COLLDATA("abdrazzaq","en",[[1,"I said to ‘Ata’: What do you think if I dip my hands into a large water vessel with a single immersion? He said: That suffices you, and the foot is likewise, but clean them."],[2,"I said to ‘Ata’: “Wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows” — is that included in what is washed? He said: Yes, there is no doubt about that."],[3,"Abu Hurayrah performed wudu’ and washed the inguinal creases. It was said to him: What do you intend by this? He said: I intend to perfect my radiance of the limbs, or he said: my loosening."],[4,"He used to wipe his head once with both palms, passing his hands forward and then back over his head once."],[5,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiped his head with his hands, moving them forward and then back. He began at the front of his head, then returned them until he came back to the place where he had begun."],[6,"He would place the palm of his right hand on the water, then not shake it off, then wipe with it from the top of his head to the forehead once, not adding to that."],[7,"He would insert his hands into the water for wudu’, then wipe with them a single wipe over the crown of the head only."],[8,"He used to wipe his head once."],[9,"He performed wudu’ and wiped his head with a single wipe."],[10,"Even if I were on the bank of the Euphrates, I would not wipe my head except once."],[11,"The Prophet ﷺ performed wudu’ and wiped his head twice."],[12,"I saw ‘Ali perform wudu’, then he took a handful of water and placed it on his head, and I saw it flowing down over all sides of his head."],[13,"The most I wipe my head is three times, not more and not less, with one hand, without making it obligatory."],[14,"If he wipes part of his head, it suffices him."],[15,"I said to Ata: How should the one with two braids wipe his head? He said: Only what is on his head from them, and he does not shave his head, nor does he wipe the ends of the hair. Then Ata placed his hand on his head and wiped the hair at its roots, and he passed his palms over what was on his head from it, and he poured his palms, and he did not return them going up against the hair. And I saw that he did not repeat the head. And I asked him about the one with long flowing hair (jumma). He said: This saying applies to both of them. And I saw Ubayd b. Umayr — and he had a jumma — and he would hold back what was over his face from it, and he did that between his ears and his head, and he would touch that which he put between his ears and his head, and he would not touch any of his jumma except what was on his head."],[16,"If a man wipes his head but does not wipe his ears, it suffices him; but if he wipes his ears and does not wipe his head, it does not suffice him."],[17,"It is enough for you to wipe your head with the leftover water on your hands from the ablution."],[18,"A man asked him, saying: I perform ablution and wash my face and forearms, so is what is on my hands enough for my head, or should I get fresh water for my head? He said: No, rather get fresh water for your head."],[19,"Ibn Umar used to get fresh water for his head."],[20,"Mamar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."],[21,"He would wipe his ears along with his face once, and he would wipe his head: he would dip his palms in the water, then wipe with them the front of his head — the crown (yafukh), then the back of the neck (qafa), then the temples (sudghayn), then wipe his ears with a single wipe — all of that with what was on his palm from that single wipe."],[22,"I said to Ata: Do you wipe your head with the leftover water from your face? He said: No, rather I dip my hands in the water and wipe with them, and I do not shake them off, nor do I wait for the water on them to dry, and I am keen to wet the hair."],[23,"The ears are part of the head."],[24,"The ears are part of the head."],[25,"The ears are part of the head."],[26,"He used to wash the backs of his ears and their inner sides, except the ear canal, along with the face once or twice. And he would insert his two fingers, after wiping his head, into the water, then insert them into the ear canal once. He said: I saw him when he was dying; he performed ablution, then inserted his two fingers into the water and tried to insert them into his ear canal, but they would not go in or reach it, until I inserted my finger into the water and then inserted them into his ear canal."],[27,"The ears are part of the head."],[28,"We used to perform ablution for Ibn Umar when he was ill, and he would command us to wipe his ears as he used to wipe."],[29,"He used to wipe his ears along with his head when performing ablution: he would insert his two fingers into the water, wipe his ears with them, then pass his thumbs behind his ears."],[30,"He would insert his hands into the ablution water and wipe with them a single wipe over the crown only, then insert his two fingers into the water, then insert them into his ears, then pass his thumbs behind his ears."],[31,"He used to wipe the ears and say: 'The ears are part of the head.'"],[32,"He used to wipe the backs of the ears and their inner sides."],[33,"Follow with your two fingers the inner sides of the ears, washing them with the leftover water from your face every time you scoop water onto your face. I said: 'What if I delay wiping them until I wipe them with the head?' He said: 'It does not harm you.'"],[34,"He performed ablution and wiped his ears, their outer and inner sides, and said: 'I saw the Prophet ﷺ do that.'"],[35,"The Prophet ﷺ wiped his ears, their outer and inner sides."],[36,"Whatever of the ears faces the face is part of the face—he said: he washes it—and their outer part is part of the head."],[37,"The ears are not part of the face, and they are not part of the head. If they were part of the head, it would be appropriate to shave the hair on them. And if they were part of the face, it would be appropriate to wash their backs and their fronts along with the face."],[38,"From where do you consider the ears? He said: From the head. He said: And I wipe them along with the face every time I pour water on my face. I said: Is it obligatory upon me to remove the dirt from the ears? He said: No."],[39,"I said to 'Ata': How does a bald person wipe? He said: He wipes his entire head, whatever has hair and whatever is bald of it; the water reaches what it reaches and misses what it misses, and he is not required to clean it thoroughly."],[40,"He was asked about a man who forgot to wipe his head until he had prayed. He said: If he wishes, he repeats the ablution and the prayer."],[41,"If you forget wiping the head and then pray, then remember, wipe your head and repeat the prayer. He said: And it reached me from him, and I do not know whether I heard him say: If there is moisture in your beard, then wipe from it."],[42,"Regarding the one who forgot wiping the head in ablution, he said: If there is moisture in his beard, let him wipe his head only, and let him repeat the prayer."],[43,"If he forgets the wiping, he wipes and repeats the prayer, and he does not repeat the ablution. And if he forgets the wiping and rain reaches his head, then it suffices him; it is purifying."],[44,"Regarding a man who forgot to rinse his nose, or wipe his ears, or rinse his mouth until he entered prayer, then remembered—should he not leave because of that? He said: But if he forgot to wipe his head and remembered while in prayer, then he leaves and wipes his head."],[45,"If he forgets wiping the head, he repeats the prayer."],[46,"Regarding a man who forgot wiping his head, then stood and said the takbir in prayer and then laughed, he said: He leaves and wipes his head, and he does not repeat the ablution because his prayer was not complete nor was his ablution complete."],[47,"If you forget wiping the head and find moisture in your beard, then wipe your head with it."],[48,"And someone other than him preferred [using] water other than that."],[49,"A man asked him, “I perform ablution and wash my face and forearms; is what is on my hands enough for the head, or should I take fresh water for my head?” He said, “Rather, take fresh water for your head.”"],[50,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib, “How does a woman wipe her head?” He said, “She removes her head covering, then wipes her head.”"],[51,"I saw Safiyya bt. Abi Ubayd performing ablution when I was a boy. When she wanted to wipe her head, she removed the head covering."],[52,"She wipes over her garments at the beginning of the day and touches water to the ends of her forelock toward the forehead."],[53,"Regarding this verse: ﴿O you who believe, when you rise to prayer, wash your faces and your hands to the elbows, and wipe your heads and your feet to the ankles﴾, they both said: “Wipe the feet.”"],[54,"Allah made two washings and two wipings obligatory. Do you not see that He mentioned dry ablution and made it take the place of the two washings as two wipings, and left the two wipings?"],[55,"Ablution is two wipings and two washings."],[56,"As for Jibril ﷺ , he came down with wiping over the feet."],[57,"I saw Ali performing ablution, and he washed the tops of his feet. He said, “Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ washing the tops of his feet, I would have thought the bottoms of the feet more deserving of washing than their tops.”"],[58,"I said to Ata, “Why should I not wipe the feet as I wipe the head, when he mentioned both of them together?” He said, “I only regard it as wiping the head and washing the feet. I heard Abu Hurayra say, ‘Woe to the heels from the Fire.’” Ata said, “Some people say it is wiping, but as for me, I wash them.”"],[59,"He returned to washing the feet in His saying: ﴿and your feet to the ankles﴾."],[60,"Wiping over the feet returned to washing in His saying: ﴿and your feet to the ankles﴾."],[61,"He mentioned to ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz the wiping over the two feet, so he said: 'It has reached me from three of the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, the least of them being your cousin al-Mughīrah b. Shuʿbah, that the Prophet ﷺ washed his two feet.'"],[62,"I saw Abū Hurayrah pass by a people performing ablution from the water container, and he said: 'Perform ablution well, may Allah have mercy on you. Have you not heard what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Woe to the heels from the Fire.'"],[63,"Woe to the heels from the Fire."],[64,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked upon us while we were performing ablution, and he said: 'Woe to the heels from the Fire.' He said: 'So we began to wash them thoroughly and rub them vigorously.'"],[65,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ washed his two feet three times, three times. Then she said to us: 'Ibn ʿAbbās entered upon me and asked me about this [hadith], and I informed him, so he said: The people refuse anything but washing, while we find in the Book of Allah, Exalted is He, the wiping.'"],[66,"When you scoop with both of your hands onto your two feet, wash the one with which you wash the sole of your foot before you put it into the water."],[67,"Pass water between your fingers before Allah passes fire between them."],[68,"A man must certainly pass water between his toes during ablution, or the Fire will certainly pass between them."],[69,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Abī Bakr performed ablution in the presence of ʿĀʾishah, and she said to him: 'Perform ablution thoroughly, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: Woe to the heels from the Fire.'"],[70,"When he washed his two feet, he would pass his fingers between his toes."],[71,"Pass water between the fingers, lest Allah stuff them with Fire."],[72,"He would pass his fingers between his toes when he performed ablution."],[73,"In his ablution he would thoroughly clean his feet, and meticulously cleanse the fingers of his hands together with the toes of his feet, and he would continue doing that until he had purified them."],[74,"Ibn Umar used to run his fingers through his toes when he performed ablution."],[75,"The Prophet ﷺ looked at a blind man performing ablution, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began saying, 'The sole of the foot,' but the blind man did not hear him. The blind man proceeded to wash the soles of his feet, and thus he was called al-Baṣīr (the seeing one)."],[76,"Ibn Umar used to wash his feet with the excess water of his ablution."],[77,"The Prophet ﷺ looked at a man with impaired sight performing ablution while he was distant from him. The Prophet ﷺ said, 'A little, a little, the soles of the feet,' so he washed the sole of the foot, and thus he was called al-Baṣīr."],[78,"I said to Ata: Regarding His saying, 'And your feet up to the ankles,' do you consider the ankles part of what must be washed of the feet? He said: Yes, there is no doubt about it."],[79,"He came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned some matters, so the Prophet ﷺ said: Perform ablution thoroughly, run your fingers through the toes, and when you rinse your nose, do it thoroughly unless you are fasting."],[80,"I set out with some companions of mine until we reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ but did not find him. Aisha fed us dates and prepared some porridge for us. Then the Prophet ﷺ came walking with a slight limp. He said: Did you feed them anything? We said: Yes. While we were in that situation, the shepherd brought the sheep into the enclosure with a newborn lamb in his hand. He said: Has it given birth? He said: Yes. He said: Then slaughter a sheep for them. Then he turned to us and said: Do not think—and he did not say 'do not think'—that we slaughtered the sheep because of you. We have one hundred sheep, and we do not want them to exceed that number. When the shepherd brings us a newborn lamb, we order him to slaughter a sheep. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me about ablution. He said: When you perform ablution, do it thoroughly, and run your fingers between the toes, and when you rinse your nose, do it thoroughly unless you are fasting. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a wife, and I mentioned her long tongue and abusive speech. He said: Divorce her. I said: O Messenger of Allah, she is a companion and has given birth. He said: Keep her and command her, for if there is any good in her, she will do it. And do not strike your wife as you would strike your slave woman."],[81,"If you dip your hand into a water channel, cleanse it and that is sufficient for you. And do not begin with your left foot before your right foot."],[82,"Regarding a man who placed his feet in a river and did not touch them with his hand, he said: It suffices him."],[83,"I said to Ata: What do you think if a person performs ablution and then steps on feces? Does he need to repeat his ablution? He said: No, but he should wash the feces off, so let him cleanse it. He said: And I say: follow this. If he steps on dung, he should rub his feet on the ground, or he said: with dust."],[84,"84. Abd al-Razzaq, from Isra'il, from al-Awwam b. Hawshab, from Ibrahim, similar to the statement of al-Shabi. Matn hidden: Regarding a man who placed his feet in a river and did not touch them with his hand, he said: It suffices him."],[85,"If a man steps in human excrement up to his ankles, he only has to wash his feet."],[86,"About a man whose body is touched by urine or blood while he is in a state of wudu, he said: He washes the trace of urine and blood and does not repeat wudu."],[87,"If you step on something foul and wet, wash it; but if it is dry, there is no harm."],[88,"I said to Ata: What do you think if I step on dry dung, should I wash the sole of my foot? He said: No. I said: What about my palm and face if any of that dry dung touches them? He said: No, by my life, when we went up with a funeral procession, the wind would blow dry dung onto our faces, and we did not perform wudu or wash our faces or anything of that."],[89,"We used to enter upon Abu al-Aliyah al-Riyahi and perform wudu, and he would say: Do you not perform wudu in your dwellings? We said: Yes, but we step in filth. He said: There is no wudu upon you. Shall I tell you of something more severe than that? The wind blows it onto your heads and beards."],[90,"If a man steps on dry dung, there is no wudu upon him, and if he touches a dog, there is no wudu upon him."],[91,"I said to Ata: Should I sprinkle water on my garment if that touches it? He said: No."],[92,"One day we went out with Ibn al-Musayyib to a mosque, and the ground had been rained on and there was mud. When we reached the mosque door, a man from the group washed his feet. Ibn al-Musayyib said to him: Had you not performed wudu in your dwelling? He said: Yes, but we passed through this mud. He said: There is no wudu upon you."],[93,"He saw al-Hasan walking in the mud. He said: The mud does not reach the top of the feet but fills the soles. When he reached the mosque door, he wiped the bottoms of his feet on the ground, then entered the mosque without washing them."],[94,"About a man whose body is touched by urine and blood while he is in a state of wudu, he said: He washes the trace of blood and urine and does not repeat wudu."],[95,"I saw Ibn Umar at Mina perform wudu, then go out barefoot and step on whatever he stepped on, then enter the mosque and pray without performing wudu again."],[96,"Alqamah and al-Aswad used to wade through water and mud in the rain, then enter the mosque and pray."],[97,"I saw Yahya b. Waththab, and Abd Allah b. Ayyash, and others among the companions of Abd Allah, wading through water that had been mixed with dung and urine. When they reached the door of the mosque, they did no more than shake off their feet, then they entered the prayer."],[98,"A man asked Abd Allah b. al-Zubayr about the mud of rain. He said: You ask me about two purifiers together. Allah said: ‘And We sent down from the sky blessed water’ (Q 50:9), and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘The earth has been made for me a mosque and a purifier.’"],[99,"Ablution is from ritual impurity, not from what is stepped on."],[100,"Ablution is from what comes out, not from what goes in, and one does not perform ablution from a place tread upon."],[101,"We used not to perform ablution from a place tread upon."],[102,"We used not to perform ablution from a place tread upon, nor to uncover a veil, nor to tie back hair."],[103,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us to uncover a veil, or tie back hair, or make a new ablution."],[104,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man who steps in impurity with his sandals. He said: ‘Dust is a purifier for them.’"],[105,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, we have a foul path in the rain. The Prophet ﷺ said: ‘Is there not beyond it a good path?’ I said: Yes. He said: ‘Then that for that.’"],[106,"A woman asked Aishah about a woman who drags the hem of her garment when she goes out to the mosque and it touches an impure place. She said: ‘Then she passes over a pure place, and it purifies it.’"],[107,"I heard Ibn Abbas on Friday on this pulpit on a rainy day saying: Pray in your dwellings, and do not come bringing filth, carrying it with your feet into the mosque. For not every water jar of the mosque is sufficient for your purification."],[108,"I carry water with me on a rainy day until I come to the door of the mosque and wash them there."],[109,"Regarding a man who performed ablution then his foot sank into filth and he found no water, he said: He should perform tayammum. He is in the position of a man who has not completed his ablution. He said: If something affects any of the parts of ablution or tayammum, he should wipe it with dust, and that takes the place of water."],[110,"A person asked ʿAṭāʾ. He said: I asked: I touched my sandal during prayer and my hand fell upon something foul. Should I repeat my prayer? He said: No."],[111,"Regarding a man who prayed while there was filth in his leather socks? He said: He should repeat."],[112,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I missed one of my feet—or forgot it until I remembered later—and I did not do anything about that. He said: Wash what you missed and do not start the ablution over from the beginning."],[113,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I forgot a small part of the ablution limbs of the body. He said: Touch it with water."],[114,"Whoever forgets something of his limbs in ablution should not repeat the ablution, whether the ablution has dried or not, but he should wash what he left out and repeat the prayer."],[115,"Whoever leaves out any of the places of ablution should repeat: he should wash what he left out, then repeat the prayer even if it was the size of a hair."],[116,"Whatever water touches of the places of purification, that place has become pure."],[117,"Whoever forgets something of the limbs of his ablution: if his ablution has not dried, he should wash what he left out, but if it has dried, he should repeat the ablution and the prayer within the time."],[118,"He saw a man praying who had left a spot the size of a fingernail on his feet, so he ordered him to repeat the ablution and the prayer."],[119,"I entered upon al-Rubayyiʿ bt. ʿAfrāʾ, and she said: Who are you? He said: I said: I am ʿAbd Allāh b. Muḥammad b. ʿAqīl b. Abī Ṭālib. She said: Who is your mother? I said: Rayṭa bt. ʿAlī—or: So-and-so daughter of ʿAlī b. Abī Ṭālib. She said: Welcome, O son of my sister. I said: I have come to ask you about the ablution of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to keep ties with us and visit us, and he used to perform ablution in this vessel—or: in a vessel like this one—and it was about a mudd. She said: He used to wash his hands, rinse his mouth, and sniff water into his nostrils, then he washed his face three times, then he washed his hands three times each, then he wiped his head twice, and wiped his ears, their outside and inside, and washed his feet three times."],[120,"He performed ablution three times each, then wiped his head, then drank the leftover water of his ablution, then said: Whoever would like to look at the ablution of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, let him look at this."],[121,"I witnessed 'Ali in al-Rahabah: he urinated, then performed ablution and washed his palms three times, rinsed his mouth and sniffed water and blew it out three times, washed his face three times, and his forearms three times each, wiped his head, and washed his feet three times. Then he stood up straight, took the leftover water of his ablution and drank it, and said: 'I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do just as you have seen me do, and I loved to show you.'"],[122,"That 'Ali in al-Kufah said to his servant: 'O Qanbar, bring me water for ablution.' So he brought it to him—al-Mughirah said, on the authority of 'Abd al-Karim: in a large wooden bowl—and he began by washing his hands before putting them into the ablution water three times, then rinsed his mouth three times and sniffed water and blew it out three times, then washed his face three times, then washed his right hand up to the elbow three times, and the left likewise. Then he scooped one handful of water with one of his hands onto his head and wiped with it—he said: in summer—as if he scooped it because of the heat. He said: then he washed his right foot up to the ankles three times, then the left likewise. Then he stood upright and drank from the leftover water of his ablution, and said: 'Whoever would like to look at the ablution of the Messenger of God ﷺ, this is how he should perform ablution.' He said: and they hold that the Prophet ﷺ drank the leftover water of his ablution standing, just as 'Ali did. Then he prayed the prescribed prayer. He said: then he did not leave his seat until he called Qanbar for water for the prayer, then scooped one handful, rinsed his mouth from it and sniffed water and blew it out, and wiped his face, forearms, head, and feet from that handful with a single wipe for each limb, dividing it: he rinsed his mouth and sniffed water and blew it out, and wiped his face, forearms, and head once, then said: 'This is the ablution of one who has not broken his state of purity,' meaning: if he wishes he may perform ablution, and if he wishes he may not."],[123,"'Ali called for water for ablution, and it was brought near to him. He washed his palms three times before putting them into his ablution water, then rinsed his mouth three times, sniffed water three times, then washed his face three times, then washed his right hand up to the elbow three times, then the left likewise, then wiped his head with one wipe, then washed his right foot up to the ankles three times, then the left likewise. Then he stood upright and said to me: 'Hand it to me.' So I handed him the vessel containing the leftover water of his ablution, and he drank from the leftover water of his ablution standing. I was astonished, and when he saw my astonishment, he said: 'Do not be astonished, for I saw your father, the Prophet ﷺ, do the same as you have seen me do,' meaning with this ablution of his and his drinking of the leftover water of his ablution standing."],[124,"That he rinsed his mouth three times, sniffed water and blew it out three times, then poured water over his face three times, and over his hands three times, then washed his feet three times each, then said: 'Thus did the Prophet ﷺ perform ablution.' He said: I was not certain about it from 'Uthman, so I did not add to it nor subtract."],[125,"I saw 'Uthman b. 'Affan perform ablution: he washed his palms three times each, rinsed his mouth, sniffed water and blew it out, washed his face three times—he said: and I think he said—and his forearms three times each, then wiped his head and his ears, their outside and inside, and washed his feet three times each, and ran his fingers between his fingers and through his beard when he washed his face before washing his feet, then said: 'I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do just as you have seen me do.'"],[126,"That he performed ablution, washing each limb once, then mentioned that the Prophet ﷺ used to do it."],[127,"That the Messenger of God ﷺ performed ablution once, once."],[128,"Shall I inform you of the ablution of the Messenger of God ﷺ? He scooped with his right hand, then poured it over the left with a single pour."],[129,"That the Messenger of God ﷺ performed ablution twice: once, and three times."],[130,"That he was asked about three scoops in ablution, and he said: 'Whoever performs ablution well, one scoop suffices him.'"],[131,"He performed ablution once, once."],[132,"One time suffices if he completes the ablution thoroughly."],[133,"If a man should perform ablution a single time and complete it well in that one time, it would suffice him."],[134,"Once suffices, and twice suffices."],[135,"He saw Umar b. al-Khattab performing ablution twice."],[136,"He saw Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ performing ablution twice, twice."],[137,"I would help Ibn Umar with his ablution sometimes twice, and sometimes three times."],[138,"A man said to Abd Allah b. Zayd, who was one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ: Can you show me how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution? He said: Yes. So Abd Allah b. Zayd called for water for ablution and poured it over his hands and washed them twice, then rinsed his mouth and snuffed water into his nose and blew it out three times, and washed his face three times, then washed his hands up to the elbows twice, then wiped his head with his hands, bringing them forward and back; he began with the front of his head, then took them to the back of his neck, then returned them until he came back to the place where he began, then washed his feet."],[139,"I saw Uthman b. Affan perform ablution. He poured water over his hands three times and washed them, then rinsed his mouth and snuffed water into his nose, then washed his face three times, then washed his right hand up to the elbow three times, then washed the left one likewise, then wiped his head, then washed his right foot three times, then the left one three times likewise. Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ablution like this ablution of mine. Then he said: Whoever performs ablution like this ablution of mine, then prays two rak'ahs without talking to himself during them, his previous sins will be forgiven."],[140,"Uthman performed ablution and poured water over his hands three times, and snuffed water into his nose and blew it out three times, and rinsed his mouth three times, and washed his face three times, and washed his right hand up to the elbow three times, and washed the left one likewise, then wiped his head, then washed his right foot three times, then the left one likewise. Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ablution like this ablution of mine. Then he said: Whoever performs ablution like this ablution of mine, then stands and prays two rak'ahs without talking to himself during them, his previous sins will be forgiven."],[141,"Uthman sat in the sitting places and called for water for ablution and performed ablution. Then he said: By Allah, I shall relate to you a hadith that were it not for a verse in the Book of Allah I would not relate to you. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: No man performs ablution and does it well except that he is forgiven for what is between him and the next prayer until he prays it. He said: I heard it from him."],[142,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and we took turns tending the camels. One day I came while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering a sermon, and part of his speech had preceded me, so I sat down next to Umar b. al-Khattab. I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: Whoever performs ablution and perfects it, then stands to pray and prays a prayer knowing what he says in it until he finishes his prayer, will be as he was on the day his mother gave birth to him. He said: I said: Excellent, excellent. Umar b. al-Khattab said: He has just said something even better than this. He said: Whoever performs ablution and perfects it, then stands and prays a prayer knowing what he says in it, until he finishes his prayer, then says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, eight gates of Paradise will be opened for him and he may enter through whichever of them he wishes."],[143,"We burn ourselves (with sins), and when we pray the prescribed prayer, the prayer expiates what came before it; then we burn ourselves again, and when we pray, the prayer expiates what came before it."],[144,"When a believing servant stands for prayer, his sins are placed upon his head, and he does not finish his prayer until they separate from him as clusters of dates fall from a palm tree, scattering right and left."],[145,"No crier from the people of the earth calls to prayer until a crier from the people of heaven calls: 'Arise, O children of Adam, and extinguish your fires.' He said: Then the muezzin stands and gives the call to prayer, then the people rise for prayer."],[146,"A wen—meaning a pustule—appeared on Adam's neck. He prayed one prayer and it descended to his chest, then he prayed one prayer and it descended to his loins, then he prayed one prayer and it descended to his palm, then he prayed one prayer and it descended to his thumb, then he prayed one prayer and it disappeared."],[147,"Prayers are expiations for what occurs between them as long as major sins are avoided."],[148,"He spent the night at Salman's observing his diligence. He said: He got up and prayed in the last part of the night, as if he did not see what he had expected. He mentioned that to him, and Salman said: Maintain these five prayers, for they are expiations for these wounds as long as no fatal blow is struck. When evening comes, people are in three categories: some have credit and no debit, some have debit and no credit, and some have neither credit nor debit. A man who seizes the darkness of night and the people's heedlessness, then stands praying until morning—that one has credit and no debit. A man who seizes the people's heedlessness and the darkness of night, then plunges headlong into sins—that one has debit and no credit. And a man who prays 'Isha then sleeps—that one has neither credit nor debit. Beware of overexertion; stick to moderation and consistency."],[149,"A man's prayer in his house is light. When a man stands for prayer, his sins are hung above him, and he does not prostrate a single prostration except that God expiates a sin from him by it."],[150,"For the praying person are three qualities: mercy scatters upon him from his feet to the canopy of the sky, angels surround him from his horn to the sky's regions, and a crier calls: If the one in intimate prayer knew whom he is addressing, he would not turn away."],[151,"Ata said to me: When he rinses his mouth, what comes out of his mouth are sins; when he sniffs water, what comes out of his nose are sins; when he washes his face, what comes out of it are sins; when he washes his hands, what falls from them are sins; when he wipes his head, what falls from it of impurities are sins; and when he washes his feet, what falls from them are sins—until he returns as on the day his mother bore him, except for a major sin."],[152,"A freedwoman of his called Umm Hashim seated him behind the curtain with an inkpot and a pen, and sent to Abu Umamah asking him about a hadith he narrated from the Messenger of God ﷺ regarding ablution. He said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: Whoever stands for ablution and washes his hands, sins depart from his hands; when he rinses his mouth, sins depart from his mouth; when he sniffs water, they depart from his nose; and so on until he washes his feet. If he goes out for an obligatory prayer, it is like an accepted Hajj, and if he goes out for a voluntary prayer, it is like an accepted 'Umrah."],[153,"O Messenger of God, which part of the night is best? He said: The last portion of the night. He said: Then prayer is accepted until the dawn prayer, then no prayer until sunrise, then prayer is accepted until the 'Asr prayer, then no prayer until the sun sets. He said: I said: O Messenger of God, how is the night prayer? He said: Two by two. I said: How is the daytime prayer? He said: Four by four. He said: And whoever sends one blessing upon me, God records for him a qirat, and the qirat is like Uhud. When a servant stands to perform ablution and washes his palms, his sins depart from his palms; then when he rinses his mouth and sniffs water, his sins depart from his nostrils; then when he washes his face, his sins depart from his face, his hearing, and his sight; then when he washes his forearms, his sins depart from his forearms; then when he wipes his head, his sins depart from his head; then when he washes his feet, his sins depart from his feet; then when he stands for prayer, he comes out of his sins as on the day his mother bore him."],[154,"He was sitting with companions of his when a man said to him: Who will narrate to us a hadith from the Messenger of God ﷺ? 'Amr said: I will. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: I heard him say: Whoever grows a grey hair in the path of God, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of God, it is for him like freeing a slave. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever frees a soul, God frees for every limb of it a limb of him from the Fire. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever frees two souls, God frees for every two limbs of them two limbs of him from the Fire. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And a hadith that if I had not heard it from him once or twice or three or four or five times, I would not narrate it to you: There is no Muslim servant who performs ablution and washes his face except that the sins of his face fall from the edges of his beard; when he washes his hands, the sins of his hands fall from between his fingernails; when he wipes his head, the sins of his head fall from the ends of his hair; when he washes his feet, the sins of his feet fall from their soles. If he goes to a mosque and prays in congregation there, his reward falls upon God; and if he stands and prays two rak'ahs, they are an expiation. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful; narrate and do not err."],[155,"He was sitting with companions of his when a man said to him: Who will narrate to us a hadith from the Messenger of God ﷺ? 'Amr said: I will. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: I heard him say: Whoever grows a grey hair in the path of God, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of God, it is for him like freeing a slave. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever frees a soul, God frees for every limb of it a limb of him from the Fire. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever frees two souls, God frees for every two limbs of them two limbs of him from the Fire. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And a hadith that if I had not heard it from him once or twice or three or four or five times, I would not narrate it to you: There is no Muslim servant who performs ablution and washes his face except that the sins of his face fall from the edges of his beard; when he washes his hands, the sins of his hands fall from between his fingernails; when he wipes his head, the sins of his head fall from the ends of his hair; when he washes his feet, the sins of his feet fall from their soles. If he goes to a mosque and prays in congregation there, his reward falls upon God; and if he stands and prays two rak'ahs, they are an expiation. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful; narrate and do not err."],[157,"He was sitting with companions of his when a man said to him: Who will narrate to us a hadith from the Messenger of God ﷺ? 'Amr said: I will. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: I heard him say: Whoever grows a grey hair in the path of God, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of God, it is for him like freeing a slave. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever frees a soul, God frees for every limb of it a limb of him from the Fire. He said: And I heard him say: Whoever frees two souls, God frees for every two limbs of them two limbs of him from the Fire. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful. He said: And a hadith that if I had not heard it from him once or twice or three or four or five times, I would not narrate it to you: There is no Muslim servant who performs ablution and washes his face except that the sins of his face fall from the edges of his beard; when he washes his hands, the sins of his hands fall from between his fingernails; when he wipes his head, the sins of his head fall from the ends of his hair; when he washes his feet, the sins of his feet fall from their soles. If he goes to a mosque and prays in congregation there, his reward falls upon God; and if he stands and prays two rak'ahs, they are an expiation. He said: Go on, may your father be for God, and be careful; narrate and do not err."],[159,"When the servant rinses his mouth, every sin he spoke with the water departs when it leaves his mouth; when he washes his face, every sin in his face departs with the water dripping from his face; when he washes his hands, the sins from his hands depart with the water dripping from his hands; and when he washes his feet, the sins from his feet depart when he washes them. Then when he leaves his house for the mosque, a sin is erased for every step and a good deed is added until he enters the mosque."],[160,"There is no servant who performs ablution and does it well, making the water flow over his face, then washes his forearms making the water flow over his elbows, then washes his feet making the water flow from his heels, then prays and does his prayer well, except that he is forgiven for what has passed."],[161,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to perform ablution for every prayer until the Day of the Conquest, when he prayed Zuhr, Asr, and Maghrib with a single ablution."],[162,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed the prayers with a single ablution and wiped over his leather socks. Umar said to him, \"O Messenger of God, you have done something you did not used to do?\" He said, \"I did it deliberately, O Umar.\""],[163,"We were with Abu Musa al-Ashari in an army on the bank of the Tigris when the prayer time arrived. His caller announced the Zuhr prayer, so the people got up to perform ablution, performed it, and he led them in prayer. Then they sat in circles. When the Asr time arrived, the Asr caller announced it, and the people arose once more to perform ablution. He ordered his caller, who announced: \"Indeed, no ablution is required except for one who has invalidated his state of purity. Knowledge is about to disappear and ignorance will prevail, to the point that a man will strike his mother with a sword out of ignorance.\""],[164,"I do not mind praying five prayers all with a single ablution, as long as I am not resisting defecation or urination."],[165,"I do not mind praying five prayers all with a single ablution, as long as I am not resisting defecation or urination."],[166,"One of us would be sufficed by ablution as long as he did not invalidate it."],[167,"I would pray Zuhr, Asr, and Maghrib with a single ablution as long as I did not invalidate it or say something objectionable."],[168,"No one's ablution is valid for more than a day and a night's prayers, whether he invalidates it or not, and whether he wipes or not. He said: And I heard Wahb say: I would pray Zuhr with the ablution of Isha."],[169,"Is ablution for every prayer? He said: No. I said: But He says: {When you rise to prayer}. He said: The first ablution suffices you. If I performed ablution for the morning prayer, I would pray all the prayers with it as long as I did not invalidate it. I said: Is it recommended to perform ablution for every prayer? He said: No."],[170,"Al-Aswad b. Yazid used to perform ablution with a bowl [of water] enough to quench a man's thirst, then pray all the prayers with that ablution as long as he did not invalidate it."],[171,"Al-Miswar b. Makhrama said to Ibn Abbas: Do you have any dispute with Ubayd b. Umayr? When he hears the call to prayer, he goes out and performs ablution. Ibn Abbas said: This is what Satan does. When he comes, inform me. So when he came, they informed him, and he said: What makes you do what you do? He said: God says: {When you rise to prayer, wash your faces}, and he recited the verse. Ibn Abbas said: It is not like that. When you perform ablution, you are pure as long as you do not invalidate it."],[172,"He used to perform ablution for every prayer."],[173,"He used to rinse his mouth and sniff water into his nose for every prayer."],[174,"He used to perform ablution for every prayer."],[175,"Abd Allah b. Umar disliked performing ablution in copper vessels. He said: The nuḍār (a type of gold) and rikāʾ (a type of silver) and a copper basin were brought to him."],[176,"He would not perform ablution in a vessel of ṣufr (brass or yellow copper)."],[177,"Ibn Umar would wash his feet in a copper basin. He said: And he disliked drinking from a cup of ṣufr."],[178,"The dislike of Ibn Umar regarding copper was mentioned to him. He said: Ablution in copper? Nothing is disliked about copper except due to its smell."],[179,"He used to perform ablution in copper vessels."],[180,"He disliked performing ablution in copper."],[181,"He used to wash his head in a copper pail belonging to one of his wives."],[182,"I asked Abd Allah b. Umar about ablution in copper. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to wash his head in a copper pail belonging to Zaynab bt. Jaḥsh. A man from the family of Jaḥsh who was present with us said: Yes, that was indeed a washing tub that belonged to us."],[183,"Pour upon me from seven waterskins whose mouths have not been untied, so that I may make a covenant with the people. Aisha said: So we seated him in a copper washing tub belonging to Hafsa, and we poured water upon him from them until he began gesturing to us that you have done enough, then he went out."],[184,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What if I am compelled during travel to use water in a container made from an untanned dead animal's skin, or water containing a dead mouse, and I have no other water — would you prefer that I purify myself with that water or with earth? He said: It is more preferable to me than earth. I said: Should we leave it in the depression? He said: Yes. I said: Suppose I performed ablution with it in the depression without knowing, then prayed the prescribed prayer, and then learned before that prayer time expired? He said: Repeat the ablution and repeat the prayer. I said: What if I learned after the time had passed? He said: Do not repeat it."],[186,"Umar b. al-Khattab went out to relieve himself in Ajyad. Then he returned and asked for water for ablution, but they did not give him any. Umm Mahzul, who was one of the nine prostitutes in the pre-Islamic era, said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, here is water, but it is in a wooden bowl, and the bowl has not been tanned.' Umar said to Khalid b. Tuhayl: 'Is it she?' He said: 'Yes.' Then he said: 'Bring it, for Allah has made water a means of purification.'"],[187,"A person asked Ata: 'May I drink and perform ablution from water that is in a container that has not been tanned?' He said: 'Is it ritually slaughtered?' He said: 'Yes, and it is not carrion.' He said: 'There is no harm in that.'"],[188,"Tanning hides is their ritual slaughter."],[189,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a sheep belonging to a freed slave woman of Maymunah and said: 'Why did you not make use of its hide?' They said: 'How can we, when it is dead, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'Only its flesh is forbidden.'"],[190,"Al-Zuhri used to reject tanning and would say: 'It may be used in any case.'"],[191,"When the hide of a dead animal is tanned, then it is sufficient; let it be used."],[192,"There was a domestic sheep belonging to one of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ, and it died. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Why did you not make use of its hide?'"],[193,"A sheep died, and the Prophet ﷺ said: 'Why did you not tan its hide?'"],[194,"The Prophet ﷺ asked for water for ablution, and it was said to him: 'We find none for you except in the skin of a dead animal.' He said: 'Did you tan it?' They said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Bring it, for that is purifying.'"],[195,"We raid the people of the East, and we are brought hides and water-skins. He said: 'I do not know what to say to you, except that I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Any hide that is tanned has become pure.'"],[196,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded that the hides of dead animals be used when they are tanned."],[197,"He was asked about a dead animal, and he said: 'Its purification is its tanning.'"],[198,"The Prophet ﷺ asked for water, and a waterskin was brought to him. It was said, \"It is from a dead animal,\" and they mentioned tanning. He said: So the Prophet ﷺ drank from it."],[199,"I asked him about a man who owns camels, cattle, and sheep, and they die; then their hides are tanned. He said: He may sell them or wear them."],[200,"He forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be spread as bedding."],[201,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May a man sell the hides of a dead sheep before they are tanned? He said: I would not like you to consume its price, even if it is tanned."],[202,"He says regarding the hides of dead animals: Their purification is their tanning. He said: And al-Ḥasan used to say: They may be used but not sold."],[203,"The ritual slaughter of hides is their tanning, so wear them."],[204,"Muḥammad b. al-Ashʿath spoke to ʿĀʾishah about making a fur blanket for her. She said: It is from a dead animal, and I will not wear anything from a dead animal. He said: We will make a tanned blanket for you, but she disliked wearing anything from a dead animal."],[205,"I heard ʿAṭāʾ being asked about the offspring of sheep that are extracted from their mothers' wombs dead, and their skins are made into furs. He said: Are they tanned? He said: Yes. He said: That suffices; wear them."],[206,"We do not make use of anything from a dead animal except its hides; when they are tanned, their tanning is their purification and their ritual slaughter."],[207,"The letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was read to us in the land of Juhaynah while I was a young boy: Do not make use of anything from a dead animal, neither hide nor sinew."],[208,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What if I am compelled during travel to use water in a container made from an untanned dead animal's skin, or water containing a dead mouse, and I have no other water — would you prefer that I purify myself with that water or with earth? He said: It is more preferable to me than earth. I said: Should we leave it in the depression? He said: Yes. I said: Suppose I performed ablution with it in the depression without knowing, then prayed the prescribed prayer, and then learned before that prayer time expired? He said: Repeat the ablution and repeat the prayer. I said: What if I learned after the time had passed? He said: Do not repeat it."],[209,"The wool of a dead animal has no ritual slaughter; wash it and make use of it. Al-Thawrī said: Do you not see that we shear it while it is alive?"],[210,"Wool, goat hair, shorn wool, and lamb's wool: there is no harm in them, nor in the feathers of a dead animal."],[211,"There is no harm in the wool of a dead animal, but it should be washed; and there is no harm in the feathers of a dead animal."],[212,"A person asked ʿAṭāʾ about the wool of a dead animal, and he disliked it and said: \"I have not heard that it is permitted except for its hide when it is tanned.\""],[213,"They mentioned that the fats of dead animals are used for lamps, and ships are greased with them, but they should not be touched. He said: \"It is taken with a stick.\" I said: \"Should things other than ships be greased with it, such as leather or something that is touched?\" He said: \"I do not know.\" I said: \"Which part of ships is greased?\" He said: \"Their exteriors, but their interiors are not greased.\" I said: \"But he must touch its fat with his hand when putting it in the lamp?\" He said: \"Then let him wash his hand if he touches it.\""],[214,"Abū had a comb and a measure of elephant bones."],[215,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"The bones of the elephant—for they claimed that the anṣāb are its bones, and it is a dead animal.\" He said: \"No use is to be made of them.\" I said: \"And likewise the bones of livestock that die?\" He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"Should anything be put in the bones of a dead animal to hold something?\" He said: \"No.\""],[216,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"The animal from which ivory combs are made is taken dead, and bracelets are made from it, for it is not slaughtered.\" He said: \"No.\" Then I reminded him and said: \"It is a sea creature from what the sea casts up.\" He said: \"Then it is among what the sea casts up.\""],[217,"There is no harm that men and women perform wudu together. They are only your counterparts, your sisters, your daughters, and your mothers."],[218,"He did not see any harm in trading in ivory."],[219,"267 267 - Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Layth ............ nothing makes it impure."],[221,"I saw under the pillow of Ṭāwūs on his bed a knife whose handle was of ivory. He said: \"And he had seen it when I lifted the pillow.\""],[222,"Abū had a comb and a measure of elephant bones."],[223,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be spread as bedding."],[224,"He forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be used as floor coverings or worn."],[225,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade the skins of beasts of prey from being ridden upon."],[226,"I was informed from Ḥabīb b. Abī Thābit, from ʿĀṣim b. Ḍamrah, the like of it. [A mount was brought to ʿAlī, and on it was a saddle with silk brocade. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade us from silk brocade, from riding on it, from sitting on it; and from leopard skins, from riding on them, from sitting on them; and from spoils of war being sold until they were divided into fifths; and from pregnant captives of the enemy being given intercourse; and from domestic donkeys; and from eating any fanged beast of prey, and eating any taloned bird; and from the price of wine, the price of carrion, the stud fee of a stallion, and the price of a dog.]"],[227,"I was informed from Ḥabīb b. Abī Thābit, from ʿĀṣim b. Ḍamrah, the like of it. [A mount was brought to ʿAlī, and on it was a saddle with silk brocade. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade us from silk brocade, from riding on it, from sitting on it; and from leopard skins, from riding on them, from sitting on them; and from spoils of war being sold until they were divided into fifths; and from pregnant captives of the enemy being given intercourse; and from domestic donkeys; and from eating any fanged beast of prey, and eating any taloned bird; and from the price of wine, the price of carrion, the stud fee of a stallion, and the price of a dog.]"],[228,"He forbade riding on a leopard skin."],[229,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade the skins of beasts of prey from being ridden upon."],[230,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade the skins of beasts of prey from being ridden upon."],[231,"He forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be used as floor coverings or worn."],[232,"He had a sanjawiyya [a garment dyed the color of the sky, then placed over a fur of foxes] made of fox furs, and he would wear it. When he wanted to pray he would take it off."],[233,"I saw on Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī a cap containing fox fur."],[234,"A man was wearing a cap of fox fur before ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, so he ordered it and it was ripped open."],[235,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb saw a man wearing a cap containing cat skins. He took it and tore it, saying: I think it is nothing but carrion."],[236,"I asked ʿAbīda about cat skins, and he disliked them, even if tanned."],[237,"There is no harm in the hides of beasts of prey: they may be sold, ridden upon, and spread."],[238,"He saw al-Sha'bi sitting on a lion skin."],[239,"I asked al-Zuhri about the skins of tigers, and he granted a dispensation for them, saying: The Messenger of God ﷺ granted a dispensation for the skins of dead animals."],[240,"There is no harm in the hides of beasts of prey when they are tanned. He says: The Prophet ﷺ granted a dispensation for the skins of dead animals."],[241,"233 'Abd al-Razzaq said: I myself heard Ibrahim and others mention from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir. There is no harm in the hides of beasts of prey when they are tanned. He says: The Prophet ﷺ granted a dispensation for the skins of dead animals."],[242,"Ibn Sirin used to ride on a saddle with a tiger skin on it. He said: And 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz used to ride on it."],[243,"That his father never had a saddle except with a tiger skin on it."],[244,"He asked al-Hasan about them, and he said: There is no harm in them. They were ridden in the time of 'Umar b. al-Khattab."],[245,"I asked 'Ata' about the ablution basin at the gate of the mosque, and he said: There is no harm in it. It existed in the time of Ibn 'Abbas, and he was the one who set it up, knowing that men and women, black and red, would perform ablution from it. He saw no harm in it, and if there had been harm, he would have forbidden it. He said: Would you perform ablution from it? I said: Yes."],[246,"I saw a person uncovered, exposed over the basin, scooping water with his hand over his private part. He said: Perform ablution, for there is nothing against you. Religion is accommodating. The Prophet ﷺ used to say: Be accommodating and it will be accommodated to you. Those who came before did not scrutinize this or press hard about it."],[247,"A man said: O Messenger of God, a new sealed clay jar is dearer to you to perform ablution from, or from what people perform ablution from is dearer? He said: The religion most beloved to God is the tolerant one (al-hanifiyya). It was said: And what is al-hanifiyya? He said: The accommodating one. He said: The spacious Islam."],[248,"Your ablution basins are dearer to me than an old woman's sealed clay jar."],[249,"Your ablution basins are dearer to me than an old woman's sealed clay jar."],[250,"I saw Jarir b. Abd Allah performing wudu from a water basin."],[251,"The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ used to perform wudu from a stone trough basan."],[252,"I said to al-Shabi: Is an old covered water skin more beloved to you that wudu be performed from it, or from these basins into which the butcher puts his hand? He said: No, rather from these basins into which the butcher puts his hand."],[253,"244 243 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from a man, from Muhammad b. Wasi, from the Prophet ﷺ something similar, except that he did not mention the tolerant Hanifiyya. Hidden text: A man said: 'O Messenger of God, is a new covered jar more beloved to you that you perform wudu from it, or from that which people perform wudu with? He said: The most beloved of religions to God is the Hanifiyya.'"],[254,"There is no harm that men and women perform wudu together. They are only your counterparts, your sisters, your daughters, and your mothers."],[255,"We used to perform wudu, we and the women, together."],[256,"I saw Umar b. al-Khattab come to basins at which men and women were all performing wudu, and he struck them with the whip. Then he said to the owner of the basin: 'Make basins for men and basins for women.' Then he met Ali and said: 'What do you think?' He said: 'I think you are only a shepherd. If you strike them for anything other than that, you have perished and caused others to perish.'"],[257,"Umar b. al-Khattab came to some water and it was said to him: 'Dogs and beasts of prey lap from it.' He said: 'They have taken away in their bellies what they lapped.'"],[258,"249 248 - ....... It was said: 'The dog lapped in the basin of Majanna.' He said: 'Did it lap except with its tongue?' So he drank from it and drew water. He said: 'And Majanna is the name of a basin.'"],[259,"Umar b. al-Khattab came to the basin of Majanna, and it was said to him: 'O Commander of the Believers, the dog just lapped in it.' He said: 'It only lapped with its tongue, so drink from it and perform wudu.'"],[260,"He was with Umar b. al-Khattab in a riding party in which Amr b. al-As was also present. They stopped at a basin, and Amr said: 'O owner of the basin, do beasts of prey come to your basin?' Umar said: 'O owner of the basin, do not inform us, for we come to the beasts of prey and they come to us.'"],[261,"252 251 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said b. Abi Said, something similar. Hidden text: He was with Umar b. al-Khattab in a riding party in which Amr b. al-As was also present. They stopped at a basin, and Amr said: 'O owner of the basin, do beasts of prey come to your basin?' Umar said: 'O owner of the basin, do not inform us, for we come to the beasts of prey and they come to us.'"],[262,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution with water left over by beasts of prey."],[263,"I was informed that the Prophet ﷺ came with Abu Bakr and Umar to a water basin. The people of the water came out and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, dogs and beasts of prey lap from this basin.' He said: 'They have what they carried in their bellies, and for us what remains is drink and purifying.'"],[264,"Someone sought water for Umar's ablution and found it only with a Christian woman. He asked her for it and brought it to Umar. Its beauty pleased him, so Umar said: 'Where is this from?' He said to him: 'From this Christian woman.' He performed ablution, then went in to her and said to her: 'Accept Islam.' She uncovered her head, and it was like white cotton-grass. She said: 'After such an age?'"],[265,"The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution — or drank — from a pool into which dog meat was thrown. He said: 'And I do not think but that he said: and carcasses.' That was mentioned to him, and he said to him: 'Water is not made impure by anything.'"],[266,"Water purifies and is not purified."],[267,"Water is not made impure by anything, and water is purifying."],[268,"When water amounts to two qullahs, it does not carry impurity or harm."],[269,"Ibn Abbas passed by a pool containing a carcass. He ordered it removed, then performed ablution from it."],[270,"When water is a dhanub or two dhanubs, nothing makes it impure. I said to him: 'What is a dhanub?' He said: 'A bucket.'"],[271,"When water and blood mix, the water is purifying."],[272,"If any blood drips into water, pour out a jug or two from it; but if the water is little, enough only for ablution, then pour it all out."],[273,"Nothing makes water impure except what changes its smell or taste, or what overcomes its smell and taste."],[274,"Indeed water is never made impure by anything; it purifies and nothing purifies it. He ﷺ said: \"And We sent down from the sky purifying water\" (Q 25:48)."],[275,"If the water is two qullahs, nothing makes it impure."],[276,"267 267 - Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Layth ............ nothing makes it impure."],[277,"If the water is a kurr, nothing makes it impure. The kurr is forty gold (coins)."],[278,"I asked al-Zuhri about a chicken that fell into a well and died. He said: There is no harm in performing ablution from it and drinking, unless it putrefies until the smell of its stench is found in the water, then it should be drained."],[279,"I asked al-Zuhri about a mouse that fell into a well. He said: If it is taken out immediately, there is no harm; but if it dies in it, it should be drained."],[280,"If an animal dies in a well, water is taken from it; but if it disintegrates in it, it should be drained."],[281,"If an animal dies in a well, forty buckets are drawn from it."],[282,"If a mouse falls into a well and disintegrates, seven buckets are drawn from it. If the mouse is in its original state and has not disintegrated, a bucket or two is drawn from it. If it is putrid, more than that, then let enough be drawn from the well to remove the smell."],[283,"If a dog falls into a well and is taken out when it falls, twenty buckets are drawn from it. If it is taken out when it has died, sixty or seventy buckets are drawn from it. If it disintegrates in it, its water is drained. If they are unable, one hundred twenty or one hundred buckets are drawn from it."],[284,"A man fell into Zamzam and died in it. Ibn Abbas ordered that its springs be blocked and it be drained. It was said to him: There is a spring in it that has overcome us. He said: It is from Paradise. So he gave them a cloak of khazz, and they stuffed it in it, then its water was drained until no stench remained in it."],[285,"About a mouse that fell into a well and dough was kneaded with its water: he said: Feed it to chickens."],[286,"I put my wudu water, then a mouse comes and drinks from it. Al-Hasan said: Pour it out, for the wicked creature does not drink from anything without urinating in it."],[287,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about a mouse falling into ghee. He said: If it is solid, throw it away and what is around it; and if it is liquid, do not go near it."],[288,"279 279 - Abd al-Razzaq said: Ma'mar also used to mention it, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah b. Utba, from Ibn Abbas, from Maymunah, and likewise Ibn Uyaynah reported it to us. Hidden matn: The Prophet ﷺ was asked about a mouse falling into ghee. He said: If it is solid, throw it away and what is around it; and if it is liquid, do not go near it."],[289,"We said to Abu Sa'id: Can it still be used? He said: Yes."],[290,"Make use of it, but do not eat it."],[291,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about a mouse falling into ghee. He said: If it is solid, take what is around it, about a handful, and eat the rest."],[292,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about a mouse falling into ghee. He said: If it is solid, take what is around it, about a handful; and if it falls into oil, use it as fuel for a lamp."],[293,"I said to Ata: A mouse falls into solid rendered fat, or non-solid. He said: It has reached us that if it is solid, take what is around it and throw it away, and eat what remains. I said: What about non-solid? He said: Nothing has reached me about it, but I think it should be used as fuel for a lamp and not eaten."],[294,"When a mouse dies in solid rendered fat, he said: It has reached us that if it is solid, take what is around it and throw it away, and eat what remains."],[295,"A mouse fell into twenty qirtal of oil, and Ibn Umar said: Use it for lamps and anoint leather with it."],[296,"I said to Amr b. Dinar: A mouse died in a person's oil which he uses for anointing. He said: I do not like it."],[297,"I asked Ata about a mouse that died in honey. He said: Honey is like solid matter: scoop out what is around it and eat the rest."],[298,"A mouse dying in melted fat or oil, and it is found to have shed its skin, or it is found that it died while it is intact, not shed. He said: It is the same if it died in it. The oil is boiled and used for anointing if you are not disgusted. I said: What about the fat—should it be boiled, heated, and then eaten? He said: What is eaten is not like something on the head that is anointed with."],[299,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A mouse fell into a jar and died in it. He said: Ablution is not performed from it. If you performed ablution without knowing, then prayed without knowing, repeat as long as you are within the time. He said: If the time has passed, still repeat. I said: If some of that jar's water touches my garment, should I wash it or sprinkle it? He said: No."],[300,"If you are compelled to use water in which a mouse has fallen, perform ablution with it and do tayammum, combining both."],[301,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A gecko dying in rendered fat, butter, oil, and the like—is it in the same rank as a mouse in that? He said: And more beloved [to avoid]."],[302,"A gecko fell into some butter belonging to the family of Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, and they mixed sawīq with it. Then they informed him, and he said: Sell it to someone who considers it lawful, then inform him."],[303,"A gecko of theirs died, and they mixed sawīq with it, and he drank from it. Then he was told of what had happened. He said: Did you know? They said: No. He clapped his hands and said: Sell the butter and the sawīq to people not of your religion, and make clear to them what had happened. One of his family said: Should we use it as lamp fuel? He said: Yes, if you wish."],[304,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A dung beetle dying in honey, or butter, or rendered fat, or water. He said: It is only a small creature with no flesh and no blood. If it falls into something solid or not solid and dies, none of it is discarded, and do not pour it out—eat it. I said to him: What is a dung beetle? He said: The black creature that rolls dung."],[305,"Regarding the dung beetle, hornet, and their like when they fall into water or land in food and drink, he said: It may be eaten, drunk, and used for ablution. Whatever is in water that has no bone, there is no harm in it."],[306,"She used to travel with Maymūnah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: We would come to a pool with dead dung beetles in it and take water from it—meaning they would drink it."],[307,"He was asked about a fly that falls into water and dies in it. He said: There is no harm in it."],[308,"None of you should urinate in stagnant water that does not flow, then perform ablution from it."],[309,"None of you should urinate in stagnant water, then perform ablution from it."],[310,"He forbade a man from urinating in stagnant water."],[311,"He forbade urinating in still water that does not flow, then bathing in it."],[312,"I asked Jabir b. Abd Allah about stagnant water: 'May I bathe in it when a ritually impure person has entered it?' He said: 'No, but draw from it by scooping.'"],[313,"If you become ritually impure and pass by a pool, draw from it by scooping and pour it over yourself. If it flows into it, do not worry, and do not enter it if you can."],[314,"About a man who becomes ritually impure and passes by a well without a bucket with him. He said: 'If he finds no way but to put his hand into it, let him put it in.' Ma'mar said: 'I heard someone say: He should wet the edge of his garment, then squeeze it over his hands to wash them.' Ma'mar said: 'If he performed tayammum and then put his hand in, that would be preferable to me.'"],[315,"About a man who forgot and put his hand into the water in which he was going to bathe while he was ritually impure, before washing his hands. He said: 'Bad is what he did, but he may bathe with it.'"],[316,"He discards, and does not perform wudu or ghusl."],[317,"I asked Ata about a ritually impure person who forgets and puts his hand into the vessel containing his bathing water before washing them. He said: 'If he forgot, it is fine; let him wash his hands.'"],[318,"There is no ritual impurity on a garment, nor on the ground, nor on a man whom a ritually impure person touches, nor on water. He says: If his hands precede him and he puts them into the water while he is ritually impure, before washing them, it is fine."],[319,"The companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to put their hands into water while they were ritually impure, and women while they were menstruating, and that did not harm them."],[320,"The companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to put their hands into water while they were ritually impure, and women while they were menstruating, and that did not harm them."],[321,"I asked al-Zuhri about a man who bathes from ritual impurity and some of the water from his skin splashes into the vessel. He said: 'There is no harm in it.'"],[322,"I said to Ata: 'What do you think about what splashes from the vessel into the basin?' He said: 'It does not harm you.'"],[323,"I said to 'Ata': I place my cup containing my wudu water in the basin from which I perform wudu, and it may be large. He said: No. I said: But I know that water from the basin splashes onto me, and the cup is not in it, and it splashes into my wudu water from the basin, and the cup is not in it. He said: None of that harms you. It is an excuse for you that this happens even though you have set it aside, and then it entered from the basin."],[324,"Where would Ibn 'Umar put the vessel from which he performed wudu? He said: Beside him."],[325,"He was asked about a man who performs ghusl or wudu from water and splashes into it. He said: He did not see any harm in it."],[326,"They were asked about a man who performs ghusl from janabah and splashes from his washing into the water from which he is washing. He said: There is no harm in it."],[327,"Maymun b. Mihran would perform ghusl from a vessel then raise it, so that nothing from his washing would splash back."],[328,"Two waters do not purify from janabah: sea water and bathhouse water."],[329,"The sea: its water is purifying and its dead animals are lawful."],[330,"320 320 - Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri, from Aban, from Anas, from the Prophet ﷺ, something similar. Hidden matn: The sea: its water is purifying and its dead animals are lawful."],[331,"Some people from Banu Mudlij asked the Messenger of God ﷺ: We ride rafts of ours, and one of us carries a little water for his lips. If we perform wudu with sea water, we feel uneasy within ourselves, and if we perform wudu with it, we become thirsty. The Prophet ﷺ said: It is the purifier; its water is purifying, its dead animals are lawful."],[332,"A man told me, one of the fishermen who are in al-Jar, and the people of Madinah used to be provisioned from al-Jar. He found scattered grain, and 'Umar began picking it up until he gathered a mudd or nearly a mudd from it. Then he said: Do I see you doing such a thing, when this is the sustenance of a Muslim man until nightfall? He said: I said to him: O Commander of the Faithful, if only you would ride to see how we fish. He said: So he rode with them, and they began fishing. 'Umar said: By God, I have never seen a livelihood more wholesome — or he said: more lawful — than today. Then he said: We prepared food for him, and I said: O Commander of the Faithful, if you wish we will give you food to drink, or if you wish water, for milk is easier for us than water; we fetch fresh water from such-and-such a place. He said: So he ate, then he called for what he wanted. Then we said: O Commander of the Faithful, we go out here and take provisions of water for our lips, then we perform wudu from sea water. He said: Glory be to God! What water is purer than sea water?"],[333,"'Umar b. al-Khattab was asked about sea water, and he said: What water is purer than sea water?"],[334,"They are two seas: 'This is fresh and sweet, and this is salty and bitter.'"],[335,"Is sea water purifying? He said: Yes."],[336,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if I find water other than sea water and al-īḍā, and I see a well—should I leave the well and al-īḍā? He said: If you purify yourself from either of them, they are both purifying. I said to him: What is al-īḍā? He said: The miṭhara (water container for purification)."],[337,"I asked Ibrāhīm about sea water—may I perform ghusl with it? He said: Yes, and fresh water is dearer to me."],[338,"A man said to him: I passed by the sea while I was in a state of major ritual impurity, so I performed ghusl from it. He said: That suffices you."],[339,"The purification of the vessel of any of you, if a dog laps in it, is to wash it seven times."],[340,"If a dog laps in a vessel, wash it seven times, the first of them with dust."],[341,"331 331 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar, from Ayyūb, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, likewise. Hidden text: If a dog laps in a vessel, wash it seven times, the first of them with dust."],[342,"331 331 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar, from Ayyūb, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, likewise. Hidden text: If a dog laps in a vessel, wash it seven times, the first of them with dust."],[343,"About a dog that laps in a vessel, he said: Do not put anything in it until you wash it seven times."],[344,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: How many times should the vessel in which a dog laps be washed? He said: I have heard all of that—seven, five, and three times."],[345,"The vessel is washed seven times if a dog laps in it."],[346,"If a dog laps in the vessel of any of you, let him wash it seven times."],[348,"I asked al-Zuhrī about a dog that laps in a vessel. He said: It is washed three times. He said: I have not heard anything about a cat."],[349,"I said to 'Ata': 'A dog lapped from a large bowl belonging to some people, in which there was milk, and they caught it at that moment, and they scooped out around the place where it lapped.' He said: 'Do not drink it.'"],[350,"He used to dislike the leftover water of a dog."],[351,"339 339 - Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri, from 'Ubayd Allah b. 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, likewise. Hidden text: He used to dislike the leftover water of a dog."],[352,"He used to dislike the leftover water of a cat."],[353,"341 341 - Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri, from 'Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, likewise. Hidden text: He used to dislike the leftover water of a cat."],[354,"I said to 'Ata': 'What about a cat?' He said: 'It is like a dog, or worse than it.'"],[355,"Regarding a cat that laps from a vessel, he said: 'It is like a dog; it should be washed seven times.'"],[356,"Regarding a cat that laps from a vessel, he said: 'Wash it once and pour it out.'"],[357,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a cat that laps from a vessel. He said: 'It should be washed once or twice.' And al-Hasan used to say: 'Once or three times.'"],[358,"He saw Abu Qatadah al-Ansari tilting a vessel for a cat to drink from it, then he performed wudu' with its leftover water."],[359,"347 347 - Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from 'Ikrimah, likewise. Hidden text: He saw Abu Qatadah al-Ansari tilting a vessel for a cat to drink from it, then he performed wudu' with its leftover water."],[360,"Abu Qatadah brought a vessel near to a cat, and it lapped from it. Then he performed wudu' with its leftover water and said: 'It is part of the household effects.'"],[361,"Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah: something similar. Hidden text: Abu Qatadah brought a vessel close to the cat, and it lapped from it; then he performed ablution from the leftover water and said, \"It is one of the household items.\""],[362,"There is no harm in performing ablution from the leftover water of a cat; it is simply one of my dependents."],[363,"'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ishaq b. 'Abd Allah b. Abi Talhah, from a woman, from her mother, who was married to Abu Qatadah: similar to the hadith of Malik. Hidden text: Abu Qatadah entered upon her, and she poured water for his ablution; then a cat came and drank, and he tilted the vessel for it until it drank. Kabshah said: He saw me looking at him, and he said, \"Are you amazed, daughter of my brother?\" She said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, 'It is not impure; it is merely one of the frequent visitors—males and females.'\" Hidden chain: 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ishaq b. 'Abd Allah b. Abi Talhah, from a woman, from her mother; Abu Qatadah said."],[364,"Abu Qatadah visited them, so they poured water for his ablution. A cat came near him, and he tilted toward it the vessel containing his ablution water, and it drank from it. Then he performed ablution with the leftover water, and they were surprised at that. Abu Qatadah said, \"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'It is not impure; it is one of those that frequent you.'\""],[365,"Abu Qatadah entered upon her, and she poured water for his ablution. A cat came and drank, and he tilted the vessel for it until it drank. Kabshah said: He saw me looking at him, and he said, \"Are you amazed, daughter of my brother?\" She said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'It is not impure; it is merely one of the frequent visitors—males and females.'\""],[366,"A cat lapped some milk belonging to the family of Abu Qays. His family wanted to pour out the milk, but he forbade them from that and ordered them to drink it."],[367,"Her mistress sent her with jashish or rice as a gift to 'A'ishah. She brought it while 'A'ishah was praying, so she set it down. A cat came near and ate from it, while some women were with 'A'ishah. When she finished praying, she called for it, and when she saw the women avoiding the spot where the cat had eaten, 'A'ishah ؓ placed her hand on the spot where the cat had eaten and said, \"It is not impure.\""],[368,"I used to perform ablution, I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, from a vessel that a cat had previously drunk from."],[369,"A woman asked about a cat lapping from my drink, and he said, \"A cat?\" She said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Do not pour out your drink or your purification water, for it does not make anything impure.\""],[370,"The cat is one of the household items."],[371,"Ibn 'Abbas was asked whether a vessel should be washed after a cat laps from it. He said, \"It is merely one of the household items.\""],[372,"The cat is one of the household items."],[373,"One day the Prophet ﷺ performed ablution and was delayed from his Companions. Then he came out, and they said, 'What delayed you?' He said, 'A small creature drank — the cat.' Sadaqah said, 'I do not know whether from his ablution water or from the leftover of his ablution water; I do not know.' A man who was with us then, one who had heard the knowledge, said, 'Rather, from his ablution water.'"],[374,"I said to 'Ata': 'A donkey drinks from my large bowl?' He said, 'Yes, and perform ablution with its leftover.' Then he recited: 'And [He created] the horses, mules and donkeys for you to ride them.' I said, 'But it is forbidden to eat it?' He said, 'Its meat is not the same as performing ablution with its leftover, so give it to drink from your bowl.'"],[375,"He used to perform ablution with the leftover of a donkey."],[376,"He saw Mujahid performing ablution with the leftover of a donkey."],[377,"I asked al-Zuhri about ablution with the leftover of a donkey, and he said, 'There is no harm in it.'"],[378,"There is no harm in using the leftover of a donkey for ablution."],[379,"I asked al-Hasan about the leftover water of a donkey, and he said, 'There is no harm in the leftover of all riding beasts.'"],[380,"There is no harm in the leftover water of a donkey."],[381,"There is no harm in performing ablution with the leftover of a donkey."],[382,"He disliked the leftover water of a donkey, a mule, and a dog, but he saw no harm in the leftover water of a horse or a sheep."],[383,"371 370 - Abd al-Razzaq from Hushaym, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, the like of it. Hidden text: He disliked the leftover water of a donkey, a mule, and a dog, and he saw no harm in the leftover water of a horse or a sheep."],[384,"He disliked performing ablution with the leftover of a donkey. He said, 'Is it anything but a donkey?'"],[385,"What is not eaten of its meat, do not perform ablution with its leftover water. Qatadah said: I have not heard anyone differ regarding that whose meat is eaten among beasts, that ablution may be performed with its leftover water and one may drink from it."],[386,"He used to dislike the leftover water of the donkey, the dog, and the cat, that ablution be performed with their leftover water."],[387,"He used to dislike the leftover water of the donkey, the dog, and the cat, that ablution be performed with their leftover water."],[388,"I asked al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyab about performing ablution with a woman's leftover water, and both of them forbade me from it."],[389,"He used to see no harm in a man and a woman sharing water for ablution, and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man from performing ablution with a woman's leftover water."],[390,"Do not perform ablution with the leftover water of my ablution."],[391,"He forbade a man from performing ablution with a woman's leftover water."],[392,"He used to see no harm in a woman's leftover drink or her leftover ablution water, and he said: She has cleaner clothes and a better scent."],[393,"He used to see no harm in a woman's leftover drink or her leftover ablution water, and he said: She has cleaner clothes and a better scent."],[394,"A person asked ʿAṭāʾ: A woman bathes without being junub; may a man bathe with her leftover water? He said: Yes."],[395,"There is no harm in a woman's leftover water, whether she is menstruating or not, as long as there is no harm in her hands."],[396,"There is no harm in a man performing ablution with a woman's leftover water as long as she is not menstruating or junub."],[397,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I met a woman at the water while she was performing ghusl with it or doing wuḍūʾ—may a man perform wuḍūʾ from her leftover? He said: Yes, if she is a Muslim. ʿAṭāʾ said: A man may perform ghusl with a woman's leftover, provided they are not both in a state of major ritual impurity."],[398,"There is no harm in a man and a woman performing ghusl from the same vessel, but if she is alone with it, then do not approach it."],[399,"There is no harm in performing wuḍūʾ from the leftover of a woman's drink or the leftover of her wuḍūʾ, as long as she is not in a state of major ritual impurity or menstruating. But if she is alone with it, then do not approach it."],[400,"A person asked ʿAṭāʾ: A woman performs ghusl while not in a state of major ritual impurity—may a man perform ghusl from the vessel with her? He said: Yes."],[401,"I would drink from the vessel while I was menstruating, and the Prophet ﷺ would take it and place his mouth on the same spot where my mouth had been and drink. And I would take a bone with meat on it and bite from it, and he would take it from me and place his mouth on the same spot where my mouth had been and bite from it."],[402,"I asked al-Zuhrī about the leftover water of a menstruating woman and of a person in a state of major ritual impurity, and he saw no harm in it."],[403,"There is no harm in the leftover water of a menstruating woman or of a person in a state of major ritual impurity—he saw no harm in it—whether for wuḍūʾ or for drinking."],[404,"There is no harm in the leftover water of a menstruating woman: one may drink it and perform wuḍūʾ from it."],[405,"He used to dislike the leftover water of a person in a state of major ritual impurity, whether for wuḍūʾ or drinking. But he saw no harm in performing wuḍūʾ with the leftover of a menstruating woman, yet he disliked the leftover of her drink."],[406,"There is no harm in the leftover water of a menstruating woman: one may drink it and perform wuḍūʾ from it."],[407,"He used to dislike the leftover of a menstruating woman and of a person in a state of major ritual impurity."],[408,"There is no harm in the leftover of a woman, whether she is menstruating or not."],[409,"A woman among the wives of the Prophet ﷺ bathed from janābah. The Prophet ﷺ came and performed wuḍūʾ from her leftover water. She said, 'I bathed from it.' He said, 'Water is not made impure by anything.'"],[410,"398 397 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Isrāʾīl, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, similar to it. Hidden text: A woman among the wives of the Prophet ﷺ bathed from janābah. The Prophet ﷺ came and performed wuḍūʾ from her leftover water. She said, 'I bathed from it.' He said, 'Water is not made impure by anything.'"],[411,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb was asked about a menstruating woman handing a man wuḍūʾ water and dipping her hand into it. He said, 'Her menstruation is not in her hand.'"],[412,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ, 'May a person in janābah perform wuḍūʾ with the leftover wuḍūʾ water of another person in janābah, and may a man and a woman each perform wuḍūʾ with the other's leftover water when both are in janābah?' He said, 'For prayer, no. But for food, drink, and sleep'—he said—'the leftover wuḍūʾ water of a person in janābah may not be used for prayer.' I said, 'Is the menstruating woman of the same status as them?' He said, 'Yes.'"],[413,"We used to bathe at the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we and our wives, from a single vessel."],[414,"There is no harm in a ritually pure woman combing her hair with leftover water of a person in janābah, or in dyeing with her leftover water. Each of them may eat and drink from the other's leftover."],[415,"There is no harm in a ritually pure woman combing her hair with the leftover water of a menstruating woman."],[416,"404 403 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar, from Jābir, from al-Shaʿbī, similar to it. Hidden text: There is no harm in a ritually pure woman combing her hair with the leftover water of a menstruating woman."],[417,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ about touching the armpit. He said, 'I would not like to touch it since I heard about it from ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb what I heard, but I do not perform wuḍūʾ because of it.'"],[418,"Whoever touches his armpit should perform wuḍūʾ. He said, 'I have not heard this ḥadīth except from him.' He said, 'We tell people about performing wuḍūʾ after touching the private part, and they do not believe us. How would it be if we told them about touching the armpit?'"],[419,"407 406 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAmr b. Dīnār, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbayd Allāh b. ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUtbah, from ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, similar to it, except that he did not mention the penis. Hidden text: Whoever touches his armpit should perform wuḍūʾ."],[420,"Ibn ʿUmar used to pass his hand over his armpit when performing wuḍūʾ, then he would not repeat the wuḍūʾ."],[421,"I saw Ibn Umar in a waist-wrapper and a cloak. I saw him place his hand on his nose, then strike his hand on his armpit while he was in prayer."],[422,"There is no wudu required from plucking the armpit hair."],[423,"Busrah bt. Safwan b. Muharrith said: I said, \"O Messenger of God, one of us performs wudu for the prayer, finishes her wudu, then inserts her hand into her garment and touches her private part. Is wudu obligatory upon her?\" He said, \"Yes. When she touches her private part, let her repeat the prayer and the wudu.\" He said: And Abd Allah b. Amr was sitting, and that did not startle Abd Allah b. Amr afterward."],[424,"He and Marwan discussed wudu from touching the private part. Marwan said, \"Busrah bt. Safwan narrated to me that she heard the Messenger of God ﷺ commanding wudu from touching the private part.\" It was as if Urwah was not convinced by his report, so Marwan sent a policeman to her. He returned and informed them that she had heard the Messenger of God ﷺ commanding wudu from touching the private part."],[425,"413 Ma'mar said: Hisham b. Urwah informed me, from his father, the like of it. Hidden text: He and Marwan discussed wudu from touching the private part. Marwan said, \"Busrah bt. Safwan narrated to me that she heard the Messenger of God ﷺ commanding wudu from touching the private part.\" It was as if Urwah was not convinced by his report, so Marwan sent a policeman to her. He returned and informed them that she had heard the Messenger of God ﷺ commanding wudu from touching the private part. Hidden chain: Ma'mar said: Hisham b. Urwah informed me, from his father: Marwan said to me, \"Busrah bt. Safwan narrated to me.\""],[426,"When one of you touches his penis, let him perform wudu."],[427,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed the morning prayer, then repeated it. It was said to him, \"You had already prayed.\" He said, \"Yes, but I touched my penis and forgot to perform wudu.\""],[428,"I was holding the mushaf for Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas once while he was reciting from memory, until my penis itched and I scratched it. When he saw me insert my hand there, he said, \"Did you touch it?\" I said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Get up and perform wudu.\""],[429,"I was presenting to my father, holding the mushaf while he was reading it. My penis itched, so I inserted my hand and scratched it, and then I realized I had touched my penis. I mentioned that to him, and he said, \"Get up and perform wudu,\" so I did."],[430,"While Umar b. al-Khattab was standing leading the people in prayer, when he began the prayer, his hand slipped onto his penis. He gestured to the people to stay, went and performed wudu, then came back and prayed. My father said to him, \"Perhaps he found pre-seminal fluid?\" He said, \"I do not know.\""],[431,"Ibn Umar prayed the afternoon prayer with them, then traveled some miles—I think he said six—then dismounted, performed wudu, and repeated the prayer. I said to him, \"Had you not already prayed?\" He said, \"Yes, but I touched my penis and prayed without performing wudu, so that is why I repeated it.\""],[432,"Ibn Umar prayed the afternoon prayer with them on the road to Makkah. Then we mounted and traveled as far as we were able to travel. Then Ibn Umar made his camel kneel, performed wudu, and prayed the afternoon prayer alone. Salim said: I said to him, \"You had already prayed the afternoon prayer for us. Did you forget?\" He said, \"I did not forget, but I had touched my penis before praying. When I remembered that, I performed wudu and repeated my prayer.\""],[433,"Ibn Jurayj said: And Hasan b. Muslim reported to me that Salim reported to him something similar to the hadith of Ibn Shihab, except that he did not mention which prayer. [Hidden text] that Ibn Umar prayed with them on the road to Makkah, then we mounted and traveled as far as we were able to travel, then Ibn Umar made his camel kneel, performed wudu, and prayed alone. Salim said: I said to him, \"You prayed a prayer for us, did you forget?\" He said, \"I did not forget, but I touched my penis before I prayed, so when I remembered that, I performed wudu and repeated my prayer.\" [Hidden chain] Ibn Jurayj said: And Hasan b. Muslim reported to me that Salim reported to him that"],[434,"My father would perform ghusl, then perform wudu, and I would say: \"Does not ghusl suffice you?\" He would say: \"Yes, but it seems to me that something comes out of my penis, so I touch it, and I perform wudu for that.\""],[435,"I said to Ata: \"What do you think if you touch your penis while you are performing ghusl?\" He said: \"Then I would repeat wudu.\""],[436,"Whoever touches his penis should perform wudu."],[437,"Whoever touches his penis should perform wudu. And that was only traced back from Ibn Umar. Qays said to him: \"O Abu Muhammad, if you touched your penis while in the prescribed prayer, would you leave and cut off your prayer to perform wudu?\" He said: \"Yes, by Allah, I would cut off my prayer and perform wudu.\""],[438,"I said to Ata: \"I touched the penis from behind the garment.\" He said: \"There is no wudu except from direct contact, then by touching.\" I said: \"With the thigh or the shin?\" He said: \"There is no wudu except with the hand.\" I said: \"What distinguishes between that?\" He said: \"It is only from the leg, and how can the leg not touch? The hand is not like the form of the leg in that.\""],[439,"I said to Ata: \"What do you think if I touch my penis and do not touch the urinary passage?\" He said: \"If you touch its back or any part of it, then perform wudu.\""],[440,"A man asked the Prophet ﷺ and said: \"I touched my penis while I was praying.\" He said: \"There is no harm; it is only a piece of you.\""],[441,"I said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what do you think of a man who performs wudu, then reaches with his hand and touches his penis, should he perform wudu?\" Then I reached with my hand and touched my penis? He said: \"It is from you.\""],[442,"A group of the companions of Muhammad ﷺ gathered, among them one who said: \"I do not care whether I touched it or my ear, or my thigh, or my knee.\""],[443,"I do not care whether I touched it or my ear, as long as I did not intend to do so."],[444,"A man asked him about touching the penis during prayer, and he said: \"I do not care whether I touched it or touched my nose.\""],[445,"I scratched my body while I was in prayer, and I reached my penis. I said to Abd Allah b. Masud, and he laughed and said: Cut it off—where will you isolate it? It is only a piece of you."],[446,"I do not care whether I touch it or my nose tip."],[447,"I said to Ata: What do you think—if I touch the penis with my forearm, should one perform wudu? He said: Yes."],[448,"I do not care whether I touch it or my thigh."],[449,"A man asked Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas about touching the penis—should one perform wudu because of it? He said: If there is any part of you that is impure, then cut it off."],[450,"Ibn Abbas said to Ibn Umar: If I knew that what you say about the penis is true, I would cut it off. Then, if I knew it to be impure, I would cut it off. I do not care whether I touch it or touch my nose."],[451,"Ali, Abd Allah b. Masud, Hudhayfa b. al-Yaman, and Abu Hurayra did not consider touching the penis to require wudu, and they said: There is no harm in it."],[452,"Whoever touches his penis, no wudu is required of him."],[453,"Al-Hasan and Qatada did not consider wudu required because of it."],[454,"One of their commanders called me and Ibn Jurayj and asked us about touching the penis. Ibn Jurayj said: He should perform wudu. I said: No wudu is required of him. When we differed, I said to Ibn Jurayj: What do you think if a man puts his hand on semen? He said: He washes his hand. I said: Which of the two is more impure—semen or the penis? He said: No, rather semen. I said: How can this be? He said: Nothing put that on your tongue except a devil."],[455,"Whoever touches the penis should perform wudu."],[456,"Whoever touches his groins must perform ablution."],[457,"Whoever touches his penis, or his testicles, or his inguinal creases must repeat the ablution."],[458,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if I touch what is around the penis and the testicles? He said: No ablution except from touching it itself."],[459,"If a man touches his testicles or his inguinal creases, he performs ablution."],[460,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A man touched his anus, the path of the privy, and did not put his hand there—should he perform ablution? He said: Yes. If you perform ablution from touching the penis, you perform ablution from touching it. He said: I said: What do you think if he touches what is around the path of the privy and does not insert his hand there?"],[461,"I heard a man say to Qatādah: A man has a prolapse, and his anus comes out from the severity of tenesmus, so he pushes it back with his hand. Is ablution incumbent on him? He said: No, but he should wash his hand."],[462,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if I touched the penis of a young boy? He said: Perform ablution."],[463,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I touched the sheath of a donkey or the penis of a camel. He said: As for the sheath of a donkey, you would perform ablution, but as for the penis of a camel, no. I said: What distinguishes between them? I said: Because of the donkey, which is more impure. He said, and I say: Consider everything impure like the donkey, whose flesh is not eaten—if one touches that part of it, ablution is incumbent on him. And everything whose flesh is eaten, like the camel—if one touches that part of it, no ablution is required from it."],[464,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A dog touched my garment—should I sprinkle water on it? He said: No."],[465,"I asked him, saying: A dog passed by and touched my ṭaylasān. He said: If something stuck to it, wash it; otherwise, no harm."],[466,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: If a man touches a dog or a donkey that is wet, should he perform ablution because of it? He said: No, and that is more putrid than the armpit."],[467,"Regarding a man who performs ablution and then touches a dog, he said: He is not required to perform ablution."],[468,"A person asked Ata: 'I touched my sandal during prayer, and my hand fell on filth in it. Should I repeat my prayer?' He said: 'No.'"],[469,"I asked him about a man who performs ablution then shakes hands with a junub, a menstruating woman, a Jew, or a Christian. He said: 'He does not have to repeat the ablution.'"],[470,"The Messenger of God ﷺ met Hudhayfah and reached out his hand toward Hudhayfah. Hudhayfah said: 'I am junub.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'The believer does not become impure.'"],[471,"There is nothing upon a man if another man in a state of janabah touches him."],[472,"There is nothing upon a man if another man in a state of janabah touches him."],[473,"Ibn Mas'ud prayed while there was rumen and blood from camels he had slaughtered on his belly, and he did not perform ablution."],[474,"Ibn Mas'ud slaughtered a camel and became smeared with its blood and rumen, then the prayer was called and he prayed without performing ablution."],[475,"Ali asked al-Mustawrid al-Ijli to repent while he was intending to pray, and said: 'I seek God's help against you.' He said: 'And I seek the Messiah's help against you.' Ali reached his hand to his neck and found a cross, so he cut it off. When he entered prayer, he put forward a man and left. Then he told the people that he had not done that because of any ritual impurity, but because he had touched those impurities and wanted to perform ablution because of them."],[476,"I said to Ata: 'Trimming the mustache and clipping the nails, does it require ablution?' He said: 'No, but let him touch with water the place where he clipped and trimmed.'"],[477,"When a man takes something from his nails or his hair, he should pass water over it."],[478,"He wipes water over it."],[479,"His ablution has been invalidated."],[480,"Regarding one who trims his nails and hair: nothing is required of him."],[481,"Nothing is required of him."],[482,"When a man is overcome by deep sleep while standing, sitting, or lying down, he should perform wudu. He said: Al-Hasan used to perform wudu several times during the night."],[483,"If he kisses, then ablution is required of him."],[484,"To perform wudu because of a foul word is more beloved to me than to perform wudu because of good food."],[485,"One of you performs wudu because of good food, but does not perform wudu because of an indecent word he utters."],[486,"I would pray Zuhr, Asr, and Maghrib with a single ablution as long as I did not invalidate it or say something objectionable."],[488,"Ablution is required from kissing."],[490,"Allah does not accept the prayer of anyone who has incurred ritual impurity until he performs ablution. A man from Hadramawt asked him: What is ritual impurity, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: Breaking wind or passing gas."],[491,"A man complained to Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that he was in doubt about ablution, saying whisperings: 'You did not wipe your head; you did not wash such-and-such.' He said: That is from the devil, so continue."],[492,"When sleep overcomes you, perform wudu, whether sitting or lying down."],[494,"When a man is overcome by deep sleep while standing, sitting, or lying down, he should perform wudu. He said: Al-Hasan used to perform wudu several times during the night."],[495,"I saw al-Hasan in the enclosure praying more than once; he would nod off with his head, then get up and pray, without performing ablution."],[496,"Wudu is obligatory upon every sleeper, except one who briefly nodded his head."],[497,"If he slept while sitting a heavy sleep, he repeats wudu; but if it was a light doze, there is no harm."],[498,"Whoever deserves sleep, upon him is wudu."],[499,"Whoever sleeps lying down, let him perform wudu."],[500,"Pus and blood are the same."],[501,"He would sleep while sitting and not perform wudu, but if he slept lying down, he would repeat wudu."],[502,"He was asked about kissing. He said: From it wudu is required, and it is part of touching."],[503,"I came to Ibn Umar while he was sitting waiting for prayer, and I greeted him, and he woke up. He said: \"Abu Thabit?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Did you greet?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"When you greet, make it audible, and when they respond to you, let them make it audible to you.\" Then he stood and prayed, and he had been sitting with his legs drawn up, having slept."],[504,"Tawus arrived on Friday while Ibn al-Dahhak was delivering the sermon to the people. He said: When we prayed and left, he said: \"What did he say while I dozed off?\""],[505,"If a man kisses with desire or touches with desire, then wudu is incumbent on him."],[506,"Regarding a man who sleeps while sitting: no wudu is required of him."],[509,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I dozed briefly during the prescribed prayer, then I startled awake and I do not know if I uttered anything. Should I repeat it, or is anything incumbent on me? He said: No."],[510,"When the insane person regains his reason, he performs wudu as he would for prayer."],[512,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A man applies depilatory paste—is wudu incumbent on him? He said: Is he not taking a bath? He said: And he must inevitably touch his penis—he was the one who said that. He said: I said: What if he applied it to his shins due to pain in them, while he was in a state of wudu—does he repeat the wudu? He said: Depilatory paste is not a ritual impurity."],[513,"He used to say: Whoever kisses his wife while he has wudu should repeat the wudu."],[514,"Kissing is part of touching, and from it wudu is required."],[515,"Ibn ʿUmar would leave for prayer having already performed wudu, then he would meet one of his children and kiss him, and then he would call for water and rinse his mouth, and he would not add to that."],[516,"A man performs wudu from sexual contact, from touching with his hand, and from kissing if he kisses his wife. About this verse: {or you have touched women} he said: It means fondling."],[517,"Kissing is part of touching, and from it wudu is required."],[518,"If a man kisses with desire or touches with desire, then wudu is incumbent on him."],[519,"If he kisses, then ablution is required of him."],[520,"Touching with the hand. He said: And from it is ablution and dry ablution if he does not find water."],[521,"Ablution is required from kissing."],[522,"Ablution is required from kissing."],[523,"I do not care whether I kissed her or smelled sweet basil."],[524,"Ubayd b. Umayr, Saʿid b. Jubayr, and ʿAtaʾ b. Abi Rabah differed concerning al-mulamasah. Saʿid and ʿAtaʾ said: It is touching and pressing. Ubayd b. Umayr said: It is intercourse. Ibn ʿAbbas came out to them while they were thus, so they asked him and told him what they had said. He said: The two clients erred, and the Arab was correct: it is intercourse. But Allah is modest and uses a metonym."],[525,"I do not care whether I kissed her or smelled sweet basil."],[526,"Umar b. al-Khattab went out to prayer and his wife kissed him, so he prayed and did not perform ablution."],[527,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution, and he would go out to prayer and kiss me, then pray without renewing ablution."],[528,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kissed me, then prayed and did not renew ablution."],[529,"He used to kiss after ablution and not repeat it. Or she said: Then he would pray."],[530,"Atikah bt. Zayd kissed Umar b. al-Khattab while he was fasting, and he did not forbid her. He said: He was intending to pray, then he proceeded and prayed and did not perform ablution."],[531,"There is no ablution required from kissing."],[534,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said, while sitting in the mosque during prayer: he grasped Aisha's foot without desire."],[535,"She is only your sweet basil."],[536,"If a person vomits or induces vomiting, ablution becomes obligatory upon him; and if he regurgitates, ablution becomes obligatory upon him."],[537,"I said to Ata': What if a man regurgitates and it reaches his chest or throat but does not reach the mouth? He said: No ablution is due for that. I said: What if it reaches the throat but he does not spit it out and swallows it back? He said: Ablution becomes obligatory when it reaches the mouth and becomes visible. I said: Do you dislike a person swallowing it back after it appears in his mouth? He said: Yes, but not because of sin; I find it disgusting."],[538,"I said to Ata': What if I belch and some food comes out of my throat that was stuck there, not from my stomach—do I perform ablution for that? He said: No. I said: What if I belch and a little food comes up from the esophagus? He said: By my life, I often expel a lot of phlegm, and then something comes from my throat and head; there is no ablution for that, except for what comes out from your stomach."],[539,"When regurgitation reaches the mouth, ablution becomes obligatory for it. But if it is dry and he feels it in his throat, he does not perform ablution for it."],[540,"If regurgitation is swallowed, let him perform ablution."],[541,"If regurgitation is swallowed, let him perform ablution."],[542,"If any of it appears on the tongue, little or much, ablution is required for it."],[543,"There is no ablution for regurgitation."],[544,"There is no ablution for regurgitation."],[545,"Ablution is required for vomiting; and if it is regurgitation that overcomes him, let him perform ablution."],[546,"The Messenger of God ﷺ induced vomiting, so he broke his fast; water was brought and he performed ablution."],[547,"I said to 'Ata': A drop that came out from urine. He said: Perform ablution from it; it is a ritual impurity."],[548,"Perform ablution from every ritual impurity: from urine, defecation, passing wind, and from every impurity that comes out of a person."],[549,"Whoever breaks wind must repeat the ablution."],[550,"When one of you breaks wind, let him perform ablution, for Allah is not ashamed of the truth."],[551,"Allah does not accept the prayer of anyone who has incurred ritual impurity until he performs ablution. A man from Hadramawt asked him: What is ritual impurity, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: Breaking wind or passing gas."],[552,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ noticed an odor while with his companions and said: 'Whoever this odor came from, let him perform ablution.' The person felt ashamed and did not stand up until he said it three times, and no one stood. Then al-'Abbas b. 'Abd al-Muttalib said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we all perform ablution?"],[553,"When one of you incurs ritual impurity during prayer, let him hold his nose and then leave."],[554,"When Satan confuses one of you during his prayer and says: 'You have incurred ritual impurity,' let him say to himself: 'You lie,' until he hears a sound with his ear or smells an odor with his nose. And when one of you prays and does not know whether he added or subtracted, let him prostrate twice while seated."],[555,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about a man who is uncertain during his prayer. He said: He should not leave unless he smells an odor or hears a sound."],[556,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'It seems to me during prayer that something comes out of my urethra, or that wind comes out of me. Should I interrupt my prayer?' He said: 'No, that is from Satan, who enters the urethra of one of you to make him imagine that wind is coming out. When one of you feels that, let him not interrupt his prayer until he finds wetness, or smells an odor, or hears a sound.'"],[557,"Satan circles around a man during his prayer to interrupt his prayer. When he tires him, he blows into his anus. When one of you feels something, let him not leave until he smells an odor or hears a sound."],[558,"Satan blows into a man's anus. When one of you feels that, let him not leave until he hears a sound or smells an odor."],[559,"It is said that the devil runs through the urethra and bites the anus. If any of you senses any of that, let him not leave until he hears a sound or finds a smell."],[560,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What if I doubt whether I have broken wind? He said: Do not stand for prayer except with certainty."],[561,"If you doubt concerning ablution before the prayer, then perform ablution; and if you doubt while you are in prayer or after the prayer, do not repeat that prayer."],[562,"If you doubt concerning ablution before entering the prayer, then perform ablution; and if you doubt while you are in prayer, then continue."],[563,"A man complained to Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that he was in doubt about ablution, saying whisperings: 'You did not wipe your head; you did not wash such-and-such.' He said: That is from the devil, so continue."],[564,"Pus and blood are the same."],[565,"Pus and blood are the same."],[566,"Perform ablution from pus, the penis, and blood. And he mentioned two things I did not mention."],[567,"Regarding a head wound on a man, he said: If blood flows, let him perform ablution; and if it appears but does not flow, then there is no ablution upon him."],[568,"ʿAṭāʾ said to me: Perform ablution from any blood that comes out and flows, and from pus and a boil, or a small blister, when it comes out and flows, ablution is required for it. He said: And if you extract a tooth and blood flows with it, then perform ablution."],[569,"I asked Ibrāhīm and Mujāhid. I said: I cut my hands and blood appeared but did not flow. Mujāhid said: Perform ablution. Ibrāhīm said: Until it flows."],[570,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ and Mujāhid about a wound on a person's hand in which blood appears but does not flow. Mujāhid said: He performs ablution. ʿAṭāʾ said: Until it flows."],[571,"Regarding a man from whom pus and blood come out, he said: He performs ablution from any blood or pus that flows or drips."],[572,"He held the same view regarding blood, and he did not consider pus to be like blood."],[573,"I asked Sa'id b. Jubayr about a pimple that was on my face; I squeezed it and blood came out, and I crushed it with my finger. He said: No ablution is required for that."],[574,"Pus and blood are the same."],[575,"He saw Ibn 'Umar squeeze a pimple between his eyes, and something came out; he crushed it between his fingers, then prayed without performing ablution."],[576,"I went to wipe with the stone. He said: I do not know except that Ayyub said: He met me with his fingernail and wounded my hands with a wound, from which blood came out, enough to cover the wound. I said to Tawus: What do you think? Should I wash it? He said: Wash it if you wish. Then he said: I consider it only a small amount, so leave it to dry."],[577,"I said to 'Ata': I insert my finger into my nose and it comes out stained with blood. He said: Then you need not perform ablution, but wash away the blood from yourself, wash your fingers, and sniff water into your nose. He said: If you insert your finger into your nose while you are in prayer and blood comes out on your finger, do not leave the prayer, but wipe your fingers with dust, and that suffices you."],[578,"I saw Abu Hurayrah insert his finger into his nose and it came out stained with blood; he crushed it, then prayed without performing ablution."],[579,"I saw Ibn al-Musayyab insert his fingers into his nose, and they came out stained with blood; he crushed it, then prayed without performing ablution. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: Ma'mar indicated how he crushed it, placing his thumb on his forefinger and then crushing."],[580,"Regarding water coming out from a wound, he said: There is nothing in it. He said: I said: Even if there is yellowness in the water? He said: No ablution is required from water."],[581,"No ablution is required from tears of the eye, nor from what flows from the nose."],[582,"Regarding a man who spits blood, he said: If blood predominates, he should perform ablution."],[583,"I asked 'Ata' about blood that comes out in the mouth. He said: If it flows in the mouth, then ablution is required for it. If the gum bleeds in the mouth until it protrudes, then perform ablution."],[584,"I saw Ibn al-Musayyib put all ten fingers into his nose, and they came out stained with blood, and he rubbed it off, then prayed without performing ablution."],[585,"I said to 'Ata': A man spits out blood; is ablution required of him? He said: No. I said: Should he rinse his mouth? He said: No, if he wishes."],[586,"I said to 'Ata': I put a stick into my mouth and blood comes out on it. He said: Then do not rinse your mouth."],[587,"Mujahid spat blood and performed ablution."],[588,"I said to 'Ata': A man with a liver ailment spits blood, or one with a chest wound discharges pus; is that a ritual impurity? He said: No, and no ablution is required of him for what is not food."],[589,"A man asked 'Alqama b. Qays, saying: I spat blood. He said: Then rinse your mouth and pray."],[590,"If blood flows from the gums into the mouth, ablution is required; and if you pull a tooth and blood flows with it until you spit, ablution is required. The gums are the flesh above the teeth."],[591,"I said to 'Ata': I spat during prayer and blood came out in the spittle. He said: Then do not rinse your mouth, if you wish. The religion is lenient. It has reached me that it used to be said: Be lenient and leniency will be shown to you."],[592,"Regarding a man who spits blood, he said: If blood is predominant, he performs ablution."],[593,"I saw 'Abd Allah b. Abi Awfa spit blood, then pray without performing ablution."],[594,"Ablution is performed on account of nosebleed when it appears and flows, whether little or much."],[595,"I said to 'Ata': A man has a nosebleed that does not stop until the prayer is about to elude him; how should he act? He said: He should plug his nostril, then stand and pray, even if he fears it might go down. I said: Then the blood will fall into his interior. He said: It will not fall into his interior, and prayer is unavoidable, even if it falls into his interior."],[596,"If a person has a nosebleed and it does not stop, he should plug his nostril and pray. If he fears it might enter his stomach, he should still pray, even if it flows."],[597,"If his nosebleed does not stop, he makes gestures (i.e., prays with minimal movement)."],[598,"If it does not stop during prayer, he stuffs it (with something)."],[599,"I said to 'Ata': I noticed blood in my nose while I was praying, but it had not come out. Should I leave the prayer? He said: No. I said: I noticed it in the nostril before the prayer, but it had not flowed. Should I blow it out? He said: If you wish, but he used to forbid touching the nose during prayer."],[600,"I had boils, so I asked my father about them. He said: If they stop bleeding (lit. are stanched), wash them and perform ablution. If they do not stop bleeding, perform ablution and pray. If something comes out, do not worry."],[601,"I and Ibn 'Abbas entered upon 'Umar when he was stabbed, and we said: The prayer! He said: No one has any share in Islam who neglects the prayer. So he prayed while his wound was flowing with blood."],[602,"Ibn 'Abbas and al-Miswar b. Makhrama entered upon 'Umar when they returned from prayer after he had been stabbed, and found that he had not prayed the dawn prayer. They said: The prayer! He said: Yes, whoever abandons the prayer has no share in Islam. So he performed ablution and then prayed while his wound was flowing with blood."],[603,"When 'Umar was stabbed, I and a group of the Ansar carried him until we brought him into his house. He remained in a single swoon until dawn broke. A man said: You will not alarm him with anything except the prayer. So we said: The prayer, O Commander of the Believers! He opened his eyes and said: Have the people prayed? We said: Yes. He said: Indeed, no one has any share in Islam who abandons the prayer. So he prayed while his wound was flowing with blood."],[604,"Zayd grew old until he lost control of his urine, so he would treat it as much as he could. When it overcame him, he would perform ablution and then pray."],[605,"A man complained to him, saying: I am in prayer and it seems to me that there is wetness on my penis. He said: May Allah curse Satan! He touches a man's penis during his prayer to make him think he has broken his ablution. When you perform ablution, sprinkle water on your private part. Then if you find something, you say: It is from the water. The man did that and it went away."],[606,"A man asked him, saying: I experience severe urine (dripping). When I say the takbir and enter prayer, I find it. Sa'id said: Obey me. Do what I command you for fifteen days: perform ablution, then enter your prayer and do not leave it."],[607,"I asked Muhammad b. Ka'b al-Qurazi: I perform ablution and then I find wetness. He said: When you perform ablution, sprinkle water on your private part. If it comes to you, say: It is from the water that I sprinkled. For it (i.e., Satan) will not leave you until he comes to you and vexes you."],[608,"When he performed wudu and finished, he took a handful of water and sprinkled it on his private part."],[609,"When he urinated and performed wudu, he sprinkled his private part."],[610,"Ibn Umar, when he performed wudu, did not wash the trace of urine, but he would sprinkle."],[611,"I saw Ibn Umar perform wudu, then sprinkle until I saw the wetness from behind him on his clothes."],[612,"I saw Ibn Umar perform wudu, then take a scoop of water and pour it between his lower garment and his belly onto his private part."],[613,"If I perform wudu, then something comes out of me after that, I do not count it with this — or he said: like this — and he placed his spit on his finger."],[614,"They did not see any harm in the wetness a man finds during the prayer as long as it does not drip."],[615,"It used to afflict me during the prayer, and I would find wetness, and it would come out of me during the prayer. I used to leave in the hour repeatedly and perform wudu. I asked Ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: Do not leave. He said: I thought that he thought it was merely being confused upon me. He said: I said: It is more than that; it reaches my foot — or he said: the ground. He said: Do not leave, and when you sense that, receive it with your garment. A brother who was sitting with him said to me: Do you know what he told you? He said: Wash your garment when you finish your prayer, and I did not hear it myself. He said: I did what he said, and it did not take long before it went away from me."],[616,"He used to regard dripping as invalidating."],[617,"A man asked him: I find wetness while I am in prayer; should I leave? He said: No, not until it is a drop. I think he said that day: Is there anyone who does not find wetness?"],[618,"I said to Ata: I found a trace of semen before Zuhr but did not look at it until I finished from Maghrib, and I found semen at the tip of my penis. He said: Repeat all of your prayer. I said: What do you think if I prayed Zuhr, Asr, and Maghrib, then turned back, and found madhy but did not have doubt before or after that? He said: Do not repeat it, for perhaps you emitted madhy after you prayed. I said: I had intercourse, then went out and found a trace before Zuhr but did not leave until I returned at Isha and found madhy that had dried on the tip of the urethra. I had dinner and did not hurry from my dinner, then went to the mosque and prayed Zuhr, Asr, and Maghrib. He said: I repeated them, and he saw that I had done correctly in what I repeated."],[619,"Qays said to Ata: What do you think about madhy; would you merely wipe it? He said: No, madhy is more severe than urine; it is washed thoroughly. Then he began to inform us at that time, saying: Aish b. Anas, the brother of Sa'd b. Layth, informed me: Ali b. Abi Talib, Ammar b. Yasir, and al-Miqdad b. al-Aswad discussed madhy. Ali said: I am a man who emits much madhy, so ask the Messenger of God ﷺ about that, for I am too shy to ask him about it because of his daughter being with me. Were it not for his daughter's position, I would ask him. Aish said: So one of the two men asked, Ammar or al-Miqdad. Qays said: Aish named for me the one of them who asked the Prophet ﷺ about that, but I forgot him. The Prophet ﷺ said: That is madhy. When one of you finds it, let him wash that part from himself, then perform wudu and do it well, then sprinkle his private part. He said: I asked Ata about the saying of the Prophet ﷺ: \"He washes that from himself.\" He said: Where the madhy is; he washes from it, or his entire penis? He said: Rather, where the madhy is from it only."],[620,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if I find pre-ejaculatory fluid and I wash my penis; should I scrutinize my private parts in that case? He said: No, that is sufficient for you."],[621,"I said to 'Ata': I have intercourse with my wife, and I find pre-ejaculatory fluid afterwards, or at the time, after intercourse but not during it. So I shake off my penis and I perform ghusl, and before noon I find something suspicious of moisture, for I find it on my thigh and on the testicles. Should I investigate whether I find anything, or do I have a dispensation not to investigate? He said: If you are someone who often has pre-ejaculatory fluid, then investigate, but if you are not, then do not investigate."],[622,"That 'Ali commanded him to ask the Prophet ﷺ about a man when he draws near to his wife and pre-ejaculatory fluid comes out of him: what is upon him? For his daughter is with me, and I am ashamed to ask him. Al-Miqdad said: So I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about that, and he said: When one of you finds that, let him sprinkle his private part and perform wudu' as he does for prayer."],[623,"'Ali said to al-Miqdad: Ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for me about a man who plays with his wife and speaks with her and then emits pre-ejaculatory fluid. Were it not that I feel ashamed, and that his daughter is under my care, I would have asked him. So al-Miqdad asked, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Let him wash his penis and perform wudu', then let him sprinkle water on his private part."],[624,"That 'Ali said: I said to al-Miqdad: Ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for were it not that his daughter is under my care, I would have asked him about that: when a man draws near to his wife and emits pre-ejaculatory fluid, but he does not control that and does not touch her. So al-Miqdad asked, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When one of you emits pre-ejaculatory fluid and does not touch her, let him wash his penis and his testicles. And 'Urwah used to say: Let him perform wudu' when he wants to pray, like his wudu' for prayer."],[625,"'Ali said: I said to al-Miqdad: Ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for were it not that his daughter is under my care, I would have asked him about that: when a man draws near to his wife and emits pre-ejaculatory fluid, but he does not control that and does not touch her. So al-Miqdad asked, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When one of you emits pre-ejaculatory fluid and does not touch her, let him wash his penis and his testicles. And 'Urwah used to say: Let him perform wudu' when he wants to pray, like his wudu' for prayer."],[626,"That 'Ali said: I was a man who often emitted pre-ejaculatory fluid, so I felt ashamed to ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. So I commanded a man, and he asked him, and he said: For it, wudu' is required."],[627,"Indeed, something like a pearl comes out of one of us, so when one of you finds that, let him wash his penis and perform wudu'."],[628,"Regarding pre-ejaculatory fluid: he should wash his penis and perform wudu' as he does for prayer."],[629,"That 'Uthman was asked about pre-ejaculatory fluid, and he said: That is the dripping; for it, wudu' is required."],[630,"Regarding pre-ejaculatory fluid: he should wash his glans."],[631,"I asked Ibn 'Abbas. He said: While I was on my riding camel, between sleeping and waking, desire took hold of me, and something came out of my penis until it filled my sandal and what was around it. He said: Wash your penis and what it touched, then perform wudu' as you do for prayer."],[632,"About pre-ejaculatory fluid (madhy), post-urinal fluid (wady), and semen (maniyy): from semen comes the major ablution (ghusl), and from pre-ejaculatory fluid and post-urinal fluid comes the minor ablution (wudu); he washes his glans and performs wudu."],[633,"They are three: pre-ejaculatory fluid (madhy), post-urinal fluid (wady), and semen (maniyy). As for pre-ejaculatory fluid, it is that which occurs with urine and after it; in it is washing the private part and wudu as well. As for semen, it is the gushing water in which there is desire, and from it comes the child; in it is ghusl."],[634,"615 612 - Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar, from Qatadah, the like of it. Hidden text: They are three: pre-ejaculatory fluid (madhy), post-urinal fluid (wady), and semen (maniyy). As for pre-ejaculatory fluid, it is that which occurs with urine and after it; in it is washing the private part and wudu as well. As for semen, it is the gushing water in which there is desire, and from it comes the child; in it is ghusl."],[635,"Indeed I find pre-ejaculatory fluid on my thigh flowing down while I am praying, and I do not care about that."],[637,"Indeed I find pre-ejaculatory fluid on my thigh flowing down while I am praying, and I do not care about that."],[638,"If it flowed on my thigh, I would not turn away."],[639,"Umar said while he was on the pulpit: Indeed something descends like pearls, or like a bead, and I do not care about it."],[640,"I said to 'Ata': \"An ulcer on my forearm.\" He said: \"Do not expose it; let the water touch it.\" I said: \"What do you think if around the wound there is blood and pus, but it has stuck to the edge of the wound?\" He said: \"Follow it with a piece of wool or cotton containing water and wash it.\" I said: \"Is there no concession for me not to touch it or clean it?\" He said: \"Do not pray while you have blood on you.\""],[641,"I said to 'Ata': \"An ulcer on my forearm. What do you think if the wound is open with its mouth?\" He said: \"Do not insert your hand into it, but let the water touch what is around it.\""],[642,"I said to 'Ata': \"A man with a broken hand bandaged over it.\" He said: \"He wipes over the bandage alone, and that suffices him. He said: There is no escape from touching the bandage. His hand's bandage is in the place of his hand; he wipes over the bandage with a wiping. If he misses anything of it, it is no problem.\""],[643,"I said to 'Ata': \"What do you think if there is a bandage on a boil on a man's forearm, or a small ulcer? Should he wipe over the bandage or remove it?\" He said: \"If it is small, I prefer that he remove the bandages.\""],[644,"About the fracture of the hand, the leg, and everything severe: if it is bandaged, then Allah is most accepting of the excuse, so he should wipe the bandages."],[645,"I asked 'Ata': \"Should I wipe over splints?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[646,"I asked Ibrahim about wiping over bandages. He said: Wipe over them; God is most excusing of excuses."],[647,"One of my forearms was broken, so I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he commanded me to wipe over the bandages."],[648,"I struck a camel of mine and wounded myself, so I asked Sa'id b. Jubayr, and he said: Wash what is around it, and do not let water come near it."],[649,"If the wound is bandaged, wipe around the bandage."],[650,"629 626 - Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar, from a man from the people of the Jazira, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, something similar. Hidden text: If the wound is bandaged, wipe around the bandage."],[651,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if my ear hurts and it becomes hard for me to wash it? He said: Do not cleanse it thoroughly; just let water touch it."],[652,"We entered upon Abu al-'Aliyah al-Riyahi while he was in pain, and they performed ablution for him. When one of his feet remained, he said: Wipe over this one, for it is sick; it had redness on it. And redness is swelling."],[653,"Regarding worms that come out of a person like gourd seeds, he said: There is no ablution required for it."],[654,"Regarding worms that come out of a person, he said: There is no ablution required for it."],[655,"Regarding worms that come out of a person, one performs ablution because of it."],[656,"635 632 - Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri, from a man, from 'Ata', who said it. Abd al-Razzaq informed us, and we adopt it. Hidden text: Regarding worms that come out of a person, one performs ablution because of it. Abd al-Razzaq said: And we adopt it."],[657,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was invited to food, so he ate a shoulder, then the muezzin came to him, and he stood for prayer and did not perform ablution."],[658,"He saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cut off a piece from a shoulder, eat it, and when the muezzin came to him, he put down the knife, then stood for prayer without performing ablution."],[659,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution, then cut off a shoulder and ate, then proceeded to prayer without performing ablution."],[660,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ eat from the shoulder of a sheep, then stand for prayer without performing ablution."],[661,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was eating meat from a bone, the muezzin came to him, so he put it down and stood for prayer without touching water."],[662,"Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, informed him that she served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a roasted side, and he ate from it, then stood for prayer without performing ablution."],[663,"Bread and meat were served to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then he called for water for ablution and performed ablution, then prayed Zuhr. Then he called for the remainder of his food and ate. Then he stood for prayer without performing ablution. He said: Then I entered with Abu Bakr, and he said: Is there anything? By Allah, he found nothing. He said: Where is your sheep? It was brought to him, and he took hold of it, then milked it for us and made for us hisba [1], so we ate. Then we stood for prayer without performing ablution. Then I entered with Umar, and here a large bowl was placed containing bread and meat, and here a large bowl containing bread and meat. Umar ate, then stood for prayer without performing ablution."],[664,"Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, the like of it. Hidden text: Bread and meat were served to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then he called for water for ablution and performed ablution, then prayed Zuhr. Then he called for the remainder of his food and ate. Then he stood for prayer without performing ablution. He said: Then I entered with Abu Bakr, and he said: Is there anything? By Allah, he found nothing. He said: Where is your sheep? It was brought to him, and he took hold of it, then milked it for us and made for us hisba, so we ate. Then we stood for prayer without performing ablution. Then I entered with Umar, and here a large bowl was placed containing bread and meat, and here a large bowl containing bread and meat. Umar ate, then stood for prayer without performing ablution."],[665,"He would not perform ablution from what fire had touched."],[666,"He heard Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayrah, and he saw Abu Hurayrah perform ablution, then say: O Ibn Abbas, do you know from what I am performing ablution? He said: No. He said: I performed ablution from pieces of aqit (dried curd) that I ate. Ibn Abbas said: I do not care from what you performed ablution. I bear witness that I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ eat a shoulder of meat, then stand for prayer without performing ablution. He said: And Sulayman was present at that from both of them."],[667,"Uthman b. Affan ate food that fire had touched, then proceeded to prayer without performing ablution. He said: I think he said: Then Uthman said: I performed ablution as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution, ate as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate, and prayed as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed."],[668,"Abu Hurayrah said: Ablution is required from what fire has touched. Marwan said: How can anyone be asked while the wives of our Prophet ﷺ and our mothers are among us? He said: So he sent me to Umm Salamah, and I asked her. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me after he had performed ablution, and I handed him a bone with meat or a shoulder, and he ate. Then he stood for prayer without performing ablution."],[669,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out for prayer and saw one of his young boys with a meaty bone. He took it, bit a piece from it, then proceeded and prayed."],[670,"Ibn Abbas would stay overnight on Friday in the house of his maternal aunt Maymunah and would narrate. A man said to him: Tell me about what fire has touched. Ibn Abbas said: I will not tell you except what I saw of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He and his companions were in his house, then the muezzin came to him and he rose for prayer. When he was at the door, he was met with a large dish containing bread and meat. He went back with his companions, and he ate and they ate. Then he returned to prayer and did not perform ablution."],[671,"Abu Bakr al-Siddiq ؓ ate a shoulder of meat or a foreleg, then stood and prayed for us, and he did not perform ablution. Ata said: I think that Jabir said: And he did not rinse his mouth nor wash his hand. He said: I think he said: He wiped his hand."],[672,"Abu Bakr ate bread and meat, then stood for prayer and did not perform ablution."],[673,"We ate with Abu Bakr bread and meat, then he stood for prayer and did not perform ablution. Ma'mar said: I believe he said: Except that he rinsed his mouth."],[674,"A large bowl was brought to us while we were with Ibn Mas'ud. He ordered it and it was placed in the road. He ate from it and we ate with him, and he began inviting whoever passed by. Then we proceeded to prayer, and he did no more than wash the tips of his fingers and rinse his mouth, then he prayed."],[675,"Umar ate from a large bowl, then stood and prayed and did not perform ablution."],[676,"A shallow bowl was brought to us from the house of Ibn Mas'ud containing bread and meat, and we ate. Ibn Mas'ud was with us, and he rinsed his mouth and washed his fingers at sunset."],[677,"Fire is only God's blessing. It does not make anything lawful or unlawful. There is no ablution from what fire has touched, and no ablution from what enters. Ablution is only from what exits from a person."],[678,"We would sometimes come to Ibn Abbas, and he would bring his dinner near sunset. He would dine and we would dine, and he would do no more than wash his palms and rinse his mouth, and he would not perform ablution. Then he would pray."],[679,"He was asked about ablution from what fire has touched, and he said: Fire only increases it in goodness."],[680,"We were with Ibn Abbas in his house, and he brought food near to us, and the call to prayer was given. He said: When this is present, begin with it. So the people ate, and some of them said: Should we not perform ablution? Ibn Abbas said to him: It is said: Ablution is from what fire has touched. He said: Fire only increases it in goodness. If fire had not touched it, you would not eat it. He said: Then he led us in prayer on a carpet - or a mat - that covered his house."],[681,"Were it not for licking the lips, I would not care to rinse my mouth."],[682,"Ablution is only from what comes out, and fasting is from what goes in, and it is not from what comes out."],[683,"I came to Madinah and had dinner with Abu Talhah before Maghrib, and with him were a group of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ, among them Ubayy b. Ka'b. When Maghrib time came, I got up to perform ablution, so they said: 'What is this Iraqi practice you have introduced? You perform ablution from good foods?' Then they all prayed Maghrib and did not perform ablution."],[684,"I asked 'Abidah about what fire has touched, so he ordered a sheep and it was slaughtered. Then a need made him hurry—I think the commander summoned him—so he called for milk, ghee, and bread, and he ate and we ate with him. Then he stood for prayer and prayed without performing ablution. Ibn Sirin said: 'I thought he only wanted to show me that.'"],[685,"He used to eat tharid and then pray without performing ablution."],[686,"I used to eat tharid and meat with Abu Umamah, and he would pray without performing ablution."],[687,"Ablution is only from what comes out. He said: 'And not from what goes in, because it enters while it is good—you have no obligation from it—and it comes out while it is impure; from it you must perform ablution and purification.'"],[688,"Abu Bakr ate a sheep's shoulder or foreleg, then stood for prayer and did not perform ablution. It was said to him: 'Shall we bring you water for ablution?' He said: 'I have not broken my wudu.'"],[689,"He entered upon Umm Habibah, and she gave him sawiq to drink. Then he stood to pray, so she said to him: 'Perform ablution, O son of my brother, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Perform ablution from what fire has touched.'"],[690,"Zayd b. Thabit and 'A'ishah used to perform ablution from what fire had touched."],[691,"Perform ablution from what fire has touched."],[693,"I passed by Abu Hurayrah while he was performing ablution, and he said: 'Do you know what I am performing ablution from? From pieces of aqit cheese that I ate. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Perform ablution from what fire has touched.'"],[694,"He found Abu Hurayrah performing ablution on the roof of the mosque, and Abu Hurayrah said: 'I am performing ablution only from pieces of aqit that I ate, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Perform ablution from what fire has touched.'"],[695,"I do not care if I plunged my hands into dung and blood or ate food that fire had touched, then prayed and did not perform ablution. He said: And this is what al-Hasan used to follow."],[696,"I saw Anas b. Malik coming out from al-Hajjaj's presence talking to himself. I said: What is the matter with you, O Abu Hamzah? He said: I came out from this man's presence when he called for food for the people, and he ate and they ate. Then they stood up to pray without performing ablution – or he said: without touching water. Anas used to perform ablution after eating what fire had changed."],[697,"He used to perform ablution after eating what fire had touched, even to the point of performing ablution after eating sugar."],[698,"Ibn Umar used to perform ablution with hot water."],[701,"Abu Hurayrah used to perform ablution after eating what fire had touched. This reached Ibn Abbas, so he sent word to him saying: What do you think if I took some good oil and applied it to my beard, would I have performed ablution? Abu Hurayrah said: O son of my brother, when you narrate a hadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, do not strike examples for it in argument."],[702,"Perform ablution after eating what fire has touched, rinse your mouth after milk, and do not rinse your mouth after fruit."],[703,"Urwah reported from Aishah that she used to perform ablution after eating what fire had touched."],[706,"He used to dislike performing ablution with milk."],[707,"He used to dislike performing ablution with milk."],[708,"I said to Ata: Would you perform ablution after eating meat and wash your hands of its traces? He said: Yes. I said: With soapwort or with water? He said: With water; soapwort is something they have innovated. I said: What do you think about grease – clarified butter, sirup, or fat – that you have eaten from: would you wash your hands of it or rinse your mouth? He said: No. I said: What about bread alone? He said: I would not rinse my mouth from it nor wash my hands."],[709,"I said to 'Ata': 'What about soft fruits and bananas?' He said: 'I would not wash my hands from them, nor rinse my mouth, unless something from them sticks to my hands and bothers me. As for anything else, no.' I said: 'Why do you rinse your mouth from meat specifically then?' He said: 'Meat gets between the molars and the teeth.' I said: 'Do you think that if you knew that none of it entered between your teeth, would you mind not rinsing your mouth?' He said: 'No, by God, I would never mind not rinsing my mouth from it.'"],[710,"Abu Bakr came to us with bread and meat and we ate. Then he called for water, rinsed his mouth, and did not perform ablution. Then he stood up for prayer."],[711,"Perform ablution after eating what fire has touched, rinse your mouth after milk, and do not rinse your mouth after fruit."],[712,"The Prophet ﷺ drank milk and rinsed his mouth, and said: 'It has fat.'"],[713,"Abu Umamah used to rinse his mouth after milk, then pray."],[714,"Ibn 'Abbas drank milk, then stood up for prayer. I said: 'Will you not rinse your mouth?' He said: 'I do not care. Be lenient and God will be lenient with you.'"],[715,"When a man has been cupped, he should bathe."],[716,"Ibn 'Abbas drank milk, then stood up for prayer. I said: 'Will you not rinse your mouth?' He said: 'I do not care at all. Be lenient and leniency will be shown to you.'"],[718,"When a man has been cupped, he should bathe."],[719,"Bilal wiped over his leather socks, and it was said to him: 'What is this?' He said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiping over the leather socks and the head covering.'"],[720,"Ibn 'Abbas drank finely ground barley drink in the mosque of Basra. Al-Ghadban b. al-Qaba'thari said to him: 'Will you not rinse your mouth?' Ibn 'Abbas said: 'Be lenient and leniency will be shown to you,' and he did not rinse his mouth."],[721,"We set out with the Messenger of God ﷺ to Khaybar until we were at al-Sahba', and between it and Khaybar was an afternoon's journey. The Messenger of God ﷺ called the people to bring their provisions, and nothing was brought except barley drink. He chewed and we chewed, then he stood up and rinsed his mouth, and we rinsed our mouths, and he prayed Zuhr or 'Asr."],[722,"I disapproved of Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas, while he was governor in Kufa, wiping over the leather socks. He said: Is there any harm on me in that? — while he was residing in Kufa. 'Abd Allah said: When he said that, I knew that he knows something about that which I do not know, so I did not answer him back. Then we met with 'Umar, and Sa'd said: Ask your father for a ruling about what you disapproved of regarding the matter of the leather socks. I said: Have you considered if one of us performs ablution while wearing leather socks on his feet — is there any harm on him in wiping over them?}"],[723,"I said to Ata: Do you rinse your mouth after drinks: nabidh, honey, sawiq, and milk? He said: No, by God, I drink nabidh in the mosque and I do not rinse my mouth until I pray. A man said to him: Sawiq with coarse flour? He said: No, that is something that sticks to the mouth."],[724,"On the night of the jinn, two men among them stayed behind and said: We will witness the dawn prayer with you, O Messenger of God. The Prophet ﷺ said: Do you have water with you? I said: I have no water with me, but I have a vessel with nabidh. The Prophet ﷺ said: Good dates and purifying water. So he performed ablution."],[725,"Do not perform ablution with milk or nabidh."],[727,"Regarding a man who has been cupped, he said: He washes off the blood and performs ablution. I said: Do you think that if someone shaves his head and has been cupped, a full bath is obligatory on him? He said: No."],[728,"I entered upon Ibrahim while he was being cupped and I said: Will you bathe today, O Abu Imran? He said: No, but I will wash the mark of the cups."],[729,"A man should bathe when he has been cupped."],[730,"Regarding the one who has been cupped: he washes the mark of the cups, then performs ablution, then prays."],[731,"I came to Aisha to ask her about wiping over the leather socks. She said: You should go to Ibn Abi Talib and ask him, for he used to travel with the Messenger of God ﷺ. So I went to him and asked him, and he said: The Messenger of God ﷺ set three days and their nights for the traveler, and one night for the resident."],[732,"Ali used to consider it recommended to bathe after cupping."],[733,"It is more preferable to me to extend it to the elbows."],[734,"A man should bathe when he has been cupped."],[735,"If a man performs ablution and finishes some of his limbs while some remain, then he breaks wind, the ablution is to be started anew."],[737,"Ata was asked about a fringed towel, whether a man may wipe water with it. He refused to allow that and said: It is something innovated. I said: What do you think if I want the towel to remove the cold of the water from me? He said: Then there is no harm in it."],[738,"They both disliked the towel after ablution for prayer."],[739,"When you perform ablution, do not towel yourself."],[740,"He disliked wiping with a towel after ablution, but did not dislike it when bathing from janabah."],[741,"Ibn Abi Layla, Mujahid, and Sa'id b. Jubayr used to dislike the towel after ablution for prayer."],[743,"Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu al-Aliyah al-Riyahi both disliked that."],[744,"Hasan b. Ali performed ablution, then called for a cloth to dry himself with. A woman saw him and said: I saw him doing that and I detested him. Then at night I saw in a dream as if I was vomiting my liver."],[745,"He had a rag with which he dried himself after ablution."],[746,"Abdullah b. al-Harith b. Nawfal had a rag and he used to dry himself with it when he performed ablution."],[747,"He repeats whatever is still within its time."],[748,"'Alqama had a clean cloth with which he would dry himself when he performed ablution."],[749,"There is no harm in a man wiping his face after ablution with a towel before he prays, or he said: with a garment."],[751,"We heard that if a man performs ablution with water with which his companion has already performed ablution, it is not valid for him; but if he performs an ablution on top of an ablution, it is valid for him."],[752,"Jarīr b. ʿAbd Allāh used to order his family to perform ablution from the leftover water of his siwāk."],[753,"If you scratch some part of your body while you are in a state of ablution, then you wipe it with saliva, wash that spot with water."],[754,"He used to order the tailor to moisten the threads with water and not moisten them with his saliva."],[755,"It has been said regarding saliva; so take the easiest course in it."],[756,"A man asked him: I insert my finger into my mouth and pass it over my teeth like a siwāk, then I insert it into my ablution water? He said: No harm."],[757,"If a vessel is left overnight uncovered with no lid on it, Iblīs spits into it, or Iblīs expectorates into it. I mentioned that to Ibrāhīm and he said: Or he drinks from it."],[758,"Regarding a man whose forearm was cut off, he said: There is no ablution for his upper arms, but only where ablution reaches on the upper arm at all."],[759,"If anything remains of the places of ablution, he washes it."],[760,"I heard that the amputee performs ablution on his extremities."],[761,"Whoever performs ablution and then finishes his ablution and says: 'Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah; I seek Your forgiveness and turn to You in repentance' - a seal is placed on it, then it is placed beneath the Throne, and it will not be broken until the Day of Resurrection. And whoever recites Surah al-Kahf as it was revealed, then encounters the Dajjal, he will not be given power over him and he will have no way against him, and a light will be raised for him from where he recites it to Makkah."],[762,"When a man performs ablution, he should say: 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His messenger. O Allah, make me among those who repent and make me among those who purify themselves.'"],[763,"Bilal wiped over his leather socks, and it was said to him: 'What is this?' He said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiping over the leather socks and the head covering.'"],[764,"A man entered upon Bilal - or he said: Usama; the doubt is from Abd al-Razzaq - while he was performing ablution under a waterspout and wiped over his leather socks. The man said to him: 'What is this?' He said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiped over the leather socks and the head covering.' I said: 'What is the waterspout?' He said: 'The spout.'"],[765,"That my foot be cut off is dearer to me than that I wipe over the leather socks."],[766,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiping over the leather socks and over the head covering."],[768,"Wipe over the leather socks or over the head covering - or a head covering."],[769,"I saw Anas b. Malik urinate, then he got up and performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks and over his turban, then he got up and prayed an obligatory prayer."],[770,"He used to perform ablution while wearing a turban, pushing it back from his head without untying it, then he wiped his head, passing water with one palm over the crown only, then he returned the turban."],[771,"Two things I do not ask anyone about: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ wiping over leather socks and the khimār."],[772,"He used to wipe over his turban. He said: He would place his hand on his forelock, then pass his hand over the turban."],[773,"I said to 'Ata': Has any allowance reached you for wiping over gloves or for raising [them]? He said: No."],[775,"He used to remove the turban, then wipe his head."],[776,"I saw Anas b. Mālik go to the privy, then come out wearing a white buttoned cap, and he wiped over the cap and over two black woolen socks of his, then prayed."],[777,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ wiping over his leather socks."],[778,"I was with the Messenger of God ﷺ on a journey. When he was partway, he fell behind and I fell behind with him carrying the water vessel. He relieved himself, then came to me and I poured water over his hands. That was at the time of the dawn prayer. When he washed his face and wanted to wash his forearms, the sleeve of his jubbah was tight, and he was wearing a Syrian jubbah. He said: So he brought his hands out from under the jubbah, washed his forearms, then performed ablution wiping over his leather socks. He said: Then we reached the people, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf had already led them in one rakʿah. I went to inform him, but he said: Leave him. Then he finished. The Prophet ﷺ stood and prayed one rakʿah. The people were alarmed at that, and he said: You did right, or he said: You did well."],[779,"He raided with the Messenger of God ﷺ in the expedition of Tabūk. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ went to relieve himself before the morning prayer, and I carried a water vessel with him. When the Messenger of God ﷺ returned to me, I began pouring from the vessel over his hands. He washed his hands three times, then rinsed his mouth and nose, and washed his face. Then he tried to pull his jubbah off his forearms, but the sleeves of his jubbah were tight, so he inserted his hands into the jubbah until he brought his forearms out from the bottom of the jubbah. Then he washed his forearms up to the elbows, and performed ablution wiping over his leather socks. Then he came, and I came with him, and found that the people had put ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf forward. The Prophet ﷺ caught one of the two rakʿahs and prayed the other rakʿah with the people. When ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said the salām, the Messenger of God ﷺ stood to complete his prayer, and that alarmed the Muslims, so they said subḥān Allāh a lot. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, he turned to them and said: You did well—or he said: You did right—commending them for praying the prayer at its proper time."],[780,"I was with the Messenger of God ﷺ on a journey. He said: Stay behind, O Mughīrah, and go on, O people. He said: Then he went and relieved himself. Then I followed him with a vessel of water. When he finished, I poured from it over him. He washed his face, then tried to bring his hands out of a Byzantine jubbah he was wearing, but the sleeves of the jubbah were tight, so he brought his hands out from under the jubbah and washed them, then wiped over his leather socks, then prayed."],[781,"I was with the Messenger of God ﷺ on a journey. He relieved himself, then I brought a vessel of water while he was wearing a Syrian jubbah. He could not bring his hand out from its sleeves, so he brought his hand out from its bottom, then performed ablution wiping over his leather socks."],[782,"I was with the Messenger of God ﷺ when he urinated standing on a people's garbage dump — meaning its refuse — then he moved aside. I brought him water, and he performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks."],[783,"I asked 'Ata' about wiping over the leather socks. He said: Three [days] for the traveler, and one day for the resident."],[784,"Wipe over them as long as your foot stays attached to them. Were the leather socks of the Muhajirun and the Ansar anything but tattered, split, and patched?"],[785,"If something emerges from any of the places of ablution, do not wipe."],[786,"Someone who heard 'Ali — when he was asked about wiping over the leather socks — informed me that he said: Yes, and over the sandals, and over the turban."],[787,"I saw Jarir b. Abd Allah performing wudu from a water basin."],[788,"Ibn Mas'ud used to wipe over his leather socks and wipe over his socks."],[789,"Additional text: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ wiping over the leather socks. Jarir said: My Islam came after Surat al-Ma'idah was revealed."],[790,"I helped the Messenger of God ﷺ perform ablution, and he wiped over his leather socks after Surat al-Ma'idah was revealed."],[791,"Ibn 'Umar saw Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas wiping over his leather socks, and 'Abd Allah disapproved of that. Sa'd said: 'Abd Allah disapproved of my wiping over my leather socks. 'Umar said: Let it not stir in the heart of any Muslim man that he may perform ablution over his leather socks, even if he has come from defecating."],[792,"'Umar said to 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar: Your paternal uncle — meaning Sa'd — knows more than I. If you put your feet into the leather socks while they are pure, then wipe over them, even if you come from defecating."],[793,"Someone who saw 'Ali wiping over his sandals informed him."],[794,"Wipe over the leather socks as long as you do not remove them; he would not set a time limit for them."],[795,"That he entered upon Ammar b. Yasir after he had come out of the privy. He performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks."],[796,"Even if he comes from defecation and urination."],[797,"When a man puts his feet into the leather socks while they are pure, then goes to relieve himself, then performs ablution for prayer, he should wipe over his leather socks. He said that Umar commanded that."],[798,"When he performed tayammum, he struck his hands once on the dust, then wiped his face, then struck another time, then wiped his arms up to the elbows, and did not shake his hands off from the dust."],[799,"He repeats whatever is still within its time."],[800,"Abu Ayyub al-Ansari used to give the ruling of wiping over the leather socks, but he himself would not wipe. It was said to him, and he said, \"Do you think I would give you a ruling whose benefit is yours and whose sin is upon me? Rather, purification has been made beloved to me.\""],[801,"I asked Ibn Tawus: How did his father speak about wiping over the leather socks? He said, \"He used to narrate the hadith of Sa'd and Ibn Umar.\""],[803,"I said to 'Ata': Has any allowance reached you for wiping over gloves or for raising [them]? He said: No."],[804,"I saw Ali urinate, then wipe over his socks and sandals, then stand and pray."],[805,"Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari used to wipe over a pair of woolen socks of his and his sandals."],[806,"He urinated while we were with him, then he wiped over his socks and sandals, then he prayed."],[809,"I saw al-Bara' b. 'Azib wiping over his socks and sandals."],[811,"I said to 'Ata': Is wiping only over the two upper parts of the leather socks? He said: Yes. I said: Should I not wipe the bottoms of the leather socks? He said: No, only their tops."],[814,"Wiping over the two socks is like wiping over the two leather socks."],[815,"I said to 'Ata': Is wiping only over the two upper parts of the leather socks? He said: Yes. I said: Should I not wipe the bottoms of the leather socks? He said: No, only their tops."],[816,"I saw 'Ali urinate standing until it foamed, then he performed ablution and wiped over his sandals, then he entered the mosque and took off his sandals, placing them in his sleeve, then he prayed. Ma'mar said: If I wished, I could narrate that Zayd b. Aslam narrated to me from 'Ata' b. Yasar, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet ﷺ did as 'Ali did, and I would have done so."],[817,"I saw 'Ali urinate while standing until it foamed, wearing a black cloak of his, then he called for water and performed ablution and wiped over his sandals, then he stood up and removed them, then he prayed Zuhr."],[818,"That my foot be cut off is dearer to me than that I wipe over the leather socks."],[819,"He used to put on the hairless leather sandals for ablution."],[820,"I said to Ibn Umar: I have seen you wearing these hairless leather sandals. He said: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ wearing them and performing ablution in them."],[821,"I asked Aisha about wiping over the leather socks. She said: Ask Ibn Abi Talib, for he used to travel with the Messenger of God ﷺ. So we asked Ali and he said: For the traveler three [days], and for the resident one night."],[822,"I came to Aisha to ask her about wiping over the leather socks. She said: You should go to Ibn Abi Talib and ask him, for he used to travel with the Messenger of God ﷺ. So I went to him and asked him, and he said: The Messenger of God ﷺ set three days and their nights for the traveler, and one night for the resident."],[823,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set three days for the traveler and one day for the resident. By God, if the questioner had continued in his questioning, he would have made it five."],[824,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set three days for the traveler and one day for the resident. By God, if the questioner had continued in his questioning, he would have made it five."],[825,"I came to Safwan b. Assal to ask him about wiping over the leather socks. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ used to command us during travel not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights, except for major ritual impurity, but [they may be wiped] from sleep, defecation, and urination."],[826,"I came to Safwan b. Assal al-Muradi and he said: What do you need? I said: I have come seeking knowledge. He said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: No one goes out of his house in search of knowledge but that the angels lower their wings in approval of what he is doing. I said: I have come to ask you about wiping over the leather socks. He said: Yes. I was in the army that the Messenger of God ﷺ sent, and he commanded us to wipe over the leather socks when we had put them on in a state of purity: three [days] when we traveled and one night when we stayed, and not to remove them because of defecation, urination, or sleep, and not to remove them except because of major ritual impurity. He said: And I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: In the west there is an open gate whose width is a journey of seventy years; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its direction."],[828,"I came to Safwan and he said: What brought you? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: The angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge in approval of what he seeks. I said: The matter of wiping over the leather socks after defecation and urination has been troubling me, and you are a man from among the companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ, so I have come to ask you about that. Have you heard anything from him about it? He said: Yes. He used to command us, when we were traveling or when we were travelers, not to remove our leather socks for three days with their nights, except because of major ritual impurity, but [they may be wiped] from defecation, urination, and sleep. I said to him: Did you hear him mention desire? He said: Yes. While I was with him on a journey, a Bedouin called out to him with a loud voice—or he said: a resounding voice; Ibn Uyayna was unsure—and said to him: O Muhammad. He answered him in a similar manner to his speech and said: Wait. What do you think of a man who loves a people but has not yet caught up with them? He said: On the Day of Resurrection he will be with those he loves. He said: And he kept talking to us until he said: In the direction of the west there is a gate whose width is a journey of seventy years; God opened it for repentance on the day He created the heavens and the earth, and He will not close it until the sun rises from its direction."],[829,"We were in Azerbaijan and Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to us to wipe over the leather socks for three [days] when we traveled, and one night when we stayed."],[830,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to the people of al-Massisa to remove the leather socks every three [days]."],[831,"The traveler wipes over the leather socks for three days and their nights, and for the resident, a day and a night."],[833,"I traveled with ʿAbd Allāh b. Masʿūd three [days] to Madinah, and he did not remove his leather socks."],[834,"For the traveler, three days, he wipes over the leather socks, and for the resident, a day."],[836,"For the resident, a day until night, and for the traveler, three nights."],[837,"Wipe over the leather socks as long as you do not remove them; he would not set a time limit for them."],[839,"We went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ on one of his journeys, until when we were at al-Bayda - or at Dhat al-Jaysh - my necklace broke. She said: The Prophet ﷺ stayed to search for it, and the people stayed with him, and they had no water. The people came to Abu Bakr and said: Do you not see what Aisha has done? She has detained the Prophet ﷺ and the people, and they have no water. She said: Then Abu Bakr came while the Prophet ﷺ had his head resting on my thigh. He said: You have detained the Prophet ﷺ and the people, and they are not at a water source, and they have no water. Abu Bakr reproached me and said to me whatever God willed that he should say, and he began poking me with his hand in my side, but nothing prevented me from moving except the place of the Messenger of God ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ slept on my thigh until morning without water. Then God revealed the verse of tayammum, and they performed tayammum. Usayd b. Hudayr said: This is not the first of your blessings, O family of Abu Bakr. She said: Then we made the camel that I had been riding get up, and we found the necklace under it."],[840,"Regarding wiping over the leather socks, he said: 'If you put them on while both are pure with fresh water, then you wipe from [the time of] the minor impurity until the same time of the next day.' He said: 'If you perform ablution at dawn and do not break your wudu until the afternoon, you may wipe over them until the afternoon of the next day.'"],[841,"I was present when Saʿd and Ibn ʿUmar argued before ʿUmar about wiping over the leather socks. ʿUmar said: 'He wipes over them until the like of that hour of his day and night.'"],[842,"We would wipe over them, then stand and pray."],[843,"He used to narrate, then wipe over two overshoes of his made of felt, wiping over them, then take them off. When he stood for prayer, he would put them on and pray."],[844,"When he takes them off, he must repeat ablution; his ablution is invalidated."],[845,"When he takes them off, he must repeat ablution; his ablution is invalidated. When a man wipes over his leather socks and then removes them, he must wash his feet."],[846,"When you remove them, wash your feet."],[847,"Ibn Abbas was asked: Which earth is best? He said: Cultivated land."],[848,"I said to Ata: 'So perform tayammum with clean earth.' He said: The cleanest around you."],[849,"I said to Ata: How is tayammum? He said: You place the palms of your hands on the ground, then shake them by striking one against the other, then wipe your face and your palms with a single wipe only for the face and palms. I said: The beard, do I wipe over it with the face? He said: Yes, with the face."],[850,"When he performed tayammum, he struck his hands once on the dust, then wiped his face, then struck another time, then wiped his arms up to the elbows, and did not shake his hands off from the dust."],[851,"I was far from water, became junub, and performed tayammum. Something arose in my heart, so I went to the house of Abū Dharr but did not find him. I went to the mosque, where his appearance had been described to me, and there he was standing praying; I recognized him by the description. I greeted him, but he did not respond until he finished. I said, “Are you Abū Dharr?” He said, “My family says that.” I said, “No one was more beloved to me to see than you.” He said, “Now you have seen me.” I said, “We stay far from water, become junub, and remain for days performing tayammum. Something in my heart troubled me about that.” He said, “Do you know Abū Dharr? I was in Madinah and disliked staying there, so the Messenger of God ﷺ ordered a small flock of sheep for me, and I went out with them. I became junub, performed tayammum with clean earth, and prayed for several days. Something in my heart troubled me until I thought I was doomed. So I ordered a young camel to be saddled and mounted it until I reached Madinah. I found the Messenger of God ﷺ in the shade of the mosque among a group of his companions. I greeted him, and he raised his head and said, ‘Glory be to God! Abū Dharr?’ I said, ‘Yes, O Messenger of God. I became junub and performed tayammum for several days; then something in my heart troubled me until I thought I was doomed.’ The Messenger of God ﷺ called for water, and a black slave woman brought it in a bowl that was shaking—meaning not full. He said, ‘Clean earth is sufficient as long as you do not find water, even for ten years. But when you find water, let it touch your skin.’ ” He said, “Abū Dharr’s janāba was from sexual intercourse.”"],[852,"When the circumcised part passes the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory."],[856,"He wipes the face, shakes his hands, strikes one of them with the other, and wipes his hands."],[857,"Ali said about tayammum: one strike for the face and one strike for the hands up to the wrists."],[859,"He strikes his two hands on the ground, then strikes his hand - i.e., shakes it - then wipes his face and his two hands."],[860,"Ammar b. Yasir used to narrate that he was with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, and Aisha was with him. Her necklace broke, and the people were delayed searching for it until morning came while they had no water with them. Then the verse of tayammum was revealed. Ammar said: They arose and wiped, striking their hands and wiping their faces with them; then they returned and struck their hands a second time, and wiped their hands with them up to the armpits - or he said: up to the shoulders."],[862,"Water is due from water (i.e., ghusl is required when semen is emitted)."],[864,"I screened the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he performed ghusl from janābah. He began by washing his hands. Then he poured water with his right hand over his left and washed his private part and what had been affected. Then he struck his hand on the wall — or the ground — then performed wuḍūʾ as for prayer, except for his feet. Then he poured water over himself, then he moved his feet aside and washed them."],[866,"We would perform tayammum anew for each prayer. Ma'mar said: Qatadah used to hold this view."],[867,"Tayammum has the status of water; that is, one may pray with it as long as one has not invalidated one's state of purity."],[868,"He performs tayammum, and all the prayers suffice him as long as he does not invalidate his state of purity. It has the status of water."],[870,"Tayammum may be performed with fresh grass or with the mountain."],[871,"We have heard that if snow falls and one cannot get to earth, or there is mud and one cannot get to earth, then he performs tayammum using the mane of his horse, its elbow, and whatever dust is on his veil."],[872,"I said to 'Ata': What about one who performs tayammum, prays, and then finds water? He said: If he finds water within the time of that prayer, then he should bathe if he was in a state of major ritual impurity, or perform wudu if he was not, and then repeat that prayer. If he finds water after the time of that prayer has passed, he does not repeat it, but he should bathe and perform wudu for the prayers he is going to offer."],[873,"About a man who has a nocturnal emission at night, performs ghusl, and in the morning finds some of it on his body: He said: He should repeat his ghusl and repeat the prayer, whether it is within the time or outside it."],[875,"About a man who has a nocturnal emission at night, performs ghusl, and in the morning finds some of it on his body: He said: He should repeat his ghusl and repeat the prayer, whether it is within the time or outside it."],[876,"About a man who has a nocturnal emission at night, performs ghusl, and in the morning finds some of it on his body: He said: He should repeat his ghusl and repeat the prayer, whether it is within the time or outside it."],[877,"If he removes them, he must repeat the wudu; his previous ablution has been invalidated."],[878,"When he takes them off, he must repeat ablution; his ablution is invalidated. When a man wipes over his leather socks and then removes them, he must wash his feet."],[879,"Concerning one who removes one of his leather socks, he said: 'That he wash both his feet is more preferable to us, but among us are those who say he should wash [only] that foot. The other opinion is more preferable to us.' Al-Thawri said: 'When you remove the leather sock from the place of wiping, then wash the foot.'"],[880,"Regarding a man who put on two leather socks, and over them another pair of leather socks, then he wiped over the upper pair, then removed them while the lower pair remained, he said: 'The ablution is invalidated when he removes the upper pair over which the wiping was done.'"],[881,"Concerning a man who wiped over his socks and then put on leather socks over them, and then broke his ablution, he said: 'He removes his leather socks and wipes over his socks.'"],[882,"They (meaning two individuals) used to, when they wanted to urinate while in a state of ablution, put on leather socks, then stand and urinate, then perform ablution and wipe over the leather socks."],[884,"I saw al-Hasan urinate, then perform ablution and wipe over his leather socks with a single wipe on their tops. He said: 'I saw the trace of his fingers on the leather sock.'"],[885,"I saw Qays b. Sa'd b. 'Ubadah urinate, then come to the Tigris and wipe over his leather socks. He wiped his fingers over the leather sock and spread them apart. He said: 'I saw the trace of his fingers on the leather sock.'"],[887,"When he performs ablution over his leather socks, he places one hand on top of the leather sock and the other beneath the leather sock."],[888,"I saw Ibn 'Umar wipe over them—meaning his leather socks—a single wipe with both his hands, [wiping] their bottoms and their tops. He had previously urinated and then performed ablution like this for a funeral he was invited to."],[889,"To wipe over them three times is more preferable to me, just as a person wipes his foot, and do not wash them. I said: 'Should I dip my palm into the water and not shake it off until I wipe with what is on it as I wipe the head?' He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'What do you think if, after three wipes, I miss a part of the leather socks?' He said: 'That will not harm you.'"],[890,"I said to 'Ata': Is wiping only over the two upper parts of the leather socks? He said: Yes. I said: Should I not wipe the bottoms of the leather socks? He said: No, only their tops."],[891,"I said to 'Ata': Has any allowance reached you for wiping over gloves or for raising [them]? He said: No."],[892,"About a man who performed ablution but forgot to wipe his head or some of the parts of ablution, then put on his leather socks, then urinated. He said: He should remove his leather socks and repeat the ablution, because he put them on without complete purification."],[893,"That my foot be cut off is dearer to me than that I wipe over the leather socks."],[894,"I said to 'Ata': Is there an allowance for the feverish or the sick not to clean thoroughly or perform ablution completely? He said: No."],[895,"For the sick person with smallpox and the like there is an allowance not to perform ablution. He recited: “And if you are ill or on a journey…” Then he said: This is among the hidden meanings of the Qur’an."],[896,"He used to say about this verse: “And if you are ill or on a journey or one of you comes from the privy…” He said: It is for the sick person who is struck by major impurity; if he fears for himself, he has the allowance of dry ablution like the traveler who finds no water."],[897,"I said to 'Ata': What about the person with smallpox, does he have an allowance not to perform ablution? I recited to him: “And if you are ill or on a journey…” while he remained silent until I reached, “then you find no water.” He said: That is when they find no water. If they find water, let them purify themselves. He said: And if the person with smallpox has a wet dream, the full bath is obligatory upon him. By God, I once had a wet dream — 'Ata' is the speaker — while I had smallpox, and I bathed. It applies to all of them when they find no water, meaning the verse."],[898,"A man stayed with me and became junub while having a wound. I asked 'Ubayd b. 'Umayr, and he said: Let him wash around it and not let water touch his wound."],[899,"A man was afflicted with major impurity and had wounds, then had a wet dream and sought a ruling. They ordered him to bathe, so he bathed and died. That was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: “What is wrong with you? You killed him; may God kill you.”"],[900,"Menstruation is greater."],[901,"For the severely ill person there is an allowance not to perform ablution and to wipe with dust. He said: “And if you find no water, then perform dry ablution with clean earth.” Tawus said: It is for the junub: “And if you are ill…” — that is, up to “or you have touched women.”"],[902,"A concession for the sick regarding ablution: tayammum with clean earth. And Ibn Abbas said: What do you think if he is bound in leather? As if asking: what would he do with it?"],[903,"If a person has smallpox, or a wound that becomes severe and he fears for himself, then he should perform tayammum with clean earth."],[904,"Regarding the one afflicted with smallpox and the menstruating woman, when they fear for their lives, they perform tayammum. He says: the one with smallpox when he is in a state of major ritual impurity."],[905,"A man had smallpox, so Ibn Mas'ud ordered that dust be brought near to him in a basin or a small vessel, and he wiped himself with the dust."],[906,"A man had smallpox and became junub. They ordered him, so he performed ghusl, and his flesh fell off and he died. That was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: They killed him, may God kill them. Is not the cure for ignorance to ask? If only he had performed tayammum with clean earth."],[907,"Indeed there is a concession for the sick to wipe with dust even while he finds water."],[908,"I said to Ata: May a man eat before performing wudu? He said: No."],[909,"The people of Taif came to the Prophet ﷺ and complained to him of the cold and asked him about the ghusl for janabah. He said: As for me, I pour water over my head three times."],[910,"They are agreed that if a man is in a cold land and becomes junub and fears death, he performs tayammum, and he is in the same position as a sick person."],[911,"He became junub while he was the commander of an army, and he abandoned ghusl because of a verse, saying: If I perform ghusl, I will die. So he prayed with those with him while he was junub. When he arrived before the Messenger of God ﷺ, he informed him of what he had done and told him his excuse, and he smiled and remained silent."],[912,"A necklace of Aisha fell, so the Prophet ﷺ sent a group to search for it. Dawn overtook them while they had no water, so they prayed without purification. They complained about that to the Prophet ﷺ, and then tayammum was revealed. Ma'mar said: Ayyub informed me, saying: Abu Bakr passed by Aisha and said: You have detained the people and caused them hardship. Ma'mar said: Hisham said from his father, and Ayyub also said it. He said: When tayammum was revealed, Abu Bakr was pleased with that and said: I did not know that you were blessed; no matter disliked by you befalls you but God - blessed and exalted - makes in it good for the Muslims."],[913,"We went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ on one of his journeys, until when we were at al-Bayda - or at Dhat al-Jaysh - my necklace broke. She said: The Prophet ﷺ stayed to search for it, and the people stayed with him, and they had no water. The people came to Abu Bakr and said: Do you not see what Aisha has done? She has detained the Prophet ﷺ and the people, and they have no water. She said: Then Abu Bakr came while the Prophet ﷺ had his head resting on my thigh. He said: You have detained the Prophet ﷺ and the people, and they are not at a water source, and they have no water. Abu Bakr reproached me and said to me whatever God willed that he should say, and he began poking me with his hand in my side, but nothing prevented me from moving except the place of the Messenger of God ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ slept on my thigh until morning without water. Then God revealed the verse of tayammum, and they performed tayammum. Usayd b. Hudayr said: This is not the first of your blessings, O family of Abu Bakr. She said: Then we made the camel that I had been riding get up, and we found the necklace under it."],[914,"If he prayed with tayammum, then found water during the time of that prayer, he does not repeat it."],[915,"If he prayed, then found water during the time, he does not repeat it."],[916,"He came with Ibn Umar from al-Jurf, and when he reached al-Mirbad he did not find water, so he performed tayammum with earth and prayed, and he did not repeat that prayer."],[917,"Ibn Umar performed tayammum and prayed Asr while he was a mile or two from Madinah, then he entered Madinah while the sun was still high, and he did not repeat it."],[918,"If you are junub, wipe yourself [with earth], then if you find water, you need not wash from your janabah if you wish. Abd al-Hamid said: I mentioned that to Ibn al-Musayyab, and he said: What does he know? If you find water, then wash."],[919,"Umm Sulaym, the mother of the sons of Abu Talhah, entered upon the Messenger of God ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, is ghusl obligatory upon a woman if she has a wet dream? He said: Yes, if she sees the fluid."],[920,"When a woman has a wet dream and emits fluid, she should perform ghusl."],[922,"Two men from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ were afflicted with that, then they found water within the time, and they washed—or he said: performed ablution—and one of them repeated the prayer while the other did not. They came to the Prophet ﷺ and told him the story. The Prophet ﷺ said to the one who repeated: You have been given your reward twice, and to the other he said: It suffices for you."],[923,"Two men became junub, so they performed tayammum and prayed, then they found water within the time and washed. One of them repeated the prayer while the other did not. They asked the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ said to the one who repeated: You have been given your reward twice, and to the other he said: It suffices for you."],[924,"A bedouin came to Abu Salamah b. Abd al-Rahman and said: I had a wet dream before dawn and did not find water, so I performed tayammum and prayed. When morning came I found water; should I wash? Abu Salamah said: If you wish, wash, and if you wish, do not wash. Abu Harmalah said: I said to Ibn al-Musayyab: Do you not hear what this man is saying? And I told him his statement. Ibn al-Musayyab said: Did he do that? I said: Yes. He said: He threw pebbles toward him, and said: Consider—if one of you does not know what a fatwa is, why does he give fatwas to people? O you, you purified yourself for your prayer, so when you find water, washing is obligatory upon you."],[925,"If a man performs tayammum, then passes by water and says: Until I come to another water—then his tayammum is invalidated, and he performs ablution for that prayer. And if he performs tayammum, then finds water before he makes the salam in his prayer, then his tayammum is nullified and he performs ablution for that prayer."],[927,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Have you ever applied depilatory paste in the bathhouse? He said: Yes, once."],[928,"If you teach a man tayammum, that tayammum is not sufficient for you to pray with unless you intend by it that you are performing tayammum for yourself. But if you teach him ablution, it suffices you."],[929,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Have you ever applied depilatory paste in the bathhouse? He said: Yes, once."],[930,"If a traveller fears thirst for himself and has water, he performs tayammum."],[931,"Allah has only made water something that purifies, not something that is purified."],[932,"906 898 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar, from Qatādah the like of it, and from Juwaybir, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk b. Muzāḥim the like of it. Hidden text: If a traveller fears thirst for himself and has water, he performs tayammum."],[933,"If he fears thirst and has water, he performs tayammum and does not perform ablution."],[934,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A man was travelling and became junub, and he had only enough water for his ablution for prayer. He said: He should perform ablution with it."],[935,"About a man who becomes junub while travelling and has only enough water for his ablution for prayer. He said: He performs ablution with it and does not perform tayammum. Maʿmar said: He performs ablution and also performs tayammum; that is preferable to me."],[936,"About a man who becomes junub while travelling and has only enough water to wash his face and hands. He said: He should wash his face and hands and pray, and he should not perform tayammum. Maʿmar said: I heard someone else say: He should wash his face and also perform tayammum."],[937,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A man becomes junub while travelling, and he has water – is it sufficient for him to wash his face and palms, when he has enough to reach his feet, hands, and forearms? He said: No, by my life, it is not sufficient for him; so he should not omit that if it reaches his feet, hands, and forearms. Then he recited the verse of wiping, and he made them both together, and assigned to it the wiping if he does not find water."],[938,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Is a single wiping with water preferable to you, or three wipings with dust? He said: Rather, one wiping with water; he should prefer water over dust. Even if water is little and not enough, he should prefer its little over dust, reaching as much of his limbs as it reaches; but if water is little, he should begin by washing his private part, even if it reaches only that."],[939,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ, “If he has enough water to wash his face, feet, and forearms, may he leave the water and wipe with dust if he wishes?” He said, “No, by my life.” I asked him, “If he has enough to wash only his face and private parts?” He said, “Let him wash his face and private parts, then wipe his palms with dust.” I asked, “And if he has enough to wash his private parts?” He said, “Let him wash his private parts and wipe his face and palms with dust.”"],[940,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ, “May a man have intercourse with his wife while traveling when he has no water?” He said, “If there are four nights or more between him and water, let him have intercourse with his wife; but if there are three nights or less between him and water, let him not have intercourse with his wife.”"],[941,"If he can reach water the same day or the next day, let him not have intercourse with her until he reaches the water. But if he is far from water with his sheep or camels, there is no harm in his having intercourse with his wife and then performing tayammum."],[942,"Regarding a man who has intercourse with his wife while traveling and has no water with him, he said, “There is no harm in that.”"],[943,"That he heard the Bedouins asking Abū al-Shaʿthāʾ, saying, “We stay away with our livestock for a month or two months; may one of us have intercourse with his wife when he has no water with him?” He said, “Yes.”"],[944,"If he is traveling, let him not approach her until he reaches water; but if he is far from water, there is no harm in his having intercourse with her, even if he has no water."],[945,"A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of God, I am in the sand for four or five months, and among us are women in post-childbirth bleeding, menstruating women, or one who is junub. What do you think?” He said, “Upon you is dust.”"],[946,"I was far from water, became junub, and performed tayammum. Something arose in my heart, so I went to the house of Abū Dharr but did not find him. I went to the mosque, where his appearance had been described to me, and there he was standing praying; I recognized him by the description. I greeted him, but he did not respond until he finished. I said, “Are you Abū Dharr?” He said, “My family says that.” I said, “No one was more beloved to me to see than you.” He said, “Now you have seen me.” I said, “We stay far from water, become junub, and remain for days performing tayammum. Something in my heart troubled me about that.” He said, “Do you know Abū Dharr? I was in Madinah and disliked staying there, so the Messenger of God ﷺ ordered a small flock of sheep for me, and I went out with them. I became junub, performed tayammum with clean earth, and prayed for several days. Something in my heart troubled me until I thought I was doomed. So I ordered a young camel to be saddled and mounted it until I reached Madinah. I found the Messenger of God ﷺ in the shade of the mosque among a group of his companions. I greeted him, and he raised his head and said, ‘Glory be to God! Abū Dharr?’ I said, ‘Yes, O Messenger of God. I became junub and performed tayammum for several days; then something in my heart troubled me until I thought I was doomed.’ The Messenger of God ﷺ called for water, and a black slave woman brought it in a bowl that was shaking—meaning not full. He said, ‘Clean earth is sufficient as long as you do not find water, even for ten years. But when you find water, let it touch your skin.’ ” He said, “Abū Dharr’s janāba was from sexual intercourse.”"],[947,"That he came to the Prophet ﷺ after becoming junub, so the Prophet ﷺ called for water. He concealed himself and washed. Then the Prophet ﷺ said to him, “Clean earth is the Muslim’s purification, even if he does not find water for ten years. But when he finds water, let him make it touch his skin, for that is better.”"],[948,"I became junub while among camels, so I rolled in the dust as an animal rolls. Then I came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and mentioned all that. He said, “Tayammum would have sufficed you for that.”"],[949,"A man from the desert came to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb and said, “O Commander of the Faithful, we stay for a month or two without finding water.” ʿUmar said, “As for me, I would not pray until I find water.” ʿAmmār b. Yāsir said, “Do you not remember when you and I were in such-and-such a land tending camels, and you know that I became junub?” He said, “Yes.” “So I rolled in the dust and mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ. He laughed and said, ‘Clean earth would have sufficed you, like this,’ and he struck the ground with his hand, blew on them, and wiped his face and forearms up to near the middle of the forearm.” ʿUmar said, “Fear God, O ʿAmmār.” ʿAmmār said, “Because of the right I have over you, O Commander of the Faithful, if you wish I will never mention it as long as I live.” ʿUmar said, “No, by God, but we will entrust you with your affair as you have taken it.”"],[950,"That Abū Dharr had intercourse with his wife and had no water with him, so he wiped his face and hands. Then something troubled him, so he went to the Prophet ﷺ, a distance of three nights’ travel. He found that the people had already prayed the dawn prayer, and he asked about the Prophet ﷺ. It turned out that he had gone out to relieve himself, so he followed him. The Prophet ﷺ turned and saw him, so he stretched his hands to the ground and placed them—he said, “I think he said: then he shook them”—then he wiped his face and hands with them, and then told him how he had wiped."],[951,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think of His saying: \"or you have touched women\" — is it intercourse? He said: Yes. I said to him: What about a junub person on a journey, if he cannot find water, how does he purify himself? He said: The purification of one who is not in a state of wudu' if he cannot find water is the same; they do not differ. They both wipe their faces and their hands."],[952,"When a man is distant from water while tending his camels or his sheep, there is no harm in his having intercourse with his wife and performing tayammum. Ma'mar said: And I heard al-Zuhri say that."],[953,"I was sitting with Ibn 'Umar when a man came to him and said: I am distant from water while tending my camels; may I have intercourse if I cannot find water? Ibn 'Umar said: As for me, I would not do that. But if you do it, then fear Allah and perform ghusl when you find water."],[954,"The Prophet ﷺ sent 'Umar b. al-Khattab and a man from the Ansar to guard the Muslims. They became junub when the cold of the pre-dawn overtook them, so 'Umar rolled in the dust, while the Ansari performed tayammum with clean earth and wiped himself with it, then they both prayed. The Prophet ﷺ said: The Ansari did it correctly."],[955,"He heard the Bedouins asking Abu al-Sha'tha', saying: We stay away from water with our livestock for a month or two months, and one of us has intercourse with his wife while he has no water. He said: Yes, he used to see no harm in that."],[956,"If I became junub and could not find water for a month, I would not pray. Sufyan said: This opinion is not to be followed."],[957,"Ibn Mas'ud retracted his earlier statement about the junub person, that he should not pray until he performs ghusl."],[958,"If you become junub, search for water as much as you can. If you cannot find any, then perform tayammum and pray. Then when you find water, perform ghusl."],[959,"Regarding a menstruating woman who becomes pure while she has no water, he said: She performs tayammum, and her husband may have intercourse with her."],[960,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about something of that sort, and he said: There is no harm in it."],[961,"I said to 'Ata': Suppose a man has a wet dream in a snowy land in winter, and he thinks that if he performs ghusl he will die, and he is unable to prepare something to wash with — should he perform ghusl? He said: Yes, even if he dies. Allah said: \"And if you are junub, purify yourselves\" (Qur'an 5:6), and Allah has not given him any excuse."],[962,"I asked al-Sha'bi and al-Hakam about snow, and they both said: One may perform wudu' with it."],[963,"If the ritually impure person finds only snow, let him melt it; but if he finds no fire and cannot perform ablution from it, then tayammum with clean earth."],[964,"If a man becomes junub, let him wait for water; but if he fears the prayer time will elapse and no water comes, let him wipe himself with dust and pray."],[965,"If a woman sees after purification something that disturbs her, like the washing of meat, or the washing of fish, or like drops of blood before a nosebleed, then that is a kick from the kicks of Satan in the womb. She should sprinkle water and perform wudu and pray. Israil added in his narration: If it is fresh blood without any doubt, then she should abstain from prayer."],[966,"Someone who was with Umar b. al-Khattab on a journey told us: He became junub and had no water with him. He said: 'Do you think that if we ride hard, we will reach water before sunrise?' They said: 'Yes.' So they spurred on their mounts and arrived before sunrise, and Umar performed ghusl and prayed."],[967,"A Muslim woman had istihadah bleeding, and she asked the Prophet ﷺ — or he was asked about her — and he said: It is only a vein. She leaves prayer for the duration of her menstruation, then combines zuhr and asr with one ghusl, maghrib and isha with one ghusl, and performs ghusl for subh."],[969,"He performed Umrah with Umar b. al-Khattab, and Umar camped at night on the way near some water sources and had a wet dream. He woke up and said: 'Do you think we can reach water before sunrise?' They said: 'Yes.' Ma'mar said: So he hastened the journey."],[970,"When the two circumcised parts meet, ghusl becomes obligatory."],[971,"Just as the hadd penalty becomes obligatory, likewise ghusl becomes obligatory."],[973,"When he sits between the four branches and then brings the circumcised part into contact with the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory."],[974,"When he sits between her four branches and then exerts himself upon her, ghusl becomes obligatory."],[975,"I asked 'A'ishah, \"What obligates ghusl?\" She said, \"Do you know what your likeness is, O Abu Salamah? The likeness of a chick that hears the rooster crow and crows with it. When the circumcised part passes the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory.\""],[977,"It obligates the prescribed punishment and does not obligate a cup of water?"],[979,"Does it obligate four thousand and not obligate a cup of water?"],[981,"When the circumcised part passes the circumcised part, ghusl has become obligatory. He said: And 'A'ishah used to say it."],[982,"He was asked about that, and he said, \"When I reach that, I perform ghusl.\""],[984,"As for me, when I have intercourse with my wife, I perform ghusl."],[985,"As for me, when I have intercourse with my wife, I perform ghusl."],[986,"The saying of the Ansar, 'Water from water,' was a concession in the beginning of Islam; then we adopted ghusl after that when the circumcised part touched the circumcised part."],[989,"The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to differ concerning a man who has intercourse with his wife and then withdraws from her before ejaculating. He mentioned that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari came to 'A'ishah and said, \"The disagreement of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a matter has become difficult for me. I hold you in too great esteem to face you with it.\" She said, \"What is it?\" several times. He said, \"A man has intercourse with his wife but does not ejaculate.\" He said, \"She said to me, 'When the circumcised part passes the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory.'\" Abu Musa said, \"I will never ask about this after you, ever.\""],[990,"Additional text: The Muhajirun used to command ghusl, while the Ansar used to say, \"Water is from water.\" Who will decide between these two? The Muhajirun said, \"When the circumcised part touches the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory.\" So they appointed 'Ali b. Abi Talib as judge between them, and they brought their dispute to him. He said, \"What do you think? If you saw a man inserting and withdrawing, would the hadd punishment be obligatory on him?\" He said, \"So the hadd would be obligatory, and yet no sa' of water would be obligatory on him?\" So he ruled in favor of the Muhajirun. That reached 'A'ishah, and she said, \"Often the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and I did that, and we got up and performed ghusl.\""],[991,"Additional text: The Muhajirun used to command ghusl, while the Ansar used to say, \"Water is from water.\" Who will decide between these two? The Muhajirun said, \"When the circumcised part touches the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory.\" So they appointed 'Ali b. Abi Talib as judge between them, and they brought their dispute to him. He said, \"What do you think? If you saw a man inserting and withdrawing, would the hadd punishment be obligatory on him?\" He said, \"So the hadd would be obligatory, and yet no sa' of water would be obligatory on him?\" So he ruled in favor of the Muhajirun. That reached 'A'ishah, and she said, \"Often the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and I did that, and we got up and performed ghusl.\""],[992,"I had intercourse with my wife and was slow to reach climax, so I did not ejaculate, and I did not perform ghusl."],[993,"What do you think if one of us has intercourse and is slow to reach climax and does not emit semen? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"He should wash what touched him of her and perform wudu'.\""],[994,"When one of you has intercourse and is slow to reach climax, he should perform wudu' as he does for prayer."],[995,"Ubayy b. Ka'b asked the Prophet ﷺ, \"One of us comes to the woman and then is slow to reach climax.\" The Prophet ﷺ said, \"Water is from water.\""],[996,"I said to Zayd b. Thabit, \"Ubayy b. Ka'b used to give fatwa to that effect.\" Zayd said, \"Ubayy retracted that before he died.\""],[998,"The Prophet ﷺ called a man of the Ansar, and he came out to him and they went toward Quba'. They passed by a small garden, and the Ansari performed ghusl. The Prophet ﷺ asked him, and he said, \"You called me while I was on my wife.\" The Prophet ﷺ said, \"When one of you is slow to reach climax or fails to ejaculate, wudu' is sufficient for him.\""],[999,"When one of you is hurried or fails to ejaculate, he should not perform ghusl."],[1001,"He married a woman who had belonged to Rafi' b. Khadij. She informed him that Rafi' used to have intercourse with her without ejaculating, and he would say: Do not perform ghusl. She had sores."],[1002,"He married the former wife of Rafi' b. Khadij. She informed him that Rafi' used to practice 'azl with her because of sores she had, so that she would not have to perform ghusl."],[1003,"Water is due from water (i.e., ghusl is required when semen is emitted)."],[1004,"I was with Ibn ʿAbbās when a woman wrote to him: “I have been suffering from istihāḍah for such and such a time, and I was told that ʿAlī used to say: ‘She should perform ghusl for every prayer.’” Ibn ʿAbbās said: “I find nothing for her other than what ʿAlī said.”"],[1005,"A menstruating woman performs ghusl as a person in janabah does."],[1007,"Water is due from water."],[1008,"Regarding a man who has intercourse with his wife in other than the front passage and ejaculates, he said: He performs ghusl, but she does not; rather, she washes whatever part of her was affected."],[1009,"She prostrates."],[1010,"He performs ghusl until the doubt is removed."],[1011,"He performs ghusl."],[1012,"When a man wakes up at night and finds wetness but does not remember a wet dream, he should perform ghusl. But if he sees that he had a wet dream and does not find wetness, there is no ghusl upon him."],[1013,"I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ghusl from a single vessel while we were junub, and I used to wash the head of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in i'tikaf in the mosque and I was menstruating, and he used to order me while I was menstruating to wrap a waist-wrapper, then he would have intimate contact with me."],[1014,"My mother sent word to ʿAlqamah: “May a menstruating woman nurse the sick?” He said, “Yes, but if you are near death, she should leave you.” I said, “May she wash me when I die?” He said, “No.”"],[1015,"Regarding a man who thinks he had a wet dream but finds no wetness, he said: There is no ghusl upon him."],[1016,"I said to Ata: A man has a wet dream, then he catches his penis before the semen comes out and holds it back, so it recedes. Is ghusl required of him? He said: If nothing comes out of him, then no ghusl is required of him."],[1017,"None of you should urinate in his bathing place and then perform wudu in it, for most whispering (waswas) comes from that."],[1018,"Urinating in the bathing place brings about affliction (lamam)."],[1019,"Whoever urinates in his bathing place will not be purified."],[1020,"I said to Ata: Do you dislike urinating in the bathing place? He said: No, and I urinate in it. If it were a bathing place on open ground, I would dislike urinating in it. But as for this plastered one, nothing remains in it, so I do not mind urinating in it. And he claimed he urinated in it."],[1021,"Allah will not purify a man who urinates in his bathing place."],[1022,"When I perform ghusl from janabah, I begin with my private part, then I rinse my mouth and sniff water into my nose, then I wash my face and hands, then I pour water over my head, then over my hands, then over my legs. He said: And I wash my feet in the bathing place, then I put on my sandals in it. Then that suffices me; I do not wash them after that. I said: What do you think if you do not put on sandals in the bathing place and you leave it barefoot? He said: Then I would wash them."],[1023,"I said to Ata: What has reached you about pouring water over the head? He said: It has reached me that it is three times. I said: What do you think if I pour water over my head three times while I have long hair, and with each pouring I soak it, and I have a concern in my mind whether I have not wetted the roots of the hair as I want? He said: That is what was said: three times is the Sunnah."],[1024,"Ghusl from janabah is when one is thorough. He said: I said: Does he clean (the body)? He said: Yes, with that."],[1025,"I said to 'Ata': 'Does the junub pass [his hand] over everything that is apparent of him?' He said: 'Yes.' A man said to him: 'Does the junub pour water over it?' He said: 'No, rather he washes [it] with a washing; should the junub wash his backside—the way of the privy—on account of janabah?' He said: 'Yes, by Allah, and indeed that is the most deserving of what should be washed of him.' I said: 'Is it not that a man passes excrement and then perfumes himself, then comes and performs wudu' and does not wash his backside?' He said: 'Janabah occurs [only] occasionally, once.'"],[1026,"A group came to 'Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ and asked him about a man's voluntary prayer in his house, about what is lawful for a man from his menstruating wife, and about ghusl from janabah. He said: 'As for a man's voluntary prayer in his house, it is light, so illuminate your houses. What good is a house in which there is no light? As for what is lawful for a man from his menstruating wife: you may have what is above the waist-wrapper, and do not look upon what is beneath it until she becomes pure. As for ghusl from janabah: perform your wudu' as for prayer, then wash your head three times, then pour water over your skin.'"],[1027,"A number of people from Kufa came to 'Umar b. al-Khattab and said: 'We have come to ask you about three matters: about a man's voluntary prayer in his house, about what is lawful for a man from his menstruating wife, and about ghusl from janabah.' He said: 'Are you sorcerers?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Are you soothsayers?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Where are you from?' They said: 'From Iraq.' He said: 'From which [part of] Iraq?' They said: 'From the people of Kufa.' He said: 'You have asked me about matters that no one has asked me about since I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about them.'"],[1028,"Whoever relieves himself or is afflicted by janabah, let him avoid touching filth with his right hand and wash with his left until he cleanses. Then let him wash his left hand, then pour water over his face and head."],[1029,"Regarding the ghusl of 'Abdullah b. 'Umar from janabah. He said: 'He would pour water over his hands and wash them, then scoop with his right hand and pour over his private part, washing it with his left hand. When he finished washing his private part, he washed the left hand, then rinsed his mouth, inhaled water into his nose, and sprinkled water into his eyes. Then he began with his face and washed it, then his head, then his right hand, then the left. Then he scooped with both hands over the rest of his body afterwards and washed it.' He said: 'Abdullah b. 'Umar would not sprinkle water into his eyes except in the ghusl of janabah. As for wudu' for prayer, no.'"],[1030,"When he performed ghusl from janabah, he would sprinkle water into his eyes and run his fingers through his beard. He said: 'Abdullah said: I do not know anyone who sprinkled water into his eyes except Ibn 'Umar.'"],[1031,"Ibn 'Umar would rub his beard, and that is because I asked him about soaking the roots of his hair with water."],[1032,"I said to Ata: What has reached you about pouring water over the head? He said: It has reached me that it is three times. I said: What do you think if I pour water over my head three times while I have long hair, and with each pouring I soak it, and I have a concern in my mind whether I have not wetted the roots of the hair as I want? He said: That is what was said: three times is the Sunnah."],[1033,"He was asked about ghusl from janabah, and he said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pour water over his head three times.' He said: 'Then 'Abdullah gestured, lowering both his palms but not joining the ends of the palms to their bases; rather, it was as if he spread them slightly. Then he scooped with them.' He said: 'And he poured over his head three times.'"],[1034,"Janabah was mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'As for me, I pour water over my head three times.' Then he gestured with his hands as if pouring with them over the head."],[1035,"The junub scoops over his head three scoops of water."],[1036,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wanted to perform ghusl from janabah, he would pour water over his hands, then perform his wudu' as for prayer, then saturate his hair with water until he reached the skin, then pour over his head three times, then pour over the rest of his body, then take the vessel and empty it over himself."],[1037,"I screened the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he performed ghusl from janābah. He began by washing his hands. Then he poured water with his right hand over his left and washed his private part and what had been affected. Then he struck his hand on the wall — or the ground — then performed wuḍūʾ as for prayer, except for his feet. Then he poured water over himself, then he moved his feet aside and washed them."],[1038,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ performed ghusl from janābah, he began by washing his hands, then performed wuḍūʾ for prayer, then immersed his hand in the water and ran his fingers through the roots of his hair until, when he thought he had thoroughly wet the skin of his head, he poured three handfuls of water over his head with his hands, then after that he poured water over all of his skin. They did not doubt — Hishām and others — that he began with the private part."],[1039,"When ʿUthmān performed ghusl from janābah, he moved away from his place and washed his feet."],[1040,"Abū al-Dardāʾ was asked about the ghusl of the junub. He said: 'He should wet the hair and cleanse the skin.'"],[1041,"Under every hair there is janābah, so wet the hair and cleanse the skin."],[1042,"The junub should pour water over his palms, perform wuḍūʾ after washing his private part, then wash his head and pour water over his body. When he is done, he should wash his feet."],[1043,"It used to be said: A man with long, thick hair should scoop three handfuls of water over his head, and then let the water soak into the roots of the hair with each handful."],[1044,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Should a man with two braids wet his braids? He said: No. But the roots of the hair are the scalp and the skin of the head. However, he should pour water over his head; whatever touches the braids touches them, and what misses them is of no harm."],[1045,"A man came to Jābir and asked him about the ghusl of janābah, how to wash his head. Jābir said: 'As for the Messenger of God ﷺ, he used to scoop three handfuls of water over his head.' The man said: 'My hair is abundant.' Jābir said: 'The hair of the Messenger of God ﷺ was more abundant and better than your hair.'"],[1046,"Whoever washes his head with sidr while he is junub has done it thoroughly, then he should wash the rest of his body afterwards."],[1047,"Shall I tell you what I heard from ʿAbdullāh? I heard him say: Any junub who washes his head with khiṭmī has done it thoroughly."],[1049,"One of them would wash his head from janabah with lote leaves, then wait a while, then wash the rest of his body."],[1050,"If you wash your head while you are junub, then wash the rest of your body afterward, it has sufficed for you."],[1051,"He used to say about a man who has a wife and a slave girl and he watches his wife with regard to ghusl: There is no harm in his washing his head, then waiting, then washing the rest of his body afterward, and not washing his head [again]."],[1052,"If the junub person washes his head with lote leaves or marshmallow while he is junub, he should not leave it until that dries."],[1053,"About a man who washes his head with marshmallow while he is junub, then leaves it until it dries: He said: I heard 'Ali b. al-Husayn say: Whatever water touches of you while you are junub, that place has become purified."],[1054,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once performed ghusl for janabah, and he saw on his shoulder a spot the size of a dirham that water had not touched. He said: So he wiped it with the hair of his beard. Or he said: With the hair of his head ﷺ."],[1055,"About a man who performs ghusl from janabah and a part of his body remains [unwashed]: He said: He should wash what the water did not reach."],[1056,"That the Prophet ﷺ performed ghusl from janabah, then came out with his head dripping, and between his shoulders or above that was a spot the size of a dirham that water had not touched. Someone said to the Prophet ﷺ: Have you performed ghusl, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. He said: But a spot the size of a dirham has not been touched by water. So the Prophet ﷺ took some water in his palm from part of his head that had it and wiped that spot with it."],[1057,"If you forget a small part of the body in wudu, then make water touch it."],[1059,"If a man becomes junub and performs ghusl, then sees moisture after having urinated, he does not have to repeat the ghusl; but if he has not urinated and sees moisture, he must repeat the ghusl."],[1060,"About a man who has a nocturnal emission at night, performs ghusl, and in the morning finds some of it on his body: He said: He should repeat his ghusl and repeat the prayer, whether it is within the time or outside it."],[1061,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I had intercourse, then I left and noticed some doubt before noon, but I did not look until I returned at night and found thin fluid that had dried on the tip of the penis, so I ate dinner and went out to the mosque, having prayed noon, afternoon, and sunset, and I had not rushed away from my dinner. He said: You did right."],[1062,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think of a person in a state of major ritual impurity who performs ghusl but does not finish it before breaking wind in the middle of it? He said: He should perform ablution on the parts of the body washed for ablution that he has already washed, and wash the remainder for his major impurity, but he does not wash again for major impurity what he has already washed. He said: There is no harm in a junub breaking wind in the middle of his ghusl if he performs ablution for prayer afterward."],[1063,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if a junub washes his head with marshmallow or lote leaves, then gets up and relieves himself, and then returns—should he wash his head again? He said: No, if he wishes; rather, he wipes it with the wiping of ablution for prayer, then washes."],[1064,"Al-Thawrī, from a man who became junub and performed the ablution for prayer, then washed his head and part of his body, and then broke wind before completing his ghusl, said: He completes his ghusl and then repeats the ablution. The event invalidated the ablution but did not invalidate the ghusl."],[1065,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think of a person in a state of major ritual impurity who performs ghusl but does not finish it before breaking wind in the middle of it? He said: He should perform ablution on the parts of the body washed for ablution that he has already washed, and wash the remainder for his major impurity, but he does not wash again for major impurity what he has already washed. He said: There is no harm in a junub breaking wind in the middle of his ghusl if he performs ablution for prayer afterward."],[1067,"I and the Messenger of God ﷺ used to perform ghusl from a single vessel of the size of a faraq."],[1068,"She informed him that she and the Prophet ﷺ both began together while they were junub from a single vessel."],[1069,"When a man and a woman are both junub and perform ghusl, if they wish they may use one vessel when they both begin and put their hands in together; but as for one washing with the leftover water of the other, no."],[1070,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if one of them puts his hand into the vessel, then withdraws it, and the other puts his hand in when this one withdraws it, not preceding him except by that? He said: That is putting in together; they have begun together. I said to him: If she preceded him by one scoop, then withdrew her hand and he put his in at that moment? He said: Then it does not harm him. I said: What do you think if one of them scoops before the other a scoop from a single vessel and does not finish his ghusl? He said: Then they did not begin together."],[1071,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did not command any woman among us to make up the prayer."],[1073,"We used to bathe at the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we and our wives, from a single vessel."],[1075,"There is no harm in a man and a woman performing ghusl together while junub in a single vessel."],[1076,"If she becomes pure at the beginning of the day, let her complete her fast; otherwise, no."],[1077,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ghusl with the leftover water of Maymunah, and that is because I asked him about two junub persons performing ghusl together."],[1078,"My father used to perform ghusl then perform wudu, so I would say: 'Does ghusl not suffice you, and what wudu is more complete than ghusl?' He said: 'And what wudu is more complete than ghusl for the junub? But it seems to me that something may come out of my penis, so I touch it, and therefore I perform wudu.'"],[1079,"If you do not touch your private parts after you finish your ghusl, then what wudu is more thorough than ghusl?"],[1080,"Ibn Umar was asked about wudu after ghusl, and he said: 'What wudu is better than ghusl?'"],[1081,"I asked Ibn Umar; I said: 'Wudu from ghusl after janabah?' He said: 'You have delved too deeply, O servant of Ashja'.'"],[1082,"A woman was mentioned to him who performed wudu after ghusl. He said: 'If she were with me, she would not have done that. And what wudu is more comprehensive than ghusl?'"],[1083,"The Prophet ﷺ, when he finished the ghusl of janabah, moved aside and washed his feet."],[1084,"Ibn al-Musayyib was asked about wudu after ghusl, and he said: 'No, but he should wash his feet.'"],[1085,"Jabir b. Abd Allah was asked about a junub person performing wudu after ghusl. He said: 'No, unless he wishes; ghusl suffices him.'"],[1086,"I said: O Messenger of God, I am a woman who tightens the braid of my head; should I undo it? He said: No, it suffices you to take with your hands three scoops, then pour water over your skin, and you will be purified."],[1087,"The women of Ibn ʿUmar did not undo their heads when they performed ghusl from janābah or ḥayḍ."],[1088,"He asked ʿĀʾishah ؓ about a woman: when she performs ghusl, should she undo her hair? ʿĀʾishah said: Even if she had spent an ūqiyyah on it? If she pours three handfuls over her head, that is sufficient."],[1089,"A woman scoops three scoops over her head. I said to ʿAmr: What about one with long thick hair? He said: I see him only as like her."],[1090,"One of us would keep her braid during the ghusl."],[1091,"I found our early women: when one of them wanted to purify from menses, she would comb with light henna, and that sufficed her for her ghusl from menses, so she did not wash her head."],[1092,"I sent a man called Sumayy to Ibn al-Musayyib to ask him about a woman who was junub, then combed with light henna; does that suffice her instead of washing her head? He said: Yes. I said: Go back to him and ask him: Was this from the Prophet ﷺ? Ibn al-Musayyib said: I will not go to lie against the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[1093,"Ḥudhayfah b. al-Yamān said to a daughter of his — or to his wife —: Saturate your head with water before God saturates it with fire; how little it remains on her."],[1094,"If a junub woman combs with light henna, that suffices her instead of washing her head for her janābah."],[1095,"It used to be said: A woman scoops three scoops over her head; each time she scoops over her head, she saturates the roots of the hair with water, and she follows with her hands until she saturates the partings of the hair."],[1096,"A woman and one with long thick hair saturate their heads when they perform ghusl from janābah. And he showed me: he placed his palms on his head together, then made as if separating what is between the hair."],[1097,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ about a woman whose husband had intercourse with her and she did not perform ghusl for her janābah until she menstruated. He said: She performs ghusl for her janābah and does not wait to become pure. He had told me before that: Ḥayḍ is more severe than janābah."],[1098,"Menstruation is greater."],[1099,"She performs ghusl."],[1100,"She performs ghusl."],[1101,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go around his wives in a single ghusl."],[1103,"I saw Salman b. Rabi'ah al-Bahili lean toward Umar and ask him about something. We said: What did you ask him about? He said: I asked him about a man who has intercourse with his wife and then wants to repeat. He said: He should perform wudu."],[1104,"I asked Ibn Umar about it, and he said: If he wants to repeat, or eat, or sleep, he should perform wudu as for prayer."],[1105,"Ata was asked: May a man warm himself with his wife while he is junub and she is likewise? He said: Yes, there is no harm in a man having intercourse with a woman twice in a single janabah."],[1106,"I love to precede her to the ghusl, so I perform ghusl, then I warm myself with her until I get warm, then I tell her to perform ghusl."],[1107,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to say this and command it."],[1108,"There is no harm in a man warming himself with his wife if he has performed ghusl from janabah before she performs ghusl."],[1110,"He used to warm himself with her after the ghusl. Al-A'mash said: I said to Ibrahim: Should he perform wudu after this? He said: Yes."],[1111,"Ibn ʿAbbās used to grant a concession to one who is not in a state of wuḍūʾ to recite more than a verse or two."],[1112,"Ibn Mas'ud used to warm himself with his wife in winter while she was junub, and he had already performed ghusl, and he would cool himself with her in summer while they were in that state."],[1113,"I said to him: The junub man has performed ghusl but his wife has not; may he have intimate contact with her if she has a waist-wrapper covering her private part? He said: Yes."],[1114,"When a man has intercourse with his wife and then sleeps without performing ghusl, he should wash his private part and perform wudu as for prayer, and when he performs wudu, he should do it thoroughly."],[1115,"When the Prophet ﷺ wanted to sleep while junub, he would perform wudu as for prayer before sleeping, and when he wanted to eat, he would wash his private part and rinse his mouth, then eat."],[1116,"Another added from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, in this hadith: He would wash his private part, then perform wudu. Al-Khurasani informed us of that from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah. The Prophet ﷺ, when he wanted to sleep while junub, would perform wudu as for prayer before sleeping, and when he wanted to eat, he would wash his private part, then perform wudu, then eat."],[1117,"He asked the Prophet ﷺ: May one of us sleep or eat while junub? He said: Yes, he should perform wudu as for prayer. Nafi' said: Ibn Umar, when he wanted to do any of that, would perform wudu as for prayer except for his feet."],[1118,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb came out of the privy and recited a verse or some verses. Abū Maryam al-Ḥanafī said to him: You came out of the privy and you are reciting? ʿUmar said to him: Did Musaylimah give you this fatwā? He had been with Musaylimah."],[1119,"I asked Aishah: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ever sleep while junub? She said: Sometimes he would perform ghusl before sleeping, and sometimes he would sleep before performing ghusl, but he would perform wudu. He said: Praise be to Allah who has placed ease in the religion."],[1120,"Umar sought a ruling from the Prophet ﷺ and said: May one of us sleep while junub? He said: Yes, let him perform wudu, then let him sleep until he performs ghusl when he wishes. He said: Abdullah b. Umar, when he wanted to sleep while junub, would pour water on his hand, then wash his private part with his left hand, then wash the hand with which he washed his private part, then rinse his mouth and sniff water and blow it out, sprinkle water in his eyes, wash his face and hands up to the elbows, wipe his head, then sleep; and when he wanted to eat something while junub he would do likewise."],[1121,"When he wanted to eat, sleep, or drink while junub, he would perform wudu as for prayer."],[1122,"I said to Ata: What do you think if I were junub and wanted to eat or drink, so I performed wudu, and when I finished I passed wind before eating - does the first wudu suffice? Ma'mar said: Then eat and drink."],[1123,"When he wanted to eat, sleep, or drink while junub, he would perform wudu as for prayer."],[1124,"The junub washes his palms, then rinses his mouth, then eats."],[1125,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would sleep junub without touching water."],[1126,"The junub washes his hands and eats."],[1127,"I asked two men about janabah. One of them said: When I want to sleep, I perform wudu and wash my private part. The other said: When I want to sleep, I wash my private part, unless I want to eat."],[1128,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wanted to eat while he was junub, he would wash his hands, then rinse his mouth, and eat."],[1129,"I said to Ata: 'May the junub and the menstruating woman touch the mushaf while it is in its covering?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'What if there is a cloth between them and its covering?' He said: 'No, and the covering is no more protective than the cloth.' I said: 'What about someone without wudu while it is in its covering?' He said: 'Yes, it does not harm him.' I said: 'May he take it closed?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1130,"Ammar b. Yasir came from a journey and his family anointed him with yellow perfume. He said: Then I came and greeted the Prophet ﷺ. He said: Peace be upon you. Go and perform ghusl. He said: So I went and performed ghusl, then returned with its trace on me. I said: Peace be upon you. He said: And peace be upon you. Go and perform ghusl. He said: So I went and took a potsherd and rubbed my skin with it until I thought I had cleaned it. Then I came to him and said: Peace be upon you. He said: And peace be upon you, sit down. Then he said: The angels do not attend the funeral of a disbeliever with good, nor a junub until he performs ghusl or performs wudu as for prayer, nor one anointed with yellow perfume."],[1131,"From Ibn Umar: He asked the Prophet ﷺ: May I sleep while junub? He said: Perform wudu as for prayer. Salim said: Ibn Umar, when he wanted to sleep or eat while junub, would wash his private part, face, and hands, not doing more than that."],[1132,"I said to Ata: 'May the junub and the menstruating woman touch the mushaf while it is in its covering?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'What if there is a cloth between them and its covering?' He said: 'No, and the covering is no more protective than the cloth.' I said: 'What about someone without wudu while it is in its covering?' He said: 'Yes, it does not harm him.' I said: 'May he take it closed?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1133,"When Sa'd was junub, he would perform wudu as for prayer, then go out for his need."],[1134,"I said to Ata: May the junub receive cupping, apply depilatory paste, trim his nails, and shave his head without having performed wudu? He said: Yes. And what is that — by my life? He was surprised."],[1135,"A woman asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a woman who has a wet dream. Aisha said to her: You have exposed women! Does a woman see that? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to her and said: Then from where does resemblance come? May your right hand be dusty! The Prophet ﷺ commanded the woman to perform ghusl if she ejaculated."],[1137,"Umm Sulaym, the mother of the sons of Abu Talhah, entered upon the Messenger of God ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, is ghusl obligatory upon a woman if she has a wet dream? He said: Yes, if she sees the fluid."],[1138,"A woman asked the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, a woman sees in her sleep what a man sees? He said: She must perform ghusl. Umm Salamah said: O Messenger of God, does a woman have wet dreams? He said: Yes, and how else does her child resemble her?"],[1139,"Umm Sulaym said: O Messenger of God, may God bless you, a woman sees what a man sees in a dream? 'A'ishah said: You have exposed women. She said: God does not shy from the truth. The Prophet ﷺ said: May your hands be dusty; from where does resemblance come?"],[1141,"A woman came to one of the Prophet's ﷺ wives and said: A woman sees a man having intercourse with her in a dream. Then she left. When the Prophet ﷺ came, his wife mentioned that to him, so he asked for her and she repeated the story. He said: If she sees moisture, she should perform ghusl."],[1143,"'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf and Abu Bakr, or Khalid b. al-Walid, went to a pool at the edge of the lava field and performed ghusl. They returned and told the Prophet ﷺ about their outing until they told him about their ghusl. He said: How did you two do it? He said: I screened him until he washed, then he screened me until I washed. He said: If you had done otherwise, I would have caused you pain with a beating."],[1144,"When the Prophet ﷺ was at al-Hudaybiyyah, with a garment covering him, the wind blew and lifted the garment from him, and there was a man bathing naked in the open. The Prophet ﷺ became angry and said: O people, fear God and be modest before the noble ones, for the angels do not leave you except at one of three times: when a man is having intercourse with his wife, when he is relieving himself—he said: I forgot the third—the Prophet ﷺ said: So when one of you performs ghusl, let him conceal himself by bathing behind a wall, or beside a camel, or let his brother screen him."],[1145,"The Prophet ﷺ saw people bathing naked in the river, with no waist-wrappers on them, so he stopped and called out at the top of his voice: \"What is wrong with you that you do not hope for God's dignity?\""],[1146,"When the Kaaba was built, the Prophet ﷺ and 'Abbas went to carry stones. 'Abbas said to the Prophet ﷺ: Put your waist-wrapper on your neck to protect you from the stones. He did so and fell to the ground, and his eyes turned toward the sky. Then he stood up and said: My waist-wrapper, my waist-wrapper. So his waist-wrapper was tied around him."],[1147,"I asked Sa'id b. Jubayr about a ring on which the remembrance of God is written, and he disliked it."],[1148,"When the House was being built, the people were carrying stones, and the Prophet ﷺ was carrying with them. He took the garment and placed it on his shoulder. He said: Then it was called out: Do not uncover your private parts. He said: So he threw down the stone and put on his garment."],[1149,"I said: O Messenger of God, what may we expose of our private parts and what must we conceal? He said: Guard your private parts except from your wife or what your right hand possesses. He said: I said: O Messenger of God, what if some of us are with others? He said: If you can avoid letting anyone see your private parts, do so. He said: I said: What do you think if one of us is alone? He said: God is more deserving that one be modest before Him. And he placed his hand over his private part."],[1150,"A man should not have skin-to-skin contact with a man, nor a woman with a woman. It is not lawful for a man to look at another man's private parts, nor for a woman to look at another woman's private parts."],[1151,"Ali came upon us while we were bathing, some of us pouring water over others. He said: You bathe without covering yourselves? By God, I fear that you may be the worst of successors."],[1152,"Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ sent Salman on a military expedition. He camped by the Euphrates while in a tent of his made of wool or a cloak. He heard the voices of people and saw that they had camped by the water. He gestured with his hand like this—he raised his hand and clenched his fingers—and said: By God, to die then be resurrected, then die then be resurrected is dearer to me than to see a Muslim's private parts or for him to see mine."],[1153,"The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered a man, and he poured a bucket of water."],[1154,"When the Prophet ﷺ was at al-Abwa, he came forward and saw a man bathing in the open on a watering trough. The Prophet ﷺ turned back and stood. When they saw him standing, they came out to him from their saddles. He said: God is modest and loves modesty, and He is veiling and loves concealment. When one of you bathes, let him conceal himself. Abdullah b. Ubayd and Yusuf b. al-Hakam then said: He also said along with that: Fear God, and he said: Let his brother or his servant pour water over him; if there is none, let him bathe beside his camel. The Prophet ﷺ said something, all of it about that."],[1155,"The Prophet ﷺ went out and saw a hired worker of his bathing in the open. He said: I see you do not feel shame before your Lord. Take your wages; we have no need of you."],[1156,"The Prophet ﷺ hired a man and saw him bathing naked in the open near a ruin. He said to him: Take your wages and go away from us."],[1157,"Hasan and Husayn entered the Euphrates, each of them wearing his waist-wrapper. Then they said: In the water there is, or: the water has, inhabitants."],[1159,"The Messenger of God ﷺ saw me with my thigh uncovered, and the Prophet ﷺ said: Cover it, for it is part of the private area."],[1160,"Fear God regarding a house called the bathhouse. They said: O Messenger of God, it removes dirt and benefits against such-and-such. He said: Whoever enters it should cover himself."],[1162,"Fear God regarding a house called the bathhouse. They said: O Messenger of God, it removes dirt and benefits against such-and-such. He said: Whoever enters it should cover himself."],[1163,"It is forbidden to enter the bathhouse without a waist-wrapper."],[1164,"You will conquer the non-Arabs and find houses called bathhouses. Men should not enter them except with a waist-wrapper – or he said: with a loincloth – and women should not enter them except if they are postpartum or ill."],[1165,"The engraving on the ring of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari was a lion between two men."],[1166,"And he should not mention the name of God in it until he leaves it."],[1167,"Ali met two men who had come out of the bathhouse, anointed with oil. He said: From which group are you? They said: From the Muhajirun. He said: You lie; rather, you are from the Muhajirun. The true Muhajir is Ammar b. Yasir."],[1168,"Al-Hasan was asked about entering the bathhouse. He said: There is no harm in it if one has a loincloth. They said: We see naked people in it. Al-Hasan said: Islam is mightier than that."],[1170,"Ibn Umar entered the bathhouse once, wearing a waist-wrapper. When he entered, he found them naked. He turned his face toward the wall and said: Bring me my garment, O Nafi'. I brought it to him, and he wrapped himself in it, covered his face, and gave me his hand. I led him until he left, and he never entered it after that."],[1171,"Why do you not enter the bathhouse? He disliked that. It was said to him: You cover yourself. He said: I dislike seeing another's private parts."],[1172,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ applied depilatory paste, he himself attended to his pubic area."],[1173,"I entered the bathhouse with Abu al-Sha'tha', and I applied depilatory paste to him. I put my hands between his legs, and he said: Uff, uff, and disliked that. He himself attended to his pubic area and lower abdomen."],[1174,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray in a qamīṣ alone? He said: Yes, if it is thick. I said: May I pray in a fur garment? He said: Yes, and what harm is there? It has been tanned."],[1175,"He disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1176,"He disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1177,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1178,"He used to write to the regions: “No Muslim woman should enter a bathhouse except due to illness. And teach your women Sūrat al-Nūr.”"],[1179,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wrote to Abū ʿUbaydah: “It has reached me that women from among the believing women and the emigrant women enter bathhouses, and with them are women from the People of the Book. Forbid that and prevent it.” Abū ʿUbaydah said while angry, although he was not given to anger or obscenity: “O God, any woman who enters a bathhouse without illness or sickness, intending thereby to whiten her face, blacken her face on the day when faces become white.”"],[1180,"ʿĀʾishah ordered me, and I applied depilatory paste to her, then I applied henna immediately after it, from the parting of her hair to her feet, in a bathhouse that belonged to a fortress she had. She said: I said to her: Were you not the one who forbade women? She said: I am ill, and I now forbid that no woman should enter a bathhouse except due to illness."],[1181,"ʿUmar wrote to Abū ʿUbaydah b. al-Jarrāḥ: “It has reached me that women from among the Muslim women in your region enter the bathhouse with polytheist women. Forbid that most strictly. It is not lawful for a woman who believes in God and the Last Day that anyone other than the people of her own religion should see her private parts.”"],[1182,"Shall I perform ghusl with water other than the bathhouse water when I come out? He said: Yes. He said: I said to him: But the hot water is in a clean place from which it is taken. He said: I do not know what woman may have been hidden from me. I said to him: I applied depilatory paste and then performed ghusl in the bathhouse. Does it suffice me for wuḍūʾ? He said: I fear that you may have omitted something of wuḍūʾ in between."],[1183,"And Maʿmar used to do that."],[1184,"The purificatory baths are six: from janābah, from the bathhouse, from washing the dead, from cupping, ghusl for Friday, and ghusl for the two festivals."],[1185,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1186,"Water purifies and is not purified."],[1187,"He heard Ibn ʿAbbās being asked about the bathhouse, whether one should perform ghusl in it. He said: Yes, and may it be destroyed."],[1188,"Ibn ʿAbbās was asked about the bathhouse pool from which both the junub and the non-junub bathe. He said: Water does not become junub."],[1189,"He was asked about performing ghusl after the bathhouse, and he said: Allah has only made water something that purifies, not something from which one must purify oneself."],[1191,"I asked al-Shaʿbī, or he was asked: Does the bathhouse ghusl suffice? He said: Yes, and I consider it the most thorough ghusl."],[1192,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1193,"She experienced istiḥāḍah, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set the duration of her menstruation at six or seven days."],[1194,"The maximum duration of menstruation is ten days; after that she is mustaḥāḍah."],[1195,"The maximum duration of menstruation is ten days; after that she is mustaḥāḍah."],[1196,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A menstruating woman sees purity, purifies herself, then sees blood after that. Is that menstruation? He said: No. If she sees purity, she should perform ghusl. If she sees blood after that, she is mustaḥāḍah, since that is during her period. He said: She should pray as long as she sees purity, then complete her normal periods. If it increases beyond that, it is the status of mustaḥāḍah, so she should pray."],[1197,"Regarding a woman whose menstruation is six days, then she menstruates for two days, then she becomes pure. He said: She performs ghusl and prays. If she sees menstrual blood after that, she refrains until she becomes pure, up to ten days. If it exceeds ten days, then she is a mustahadah; she makes up the days that exceeded her normal period."],[1198,"She extends her purification for one day beyond her menstruation, then she is a mustahadah."],[1199,"Regarding a woman whose menstruation is seven days, she remains menstruating for two days, then she sees purity and fasts for one day, then she sees blood the next day, then the blood continues with her until the completion of ten days, then she becomes pure. She must make up that day because she fasted it during the days of her menstruation. If it exceeds ten days, then she is a mustahadah. He also said regarding a woman whose normal period was six days and then it exceeded her normal period up to ten: If she becomes pure at the completion of ten, she does not have to make up the prayers. If it exceeds ten, she makes up the days that exceeded her normal period."],[1200,"The mustahadah abstains from prayer for the duration of her normal periods, then she extends her purification by one day, then she prays."],[1201,"I said to Ata: What if her periods vary? He said: She completes the maximum of that, then she extends her purification by one day beyond the maximum."],[1202,"I said to Ata: What is purification? He said: The white discharge that is light, with which there is no yellowness or fluid — the light white discharge."],[1203,"Some women asked Aisha about a menstruating woman: should she perform ghusl when she sees yellowness and then pray? Aisha said: No, not until she sees the white discharge."],[1204,"I said to Ata: She sees during the days of her menstruation, along with her menstruation, yellowness preceding the blood, or fluid. Is that menstruation? He said: No, and she should not abstain from prayer until she sees the blood. I fear that it may be from Satan, to prevent her from prayer."],[1205,"If a woman sees after purification something that disturbs her, like the washing of meat, or the washing of fish, or like drops of blood before a nosebleed, then that is a kick from the kicks of Satan in the womb. She should sprinkle water and perform wudu and pray. Israil added in his narration: If it is fresh blood without any doubt, then she should abstain from prayer."],[1206,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1207,"I said to Ata: Suppose she menstruates and the blood ceases, but she sees fluid or a thin discharge? He said: She should not pray until she sees the clean white discharge."],[1208,"I suffered from istihadah for seven years and complained about that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said: That is not menstruation, but it is a vein, so perform ghusl. She used to perform ghusl at every prayer, and she used to bathe in a large basin and would see the blood in the basin."],[1210,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1212,"Concerning a pregnant woman who sees blood: They said: She is like a woman with istihadah; she performs ghusl once a day at the time of Zuhr prayer."],[1213,"If you are certain that it is in one part of the garment, wash that part and sprinkle the other part."],[1214,"I asked him about the mustahadah. He said: She sits for the days of her regular periods, then performs ghusl from noon to noon, uses a waist-wrapper, fasts, and her husband may have intercourse with her."],[1216,"She was asked about the mustahadah and said: She sits for the days of her regular periods, then performs one ghusl and performs wudu for every prayer."],[1217,"Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine."],[1218,"She waits for the days of her regular periods, then performs one ghusl for zuhr and asr, delaying zuhr and hastening asr. She performs one ghusl for maghrib and isha, delaying maghrib and hastening isha. She performs ghusl for fajr, and she does not fast, her husband does not have intercourse with her, and she does not touch the mushaf."],[1219,"He used to pray in his sandals."],[1220,"I used to have prolonged, heavy istihadah bleeding. She said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ to ask his ruling and inform him. I found him in the house of my sister Zaynab. I said: O Messenger of God, I have a need to bring to you. He said: What is it? I said: I feel shy about it. He said: What is it, O you? She said: I said: I have prolonged, heavy istihadah bleeding that has prevented me from prayer and fasting. What do you think about it? He said: I prescribe for you cotton, for it removes the blood. She said: I said: It is more than that. He said: Then use a cloth as a waist-wrapper. I said: It is more than that. He said: Then use a garment. I said: It is more than that, it gushes out. He said: I will command you two things; whichever of them you do, God will suffice you from the other. If you are able to do both, you know best. Then he said: This is only a kick from the kicks of Satan. He said: Menstruate for six or seven days according to God's knowledge, then perform ghusl. When you see that you have become pure and are certain, pray and fast for twenty-four nights and their days, and that will suffice you. Do likewise every month, as women menstruate and become pure according to the timing of their menstruation and purity. If you are able to delay zuhr and hasten asr, perform ghusl for both together, then delay maghrib and hasten isha, perform ghusl for both and combine the two prayers, and perform ghusl with fajr, then pray. Do likewise and fast if you are able. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: This is the more preferable of the two matters to me."],[1221,"She used to have constant bleeding and had asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he commanded her to perform ghusl for every prayer."],[1222,"A Muslim woman had istihadah bleeding, and she asked the Prophet ﷺ — or he was asked about her — and he said: It is only a vein. She leaves prayer for the duration of her menstruation, then combines zuhr and asr with one ghusl, maghrib and isha with one ghusl, and performs ghusl for subh."],[1223,"She should wait for the days she used to menstruate, then perform ghusl and pray."],[1224,"Ibn 'Abbas prayed on a cushion that covered the house."],[1226,"A mustahāḍah woman sent to Ibn al-Zubayr a slave of hers—or a mawlā of hers—saying: “I am afflicted; I have not prayed for such and such a time—I think he said: for two years—and I adjure you by Allah to clarify for me my religion.” And she wrote to him that she had been advised to perform ghusl for every prayer. Ibn al-Zubayr said: “I find nothing for her other than that.”"],[1227,"ʿAṭāʾ was asked about a woman whose menstruation left her for a long time, then the bleeding returned. He said: “She should wait; if it is menstruation, then it is menstruation, and if it is istihāḍah, then she has a similar case, but she should not abandon prayer. When she sees blood, she should perform ghusl for every prayer and then pray. Then, when she knows, she should abandon prayer. I fear it may be istihāḍah.”"],[1228,"He was asked about a woman whose menstruation left her for thirty years, then she experienced istihāḍah. He ordered that she be treated as a mustahāḍah."],[1229,"A woman used to suffer excessive bleeding, and Umm Salamah asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about her. He said: “She should wait for the number of nights and days she used to menstruate before what afflicted her occurred, and she should abandon prayer for that amount of the month. When she passes that, she should perform ghusl, then wrap herself with a cloth, then pray.”"],[1231,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “If she extracts blood, should she perform ghusl like the mustahāḍah?” He said: “No.” I said: “Are they different?” He said: “In the case of the mustahāḍah, what comes out of her comes from inside her.”"],[1232,"The mustahāḍah may pray and circumambulate the House."],[1234,"Regarding the mustahāḍah: she may fast, and her husband may have intercourse with her."],[1235,"He was asked about the mustahāḍah: may she be have intercourse with? He said: “Prayer is greater than intercourse.”"],[1236,"He was asked about the mustahāḍah: may her husband have intercourse with her? He said: “Yes, even if blood flows onto her heel.”"],[1238,"A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'I have been afflicted with istihadah outside my period.' He said: 'Use a piece of cotton. If it returns, use a piece of cotton, and fast and pray and make up what you owe.'"],[1239,"Sulayman b. Yasar was asked: 'May a husband have intercourse with a mustahadah?' He said: 'We have only heard of the concession for her regarding prayer.'"],[1240,"Sulayman b. Yasar was asked: 'May a husband have intercourse with a mustahadah?' He said: 'We have only heard of the concession for her regarding prayer.'"],[1241,"They used to pray in their fur-lined coats, hooded cloaks, and shawls without taking their hands out of them. We said to him, 'What is a mustaqa?' He said, 'It is a cloak made by the people of Syria with two long sleeves, its opening is on the chest, they wear it and tie its sleeves when they put it on.'"],[1242,"Regarding the mustahadah: her husband should not approach her."],[1243,"She waits for the days of her regular periods, then performs one ghusl for zuhr and asr, delaying zuhr and hastening asr. She performs one ghusl for maghrib and isha, delaying maghrib and hastening isha. She performs ghusl for fajr, and she does not fast, her husband does not have intercourse with her, and she does not touch the mushaf."],[1244,"Ata' was asked about the mustahadah and he said: 'She prays and fasts and recites the Quran, and she should use a cloth as a waist-wrapper, then she circumambulates.' Sulayman b. Musa said to him: 'Is it lawful for her husband to have intercourse with her?' He said: 'Yes.' Sulayman said: 'Is that opinion or knowledge?' He said: 'We have heard that when she prays and fasts, it is lawful for her husband to have intercourse with her.'"],[1245,"He was sitting with Abd Allah b. Umar when a woman came seeking his ruling, and she said: 'I set out intending to circumambulate the House, and when I reached the door of the mosque, I had a discharge of blood, so I returned until it stopped. Then I set out again and when I reached the door of the mosque, I had a discharge of blood, until I did that three times.' Ibn Umar said: 'It is a kick from Satan, so perform ghusl, use a cloth as a waist-wrapper, and circumambulate.'"],[1246,"If a virgin is not purified of her lochia in seven days, then [she waits] fourteen and twenty-one days, and the maximum of that is forty nights."],[1248,"If a virgin gives birth and the blood is prolonged, she should wait forty nights, then perform ghusl."],[1249,"She should wait seven nights, or fourteen, then perform ghusl and pray."],[1250,"If a virgin gives birth, she should wait like any woman among her womenfolk."],[1252,"Uthman b. Abi al-As used to say to a woman among his wives when she had given birth: Do not come near me for forty nights."],[1253,"I heard: When she menstruates, she sits for a period similar to [that of] her fellow women. Sufyan said: The yellowish discharge and the blood during the days of menstruation are the same."],[1254,"I said to Ata: Is there a prescribed ghusl for a menstruating woman? He said: No, except that she cleanses herself; she pours three handfuls on her head or more, for menstruation is more severe than janabah."],[1255,"Ata said to me: When a woman purifies herself from menstruation, she should wash her body with sidr. I said: Should she undo her hair? He said: No, and if she finds nothing but the ground, that suffices her; and if she finds no water, she may wipe herself with dust."],[1256,"He was asked about praying in the midst of graves. He said: 'It was mentioned to me that the Prophet ﷺ said: The Children of Israel took the graves of their prophets as places of worship, so Allah, the Exalted, cursed them.'"],[1257,"She used to instruct women, when they had purified from menstruation, to follow the traces of blood with yellowish discharge."],[1258,"Excellent are the women of the Ansar; shyness did not prevent them from understanding the religion and asking about it. When Surah al-Nur was revealed, they tore the borders or waistbands of their wrappers and made them into veils. Then so-and-so came and said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah is not shy of the truth; how should I perform ghusl from menstruation? He said: Let one of you take her sidr and water, then purify herself and perform purification well, then pour water over her head and press it firmly into the roots of her head, then pour water over her body, then take a piece of cotton perfumed with musk, or a piece (Abu Bakr was unsure), and purify herself with it, meaning the piece—the uncertainty—and some said the powdered perfume. She said: How should I purify myself with it? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shy of her and covered himself from her, and said: Glory be to Allah! Purify yourself with it. Aisha said: I understood what he meant, so I took hold of the opening of her garment and said: Follow the traces of blood with it."],[1259,"When a pregnant woman sees blood and her menstruation is according to her usual periods, she should refrain from prayer as a menstruating woman does."],[1260,"Concerning a pregnant woman who sees blood: They said: She is like a woman with istihadah; she performs ghusl once a day at the time of Zuhr prayer."],[1261,"If she sees it after purification, she performs ghusl."],[1262,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: 'May a person in a state of major impurity pass through the mosque?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1263,"Regarding the pregnant woman who sees blood, he said: She performs wudu and prays as long as she has not given birth. And if the blood flows, she is not required to perform ghusl; she is only required to perform wudu."],[1264,"When the pregnant woman sees yellowish discharge, she performs wudu and prays. And when she sees blood, she performs ghusl and prays. She should not abandon prayer under any circumstances."],[1265,"I wrote to Nafi': 'Ask Sulayman b. Yasar about a woman'—I think he said: 'who sees blood while she is pregnant.' Nafi' wrote back to me: 'I asked him and he said: If she sees blood that is not menstrual and not [at the time of] two periods, then she should perform ghusl, secure a cloth, and pray.'"],[1266,"We did not consider yellowish or dusky discharge to be anything."],[1267,"I asked Thawban about yellowish discharge. He said: There is no harm in it; she performs wudu and prays. He said: I said: 'Is this something you say yourself or something you heard?' He said: His eyes welled up and he said: 'Rather, I heard it.'"],[1269,"Ata was asked about a menstruating woman for whom medicine is prepared so that her menstruation ceases, while she is in her period as it is—may she perform tawaf? He said: Yes, if she sees purity. But if she sees a flicker and does not see the white discharge, then no."],[1270,"He asked Ibn Umar about a woman whose menstrual blood persisted, and she wanted to drink a medicine that would cut off the blood. Ibn Umar did not see any harm in it, and he described the water of the arak tree. Ma'mar said: And I heard Ibn Abi Najih being asked about that, and he did not see any harm in it."],[1271,"I said to him: 'Did your father command women at the time of prayer to perform purification and remembrance?' He said: 'No.'"],[1272,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: 'May a person in a state of major impurity pass through the mosque?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1273,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about menstrual blood that gets on a garment. He said: 'She should scrape it off with water, then sprinkle it and pray.'"],[1274,"The menstruating woman is not required to wash her clothes unless she wishes to."],[1275,"The menstruating woman is not required to wash her clothes unless she wishes to."],[1277,"That Aisha was asked about menstrual blood that is washed with water but its trace does not go away. She said: Allah has made water purifying."],[1278,"That she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about menstrual blood that gets on a garment. He said: Wash it with water and lote-tree leaves, and rub it with a rib."],[1279,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: 'May a person in a state of major impurity pass through the mosque?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1280,"One of us would menstruate and there would be blood on her garment, so she would rub it with a stone, or with a stick, or with a bone, then sprinkle it with water and pray."],[1282,"One of us would wash menstrual blood with her saliva, scraping it with her fingernail."],[1283,"I said to Ata, 'What do you think about a dog passing through the mosque — should its trace be sprinkled with water?'"],[1284,"Al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin used to dislike a man, after urinating, sitting in the mosque while not in a state of purity; but he may pass through and not sit. He said: Jabir b. Zayd saw no harm in sitting in it while not in a state of ablution."],[1285,"Enter the mosque in every condition as long as you are not junub."],[1286,"I entered upon Ibn Abbas, and Maymunah had sent me to him. I found two beds in his house. I returned to Maymunah and said: I see Ibn Abbas as nothing but one who has abandoned his wife. She sent to Bint Mishrah al-Kindi, the wife of Ibn Abbas, asking her. She said: There is no abandonment between me and him, but I am menstruating. So Maymunah sent to Ibn Abbas: Do you turn away from the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to have intimate contact with a woman from among his wives while she was menstruating, with a cloth on her reaching to the knee, or to the middle of the thigh."],[1287,"Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Ibn Shihab, from Habib the client of Urwah, from Nudbah. Matn: I entered upon Ibn Abbas, and Maymunah had sent me to him. I found two beds in his house. I returned to Maymunah and said: I see Ibn Abbas as nothing but one who has abandoned his wife. She sent to Bint Mishrah al-Kindi, the wife of Ibn Abbas, asking her. She said: There is no abandonment between me and him, but I am menstruating. So Maymunah sent to Ibn Abbas: Do you turn away from the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to have intimate contact with a woman from among his wives while she was menstruating, with a cloth on her reaching to the knee, or to the middle of the thigh. Isnad: Abd al-Razzaq said: And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Ibn Shihab, from Habib the client of Urwah, from Nudbah, from Maymunah."],[1288,"I was with the Prophet ﷺ under his blanket and I menstruated, so I slipped away from him. He said: What is the matter with you? Have you started menstruating? - meaning the menses. She said: Yes. He said: Tie your garments firmly around yourself. She said: So I tied my menstrual garments firmly around myself, then I returned and lay down with the Prophet ﷺ."],[1289,"I menstruated while I was lying down with the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ ordered her to adjust her garments over her, then he ordered her to lie down with him on a single bed while she was menstruating, with a garment over her private part."],[1290,"Enter the mosque in every condition as long as you are not junub."],[1291,"A group of people from the people of al-Kufah came to Umar b. al-Khattab and asked him about a man's voluntary prayer in his house, about what is lawful for a man from his wife when she is menstruating, and about the ghusl from janabah. He said: As for a man's voluntary prayer in his house, it is light, so illuminate your houses, and what good is a house in which there is no light? As for what is lawful for a man from his wife when she is menstruating, everything above the waist-wrapper is lawful; he must not look at what is beneath it until she becomes pure. As for the ghusl from janabah, perform wudu as you do for prayer, then pour water over your head three times and rub, then pour water over your skin."],[1293,"A man may have intimate contact with his wife when she is menstruating if she places a cloth over her private part."],[1294,"Ibn Umar sent to A'ishah asking her for a ruling about the menstruating woman: may a man have intimate contact with her? A'ishah said: Yes, if she places a cloth over her private part."],[1295,"A husband may have intimate contact with his menstruating wife if there is a waist-wrapper over her lower part. We heard that."],[1296,"What is beneath the waist-wrapper when the woman is menstruating is unlawful."],[1299,"One who has not become pure holds the status of a menstruating woman until she becomes pure."],[1300,"A'ishah used to comb the head of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in i'tikaf and she was menstruating. He said: He would lean his head toward her and she was in her room, and the Prophet ﷺ was in the mosque."],[1301,"Enter the mosque in every condition as long as you are not junub."],[1302,"Ibn 'Umar used to perform ablution in the mosque."],[1303,"A menstruating woman used to serve my father, and he would say, “Her menstruation is not in her hand.”"],[1304,"Ibn 'Umar used to perform ablution in the mosque."],[1305,"The Prophet ﷺ would place his head in my lap while I was menstruating, and then he would recite the Qurʾān."],[1306,"Mujahid forbade me from sleeping in the mosque."],[1307,"A menstruating woman may place something in the mosque and take something from it."],[1308,"The slave girls of ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUmar used to wash his feet while they were menstruating, and they would hand him the small mat."],[1309,"My mother sent word to ʿAlqamah: “May a menstruating woman nurse the sick?” He said, “Yes, but if you are near death, she should leave you.” I said, “May she wash me when I die?” He said, “No.”"],[1310,"Ibn ʿUmar used to have a menstruating woman wash his feet, and he would pray over a menstruating woman."],[1311,"The Prophet ﷺ said to her, “Hand me the small mat.” She said, “I am menstruating.” He said, “It is not in your hand.”"],[1312,"Abū Ẓibyān sent word to Ibrāhīm asking him about a menstruating woman performing ablution for me, and then I lean against her and pray. He said, “No.”"],[1313,"“O Mother of the Believers, what is lawful for a man from his wife when she is menstruating?” She said, “What is above the waist-wrapper.” Masrūq nudged a man who was with him with his hand, meaning, “Listen.” I said, “And what is lawful for me from her when I am fasting?” She said, “Everything except intercourse.”"],[1314,"If he has intercourse with her while she is menstruating, he should give a dinar in charity."],[1315,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ after having intercourse with his wife while she was menstruating, and he commanded him to give half a dinar in charity."],[1316,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded a man who had intercourse with his wife while she was menstruating to give half a dinar in charity."],[1317,"Whoever has intercourse with his wife during her menstruation should give a dinar in charity, and whoever has intercourse with her when the blood has stopped but she has not yet performed ghusl should give half a dinar."],[1322,"There is nothing upon him; he should seek forgiveness from Allah."],[1323,"There is nothing upon him; he should seek forgiveness from Allah."],[1324,"I asked him about a man who has intercourse with his menstruating wife. He said: I have not heard of any specific expiation for it, so he should seek forgiveness from Allah."],[1325,"A man said to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq: I saw in a dream that I was urinating blood. He said: You are a man who has intercourse with his wife while she is menstruating, so seek forgiveness from Allah and do not do it again."],[1327,"Regarding His saying: \"And do not approach them until they are pure. And when they have purified themselves, then come to them from where...\" He said: Women have two purities: purity, as in His saying \"until they are pure,\" meaning when they become pure from blood before performing ghusl; and His saying \"when they have purified themselves,\" meaning when they have performed ghusl. She is not lawful for her husband until she performs ghusl. He says: \"then come to them from where Allah has commanded you,\" from where the blood comes out. If he does not come to her from where he was commanded, then he is not among the repentant nor among those who purify themselves."],[1328,"A person asked ʿAṭāʾ: the menstruating woman who sees purity but does not perform ghusl—is she lawful for her husband? He said: No, until she performs ghusl."],[1329,"Sālim b. ʿAbdallāh and Sulaymān b. Yasār were asked about the menstruating woman: may her husband have intercourse with her when she sees purity before she performs ghusl? They both said: No, until she performs ghusl."],[1330,"I said to him: Should the menstruating woman make up the prayer? He said: No, that is an innovation."],[1331,"He was asked: Should the menstruating woman make up the prayer? He said: No, that is an innovation."],[1332,"I asked ʿĀʾishah: What is the matter with the menstruating woman that she makes up the fast but does not make up the prayer? She said: Are you a Ḥarūriyyah? I said: I am not a Ḥarūriyyah, but I ask. She said: That used to happen to us with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we were commanded to make up the fast but were not commanded to make up the prayer."],[1333,"I asked ʿĀʾishah: What is the matter with the menstruating woman that she makes up the fast but does not make up the prayer? She said: Are you a Ḥarūriyyah? I said: I am not a Ḥarūriyyah, but I ask. She said: That used to happen to us with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we were commanded to make up the fast but were not commanded to make up the prayer."],[1335,"The menstruating woman makes up the fast. I said: From whom? He said: This is what the people have agreed upon, and we do not find a chain of transmission for everything."],[1336,"If the menstruating woman becomes pure before nightfall, she prays ʿaṣr and ẓuhr; and if she becomes pure before dawn, she prays maghrib and ʿishāʾ."],[1337,"Mujahid forbade me from sleeping in the mosque."],[1338,"Indeed I recite my portion - or she said: my hizb - while I am lying down on the bed."],[1339,"They disliked touching the mushaf while not in a state of wudu."],[1340,"If she becomes pure at the beginning of the day, let her complete her fast; otherwise, no."],[1341,"If a woman becomes pure before sunset, she should pray the entire daytime prayer, and if she becomes pure before dawn breaks, she should pray the entire nighttime prayer."],[1342,"If she becomes pure at the time of ʿAṣr, she prays ʿAṣr and does not pray Ẓuhr."],[1343,"If a menstruating woman becomes pure at the time of a prayer, she prays that prayer, and if she does not become pure during its time, she does not pray that prayer."],[1344,"If a woman sees purity at the time of a prayer but does not perform ghusl until its time has passed, she should repeat that prayer, making it up. Al-Thawrī said this."],[1345,"If a woman menstruates at the time of a prayer that she had not yet prayed, she makes it up when she becomes pure."],[1346,"Qatādah was asked about a woman who slept through the last ʿIshāʾ prayer and woke up while she was menstruating. He said: 'When she becomes pure, she should make it up.'"],[1347,"Regarding a menstruating woman who sees purity at night but does not perform ghusl until morning, he said: 'She performs ghusl and completes her fast, and no makeup is required of her.'"],[1348,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: 'A woman begins the morning menstruating, then becomes pure during part of the day; should she complete the fast?' He said: 'No, she is to make it up.'"],[1349,"They used to dislike hanging anything with the Quran."],[1350,"If she menstruates before sunset in Ramaḍān, she may eat and drink."],[1351,"If she menstruates after ʿAṣr while she is fasting, she breaks her fast and makes it up."],[1352,"If she menstruates before night, there is no fast for her, and if she begins the morning menstruating then becomes pure during part of the day, there is no fast for her."],[1353,"I said to 'Ata': A woman who wakes up menstruating and does not see anything until she becomes pure. He said: 'She repeats it.' I said: 'What about a woman who menstruates at the end of the day? Does she complete what remains?' He said: 'No, she has menstruated, so she must repeat it.'"],[1354,"When a man has intercourse with his wife and she does not perform ghusl until she menstruates, he said: 'She performs ghusl.' He also said regarding menstruation: 'It is more severe than major ritual impurity. The junub may pass through the mosque, but the menstruating woman may not.'"],[1355,"Menstruation is greater."],[1356,"He disliked that a complete verse be written on a seal ring, except part of it."],[1357,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying in a single garment, wrapping himself in it and crossing its two ends."],[1358,"She washes her private part, and that suffices her."],[1360,"I said to 'Ata': 'What may the menstruating woman and the junub recite of the Qur'an?' He said: 'As for the menstruating woman, she may not recite anything. As for the junub, [he may recite] a verse [or part of it].'"],[1361,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray in a qamīṣ alone? He said: Yes, if it is thick. I said: May I pray in a fur garment? He said: Yes, and what harm is there? It has been tanned."],[1362,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1363,"I witnessed 'Ali b. Abi Talib urinate, then he said: 'Recite the Qur'an as long as one of you is not junub. If he is junub, then not even a single letter.'"],[1364,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1365,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib: 'May the junub recite anything of the Qur'an?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1366,"The junub may glorify and praise Allah, and supplicate, but he does not recite even a single verse."],[1367,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What may one who is not in a state of wuḍūʾ recite? He replied: Five verses and four."],[1368,"One who is not in a state of wuḍūʾ may recite verses, but he did not specify their number."],[1369,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: If a man wants to review the Quran and recite outside of prayer, should he perform wuḍūʾ like the wuḍūʾ for prayer in terms of thoroughness and wiping the head? He said: Yes."],[1370,"Ibn ʿAbbās used to grant a concession to one who is not in a state of wuḍūʾ to recite more than a verse or two."],[1372,"If you know that you had a wet dream in your garment and you do not know where it is, wash the whole garment. If you do not know whether it hit it or not, sprinkle it with water."],[1374,"Sometimes I would hear Abū Hurayrah reciting a sūrah rapidly, while he was not in a state of wuḍūʾ."],[1375,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb came out of the privy and recited a verse or some verses. Abū Maryam al-Ḥanafī said to him: You came out of the privy and you are reciting? ʿUmar said to him: Did Musaylimah give you this fatwā? He had been with Musaylimah."],[1376,"Ibn Masʿūd would prompt a man who was reciting, then he stood up and urinated, and the man stopped reciting. Ibn Masʿūd said to him: ...."],[1378,"Recite the Quran in every state, as long as you are not in a state of major ritual impurity (junub)."],[1379,"Indeed I recite my portion - or she said: my hizb - while I am lying down on the bed."],[1380,"Recite the Quran in every state, as long as you are not in a state of major ritual impurity (junub)."],[1381,"We entered upon Salman, and he recited to us verses from the Quran while he was without wudu."],[1382,"We came to Salman al-Farisi, and he came out to us from a privy of his. We said to him: 'If only you would perform wudu, O Abu Abd Allah, then recite to us such-and-such a surah.' He said: 'Allah only said: \"In a protected book. None touch it except the purified\" (Q 56:78-79). It is the Remembrance that is in heaven; none touch it except the angels.' Then he recited to us from the Quran whatever we wished."],[1383,"He was asked about a man who recites and then breaks wind. He said: 'Let him stop reciting until the wind passes from him.'"],[1384,"I said to Ata: 'I relieved myself in one of these mountain passes. May I wipe myself with earth and then recite?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1385,"In the letter of the Prophet ﷺ to Amr b. Hazm: The Quran is not to be touched except in a state of purity."],[1386,"Someone who was with Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ on a journey told me, and he had no water with him, and he was struck by janabah. He said: Do you think that if we set out we would reach the water before sunrise? Umar bathed and began washing what of the janabah had hit his garment. Amr b. al-As - or al-Mughirah - said to him: O Commander of the Faithful, if only you prayed in this garment. He said: O son of Amr - or al-Mughirah - do you want that I not pray in a garment that janabah has hit? Then it would be said: Umar did not pray in a garment that janabah had hit. No, rather I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."],[1387,"There is no harm in a person without wudu taking the mushaf and carrying it from one place to another."],[1388,"He did not consider the blood of fleas to be a problem."],[1389,"I said to Ata: 'May the junub and the menstruating woman touch the mushaf while it is in its covering?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'What if there is a cloth between them and its covering?' He said: 'No, and the covering is no more protective than the cloth.' I said: 'What about someone without wudu while it is in its covering?' He said: 'Yes, it does not harm him.' I said: 'May he take it closed?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1390,"Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine."],[1391,"They disliked touching the mushaf while not in a state of wudu."],[1392,"Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine."],[1394,"Ibn Sirin sent me to ask al-Qasim b. Muhammad about dirhams that contain the mention of God: do people buy with them while they contain the writing? So I asked him, and he said: There is no harm in the writing with which they transact; they only transact with gold and silver. If you went with the writing on a patch, they would not give you anything for it. But dirhams that contain the mention of God should not be touched except in a state of wudu."],[1396,"One without wudu should not touch dirhams."],[1399,"I asked Ibrahim: Should I write a letter without wudu? He said: Yes."],[1400,"It is disliked for a junub to write 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim'."],[1401,"It used to be considered recommended not to recite the hadiths of the Prophet ﷺ except in a state of wudu."],[1403,"I saw Saʿīd b. Jubayr urinate, then wash his face, then take the mushaf and recite from it."],[1405,"Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine."],[1406,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about seeking refuge (istiʿādha) that is on a menstruating woman or a junub person. He said: \"There is no harm in it if it is in a tube or on a piece of parchment that she passes over.\""],[1408,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"A ring on the hand of a menstruating woman or a junub person?\" He said: \"It does not harm him; in the ring there is only a letter or a small amount.\" I said: \"What about someone not in wudu who goes to the toilet with it on his hand?\" He said: \"It does not harm him.\""],[1409,"Ibn al-Musayyib was asked about a ring containing the name of God when a person becomes junub. He said: \"There is no harm in it.\" I said: \"I enter toilets and become junub.\" He said: \"There is no harm in it.\" And he said: \"Saʿīd b. al-Musayyib gave me this ruling.\""],[1410,"The inscription on the Prophet's ring ﷺ was 'Muhammad'."],[1413,"The inscription on his ring was 'There is no god but God,' and he did not wear it."],[1414,"Ibn ʿUmar inscribed his name on his ring, and he did not wear it."],[1415,"He disliked that a complete verse be written on a seal ring, except part of it."],[1416,"Abdullah b. Muhammad b. Aqil brought out to us a ring whose engraving was an image, and he informed us that the Prophet ﷺ wore it once or twice. He said: Then some of those who were with us washed it and drank the water."],[1417,"On the ring of Ibn Mas'ud there was a tree, or between two flies."],[1418,"The engraving on the ring of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari was a lion between two men."],[1419,"The engraving on the ring of Anas b. Malik was a crane—or he said: a bird with two heads—and the engraving on the ring of Abu 'Ubayda b. al-Jarrah was 'The fifth belongs to God.'"],[1420,"I asked Sa'id b. Jubayr about a ring on which the remembrance of God is written, and he disliked it."],[1421,"Al-Hasan and al-Husayn engraved the remembrance of God on their rings."],[1423,"He disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1424,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying in a single garment, wrapping himself in it and crossing its two ends."],[1425,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying in a single garment, wrapping himself in it and crossing its two ends."],[1426,"The last prayer the Messenger of God ﷺ prayed was in a single garment, crossing its two ends, behind Abu Bakr."],[1427,"He disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1428,"He disliked letting the garment hang down."],[1429,"That they both disliked it. Mujahid."],[1430,"If the garment is wide, pray in it wrapped around the body; and if it is small, pray in it as a waist wrapper."],[1431,"Umar b. al-Khattab saw a man praying in a single garment, wrapping himself in it, and said: 'Do not imitate the Jews. If one of you has only one garment, let him tie it around his waist.'"],[1432,"1386 Abu Bakr said: I narrated it to Ma'mar, and he said: I heard Yahya mention it. Hidden text: A man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I sometimes pray in a single garment.' The Prophet ﷺ remained silent until the prayer was about to begin, then he matched his two garments together and prayed in them. Hidden chain: Abu Bakr: I narrated it to Ma'mar, and he said: I heard Yahya from Qays b. Talq."],[1434,"None of you should pray in a single garment with nothing of it on his shoulder."],[1435,"That they both disliked it. Mujahid."],[1436,"There is no impurity on the garment."],[1437,"Jabir b. 'Abd Allah led us in prayer in a single garment. He said: I think he said: He tied it around his waist."],[1438,"I saw Jabir b. 'Abd Allah praying in a single garment. I said: 'Do you pray in a single garment while clothes are beside you?' He said: 'Yes, because of a fool like you.'"],[1439,"I saw Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh praying in a single garment, placing one end of it over the opposite shoulder."],[1442,"Khālid b. al-Walīd led us in prayer in a travel garment, wrapping himself in it."],[1443,"Pus is in the same category as blood."],[1444,"Ubayy b. Kaʿb and ʿAbd Allāh b. Masʿūd differed about prayer in a single garment. Ubayy said: There is no harm in it; the Prophet ﷺ prayed in a single garment, so prayer in it is permissible. Ibn Masʿūd said: That was only when people could not find garments; but when they find them, prayer should be in two garments. So ʿUmar stood on the pulpit and said: The correct view is what Ubayy said, and Ibn Masʿūd did not fall short."],[1445,"A man stood up to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, may a man pray in a single garment? He said: Does every one of you find two garments? Then, when it was in the time of ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, a man stood up to him and said: Should I pray ʿaṣr in a single garment? ʿUmar said: When God grants you abundance, then be generous to yourselves. A man gathers his garments upon himself; he may pray in a waist-wrapper and a cloak, in a shirt and a waist-wrapper, in a waist-wrapper and an outer garment with sleeves, in trousers and an outer garment with sleeves ... He said: And I think he said: in short trousers and a cloak, in short trousers and a shirt, in short trousers and an outer garment with sleeves."],[1446,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What is the least clothing in which I may pray a supererogatory prayer? He said: In a single garment. I said: Wrapped around? He said: Yes."],[1447,"In the letter which the Messenger of God ﷺ wrote to ʿAmr b. Ḥazm: None of you should pray in a single garment unless he places one end over the opposite shoulder."],[1448,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed in a striped cloak with markings. When he finished his prayer, he said: Take this striped cloak to Abū Jahm b. Ḥudhayfah and bring me his plain heavy cloak, for it distracted me just now from my prayer."],[1449,"Extra text: Ibn ʿUmar clothed him with two garments when he was a boy. He said: He entered the mosque and found him praying wrapped in a single garment. He said: Do you not have two garments to wear? I said: Yes, I do. He said: Do you think that if I sent you to the back of the house you would wear them? He said: Yes. He said: Is God more deserving that you adorn yourself for Him, or people? Nāfiʿ said: I said: Rather God. Then he informed him from the Messenger of God or from ʿUmar—Nāfiʿ was certain it was from one of the two, and I think it was from the Messenger of God ﷺ—that he said: None of you should wrap himself in prayer as the Jews wrap themselves. Let him drape it. Whoever has two garments should wear one as a waist-wrapper and then pray."],[1450,"Ibn ʿUmar saw me praying in a single garment and said: Did I not clothe you with two garments? I said: Yes. He said: Do you think that if I sent you to so-and-so you would go in this garment? I said: No. He said: God is more deserving that you adorn yourself for Him—or: that you adorn yourself for Him."],[1451,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Should I wrap myself in the garment? He said: No, the tawashshuḥ (wrapping over one shoulder and under the other) is more concealing—a man folds his waist-wrapper over his private parts twice. And he disliked ever praying in it when it was small. I said: What do you think if there were two men wearing waist-wrappers and they had one cloak, and they stood to pray—would you prefer that they both share that cloak while wearing their waist-wrappers, or that they wrap themselves in their waist-wrappers and leave the cloak? He said: Rather, they should pray in their waist-wrappers, and the cloak together is more beloved to me."],[1452,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in a shamlah (a small cloak) or a burdah (striped cloak) which he tied around himself."],[1453,"When a man prays in a garment folded over his private parts, there is no harm."],[1454,"I saw Ibn Ṭāwūs praying in a jubbah (outer garment) with no waist-wrapper or cloak on him. I asked him, and he informed me that his father saw no harm in praying in a jubbah alone, or a qamīṣ (shirt) alone, if it did not describe [the body]."],[1455,"When Ṭāwūs was asked about a single garment in prayer, he said: Can every person find two garments? He would say: A man may pray in a jubbah alone or a qamīṣ alone if it is thick. If the waist-wrapper is small and cannot be wrapped around, let him use it as a waist-wrapper."],[1456,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray in a qamīṣ alone? He said: Yes, if it is thick. I said: May I pray in a fur garment? He said: Yes, and what harm is there? It has been tanned."],[1457,"A man may pray in a single qamīṣ if it is tight; there is no harm."],[1458,"ʿAṭāʾ and Mujāhid entered upon ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd b. ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr, and he asked them: [What about] a man praying in a single qamīṣ? ʿAṭāʾ said: Yes. Mujāhid said: It is more beloved to me that he tie something around his waist."],[1459,"Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh led them in prayer in a qamīṣ with no waist-wrapper or cloak on him, and Jābir said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying in a qamīṣ."],[1460,"Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine."],[1461,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray in a garment I borrowed, not knowing whether it is pure or not? He said: Yes."],[1462,"If a man buys from a Muslim a garment from a polytheist or borrows it, let him pray in it and not wash it unless he recognizes something in it."],[1464,"There is no harm in the urine of camels. Some of them used to inhale water into the nose from it. He said: And they did not consider there to be any harm with cows and sheep."],[1465,"The bow is a cloak."],[1466,"When one of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ did not find a cloak to pray in, he would throw a rope on his shoulders—or he said: on his upper arm."],[1467,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think about that which you would eat—do you wash your garment from its droppings or its urine? He said: And what is that? I said: Camels. I said: And cows, sheep, game, and birds. He said: I would not wash my garment from that unless I found its odor disgusting or it was seen on my garment. I said: What about the horse, since its meat used to be eaten? He said: Perhaps I would wash my garment from its dung or its urine. There is no harm upon me in that if I left it. He said: Wipe it and sprinkle water on it."],[1468,"There is no harm in letting the garment hang down."],[1469,"If you have a wet dream in your garment and you do not know its place, sprinkle it with water."],[1470,"He used to let it hang down."],[1471,"I saw al-Ḥasan and Ibn Sīrīn letting their garments hang down over their shirts."],[1473,"He disliked that a man should wrap his cloak around his shoulders; he said: he should spread it out."],[1474,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by a man who had let his garment hang down while praying, so he folded his garment back over him."],[1476,"Additional text: That his father disliked letting the garment hang down in prayer. Abu Ubayda said: My father used to mention that the Prophet ﷺ forbade it."],[1477,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down in prayer."],[1479,"There is no harm in letting the garment hang down."],[1480,"That he disliked letting the garment hang down unless he held its two ends."],[1481,"He was asked about flea blood on a garment, and he said: 'Wash what you can.'"],[1482,"He saw a group of people letting their garments hang down and said: They are like the Jews coming out of their synagogues."],[1483,"If he lets his garment hang down while praying, let him wrap his cloak over himself. I mentioned that to Tawus, and he said: That is better and more beautiful."],[1484,"I saw Tawus praying while he had placed his cloak under his upper arm."],[1485,"That he saw no harm in a man letting his garment hang down when he was wearing a shirt, but if he was wearing only a waist-wrapper, he should not let it hang down."],[1486,"He used to dislike letting the garment hang down and he would trace it back as a hadith; then he mentioned the Prophet ﷺ."],[1488,"Ibn ʿUmar was asked whether one may pray in the garment in which one has intercourse. Ibn ʿUmar said: “I had intercourse in the garment I am wearing last night, and I pray in it.”"],[1490,"I asked ʿĀʾishah about a man who has intercourse with a woman in a garment and then sweats in it. She said: “When that happened, the woman would prepare a rag or rags and wipe with it, and the man would wipe with it, and she saw no harm in it—meaning in praying in it.”"],[1491,"ʿĀʾishah was asked about the garment in which the menstruating woman sweats. She said: “There is no harm in it—meaning in praying in it.”"],[1492,"He forbade praying in a woman’s undergarment."],[1493,"She used to dislike praying in it."],[1494,"Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine."],[1495,"A man said to Ibn Abbas: 'May I place the mushaf on a bed on which I have intercourse, have wet dreams, and sweat?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1496,"No washing or sprinkling is required for the garment of the menstruating woman or the junub."],[1497,"About semen that gets on a garment: “If you do not find it filthy, remove it with a blade of idhkhir grass.”"],[1498,"If you have a wet dream in your garment, remove it with a sprig of idhkhir or a rag, and do not wash it, if you wish, unless you are disgusted or dislike that it be seen in your garment."],[1499,"Aisha sent for a guest of hers, inviting him. They said to her: He is washing a janabah in his garment. She said: And why does he wash it? I used to rub it from the garment of the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[1500,"Remove the semen with a sprig of idhkhir or a stone from your garment."],[1501,"If you know that you had a wet dream in your garment and you do not know where it is, wash the whole garment. If you do not know whether it hit it or not, sprinkle it with water."],[1502,"A woman came with a son of hers on whom she had hung amulets, fearing that he might have the 'udhra. The Prophet ﷺ said: On what do you medicate your children with these amulets? You should use this Indian wood—meaning costus—for in it there are four cures, among them pleurisy. Then the Prophet ﷺ took her boy and placed him in his lap, and he urinated on him. He called for water and sprinkled it, and the boy had not yet reached the age of eating food."],[1503,"Ibn 'Umar used to have ḥulal (ornate suits) made for the companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, a single ḥulla reaching seven hundred to a thousand dirhams."],[1504,"If you are certain that it is in one part of the garment, wash that part and sprinkle the other part."],[1505,"He performed umrah with Umar b. al-Khattab in a group that included Amr b. al-As. Umar halted for the night on some part of the road near some waters, then he had a wet dream. He woke when dawn had almost broken, so he mounted, and the journey continued until he came to the water. He sat by the water washing what he saw of the wet dream until it became bright. Amr said: You have reached the morning, and we have garments with us. Wear them and leave your garment to be washed. Umar said: How amazing you are, O Amr! If you can find garments, does every person find garments? By God, if I did that, it would become a sunnah. No, rather I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."],[1506,"Umar was struck by janabah while on a journey. When morning came, he said: Do you think we can reach the water before sunrise? They said: Yes. He hurried the travel until he reached it, then bathed and began washing what he saw of the janabah in his garment. Amr b. al-As said: If only you wore a garment other than this and prayed. Umar said to him: If I found a garment, would every human find one? If I did that, it would become a sunnah, but I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."],[1507,"Someone who was with Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ on a journey told me, and he had no water with him, and he was struck by janabah. He said: Do you think that if we set out we would reach the water before sunrise? Umar bathed and began washing what of the janabah had hit his garment. Amr b. al-As - or al-Mughirah - said to him: O Commander of the Faithful, if only you prayed in this garment. He said: O son of Amr - or al-Mughirah - do you want that I not pray in a garment that janabah has hit? Then it would be said: Umar did not pray in a garment that janabah had hit. No, rather I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."],[1508,"He performed umrah with Umar b. al-Khattab in a group that included Amr b. al-As. Umar halted for the night near some of the waters, then he had a wet dream. He woke and morning had come, and he found no water in the group, so he mounted, and the journey continued until he came to the water. He sat by the water washing what was in his garment from the wet dream. When it became bright, Amr b. al-As said to him: You have reached the morning. Leave your garment to be washed and wear some of our garments. He said: How amazing you are, O Amr! If you can find garments, does every Muslim find garments? By God, if I did it, it would become a sunnah. Rather, I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."],[1509,"If you have a wet dream in your garment and you do not know its place, sprinkle it with water."],[1510,"There is no ritual impurity on a garment, nor on the ground, nor on a man whom a ritually impure person touches, nor on water. He says: If his hands precede him and he puts them into the water while he is ritually impure, before washing them, it is fine."],[1511,"Regarding semen that gets onto a garment and its location is unknown, he said: The garment should be sprinkled with water."],[1512,"Pus, blood, urine, and pre-seminal fluid that get onto a garment are all the same: one should rub it, then sprinkle it with water."],[1513,"I said to al-Zuhrī: A man sees a small or large amount of blood on his garment. He said: Sālim informed me that Ibn ʿUmar would turn away because of a small or large amount of it, then he would resume based on what he had already prayed, unless he spoke, in which case he would repeat the prayer."],[1514,"I was told that the Prophet ﷺ prayed in his sandals, then removed them and placed them on his left side. When he finished, he said: \"Why did you remove your sandals?\" They said: \"We saw you remove your sandals, so we removed ours.\" He said: \"I only removed them because Gabriel came to me and said that there was some impurity on them. So when you come to the doors of the mosque—or mosques—check them, and if there is impurity on them, scrape it off, then enter and pray in your sandals.\""],[1515,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Does the garment require washing? You informed me from ʿĀʾishah that she used to rub blood, until he said: That suffices him. I said: What about blood, pus, and everything similar — if it is rubbed, does that suffice? He said: Yes, rub it, then sprinkle it, and it suffices you. I said to him: I rubbed blood from my garment but it would not come out, and it defeated me. He said: Then sprinkle water on it and it suffices, even if you do not wash it."],[1516,"A man asked ʿAṭāʾ, saying: On my back there is skin with sores whose pus has filled my clothes, and washing has become burdensome for me. He said: Can you put a drying powder on it to dry them? He said: No. He said: Then pray and do not wash your garment, for God is most accepting of excuses."],[1517,"Regarding a garment that gets blood on it, he said: If it is excessive, one should turn away; if it is little, one should not turn away. He said: And he used to say: The area of a dirham is excessive."],[1518,"There is no harm in praying on a carpet and a small mat."],[1520,"He did not consider the blood of fleas to be a problem."],[1521,"He was asked about the blood of fleas on a garment, and he said: There is no harm in it."],[1522,"He did not consider the blood of fleas to be a problem."],[1523,"1477 1462 - Abd al-Razzaq reported from Hushaym, from al-Hajjaj b. Arta'ah, from Abu Ja'far Muhammad b. 'Ali, something similar. Hidden text: that he saw no harm in the blood of fleas."],[1524,"He was asked about flea blood on a garment, and he said: 'Wash what you can.'"],[1525,"Pus is in the same category as blood."],[1526,"When he prayed in a garment containing blood, he would not repeat the prayer."],[1527,"'Alqamah was wearing a burd - or he said: a garment - in which there was a trace of blood that had been washed but did not go away, and he used to pray in it. Someone said to him: 'If only you would set it aside and wear something else.' He replied: 'Among the things that make prayer in it beloved to me is that I see the blood of Mi'dad in it.' He said: 'We were besieging a fortress in Adharbayjan when a stone was thrown and struck him, cutting his head and causing blood to flow down his face. I implored him, so he took this burd of mine, wrapped it around his head as a turban, and began wiping the blood while saying: \"By Allah, it is only a minor wound, and Allah, Blessed and Exalted, blesses even a minor wound.\" He said: 'And his skull was split by a sword.' He said: 'Mi'dad later died from that wound.'"],[1528,"Regarding a garment that gets blood on it, he said: 'If it is excessive, he should stop the prayer; if it is little, he should not stop.' He used to say: 'The size of a dirham is excessive.'"],[1530,"'Ata' said to me: 'I have prayed many times in this garment of mine while it had blood on it, and I forgot to wash it.'"],[1531,"I saw al-Qasim b. Muhammad take off his shirt because of blood he noticed in it, and I forgot to wash it."],[1532,"Al-Sha'bi was asked about bat urine in the mosque and did not see any harm in it."],[1533,"I was with Ibn Sirin when bat urine fell on him, and he sprinkled water on it, saying: 'I did not consider sprinkling to be anything until it reached me from six of the Companions of Muhammad ﷺ.'"],[1534,"I asked Hammad about chicken droppings getting on a garment, and he said: 'When it dries, he should rub it off.'"],[1535,"He was asked about rainwater mud that gets on a garment. He said: He prays in it, and when it dries, let him scrape it off."],[1536,"There is no harm in dung that is on the two sandals, then he prays in them."],[1537,"I asked about a man who stepped on wet dung. He said: If he wishes, he wipes his feet on the ground."],[1538,"He was asked about a man who walks behind camels and gets splashed by their urine. He said: He sprinkles water."],[1540,"There is no harm in the urine of camels. Some of them used to inhale water into the nose from it. He said: And they did not consider there to be any harm with cows and sheep."],[1541,"There is no harm in the urine of beasts, except a stagnant pool."],[1542,"If the heat harms me, I do not mind prostrating on my garment. But as for prostrating on a person, no."],[1543,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked: Should we pray at camel watering places? He said: No. He said: Should we pray in sheep pens? He said: Yes. He said: Should we perform ablution from sheep meat? He said: No."],[1544,"There is no harm in the urine of that which has a stomach."],[1545,"I said to Ata, 'What do you think about a dog passing through the mosque — should its trace be sprinkled with water?'"],[1546,"A woman came with a son of hers on whom she had hung amulets, fearing that he might have the 'udhra. The Prophet ﷺ said: On what do you medicate your children with these amulets? You should use this Indian wood—meaning costus—for in it there are four cures, among them pleurisy. Then the Prophet ﷺ took her boy and placed him in his lap, and he urinated on him. He called for water and sprinkled it, and the boy had not yet reached the age of eating food."],[1547,"Ibn 'Umar used to perform ablution in the mosque."],[1548,"The urine of a girl is washed, and the urine of a boy is sprinkled. Sufyān said: We say: as long as he has not eaten food."],[1549,"The urine of a girl is washed, and the urine of a boy is sprinkled as long as he has not eaten."],[1550,"A boy was brought to the Prophet ﷺ and urinated on him, so he poured water over it."],[1551,"Regarding the urine of a boy, he said: An equal amount of water is poured over it. He said: This is what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did with the urine of al-Ḥusayn b. 'Alī."],[1552,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sleeping in my house when Ḥusayn b. 'Alī came toddling. I feared he would wake him, so I distracted him with something. She said: Then I became heedless of him, and he sat on the Prophet's ﷺ belly and placed the tip of his penis in the navel of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and urinated in it. She said: I was alarmed at that, so the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Bring me water,\" and he poured it over it. Then he said: \"The urine of a boy is sprinkled, and the urine of a girl is washed.\""],[1553,"I said to 'Aṭā': \"A boy who has not eaten food – do you wash his urine or his excrement from your garment?\" He said: \"I do not wash; I sprinkle on it or pour on it?\" I said: \"A boy licks ghee and honey before eating food, and that is his food.\" He said: \"Sprinkle or pour.\""],[1554,"'Umar b. al-Khaṭṭāb intended to forbid ḥibara (striped Yemeni cloth) dyed with urine, but a man said to him: \"Did you not see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wearing it?\" 'Umar said: \"Yes.\" The man said: \"Did not Allah say: 'Indeed, in the Messenger of Allah you have a good example'?\" So 'Umar abandoned that."],[1555,"Additional text: 'Umar intended to forbid ḥibara garments because they are dyed with urine, then he said: \"We were forbidden from excessive investigation.\""],[1556,"'Umar said: \"Would that we were forbidden from this 'aṣab (striped cloth), for it is dyed with urine.\" Ubayy b. Ka'b said: \"By Allah, that is not for you.\" He said: \"What?\" He said: \"We wore it during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while the Qur'an was being revealed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in it.\" 'Umar said: \"You have spoken the truth.\""],[1557,"I saw al-Zuhrī wearing what had been dyed with urine."],[1559,"Ibn ʿUmar, or ʿUmar, used to forbid dyeing with urine. He said: And ʿUmar used to commission garments for the companions of Muhammad ﷺ, and a garment reached a thousand dirhams or more than that."],[1560,"Ibn 'Umar used to perform ablution in the mosque."],[1561,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying in his sandals."],[1562,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Does a man pray in sandals? He said: Yes, that has reached me from the Prophet ﷺ, that he prayed in them and there is no harm in them, and in leather socks too."],[1565,"A man said to him: O Abū Hurayrah, did you forbid people to fast on Friday? He said: No, by your life, I did not forbid people to fast on Friday, except that I, by the Lord of this sanctuary—he said it three times—heard the Prophet of God ﷺ say: None of you should single out Friday for fasting unless he fasts other days as well. He said: I did not leave him until another came to him and said: O Abū Hurayrah, did you forbid people to pray in their sandals? He said: No, by God, I did not forbid people to pray in their sandals, except that I, by the Lord of this sanctuary—until he said it three times—saw the Prophet ﷺ here at the Maqām praying with his sandals on, then he left with them on."],[1566,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying in patched sandals."],[1567,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying in his sandals, and he pointed to the Maqām."],[1568,"Abū Mūsā led them and removed his sandals. ʿAbdullāh said to him: Why did you remove your sandals? Are you in the sacred valley?"],[1569,"I saw Ibn ʿAbbās praying in his sandals."],[1570,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the mosque, a bedouin entered and urinated in a corner of the mosque. The companions of the Prophet ﷺ shouted at him and wanted to remove him, but the Prophet ﷺ forbade them. When he finished, the Prophet ﷺ ordered that water be poured over his urine, or it was said: a bucket of water. Then he said: This is a place where one should not urinate; it was only built for prayer."],[1571,"Ibrahim, when the prayer was established, would put on his sandals and pray in them."],[1574,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people, then removed his sandals, so the people removed their sandals. When he finished, he said: \"What is the matter with you?\" They said: \"We saw you remove yours, so we removed ours.\" He said: \"Whoever wishes may pray in his sandals, and whoever wishes may remove them.\""],[1575,"I was told that the Prophet ﷺ prayed in his sandals, then removed them and placed them on his left side. When he finished, he said: \"Why did you remove your sandals?\" They said: \"We saw you remove your sandals, so we removed ours.\" He said: \"I only removed them because Gabriel came to me and said that there was some impurity on them. So when you come to the doors of the mosque—or mosques—check them, and if there is impurity on them, scrape it off, then enter and pray in your sandals.\""],[1577,"The Prophet ﷺ: while he was praying one day, he removed his sandals, so the people removed their sandals. When he finished, he said: \"What is the matter with you that you removed your sandals?\" They said: \"We saw you remove yours, so we removed ours.\" He said: \"Gabriel came to me and informed me that there was some filth on them. So when one of you comes to the mosque, let him look at his sandals, and if there is filth on them, let him rub them on the ground.\""],[1578,"A bedouin urinated in the mosque, so the people stood up to him and rebuked him severely. The Prophet ﷺ said: Leave him, and pour over his urine a bucket of water—or a pail of water—for you have been sent as facilitators, not as hardship-makers. Then the Prophet ﷺ stood up, with the bedouin behind him. While they were praying, the bedouin said: O Allah, have mercy on me and Muhammad, and do not have mercy on anyone else with us. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, he said to him: You have constricted something vast."],[1579,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed on the day of the conquest, then removed his sandals and placed them on his left side."],[1580,"When one of you prays in his sandals and wants to remove them, let him remove them between his feet, and not place them beside him, causing annoyance to anyone with them."],[1581,"Ibn Munabbih said to him: \"Why do you place your sandals on your left side and annoy your companion with them?\" His father heard that and said: \"Yes, place them between your feet.\" Thus Ibn Tawus would never place them except between his feet."],[1582,"He disliked that any part of his feet be visible from his sandals."],[1583,"He would look at his sandals when he came to the door of the mosque, [to see] whether there was dirt on them."],[1584,"We said to ʿAṭāʾ: A man prays in the mirḍaba that a person throws, while it is on him, and [with] rings? He said: He should remove them. We said: That involves trouble in tying the mirḍaba. He said: Even if it is only for the prescribed prayer. But if he prays in them, there is no harm, though I prefer that he not do that. He said: I said to him: What is the mirḍaba? He said: It is the nadwa. We said: And the rings? He said: The finger-rings that are on the fingers when one shoots."],[1585,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray while there is yarn in my waist-wrapper? He said: Yes, it is just like your garment. I said: And other than that, such as a stick or pages containing writings of truth? He said: Yes, but I prefer that he place it on the ground."],[1586,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray while there is gold or silver in my waist-wrapper? He said: No; put them on the ground, even if they are in a purse. I said: They are loose in my waist-wrapper. He said: Pour them out onto your sandals. I said: What is the matter with gold and silver among all that? He said: Because they have a form that those others do not."],[1587,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Ornamented swords—may I pray while wearing them? He said: I dislike that in Makkah, but in other places I do not dislike praying while wearing them. I said: Even if there is no fear? He said: Yes."],[1588,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray on smooth rock when I could find pebbles nearby if I wished? He said: No. I said: Would a dirt floor without pebbles, a place of hard earth with dust, suffice for me instead of pebbles, when I could find pebbles nearby if I wished? He said: If there is dust, it is enough for you."],[1589,"The Prophet ﷺ saw Ṣuhayb prostrating as if he were avoiding the dust, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him: “Put your face in the dust, O Ṣuhayb.”"],[1590,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I pray in my house on a plastered floor, or on marble, where there is no dust or pebbles? He said: I do not like that; pebbles are more beloved to me. I said: What if there were a handful of pebbles just where I place my face—would that suffice me? He said: Yes, if it is the size of his face, or his nose and forehead. I said: Even if there are no pebbles under his hands? He said: Yes. I said: Would you prefer that I make the entire place of prostration pebbles? He said: Yes."],[1591,"I said to Nāfiʿ, the freedman of Ibn ʿUmar: Did Ibn ʿUmar dislike praying on hard, smooth ground and seek out pebbles and dust? He said: He did not care."],[1592,"A man said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think—if I pray on hard, smooth ground, should I scrape away the dust from my face? He said: Yes."],[1593,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May I designate a spot in my house that is not a mosque, and I do not know of any impurity in it, and pray there? He said: Yes. I said: And I should not sprinkle it [with water]? He said: No, unless you fear that there may be impurity in it; then if you wish, sprinkle it."],[1594,"ʿAṭāʾ said to me: Take a place in your house as a mosque, for Zayd b. Khālid al-Juhanī said: Do not make your houses into graveyards; make places of prayer in them."],[1595,"Honor your houses with some of your prayers, and do not make them graves."],[1596,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think of a person praying on a small mat and a soft covering? He said: There is no harm in that, as long as it is not under his face and hands; but if it is under his knees, that is fine, because he prostrates on his bare face."],[1597,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think of a person praying while wearing a cloak in cold weather, and he prostrates on his cloak without taking out his hands? He said: It does not harm him. I said: What if it is not cold? He said: I prefer that he put both of them on the ground; but if he does not do that, there is no harm. I said: Do you prefer that he not pray on anything except the ground, and leave all that? He said: Yes."],[1598,"Ibn 'Umar used to pray on a small mat, under which was the mat of his house, not in a mosque, and he would prostrate on it and stand on it."],[1599,"I asked al-Zuhri about saffron in the mosque, and he said: It is good; it is the perfume of the mosque."],[1600,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed on a mat."],[1601,"I saw 'Umar b. al-Khattab praying on an 'abqari. I said: What is an 'abqari? He said: I do not know."],[1602,"Ibn 'Abbas prayed on a cushion – or a carpet – that covered his house."],[1605,"Ibn 'Abbas prayed on a cushion that covered the house."],[1606,"Ibn 'Abbas led them in prayer wearing a single garment, crossing its two ends over his shoulders, on a cushion that covered the house."],[1607,"I asked al-Zuhri about prostrating on a cushion. He said: There is no harm in that. The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray on a small mat."],[1608,"Ibn ʿUmar used to have a menstruating woman wash his feet, and he would pray over a menstruating woman."],[1609,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in the mosque and saw mucus in the qiblah. When he finished his prayer, he said: When one of you prays, he is conversing with his Lord, and Allah turns toward him with His face. So let none of you spit in the qiblah, nor to his right. Then he called for a stick and scraped it with it. Then he called for khaluq perfume and applied it – I think."],[1610,"We were with Hudhayfah, and Shabath b. Rib'i stood up to pray and spat in front of him. When he finished, Hudhayfah said: O Shabath, do not spit in front of you, nor to your right, for on your right is the writer of good deeds. Spit to your left or behind you. Indeed, when a man performs ablution well and stands for prayer, Allah faces him with His countenance, conversing with him, so He does not turn away from him until he himself turns away or commits a bad deed."],[1611,"I saw my father: a carpet was spread for him and he prayed on it, and I thought that was because the place was dirty."],[1612,"He was in a house and it leaked on him, so he pulled a leather mat and prayed on it."],[1613,"Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq used to prostrate, or pray, on the ground with his face directly on it."],[1614,"Ibn Masʿūd would not prostrate—or he said: would not pray—except on the ground."],[1615,"He used to stand on the papyrus mat and prostrate on the ground. We said: What is the papyrus mat? He said: The reed mat."],[1616,"I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shielding his face with anything, meaning during prostration."],[1617,"If the crowd becomes intense on Friday, then one of you should prostrate on the back of his brother."],[1618,"Whoever is troubled by the heat on Friday, let him spread out his garment and prostrate on it. And whoever is crowded by people on Friday so that he cannot prostrate on the ground, let him prostrate on a man's back."],[1621,"If there is a crowd, then prostrate on a person. Sufyan said: And if he is unable to prostrate on a person, he remains until the people stand, then he prostrates and follows them."],[1624,"If the heat harms me, I do not mind prostrating on my garment. But as for prostrating on a person, no."],[1625,"When Tawus spat in the mosque, he would dig a small trench for it, then bury it."],[1626,"There is no harm in prostrating on the coil of the turban."],[1627,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to prostrate on the coil of his turban."],[1628,"1580 Ibn Muharrir said: And Sulayman b. Musa reported to me, from Makhul, from the Prophet ﷺ the like of that. Hidden text: The Messenger of God ﷺ used to prostrate on the coil of his turban."],[1629,"I saw Makhul prostrating on his turban, so I said: Why do you prostrate on it? He said: I guard against the cold on my head."],[1631,"Shurayh used to prostrate on his burnoose, and Abd al-Rahman b. Yazid used to prostrate on his turban."],[1633,"I suffered a wound on my face and bandaged it. I asked ʿAbīdah al-Salmānī, 'Should I prostrate on it?' He said, 'Remove the bandage.'"],[1634,"Ibn ʿUmar disliked prostrating on the fold of his turban until he uncovered it."],[1635,"They used to pray in their fur-lined coats, hooded cloaks, and shawls without taking their hands out of them. We said to him, 'What is a mustaqa?' He said, 'It is a cloak made by the people of Syria with two long sleeves, its opening is on the chest, they wear it and tie its sleeves when they put it on.'"],[1636,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade uncovering a curtain, tying back hair, or performing ablution anew."],[1637,"We never used to uncover a garment. He said: Ibn ʿUmar used to bring out his hands, and al-Ḥasan would not do that."],[1638,"Abū Bakr addressed us and forbade us—or he forbade—praying on saddle blankets."],[1640,"Ibn ʿUmar disliked relieving oneself on the road or praying on it."],[1641,"I was with Ibrāhīm, and he led me in the dawn prayer and had me stand on his right, and he moved away from the road. Sulaymān said: It was recommended for a man to dismount on the right side of the road or to pray on the right side of the road."],[1642,"I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, which mosque was first placed on the earth?' He said, 'The Sacred Mosque.' I said, 'Then which?' He said, 'Then the Aqṣā Mosque.' He said, I said, 'How long between them?' He said, 'Forty years.' He said, 'Then wherever the prayer overtakes you, pray, for it is a mosque.'"],[1643,"A man said to Ata: 'Was flogging in the mosque forbidden?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1644,"Do not pray while there is a grave between you and the qiblah, but if there is a screen of a cubit between you and it, then pray."],[1645,"Umar b. al-Khattab saw me while I was praying at a grave. He began saying: 'The grave.' He said: 'I thought he was saying: the moon.' He said: 'So I began raising my head to the sky and looking.' He said: 'I only mean the grave. Do not pray towards it.' Thabit said: 'Anas b. Malik used to take me by the hand when he wanted to pray and then move away from the graves.'"],[1646,"The entire earth is a place of prostration except for the grave and the bathhouse."],[1647,"They used to dislike taking three types of structures as a qiblah: the grave, the bathhouse, and the toilet."],[1648,"Do not pray towards a toilet, nor a bathhouse, nor in the graveyard."],[1649,"Do not pray towards a toilet, nor in the bathhouse, nor in the graveyard."],[1650,"Among the worst of people is one who takes graves as places of worship."],[1651,"O Allah, do not make my grave an idol that is prayed towards, for Allah's wrath is severe upon a people who took the graves of their prophets as places of worship."],[1652,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on his deathbed, he began placing a corner of his cloak over his face, and when he felt distressed by it, he would uncover his face and say: 'Allah's curse be upon the Jews and Christians; they took the graves of their prophets as places of worship.' Aishah said: 'He was warning against the like of what they did.'"],[1653,"May Allah destroy the Jews; they took the graves of their prophets as places of worship."],[1655,"He was asked about praying in the midst of graves. He said: 'It was mentioned to me that the Prophet ﷺ said: The Children of Israel took the graves of their prophets as places of worship, so Allah, the Exalted, cursed them.'"],[1656,"I do not know but that he strongly disliked praying in the midst of graves."],[1657,"I said to Nafi': Did Ibn Umar dislike praying in the midst of graves? He said: We prayed over Aisha and Umm Salama in the midst of al-Baqi'. He said: The imam on the day we prayed over Aisha ؓ was Abu Hurayra, and Abdullah b. Umar attended that."],[1658,"A man said to Ata: 'Was flogging in the mosque forbidden?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1659,"One may pray in sheep pens, but one may not pray at camel watering places."],[1660,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked: Should we pray at camel watering places? He said: No. He said: Should we pray in sheep pens? He said: Yes. He said: Should we perform ablution from sheep meat? He said: No."],[1661,"1613 1597 - Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from a man, from Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla, from al-Bara' b. Azib, from the Prophet ﷺ the like of it. Hidden text: The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked: Should we pray at camel watering places? He said: No. He said: Should we pray in sheep pens? He said: Yes. He said: Should we perform ablution from sheep meat? He said: No."],[1662,"Umar b. al-Khattab ate camel meat and then prayed without performing ablution."],[1663,"Pray in sheep pens and wipe away their nasal discharge, for they are among the beasts of Paradise. He said: meaning the ewes among them. We said: What is their rugham? He said: What is in their nostrils."],[1664,"Be good to your sheep, wipe the nasal discharge from them, and pray in their vicinity - or he said: in their pens - for they are among the beasts of Paradise."],[1665,"Pray in sheep pens and wipe away their nasal discharge, for they are among the beasts of Paradise."],[1666,"If the prayer overtakes you in sheep pens, then pray; and if it overtakes you at camel watering places, then go out to open ground, for they are from the creation of Satan, or he said: from the visible presence of Satan."],[1667,"I said to Ata: May I pray in a sheep resting place? He said: Yes. I said: Do you dislike it because of dog urine among them? He said: Then do not pray there."],[1668,"Attend to your prayers as much as you are able, and the most to be guarded against for it are dog resting places."],[1669,"A man said to Ata: 'Was flogging in the mosque forbidden?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[1670,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī prayed with us in Dār al-Barīd on a spot where there was dung."],[1671,"They were with Ṭāwūs on a journey, and they wanted to stop at a place. He saw the trace of a dog and disliked stopping there, so he moved on—or he said: 'He turned away from it.'"],[1673,"He used to dislike praying in a church if it contained images."],[1674,"Do not learn the jargon of the non-Arabs, and do not enter their churches on their festival day, for wrath descends upon them."],[1675,"When ʿUmar came to Syria, a man from among the leading Christians prepared food for him and invited him. ʿUmar said: 'We do not enter your churches because of the images in them.'"],[1676,"When ʿUmar came to Syria, a Christian man prepared food for him and said to ʿUmar: 'I would love for you to come to me, and you and your companions honor me.' He was a man from among the leading Christians. ʿUmar said: 'We do not enter your churches because of the images in them.'"],[1677,"Salmān al-Fārisī was seeking a place to pray in, and a non-Arab woman said to him: 'Seek a pure heart, and pray wherever you wish.' He said: 'You have spoken wisely.'"],[1678,"He used to permit a person in a state of major impurity to pass through the mosque as a passerby, and I only know that he recited: '...nor in a state of major impurity, except those passing through.'"],[1679,"It is disliked to use a tooth-stick in the mosque, and to clip one's nails in it."],[1681,"A man said to ʿAṭāʾ: Was it forbidden to draw a sword in the mosque? He said: Yes, and it was forbidden to pass arrows through the mosque unless their tips were held."],[1682,"A person in a state of major impurity may not enter the mosque unless compelled to do so."],[1683,"A person in a state of major impurity may not pass through the mosque unless he finds no alternative; he should perform tayammum and then pass through it."],[1684,"They used to see no harm in burying a louse in the ground while in the mosque."],[1685,"A group from Thaqif came to the Prophet ﷺ, and the prayer was commenced. It was said, 'O Prophet of Allah, these are polytheists.' He said, 'The earth is not defiled by anything.'"],[1686,"The Prophet ﷺ said to 'Uthman."],[1687,"The Prophet ﷺ lodged the delegation of Thaqif in the mosque and set up tents for them there. They saw the people when they prayed and heard the Qur'an."],[1688,"We passed with Ali by the place of the earth's collapse at Babil, and he disliked praying there until he had gone beyond it."],[1689,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by al-Hijr, he said, 'Do not enter the dwellings of those who wronged themselves unless you are weeping, lest you be struck by the like of what struck them.' Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ covered his head and hastened the pace until he had crossed the valley."],[1690,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by al-Hijr, he said to us, 'Do not enter upon these punished people unless you are weeping. If you are not weeping, then do not enter upon them, lest you be struck by the like of what struck them.'"],[1691,"I said to Ata, 'What do you think about a dog passing through the mosque — should its trace be sprinkled with water?'"],[1692,"The Prophet ﷺ was concerned about the adhan, so much so that he considered ordering men to stand on the towers of Madinah and call to prayer, until they made a clapper or nearly did so. He said: A man from the Ansar called Abd Allah b. Zayd saw a man on the wall of the mosque wearing two green garments, saying: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; hayya ‘ala al-salah; hayya ‘ala al-salah; hayya ‘ala al-falah; hayya ‘ala al-falah; Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; la ilaha illa Allah. Then he sat for a while and repeated it, then said: Qad qamat al-salah twice – the iqamah. Then he went to the Prophet ﷺ and told him. He said, “Teach it to Bilal.” Then Umar stood up and said, “The same vision that came to Abd Allah came to me last night, but he preceded me.”"],[1693,"I said to Ata, 'May a menstruating woman pass through the mosque?' He said, 'No.' I said, 'May she enter her private prayer area in the house?' He said, 'No, she should keep away from it.' I said, 'If she did enter, should she sprinkle it with water?' He said, 'No.'"],[1694,"It is disliked for a woman to enter her mosque while she is menstruating, but she may place in it whatever she wishes."],[1695,"The slave girls of ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUmar used to wash his feet while they were menstruating, and they would hand him the small mat."],[1696,"I said to Ata': 'Should the mu'adhdhin face the qiblah?' He said: 'Yes. If he is in a village, he should turn to his right, his left, and behind him, and call the people with the adhan. If he is on a journey, and there are not many people with him, such as with a caliph, or there is no one among the people to call them to the adhan, then he should face the qiblah throughout his entire call.'"],[1697,"He used to urinate, then enter the mosque."],[1698,"Ibn Sirin used to sit at the edge of the mosque when he came out of the privy, with his legs on the ground, then perform ablution."],[1699,"I saw Ibn Sirin come out of the privy and sit on the wall of the mosque, having put his legs out, while performing ablution."],[1700,"A man should not call the adhan except in a state of ablution."],[1701,"They used to see no harm in a mu'adhdhin calling the adhan without ablution."],[1702,"A man said to 'Ata': 'A man goes out and urinates, then comes to Zamzam and performs ablution.' He said: 'There is no harm in that. And if he wants to relieve himself, let him enter; if he wishes, let him perform ablution at Zamzam. The religion is lenient and easy.' A man said to him: 'I see people performing ablution in the mosque.' He said: 'Sit down; there is no harm in that.' I said: 'Do you perform ablution in it?' He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'Do you rinse your mouth and sniff water?' He said: 'Yes, and I complete my ablution in the mosque of Makkah.'"],[1703,"I saw Abu Bakr b. Muhammad b. 'Amr b. Hazm performing ablution in the mosque of Makkah, and Tawus used to perform ablution in the Sacred Mosque."],[1704,"1844 1827 - Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj who said: Ibn Muslim informed me that a man asked Tawus, who was sitting with the people, 'O Abu Abd al-Rahman, when was \"prayer is better than sleep\" said?' Tawus replied, 'It was not said during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but Bilal heard it during the time of Abu Bakr after the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from a man who was not a muezzin, so he took it from him and used it in the call to prayer. Abu Bakr did not remain long before Umar said, \"If only we had forbidden Bilal from this innovation,\" but it was as if he forgot it, and the people have used it in the call to prayer until today.'"],[1705,"Regarding ablution in the mosque, he said: 'If it is not urine, there is no harm in it.'"],[1707,"I saw Abd al-Rahman b. al-Baylamani performing ablution in the great mosque of Sanaa."],[1708,"I saw Tawus performing ablution in the mosque. Abu Bakr said: And I myself saw Ibn Jurayj performing ablution in the Sacred Mosque while he was seated on a cushion of his; he rinsed his mouth and sniffed water."],[1710,"A man during the lifetime of the Messenger of God ﷺ, when he saw a dream, would relate it to the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: So I wished for a dream that I could relate to the Prophet ﷺ. He said: I was an unmarried youth, and I used to sleep in the mosque at the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. Then I saw in sleep as if two angels took me and went with me to the Fire, and behold, it was folded like the folding of a well, and the Fire had something like the two horns of a well—meaning by the two horns of a well: the two posts of the well—and behold, there were people in it whom I recognized. So I began saying: I seek refuge in God from the Fire. Then another angel met them and said: You shall not be frightened. Then I related it to Hafsa, and Hafsa related it to the Messenger of God ﷺ, so he said: What an excellent man is Abdullah, if only he would pray at night. Salim said: So after that Abdullah would not sleep at night except a little."],[1713,"My father sent me to Said b. al-Musayyab to ask him about sleeping in the mosque, so he said: Where else would the people of the Suffa sleep? And he did not see any harm in it."],[1714,"The people of the Suffa used to spend the night in the mosque. Alqama said: Then a man among them died, and his lower garment was opened and two dinars were found in it, so the Prophet ﷺ said: Two cauterizations."],[1715,"I said to Ata: Do you dislike that one spend the night in the mosque? He said: Rather, I love it—I love that one sleep in it."],[1716,"Ata used to sleep in the mosque for thirty years, then he would get up for circumambulation and prayer."],[1718,"I asked Ibn Abbas about sleeping in the mosque. He said: If you sleep for prayer or tawaf, there is no harm."],[1719,"Abdullah b. Masud used to patrol the mosque and would not leave any dark figure except that he expelled him, unless he was a praying man."],[1720,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us while we were lying down in his mosque and struck us with a palm branch that was in his hand, and said: Get up, do not sleep in the mosque."],[1721,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ invited me and a group with me from the people of the Suffah, and we had dinner with him. Then he said: If you wish, you may sleep here, or if you wish, in the mosque. We said: In the mosque. He said: So we used to sleep in the mosque."],[1722,"I said to Ata: If a man urinates in the mosque of Makkah or in his mosque at the House deliberately, not while sleeping, he said: It is preferable to me that he not do that. I said: And if he did, is there any sprinkling? He said: No."],[1723,"A bedouin urinated in the mosque, so the people stood up to him and rebuked him severely. The Prophet ﷺ said: Leave him, and pour over his urine a bucket of water—or a pail of water—for you have been sent as facilitators, not as hardship-makers. Then the Prophet ﷺ stood up, with the bedouin behind him. While they were praying, the bedouin said: O Allah, have mercy on me and Muhammad, and do not have mercy on anyone else with us. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, he said to him: You have constricted something vast."],[1724,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he heard the muezzin, said as he said."],[1725,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the mosque, a bedouin entered and urinated in a corner of the mosque. The companions of the Prophet ﷺ shouted at him and wanted to remove him, but the Prophet ﷺ forbade them. When he finished, the Prophet ﷺ ordered that water be poured over his urine, or it was said: a bucket of water. Then he said: This is a place where one should not urinate; it was only built for prayer."],[1726,"A bedouin urinated in the mosque, and the companions of the Prophet ﷺ stood up to him. He said: Do not rush him. When he finished, the Prophet ﷺ ordered a bucket of water to be poured over his urine."],[1728,"A bedouin urinated in the mosque, and the people were about to attack him. The Prophet ﷺ said: Dig up the place, and pour over it a pail of water; teach and make things easy, and do not make things difficult."],[1729,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque, he would say: Peace be upon the Prophet and the mercy of Allah. O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy and Paradise. And when he left, he would say: Peace be upon the Prophet and the mercy of Allah. O Allah, protect me from Satan and from all evil."],[1730,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque, he would say: \"O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad. O Allah, forgive me my sins and open for me the gates of Your mercy.\" And when he left, he would say the like of it, except that he would say: \"the gates of Your bounty.\""],[1731,"When you enter the mosque, say: \"O Allah, open for us the gates of Your mercy.\" And when you leave, say: \"O Allah, we ask You of Your bounty.\""],[1732,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque, he would say: \"In the name of Allah. O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy and make easy for me the gates of Your provision.\""],[1734,"When you enter the mosque, greet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. And when you enter upon your family, say: \"Peace be upon you.\" And when you enter a house in which there is no one, say: \"Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah.\""],[1735,"I asked Alqamah, \"What do you say when you enter the mosque?\" He said, \"I say: 'Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. May Allah and His angels send blessings upon Muhammad.'\""],[1736,"Ka'b said to Abu Hurayrah: \"Preserve two things from me. When you enter the mosque, greet the Prophet ﷺ and say: 'O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy.' And when you leave, say: 'O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad. O Allah, protect me from Satan.'\""],[1740,"No provision is to be taken for the call to prayer."],[1741,"If you enter the mosque and bow (pray two rak'ahs), then leave and enter again, the first bowing suffices you."],[1742,"A man said to 'Ata': Was it ever said: When a man passes by the mosque, let him pray two rak'ahs in it? He replied: I have not heard that about it, but it is good."],[1743,"A man entered the mosque, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: Do not sit until you pray two rak'ahs."],[1744,"Among the signs of the Hour is that a man will pass through the mosque and not pray two rak'ahs."],[1745,"I saw Ibn 'Umar enter the mosque and leave it without praying in it."],[1746,"When you pray, you are conversing with your Lord, so do not spit in front of you, nor to your right, but to your left; and if there is something on your left that obstructs you, then spit under your foot."],[1747,"The adhan is the banner of faith."],[1748,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in the mosque and saw mucus in the qiblah. When he finished his prayer, he said: When one of you prays, he is conversing with his Lord, and Allah turns toward him with His face. So let none of you spit in the qiblah, nor to his right. Then he called for a stick and scraped it with it. Then he called for khaluq perfume and applied it – I think."],[1749,"The Prophet ﷺ scraped it off, then sprinkled its trace with saffron which he called for."],[1750,"I asked al-Zuhri about saffron in the mosque, and he said: It is good; it is the perfume of the mosque."],[1751,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in the mosque and saw mucus in the qiblah. When he finished his prayer, he said: When one of you prays, his Lord converses with him. Then he said: Who is your imam? They said: Abu so-and-so. So he removed him. Then his wife was informed, and she ordered water and washed it, prepared it, and – I think he said – perfumed the mosque with incense. When the Prophet ﷺ entered the mosque, he said: Who did this? They said: The wife of so-and-so. So he restored her husband as imam."],[1752,"When any of you stands for prayer, let him not spit in front of him, for he is conversing with Allah as long as he is in his prayer place; nor to his right, for on his right there is an angel; but let him spit to his left or under his feet."],[1753,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying, then he spat under his foot and rubbed it with his sandal while it was on his foot."],[1754,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: When you pray, do not spit in front of you, nor to your right, but spit to your left if it is empty; otherwise, under your foot. And he pointed with his foot and scraped the ground."],[1755,"We were with Hudhayfah, and Shabath b. Rib'i stood up to pray and spat in front of him. When he finished, Hudhayfah said: O Shabath, do not spit in front of you, nor to your right, for on your right is the writer of good deeds. Spit to your left or behind you. Indeed, when a man performs ablution well and stands for prayer, Allah faces him with His countenance, conversing with him, so He does not turn away from him until he himself turns away or commits a bad deed."],[1756,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque, so he scraped it off with a pebble or something, then said: Does this person not fear that it may become a brand between his eyes? One of them said: Then the Prophet ﷺ called for perfume or saffron and smeared it on the spot."],[1757,"The mosque shrinks from phlegm as a piece of flesh or a hide shrinks in the fire."],[1758,"When one of you prays, he should not spit in front of him or to his right, but to his left; and if he cannot, let him spit into the edge of his garment. And he said: Like this, and he folded his garment and rubbed it in it."],[1759,"Ata said to me: A man may spit during prayer to his left; if he finds no place, let him lift his left foot and spit under it."],[1760,"When Tawus spat in the mosque, he would dig a small trench for it, then bury it."],[1761,"I asked a man from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ about spitting in the mosque. He said: It is a sin, and its expiation is to bury it."],[1762,"A man from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ spat at night, then brought a lamp and buried it."],[1763,"Phlegm in the mosque is a sin, and its expiation is to bury it."],[1764,"When he spat in the mosque, he would make a deep hole for it, then bury it."],[1765,"We were with Abdullah b. Mas'ud, and he wanted to spit while the space to his right was empty, but he disliked spitting to his right even though he was not in prayer."],[1766,"He was ill and spat to his right, or wanted to spit, and said: 'I have not spat to my right since I became Muslim.'"],[1767,"He heard Umar b. Abd al-Aziz say to his son Abd al-Malik, who had spat to his right while on a journey, and he forbade him from that and said: 'You harm your companion. Spit to your left.'"],[1769,"I said to 'Ata': Has it reached you that it used to be said: It is incumbent upon the imam not to supplicate for himself for anything without supplicating for those behind him with the like of it? He said: Yes. I said: What is his right upon them? He said: They supplicate and seek forgiveness for themselves and for the believing men and women, and they do not single him out for anything except among the believers. I said: How does he supplicate? He said: He says: O Allah, forgive us; O Allah, have mercy on us. Then he includes the believing men and women, so he starts with them and singles them out, saying: O Allah, forgive us; O Allah, have mercy on us. This is specifically for them. Then he includes the believing men and women after that, and he does not name those behind him except in that way."],[1771,"I saw al-Shabi beat a man who had slandered another man in the courtyard (al-rahba), and he did not beat him in the mosque."],[1773,"Marwan was asked about beating in the mosque. He said: 'The mosque has sanctity.'"],[1774,"The first call of the adhan is only to notify the people. He said: It is a binding duty that is indispensable, and it is not permissible otherwise. When one hears the adhan, he must come and attend the prayer. Then he told me about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: What is the matter with men who hear the call to prayer and then stay behind? I was determined to establish the prayer and then no one would stay away from it except that I would burn his house, or burn it down upon him. A man came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, I am blind and it is hard for me not to attend the prayer. The Prophet ﷺ said: Attend it. He said: I am blind, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. He said: Then attend it. I said: What was his impairment? He said: I think he was blind or had weak eyesight, and he asked for a concession regarding the night prayer. Ibn Jurayj said: Someone I trust told me that the man was Ibn Umm Maktum."],[1775,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade reciting poetry, recounting wounds, and carrying out prescribed punishments in the mosque."],[1777,"Do not make much idle talk - meaning in the mosque. He said: One day he entered the mosque and found two men whose voices had risen, so they rushed to him and he caught one of them and beat him and said: 'Who are you from?' He said: 'From Thaqif.' He said: 'In this mosque of ours the voice is not raised.'"],[1778,"Umar heard a man raise his voice, so he said: \"Where are you from?\" The man said: \"From Thaqif.\" He said: \"From which land?\" He said: \"From the people of al-Ta'if.\" He said: \"Indeed, if you had been from the people of this city of ours, I would have given you a painful beating. In this mosque of ours, the voice is not to be raised.\""],[1779,"When Umar went out for prayer, he would call out in the mosque: \"Beware of idle talk.\" And he used to say: \"Raise your voices in the mosque.\""],[1780,"I said to Ata': \"What about shouting in the mosque?\" He said: \"As for talk that has no harm in it, it is fine; but as for obscene speech or insults, then no.\""],[1781,"Ubayy b. Ka'b heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque. He said: \"He bit him.\" The man said: \"O Abu al-Mundhir, you were not one to use obscene language.\" He said: \"We were commanded to do this.\""],[1782,"Hassan b. Thabit recited poetry in the mosque, and Umar passed by him and gave him a look. Hassan said: \"By Allah, I recited poetry in it when there was one better than you in it.\" Umar feared that he might be referring to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he let it pass and left him alone."],[1783,"A poet came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in the mosque and said: \"Shall I recite poetry to you, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"No.\" The poet said: \"Yes, so give me permission.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Then leave the mosque.\" He left the mosque. The Prophet ﷺ gave him a garment and said: \"This is in place of the praise you gave your Lord.\""],[1784,"It is disliked to use a tooth-stick in the mosque, and to clip one's nails in it."],[1785,"I said to Ata: \"What do you think of someone from the people of the village who does not hear the call?\" He said: \"If he wishes, he may come, and if he wishes, he may not.\" He said: I said: \"Even if he is close to the mosque?\" He said: \"If he wishes, let him come, and if he wishes, let him sit.\" I said: \"What do you think if I am in a dwelling where sometimes I hear it and sometimes I do not hear it? Do I have a concession to sit when I do not hear it?\" He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"Even if I know that the time of prayer has come, the time at which I think it is prayed?\" He said: \"Yes, if you do not hear the call.\""],[1786,"A man announced his lost animal in the mosque, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"May he not find his lost animal.\""],[1787,"I asked al-Hasan: I said, 'We pass by the mosque and hear the iqama and want to go beyond it to another.' He said, 'A Muslim man would say to his brother when he heard the iqama: \"You have been detained.\"'"],[1788,"The Prophet ﷺ heard a man announcing a lost camel of his that was red in the mosque, saying: \"Who will lead me to the red camel?\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"May you not find it. Mosques were built only for what they were built for.\""],[1789,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"O seeker, may someone other than you find it. Mosques were not built for this.\""],[1790,"The Prophet ﷺ heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, and he said: \"O seeker, may someone other than you find it.\""],[1791,"Ibn Mas'ud heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, so he seized him, rebuked him, and said: We have been forbidden from this."],[1792,"It used to be said: When someone announces a lost item in the mosque, say: 'May Allah not return it to you.' And when someone buys or sells in the mosque, it is said: 'May Allah not profit your trade.'"],[1793,"Keep away from your mosques your insane, your children, raising your voices, unsheathing your swords, your selling, your buying, establishing your prescribed punishments, and your disputes; perfume them on the day of your Friday gatherings, and place your means of purification at their doors."],[1795,"Keep your mosques away from children and the insane."],[1796,"Whoever would be pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him maintain these prescribed prayers where the call to them is given, for they are among the sunan of guidance, and Allah has prescribed for your Prophet ﷺ the sunan of guidance. By my life, I think none of you has not taken a mosque in his house, and if you were to pray in your houses as this laggard prays in his house, you would abandon the sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ, and if you abandoned the sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ you would go astray. I have seen us when none stayed away from it except a hypocrite whose hypocrisy was known or one whose hypocrisy was acknowledged, and I have seen a man being supported between two men until he was placed in the row. No man performs ablution and does it well, then takes a step intending by it a mosque for Allah, except that Allah writes for him a good deed by it, raises him a degree by it, and erases a sin from him by it, until we used to take short steps."],[1797,"If I had any authority over the people's affairs, I would not leave two people disputing in the mosque."],[1798,"He saw Shurayḥ adjudicating in the mosque, and I myself saw Ibn Abī Laylā adjudicating in the mosque."],[1799,"A man said to ʿAṭāʾ: Was it forbidden to draw a sword in the mosque? He said: Yes, and it was forbidden to pass arrows through the mosque unless their tips were held."],[1800,"Jabir b. Abd Allah was asked about unsheathing a sword in the mosque. He said: We used to dislike that. A man used to give arrows as charity in the mosque, so the Prophet ﷺ ordered him not to pass through the mosque with them unless he was grasping all of their blades."],[1802,"He used to dislike drawing the sword in the mosque."],[1803,"When you pass by arrows in the markets of the Muslims or their mosques, then hold them by their points, so that you do not wound anyone with them."],[1804,"Whoever eats of this plant—meaning garlic—let him not approach this mosque of mine."],[1805,"I said to 'Ata': 'What do you think about the one you mentioned being prohibited in the mosque—is it in all mosques or specifically in the Sacred Mosque to the exclusion of others?' He said: 'Rather, in all mosques.'"],[1806,"Whoever eats of this plant—meaning garlic—let him not cause us harm in our mosque."],[1807,"Whoever eats this plant—meaning garlic—let him not come near this mosque of mine, and let him not come to us wiping his forehead. He said: 'I said: O Abu Sa'id, is it forbidden?' He said: 'No, the Prophet ﷺ only disliked it because of its smell.'"],[1808,"Whoever eats this foul plant, let him not come near this mosque of ours."],[1809,"Whoever eats this foul plant, let him not cause us harm in our mosque, and let him sit in his house."],[1811,"If you plaster a mosque with clay mixed with dung, do not pray in it until you wash it, if it becomes pure for it."],[1812,"'Ubayd b. 'Umayr saw a louse on Ibn 'Umar in the mosque, so he took it and buried it, while Ibn 'Umar was looking at him and did not object to that."],[1813,"May God have mercy on you! Do you think every hadith of the Prophet ﷺ reached Ibn 'Umar? Then Yahya said: It reached me that the Prophet ﷺ said: 'If one of you sees a louse, let him not kill it in the mosque; rather, let him wrap it in his garment, and when he goes out, let him kill it.'"],[1814,"Abu Umama saw a louse on his clothes while he was in the mosque, so he took it and buried it in the mosque, while Abu Ghalib was looking at him."],[1815,"Abu Umama used to delouse himself in the mosque."],[1816,"Ibn Mas'ud took a louse and buried it in the mosque, then said: 'Have We not made the earth a container, for the living and the dead?'"],[1817,"They hope for a man, when he walks to the mosque—meaning for prayer in the dark night—for forgiveness."],[1818,"He saw Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī killing a louse in the mosque between two pebbles."],[1819,"He saw Abū Hurayrah burying a louse in the mosque and saying: “Phlegm is worse than it.”"],[1821,"I saw Muʿādh b. Jabal killing lice and fleas during prayer."],[1822,"There is no ablution required for killing a louse. He said: Ibn Sīrīn used to consider ablution [necessary]."],[1823,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded us to kill the two black ones during prayer: the snake and the scorpion."],[1825,"He was asked about a man killing a scorpion during prayer. He said: “In prayer there is indeed enough to occupy one.”"],[1826,"Do not struggle against the two foul things during prayer: defecation and urination."],[1827,"That I carry it in the corner of my cloak is dearer to me than struggling against defecation and urination."],[1828,"We were with ʿAbd Allāh b. al-Arqam al-Zuhrī when the prayer was called, then he went to relieve himself. It was said to him: “What is this?” He said: “I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: ‘When the prayer is called and one of you needs to relieve himself, let him begin with relieving himself.’”"],[1829,"We were with him on a journey, and he was leading them. When the prayer time came, he said: \"Let one of you lead you, for I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'When the prayer time comes and one of you needs to relieve himself, let him begin with relieving himself.'\""],[1830,"He said: \"We went out for Hajj—or 'Umrah—with 'Abd Allah b. al-Arqam al-Zuhri. He called the Iqamah for prayer, then said: 'Pray,' and went to relieve himself. When he returned, he said: 'The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"When the Iqamah is called and one of you needs to defecate, let him begin with defecating.\"'\""],[1831,"\"Do not resist the two impure things during prayer: defecation and urination.\""],[1832,"\"I guard against one of them just as I guard against the other: defecation and urination.\""],[1833,"\"We would restrain it (the urge) severely.\""],[1835,"\"He used to see no harm in that as long as he did not fear that it would distract him from his prayer or that he would be overtaken (by the urge).\""],[1836,"\"Let none of you pray while he is resisting urination or defecation.\""],[1837,"\"Prayer was made obligatory as fifty, then it was reduced until it was made five. Then it was proclaimed: 'O Muhammad, the word with Me is not changed, and for you with these five are fifty.'\""],[1838,"\"Prayer was made obligatory upon the Prophet ﷺ on the night he was taken on the Night Journey: fifty prayers. Then it was reduced until it was made five. Then God said: 'For you with the five are fifty; the good deed is multiplied tenfold.'\""],[1839,"\"Fifty prayers were made obligatory upon the Prophet ﷺ on the night he was taken on the Night Journey, then they were reduced to five.\" Al-Hasan said: \"Then it was proclaimed: 'I have decreed My obligation and lightened the burden on My servants, and for you with these five are fifty.'\""],[1840,"\"Establish prayer at the two ends of the day\" until the end of the verse. He said: \"The first prayer the Messenger of God ﷺ prayed was the Zuhr. Gabriel came to him and said: '\"Verily, we stand in rows, and verily, we are the ones who glorify.' He said: 'Gabriel stood in front of the Messenger of God ﷺ, with the Prophet ﷺ behind him, then the people behind the Messenger of God ﷺ, and the women behind the men.' He said: 'He led them in the Zuhr prayer—that is, four rak'ahs. When it was time for 'Asr, Gabriel stood and did the same. Then Gabriel came when the sun had set and led them in three rak'ahs, reciting aloud in the first two rak'ahs, and not being heard in the third.' Al-Hasan said: 'This is the odd-numbered prayer of the day.' He said: 'When it was time for 'Isha', and the twilight had disappeared and darkness had set in, Gabriel came to him and stood before him and led the people in four rak'ahs, reciting aloud in the two rak'ahs. When morning came the next night, he led him and the people with him in a manner similar to what he had done: he led them in two rak'ahs, reciting in them and prolonging the recitation. The Prophet ﷺ did not die until he had defined for the people their prayers.' Then al-Hasan mentioned the Friday prayer. He said: 'He led them in two rak'ahs and removed two rak'ahs from them because of the gathering of the people on that day and for the sermon.' God said: 'Establish prayer at the two ends of the day and in some watches of the night; indeed, good deeds remove evil deeds—that is a reminder for those who remember.' And he mentioned the two ends of the day as being from the morning prayer to the Fajr prayer, and 'in some watches of the night' as the Maghrib and 'Isha' prayers."],[1841,"Nafi' b. al-Azraq disputed with Ibn al-'Abbas and said: Do you find the five prayers in the Quran? He said: Yes. Then he recited to him: “So glory be to Allah when you enter the evening and when you enter the morning”—that is Maghrib and Fajr—“and in the afternoon”—that is 'Asr—“and when you reach midday”—that is Zuhr. He said: “And after the 'Isha prayer.”"],[1842,"When the Prophet ﷺ awoke from the night in which he was taken on the Night Journey, nothing startled him except Gabriel. He descended when the sun had declined — for that reason it was called the first. He stood and called to his companions: Prayer in congregation! So they gathered, and Gabriel prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with the people. He lengthened the first two rak‘ahs, then shortened the remaining two. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people. Then he descended for Asr likewise, and they did the same as they had done for Zuhr. Then he descended at the beginning of the night and called out: Prayer in congregation! So Gabriel prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people. He lengthened the first two and shortened the third. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people. Then when a third of the night had passed, he descended and called to the people: Prayer in congregation! So they gathered, and Gabriel prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people. He recited in the first two and lengthened and recited aloud, and shortened the remaining two. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people. Then when dawn rose, Gabriel came in the morning ...................... with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people. He recited in them and recited aloud, lengthened and raised his voice. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people."],[1843,"The Muslims were concerned about something by which they could gather for their prayer. Some of them said: a bell; others said: a trumpet. 'Abdullah b. Zayd al-Ansari was shown in a dream that a man passed by him with a bell. 'Abdullah said to him: Will you sell this? The man said: What will you do with it? He said: We will strike it for our prayer. He said: Shall I not show you something better? He said: Yes. He said: Say: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah, ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; hayya 'ala al-salah, hayya 'ala al-salah; hayya 'ala al-falah, hayya 'ala al-falah; Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; la ilaha illa Allah. He said: 'Umar b. al-Khattab saw the same in his dream. When 'Abdullah prayed the morning prayer, he went to the Prophet ﷺ to inform him, and 'Umar went too, and he found that the Ansari had preceded him, and he found that the Prophet ﷺ had already ordered Bilal to give the call to prayer."],[1844,"The Prophet ﷺ and his companions deliberated how they could make something by which, when they wanted to gather for prayer, they would assemble for it. They deliberated about a bell. He said: While 'Umar b. al-Khattab was about to buy two pieces of wood for the bell, he saw in a dream: Do not make a bell; rather, call to prayer with the adhan. He said: 'Umar went to the Prophet ﷺ to inform him of what he had seen, and the revelation had already come to the Prophet ﷺ about that. Nothing startled 'Umar except Bilal giving the call to prayer, and the Prophet ﷺ said: The revelation has preceded you in that, when 'Umar informed him of it."],[1845,"When the Muslims came to Madinah, they would gather and estimate the time of prayer, and no one called to it. One day they discussed that. Some of them said to others: Take a bell like the bell of the Christians; others said: Rather a horn like the horn of the Jews. 'Umar said: Why not send a man to call to prayer? The Prophet ﷺ said: O Bilal, stand and give the call to prayer."],[1846,"The end of the adhan is: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, la ilaha illa Allah."],[1847,"The Muslims were concerned about something by which they could gather for their prayer. Some of them said: a bell; others said: a trumpet. 'Abdullah b. Zayd al-Ansari was shown in a dream that a man passed by him with a bell. 'Abdullah said to him: Will you sell this? The man said: What will you do with it? He said: We will strike it for our prayer. He said: Shall I not show you something better? He said: Yes. He said: Say: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah, ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; hayya 'ala al-salah, hayya 'ala al-salah; hayya 'ala al-falah, hayya 'ala al-falah; Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; la ilaha illa Allah. He said: 'Umar b. al-Khattab saw the same in his dream. When 'Abdullah prayed the morning prayer, he went to the Prophet ﷺ to inform him, and 'Umar went too, and he found that the Ansari had preceded him, and he found that the Prophet ﷺ had already ordered Bilal to give the call to prayer."],[1848,"The Muslims were concerned about something by which they could gather for their prayer. Some of them said: a bell; others said: a trumpet. 'Abdullah b. Zayd al-Ansari was shown in a dream that a man passed by him with a bell. 'Abdullah said to him: Will you sell this? The man said: What will you do with it? He said: We will strike it for our prayer. He said: Shall I not show you something better? He said: Yes. He said: Say: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah, ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; hayya 'ala al-salah, hayya 'ala al-salah; hayya 'ala al-falah, hayya 'ala al-falah; Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; la ilaha illa Allah. He said: 'Umar b. al-Khattab saw the same in his dream. When 'Abdullah prayed the morning prayer, he went to the Prophet ﷺ to inform him, and 'Umar went too, and he found that the Ansari had preceded him, and he found that the Prophet ﷺ had already ordered Bilal to give the call to prayer."],[1849,"I went out with ten youths with the Prophet ﷺ to Hunayn, and he was the most hateful of people to us. They gave the call to prayer, and we stood making the call in mockery of them. The Prophet ﷺ said: Bring me these youths. He said: Give the call to prayer. They gave it, and I was the last of them. The Prophet ﷺ said: How excellent is this one whose voice I heard! Go and give the call to prayer for the people of Makkah, and tell 'Attab b. Asid: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded me to give the call to prayer for the people of Makkah. He wiped his forelock and said: Say: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, twice; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah, ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah, twice; hayya 'ala al-salah, hayya 'ala al-salah; hayya 'ala al-falah, hayya 'ala al-falah, twice; Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; la ilaha illa Allah. And when you give the first call of Fajr, say: Al-salatu khayrun min al-nawm, twice. And when you give the iqamah, say it twice: Qad qamat al-salah, qad qamat al-salah. He heard it. He said: Abu Mahdhurah never cut his forelock nor parted it, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiped over it."],[1850,"During the governorship of Ibn al-Zubayr, he used to give the first call: ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah, ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; hayya 'ala al-salah twice; hayya 'ala al-falah twice. I said to 'Amr: In the iqamah twice? He said: I do not know how they used to say the iqamah."],[1851,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to 'Uthman b. Abi al-'As when he appointed him over Ta'if: If a man comes to you wanting to give the call to prayer, do not prevent him."],[1852,"A mu'adhdhin gave the call to prayer for Mu'awiyah in Makkah, and Abu Mahdhurah carried him and threw him into the well of Zamzam."],[1853,"Ibn Umar passed by a muezzin and said: Make your adhan odd (in number), for the adhan is odd."],[1854,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to his governors: The most important of your matters to me is prayer. Whoever guards it and is constant in it guards his religion, and whoever neglects it is more neglectful of other things. Then he wrote: Pray Dhuhr when the shade is a forearm's length until the shadow of one of you equals his height; Asr while the sun is high, white and clear, for the time a rider travels two or three farsakhs; Maghrib when the sun sets; Isha when the twilight disappears until one-third of the night. Whoever sleeps, may his eyes not sleep; whoever sleeps, may his eyes not sleep. And Subh while the stars are visible and intertwined."],[1855,"When Ibn Umar said in the adhan, “Hayya ‘ala al-falah,” he said, “Hayya ‘ala al-‘amal,” then he said, “Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, la ilaha illa Allah.”"],[1856,"Abd Allah b. Zayd, the brother of the Banu al-Harith b. al-Khazraj, reported that while he was sleeping, he saw a man carrying two pieces of wood. He said: I said to him in the dream, “The Prophet ﷺ wants to buy these two pieces of wood to make a bell to be struck for prayer.” He said: The man with the two pieces of wood turned his head and said, “I shall guide you to what is better than this.” Then the dream was conveyed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ordered him to give the adhan. Abd Allah b. Zayd woke up. He said: And Umar saw a vision similar to that of Abd Allah b. Zayd, but Abd Allah b. Zayd preceded him to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of that. The Prophet ﷺ said to him, “Arise and give the adhan.” He said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have a terrible voice.” He said to him, “Then teach Bilal what you saw.” So he taught him, and Bilal used to give the adhan."],[1857,"The Prophet ﷺ was concerned about the adhan, so much so that he considered ordering men to stand on the towers of Madinah and call to prayer, until they made a clapper or nearly did so. He said: A man from the Ansar called Abd Allah b. Zayd saw a man on the wall of the mosque wearing two green garments, saying: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah; hayya ‘ala al-salah; hayya ‘ala al-salah; hayya ‘ala al-falah; hayya ‘ala al-falah; Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; la ilaha illa Allah. Then he sat for a while and repeated it, then said: Qad qamat al-salah twice – the iqamah. Then he went to the Prophet ﷺ and told him. He said, “Teach it to Bilal.” Then Umar stood up and said, “The same vision that came to Abd Allah came to me last night, but he preceded me.”"],[1858,"He gave the adhan for us at Mina and said: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar; ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah twice; ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah ﷺ twice. Then he did as was mentioned in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla regarding the adhan and iqamah, exactly like that hadith."],[1859,"Bilal used to repeat the adhan in pairs and repeat the iqamah in pairs, and he used to begin with the takbir and end with the takbir."],[1861,"He made the iqamah twice twice."],[1862,"The iqamah being once once was mentioned to him, and he said: This is something the commanders have made light; the iqamah is twice twice."],[1863,"Bilal used to repeat the adhan in pairs and make the iqamah odd, except for his saying, “Qad qamat al-salah, qad qamat al-salah.”"],[1864,"Bilal was commanded to make the adhan even and the iqamah odd."],[1865,"That Sa'd called the adhan for the Prophet ﷺ at Quba', and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"You have done well, my son. When you come, then call the adhan.\" So Sa'd used to call the adhan at Quba', and Bilal did not call the adhan."],[1867,"He used to say when he called the adhan: 'Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest,' raising his voice with it. Then he said, lowering his voice: 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Come to prayer, come to success.' Then he would repeat it, raising his voice, saying: 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Come to prayer, come to prayer, come to prayer. Come to success, come to success. Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest. There is no god but Allah.' He said: And Ibn Sirin used to say something like that."],[1868,"Ata' said to me: \"It is a right and an established sunnah that a mu'adhdhin should not call the adhan except in a state of ablution.\" He said: \"It is part of the prayer, and it is the opening of the prayer, so he should not call the adhan except in a state of ablution.\""],[1869,"Cool down Zuhr, for the intense heat is from the scorching of Hellfire—though one of them said: 'from the scorching of Jahannam.'"],[1870,"They used to see no harm in a mu'adhdhin calling the adhan without ablution."],[1871,"I said to Ata': 'Should the mu'adhdhin face the qiblah?' He said: 'Yes. If he is in a village, he should turn to his right, his left, and behind him, and call the people with the adhan. If he is on a journey, and there are not many people with him, such as with a caliph, or there is no one among the people to call them to the adhan, then he should face the qiblah throughout his entire call.'"],[1872,"When he calls the adhan and is not in a congregation, he should not turn. When he calls the adhan in a congregation, calling someone with his adhan, he should face the House, until he begins, so he faces it, until he says: 'I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.' Then he should turn and call to the right and the left if he wishes. And Abd al-Karim mentioned it from al-Nakha'i."],[1873,"They used to pray Asr at evening time."],[1876,"I saw Bilal calling the adhan and turning, so I followed his mouth here and here, and his fingers were in his ears. He said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in a red tent of his. He said: Bilal went out in front of him with a spear, and he fixed it in the ground at al-Abtah. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr and Asr facing it, while dogs, donkeys, and women passed in front of him. He was wearing a red garment, and it is as if I am looking at the sheen of his shins."],[1877,"That the mu'adhdhin places his forefingers in his ears."],[1878,"Bilal and Abu Mahdhurah used to place their fingers in their ears during the call to prayer."],[1879,"They used to dislike for the muezzin, once he had begun his call to prayer, to speak until he had finished, and likewise during the iqamah, and he should face the qiblah for the takbir and the shahadah. Ibrahim said: 'And his feet should remain in place.'"],[1880,"They used to pray Asr at evening time."],[1882,"I said to 'Ata': 'May the muezzin speak in the midst of his call to prayer?' He said: 'It is better for him not to speak, but if he does speak there is no harm.'"],[1883,"It is disliked for the muezzin to give the call to prayer while sitting, and it is disliked for a boy to give the call to prayer until he has reached puberty."],[1884,"He was asked about a boy who has not reached puberty: may he give the call to prayer for the people and lead the prayer? He said: 'Yes.'"],[1885,"I said to 'Ata': 'May the muezzin give the call to prayer without standing?' He said: 'No, unless due to illness.' I said: 'Due to drowsiness or laziness?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'May a boy who has not reached puberty give the call to prayer?' He said: 'No.'"],[1886,"I saw Ibn 'Umar give the call to prayer while he was riding. He said: 'I said to him: 'Did he place his fingers in his ears?' He said: 'No.'"],[1887,"I was with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, and the time for the morning prayer came. He said: 'Give the call to prayer, O brother of Suda'.' So I gave the call to prayer while I was on my mount."],[1888,"I would not like your muezzins to be your blind men — I think he said: 'nor your reciters.'"],[1889,"Ibn Umm Maktum used to give the call to prayer for the Prophet ﷺ while he was blind. He would not give the call to prayer until it was said to him: 'It is morning.'"],[1891,"Indeed Bilal gives the call to prayer at night, so whoever intends to fast, Bilal's call should not prevent him until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the call. He said: He was blind, so he would not give the call until it was said to him, 'It is morning.' One night Bilal gave the call, then came to wake the Prophet ﷺ, but it was said to him, 'He is sleeping.' So Bilal called out, 'Prayer is better than sleep,' and it was established in the dawn prayer."],[1892,"I used to give the call to prayer for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the dawn prayer, and I would say, when I said in the first call, 'Come to success': 'Prayer is better than sleep, prayer is better than sleep.'"],[1893,"He used to say: The twilight (shafaq) is the red glow."],[1894,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded Bilal to say the tathwib in the dawn prayer and not to say it in any other prayer."],[1896,"He used to forbid his muezzin from saying the tathwib except in the night prayer and the dawn prayer."],[1897,"In the tathwib, when he said in the call to prayer, 'Come to success, come to success,' he would say, 'Prayer is better than sleep.'"],[1898,"1844 1827 - Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj who said: Ibn Muslim informed me that a man asked Tawus, who was sitting with the people, 'O Abu Abd al-Rahman, when was \"prayer is better than sleep\" said?' Tawus replied, 'It was not said during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but Bilal heard it during the time of Abu Bakr after the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from a man who was not a muezzin, so he took it from him and used it in the call to prayer. Abu Bakr did not remain long before Umar said, \"If only we had forbidden Bilal from this innovation,\" but it was as if he forgot it, and the people have used it in the call to prayer until today.'"],[1899,"I asked Ata, 'When was \"prayer is better than sleep\" said?' He replied, 'I do not know.'"],[1900,"Sa'd was the first to say 'prayer is better than sleep' during the caliphate of Umar, and he called it an innovation, then he abandoned it. Indeed, Bilal did not give the call to prayer for Umar."],[1901,"The muezzin of the Messenger of God ﷺ would give the adhan, then wait and would not give the iqamah until he saw the Prophet of God ﷺ come out, then he would give the iqamah for the prayer when he saw him."],[1902,"I said to Ata, 'What about what is reported from you that when the muezzin gives the call at night or during the day, he waits for a while after finishing the call, then he shouts in a loud voice, \"Come to prayer\" several times?' He said, 'I do not know, and nothing has reached me about it.'"],[1903,"I was with Ibn Umar when he heard a man performing the tathwib in the mosque, so he said: \"Take us away from this innovator.\""],[1904,"I was with the Messenger of God and he commanded me, so I gave the call to prayer for Fajr. Then Bilal came, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"O Bilal, the brother of Suda' has already given the call to prayer, and whoever gives the call to prayer, he gives the iqamah.\""],[1905,"Umar said to Abu Mahdhura: \"When you give the first call to prayer, give the adhan, then perform the tathwib and I will come to you.\""],[1906,"Umar said to Abu Mahdhura: \"When you give the first call to prayer, pray two rak'ahs, then give the iqamah, for I will come out to you.\" He said: \"And he used to give the call to prayer on the bench of Zamzam.\""],[1908,"The muezzin of the Messenger of God ﷺ would give the adhan, then wait and would not give the iqamah until he saw the Prophet of God ﷺ come out, then he would give the iqamah for the prayer when he saw him."],[1909,"The imam is a guarantor, and the muezzin is a trustee. O God, guide the imams and forgive the muezzins."],[1910,"The muezzins are the trustees, and the imams are the guarantors. May God guide the imams and forgive the muezzins."],[1912,"When he heard the muezzin, he would say as he says, and when he said: \"I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God,\" he would say: \"And I too.\""],[1914,"When he heard the muezzin giving the adhan, he would say: \"God is most great,\" and he said: \"God is most great.\" When he said: \"I bear witness that there is no god but God,\" he said: \"I bear witness that there is no god but God.\" When he said: \"I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God,\" he said the like of that. When he said: \"Come to prayer,\" he said: \"There is no might and no power except with God, the Most High, the Most Great.\""],[1915,"We entered upon Mu'awiya, and the caller announced the prayer. He said: Allah is greatest, Allah is greatest. Mu'awiya said the same as he said. Then he said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. He said the same again. Then he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. He said the same. Then he said: Thus I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say."],[1916,"That he heard Abu Umama b. Sahl b. Hunayf, when he heard the muezzin, say the takbir and testify as he testified. Then he said: Thus Mu'awiya narrated to us that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: as the muezzin said. When he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, he said: And I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, then he fell silent."],[1917,"When he heard the muezzin, he would say as he says, and when he said: \"I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God,\" he would say: \"And I too.\""],[1919,"Whoever says as the muezzin says, and when he says: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, he says: And I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, those who deny Muhammad are liars, shall have a reward equal to those who deny Muhammad ﷺ."],[1920,"That some people in the past used to remain silent for the call to prayer as they remained silent for the Quran. The muezzin would not say anything but they said the like of it, until when he said: Come to prayer, they said: There is no might and no power except with Allah, the Most High, the Most Great. When he said: Come to success, they said: Whatever Allah wills."],[1921,"They used to dislike that a man should rise for prayer when the muezzin began the iqama."],[1922,"He was in her house and heard the muezzin, and said as he said. When he said: Come to prayer, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose for prayer."],[1923,"I saw Ibn Umar hastening between al-Safa and al-Marwa, and with him were some people. A man with a long beard came to him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I love you for the sake of Allah. Ibn Umar said: But I hate you for the sake of Allah. It was as if Ibn Umar's companions reproached him and spoke to him. He said: He seeks excessive payment in his call to prayer and takes a wage for it."],[1924,"That a man said: I love you for the sake of Allah. He said to him: But I hate you for the sake of Allah. He said: Why? He said: You seek excessive payment in your call to prayer and take a wage for the Book of Allah."],[1925,"That Umar came to Makkah and Abu Mahdhura gave the call to prayer. Umar said to him: Were you not afraid that your voice would tear? He said: O Commander of the Faithful, you have arrived and I wished to make you hear it. Umar said: Your land, O people of Tihama, is a hot land, so delay, then delay — meaning the noon prayer — then give the call, then give the iqama, I come to you [1]."],[1926,"No provision is to be taken for the call to prayer."],[1927,"He disliked taking a fee for his adhan unless he was given something without stipulation."],[1928,"The first to give stipends to the mu'adhdhins was Uthman."],[1929,"The adhan is the banner of faith."],[1930,"The first call of the adhan is only to notify the people. He said: It is a binding duty that is indispensable, and it is not permissible otherwise. When one hears the adhan, he must come and attend the prayer. Then he told me about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: What is the matter with men who hear the call to prayer and then stay behind? I was determined to establish the prayer and then no one would stay away from it except that I would burn his house, or burn it down upon him. A man came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, I am blind and it is hard for me not to attend the prayer. The Prophet ﷺ said: Attend it. He said: I am blind, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. He said: Then attend it. I said: What was his impairment? He said: I think he was blind or had weak eyesight, and he asked for a concession regarding the night prayer. Ibn Jurayj said: Someone I trust told me that the man was Ibn Umm Maktum."],[1931,"The mu'adhdhins will have the longest necks of people on the Day of Resurrection, and they will not be eaten by worms in their graves."],[1932,"The mu'adhdhins will have the longest necks of people on the Day of Resurrection, and they will not be eaten by worms in their graves."],[1933,"The longest of people in necks on the Day of Resurrection are the mu'adhdhins."],[1934,"Indeed, the mu'adhdhin is forgiven as far as his voice reaches, and every moist and dry thing that hears him confirms him, and for the one who witnesses it are twenty-five good deeds."],[1935,"Allah forgives the mu'adhdhin as far as his voice reaches, and every moist and dry thing that hears him confirms him."],[1936,"I was in the lap of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and he said: O my son, if you are in the deserts, raise your voice with the adhan, for I heard him—meaning the Prophet ﷺ—say: There is no jinn, no human, no stone, and no tree except that it bears witness for him."],[1937,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey, he heard a man saying: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar. The Prophet ﷺ said: Upon the fitrah, upon the fitrah is this one. He said: Ash-hadu an la ilaha illa Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said: He is free from shirk, this one. He said: Ash-hadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said: He has entered Paradise, this one. He said: Hayya 'ala al-salah, hayya 'ala al-falah. The Prophet ﷺ said: Islam—or he said: faith—has appeared. By the Lord of the Ka'bah, you will find this one to be a shepherd, or a hunter, or a man who went out from his family to live in the desert. The people hurried to inform him of what they heard and found him to be a man from Aslam who had gone out from his family to live in the desert."],[1938,"The mu'adhdhin who seeks reward is like the one who brandishes his sword in the way of Allah."],[1939,"Meats forbidden to the Fire. Then he mentioned the muʾadhdhins."],[1940,"If I were able to give the adhan along with the khullayfā, I would have given it."],[1941,"Were it not that I fear it would become a sunnah, I would not abandon the adhan."],[1942,"\"Who are your muʾadhdhins today?\" They said, \"Our freedmen and slaves.\" He said, \"That is a great deficiency in you.\""],[1943,"A boy should not lead the prayer until he has a wet dream, and let the best among you give the adhan for you."],[1944,"The adhan is never given among a people at night except that they are safe from punishment until morning, nor in the daytime except that they are safe from punishment until evening."],[1945,"I would never like to lead anyone in prayer except my own household, because if there is any deficiency in the prayer, then upon him is the sin of what is deficient in his prayer and their prayer, and there are things incumbent upon the imam, and I saw him fear that he may not fulfill them."],[1946,"Mujahid went out with a man to al-Taʾif, and each of them disliked leading the other in prayer, so each prayed alone until they returned."],[1947,"Three will recline on dunes of musk on the Day of Resurrection in Paradise: a man who called to the five prayers day and night seeking thereby the face of God, a man who learned the Book of God and led people in it while they were pleased with him, and a slave whose worldly bondage distracted him from obedience to God."],[1948,"Take the initiative in the adhan, but do not vie for leadership in prayer. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Take the initiative in leading prayer through the adhan, because of its surpassing merit.\""],[1949,"If you can avoid leading anyone in prayer, do so, for if the imam knew what is upon him, he would not lead."],[1950,"The prayer was commenced, and the people deferred to one another. Hudhayfah said: \"Either you must appoint an imam for it, or you will pray individually.\""],[1951,"That a people performed the prayer and one of them kept saying to another, \"Go forward,\" and the other said to him, \"Go forward,\" until they were swallowed up by the earth."],[1952,"He heard the mu'adhdhin of the Prophet ﷺ on a rainy night saying: 'Hayya ala al-salah, hayya ala al-falah, pray in your dwellings.'"],[1953,"I said to 'Ata': Has it reached you that when the imam shortens the prayer, the sin of those behind him is upon him? He said: Yes."],[1954,"I said to 'Ata': Has it reached you that it used to be said: It is incumbent upon the imam not to supplicate for himself for anything without supplicating for those behind him with the like of it? He said: Yes. I said: What is his right upon them? He said: They supplicate and seek forgiveness for themselves and for the believing men and women, and they do not single him out for anything except among the believers. I said: How does he supplicate? He said: He says: O Allah, forgive us; O Allah, have mercy on us. Then he includes the believing men and women, so he starts with them and singles them out, saying: O Allah, forgive us; O Allah, have mercy on us. This is specifically for them. Then he includes the believing men and women after that, and he does not name those behind him except in that way."],[1955,"Abu Bakr said: And someone who heard Muhammad b. Ishaq narrating from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, informed me the like of Ma'mar's hadith. Hidden text: The Prophet ﷺ said: Bilal gives the call to prayer at night, so whoever intends to fast should not be deterred by Bilal's call until he hears the call of Ibn Umm Maktum."],[1958,"I once gave the call to prayer, then I went to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I have given the call, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do not give the call until dawn breaks. Then I came to him again and said: I have given the call. He said: Do not give the call until you see it like this, and he joined his hands and then separated them."],[1959,"Bilal once gave the call to prayer at night, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: Go out and announce that the servant has slept. So he went out saying: Would that Bilal's mother had lost him, and may he be soaked from the sweat of his forehead's blood. Then he announced that the servant had slept."],[1960,"When the muezzin gave the call to prayer at night, they would come to him and say: Fear Allah, and repeat your call."],[1961,"They used to dislike the muezzin giving the call to prayer before the break of dawn."],[1962,"Ibn Umm Maktūm and Bilāl used to give the adhān for the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “Ibn Umm Maktūm is blind, so when Ibn Umm Maktūm gives the adhān, eat, and when Bilāl gives the adhān, stop and do not eat.”"],[1965,"The mu'adhdhin of the Prophet ﷺ gave the call on a cold night while I was under my blanket, and I wished that Allah would cast upon his tongue, 'And there is no harm.' He said: 'And there is no harm.'"],[1966,"The mu'adhdhin of the Prophet ﷺ gave the call on a cold night while I was under my blanket, and I wished that Allah would cast upon his tongue, 'And there is no harm.' He said: 'And there is no harm.'"],[1968,"I said to Nāfiʿ: How much did Ibn ʿUmar give the adhān while traveling? He said: Two adhāns; when dawn broke, he gave the first adhān. As for the other prayers, it was an iqāmah, an iqāmah for each prayer. He used to say: The adhān is only for an army or a traveling group that has a commander, who calls to prayer so that they gather for it. As for a group like this, it is only the iqāmah."],[1969,"If you are in the city, the iqama of the city suffices you, even if you do not hear it."],[1971,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: The caliph while traveling, with him the like of the pilgrims—how much adhān is given for him? He said: An adhān and an iqāmah for every prayer. I said: Do you think that one who hears the iqāmah while traveling is obligated to attend the prayer just as one who hears the call in a settled area is obligated to attend the prayer? He said: Yes, unless he is on his mount. I said: Then it would be only fatigue and slackness? He said: He laughed and said: Yes, by my life, it is indeed an obligation upon him to attend it."],[1972,"Ibn ʿUmar gave the adhān while he was at Ḍajnān, between Makkah and Madinah, on an evening of wind and cold. When he finished the call, he said to his companions: “Pray in your dwellings.” Then he narrated that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to command that on a cold or rainy night; when he finished his adhān, he would say: “Pray in your dwellings” twice."],[1973,"Ibn ʿUmar gave the adhān at Ḍajnān, between Makkah and Madinah, and said: “Pray in your dwellings.” Then he said: “The Prophet ﷺ used to command his caller on a cold, rainy, or windy night to say: ‘Pray in your dwellings.’”"],[1974,"He was overtaken by rain while they were on a journey, and he said to his companions: Pray in your dwellings. I said to ‘Ata’: Do they pray following his prayer? He said: Yes, I think so."],[1975,"I said to Ata: A group of travelers who have rain ahead of them, may they pray on their riding animals? He said: Yes, if they wish. I said: Do they perform tayammum with earth if they find no water? He said: Yes."],[1976,"I said to ‘Ata’: If one hears the iqama while traveling and thinks he can catch it or part of it, is he obliged to attend it? And whoever thinks he will not catch it has no obligation? I said: What do you think about someone who hears the iqama on the evening of ‘Arafa—is he obliged to come to the prayer when he hears it? He said: Yes, unless he is occupied in his dwelling."],[1977,"Regarding a man who forgot the iqama until he stood up to pray, he said: Ibn Umar's practice was: if he was in a city where the prayer is called with an iqama, that sufficed for him."],[1978,"Whoever belongs to a village that does not hold the Friday prayer, they should have an adhan and iqama for every prayer. I said: How about the residents of ‘Arafa? He said: They should have an adhan and iqama for every prayer; if they have an imam who gathers them, then they should have an adhan and iqama for every prayer."],[1979,"I said to ‘Ata’: I have a neighbor in the desert who settled before me, or I settled before him? He said: It is not obligatory for either of you to go to the other; you are the imam of your family, and he is the imam of his family."],[1980,"I said to ‘Ata’: An imam of a people in the desert gives the adhan for the night prayer in his house and does not come out or appear to them. He said: Then they should not go to him. He said: For then he does not want them to come to his house."],[1981,"Supplication is not rejected between the adhan and the iqama."],[1982,"There are two hours in which the gates of heaven are opened, and rarely is a suppliant turned away: at the presence of the call to prayer, and in the ranks for the cause of Allah."],[1983,"Whoever says at the iqama: ‘O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and the established prayer, grant our master Muhammad the means and raise him in degrees,’ intercession by the Prophet ﷺ is assured for him."],[1984,"The virtue of prayer in congregation is twenty-five times greater."],[1985,"Ibn Umm Maktum came to the Prophet ﷺ after he had suffered damage to his eyes and said: Do you find any concession for me to pray in my house? The Prophet ﷺ said to him: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. He said: I find no concession for you."],[1986,"Whoever hears the call and does not respond, there is no prayer for him. Ibn Abbas said: \"Except due to an illness or an excuse.\""],[1987,"There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque."],[1988,"Whoever hears the call from among the neighbors of the mosque and does not respond while he is healthy and without excuse, there is no prayer for him."],[1989,"Whoever hears the call and does not respond has not desired good, and good is not desired for him."],[1990,"Whoever hears 'Hayya ala al-salah, hayya ala al-falah' and does not respond, he has not increased in good by it."],[1991,"The virtue of prayer in congregation is twenty-five times greater."],[1992,"I said to Ata: \"What do you think of someone from the people of the village who does not hear the call?\" He said: \"If he wishes, he may come, and if he wishes, he may not.\" He said: I said: \"Even if he is close to the mosque?\" He said: \"If he wishes, let him come, and if he wishes, let him sit.\" I said: \"What do you think if I am in a dwelling where sometimes I hear it and sometimes I do not hear it? Do I have a concession to sit when I do not hear it?\" He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"Even if I know that the time of prayer has come, the time at which I think it is prayed?\" He said: \"Yes, if you do not hear the call.\""],[1993,"Umar b. al-Khattab missed a man for some days, and either he entered upon him or met him. He said: \"Where do you think you have been?\" He said: \"I was ill, so I did not go out for prayer or anything else.\" Umar said: \"If you respond to anything, then respond to al-falah (success).\""],[1994,"I said to Ata: \"What about someone who hears the iqama in the city but did not hear the first call?\" He said: \"If he thinks that he can catch it, then it is obligatory for him to come to it.\""],[1995,"Ibn Abbas ordered his caller on a rainy Friday: when you reach 'Hayya ala al-falah,' say: 'Indeed, pray in your dwellings.' It was said to him: 'What is this?' He said: 'One better than me did it.'"],[1996,"We prayed Isha in Basra and it rained on us. Then I came to open (the door), and my father Usama said to me: 'I saw us with the Messenger of God ﷺ at the time of Hudaybiyya, and it rained on us but the sky did not wet the bottoms of our sandals. Then the caller of the Prophet ﷺ announced: Pray in your dwellings.'"],[1997,"He heard the mu'adhdhin of the Prophet ﷺ on a rainy night saying: 'Hayya ala al-salah, hayya ala al-falah, pray in your dwellings.'"],[1998,"I heard the mu'adhdhin of the Prophet ﷺ on a cold night while I was under my blanket, and I wished he would say, 'Pray in your dwellings.' When he reached 'Hayya 'ala al-falah,' he said, 'Pray in your dwellings.' Then I asked about it, and indeed the Prophet ﷺ had commanded that."],[1999,"The mu'adhdhin of the Prophet ﷺ gave the call on a cold night while I was under my blanket, and I wished that Allah would cast upon his tongue, 'And there is no harm.' He said: 'And there is no harm.'"],[2002,"After the mu'adhdhin gave the iqama and they fell silent, the Prophet ﷺ used to speak about needs and attend to them. So a stick was fixed for him in the qibla, like a peg, to support himself on for that."],[2003,"The prayer would be announced, and a man would speak to the Prophet ﷺ about a need he had, so he would stand between him and the qibla, and he would keep standing talking to him. Sometimes I saw some of the people dozing off from the length of the Prophet's ﷺ standing."],[2005,"He came out to them when the prayer had been announced while they were standing, and said, 'What is the matter with you, standing idle [samidun]?'"],[2006,"I asked him, 'Do you wait for the imam standing or sitting?' He said, 'Rather sitting.'"],[2007,"I came to Abu Ishaq, who was a neighbor of the mosque and would not go out until he heard the iqama. He said: And I saw men doing that."],[2008,"I said to 'Ata', 'It is said that when the mu'adhdhin says, \"Qad qamat al-salah,\" the people should stand then.' He said, 'Yes.'"],[2009,"From Husayn b. 'Ali b. Abi Talib. He said: I saw him at the basin of Zamzam from which the pilgrims are given water, and the basin at that time was between the Corner and Zamzam. The mu'adhdhin announced the prayer, and when he said, 'Qad qamat al-salah,' Husayn stood, and that was after the death of Mu'awiya, while the people of Makkah had no imam. Someone said to him, 'Sit until the people are lined up, for he says, \"Qad qamat al-salah.\"'"],[2010,"I saw Husayn b. Ali wading in Zamzam, and something occurred between Ibn al-Zubayr and a man at the iqama of the prayer. I saw Husayn standing in the basin and it was said to him, 'Sit,' and he said, 'The prayer has been established' twice."],[2011,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz sent men to the mosque: when the iqama is called, then get up for it."],[2012,"We were sitting with Ibn Umar. When the muezzin began the iqama, we stood, but Ibn Umar said, 'Sit. When he says, \"The prayer has been established,\" then stand.'"],[2013,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz used to appoint guards so that when the muezzin began the iqama, they would make the people stand for the prayer until he said the takbir."],[2015,"I asked al-Hasan: I said, 'We pass by the mosque and hear the iqama and want to go beyond it to another.' He said, 'A Muslim man would say to his brother when he heard the iqama: \"You have been detained.\"'"],[2016,"I asked al-Hasan: I said, 'We pass by the mosque and hear the iqama and want to go beyond it to another.' He said, 'A Muslim man would say to his brother when he heard the iqama: \"You have been detained.\"'"],[2017,"A man came to Ibn al-Musayyib while he was in the mosque and asked him about a need, then went to leave. Ibn al-Musayyib said, 'Where do you intend?' He said, 'My companions are waiting for me.' Ibn al-Musayyib said, 'The adhan has been called, so do not leave.' The man said, 'They are on their mounts, and I dislike detaining them.' Ibn al-Musayyib said, 'Do not leave until you pray.' The man slipped away while Ibn al-Musayyib was distracted. Ibn al-Musayyib turned and said, 'Where is the man?' They said, 'He has gone.' He said, 'I do not think he will meet with good on this journey of his.' The man traveled only a few miles before falling from his mount, and his leg was broken."],[2018,"I was with Ibn al-Musayyib when a man came to him and asked him about some matter. The muezzin called, and the man intended to leave. Sa'id said to him, 'The prayer has been called.' The man said, 'My companions have gone on, and this is my mount at the door.' Sa'id said to him, 'Do not leave, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"No one leaves the mosque after the call to prayer except a hypocrite, unless a man leaves for his need and intends to return to the prayer.\"' The man refused to do anything but leave. Sa'id said, 'Take note of that man.' One day someone came to him and said, 'O Abu Muhammad, did you not see this man who refused to do anything but leave? He fell from his mount and broke his leg.' Sa'id said, 'I thought something would befall him.'"],[2019,"We were with Abu Hurayrah in the mosque when the muezzin called for Asr and a man left. Abu Hurayrah said, 'As for this one, he has disobeyed Abu al-Qasim.'"],[2021,"Ibn Umar and I came while the people were in prayer, and we sat by the gardens until they finished."],[2022,"Any man who goes out into a desolate land - meaning a wilderness - let him choose a place for prayer, and let him cast his gaze right and left to see which is most level underfoot and most pleasant for his place of prayer, for the spots of ground vie for the Muslim man: every spot loves that God be remembered in it. So if he wishes, he may call the adhan, and if he wishes, he may give the iqama."],[2023,"If a man is in a wasteland of the earth and calls the adhan, gives the iqama, and prays, four thousand angels pray with him - or four thousand thousand angels."],[2024,"If a man prays and gives the iqama, his two angels pray with him; and if he calls the adhan and gives the iqama, many angels pray with him."],[2025,"If a man gives the iqama for himself, his two angels pray with him; and if he calls the adhan and gives the iqama, as many angels as fill the earth pray with him."],[2026,"Whoever prays in a wasteland and gives the iqama, an angel prays on his right and an angel on his left; and whoever calls the adhan and gives the iqama, angels like mountains pray with him."],[2027,"If a man is in a desolate land and the time for prayer arrives, let him perform ablution; and if he does not find water, let him perform tayammum. If he gives the iqama, his two angels pray with him; and if he calls the adhan and gives the iqama, behind him pray hosts of God whose two ends cannot be seen."],[2028,"I said to 'Ata': 'I forgot the two rak'ahs of dawn until the iqama for prayer was given.' He said: 'Pray them, then pray, and do not repeat the iqama; the first one suffices you.'"],[2030,"I said to 'Ata': 'I prayed the obligatory prayer alone and forgot to give the iqama for it.' He said: 'Return to your prayer: give the iqama for it, then repeat.'"],[2031,"Whoever forgets the iqama until he has prayed does not repeat his prayer."],[2032,"I said to Ibrahim: 'I prayed without an iqama.' He said: 'It suffices you.'"],[2033,"Ibn Mas'ud prayed with his companions in his house without an iqama and said: 'The iqama of the town suffices.'"],[2034,"Ibn Mas'ud, Uthman, and al-Aswad prayed without an adhan or an iqama."],[2035,"Regarding a man who forgot the iqama until he stood up to pray, he said: Ibn Umar's practice was: if he was in a city where the prayer is called with an iqama, that sufficed for him."],[2037,"Ibn Umar's practice was: when he prayed in a land where the prayer is called with an iqama, he would pray according to their iqama and would not make an iqama for himself."],[2038,"I asked Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla: I came to the mosque and they had already prayed. Should I say the iqama? He said: You have been sufficed."],[2039,"I saw Anas enter a mosque where prayer had already been performed, and he called the adhan and the iqama."],[2040,"There is no iqama upon women. He said: Whoever forgets the iqama while traveling does not have to repeat, and whoever forgets rinsing the mouth and sniffing water does not repeat."],[2041,"I said to Ibrahim: I forgot the iqama while traveling. He said: Your prayer suffices you."],[2042,"If you are traveling, do not pray except with the iqama. If you forget the iqama, return to your prayer, say the iqama, then resume."],[2043,"I said to Ata: What do you think if a man hears the call or the iqama while he is performing the obligatory prayer, should he cut off his prayer and go to the congregational mosque? He said: If he thinks he can catch something of the obligatory prayer, then yes. I said: What do you think if I hear the iqama, is it obligatory for me to come to the prayer just as it is obligatory when I hear the call? He said: Yes."],[2045,"I saw Sa'id b. Jubayr come to us after we had prayed, and he heard a muezzin, so he went out to him."],[2047,"He used to come to the mosque when they had already prayed in it, and he would hear the mu'adhdhins, so he would pray in his own mosque which he had entered."],[2048,"Regarding a man who prayed one rak'ah of the obligatory prayer and then heard the iqamah, he said: he adds another to it, then goes to the imam and prays with him in congregation, and if he is in the mosque he joins them."],[2050,"He was asked about a man who called the adhan and forgot, so he made it the iqamah. Al-Sha'bi said: he should call the adhan and the iqamah. He said: its interpretation according to us is that he makes the iqamah an adhan, then calls the iqamah."],[2051,"Whoever would be pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him maintain these prescribed prayers where the call to them is given, for they are among the sunan of guidance, and Allah has prescribed for your Prophet ﷺ the sunan of guidance. By my life, I think none of you has not taken a mosque in his house, and if you were to pray in your houses as this laggard prays in his house, you would abandon the sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ, and if you abandoned the sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ you would go astray. I have seen us when none stayed away from it except a hypocrite whose hypocrisy was known or one whose hypocrisy was acknowledged, and I have seen a man being supported between two men until he was placed in the row. No man performs ablution and does it well, then takes a step intending by it a mosque for Allah, except that Allah writes for him a good deed by it, raises him a degree by it, and erases a sin from him by it, until we used to take short steps."],[2052,"A man was negligent — or fell behind — from the prayer until the imam pronounced the takbir. Ibn Mas'ud and Ibn Umar said: \"What you missed of it is better than a thousand.\""],[2053,"The farthest of you in house has the greatest reward. They said: how, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: because of the many steps, Allah writes for him a good deed with one of his two steps, and erases from him a sin with the other."],[2054,"Banu Salimah complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the distance of their homes from the mosque, so Allah revealed: 'And We record what they have sent before and their traces.' The Prophet ﷺ said: keep to your homes, for your traces are recorded."],[2055,"Zayd b. Thabit placed his hand on me while intending to pray and began to shorten his steps."],[2056,"2071 2051 - 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Hammam b. Munabbih, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said something similar. Hidden textCool down Zuhr, for the intense heat is from the scorching of Hellfire."],[2057,"I had intended to order my young men to gather for me bundles of firewood, then order a man to lead the people in prayer, then go and burn down the houses of a people who do not attend the prayer."],[2059,"If one of you were told that if he attended the 'Isha' prayer he would find two small arrows or a fat bone, he would attend it. No prayer is harder on the hypocrites than these two prayers: the morning prayer and the 'Isha' prayer; they cannot bear them."],[2060,"Umar b. al-Khattab went out to the prayer and faced the people, then ordered the muezzin and he called the iqamah, and he said: We will not wait for anyone for our prayer. When he finished his prayer, he turned to the people and said: What is the matter with people whose staying behind causes others to stay behind? By Allah, I was about to send for them and have them brought by their necks, then it would be said: Attend the prayer."],[2061,"I witnessed a man who stayed with Ibn Abbas for a month asking him about this question every day: What do you say about a man who fasts during the day and prays at night but does not attend the congregation or the Friday prayer; where is he? He said: In the Fire."],[2062,"A man asked Ibn Abbas, saying: A man fasts during the day and prays at night but does not attend the congregation or the Friday prayer; where is he? He said: In the Fire. Then he came the next day and asked him about that, and he said: He is in the Fire. He kept coming to him for nearly a month asking him about that, and Ibn Abbas kept saying: He is in the Fire."],[2063,"He entered upon Uthman b. Affan while he was besieged, and Ali was leading the people in prayer. He said: O Commander of the Faithful, I am reluctant to pray with these people while you are the imam. Uthman said: Prayer is the best thing people do. When you see people doing good, do good with them; and when you see them doing evil, avoid their evil."],[2064,"There is no step a Muslim takes to the mosque except that Allah records for him a good deed for it and erases from him a bad deed for it."],[2065,"Shall I not guide you to that by which Allah expiates sins and raises ranks? Steps to the mosques, performing ablution thoroughly in difficult circumstances, and waiting for prayer after prayer. That is the stationed guard; that is the stationed guard."],[2066,"The stationed guard—the best stationed guard is prayer after prayer and keeping to the gatherings of remembrance. No servant prays and then remains in his place except that the angels pray for him until he breaks his ablution."],[2067,"They hope for a man, when he walks to the mosque—meaning for prayer in the dark night—for forgiveness."],[2068,"Whoever goes to the mosque in the morning or evening, Allah determines for him provision from the heaven and the earth—or he said: the heavens."],[2069,"Indeed Satan is the wolf of the son of Adam, like the wolf of the sheep; he takes the sheep from the side and the far side. So you must adhere to the congregation and the mosques."],[2070,"I was about to order some young men to gather firewood, then order a man to lead the people in prayer, and then go to the houses of people who did not attend the prayer and burn them down. By Allah, if it were said to any one of them that he would find a fat meaty bone or two, or a bone with meat or two, he would attend it."],[2071,"The virtue of prayer in congregation is twenty-five times greater."],[2072,"A prayer with the imam is better than twenty-five prayers performed alone."],[2073,"The virtue of praying in a group over praying alone is twenty-five degrees, and the angels of the night and the angels of the day gather in the dawn prayer. Abu Hurayrah says: And recite if you wish: 'And the recitation of dawn, indeed the recitation of dawn is witnessed.' Ma'mar said: Qatadah said: The angels of the night and the angels of the day witness it."],[2074,"A man's prayer in congregation surpasses his prayer alone by twenty-four prayers."],[2075,"The virtue of congregational prayer over a man's prayer alone is twenty-some degrees."],[2076,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the dawn prayer, and when he said the salam he said: 'Is so-and-so present?' They said: 'Yes, but he did not attend.' He said it three times. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The heaviest prayers for the hypocrites are the 'Isha' prayer and the Fajr prayer. If they knew what is in them, they would come to them even if they had to crawl. The first row is like the row of the angels. If you knew its virtue, you would race to it. Your prayer with a man is purer than your prayer alone, and your prayer with two men is purer than your prayer with one man, and the more there are, the more beloved it is to Allah.'"],[2077,"And the virtue of a man's prayer in congregation over his prayer alone is twenty-five degrees."],[2078,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the dawn prayer, and when he said the salam he said: 'Is so-and-so present?' They said: 'Yes, but he did not attend.' He said it three times. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The heaviest prayers for the hypocrites are the 'Isha' prayer and the Fajr prayer. If they knew what is in them, they would come to them even if they had to crawl. The first row is like the row of the angels. If you knew its virtue, you would race to it. Your prayer with a man is purer than your prayer alone, and your prayer with two men is purer than your prayer with one man, and the more there are, the more beloved it is to Allah.'"],[2079,"If the people knew what is in the call to prayer and the first row, and then found no way except to draw lots for it, they would draw lots. If they knew what is in going early to prayer, they would race to it. If they knew what is in attending the 'Isha' prayer and the morning prayer, they would come to them even if they had to crawl."],[2080,"Whoever prays 'Isha' in congregation, it is as if he stood in prayer half the night, and whoever prays 'Isha' and Fajr in congregation, it is as if he stood in prayer the whole night."],[2081,"'Uthman went out for the later 'Isha' prayer and found the people few, so he lay down for a while at the back of the mosque until the people became numerous. He said: So I lay down, and he asked me: 'Who are you?' I told him. Then he asked me: 'How much of the Qur'an do you have with you?' I told him. 'Uthman said: 'Indeed, whoever attends the 'Isha' prayer, it is as if he stood half the night, and whoever attends the Fajr prayer, it is as if he stood the whole night.'"],[2082,"Shifa, one of the women of Banu Adi b. Ka'b, came to Umar during Ramadan and he said: \"Why do I not see Abu Huthmah — meaning her husband — attending the morning prayer, when he is one of the men of Banu Adi b. Ka'b?\" She said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, he strove through the night (in worship), then grew too tired to go out, so he prayed the morning prayer (at home), then slept.\" He said: \"By Allah, had he attended it (in congregation), it would be dearer to me than his striving through the night.\""],[2083,"Umar b. al-Khattab entered my house and found two men sleeping there. He said: \"What is the matter with these two that they did not attend the prayer with me?\" I said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, they prayed with the people — and that was in Ramadan — and they kept praying until morning, then they prayed the morning prayer and slept.\" Umar said: \"That I pray the morning prayer in congregation is dearer to me than that I pray through the night until morning.\""],[2084,"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said to a man of the Ansar: \"Attending the two of them — the Isha and the Fajr prayer — is better than standing (in prayer) throughout what is between them.\""],[2086,"The Fajr prayer in congregation used to be deemed equal to standing in prayer the entire night, and the Isha prayer to half the night."],[2087,"Attending an obligatory prayer, whatever it may be, is dearer to me than standing in prayer for a night and fasting for a day."],[2089,"Whoever prays the Maghrib and Isha in congregation, the good of the Night of Decree will not miss him."],[2090,"Whoever attends the five prayers for forty nights in congregation, catching the first takbirah, Paradise becomes incumbent for him."],[2091,"Whoever does not miss the first rak'ah of the prayer for forty days, two declarations of innocence are written for him: an innocence from the Fire and an innocence from hypocrisy."],[2092,"A man was negligent — or fell behind — from the prayer until the imam pronounced the takbir. Ibn Mas'ud and Ibn Umar said: \"What you missed of it is better than a thousand.\""],[2093,"He said to his son: \"Did you catch the prayer with us?\" He said: \"Did you catch the first takbirah?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"What you missed of it is better than a hundred camels, all of them black-eyed.\""],[2094,"That I pray behind an imam who recites: “Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming?” is dearer to me than that I recite a hundred verses in my prayer."],[2095,"No hypocrite attends them — meaning Fajr and Isha."],[2096,"Three is a congregation."],[2097,"They used to pray Asr at evening time."],[2098,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the morning prayer, he sat in his place until the sun rose."],[2099,"That I pray the morning prayer, then sit in my place remembering Allah until the sun rises, is dearer to me than raiding on fine horses in the path of Allah."],[2100,"That I pray the morning prayer and sit remembering Allah until the sun rises is dearer to me than everything the sun rises and sets upon."],[2101,"He used to say: The twilight (shafaq) is the red glow."],[2102,"He used to say: The twilight (shafaq) is the red glow."],[2103,"When the Prophet ﷺ awoke from the night in which he was taken on the Night Journey, nothing startled him except Gabriel. He descended when the sun had declined — for that reason it was called the first. He stood and called to his companions: Prayer in congregation! So they gathered, and Gabriel prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with the people. He lengthened the first two rak‘ahs, then shortened the remaining two. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people. Then he descended for Asr likewise, and they did the same as they had done for Zuhr. Then he descended at the beginning of the night and called out: Prayer in congregation! So Gabriel prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people. He lengthened the first two and shortened the third. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people. Then when a third of the night had passed, he descended and called to the people: Prayer in congregation! So they gathered, and Gabriel prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people. He recited in the first two and lengthened and recited aloud, and shortened the remaining two. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people. Then when dawn rose, Gabriel came in the morning ...................... with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people. He recited in them and recited aloud, lengthened and raised his voice. Then Gabriel greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ greeted the people."],[2104,"Prayer times. He said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: Prayer times. He said: Attend the prayer with me today and tomorrow. So he prayed Zuhr when the sun had declined. He said: Then he prayed Asr early. Then he prayed Maghrib when night entered, when the fasting person breaks his fast. As for Isha, I do not know when he prayed it. Someone other than Ata said: Until the twilight had disappeared. Ata said: Then he prayed Fajr when dawn rose. Then he prayed Zuhr the next day, but he did not pray it until it had cooled. I said: The first cooling? He said: Later, and later, in the evening. He said: Then he prayed Asr after that, delaying it. I said: What delay? He said: In the evening before the sun turned yellow. He said: Then he prayed Maghrib when the twilight had disappeared. He said: He said: And I do not know what time he prayed Isha. Someone else said: He prayed in the first third of the night. Ata said: Then he prayed Fajr when it had brightened, and he brightened it greatly. I said: What time? He said: Before the time of neglecting it, before sunrise was due. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: Where is the one who asked me about the time of the prayer that is proper? So he was brought, and the Prophet ﷺ said: Did you attend the prayer with me today and yesterday? He said: So pray it between those times. He said: Then Ali turned and said: I think he used to pray it between those times — meaning the Prophet ﷺ."],[2105,"He used to say: The twilight (shafaq) is the red glow."],[2106,"Gabriel descended and prayed Zuhr with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed the prayer when the sun had declined. Then he prayed Asr when the shadow of every thing was equal to it. Then he prayed Maghrib when the sun had set. Then he prayed Isha after that, as if he meant the disappearance of the twilight. Then he prayed Fajr in the dark, when dawn broke. He said: Then Gabriel descended the next day and prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr with the people when the shadow of every thing was equal to it. Then he prayed Asr when the shadow of every thing was twice its length. Then he prayed Maghrib when the sun had set, at the same time. Then he prayed Isha after a portion of the night had passed. Then he prayed Fajr after it had become very bright. Then he said: Between these two times is the time."],[2107,"Prayer has a fixed time like the time of Hajj, so pray each prayer at its time."],[2108,"He used to dislike sleeping before the late evening prayer and staying awake after it."],[2109,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari: Pray Dhuhr when the sun declines, Asr while the sun is white and clear before yellowness enters it, Maghrib when the sun sets, delay Isha as long as you do not sleep, and pray Subh while the stars are visible and intertwined, and recite in it two long surahs from the Mufassal."],[2110,"Umar wrote to the people of the garrisons: Pray Dhuhr when the sun declines until the shadow of everything equals its length; Asr while the sun remains for the time a rider travels two or three farsakhs; Maghrib when the sun sets and night begins; Isha when the twilight disappears until one-third of the night. Do not be distracted from prayer. Whoever sleeps, may his eyes not sleep; whoever sleeps, may his eyes not sleep."],[2111,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to his governors: The most important of your matters to me is prayer. Whoever guards it and is constant in it guards his religion, and whoever neglects it is more neglectful of other things. Then he wrote: Pray Dhuhr when the shade is a forearm's length until the shadow of one of you equals his height; Asr while the sun is high, white and clear, for the time a rider travels two or three farsakhs; Maghrib when the sun sets; Isha when the twilight disappears until one-third of the night. Whoever sleeps, may his eyes not sleep; whoever sleeps, may his eyes not sleep. And Subh while the stars are visible and intertwined."],[2112,"Whoever prays the morning prayer at night must repeat it when dawn rises, and repeat the iqamah."],[2113,"It is Asr."],[2114,"He asked Abu Hurayrah about the prayer time. Abu Hurayrah said: I will inform you. Pray Dhuhr when your shadow equals your height; Asr when your shadow is twice your height; Maghrib when the sun sets; Isha between then and one-third of the night, and if you sleep until midnight, may your eyes not sleep; and pray Subh in the darkness."],[2115,"Those before you were more hastening in Dhuhr and more delaying in Asr than you."],[2116,"The companions of Abdullah b. Mas'ud used to hasten Dhuhr, delay Asr, hasten Maghrib, and delay Isha."],[2117,"It is Asr."],[2118,"O Mughirah, I knew—Jibril descended and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, and the people prayed five times, by his word he would utter. Then he said: 'Thus have I been commanded.'"],[2119,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr when the sun had begun to decline."],[2120,"I said to 'Ata': 'Which time do you prefer for me to pray Zuhr, whether as imam or alone?' He said: 'When it cools down, or after it has cooled down, though do not delay it into the evening.' I said: 'What about in the winter?' He said: 'When it cools down, and before the time you would pray it in summer, because of the cold.' I said: 'What if I pray it in a house, in the shade?' He said: 'When it cools down is preferable to me.'"],[2121,"Cool down the prayer, for the intense heat is from the scorching of Hellfire."],[2122,"It is Asr."],[2123,"I asked Ubaydah about the middle prayer, and he said: 'It is Asr.'"],[2124,"It is Asr."],[2125,"The sun's duluk is its decline after midday, and that is the time of Zuhr."],[2127,"I never saw anyone more keen to hasten Zuhr than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[2128,"We complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the burning ground, and he did not respond to our complaint—he said: 'regarding the Zuhr prayer.'"],[2129,"Zuhr is like its name—he said: 'at midday.'"],[2130,"We used to pray Zuhr in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ in winter, and we would not know whether more of the day had passed or more remained."],[2131,"I used to pray Zuhr with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and I knew its time in the sky and the earth from the sun: he used to pray it when the sun had declined."],[2132,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to pray Zuhr when the sun declined."],[2133,"Umar came to Makkah and Abu Mahdhura gave the call to prayer for him. He said to him, 'Did you not fear that your cloak would be torn?' He said, 'O Commander of the Faithful, you have arrived, and I wanted to let you hear my call to prayer.' Umar said to him, 'Your land, O people of Tihama, is hot, so wait for it to cool, then wait for it to cool twice or thrice, then give the call to prayer, then give the iqama and I will come to you.'"],[2134,"I will not fail you regarding the time. He said: He prayed Zuhr with them—I think he said—when the sun had declined."],[2135,"It is Asr."],[2136,"The Messenger of God ﷺ would not stop at a place on a journey and depart until he prayed Zuhr, and the earliest he would pray was when the sun had declined."],[2138,"If news reached him of a large number while on a journey and the sun had declined while he was at his stopping place, he would mount before praying and travel for miles, then make his camel kneel and pray Zuhr."],[2139,"When we alighted at a place, we would not cease glorifying God until the saddles were untied."],[2140,"I did not find the people except that they used to pray Zuhr in the late afternoon."],[2141,"I asked Ata about the declining of the sun, and he said: Its declining is its inclination. I said to Ata: If I stand for Zuhr and pray it and I prolong it before the sun declines, and I do not bow until it has declined? He said: I do not like that. Then he recited: 'at the declining of the sun'."],[2143,"He used to pray ʿAṣr before the sun rose out of my room, while it was rising."],[2144,"I asked 'A'ishah, \"What obligates ghusl?\" She said, \"Do you know what your likeness is, O Abu Salamah? The likeness of a chick that hears the rooster crow and crows with it. When the circumcised part passes the circumcised part, ghusl becomes obligatory.\""],[2145,"He used to pray the ʿAṣr prayer while the sun was in her room before it appeared, and the shadow had not yet appeared from her room."],[2146,"It is Asr."],[2147,"Indeed, the one who misses ʿAṣr—it is as if he has been deprived of his family and property."],[2148,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wrote: Pray while the sun is white and clear, for as long as a rider can travel two farsakhs until the sun sets."],[2149,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray ʿAṣr when the sun came out of my room, and my room was spacious."],[2150,"I heard ʿAmr b. Maymūn al-Awdī, as I was leaving the mosque during the governorship of Bishr b. Marwān, say: Have you prayed ʿAṣr? He said: I said: Just now I prayed Ẓuhr? He said: I used to pray ʿAṣr with ʿUmar at this very time."],[2151,"We used to pray ʿAṣr, and a man would go out to Banū ʿAmr b. ʿAwf and find them praying ʿAṣr."],[2152,"We entered upon Anas b. Mālik after Ẓuhr, and he came forward to pray ʿAṣr. When he finished, we reminded him about praying early—or he mentioned it—and he said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: That is the prayer of the hypocrites—three times—one of them sits until when the sun turns yellow, and it is between the two horns of, or on the horn of, Satan, then he stands and pecks four times, not remembering God therein except a little."],[2154,"He used to delay Asr until the sun became very yellow. I said to Ibrahim, 'Is this something you saw?' He said, 'Rather, he used to count for that. He would stay in Makkah for two or three days in order to pray. That is how it was.' Ibn Jurayj said: 'Ibn Tawus used to sometimes hasten it and sometimes delay it.'"],[2155,"I said to Ata, 'Which time do you prefer for me to pray Asr, as an imam or alone?' He said, 'To hasten it.'"],[2156,"When did Ibn Umar pray Asr? He said, 'While the sun was white and had not changed. Whoever traveled quickly could go five miles before nightfall.'"],[2157,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the Asr prayer one day while it was still daylight."],[2158,"Umar b. al-Khattab found al-Munkadir praying after Asr, so he sat beside him with the whip. He said, 'What is this prayer? Leave. I missed two rak'ahs of Asr.' He said, 'If any of you misses Asr or part of it, he should not prolong it until the yellowness of the sun reaches him.'"],[2159,"I asked Ubaydah about the middle prayer, and he said: 'It is Asr.'"],[2162,"Men from Banu Salima used to attend Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then they would return to their families while they could still see the places where arrows fell."],[2163,"We used to pray Maghrib with the Prophet ﷺ, then return to our homes, which were a mile away, and I could see the places where arrows fell."],[2164,"Pray this prayer of yours while the mountain passes are still bright for Maghrib."],[2165,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wrote to the commanders of the garrison cities: \"Do not be among those who delay your breaking of the fast, nor among those who wait for the interlacing of the stars for your prayer.\""],[2166,"I was informed that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to say: Pray Maghrib when the sun sets. Ibn Jurayj said: And Tawus used to pray it when the night began. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Ata: What is the ghasq of the night? He said: Its beginning, when it enters; so what I prefer is to pray Maghrib when the first part of Maghrib enters."],[2167,"He used to pray Maghrib when the sun set, and say: This, by God, is its time. And he would not swear about any of the prayers other than it."],[2168,"Abdullah b. Masud used to pray Maghrib when the edge of the sun disappeared, and he swore that it was the time about which God, Blessed and Exalted, said: 'Establish prayer at the sun's decline until the darkness of the night' (Q 17:78). He said: He mentioned all the prayers but I did not memorize them."],[2171,"There is no prayer I fear missing more than Maghrib."],[2172,"There is no harm in a traveler or someone with an excuse delaying Maghrib by the amount that the pilgrim prays it at Muzdalifah."],[2173,"That the Messenger of God ﷺ: the sun set for him at Sarif, and he did not pray Maghrib until he entered Makkah."],[2174,"I said to Salim: What is the furthest Ibn Umar delayed Maghrib? He said: From Dhat al-Jaysh to Dhat al-Ufuq; between them eight miles."],[2175,"Al-Rabi b. Khuthaym used to say to the muezzin in the evening when there were clouds: Delay the prayer until it is dark."],[2176,"He left his land from Marr when the fasting person broke his fast, intending Madinah, and he did not pray Maghrib until he reached the main road from al-Zahran, combining it with 'Isha'. And it was said to him: The prayer."],[2177,"Wahb used to recognize the sun at al-Rahbah, then he would mount and not pray Maghrib except at his house; he did that more than once."],[2178,"The Prophet ﷺ was visited by Jibril to prescribe the prayer, and he prayed every prayer at two times, except Maghrib, which he prayed at one time when the sun had set."],[2179,"Were it not that I would make things difficult for my Ummah, I would command them to use the siwak with wudu. And were it not that I would make things difficult for my Ummah, I would delay the Isha prayer until a third of the night or half of the night, for Allah—or he said: our Lord—Blessed and Exalted descends to the lowest heaven and says: Who asks Me, that I may give him? Who seeks My forgiveness, that I may forgive him? Who calls upon Me, that I may answer him?"],[2180,"Were it not that I would make things difficult for the believers, I would command them to use the siwak for every wudu, and to delay Isha—meaning the late evening prayer."],[2181,"Umar wrote to Abu Musa: Pray the Isha prayer between your time and a third of the night; if you delay, then up to half the night, and do not be among the heedless."],[2182,"Whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before sunrise has caught it, and whoever catches two rak'ahs of Asr before sunset has caught it."],[2183,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote: Pray the Isha prayer when the whiteness of the horizon has disappeared, between your time and a third of the night; whatever you hasten after the disappearance of the horizon is better."],[2184,"Ubada b. al-Samit and Shaddad b. Aws used to pray the later Isha when the redness had gone. Makhul said: And that is the twilight."],[2185,"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ once delayed the Isha prayer until people slept, woke up, slept, and woke up. Then Umar b. al-Khattab stood up and said: The prayer! So the Prophet ﷺ came out and it is as if I can see him now with his head dripping water, placing his hand ﷺ on the side of his head. He said: Were it not that I would make things difficult for my Ummah, I would command them to pray it like this."],[2186,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once delayed the Isha prayer at night, then came out with his head dripping water and said: Were it not that I would make things difficult for my Ummah, I would have loved to pray this prayer at this time."],[2187,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once delayed the Isha prayer until most of the night had passed and the people of the mosque had slept. Then he came out and prayed, and said: This is indeed its time, were it not that I would make things difficult for my Ummah."],[2188,"The Prophet ﷺ was once occupied at night and delayed it until we slept, then woke up, then slept, then woke up. Then the Prophet ﷺ came out to us and said: There is no one on earth waiting for this prayer except you."],[2189,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once delayed the Isha prayer at night, and Umar called out to him: The women and children have slept. So he came out to them and said: No one on earth is waiting for this prayer except you. Al-Zuhri said: And at that time no one prayed it except those in Madinah."],[2190,"Let him pray it when he remembers it."],[2191,"Ibn Umar used to dislike stones in the mosque."],[2192,"Pray isha after the twilight disappears, between that and the middle of the night."],[2194,"We set out with Makhul to Makkah. He said: Thawr b. Yazid used to give the call to prayer for him, and he would command him not to call for isha until the red glow had gone, and he said: That is the twilight (shafaq)."],[2195,"A spear used to be carried with the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Eid, and he would pray toward it. And when he traveled, it was carried with him and he would pray toward it."],[2196,"I saw Tawus praying maghrib, then performing tawaf seven circuits, then praying two rak'ahs, then praying the late evening prayer (isha), then leaving. He said: When he was at Mina, after praying maghrib he would pray two rak'ahs, then pray the late evening prayer, then leave. He said: I do not know that except that it was before the setting of the twilight."],[2197,"When Anas b. Malik wanted to pray isha, he said to a servant of his—or a freedman of his—: Look whether the two horizons have become equal."],[2198,"Were it not for the weakness of the weak and the illness of the sick, I would have delayed the isha prayer."],[2199,"I saw Mu'awiya praying maghrib, then I could only do seven or seventy circuits before he came out and prayed isha, while the twilight had not yet disappeared."],[2200,"I saw Tawus praying maghrib, then performing tawaf seven circuits only, then praying isha, then leaving."],[2201,"Pray isha within the first third of the night. Whoever sleeps after the first third of the night, may his eyes not sleep."],[2202,"Pray the late evening prayer before the sick person sleeps and the worker becomes lazy."],[2203,"There is no night talk after the late evening prayer except for one who is praying or a traveler."],[2204,"He disliked - or forbade - sleeping before it and talking after it."],[2205,"I saw Ibn Umar praying toward his camel."],[2206,"Umar b. al-Khattab passed by a group engaged in night talk and greeted them and said: By the One besides whom there is no deity, there is no deity but Allah, and I enjoin you to fear Allah."],[2207,"Umar b. al-Khattab saw people who stayed up talking after the late evening prayer, so he dispersed them with a whip and said: Night talk from its beginning and sleep at its end?"],[2208,"Abu Khalaf al-A'ma asked Anas about a woman from his family who sleeps before the late evening prayer. He said: I command her not to pray after sleeping - meaning she should not sleep until she prays. He said: He only commands one of her family to wake her when the muezzin calls the adhan. He said: Command her. We said: Command the one whom I commanded to wake her, so he should not let her sleep."],[2209,"I sought Hudhayfah, so he said: Why did you seek me? He said: I said: For hadith. He said: Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ used to warn against hadith after the sleep prayer."],[2210,"She heard Urwah talking after the late evening prayer, so she said: What is this talk after the late evening prayer? I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sleeping before it or talking after it. Either he was praying and gaining reward, or sleeping and being safe."],[2211,"Indeed this verse was revealed concerning that: They remember Allah - {Their sides forsake their beds}."],[2212,"Beware of night talk after the late evening prayer, and when donkeys bray at night, seek refuge in Allah from Satan."],[2213,"When donkeys bray at night, say: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. I seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan."],[2215,"Whoever sleeps before the late evening prayer, may his eye never sleep."],[2216,"There is no harm in staying awake after the late evening prayer for the sake of fiqh."],[2217,"If I sleep past the late evening prayer, that is dearer to me than engaging in idle talk after it."],[2218,"If I sleep past the late evening prayer, that is dearer to me than engaging in idle talk after it."],[2220,"Ibn ʿUmar would sometimes sleep past the late evening prayer and would tell his family to wake him."],[2221,"ʿAlī would have supper, then sleep in his clothes before the late evening prayer."],[2223,"I was conversing after the late evening prayer when ʿĀ’ishah called out to me: “Will you not give your two scribes a rest, O ʿUrayyah? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not sleep before it nor converse after it.”"],[2224,"Whoever fears that he may sleep before the late evening prayer, there is no harm in praying before the twilight disappears."],[2225,"It is the ʿIshā’ prayer, so do not let the Bedouins prevail over you regarding the name of your prayer, for they delay it on account of the camels."],[2226,"Indeed, do not let the Bedouins prevail over you regarding the name of your prayer. Indeed, it is al-ʿIshā’, and they delay it on account of the camels—or he said: the camels."],[2227,"O 'Abd al-Rahman, do not be overcome regarding the name of your prayer, for Allah has named it al-'isha', and the Bedouins only call it al-'atamah because of the lateness of milking their camels."],[2228,"Ibn 'Umar, when he heard them saying 'al-'atamah,' would become angry and shout at them."],[2230,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the morning prayer one day, then prayed it the next day, then said: 'Between these two is a time.'"],[2231,"If it is adjacent to it, then it will not cut off your prayer."],[2232,"A man asked the Prophet ﷺ about the time of the morning prayer. He said: 'Pray it today with us and tomorrow.' When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was at Qa' Namirah near al-Juhfah, he prayed it when the first light of dawn appeared. Then when he was at Dhu Tuwa, he delayed it until the people said: 'Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away or has he prayed it?' He prayed it just before sunrise. Then he turned to the people and said: 'What did you say?' They said: 'We said: If only we had prayed.' He said: 'If you had done so, punishment would have befallen you.' Then he called the questioner and said: 'Its time is between my two prayers.'"],[2233,"Make the morning prayer bright."],[2234,"'Abdullah used to make the morning prayer bright."],[2235,"We prayed the morning prayer with Ibn Mas'ud, and we began turning around when we finished. He said: 'What is the matter with you?' We said: 'We think the sun is rising.' He said: 'This, by the One besides whom there is no god, is the time of this prayer: \"Establish prayer at the declining of the sun to the darkness of the night\" (Quran 17:78). This is the declining of the sun, and this is the darkness of the night.'"],[2236,"'Abdullah b. Mas'ud used to pray the morning prayer in the dark as Ibn al-Zubayr did, and he would pray the sunset prayer when the sun set, and he would say: 'By Allah, it is as Allah said: \"to the darkness of the night and the Quran at dawn; indeed, the Quran at dawn is ever witnessed\" (Quran 17:78-79).'"],[2238,"Al-Hakam al-Ghifari led the people in prayer on a journey, with a spear in front of him, and donkeys passed in front of his companions. He repeated the prayer with them. They said: He wanted to do as al-Walid b. Uqba did when he prayed four rak'ahs with his companions in the morning, then said: \"Shall I add more for you?\" He said: I caught up with al-Hakam and mentioned that to him. He stopped until the people caught up, then said: I repeated the prayer with you because of the donkeys that passed in front of you, and you compared me to Ibn Abi Mu'ayt. I ask God to make your journey good, to make your delivery good, to grant you victory over your enemy, and to separate me from you. He said: They went on and did not see anything on their faces except what pleased them, and when they finished, he died."],[2241,"Ibrahim said to me, and I was a muezzin: 'Let it be bright, let it be bright.'"],[2242,"'Umar b. al-Khattab would pray the morning prayer in the dark and also when it was bright, and he would pray it between those times."],[2243,"I said to 'Ata': Which time do you prefer for me to pray the morning prayer, whether as imam or alone? He said: When the second dawn breaks, then you prolong the recitation, bowing, and prostration until you finish and the dawn has spread and people have gathered."],[2244,"A woman does not invalidate the prayer of a woman."],[2245,"I would pray the morning prayer with 'Umar b. al-Khattab, and if my son had been beside me, I would not have recognized his face."],[2246,"I would pray with 'Umar, then leave and not recognize the face of my companion."],[2247,"I would pray the morning prayer with Ibn al-Zubayr, then go to Ajyad and relieve myself, and it would still be dark."],[2248,"Ibn 'Umar would pray the morning prayer with Ibn al-Zubayr, then return to his home with the prayer, because Ibn al-Zubayr would pray at night—or he said: in the dark—"],[2249,"A woman does not invalidate the prayer of a woman."],[2250,"Nothing invalidates the prayer; ward off what you can. He said: “He would not pray except facing a sutrah.”"],[2251,"Ibn Umar, when the dawn became clear to him, would make his camel kneel and pray the morning prayer."],[2252,"When al-Hajjaj encamped against Ibn al-Zubayr, he prayed the morning prayer at Mina, then he delayed it until the sky was very bright. Ibn Umar sent to him: 'What makes you delay the prayer until this time?' He said: 'We are at war and fearful.' Ibn Umar replied to him: 'There is no fear upon you in praying the prayer at its proper time, so do not delay it until this hour.' And Ibn Umar prayed with him."],[2253,"I said to Ata: 'And the Quran of the dawn?' He said: 'It is the morning prayer.' I said: 'It is witnessed.' He said: 'The angels and goodness witness it.'"],[2254,"I said to Ata: 'I stood for the morning prayer before the rising of the dawn, and I did not bow until the dawn rose.' He said: 'I do not love that.' He said: 'And the Quran of the dawn, indeed the Quran of the dawn is witnessed.'"],[2255,"We, the women, used to attend the morning prayer with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and we would leave wrapped in our cloaks, unrecognizable because of the darkness. She said: When the Prophet ﷺ said the salam, he would remain in his place for a little while, and they thought that was so the women could leave before the men."],[2256,"Perform the morning prayer when it is bright, for it is greater in reward."],[2257,"If the evening meal is brought and the call to prayer is made, then begin with the evening meal, then pray."],[2259,"Maymun b. Mihran invited us to a meal, and the call to prayer was made. We stood and left his food, and he seemed to be upset. He said: 'By God, something like this happened during the time of Umar, and he began with the food.'"],[2260,"Yasar invited us to a meal, and we wanted to stand when the prayer time came. He said: 'Umar used to order us, when the prayer time came and food was placed, to begin with the food.'"],[2261,"I was with Ubayy b. Kab, Ibn Talha, and some men of the Ansar, and the call to prayer was made while we were having our food. Anas said: I turned to go out, but they held me back and said: 'Is this an Iraqi legal opinion?' They criticized me for it until I sat down."],[2262,"If one of you is having his evening meal or his food, and the call to prayer is made, he should not rush away from it until he finishes."],[2263,"Additional text: Ibn Umar sometimes we would meet him while he was fasting, and dinner would be brought to him after the call to Maghrib prayer had been made, and then the iqamah would be given while he could hear—meaning the prayer—yet he would neither leave his dinner nor hurry until he finished his dinner, then he would go out and pray. And he said: 'The Prophet of God ﷺ used to say: Do not hasten away from your dinner when it is brought to you.'"],[2264,"He would be at his food while listening to the imam's recitation, and he would not get up until he finished his food."],[2265,"The one who misses the ʿAṣr prayer—it is as if he has been deprived of his family and property. He said: Ibn ʿUmar used to think that it is the middle prayer."],[2266,"Ask Ali about the middle prayer. So he asked him, and he said: 'We used to think that it is the Asr prayer, until I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say on the day of the Trench: They kept us occupied from the middle prayer, the Asr prayer. May God fill their graves and their bellies with fire.'"],[2267,"The Prophet ﷺ said on the day of the Confederates: 'May God fill their graves and their homes with fire, as they kept us occupied from the middle prayer until the sun set.' And on that day he had not prayed Zuhr and Asr until the sun had set."],[2268,"On the day of the Confederates, we prayed Asr between Maghrib and Isha. May God fill their graves and their bellies with fire. They kept us occupied from the middle prayer, the Asr prayer. May God fill their graves and their bellies with fire."],[2270,"I said to ʿAbīdah: What invalidates the prayer? He said: \"Wickedness invalidates it, and righteousness completes it, and a barrier like the rear part of a saddle is sufficient for you.\""],[2271,"I came to Abu Hurayrah while he was sitting in the Sacred Mosque. He said: I said: Describe him to me. He said: He was a dark-skinned man with two braids, broad-shouldered, and hollow-cheeked. I said: Tell me about the most important of matters that springs from our prayer, which we cannot do without. He said: Who are you? He said: From a people who were pleased with their obedience and embraced it. He said: From whom are you? I said: From Thaqif. He said: Where are you in relation to Amr b. Aws? He said: I said: I saw that Amr was there, but I came to ask you. He said: Do you recite anything from the Quran? I said: Yes. He said: I recited to him the Opening of the Book, and he said: This is the Seven Oft-Repeated Verses about which Allah Most High says: \"And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated verses and the great Quran.\" Then he said to me: Do you recite Surah al-Ma'idah? I said: Yes. He said: Recite to me the verse of ablution. I recited it. He said: I think you have learned the ablution for prayer. Have you not heard Allah say: \"Establish prayer at the sun's decline\"? Do you know what the sun's decline is? I said: No. He said: When the sun moves from the meridian — or from the belly of the sky — after midday. He said: Yes, pray Dhuhr then. Pray Asr while the sun is white and clear, and you can feel its heat. He said: Do you know what the darkness of night is? I said: Yes, sunset. He said: Yes, so follow it immediately, then follow it immediately. Pray Isha when the twilight disappears and the night grows dark from here — and he pointed to the east — between that and one-third of the night. Whatever you hasten after the whiteness of the horizon disappears is better. Pray Fajr when dawn rises. Do you know dawn? He said: I said: Yes. He said: Not all people know it. I said: When the whiteness flashes. He said: Yes, pray it then until the darkness, then until the darkness. And he said in his hadith: Beware of sitting with legs drawn up, and guard against heedlessness until you finish. I said: Tell me about the middle prayer. He said: Have you not heard Allah say: \"Establish prayer at the sun's decline until the darkness of the night and the Quran at dawn,\" the verse, and \"And after the Isha prayer are three times of privacy for you\"? He mentioned all the prayers, then said: \"Guard strictly the prayers, especially the middle prayer.\" Indeed, it is Asr. Indeed, it is Asr."],[2273,"It is Zuhr."],[2274,"Zayd b. Thabit sent his freedman Harmalah to Aisha to ask her about the middle prayer. She said: 'It is Zuhr.' She said: Zayd used to say: 'It is Zuhr.' I do not know whether he took it from her or from someone else."],[2275,"I read in the copy of the Qur'an belonging to 'A'ishah ؓ: (Guard strictly the prayers, and the middle prayer, and the 'Asr prayer, and stand before Allah devoutly)."],[2276,"Hafsah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, gave a copy of the Qur'an to a client of hers to write it, and said: When you reach this verse, \"Guard strictly the prayers and the middle prayer,\" inform me. When he reached it, he came to her, and she wrote with her own hand: (Guard strictly the prayers, and the middle prayer, and the 'Asr prayer, and stand before Allah devoutly)."],[2278,"Umm Salamah ordered me to write a copy of the Qur'an for her, and said: When you reach \"Guard strictly the prayers and the middle prayer,\" inform me. I informed her, and she said: Write: (Guard strictly the prayers, and the middle prayer, and the 'Asr prayer, and stand before Allah devoutly)."],[2279,"I asked 'Ata' about the middle prayer. He said: I think it is the dawn prayer. Do you not hear His saying: \"And the Qur'an of dawn; indeed, the Qur'an of dawn is ever witnessed\"?"],[2280,"It is intermediate, and it is between the night and the day."],[2282,"We prayed the morning prayer with the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When we finished, I said: Which prayer is the middle prayer? He said: The one you have just prayed."],[2283,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the 'Asr prayer. When he finished it, he turned and said: This prayer was made obligatory on those before you, but they neglected it and it became burdensome for them. It is preferred over all others by twenty-six degrees."],[2284,"One of you remains in prayer as long as he is waiting for the prayer, and the angels continue to pray for one of you as long as he is in the mosque, saying: O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him."],[2285,"[Additional text:] One of you remains in prayer as long as he is waiting for it, and the angels continue to pray for one of you as long as he is in the mosque, saying: O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him, as long as he does not invalidate his ablution. A man from Hadramawt said: What is the invalidating thing, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: Passing wind silently or audibly."],[2286,"I said to 'Ata': When is the time for the dawn prayer neglected? He said: Until its rising becomes clear. I said to him: When is the time for the noon prayer neglected? He said: There is no neglect for it until the sun becomes yellow. I said: And the 'Asr prayer? He said: Until the sun becomes yellow."],[2287,"I said to 'Ata': It used to be said: The 'Isha' prayer is between us and the middle of the night, and whatever is beyond that is neglect, and the Maghrib is similar to that. He said: It is neglect for it until the first half of the night."],[2288,"That Ibn Abbas left his land after the fasting person broke the fast, heading for Madinah, and he did not pray Maghrib until he reached the main road from al-Zahran, then he combined it with Isha. And it was said to him before that: the prayer. He said: Push on."],[2289,"When the sun declines from the middle of the sky, the Zuhr prayer is due until 'Asr arrives, and the 'Asr prayer is due until the twilight disappears, and whatever is after that is exceeding the bounds. And the 'Isha' prayer is due until midnight, and whatever is after that is exceeding the bounds. And the Fajr prayer is due until the rising of the sun's horn, and whatever is after that is exceeding the bounds."],[2290,"I heard Abu Hurayrah, and a man asked him about neglect in prayer. He said: That you delay it until the time of the next prayer. Whoever does that has neglected it."],[2291,"From Umm Farwah, who had pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, she said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, 'Which deeds are best?' He said: 'Prayer at the beginning of its time.'"],[2292,"I said to Ata': \"Was it forbidden to wipe away dust from one's face?\" He said: \"Yes.\" And it is said: \"If you see something you dislike, then postpone it.\" I said: \"What thing?\" He said: \"We have heard that, and it is more beloved to me that you do not wipe it.\" I said: \"What do you think if I wiped?\" He said: \"Then do not do it again, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.\""],[2293,"I prefer that you do not wipe until you finish."],[2294,"For one of you to be deprived of his family and property is better for him than to miss the time of a prayer."],[2295,"I asked Tawus: When is the 'Isha' prayer missed? He said: Until dawn, without taking that as a habit, and do not say that you are better than anyone."],[2296,"Tawus would not pray Maghrib at Jam' until the twilight disappeared. He said: And Tawus used to say: Zuhr and 'Asr are not missed until nightfall, and Maghrib and 'Isha' are not missed until dawn, and Subh is not missed until the sun rises."],[2297,"He used to say: Zuhr and 'Asr are not missed until nightfall, and Maghrib and 'Isha' are not missed until dawn, and Subh is not missed until the sun rises."],[2298,"Whoever catches one rak'ah of 'Asr before the sun sets has caught it, and whoever catches one rak'ah of Subh before the sun rises has caught it."],[2299,"Indeed, one of you—or indeed, the man among you—prays, and what has escaped him of its time is better for him than the like of his family and his wealth."],[2300,"The daytime prayers, Zuhr and 'Asr, are not missed until nightfall, and the nighttime prayers, Maghrib and 'Isha', are not missed until daytime, and the time of Subh is not missed until the sun rises."],[2301,"Whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before sunrise has caught it, and whoever catches two rak'ahs of Asr before sunset has caught it."],[2302,"Whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before sunrise has caught it, and whoever catches two rak'ahs of Asr before sunset has caught it."],[2303,"Whoever catches a rak'ah of Fajr before sunrise has caught it, and whoever catches two rak'ahs of Asr before sunset has caught it."],[2304,"Al-Miswar b. Makhramah entered upon Ibn Abbas and spoke with him while he was reclining on a cushion. Ibn Abbas fell asleep, and al-Miswar b. Makhramah slipped away from him. He did not wake up until morning. He said to his servant: Do you think I can pray before the sun rises—four, meaning Isha, and three, meaning Witr, and two, meaning Fajr, and one, meaning one rak'ah of Subh? He said: Yes. So he prayed them."],[2305,"Al-Miswar b. Makhramah entered upon Ibn Abbas, and I covered a cushion for Ibn Abbas. He slept on it, and al-Miswar b. Makhramah spoke with him a little, then left. Ibn Abbas slept through Isha and Witr until morning. He said to his servant: Do you think I should pray Isha and Witr, and the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and one rak'ah before sunrise? He said: Yes. He said: So Ibn Abbas prayed Isha, then prayed Witr, and prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then prayed Subh, and the sun had almost risen."],[2306,"If you fear that Asr will be missed, then shorten the first two rak'ahs. If the night overtakes you with them, then complete the last two and lengthen them if you wish."],[2307,"If you fear that Subh will be missed, then race the sun with the first rak'ah. If you beat the sun with it, then do not hasten the last one but complete it."],[2308,"When the servant prays at its proper time, a brilliant light shines forth for it in the sky, and it says: You have preserved me, may God preserve you. And when he prays it at other than its time, it is folded up like a worn-out garment is folded, and it is struck with it in his face."],[2309,"If not for the fact that a man prayed two rak'ahs before the morning prayer and then died, he would have prayed the morning prayer."],[2310,"If he fears sunrise, he shortens the first rak'ah and lengthens the last one if he wishes."],[2311,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ returned from Khaybar, he traveled at night until, when it was the last part of the night, he turned off the road and then halted to rest. He said, “Who will keep watch over our prayer?” Bilal said, “I will, O Messenger of God.” So he sat and kept watch over them. Then the Prophet ﷺ and his companions slept. While Bilal was sitting, sleep overcame him, and nothing woke them except the heat of the sun. They were startled, and the Prophet ﷺ said, “Did you sleep, O Bilal?” He said, “O Messenger of God, the One who took my soul took your souls.” He said, “So they hastened to their mounts and moved away from the place where the heedlessness had befallen them. Then he led them in the morning prayer. When he finished, he said, “Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, for God, Exalted is He, says: “Establish prayer for My remembrance” [Q 20:14].”"],[2312,"While the Prophet ﷺ was on one of his journeys, he traveled through the night until, when it was the last part of the night, they dismounted to rest. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Who will wake us for the morning prayer?” Bilal said, “I will.” So Bilal leaned on the arm of his camel and they did not wake until the sun had risen. The Prophet ﷺ stood up, performed ablution, and prayed two rak‘ahs in his resting place. Then he traveled for a while, then prayed the morning prayer."],[2314,"The Messenger of God ﷺ slept and did not wake except due to the heat of the sun. Then he traveled until he crossed the valley and said, “We shall not pray where Satan made us forget.” He said, “So he prayed two rak‘ahs, and commanded Bilal to give the call to prayer, and he gave the iqamah and prayed.”"],[2315,"Abu Qatadah said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said to me while we were traveling at night, and sleep had overtaken him: “Move off the road and make your camel kneel.” So the Messenger of God ﷺ made his camel kneel, and we made ours kneel. He said, “Each man of us leaned on the arm of his mount, and we did not wake until the sun shone brightly, and we woke only to the sound of a bird. We said, “O Messenger of God, we are ruined.” He said, “You are not ruined. Prayer does not escape the sleeper; it only escapes the one who is awake.” He said, “So he performed ablution, commanded Bilal to give the call to prayer, and prayed two rak‘ahs. Then he moved from that place, then commanded him and he gave the iqamah, and he led us in the morning prayer.”"],[2316,"When we slept through the prayer and then woke up, we said, “O Messenger of God, shall we not pray such-and-such prayer?” He said, “Does our Lord forbid us from usury and accept it from us? Negligence is only when one is awake.”"],[2317,"A man came to Ibn Mas‘ud and said, “I slept through the morning prayer until the sun rose.” ‘Abd Allah said, “Go and perform ablution as best as you can, and pray as best as you can.” Then he repeated the words to him, and he said the same to him. His soul did not leave him from the magnitude of his sin in his own eyes until he said, when those with him had dispersed, “O Abu ‘Abd al-Rahman...” So he repeated the words to him. He said, “Then ‘Abd Allah took his hand and said, “You are only told this so that you do it. Go and perform ablution as best as you can, and pray as best as you can.”"],[2318,"Regarding a man who forgot the noon prayer until he had prayed the afternoon prayer, he said, “His afternoon prayer is valid, and he prays the noon prayer.”"],[2319,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered upon ‘Ali and Fatimah while they were sleeping and said, “Will you not pray?” ‘Ali said, “O Messenger of God, our souls are in God’s hand. If He wishes to send them, He sends them.” So he turned away from them, saying, “And man is ever most disputatious” [Q 18:54]."],[2320,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ returned from Khaybar, he traveled at night until, when it was the last part of the night, he turned off the road and then halted to rest. He said, “Who will keep watch over our prayer?” Bilal said, “I will, O Messenger of God.” So he sat and kept watch over them. Then the Prophet ﷺ and his companions slept. While Bilal was sitting, sleep overcame him, and nothing woke them except the heat of the sun. They were startled, and the Prophet ﷺ said, “Did you sleep, O Bilal?” He said, “O Messenger of God, the One who took my soul took your souls.” He said, “So they hastened to their mounts and moved away from the place where the heedlessness had befallen them. Then he led them in the morning prayer. When he finished, he said, “Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, for God, Exalted is He, says: “Establish prayer for My remembrance” [Q 20:14].”"],[2321,"I prefer that you do not wipe until you finish."],[2322,"Regarding a man who forgot a daytime prayer until he remembered it at night: “Let him pray it when he remembers it.”"],[2324,"Pray it when you remember it."],[2325,"Abu Bakrah came to them in a garden of theirs, and he slept through the 'Asr prayer. He said: We thought that he had prayed, but he had not prayed. Then he stood up, performed wudu', and did not pray until the sun had set."],[2326,"He slept through Fajr until the sun rose. He said: I stood up to pray, but he called me and made me sit down – meaning Ka'b – until the sun had risen high and become bright. Then he said: 'Stand up and pray.'"],[2327,"Regarding a man who forgot a prayer until the time of the next one entered, and he feared that if he prayed the first prayer he would miss this one, he said: 'He should pray this prayer that he fears missing, and he has not lost it twice.'"],[2330,"Regarding a man who entered with a group praying 'Asr while he thought it was Zuhr, he said: 'He should pray Zuhr, then 'Asr, and he does not repeat what he prayed, so that he gives precedence to what Allah gave precedence to.'"],[2331,"I arrived in Madinah while they were praying 'Asr, and I had not prayed Zuhr. He said: 'So I prayed with them, thinking it was Zuhr.' He said: 'When I finished, I learned that it was 'Asr.' He said: 'So I prayed Zuhr, then I prayed 'Asr.' He said: 'Then I asked in Madinah, and everyone ordered me to do what I did.' Ibn Sirin said: 'And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were there at that time.'"],[2332,"Regarding a man who entered with a group in 'Asr while he had not prayed Zuhr, he said: 'Allah prescribed Zuhr before 'Asr, so he should pray Zuhr, then pray 'Asr.'"],[2333,"If you catch 'Asr, make the one you caught with the imam Zuhr, and pray 'Asr after that. He said: 'He used to do that.'"],[2334,"If he forgot 'Asr and remembered during Maghrib that he had not prayed it, he should make it 'Asr. He said: 'And if he remembers after finishing, he should pray 'Asr.'"],[2336,"About a man who slept through Zuhr until it was Asr, and he is the imam of a people, then he led them while intending it as Zuhr and they intended it as Asr, he said: \"It suffices for his own prayer, and he relies on that, but they must repeat Asr.\""],[2337,"One prayer does not suffice for various intents."],[2338,"Abu al-Darda' came to the people of Hims while they were praying Isha, and he thought it was Maghrib. When the imam said the salam, he stood and prayed one more rak'ah, counting three for Maghrib and making the two rak'ahs voluntary; then he prayed Isha after that."],[2339,"Mu'adh b. Jabal used to pray with the Prophet ﷺ the prayer people call 'Atamah, then he would go and lead them in the last Isha as well. So it was voluntary for him, but obligatory for them."],[2340,"The rows most deserving of completion are their first; indeed, God and His angels bless the first row."],[2341,"If you pray in your house and then find people praying it, pray with them. If you find them in Maghrib, add one rak'ah."],[2342,"When a man comes to the night prayer of Ramadan and has not prayed the obligatory prayer, he prays with them and counts it as the obligatory prayer with them."],[2343,"One prayer does not suffice for various intents."],[2344,"He does not repeat."],[2345,"He said to his people: “Gather so that I may lead you in the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ.” When they gathered, he said: “Is there anyone among you who is not one of you?” They said: “No, except a nephew of ours.” He said: “A people’s nephew is one of them.” Then he called for a bowl containing water, washed his hands, rinsed his mouth, snuffed water into his nose, washed his face three times, and his forearms three times each, wiped his head, and washed his feet. Then he led them in the noon prayer, saying in the two rak‘ahs twenty-two takbirs: he said the takbir when he prostrated and when he raised his head from prostration, and he recited in the first two rak‘ahs the Opening of the Book, letting those next to him hear."],[2346,"When the obligatory prayer is mixed with the voluntary, it is like speech [i.e., invalid]."],[2347,"Those who passed away used to set up the back part of the saddle when they prayed. I said: And how much did you hear it was? He said: \"The measure of the back part of the saddle.\" He said: \"A cubit.\""],[2348,"Ibn Umar would not pray except toward a sutrah. He said: The measure of the back part of his saddle was a cubit. He said: \"He would pray, and sometimes he would make his camel lie down across and pray toward it.\""],[2349,"Ibn Umar would place his saddle during travel, and he would make its back part a third of it if there was nothing else, or he would make his riding camel lie down across and place it between him and the qiblah, then pray toward it."],[2351,"If there is between you and the road the like of the back part of a saddle, then whoever passes by you will not harm you."],[2352,"If there is between you and the road the like of the back part of a saddle, then whoever passes by you will not harm you."],[2353,"Ibn 'Umar used to say the takbir in prayer when he began, when he bowed, when he leaned forward to prostrate before placing his head, when he raised from the prostration, then when he placed again to prostrate before putting his face, when he raised his head from the prostration, then when he stood upright after the second."],[2354,"Ibn Umar would not pray toward these mile-markers that are between Makkah and Madinah, which were made of stones. It was said to him: Why do you dislike that? He said: \"I likened them to the standing stones (ansab).\""],[2357,"He would go out with the spear on the day of Fitr and Adha, in order to plant it and pray toward it."],[2358,"He saw Suwayd b. Ghafalah on the road to Makkah making his camel kneel and praying toward it."],[2359,"A spear used to be carried with the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Eid, and he would pray toward it. And when he traveled, it was carried with him and he would pray toward it."],[2360,"I saw Ibn Umar praying toward his camel."],[2362,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed toward a camel, then he took a hair from the top of its hump and said: 'Nothing of what Allah has granted you as spoils is lawful except the khums, and even the like of these hairs is returned to you.'"],[2363,"When one of you prays, let him pray toward something. If he cannot find anything, let him set up a stick. If he cannot find a stick, let him draw a line in front of him, and then whatever passes in front of him will not harm him."],[2364,"When one of you prays, let him pray toward something. If he cannot find anything, let him set up a stick. If he cannot find a stick, let him draw a line in front of him, and then whatever passes in front of him will not harm him."],[2367,"It does not harm you when there is a barrier in front of you, even if it is thinner than a hair."],[2368,"If it is the size of the back of a saddle—or he said: the rear of a saddle—even if it is the size of a hair, it suffices."],[2369,"Like the rear of a saddle in the extent of the whip's handle—meaning the barrier."],[2370,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked: 'What screens the worshipper from animals?' He said: 'The like of the rear of a saddle in front of him.'"],[2371,"The like of the rear of a saddle or a stick if he does not have the rear of a saddle with him."],[2372,"We used to screen ourselves with an arrow or a stone in prayer—or he said: One of us would screen himself with an arrow or a stone in prayer."],[2373,"I said to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: 'What screens the worshipper?' He said: 'The like of the rear of a saddle, and a stone suffices for that, and an arrow that you plant in front of you.'"],[2374,"The takbir of the rak'ah suffices for him."],[2375,"If you are in open ground and have something with you to plant, plant it in front of you. If you have nothing with you, then draw a line in front of you."],[2376,"He was asked about a reed and reeds that a man places in front of him while praying. He said: \"It screens him if it is a cubit and a span.\""],[2377,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"What do you think if I have a staff that is only a cubit, of which four fingers are in the ground, so that its visible part above the ground is less than a cubit?\" He said: \"No, not until its visible part above the ground is a cubit.\""],[2378,"The measure of the rear part of a saddle, and if what is in front of you screens you, it is better for your soul."],[2379,"Someone informed me that he saw ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb praying toward his cap, which he had made a screen for himself."],[2380,"When one of you prays, let him pray toward a screen and draw near to it, for Satan passes between him and it."],[2381,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb saw a man praying with no screen in front of him, so he sat down in front of him and said: \"Do not rush your prayer.\" When he finished, ʿUmar said to him: \"When one of you prays, let him pray toward a screen, so that Satan does not come between him and his prayer.\""],[2383,"None of you should pray while there is a gap between him and the qiblah."],[2384,"I saw ʿAbd Allāh b. Mughaffal praying with about seven cubits between him and his screen."],[2385,"It is said: The least that suffices you between you and the pillar is three cubits."],[2386,"Umar b. al-Khattab passed by a youth while he was praying, and Umar said: \"Young man, young man,\" three times, until Umar saw that he had recognized his voice: \"Move forward to the pillar, so that Satan does not toy with your prayer. It is not an opinion I am stating, but I heard it from the Messenger of God ﷺ.\""],[2387,"If between you and that which cuts off your prayer there is the measure of a stone, it will not cut off your prayer."],[2388,"If between you and that which cuts off your prayer there is the measure of a stone, it will not cut off your prayer."],[2389,"If you are praying on a roof over which people pass, and you are where the people are not seen when they pass—Sufyan said: \"Then the thing that prevents you from seeing them is what screens you.\""],[2390,"I said to Ata: I prayed facing a stick planted upright on the ground, a cubit or more, and behind me were thirty men, with the row extending from here and here. Does it suffice for me and them against what cuts off the prayer? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: What if someone passes in front of them but behind me? He said: \"It cuts off their prayer.\""],[2391,"I saw Bilal come out with the spear and plant it in front of the Messenger of God ﷺ in al-Batha, and he prayed the noon and afternoon prayers facing it, while dogs, donkeys, and women passed beyond it."],[2392,"I saw myself in rows behind Umar, and he prayed with the spear in front of him, and women in howdahs passed in front of him, and that did not cut off his prayer."],[2393,"Umar would sometimes plant the spear and pray facing it while women in howdahs passed in front of him."],[2395,"Al-Hakam al-Ghifari prayed with his companions, having planted a spear in front of him, and a dog or a donkey passed in front of them. He turned to his companions and said: \"Indeed, it did not cut off my prayer, but it cut off your prayer.\" Then he repeated the prayer with them."],[2396,"When what cuts off the prayer passes in front of the people, it cuts off the prayer of the first row, but it does not cut off the rows behind them.\""],[2397,"Al-Hakam al-Ghifari led the people in prayer on a journey, with a spear in front of him, and donkeys passed in front of his companions. He repeated the prayer with them. They said: He wanted to do as al-Walid b. Uqba did when he prayed four rak'ahs with his companions in the morning, then said: \"Shall I add more for you?\" He said: I caught up with al-Hakam and mentioned that to him. He stopped until the people caught up, then said: I repeated the prayer with you because of the donkeys that passed in front of you, and you compared me to Ibn Abi Mu'ayt. I ask God to make your journey good, to make your delivery good, to grant you victory over your enemy, and to separate me from you. He said: They went on and did not see anything on their faces except what pleased them, and when they finished, he died."],[2398,"The Prophet ﷺ, while he was praying with the people, a lamb or a young she-goat passed by to cross in front of him. He began to draw near to the pillar and kept drawing near until he got ahead of it, and he pressed his belly against the pillar. It then passed between him and the people, and he did not tell the people to do anything."],[2399,"Zayd b. Khalid sent me to Abu Juhaym al-Ansari to ask him: What did you hear from the Messenger of God ﷺ concerning a man passing in front of a worshiper? He said: I heard him say: \"For one to stand in his place for forty is better for him than to pass in front of a worshiper.\" He said: I do not know whether he said forty years or forty days."],[2400,"If the one who passes in front of a worshiper knew what is upon him, it would be better for him to be swallowed up by the earth than to pass in front of a worshiper."],[2401,"If the one who passes in front of a worshiper knew what is upon him, it would be better for him to stand for a year than that, when there is no screen in front of the worshiper."],[2402,"Do not let anyone pass in front of you while you are praying. If he insists except that you fight him, then fight him."],[2404,"Ibn Umar would not let anything pass in front of him while he was praying, and he himself would not pass in front of men or women."],[2405,"While Abu Said al-Khudri was praying, a young man came wanting to pass close to his screen, and the governor of Madinah at that time was Marwan. He said: Abu Said pushed him until he knocked him down. He said: The young man went until he entered upon Marwan and said: Here is a crazy old man who pushed me until he knocked me down. He said: Do you know him? He said: Yes. He said: The Ansar used to enter upon him on Friday. He said: Abu Said entered upon him, and Marwan said to the young man: Do you know him? He said: Yes, is it this old man? Marwan said to the young man: Do you know who this is? He said: No. He said: This is a companion of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: Marwan welcomed him and brought him near until he sat close to his seat. Then he said to him: This young man claims that you pushed him until you knocked him down. He said: What did I do? He repeated it to him, and he said: I only pushed a devil. He said: Then he said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"If one of you wants to pass between you and your screen, push him back. If he refuses, shove him. If he refuses, fight him, for he is only a devil.\""],[2406,"A relative of Marwan went to pass in front of Abu Said al-Khudri, and he forbade him and pushed him. He complained about him to Marwan, and Marwan said to Abu Said: What made you do what you did? He said: The Prophet ﷺ commanded us not to let anyone pass in front of us, and if he refuses, to push him."],[2407,"A man from the Banu Marwan passed in front of him while he was praying, and he pushed him three times. He said: He complained to Marwan, and he mentioned that to him, and he said: \"If he had refused, I would have grabbed him by his hair.\""],[2408,"Dawud b. Marwan wanted to pass in front of Abu Said while he was praying, wearing a fine suit of his, and Marwan was then governor of the people in Madinah. He pushed him back, but he seemed to refuse, so he struck him in the chest. The young man went to his father and informed him. Marwan called Abu Said, thinking he struck him because of his fine suit. He said: He mentioned that. He said: He said: Yes, the Prophet ﷺ said: Push him back, and if he refuses, then struggle with him."],[2410,"While we were with the Messenger of God ﷺ in part of the upper valley, intending to pray, he had stood and we stood, when a donkey came out of the ravine of Abu Dubb, the ravine of Abu Musa. The Prophet ﷺ held back and did not say the takbir, and Ya'qub b. Zam'a, the brother of Banu Asad, went over to it until he turned it back."],[2411,"A dog came while the Prophet ﷺ was praying the afternoon prayer with the people, about to pass in front of them. A man among the people said, 'O God, stop it,' and the dog died. When the Prophet ﷺ finished, he said, 'Which of you supplicated against it?' The man said, 'I did, O Messenger of God.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'If he had supplicated against a nation among the nations, it would have been answered for him.'"],[2412,"I saw 'Uthman — or he said: 'Uthman was — praying while he was fending off a sheep from passing in front of him."],[2413,"I passed by the side of Ibn 'Umar and he thought I was passing in front of him, so he burst out in a way that alarmed me and pushed me aside."],[2414,"I went to pass in front of Ibn 'Umar while he was sitting and praying. He said: 'He rebuked me, and he was severe with anyone who passed in front of him.'"],[2415,"The Messenger of God ﷺ led us in the dawn prayer. He began reaching with his hands in front of him while in prayer. The people asked him when he finished, and he said: 'Satan was throwing sparks of fire at me to distract me from the prayer. I grabbed at him. If I had caught him, he would not have escaped me until he was tied to one of the pillars of the mosque, and the children of the people of Madinah would look at him.'"],[2416,"'Umar b. al-Khattab passed by a man praying without a sutra. When he finished, he said: 'If the passer and the one passed in front of knew what is upon them, they would not do it.'"],[2417,"Whoever among you can avoid having someone pass in front of him while he is praying, let him do so. For the one passing in front of the worshipper is more deficient in reward than the one passed in front of."],[2418,"The Messenger of God ﷺ rushed toward a tomcat or a she-cat that was heading toward the qibla."],[2419,"When someone wants to pass in front of you while you are praying, do not let him, for he casts away half of your prayer."],[2420,"When a passerby goes past you in your prayer, do not push him back a second time."],[2422,"A man passed in front of Salim b. 'Abd Allah, and he pulled him back after he had tried to go on, until he returned."],[2423,"Do not let him pass in front of you, for with him is his devil."],[2424,"Five things are of boorishness: that a man prays in the mosque while people are passing in front of him; that he urinates standing; that the prayer is established while he is beside the mosque and he does not respond; that he wipes dust from his face during the prayer before he has said the salam; and that he eats along with someone who is not of his religion."],[2425,"I said to 'Ata': \"Consider if I enter before the prayer and begin, seeking refuge, and recite until the prayer is established. Should I seek refuge for the obligatory prayer as well? Then I finish the obligatory prayer, and afterward I pray again—should I seek refuge also?\" He said: \"The first seeking of refuge suffices you, but if you seek refuge for each one, that is good.\""],[2426,"The black dog interrupts the prayer.” He said: I think he said: “And the menstruating woman.” I said to Abu Dharr: “What is it about the black dog?” He said: “Indeed, I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about that. He said: ‘It is a devil.’”"],[2427,"If the person praying is not praying toward a sutrah, there is no sin upon you to pass in front of him."],[2430,"The prayer is invalidated by the dog, the pig, the Jew, the Christian, the Magian, and the menstruating woman."],[2431,"I said: \"O Messenger of God, Satan came between me and my recitation.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"That is a satan called Khinzab. When you sense him, seek refuge with God and spit to your left three times.\""],[2435,"I and al-Fadl b. Abbas passed in front of the Prophet ﷺ, riding a female donkey, while he was praying on the day of Arafah, with nothing between us and him to act as a barrier."],[2436,"The Prophet ﷺ visited Abbas while we were in our desert area. He stood to pray — I think he said: the afternoon prayer — while in front of him there was a female dog of ours and a donkey grazing, with nothing between him and them to act as a barrier."],[2437,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage — or he said: on the day of the conquest — while he was praying, and al-Fadl b. Abbas and I were riding a female donkey. We cut through the row, dismounted, then joined the row, and the donkey passed in front of them, yet it did not invalidate their prayer."],[2438,"What invalidates the prayer was mentioned to Ibn Abbas. It was said to him: the woman and the dog? Ibn Abbas said: “To Him ascend the good words, and righteous deeds raise them” (35:10). So what invalidates this?"],[2440,"A man wanted to pass in front of Humayd b. Abd al-Rahman b. Awf, so he took him to Uthman. Uthman said to the man: “What harm would it have done you to turn back when he stopped you?” Then he turned to Humayd and said: “What harm would it have done you if he passed in front of you? Indeed, nothing invalidates the prayer except speech and ritual impurity.”"],[2441,"Nothing invalidates the prayer. He said: Sometimes I saw a man whom I had forbidden to pass in front of Amir while he was praying, so he would take him by the hand and make him walk in front of him."],[2443,"Nothing invalidates the prayer; ward off what you can.””"],[2444,"Nothing invalidates the prayer; ward off what you can.””"],[2445,"Nothing invalidates the prayer; ward off what you can.””"],[2447,"Nothing invalidates a Muslim's prayer, and ward off what you can."],[2449,"I said to ʿAbīdah: What invalidates the prayer? He said: \"Wickedness invalidates it, and righteousness completes it, and a barrier like the rear part of a saddle is sufficient for you.\""],[2450,"O God, I seek refuge with You from Satan, from his prodding, his spitting, and his blowing. They said: \"How often you seek refuge from this one! Who is this one?\" He said: \"As for his prodding, it is madness; as for his blowing, it is pride; and as for his spitting, it is poetry.\""],[2451,"The Prophet ﷺ was praying while I was lying crosswise on the bed between him and the qiblah. I said: \"Was there a wall of the mosque between them?\" He said: \"No, except she was in the house up to its walls.\""],[2452,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying while I was lying crosswise between him and the qiblah, like the lying of a funeral bier."],[2454,"I used to sleep in front of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with my legs in his qiblah direction. When he wanted to prostrate, he nudged me and I drew my legs in, and when he stood up I stretched them out.\" She said: \"And there were no lamps in the houses in those days.\""],[2455,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed with a small striped cloak of these Yemeni cloaks on him, part of it on me and part on him."],[2457,"The Prophet ﷺ was praying with Umāmah bt. Zaynab bt. the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she was the daughter of Abū al-ʿĀṣ b. al-Rabīʿ b. ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, on his neck. When he bowed he put her down, and when he rose from prostration he took her and put her back on his neck.\" ʿĀmir said: \"And I did not ask him which prayer it was.\""],[2459,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to take Husayn during the prayer and make him stand, until when he prostrated he would put him down. I said: 'In the prescribed prayer?' He said: 'I do not know.'"],[2460,"The Prophet ﷺ used to prostrate, and Husayn would climb onto his back. When he raised his head he would move him back, and when he prostrated he would return and climb onto his back. He said: 'When he raised his head he would move him back.'"],[2461,"When the prayer was commenced, the Messenger of God ﷺ would be approached by al-Hasan, al-Husayn, and Umamah, who would rush to him. When he sat, they would sit in his lap and on his back. When he stood, he would place them thus, and so it continued until his prayer was completed."],[2462,"A man brought to Uthman b. Affan a man whose nose had been broken, and said to him: 'He passed in front of me during the prayer while I was praying, and I had heard what you said about one who passes in front of a worshipper.' Uthman said to him: 'What you did was worse, O son of my brother. You ruined the prayer and broke his nose.'"],[2463,"Nothing interrupts the prayer in Makkah. It does not harm you if a woman passes in front of you."],[2464,"I saw Ibn al-Zubayr praying in the mosque, and a woman wanted to pass in front of him while he was about to prostrate. When she had passed, he prostrated at the place of her feet."],[2465,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying in the Sacred Mosque while the people were circumambulating the House between him and the qiblah, in front of him, with no screen between him and them."],[2467,"I saw Muhammad b. al-Hanafiyyah praying in the mosque of Mina while people were passing in front of him, and a young man from his family came and sat in front of him."],[2469,"I said to the Commander of the Faithful: 'We live in the desert, and if I go out I settle in one place, and if my wife goes out she settles in another.' He said: 'Then separate yourself from her with a cloth, then pray and let her pray.'"],[2470,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray while some of his wives were on his right and on his left, and they were menstruating."],[2471,"I asked Tawus, and he said: \"What is the matter with the people? No one remains but that a man prays while another man faces him?\" He said: \"Because of a man who vowed to kiss the forehead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" Then Tawus informed the man of that report. Al-Hasan said: \"So I asked Tawus about that, and he concealed it from me, saying: 'You only want to say, Tawus told me.'\" He said: \"So I commanded a man from among the pilgrims, with myself between him and Tawus, and I said to him: 'Ask him: Was there a man who vowed to kiss the forehead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? Did he come to prostrate upon his forehead?' He said: 'Come here.' So he came until the man faced the qiblah, and the Prophet ﷺ faced the man. The Prophet ﷺ inclined his head until he made his forehead accessible, and he prostrated upon it, while both of them were facing the qiblah, and neither of them was in prayer.\" Al-Hasan said: \"The one who informed him made a mistake; he said: 'to kiss.' And I knew that the report was only as Tawus said, and I knew that he only disliked—meaning a man's prayer while facing another man—because of that.\""],[2472,"That Khuzaymah b. Thabit vowed to prostrate upon the forehead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked that, and he was uneasy about the man. And this was the report.\""],[2473,"A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: \"I have vowed to prostrate upon your face.\" So he faced the qiblah, then the man inclined his head from behind him, and the man prostrated behind the Prophet ﷺ while facing the qiblah."],[2474,"Umar saw a man praying while another man faced him. He turned to this one with the whip and said: \"You pray while this one faces you?\" And he turned to that one with the whip and said: \"You face him while he is praying?\""],[2475,"I said to Ata': \"Was it forbidden to wipe away dust from one's face?\" He said: \"Yes.\" And it is said: \"If you see something you dislike, then postpone it.\" I said: \"What thing?\" He said: \"We have heard that, and it is more beloved to me that you do not wipe it.\" I said: \"What do you think if I wiped?\" He said: \"Then do not do it again, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.\""],[2476,"When one of you stands for prayer, mercy faces him, so do not move the pebbles."],[2477,"When one of you stands for prayer, mercy faces him, so let him not wipe the pebbles."],[2478,"Whoever sets out to attend the prayer, and the iqamah is called while he is on the road, let him not hurry, nor increase upon his previous manner of walking. Whatever he catches, let him pray with the imam, and whatever he does not catch, let him complete it. And let him not wipe his face when he prays. If he wipes, then one time; and if he refrains from it, that is better for him than a hundred black-eyed camels."],[2479,"A single wiping for prostration is permitted, and leaving it is better than a hundred black-eyed camels."],[2480,"When the prayer approaches, walk in your usual manner, pray what you catch, and complete what you missed. Do not wipe the ground except once, and to refrain from it is better for you than a hundred camels, all of them black-eyed."],[2481,"I asked the Prophet ﷺ about everything, until I asked him about wiping away pebbles. He said: \"One time, or leave it.\""],[2482,"I asked my close friend about everything, even wiping away pebbles. He said: \"One time.\""],[2483,"Abu Dharr passed by while I was praying and said: \"The earth should not be wiped except with one wipe.\""],[2485,"Abdullah b. Zayd would level the pebbles with his hand once when he wanted to prostrate, and he would say in his prostration: \"At Your service, O God, at Your service, and I obey You.\""],[2488,"I was with Uthman when the prayer commenced, and I was speaking to him about granting me a stipend. I kept speaking to him while he was leveling the pebbles with his hand, until some men whom he had entrusted with straightening the rows came and informed him that they were now straight. He said to me: \"Stand in the row,\" and then he said the takbir."],[2489,"Tawus would wipe the dust from his face with one wipe when he wanted to prostrate."],[2490,"I said to Ata: \"Should I prostrate on a stone that hurts my face?\" He said: \"Cast it aside and prostrate with your face until it touches the ground, or turn your face.\""],[2491,"The Prophet ﷺ heard a man turning pebbles during prayer in the mosque. When he finished, he said: \"Who was the one turning pebbles during prayer?\" The man said: \"I, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"That is your share of your prayer.\""],[2492,"He used to begin with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim.'"],[2493,"I said to Ata: \"They used to be strict about wiping the pebbles from the place of the forehead, but not as strict about wiping dust from the face.\" He said: \"Yes, by God, indeed.\""],[2494,"I said to Ata: \"I shook the dust off my hands before finishing the prayer.\" He said: \"I do not like that.\""],[2495,"He would wipe his forehead when he finished the prayer, before he said the salam."],[2497,"It was recommended for a man, when he finished his prayer, to wipe the dust from his face, then say: 'In the name of Allah, besides whom there is no deity, Knower of the unseen and the witnessed. O Allah, remove sorrow from me.'"],[2498,"It is said: 'If you can avoid wiping your face from the dust until you finish your prayer, do so; and if you wipe, there is no harm. I prefer that you do not wipe until you finish.' Ata' said: 'I do all of that. Sometimes I wipe before finishing my prayer, and sometimes I do not wipe until I finish my prayer.'"],[2499,"I said to Ata': 'What do you think if I wipe my face after saying: \"Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah,\" and I recite the tashahhud before the imam says the taslim?' He said: 'It does not harm you.'"],[2500,"I said to him: Did Ibn al-Zubayr say amin right after the Mother of the Qur'an? He said: \"Yes, and those behind him would say amin until the mosque resounded with a loud murmur.\""],[2501,"He disliked that a man wipe his face from the dust during the prayer. He said: I mentioned that to al-Hasan, and he had been wiping his face before saying the taslim. He said: 'Should I then leave the dust on my face?'"],[2502,"They did not form rows until this was revealed: 'And indeed, we are those who stand in rows' (37:165), 'And indeed, we are those who glorify' (37:166)."],[2503,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said... then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Zuhri. [Hidden text: When the imam says: 'Not the path of those who have incurred wrath, nor of those who have gone astray,' then say: Amin, for the angels say Amin, and the imam says Amin. Whoever's Amin coincides with the Amin of the angels, his previous sins are forgiven.]"],[2508,"He used not to recite in the last two rakʿahs."],[2509,"The first of the prayers is longer in recitation."],[2510,"The Prophet ﷺ used to wipe our chests in prayer from here to here, saying: \"Straighten your rows; do not differ, lest your hearts differ. Indeed, God and His angels send blessings upon the first row—or he said: the rows—and whoever gives a gift of silver or milk, or gives away a small skin of water, is equal to freeing a slave.\""],[2511,"The first of the prayers is longer in recitation."],[2512,"We used to pray with Umar, and he would say: \"Close up your rows so that your shoulders touch, lest Satan pass through you, like small black sheep.\""],[2513,"The first of the prayers is longer in recitation."],[2516,"Umar used to send a man to straighten the rows, and he would not say the takbir until the man came and told him that the rows were even."],[2517,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to command the straightening of the rows, and when they came and told him that they were straight, he said the takbir."],[2518,"Umar would not say the takbir until the rows were straight; he assigned men to that task."],[2519,"Straighten your rows, align your shoulders, help your female slaves, and restrain yourselves. For the believer restrains himself and helps his female slaves, while the hypocrite does not help his female slaves nor restrain himself. And do not impose a tax on a slave who is not a craftsman, for if he cannot find his tax he will steal; and do not impose a tax on a slave woman who is not a craftswoman, for if she cannot find anything she will seek it with her private parts."],[2520,"Straighten the rows, align the shoulders, and listen silently, for the reward of the silent listener who does not hear is like the reward of the silent listener who hears."],[2522,"He used to say in his sermon: It is rare for the imam to omit saying when he stands: Listen and be silent, for the silent one who does not hear the sermon has the like of what the listening silent one has. When the prayer is established, straighten the rows and align the shoulders, for the straightness of the row is part of the completeness of the prayer. Then he does not say takbir until men come whom he has entrusted with straightening the rows and inform him that they are straight, then he says takbir."],[2523,"Straighten the rows, arrange the feet, align the shoulders, and listen and be silent, for the silent listener who does not hear has the like of what the silent listener who hears has."],[2524,"I saw the guards of one of the commanders of Makkah ordering the straightening of the rows, but they did not pray with the people. I said to 'Ata': 'Did you approve of that from the guards?' He said: 'No, by God, unless they pray with the people. Glory be to God.'"],[2525,"I said to 'Ata': 'What do you think of a person leaving the row when they sit for the last tashahhud, so that he is separated from the row?' He said: 'I do not like it except after the taslim, and I prefer that he remain. And if he leaves due to crowding, there is no harm after the taslim also.'"],[2526,"The Prophet ﷺ said about the one who leaves the rows: 'That is the seat of Satan.' And about the one who raises his head before the imam, he said: 'His head is in the hand of Satan, who raises it and lowers it.'"],[2527,"I said to 'Ata': 'Do the people crowd after the imam says the takbir?' He said: 'No, unless one walks holding the hand of someone among the people, and exits from his row to the row behind him. Is it excused for him to walk behind?' He said: 'There is no harm in that.' I said: 'He exits turning his back on the qiblah, facing the row behind him?' He said: 'I do not like that.' I said: 'And does he not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness?' He said: 'No, he only turns out of fear of bumping into someone.'"],[2528,"I said to him: 'Is it disliked for a person to walk through the rows after the imam says the takbir?' He said: 'No, unless he walks directly in front of someone.' Then he said afterwards: 'If he breaks through the rows to an empty space, he has done well, and it is a duty upon the people to close up the rows so that there are no gaps between them.' Then he said: 'Indeed, God loves those who fight in His way in ranks as if they were a solid structure (Quran 61:4). So prayer is more worthy of having that.'"],[2529,"Indeed, God and His angels bless the first row."],[2530,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed the 'Isha' prayer, then said: 'Indeed, God and His angels bless the front row.'"],[2531,"The rows most deserving of completion are their first; indeed, God and His angels bless the first row."],[2532,"He heard Ibn Umar reciting in Maghrib: Qaf. By the Glorious Quran."],[2533,"The Prophet of God ﷺ used to seek forgiveness for the first front row three times, and for the second once."],[2534,"A people will not cease falling behind from the first row until Allah makes them fall behind in the Fire."],[2535,"Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who advance to the front rows through their prayer."],[2536,"I saw al-Miswar b. Makhramah making his way through the rows until he reached the first and the second."],[2539,"Umar would command the straightening of the rows, then say: \"Come forward, O so-and-so; come forward, O so-and-so; move back, O so-and-so.\""],[2541,"When I arrived in Madinah, I entered the mosque for the Asr prayer. I advanced to the first row, then a man came and took hold of my shoulder and moved me back, then stood in my place after the imam had said the takbir and I had said the takbir. When we finished the prayer, he turned to me and said: \"I only moved you back because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us that the Muhajirun and the Ansar should pray in the first row, and I knew that you were not one of them, so I moved you back.\" I said: \"Who is this?\" They said: \"Ubayy b. Ka'b.\""],[2542,"Hudhayfah saw a man in the first row and moved him back, saying: \"You are not one of them.\""],[2543,"When the prayer was called, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stand at his place of prayer, then turn to face the people and say: \"Straighten your rows, for I see you from behind me.\""],[2545,"He led us in prayer, and when he stood to lead us he said: \"Straighten the rows, for part of the perfection of prayer is establishing the row.\""],[2546,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Did the Prophet ﷺ straighten the rows of the people himself, from behind them? He said: “I heard him say: I thought it was incumbent upon the imams to command their guards to do that, straightening the people?” He said: “Yes.” Then he said: “Rather, they are commanded and it suffices them. The people in that time were few, recently out of unbelief, so they were taught.”"],[2547,"When the people were few, Ibn al-Zubayr placed them behind the Maqām, and that was criticized against him."],[2548,"He used to dislike a man standing in the second row until the first row was complete, and he disliked standing in the third row until the second row was complete. The imam ought to command them to do that."],[2549,"Whoever releases a Muslim from a sale, Allah will release him from his own reckoning on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever joins a row, Allah will join his steps on the Day of Resurrection."],[2550,"Whoever joins a row in the path of Allah or in prayer, Allah will join his steps on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever releases a remorseful person, Allah will release him from his own reckoning on the Day of Resurrection."],[2552,"No man takes a step of greater reward than a step he takes toward a gap in a row to fill it."],[2553,"That my two front teeth fall out is dearer to me than seeing a gap in the row in front of me and not joining it."],[2554,"That my two front teeth fall out is dearer to me than seeing a breach in the row and not filling it."],[2556,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to recite Surat Yusuf in the latter night prayer. He said: “And I was at the back of the row, until when he mentioned Yusuf, I heard his sobbing, while I was at the back of the rows.”"],[2557,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: If I am between two men, and between each of them there is a gap, should I stick to one of them or stand evenly between them? He said: Stand evenly between them, unless the one between your knees is adjacent, then stick between them. I said: I find rows broken—am I not most entitled to join the one next to me from the congregation of people? He said: “Yes.”"],[2558,"Keep to the right sides of the rows, and beware of the spaces between the pillars, and keep to the first row."],[2559,"It pleases me to pray on the right side of the Prophet ﷺ, because when he said the salam, he would turn his face toward us — or he said — he would begin with us in greeting."],[2560,"He saw al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin praying on the left side of the mosque, because their dwellings were on that side."],[2562,"I said to 'Ata': Is it disliked for a man to stand alone behind the row? He said: \"Yes, and [so are] two men and three, except in the row, for in it there are gaps.\" I said to 'Ata': What do you think if I find the row crowded and I see no gap — should I stand behind them? He said: \"Allah does not burden any soul beyond its capacity. But, by Allah, it is more beloved to me that I enter into it.\""],[2563,"It is said: \"If the row is crowded and there is no entry in it, then let him bring out a man from that row and stand with him. If he does not do so, then that prayer of his is the prayer of one person, not a congregational prayer.\""],[2564,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying alone behind the row, so he ordered him, and he repeated the prayer."],[2565,"Regarding a man who finds the row complete, he said: \"He should move a man back. If he does not, his prayer is not valid.\""],[2566,"I asked al-Hakam b. 'Utaybah and Hammad about that. Al-Hakam said: \"He repeats it,\" and Hammad said: \"He does not repeat it.\""],[2567,"If he stands parallel to the imam, he does not repeat."],[2568,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the morning prayer in Makkah. He began Surat al-Mu'minun until, when he came to the mention of Musa and Harun, or the mention of 'Isa—Ibn 'Abbad was uncertain, or they disagreed about it—the Prophet ﷺ coughed, so he bowed."],[2569,"Do not form rows between the pillars, and do not follow people while they are talking."],[2570,"Do not form rows between the pillars, and do not pray while before you there are people disputing — or he said: engaging in idle talk."],[2571,"I was with Anas b. Malik, and we stood between the pillars, so we moved back. When we had prayed, Anas said: “We used to avoid this in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ.”"],[2572,"He disliked the row between the pillars."],[2573,"I was forbidden to pray behind those sleeping and those conversing."],[2574,"I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah and heard him say the takbir when he began, when he bowed, when he lowered himself for prostration, then when he raised his head, then when he lowered his head, then when he lowered his head to prostrate the second time, then when he raised his head, then when he stood upright after the two rak‘ahs. He said to me: “Such is the takbir in every prayer.”"],[2577,"Abu Hurayrah used to say the takbir with us: he would say it when he stood, when he bowed, when he intended to prostrate, after finishing the prostration, when he sat, and when he intended to stand for the two rak‘ahs he would say the takbir. And he would say the takbir likewise in the other two rak‘ahs. When he said the salam, he said: “By the One in whose hand is my soul, I am the closest of you in resemblance to the Messenger of God ﷺ — meaning in prayer. This remained his prayer until he departed from this world.”"],[2578,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ stood for prayer, he would say the takbir when he stood, and he would say the takbir when he bowed. Then he would say: “God hears the one who praises Him” when he raised his back from the bowing. Then he would say while standing: “Our Lord, to You belongs praise.” Then he would say the takbir when he went down to prostrate, then he would say the takbir when he raised his head. Then he would do that in all the prayers until he completed them, and he would say the takbir when he stood from the two rak‘ahs after sitting. Then Abu Hurayrah would say: “I am the one among you whose prayer most resembles that of the Messenger of God ﷺ.”"],[2579,"He used to say the takbir every time he lowered and raised, and that remained his prayer until he met God."],[2580,"‘Imran b. Husayn and I prayed in Kufa behind ‘Ali b. Abi Talib, who said this takbir when he bowed and when he prostrated, saying all of it. When we finished, ‘Imran said to me: “I have not prayed for a while — or since such and such — a prayer more resembling the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ than this prayer.”"],[2581,"He said to his people: “Gather so that I may lead you in the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ.” When they gathered, he said: “Is there anyone among you who is not one of you?” They said: “No, except a nephew of ours.” He said: “A people’s nephew is one of them.” Then he called for a bowl containing water, washed his hands, rinsed his mouth, snuffed water into his nose, washed his face three times, and his forearms three times each, wiped his head, and washed his feet. Then he led them in the noon prayer, saying in the two rak‘ahs twenty-two takbirs: he said the takbir when he prostrated and when he raised his head from prostration, and he recited in the first two rak‘ahs the Opening of the Book, letting those next to him hear."],[2582,"Ibn Mas'ud used to say the takbir whenever he lowered and raised."],[2584,"Whoever prays a rak'ah and does not recite in it the Mother of the Qur'an has not prayed, except with the imam."],[2585,"He used to say the takbir every time he lowered and raised, and that remained his prayer until he met God."],[2586,"Ibn 'Umar used to say the takbir in prayer when he began, when he bowed, when he leaned forward to prostrate before placing his head, when he raised from the prostration, then when he placed again to prostrate before putting his face, when he raised his head from the prostration, then when he stood upright after the second."],[2588,"A man came to Ibn 'Abbas and said: I prayed with so-and-so and he said twenty-two takbirs, as if he intended to criticize him. Ibn 'Abbas said: \"Woe to you! That is the sunnah of Abu al-Qasim.\""],[2590,"'Adi b. Artah ordered al-Hasan to lead the people in prayer: \"He said this takbir when lowering and when raising, and the people made mistakes, so he said with them the takbir of the imams of that day.\""],[2591,"I heard al-Hasan, and a man said to him: O Abu Sa'id, we have an imam who says the takbir in prayer when he raises and when he lowers. Al-Hasan said: \"By the One besides whom there is no god, it is indeed the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\""],[2592,"We discussed the addition of this takbir in prayer. Abu al-Sha'tha' said: \"I prayed behind Ibn 'Abbas and did not hear him say it.\""],[2594,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz said to me: Are 'Umar and Ibn 'Umar just in your view? He said: I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"They did not say this takbir.\""],[2595,"Qasim b. Muhammad led us in Maghrib prayer without saying this takbir when rising and when prostrating. When he finished, I said to him: 'Nafi' told me that he prayed behind Abu Hurayrah and he said the takbir when rising and when prostrating.' He became angry and said: 'May you be without a father! Do you think I am obliged to do everything Abu Hurayrah used to do? Why did you not ask him whether 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar used to do it?' So I asked Nafi', and he said: 'No one who understands the prayer abandoned it.'"],[2596,"'Umar b. al-Khattab led them and did not say this takbir."],[2597,"I said to 'Ata': 'Should we say the takbir in voluntary prayer just as we say it in the obligatory prayer?' He said: 'Yes, make the voluntary like the obligatory if you are able in all of that. It is only something by which you seek the face of God and the Hereafter.'"],[2598,"The essence of the prayer is that a man says the takbir with his hands and his face and in it, and he raises his head a little when he begins, and when he bows, and when he raises his head."],[2599,"I said to Nafi': 'Did you ever see 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar, when he said the takbir in prayer, raise his head and his face toward the sky?' He said: 'Yes, a little.'"],[2600,"Ibn 'Umar, when he stood for prayer, raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders, and when he bowed he raised them, and when he raised his head from the rak'ah he raised them, and when he stood from the second rak'ah he raised them, and he did not do that in prostration. He then informed them that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do it."],[2601,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ stood for prayer, he raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders, then said the takbir. When he wanted to bow, he did the same. When he raised from bowing, he did the same, and he did not do it when he raised his head from prostration."],[2603,"Ibn 'Umar used to say the takbir with his hands when he began the prayer, when he bowed, when he said 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah', when he raised his head from the rak'ah, and when he stood upright from the second rak'ah. He did not say the takbir with his hands when he raised his head from the two prostrations. I said to Nafi': 'Did Ibn 'Umar make the first of them the highest?' He said: 'No, all the same.' I said: 'Did he bring any of them past his ears?' He said: 'No, nor did it reach his face.' He then gestured to me toward the chest or lower than that."],[2604,"He used to raise his hands when he bowed and when he raised his head from bowing until they were level with his ears."],[2605,"I watched the Prophet ﷺ and he raised his hands in prayer when he said the takbir, then when he said the takbir he raised his hands, then when he said 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah' he raised them. Then he sat and spread his left leg, placed his left hand on his left knee and his right forearm on his right thigh, then he pointed with his index finger, placed his thumb on his middle finger making a circle with it, and closed the rest of his fingers. Then he prostrated and his hands were level with his ears."],[2606,"I saw Ibn 'Abbas when he began the prayer raise his hands, and when he bowed, and when he raised his head from bowing."],[2607,"I saw Wahb b. Munabbih, when he pronounced the takbir in prayer, raise his hands until they were level with his ears, and also when he bowed, and when he raised his head from bowing."],[2608,"I heard Tawus, when he was asked about raising the hands in prayer, say: \"I saw Abd Allah, and Abd Allah, and Abd Allah raising their hands in prayer.\""],[2609,"The first takbir, which is for opening the prayer, involves raising the hands higher than any other takbir. He said: until they pass behind the head. Ibn Jurayj said: I myself saw Ibn Tawus pass his hands behind his head."],[2610,"I said to Ata: I have seen you pronounce the takbir with your hands when you open the prayer, when you bow, when you raise your head from the first prostration and from the last, and when you straighten up from the second unit. He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Has it reached you that the takbir for opening the prayer with the hands is greater than the others? He said: \"No.\" I said: Does one pass the hands behind the ears? He said: \"No.\" He said: \"It has reached me from Uthman that he used to pass his hands behind his ears.\""],[2611,"I heard Abd Allah b. Ubayd b. Umayr mention that from Uthman. [Hidden text: I said to Ata: I have seen you pronounce the takbir with your hands when you open the prayer, when you bow, when you raise your head from the first prostration and from the last, and when you straighten up from the second unit. He said: Yes. I said: Has it reached you that the takbir for opening the prayer with the hands is greater than the others? He said: No. I said: Does one pass the hands behind the ears? He said: No. He said: It has reached me from Uthman that he used to pass his hands behind his ears.]"],[2612,"I said to Ata: And in voluntary prayer, are the hands raised in the same way as in the obligatory prayer? He said: \"Yes, in every prayer.\""],[2613,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced the takbir, he raised his hands until his thumb was seen close to his ears."],[2614,"2552 2531 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyayna, from Yazid, from Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla, from al-Bara b. Azib, the like of it, and he added: \"Once only; then do not repeat raising them in that prayer.\" [Hidden text: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced the takbir, he raised his hands until his thumb was seen close to his ears. Al-Bara b. Azib said: Once only; then do not repeat raising them in that prayer.]"],[2616,"He used to raise his hands at the beginning, then not raise them afterwards."],[2617,"I heard ʿUbayd Allāh b. ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUtbah reciting in the zuhr and ʿasr prayers along with the imam. I asked Ibrāhīm, and he said: Do not recite unless the imam falters. And I asked Mujāhid, and he said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr reciting."],[2619,"I said to Ata: What do you think if I forget to say the takbir with my hands in some of that—do I repeat the prayer? He said: \"No.\""],[2620,"I asked Hammad about a man who forgot the opening takbir. He said: \"He repeats his prayer.\""],[2622,"The key to prayer is purification; its consecration is the takbir, and its release is the taslim."],[2623,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to begin his prayer with the takbir and begin his recitation with 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin.'"],[2624,"About a man who forgets the opening takbir of the prayer, they both said: \"He does not repeat—he has already said takbir when he bowed and when he prostrated.\""],[2625,"The takbir of the rak'ah suffices for him."],[2626,"A man said to Ata: I forgot the takbir—should I repeat? He said: \"No. You say takbir when you sit and in between. You only repeat if you forget a rak'ah or a prostration.\""],[2627,"I said to Ata: What do you think if I forget some of the takbir, that I should utter it with my mouth? He said: \"Do not repeat, and do not prostrate the prostration of forgetfulness—you will say takbir.\""],[2628,"I said to Ibn al-Musayyib: I prostrate on Friday, and Satan says to me: You did not say the opening takbir. Ibn al-Musayyib said: \"You said takbir before and after.\""],[2629,"If you are aligned in the row and you did not say the takbir until the imam bows and raises his head from the rak'ah, then bow and count it. But if you were not aligned in the row, do not count it."],[2630,"If a man forgets to say the takbir in prayer, and then says: \"Glory be to God,\" then opening with the remembrance of God suffices him."],[2631,"If a man says the takbir before the imam, he should repeat the takbir, and if he does not repeat it until he finishes the prayer, he should repeat the prayer."],[2632,"I said to him: Suppose it seemed to me that the imam had already said the opening takbir, so I said the takbir, and then he said the takbir afterwards? He said: \"Say the takbir with him.\""],[2633,"I asked Ibrahim: When does the imam say the takbir? When the muezzin finishes or before he finishes? He said: \"Whichever you do, there is no harm.\""],[2635,"He said the takbir once when the muezzin said: The prayer has been established."],[2636,"I said to Ibrahim: When the muezzin says 'the prayer has been established,' should I say the takbir where I am, or when he finishes? He said: \"Whichever you wish.\""],[2637,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at night and began his prayer, he said the takbir, then said: \"Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god other than You.\" Then he said tahlil three times and takbir three times, then said: \"I seek refuge in Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from Satan the accursed.\""],[2638,"Umar used to teach the people that when a man stands for prayer, he should say before the recitation: \"Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty; there is no god other than You.\""],[2639,"He would begin the prayer with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim'."],[2640,"He used to say amin quietly."],[2641,"They used to say when they began the prayer: \"Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god other than You.\""],[2642,"A man came while the people were in prayer, and when he reached the row he said: \"Allah is greater, greatly; praise be to Allah, abundantly; and glory be to Allah morning and evening.\" When the Prophet ﷺ finished the prayer, he said: \"Who is the one who said those words?\" The man replied: \"I, O Messenger of Allah. By Allah, I intended nothing but good by them.\" He said: \"I saw the gates of heaven opened for them.\" Ibn Umar said: \"I have not abandoned them since I heard them.\""],[2643,"He saw Ibn Umar and prayed beside him, and he said: \"Allah is greater, Allah is greater, greatly; praise be to Allah, abundantly; glory be to Allah morning and evening; O Allah, make You the most beloved thing to me and the best thing in my sight.\""],[2644,"A man entered while the Prophet ﷺ was in his prayer, out of breath. He said upon entering: 'Praise be to Allah, much, blessed, and good.' When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had finished his prayer, he said: 'Who is the owner of these words?' twice. A man said: 'I, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'I saw twelve angels racing to be the one to ascend with it, so as to greet Allah, blessed and exalted.' He said: The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Why do I hear your breathing?' He said: 'The prayer was called, so I hurried.' He said: 'When you hear the iqamah, walk at your normal pace. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you miss, make up.'"],[2645,"When one of you rises at night, let him open his prayer with two light rak'ahs."],[2646,"I used to sleep in the room of the Prophet ﷺ and I would hear him when he rose at night to pray saying: 'Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds,' then after a while, he would say: 'Glory be to Allah the Almighty and with His praise,' and after a while. I said to him: 'What is the pause?' He said: 'He would supplicate for a while.'"],[2647,"Ibn Tawus said to me: He did not know his father except that the imam and those behind him would say it."],[2648,"When Ali began the prayer, he would say: 'Allah is greatest. There is no god but You. Glory be to You. I have wronged my soul, so forgive me. None forgives sins but You. At Your service and with Your help, and good is in Your hands, and evil is not attributed to You. The guided one is he whom You guide. I am Your servant before You, and Your servant before You, and from You and to You. There is no refuge and no escape from You except to You. Blessed and exalted are You. Glory be to You, Lord of the House.'"],[2649,"He used not to recite in the last two rakʿahs."],[2650,"Ibrahim said: And Ibn al-Munkadir narrated to me from Ali b. Abi Talib something similar. Hidden matn: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose for the obligatory prayer, he said the takbir and raised his hands level with his shoulders. Then he said: 'I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth, as a hanif' — the verse — 'and the two verses after it, up to the Muslims.' Then he would say: 'You are the King. There is no god but You. Glory be to You. You are my Lord and I am Your servant. I have wronged my soul and I acknowledge my sin, so forgive me all my sins. None forgives sins but You. Guide me to the best of character; none guides to its best but You. Turn away from me its evil; none turns away from me its evil but You. At Your service and with Your help. I am through You and to You. There is no refuge and no escape from You except to You. Blessed and exalted are You. I seek Your forgiveness and turn to You in repentance.'"],[2651,"When he began the prayer, he would say: 'Allah is greatest, greatly. And praise be to Allah, much, good, and blessed therein.' Then he would say: 'My Lord, Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will not call upon any god other than Him. Then we would have uttered an enormity. Allah is greatest, Allah is greatest. I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth...' up to '...and I am of the Muslims.' Then he would say: 'My Lord, Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will not call upon any god other than Him. Then we would have uttered an enormity. Allah is greatest. Praise be to Allah. There is no god but Allah. Glory be to Allah. Blessed is Allah. Exalted is Allah. Whatever Allah wills. There is no might and no power except with Allah. I bear witness that Allah has power over all things, and that Allah, glory be to Him... Glory be to my Lord Most High. Glory be to the King, the Holy, the Mighty, the Wise. My Lord, forgive me. My Lord, have mercy on me. My Lord, I seek refuge with You from the spurs of the devils, and I seek refuge with You, my Lord, from their presence. I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed devil. Allah is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.' He said: He used to say: 'This is the voluntary prayer.'"],[2652,"And often I fall short of that. He said: And I prefer that it be in the obligatory prayer and the voluntary. I said to him: 'But it is disliked for a person to seek forgiveness while standing in the obligatory prayer.' He said: 'Rather he glorifies and remembers Allah.' He said: 'For I have not yet recited nor prayed yet. This is only before the recitation.' I said: 'Could I be supplicating against a person at that time, naming him?' He said: 'No. I arose only for my own need. As for anything else, no.' Then a person said to him: 'Would you mind if I spoke at that time, after the takbir and before the recitation?' He said: 'By my life, after I say the takbir? There is no speech at that time, after the takbir and before the recitation.'"],[2653,"I said to Ata': 'What do you think if I do not add more than a single takbir in the obligatory prayer and do not say this saying? Has my prayer been done properly or has it become deficient?' He said: 'No. Then he said: 'What do you think if you had a need from a person, would you not praise him before asking?'"],[2654,"I said to Ata': 'What do you think if I say: I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth... up to: the Muslims?' He said: 'That is something people have innovated.' Ata' said: 'Among those who used to experience this, when one of them prayed the night prayer, he would begin with the takbir, then remember Allah, then ask, then recite, then bow two rak'ahs, then stand and pray or begin his prayer anew.'"],[2655,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up at night, he would say Allahu Akbar three times, and he would extol God three times, and he would declare God's oneness three times. Then he would say: \"O God, I seek refuge with You from Satan, from his prodding, his blowing, and his spitting.\" They said: \"How often you seek refuge from this one!\" He said: \"As for his prodding, it is madness; as for his blowing, it is poetry; and as for his spitting, it is pride.\""],[2656,"When the Prophet ﷺ stood up at night, he would say: \"God is greater, greatly,\" twice, then he would say: \"God is greater, greatly,\" then he would say: \"There is no god but God\" three times. Then he would say: \"O God, I seek refuge with You from Satan, from his blowing, his spitting, and his prodding.\""],[2657,"The Asr used to be made lighter than the Zuhr in recitation."],[2658,"I said to 'Ata': \"Consider if I am overtaken by verses and I recite them to you, should I seek refuge?\" He said: \"No, if you wish. But if you present the Qur'an, and you begin a recitation that you recite in prayer or otherwise, then seek refuge for it.\" I said: \"Consider if I pray two light rak'ahs, should I seek refuge for them?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[2659,"I said to 'Ata': \"Consider if I enter before the prayer and begin, seeking refuge, and recite until the prayer is established. Should I seek refuge for the obligatory prayer as well? Then I finish the obligatory prayer, and afterward I pray again—should I seek refuge also?\" He said: \"The first seeking of refuge suffices you, but if you seek refuge for each one, that is good.\""],[2660,"I asked Nafi', the freedman of Ibn 'Umar, whether he knew how Ibn 'Umar used to seek refuge. He said: \"He used to say: 'O God, I seek refuge with You from Satan the accursed.'\""],[2661,"When he began the prayer, he would say: 'Allah is greatest, greatly. And praise be to Allah, much, good, and blessed therein.' Then he would say: 'My Lord, Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will not call upon any god other than Him. Then we would have uttered an enormity. Allah is greatest, Allah is greatest. I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth...' up to '...and I am of the Muslims.' Then he would say: 'My Lord, Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will not call upon any god other than Him. Then we would have uttered an enormity. Allah is greatest. Praise be to Allah. There is no god but Allah. Glory be to Allah. Blessed is Allah. Exalted is Allah. Whatever Allah wills. There is no might and no power except with Allah. I bear witness that Allah has power over all things, and that Allah, glory be to Him... Glory be to my Lord Most High. Glory be to the King, the Holy, the Mighty, the Wise. My Lord, forgive me. My Lord, have mercy on me. My Lord, I seek refuge with You from the spurs of the devils, and I seek refuge with You, my Lord, from their presence. I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed devil. Allah is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.' He said: He used to say: 'This is the voluntary prayer.'"],[2662,"Abu Dharr stood up to pray, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"O Abu Dharr, seek refuge with God from the satan of humans and jinn.\""],[2663,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up at night, he would say Allahu Akbar three times, and he would extol God three times, and he would declare God's oneness three times. Then he would say: \"O God, I seek refuge with You from Satan, from his prodding, his blowing, and his spitting.\" They said: \"How often you seek refuge from this one!\" He said: \"As for his prodding, it is madness; as for his blowing, it is poetry; and as for his spitting, it is pride.\""],[2664,"His prodding is al-mu'tah, meaning madness; his blowing is pride; and his spitting is poetry."],[2665,"I said: \"O Messenger of God, Satan came between me and my recitation.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"That is a satan called Khinzab. When you sense him, seek refuge with God and spit to your left three times.\""],[2666,"I said to 'Ata': \"What about the verse 'And say: My Lord, I seek refuge with You from the proddings of the satans'?\" He said: \"That is a statement from the Qur'an, not obligatory in prayer.\""],[2667,"I said to 'Ata': “A man forgets the opening takbir of the prayer, so I sought refuge with two rak'ahs, then another, then another, so I seek refuge for every prayer up to the seven?” He said: “The first suffices you, but if you also seek refuge, that is good.” I said: “I prayed, and while I was praying a person came to me for a need, so I turned to him and fulfilled his need, then I stood to pray another time.” He said: “The first suffices you, but if you also seek refuge, that is good.”"],[2668,"The seeking of refuge in everything suffices you, and if you add more, there is no harm."],[2669,"The seeking of refuge at the beginning of anything suffices you."],[2670,"He used to seek refuge once at the beginning of his prayer."],[2671,"He used to seek refuge before reciting Umm al-Qur'an."],[2672,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at night and began his prayer, he said the takbir, then said: \"Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god other than You.\" Then he said tahlil three times and takbir three times, then said: \"I seek refuge in Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from Satan the accursed.\""],[2673,"He used to seek refuge from Shaytan in the prayer before reciting Umm al-Qur'an, and after reciting Umm al-Qur'an."],[2674,"Al-Hasan used to seek refuge once when he opened his prayer before reciting the Fatihah of the Book: “I seek refuge in Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from the accursed Shaytan.”"],[2675,"The Asr used to be made lighter than the Zuhr in recitation."],[2676,"He used to seek refuge after the Fatihah of the Book."],[2677,"The companions of Ibn Mas'ud used to seek refuge after the Fatihah of the Book."],[2678,"I said to 'Ata': “I forgot the seeking of refuge.” He said: “I do not repeat, nor do I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness; I will seek refuge later.” I said: “We have been commanded to seek refuge just as we have been commanded to perform ablution?” He said: “It is not like ablution. It is speech; I will say it when I remember in my prayer.” I said: “I did not remember until I finished.” He said: “That is fine; when I finish, I seek refuge.”"],[2679,"Four things the imam keeps silent: Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim, the seeking of refuge, Amin, and when he says 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah,' he says 'Rabbana laka al-hamd.'"],[2680,"Five things are kept silent: 'Subhanaka Allahumma wa bihamdika,' the seeking of refuge, 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim,' 'Amin,' and 'Allahumma Rabbana laka al-hamd.'"],[2681,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman reciting 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin.'"],[2682,"The Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and 'Umar used to begin with 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin.' He said: I said: 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim.' He said: 'After it,' he says: 'After it,' he says: 'Keep it silent.'"],[2683,"I recited 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim,' and my father said to me: 'Beware of introducing something new, O my son, for I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, 'Umar, and 'Uthman, and they used to recite 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin.'"],[2684,"He did not say 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' out loud; he said 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin' out loud."],[2685,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to begin his prayer with the takbir and begin his recitation with 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin.'"],[2687,"He prayed behind 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz and I heard him begin the recitation with 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin.'"],[2689,"I asked him about 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim'—should I say it out loud? He said: 'The Sunnah is 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbil-'alamin,' and if it were a matter of opinion, 'Al-hamdu lillah' is better than 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim.'"],[2690,"Saying 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' out loud is the recitation of the Bedouins."],[2691,"It suffices you to say 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' at the first thing, and the seeking of refuge at the first thing."],[2692,"He used to begin with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim.'"],[2693,"He would not omit 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim'; he would begin the recitation with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim'."],[2694,"\"And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated [verses]\" — meaning the Mother of the Qur'an — \"and I recited it to Sa'id just as I recited it to you.\" Then he said: \"Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim\" is the seventh verse. Ibn 'Abbas said: \"God has brought it out for you, and He did not bring it out for anyone before you.\""],[2696,"He would begin with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' in the prayer."],[2697,"He would begin with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' and say: \"It is a verse from the Book of God Most High that people have abandoned.\""],[2699,"He would recite 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' aloud in every rak'ah."],[2701,"He said to 'Ata': If I forget it in an obligatory prayer, should I repeat the prayer or prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: \"Yes, by my life, we do omit from the Qur'an and then we make it frequent.\" A man said to him: And Bara'ah? He said: \"Yes, it and al-Anfal are one, and that I do not fail to recite it: Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim.\""],[2702,"The believers in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ did not know the end of a surah until 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' came down. When 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' came down, they knew that a surah had come down and the previous one had ended."],[2703,"Mu'awiyah led the people in the evening prayer in Madinah, and he did not recite 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim,' nor did he say some of the takbirs that the people say. When he finished, those of the Muhajirun and Ansar who heard that called out to him and said: \"O Mu'awiyah, did you steal the prayer or forget? Where is 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' and 'Allahu akbar' until you go down in prostration?\" Mu'awiyah never did that again."],[2705,"That Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar used to begin with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim'."],[2706,"What have you heard about reciting 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim'? He said: Abu Wa'il informed me that he heard Umar b. al-Khattab begin with 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabb il-alamin' (Praise be to Allah, Lord of the Worlds)."],[2707,"I said to Ata': Is reciting the Umm al-Qur'an obligatory? He said: As for me, I do not omit it, in the prescribed prayer or the supererogatory, the Fatihah of the Qur'an. He said: As for me, I heard Abu Hurayrah say: When one of you recites the Umm al-Qur'an, if he stops at it, it suffices him; and if he adds to it, it is better."],[2708,"There is no prayer for one who does not recite the Umm al-Qur'an or more."],[2709,"He heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri recite the Umm al-Qur'an in every rak'ah—or he said: in every prayer."],[2711,"I would be ashamed before the Lord of this little structure to pray a prayer in which I do not recite the Umm al-Qur'an and something along with it."],[2713,"Do not pray any prayer until you recite the Fatihah of the Book in every rak'ah."],[2714,"I said to Ata': Does it suffice for me in every rak'ah to recite 'Inna a'taynaka al-kawthar' (Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar) without the Umm al-Qur'an in the prescribed prayer? He said: No, nor Surah al-Baqarah. He said: 'Wa laqad ataynaka sab'an mina al-mathani' (And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated verses). He said: It is the seven. I said: Where is the seventh? He said: 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim', and he regards the Umm al-Qur'an as obligatory in every rak'ah."],[2715,"About a man who recites the Qur'an in all of his prayer but does not recite the Umm al-Qur'an—or he said: the Fatihah of the Book—he said: He does not have to repeat it; he has recited Qur'an."],[2716,"I said to Ata: What do you think—if I were to begin with Surat Maryam, recite the Mother of the Qur'an, then reach the prostration and prostrate, and then stand and recite the Mother of the Qur'an again as well? He said: \"No, you are still in the same rak'ah until now, so do not recite in it if you wish.\""],[2717,"When the Messenger of God said: [Not the path of those who have incurred wrath, nor of those who are astray], he would say: \"Amin,\" so that those next to him could hear him."],[2718,"When the Messenger of God said: [Not the path of those who have incurred wrath, nor of those who are astray], he would say: \"Amin,\" so that those next to him could hear him."],[2719,"I prayed with Abu Hurayrah. When he said: [Not the path of those who have incurred wrath, nor of those who are astray], he would say: \"Amin,\" so that we could hear him, and those behind him would say amin.\" He said: \"And he would pronounce the takbir with us—this takbir—when he bowed and when he prostrated.\""],[2721,"Bilal said to the Prophet ﷺ: Do not get ahead of me with amin."],[2722,"He was the mu'adhdhin of al-Ala b. al-Hadrami in Bahrain, and he stipulated with him that he would not get ahead of him with amin."],[2723,"He was the mu'adhdhin of al-Ala b. al-Hadrami, and Abu Hurayrah said to him: You must let me say amin, or I will not call the adhan for you."],[2724,"Abu Hurayrah entered the mosque while the imam was [leading prayer], and Abu Hurayrah called out to him: Do not get ahead of me with amin."],[2725,"The Asr used to be made lighter than the Zuhr in recitation."],[2726,"When he finished the Mother of the Qur'an, he would say: \"Amin.\" He would not fail to say amin when he finished it, and he urged them to say it. He said: And I heard from him a report about that."],[2728,"I said to 'Ata': 'Amin?' He said: 'I never abandon it.' He said: 'Immediately after the Umm al-Qur'an in the prescribed and supererogatory prayers?' He said: 'And I used to hear the imams saying Amin immediately after the Umm al-Qur'an, they themselves and those behind them, until the mosque had a loud murmur.'"],[2729,"When the imam says: 'Not the path of those who have incurred wrath, nor of those who have gone astray,' then say: Amin, for the angels say Amin, and the imam says Amin. Whoever's Amin coincides with the Amin of the angels, his previous sins are forgiven."],[2731,"When the Amin on earth coincides with the Amin in heaven, his previous sins are forgiven."],[2732,"When the imam says: 'Not the path of those who have incurred wrath, nor of those who have gone astray,' then say: Amin; God will answer you."],[2733,"The rows of the people of earth are aligned with the rows of the people of heaven. When an Amin on earth coincides with an Amin in heaven, he is forgiven."],[2734,"The Jews have never envied you over anything as much as they have envied you over Amin and the salam, that you greet one another."],[2737,"'Ata' said to me: 'I marvel at a person who supplicates and makes his supplication a continuous recitation, without saying Amin to his supplication.' He said: 'He should say: Amin.'"],[2738,"I said to 'Ata': 'What do you think when the imam recites the Umm al-Qur'an in the last rak'ah of Maghrib and the last two rak'ahs of Isha' — how should he say Amin?' He said: 'He should say Amin quietly to himself.'"],[2739,"I said to 'Ata': 'I forgot to say Amin.' He said: 'Do not repeat it, and do not perform the prostration of forgetfulness.'"],[2740,"The Asr used to be made lighter than the Zuhr in recitation."],[2741,"Ibn Umar used to recite in the first rak'ah of Zuhr [surah] al-Dhariyat."],[2744,"Recite in the first two rakʿahs the Opening of the Book and a surah, and in the last two glorify."],[2745,"He would not fail to recite in the latter night prayer the small prostration surah, “Alif Lam Mim. The revelation” (32:1-2), and “Blessed is He in whose hand is the dominion” (67:1)."],[2747,"I asked Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh, and he said: \"As for me, I recite in the first two rakʿahs of the noon and afternoon prayers the Opening of the Book and a surah, and in the last two only the Opening of the Book.\""],[2749,"She used to recite in the last two rakʿahs the Opening of the Book."],[2750,"I recite in the first two rakʿahs of the noon, afternoon, and the last evening prayer in every rakʿah the Mother of the Book and a surah, and in the last rakʿah of the sunset prayer the Mother of the Book."],[2751,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Does the Mother of the Book ever suffice me in the prescribed prayer in all four rakʿahs? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Should I add in the noon and afternoon prayers to the Mother of the Book? He said: \"Yes, ﴿Say: He is God, One﴾ and something like that.\" I said: Should I add in the last rakʿah of the sunset prayer and the last two rakʿahs of the evening prayer to the Mother of the Book? He said: \"Yes, ﴿Say: He is God, One﴾ and something like that.\""],[2752,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if I do not recite in the prescribed prayer in the divided portion, and I recite part of a surah from its beginning, middle, or end? He said: \"It does not harm you; all of it is Quran.\""],[2753,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the morning prayer in Makkah. He began Surat al-Mu'minun until, when he came to the mention of Musa and Harun, or the mention of 'Isa—Ibn 'Abbad was uncertain, or they disagreed about it—the Prophet ﷺ coughed, so he bowed."],[2754,"Ibn Masʿūd prayed on a rough haircloth."],[2755,"The Prophet recited in Fajr on Friday Surah al-Rum."],[2756,"Was Ibn 'Umar one who would recite in a rak'ah of an obligatory prayer part of a long surah and then bow? He said, \"No.\""],[2758,"If he does not recite in the [first] two rak'ahs of maghrib, he must repeat."],[2760,"The first of the prayers is longer in recitation."],[2761,"The first of the prayers is longer in recitation."],[2762,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray Zuhr with us, and sometimes he would make us hear a verse. He used to lengthen the first rak'ah of the dawn prayer and lengthen the first rak'ah of the Zuhr prayer. We thought that by that he intended people to catch the first rak'ah."],[2763,"I heard ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr reciting behind the imam in the zuhr and ʿasr prayers."],[2764,"The companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ watched him in Zuhr and estimated his recitation in the first rak'ah of Zuhr to be [of the length of] Tanzil al-Sajdah."],[2765,"The Prophet ﷺ prostrated in the Zuhr prayer, then stood and recited. They think that he recited \"Alif Lam Mim. The revelation...\" [Q 32:1-2], while he was praying with his companions."],[2766,"Ibn Umar used to pray and recite in Zuhr [surah] Qaf and Iqtarabat."],[2767,"Ibn Umar used to pray and recite in Zuhr [surah] Qaf and Iqtarabat."],[2768,"He used to recite in Zuhr [surah] Alladhina Kafaru and in ... [surah] Inna Fatahna Laka."],[2770,"I heard ʿUbayd Allāh b. ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUtbah reciting in the zuhr and ʿasr prayers along with the imam. I asked Ibrāhīm, and he said: Do not recite unless the imam falters. And I asked Mujāhid, and he said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr reciting."],[2771,"I heard ʿUbayd Allāh b. ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUtbah reciting in the zuhr and ʿasr prayers along with the imam. I asked Ibrāhīm, and he said: Do not recite unless the imam falters. And I asked Mujāhid, and he said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr reciting."],[2772,"I liken the daytime prayer to the night prayer, the midday prayer."],[2774,"“As for me, I recite with the imam in Zuhr and Asr the Opening of the Book and a short surah, then I say tahlil and tasbih.” I said, “Should the one beside me hear my recitation?” He said, “With the imam?” I said, “Yes.” He said, “No.”"],[2775,"Anas used to lead us in Zuhr and Asr, and sometimes he would let us hear from his recitation [surah] Idha al-Sama' Infatarat and [surah] Sabbih Isma Rabbika al-A'la."],[2776,"He used to recite in the first two rak'ahs of the Asr prayer [surah] Idha al-Sama' Inshaqqat and [surah] Wa al-Sama' Dhat al-Buruj."],[2777,"We prayed Asr with Ibn Umar, and he recited [surah] al-Mursalat and [surah] 'Amma Yatasa'alun."],[2779,"Why do you recite the short ones of the mufassal in Maghrib? The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the Maghrib prayer the longer of the two long ones. He said: I said: What is the longer of the two long ones? He said: Al-A'raf. He said: I said to Ibn Abi Mulaykah: What are the two long ones? He said: It was as if he said, from his own opinion: Al-An'am and Al-A'raf."],[2780,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting in Maghrib Al-Tur."],[2781,"The Prophet ﷺ recited in Maghrib Al-Tur."],[2782,"The last thing I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite in Maghrib was Surat al-Mursalat."],[2783,"He heard Ibn Umar reciting in Maghrib: Qaf. By the Glorious Quran."],[2784,"He heard Ibn Umar recite in Maghrib: Indeed, We have given you a clear victory."],[2785,"Umar b. al-Khattab led us in the Maghrib prayer, and in the first rak'ah he recited By the fig and the olive and Mount Sinai, and in the last rak'ah he recited Alam tara and Li-ilafi Quraysh both together."],[2786,"He prayed behind Abu Bakr al-Siddiq the Maghrib prayer, and in the first two rak'ahs he recited the Mother of the Quran and two suras from the short ones of the mufassal. Then he stood for the third rak'ah. He said: I drew near to him until my clothes almost touched his clothes, and I heard him reciting the Mother of the Quran and this verse: Our Lord, do not let our hearts deviate after You have guided us, up to al-Wahhab."],[2787,"I prayed behind Abu Bakr the Maghrib prayer where my clothes touched his clothes. When it came to the last rak'ah, he recited the Opening of the Book, then recit ed: Our Lord, do not let our hearts deviate, up to al-Wahhab."],[2788,"He heard Abu Bakr recite it in the third rak'ah, and Makhul said to him: It was not a recitation from Abu Bakr; it was only a supplication from him."],[2790,"Ibn Mas'ud prayed the last 'isha' prayer with us and began Surah al-Anfal until, when he reached, \"Excellent is the protector and excellent is the helper\" [Q 8:40], he bowed. Then in the second [rak'ah] he recited a surah of the mufassal."],[2791,"Ali used to forbid recitation behind the imam."],[2792,"I used to prompt Ibn Umar in prayer, and he would say nothing."],[2793,"Ibn Umar used to recite three surahs in a rak'ah sometimes."],[2794,"I saw Tawus, more times than I can count, reciting in the latter night prayer “Alif Lam Mim. The revelation” of the prostration, and “Blessed,” and he prostrated in it. One night he did not prostrate in it, and I thought that he bowed when he reached the prostration verse. He recited it in two rak'ahs."],[2795,"Tawus used to recite Qul huwa Allahu ahad along with the Mother of the Quran in every rak'ah."],[2796,"The Prophet ﷺ recited in the night prayer, in one of the two rak'ahs, “By the fig and the olive” (95:1) while traveling."],[2797,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the morning prayer in Makkah. He began Surat al-Mu'minun until, when he came to the mention of Musa and Harun, or the mention of 'Isa—Ibn 'Abbad was uncertain, or they disagreed about it—the Prophet ﷺ coughed, so he bowed."],[2798,"Ali led us in the dawn prayer and recited the Prophets. He left out a verse, then recited for a while, then returned to the verse and recited it, then he repeated his recitation, and went back to what he had been reciting."],[2799,"Umar recited Surat Yusuf in the dawn prayer and became confused, so he went back to its beginning, then recited and continued in his recitation."],[2800,"Umar recited in the dawn prayer al-Kahf and Yusuf—or Yusuf and Hud. He said: He became confused in Yusuf, and when he became confused, he went back to the beginning of the surah and recited, then continued through all of it."],[2801,"I prayed the dawn prayer behind Abu Bakr, and he began al-Baqarah and recited it in two rak'ahs. When he finished, Umar stood up and said: May Allah forgive you! The sun had almost risen before you said the salam. He said: If it had risen, it would have found us not heedless."],[2802,"I prayed the dawn prayer behind Abu Bakr, and he began al-Baqarah and recited it in two rak'ahs. When he finished, Umar stood up and said: May Allah forgive you! The sun had almost risen before you said the salam. He said: If it had risen, it would have found us not heedless."],[2805,"I memorized Surat Yusuf and Surat al-Hajj only from Umar, on account of how often he would recite them in the Fajr prayer. He said: \"He would recite them with slow recitation.\""],[2806,"I heard Umar's sobbing while I was in the row behind him in prayer as he was reciting Surat Yusuf, until he reached: \"I only complain of my grief and sorrow to Allah.\""],[2807,"Umar led us in the Fajr prayer, and he did not finish until every mindful person knew that the sun had risen. He said: It was said to him: 'You did not finish until the sun almost rose.' He said: 'Had it risen, it would have found us not heedless.'"],[2808,"Umar b. al-Khattab recited in Fajr Surat Al Imran."],[2809,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting in the first rak'ah of the Fajr prayer: \"And tall palm trees with clustered dates.\""],[2810,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray prayers similar to the prayer you pray today, but he would keep it light. His prayer was lighter than yours. He would recite in Fajr al-Waqi'ah and similar surahs."],[2811,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting in Fajr: \"By the night when it darkens.\""],[2813,"He used to recite in Fajr ten surahs from the beginning of al-Mufassal, one surah in each rak'ah."],[2814,"Umar recited in Fajr Yusuf, then in the second rak'ah he recited al-Najm and prostrated, then he stood and recited: \"When the earth is shaken.\""],[2815,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed the Fajr prayer and recited Surah al-Rum, and he became confused in it. When he finished, he said: \"What is the matter with people who pray with us without purification? Whoever prays with us should perfect his purification, for those are the ones who confuse the Qur'an for us.\""],[2816,"Adi b. Artah ordered al-Hasan to lead the people in prayer. In Fajr he recited: \"O Prophet, when you [plural] divorce women\" and \"O Prophet, why do you prohibit [yourself]\"."],[2817,"Abu Wa'il recited in one of the two rak'ahs of the morning prayer the Mother of the Qur'an and a verse."],[2818,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite on Friday at dawn “The revelation of the Prostration” and “Has there come upon man?” And he used to recite in the Friday prayer Surat al-Jumu'ah and “When the hypocrites come to you.”"],[2821,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite in the Fajr prayer on Friday: \"The revelation of the prostration\" and \"Has there come upon man\"."],[2822,"The Prophet ﷺ recited in the morning prayer: \"Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory.\""],[2823,"I prayed with Umar at Dhu al-Hulayfah while he was heading to Makkah the Fajr prayer, and he recited: \"Say, O disbelievers\" and \"The One, the Eternal.\""],[2824,"I was with Umar between Makkah and Madinah, and he led us in Fajr. He recited: \"Have you not seen how your Lord dealt\" and \"For the security of Quraysh.\" Then he saw some people stopping and praying in a mosque. He asked about them, and they said: \"A mosque where the Prophet ﷺ prayed.\" He said: \"Those before you were destroyed only because they took the relics of their prophets as places of worship. Whoever passes by any mosque and the prayer time comes should pray; otherwise, let him move on.\""],[2825,"I accompanied Umar b. al-Khattab on a journey, and he recited: \"Say, O disbelievers\" and \"Say, He is God, the One.\""],[2826,"I prayed with Umar in the year he was killed, in Makkah, the morning prayer, and he recited: \"I swear by this land\" and \"By the fig and the olive.\""],[2827,"He used to recite in the morning prayer while traveling Surat Sabbih and \"Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming?\" and the like of them."],[2828,"Ibrahim al-Nakha'i led them in prayer while traveling, and he recited in the dawn prayer \"When the earth is shaken\" and \"Indeed, We sent it down during the Night of Decree.\""],[2829,"I was with Anas b. Malik, and he was returning from his land heading to al-Basrah, and between it and al-Basrah were three miles—or three parasangs. The dawn prayer became due, so a son of his called Abu Bakr stood up and led us in prayer, and he recited Surat Tabarak. When he said the salam, Anas said to him: \"You made it long for us.\""],[2830,"On the day Umar was killed, I prayed the morning prayer, and nothing prevented me from standing in the first row except awe of Umar. He said: The people surged, so they put forward Abd al-Rahman b. Awf, and he recited \"When the victory of God and the conquest comes\" and \"Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar.\""],[2831,"They used to recite in the dawn prayer while traveling \"When the sky is split open\" and \"Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming?\""],[2834,"Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite the Mother of the Qur'an, it is deficient, it is deficient, it is deficient: not complete."],[2835,"Whoever prays a rak'ah and does not recite in it the Mother of the Qur'an has not prayed, except with the imam."],[2836,"The Prophet ﷺ used to lead us in prayer, and he would recite aloud and quietly, so we would recite aloud in what he recited aloud, and quietly in what he recited quietly. I heard him say: There is no prayer except with recitation."],[2837,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I am unable to learn the Qur'an, so what will suffice me? He said: \"You say: Glory be to God, praise be to God, there is no might and no power except with God, there is no god but God, and God is greatest.\" He said: The man said: Like this—and he gathered his five fingers—so he said: \"This is for God.\" He said: \"You say: O God, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, and provide for me.\" He said: The man clasped both his palms, and the Prophet ﷺ said: As for this one, he has filled his hands with good."],[2839,"A man came to him and said: \"I prayed but did not recite.\" He said: \"Did you complete the bowing and the prostrations?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then your prayer is complete.\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Not everyone excels at recitation.\""],[2840,"A Muslim man must learn six surahs for prayer: two surahs for the dawn prayer, two for the sunset prayer, and two for the night prayer."],[2841,"I prayed the sunset prayer behind Umar b. al-Khattab, and he did not recite anything in the first rak'ah. Then in the second rak'ah he recited the Opening of the Book twice and two surahs. Then he prostrated twice before the salutation."],[2842,"Umar b. al-Khattab prayed the late night prayer at al-Jabiyah and did not recite during it until he finished. When he finished, he entered, and Abd al-Rahman b. Awf went around to him and cleared his throat until Umar b. al-Khattab heard his sound and knew he had a need. He said: \"Who is this?\" He said: \"Abd al-Rahman b. Awf.\" He said: \"Do you have a need?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then enter.\" So he entered and said: \"Do you think that what you did just now—did the Messenger of God ﷺ enjoin it upon you, or did you see him do it?\" He said: \"And what is that?\" He said: \"You did not recite during the night prayer.\" He said: \"Did I do that?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"I was distracted. I prepared a caravan from Syria until it arrived in Madinah.\" He said: \"Who is the muezzin?\" So he performed the iqamah for prayer. Then he returned and prayed the night prayer for the people. When he finished, he gave a sermon and said: \"There is no prayer for one who does not recite in it. As for what I did just now, I was distracted. I prepared a caravan from Syria until it arrived in Madinah, and I distributed it.\""],[2843,"Umar b. al-Khattab prayed the night prayer with us, and I did not hear him recite in it. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari said to him: \"What is the matter with you, O Commander of the Faithful, that you did not recite?\" He said: \"Is it so, O Abd al-Rahman b. Awf?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then he ordered the muezzin and he performed the iqamah, and he recited a recitation that I heard while I was at the back of the rows. When he finished, he said: \"I was praying and talking to myself about a caravan I had sent from Madinah with its saddles and saddlecloths—when would it arrive? And indeed, there is no prayer except with recitation.\""],[2844,"Umar prayed the sunset prayer and did not recite. So he ordered the muezzin, and he repeated the adhan and the iqamah, then he repeated the prayer."],[2845,"Umar prayed the night prayer with the people, and I did not hear him recite in it. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari said to him: \"What is the matter with you, O Commander of the Faithful, that you did not recite?\" He said: \"Is it so, O Abd al-Rahman b. Awf?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Did I do that?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"You have spoken the truth.\" He said: \"I prepared a caravan from Madinah until it reached Syria, and I was moving it stage by stage.\" Then he repeated the prayer for them."],[2847,"I asked Alqamah. I said: \"I forgot in the first two rak'ahs, then I recited in the last two rak'ahs. Will it suffice for me in my prayer?\" He said: \"Yes, God willing.\""],[2848,"He used to dislike that one raise the head excessively or lower it in bowing."],[2853,"I said to 'Ata': \"If I forget the recitation in a rak'ah of Umm al-Qur'an and the surah after it, and I do not recite anything in the rak'ah?\" He said: \"Then you do not need to repeat, but prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.\""],[2855,"If he does not recite in a rak'ah until he bows, then he raises his head when he remembers and recites, then prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness. If he prostrated, he should continue."],[2856,"\"Do you recite behind me while I am reciting?\" He said: They remained silent until he asked them three times. They said: \"Yes, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Then do not do that, unless one of you recites the Opening of the Book silently to himself.\""],[2857,"\"Perhaps you recite while the imam is reciting?\" Twice or three times. They said: \"Yes, O Messenger of God, we do.\" He said: \"Then do not do so, except that one of you recites the Opening of the Book.\""],[2858,"\"Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite the Mother of the Qur'an, it is deficient, it is deficient, not complete.\" Abu al-Sa'ib said: \"I am sometimes behind the imam.\" Abu al-Sa'ib said: Abu Hurayrah nudged my arm and said: \"O bedouin, recite it to yourself, for I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'God said: I have divided the prayer between Me and My servant into two halves; half is for Me and half is for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks for.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Recite: the servant stands and says: 'Praise be to God, Lord of the worlds,' and God says: My servant has praised Me. The servant says: 'The Compassionate, the Merciful,' and God says: My servant has extolled Me. The servant says: 'Master of the Day of Judgment,' and God says: My servant has glorified Me. And He said: This is between Me and My servant. The servant says: 'You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help,' and God says: Its reward is for My servant, and he shall have what he asks for. My servant says: 'Guide us to the straight path,' to the end of the surah, and God says: This is for My servant, and he shall have what he asks for.'\""],[2859,"\"Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite the Mother of the Qur'an, it is deficient, it is deficient, not complete.\" He said: I said to him: \"O Abu Hurayrah, I am sometimes behind the imam.\" He said: He nudged my arm and then said: \"Recite it, O Persian, to yourself. For I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'God said: I have divided the prayer between Me and My servant into two halves; half is for Me and half is for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks for.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Recite: the servant stands and says: 'Praise be to God, Lord of the worlds,' and God, Blessed and Exalted, says: My servant has praised Me. The servant says: 'The Compassionate, the Merciful,' and God says: My servant has extolled Me. The servant says: 'Master of the Day of Judgment,' and God says: My servant has glorified Me. He said: This verse is between Me and My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks for. The servant says: 'You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help,' to the end of the surah, and these are for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks for.'\""],[2860,"He used to recite the Opening of the Book in prayers in which the imam recites aloud and in those in which he does not recite aloud."],[2861,"\"Recite the Mother of the Qur'an in every prayer\"—or he said: \"in every rak'ah.\" He said: I said: \"Do you recite it, O Abu al-Walid, with the imam?\" He said: \"I do not omit it, whether as imam or as one led in prayer.\""],[2862,"I prayed beside 'Ubadah b. al-Samit, and I heard him reciting behind the imam. When we finished our prayer, we said: \"O Abu al-Walid, do you recite with the imam?\" He said: \"Woe to you, for there is no prayer except with it.\""],[2863,"Ubayy b. Kaʿb used to recite behind the imam in the zuhr and ʿasr prayers."],[2864,"It is necessary to recite the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb behind the imam, whether he recites aloud or not."],[2866,"I heard ʿUbayd Allāh b. ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUtbah reciting in the zuhr and ʿasr prayers along with the imam. I asked Ibrāhīm, and he said: Do not recite unless the imam falters. And I asked Mujāhid, and he said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr reciting."],[2868,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb commanded us to recite behind the imam."],[2869,"If the imam does not make you hear, then recite."],[2870,"It is a Sunnah: when you raise your head from the rak'ah or the sajdah, stand upright until every bone returns to its joint. If you do that, it suffices you. And it was said—I do not know whether the Prophet ﷺ said it—after he raised his head from the rak'ah and stood upright: \"Say: O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling whatever You wish after that. There is no one who can withhold what You give, and no one can give what You withhold, and no one's fortune can avail him against You.\""],[2871,"ʿUthmān said: The silent listener who does not hear has a share like that of the attentive listener."],[2872,"Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Muṣʿab, from Muḥammad b. ʿUthmān [1], except that he said: \"of the reward.\" [The hidden matn:] The silent listener who does not hear has a share like that of the attentive listener: of the reward. [1] Thus in the al-Maktab al-Islāmī edition; perhaps there is a scribal error, and the correct reading is: Muḥammad (from) ʿUthmān."],[2873,"Straighten the rows, align the shoulders, and listen silently, for the reward of the silent listener who does not hear is like the reward of the silent listener who hears."],[2874,"The imam recites the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb and another surah in the zuhr and ʿasr prayers in the first two rakʿahs."],[2875,"He raises his head from prostration until every part settles into place."],[2876,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, he would spread his arms so that the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."],[2877,"“As for me, I recite with the imam in Zuhr and Asr the Opening of the Book and a short surah, then I say tahlil and tasbih.” I said, “Should the one beside me hear my recitation?” He said, “With the imam?” I said, “Yes.” He said, “No.”"],[2878,"“Whoever prays an obligatory prayer or a voluntary prayer should recite the Opening of the Book and Quran with it. If he reaches the Opening of the Book, it suffices for him. Whoever is with the imam should recite before him or when he is silent. Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite, it is deficient” three times."],[2879,"When the imam recites aloud, he should hasten with the Opening of the Book, or he may recite after he falls silent. When he recites, they should listen attentively, as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has said."],[2880,"It is necessary to recite the Opening of the Book with the imam, but those who came before, when the imam said the takbir, he was silent for a while, not reciting, long enough for them to recite the Opening of the Book."],[2881,"Recite the Opening of the Book whether the imam recites aloud or not. When he recites aloud and finishes the Opening of the Book, then you recite it yourself."],[2883,"Samurah b. Jundub used to lead the people in prayer and he would pause twice: when he said the takbir for the prayer, and when he finished reciting the Opening of the Book. The people criticized him for that, so he wrote to Ubayy b. Ka'b about it, saying that the people had criticized him, and “I forgot and they remembered, or I remembered and they forgot.” Ubayy wrote back to him: “Rather, you remembered and they forgot.” Al-Hasan used to say: “When the imam finishes reciting the Opening of the Book, then you recite it yourself.”"],[2886,"It is necessary to recite the Opening of the Book with the imam, but those who came before, when the imam said the takbir, he was silent for a while, not reciting, long enough for them to recite the Opening of the Book."],[2887,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer in which he recited aloud, then after he had said the salam he turned to the people and said to them: “Did any of you recite with me just now?” They said, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “I say: why am I being contended with in the Quran?” So the people refrained from reciting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in what he recited aloud when they heard that from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[2888,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer in which he recited aloud, then after he had said the salam he turned to the people and said to them: “Did any of you recite with me just now?” They said, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “I say: why am I being contended with in the Quran?” So the people refrained from reciting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in what he recited aloud when they heard that from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[2889,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr or Asr, and a man began reciting behind the Prophet ﷺ while another man was forbidding him. When he finished, he said: \"O Messenger of God, I was reciting while this man was forbidding me.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said to him: \"Whoever has an imam, the imam's recitation is a recitation for him.\""],[2890,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr with his companions. When he finished, he said: \"Did any of you recite 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High'?\" A man said: \"I recited it.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"I said: Why am I being contested over it?\""],[2891,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed Zuhr with his companions. When he finished his prayer, he said: \"Which of you recited 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High'?\" Some of the people said: \"I, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"I knew that some of you were disputing it with me.\""],[2892,"A man recited 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High' behind the Prophet ﷺ, and that was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, so he said: \"It has been mentioned that some of you were disputing it with me.\""],[2893,"Whoever recites behind the imam has erred in the fitrah."],[2895,"A man came to Abd Allah and said: \"O Abu Abd al-Rahman, should I recite behind the imam?\" He said: \"Listen to the Quran, for in the prayer there is enough engagement, and the imam will suffice you for that.\""],[2898,"Ali used to forbid recitation behind the imam."],[2901,"I wish that the one who recites behind the imam had his mouth filled—he said: I think he said—with dust or pebbles."],[2902,"I wish that the one who recites behind the imam had his mouth filled—he said: I think he said—with dust or pebbles."],[2903,"I wish that the one who recites behind the imam, when he recites aloud, would bite down on a live coal."],[2904,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade recitation behind the imam. He said: And our elders informed me that Ali said, \"Whoever recites behind the imam has no prayer.\" He said: And Musa b. Uqba informed me that the Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to forbid recitation behind the imam."],[2905,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade recitation behind the imam. He said: And our elders informed me that Ali said, \"Whoever recites behind the imam has no prayer.\" He said: And Musa b. Uqba informed me that the Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to forbid recitation behind the imam."],[2906,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade recitation behind the imam. He said: And our elders informed me that Ali said, \"Whoever recites behind the imam has no prayer.\" He said: And Musa b. Uqba informed me that the Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to forbid recitation behind the imam."],[2907,"When one of you prostrates, let him place his hands with his face, for the hands prostrate as the face prostrates; and when he raises his head, let him raise them with it."],[2908,"The imam's recitation is sufficient for you in what he recites aloud during the prayer."],[2910,"I asked Ibn Umar, \"Should I recite with the imam?\" He said, \"You are indeed big-bellied! The imam's recitation [is enough].\""],[2912,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade recitation behind the imam. He said: And our elders informed me that Ali said, \"Whoever recites behind the imam has no prayer.\" He said: And Musa b. Uqba informed me that the Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to forbid recitation behind the imam."],[2914,"The imam's recitation suffices for those behind him. I said, \"From whom do you report this?\" He said, \"I heard it, but I prefer that you adopt the virtuous practices; I prefer that you recite along with him.\""],[2915,"They did not recite behind the imam until Ibn Ziyad's time. Then it was said to them, \"If he does not recite aloud, he does not recite silently to himself,\" so the people recited."],[2916,"I said to Ata, \"Does the imam's recitation suffice for those behind him in what he raises his voice and in what he is silent?\" He said, \"Yes.\""],[2917,"I asked Jabir b. Abdullah, \"Do you recite anything behind the imam in the zuhr and asr prayers?\" He said, \"No.\""],[2918,"Samura used to lead the people in prayer, pausing twice: when he said the takbir for the prayer, and when he finished reciting the Qur'an. The people criticized that about him, so he wrote to Ubayy b. Ka'b concerning that: 'The people have criticized me; perhaps I forgot and they remembered, or I remembered and they forgot.' Ubayy wrote back to him: 'Rather, you remembered and they forgot.'"],[2919,"People should not prompt the imam while he is reciting, for it is speech."],[2920,"Do not prompt an imam while you are in prayer."],[2921,"When the imam falters, do not correct him, for it is speech."],[2922,"ʿAbdullāh used to rise on the tops of his feet from the final prostration and in the first and third rakʿahs."],[2924,"I came to the mosque and there was a man praying behind the Maqam, pleasant smelling, well dressed, reciting, and a man beside him prompting him. I said, 'Who is this?' They said, 'Uthman.'"],[2925,"A man from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the Prophet ﷺ and recited Sūrat al-Baqarah. He recited it beautifully, clearly, and eloquently. He did not pass by any verse mentioning Paradise except that he asked about it, nor any verse mentioning the Fire except that he sought refuge at it. When he finished it, he bowed and said: 'Glory be to the Lord of dominion, might, pride, and greatness.' Then he raised his head and said the like of that when he raised his head. Then he prostrated and remained for a while saying the like of what he had remained saying after raising his head from bowing. Then he raised his head and stood, and recited Āl ʿImrān in the same manner. Then he finished it and did the same as he had done in bowing and prostration, and when raising his head from bowing and prostration, saying that in all of it as he had done in the first rakʿah. The man said to him when morning came: 'O Prophet of Allah, I wanted to pray a prayer like that but I could not.' He said: 'You will not be able to do what I can do. I fear for you for the sake of Allah.'"],[2926,"Ibn Umar prayed Maghrib, and when he recited: 'not of those who earn Your anger nor of those who go astray,' he began reciting: 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful' repeatedly and kept repeating it. So I said, 'When the earth is shaken,' and he recited it. When he finished, he did not criticize me for that."],[2927,"Marwan b. al-Hakam used to be prompted at that time, and he had appointed men for that; if he made a mistake, they would prompt him, while the Companions of Muhammad ﷺ were at that time in Madinah."],[2928,"When he raised his head from bowing, he would say: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him.\" Then he would say: \"O Allah, our Lord, and to You belongs praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling whatever You wish after that.\""],[2931,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade me from reciting while bowing and prostrating, from wearing a gold ring, from wearing qassī clothing, and from wearing garments dyed with safflower. I asked him: 'What is qassī?' He said: 'Silk.'"],[2933,"When the Prophet ﷺ prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits would be visible."],[2935,"O ʿAlī, I love for you what I love for myself, and I dislike for you what I dislike for myself. Do not wear qassī, nor garments dyed with safflower, and do not ride on red saddle cloths, for they are the mounts of Satan. Do not recite while you are prostrating, do not tie up your hair while you are praying, for that is the share of Satan. Do not recite while you are bowing, do not recite while you are prostrating, do not prompt the imam of a people, and do not fidget with pebbles during prayer."],[2937,"Do not recite in bowing or in prostration; bowing and prostration were only appointed for glorification."],[2938,"The Messenger of God ﷺ lifted the curtain and saw the people in rows behind Abū Bakr. He said: 'Nothing remains of the glad tidings of prophethood except a righteous dream that a Muslim sees or is seen for him. I have been forbidden to recite while bowing and prostrating. As for bowing, in it the Lord is magnified. As for prostration, strive in supplication during it, for it is worthy that you be answered.' He said: 'That is, it is fitting.'"],[2939,"I said to Ata, \"Is there a position for the two palms that is commanded in prostration?\" He said, \"No.\""],[2941,"He passed by the Prophet ﷺ one night while he was praying in the mosque in Madinah. He said: 'I stood to pray behind him, and it seemed to me that he did not know. He began Sūrat al-Baqarah. I said: \"When he reaches one hundred verses, he will bow.\" He reached it but did not bow. I said: \"When he reaches two hundred verses, he will bow.\" He reached it but did not bow. When he finished it, he would bow. He finished it but did not bow. When he finished, he said: \"O God, to You belongs praise; O God, to You belongs praise,\" an odd number of times. Then he began Āl ʿImrān. I said: \"If he finishes it, he will bow.\" He finished it but did not bow, and said: \"O God, to You belongs praise,\" three times. Then he began Sūrat al-Māʾidah. I said: \"When he finishes, he will bow.\" He finished it and bowed. I heard him say: \"Glory be to my Lord, the Magnificent,\" and he moved his lips, so I knew he was saying something else. Then he prostrated, and I heard him say: \"Glory be to my Lord, the Most High,\" and he moved his lips, so I knew he was saying something else, but I could not understand anything besides that. Then he began Sūrat al-Anʿām, so I left him and went away.'"],[2942,"He spent the night with him. The Prophet ﷺ got up at night, relieved himself, then came to the water skin and poured water, washing his hands three times. Then he rinsed his mouth and performed ablution, and recited the seven long surahs in a single rakʿah."],[2944,"Uthman recited the seven long surahs in a single rak'ah."],[2945,"Ibn Umar used to recite three surahs in a rak'ah sometimes."],[2946,"Ibn Umar used to recite two or three surahs in a single rak'ah."],[2949,"Prostrate on your nose."],[2950,"My father used to combine Sabbih isma rabbika al-a'la and wa al-layli idha yaghsha in one rak'ah, and wa al-duha with alam nashrah in one rak'ah in the prescribed prayer."],[2953,"He prostrates on his nose."],[2954,"I said to Ibn Umar - or someone else said - 'I recited the Mufassal in a single rak'ah.' He said: 'Did you do that? If Allah had willed, He would have sent it down as one whole. Give each surah its due of bowing and prostration.'"],[2955,"A prayer in which a man does not make his back straight in bowing and prostration does not suffice."],[2956,"I said to Ata: What do you think of people who place their hands below their knees when they bow? He said: \"This is an innovation. No, except above the two knees.\""],[2957,"A person said to Ata: I see people who, when they bow, lower their heads until they put their chins between their legs. He said: \"No, this is an innovation; those who came before did not do that.\" He said: Then how? He said: \"A moderate bowing, like the bowing of people today.\""],[2958,"When you stand for prayer and bow, place your hands on your knees and spread your fingers apart. Then raise your head until every limb returns to its joint. When you prostrate, place your forehead firmly on the ground and do not peck."],[2959,"When you bow, place your hands on your knees and spread your fingers apart."],[2961,"None of you should blow during his prayer."],[2962,"I saw an old sheikh wearing a burnus. Ibn Uyaynah said: Meaning al-Aswad b. Yazid. When he bowed, he joined his hands between his knees. He said: We went to Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami and told him, and he said: Yes, those are the companions of Abd Allah b. Masud, but Umar has prescribed for you the knees, so take to the knees."],[2963,"I prayed beside my father and I applied tatbiq, and he said: My father forbade me and said: We used to do it, but we were forbidden from it."],[2964,"Abd Allah used to apply tatbiq: when he bowed, he placed his hands between his knees and spread his forearms and thighs. I said to Ibrahim: What prevented you from that? He said: \"He used to place his hands on his knees.\""],[2965,"We prayed with Abd Allah, and when he bowed, he applied tatbiq with his palms and placed them between his knees, and he struck our hands, so we did that. Then we later met Umar, and he prayed with us in his house. When he bowed, we applied tatbiq with our palms as Abd Allah had done, while Umar placed his hands on his knees. When he finished, he said: \"What is this?\" We told him about Abd Allah's practice. He said: That is something that used to be done and then was abandoned."],[2966,"Ata said to me: Keep your hands firmly on your knees, and keep your back straight, and that suffices for the completeness of bowing."],[2967,"Be still in bowing until every part of you settles into place."],[2968,"I have become heavy, so whoever misses the bowing may catch me in the slowness of my standing."],[2969,"A man said to 'Ata': It used to be said: A person should not lower his head in bowing, nor raise it excessively. He said: \"No, and why would he lower it?\" A man said to him: What is excessive raising (al-iqna')? He said: Raising his head in bowing."],[2970,"He used to dislike squatting and sitting on the left buttock with the right foot up through the left thigh."],[2971,"The Messenger of God ﷺ, his bowing, his prostration, and his standing after the rak'ah were nearly equal in duration. He said: \"And if a bowl of water were placed on the Prophet's back, it would not spill because of how level he was when bowing.\""],[2972,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would spread out his left leg—or he said his left foot—for the right. He said: \"And she would forbid us from the heel of the Shaytan.\""],[2973,"He heard the Prophet ﷺ say while bowing: Glory be to my Lord, the Magnificent, moving his lips back and forth, and I knew he was saying something other than that."],[2974,"When the Prophet ﷺ bowed, he said: Glory be to my Lord, the Magnificent, and when he prostrated, he said: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High."],[2975,"When the Prophet ﷺ bowed, he would say: \"O God, I have bowed, and in You I have believed. You are my Lord, and upon You I have relied.\" And in prostration: \"Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.\""],[2976,"Among the most beloved speech to God, Mighty and Majestic, is for the servant to say: My Lord, I have wronged myself, so forgive me."],[2977,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to say frequently in his bowing and prostration: Glory be to You, O God, our Lord, and with Your praise. O God, forgive me, interpreting the Qur'an, meaning when 'When the help of God and the victory comes' was revealed."],[2978,"When 'When the help of God and the victory comes' was revealed, the Prophet ﷺ used to say frequently: Glory be to You, O God, and with Your praise. O God, forgive me, for You are the Ever-Relenting."],[2979,"Ibn Mas'ud, when he bowed, would say: 'Glory be to my Lord, the Magnificent' — three times or more — and when he prostrated, he would say: 'Glory be to my Lord, the Most High, and with His praise' — three times or more.\" Abu 'Ubaydah said: \"My father used to mention that the Prophet ﷺ used to say it.\""],[2980,"A'ishah got up one night looking for the Prophet ﷺ in the middle of the night. He said: Her hand fell upon the sole of the Prophet's foot ﷺ while he was prostrating, and he was saying: 'Glory be to my Lord, Possessor of the dominion, and the might, and the pride, and the greatness. I seek refuge in Allah — by Your pleasure from Your wrath, and I seek refuge in Your forgiveness from Your punishment, and I seek refuge in You from You. I cannot enumerate praise upon You; You are as You have praised Yourself.'"],[2981,"Fatimah bt. Abi Hubaysh said: O Messenger of God, I am a woman with istihadah and I do not become pure. Should I abandon prayer? The Prophet ﷺ said: That is only a vein, not menstruation. When menstruation comes, abandon prayer, and when menstruation departs, wash the blood from yourself, then pray."],[2982,"A'ishah missed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night, so she went feeling with her hand and it fell upon the hollow of his foot while he was prostrating, and he was saying: 'I seek refuge in Your pardon from Your punishment, and I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, and I seek refuge in You from You. I cannot attain to praising You, nor enumerate praise upon You; You are as You have praised Yourself.'"],[2983,"He used to say in his bowing and in his prostration: 'Glorified, Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit.'"],[2984,"'Umar b. al-Khattab used to say in his bowing and in his prostration about five glorifications: 'Glory be to Allah, and with His praise.'"],[2985,"Bow until your palms are firmly placed on your knees for about three glorifications, then raise your back until every limb of yours returns to its place."],[2986,"It suffices in bowing and prostration: 'Glory be to Allah, and with His praise' three times."],[2987,"When he prostrated, he would say: 'My face has prostrated to the One who created it and fashioned it, and made its hearing and its sight.'"],[2988,"We used to pray with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and people would say: “Peace be upon God, peace be upon Jibrīl, peace be upon Mīkāʾīl, peace be upon the angels brought near.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not say 'Peace be upon God', for God is al-Salām.” He said: So he taught them the tashahhud, saying: “Say: 'Greetings are for God, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of God and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of God. I bear witness that there is no god but God, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.'”"],[2989,"He performs the tashahhud."],[2990,"We differed, so Abu al-Aswad said: 'Abdullah used to say in his prostration: 'Glory be to You, there is no lord besides You.' And Shaddad said: He used to say: 'Glory be to You, there is no god but You.'"],[2992,"He performs the tashahhud."],[2993,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the woodcutters when they asked him: 'Three glorifications in bowing, and three glorifications in prostration.'"],[2994,"I entered the mosque and found ʿAbd Allah praying. He bowed, so I began reciting Sūrat al-Aʿrāf, and I finished before he prostrated."],[2995,"When he places his hands on his knees, he has completed it; and when he enables his forehead to touch the ground, he has completed it."],[2996,"A man from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ prayed with the Prophet ﷺ and recited Sūrat al-Baqarah. He recited it beautifully, clearly, and eloquently. He did not pass by any verse mentioning Paradise except that he asked about it, nor any verse mentioning the Fire except that he sought refuge at it. When he finished it, he bowed and said: 'Glory be to the Lord of dominion, might, pride, and greatness.' Then he raised his head and said the like of that when he raised his head. Then he prostrated and remained for a while saying the like of what he had remained saying after raising his head from bowing. Then he raised his head and stood, and recited Āl ʿImrān in the same manner. Then he finished it and did the same as he had done in bowing and prostration, and when raising his head from bowing and prostration, saying that in all of it as he had done in the first rakʿah. The man said to him when morning came: 'O Prophet of Allah, I wanted to pray a prayer like that but I could not.' He said: 'You will not be able to do what I can do. I fear for you for the sake of Allah.'"],[2997,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: 'Has any statement reached you that is said in bowing?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'Then what do you say?' He said: 'If I am not in a hurry and nothing distracts me, I say a statement, and when I reach it, that is it. I say: \"Glory be to You and with Your praise; there is no god but You,\" three times; \"Glory be to our Lord, indeed the promise of our Lord is surely fulfilled,\" three times; \"Glory be to Allah, the Mighty,\" three times; \"Glory be to Allah and with His praise,\" three times; \"Glory be to the King, the Holy,\" three times; \"Glorified, Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit; the mercy of my Lord precedes His anger,\" three times.' I said: 'Then has it reached you that he used to say any of these in bowing?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'Then what do you follow in that?' He said: 'As for \"Glory be to You and with Your praise; there is no god but You,\" [it has reached me].'"],[2998,"If you miss a rak'ah with the imam and he sits, reciting the tashahhud in a shaf' while you are in a witr, then when the imam finishes, complete what remains of your prayer, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. I said: 'Why should I prostrate them?' He said: 'Because one does not sit in a witr nor recite the tashahhud in it, and because he sat in a witr.' I said: 'Is that treated like forgetfulness and error?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[2999,"Ibn Ṭāwūs was asked about the completeness of prostration. He gestured with his hand and said: 'Three glorifications.'"],[3000,"I used to hear Ibn al-Zubayr often saying in his prostration: 'Glorified, Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit; the mercy of my Lord precedes His anger.'"],[3001,"ʿAlī used to say when he bowed: 'O Allah, to You I have humbled myself, to You I have bowed, to You I have submitted, in You I have believed, and You are my Lord. My hearing, my sight, my flesh, my blood, my marrow, my bones, my sinews, my hair, and my skin have humbled themselves to You. Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah.' And when he said: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' he said: 'O Allah, our Lord, to You be praise.' And when he prostrated, he said: 'O Allah, to You I have prostrated, to You I have submitted, in You I have believed, upon You I have relied, and You are my Lord. My hearing, my sight, my flesh, my blood, my bones, my sinews, my hair, and my skin have prostrated to You. Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah.'"],[3002,"2929 2904 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Ubayy [1], from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Ali, the like of it. Hidden text: The speech of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in his bowing was that he would say: \"O Allah, for You I have bowed, and in You I have believed, and to You I have submitted, and You are my Lord. My hearing, my sight, my brain, my bones, and my sinews have humbled themselves to Allah, Lord of the worlds.\" Then when he raised his head from bowing he said: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him.\" Then he would follow it with: \"O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling whatever You wish after that.\" Then when he prostrated he said in his prostration: \"O Allah, for You I have prostrated, and in You I have believed, and to You I have submitted, and You are my Lord. My face has prostrated to the One who created it and fashioned its hearing and sight. Blessed is Allah, the best of creators.\" [1] Perhaps there is an omission; the correct reading is: (Ibn Abi Yahya, that is Ibrahim b. Muhammad b. Abi Yahya), as it is explicitly stated in the previous narration."],[3003,"A man said to the Prophet ﷺ: 'O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you with salam, but how do we send blessings upon you?' He said: 'Say: O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed Ibrahim. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, grant barakah to Muhammad, as You granted barakah to Ibrahim. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.'"],[3004,"Tawus used to say in his bowing: \"O Allah, for You I have bowed, and for You I have humbled myself, and to You I have submitted, and in You I have believed, and upon You I have relied, and to You I have turned, and to You is the final return.\""],[3005,"It is a Sunnah: when you raise your head from the rak'ah or the sajdah, stand upright until every bone returns to its joint. If you do that, it suffices you. And it was said—I do not know whether the Prophet ﷺ said it—after he raised his head from the rak'ah and stood upright: \"Say: O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling whatever You wish after that. There is no one who can withhold what You give, and no one can give what You withhold, and no one's fortune can avail him against You.\""],[3006,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said \"Allah hears the one who praises Him,\" he would say: \"O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling whatever You wish after that.\""],[3007,"When he raised his head from bowing, he would say: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him.\" Then he would say: \"O Allah, our Lord, and to You belongs praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling whatever You wish after that.\""],[3009,"I was sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when a man came and said: 'We know how to greet you with salam, but how do we send blessings upon you?' He said: 'Say: O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed the family of Ibrahim. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.'"],[3012,"When the imam says, \"Allah hears the one who praises Him,\" then say, \"Our Lord, to You belongs praise; may Allah hear you.\" For Allah decreed upon the tongue of His Prophet: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him.\""],[3014,"He used to say, when he said \"Allah hears the one who praises Him\": \"O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs much praise,\" then he would prostrate. \"So that I may grant him such and such.\" He said: \"O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise. O Allah, by Your might and power I stand and I sit.\""],[3015,"When the imam says 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' let the one behind him say: 'Our Lord, to You belongs praise.'"],[3016,"He heard Abu Hurayrah, while he was leading the people in prayer, saying: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him. O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs all praise. Allah is most great,\" raising his voice with that, and we followed him together."],[3017,"When the imam raises his head from bowing and says: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him,\" then say: \"Our Lord, to You belongs all praise.\""],[3018,"When the Prophet ﷺ raised his head from bowing, he would say: \"Praise be to Allah, Possessor of sovereignty, majesty, pride, and greatness.\""],[3019,"A man said when he raised his head from the rak'ah: \"Our Lord, to You belongs abundant, good, blessed praise.\" When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, he said: \"Who said these words?\" The man remained silent. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Who said them?\" The man said: \"I did, O Messenger of Allah.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"We saw twelve angels, all of them writing it down.\""],[3020,"If you are with an imam and he says: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him,\" then if you also say: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him,\" it is good. If you do not say with the imam \"Allah hears the one who praises Him,\" it is sufficient for you. And to combine them both with the imam is more beloved to me."],[3021,"He said: \"What do you think if the imam does not make me hear his saying 'Allah hears the one who praises Him'?\" He said: \"Say the like of what he says when you hear him.\" He said: \"And the imam praises when he says 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' and the person praying alone praises; and they both praise while standing upright before prostrating. For the imam and others are commanded to praise when he says 'Allah hears the one who praises Him.' And whoever is behind the imam says what I have written.\""],[3022,"And if you say, when you raise your head from the rak'ah: \"Praise be to Allah,\" it is sufficient for you. If you praise with any praise, it is enough for you."],[3023,"He raises his head from prostration until every part settles into place."],[3024,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, he would spread his arms so that the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."],[3025,"My father narrated to me that he was with his father at al-Qāʿ from Namirah — or he said: from Tamirah. He said: \"A mounted group passed by us and made their camels kneel at the side of the road. My father said to me: 'My son, stay with our lambs until I draw near to this mounted group.' He said: So he drew near and I drew near with him. The prayer was commenced, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among them. He said: I was looking at the whiteness of the armpits of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whenever he prostrated.\""],[3029,"He used to dislike prolonging the prostration or pausing too long, but rather a moderate balance between that."],[3030,"Ibn 'Umar saw me praying without keeping my forearms off the ground, and he said: 'O nephew, do not spread out like a beast of prey. Lean on the palms of your hands, and keep your upper arms away from your sides, for if you do that, every limb of yours prostrates.'"],[3031,"The companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ complained about supporting themselves with their hands in prostration, so he permitted them to use their hands to help themselves on their knees in prostration."],[3032,"He used to command that one be upright in prostration and that a man should not prostrate with his forearms spread out like a dog."],[3033,"When one of you prostrates, let him be upright, and not spread his forearms as a dog spreads them."],[3034,"The Muslims complained to the Messenger of God ﷺ about spreading the arms in prayer, so they were commanded to help themselves with their knees."],[3035,"When Ibn 'Umar saw a man spreading his fingers during prostration in prayer, he forbade him. He said: 'He would close his fingers tightly and lay them flat.'"],[3036,"I prayed next to Ibn 'Umar and spread my fingers when I prostrated. He said: 'O nephew, close your fingers when you prostrate, and face the qiblah, and point the palms toward the qiblah, for they prostrate along with the face.'"],[3037,"When one of you prostrates, let him raise his hands, for the hands prostrate with the face."],[3038,"When one of you prostrates, let him place his hands with his face, for the hands prostrate as the face prostrates; and when he raises his head, let him raise them with it."],[3039,"When one of you prostrates, let him raise his hands, for the hands prostrate with the face."],[3040,"I have not seen anyone praying with the posture of 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar, more intensely facing the Ka'bah with his face, his palms, and his feet."],[3041,"Ibn 'Umar used to like to be straight in prayer, even his fingers toward the qiblah."],[3042,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to forbid any of us from spreading his forearms like the spreading of a dog or a beast of prey."],[3043,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ about leaning on the hands in prostration, and he said: \"It is forbidden.\" I said: \"Then I place my elbows.\" He said: \"If you wish, then on your knees, and if you wish, do not place them on them. As long as you do not lean, it does not harm you wherever you place them.\""],[3044,"He used to forbid a man from placing his forearms on the ground when he prostrated, up to the palms."],[3045,"He saw a man who drew aside when he prostrated. He said: \"No, do not turn away your form. He says: do not prefer it.\" I said: \"What is 'do not turn away your form'?\" He said: \"Do not alter, do not hold back.\""],[3046,"When one of you prostrates, let him not prostrate sitting on his left hip or lying down, for if he perfects the prostration, all his bones prostrate."],[3047,"He saw a man who, when he prostrated, raised his legs toward the sky, and he said: \"The prayer of this one is not complete.\""],[3048,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded placing the palms and setting the feet upright."],[3049,"Enable your knees and the tops of your feet to touch the ground during prostration."],[3050,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"What do you think if I do not straighten my back in the prostration of the prescribed prayer, and I do not keep my face prostrating in some of that?\" He said: \"Do not repeat it, and do not perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness.\""],[3051,"He disliked for a man, when he prostrated, to press his private part directly against the ground. He said: \"And its explanation is that there should be a garment between him and the ground.\""],[3052,"I observed the Messenger of God ﷺ, and when he prostrated, his hands were in line with his ears."],[3053,"Ibn ʿUmar used to place his hands, when he prostrated, in line with his ears."],[3054,"Ibn Umar was asked whether a man should place his hand when he prostrates. He said, \"Place them wherever they fall.\""],[3055,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: How has it reached you concerning the beginning of the taslim? He said: \"I do not know, except that the first to raise his voice with the taslim was ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb ؓ.\" He said: \"They used to greet themselves without raising their voices with the taslim.\" I said: \"So they would depart upon the greeting of the tashahhud?\" He said: \"No, but they would say within themselves: 'Peace be upon you,' then stand until ʿUmar raised his voice.\""],[3056,"Ibn Mas'ud bowed and clasped his hands, placing them between his knees."],[3057,"I bowed and clasped my hands, placing them between my knees, but my father forbade me and said, \"We used to do that, but we were forbidden from it.\""],[3058,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ stood for prayer, he would say the takbir when he stood, and he would say the takbir when he bowed. Then he would say: “God hears the one who praises Him” when he raised his back from the bowing. Then he would say while standing: “Our Lord, to You belongs praise.” Then he would say the takbir when he went down to prostrate, then he would say the takbir when he raised his head. Then he would do that in all the prayers until he completed them, and he would say the takbir when he stood from the two rak‘ahs after sitting. Then Abu Hurayrah would say: “I am the one among you whose prayer most resembles that of the Messenger of God ﷺ.”"],[3059,"Umar, when he bowed, would go down as a camel goes down, his knees before his hands, and he would say the takbir and go down."],[3060,"Regarding the man who puts his hands down before his knees, Ibrahim said, \"Does anyone do that except a madman?\""],[3061,"Regarding the man who puts his hands down before his knees, Ibrahim said, \"Does anyone do that except a madman?\""],[3062,"When he prostrated, he placed his knees, then his hands, then his face. When he wanted to get up, he raised his face, then his hands, then his knees."],[3063,"He did not say the takbir except while going down, and his rising for standing."],[3064,"He saw Mu'awiya in the third rak'ah – as al-Dabari read it – and the third from bowing, when he raised his head from prostration he did not pause. He said, \"He rises while saying the takbir in his rising for standing.\""],[3065,"He used to dislike leaning on his hands when sitting between the two rak'ahs and when rising."],[3066,"He did not see any harm in a man supporting himself with his hands when rising in prayer."],[3067,"Regarding a man who rises to stand: should he raise his hands before his knees? He said, \"He considers whichever of that is easier for him.\""],[3069,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ, 'I see some people, when one of them stands, bending his leg.' He said, 'With its foot, then he places his hand on his thigh and then stands like that, or he places his hand on the ground and then stands upon it.' He said, 'This manner of standing is closer to pride. Nothing but humility is appropriate in prayer.'"],[3070,"I watched ʿAbdullāh b. Masʿūd in prayer and saw him rise without sitting. He said, 'He rises on the tops of his feet in the first and third rakʿahs.'"],[3071,"ʿAbdullāh used to rise on the tops of his feet from the final prostration and in the first and third rakʿahs."],[3074,"I was commanded to prostrate on seven parts and not to tuck up hair or clothing: on the forehead and the nose—then he passed his hands over his forehead and nose—and on the two hands, the two knees, and the two feet."],[3075,"The Prophet ﷺ was commanded to prostrate on seven: his forehead, his two hands, his two knees, and his two feet, and was forbidden to tuck up hair or clothing."],[3076,"I was commanded to prostrate on seven bones and not to tuck up hair or clothing. He said, 'The forehead—then he places his hand on it and then passes it to his nose—and the two hands, the two knees, and the two feet.'"],[3079,"The Prophet ﷺ was commanded to pray on seven parts: on his palms, his knees, the tips of his feet, and his forehead. Then Tawus passed by wiping—when he said, 'and his forehead,' he continued until he wiped his nose—'and not to tie back hair or clothes.'"],[3080,"Those who came before used to say: A person prostrates on his face, his palms, his knees, and his feet, and he does not tie back hair or a garment."],[3082,"Place your nose until the dust comes out of it. I said: What is the dust? He said: 'Pride.'"],[3083,"When you prostrate, press your nose to the ground."],[3084,"We discussed Laylat al-Qadr among a group of Quraysh. I went to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who was a friend of mine, and said, 'Will you come out with us to the palm groves?' He said, 'Yes.' He came out wearing a cloak of his. I said to him, 'Did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention Laylat al-Qadr?' He said, 'Yes. We observed i'tikaf with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the middle ten days of the month of Ramadan, and we came out on the morning of the twentieth.' He said, 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us and said: I saw Laylat al-Qadr, then I was made to forget it. So seek it in the last ten days, on an odd night. And I saw myself prostrating in water and mud. Whoever observed i'tikaf with me, let him return to his place of i'tikaf.' He said, 'We returned, and there was not a cloud in the sky. Then a cloud came and we were rained upon until the roof of the mosque leaked, and it was made of palm branches. The prayer was established, and when he finished, I saw the trace of mud on the forehead and nose of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, meaning the night of the twenty-first.'"],[3086,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a woman prostrating while raising her nose, and he said something severe about her, disapproving of her raising her nose."],[3090,"If you do not place your nose with your forehead, that prostration will not be accepted from you."],[3091,"What I have not forgotten, among what I have forgotten from the Messenger of God ﷺ, is that he would say the taslim to his right: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God,' until we could see the whiteness of his cheek, and to his left: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God,' until we could see the whiteness of his cheek as well."],[3093,"I said to him: \"Placing the nose with the forehead?\" He said: \"Indeed I prostrate on it sometimes, and sometimes I do not prostrate on it. And that I prostrate on it is more beloved to me.\""],[3094,"Some of them used to say: \"Whoever says that prostration is on the nose, and then prostrates on his forehead and does not prostrate on his nose, it suffices him. And whoever says that there is no prostration on his nose, and then prostrates on the nose and does not prostrate on the forehead, it does not suffice him.\""],[3095,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man to pray while his head is tied back."],[3096,"He passed by Hasan b. Ali while Hasan was praying standing, having fastened his braid at the back of his neck. Abu Rafi' undid it, so he turned to him angrily. Abu Rafi' said to him: \"Attend to your prayer and do not get angry, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'That is the devil's share.'\""],[3097,"Umar b. al-Khattab passed by a son of his while he was praying with his head tied back, and he pulled him until he threw him down."],[3098,"O ʿAlī, I love for you what I love for myself, and I dislike for you what I dislike for myself. Do not wear qassī, nor garments dyed with safflower, and do not ride on red saddle cloths, for they are the mounts of Satan. Do not recite while you are prostrating, do not tie up your hair while you are praying, for that is the share of Satan. Do not recite while you are bowing, do not recite while you are prostrating, do not prompt the imam of a people, and do not fidget with pebbles during prayer."],[3099,"It is disliked for a man to pray while his head is tied back, or to fidget with pebbles, or to spit in front of his face, or to his right."],[3100,"Hudhayfah passed by his son while he was praying, and he had two braids which he had tied back. He called for a blade and cut one of them, then said: \"If you wish, do the same to the other, and if you wish, leave it.\""],[3101,"Abdullah b. Mas'ud passed by a man prostrating with his head tied back, and he untied it. When he finished, Abdullah said to him: \"Do not tie it back, for your hair prostrates, and for every hair there is a reward.\" He said: \"I only tied it back so that it would not get dusty.\" He said: \"That it gets dusty is better for you.\""],[3102,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a man prostrating and holding back his hair with his hand, so the Prophet ﷺ said: \"O Allah, erase his hair.\" He said: \"Then his hair fell out.\""],[3103,"If the imam gives you opportunity, then recite in the two rak'ahs that remain, a surah in each, and make them the beginning of your prayer."],[3106,"I said: 'I pray in the rain in my cloak, and the water flows beside me?' He said: 'Do not fold it back.' He said: 'Then it will be spoiled.' He said: 'Even so, leave it in the water.'"],[3107,"I said to 'Ata': 'A man removing his cloak from under him, then not lifting it from the ground—is that holding it back by removing it?' He said: 'No harm if he sits; that only applies in prostration.'"],[3108,"I said to him: 'A man holds back his hair for something other than prayer, then the prayer is established.' He said: 'Let him loosen his head and let it hang.'"],[3109,"I said to 'Ata': 'A man from the desert people—one of them holds back his hair for a long time, because of his work with his livestock and his labor.' He said: 'No harm; this one holds it back because of his work, and it is only holding back the hair for prayer that is forbidden.'"],[3110,"I said to 'Ata': 'Is it feared that the turban is a holding back of the hair?' He said: 'That only comes down to intention.'"],[3111,"I said to 'Ata': 'Should a man braid his two horns?' He said: 'No; that is for something other than holding it back for prayer—turbans and braiding the two horns.'"],[3112,"I do not like for the one with two horns to place his two braids, when they are long, on his back. He said: 'So where?' He said: 'On his chest.'"],[3114,"I said to 'Ata': 'What do you think if I put my forearms on the ground and hold back my hair and garment?' He said: 'Do not repeat it, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.'"],[3115,"He raises his head from prostration, then sits until every part of him settles in its place."],[3116,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would sometimes raise his head from the prostration and the rak'ah, and remain between them until one would say something."],[3117,"He used to say between the two prostrations: 'Lord, forgive me, have mercy on me, restore me, and provide for me.'"],[3118,"He used to say between the two prostrations: 'O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, provide for me, and mend me.'"],[3119,"He performs the tashahhud."],[3122,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ bowed, he neither lowered his head nor raised it excessively, and when he raised his head from bowing, he did not prostrate until he stood upright."],[3123,"He said: O Messenger of God, we know how to greet you with peace, so how should we pray for you? He said: \"Say: O God, bestow prayer upon the family of Muhammad; O God, bless the family of Muhammad as You blessed and prayed upon Abraham and the family of Abraham. Truly You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.\""],[3125,"Whoever blows during prayer has spoken."],[3128,"He would begin with 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' in the prayer."],[3130,"I do not care whether I blew or spoke."],[3131,"They used to dislike blowing because it harms the person sitting next to him."],[3132,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī and Ayyūb about a man who squats when he raises his head from prostration until he prostrates the other. Ayyūb said: \"Al-Ḥasan and Ibn Sīrīn would not squat.\" ʿAṭāʾ said: \"That is what we used to hear until the people of Makkah came to us with something other than that.\""],[3133,"When one of you prays, let him not squat like a dog squats."],[3134,"Abū Hurayrah said to him: Beware of the dog's sitting posture and squatting, and guard against heedlessness until you finish the obligatory prayer."],[3135,"What I have not forgotten, among what I have forgotten from the Messenger of God ﷺ, is that he would say the taslim to his right: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God,' until we could see the whiteness of his cheek, and to his left: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God,' until we could see the whiteness of his cheek as well."],[3136,"Recite in what you make up."],[3137,"He saw Ibn ʿUmar, Ibn al-Zubayr, and Ibn ʿAbbās squatting between the two prostrations."],[3138,"It is from the Sunnah that your heels touch your buttocks in prayer between the two prostrations."],[3139,"He saw ʿUmar and Ibn ʿUmar squatting between the two prostrations."],[3140,"Squatting in prayer is the Sunnah."],[3141,"It is from the Sunnah that your heels touch your buttocks."],[3142,"He saw Ibn Umar perform in the first prostration of the even and odd (prayers) two characteristics. He said: 'I saw him once squatting on the ends of both his feet, kneeling on them, and once folding his left leg and spreading it, sitting on it, while his right leg he stood upon, arching it on the ends of both his feet.' He said: 'I saw him do that in the first of the two prostrations, and in the third prostration of the witr, then he would remain and stand.'"],[3143,"We said to Ibn Abbas about squatting on the two feet. He said: 'It is the sunnah.' We said: 'We consider it harsh for a man.' Ibn Abbas said: 'Rather, it is the sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ.'"],[3145,"Recite in what you make up."],[3146,"I watched the Prophet ﷺ and he raised his hands in prayer when he said the takbir, then when he said the takbir he raised his hands, then when he said 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah' he raised them. Then he sat and spread his left leg, placed his left hand on his left knee and his right forearm on his right thigh, then he pointed with his index finger, placed his thumb on his middle finger making a circle with it, and closed the rest of his fingers. Then he prostrated and his hands were level with his ears."],[3147,"I saw Ibn Umar sit in pairs, sitting on his left (leg), spreading it and sitting on it, and squatting on the toes of his right (foot), kneeling on them, coming behind him on all its toes."],[3148,"Ibn ʿUmar, when he missed a rakʿah or something of the prayer with the imam and the imam said the taslīm, he stood as soon as the imam said the taslīm, and he did not wait for the imam to stand."],[3149,"Ibn Umar sat cross-legged in his prayer and said: 'It is not from the sunnah of prayer, but I complain of my leg.'"],[3150,"I heard a man ask Ata': 'Was it recommended for a man to sit on his left leg in prayer?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[3151,"Ibn Umar prayed and sat cross-legged. I did that while I was young in age, and he said: 'Why do you do that?' He said: I said: 'Because you do it.' He said: 'It is not from the sunnah of prayer; rather, the sunnah of prayer is to fold the left (leg) and erect the right.' He said: And Abdullah said: 'My legs do not carry me.'"],[3152,"He saw Ibn Umar sit cross-legged in two prostrations of the prayer on the tops of his feet. That was mentioned to him, and he said: 'It is not from the sunnah of prayer, but I do that because I complain.'"],[3154,"When he sat in the prayer in the first two rak'ahs, he would erect his right foot and spread out his left—and he pointed with the finger next to the thumb—and when he sat in the last two, he would place his buttocks on the ground and erect his right foot."],[3155,"Recite in what you make up."],[3156,"I prayed beside Ibn Umar, and I was fidgeting with the pebbles during the prayer. When he finished, he said: \"Fidgeting with pebbles during the prayer is from the Shaytan, but do as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do: when he sat in the prayer, he placed his left palm on his left thigh and his right palm on his right thigh, clenched his fingers, and pointed with the finger next to the thumb.\""],[3157,"When he sat in the second rak'ah, he would tuck his left foot under him and sit on it, placing his foot under his buttock until the top of his foot became dark from the pebbly ground."],[3158,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would spread out his left leg—or he said his left foot—for the right. He said: \"And she would forbid us from the heel of the Shaytan.\""],[3159,"Ata was complaining of pain in his left leg, so he would extend his right leg while his left was folded, keeping it upright. I said: \"Will you not sit cross-legged?\" He said: \"I dislike that.\" I said: \"What if I sit cross-legged or extend my leg in front of me in the prayer?\" He said: \"Prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.\""],[3160,"Sitting on two embers is better for me than sitting cross-legged in the prayer."],[3161,"I said to Ata: \"May I sit cross-legged between the two rak'ahs while I am young?\" He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"I want to sit cross-legged before the tashahhud.\" He said: \"Do not do so until you have performed the tashahhud. Once you have performed it, then sit cross-legged or sit with your legs drawn up or do whatever you wish. If you do it before the tashahhud in an obligatory prayer, prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. In a voluntary prayer, if you do it, do not prostrate.\" He said: \"What I prefer is that you perform the tashahhud tucking your left side under you and erecting the other, sitting in iq'a on it, with its toes in the dust, like the sitting of Ibn Umar.\" I said: \"Then should I place my left hand like that before the tashahhud?\" He said: \"No, and I do not like that.\""],[3162,"Ibn ‘Umar would follow him (in prayer) and would not sit."],[3163,"Ibn ‘Umar would follow him (in prayer) and would not sit."],[3164,"He saw a man sitting leaning with his hand on the ground and said: \"You have sat in the sitting position of a people who were punished.\""],[3165,"Concerning a man placing his left hand when sitting in the prayer, he would say: \"It is the sitting position of those upon whom is wrath.\""],[3166,"Ibn ʿAbbās heard a man, when he sat in prayer, saying: “Praise be to God” before the tashahhud. He rebuked him, saying: “Begin with the tashahhud.”"],[3167,"I know nothing after the two rakʿahs except the tashahhud."],[3168,"Some of his companions came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Prophet of Allah, your companions—meaning the earlier companions—have surpassed us in deeds. He said: \"Shall I not inform you of something you can do after the obligatory prayers, by which you will catch up with those who preceded you and surpass those who come after you?\" They said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. So he commanded them to say Allāhu Akbar thirty-four times, Subḥān Allāh thirty-three times, and Alḥamdu lillāh thirty-three times. He said: Then a man informed us about that; he said: The poor came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, the wealthy have overtaken us in reward, so command us with an action by which we can catch up with their deeds. He told them the same as what ‘Aṭā’ said. When the owners of wealth learned of it, they took it up. When the poor saw that, they came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, and he said: \"These are the virtues.\""],[3169,"We did not know what to say in prayer, so we used to say: “Peace be upon God, peace be upon Jibrīl, peace be upon Mīkāʾīl.” The Prophet ﷺ taught us and said: “Do not say 'Peace be upon God', for God is al-Salām. When you sit after two rakʿahs, say: 'Greetings are for God, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of God and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of God.'” Abū Wāʾil said in the ḥadīth of ʿAbdullāh, from the Prophet ﷺ: “When you say it, it reaches every righteous servant in the heaven and the earth.” Abū Isḥāq said in the ḥadīth of ʿAbdullāh: “When you say it, it reaches every angel brought near, or prophet sent, or righteous servant. I bear witness that there is no god but God, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.”"],[3170,"Rabīʿ b. Khuthaym came to ʿAlqamah to consult him about adding “and His forgiveness” to it. ʿAlqamah said: “We only stop at what we have been taught.”"],[3171,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was taught the openings of good and its comprehensive words—or the comprehensive words of good and its conclusions—and we did not know what to say in our prayer until he taught us. He said: “Say: 'Greetings are for God, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of God and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of God. I bear witness that there is no god but God, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.'”"],[3172,"We used to pray with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and people would say: “Peace be upon God, peace be upon Jibrīl, peace be upon Mīkāʾīl, peace be upon the angels brought near.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not say 'Peace be upon God', for God is al-Salām.” He said: So he taught them the tashahhud, saying: “Say: 'Greetings are for God, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of God and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of God. I bear witness that there is no god but God, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.'”"],[3173,"Abū Mūsā led his companions in a prayer. He said: When he sat, a man among the people said: “Prayer has been joined with righteousness and zakāh.” He said: When Abū Mūsā finished his prayer, he said: “Which of you said such-and-such a word?” The people remained silent. Abū Mūsā said: “O Ḥiṭṭān, perhaps you said it?” He said: I said: “No, by God, I did not say it, and I feared you would rebuke me for it.” A man among the people said: “I said it, and I intended only good by it.” Abū Mūsā said: “Do you not know how to pray? The Messenger of God ﷺ addressed us, explained to us our ways, and taught us our prayer. He said: 'When you pray, straighten your rows, then let one of you lead you. When he says the takbīr, say the takbīr. When he says: “not of those who incur anger nor of those who go astray,” say: “Āmīn,” and God will answer you. When he says the takbīr and bows, say the takbīr and bow, for the imam bows before you and rises before you.'” The Prophet of God ﷺ said: “That is for that. When he says: 'God hears the one who praises Him,' say: 'Our Lord, to You be praise,' and God will hear you, for He decreed upon the tongue of His Prophet ﷺ 'God hears the one who praises Him.' When it comes to the sitting, let one of you say first when he sits: 'Greetings are for God, the good words; prayers are for God. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of God and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of God. I bear witness that there is no god but God, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.'”"],[3174,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was taught the openings of good and its comprehensive words—or the comprehensive words of good and its conclusions—and we did not know what to say in our prayer until he taught us. He said: “Say: 'Greetings are for God, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of God and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of God. I bear witness that there is no god but God, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.'”"],[3175,"I witnessed ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb teaching the tashahhud. He said: “Greetings are for God, the pure words are for God, the good words are for God. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of God and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of God. I bear witness that there is no god but God, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.” ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: “Maʿmar adopted it, and I adopt it.”"],[3176,"and the mercy of God. Peace be upon us"],[3178,"I heard Ibn ʿAbbas and Ibn al-Zubayr say in the tashahhud during prayer: “Al-taḥiyyātu al-mubārakātu lillāh, al-ṣalawātu al-ṭayyibātu lillāh, al-salāmu ʿalā al-nabiyyi wa-raḥmatu Allāhi wa-barakātuhu, al-salāmu ʿalaynā wa-ʿalā ʿibādi Allāhi al-ṣāliḥīn, ashhadu an lā ilāha illā Allāh, wa-ashhadu anna Muḥammadan ʿabduhu wa-rasūluhu.” He said: “I heard Ibn al-Zubayr saying them on the minbar, teaching them to the people.” He said: “And I heard Ibn ʿAbbas saying them likewise.” I said: Did Ibn ʿAbbas and Ibn al-Zubayr not differ concerning them? He said: “No.”"],[3179,"He used to say in the tashahhud: “In the name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful. Al-taḥiyyātu al-mubārakātu wa-al-ṣalawātu al-ṭayyibātu lillāh, al-salāmu ʿalayka ayyuhā al-nabiyyu wa-raḥmatu Allāhi wa-barakātuhu, al-salāmu ʿalaynā wa-ʿalā ʿibādi Allāhi al-ṣāliḥīn, ashhadu an lā ilāha illā Allāh, wa-ashhadu anna Muḥammadan ʿabduhu wa-rasūluhu.” Ṭāwūs said concerning the tashahhud: “It used to be taught as the Qurʾan is taught.”"],[3180,"In the tashahhud as Ibn Ṭāwūs informed me, except that he did not include in it “In the name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful.” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I mentioned that to Saʿīd b. Jubayr, and he said: “Ṭāwūs has retracted part of it.” So I informed Ṭāwūs of that, and he denied having retracted any of it, saying: “Even if I had heard ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbbas only once or twice.”"],[3181,"I said to Nāfiʿ: How did Ibn ʿUmar use to perform the tashahhud? He said: He used to say: “In the name of God. Al-taḥiyyātu lillāh, al-ṣalawātu lillāh, al-zākiyātu lillāh, al-salāmu ʿalayka ayyuhā al-nabiyyu wa-raḥmatu Allāhi wa-barakātuhu, al-salāmu ʿalaynā wa-ʿalā ʿibādi Allāhi al-ṣāliḥīn.” Then he would testify: “I testify that there is no god but God, I testify that there is no god but God, I testify that Muḥammad is the Messenger of God,” following these with the salutation."],[3182,"He did not perform the salutation in the first two-unit portion; he considered that invalidating for his prayer.” Al-Zuhrī said: “As for me, I do perform the salutation.”"],[3184,"While the Prophet ﷺ was teaching the tashahhud, a man said: “And I testify that Muḥammad is His Messenger and His servant.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “I was a servant before I was a messenger. Say: And I testify that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger.”"],[3185,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ in a dream; he came to me, and I said: O Messenger of God, we have differed concerning the tashahhud. So-and-so said such and such, and so-and-so said such and such, and Ibn Masʿūd said such and such. He said: “The Sunnah is the Sunnah of Ibn Masʿūd.”"],[3186,"Regarding a man who forgot the tashahhud at the end of his prayer until he left, they said: “He does not repeat it; his prayer is complete.”"],[3187,"No prescribed prayer nor voluntary prayer is valid without a tashahhud.” I said: What if I forgot the tashahhud in the dawn prayer? He said: “Do not repeat it, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness; perform the tashahhud when you remember.”"],[3188,"No prayer is valid except with a tashahhud."],[3189,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to open his prayer with the takbir and end it with the taslim."],[3190,"He used to teach them the tashahhud, then say: \"O Allah, I ask You for all good, what I know of it and what I do not know, and I seek refuge in You from all evil, what I know of it and what I do not know. O Allah, I ask You for the best of what Your righteous servants ask You, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what Your righteous servants seek refuge from. O Allah, our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire. Our Lord, forgive us our sins, expiate our evil deeds, and cause us to die with the righteous. Our Lord, grant us what You promised us through Your messengers, and do not disgrace us on the Day of Resurrection. Indeed, You do not break Your promise.\""],[3191,"I asked him about the imam. He said: \"He says the takbir, then says: 'Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise,' then seeks refuge quietly, and recites 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful' quietly, then recites aloud 'Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds.' Then he says the takbir as he goes down [into ruku'], then rises and says the takbir as he goes down [into sujud]. Then he sits in the first two [rak'ahs] for the tashahhud and does not add to it. In the last two [rak'ahs] there is the tashahhud and five comprehensive words.\" Al-Thawri said: Mansur informed me, saying: I said to Ibrahim: \"The prayer upon the Prophet ﷺ?\" He said: \"They would not add to these.\""],[3194,"He used to say after the tashahhud in the final two rak'ahs some words which he taught very earnestly. He said: \"I seek refuge in Allah from the punishment of Hell, and I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of the false messiah, and I seek refuge in Allah from the punishment of the grave, and I seek refuge in Allah from the trial of life and death.\""],[3195,"He said to a man: \"Did you say them in your prayer?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then repeat your prayer.\""],[3196,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: \"O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave, and from the trial of life and death, and from the evil of the trial of the false messiah.\""],[3197,"When he performed the tashahhud, he would cast off his cloak after the tashahhud in the last rak'ah."],[3198,"It does not harm you on which of your two sides you depart."],[3200,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to raise his hands to his chest in supplication, then wipe his face with them."],[3201,"If you are seated in a witr while the imam is in an even-numbered rak'ah, perform the tashahhud but do not say the salam. Say: 'Greetings are for Allah, blessed things are for Allah, prayers and good things are for Allah.' And glorify and supplicate, and perform the tashahhud in this manner. I said: 'Should I glorify and say tahlil and takbir?' He said: 'No, if you wish.'"],[3205,"He did not perform the salutation in the first two-unit portion; he considered that invalidating for his prayer.” Al-Zuhrī said: “As for me, I do perform the salutation.”"],[3206,"If you miss a rak'ah with the imam and he sits, reciting the tashahhud in a shaf' while you are in a witr, then when the imam finishes, complete what remains of your prayer, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. I said: 'Why should I prostrate them?' He said: 'Because one does not sit in a witr nor recite the tashahhud in it, and because he sat in a witr.' I said: 'Is that treated like forgetfulness and error?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[3207,"Ibn al-Zubayr missed two rak'ahs of Zuhr. When the imam said the salam, Ibn al-Zubayr stood and completed the rak'ah. When he said the salam, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3209,"I saw Ibn Umar miss a rak'ah, so he sat in a witr while the imam was in a shaf'. When the imam said the salam, he stood and completed what remained upon him, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3210,"When any one of you is in prayer let him not raise his gaze to the sky lest his sight be taken away."],[3211,"When the prayer is established, do not come to it running, but come to it walking, and upon you is tranquility. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you miss, complete.' He did not mention its prostration."],[3212,"He used to say: 'O Allah, bless Muhammad and his household, his wives, and his offspring, as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And grant barakah to Muhammad and his household, his wives, and his offspring, as You granted barakah to Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.'"],[3213,"He used to say: 'O Allah, accept the great intercession of Muhammad, raise his elevated degree, and grant him his request in the Hereafter and the first life, as You granted Ibrahim and Musa.'"],[3214,"I consider it a favor to me from a man that he would divert me during the prayer."],[3215,"That he disliked it."],[3216,"He said: O Messenger of God, we know how to greet you with peace, so how should we pray for you? He said: \"Say: O God, bestow prayer upon the family of Muhammad; O God, bless the family of Muhammad as You blessed and prayed upon Abraham and the family of Abraham. Truly You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.\""],[3217,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came to us and sat with us in the gathering of Saʿd b. ʿUbādah. Bashīr b. Saʿd, who is Abū al-Nuʿmān b. Bashīr, said to him: God has commanded us to pray for you, so how should we pray for you? He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ remained silent until we wished he had not asked him. Then he said: \"Say: O God, bestow prayer upon Muḥammad and upon the family of Muḥammad, as You bestowed prayer upon Abraham; and bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You blessed the family of Abraham among all the worlds. Truly You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And the greeting of peace is as you have learned.\""],[3218,"He used to say: O God, make Your prayers, Your mercy, and Your blessings upon the Master of the Messengers, the Leader of the God-fearing, the Seal of the Prophets, Muḥammad Your servant and Messenger, the Imam of good, the Guide of good, the Messenger of mercy. O God, raise him to a praised station that the first and the last will envy. O God, bestow prayer upon Muḥammad and upon the family of Muḥammad, as You bestowed prayer upon the family of Abraham. Truly You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O God, bless Muḥammad and upon the family of Muḥammad, as You blessed the family of Abraham. Truly You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."],[3219,"A man asked him about the saying: O God, bestow prayer upon Muḥammad and upon the family of Muḥammad. He said: \"They differed about them. Some of them said: The family of Muḥammad are his household. Others said: Those who obey him.\""],[3220,"You are presented to me with your names and your appearances, so make the prayer upon me excellent."],[3222,"I entered upon the Prophet ﷺ one day and found him cheerful. I said: O Messenger of God, I do not know when I have seen you with a better expression or a more pleasant disposition than today. He said: \"What prevents me? Gabriel left me just now and brought me glad tidings that for every servant who prays for me one prayer, ten good deeds are written for him, ten bad deeds are erased from him, ten degrees are raised for him, it is presented to me as he said it, and it is returned to him with the like of what he prayed.\""],[3223,"A messenger came to me from my Lord and said: No servant prays for you a prayer except that God prays upon him tenfold. A man said: O Messenger of God, should I make half of my supplication for you? He said: \"If you wish.\" He said: Should I make all of my supplication for you? He said: \"Then God will suffice you for the concerns of this world and the Hereafter.\""],[3224,"Whoever prays for me one prayer, God prays upon him, so do it a lot or a little."],[3225,"God has angels who roam the earth, conveying to me the greetings of peace from my community."],[3226,"Do not make me like a rider's bowl, for when a rider wants to set off, he hangs up his provisions and fills a bowl with water. If he needs to perform ablution, he performs ablution; if he needs to drink, he drinks; otherwise he pours it out. So place me in the middle of supplication, at its beginning, and at its end."],[3227,"When a man says to his brother: May God reward you well, he has reached the utmost in praise."],[3228,"Prayer upon no one is appropriate except upon the prophets."],[3231,"It is part of coarseness that I be mentioned in a man's presence and he does not invoke blessings upon me."],[3232,"That he disliked it."],[3233,"There is no servant who supplicates for the believing men and women but that God returns to him for every believing man and woman who has passed away or is to come until the Day of Resurrection the like of what he supplicated."],[3234,"That he disliked it."],[3235,"That he disliked it."],[3236,"Ibn Ṭāwūs caught up with me during circumambulation and struck my shoulder, and said to his father: Your companion intends to make the taslim aloud—meaning Ibn Hishām. He said: The first to make the taslim aloud was ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, and the Anṣār disapproved of that and said: 'And upon you'—that is, 'upon you be peace'—'what is the matter with you?' He said: \"I wanted it to be my announcement.\""],[3237,"What I have not forgotten, among what I have forgotten from the Messenger of God ﷺ, is that he would say the taslim to his right: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God,' until we could see the whiteness of his cheek, and to his left: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God,' until we could see the whiteness of his cheek as well."],[3238,"When the Prophet ﷺ said the taslim, the whiteness of his left cheek could be seen."],[3240,"Ibn Masʿūd would say the taslim to his right: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God and His blessings,' and to his left: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of God and His blessings,' making both of them aloud."],[3241,"That Ali used to give the salutation to his right and to his left: \"Peace be upon you, peace be upon you.\""],[3242,"It is disliked for a man to play with pebbles while praying."],[3243,"She forbade a man to place his fingers on his waist during the prayer, as the Jews do."],[3244,"That Ammar b. Yasir used to give the salutation to his right: \"Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings,\" and to his left the like of that."],[3245,"We used to pray with the Prophet ﷺ and we would say with our hands: \"Peace be upon you.\" He said: \"What is the matter with people who throw their hands as if they were the tails of restive horses? Is it not enough for one of you—or it is only enough for one of you—to place his hand on his thigh and then greet his brother, to his right and to his left?\""],[3246,"He disliked it."],[3247,"He disliked it."],[3248,"He disliked it."],[3249,"He disliked it."],[3250,"He disliked it."],[3251,"When you pray alone, give the salutation to your right: \"Peace,\" and to your left: \"Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah.\" When you are in a row with people on your right and on your left, say: \"Peace be upon you,\" and to your left say: \"Peace be upon us.\" When you are at the edge of the row with people on your right and no one on your left, say to your right: \"Peace be upon you,\" and to your left: \"Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah.\" And if there are people on your left and no one on your right, say: \"Peace be upon you,\" and to your left: \"Peace be upon you.\""],[3252,"He disliked it."],[3255,"The Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman used to say one taslim. Al-Salt said: 'I prayed behind 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz and he said one taslim.'"],[3256,"He disliked it."],[3257,"He disliked it."],[3258,"He disliked it."],[3259,"He disliked it."],[3260,"Ibn 'Umar was asked about leaning on walls during the prayer, and he said: \"We do it, but that diminishes the reward.\""],[3261,"I said to 'Ata': 'What if I delay a little and do not respond to the imam until I finish my need? Is there any harm?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'I saw you do that.' He said: 'Yes, I do not respond to him until the departure is with the taslim.' He said: 'Whichever you do, it does not harm you; it is all the same.' Ibn Jurayj said: I said to 'Ata': 'What if the one on my side greets me—should I make it my taslim of departure and respond to his greeting together, or respond to him and then say the taslim of departure after?' He said: 'Whichever you do, it does not harm you; it is all the same.' Ibn Jurayj said: 'And I saw him do all of that.'"],[3262,"I said to 'Ata': 'What if I delay a little and do not respond to the imam until I finish my need? Is there any harm?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'I saw you do that.' He said: 'Yes, I do not respond to him until the departure is with the taslim.' He said: 'Whichever you do, it does not harm you; it is all the same.' Ibn Jurayj said: I said to 'Ata': 'What if the one on my side greets me—should I make it my taslim of departure and respond to his greeting together, or respond to him and then say the taslim of departure after?' He said: 'Whichever you do, it does not harm you; it is all the same.' Ibn Jurayj said: 'And I saw him do all of that.'"],[3263,"If the imam is on your right, then you say the taslim to your right and intend the imam in that. If he is on your left, you say the taslim and also intend the imam in that. If he is in front of you, you greet him within yourself, then you say the taslim to your right and to your left."],[3265,"It is disliked for a man to pray leaning against a wall except for an excuse."],[3267,"I said to 'Ata': A rak'ah with the imam eludes me, then the imam gives the taslim; should I stand up and make it up, or should I wait for him to stand? He said: \"Wait a little; if he remains seated, then stand and leave him.\""],[3268,"Whenever Ibn 'Umar missed part of the prayer, when the imam gave the taslim he would stand up to make up what he had missed; if he had not missed anything, he would not stand until the imam stood up."],[3269,"She saw a woman supplicating while raising her finger next to her two thumbs, so 'A'ishah said to her: 'He is but God, one God,' forbidding her from that."],[3270,"I prayed one rak'ah of Fajr with the people of Madinah, and when the imam gave the taslim I stood up to make up my rak'ah, but they pulled me back. I mentioned that to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and he said: \"That is what they used to do, but they feared the sword.\""],[3271,"The one whom the imam preceded should not make up until the imam turns away from his innovation; a man is only ordered to sit for fear that the imam may have made an error.\" He said: \"His innovation is facing the qiblah after the taslim."],[3272,"Whatever you catch with the imam is the beginning of your prayer, and make up what he preceded you with in recitation."],[3273,"Three things that people have introduced: shortening the prostration, raising the hands, and raising the voice in supplication."],[3274,"When one of you prays, let him not turn around. He is conversing intimately with his Lord; indeed his Lord is before him, and he is conversing intimately with Him. He said: 'And it has reached us that the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, says: O son of Adam, to whom do you turn? I am better for you than the one you turn to.'"],[3275,"If the imam gives you opportunity, then recite in the two rak'ahs that remain, a surah in each, and make them the beginning of your prayer."],[3277,"Jundub and Masruq caught one rak'ah of Maghrib. Jundub recited, and Masruq did not recite behind the imam. When the imam gave the taslim, they both stood up to make up. Masruq sat in the second and third, while Jundub stood in the second and did not sit. When they finished, they discussed that and went to Ibn Mas'ud. He said: \"Each has done right—or each has done well—and we do as Masruq did.\""],[3278,"Jundub and Masruq caught one rak'ah of Maghrib. One of them recited in the other two rak'ahs what he had missed of recitation, and the other did not recite in either rak'ah. Ibn Mas'ud was asked, and he said: \"Both of you have done well, and I do as this one who recited in the two rak'ahs did.\""],[3280,"He prays with the imam whatever he catches, and he makes up what he was preceded in with the imam regarding the recitation."],[3281,"That he disliked it."],[3283,"He used to be commanded: if he turned around a lot during the prayer, he should close his eyes."],[3284,"He disliked it."],[3285,"He disliked it."],[3286,"He disliked it."],[3287,"In the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, when a man came and had missed something of the prayer, the people gestured to him, so he prayed what he had missed and then entered the prayer, until one day Muʿādh b. Jabal came. They gestured to him, but he entered and did not wait for what they said. When the Prophet ﷺ had prayed, they mentioned that to him, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"Muʿādh has established a practice for you.\""],[3288,"He disliked it."],[3289,"He disliked it."],[3290,"He disliked it."],[3291,"Ibn Umar would catch the imam while prostrating and would perform the two prostrations with him, but he would not count them."],[3292,"Whoever sets out to attend the prayer, and the iqamah is called while he is on the road, let him not hurry, nor increase beyond his initial walking pace. What he catches, let him pray with the imam; and what he does not catch, let him complete it."],[3293,"Tell me about a prayer in which you sit throughout?\" He said: So we said to him, and he said: \"It is Maghrib; you caught one rak‘ah of it, so you sat with the imam, then you broke (your prayer) and prayed a rak‘ah, then you sat, then you prayed another rak‘ah and sat in it, and he did not mention any prostration in it."],[3294,"I caught one rak‘ah of Maghrib—should I pair it with another, then start my prayer afresh? He said: \"The Sunnah is better: pray what you caught, and complete what you missed.\" He said: I said, \"Should I recite?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[3295,"From the Prophet ﷺ after his prayer: thirty-three takbirs, thirty-three tasbihs, thirty-three tahmids, and one lā ilāha illā Allāh."],[3296,"Some of his companions came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Prophet of Allah, your companions—meaning the earlier companions—have surpassed us in deeds. He said: \"Shall I not inform you of something you can do after the obligatory prayers, by which you will catch up with those who preceded you and surpass those who come after you?\" They said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. So he commanded them to say Allāhu Akbar thirty-four times, Subḥān Allāh thirty-three times, and Alḥamdu lillāh thirty-three times. He said: Then a man informed us about that; he said: The poor came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, the wealthy have overtaken us in reward, so command us with an action by which we can catch up with their deeds. He told them the same as what ‘Aṭā’ said. When the owners of wealth learned of it, they took it up. When the poor saw that, they came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, and he said: \"These are the virtues.\""],[3297,"He commanded a man to say Subḥān Allāh thirty-three times after the prayer, Alḥamdu lillāh thirty-three times, and Allāhu Akbar thirty-four times."],[3298,"They pray alone."],[3299,"Some of the poor believers said: O Messenger of Allah, the wealthy have taken the rewards: they give charity, but we cannot give charity; they spend, but we cannot spend. He said: \"Do you think that if the wealth of this world were piled up one on top of another, it would reach the sky?\" They said: No, O Messenger of Allah. He said: \"Shall I not tell you of something whose root is in the earth and whose branch is in the sky? It is to say after every prayer: Lā ilāha illā Allāh, Allāhu Akbar, Subḥān Allāh, and Alḥamdu lillāh ten times. Their root is in the earth and their branch is in the sky.\""],[3300,"Two qualities, no Muslim man keeps count of them except that he enters Paradise, and they are easy, but few people act upon them.\" They said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: \"Each of you glorifies Allah ten times, praises Him ten times, and magnifies Him ten times after every prayer—that is one hundred and fifty on the tongue and one thousand five hundred in the balance. And when one of you goes to his bed, he magnifies Allah, praises Him, and glorifies Him one hundred times—that is one hundred on the tongue and one thousand in the balance. So which of you commits two thousand five hundred sins in his day and night?\" He said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ counting like this—and he counted with his fingers. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how could we not keep count of them? He said: \"Satan comes to one of you during his prayer and says to him: Remember such-and-such a need and such-and-such a need, until he leaves without having remembered. And he comes to him at his sleep and makes him fall asleep without having remembered.\""],[3301,"Two qualities—whoever guards them will enter Paradise: whoever glorifies Allah ten times after every prayer."],[3302,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say after a prayer: O Allah, I ask You for good provision, accepted deeds, and beneficial knowledge."],[3303,"Whoever says after every prayer—Ibn Abi Husayn said in his hadith, while he is folding his leg—before speaking: There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise. He gives life and causes death. In His hand is all good, and He is capable of all things, ten times. Allah writes for him ten good deeds for each one, erases ten bad deeds from him, raises him ten degrees, and for each one he says, he has the equivalent of freeing a slave from the descendants of Ismail. They are a barrier and a guard against Satan and a protection from every disliked thing. No deed will overcome them unless he associates partners with Allah."],[3304,"They are follow-up phrases whose sayer—or he said doer—will not be disappointed: whoever glorifies Allah after every prayer thirty-three times, praises Him thirty-three times, and magnifies Him thirty-four times."],[3305,"Whoever declares Allah's oneness after the obligatory prayer one hundred times, glorifies Him one hundred times, praises Him one hundred times, and magnifies Him one hundred times, his sins will be forgiven even if they are like the foam of the sea."],[3306,"Whoever says after every prayer: I seek forgiveness from Allah, besides whom there is no god, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer, and I turn to Him in repentance, three times, Allah will expiate his sins even if he fled from the battlefield."],[3307,"Whoever is pleased to measure with the fullest measure, let him say when he finishes his prayer: 'Glory to your Lord, the Lord of Might, above what they describe' (Q 37:180), 'Peace be upon the messengers' (Q 37:181), 'And praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds' (Q 37:182)."],[3308,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prayer, he said: O Allah, You are Peace, and from You is peace. Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor."],[3309,"He used to say when he finished his prayer: With the praise of my Lord I depart, and with my sins I confess. I seek refuge in my Lord from the evil of what I have committed. O Turner of hearts, turn my heart to what You love and are pleased with."],[3310,"Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: When a servant's praise of Me occupies him from asking Me, I give him the best of what I give to those who ask."],[3311,"Whoever says: There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise, and He is capable of all things, one hundred times, he will surpass every deed except for one who does more."],[3312,"There is no harm in counting the takbir and tasbih in prayer according to what has come in the hadiths."],[3313,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the morning prayer, he sat in his place until the sun rose."],[3314,"Is a man who sits in his place of prayer after Fajr more beloved to you, or the one who comes to the obligatory prayers? He said: \"Rather, the one who sits in his place is more beloved to me.\""],[3315,"I said to 'Ata': The number of glorifications, takbirs, and praises after the prescribed prayer that you mentioned—is that more beloved to you, or should we add to it? He said: \"Yes.\" He said: I said: Is it more beloved to you not to stand until you finish your glorifications? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Why? He said: \"Because they say: The angels do not cease to pray for a person as long as he does not rise from his place of prayer in which he prayed, as long as he does not invalidate his ablution.\" He said: \"And I love for that to be at the end of the prescribed prayer.\" I said: Do you deem it desirable not to speak until you finish it? He said: \"Yes, by Allah, but they do not let us.\""],[3316,"I said: Has anything reached you from those who came before regarding sitting after the taslim? He said: \"No.\" I said: I saw you sitting. He said: \"Glory be to Allah, I remember Allah.\" I said: Could you not finish your need before you say the taslim, so that when you have said it, there is nothing but standing? He said: \"Rather, I say the taslim and rest, then I devote myself to declaring Allah's oneness, glorifying Him, praising Him, and remembering Him.\""],[3318,"The Prophet ﷺ would depart sometimes on his right and sometimes on his left, and he would hold his right hand over his left in prayer."],[3319,"Let none of you give the devil a share of himself, thinking that he must depart on his right. He said: \"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ most often departing on his left.\""],[3320,"When the imam says the taslim, depart wherever your need is, right or left, and do not turn around like a donkey."],[3322,"Ibn 'Umar did not care on which side he departed, his right or his left. He said: \"That is because I asked him about that.\""],[3323,"I prayed and saw Ibn 'Umar sitting, so I turned to my left and sat with him. He said: \"What prevented you from turning to your right?\" He said: I said: I saw you and turned to you. He said: \"You did well. Some people say: Do not turn except to your right.\""],[3324,"It does not harm him whether he departs on his right or on his left. I said: Which of the two is preferred? He said: \"They are equal.\""],[3325,"Abu Bakr, when he said the taslim to his right and to his left, said: \"Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah,\" then he would depart at that moment as if he had been sitting on hot stones."],[3326,"Abu Bakr, when he said the taslim, was as if he were on hot stones until he got up."],[3327,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr or 'Asr and said the taslim after two rak'ahs, then he left. The swift among the people went out and said: \"Has the prayer been lightened for us?\" Dhu al-Shimalayn said: \"O Messenger of God, has the prayer been lightened?\" He said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"What does Dhu al-Yadayn say?\" They said: \"He has spoken the truth.\" He said: So he prayed the two rak'ahs that he had left, then prostrated two prostrations after the taslim while sitting."],[3329,"When a man is uncertain in his prayer and does not know whether he has prayed three or two, let him build upon the more certain of that, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3330,"When the imam says the taslim, he should get up, or else he should turn away from his place. I said, 'Is it sufficient for him to turn away from his place and face the qiblah?' He said, 'Turning away is that he turn east or west, according to more than one.'"],[3331,"Mujahid prayed behind Ibrahim al-Nakhaʿi, and when he had said the taslim he turned away, and he said, 'It is not from the Sunnah that you sit until you stand, then you may sit afterwards if Allah wills.'"],[3332,"It is not from the Sunnah that he sit until he stands. When he had finished, he stood, then he sat down—meaning he turned east or west. As for facing the qiblah, no."],[3333,"From Ibn Masʿud that when he said the taslim, he would get up from his place, or turn away eastward or westward."],[3334,"When you are behind the imam, do not bow until he bows, do not prostrate until he prostrates, and do not raise your head before him. If the imam finishes and does not get up or turn away, and you have a need, then go and leave him, for your prayer is complete."],[3335,"And he should not leave until the imam gets up. Al-Zuhri said, 'The imam is appointed only to be followed, and he should not leave.'"],[3336,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say when he said the taslim: 'There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise. O Allah, none can withhold what You give, and none can give what You withhold, and no possessor of fortune can benefit from You his fortune.' Warrad said, 'Then afterwards I came as a delegate to Muʿawiyah, and I heard him on the minbar commanding the people to say that and teaching them.'"],[3337,"That raising the voice in dhikr when the people finished the obligatory prayer was practiced in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Ibn ʿAbbas said, 'I would know when they had finished by that when I heard it.'"],[3338,"ʿAbidah was holding my hand when he heard the voice of al-Musʿab b. al-Zubayr saying, 'There is no god but Allah and Allah is greater,' facing the qiblah after he had said the taslim from the prayer. ʿAbidah said, 'What is wrong with him? May Allah fight him! A loud proclaimer of innovations.'"],[3339,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he finished his prayer, remained seated for a little while. They used to think that this was so that the women could leave before the men."],[3342,"The imam speaks if he sits down. If he speaks and does not stand up with him, if he wishes.\" I said: \"Does he leave his speech in the status of his speech?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[3343,"I prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and the moment he said the taslim he stood up. Then I prayed behind Abu Bakr, and when he said the taslim he sprang up, as if he were rising from a hot stone."],[3344,"If a man recites the tashahhud and fears that he may invalidate his ablution before the imam says the taslim, let him say the taslim, for his prayer is complete."],[3345,"The Prophet ﷺ supplicated against a people and raised his hands - Amr gestured to me and held his hands upright - very high toward the sky. The she-camel moved, so he held it with one of his hands while the other remained raised toward the sky."],[3346,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to raise his hands to his chest in supplication, then wipe his face with them."],[3347,"The Prophet ﷺ was supplicating while the nose-rein was between his two fingers. The nose-rein fell, so he bent down to pick it up, and he pointed with the finger next to the thumb and raised it."],[3348,"The Prophet ﷺ used to say in his prayer like this, and he pointed with his index finger."],[3349,"When he sat in prayer, he placed his hands on his knees and raised his right finger, the one next to the thumb, and supplicated with them, while his left hand was on his knee, spread out upon it."],[3350,"'Umar saw me while I was toying with pebbles during the prayer. When he finished, he forbade me and said: 'Do as the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do. When he sat in the prayer, he would place his right palm on his right thigh, clench his fingers, point with the finger next to the thumb, and place his left palm on his left thigh.'"],[3351,"A man next to him—while they were with the judge—when the judge supplicated, the man raised his hands. Ibn 'Umar nudged him and gestured to him with a finger toward the ground. Then the judge supplicated another time, and the man forgot and again raised his hand. Ibn 'Umar nudged him and gestured to him in the same way."],[3352,"Ibn 'Umar saw a man pointing with two fingers, so Ibn 'Umar said to him: 'God is but one God. Point with one finger when you point.'"],[3353,"He would point with his finger when he supplicated without moving it, and the Prophet ﷺ leaned with his left hand on his left leg, and that is in pairs."],[3354,"She saw a woman supplicating while raising her finger next to her two thumbs, so 'A'ishah said to her: 'He is but God, one God,' forbidding her from that."],[3357,"Part of seventy parts of prophethood is delaying the pre-dawn meal, hastening the breaking of the fast, and a man's pointing with his finger during the prayer."],[3358,"Supplication with earnest entreaty is like this—and he spread his hands with their backs toward his face—and supplication is like this—and he raised his hands up to his beard—and sincerity is like this,' pointing with his finger."],[3359,"The Messenger of God ﷺ would raise his hands supplicating until I would tire for him from how much he raised them: 'O God, I am only a human, so do not punish me for insulting a man whom I insulted or harmed.'"],[3360,"The Messenger of God ﷺ passed by a group of Bedouins who had accepted Islam, and the confederates had destroyed their lands. The Messenger of God ﷺ raised his hands supplicating for them, spreading his hands in front of his face. A Bedouin said to him: 'Stretch out, O Messenger of God, may my father and mother be your ransom.' He said: So the Messenger of God ﷺ stretched out his hands in front of his face and did not raise them toward the sky."],[3361,"Your Lord is modest and generous; He is too modest, when a servant raises his hands to Him, to turn them back empty until He places good in them."],[3362,"Three things that people have introduced: shortening the prostration, raising the hands, and raising the voice in supplication."],[3363,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered the mosque and saw them raising their hands in prayer. He said: \"What is the matter with them raising their hands as if they were the tails of restive horses? Be still in prayer.\""],[3364,"The Prophet ﷺ saw some people raising their hands above their heads in prayer, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Thawri. The hidden text: The Messenger of God ﷺ entered the mosque and saw them raising their hands in prayer. He said: \"What is the matter with them raising their hands as if they were the tails of restive horses? Be still in prayer.\""],[3365,"Regarding a man who forgot and stood up in the two rakʿahs of sitting: he sits as long as he has not become fully upright."],[3366,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by a man supplicating with two fingers, so he took one of them and said: \"Make it one, make it one.\""],[3367,"Ibn Umar used to spread out his hands along with the forearm. They mentioned that those who have passed used to supplicate and then pass their hands over their faces to bring back the supplication and blessing."],[3368,"When any one of you is in prayer let him not raise his gaze to the sky lest his sight be taken away."],[3370,"He utters the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness and gives taslim."],[3371,"We said to him: Where should the gaze go in prayer? He said: \"If it is where one prostrates, then that is good.\""],[3372,"You forgot as I forgot."],[3373,"The Prophet ﷺ used to raise his head to the sky while praying until God revealed: \"Those who are humble in their prayers,\" or other than it. If it was not that, I do not know what it was. Then he lowered his head."],[3375,"He was asked about His saying: \"Those who are humble in their prayer.\" He said: \"Do not turn around in your prayer, and that you relax your shoulder for the Muslim man.\""],[3376,"When a man did not see such-and-such, he would be commanded to close his eyes."],[3377,"When the servant turns around in his prayer, God says: 'I am better for you than the one you turn to.' If he does it a second time, He says the like of that; if he does it a third time, He turns away from him."],[3378,"I said to Ata: 'I look to my right and to my left during the prayer. Does turning around cut off the prayer?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'Should I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness?' He said: '...'"],[3379,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May one count the obligatory prayer with pebbles and lines? He said: \"There is no harm.\""],[3380,"He disliked it."],[3381,"He disliked it."],[3382,"He disliked it."],[3383,"When the Prophet ﷺ entered the prayer, he cast his gaze right and left without turning his neck."],[3384,"He disliked it."],[3385,"He saw al-Qasim or Salim praying while he was looking to his right and to his left."],[3386,"When the servant performs ablution and does it well, then stands for prayer, God faces him with His face, conversing intimately with him, and He does not turn away from him until he is the one who turns away or turns right or left."],[3387,"When the servant turns around during the prayer, it is only a devil twisting his neck."],[3388,"I was praying with Ibn Umar behind me, and I was not aware of him. He placed his hand on the back of my neck and squeezed me."],[3389,"I asked 'A'ishah about turning around during the prayer, and she said: 'It is a snatching away that Satan snatches from the prayer.'"],[3391,"Whoever catches a rak'ah of the prayer has certainly caught the prayer."],[3392,"I saw the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and one of them would bear witness while standing in prayer."],[3393,"'A'ishah would order her servant to divide the broth, and she would pass by her while she was praying, and she would gesture to her: 'Add more.'"],[3394,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed in the house of Umm Salamah. 'Umar b. Abi Salamah came to pass in front of him, so he gestured to him, and he went back. Then Zaynab bt. Abi Salamah came, and he gestured to her, but she went on. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'You women are more disobedient.'"],[3395,"I consider it a favor to me from a man that he would divert me during the prayer."],[3396,"I saw Ibn 'Umar gesturing to me and to a man in the row when he saw a gap: 'Move forward.'"],[3397,"Regarding a man who was praying and a man passed by him, and he said to him: 'You did such and such,' and he became distressed, he said: 'Let him complete his prayer and prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.'"],[3398,"I said to 'Ata': A person passes by me and I say 'Subhan Allah' two or three times, and he comes forward, and I say with my hand 'go away,' and he says: 'I have such and such,' while I am in the prescribed prayer. Has my prayer been interrupted? He said: 'No, but I dislike it.' I said: 'Should I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness?' He said: 'No. It has reached us that a person does not fear anything more severe for him than his prayer, so I fear that that would be a deficiency in it.'"],[3399,"I said to 'Ata': Do you dislike every kind of gesturing in the prescribed prayer? If a man comes and says: 'Have you prayed the prayer?' I dislike to gesture to him with my head? He said: 'Yes, I dislike all of that.'"],[3401,"He said: A person said to 'Ata': A person comes to me while I am in the prescribed prayer and tells me some news, and I listen to him. He said: 'I do not like that, unless it happens absentmindedly. It is only the prescribed prayer, so devote yourself to it until you finish it.'"],[3402,"That a person sought permission to approach the Prophet ﷺ with a gift, and the Prophet ﷺ took it with his hand while he was in prayer."],[3403,"About a man who was praying and feared that his mount would go away, or that a wild beast would attack it? They both said: 'He should leave the prayer.' It was said: 'Should he then complete what he has already prayed?'"],[3404,"That Abu Barzah al-Aslami feared a lion for his mount, so he walked toward it while he was in prayer and took hold of it."],[3405,"That Abu Barzah al-Aslami was praying, and he feared for his mule, so he walked toward it until he took hold of it while he was praying."],[3406,"I asked him. He said: I said: A man is praying and sees a child at a well, fearing that he may fall into it—should he leave the prayer? He said: 'Yes.' I said: And if he sees a thief intending to take his mule? He said: 'He should leave the prayer.'"],[3407,"A man asked him, saying: A sheep enters my house while I am praying, so I lower my head and take a stick and strike it. He said: 'There is no harm in that.'"],[3408,"That Abu Barzah al-Aslami's mount got loose while he was in prayer, so he left the prayer and took hold of it."],[3409,"He disliked that a man should crack his fingers during the prayer."],[3410,"When one of you is coming to the prayer and the iqamah is called, he should walk calmly, for he is in prayer. Whatever he catches up with, he prays, and whatever he misses, he makes up afterwards.\" 'Ata' said: \"And I find this within myself.\" I said: \"So you do not hurry when the iqamah is called, even if you are performing ablution or bathing?\" He said: \"Yes, I do not hurry away from that.\""],[3411,"I said to 'Ata': A man stretches during the prayer? He said: 'Nothing has reached me about it, but I do not like it.' I said: What about cracking the neck and the fingers and the like during the prayer? He said: 'I dislike it.' I said: What about clearing the throat, blowing the nose, spitting, and a man putting his hand into his nose? He said: 'Do not do that in the prayer.' I said: What about scratching oneself during the prayer, and putting on a cloak, and wrapping a waist-wrapper during the prayer? He said: 'All of that you should not do in the prayer.'"],[3412,"And I dislike that he move about excessively. I said: If I did something of what I mentioned to you, should I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: \"No.\""],[3413,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Is wiping the two feet disliked during the obligatory prayer? He said: \"Indeed I prefer that a man minimize movement.\""],[3415,"Ibn ʿUmar would recite al-Baqarah in a single rakʿah, and he was slow in recitation, so he would tap the ground with his toes."],[3416,"Indeed I prefer that one minimize movement in prayer, and that he stand upright on his feet, unless he is an elderly person who cannot do that. As for long standing, one must lean on one hip at times and on the other at times."],[3418,"When ʿAbdullāh prayed, he was like a garment cast down."],[3419,"When al-Zubayr prayed, he was like a firmly fixed ankle."],[3420,"\"Make the prayer tranquil\"—he said: \"Be still, be at ease.\""],[3421,"Begin with that which you sought."],[3422,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: May a man bow while his feet are side by side, one of them extending beyond the other? He said: \"There is no harm in that.\""],[3423,"Ibn al-Musayyib saw a man playing with his beard during prayer and said: \"Indeed I think that if this man's heart were humble, his limbs would be humble.\""],[3424,"Ibn al-Musayyib saw me playing with pebbles during prayer and said: If this one's heart were humble, his limbs would be humble."],[3425,"He used to dislike every kind of idle play during prayer."],[3426,"It is disliked for a man to play with pebbles while praying."],[3429,"Masruq saw me playing with pebbles with my hand during prayer, and he struck my hand."],[3430,"He saw a man moving pebbles while he was in prayer, and 'Abd Allah said: When you ask your Lord in prayer, do not ask Him while there is a stone in your hand."],[3431,"It used to be said regarding wiping the beard during prayer: Once, or leave it. He said: I asked Mujahid about rain mud that gets on a garment. He said: Scrape it off when it dries."],[3433,"Yawning is disliked during prayer and at other times. He said: He said: Satan plays with a person. He said: And it is more intense during prayer."],[3434,"Seven things are from Satan: nosebleed, vomiting, severe sneezing, yawning, drowsiness at the time of exhortation, anger, and private conversation."],[3435,"Satan has a bottle containing a puff of air, so when people stand for prayer he makes them smell it, and they yawn. Whoever experiences that is commanded to press his lips and nostrils together."],[3436,"When a man is reciting and yawns, he should stop the recitation."],[3437,"Begin with that which you sought."],[3438,"When one of you yawns during the prayer, let him place his hand over his mouth, for it is from Shaytan."],[3439,"When one of you yawns, let him close (his mouth) as much as he is able."],[3440,"When one of you yawns, let him place his hand over his mouth, for Shaytan enters with the yawn."],[3441,"When a person says during yawning, 'hah, hah,' Shaytan laughs from inside him."],[3442,"He disliked that a man should crack his fingers during the prayer."],[3444,"It is disliked that a man close his eyes during the prayer as the Jews close them."],[3445,"When a man did not see such-and-such, he would be commanded to close his eyes."],[3446,"There is no man who performs ablution in his house, then goes out intending the prayer, except that he is in prayer until he completes his prayer. So he should not interlace his fingers during the prayer."],[3447,"When one of you performs ablution in his house, then goes out intending the prayer, he remains in a state of prayer until he returns. So do not say 'like this' — then he interlaced the fingers, the fingers of one hand with the other."],[3448,"When you perform wudu and do it well, then you head to the mosque, you are indeed in prayer, so do not interlace your fingers."],[3449,"When you perform wudu then go out heading to the mosque, do not interlace your fingers, for you are in prayer."],[3451,"3363 3335 - Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn 'Ajlan, from Ibn al-Musayyib, similar to it, except that he did not trace it back to the Prophet ﷺ. Hidden text: When you perform wudu then go out heading to the mosque, do not interlace your fingers, for you are in prayer."],[3452,"The Prophet ﷺ met a man while he was interlacing one of his hands with the other. He said, \"Where do you intend to go?\" The man said, \"The mosque.\" The Prophet ﷺ separated the man's fingers, then said, \"When one of you leaves his house for the mosque, he should not do this interlacing.\""],[3453,"He disliked that a man interlace his fingers during the prayer, and that he pray while having his hair tied."],[3454,"She forbade a man to place his fingers on his waist during the prayer, as the Jews do."],[3455,"When one of you stands for prayer, he should not place his hand on his waist, for Satan attends that."],[3456,"A person placing his hand on his hip is the posture of the people of the Fire."],[3458,"Placing the hand on the waist is the posture of the people of the Fire."],[3459,"God has disliked for you three things: idle talk during the Quran, raising the voice in supplication, and putting the hand on the waist during prayer."],[3460,"While I was sitting with him, he saw a man in prayer taking his hand out of his garment behind his back. He said to me, \"Stand up to this man and tell him to put his hand from the place of the shackle.\" He said, \"And he saw a man standing in prayer who had put his hand on his hip. He said to me, \"Stand up to this man and tell him to put his hand from the place of the poet's hand.\""],[3461,"He used to dislike a man placing his left hand at his side and placing his right palm between his left upper arm and his side, and he disliked him gripping with his right palm his left upper arm, or with his left palm his right upper arm."],[3462,"I said to 'Ata': Should I grip with my two palms one upon the other, or at the head of the forearm, then let them hang down? He said: \"There is no harm in that.\""],[3463,"He used to pray letting his hands hang down."],[3465,"Regarding a man who prayed Zuhr with five [rak'ahs], he said: \"He prostrates twice while sitting.\""],[3466,"There is no harm in shifting from one foot to the other during the prayer."],[3467,"He saw Jabir b. 'Abd Allah praying while leaning on a wall."],[3469,"Ibn 'Umar was asked about leaning on walls during the prayer, and he said: \"We do it, but that diminishes the reward.\""],[3470,"I know that placing one's hand on the wall during the prayer diminishes the reward."],[3471,"It is disliked for a man to pray leaning against a wall except for an excuse."],[3472,"Zayd b. Thabit and Ibn 'Umar used to give the legal opinion that when a man reaches people while they are bowing, he should say one takbir, and he has caught the rak'ah. They said: \"And if he finds them prostrating, he prostrates with them but does not count that.\""],[3473,"If one stands when one should sit, then when one finishes one's prayer one performs the two prostrations of forgetfulness and utters two tashahhuds."],[3474,"One takbir suffices him, and if he says two takbirs it is more beloved to us."],[3477,"If you enter the mosque while the imam is bowing and you say the takbir, then you do not bow until he raises his head, so do not count it (that rak’ah)."],[3478,"He forbade that the skins of beasts of prey be used as floor coverings or worn."],[3479,"If you catch the imam while he is bowing and you bow before he raises, then you have caught (the rak’ah), and if he raises before you bow, then it has missed you."],[3481,"About a man who said the takbir with the imam at the beginning of the prayer, then dozed until the imam had prayed one rak’ah or two. He said: “When he wakes, he bows and prostrates for what the imam preceded him in, and he follows the imam for what remains, bowing and prostrating without recitation.”"],[3482,"About a man who entered with a people in their prayer and dozed until the imam bowed. He said: “He follows the imam.”"],[3483,"About a man who entered with the imam in prayer, then from his drowsiness he bowed and prostrated, then woke. He said: “He follows the imam.”"],[3484,"I said to him: If I said the takbir with the imam to open the prayer, then the imam bowed and I forgot and did not bow until the imam bowed? He said: “Then you have caught it, so count it.”"],[3485,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I dozed and remained standing until the people bowed and prostrated, and a man pulled me, so I sat down as I was? He said: “Complete that rak’ah.”"],[3486,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: The prayer was established while I was with the people, and the imam said the takbir and raised from the rak’ah, and I did not say the takbir in that. He said: “If you had straightened yourself in the row, then count it, and if you had not ceased moving until he bowed and raised his head from his rak’ah, then say the takbir, then rise, and count it, and if you had not straightened yourself in the row, then no.”"],[3492,"A man entered the mosque while the Prophet ﷺ was in prayer, and he heard the sound of his sandals. When he finished, he said, 'In what state did you find us?' He said, 'Prostrating, so I prostrated.' He said, 'Do likewise, and do not count the prostration unless you catch the rak'ah. If you find the imam standing, then stand; or sitting, then sit; or bowing, then bow; or prostrating, then prostrate; or sitting, then sit.'"],[3494,"There is no tashahhud and no taslim in them."],[3495,"Abu Bakrah entered the mosque while the imam was bowing, so he bowed before reaching the row. The Prophet ﷺ said to him, 'May Allah increase your keenness, but do not do it again.'"],[3497,"The Prophet ﷺ heard a man hurrying to the prayer while he was bowing, and he said, 'May Allah increase your keenness, but do not do it again.'"],[3498,"There is no tashahhud and no taslim in them."],[3500,"Zayd b. Thabit would bow and then walk while still bowing."],[3501,"I and Ibn Mas'ud entered the mosque while the imam was bowing, so we bowed, then we proceeded until we stood straight in the row. When the imam finished, I stood up to pray and he said, \"You have caught it.\""],[3502,"There is no harm in bowing before the row."],[3504,"I saw Sa'id b. Jubayr enter while the imam was bowing and he bowed before passing behind, then he proceeded as he was, still bowing."],[3506,"If the imam makes a mistake and does not prostrate, then there is no forgetfulness or prostration upon those behind him."],[3507,"Ibn Mas'ud caught up with a group of people sitting at the end of their prayer, so he said, \"I have caught it, if God wills.\""],[3508,"I saw 'Uthman b. 'Affan perform ablution: he washed his palms three times each, rinsed his mouth, sniffed water and blew it out, washed his face three times—he said: and I think he said—and his forearms three times each, then wiped his head and his ears, their outside and inside, and washed his feet three times each, and ran his fingers between his fingers and through his beard when he washed his face before washing his feet, then said: 'I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do just as you have seen me do.'"],[3510,"When he reaches them while they are prostrating, he prostrates with them and says the takbir. If it is in an even-numbered prostration, he stands with another takbir, and if it is in an odd-numbered one, he stands without takbir."],[3511,"Regarding a man who enters while the imam is prostrating, or just as he raises his head from a rak'ah, or sitting doing the tashahhud—does he say the opening takbir of the prayer? He said: \"If he wishes he may say the takbir, and if he wishes he may not. But when the imam stands and says the salam, then he says the takbir and begins his prayer.\""],[3512,"I said: A man came when the imam had already bowed in the last rak'ah of the obligatory prayer, so he prostrated with him two prostrations and did the tashahhud with the imam, then the imam said the salam. May the man then speak if he wishes and go to another place of prayer? He said: \"Yes indeed, the rak'ah has passed him by, so he may speak if he wishes, for he is not in prayer.\""],[3513,"Concerning a man who arrives after the imam has already said the salam (and he is still supplicating), should he begin with the opening takbir? He said: \"He sits as long as the imam remains seated.\""],[3514,"If you find the imam and the people sitting in the final part of the prayer, say the takbir while standing, then sit and say the takbir when you sit. That makes two takbirs: the first while you are standing is for the opening of the prayer, and the other when you sit is as if for the prostration. Then do not speak, for the prayer has become obligatory upon you, and you have begun it. However, do not count your sitting with them, and say what they say while you are sitting with them.\""],[3515,"I saw Sa'id b. Jubayr enter while the imam was bowing and he bowed before passing behind, then he proceeded as he was, still bowing."],[3516,"Ibn 'Umar, when he caught one sajdah with the imam, would add another to it, and when he finished his prayer he would perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness.\""],[3517,"If the imam makes a mistake, those behind him prostrate. If one of those behind him makes a mistake, there is nothing upon them, so that no mistake along with the imam harms them."],[3519,"\"Whatever you catch up with, pray it, and whatever you miss, make it up,\" and he did not mention any prostration."],[3520,"If you catch one sajdah with the imam, then prostrate with him, then rise from it without adding another to it, and do not count it.\""],[3521,"If you catch the imam while he is prostrating, he (the latecomer) says one takbir, intending by it the opening of the prayer, and prostrates with them, but when he stands up he says the takbir.\""],[3522,"When one of you is coming to the prayer and the iqamah is called, he should walk calmly, for he is in prayer. Whatever he catches up with, he prays, and whatever he misses, he makes up afterwards.\" 'Ata' said: \"And I find this within myself.\" I said: \"So you do not hurry when the iqamah is called, even if you are performing ablution or bathing?\" He said: \"Yes, I do not hurry away from that.\""],[3523,"When the call to prayer is given, come to it walking, with tranquility upon you. Whatever you catch up with, pray, and whatever you miss, complete."],[3524,"When the prayer is established, do not come to it running, but come to it walking, and upon you is tranquility. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you miss, complete.' He did not mention its prostration."],[3525,"You forgot as I forgot."],[3526,"A man entered while the Prophet ﷺ was praying, and he was out of breath. He said: 'Praise be to Allah, much, good and blessed in it.' When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, he said: 'Who is the speaker of these words?' twice. A man stood up to him and said: 'I am, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Twelve angels raced to it, which of them would ascend with it, and thereby greet Allah, the Blessed and Exalted.' He said: 'Why do I hear your breath?' He said: 'The prayer was called, so I hurried.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'When you hear the iqamah, walk at your normal pace. What you catch, pray; and what you miss, complete.'"],[3527,"The prayer was called while Anas b. Malik had placed his hand on me. He said: I was reluctant to lift his hand from me, and he began taking short steps. We reached the mosque, and we had been preceded by one rak'ah. We prayed with the imam and completed what we had missed. Anas b. Malik said to me: 'O Thabit, act according to what I did with you.' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'My brother Zayd b. Thabit did it with me.'"],[3528,"3566 3538 - Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from someone other than him, from Ibrahim, the like of al-Hasan's statement. Hidden text: Concerning a man who misses part of the prayer with the imam during the days of Tashriq, Ibrahim said: He says the takbir with the imam when he says takbir, then he stands and makes up what he missed."],[3529,"Ibn Mas'ud hastened to the prayer, and it was said to him. He said: 'Is not the prayer the most deserving of what I hasten to?'"],[3530,"Ibn Umar heard the iqamah while he was at al-Baqi', so he quickened his walk to the mosque."],[3531,"Whoever sets out to attend the prayer, and the iqamah is called while he is on the road, let him not hurry, nor increase beyond his initial walking pace. What he catches, let him pray with the imam; and what he does not catch, let him complete it."],[3532,"When the prayer approaches, walk in your usual manner, pray what you catch, and complete what you missed. Do not wipe the ground except once, and to refrain from it is better for you than a hundred camels, all of them black-eyed."],[3534,"I said to Ata: 'A group of people entered the mosque of Makkah outside the prayer, by night or day; they now disapprove of that.'"],[3535,"Anas b. Malik passed by us, and with him were some companions. He said: 'Have you prayed?' We said: 'Yes.' He said: Then he dismounted and led his companions in prayer, so he went forward and prayed with them. Abu Uthman said: Then he sat, and we placed for him a blanket and two cushions. He narrated to us a good hadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then he mounted and left."],[3536,"Anas b. Malik passed by us, and with him were companions, about ten in number, and we had prayed the morning prayer. He said: 'Have you prayed?' We said: 'Yes.' He said: Then he ordered one of them, and he called the adhan, and he prayed two rak'ahs. Then he ordered him, and he called the iqamah. Then Anas went forward and prayed two rak'ahs with his companions. Then he departed, and they had placed for him a cushion and a pillow. He narrated to us, and among what he narrated to us, he said: 'My mother, Umm Sulaym, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, may my mother and father be your ransom, O Messenger of Allah, if only you would supplicate for him.' He said: 'I have supplicated for him with three supplications. I have seen two of them, and I hope for the third.'"],[3538,"Ibrahim disliked leading them in prayer in a mosque where prayer had already been offered."],[3539,"I accompanied Ayyub from Makkah to Basra, and we came to the mosque of a people at a watering place where prayer had already been offered. Ayyub called the adhan and the iqamah, then stepped forward and prayed with us."],[3540,"I entered with Ibn Sabit, and some of us prostrated while he forbade some of us from prostrating. When he had said the salam, Ibn Sabit stood up and prayed with his companions. He said: I mentioned that to Ata, and he said, 'That is how it should be.' He said: I said, 'This is not done among us.' He said, 'They split up.'"],[3541,"Regarding a group of people who enter the mosque and catch one rak'ah with the imam, he said: 'They stand and make up what remains for them, with one of them leading them while he stands with them in the row; they pray according to his prayer.'"],[3545,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying alone and said, 'Who will give charity to this man by praying with him?'"],[3546,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying alone and said, 'Will no one seek reward by praying with this man?'"],[3547,"When you enter a mosque where prayer has already been offered, then establish the prayer and pray, whether the iqamah has been called or not."],[3549,"When two men enter the mosque after the prayer, they pray together, one of them leading his companion."],[3550,"I heard a man ask Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla: I came to a people who had already prayed; should I perform the iqamah? He said: You have been spared. He said: Should I offer voluntary prayer? He said: Begin with that for which you came."],[3553,"When you come to the mosque and find that they have already prayed, do not pray except the prescribed prayer."],[3555,"A person asked Ata: I come to the mosque and the imam has already prayed the prescribed prayer. Should I pray two rak'ahs before performing the obligatory? He said: Rather begin with the prescribed prayer, for the right is first. Then pray afterwards whatever you wish. I said: What about in my desert place? He said: Pray before it if you wish in your desert place."],[3556,"When you come to the mosque and they have already prayed, begin with the prescribed prayer."],[3557,"Begin with that which you sought."],[3559,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr or Asr and made a mistake in two rak'ahs and finished. Dhu al-Shimalayn b. Abd Amr—who was an ally of Banu Zuhrah—said to him: Has the prayer been shortened or did you forget? The Prophet ﷺ said: What is Dhu al-Yadayn saying? They said: He has spoken the truth, O Prophet of God. So he completed with them the two rak'ahs that he had missed."],[3560,"He prayed two rak'ahs in the 'Asr prayer or the Zuhr prayer, then he said the taslim. Dhu al-Shimalayn b. 'Abd 'Amr said to him: \"O Prophet of God, has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget?\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"It has not been shortened, nor did I forget.\" Dhu al-Shimalayn said to him: \"Indeed, by my father, O Prophet of God, some of that has happened.\" The Prophet ﷺ turned to the people and said: \"Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken the truth?\" They said: \"Yes, O Prophet of God.\" Then the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up for the prayer when he was certain."],[3561,"The Prophet ﷺ once prayed part of a four-rak'ah prayer, praying two rak'ahs and then saying the taslim. A man stood up to him and said: \"Have you lightened the prayer for us, O Prophet of God?\" He said: \"And what is that?\" He said: \"You said the taslim after two rak'ahs.\" He said: \"No.\" Then he stood and prayed two rak'ahs, completing them, and he did not restart the prayer in full. When he said the taslim, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3562,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed 'Asr as two rak'ahs, then said the taslim and went to his family. I said: \"And he turned away?\" He said: \"And he turned away.\" Dhu al-Yadayn, the brother of Banu Sulaym, caught up with him and said: \"O Prophet of God, did you forget, or have you lightened the prayer for us?\" He said: \"And what is that?\" He said: \"You prayed 'Asr as two rak'ahs.\" He said: \"Has Dhu al-Yadayn, the brother of Banu Sulaym, spoken the truth?\" The people said: \"Yes.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Come to success, come to success.\" The prayer has been established. Then he prayed two rak'ahs with them, then he left."],[3563,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed and then said the taslim. A man said to him: \"Did you forget, O Prophet of God, or have you lightened the prayer for us?\" He said: \"What did Dhu al-Yadayn say?\" They said: \"Yes.\" So he returned and prayed what remained. He said: \"Did he inform you that he prostrated two prostrations after the taslim?\" He said: \"I do not know.\""],[3564,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed part of a four-rak'ah prayer and said the taslim after two prostrations. Dhu al-Yadayn said to him: \"Did you forget, or have you lightened the prayer for us, O Prophet of God?\" He said: \"Did I do that?\" They said: \"Yes.\" So he returned and prayed two rak'ahs, then prostrated two prostrations while sitting."],[3565,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr or 'Asr and said the taslim after two rak'ahs, then he left. The swift among the people went out and said: \"Has the prayer been lightened for us?\" Dhu al-Shimalayn said: \"O Messenger of God, has the prayer been lightened?\" He said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"What does Dhu al-Yadayn say?\" They said: \"He has spoken the truth.\" He said: So he prayed the two rak'ahs that he had left, then prostrated two prostrations after the taslim while sitting."],[3566,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed the 'Asr prayer and said the taslim after two rak'ahs. Dhu al-Yadayn stood up and said: \"Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of God, or did you forget?\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"None of that happened.\" He said: \"Some of that has happened, O Messenger of God.\" The Prophet ﷺ turned to the people and said: \"Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken the truth?\" They said: \"Yes.\" So the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up and completed what remained of the prayer, then prostrated two prostrations while sitting after the taslim."],[3567,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed one of the two evening prayers for us. He stood up after two rak'ahs and did not sit. When he was at the end of his prayer, we waited for him to say the taslim, but he prostrated two prostrations before the taslim, then said the taslim."],[3568,"The Prophet ﷺ stood up in Zuhr while he was supposed to sit. When he completed his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations while sitting before saying the taslim. He said the takbir in every prostration, and the people prostrated them with him in place of the sitting that he had forgotten."],[3569,"The Prophet ﷺ stood up in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr or 'Asr and did not sit. When he finished his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations before saying the taslim."],[3570,"He stood up in the first two rak'ahs, and they glorified Allah to him, but he did not sit. When he finished his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations after the taslim, then said: \"This is what the Messenger of God ﷺ did.\""],[3571,"The taslim is after the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3572,"Concerning a man who forgets the two prostrations of forgetfulness, he said: If he does not remember them until he has finished and has not prostrated them, his prayer is complete. But if he remembers them while he is sitting and has not stood, he prostrates them."],[3573,"He prayed five [rak'ahs], and it was said to him: O Abu Shibl, you prayed five. He said: And do you say that — to Ibrahim — O one-eyed? He said: I said: Yes. He said: \"So he bent his leg and prostrated twice.\" Then he said: \"This is how the Messenger of God ﷺ did.\""],[3574,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr or Asr with five [rak'ahs], then he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"These two prostrations are for whoever of you thinks he added or omitted.\""],[3575,"Regarding a man who prays Ẓuhr two rakʿahs, then says salām and leaves. He said: \"He returns to it complete, unless the Prophet ﷺ did what they say.\""],[3576,"Regarding a man who prayed Zuhr with five [rak'ahs], he said: \"He prostrates twice while sitting.\""],[3578,"He returns the greeting while in prayer."],[3579,"\"When a man prays five and does not sit in the fourth, he adds a sixth, then says the taslim, and then repeats his prayer anew.\""],[3581,"When the muezzin calls, Satan turns away with flatulence so he will not hear the call. When he falls silent, he comes back. When the iqamah is given, he turns away with flatulence. When he falls silent, he comes back. He whispers between a person and his heart, saying: Remember such-and-such, remember such-and-such — things he had not remembered before — so the man remains not knowing how much he has prayed. If any of you experiences that, let him prostrate twice while sitting."],[3582,"I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri [narrate], and I said: One of us prays and does not know how much he has prayed? He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: If any of you does not know, let him prostrate twice while sitting. If Satan comes to any of you and says: You have broken your ablution, let him say to himself: You lie — until he hears a sound with his ear or finds an odor with his nose."],[3583,"Satan comes to one of you in his prayer and confuses him so he does not know how much he has prayed. If he experiences that, let him prostrate twice while sitting."],[3584,"Satan comes to one of you and confuses him in his prayer — did he add or omit? If he experiences that, let him prostrate twice while sitting.\""],[3586,"When one of you is uncertain in his prayer and does not know how many he has prayed, whether three or four, let him stand and pray a rak'ah to complete it with, then prostrate two prostrations while sitting before he says the salam. If the rak'ah he prayed was a fifth, he pairs it with these two prostrations; and if it was the fourth, then the two prostrations are a humiliation for Satan."],[3587,"When you do not know whether you have prayed four or three, aim for what is correct, then stand and bow a rak'ah, then prostrate two prostrations. Allah does not punish for addition."],[3588,"When a man is uncertain in his prayer and does not know whether he has prayed three or two, let him build upon the more certain of that, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3589,"When a man is uncertain in his prayer and does not know whether it was three or four, let him build upon the more complete of that in his own mind, and no prostration is due from him."],[3590,"When one of you is uncertain in his prayer, let him aim until he knows that he has completed it, then prostrate two prostrations while sitting."],[3591,"When one of you is uncertain in his prayer, let him build upon the more certain of that in his own mind, then prostrate two prostrations while sitting."],[3592,"He starts his prayer over, whether he spoke or did not speak."],[3593,"I said to 'Ata': If it becomes confused for the imam and he does not know how many he has prayed while he is standing, what should he do? He said: 'He is about to know by the knowledge of those behind him.'"],[3595,"Concerning addition in prayer: 'He prostrates two prostrations for forgetfulness, and if he does not remember how many he prayed, he builds upon the more complete of that in his own mind, and prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness.'"],[3596,"I was with 'Umar reminding him of the prayer, when 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf entered upon us and said: Shall I not relate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? We said: Yes. He said: I bear witness with the testimony of Allah that I heard him say: When one of you is in doubt about the prayer regarding deficiency, let him pray until he is in doubt regarding addition."],[3598,"If you do not know how much you have prayed, repeat your entire prayer. If you are certain that you prayed two rakʿahs but do not know how much you prayed beyond them, repeat only what you are unsure about, and do not repeat the two rakʿahs about which you are certain. Then prostrate twice while sitting. But if you doubt a second time, do not repeat; repetition is only once."],[3599,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: If I pray an obligatory prayer and then doubt, and I repeat it, then I doubt again? He said: 'Do not repeat.' I said: I am certain that I prayed five rakʿahs. He said: 'Do not repeat, even if you prayed ten rakʿahs. Prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.'"],[3601,"I asked Abū Hurayrah, saying: I doubted in my prayer. He said: 'They say: You prostrate twice while sitting.' And I asked ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUmar, and he said: 'Repeat your prayer until you are certain.'"],[3602,"I said to Muḥārib b. Dithār: I heard ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUmar say: 'Count the prayer as much as you can, and do not repeat'? He said: 'Yes.'"],[3603,"The prayer is not to be repeated."],[3604,"He repeats, but they do not repeat."],[3605,"If a man stands up in the first two rakʿahs, they should say subḥān Allāh to him. If he has become fully upright, he should not sit; but if he has not yet become fully upright, he should sit."],[3606,"He repeats, but they do not repeat."],[3607,"He stood up in the first two rak'ahs, and they glorified Allah to him, but he did not sit. When he finished his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations after the taslim, then said: \"This is what the Messenger of God ﷺ did.\""],[3608,"He led a people in prayer and had a nosebleed, then left and gestured to a man to come forward. Then he returned and completed the rest of his prayer."],[3609,"He led the people in prayer and forgot, so he stood up in the first two rakʿahs and did not perform the tashahhud. The people said subḥān Allāh, so he gestured to them to stand up, and they stood up."],[3610,"He rose up on his legs, so they said subḥān Allāh to him, and he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3611,"We were with him, and he prayed ʿaṣr. He made a movement to stand up, so they said subḥān Allāh, and he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3612,"Ibn al-Zubayr stood up after two rak'ahs of Maghrib—or intended to stand. He said: I only recall Tawus being uncertain which he did: he rose or intended to rise—then he performed two prostrations while seated. He said: I mentioned that to Ibn 'Abbas, and he said: 'He did right, by my life.' I said: And did he inform you that he prostrated before the taslim or after it? He said: 'I do not know.'"],[3614,"Ibn al-Zubayr led us in Maghrib one day. I said: And you attended that? He said: Yes. He gave taslim after two rak'ahs. The people said: Subhan Allah, Subhan Allah. Then he stood and prayed the third. When he gave taslim, he performed the two prostrations of forgetfulness, and the people performed them with him. He said: Some companions of ours entered upon Ibn 'Abbas and one of them mentioned that, as if he wanted to criticize Ibn al-Zubayr for it. Ibn 'Abbas said: 'He did right, and they did right.'"],[3615,"If one stands when one should sit, then when one finishes one's prayer one performs the two prostrations of forgetfulness and utters two tashahhuds."],[3616,"If one gives taslim at the end of two rak'ahs and then remembers, let him complete what has passed and perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3617,"If you stand where one should sit, or sit where one should stand, or recite aloud in what should be recited quietly, or recite quietly in what should be recited aloud, forgetfully, you perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness. If you deliberately recite aloud in what should be recited quietly, or deliberately do any of that, you do not perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness. If you forget something from a daytime prayer and make it up at night, recite as you would during the day from the prescribed prayer."],[3618,"I said to 'Ata': A man prayed Zuhr as two rak'ahs, then stood and had not left; then he remembers. He said: 'He completes what has passed.' A man said to him: I stand in the prescribed prayer and become heedless until I gesture to a person with my hand, but I do not speak. He said: 'Sit and perform two prostrations.'"],[3620,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if one forgets and stands, and had not left, then remembers? He said: 'He completes what has passed.'"],[3621,"He uttered the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3622,"He forgot in his prayer and performed two prostrations while seated. He said: We said to him: Was there any tashahhud? He said: 'Yes,' and Ibrahim gave taslim in them."],[3623,"He utters the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness and gives taslim."],[3624,"Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla made a mistake in his prayer, so he said the taslim, prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then said the taslim another time. He said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad, and they said: He performs the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3625,"There is no recitation, no bowing, and no tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness. I said: Do you think that when I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, I should make my rising a standing? He said: Rather sit, for that is more beloved to me and more complete for them."],[3627,"I said to Ata: When does one prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: When he says the taslim. I do not like that anything be placed between them and 'peace be upon you.' I said: Do I say takbir when I lower my back for prostration and when I raise it? He said: Yes, there is nothing in them except that, unless a person remembers his Lord, so he completes their prostration. When he raises his back, let him set it upright until every bone returns to its joint."],[3628,"If the imam makes a mistake and does not prostrate, then there is no forgetfulness or prostration upon those behind him."],[3629,"There is no forgetfulness upon those behind the imam. He said: I said: Even if he prostrates three prostrations in every rak'ah? He said: There is no forgetfulness upon them."],[3632,"If a man sees blood or impurity on his garment, or he prayed not facing the qiblah, or he performed tayammum and then found water within the time, he does not have to repeat [the prayer]."],[3633,"Regarding a man who misses part of the prayer, then says the taslim out of forgetfulness, he said: He stands and continues, then prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3634,"Regarding a man who misses part of the prayer while the imam has already made a mistake before he arrived, he said: When the imam says the taslim and prostrates, let him prostrate with him. Then when he finishes, let him stand and make up what he missed."],[3635,"Whoever leads the people in prayer should consider the weakest among them, for among them are the weak, the elderly, and the one with a need."],[3636,"He saw two men praying: one of them letting his lower garment hang down, and the other not completing his bowing or prostration. He laughed. They said: What makes you laugh, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman? He said: I marvel at these two men. As for the one letting his lower garment hang down, Allah will not look at him; and as for the other, Allah will not accept his prayer."],[3637,"If he forgot a rak'ah of the obligatory prayer until he entered into the voluntary prayer, then he remembered, so he prayed the rest of the obligatory prayer, then he prostrated twice while sitting."],[3638,"If he forgot something of the obligatory prayer until he entered into the voluntary prayer, then he remembered, he would turn away on an even number and start his prayer over. And he used to say: 'The voluntary prayer is equivalent to speech.'"],[3640,"If the imam was sitting in the fourth [rak'ah] while the man had missed a rak'ah, and the man stood up to make up [what he missed], thinking that the imam had said the salam, and he completed the fourth [rak'ah], then the imam said the salam, he should not repeat it. Rather, he should make up that rak'ah after the imam says the salam."],[3641,"We said: Bas bas [1], if a man forgot in the first rak'ah, and when he had prayed two rak'ahs he thought he had prayed four, so he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then he remembered, so he stood and completed four, he must repeat his prayer because he made, in the midst of his prayer, a voluntary act - meaning the two prostrations of forgetfulness. He said: But we say: No."],[3642,"If his doubt is after finishing [the prayer], there is nothing wrong with it. And if he doubts whether he prayed or not, then if it is within the time he should repeat it, and if [the time] has passed he does not repeat it."],[3643,"Concerning a man who does not know whether he prayed or not, he said: 'He repeats it as long as it is within the time of that prayer. Once the time has passed, he is not obligated to repeat it.'"],[3645,"Concerning a man who recited in the obligatory prayer a surah containing a prostration, then he forgot and did not prostrate until he bowed and prostrated for it. He said: 'He should not recite [it], and he should prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.'"],[3646,"I asked Hammad about a man who prayed and recited the prostration, then bowed with it, and forgot to prostrate until he raised his head. He said: 'He prostrates the two prostrations of the rak'ah, so that when he finishes his prayer, he prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness.'"],[3647,"If you doubt about the prostration, do not repeat, but prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. And if you are certain that you prostrated three prostrations in one rak'ah, do not repeat, but prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. I said: Then for bowing, is it not like that? He said: Bowing is more serious. If you forget a rak'ah, then once you are certain, repeat it."],[3648,"Concerning a man who stood up and recited, then bowed, then prostrated one prostration, then stood and recited, then bowed, then remembered while he was prostrating that he had not prostrated in the first rak'ah except one prostration. He said: 'He should not count this rak'ah in which he remembered while prostrating, but he should raise his head and prostrate the missed prostration, and prostrate the two prostrations of the rak'ah he is in, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness when he finishes his prayer.' He said: 'If he remembers after he has prostrated one prostration, he counts it, then prostrates his missed prostration, then prostates another one to his first prostration. If he remembers while standing, he prostrates, then returns standing to where he was in his recitation. If a man forgets the bowing, he does not count his prostration, and he makes up the bowing and prostration anew.'"],[3649,"Concerning a man who forgot a prostration in the beginning of his prayer until he had prayed three rak'ahs or four. He said: 'When he remembers it, he falls prostrating, and if he remembers after he has bowed, he continues in his bowing and prostrates three prostrations.'"],[3650,"Concerning a man who bowed, then forgot and prostrated one prostration, then remembered while standing. He said: 'He completes his prayer, and when he has said the salam, he prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness.' I said: 'Does he not fall prostrating when he remembers?' He said: 'As for after his standing, no.'"],[3651,"Concerning a man who sat in the fourth rak'ah, then remembered that he had forgotten a prostration from every rak'ah. He said: 'He prostrates four prostrations consecutively, then recites the tashahhud, then says the salam, then prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness.'"],[3652,"He saw Abū Hurayrah burying a louse in the mosque and saying: “Phlegm is worse than it.”"],[3653,"I prayed behind Ibrahim al-Nakha'i one of the two evening prayers, and when he said the salam, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness. I said to him: 'What is the matter with you, Abu 'Imran?' He said: 'I feared that I had forgotten.' He said: I said: 'If you had forgotten, we would have said subhan Allah.' He said: 'I feared that you had forgotten as I forgot.'"],[3654,"Prayer is a measure; whoever gives full measure will be given full measure, and whoever gives short measure, you have known what is for the defrauders."],[3655,"If you prostrate them for what is not incumbent upon you, it is better for you than omitting them for what is incumbent upon you, meaning the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3656,"For every forgetfulness there are two prostrations after the salam."],[3657,"A man missed one of the two evening prayers and does not know whether it was Zuhr or 'Asr. He said: 'He must pray Zuhr then 'Asr.'"],[3658,"Concerning a man who forgot on Saturday the Zuhr prayer or the 'Asr prayer, and does not know which of them he forgot on Sunday. He said: 'He prays Zuhr and 'Asr, then prays Zuhr again.'"],[3659,"Concerning a man who forgot one prayer from the daytime prayers, and does not know which of them he forgot. He said: 'He prays Fajr, then Zuhr, then 'Asr, each prayer with an iqamah. If he forgot a prayer from the nighttime prayers and does not know which of them it is, let him pray Maghrib with an iqamah and 'Isha with an iqamah. If he does not know whether the prayer he forgot is from the nighttime prayers or the daytime prayers, let him pray the prayers with an iqamah for each.'"],[3660,"Al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin differed concerning a man who misses part of the prayer with the imam during the days of Tashriq. Al-Hasan said: He says the takbir with the imam when he says takbir, then he stands and makes up what he missed. Ibn Sirin said: He stands and makes up, and when he finishes his prayer he says takbir afterwards. In Sufyan's view, the statement of Ibn Sirin is preferable. He said: He stands and makes up."],[3661,"If a man laughs during prayer, he should restart the ablution and restart the prayer."],[3662,"Concerning the man who misses part of the prayer during the days of Tashriq with the imam, he said: He stands and makes up, and when he finishes his prayer he says takbir afterwards, like the statement of Ibn Sirin."],[3663,"In every case where you are required to prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, if you forget them until you stand, then pray two rak'ahs when you remember in the prescribed prayer."],[3664,"Concerning a man who forgets the two prostrations of forgetfulness, he said: If he does not remember them until he has finished and has not prostrated them, his prayer is complete. But if he remembers them while he is sitting and has not stood, he prostrates them."],[3665,"I said to Ata: I forgot the two prostrations of forgetfulness and spoke, or I realized and did not stand. He said: Prostrate them. He said: If it was when you had finished and had not spoken, then you remembered, he said: Sit, sit, and prostrate them."],[3666,"I made an error and went to al-Dahhak b. Muzahim at his house and said: I made an error. He said: Prostrate them now. Al-Thawri said: As for others, they used to prefer that if he remembers them while he is in the mosque, he should prostrate them; otherwise no."],[3667,"Alqamah b. Qays made an error in his prayer and spoke after saying the salam before prostrating the two prostrations of forgetfulness. He was told, so he moved aside and prostrated them."],[3668,"Alqamah made a mistake in his prayer and said the salam. A man said: You made a mistake. He said: Is it so? He said: Yes. So he bent his leg and prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness. Ma'mar said: I heard someone mention that he turned away, and a man said to him: You did not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. So he turned to the qiblah and prostrated them."],[3671,"If you err in voluntary prayer and do not know how much you have prayed, do not repeat, but go with what is more likely in your mind, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3672,"Whoever takes knowledge all at once, loses it all at once."],[3673,"If you laugh deliberately during prayer, and then guffaw, you have interrupted your prayer.\" I said: \"What do you think if I laugh forgetfully in two prostrations, while I think that I have finished?\" He said: \"I do not know. Perhaps if you complete what remains upon what has passed, and then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, that will suffice you. Rather, it is his saying: it will be made up for you.\""],[3674,"I saw him praying voluntary prayer, then he prostrated while sitting. I said to him, 'What is this?' He said, 'I frequently forget.' I said, 'Is there forgetfulness in voluntary prayer?' He said, 'Ayyub told me from Ibn Sirin that he did not consider one who forgot in voluntary prayer to owe anything.' He said, 'And al-Hasan considered it forgetfulness and prostrated in it as he prostrates in the obligatory prayer.'"],[3677,"When he forgot in voluntary prayer, he performed the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[3679,"There is no forgetfulness in voluntary prayer."],[3680,"If you laugh deliberately during prayer, and then guffaw, you have interrupted your prayer.\" I said: \"What do you think if I laugh forgetfully in two prostrations, while I think that I have finished?\" He said: \"I do not know. Perhaps if you complete what remains upon what has passed, and then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, that will suffice you. Rather, it is his saying: it will be made up for you.\""],[3683,"I said to him, 'If I forget to perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness in voluntary prayer until I return to my family.' He said, 'Then do not perform them, because they are voluntary.'"],[3684,"I entered the Sanctuary of Jerusalem and found a man there who prostrated a great deal. I felt some resentment at that. When he finished, I said, 'Do you know whether you finished on an even number or an odd number?' He said, 'Even if I do not know, Allah knows.' Then he said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me,' then he wept, then said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me,' then he wept, then said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me,' then he wept, then said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim ﷺ informed me that no servant prostrates to Allah a single prostration except that Allah raises him a degree by it, removes a sin from him by it, and writes a good deed for him by it.' He said, 'I said, 'Tell me who you are, may Allah have mercy on you.' He said, 'Abu Dharr, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' He said, 'Then my soul felt humbled.'"],[3685,"I was walking with Kaʿb and we passed by a man performing rukūʿ and sujūd, not knowing whether he was on an even or odd number. He said: I said: I will surely guide this man, so I stayed behind and said: O Abū ʿAbd Allāh, are you on an even or odd number? He said: \"I have been sufficed.\" I said: Who sufficed you? He said: \"The noble recording angels.\" He said: Then he said: \"Whoever prostrates to Allah one prostration, Allah writes for him thereby a good deed, raises him thereby a degree, and erases from him thereby a sin.\" He said: Then I said: Who are you? He said: Abū Dharr. He said: I said: \"May Muṭarrif's mother be bereaved of him! Abū Dharr knows the sunnah.\" He said: Kaʿb said: \"Where is Muṭarrif?\" He said: It was said: He stayed behind to guide a man whom he saw not knowing whether he was on an even or odd number. Kaʿb said: \"Whoever prostrates to Allah one prostration, Allah writes for him thereby a good deed, raises him thereby a degree, and erases from him thereby a sin.\""],[3686,"I asked al-Shaʿbī about a man who said in place of 'Samiʿa Allahu liman hamidah': 'Allahu akbar.' He said: \"No forgetfulness is upon him.\""],[3687,"Smiling does not interrupt the prayer until one guffaws or laughs loudly."],[3688,"I said to 'Ata': What about one who delays the prayer until he prays it negligently? He said: \"Praying the congregation with him is more beloved to me.\" I said: Why do you not stop at the statement of Ibn Mas'ud regarding that? He said: \"The congregation is more beloved to me as long as it is not missed.\" I said: Even if the sun turns yellow for setting and reaches the tops of the mountains? He said: \"Yes, as long as it has not set.\""],[3689,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if I became forgetful in the obligatory prayer and then spoke? He said: \"With a word?\" I said: Yes. He said: \"Your prayer is interrupted, so repeat it anew.\""],[3690,"ʿUrwah b. al-Zubayr led them in the Maghrib prayer and performed two rakʿahs. A young son of his came and sat next to him, and ʿUrwah spoke to him, thinking he had completed. He said: We said subḥān Allah to him, so he stood and performed the third rakʿah, then prostrated two prostrations while sitting."],[3691,"If he purposely speaks, let him complete his prayer fully. And he said: \"The Prophet ﷺ spoke only because he forgot, thinking he had completed, and not purposely.\""],[3692,"Regarding a man who prays Ẓuhr two rakʿahs, then says salām and leaves. He said: \"He returns to it complete, unless the Prophet ﷺ did what they say.\""],[3693,"He was asked about a man who prayed then spoke while one rakʿah remained upon him. He said: \"He restarts his prayer.\" He said: And I asked him about a man who prayed and his penis became erect? He said: \"It does not harm him.\""],[3695,"While the Prophet ﷺ was praying with his companions on the road to Makkah, a man passed by driving some shawl of his, so the Prophet ﷺ gestured to him but he did not understand. ʿUmar shouted at him, saying: O owner of the shawl, turn back your camels. So he turned them back. When the Prophet ﷺ finished praying, he said: \"Who was the speaker?\" They said: ʿUmar. He said: \"How excellent a jurist you are, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb!\" I said to him: What is shawl? He said: A group of camels."],[3696,"Regarding a man who forgot in his prayer and then spoke. They said: \"He repeats his prayer.\""],[3697,"They used to speak during the prayer, and a man would instruct his brother, until this verse was revealed: “And stand before Allah, devoutly obedient” (2:238). So they stopped speaking. He said: “Qunūt is silence, and qunūt is obedience.”"],[3698,"If you sneeze while you are praying, then praise Allah within yourself."],[3699,"A person sneezed, and another said “may Allah have mercy on you” to him while praying. The people said: “That is not to be done during prayer.”"],[3700,"A man sneezed during the prayer, and a Bedouin next to him said to him: “May Allah have mercy on you.” The Bedouin said: “The people looked at me, so I said: ‘Woe to me! What is wrong with them looking at me?!’ They struck their thighs with their hands. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, he called me. The Bedouin said: ‘By my father and mother, I have never seen a teacher better than him. By Allah, he did not rebuke me nor insult me.’ He said: ‘Indeed, prayer is not suitable for any of the speech of people. It is only glorification (tasbīḥ), magnification (takbīr), declaration of oneness (tahlīl), and recitation of the Qur’an,’ or as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said.”"],[3701,"One should not eat or drink during prayer. I said: “What if I drank forgetfully?” He said: “If you have not spoken, then complete what remains based on what has passed, then prostrate two prostrations of forgetfulness (sajdatay al-sahw). If you drank deliberately, then your prayer is invalidated, so repeat the prayer.”"],[3702,"He should not eat or drink while praying. If he does, he must repeat [the prayer]."],[3703,"May I eat and drink in voluntary prayer, even a sip? He said: “No, by my life, but leave and drink.”"],[3706,"The most entitled of the people to lead them is the one who recites the Book of Allah best. If they are equal in recitation, then the most knowledgeable in the Sunnah. If they are equal in the Sunnah, then the one who made hijrah earliest. If they are equal in hijrah, then the eldest. And no man should lead another in his sphere of authority, nor sit on his seat of honor in his house except with his permission."],[3707,"It is disliked that he have dirhams or something in his mouth while praying. Sufyān said: “And there is no harm in a man praying while he has food or something in his belt, according to more than one [authority].”"],[3708,"It is disliked that he have dirhams or something in his mouth while praying. Sufyān said: “And there is no harm in a man praying while he has food or something in his belt, according to more than one [authority].”"],[3710,"I said to 'Ata': 'I forgot and leaned during a two-rak'ah prayer, or before performing the final tashahhud salam?' He said: 'Pray what remains if you have not spoken, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness.' He said: 'But if you did that deliberately, your prayer is cut off.'"],[3712,"'Uthman b. Maz'un greeted the Prophet ﷺ while he was sitting in prayer, so the Prophet ﷺ returned the greeting."],[3713,"Ibn Mas'ud greeted the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah while the Prophet ﷺ was praying, so he returned the greeting."],[3714,"When the emigrants to Abyssinia returned, the Prophet ﷺ gave that up. He would be greeted during prayer but would not respond. It was said to him: 'O Prophet of Allah, you used to respond while you were in Makkah during prayer.' He said: 'In prayer there is occupation.'"],[3715,"People used to return greetings to one another during prayer, until Ibn Mas'ud greeted the Prophet ﷺ and he did not return it. He sat down sad, imagining that something had been revealed concerning him. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, Ibn Mas'ud mentioned that to him, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'In prayer there is occupation,' or 'prayer is enough of an occupation.' He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ said: 'Shall I not teach you the greetings?' meaning the tashahhud."],[3716,"We used to greet the Prophet ﷺ until we returned from the Negus. We greeted him and he did not return our greeting, and said: 'In prayer there is occupation.'"],[3717,"Ibn Mas'ud came to the Prophet ﷺ upon his return from Abyssinia while he was praying, and greeted him, but he did not return the greeting until he finished. Then he said: 'In prayer there is occupation.'"],[3718,"We used to greet the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying and he would return our greeting. When I came from the land of Abyssinia and greeted him, he did not return my greeting. I was seized by what is past and what is to come. Then I waited for him, and when he finished his prayer I mentioned that to him. He said: 'Allah brings about ease in His command, and He has decreed'—or he said 'He has brought about'—'that you should not speak during prayer.'"],[3719,"Ibn 'Umar passed by a man praying and greeted him, and the man returned the greeting. Ibn 'Umar said to him: 'When one of you is in prayer and is greeted, he should not speak, but should gesture with a sign; that is his return.'"],[3722,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque of Banu 'Amr b. 'Awf to pray in it, and Suhayb entered with him. Men from the Ansar came in to greet him. 'Abdullah said: I asked Suhayb how the Prophet ﷺ responded when greeted during prayer. He said: 'He would gesture with his hand.'"],[3723,"I saw Musa b. Jamil, who was a man devoted to prayer, and Ibn Abbas praying at night facing the Kabah. He said: I saw Musa pray, then he would repeat, then he departed and passed by Ibn Abbas and greeted him, so Ibn Abbas grasped Musa's hand like this—and Ata clasped his palm over his palm. Ata said: That was from him as a greeting, and I did not see Ibn Abbas speak."],[3724,"Amr b. Dinar saw no harm in that."],[3725,"If I passed by people praying, I would not greet them."],[3726,"As for me, I dislike greeting people who are praying, lest I disturb them. He said: And if someone greets me while I am sitting in the tashahhud, I return it then."],[3727,"I said to Ata: If you were standing to pray, would you return the greeting if someone greeted you? He said: No, but I would wait until I finish, then return it to him."],[3728,"If someone greets you during the prayer, do not return it; then when you finish, if he is still nearby, return it, and if he has gone, send the greeting after him."],[3729,"If a person sees blood on his garment while in prayer and leaves to wash it, he completes what remains based on what has passed, as long as he has not spoken."],[3731,"If someone experiences wind, a nosebleed, or vomiting, he should leave, place his hand on his nose, and perform ablution. If he speaks, he should start the prayer over; otherwise, he counts what he has already prayed."],[3732,"If a person sees blood on his garment while in prayer and leaves to wash it, he completes what remains based on what has passed, as long as he has not spoken."],[3733,"If one of you experiences wind from stool or urine, he should leave and perform ablution without speaking or straying—meaning doing anything else—then return to the verse he was reciting."],[3734,"If a person sees blood on his garment while in prayer and leaves to wash it, he completes what remains based on what has passed, as long as he has not spoken."],[3735,"From ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUmar that he would give a legal opinion to a man who had a nosebleed during prayer, or was overcome by vomiting, or found pre-seminal fluid, that he should leave, perform ablution, and then complete the remainder of his prayer as long as he has not spoken."],[3736,"Vomiting and nosebleed are the same; one performs ablution for both of them, even if he has not spoken."],[3737,"Ibn ʿUmar had a nosebleed while he was praying, so he entered his house and pointed to ablution water. It was brought to him, and he performed ablution. Then he entered and completed [the prayer] based on what had preceded of it, and he did not speak during that."],[3738,"Regarding one who has a nosebleed during prayer, he said: 'He leaves, performs ablution, and then completes the remainder of his prayer as long as he has not spoken.'"],[3739,"If you have a nosebleed during prayer, pinch your nostril and pray as you are. If any blood comes out, perform ablution, then do not speak until you build upon what has preceded."],[3740,"If it does not stop during prayer, he stuffs it (with something)."],[3741,"If a person sees blood on his garment while in prayer and leaves to wash it, he completes what remains based on what has passed, as long as he has not spoken."],[3742,"If a person has a nosebleed during prayer and then does not speak until he performs ablution and prays, he should pray the remainder based on what has preceded, as long as he has not spoken. But ʿAmr says: 'If he deliberately speaks, he must start his prayer over completely.' He also said: 'The Prophet ﷺ only spoke when he forgot, thinking he had completed [the prayer], so he did not repeat it.'"],[3743,"When one of you has a nosebleed during prayer or is overcome by vomiting, if it is a small amount, he should wash it; or if he finds pre-seminal fluid, he should leave, perform ablution, then return to the remainder of his prayer and not start it anew. Along with that, he should not speak until he returns to the remainder of his prayer."],[3744,"When a man has a ritual impurity during his prayer and then does not speak until he performs ablution, he completes the remainder of his prayer based on what has preceded of it. But if he speaks, he starts it over afresh."],[3745,"He repeats the prayer and does not count anything that preceded of it in the case of nosebleed."],[3746,"He starts his prayer over, whether he spoke or did not speak."],[3747,"They say: he should resume as long as he has not spoken, but if he has spoken—until I am not in doubt—that is dearer to me."],[3748,"Laughing, urinating, and passing wind necessitate repeating ablution and prayer; vomiting and nosebleed he builds upon [his prayer] if he has not spoken, but if he has spoken he starts over."],[3749,"Three things require repeating ablution and prayer: laughing, urinating, and passing wind. Three things require repeating prayer but not ablution: speaking, eating, and drinking. Three things require repeating ablution but not prayer unless he has spoken: vomiting, nosebleed, and whatever flows from wounds or sores."],[3750,"Any man who breaks his state while in prayer should leave, perform ablution, then complete what remains, even if he has spoken."],[3751,"Do not cut off [the prayer] except for three: a nosebleed, breaking wind, or the closing salutation of departure."],[3752,"If you pray and then finish, and it turns out you did not face the qiblah but the prayer time has not elapsed, then repeat your prayer. He said: \"But if that prayer time has elapsed and you did not remember, then do not repeat.\""],[3753,"He repeats it as long as it is within the time."],[3754,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: 'People crowd me in their multitude and turn me away from the direction of the House, so much so that I can hardly face the qiblah, or I can hardly face any part of the House.' He said: \"Strive to face it, but if the matter overcomes you, there is no harm.\""],[3755,"He saw two men praying: one of them letting his lower garment hang down, and the other not completing his bowing or prostration. He laughed. They said: What makes you laugh, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman? He said: I marvel at these two men. As for the one letting his lower garment hang down, Allah will not look at him; and as for the other, Allah will not accept his prayer."],[3756,"When they saw a man not praying well, they taught him."],[3757,"I said to Mujāhid: 'I prayed turned away from the qiblah?' He said: \"It suffices you.\""],[3762,"About a man in the land of Abyssinia on a cloudy day who did not know whether the time of prayer had come or not. He said: \"I will pray, and if the time had come, you would have prayed; otherwise, you will repeat it.\" He said: \"So he had prayed within the time.\" He said: \"That suffices him.\""],[3763,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari led us in the afternoon prayer on a rainy day. When it cleared up, he found that he had prayed it at the wrong time, so he repeated the prayer."],[3764,"I said to Ata: 'I prayed zuhr before the sun declined, or subh before dawn, and I did not know until the time had passed.' He said to me: 'What is this? Why do you not know? How can you not know?'"],[3766,"I said to Ata: 'During the days of Hajj and other times, I am at a distance from the imam. Does the imam raising his head from bowing and prostration suffice me, or should I wait for the raising of those near me among the people?' He said: 'Rather, one raising suffices you, and the stronger of that in your mind is to match the imam's raising. Follow him as much as you can.'"],[3767,"The prayer was called, and the Prophet ﷺ came out until when he stood at his prayer place, he remembered that he had not performed ghusl. He said to the people: 'Stay where you are,' then he entered and performed ghusl, then he came out to the people while they were standing in rows, and his head was dripping water."],[3768,"I said to Ata: 'The most knowledgeable of the people is in a state of janabah and cannot find water; should he lead them?' He said: 'No, by my life, even if he is a commander, he should not lead them.'"],[3769,"We went out with Umar b. al-Khattab to al-Jurf. He looked and saw that he had had a wet dream. He prayed without performing ghusl. He said: 'By God, I think I had a wet dream without realizing it, and I prayed without realizing it.' He said: 'So he performed ghusl and washed what he saw on his garment, and sprinkled water on what he did not see. Then he called the adhan and iqamah, and then prayed after the forenoon had risen, unhurriedly.'"],[3771,"I and Umar b. al-Khattab were sitting, with a small stream between us. He said: Umar saw janabah on his garment, so he said: 'Wet dreams have come upon us ever since we ate this fat.' Then he washed what he saw on his garment, performed ghusl, and repeated the prayer."],[3772,"I said to Ata': I prayed in a state of janabah or without wudu without knowing, until that prayer had passed. He said: 'Perform wudu, then repeat your prayer.'"],[3774,"Umar b. al-Khattab led them while he was junub, or without wudu, so he repeated the prayer, but those behind him did not repeat."],[3775,"Ibn Umar prayed the Asr prayer with his companions while he was without wudu, so he repeated, but his companions did not repeat."],[3776,"If a man laughs during prayer, he should restart the ablution and restart the prayer."],[3777,"They used to pray with the rulers even if they delayed."],[3778,"If the imam of a people prays with them without wudu, and he remembers when he has finished, he said: 'He repeats, and they repeat. But if he remembers only after that prayer has passed, then he repeats but they do not repeat.'"],[3779,"I said: What if he prayed with them while junub, and neither they nor he knew until that prayer had passed? He said: 'Then they should repeat, for janabah is not like wudu.'"],[3780,"\"How can I lead them when they straighten me toward the qiblah?\"—when he became blind."],[3781,"Amr b. Dinar saw no harm in that."],[3782,"The son of adultery may marry, and his daughter may be given in marriage, his testimony is valid, and he may lead."],[3783,"Al-Shaʿbī was asked about a man who would lead a people in prayer and prayed one or two rakʿahs, then saw something, was startled, and broke his prayer. He said: “They begin anew.”"],[3784,"If the imam’s prayer is invalidated, the people’s prayer is invalidated."],[3786,"From ʿAlī that he led the people in prayer while in a state of janābah, then ordered Ibn al-Nabbāḥ to call out: “Whoever prayed the morning prayer with the Commander of the Faithful should repeat the prayer, for he led the people in prayer while in a state of janābah.”"],[3788,"ʿUmar led the people in prayer while in a state of janābah, and he repeated but the people did not. ʿAlī said to him: “It was proper for those who prayed with you to repeat.” He said: They went down to ʿAlī’s statement. He said: I said: What does “went down” mean? He said: They returned."],[3789,"ʿAlī led the people in prayer while in a state of janābah or without wuḍūʾ, and he repeated and ordered them to repeat."],[3790,"I asked him about the imam of a people who is struck by janābah and finds no water for purification. He said: “He performs tayammum and advances to lead them in prayer, for God has purified him.”"],[3791,"Tayammum is equivalent to water."],[3792,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ, saying: If the most knowledgeable of a people is struck by janābah or relieves himself and wipes with dust, may he lead them? He said: “No, he should not lead them, even if he is their amīr.”"],[3793,"Regarding the imam of a people who is struck by janābah and finds no water, he said: “Let him put forward someone else.”"],[3794,"One who performs tayammum does not lead those who have purified themselves with water. He said: And ʿAlī said: “A shackled person does not lead those who are unshackled.”"],[3795,"He led a people in prayer and had a nosebleed, then left and gestured to a man to come forward. Then he returned and completed the rest of his prayer."],[3796,"Ali led us in prayer and then had a nosebleed. He took a man, brought him forward, and stepped back."],[3797,"Concerning a man who led a people in prayer and then had a hadath during his prayer, they said: \"He should bring forward a man to lead them in what remains of their prayer.\""],[3798,"A boy who has not reached puberty should not lead."],[3799,"If the imam has a hadath at the end of his prayer when he is settling into the sitting position, then his prayer is complete, and the prayer of those behind him is like his prayer."],[3800,"Concerning a man who had a hadath during his prayer before performing the tashahhud, he said: \"That is sufficient for him, so he should not repeat it.\""],[3801,"If the imam raises his head from prostration at the end of his prayer, then his prayer is complete even if he has a hadath."],[3802,"Concerning a man who has a hadath in the middle of his prayer, he said: \"If he has completed the bowing and prostration, his prayer is complete.\""],[3803,"I said to Ibrahim: A man has a hadath when he finishes the prostration in the fourth rak'ah and before the tashahhud? He said: \"His prayer is already complete.\""],[3804,"Concerning a man who has a hadath at the end of his prayer before the tashahhud, he said: \"He does not repeat it.\""],[3806,"His prayer is not complete until he performs the taslim. The sanctification of the prayer is the takbir, and its seal is the taslim — or he said: its end is the taslim."],[3807,"Abdullah b. al-Zubayr used to lead al-Zubayr and Talha."],[3808,"Regarding a man who has a hadath during the last prostration of the prayer, he said: \"He should leave, perform wudu, then return and say the tashahhud as long as he has not spoken.\" He said: \"But if he spoke, he must repeat it.\""],[3809,"Regarding a man who has a hadath after sitting in the fourth [rak'ah] before saying the tashahhud, he said: \"He should leave, perform wudu, then return and say the tashahhud.\""],[3811,"No prayer is valid without tashahhud."],[3812,"If a man recites the tashahhud and fears that he may invalidate his ablution before the imam says the taslim, let him say the taslim, for his prayer is complete."],[3815,"Mu'awiyah led the people in prayer and bowed, then he was stabbed while prostrating or bowing, so he said the salam, then said: \"Complete your prayer.\" So each man prayed by himself, and he did not appoint anyone [to lead]."],[3816,"Regarding a man who led a group in prayer and prayed one or two rak'ahs with them, then had a hadath and appointed a man who had not caught the beginning of the prayer, he said: \"The one appointed should lead them in the imam's prayer, then retreat while sitting, and appoint another man by crawling, and he [the new imam] should say the salam with them. Then he [the appointed one] stands and completes [his own prayer].\""],[3817,"I said to 'Ata': I prayed in an impure waist wrapper and I found out before that prayer's time elapsed, or after it had elapsed. He said: \"Do not repeat it. What concern is the garment? What concern is that?\""],[3820,"If a man sees blood or impurity on his garment, or he prayed not facing the qiblah, or he performed tayammum and then found water within the time, he does not have to repeat [the prayer]."],[3822,"He spent the night at his maternal aunt Maymunah's. He said: 'I lay down across the width of the cushion, while the Prophet ﷺ and his wife lay along its length. The Prophet ﷺ slept until the middle of the night, or a little before or after it. Then he woke up and sat, wiping sleep from his face with his hands. Then he recited the ten final verses of Surah Al 'Imran. Then he went to a hanging water-skin, performed ablution from it, and did his ablution well. Then he stood to pray. I did the same as he had done, then I went and stood beside him. He placed his hands on my head and took hold of my ear, twisting it. Then he prayed two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then he prayed witr. Then he lay down until the mu'adhdhin came, so he got up and prayed two brief rak'ahs, then he went out and prayed the morning prayer.'"],[3823,"I saw Saʿīd b. Jubayr praying in the imam's arch."],[3824,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ and Mujāhid about a man who prays in a garment that is not pure. They both said: He should not repeat it."],[3827,"I asked ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī. I said: Blood got on my garment, and I came to know of it after I had said the salām. He said: Do not repeat it, even if you had known of it."],[3828,"If you see blood on your garment while you are in prayer, if it is little, continue; if it is much, put it aside, and do not repeat."],[3829,"If a person sees blood on his garment while in prayer and leaves to wash it, he completes what remains based on what has passed, as long as he has not spoken."],[3832,"I saw al-Hasan come to Thabit al-Bunani. He said: I think he visited him. He said: When the prayer was due, Thabit said: 'Go forward, O Abu Sa'id.' Al-Hasan said: 'You, for you are more entitled.' Thabit said: 'By Allah, I will never go before you.' He said: So al-Hasan went forward and kept away from the arch, not praying in it."],[3833,"He used to pause at length in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and make the last two light, and likewise in Maghrib and Isha; and all that was in the standing. As for the bowing and prostration, no.\" I said: \"Should we then make the last two light in the standing?\" He said: \"Or is there any doubt? As for this, no.\""],[3834,"I was sitting with Umar b. al-Khattab when the people of Kufa came complaining about Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas. They said: \"He does not pray well.\" While they were thus, Sa'd passed by them, so he called him and said: \"These people complain about you and claim that you do not pray well.\" He said: \"As for me, I lead them in the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I lead them in the Isha prayer and pause at length in the first two rak'ahs and make the last two light.\" Umar said: \"That is what is expected of you, O Abu Ishaq.\""],[3835,"The people of Kufa complained about Sa'd to Umar and said: \"He does not pray well.\" He said: \"So Umar asked him, and he said: 'I lead them in the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I pause at length in the first two and make the last two light.' He said: 'That is what is expected of you, O Abu Ishaq.'\""],[3836,"They used to dislike that an imam leads them while reading from the mushaf, for they would be imitating the People of the Book."],[3837,"Tanning hides is their ritual slaughter."],[3838,"When you go out from your house, you are in a state of prayer."],[3839,"Did Ibn Umar make the standing equal in Zuhr, Asr, and the last Isha? He said: \"He made all of that equal, until hardly any part of his prayer was longer than another.\""],[3840,"When one of you leads the people in prayer, he should lighten the prayer, for among them are the elderly, the weak, and the sick. But when he prays alone, he may lengthen his prayer as he wishes."],[3841,"When one of you prays with the people, he should lighten it, for among them are the sick, the elderly, and the one with a need."],[3842,"Whoever leads the people in prayer should consider the weakest among them, for among them are the weak, the elderly, and the one with a need."],[3843,"When you go out from your house, you are in a state of prayer."],[3844,"When you go out from your house, you are in a state of prayer."],[3845,"The Prophet ﷺ had appointed him over al-Ta'if. He said: And the last thing the Messenger of God ﷺ enjoined upon me was to lighten the prayer for the people."],[3846,"I never prayed after the Messenger of God ﷺ any prayer lighter than the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ while complete in bowing and prostration."],[3847,"We visited Abu Waqid al-Bakri during the illness in which he died, and I heard him say: The Messenger of God ﷺ was the lightest in prayer upon the people and the longest of people in prayer for himself."],[3848,"I do pass over in my prayer when I hear crying — or he said: when I hear the crying of a child."],[3849,"Indeed I saw ʿUmar striking for it the heads of the ropes, meaning two rakʿahs after al-ʿAṣr."],[3850,"I do lighten the prayer when I hear the crying of a child, fearing that his mother might be distracted."],[3851,"I hear the sound of a child behind me and lighten the prayer, out of concern that his mother might be distracted."],[3852,"The Prophet ﷺ recited in the dawn prayer in the first rak'ah sixty verses. Then he stood for the second rak'ah and heard the sound of a child, so he recited in it three verses."],[3853,"While a young man of the Ansar was feeding his water-carrying camel, Mu'adh b. Jabal began the night prayer. The young man set down his fodder, stood and performed ablution, and attended the prayer. Mu'adh started with Surah al-Baqarah. The young man prayed and then left Mu'adh, went back to his camel and fed it — or fed her. When Mu'adh finished, the young man came and reviled him and belittled him. Then he said: \"I will go to the Prophet of God ﷺ and tell him about you.\" So we woke up and they both met with the Prophet ﷺ. Mu'adh mentioned his situation to him. The young man said: \"We are people of work and occupation, and he prolonged for us; he began with Surah al-Baqarah.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"O Mu'adh, do you want to be a tempter? When you lead the people, recite 'Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High,' 'By the night when it covers,' 'Recite in the name of your Lord,' 'By the morning brightness,' and the like of these.\" Then 'Abd Allah b. 'Ubayd b. 'Umayr said: The Prophet ﷺ called the young man and said: \"O Mu'adh, supplicate,\" and he did. Then he said to the young man: \"Supplicate.\" He said: \"By God, I do not know what this murmuring of yours is, but by God, if I meet the enemy, I will be true to God.\" He met the enemy and was martyred. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"He was true to God, and God was true to him.\""],[3854,"While a young man of the Ansar was feeding his water-carrying camel, Mu'adh b. Jabal began the night prayer. The young man set down his fodder, stood and performed ablution, and attended the prayer. Mu'adh started with Surah al-Baqarah. The young man prayed and then left Mu'adh, went back to his camel and fed it — or fed her. When Mu'adh finished, the young man came and reviled him and belittled him. Then he said: \"I will go to the Prophet of God ﷺ and tell him about you.\" So we woke up and they both met with the Prophet ﷺ. Mu'adh mentioned his situation to him. The young man said: \"We are people of work and occupation, and he prolonged for us; he began with Surah al-Baqarah.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"O Mu'adh, do you want to be a tempter? When you lead the people, recite 'Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High,' 'By the night when it covers,' 'Recite in the name of your Lord,' 'By the morning brightness,' and the like of these.\" Then 'Abd Allah b. 'Ubayd b. 'Umayr said: The Prophet ﷺ called the young man and said: \"O Mu'adh, supplicate,\" and he did. Then he said to the young man: \"Supplicate.\" He said: \"By God, I do not know what this murmuring of yours is, but by God, if I meet the enemy, I will be true to God.\" He met the enemy and was martyred. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"He was true to God, and God was true to him.\""],[3855,"A man said to the Prophet ﷺ: \"I do not attend the prayer because of how long so-and-so makes it for us.\" He said: I never saw the Prophet ﷺ angrier in an exhortation than he was that day. He said: \"Whoever leads the people should lighten, for behind him are the weak, the elderly, and the one with a need.\""],[3856,"Talhah and al-Zubayr arrived, and Talhah led us in prayer and lightened it. We said: \"What is this?\" He said: \"I hastened against the whispering.\""],[3857,"Trim this prayer before the whispering of the devil."],[3858,"My father used to lengthen the prayer in his house, and shorten it when with people. I said: O my father, why do you do this? He said: “Indeed, we are imams who are followed.”"],[3859,"Al-Zubayr led us in a prayer and shortened it. It was said to him, so he said: “I hasten to ward off whispering.”"],[3860,"If a man took a ewe with abundant milk, he would not finish milking it before I had prayed the five prayers, completing their bowing and prostration."],[3861,"We were sitting with Hudhayfah in the mosque, and he saw a man praying a prayer without completing its bowing or prostration. When he finished, he called him and said to him: “Since when have you been praying this prayer?” He said: For forty years. Hudhayfah said: You have not prayed since you began, and if you were to die upon this, you would die upon other than the natural disposition of Muhammad the Prophet ﷺ, upon which he was created."],[3862,"We were with Hudhayfah, and a man came to him from the gates of Kindah. He prayed a prayer in which he pecked and did not complete his bowing. Hudhayfah said to him: “Since when have you been praying this prayer?” He said: For forty years. He said: “You have not prayed for forty years, and if you were to die, you would die upon other than the natural disposition upon which Muhammad ﷺ was created.” Then Hudhayfah said: “A man may shorten, then he completes his bowing and prostration.”"],[3863,"He passed by a man who did not complete his bowing or prostration, and said: “Something is better than nothing.”"],[3864,"He saw two men praying: one of them letting his lower garment hang down, and the other not completing his bowing or prostration. He laughed. They said: What makes you laugh, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman? He said: I marvel at these two men. As for the one letting his lower garment hang down, Allah will not look at him; and as for the other, Allah will not accept his prayer."],[3865,"A prayer in which a man does not make his back straight in bowing and prostration does not suffice."],[3866,"Indeed, I see behind me in prayer just as I see in front of me, so straighten your rows and perfect your bowing and prostration."],[3867,"Whoever performs the prayer well when people see him, then performs it badly when alone, that is contempt with which he treats his Lord."],[3868,"While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque, a man entered and prayed two rak'ahs, and the Prophet ﷺ was watching him. Then he came and greeted him, and he returned the greeting. Then he said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He returned and prayed, then came and greeted him, and he returned the greeting. Then he said: “Go back and pray, for you have not prayed.” He returned, then came and greeted him, and he returned the greeting. Then he said: “Go back and pray, for you have not prayed.” He repeated the statement to him a third or fourth time, and the Prophet ﷺ kept saying the same as his first statement. The man said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransomed for you, by the One who sent down the Book upon you, I have striven and been eager, so show me and teach me. He said: “When you intend to pray, perfect your ablution, then face the qiblah and say the takbir, then recite, then bow until you are at rest in bowing, then rise until you are standing upright, then prostrate until you are at rest in prostration, then rise until you are at rest sitting, then prostrate until you are at rest in prostration, then rise. If you complete it upon this, you have completed it; and whatever you diminish from this, you diminish from yourself.”"],[3869,"What do you say about the thief, the adulterer, and the wine drinker? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: \"These are immoralities, and in them there are punishments, and the worst theft is a man's theft of his prayer.\" They said: O Messenger of Allah, how does he steal his prayer? He said: \"He does not complete its bowing or its prostration.\""],[3870,"Indeed the angels write the deeds of the children of Adam and say: so-and-so decreased from his prayer a quarter, and so-and-so decreased half, and so-and-so increased such and such."],[3871,"He interrupts his prayer and joins the people."],[3872,"People in prayer are three: one bridled, one muzzled, and one protected. As for the bridled, it is the one who strikes his hand on his chest, then thinks about his worldly affairs until he finishes his prayer. As for the muzzled, it is the one who twists his neck right and left. As for the protected, it is the one who turns to his prayer, nothing else concerns him until he finishes it."],[3874,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if, after I finish my prayer, I am not satisfied with its completeness, should I repeat it? He said: \"Yes, by Allah, then repeat it, for if it fell short of seeking the face of Allah, I hope Allah will not reject it from you.\""],[3875,"I said to 'Ata': What is it that, if a person reaches it in prayer as completion, nothing less will suffice him? He said: Ablution, nothing suffices from it except thorough washing, and from recitation, the Mother of the Qur'an. He said: I said: Does it suffice if he ends at it? He said: \"Yes.\""],[3876,"I scratched my body while I was in prayer, and I reached my penis. I said to Abd Allah b. Masud, and he laughed and said: Cut it off—where will you isolate it? It is only a piece of you."],[3877,"Indeed prayer is three thirds: a third purification, a third bowing, and a third prostration. Whoever guards them, they are accepted from him, and whoever decreases, he only decreases from himself."],[3878,"Whoever guards them, they are accepted from him along with what is besides them, and whoever neglects them, they are rejected from him along with what is besides them."],[3879,"Prayer is a measure; whoever gives full measure will be given full measure, and whoever gives short measure, you have known what is for the defrauders."],[3880,"What assures the one who raises his head before the imam that Allah will not turn his head into the head of a donkey?"],[3881,"A man is not safe, when he raises his head before the imam, from his head returning as the head of a dog. Some people will certainly stop raising their eyes to the sky, or they will not return to them."],[3882,"The one who raises his head before the imam and lowers it before him, his forelock is in the hand of Satan."],[3883,"When he raised and said, 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' no man among us would bend his back until the Prophet ﷺ fell prostrate, then we would fall in prostration."],[3884,"I have become heavy, so do not hasten in standing, and do not hasten in prostration."],[3885,"Ibn Mas'ud came to us while the imam was praying Fajr, so he prayed two rak'ahs by a pillar, and he had not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr."],[3886,"Do not hasten ahead of your imams in bowing or in prostration. If one of you precedes, let him lower himself by the amount he preceded."],[3887,"Any man who raises his head before the imam in bowing or in prostration, let him lower his head by the amount he raised it."],[3889,"Supplicate in the obligatory prayer with what is in the Qur'an."],[3890,"He disliked that a man pray while covering his mouth."],[3892,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying with the people, a man with poor eyesight came and fell into a well near the mosque. The Prophet ﷺ commanded: 'Whoever laughed, let him repeat the ablution and repeat the prayer.'"],[3893,"Tasbih is for men, and clapping is for women in seeking permission."],[3894,"I asked al-Zuhri about that. He said: \"Laughter does not require ablution.\""],[3896,"If a man laughs during prayer, he should restart the ablution and restart the prayer."],[3897,"Let no man after me lead the prayer while sitting."],[3898,"He saw a man laughing and ordered him to repeat the prayer."],[3899,"He ordered his companions, because of laughter, to repeat the prayer but not to repeat the ablution."],[3900,"If you laugh deliberately during prayer, and then guffaw, you have interrupted your prayer.\" I said: \"What do you think if I laugh forgetfully in two prostrations, while I think that I have finished?\" He said: \"I do not know. Perhaps if you complete what remains upon what has passed, and then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, that will suffice you. Rather, it is his saying: it will be made up for you.\""],[3901,"Smiling does not interrupt the prayer. He said: I said: \"Should I prostrate with it the two prostrations of forgetfulness?\" \"If you guffaw and you have an odd number, then make it even with a rak'ah, then start your prayer anew.\""],[3902,"If you guffaw with the imam, you have interrupted your prayer, so then start your prayer anew with him."],[3903,"I said to Ata: \"I begin the prayer standing, then I pray and recite while sitting without having bowed or prostrated?\" He said, \"Yes.\" I said, \"I bow for one rak'ah, then I sit and recite?\" He said, \"No, I dislike that you sit after an odd number.\" I said, \"Then I begin the prayer, then I sit without bowing or prostrating?\" He said, \"Yes, if you wish. You are not now after an odd number.\" I said, \"What if I sit after one rak'ah?\" He said, \"Prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. But sit after two rak'ahs whenever you wish.\""],[3904,"Smiling does not interrupt the prayer, but guffawing interrupts it."],[3906,"Smiling does not interrupt the prayer until one guffaws or laughs loudly."],[3907,"If you smile and your teeth become visible, that does not interrupt your prayer."],[3908,"If he bares his teeth, it does not harm him until he laughs loudly. I said to him: What is baring the teeth? He said: His teeth become visible."],[3909,"Urwah used to pray on something above the ground."],[3910,"I asked Abd Allah b. al-Samit, who was the nephew of Abu Dharr, about the rulers when they delay the prayer. He struck my knee and said: I asked Abu Dharr about that, and he did to me what I did to you, struck my knee, and told me that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did to him what he did to me, struck his knee as he struck my knee, and said: Pray the prayer at its time. Then he said: If you catch it with them, then pray, and let none of you say: I have already prayed, so he should not pray."],[3911,"Ubayd Allah b. Ziyad delayed the prayer. I asked Abd Allah b. Samit, and he struck my thigh, then said: I asked my close friend Abu Dharr, and he struck my thigh, then said: I asked my close friend, meaning the Prophet ﷺ, and he struck my thigh and said: Pray the prayer at its time. If you catch it, then pray with them, and let none of you say: I have already prayed, so he should not pray."],[3912,"We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: There will come rulers who will be distracted by things until they do not pray the prayer at its appointed time. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, should I then pray with them? He said: Yes."],[3913,"There will be rulers who pray the prayer at its time and delay it from its time. Pray the prayer at its time. If they delay it, then you have secured your prayer."],[3914,"Concerning the insane person who sometimes regains his sanity. He said: \"He does not make up the prayer once he is in his right mind.\""],[3915,"There will be rulers over you who delay the prayer. Pray the prayer at its time. If you catch it, then pray and make your prayer with them a supererogatory prayer."],[3916,"Ibn Masud said to his companions: I am not falling short with you regarding the time. Then he led them in the noon prayer — I think he said when the sun had passed its zenith. Then he said: There will be rulers over you who delay the prayer. Pray the prayer at its time. If you catch it with them, then pray."],[3917,"You are in a time of few preachers and many scholars. They lengthen the prayer and shorten the sermon. A time will come upon you of many preachers and few scholars. They lengthen the sermon and delay the prayer until it is said: This is the choking of the dead. I said to him: What is the choking of the dead? He said: When the sun turns very yellow. Whoever catches that should pray the prayer at its time. If he is detained, then let him pray with them and make his prayer alone the obligatory one and his prayer with them voluntary."],[3918,"How will it be for you, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, when there are rulers over you who extinguish the Sunnah and delay the prayer beyond its time? He said: So what do you command me, O Messenger of Allah? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"You ask me, Ibn Umm 'Abd, how to act? There is no obedience to a creature in disobedience to God.\""],[3919,"How will you be, O Mahdi, when your best are passed over, your youth are appointed over you, and prayer is performed at other than its time? He said: I said: I do not know. He said: \"Do not be a tax collector, nor an 'arif, nor a policeman, nor a courier, and pray the prayer at its time.\""],[3920,"Al-Walid b. 'Uqbah delayed the prayer once, so Ibn Mas'ud ordered the muezzin and he called the iqamah for prayer, then he came forward and led the people in prayer. Al-Walid sent to him: What have you done? Has something come to you from the Commander of the Faithful, or have you innovated? Ibn Mas'ud said: None of that happened, but God and His Messenger refused that we wait for you with our prayer while you are busy with your need."],[3921,"There will come after you officials who do not pray the prayer at its time. When they do that, pray it at its time."],[3922,"Slow, measured rhythmic tones, slow, measured rhythmic tones."],[3923,"They used to pray Zuhr and 'Asr with al-Hajjaj, and he would delay until evening."],[3924,"Al-Hajjaj delivered the khutbah on Friday and delayed the prayer, so a man wanted to leap at him, and the people restrained him."],[3926,"Al-Walid once delayed the Friday prayer until evening. He said: So I prayed Zuhr before I sat down, then I prayed 'Asr while I was sitting and he was delivering the khutbah. He said: I placed my hands on my knees and gestured with my head."],[3927,"I saw Sa'id b. Jubayr and 'Ata' b. Abi Rabah. He said: \"Al-Walid once delayed the prayer, and I saw them gesturing with their heads while seated.\""],[3928,"They used to pray Zuhr when the time for Zuhr arrived, and when the time for 'Asr arrived, they prayed 'Asr in the mosque where they were. Ibn Ziyad used to delay Zuhr and 'Asr."],[3929,"He used to combine prayers with al-Mukhtar the liar."],[3930,"He used to command us to pray Jumu'ah in our homes, then come to the mosque, because al-Hajjaj used to delay the prayer."],[3932,"Hasan and Husayn would hasten when they heard Marwan's caller, even while they were insulting him, and pray with him."],[3933,"I asked Yahya b. Abi Kathir, when the Kharijites had gained power over us, 'O Abu Nasr, what do you think about praying behind these people?' He said, 'The Qur'an is your imam. Pray with them as long as they pray it at its proper time.'"],[3934,"I saw Ibn 'Umar, Ibn al-Zubayr, Najdah, and al-Hajjaj, while Ibn 'Umar said, 'They are tumbling into the Fire just as flies tumble into broth.' But when he heard the mu'adhdhin, he would hasten to him—meaning their mu'adhdhin—and pray with him."],[3935,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[3937,"I said to 'Ata': 'A group of people gathered on a journey—a Qurayshi, an Arab, a client, a slave, and a bedouin from the desert. Which of them should lead his companions?' He said, 'The most learned in fiqh among them should lead them. If they are equal in fiqh, then the one who recites the Qur'an best. If they are equal in fiqh and recitation, then the eldest among them.' I said, 'If they are equal in fiqh and recitation, and the slave is the eldest among them, should he lead them because of his age, leading the Qurayshi and others?' He said, 'Yes. And why should not the most knowledgeable among them, the best reciter, and the eldest lead them, whoever he may be?'"],[3938,"Salim, the client of Abu Hudhayfah, used to lead the first Muhajirun and the Ansar from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ in the mosque of Quba, among them Abu Bakr, 'Umar, Abu Salamah, Zayd, and 'Amir b. Rabi'ah."],[3939,"The people should be led by the one who recites the Qur'an best. If they are equal in recitation, then the one who made hijrah earliest. If they are equal in hijrah, then the most knowledgeable in the Sunnah. If they are equal in knowledge, then the eldest. And no man should lead another in his sphere of authority, nor sit on his seat of honor in his house except with his permission."],[3940,"The most entitled of the people to lead them is the one who recites the Book of Allah best. If they are equal in recitation, then the most knowledgeable in the Sunnah. If they are equal in the Sunnah, then the one who made hijrah earliest. If they are equal in hijrah, then the eldest. And no man should lead another in his sphere of authority, nor sit on his seat of honor in his house except with his permission."],[3941,"No one should lead the people except the one who recites the Qur'an best."],[3942,"A delegation of Jarm came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered Amr b. Salimah to lead them in prayer. He was the youngest of them in age, because he had memorized the most Quran among them."],[3943,"When three people are on a journey, the one who knows the most Quran should lead them in prayer. If he is the youngest of them, then when he leads them, he is their leader.\" Abu Salamah said: \"That is a leader appointed by the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[3944,"Umar b. al-Khattab met a group of riders heading for the House. He asked, \"Who are you?\" The youngest of them answered, \"Servants of God, the Muslims.\" He asked, \"Where have you come from?\" He said, \"From the deep mountain pass.\" He asked, \"Where are you heading?\" He said, \"To the Ancient House.\" Umar said, \"By God, he interpreted it well.\" Then Umar asked, \"Who is your leader?\" They pointed to an elder among them. Umar said, \"Rather, you are their leader,\" referring to the youngest one who had answered him well."],[3945,"I said to Ata: If the most knowledgeable in jurisprudence among the people puts forward another besides himself? He said: \"There is no harm in that. Indeed, I do it.\""],[3946,"A delegation came to us from the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he taught them the prayer. Then he said to us: \"Let the one among you who knows the most Quran lead you.\" So Amr b. Salimah led them in prayer, even though he had not yet reached puberty."],[3947,"The owner of the dwelling leads those who come to him.\" I said to him: \"What is the dwelling?\" He said: \"His home.\""],[3948,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[3949,"Abu Said prepared food and invited Abu Dharr, Hudhayfah, and Ibn Masud. When the prayer time came, Abu Dharr stepped forward to lead them in prayer. Hudhayfah said to him: \"Step back; the master of the house has more right to lead the prayer.\" Abu Dharr asked: \"Is that so, O Ibn Masud?\" He said: \"Yes.\" So Abu Dharr stepped back."],[3950,"I said to Ata: What about a boy who has not reached puberty, if he is visited in his dwelling? He said: \"He may lead them if he has not reached puberty, but it is said that he has a right. If he wishes, he may lead them by virtue of his right, and if he wishes, he may give his right to someone else among them.\""],[3951,"Ibn Umar came to Makkah, and people came to him at his lodging. When the prayer time came, he led them. When he said the salam, he said: \"Complete [your prayer].\""],[3952,"I went looking for Ibn Masud at his house and was told, \"He is at Abu Musa al-Ashari's.\" I went there and found Abdullah and Hudhayfah. Abdullah said to Hudhayfah: \"Are you the one who made that statement?\" Hudhayfah said: \"Yes, by God, I said it because I disliked that it be said, 'So-and-so recited and so-and-so recited,' as the Children of Israel became divided.\" Then the prayer was called, and Abu Musa stepped forward and led them because they were in his house."],[3953,"From Abu Sad, the freedman of Banu Usayd: He said: I married while I was a slave and invited the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ—Abu Dharr, Ibn Masud, and Hudhayfah. When the prayer time came, Hudhayfah stepped forward to lead us. Abu Dharr or someone else said to him: \"That is not for you.\" So they put me forward, even though I was a slave, and I led them in prayer."],[3954,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: If the slave and the Bedouin do not recite the Qurʾān, may they lead in prayer those who come to them in their quarter? He said: \"No, by my life, they may not lead.\" I said: If they recite only the Opening of the Qurʾān (Umm al-Qurʾān)? He said: \"I fear that with it they have no understanding (fiqh), and that they are coarse and know nothing.\""],[3955,"They used to come to ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers, in the upper part of the valley—he and his father, and ʿUbayd b. ʿUmayr, and al-Miswar b. Makhramah, and many people. Abū ʿUmar, the freedman of ʿĀʾishah, would lead them in prayer, and Abū ʿAmr, her slave who had not been manumitted. The imam of her household was Muḥammad b. Abī Bakr, and ʿUrwah, and their households, except that ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Abī Bakr would hold back from him. Abū ʿAmr said: ʿĀʾishah said: \"When Abū ʿAmr lowers me into my grave and lets me down, he is free.\""],[3956,"ʿĀʾishah's slave named Dhakwān used to lead her in prayer. Maʿmar said: Ayyūb said, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah: He used to lead whoever entered upon her, except that when ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Abī Bakr entered, he would pray with her."],[3957,"ʿĀʾishah's slave named Dhakwān used to lead her in prayer. Maʿmar said: Ayyūb said, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah: He used to lead whoever entered upon her, except that when ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Abī Bakr entered, he would pray with her."],[3958,"I asked Ibrāhīm about the slave: May he lead in prayer? He said: \"Yes, if he establishes the prayer.\""],[3959,"Men among the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—he said, I think he said: among the companions of Badr—lost their sight, and they used to lead their clans in prayer. Among them were ʿAbd Allāh b. Umm Maktūm, ʿItbān b. Mālik, and Muʿādh b. ʿAfrāʾ."],[3960,"The Prophet ﷺ appointed Ibn Umm Maktūm as his deputy on the day of the expedition of Tabūk, and he used to lead the people in prayer while he was blind."],[3961,"When he traveled, he would appoint Ibn Umm Maktūm as his deputy over Madinah."],[3962,"The Prophet ﷺ went out on an expedition and ordered ʿAbd Allāh b. Umm Maktūm to lead his companions in prayer, as well as those who stayed behind from the Prophet ﷺ among the chronically ill and those unable to go out."],[3963,"ʿAṭāʾ was asked about the blind man: May he lead the people in prayer? He said: \"What is wrong with it if he is the most learned in jurisprudence among them?\" A man said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"Unless he errs in the qiblah?\" He said: ʿAṭāʾ said: \"If he errs, they should straighten him. He may lead them if he is the most learned in jurisprudence among them.\""],[3964,"I asked Ibrāhīm about the blind man: May he lead in prayer? He said: \"Yes, if he establishes the prayer.\""],[3965,"\"How can I lead them when they straighten me toward the qiblah?\"—when he became blind."],[3966,"Ibn 'Abbas prayed on a cushion or a carpet that covered his house."],[3970,"I asked Ibrahim: about the son of adultery, the bedouin, the slave, and the blind man—may they lead? He said: \"Yes, if they establish the prayer.\""],[3971,"The son of adultery may marry, and his daughter may be given in marriage, his testimony is valid, and he may lead."],[3972,"I asked al-Zuhri: May the son of adultery lead? He said: \"Yes, and what is his fault?\" I said: What about the effeminate man? He said: \"No, and no honor, and he is not to be followed.\""],[3973,"The tashahhud suffices you, even if you pray a hundred rak'ahs."],[3974,"I was with Anas b. Malik, and he came out from his land heading for Basra, and between it and Basra were three miles or three parasangs. The prayer came, so he put forward a son of his called Abu Bakr, and he led us in the dawn prayer, and he recited Surat Tabarak. When he finished, he said to him: \"You made it long for us.\""],[3975,"Al-Zubayr used to pray behind his son Abdullah."],[3976,"Abdullah b. al-Zubayr used to lead al-Zubayr and Talha."],[3977,"A boy should not lead the prayer until he has a wet dream, and let the best among you give the adhan for you."],[3979,"A boy should not lead the prayer until he has a wet dream, and let the best among you give the adhan for you."],[3980,"Muhammad b. Abi Suwayd appointed him for the people as a boy in al-Ta'if in the month of Ramadan to lead them in prayer, and he wrote about that to Umar, bringing him good news. Umar became angry and wrote to him: It was not your place to put forward for the people a boy upon whom the legal punishments are not incumbent."],[3981,"Al-Dahhak b. Qays ordered a boy before he had a wet dream, and he led the people in prayer. It was said to him: Why did you do that? Al-Dahhak said: He has with him of the Quran what is not with me, so I only gave precedence to the Quran."],[3982,"The Arabs used to say: Look at this one and what he will do and his people—meaning the Prophet ﷺ. When the Prophet ﷺ conquered Makkah, delegations of people came to him. There was a boy from Jarm called Amr b. Salimah. Whenever anyone of those who had come as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him, he would learn Quran from him. He said: He had more Quran than the rest of his people, so he used to lead them in prayer while he was a young boy who had not had a wet dream. He had a worn-out waist-wrapper, and an old woman of the tribe would say: Will you not clothe your imam? He said: They bought a waist-wrapper for me for three dirhams. He said: I rejoiced at it greatly."],[3983,"The prayer was commenced in a mosque in a district of Madinah. He said: Abdullah b. Umar had land near that mosque which he worked. He said: The imam of the people of that mosque was a client, and the dwelling of that client and his companions was there. When Abdullah b. Umar heard them and they commenced the prayer, he came to attend the prayer with them. The client, the owner of the mosque, said to Ibn Umar: Come forward and lead. Abdullah said: You have more right to lead in your mosque. So the client led the prayer."],[3984,"I said to Ata: A traveller passed by some people at a watering place, and the time for prayer came, so they put him forward; they had no imam. May he lead them? He said: There is no harm in that."],[3985,"A group gathered at some water around Makkah—he said: I think he said: in the upper part of the valley here—he said: during the Hajj. The time for prayer came, so a man from the family of Abu al-Sa'ib al-Makhzumi, whose tongue was non-Arab, came forward. He said: Al-Miswar b. Makhramah put him back and brought forward another, and Umar b. al-Khattab was absent so he did not let him know about anything until he came to Madinah. When he came to Madinah, he let him know about that. Al-Miswar said: Grant me respite, O Commander of the Believers. The man's tongue was non-Arab, and it was during the Hajj, so I feared that some of the pilgrims would hear his recitation and adopt his foreign accent. He said: Is that where you were going? He said: Yes. He said: You did right."],[3986,"I said to Ata: A person is brought in his quarter and leads the people in prayer, and then he recites something of the Quran and adds rhymed prose to it. He said: Then he should not lead you, so do not pray with them. But if he mixes from the Quran from here and there, then pray with his prayer."],[3987,"Ibn Mas'ud passed by some people at a watering place when the prayer had been commenced, so he entered with them. A man among them led them, and he recited and added to his recitation: We make pilgrimage to the house of our Lord and we pay the debt. Someone other than Qatadah added: And they are like sandgrouse hurrying down. Ibn Mas'ud said: {We have not heard of this in the latter religion; this is nothing but a fabrication}. He said: The bedouin stepped back, and Ibn Mas'ud came forward and led them in prayer."],[3988,"Ibn Mas'ud passed by a mosque of ours, and a man among them came forward and recited the Opening of the Book, then said: We make pilgrimage to the house of our Lord and we pay the debt, and he is like sandgrouse hurrying down. Abdullah said: {We have not heard of this in the latter religion; this is nothing but a fabrication}. He said: Abdullah left."],[3989,"Ibn Mas'ud prayed behind the bedouin, and the bedouin recited the Mother of the Quran. When he finished it and said: {not of those who incurred wrath nor of those who went astray}, he said: We make pilgrimage to the house of our Lord and we pay it the debt, on the like of sandgrouse hurrying down. Ibn Mas'ud said: {We have not heard of this in the latter religion; this is nothing but a fabrication}. He said: The bedouin stepped back until Ibn Mas'ud came forward, knowing that he was more learned than him. Ibn Mas'ud said: I have not seen a bedouin more learned than him."],[3990,"I said to Ata: Is it not the case that, if the imam wishes, when he leads the people in prayer in what the voice is raised with of recitation, he raises his voice with the Mother of the Quran in every rak'ah only, not adding anything to it? He said: Yes, and it is more beloved to me that he raises his voice with it along with a surah."],[3991,"I said to 'Ata': Was the imam commanded to raise his voice with the Qur'an? He said: 'Yes, and al-Zubayr used to raise his voice in recitation until his recitation in the mosque had a loud murmur.' I said: 'Do you think that if a man is an imam and does no more than make them hear something, that suffices him?' He said: 'It is sufficient for him.'"],[3992,"'Umar's recitation in the morning prayer used to be heard from the house of Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas."],[3993,"'Umar's recitation used to be heard from al-Balat."],[3994,"I spent the night at my maternal aunt Maymunah's, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ got up to pray voluntarily during the night. He went to the water-skin and performed ablution, then he stood to pray. When I saw him do that, I got up and performed ablution from the water-skin, then I stood on his left side. He took hold of my hand from behind his back and positioned me thus from behind his back to his right side.' I said: 'Was that in voluntary prayer?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[3995,"He gestures with one rakʿah."],[3996,"It was mentioned to us from Ibn 'Abbas that it was mentioned to him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept, and he said: 'Indeed the Prophet ﷺ was protected [from sleeping deeply].'"],[3998,"I stood up to [join] the Prophet ﷺ, and he turned me around and placed me on his right side."],[3999,"He spent the night at his maternal aunt Maymunah's. He said: 'I lay down across the width of the cushion, while the Prophet ﷺ and his wife lay along its length. The Prophet ﷺ slept until the middle of the night, or a little before or after it. Then he woke up and sat, wiping sleep from his face with his hands. Then he recited the ten final verses of Surah Al 'Imran. Then he went to a hanging water-skin, performed ablution from it, and did his ablution well. Then he stood to pray. I did the same as he had done, then I went and stood beside him. He placed his hands on my head and took hold of my ear, twisting it. Then he prayed two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then he prayed witr. Then he lay down until the mu'adhdhin came, so he got up and prayed two brief rak'ahs, then he went out and prayed the morning prayer.'"],[4000,"I prayed with my father, and his wife stood behind us."],[4001,"I was in Maymunah's house, and the Prophet ﷺ got up to pray at night. I stood up with him on his left side, so he took hold of my hand and placed me on his right side. Then he prayed thirteen rak'ahs, and I estimated that his standing in each rak'ah was about the length of [Surah] 'O you wrapped in garments' (Al-Muzzammil)."],[4002,"He stood alone to the left of Ibn 'Umar, and he pulled him with his right [hand] until he pulled him to his right side."],[4003,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think about a man praying with another man—where should he stand in relation to him? He said: Thus, on his right side. I said: Should he align with him so that they form a row together, neither one being ahead of the other? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Is it necessary for him to stand close to him so that there is no gap between them? He said: Yes, by God, indeed."],[4004,"I prayed with Anas b. Mālik and he placed me on his right, while Jamīlah, the mother of his child, stood behind us."],[4005,"From Qatādah regarding two men who are accompanied by a woman: he said: The man stands on the right of his companion, and the woman stands behind them both."],[4006,"Regarding His saying, “If you fear, then on foot or mounted”: He said: “Two takbīrs suffice, wherever he is facing.”"],[4007,"The man stands at the corner of the imam, and the two women stand behind them. I said: What if there are several women? He said: Likewise as well, the man at the corner of the man, and the women behind them both."],[4008,"I prayed beside the Prophet ﷺ while ʿĀʾishah was behind us praying with us, and I was beside the Prophet ﷺ praying with him."],[4009,"From Qatādah regarding two men who are accompanied by a woman: he said: The man stands on the right of his companion, and the woman stands behind them both."],[4010,"She invited the Prophet ﷺ to a meal she had prepared, and he ate. Then he said: Arise, and let us pray for you. He said: I went to a mat of ours that had turned black from long use, and I sprinkled it with water. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood, and the orphan and I lined up behind him, and the old woman behind us, and he prayed two rakʿahs for us, then departed."],[4011,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if they are three? He said: Some people say: two stand at his corner, and another stands behind him. He said: I said: What do you say? He said: I say the three are a congregation, so when they are three, one of them should lead them, and the two others should step back and stand."],[4012,"Ibn Umar used to shorten the prayer at the nearest point he considered for it: a property of his that he inspected from Khaybar, which is a journey of three moderate marches. He would not shorten for anything less than that. I said, 'How far is Khaybar?' He said, 'Three moderate marches.' I said, 'And al-Ta'if?' He said, 'Yes, it is on the easy route and a little farther.'"],[4015,"Three is a congregation."],[4016,"Alqama and al-Aswad came with Ibn Mas'ud to a mosque, and the people met them having already prayed. He took them to the Ka'ba, placing one on his right and the other on his left, and then prayed with them."],[4019,"Three is a congregation."],[4020,"Regarding the imam who attends the prayer with only one man besides him: he said, 'He places him on his right, then when a third comes, he steps back and the two stand behind him.'"],[4021,"He shortens the prayer on a journey of two days."],[4022,"His father Abd Allah b. Utba entered upon Umar and found him praying a voluntary prayer, so he stood on his left, and Umar moved him to his right. Then Yarfa', the freedman of Umar, came, so I stepped back with him, and I and Yarfa' prayed behind him."],[4023,"When the prayer is established and there is no one in the mosque except the imam and one man, he stands behind him until the rak'a is nearly over; if someone comes, then he may stand behind, otherwise he moves forward to his right."],[4024,"I would stand behind al-Aswad until the muezzin descended."],[4025,"If you stay in a land for four days, then pray four."],[4026,"I would stand behind al-Aswad until the muezzin descended."],[4027,"Whoever leads a people while they dislike him, his prayer will not pass beyond his collarbone."],[4028,"Whoever leads a people while they dislike him, his prayer will not pass beyond his collarbone."],[4030,"Shurayḥ used to lead his people in prayer. When the affair of Ḥujr b. ʿAdī took place, they suspected Shurayḥ concerning his matter. When he stepped forward to lead them in prayer, they said: “Step back.” He said: “Are all of you upon this?” They said: “Yes.” So Shurayḥ stepped back."],[4031,"I saw Saʿīd b. Jubayr praying in the imam's arch."],[4032,"About a man who entered a mosque thinking that they had already prayed, so he prayed two rakʿahs of the obligatory prayer, and then the iqāmah was called. He said: “He enters with the imam and prays two rakʿahs, then says the salām, then makes the remaining two voluntary.” Al-Zubayr said: “I said to Ibrāhīm: ‘I did not know that anyone did this.’ He said: ‘Those before you used to do this.’”"],[4034,"I saw al-Hasan come to Thabit al-Bunani. He said: I think he visited him. He said: When the prayer was due, Thabit said: 'Go forward, O Abu Sa'id.' Al-Hasan said: 'You, for you are more entitled.' Thabit said: 'By Allah, I will never go before you.' He said: So al-Hasan went forward and kept away from the arch, not praying in it."],[4035,"The first association of partners in this misguidance was these prayer arches."],[4036,"There will be at the end of time a people whose lifespans are shortened, and they will adorn their mosques, and they will take therein altars like the altars of the Christians. When they do that, tribulation will be poured upon them."],[4037,"Sulayman saw Hudhayfah leading them in prayer on a bench of plaster, so he said: \"Step back, for you are only a man of the people, so do not elevate yourself above them.\" He said: \"You have spoken the truth.\""],[4038,"And al-Hasan used to do that."],[4039,"Ibn Mas'ud came to us at our mosque, and the prayer was commenced. It was said to him, 'Come forward,' so he said, 'Let your imam lead you.' It was said to him, 'The imam is not here.' He said, 'Then let a man from among you come forward.' So he came forward, and he wanted to stand on something like a bench, but 'Abdullah forbade him."],[4040,"that he saw Ibn 'Abbas praying in the enclosure with Mu'awiya."],[4041,"I saw Anas b. Malik praying with 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz in the enclosure."],[4042,"And al-Hasan used to do that."],[4043,"I saw al-Hasan in the enclosure praying more than once; he would nod off with his head, then get up and pray, without performing ablution."],[4044,"Ibn 'Umar was asked about the enclosure, and he said, 'They only did that for fear that they would be stabbed.'"],[4045,"And al-Hasan used to do that."],[4047,"Whoever prays the obligatory prayer, then decides to offer voluntary prayers, let him speak or walk, and let him pray in front of that. He said: And Ibn 'Abbas said, 'I say to the slave girl, \"See how much of the night has passed,\" only to separate between them.'"],[4048,"He saw Ibn 'Umar, and a man prayed the obligatory prayer, then stood in the place where he had prayed the obligatory prayer to offer voluntary prayers, and Ibn 'Umar pushed him. When he finished, Ibn 'Umar said to him, 'Do you know why I pushed you?' He said, 'No, except that I think you only pushed me for good.' He said, 'Yes, because you did not speak since you finished the obligatory prayer, and you did not pray in front of you.'"],[4049,"I prayed the Friday prayer with Mu'awiya in the enclosure, and when he said the salam, I stood in my place and prayed. When he entered, he sent for me and said, 'Do not repeat what you did. When you pray the Friday prayer, do not connect it until you speak or leave, for the Prophet of Allah ﷺ commanded that.'"],[4050,"It is not proper for the imam to pray in the place where he led the people until he moves or separates with speech."],[4051,"The imam should not offer voluntary prayers where he prays the obligatory prayer."],[4052,"When one of you prays the obligatory prayer and then wants to offer voluntary prayers, let him move forward a little, or move back a little, or to his right, or to his left."],[4053,"The imam should not offer voluntary prayers where he prays the obligatory prayer."],[4055,"He disliked that a man pray while covering his mouth."],[4056,"He used to lead them in prayer, then offer voluntary prayers in his place. He said: 'When he prayed the obligatory prayer, he would glorify Allah in his place.'"],[4057,"We asked Aishah about the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ. She said: \"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray for a long time at night standing, and for a long time sitting.\" I said: \"How did he do it?\" She said: \"When he recited while standing, he bowed while standing, and when he recited while sitting, he bowed while sitting.\""],[4058,"Abdullah came to us, and we wanted to put him forward as imam, but he said: 'Let one of you go forward.' Abdullah was asked about a man who prays the obligatory prayer, can he offer voluntary prayers in his place? He said: 'Yes.'"],[4059,"Ibn Mas'ud saw no harm in that."],[4060,"Does a man move from his place after praying the obligatory prayer to offer voluntary prayers? Tawus said: 'Are you teaching Allah your religion?'"],[4061,"They used to dislike that an imam leads them while reading from the mushaf, for they would be imitating the People of the Book."],[4062,"He used to pray sitting cross-legged."],[4063,"There is no harm in a man leading the prayer during Ramadan while reading from the mushaf."],[4064,"She used to recite from the mushaf while praying."],[4065,"Ibn Sirin used to pray while the mushaf was at his side, and if he hesitated he would look at it.\""],[4066,"I prayed Zuhr and Asr in my house, then I came to the Prophet ﷺ and sat with him. The prayer was called, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed while I did not pray. When he finished, he said: \"Are you not a Muslim?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"What is wrong with you that you did not pray?\" I said: \"I prayed in my house.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"When the prayer is called, pray, even if you have already prayed.\""],[4067,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ and spoke to him about a need. Then the prayer was called while I was sitting. The Prophet ﷺ prayed with the people and then finished. He found me sitting and said to me: \"Are you not a Muslim?\" I said: \"Yes, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"What prevented you from praying with us?\" I said: \"I prayed in my camp.\" He said: \"Even if you had prayed in your camp.\""],[4068,"A man prays sitting cross-legged."],[4069,"I went out with Hudhayfa. He passed by a mosque and prayed Maghrib with them, adding one rak'a, and he had already prayed."],[4070,"When I pray the obligatory prayer and then catch it with the people, I make the one I prayed in my house a supererogatory prayer and make my prayer with the imam the obligatory one. I said: \"What if you only caught one rak'a?\" He said: \"The same also.\""],[4071,"I asked him about Asr: should one repeat it when the congregation comes? Ma'mar said: Ibn al-Musayyib said: \"Pray with the people, for your prayer with them is better than your prayer alone by twenty-four prayers, or twenty-some prayers.\""],[4072,"A man asked Ibn al-Musayyib: \"I prayed in my house, then I came and found the people praying. Which of the two should I make my prayer?\" He said: \"Is that up to you? That is only up to God.\""],[4073,"If you have prayed among your family and then catch the prayer in the mosque with the imam, pray with him, except for the Fajr prayer and the Maghrib prayer, which is called the Isha prayer, for those two are not prayed twice."],[4074,"When he prayed in his house and then went out and found the imam praying, he would pray with him except for Fajr and Maghrib."],[4078,"I prayed the later 'Isha' and performed Witr, then I entered the mosque while the imam was in the last rak'ah. I went to make it even, but I did not finish until the imam bowed and rose from the last rak'ah. He said: \"Do not repeat it, but perform Witr.\""],[4079,"I said to 'Ata': \"What if I pray one rak'ah alone and then they stand, and I fear that I will not be able to make my rak'ah even with another before they finish—should I pray with them?\" He said: \"Rather make it even with a rak'ah, then depart and pray with them.\""],[4080,"I said to him: The sick person is lying down and cannot sit. He said: 'Let him pray turned to the side; if he cannot, let him pray lying down, gesturing with his head.' He said: I said: Should he place his hands on his knees when he bows and prostrates? He said: 'No, but let him gesture with his head and hands, and for the takbir with his hands.'"],[4081,"I heard that voluntary prayer is disliked in the middle of the day until the sun declines, and when the sun is rising, and when it is setting.\" He said: \"It reached me that it rises between the two horns of Satan, and sets between his two horns.\""],[4082,"He asked the Prophet ﷺ, saying: \"What are you?\" He said: \"A prophet.\" He said: \"To whom were you sent?\" He said: \"To the red and the black.\" He said: \"At what time is prayer disliked?\" He said: \"From the time you pray Subh until the sun rises a spear's length, and from when the sun turns yellow until its setting.\" He said: \"Which supplication is most heard?\" He said: \"In the latter part of the night, and following the obligatory prayers.\" He said: \"When is the sun's setting?\" He said: \"From the first moment the sun turns yellow—when yellowness enters it—until the sun sets.\""],[4083,"O Messenger of God, which part of the night is most heard? He said: \"The middle of the latter part of the night.\" He said: \"Then prayer is accepted until dawn rises. Then there is no prayer until the sun is a spear's length or two, then there is no prayer until the sun sets.\""],[4084,"Indeed, the sun rises between the two horns of a devil—or he said: a devil's horn rises with it—and when it is high, it leaves it; when it is in the middle of the sky, it accompanies it; when it declines—or he said: when it passes the zenith—it leaves it; and when it nears setting, it accompanies it. So do not pray in these three hours."],[4085,"Let none of you deliberately seek to pray at sunrise or at sunset."],[4086,"Do not deliberately seek sunrise or sunset, for a devil's two horns rise with its rising and set with its setting.\" He said: \"'Umar used to strike men for these two.\""],[4088,"Indeed this prayer that was prescribed upon those before you — meaning al-ʿAṣr — they neglected it. So whoever guards it today, for him is its reward twice, and there is no prayer after it until the witness is seen, and the witness is the star."],[4089,"I do not love that a man's prayer, when the sun reddens — or he said: yellows — be with two fals until it rises the height of a palm tree."],[4090,"Look at these who have abandoned the prayer, so that when it is the hour in which prayer is disliked, they stand up praying. He said: 'And that is when the storyteller stood up early.'"],[4091,"He should perform the major ritual bath."],[4092,"When Abu Musa sat with Umar b. al-Khattab, he would sometimes say to him: 'Remind us of our Lord, O Abu Musa.' He said: 'Then he would recite.'"],[4093,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4094,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4095,"I used to pray two rakʿahs after al-ʿAṣr, then Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī met me and forbade me from them. He said: 'Should I leave them for you?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[4096,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade prayer in two hours: after al-ʿAṣr until the sun sets, and after al-Ṣubḥ until the sun rises."],[4097,"I saw Ibn al-Zubayr praying two rakʿahs after al-ʿAṣr, and I said: 'What is this?' He said: 'ʿĀʾishah informed me that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray two rakʿahs after al-ʿAṣr.' He said: 'So I went to ʿĀʾishah and asked her, and she said: 'He spoke the truth.' I said: 'I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'There is no prayer after al-ʿAṣr until the sun sets, and none after al-Fajr until the sun rises.' So the Messenger of God ﷺ does what he was commanded, and we do what we were commanded.'"],[4099,"ʿUmar struck al-Munkadir when he saw him performing supererogatory prayer after al-ʿAṣr."],[4101,"I saw Umar b. al-Khattab beating the people for [praying] the two rak'as after Asr."],[4102,"Ali prayed two rak'as after Asr while traveling, and Umar became angry with him and said: \"By Allah, you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid this.\""],[4103,"I said to him: \"Did you see Ibn Umar praying on the Day of Sacrifice in the early part of the day?\" He said: \"No, not on the Day of Sacrifice or any other day, until the sun had risen high.\" He said: \"Ibn Umar used to say: 'As for me, I pray as I saw my companions praying, but I do not forbid anyone from praying at night or during the day, as long as he does not intend the rising of the sun or its setting, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade that and said: \"The horn of Satan rises with the rising of the sun, so let no one intend the rising of the sun or its setting.\"'\","],[4105,"I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray after Asr at all, except once when some people came to him after Zuhr and kept him busy with something, and he did not pray anything after Zuhr until he had prayed Asr. She said: \"When he had prayed Asr, he entered my house and prayed two rak'as.\""],[4106,"For you is the fill of your house."],[4107,"Umar b. al-Khattab, while he was caliph, saw him [Zayd b. Khalid] praying two rak'as after Asr, and he walked over to him and struck him with the whip while he was still praying. When he finished, Zayd said: \"Strike me, O Commander of the Believers, for by Allah I will never abandon them after I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray them.\" He said: \"So Umar sat down beside him and said: 'O Zayd b. Khalid, were it not that I feared the people would take them as a means to pray until the night, I would not have struck you for them.'\""],[4108,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Asr, and a man stood up to pray after it. Umar b. al-Khattab grabbed his cloak—or his garment—and said: \"Sit down, for the People of the Book before you were destroyed because there was no break between their prayers.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Ibn al-Khattab has spoken the truth.\""],[4109,"I asked Ibn Abbas about praying after Asr, and he said: \"Pray as much as you like until nightfall.\" He said: \"And I saw Umar beating a man whom he saw praying after Asr.\""],[4111,"Tawus made me stand at the Khayf of Mina between him and the people, so that he could pray two rak'as after Asr. He said: \"So he prayed two rak'as and said to me: 'Do you pray after Asr?' He said: 'By Allah, I was forced to.'\""],[4112,"Abu Ayyub al-Ansari used to pray two rak'ahs after 'Asr before Umar became caliph. When Umar became caliph, he abandoned them. When he died, he resumed praying them. It was said to him, 'What is this?' He said, 'Umar used to beat people for them.'"],[4114,"We used to pray 'Asr with Ibn al-Zubayr in the Sacred Mosque, and he would pray two rak'ahs after 'Asr, and we would pray them with him, standing in a row behind him."],[4118,"The Prophet ﷺ would come out to us after the sun had set and night had fallen, and before the call for Maghrib was made, while we were praying. He did not forbid us nor command us."],[4120,"Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman did not pray the two rak'ahs before Maghrib."],[4121,"I saw the elite of the companions of Muhammad ﷺ, when the call for Maghrib was made, rush to the pillars to pray two rak'ahs before Maghrib."],[4124,"When the prayer has been established, there is no prayer except the obligatory one."],[4125,"He heard Muslim b. ʿAqīl say to the people while they were praying, and the prayer had already been established: “Woe to you! There is no prayer once the prayer has been established.”"],[4127,"He said: “Should I pray two rakʿahs while the muʾadhdhin is calling the iqāmah?” He replied: “Can you handle that?”"],[4129,"When the prayer has been established, there is no prayer. If the imam comes out while you are bowing, then add another light rakʿah to it, then say the salām."],[4130,"The Messenger of God ﷺ passed by Ibn al-ʿUshb while he was praying two rakʿahs when the prayer had been established. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Two prayers together?”"],[4131,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “The imam has come out while I am praying a voluntary prayer—should I complete it?” He said: “Join it with it.” I said: “I have not yet said the salām of departure.” He said: “Have you not already recited the tashahhud?” I said: “Yes.” He said: “That is enough for you—join it with it.”"],[4132,"I was standing in prayer when I passed by a verse of prostration, so I fell down prostrating for that prostration. He said: “Join it with it.” I said: “Should I say the takbīr?” He said: “Yes.” I said: “Should I seek refuge?” He said: “Yes.” I said: “Is my seeking refuge for the voluntary prayer not enough?” He said: “It is, but I prefer that you seek refuge.”"],[4133,"Nāfiʿ b. Jubayr used to join the voluntary prayer with the obligatory one. He said: “I recognized that,” and he said: “It is not his own opinion.”"],[4134,"They both used to do that: join the voluntary prayer with the obligatory one."],[4135,"About a man who entered a mosque thinking that they had already prayed, so he prayed two rakʿahs of the obligatory prayer, and then the iqāmah was called. He said: “He enters with the imam and prays two rakʿahs, then says the salām, then makes the remaining two voluntary.” Al-Zubayr said: “I said to Ibrāhīm: ‘I did not know that anyone did this.’ He said: ‘Those before you used to do this.’”"],[4136,"He interrupts his prayer and joins the people."],[4137,"If you connect the voluntary prayer to the prescribed one, it is like speech. He said: \"Rather, say the salam and join them.\""],[4138,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out while the muezzin was making the iqamah, and he prayed Fajr. He found two men praying, and he said: \"Two prayers together?\""],[4141,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying two rak'ahs when the Fajr prayer had already been established, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Which of them is your prayer? The one you prayed alone, or the one you prayed with us?\""],[4143,"I said to Ata: 'I entered the mosque while the imam was in prayer and I had not prayed the two rak'ahs.' He said: 'Pray them in the mosque, unless you fear missing the rak'ah the imam is in.'"],[4144,"I said to Ata: 'Is it more beloved to you that I pray the two rak'ahs on the road?' He said: 'I do not care where you pray them, as long as you pray them before the prayer.'"],[4145,"I said to Ata: 'What do you think if I fear missing Subh?' He said: 'Then leave them, and do not miss any part of Subh.' He said: 'Then I informed him of what Abu Sa'd al-A'ma narrated to us about the one who prayed them after Subh in the time of the Prophet ﷺ.'"],[4147,"If you miss praying them before Fajr, then pray them after Fajr."],[4148,"When the prayer has been established and you have not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, pray with the imam. Then when he finishes, pray them after Fajr."],[4149,"And I saw Ibn Jurayj pray them after Fajr in the mosque of Sana'a after the imam had said the salam."],[4150,"He went out for Fajr, and the Prophet ﷺ entered into the Fajr prayer, and he had not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. So he prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, then when the Fajr prayer finished he stood up and prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. The Prophet ﷺ passed by him and said, 'What is this prayer?' So he told him, and the Prophet ﷺ remained silent and went on without saying anything."],[4151,"He entered the mosque while the people were in prayer, and he had not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. So he entered the prayer with the people, then he sat until the sun rose for him, then he made them up. He said: And when the prayer was established while he was in the streets, he would pray them in the street."],[4152,"He prayed two rak'ahs at Duha, though he had never prayed the Duha prayer. It was said to him, 'We have never seen you pray this prayer.' He said, 'I had forgotten the two rak'ahs of Fajr, so I prayed them now.'"],[4153,"While he was getting dressed for Fajr, he heard the iqamah, so he prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr in the room, then he went out and prayed with the people. He said: 'And Ibn Umar, when he found the imam praying and he had not prayed them, would enter the prayer with the imam, then pray them after sunrise.'"],[4154,"He used to say: 'Yes, by Allah, if I enter while the people are in prayer, I will head to one of the pillars of the mosque, then I will pray the two rak'ahs and complete them, and I will not rush to complete them, then I will walk to the people and pray Fajr with them.'"],[4155,"Ibn Mas'ud came to us while the imam was praying Fajr, so he prayed two rak'ahs by a pillar, and he had not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr."],[4156,"He witnessed Sa‘īd b. al-‘Āṣ in a campaign called Dhāt al-Khashab, and with him was Ḥudhayfah. Sa‘īd said: “Which of you witnessed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of God ﷺ?” Ḥudhayfah said: “I did.” So Ḥudhayfah ordered them, and they put on their weapons. Then he said: “If an attack comes upon you, fighting is permitted for you.” He said: Then he prayed one rak‘ah with one of the two groups, while the other group faced the enemy. Then those departed and took the position of those, and those came, and he prayed another rak‘ah with them. Then he said the salām to them."],[4157,"The traveler prays two rak'ahs until he returns, unless he enters a city of the cities of the Muslims, in which case he prays the full prayer."],[4158,"Masruq used to pray them while the imam was standing praying in the mosque."],[4159,"When you enter the mosque and the imam is in prayer, and you have not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, pray them, then join the imam."],[4160,"If he has not made up the two rak'ahs of Fajr, there is nothing upon him."],[4161,"He heard the Prophet ﷺ stand in the Fajr prayer when he raised his head from bowing and say: \"Our Lord, and to You belongs praise,\" in the last rak'ah. He said: \"O Allah, curse so-and-so and so-and-so,\" supplicating against some of the hypocrites. He said: Then Allah revealed: \"You have no part in the matter, whether He turns to them in mercy or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers.\""],[4162,"He shortens the prayer on a journey of two days."],[4163,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed qunut for a month in the dawn prayer, supplicating against some tribes of the Arabs: 'Usayyah, Dhakwan, Ri'l, and Lihyan—all of them from Banu Sulaym."],[4164,"A dog came while the Prophet ﷺ was praying the afternoon prayer with the people, about to pass in front of them. A man among the people said, 'O God, stop it,' and the dog died. When the Prophet ﷺ finished, he said, 'Which of you supplicated against it?' The man said, 'I did, O Messenger of God.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'If he had supplicated against a nation among the nations, it would have been answered for him.'"],[4165,"'Ayyash b. Abi Rabi'ah, Salamah b. Hisham, and al-Walid b. al-Walid b. al-Mughirah fled from the polytheists to the Prophet ﷺ. 'Ayyash and Salamah were shackled, riding on a camel, and al-Walid was driving them. Al-Walid's finger was wounded, and he said: \"Are you but a finger that has bled, and in the path of...\" The Prophet ﷺ knew of their coming to him and their situation before the people knew. He prayed the dawn prayer and bowed in the first rak'ah. When he raised his head, he supplicated for them before prostrating, saying: \"O Allah, save 'Ayyash b. Abi Rabi'ah. O Allah, save Salamah b. Hisham. O Allah, save al-Walid b. al-Walid. O Allah, save the weak among the believers. O Allah, tighten Your grip on Mudar and make it upon them like the years of Yusuf.\""],[4166,"I said to him: I supplicated in the obligatory prayer against a man and named him. He said: Your prayer is severed. Then he informed me then, saying: The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for 'Ayyash b. Abi Rabi'ah and bowed. When he raised his head from the bowing, he said while standing: \"O Allah, save 'Ayyash b. Abi Rabi'ah, al-Walid b. al-Walid b. al-Mughirah, Salamah b. Hisham, and the weak among Your servants.\" I said: So he supplicated with this and named what he named? He said: I do not know whether it was in a voluntary prayer or an obligatory one. I said: What do you think if the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for them in the obligatory prayer? He said: I do not know. Perhaps the Prophet ﷺ was commanded to do that, and we are not like him. Ibn Jurayj said: 'Ata' said: He supplicated for them, then, as far as I have heard, he did not supplicate after that."],[4167,"I said: A man supplicates in the obligatory prayer, seeking forgiveness from his Lord and asking Him. He said: \"I do not like it.\" I said: Does that sever his prayer? He said: \"No.\" I said: Should he perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: \"No.\" I said: Do you ever supplicate in the obligatory prayer before you greet from the final tashahhud? He said: \"Yes.\" He said: Sometimes desire takes hold of me in the obligatory prayer, so I seek forgiveness and ask, but only a little. He said: It is not the same—supplication for worldly matters and its desires is more severe than supplication for the Hereafter and seeking forgiveness."],[4168,"When you are in the obligatory prayer, do not supplicate for anything until the imam finishes."],[4169,"Ubayd Allah b. Umar wrote to Ibn Umar while he was in the land of Persia: We are staying until the new moon. So he wrote: Pray two rak'ahs."],[4170,"If you stay in a land for four days, then pray four."],[4171,"Supplicate in the obligatory prayer with what is in the Qur'an."],[4172,"Supplicate in the obligatory prayer with what is in the Quran."],[4173,"He prays two rak'ahs even if he stays a year."],[4174,"Ibn Mas'ud used to say: Carry your needs upon the prescribed prayer."],[4175,"He used to supplicate for al-Zubayr and Asma', his mother, naming them by their names in the prayer."],[4176,"He says in his prayer while prostrating: O Allah, forgive al-Zubayr b. al-'Awwam and Asma' bt. Abi Bakr."],[4177,"I said to 'Ata': Do you dislike asking forgiveness in the supererogatory prayer? He said: Yes, until he sits and bears witness, then asks forgiveness while seated. He said: \"And establish prayer for My remembrance.\""],[4178,"It has reached me that the Muslims used to speak during the prayer as the Jews and Christians speak, until it was revealed: \"And when the Quran is recited, listen to it and be silent.\""],[4179,"I prayed beside 'Abdullah, and I did not know what he was reciting until I heard him say: \"Increase me in knowledge,\" and I knew that he was in Ta-Ha."],[4180,"The Prophet ﷺ, when he passed by a verse of fear, sought refuge, and when he passed by a verse of mercy, he asked."],[4181,"They both held that there is no harm in a man supplicating in the supererogatory prayer, if he passes by a verse in which there is mention of Paradise or the Fire, to stop at it and ask and seek refuge."],[4182,"'A'ishah passed by this verse: \"So Allah conferred favor upon us and protected us from the punishment of the scorching fire,\" and she said: My Lord, confer favor upon me and protect me from the punishment of the scorching fire."],[4183,"I heard Ali: he recited in prayer, “Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High” (Q 87:1), and said, “Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.”"],[4184,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari recited in the Friday prayer, “Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High” (Q 87:1), and said, “Glory be to my Lord, the Most High,” and “Has there come to you the account of the Overwhelming?” (Q 88:1)."],[4185,"When he recited, “Is not that [Creator] able to give life to the dead?” (Q 75:40), he would say, “Glory be to You, O Allah: yes indeed!” And when he recited, “Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High” (Q 87:1), he would say, “Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.”"],[4186,"When the Prophet ﷺ recited “al-Tin” and reached, “Is not Allah the most just of judges?” (Q 95:8), he would say, “Yes indeed.” And when he recited, “Is not that [Creator] able to give life to the dead?” (Q 75:40), he would say, “Yes indeed.” And when he recited, “Then in what statement after it will they believe?” (Q 77:50), and in what has been sent down — or he said: “We believe in Allah and in what He has sent down.”"],[4187,"I spent the night at Hujr al-Madari's. I heard him praying at night. He recited and passed by this verse: “Have you seen that which you emit? Is it you who creates it, or are We the Creator?” (Q 56:58–59). He said, “Rather You, O Lord; rather You, O Lord; rather You, O Lord” — three times. Then he recited, “Have you seen that which you cultivate? Is it you who makes it grow, or are We the grower?” (Q 56:63–64). He said, “Rather You, O Lord; rather You, O Lord; rather You, O Lord” — three times. Then he said, “Have you seen the water that you drink? Is it you who brought it down from the clouds, or are We the bringer down?” (Q 56:68–69). He said, “Rather You, O Lord; rather You, O Lord; rather You, O Lord” — three times. Then he said, “Have you seen the fire that you kindle? Is it you who produced its tree, or are We the producer?” (Q 56:71–72). He said, “Rather You, O Lord” — he said it three times."],[4188,"He disliked that a man should pass by the mention of the Fire and seek refuge from it in the obligatory or voluntary prayer."],[4189,"It was disliked that when the imam passed by a verse of warning or a verse of mercy, those behind him should say anything."],[4190,"“So when the Quran is recited, listen to it and pay attention” (Q 7:204). He said, “This is in prayer.”"],[4191,"When the servant is occupied with My Book instead of asking Me, I give him better than what I give those who ask."],[4192,"I said: Is supplication in voluntary prayer like it is in the prescribed prayer — if I name a person, does it cut off my prayer? He said, “Yes. If you say it while you have an odd number, then make it even with a rak'ah, then depart and start your prayer afresh.”"],[4193,"Ata was asked: May a man pray while covering his mouth? He said: I prefer that you remove it from your mouth. I heard Abu Hurayrah say: When you pray, you are confiding in your Lord."],[4194,"He disliked that a man should put his hand or his garment over his mouth or over his nose during prayer."],[4195,"He disliked that a man pray while covering his mouth."],[4196,"Ibn Umar used to dislike that a man pray while covering his mouth."],[4197,"He disliked that a man pray while covering his mouth, and he used to say: \"If a man sneezes during prayer, let him praise Allah silently.\" I asked him about a man who sneezes in the toilet. He said: \"He praises Allah, for it ascends.\""],[4198,"Jada b. Hubayra saw a man wearing a face-guard while praying, so he sent a man to him: \"Uncover the face-guard from your mouth.\""],[4199,"Al-Hasan allowed a man to pray while covering his mouth if it was due to cold or an excuse."],[4200,"I said to Ata: \"In the prescribed prayer, should I say 'Subhan Allah, Subhan Allah' and point with my hand, then return to the row?\" He said: \"Yes, that is good.\""],[4201,"\"Tasbih is for men, and clapping is for women; saying 'iss iss' in prayer.\" Ata said: \"Abu Hurayra spoke with 'iss iss' in prayer.\" He said: \"Abu Hurayra said in prayer that this is from the speech of men and women, and Ata preferred that they say tasbih rather than clap or say 'iss iss.'\" Ata said: \"And Abu Hurayra clapped with his hands.\""],[4204,"Tasbih is for men, and clapping is for women."],[4206,"I was with Sahl b. Sa'd al-Sa'idi when he was told: \"There was something between the Banu Amr b. Awf and the people of Quba.\" He said: \"That was long ago. We were in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he was told that there was something between the people of Quba, so the Prophet ﷺ went to them to make peace between them. He was delayed from the people, so Bilal said to Abu Bakr: 'Shall I call the iqama for prayer?' He said: 'As you wish.' So Bilal called the iqama, and the people put Abu Bakr forward. While he was praying, the Prophet ﷺ came and began to pass through the rows until he stood behind Abu Bakr. They began to clap, and Abu Bakr would not turn around during prayer, but when they clapped a lot, he turned around and saw the Prophet ﷺ standing behind him. The Prophet ﷺ gestured to him to continue praying as he was, so Abu Bakr stepped back, and the Prophet ﷺ came forward and prayed. When he finished, he said: 'What prevented you from remaining as you were when I ordered you?' He said: 'It is not fitting for the son of Abu Quhafa to lead the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in prayer.' Then the Prophet ﷺ said: 'What is this clapping in prayer? Tasbih is for men, and clapping is for women.'"],[4207,"The Prophet ﷺ went out one day to the mosque and said: “Where is the Dawsī youth?” He said: “There he is, O Messenger of God, suffering from fever at the back of the mosque.” The Prophet ﷺ came to me, wiped my head, and said something good to me. Then he turned to the people and said: “If I forget something in my prayer, let the men say tasbīḥ and let the women clap.” He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ prayed and did not forget anything in his prayer. With the Prophet ﷺ were two rows and a half of men, and two rows of women—or two rows of men and two rows and a half of women."],[4208,"The Prophet ﷺ fell ill, so he ordered Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer. The Prophet ﷺ prayed for the people sitting, and he placed Abū Bakr behind him, between him and the people. He said: And the people prayed behind him standing. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: “If I could start my affair over again, I would not have you pray except sitting, following the prayer of your imām. As long as he prays standing, then pray standing, and if he prays sitting, then pray sitting.”"],[4209,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed and ordered Abū Bakr, so he stood level with him at his side, and he recited. When he finished the recitation and it was the time for bowing, the Prophet ﷺ stood up and bowed and prostrated with the people. I said: “How many rakʿahs did he pray, and which prayer was that?” He said: “I do not know, except that it was a prayer with recitation.”"],[4210,"The Prophet ﷺ came out one day while Abū Bakr was leading the people in prayer. Abū Bakr started to step back, but the Prophet ﷺ gestured to him to keep praying as he was. He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ came and sat at his side. The people prayed following the prayer of Abū Bakr, and Abū Bakr prayed following the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ, while the Prophet ﷺ was sitting."],[4211,"The Prophet ﷺ came during his illness and sat in his prayer place, and Abū Bakr stood at his side and prayed standing, following the Prophet ﷺ, while the people followed Abū Bakr."],[4212,"The Prophet ﷺ fell from a horse and injured his right side. They came to him and he prayed with them sitting, and he gestured to them to sit. When he said the salām, he said: “The imām is appointed only to be followed. When he says takbīr, then say takbīr; when he bows, then bow; when he says, ‘God hears the one who praises Him,’ then say, ‘Our Lord, and to You is the praise’; when he prostrates, then prostrate; and when he prays sitting, then all of you pray sitting.”"],[4213,"The Prophet ﷺ fell from a horse and injured his right side. He prayed with them sitting, and they prayed with him sitting. When he finished, he said: “The imām is appointed only to be followed. When he prays standing, then pray standing; when he bows, then bow; when he says, ‘God hears the one who praises Him,’ then say, ‘Our Lord, and to You is the praise’; and when he prays sitting, then all of you pray sitting.”"],[4214,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed sitting while leading the people. The people stood behind him, so he stretched his hand back to them, gesturing to them to sit."],[4215,"The Prophet ﷺ fell ill, and ʿUmar and a group with him entered to visit him. The prayer time came, so he prayed with them sitting while they were standing, and he gestured to them with his hand to sit. When he finished, he said: “The Persians—their kings were given superiority over them because they sit while others stand for them. So do not do that.” And he gestured with his hand behind him without raising them above his shoulders."],[4216,"“The imām is appointed only to be followed, so do not differ from him. When he says takbīr, then say takbīr; when he bows, then bow; when he says, ‘God hears the one who praises Him,’ then say, ‘O God, our Lord, to You is the praise’; when he prostrates, then prostrate; and when he prays sitting, then all of you pray sitting.”"],[4217,"“The imām is a commander. If he prays sitting, then pray sitting; and if he prays standing, then pray standing.”"],[4218,"Their imām fell ill during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: “He would lead us sitting, and we would be sitting.”"],[4219,"Usayd b. Hudayr fell ill, and he used to lead his people in prayer while sitting."],[4220,"It is more beloved to me, when the imam is ill, that he appoint someone to lead the people in prayer if he is unable to pray except sitting.\" He said: \"And if the imam prays sitting, that is the sunnah.\" I said: \"If he prays sitting, should I pray with him or leave him?\" He said: \"Rather pray with him. Would you turn away from the sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ?\" He said: \"And it is more beloved to me that they put forward someone else from among them.\""],[4223,"I did not see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray sitting until a year or two before his death. He used to pray his voluntary prayer sitting, and he would recite the surah slowly until it became longer in recitation than it actually was."],[4224,"Slow, measured rhythmic tones, slow, measured rhythmic tones."],[4225,"By the One who took his soul, meaning the Prophet ﷺ, he did not pass away until much of his prayer was offered sitting, except for the prescribed prayer. And the most beloved deed to him was that which its doer persists in, even if it is small."],[4226,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was very hard on his body in worship, except that when he entered old age and became heavy of flesh, most of his prayer was offered while sitting."],[4229,"The Prophet ﷺ did not recite in any of the night prayer while sitting until he entered old age. When thirty or forty verses remained for him, he would stand and recite them, then prostrate."],[4230,"We asked A'ishah about the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ. She said: \"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed standing, he bowed standing; and when he prayed sitting, he bowed sitting.\""],[4231,"We asked Aishah about the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ. She said: \"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray for a long time at night standing, and for a long time sitting.\" I said: \"How did he do it?\" She said: \"When he recited while standing, he bowed while standing, and when he recited while sitting, he bowed while sitting.\""],[4232,"It is recommended for a man, when he wants to pray sitting, to begin his prayer with two rak'ahs standing.\""],[4233,"A man may pray while sitting in voluntary prayer, if he wishes cross-legged, and if he wishes with his legs drawn up and wrapped.\" He said: \"And stretch out your leg if you wish after you have performed the tashahhud.\" He said: I said: \"And reclining?\" He said: \"No.\""],[4234,"He used to sit with his legs drawn up and wrapped at the end of his voluntary prayer."],[4235,"When he wanted to prostrate, he folded his leg and prostrated."],[4236,"When a man wants to pray sitting cross-legged, then when he wants to bow, he folds his thigh as he sits in prayer, then bows and prostrates,\" and \"the saying of Ibn al-Musayyab is more beloved to Sufyan.\""],[4237,"'And We have recited it in slow, measured rhythmic tones' (Q 25:32). He said: Part of it following upon part."],[4240,"Sitting on two embers is better for me than sitting cross-legged in the prayer."],[4243,"Ata prayed while sitting with his legs drawn up and wrapped in his garment, and Sa'id b. Jubayr passed by him and said, \"You look like people sitting around conversing.\" Then he released his own sitting posture. When he left, Ata released the sitting posture while he was praying."],[4245,"Slow, measured rhythmic tones, slow, measured rhythmic tones."],[4246,"Ata al-Khurasani would sit with his legs drawn up and wrapped in his garment during voluntary prayer. I said to him, \"From whom did you take this?\" And I told him the hadith of al-Zuhri from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said, \"I think I only took it from Ibn al-Musayyab.\""],[4249,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4250,"I said to Ata: \"I began the prayer standing, bowed for one rak'ah and prostrated, then stood up. May I sit if I wish without bowing or prostrating?\" He said, \"No.\""],[4251,"He saw Ibn Umar say: While a man was praying sitting with his legs drawn up and wrapped in his garment, he had placed his hands between his knees, clasping one hand with the other. Ibn Umar pulled him and then gestured to him: \"Place your palms on your knees.\""],[4252,"We arrived in Madinah, and a severe epidemic fever from Madinah afflicted us. People were frequently praying their voluntary prayers sitting. The Prophet ﷺ came out to them at noon while they were praying their voluntary prayers sitting, and said: \"The prayer of one sitting is half the prayer of one standing.\" He said: Then people began to force themselves to stand."],[4253,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4254,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4255,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying sitting down, and I said: O Messenger of God, I was told that you said: 'The prayer of one sitting is half of the prayer of one standing,' and you are praying sitting? He said: 'Yes, but I am not like any one of you.'"],[4256,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4257,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4258,"It was said to him: What is the sign of what the sick person prays sitting? He said: 'If he is unable to stand for his worldly affairs, let him pray sitting.'"],[4259,"I asked Ibrahim: How does the sick person pray? He said: 'His standing should be cross-legged.'"],[4260,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Usfan. He said: The polytheists, led by Khalid b. al-Walid, faced us, and they were between us and the qiblah. The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr, and they said: They were in a state where, had we caught them off guard... Then they said: A prayer will come upon them now that is dearer to them than their children and their own selves. He said: Then Gabriel descended with these verses between Zuhr and Asr: \"And when you are among them and lead them in prayer...\" He said: When the prayer time came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered them to take up their weapons, and we lined up behind him in two rows. He said: Then he bowed, and we all bowed. He said: Then he raised his head, and we all raised our heads. Then the Prophet ﷺ prostrated with the row next to him, and the others remained standing guarding them. When they had prostrated and stood up, the others sat down and prostrated in their place. Then these advanced to the positions of those, and those came to the positions of these. Then they all bowed, then he raised his head, and they all raised their heads. Then the Prophet ﷺ prostrated with the row next to him, and the others stood guarding them. When they sat down, the others sat down and prostrated. Then he said the taslim upon them, and then he departed. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed it twice: once at Usfan and once in the land of Banu Sulaym."],[4261,"I entered upon Ibrahim while he was sick and he was praying lying on his right side, gesturing for the noon prayer. He said: Other jurists used to say: 'He was lying on his back, with his feet facing the qiblah as much as if he stood, he would face the qiblah.'"],[4262,"The sick person prays lying on his back, with his feet facing the qiblah."],[4263,"If the sick person is unable to pray except lying down, he prays on his side facing the qiblah, gesturing."],[4264,"I said to him: The sick person is lying down and cannot sit. He said: 'Let him pray turned to the side; if he cannot, let him pray lying down, gesturing with his head.' He said: I said: Should he place his hands on his knees when he bows and prostrates? He said: 'No, but let him gesture with his head and hands, and for the takbir with his hands.'"],[4265,"If the sick person prays sitting, then when he bows, he places his hands on his knees, and when he prostrates, he places his hands on the ground if he can."],[4266,"When the sick person bows, he places his hands on his knees, and when he prostrates, he places his hands on the ground."],[4267,"He was asked about a sick person who has chronic diarrhea or something similar: how should he pray? He said: \"In every situation: lying on his back or on his side. If he can face the qiblah and cannot do more than that, he should gesture, making his prostration lower than his bowing.\""],[4268,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: If he is unable to prostrate on the ground, may he prostrate on a mat, or have pebbles brought to him on a small mat and prostrate on them? He said: \"No, but he should gesture with his head, making the prostration lower than the bowing.\""],[4269,"Ibn ʿUmar entered upon Ṣafwān al-Ṭawīl while he was praying on a cushion. He forbade him from praying on pebbles or on a cushion, and commanded him to gesture."],[4270,"Ibn ʿUmar entered upon Ibn Ṣafwān al-Ṭawīl and found him prostrating on a cushion. He forbade him and said: \"Gesture, and make the prostration lower than the bowing.\""],[4271,"I heard Ibn ʿUmar being asked whether a man may pray on a piece of wood while he is sick. He said: \"I do not command you to take idols besides God. Whoever is able to pray standing, let him pray standing; if he cannot, then sitting; if he cannot, then lying on his side, gesturing.\""],[4272,"When a sick person cannot prostrate on the ground, he should gesture."],[4273,"When a sick person cannot bow or prostrate, he should gesture with his head for the bowing and the prostration, and he says the takbīr."],[4274,"When a sick person cannot bow or prostrate, he should gesture with his head for the bowing and the prostration, and he says the takbīr."],[4275,"My father was afflicted with hemiplegia, so he sent me to Ibn ʿUmar: Should something be raised to him when he prays? Ibn ʿUmar said: \"Again? Between your eyes — gesture.\""],[4276,"He gestures with one rakʿah."],[4277,"I saw Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, prostrating on a cushion while she was seated — meaning she was praying sitting down."],[4278,"I asked Ibn ʿAbbās about a sick person who prostrates on a pure cushion. He said: \"There is no harm in it.\""],[4280,"There is no harm in the sick person gathering the garment and prostrating on it."],[4281,"The prayer, when it was first prescribed, was prescribed as two rakʿahs; then Allah completed the prayer for the resident, and the two rakʿahs were retained in their original form for travel.\" He said: I said to ʿUrwah: \"What led ʿĀʾishah to pray four rakʿahs while traveling, when she knew that Allah had prescribed it as two?\" ʿUrwah said: \"She interpreted it as ʿUthmān interpreted the completion of the prayer at Minā.\""],[4282,"He used to grant a concession for the sick person to pray on his mount facing the qiblah."],[4283,"There is no harm in the sick person praying on his mount, facing the House and not turning his back on it."],[4284,"Ibn Umar lost consciousness for a day and a night and did not make up what he had missed."],[4285,"Ibn Umar lost consciousness for a month and did not make up what he had missed, and he prayed on the day on which he regained consciousness."],[4286,"When a sick person loses consciousness and then regains his intellect, he does not repeat the prayer."],[4288,"Ammar b. Yasir was struck and lost consciousness during Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha, then he regained consciousness halfway through the night and prayed Zuhr, then Asr, then Maghrib, then Isha."],[4289,"When a sick person is overcome in his intellect and then regains consciousness, he should pray what he missed when he regains his intellect, praying for each day and night accordingly."],[4290,"Ibn Umar fell ill once and lost his mind so that he abandoned the prayer; then he recovered, and he did not make up the prayers that he had missed."],[4291,"Concerning a man who lost consciousness and missed a single prayer, not knowing which one it was. He said: \"He should start and pray Maghrib, then Fajr, then Zuhr, then intend with it Zuhr, Asr, and Isha. Whichever one it was, it is four (rak'ahs).\""],[4294,"Concerning the madman who regains his sanity. He said: \"He should perform ablution.\""],[4295,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to travel from Madinah to Makkah, fearing none but Allah, and he would pray two rakʿahs."],[4297,"I said to Ata: \"Recitation with singing?\" He said: \"There is no harm in that. I heard Ubayd b. Umayr say: 'Dawud the Prophet ﷺ used to take the lyre and play it, repeating his voice with it, seeking thereby to weep and to make others weep.'\""],[4298,"Allah has not permitted for any prophet what He permitted for a prophet: to chant the Quran melodiously."],[4300,"Allah has not permitted for anything what He permitted for a person with a beautiful voice to chant the Quran."],[4302,"Abd Allah b. Umar al-Qari and al-Mutawakkil b. Abi Nuhayk entered upon Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas. Sa'd said to Abd Allah: 'Who is this?' He said: 'Al-Mutawakkil b. Abi Nuhayk.' He said: 'Yes, traders who earn, traders who earn; they delay. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: He is not of us who does not sing the Quran.'"],[4303,"He is not of us who does not sing the Quran."],[4304,"Indeed Allah grants permission to a man who has a good voice — he said: I think — to sing the Quran."],[4305,"For everything there is an ornament, and the ornament of the Quran is the beautiful voice."],[4306,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz had a beautiful voice. He went out one night to pray in the mosque and raised his voice, so the people gathered. Sa'id b. al-Musayyib sent word to him: 'You have beguiled the people.' He did not do that again."],[4307,"Indeed Allah and His angels bless the first rows. Adorn the Quran with your voices. Whoever gives a ewe's milk or a gift of silver, or gifts a lane, it is like the equivalent of freeing a slave."],[4308,"Ma'mar reported to us, from Mansur, from Talha, from Abd al-Rahman b. Awsaja, from al-Bara' b. Azib, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Adorn your voices with the Quran.' Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Thawri. [Hidden matn: Indeed Allah and His angels bless the first rows. Adorn the Quran with your voices. Whoever gives a gift of milk or a gift of silver, or is gifted a lane, it is like the equivalent of freeing a slave.]"],[4309,"The Prophet ﷺ heard the voice of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari while he was reciting, and he said: 'Abu Musa has been given one of the flutes of the family of David.'"],[4311,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard the voice of al-Ash'ari, Abu Musa, while he was reciting, and he said: 'This one has been given a flute from the flutes of the family of David.' He told him that, and he said: 'Now you are a true friend to me, since you informed me of this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' He said: 'If I had known that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was listening to my recitation, I would have embellished it beautifully.' He said: 'And the Prophet ﷺ heard another voice, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Do you say it to show off?\" I did not answer the Prophet ﷺ with anything until he repeated it to me once or twice or three times. After two or three times I said: \"Do you say it to show off? Rather, he is repentant.\"' He said: 'And he heard another one supplicating: \"O Allah, I ask You by the fact that I bear witness that You are Allah, there is no god but You, the One, the Eternal Refuge, who did not beget and was not begotten, and there is none equal to You.\" He said: \"He has asked Allah by His name by which if He is called upon He answers, and if He is asked by it He gives.\""],[4312,"When Abu Musa sat with Umar b. al-Khattab, he would sometimes say to him: 'Remind us of our Lord, O Abu Musa.' He said: 'Then he would recite.'"],[4313,"Umar used to say to Abu Musa while he was sitting with him in a gathering: 'Remind us, O Abu Musa.' He said: 'Then he would recite.'"],[4314,"He shortens the prayer on a journey of two days."],[4315,"Last night, O Messenger of God, while I was reciting on the roof of my house, something like a cloud covered me, while my wife was pregnant and the horse was tethered. I feared that my wife would give birth and my horse would startle. He said: Recite, Usayd, for it was the angel listening to the Qur'an. He said it three times."],[4316,"Ali b. Abi Talib urged the people to use the toothstick, and said: When a man stands to pray, the angel draws near to listen to the Qur'an, and he keeps drawing near until he puts his mouth upon his mouth. He does not utter a verse except that it falls into the angel's interior. He said: So pay attention to that, and Ali's love for the toothstick."],[4317,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked: Who among the people has the best recitation? He said: The one who, when you hear his recitation, you see that he fears God."],[4318,"He heard a man—they mentioned that he was al-Hakam al-Ghifari—saying: O plague, take me tonight. Abu Hurayrah said: Did you not hear, O Abu so-and-so, the Messenger of God ﷺ [say]: Then let none of you pray for death, for he does not know what state he will be in. He said: Yes, but I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ mention six things that I fear some of them will overtake me. He said: The selling of judgments, the wasting of blood, the rule of fools, the abundance of police, the severing of family ties, and people who take the Qur'an as reed-pipes, chanting with it."],[4319,"I said to Ibn Abbas: I am a man who is hasty in my speech and recitation. Ibn Abbas said: For me to recite al-Baqarah and recite it slowly is dearer to me than to rattle off the whole Qur'an."],[4320,"A man recited al-Baqarah and Al Imran in one rak'ah; their standing was one, their prostrations and bowings were one, and their sittings were one. Which of the two is more virtuous? He said: The one who recited al-Baqarah. Then Mujahid recited: 'And a Qur'an which We have divided, that you might recite it to the people at intervals' (Q 17:106). He said: At leisure."],[4321,"I said to Ata: What is His saying: 'And We have recited it in slow, measured rhythmic tones' (Q 25:32)? He gestured with his hand: it is discarding, it is casting away. Then he did not like the slow recitation. He said: I think that by that he means invigorating a person."],[4322,"'And We have recited it in slow, measured rhythmic tones' (Q 25:32). He said: Part of it following upon part."],[4323,"I said to 'Ata': Their saying, 'Do not shorten the prayer except in the path of Allah.' He said: 'I indeed think that is the case.' I said: 'Why?' He said: 'Because the Leader of the God-fearing did not shorten the prayer except on a path among the paths of Allah—Hajj, or 'Umrah, or a military expedition. And the leaders after him, which of them used to travel the earth seeking worldly gain?' I said: 'Do you think Ibn 'Abbas went out for other than Hajj or 'Umrah?' He said: 'No, except his going out to al-Ta'if.' I said: 'What about Jabir, Ibn 'Umar, and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri?' He said: 'Not one of them.' I said: 'So what is your view?' He said: 'I hold that one should not shorten except in the path of Allah, in the path of good. And before that he used not to say this; he used to shorten in all of that.'"],[4324,"Regarding the slow recitation, he said: I recited it until you understand it."],[4325,"I said to Ata: What do you think if I utter the Qur'an in the prescribed prayer and the supererogatory prayer, without repeating any of it and hurrying? He said: That is sufficient for you."],[4326,"The one skilled in the Qur'an is with the noble, righteous scribes, and the one who recites it while it is hard for him has two rewards."],[4327,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ, 'What do you think if I repeat something from it?' He said, 'I dislike that in the prayer, so do not repeat anything from it in the voluntary or the prescribed prayer.' He said: I said, 'What do you think if it is presented to a person and I repeat?' He said, 'That is only disliked in the prayer.'"],[4328,"I saw Saʿīd b. Jubayr leading them in Ramadan, repeating this verse: \"When the shackles are on their necks,\" \"O mankind, what has deceived you concerning your Lord, the Generous,\" \"Who created you and proportioned you,\" repeating it twice or thrice."],[4330,"Did Ibn ʿUmar make you hear the recitation in voluntary prayer during the day? He said, 'Yes, a little of the surah, and it was little.'"],[4331,"The daytime prayer is silent; the voice is not raised in it except for Friday and the morning prayer, and what is raised."],[4332,"I said to 'Ata': Their saying, 'Do not shorten the prayer except in the path of Allah.' He said: 'I indeed think that is the case.' I said: 'Why?' He said: 'Because the Leader of the God-fearing did not shorten the prayer except on a path among the paths of Allah—Hajj, or 'Umrah, or a military expedition. And the leaders after him, which of them used to travel the earth seeking worldly gain?' I said: 'Do you think Ibn 'Abbas went out for other than Hajj or 'Umrah?' He said: 'No, except his going out to al-Ta'if.' I said: 'What about Jabir, Ibn 'Umar, and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri?' He said: 'Not one of them.' I said: 'So what is your view?' He said: 'I hold that one should not shorten except in the path of Allah, in the path of good. And before that he used not to say this; he used to shorten in all of that.'"],[4333,"The daytime prayer is silent."],[4334,"Abū ʿUbaydah sent me to a man whom he heard reciting aloud during the day, and he said, 'The daytime recitation is silent.'"],[4335,"He has not spoken inaudibly who makes himself hear."],[4337,"A man desires to conceal his recitation. He said, 'Then he makes himself hear.'"],[4339,"The Messenger of God ﷺ passed by ʿAbd Allāh b. Ḥudhāfah while he was praying, and he raised his voice. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: “Do not make me hear you, O Ḥudhāfah, but make God Most High hear.”"],[4340,"A man asked her: Did the Messenger of God ﷺ raise his voice at night when he recited? She said: “Sometimes he raised it and sometimes he lowered it.” He said: Praise be to God who has made the religion spacious. He said: Did he perform witr in the first part of the night? She said: “Yes, sometimes he performed witr in the first part of the night and sometimes in its last part.” He said: Praise be to God who has made the religion spacious. He said: Did he sleep while in a state of major ritual impurity? She said: “Sometimes he bathed before sleeping and sometimes he slept before bathing, but he performed ablution before sleeping.” He said: Praise be to God who has made the religion spacious."],[4341,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by Abū Bakr while he was praying quietly, and he passed by ʿUmar while he was reciting aloud, and he passed by Bilāl while he was mixing. They all came to him in the morning, and he said: “I passed by you, O Abū Bakr, and saw you reciting quietly.” He said: Yes, may my father and mother be your ransom. He said: “Raise it a little.” He said: “I passed by you, O ʿUmar, while you were reciting aloud.” He said: May my father and mother be your ransom; I make the Merciful hear and wake the sleeper. He said: “Less—or he said—lower it a little.” He said: “And I passed by you, O Bilāl, while you were mixing.” He said: Yes, may my father and mother be your ransom; I mix the good with the good. He said: “Recite each surah in its proper way.”"],[4342,"The Prophet ﷺ said to Abū Bakr: I passed by you, O Abū Bakr, while you were reciting quietly. He said: I make the One I converse with hear. He said: “And I passed by you, O ʿUmar, while you were reciting aloud.” He said: I drive away Satan and wake the drowsy. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Lower it a little.” He said: “And I passed by you, O Bilāl, while you were reciting this surah and from that surah.” He said: O Messenger of God, I mix the good with the good. He said: “Recite the surah in its proper way.”"],[4343,"ʿAmrah bt. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān spent the night at my place, and I got up to pray at night and recited quietly. She said: “Raise your voice, for Muʿādh the reciter and Aflaḥ, the freedman of Abū Ayyūb, used to wake us at night by raising their voices.”"],[4346,"For you is the fill of your house."],[4347,"No crier from the people of the earth calls to prayer until a crier from the people of heaven calls: 'Arise, O children of Adam, and extinguish your fires.' He said: Then the muezzin stands and gives the call to prayer, then the people rise for prayer."],[4348,"The Messenger of God ﷺ observed iʿtikāf in the mosque, and he heard them reciting aloud while he was in a dome of his. He drew aside the curtains and said: “Indeed, each one of you is conversing with his Lord, so let none of you annoy another, and let none of you raise his voice over another in recitation—or he said: in prayer.”"],[4349,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was in a dome in the month of Ramadan, while a man was leading the people in prayer. He looked out at them and said: “Whatever God wills,” then said: “The one praying converses with his Lord. When one of you prays, let him consider what he is conversing with his Lord, and let not some of you raise their voices over others with the Quran.”"],[4350,"The Prophet ﷺ one night listened to Abu Bakr and found him reciting quietly in his prayer. He listened to Umar and found him raising his voice. He listened to Bilal and found him taking from this surah and from that surah. He said: \"I listened to you, O Abu Bakr, and you lower your voice.\" He said: \"I lower it to confide in my Lord.\" He said: \"And I listened to you, O Umar, and you raise your voice.\" He said: \"I frighten the devil and wake the sleeper.\" He said: \"And I listened to you, O Bilal, and you take from this surah and from that surah.\" He said: \"I combine the good with the good; I mix some of it with some.\" He said: \"All of this is good.\""],[4351,"Drowsiness in prayer is from Satan, but drowsiness in battle is a security from Allah."],[4352,"I said: \"O Messenger of Allah, Satan came between me and my prayer and my recitation.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"That is a devil called Khinzab. When you sense him, seek refuge in Allah from the accursed devil and spit dryly to your left three times.\""],[4353,"When one of you rises at night and the Qur'an becomes difficult on his tongue so that he does not know what he is saying, let him turn away and lie down."],[4354,"When one of you becomes drowsy while praying, let him sleep on his bed, for he does not know whether he is supplicating against himself or for himself."],[4355,"Do not struggle against this night, for you cannot bear it. When one of you becomes drowsy in his prayer, let him turn away and sleep on his bed, for that is safer for him."],[4356,"There is no man who intends to stand for a portion of the night but his eyes overcome him, except that Allah records for him its reward, and his sleep is a charity that Allah bestows upon him."],[4364,"The tashahhud suffices you, even if you pray a hundred rak'ahs."],[4365,"On the Day of the Confederates, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not pray Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, or Isha until a portion of the night had passed. He said: That was before the prayer of fear was revealed. He ordered Bilal, who gave the call to prayer, then he commenced the Zuhr prayer and prayed it as he used to pray it at its time. Then he ordered him, and he commenced the Asr prayer and prayed it as he used to pray it at its time. Then he ordered him, and he commenced the Maghrib prayer and prayed it at its time as he used to pray it at its time. Then he ordered him, and he commenced the Isha prayer and prayed it as he used to pray it at its time."],[4366,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed the Zuhr prayer with his companions before the prayer of fear was revealed. He said: The polytheists regretted that they had not attacked him. He said: A man said: They have a prayer before sunset that is dearer to them than their own selves. They said: If they were to pray later, we would attack them. So they lay in wait for that. Then the prayer of fear was revealed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in the prayer of fear for the Asr prayer."],[4367,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not pray the prayer of fear except twice: once at Dhat al-Riqa' in the land of Banu Sulaym, and once at Usfan, while the polytheists were at Dajnan, between them and the qiblah. He said: The Prophet ﷺ lined up all his companions behind him, while they were at Usfan. Then he advanced and prayed, and he bowed with all of them. Then he prostrated with those next to him, and the others stood behind him guarding him. When he had prostrated two prostrations with them, they stood up, and those behind him prostrated. Then they advanced to the front row, and these withdrew. Then he bowed with all of them, then he prostrated with those next to him, and the others stood guarding them. When they raised their heads from the prostration, those prostrated. Then the Prophet ﷺ said the taslim upon all of them, and their prayer was complete."],[4368,"Regarding His saying: \"If you fear that those who disbelieve may persecute you\" – it was revealed on the day the Prophet ﷺ was at Usfan and the polytheists at Dajnan. They faced each other. The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr with his companions, four rak'ahs, with their bowing, prostration, and standing all together as one. The polytheists were tempted to raid their belongings and fight them. Then Allah, exalted is He, revealed to him: \"Let a party of them stand [in prayer] with you.\" The Prophet ﷺ prayed Asr, and he lined up his companions in two rows. He said the takbir with all of them, and the first row prostrated with him, while the others remained standing and did not prostrate, until the Prophet ﷺ and the first row stood up. Then he said the takbir with them, and they all bowed. Then the back row advanced and the front row withdrew, and they alternated in prostration as they had done the first time. The Prophet ﷺ completed the Asr prayer in two rak'ahs."],[4369,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Usfan. He said: The polytheists, led by Khalid b. al-Walid, faced us, and they were between us and the qiblah. The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr, and they said: They were in a state where, had we caught them off guard... Then they said: A prayer will come upon them now that is dearer to them than their children and their own selves. He said: Then Gabriel descended with these verses between Zuhr and Asr: \"And when you are among them and lead them in prayer...\" He said: When the prayer time came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered them to take up their weapons, and we lined up behind him in two rows. He said: Then he bowed, and we all bowed. He said: Then he raised his head, and we all raised our heads. Then the Prophet ﷺ prostrated with the row next to him, and the others remained standing guarding them. When they had prostrated and stood up, the others sat down and prostrated in their place. Then these advanced to the positions of those, and those came to the positions of these. Then they all bowed, then he raised his head, and they all raised their heads. Then the Prophet ﷺ prostrated with the row next to him, and the others stood guarding them. When they sat down, the others sat down and prostrated. Then he said the taslim upon them, and then he departed. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed it twice: once at Usfan and once in the land of Banu Sulaym."],[4370,"The Prophet ﷺ led them in a prayer like this one, except that he did not mention the descent of Gabriel. He said: And Jabir said: Just as your commanders do this."],[4371,"The front row stepped backward when they raised their heads from prostration, and the back row advanced and prostrated in the positions of the first row."],[4372,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed the Zuhr prayer, four rak'ahs, while he and the enemy were in one open plain. The enemy said: They have another prayer that is dearer to them than the world and all it contains. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up to pray Asr, and they stood behind him in two rows. The Prophet ﷺ bowed, and the first row bowed while the back row remained standing. Then they stood up, and the first row stepped backward and moved to the position of the back row, while the back row advanced until they stood in their position. Then the Prophet ﷺ bowed, and the first row bowed. Thus the Prophet ﷺ had two rak'ahs, and each row had one rak'ah. Then they prayed one rak'ah each in their positions."],[4373,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the prayer of fear with one of the two groups, one rak'ah, while the other group faced the enemy. Then they departed and stood in the position of their companions, facing the enemy. Then those came, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed one rak'ah with them. Then the Prophet ﷺ said the taslim. Then these made up one rak'ah and those made up one rak'ah."],[4374,"‘Abd Allah b. ‘Umar used to narrate that he prayed it with the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said the takbīr, and a group of us formed a row behind him, while another group faced the enemy. The Prophet ﷺ led them in one rak‘ah with two prostrations, performing the prostrations like half of the morning prayer. Then they departed and faced the enemy, and the other group came and formed a row with the Prophet ﷺ, and they did the same. Then he said the salām, and each man of the two groups stood and prayed one rak‘ah with two prostrations for himself."],[4375,"From the Prophet ﷺ regarding the prayer of fear: He said: Each man of the people prayed one rak‘ah with the Prophet ﷺ, then each man prayed one rak‘ah for himself."],[4376,"One group advances with the imam, while another group faces the enemy. The imam leads them in one rak‘ah with two prostrations. Then the group that prayed with the imam departs and takes the position of their companions, and those others come and join the imam’s prayer. He leads them in one rak‘ah, then the imam says the salām. Then they stand and pray one rak‘ah in their place. Then they leave and take the position of their companions, and those others come and pray one rak‘ah."],[4377,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he formed a row behind him and a row facing the enemy. He said: “And they were all in prayer.” He said: “He said the takbīr and they all said the takbīr. He prayed one rak‘ah with the row that was next to him, while the other row faced the enemy. Then those went and these came, and he prayed one rak‘ah with them. Then those with whom he prayed the second rak‘ah stood and formed their row, and those went to the positions of these, and these came to those, and they made up one rak‘ah.”"],[4378,"A row stands behind the imam and a row facing the enemy, not in prayer. He said: “The imam prays one rak‘ah with those who are with him. Then those go to the positions of those, and those come, and he prays one rak‘ah with them. Then the imam says the salām. Then those return to the positions of those, and those return and form a row and pray one rak‘ah. Then those depart to the positions of those, and those return and make up one rak‘ah. Thus the imam has two rak‘ahs, and each of the two groups has one rak‘ah with the imam and one rak‘ah alone. However, the first group begins the makeup because they had begun the prayer, and they do not speak until they finish all of their prayer, because they are in prayer.”"],[4379,"The imam stands in the prayer of fear, and a row stands behind him and a row facing the enemy. He said: “He prays one rak‘ah with these. When he has prayed one rak‘ah with them, they stand in their place while the imam remains standing, and they make up one rak‘ah. Then they go to the positions of those, and those come, and he prays one rak‘ah with them. Then they stand in their place and make up one rak‘ah.”"],[4380,"He witnessed Sa‘īd b. al-‘Āṣ in a campaign called Dhāt al-Khashab, and with him was Ḥudhayfah. Sa‘īd said: “Which of you witnessed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of God ﷺ?” Ḥudhayfah said: “I did.” So Ḥudhayfah ordered them, and they put on their weapons. Then he said: “If an attack comes upon you, fighting is permitted for you.” He said: Then he prayed one rak‘ah with one of the two groups, while the other group faced the enemy. Then those departed and took the position of those, and those came, and he prayed another rak‘ah with them. Then he said the salām to them."],[4381,"We were with Sa‘īd b. al-‘Āṣ – I think he said at Ṭabaristān – and he said: “Which of you witnessed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of God ﷺ?” Ḥudhayfah said: “I did.” He said: Then a row stood behind him and a row facing the enemy. He said: “He prayed one rak‘ah with them. Then those went to the positions of those, and he prayed one rak‘ah with them. Then he departed.”"],[4382,"I asked him about the prayer of fear. He said: “The Messenger of God ﷺ stood and prayed with them. A row stood behind him and a row facing the enemy. He prayed one rak‘ah with them.” He said: “Then those went to the positions of those, and those came, and he prayed one rak‘ah with them. Then he departed.”"],[4383,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed the prayer of fear at Dhū Qarad. He formed a row behind him and a row facing the enemy. He said: “He prayed one rak‘ah with the row that was with him. Then those went to the positions of those, and he prayed one rak‘ah with them. Then he said the salām to them all, and they departed. Thus the Prophet ﷺ had two rak‘ahs, and each of the two groups had one rak‘ah.”"],[4384,"And how can it be shortened? That is, if each of the two groups has two rak‘ahs, it is not a shortening."],[4385,"The prayer of fear: He said: The imam stands and behind him stands one row, and another row facing the enemy not in prayer. He prays with the row behind him one rakʿah, then they depart on their heels to face the enemy, and the other row comes and prays with the imam one rakʿah. Then they stand and go to their positions while the imam is sitting. The first group comes while the imam is sitting, and they bow and prostrate, and they do not recite, and they sit with the imam. Then he rises with them and prays with them the second [rakʿah], then the imam says the taslim, and they depart to their positions, and the others come and pray one rakʿah in which they recite, then they sit and perform the tashahhud, then they stand in their place and pray another rakʿah in which they do not recite except with the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb if they wish, and they perform the tashahhud and say the taslim."],[4386,"“That you shorten the prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve may put you to trial.” He said: Its shortening is in fear and fighting—prayer in every direction, mounted and on foot. He said: “As for the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ of these two rakʿahs, and the prayer of the people on journey of two rakʿahs, it is not shortening; it is its completion.” Ṭāwūs used to say that."],[4387,"A Muslim pursues the enemy in his tracks and prays while pursuing him, turning away from the House. He said: “Does he pray on his mount like that?” He said: “No, but if the Muslim is the one being pursued and the enemy is pursuing him, then let him perform it like that.”"],[4388,"Ibn 'Umar went out to Khaybar and shortened the prayer."],[4389,"If the fear is more severe than that, let them pray standing and mounted, wherever they are facing."],[4390,"When the enemies overshadow them, it is lawful for them to pray facing any direction, on foot or mounted, two rakʿahs, gesturing with gestures."],[4391,"Regarding His saying, “If you fear, then on foot or mounted”: He said: “Two rakʿahs, gesturing with his head with gestures, wherever his face is.”"],[4393,"Regarding His saying, “on foot or mounted”: He said: “That is at the striking with the sword: you pray one rakʿah with gestures, wherever your face is, whether you are mounted, on foot, or hastening.”"],[4394,"Regarding His saying, “If you fear, then on foot or mounted”: He said: “Two takbīrs suffice, wherever he is facing.”"],[4396,"When there is sword fighting, it is only one rakʿah, gesturing with gestures, wherever his face is, on foot or mounted."],[4397,"Two rakʿahs, making gestures with them wherever his face was directed."],[4398,"The prayer, when it was first prescribed, was prescribed as two rakʿahs; then Allah completed the prayer for the resident, and the two rakʿahs were retained in their original form for travel.\" He said: I said to ʿUrwah: \"What led ʿĀʾishah to pray four rakʿahs while traveling, when she knew that Allah had prescribed it as two?\" ʿUrwah said: \"She interpreted it as ʿUthmān interpreted the completion of the prayer at Minā.\""],[4399,"He shortens the prayer on a journey of two days."],[4400,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān at the beginning of his caliphate used to pray at Makkah and at Minā two rakʿahs; then ʿUthmān prayed it as four.\" This reached Ibn Masʿūd, and he said: \"Indeed we belong to Allah and to Him we return.\" Then he stood and prayed four. It was said to him: \"You said 'Indeed we belong to Allah and to Him we return,' and then you prayed four?\" He said: \"Dissent is evil.\""],[4402,"Ubayd Allah b. Umar wrote to Ibn Umar while he was in the land of Persia: We are staying until the new moon. So he wrote: Pray two rak'ahs."],[4403,"I said: \"For what reason was shortening instituted in fear when the people are now safe?\" He said: \"The Sunnah.\" I said: \"And is it a concession?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[4405,"As for His saying: \"If you fear that those who disbelieve will put you to trial,\" he said: \"That is only when they feared those who disbelieved. Then the Prophet ﷺ established two rakʿahs after that, and it is not a shortening, but rather a fulfillment.\""],[4406,"I said to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb: \"Allah only said: 'That you shorten the prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve will put you to trial,' but the people are now safe.\" ʿUmar said: \"I wondered at what you wondered at, so I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'It is a charity that Allah has bestowed upon you, so accept His charity.'\""],[4407,"We find the prayer of fear and the prayer of residence in the Qurʾān, but we do not find the prayer of the traveler. Ibn ʿUmar said: \"Allah sent His Prophet when we were the most ignorant of people, so we do as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did.\""],[4408,"He prays two rak'ahs even if he stays a year."],[4409,"The prayer of the day of sacrifice is two rak'ahs, and the prayer of the day of breaking the fast is two rak'ahs, and the prayer of the traveler is two rak'ahs, complete and not shortened, according to the words of the Prophet ﷺ."],[4410,"I met Ibn Umar and said, \"Prayer while traveling.\" He said, \"Two rak'ahs.\" I said, \"What do you think here in Mina?\" He said, \"Woe to you! Have you heard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" I said, \"Yes, and I believe in Allah.\" He said, \"He used to pray two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, so pray if you wish or leave.\""],[4411,"The prayer of the traveler is two rak'ahs."],[4413,"The traveler prays two rak'ahs until he returns, unless he enters a city of the cities of the Muslims, in which case he prays the full prayer."],[4414,"Salman came forward with twelve riders - or thirteen - from the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the prayer became due, they said, \"Lead us, O Abu Abd Allah.\" He said, \"We will not lead you in prayer, and we will not marry your women. Allah has guided us through you.\" So a man from the people came forward and prayed four rak'ahs. When he had said the salutation, Salman said, \"What do we have to do with the fourfold prayer? Half of the fourfold would have sufficed us, and we are more in need of the concession.\""],[4415,"Uthman wrote to one of his officials: \"The resident does not pray two rak'ahs, nor the second, nor the merchant. Only the one who has provisions and water skins prays two rak'ahs.\""],[4416,"Uthman wrote to the people of Basra: \"To proceed: It has reached me that some of you are in a pasture or engaged in trade or are a tax collector and shorten the prayer. Only the one who is traveling or in the presence of the enemy shortens the prayer.\""],[4418,"Do not be deceived by your trades and your pastures, and travel to the farthest part of the cultivated land, saying, 'We are travelers.' Travelers are only those who travel from one horizon to another."],[4419,"They both used to say to the people of Kufa: \"Do not let your pasture or your cultivated land deceive you. Do not shorten the prayer until you reach the cultivated land.\" He said, \"And between them and the cultivated land was thirty parasangs.\""],[4420,"I do not think you should shorten the prayer except in a path from the paths of Allah. And before that he did not say this; he used to say, 'He shortens it in all of that.'"],[4421,"I said to 'Ata': Their saying, 'Do not shorten the prayer except in the path of Allah.' He said: 'I indeed think that is the case.' I said: 'Why?' He said: 'Because the Leader of the God-fearing did not shorten the prayer except on a path among the paths of Allah—Hajj, or 'Umrah, or a military expedition. And the leaders after him, which of them used to travel the earth seeking worldly gain?' I said: 'Do you think Ibn 'Abbas went out for other than Hajj or 'Umrah?' He said: 'No, except his going out to al-Ta'if.' I said: 'What about Jabir, Ibn 'Umar, and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri?' He said: 'Not one of them.' I said: 'So what is your view?' He said: 'I hold that one should not shorten except in the path of Allah, in the path of good. And before that he used not to say this; he used to shorten in all of that.'"],[4422,"Ibn 'Umar used to shorten the prayer going to property of his at Khaybar, attending to it. Now it was not Hajj, nor 'Umrah, nor a military expedition."],[4423,"As for me, whenever I prepared to leave from a town saying, 'I will depart now, now,' I would shorten the prayer. But if I decided to stay, I would complete it. I said, 'I am staying ten?' He said, 'Then complete it.'"],[4424,"Ibn 'Umar bought something from a man—I think it was a she-camel—and he went out to look at it and shortened the prayer. That was a journey of a full day, or four—like that—barids."],[4425,"Ibn 'Umar used to pray each prayer at its time while traveling, except for one prayer when he was informed of his wife's illness; he combined the sunset and night prayers. He was asked about it, and he said: 'This is how the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do: when travel urged him on, he would combine the sunset and night prayers.' In some of their hadiths: he delayed both of them together until a quarter of the night."],[4427,"I asked Ibn 'Abbas, saying: 'Should I shorten the prayer to 'Arafah or to Mina?' He said: 'No. But to al-Ta'if and to Jeddah. And do not shorten the prayer except in a full day's journey. Do not shorten in less than a day. If you go to al-Ta'if, or to Jeddah, or to a similar distance of land, to land of yours or to livestock, then shorten the prayer. And when you arrive, then complete it.'"],[4428,"I asked Ibn 'Abbas: 'Should I shorten the prayer to 'Arafah?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'To Mina?' He said: 'No. But to Jeddah, to 'Usfan, and to al-Ta'if. If you arrive at family of yours, or at livestock, then complete the prayer.'"],[4430,"When you travel a day until the evening, then complete the prayer; but if you go further, then shorten."],[4435,"Ibn Umar used to shorten the prayer at the nearest point he considered for it: a property of his that he inspected from Khaybar, which is a journey of three moderate marches. He would not shorten for anything less than that. I said, 'How far is Khaybar?' He said, 'Three moderate marches.' I said, 'And al-Ta'if?' He said, 'Yes, it is on the easy route and a little farther.'"],[4436,"When you travel three [days], then shorten the prayer."],[4438,"When the journey is two nights or more, then shorten the prayer."],[4439,"I said to him: At what distance is the prayer shortened? Then he mentioned the hadith of Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, and we have written it down."],[4440,"When you travel three [days], then shorten the prayer."],[4441,"I was with Hudhayfah at al-Mada'in, and I asked permission to go to my family in al-Kufah. He granted permission, and stipulated that I should not break the fast or pray two rak'ahs until I returned to him."],[4442,"Ibn 'Umar was informed of his wife's illness while he was on a journey, so he delayed the sunset prayer. He was told: 'The prayer,' but he remained silent and delayed it until after the twilight had disappeared and a portion of the night had passed. Then he dismounted and prayed the sunset and night prayers together, then said: 'This is how the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do when travel urged him on, or when the journey urged him on.'"],[4443,"I asked Shaqiq b. Salamah. He said: I said, 'I go out to al-Mada'in or to Wasit.' He said, 'Do not shorten the prayer.'"],[4444,"I saw Amir al-Sha'bi traveling to Wasit, and he shortened the prayer and broke the fast."],[4445,"I said to Ata': What about someone who takes the mountain passes for Hajj or Umrah, and someone who takes the easy route on the road to al-Ta'if? Does he shorten? He said, 'Yes.'"],[4446,"When you go out and spend the night away from your family, then shorten; but when you come to your family, then complete."],[4447,"Prayer may be shortened up to the distant cities, and it is twenty-seven farsakhs from Kufa."],[4448,"I prayed with the Messenger of God ﷺ in Madinah four [rakʿahs], and I prayed with him the afternoon prayer at Dhu al-Hulayfah two rakʿahs, and he had set out as a traveler."],[4449,"He prayed with the Prophet ﷺ in Madinah the noon prayer four [rakʿahs], and I prayed with him at Dhu al-Hulayfah the afternoon prayer two [rakʿahs], while the Prophet ﷺ was intending Makkah."],[4450,"I prayed with the Messenger of God ﷺ the noon prayer in Madinah four [rakʿahs], and the afternoon prayer at Dhu al-Hulayfah two [rakʿahs]."],[4451,"She used to instruct women to combine the two prayers during travel."],[4452,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ traveled one farsakh, he would stop and shorten the prayer."],[4453,"When Ali went out to Basra, he saw a reed hut and said: \"Were it not for this hut, we would have prayed two rakʿahs.\" I said, \"What is a reed hut?\" He said, \"A house of reeds.\""],[4454,"We went out with Ali while we were watching Kufa, and he prayed two rakʿahs. Then he returned and prayed two rakʿahs while he was looking at the village. We said to him, \"Will you not pray four?\" He said, \"Until we enter it.\""],[4455,"We went out with Ali to Siffin, and he prayed two rakʿahs between the bridge and the dam."],[4456,"He used to shorten the prayer when he left the houses of Madinah, and he would shorten when he returned until he entered its houses."],[4457,"When Ibn Umar went out for Hajj or Umrah, he would shorten the prayer at Dhu al-Hulayfah."],[4458,"I went out with my father, and with Alqamah, al-Aswad, and Amr b. Maymun, and they shortened when they left the houses."],[4460,"When you intend to travel and you cross the bridge or the trench, then pray two rak'ahs."],[4461,"'Alqama used to shorten at al-Najaf, and al-Aswad used to shorten at al-Qadisiyya when they were heading to Makkah."],[4462,"When a man goes out as a pilgrim and he has not left the houses of the village until the prayer becomes due, then if he wishes he may shorten, and if he wishes he may complete, and I have not heard anything about that."],[4463,"When a man leaves his house going in his direction and he has not left the village until the prayer becomes due, then let him shorten it. Likewise, when he enters the village returning from his journey and then the prayer becomes due, let him shorten it until he enters his house."],[4465,"When he goes out at the time of prayer, he prays two rak'ahs, just as if he entered the village at the time of prayer he would pray four."],[4466,"When I reside in a land for ten days, I complete the prayer. If I say, 'I will leave today or tomorrow,' then I pray two rak'ahs. When I reside for a month, I pray two rak'ahs."],[4467,"‘Umar b. al-Khattab combined Zuhr and ‘Asr on a rainy day."],[4468,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ resided in Tabuk for twenty days, shortening the prayer."],[4469,"We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he shortened the prayer until he came to Makkah, then he resided there for ten days shortening until we returned."],[4470,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ resided in Makkah for seventeen nights, shortening the prayer."],[4471,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Khaybar for forty nights shortening the prayer."],[4472,"Ibn Umar stayed in Azerbaijan for six months shortening the prayer. He said: And he used to say: “If you resolve to stay, then complete it.”"],[4473,"If I arrived in a land, I would pray two rak'ahs as long as I had not resolved to stay, even if I stayed twelve nights."],[4474,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set out in the year of the conquest in the month of Ramadan and fasted until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke his fast. He said: They used to follow the latest of the affairs of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and the latest of his affair."],[4476,"When Ibn Umar came to Makkah and wanted to stay fifteen nights, he let his mount loose and completed the prayer."],[4478,"Ibn ʿUmar used to perform supererogatory prayers at night, but he did not perform supererogatory prayers during the day while traveling, and he used to pray toward his camel."],[4480,"I was with him when a group from the people of al-Jazira came to him and said: O Abu Muhammad, we travel in palanquins, and we are spared the hardship. Should we fast? He said: No. They said: We are able to do that. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ was stronger and better than you. He said: The best of you are those who, when they travel, shorten the prayer and do not fast."],[4481,"If you resolve to stay fifteen nights, then complete it."],[4482,"We arrived in Madinah, so I sent to Ibn al-Musayyab: We are staying some days in Madinah; should we shorten? He said: “Yes.”"],[4483,"We were with him in Syria for two months; we used to complete [the prayer] while he used to shorten it. So we said to him, and he said, \"We are more knowledgeable.\""],[4484,"That Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas came as a delegate to Mu'awiya and stayed with him for a month, shortening [the prayer], or the month of Ramadan, and he did not fast it."],[4485,"We were with him in some of the lands of Persia for two years, and he would not combine [prayers] nor add more than two rak'ahs."],[4488,"That he stayed in Khwarizm for two years and prayed two rak'ahs."],[4489,"We were with Masruq at al-Silsila for years, and he was governor over it, and he led us in prayer two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until he departed, seeking thereby the Sunnah."],[4490,"That he went out with Masruq to al-Silsila, and he shortened [the prayer], and he stayed for years shortening it. He said: I said to him, 'O Abu A'isha, what makes you do this?' He said, 'Seeking the Sunnah,' and he shortened until he returned."],[4491,"We stayed with a governor - he said: I think in Sijistan - for two years, and with us were men from the companions of Ibn Mas'ud, and he led us in prayer two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until he departed. Then he said, 'Thus Ibn Mas'ud used to do.'"],[4492,"I go out as a traveler and stay for years, bound and without means; should I shorten [the prayer]? He said, 'It is not shortening but completeness, so pray two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs.'"],[4494,"I asked al-Sha'bi at the time of the Hajj. He said: I said, 'I come to Kufa where my grandmother and my family are?' He said: Which of the garrison cities is better, or he said greater? Then he answered me, saying, 'Is it not Madinah?' I said, 'Yes.' He said, 'I asked Ibn Umar about that, and he said, \"I come to the house in which I was born - meaning Makkah - and I do not add more than two rak'ahs.\"' Al-Sha'bi said, 'I would stay a year or two praying two rak'ahs,' or he said, 'I do not add more than two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs.'"],[4495,"Ibn Sirin used to fast one day and break the fast one day, both when resident and when traveling."],[4496,"Amir al-Sha'bi stayed with us at al-Nahrayn for four months, not adding more than two rak'ahs."],[4497,"I was sitting with Ibn 'Umar when a man entered upon him and said, 'O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, what is associating partners with Allah?' He said, 'That you make another god besides Allah.' He said again, 'O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, what is associating partners with Allah?' He said, 'That you take rivals besides Allah.' He said again, 'O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, what is associating partners with Allah?' He said, 'I adjure you, if you are a Muslim, to leave me.' So the man left, and Ibn 'Umar became very angry. He said: I rose to leave when I saw the intensity of his anger, but he put his hand on my knee and said, 'Sit; for I hope you are not one of them.' He said: I said, 'O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, I come to al-Madinah seeking a need, and I stay there seven or eight months. How should I pray?' He said, 'Pray two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs.'"],[4498,"How did Ibn 'Umar do? He said: When he has set out at noon, and said: We are staying, he completed the prayer. And he said: And when he said today and tomorrow, he shortened the prayer, even if he stayed twenty nights."],[4499,"As for me, whenever I prepared to leave from a town saying, 'I will depart now, now,' I would shorten the prayer. But if I decided to stay, I would complete it. I said, 'I am staying ten?' He said, 'Then complete it.'"],[4500,"When you put down your luggage in a land, then complete the prayer."],[4501,"A person traveling in the desert for nearly a month, moving to new pasture every day—should he shorten? He said: No. They are people traveling among their wealth, staying here and there."],[4504,"'Umar prayed two rak'ahs with the people of Makkah, then said, 'O people of Makkah, we are travelers, so complete the prayer.'"],[4505,"Ibn 'Umar entered upon a man of the people of Makkah to visit him in his illness. When the time for prayer came, he prayed two rak'ahs with them, then turned to them and said, 'Complete.'"],[4506,"'Abd Allah b. 'Umar came to visit 'Abd Allah b. Safwan, and he prayed two rak'ahs with us, then left. So we stood up and completed."],[4507,"Regarding a traveler who prayed these two [rak'ahs] and then lost his ablution. He brought forward a traveler who led them in four [rak'ahs]. He said: \"They repeat.\""],[4508,"Regarding a traveler who led a group of residents in prayer and prayed four [rak'ahs] with them. He said: \"It does not suffice them. They start over. He has shortened his own prayer.\""],[4510,"When a traveler leads residents in prayer and prays one rak'ah with them, then loses his ablution and brings forward a man who missed one rak'ah—it would have been proper for him not to bring forward anyone except one who caught [the prayer from the beginning], but he brought this one forward. He prays the remainder of his prayer with them, then withdraws and brings forward a man from those who caught the entire prayer, and he says the salam. Then he stands and makes up what he missed."],[4511,"When a traveler prays one rak'ah with residents, and behind him are a traveler and residents, and he brings forward a traveler. Then it occurs to that traveler to stay. He should pray the remainder of the traveler's prayer with them, then step back and bring forward a man from the travelers, and he concludes with them with the salam. Then he and the residents stand and complete the remainder of their prayer without an imam."],[4512,"Regarding a man from Makkah who wants to go to Kufah, so he travels until he reaches Yabrin al-Murtafi' or the like, then a need arises for him and he returns. He said: \"He completes the prayer, because he has not reached a journey in which prayer may be shortened.\""],[4513,"When you are on a journey and you have prayed one rak'ah, then it occurs to you to stay in that town, then complete your prayer. But if it occurs to you to leave after you intended to stay, you must complete [the prayer] until you leave that city."],[4514,"I caught one rak'ah of the residents' prayer while I was a traveler. He said: \"Pray according to their prayer.\""],[4515,"Regarding a traveler who caught one rak'ah of the residents' prayer in Zuhr. He said: \"He adds three to it. If he catches them sitting, he prays two rak'ahs.\""],[4517,"Regarding a traveler who catches one rak'ah of the residents' prayer. The two said: \"He prays according to their prayer. If he catches them sitting, he prays two rak'ahs.\""],[4519,"Regarding a traveler who catches one rak'ah of the residents' prayer. The two said: \"He prays according to their prayer. If he catches them sitting, he prays two rak'ahs.\""],[4521,"Whoever forgets a prayer while resident and remembers it while traveling prays four, and if he forgets a prayer while traveling and remembers it while resident, he prays two rak'ahs."],[4522,"Whoever forgets the prayer of residence until he travels, he prays it as four; and if he forgets a prayer while traveling until he comes to residence, he prays four. Hammad said: He prays two rak'ahs. And the statement of al-Hasan is more beloved to Ma'mar: He completes it so that he is not in doubt."],[4523,"If he forgets the prayer of residence and remembers while he is a traveler, he prays four."],[4524,"Regarding a man who was ignorant and prayed Maghrib as two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until he returned, he said: He repeats what he remembers."],[4526,"He saw Abu al-Shatha gesturing in prayer in water and mud."],[4528,"Anas b. Malik shortened on the ship."],[4530,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ combined Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha during his expedition to Tabuk."],[4531,"The Messenger of God ﷺ combined the noon and afternoon prayers, and the sunset and night prayers, during the expedition of Tabuk."],[4532,"They went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ to Tabuk. He said: The Prophet ﷺ used to combine the noon and afternoon prayers, and the sunset and night prayers. He said: One day he delayed the prayer, then came out and prayed the noon and afternoon prayers together, then came out and prayed the sunset and night prayers together. Then he said: 'Tomorrow, God willing, you will come to the spring of Tabuk, and you will come to it in the forenoon. Whoever comes to it, let him not touch any of its water until I arrive.' He said: We came to it, and two men had already preceded us to it. The spring was like a sandal strap, trickling with a little water. The Messenger of God ﷺ asked them: 'Have you touched any of its water?' They said: 'Yes.' He said: He rebuked them and said to them whatever God willed him to say. Then they scooped a little water from the spring with their hands until it gathered in a vessel. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ washed his face and hands in it, then returned it to the spring, and the spring flowed with abundant water, and the people drew water. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'It is likely, O Mu'adh, if your life is prolonged, that you will see this place filled with gardens.'"],[4533,"That Safiyya bt. Abi 'Ubayd, his wife, was dying. He said: He traveled until it became dark, and we thought that he had forgotten. He said: We kept saying: 'The prayer,' and he did not answer us, until about a quarter of the night had passed, about the time it takes heavily laden travelers to go from 'Arafa to Muzdalifa. Then he dismounted and prayed the sunset prayer, then turned to us and said: 'The Messenger of God ﷺ, when travel urged him on or he resolved to travel, would combine these two prayers.' Then he prayed the night prayer."],[4534,"Ibn 'Umar combined the two prayers once. He said: News came to him about Safiyya bt. Abi 'Ubayd that she was ill, so he departed after praying the afternoon prayer, then hastened the journey. He traveled until the time for the sunset prayer came. A man from his companions spoke to him and said: 'The prayer,' but he did not respond to him. Then another spoke to him, but he did not respond to him. Then another spoke to him, but he did not respond to him at all. Then another spoke to him, and he said: 'I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ, when he was in a hurry, delay this prayer until he combined the two prayers.'"],[4535,"Ibn 'Umar was informed of his wife's illness while he was on a journey, so he delayed the sunset prayer. He was told: 'The prayer,' but he remained silent and delayed it until after the twilight had disappeared and a portion of the night had passed. Then he dismounted and prayed the sunset and night prayers together, then said: 'This is how the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do when travel urged him on, or when the journey urged him on.'"],[4537,"Ibn 'Umar used to pray each prayer at its time while traveling, except for one prayer when he was informed of his wife's illness; he combined the sunset and night prayers. He was asked about it, and he said: 'This is how the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do: when travel urged him on, he would combine the sunset and night prayers.' In some of their hadiths: he delayed both of them together until a quarter of the night."],[4538,"He used to combine the two prayers while traveling: the noon and afternoon prayers, and the sunset and night prayers, and he was not pursuing an enemy nor being pursued by one."],[4539,"He used to make the witr precise."],[4540,"Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas and I accompanied each other from Kufa to Makkah, and we set out as delegates. Sa'd used to combine the noon and afternoon prayers, and the sunset and night prayers, advancing one a little and delaying the other a little, until we came to Makkah."],[4541,"Sa'id b. Zayd and Usama went out and they used to combine the noon and afternoon prayers, and the sunset and night prayers."],[4543,"Ibn 'Abbas combined the sunset and night prayers on the night he left his land. He said: 'Whoever combined them would delay the noon prayer and advance the afternoon prayer, then combine them, and delay the sunset prayer and advance the night prayer, then combine them.'"],[4544,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ, “What do you think if a person prays the two prayers at the time of one of them?” He said, “It does not harm him.”"],[4545,"Ṭāwūs used to combine the two prayers from al-Jund until he reached Makkah, and he would pray between them and with them whatever he used to pray while resident."],[4546,"Witr is between the two prayers."],[4547,"I was a hired worker for Sālim b. ʿAbd Allāh. He said: He would set out from Madinah to Makkah, and Sālim would instruct his wives to combine ẓuhr and ʿaṣr, then I would travel with them, while he himself would remain behind at the stopping place. I do not know what he would do."],[4549,"A woman came to Ṭāwūs and said, “I dislike that my father made me combine the two prayers.” He said, “It does not harm you. Do you not see that people combine the midday and ʿaṣr at ʿArafah, and maghrib and ʿishāʾ at Jamʿ?”"],[4550,"She used to instruct women to combine the two prayers during travel."],[4551,"I heard that prayer was combined because of His saying: “Establish prayer at the sun’s decline until the darkness of the night” (17:78). The darkness of the night is maghrib and ʿishāʾ."],[4552,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: People who are not on Hajj, nor ʿumrah, nor a military expedition—do they combine the two prayers? He said, “Yes, glory be to God! I circumambulate here seven rounds, then I pray ʿishāʾ—or seventy.”"],[4554,"The witr of the night is like the witr of the day — the maghrib prayer is three."],[4555,"And he prayed fajr that day before its time."],[4556,"Umar wrote to Abu Musa: 'Know that combining two prayers is one of the major sins, except with an excuse.'"],[4558,"He would dismount at the time of each prayer, even if he dismounted on a stone."],[4561,"Witr is an obligation upon every Muslim. Whoever wishes to pray witr with five rak'ahs, let him do so. Whoever wishes to pray witr with three, let him do so. Whoever wishes to pray witr with one, let him do so. And whoever is unable except to gesture, let him gesture."],[4564,"May Allah have mercy on Abdullah b. Rawahah; he would dismount during travel at the time of each prayer."],[4565,"How far did Ibn Umar delay Maghrib? He said: 'From Dhat al-Jaysh to Dhat al-Sufuq, and between them are eight miles.'"],[4566,"The sun set for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Sarif, and he did not pray Maghrib until he entered Makkah."],[4567,"Wahb b. Munabbih would have the sun set while he was at the village of al-Rahabah, and he would ride his mount until he reached his house in Sana'a."],[4568,"The Messenger of God ﷺ combined the Zuhr and ‘Asr prayers and the Maghrib and ‘Isha prayers in Madinah without traveling and without rain. He said: I said to Ibn ‘Abbas, 'Why do you think he did that?' He said, 'I think he did it to make things easier for his community.'"],[4569,"The Messenger of God ﷺ combined the Zuhr and ‘Asr prayers in Madinah without traveling and without fear. He said: I said to Ibn ‘Abbas, 'Why do you think he did that?' He said, 'He wanted not to impose hardship on anyone of his community.'"],[4570,"I prayed behind the Messenger of God ﷺ eight rak‘ahs together and seven rak‘ahs together in Madinah. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Abu al-Sha‘tha', 'I think the Prophet ﷺ delayed Zuhr a little and brought ‘Asr forward a little.' Abu al-Sha‘tha' said, 'I think so too.'"],[4571,"The Messenger of God ﷺ combined for us while resident, not traveling, between Zuhr and ‘Asr and Maghrib. A man said to Ibn ‘Umar, 'Why do you think the Prophet ﷺ did that?' He said, 'So as not to impose hardship on his community if a man combines.'"],[4572,"When the commanders combined two prayers—Maghrib and ‘Isha—during rain, he would combine with them."],[4577,"Ibn ‘Umar prayed one of the daytime prayers while traveling, and he saw some of them performing supererogatory prayer. Ibn ‘Umar said, 'What are they doing?' It was said to him, 'They are performing supererogatory prayer.' He said, 'If I were to perform supererogatory prayer, I would complete the prayer. I performed Hajj with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he did not perform supererogatory prayer during the day; I performed Hajj with Abu Bakr, and he did not perform supererogatory prayer during the day; I performed Hajj with ‘Umar, and he did not perform supererogatory prayer during the day; and I performed Hajj with ‘Uthman, and he did not perform supererogatory prayer during the day.' Then Ibn ‘Umar said, 'You have in the Messenger of God an excellent example.'"],[4578,"Ali used to consider it recommended to bathe after cupping."],[4579,"He used not to perform supererogatory prayer while traveling, and he would say, 'If I performed supererogatory prayer, I would complete it.' He would pray the night supererogatory prayer while traveling."],[4581,"Anas b. Malik shortened on the ship."],[4582,"Ibn ʿUmar would not pray the two rakʿahs of Fajr while traveling, and he would not leave them while at home."],[4584,"I accompanied Mujāhid on journeys several times, and he would not perform supererogatory prayers before it or after it."],[4585,"I traveled with Ayyūb, and he would not perform any supererogatory prayers in the Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, not adding more than two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs, except that he used to pray two rakʿahs before Fajr, and two rakʿahs after Maghrib, and he used to pray some rakʿahs after ʿIshāʾ, and he would perform witr before sleeping."],[4586,"If I travel and shorten the prayer, should I pray before it if I wish or after it? He said: \"Yes, I take the concession and the sunnah, so I shorten, then I love extra good, so I perform supererogatory prayers.\""],[4588,"And I myself saw al-Thawrī do that."],[4589,"I saw al-Shabi beat a man who had slandered another man in the courtyard (al-rahba), and he did not beat him in the mosque."],[4592,"I do not know anyone among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ who used to complete the prayer while traveling except Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas. He said: \"And 'A'ishah used to complete the prayer while traveling and fast.\" He said: And Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas traveled with a group of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, and Sa'd completed the prayer and fasted, while the people shortened and broke their fast. They said to Sa'd: \"How is it that they break their fast and shorten while you complete it and fast?\" He said: \"Keep to your own affair; for I know better about my own situation.\" He said: Sa'd did not forbid it to them, nor did he prohibit them from it."],[4593,"I said to 'Ata': \"Which of that is more beloved to you?\" He said: \"Shortening it, and all of that the righteous and the good have done.\""],[4594,"She used to fast while traveling and pray four [rak'ahs], or he said: \"and complete [the prayer].\""],[4595,"That she used to complete [the prayer] while traveling."],[4596,"Whoever prays four [rak'ahs] while traveling, it is good; and whoever prays two [rak'ahs], it is good. God will not punish you for the increase, but He will punish you for the deficiency."],[4598,"A man came to Ibn 'Umar and said: \"I and a companion of mine were on a journey, and I completed [the prayer] while he shortened it.\" Ibn 'Umar said: \"Rather, he completed it, and you shortened it.\""],[4599,"Whoever prays four [rak'ahs] while traveling must repeat the prayer."],[4601,"Pray on the ship, and do not cause hardship to your companions."],[4602,"The traveler breaks his fast when he has gone far, and that is a journey of two days."],[4603,"He used to make the witr precise."],[4605,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set out in the year of the conquest in the month of Ramadan and fasted until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke his fast. Al-Zuhri said: Breaking the fast was the latter of the two matters."],[4606,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set out in the year of the conquest in the month of Ramadan and fasted until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke his fast. He said: They used to follow the latest of the affairs of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and the latest of his affair."],[4607,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set out in the year of the conquest in the month of Ramadan and fasted until he passed by a pond on the road, and that was around midday. He said: The people became thirsty and began stretching their necks and their souls longed for it. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ called for a cup containing water and held it in his hand until the people saw it, then he drank, and the people drank."],[4608,"When the Prophet ﷺ was on his expedition for the conquest at Usfan or al-Kadid — Abd al-Malik was uncertain — a cup was handed to him while he was on his mount in the month of Ramadan. The traveling groups began passing by him while the cup was in his hand, then he drank. Afterwards he learned that some people had fasted, and he said: Those are the disobedient ones, three times."],[4610,"I never saw Ibn Umar fast while traveling at all except one day. I saw him break his fast when evening came, and we said: Were you fasting? He said: Yes, I thought I would enter Makkah today, and I disliked that the people be fasting while I was not fasting, and that was in Ramadan."],[4611,"A man asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about prayer and breaking the fast in the month of Ramadan while traveling. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: Break your fast. He said: I am able to fast, O Messenger of God. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: Are you stronger or is God? God gave as charity the breaking of the fast of the fasting person to the sick of my community and its travelers. Would any of you like to give charity to someone and then keep rejecting it back to him?"],[4612,"He came to Madinah and entered upon the Prophet ﷺ for a need, and found the Prophet ﷺ eating. The Prophet ﷺ said: Come close. The man said: I am fasting. The Prophet ﷺ said: The traveler has been relieved of fasting and half of the prayer, and so have the pregnant and the nursing."],[4613,"He came to Madinah and entered upon the Prophet ﷺ for a need, and found the Prophet ﷺ eating. The Prophet ﷺ said: Come close. The man said: I am fasting. The Prophet ﷺ said: The traveler has been relieved of fasting and half of the prayer, and so have the pregnant and the nursing."],[4614,"I was with him when a group from the people of al-Jazira came to him and said: O Abu Muhammad, we travel in palanquins, and we are spared the hardship. Should we fast? He said: No. They said: We are able to do that. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ was stronger and better than you. He said: The best of you are those who, when they travel, shorten the prayer and do not fast."],[4615,"The best of my community are those who bear witness that there is no god but Allah, alone without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger; those who, when they do good, rejoice, and when they do evil, seek forgiveness; and when they travel, they shorten the prayer and break the fast. The worst of my community are those who were born in luxury and nourished by it; their concern—or he said: their preoccupation—is soft clothing, delicious food, and wickedness in speech."],[4616,"Ibn Abbas said to me: Whatever you disobey me in concerning anything, do not disobey me in three things: when you go out as a traveler, pray two rak'ahs until you return to your family; do not fast until you return to your home; and do not enter Makkah except in a state of ihram."],[4618,"4516 4484 - Ibn Uyaynah said: And Amr b. Dinar informed me, from Kulthum b. Jabr, from a man, from Umar, something similar. [Hidden matn]: Umar b. al-Khattab ordered a man who fasted the month of Ramadan while traveling to make it up."],[4619,"I asked Ata b. Abi Rabah about the one fasting while traveling, and he said: As for the obligatory fast, no; as for the voluntary fast, there is no harm in it."],[4620,"He used to say: Whoever accompanies us should not fast. He said: And he would not fast while traveling."],[4621,"There is no witr after the morning prayer."],[4622,"A man who was fasting on the day of travel was with him, and Ibn Umar would order his pre-dawn meal to be prepared for him, and when it was time to break the fast, he would stop and detain him until he broke his fast. He said: Then one day the man suffered severe hardship from thirst, and Ibn Umar said to him: If you enter the Fire after what I see, you will have seen a pure one."],[4623,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz summoned Salim b. Abdullah and Urwah b. al-Zubayr and asked them about the traveler in Ramadan: should he fast or break the fast? Urwah said: I only took it from Aishah, and Salim said: I only took it from Abdullah b. Umar. He said: When they disagreed and their voices rose, Umar said: O Allah, forgive; O Allah, forgive. I fast it in ease and break it in hardship."],[4625,"Pray the witr even if you have prayed the morning prayer."],[4626,"4524 4492 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, something similar, and he said: \"Take whichever of them is easier for you.\" Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: \"Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship.\" [Hidden matn]: The Prophet ﷺ fasted while traveling and broke the fast, and said: Take whichever of them is easier for you. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: \"Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship.\""],[4627,"They set out for Makkah, and al-Aswad b. Yazid was with them. The crescent of the month of Ramadan appeared to them, so they fasted on the road. He said: \"We passed by the well of Maymun, and he ordered them to perform ghusl.\""],[4628,"We sighted the crescent of Ramadan at Hulwan, or at al-Mada'in, and among us were men from the Companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ. Their commander called out: \"Whoever of you wishes to fast, let him fast; and whoever of you wishes to break the fast, let him break it. For the Messenger of God ﷺ fasted while traveling and broke the fast.\""],[4629,"I returned with Ali b. Abi Talib from Yanbu. He said: Ali fasted, and Ali was riding, while I broke the fast because I was walking, until we arrived in Madinah at night. We passed by the house of Uthman b. Affan, and he was reciting. He said: Ali stopped to listen to his recitation, then Ali said: \"He is reciting while in a surah,\" or he said: \"in Surat al-Nahl.\""],[4630,"She used to fast while traveling and pray four [rak'ahs], or he said: \"and complete [the prayer].\""],[4631,"Fasting for the traveler was only disliked because people would say: Depart for his sake, for he is fasting; and feed his mount, for he is fasting."],[4632,"4524 4492 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, something similar, and he said: \"Take whichever of them is easier for you.\" Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: \"Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship.\" [Hidden matn]: The Prophet ﷺ fasted while traveling and broke the fast, and said: Take whichever of them is easier for you. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: \"Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship.\""],[4633,"Take the easier of the two for yourself; God desires only ease."],[4634,"Some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ fasted while traveling, and some of them broke the fast, and none of them found fault with the others. He said: \"This one took God's concession, and this one fulfilled God's obligation.\""],[4636,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, I have been fasting continuously, and I intend to travel. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: If you wish, fast; and if you wish, break the fast."],[4637,"Hamzah al-Aslami asked the Prophet ﷺ about fasting while traveling, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: If you wish, fast; and if you wish, break the fast."],[4638,"When a man sets out as a traveler in the month of Ramadan having begun the day fasting, he may break the fast if he wishes when he sets out."],[4639,"The fasting person does not break the fast today except if thirst becomes severe for him. If he fears for himself, he may break the fast."],[4640,"He does not break the fast on that day."],[4641,"Amr b. Shurahbil set out as a traveler during the day. When he crossed the Euphrates, he ordered his servant, who gave him water to drink, and he broke the fast."],[4642,"A man does not pray the prescribed prayer on a riding animal facing the House, nor with his back to it, unless he is sick or fearful. Then he may pray on his riding animal facing the House, but not with his back to it."],[4643,"I said to Ata: A group of travelers who have rain ahead of them, may they pray on their riding animals? He said: Yes, if they wish. I said: Do they perform tayammum with earth if they find no water? He said: Yes."],[4644,"The Messenger of God ﷺ, when he wanted to pray the prescribed prayer, dismounted from his mount and faced the qibla."],[4645,"I was with Anas b. Malik on a rainy day until we reached Atit, and the ground was soft and muddy. He led us in the afternoon prayer on his donkey, gesturing with his head, and he made the prostration lower than the bowing."],[4646,"He was traveling through water and mud, and the prescribed prayer arrived, but he could not get out of that water. He said: We feared that the prayer would elapse us, so we sought guidance from God and faced the qibla, and we gestured on our riding animals."],[4647,"He saw Abu al-Shatha gesturing in prayer in water and mud."],[4648,"They used to pray on the backs of their riding animals wherever they were facing, except for the obligatory prayer and the witr."],[4650,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray voluntary prayers on his mount wherever it turned him. When he wanted to pray the prescribed prayer, he dismounted from his mount and faced the qibla."],[4651,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying on the back of his riding camel, facing in every direction."],[4652,"He used to pray on his riding camel voluntarily, in whatever direction it faced, and he informed them that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do that. He said: I asked Nafi‘: How was the witr? He said: “He used to perform the witr on his riding camel, and sometimes he would dismount and perform the witr on the ground.”"],[4654,"He used to pray voluntarily on his riding camel in whatever direction it faced, and he would make the prostration lower than the bowing."],[4655,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying voluntary prayers on his riding camel in every direction, but he would lower the prostration compared to the bowing, gesturing with a slight nod."],[4656,"The Messenger of God ﷺ sent me on an errand. I came and found him praying toward the east, nodding his head slightly on his riding camel, with the prostration lower than the bowing. I greeted him, but he did not return my greeting. When he finished his prayer, he said: “What did you do about such-and-such errand? I was praying.”"],[4657,"I saw Anas b. Malik on a journey, praying on a donkey while facing away from the qiblah, bowing and prostrating by nodding his head without placing his face on anything."],[4658,"I saw Anas b. Malik praying voluntarily on his riding camel while heading toward Syria."],[4660,"Pray the witr even if you have prayed the morning prayer."],[4661,"A man may pray on his mount, turning his back toward Syria and Yemen. He said: I said: Even if he is on a journey for worldly matters? He said: “Yes; he begins by saying the takbir, then recites, then bows, then prostrates, then says the tashahhud.”"],[4662,"One may pray on all mounts: on the camel, the horse, the mule, and the donkey.” He said: I said: Even on the donkey? He said: “Yes.”"],[4663,"When you bow, you place your hands on your knees, then bow and lower your head, then you make the prostration lower than the bowing.\" I said: Like the bowing and prostration of a sick person? He said: \"Yes.\""],[4664,"I said to Ata': Has a reliable report reached you about praying on a mount while facing away from the qiblah? He said: \"Yes.\" Then he said regarding that: \"And to Allah belong the east and the west, so wherever you turn, there is the Face of Allah.\""],[4665,"He used to pray on his riding camel voluntarily, in whatever direction it faced, and he informed them that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do that. He said: I asked Nafi‘: How was the witr? He said: “He used to perform the witr on his riding camel, and sometimes he would dismount and perform the witr on the ground.”"],[4667,"Ibn Umar used to pray witr on his mount."],[4670,"A man lagged behind while we were traveling, and Ibn Umar said to him: What made you lag behind? He said: I prayed witr. He said: \"One better than you prayed witr on a camel, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\""],[4671,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed witr on his mount."],[4672,"Ali used to pray witr on his mount."],[4673,"I asked him — and he was spending the night at the home of Abdullah b. Mas'ud — when did Abdullah pray witr? He said: He used to pray witr when there remained of the night the same amount as had passed of the night when he prayed maghrib. He said: And Abdullah's recitation could be heard by the people of the house during the night."],[4675,"Ibn Umar, when he wanted to perform witr, would dismount from his mount and perform witr on the ground."],[4676,"I said to 'Ata': 'Should I separate between witr and what precedes it with a taslim?' He said: 'It is as if you are Bedouins. Do you not give the taslim of departure? Everything suffices you. If you wish, pray a hundred rak'ahs, or do not separate between witr and what precedes it of bowing.' He said: I said: 'And is the imam also like that in the month of Ramadan?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[4677,"I said to Qatadah: May a man pray while driving his animal not facing the qiblah? He said: No, unless he is in fear."],[4678,"A man prays like that; when he wants to bow and prostrate, he faces the qiblah."],[4679,"I said to him: People on a ship—do they shorten? He said: No, unless they fear drowning. He said: I said: What about one who is working on it—does he shorten? He said: Yes."],[4680,"He shortened on a ship and prayed in it seated, and those with him prayed seated."],[4681,"Anas b. Malik led us in prayer on the ship seated on a mat, and he shortened the prayer."],[4682,"Ibn Sirin shortened on the ship; when he reached Wasit, he completed the prayer."],[4683,"Anas b. Malik shortened on the ship."],[4684,"They pray on the ship standing, unless they fear drowning; then they pray seated, following the qiblah wherever it shifts."],[4685,"I said to Ata: What do you think if there is a shore near me—should I disembark and pray there? He said: If it does not delay your companions, then yes."],[4686,"Pray on the ship, and do not cause hardship to your companions."],[4687,"Recite in them whatever you wish; there is nothing fixed in them."],[4688,"Pray in the ship, if you wish standing and if you wish sitting; prostrate on a firm part of it or on a mat."],[4689,"Anas b. Malik prayed with his companions in the ship sitting on a mat."],[4690,"We used to pray in the ship sitting."],[4691,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, Rafi' b. Khadij, and Anas b. Malik used to lie down before the two rak'ahs of Fajr and commanded others to do that."],[4692,"I was with Jabir b. Abd Allah, Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and Abu al-Darda'—and I think he mentioned Abu Hurayrah—in a ship. The one who led us in prayer led us standing, and if we had wished to go out we would have gone out."],[4694,"Masruq used to carry with him a brick in the ship to prostrate on it."],[4696,"Ata was asked about a man who comes out of the sea naked. He said: \"He prays sitting.\""],[4697,"If one of them leads them, let their imam stand in prayer in the middle of the row, and let them make one row, if they wish standing and if they wish sitting, and let some of them lower their gaze from others."],[4698,"When a man comes out of the sea naked, he prays sitting."],[4699,"When people come out of the sea naked and one of them leads them in prayer, they pray sitting, and their imam is with them in the row, and they gesture by making signs."],[4703,"Witr is a duty, but it is not like maghrib."],[4704,"Witr is not obligatory like the prescribed prayer, but it is a sunnah established by the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[4705,"A man asked Ibn al-Musayyib about witr, and he said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed witr, and if you leave it, there is nothing upon you. And pray the duha prayer, and if you leave it, there is nothing upon you. And pray two rak'ahs before zuhr and two rak'ahs after it, and if you leave them, there is nothing upon you. And the Messenger of God ﷺ offered a sacrifice, and if you leave it, there is nothing upon you.\" He said: I said: O Abu Muhammad, we have come to know all of this except witr. He said: It has reached me that the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Indeed, God is odd (witr) and loves oddness (witr).\""],[4706,"Perform witr, O people of the Quran, for indeed God is odd (witr) and loves oddness (witr).\" Then a bedouin said: What is the Messenger of God ﷺ saying? The Prophet ﷺ said: \"It is not for you or your companions.\""],[4707,"I was commanded to perform witr and to offer sacrifices, but it was not made binding upon me."],[4708,"Three things are obligatory upon me and voluntary for you: the sacrificial animal, the duha prayer, and witr."],[4710,"It was said to Ubada b. al-Samit, or I said to him: Abu Muhammad says that witr is obligatory. Ubada said: Abu Muhammad has lied. I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"Five prayers God has prescribed for His servants. Whoever performs them without diminishing anything from them, treating their right with contempt, it is a right upon God to admit him to Paradise. And whoever does not perform them has no covenant with God; if He wills, He forgives him, and if He wills, He punishes him.\""],[4711,"They used to recommend lying down or sleeping after witr."],[4712,"There is no witr except upon one who has recited the Qur'an."],[4713,"I would not love to have abandoned witr for a night even if I had red camels."],[4716,"Ibn Sirin used to prefer odd numbers in everything, even to eat an odd number."],[4717,"When dawn broke, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray two brief rak'ahs, then lie on his right side until the mu'adhdhin came and informed him that the prayer was due. He did not lie down as a sunnah, but because he would toil during his night and thus rest. He said: Additional text: Ibn 'Umar would throw pebbles at them when he saw them lying on their right sides."],[4718,"The Prophet ﷺ came out to his companions and said: Indeed Allah has added a prayer to your prayers, so guard it, and it is witr."],[4720,"Ibn 'Abbas could not see, and someone would look for his sake. When dawn broke, he would pray two rak'ahs and then sit."],[4721,"He used to deem witr obligatory and say: Whoever misses witr until morning, let him pray witr when he remembers."],[4722,"Ibn Mas'ud passed by two men talking after the break of dawn, and he said: \"O you two, either pray, or be silent.\""],[4725,"Pray the witr before dawn breaks."],[4726,"He was asked about a man who did not pray witr until dawn broke. He said: \"He will pray the witr the next day.\""],[4727,"Whoever is overtaken by dawn and has not prayed witr, there is no witr for him."],[4728,"He was asked about a man who did not pray witr until dawn broke. He said: \"The witr has passed him by, so he should not pray it.\" It was said to him: \"Is this knowledge or opinion?\""],[4729,"We said to him: \"Tell us about the voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" He said: \"And who can bear it?\" We said to him: \"Tell us, and we will bear of it what we can.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would wait, and when the sun rose and appeared, its height from the east being like its height at 'Asr, he would pray two rak'ahs, separating them with a greeting upon the angels brought near, the prophets, and those who followed him among the believers and Muslims. Then he would wait until the forenoon was high, and its height from the east being like its height at Zuhr, he would pray four rak'ahs, separating them with the greeting as he did in the first ones. Then when the sun declined, he would stand and pray four rak'ahs, separating them with a greeting upon the angels brought near, the prophets, and those who followed him among the believers and Muslims. Then he would pray two rak'ahs after Zuhr like that, then he would pray four rak'ahs before 'Asr, separating them with the like of that.\""],[4730,"Pray the witr as long as the sun has not risen."],[4731,"\"And he would pray four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, and four before 'Asr, so these are sixteen rak'ahs.\""],[4732,"Ibn Abbas prayed witr after the break of dawn."],[4734,"I saw Umar b. al-Khattab praying four rak'ahs before Zuhr in his house."],[4737,"He saw some people praying after the sun had risen, and he said: 'If these people had caught the early predecessors, they would have learned that a prayer other than this one is better than it: the prayer of the oft-returning penitents when the young camels feel the heat of the sun.'"],[4738,"A group came to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari and asked him about Witr. He said, \"No Witr after the adhan.\" They went to 'Ali and told him, and he said, \"He has gone too deep in the matter and gone too far in giving the ruling. Witr is between you and the Fajr prayer.\""],[4739,"A man came to 'Ali and said, \"Abu Musa says: No Witr after the adhan.\" 'Ali said to him, \"He has gone too deep in the matter and gone too far in giving the ruling. Witr is between the two prayers.\""],[4740,"I was told from Abu al-Darda' that he said, \"There is no Witr for one whom the morning overtakes.\" That was mentioned to 'A'ishah, and she said, \"Abu al-Darda' lied. The Prophet ﷺ would reach the morning and then perform Witr.\""],[4741,"When Satan sees the son of Adam prostrating, he says: O woe to him! Woe to Satan! God commanded the son of Adam to prostrate and for him is Paradise, and he obeyed. And He commanded me to prostrate, and I disobeyed, so for me is the Fire."],[4742,"Witr is between the two prayers."],[4744,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, \"I did not perform Witr until morning came.\" The Prophet ﷺ said, \"Witr is only at night.\" He repeated it to him, and he ordered him to perform Witr."],[4745,"Whoever reaches the morning without having performed Witr, he reaches the morning with a rope of seventy cubits on his head."],[4746,"Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas was once delayed from the prayer. It was said to him, \"You were slow for the people.\" He said to him, \"Morning overtook me before I performed Witr, so I performed Witr.\""],[4748,"I asked 'Abidah about a man who wakes up at the iqamah and has not performed Witr. He said, \"He performs Witr.\""],[4749,"A man asked Ibn Umar about witr. He said: While Ibn Umar was circumambulating the House one night, dawn overtook him, so he performed witr."],[4750,"When dawn breaks, all night prayer and witr have passed, so perform witr before dawn."],[4752,"Abu Bakr and Umar discussed witr in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ. Abu Bakr said: As for me, I sleep on witr, and if I wake I pray pairs until morning. Umar said: But I sleep on an even number, then perform witr in the pre-dawn. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abu Bakr: “This one is cautious,” and to Umar: “This one is strong.”"],[4753,"Abu Bakr used to perform witr at the beginning of the night, and Umar at the end of the night. The Prophet ﷺ asked them about their witr and they told him. He said: “This one is strong, and this one is cautious.” He said: And the Prophet ﷺ said: “I will strike for you two the example of two men who set out on a desert by night. One of them said: I do not want to sleep until I cross it. The other said: I will sleep a little, then get up and cross it. They both reached the destination together.”"],[4754,"The Prophet ﷺ said to Abu Bakr: “When do you perform witr?” He said: Before I sleep. He said: “You have taken hold of the firm handhold.” He said to Umar: “When do you perform witr?” He said: At the end of the night when I finish my prayer. He said: “You have done the deed of those endowed with strength.”"],[4755,"The Prophet ﷺ enjoined upon me three things that I will not abandon, neither while traveling nor while residing: sleeping upon Witr, fasting three days of every month, and the Duha prayer."],[4757,"I heard Rafi' b. Khadij being asked about witr. He said: As for me, I perform witr at the beginning of the night, and if I am granted anything in its latter part, I pray two rak'ahs at a time until morning.” Or he said: “Until dawn overtakes me.”"],[4758,"I was sitting with Ammar b. Yasir when a man asked him: O Abu al-Yaqzan, what do you say about witr? Ammar said: As for me, I perform witr before I sleep, and if Allah grants me something, I pray pairs until morning.”"],[4759,"Tell me how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform witr. He remained silent. I asked him a second time and he remained silent. I asked him a third time, and he said: If you wish, I will tell you on the authority of Abu Hurayrah. As for me, I perform it here with five, then I return and sleep, and if I wake I pray pairs until morning."],[4760,"When one of you prostrates, let him be moderate and not spread his forearms like a dog. He said: And the Prophet ﷺ said: Whoever of you fears that he will not wake at the end of the night, let him perform witr at the beginning of the night; and whoever of you hopes to wake at the end of the night, let him perform witr at the end of the night, for the recitation at the end of the night is witnessed, and that is better."],[4762,"Al-Hasan b. 'Ali saw a man praying four rak'ahs after maghrib and said to him: 'Have you missed anything from the obligatory prayer?' He said, 'No.' He said: 'Then it is only two rak'ahs after the prostrations.'"],[4764,"I asked him — and he was spending the night at the home of Abdullah b. Mas'ud — when did Abdullah pray witr? He said: He used to pray witr when there remained of the night the same amount as had passed of the night when he prayed maghrib. He said: And Abdullah's recitation could be heard by the people of the house during the night."],[4766,"He was asked about witr, and he said: The witr of the prudent is at the beginning of the night, and the witr of the strong is at the end of the night. I said: What do you do? He said: As for me, if I can be among the prudent, I am."],[4767,"Ali came out when Ibn al-Nabbah gave the iqamah and said: \"By the night when it darkens, and by the dawn when it breathes\" (Q 81:17–18). What an excellent time for witr this is! Where are those who ask about witr?"],[4768,"Ali came out to us at the break of dawn and said: \"By the night when it darkens\" (Q 81:17) — he pointed with his hand toward the east — then said: Where are those who ask about witr? What an excellent time for witr this is!"],[4770,"Witr is an obligation upon every Muslim. Whoever wishes to pray witr with five rak'ahs, let him do so. Whoever wishes to pray witr with three, let him do so. Whoever wishes to pray witr with one, let him do so. And whoever is unable except to gesture, let him gesture."],[4772,"The witr of the night is like the witr of the day — the maghrib prayer is three."],[4773,"I prayed with Anas and spent the night at his place. He said: I saw him pray in pairs, until at the end of his prayer he performed the witr with three, like the Maghrib."],[4774,"Abdullah used to perform the witr with three or more."],[4775,"The witr is three, five, seven, nine, and eleven."],[4776,"When Umar had buried Abu Bakr and finished with him, he had prayed the last Isha prayer and then performed the witr with three rak'ahs, and some Muslims performed the witr with him."],[4778,"Ibn Abbas came as a delegate to Mu'awiya in Syria. They used to converse at night until midnight or more. He said: One night Ibn Abbas attended the last Isha prayer with Mu'awiya in the maqsura. When Mu'awiya finished, he prayed one rak'ah and did not add to it, while I was looking at him. He said: I went to Ibn Abbas and said to him: Shall I not laugh at Mu'awiya? He prayed Isha, then performed the witr with one rak'ah and did not add to it. He said: He did right, my son. None of us is more knowledgeable than Mu'awiya. It is only one, five, seven, or more than that; he performs the witr with whatever he wishes. I informed Ata of this report of Utba, and he said: We only heard that he said: He did right. Is not the Maghrib—Ata is the speaker—three rak'ahs?"],[4779,"Ata was asked about a rak'ah with which one performs the witr. He said: It is good. It has reached me that Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas used to perform the witr with one rak'ah."],[4780,"Sa'd used to pray Isha, then perform the witr with one rak'ah."],[4781,"Whatever is after the 'Isha' prayer is nash'ah (night rising)."],[4783,"I saw Sa'd b. Malik pray Isha, then pray one rak'ah after it, then perform the witr after that."],[4784,"You perform the witr with one rak'ah? He said: Yes, I make it light for myself. Three are dearer to me than one, five are dearer to me than three, and seven are dearer to me than five."],[4785,"The witr is seven or five; three is butayra' (deficient), and I dislike it being butayra'."],[4786,"Three is more beloved to me than one, and seven is more beloved to me than five; whatever is more numerous is more beloved to me."],[4787,"The witr is one, three, five, seven, nine, and eleven rak'ahs; the most pleasing of them to me is three."],[4788,"'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud said to Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas: \"Do you perform witr with one?\" He replied, \"Is not the witr only one?\" So 'Abd Allah said, \"Yes, but three is better.\" He said, \"I will not add to it.\" He said, \"'Abd Allah became angry, so Sa'd said, \"Do you get angry at me for performing witr with one rak'ah while you make three grandmothers inherit? Why do you not make Hawwa', the wife of Adam, inherit?\""],[4790,"A man asked 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Uthman al-Taymi about the prayer of Talhah b. 'Ubayd Allah. He said, \"If you wish, I will tell you about the prayer of 'Uthman b. 'Affan.\" He said, \"Yes.\" He said: I said, \"Tonight I will certainly prevail over the group at the Hijr\"—meaning the Maqam. He said: \"When I got up, there was a man pressing against me, veiled.\" He said: I looked and it was 'Uthman, so I moved back from him. He prayed, and he was prostrating the prostrations of the Qur'an until I said, \"This is the call to dawn.\" Then he performed witr with one rak'ah and did not pray anything else besides it, then he left."],[4791,"I was praying behind the Maqam when a veiled man came and recited the seven long surahs, then bowed for two rak'ahs, then turned away. I looked, and it was 'Uthman."],[4792,"I saw 'Uthman one night while he was praying until at the end of the night he performed witr, so I followed him to see who he was, and it was 'Uthman."],[4793,"Additional text: I said to Miqsam, \"I perform witr with three, then I go out to the morning prayer for fear of missing the prayer.\" He disliked that one should perform witr except with five or seven. I said, \"From whom is this?\" He said, \"From the trustworthy one, from Maymunah and 'A'ishah, from the Prophet ﷺ.\""],[4794,"A person asked 'Ata' about the minimum that suffices for a traveler. He said, \"One rak'ah, if he wishes.\" I said, \"And the resident?\" He said, \"One rak'ah suffices him if he wishes; he did not add to them.\""],[4795,"'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud and Hudhayfah b. al-Yaman stayed up talking at night in the presence of al-Walid b. 'Uqbah b. Abi Mu'ayt, then left him. They stood conversing until they saw the first signs of dawn, and each of them performed witr with one rak'ah."],[4797,"If a man misses something of the night and does not pray it, there is no harm in lengthening the two rak’ahs of dawn."],[4798,"Ubayy b. Ka'b used to pray witr with three (rak'ahs)."],[4800,"I prayed with Anas and spent the night at his place. He said: I saw him pray in pairs, until at the end of his prayer he performed the witr with three, like the Maghrib."],[4801,"I said to 'Ata': 'Should I separate between witr and what precedes it with a taslim?' He said: 'It is as if you are Bedouins. Do you not give the taslim of departure? Everything suffices you. If you wish, pray a hundred rak'ahs, or do not separate between witr and what precedes it of bowing.' He said: I said: 'And is the imam also like that in the month of Ramadan?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[4802,"He prayed witr with five (rak'ahs), not sitting except in the witr."],[4805,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray witr with five or seven (rak'ahs), not separating between them with speech or taslim."],[4808,"Witr is like the Maghrib prayer, except that one does not sit except in the third (rak'ah)."],[4811,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the night prayer, and he said: 'Two by two. When you fear the dawn, then pray witr with one, making odd what preceded it.'"],[4812,"'The night prayer is two by two, and the witr is one rak'ah at the end of the night.' He said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Maghrib is the witr of the daytime prayer, so make the night prayer odd.'"],[4814,"'The night prayer is two by two, and the witr is one rak'ah at the end of the night.' He said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Maghrib is the witr of the daytime prayer, so make the night prayer odd.'"],[4817,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the night prayer, and he said: 'Two by two. When you fear the dawn, then one.'"],[4818,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the night prayer, and he said: 'One of you prays two by two, until when he fears the dawn, he prays witr with one, making odd for him what he has prayed.'"],[4820,"When he slept after praying witr and then got up to pray during the night, he would pray one rak'ah to pair with his witr, making it even, then he would pray witr after that at the end of his prayer. Al-Zuhri said: 'That reached Ibn 'Abbas and he did not like it, and he said: Ibn 'Umar prays witr three times in one night.'"],[4821,"There is no harm in a man praying witr and then sleeping. When he gets up during the night, he pairs with one rak'ah to his witr, then he prays witr at the end of his prayer."],[4822,"Pray after the break of dawn as much as you wish."],[4824,"When you pray witr at the beginning of the night, pray an even number until morning."],[4825,"The matter of a man who prays witr and then wakes up and makes it even with a rakʿah was mentioned to her. She said: \"That is playing with his witr.\" He said: And I asked ʿĀʾishah about turning during prayer, and she said: \"It is a snatching which Satan snatches from the prayer.\""],[4826,"I said to him: \"A man prays witr during the night, then wakes up while there is still night left.\" Ḥasan said: \"They used to like that the last of their prayer be witr.\""],[4827,"When he prayed witr during the night, he would pray an even number until morning."],[4828,"Ṭāwūs, when he prayed witr during the night, would not make it even; he would pray an even number until morning."],[4829,"I asked ʿAmr b. Maymūn al-Awdī about making up the witr. He said: \"When you pray witr and then get up during the night, make it even with a rakʿah.\" He said: I mentioned that to ʿAlqamah, and he said: \"ʿAmr does not know. Witr is only one. When you pray witr and then wake up during the night, pray an even number until morning.\""],[4830,"When he slept after witr and then woke up, he would pray an even number until morning."],[4831,"When he prayed witr and there was still night remaining, he would sit and recite until morning."],[4832,"It was considered recommended to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr: \"Say, O disbelievers\" and \"Say, He is God, the One.\""],[4833,"When you have said the salam, there is nothing like it."],[4835,"He used to recite in the last three rak'ahs: in the first, “Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High”; in the second, “Say: O disbelievers”; and in the third, “Say: He is God, the One,” “Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the dawn,” and “Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind.”"],[4836,"He used to perform witr with “Indeed, We sent it down on the Night of Decree,” “When the earth is shaken,” and “Say: He is God, the One.”"],[4838,"Recite in them whatever you wish; there is nothing fixed in them."],[4839,"You say the takbir when you have finished the recitation in the last rak'ah of witr, then you perform qunut and raise your voice, then when you wish to bow you say the takbir."],[4840,"He used to perform witr with thirteen rak'ahs, including two rak'ahs before dawn. I said: How did he pray them? He said: “I do not know.”"],[4841,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray eleven rak'ahs at night. When dawn broke, he prayed two light rak'ahs, then he leaned on his right side until the muezzin came to call him to prayer."],[4842,"Mu'adh b. Jabal said: Who will go ahead and draw water for us? He said: I said: I will. That was on their return from al-Hudaybiyyah. Jabir said: I arrived at Athayah and drew water and filled the basin. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ arrived and said: “Are you giving water to drink?” I said: Yes, may my father be sacrificed for you. So he drank. Then I took his nose ring or rein and led him to an open flat area where he dismounted and prayed thirteen rak'ahs after the last evening prayer, while I was beside him. He said: I think he said: He prayed the last evening prayer and then prayed them."],[4843,"I was in the house of Maymunah, and the Prophet ﷺ got up to pray at night. I got up with him on his left, and he took my hand and placed me on his right. Then he prayed thirteen rak'ahs, until I estimated the length of his standing in each rak'ah as the length of “O you wrapped in garments.”"],[4844,"I slept at my maternal aunt Maymunah bint al-Harith's. The Prophet ﷺ got up during the night, went to relieve himself, then came and washed his face and hands, then slept. Then he got up during the night, went to the water skin, untied its strap, and performed a moderate ablution, not excessive but complete. Then he got up to pray. I stretched out, disliking that he should see that I was seeking him out, meaning watching him. He said: Then I got up and did as he did, then I stood on his left. He took hold of what was near my ear and turned me until I was on his right. His prayer was completed as thirteen rak'ahs, including the two rak'ahs of dawn. Then he lay down and slept until he snored. Then Bilal came and called him to prayer, so he got up and prayed without performing ablution."],[4845,"He spent the night at his maternal aunt Maymunah's house. He said: I lay down across the width of the cushion, and the Prophet ﷺ and his wife lay down along its length. He slept until midnight, or a little before or a little after it. Then the Prophet ﷺ woke up and sat up, wiping sleep from his face. Then he recited the last ten verses of Surah Al 'Imran. Then the Prophet ﷺ went to a hanging water skin, performed ablution, and did it well. Then he stood and prayed, and I stood and did the same as he had done. Then I went to his side, and he placed his hand on my head and took hold of my ear, twisting it. He prayed two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then two rak'ahs, then performed witr. Then he lay down until the muezzin came to him. Then he stood and prayed two brief rak'ahs, then went out and prayed the morning prayer (Subh)."],[4846,"He asked Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, about the Prophet's ﷺ night prayer. She said: He would pray the later 'Isha', then glorify Allah, then pray after it as much of the night as he wished. Then he would finish and sleep for a time similar to what he had prayed. Then he would wake from that sleep and pray for a time similar to what he had slept. That last prayer of his would continue until morning."],[4847,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray seventeen rak'ahs during the night."],[4848,"He asked 'A'ishah: How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Ramadan? She said: The Prophet ﷺ did not exceed eleven rak'ahs in Ramadan or at any other time. He would pray four rak'ahs – do not ask about their beauty and length – then pray four rak'ahs – do not ask about their beauty and length – then pray three. 'A'ishah said: I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, do you sleep before performing witr?' He said, 'O 'A'ishah, my eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep.'"],[4849,"I will certainly observe the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: I rested my head on his threshold or his tent. The Prophet ﷺ stood and prayed two brief rak'ahs, then prayed two long rak'ahs, then prayed two rak'ahs shorter than the two before them, then performed witr. That is thirteen rak'ahs."],[4850,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform witr with nine rak'ahs, two of them while sitting. When he became weak, he performed witr with seven, two of them while sitting."],[4851,"The character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the Qur'an. He said: I was about to get up, but then I changed my mind and said to her: Tell me about the night prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: Do you not recite this surah, 'O you wrapped in garments'? He said: I said, 'Yes.' She said: Allah made night prayer obligatory at the beginning of this surah. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ and his companions stood in prayer for a year until their feet became swollen. Allah withheld the conclusion of it for twelve months. Then Allah sent down the alleviation at the end of the surah, and night prayer became voluntary after having been obligatory. I was about to get up, but then I changed my mind and asked her: I said, 'O Mother of the Believers, tell me about the witr of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' She said: We used to prepare for him his siwak and his water for purification at night. Allah would awaken him whenever He willed. Then he would use the siwak and perform ablution, then pray nine rak'ahs, not sitting in them except at the eighth. He would praise Allah, remember Him, and supplicate to Him. Then he would stand and not give the salam until he prayed the ninth. Then he would sit and praise Allah, remember Him, and supplicate to Him, then give a salam loud enough for us to hear. Then he would pray two rak'ahs while sitting, after giving the salam. That is eleven rak'ahs, my dear son. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grew older and gained weight, he performed witr with seven, praying two rak'ahs while sitting after giving the salam. That is nine, my dear son. Whenever the Prophet of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer, he loved to persist in it. And whenever sleep or pain overcame him and prevented him from praying at night, he would pray twelve rak'ahs during the day. I do not know that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ ever recited the entire Qur'an in a single night, nor prayed the whole night until morning, nor fasted an entire month other than Ramadan. He said: I went to Ibn 'Abbas and told him her hadith. He said: She has spoken the truth. If I were able to visit her, I would have spoken to her face to face. Hakim b. Aflah said: If I had known that you would not visit her, I would not have told you her hadith."],[4852,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray nine rak'ahs at night. When he became heavy and aged, he prayed seven."],[4853,"I said to him: Should we limit ourselves to the witr of the Prophet ﷺ? He said: 'Rather, adding more good is dearer to me.'"],[4854,"They used to count four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two rak'ahs after it as part of the Sunnah. He said: And they used to pray two rak'ahs before 'Asr but did not count them as part of the Sunnah, and two rak'ahs after Maghrib, and two rak'ahs after 'Isha, and two rak'ahs before Fajr."],[4855,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray at night. When he wanted to perform witr, if I was awake he would talk to me; otherwise he would lie down."],[4856,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, Rafi' b. Khadij, and Anas b. Malik used to lie down before the two rak'ahs of Fajr and commanded others to do that."],[4858,"When dawn broke, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray two brief rak'ahs, then lie on his right side until the mu'adhdhin came and informed him that the prayer was due."],[4859,"When dawn broke, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray two brief rak'ahs, then lie on his right side until the mu'adhdhin came and informed him that the prayer was due. He did not lie down as a sunnah, but because he would toil during his night and thus rest. He said: Additional text: Ibn 'Umar would throw pebbles at them when he saw them lying on their right sides."],[4860,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out one night in the middle of the night and prayed in the mosque. Some men gathered and prayed with him following his prayer. When morning came, they spoke about how the Prophet ﷺ had gone out and prayed in the mosque in the middle of the night. The following night, more people gathered than before. The Prophet ﷺ went out in the middle of the night and prayed, and they prayed with him following his prayer. Then morning came and they spoke about that. On the third night, many people gathered, until the people filled the mosque. She said: The Prophet ﷺ went out in the middle of the night and prayed, and they prayed with him. She said: When it was the fourth night, the people gathered until the mosque was almost unable to accommodate its people. She said: The Prophet ﷺ sat and did not come out. She said: Until I heard some of them saying, 'The prayer!' But the Prophet ﷺ did not come out to them. When he had prayed the dawn prayer, he said the taslim, then stood among the people and recited the tashahhud, then said: 'Now then, your situation last night was not hidden from me, but I feared that it would be made obligatory upon you and you would be unable to perform it.'"],[4862,"What an excellent time of heedlessness is the period between maghrib and 'isha—meaning prayer."],[4863,"Pray between maghrib and 'isha, for it lightens one's portion and removes from him the vain talk of the early night. Indeed, the vain talk of the early night is a cause of slackness for its latter part."],[4864,"Whoever prays four rak'ahs after 'isha, performing well the recitation, bowing, and prostration in them, will have the like of the reward of Laylat al-Qadr."],[4865,"Whoever bows four rak'ahs after maghrib is like one who follows up one military expedition with another."],[4867,"Tahajjud is only after sleep."],[4871,"Whatever is after the 'Isha' prayer is nash'ah (night rising)."],[4872,"Whoever prays two rak'ahs before dawn is among those who seek forgiveness in the early morning hours."],[4873,"The virtue of the night prayer over the day prayer is like the virtue of secret charity over open charity."],[4874,"The virtue of the night prayer over the day prayer is like the virtue of the obligatory prayer over the voluntary prayer."],[4875,"He spent a night with Salman to observe his diligence in worship. He said: He got up to pray in the last part of the night, but it seemed he did not see what he had expected. He mentioned that to him, and Salman said: \"Guard these five prayers, for they are expiations for these wounds as long as the fatal blow is not struck. When people pray the 'Isha' prayer, they depart into three stations: among them is one who has a burden and no reward, among them is one who has a reward and no burden, and among them is one who has neither reward nor burden. As for the one who has a burden and no reward, he is a man who takes advantage of the darkness of the night and people's heedlessness and plunges headlong into sins—that is against him and not for him. As for the one who has a reward and no burden, he is a man who takes advantage of the darkness of the night and people's heedlessness and rises to pray—that is for him and not against him. As for the one who has neither reward nor burden, he is a man who prays and then sleeps—that is neither for him nor against him. Beware of extremism, and adhere to moderation and consistency.\""],[4876,"When a man rises during the night and wakes his wife, and they pray two rak'ahs, they are recorded that night among the men and women who remember Allah much."],[4877,"When one of you rises during the night, let him wake his family; and if she does not wake, let him sprinkle water on her face."],[4878,"Indeed, the most beloved of My servants to Me are those who love one another for the sake of the religion, who frequent My mosques, and who seek forgiveness in the early morning hours. Those are the ones whom, when I mention My creation with punishment, I remember them, and I turn My punishment away from My creation."],[4879,"Regarding His saying: 'Protect yourselves'—he said: \"Teach yourselves good.\""],[4880,"Accustom yourselves to good, for good is only through habit."],[4881,"Umar used to pray during the night as much as Allah willed, until when it was the last part of the night, he would wake his family, saying: 'The prayer, the prayer,' and he would recite this verse: 'And enjoin prayer upon your family and be steadfast therein. We ask you not for provision; We provide for you, and the outcome is for the righteous.'"],[4882,"The Prophet ﷺ, when some hardship in provision came upon him, would command his household to pray, then he would recite this verse: “And command your family to pray and be steadfast in it.”"],[4883,"When a man wakes up at night and remembers God, then gets up, performs ablution, prays, and supplicates to God, He answers him."],[4884,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray until his feet became cracked. It was said to him, “Has God not forgiven you your past and future sins?” He said, “Should I not be a grateful servant?”"],[4885,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray until his feet became swollen. They said, “O Messenger of God, you do this when your feet have become swollen and God Almighty has forgiven you your past and future sins?” He said, “Should I not be a grateful servant?”"],[4886,"Whoever sleeps through his portion” — or he said, “through his part of the night — and recites it between the dawn prayer and the noon prayer, it is as if he recited it at night.”"],[4887,"A man saw Umar b. al-Khattab praying at a time of day when he did not usually pray. He said to him: “I missed part of the night,” and God has said: “And it is He who made the night and day to alternate for one who desires to remember or desires to be thankful.”"],[4888,"He used to prefer, if he missed the four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, to pray those four rakʿahs after Ẓuhr."],[4889,"The Messenger of God ﷺ, when he had not prayed anything at night, would pray twelve rak’ahs during the day."],[4890,"If a man misses something of the night and does not pray it, there is no harm in lengthening the two rak’ahs of dawn."],[4891,"I asked Ata: “Do you dislike praying when dawn has spread over the mountaintops except the two rak’ahs of dawn?” He said: “Yes.”"],[4892,"I said: I came to the mosque after dawn, and began praying repeatedly. Ibn Umar said: “What is this?” I said: “I did not pray last night.” Ibn Umar said: “Do you want to inform me now? It is only two rak’ahs.”"],[4893,"He saw a man repeating the bowing after dawn broke and forbade him. The man said: “O Abu Muhammad, will God punish me for praying?” He said: “No, but He will punish you for opposing the Sunnah.”"],[4896,"Ibn 'Abbas could not see, and someone would look for his sake. When dawn broke, he would pray two rak'ahs and then sit."],[4897,"Mujahid said to Tawus: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, I saw Ibn 'Abbas, after he had lost his sight, asking his servant about the dawn. When the servant told him that it had broken, he prayed two rak'ahs and then sat."],[4899,"Pray after the break of dawn as much as you wish."],[4900,"I saw Tawus enter the mosque of Mina after dawn had broken and pray eight rak'ahs. I asked him about that, and he said: 'A prayer from the night that I used to pray, but I slept through it.'"],[4903,"They are the two dawns. As for the dawn that spreads vertically in the sky, it is nothing, and it does not make anything forbidden. Rather, the dawn that spreads over the mountain tops is the one that makes things forbidden. 'Ata' said: As for when it extends vertically into the sky—its vertical extension being that it goes upward in the sky—then no drink is forbidden for fasting, no prayer is affected, and no Hajj is missed. But when it spreads over the mountain tops, drinking is forbidden for the fast and the Hajj is missed. 'Umar said: The dawn that is like the tail of the wolf—meaning that vertical one in the sky."],[4904,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if I come to the mosque when the dawn has spread—should I lengthen the two rak'ahs or shorten them? He said: 'Lengthen them if you wish, as long as the imam has not come out.'"],[4905,"The two rak'ahs before the Fajr prayer were made light."],[4906,"The Witr is part of the night, and it is recommended that it be in the last part of the night. It is recommended to start the two rak'ahs with the takbir at dawn, and they are part of the daytime prayer."],[4907,"When dawn broke, the Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray two brief rak'ahs, then lie down on his right side."],[4908,"When dawn broke, the Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray two brief rak'ahs, then lie down on his right side."],[4909,"When dawn broke, he used to pray two brief rak'ahs."],[4911,"The muezzin came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he prayed two brief rak'ahs, then went out and prayed the morning prayer."],[4912,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to make them brief."],[4914,"I said to 'Ata': \"Are the two rak'ahs of Duha, or the Witr, or any voluntary prayer before or after the obligatory prayers obligatory?\" He said: \"No.\""],[4915,"I never saw the Messenger of God ﷺ hasten to anything more than he hastened to the two rak'ahs before the morning prayer, nor to any booty he sought."],[4916,"The two rak'ahs of Fajr are more beloved to me than the world and all that it contains."],[4917,"The two rak'ahs of Fajr are more beloved to me than the world and all that it contains. He said: And 'Umar b. al-Khattab said: They are more beloved to me than red camels."],[4918,"The two rak'ahs of Fajr escaped 'Abd Allah b. Abi Rabi'ah, so he freed a slave."],[4919,"Ibn 'Umar said to Humran: \"O Humran, fear God, and do not die while you owe a debt, for it will be taken from your good deeds, and there will be no dinar or dirham there. And do not disown your child, lest you disgrace him and God disgraces you through him on the Day of Resurrection. And hold fast to the two rak'ahs of Fajr, for in them lies the desire of all time.\""],[4920,"If you do not make up the two rak'ahs of Fajr, there is nothing upon you—he said: if they pass you by."],[4921,"Whoever prays the two rak'ahs of Fajr and prays the morning prayer in congregation, his prayer that day is recorded among the prayer of the oft-returning, and he is recorded that day among the delegation of the God-fearing."],[4922,"If not for the fact that a man prayed two rak'ahs before the morning prayer and then died, he would have prayed the morning prayer."],[4923,"He used to perform witr at the adhan and pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr at the iqamah."],[4924,"The two rak'ahs of Fajr are more beloved to me than the world and all that it contains."],[4925,"They used to prefer to pray after Maghrib with “Say: O disbelievers” and “Say: He is Allah, the One,” and after Isha in two rak’ahs with the last part of Surat al-Baqarah “The Messenger believes” and with “Say: He is Allah, the One,” and before Fajr with “Say: O disbelievers” and “Say: He is Allah, the One.”"],[4926,"The Messenger of God ﷺ recited quietly in the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and in them he recited: \"Say, O disbelievers\" and \"Say, He is God, the One.\""],[4927,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ more than twenty-five or he said: more than twenty times—Abu Bakr was uncertain—reciting in the two rak'ahs of Fajr: \"Say, O disbelievers\" and \"Say, He is God, the One.\""],[4929,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and I would say: Did he recite the Opening of the Book or not?"],[4930,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to stand for the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and I would say: Did he recite in them the Mother of the Qur'an or not, because of how light he made them?"],[4931,"Talking during the approach of dawn is disliked. I said: From among all the prayers? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Why? He said: \"Do you not hear Him say: 'And the Qur'an at dawn. Indeed, the Qur'an at dawn is ever witnessed' because it is witnessed and attended?\" I said: Then is one informed before dawn? And he disliked that as well."],[4932,"Ibn Mas'ud came out upon a people who were conversing and forbade them from talking, saying: \"You have only come for prayer; either pray, or be silent.\""],[4933,"Ibn Mas'ud passed by two men talking after the break of dawn, and he said: \"O you two, either pray, or be silent.\""],[4934,"It was hard for Abdullah b. Mas'ud to speak after the break of dawn except with the remembrance of God."],[4935,"Abdullah came at dawn while they were leaning, their backs toward the qiblah, and he said: \"Move back from the qiblah; do not come between the angels and the qiblah, for it is the prayer of the angels.\""],[4936,"Ibn Mas'ud entered the mosque before the Fajr prayer and saw a people who had leaned their backs toward the qiblah and faced the people. He said: \"Do not come between the angels and their prayer, for it is the prayer of the angels.\""],[4940,"When dawn breaks, let them be silent, even if they are riding, and if they have not prayed the two rak'ahs, let them be silent."],[4941,"I said to Ata': I hear them mention two rak'ahs before Zuhr and after it, and after Maghrib two rak'ahs and after Isha'. He said: It has reached me that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray after the last Isha' thirteen rak'ahs, among them two rak'ahs before Subh."],[4942,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \"How many prayers are there?\" He said: \"Five,\" and the Prophet ﷺ named them. Then he said: \"And Ramadan.\" The questioner said: \"I will never add anything to them.\" Then he turned away, and they laughed at him. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"If he is truthful, he will enter Paradise.\""],[4943,"I said to 'Ata': \"Is any part of voluntary prayer obligatory?\" He said: \"No.\""],[4944,"We said to him: \"Tell us about the voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" He said: \"And who can bear it?\" We said to him: \"Tell us, and we will bear of it what we can.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would wait, and when the sun rose and appeared, its height from the east being like its height at 'Asr, he would pray two rak'ahs, separating them with a greeting upon the angels brought near, the prophets, and those who followed him among the believers and Muslims. Then he would wait until the forenoon was high, and its height from the east being like its height at Zuhr, he would pray four rak'ahs, separating them with the greeting as he did in the first ones. Then when the sun declined, he would stand and pray four rak'ahs, separating them with a greeting upon the angels brought near, the prophets, and those who followed him among the believers and Muslims. Then he would pray two rak'ahs after Zuhr like that, then he would pray four rak'ahs before 'Asr, separating them with the like of that.\""],[4945,"\"And he would pray four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, and four before 'Asr, so these are sixteen rak'ahs.\""],[4946,"The Prophet ﷺ \"would pray two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, two after Jumu'ah, two after Maghrib, and two after 'Isha'.\" And it was mentioned to me - Ibn 'Umar was the one who said it - that the Prophet ﷺ would pray two rak'ahs before Subh, but I did not see it."],[4947,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two rak'ahs before Zuhr, two after Zuhr, two after Maghrib, two after 'Isha', and two after Jumu'ah. As for Jumu'ah, Maghrib, and 'Isha', they were in his house."],[4948,"He would pray two rak'ahs after Maghrib in his house, and he would not pray anything after Jumu'ah until he entered his house, then he would pray two rak'ahs in it."],[4949,"\"I memorized from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ten rak'ahs that he would pray by night and day: two rak'ahs before Zuhr, two after it, two after Maghrib, and two after the last 'Isha'. And Hafsah told me that he would pray two rak'ahs after Subh.\""],[4950,"Uthman was killed while no one prayed it, and people did not introduce anything more beloved to me than it."],[4951,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, two after Maghrib, and two after 'Isha'.\" He said: \"And Hafsah said: 'And he would pray two rak'ahs when the call was given, and no one would enter upon him at that time.'\""],[4952,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray four rak'ahs before Zuhr. It was said to him: \"You pray a prayer that you persist in.\" He said: \"The gates of heaven are opened when the sun declines, and they are not closed until the Zuhr prayer is performed, so I love that something good ascend for me to heaven.\""],[4953,"The voluntary prayer of 'Abdullah, from which he did not omit, was: four rak'ahs before Zuhr, two after it, two after Maghrib, two after 'Isha', and two before the morning prayer."],[4955,"I went on eighteen military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ abandon the two rak'ahs when the sun declines, whether at home or on a journey."],[4956,"When the shadows lengthen and the winds blow, mention your needs, for it is the hour of the oft-returning."],[4957,"Umar used to dislike praying immediately after an obligatory prayer alone with the like of it."],[4958,"Do not pray immediately after every obligatory prayer the like of it."],[4960,"Ali used to pray two rak'ahs after Isha."],[4962,"I memorized from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he used to pray two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, two after Jumu'ah, two after Maghrib, and two after Isha. He said: And Hafsah told me that when dawn broke, he prayed two brief rak'ahs."],[4963,"When the sun declined, he would stand and pray four rak'ahs, reciting in them two surahs from the Mi'in (the long surahs); then when the muezzins called the adhan together, he would tie his garment tightly and go out to the prayer."],[4965,"No prayer after the obligatory prayer is more virtuous than four rak'ahs before Zuhr."],[4966,"Whoever prays four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, Allah forbids the Fire upon him."],[4967,"The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ were not more persistent in anything than in four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two rak'ahs before Fajr."],[4968,"They used to count four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two rak'ahs after it as part of the Sunnah. He said: And they used to pray two rak'ahs before 'Asr but did not count them as part of the Sunnah, and two rak'ahs after Maghrib, and two rak'ahs after 'Isha, and two rak'ahs before Fajr."],[4969,"He used to prefer, if he missed the four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, to pray those four rakʿahs after Ẓuhr."],[4970,"He saw some people praying after the sun had risen, and he said: 'If these people had caught the early predecessors, they would have learned that a prayer other than this one is better than it: the prayer of the oft-returning penitents when the young camels feel the heat of the sun.'"],[4971,"Whoever prays two rak'ahs after Maghrib before speaking, they are recorded — or raised — in 'Illiyyin."],[4972,"They used to prefer to pray after Maghrib with “Say: O disbelievers” and “Say: He is Allah, the One,” and after Isha in two rak’ahs with the last part of Surat al-Baqarah “The Messenger believes” and with “Say: He is Allah, the One,” and before Fajr with “Say: O disbelievers” and “Say: He is Allah, the One.”"],[4973,"A man's voluntary prayer in his house exceeds his voluntary prayer in the presence of people as the merit of the congregational prayer over a man's prayer alone."],[4975,"When one of you prays a prayer in the mosque, let him allocate a portion of his prayer for his house. Indeed, Allah places in his house good from his prayer."],[4976,"We used to sit in the mosque after the qiyām of ʿAbd Allāh b. Masʿūd, helping the people to be firm in the recitation. When we wanted to return, we prayed two rakʿahs. This reached ʿAbd Allāh, and he said: 'You are burdening the people with what God has not burdened them with. They see you praying, and they will think that it is obligatory upon them. If you must do it, then do it in the houses.'"],[4977,"He saw a group of people at the grave and forbade them, and said: The Prophet ﷺ said: Do not take my house as a place of festival, and do not make your houses graves, and send prayers upon me wherever you are, for your prayers reach me."],[4978,"I never saw al-Rabi' b. Khuthaym performing voluntary prayer in the mosque of the tribe at all."],[4979,"I never saw al-Rabi' b. Khuthaym performing voluntary prayer in the mosque of the tribe at all."],[4980,"I saw the best of the companions of 'Ali: Zadhan, Maysarah, and Abu al-Bakhtari. They preferred the mosque over their families during the month of Ramadan (i.e., they devoted themselves to worship in the mosque rather than staying with their families)."],[4981,"I asked Abu Umamah about the supererogatory prayer. He said: It was a supererogatory act for the Prophet ﷺ, but for you it is a virtue."],[4982,"A man said to the Prophet ﷺ: O Messenger of God, which of the believers is most excellent in faith? He said: The best of them in character. He said: Then which faith is most excellent? He said: Patience and forbearance. He said: Then which emigration (hijrah) is most excellent? He said: Whoever abandons what God has forbidden. He said: Then which jihad is most excellent? He said: Whoever's horse is hamstrung and his blood is shed. He said: Then which charity is most excellent? He said: The effort of the one with little. He said: Then which prayers are most excellent? He said: Lengthy qunut (standing in supplication)."],[4983,"It was said: Which jihad is most excellent? He said: Whoever's horse is hamstrung and his blood is shed. It was said: Then which prayers are most excellent? He said: Lengthy qunut. It was said: Then which charity is most excellent? He said: The effort of the one with little. It was said: Then which emigration is most excellent? He said: Whoever abandons what God and His Messenger have forbidden. It was said: Then which of the people is most judicious? He said: The one who judges for people as he judges for himself. It was said: Then which of the people is most knowledgeable? He said: The one who gathers the knowledge of people to his own knowledge."],[4984,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked: Which prayer is most excellent? He said: Lengthy qunut."],[4985,"There is no servant who prostrates to God a single prostration except that God raises him by it a degree and removes from him by it a sin."],[4986,"I entered the Sanctuary of Jerusalem and found a man there who prostrated a great deal. I felt some resentment at that. When he finished, I said, 'Do you know whether you finished on an even number or an odd number?' He said, 'Even if I do not know, Allah knows.' Then he said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me,' then he wept, then said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me,' then he wept, then said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me,' then he wept, then said, 'My beloved Abu al-Qasim ﷺ informed me that no servant prostrates to Allah a single prostration except that Allah raises him a degree by it, removes a sin from him by it, and writes a good deed for him by it.' He said, 'I said, 'Tell me who you are, may Allah have mercy on you.' He said, 'Abu Dharr, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' He said, 'Then my soul felt humbled.'"],[4987,"When Satan sees the son of Adam prostrating, he says: O woe to him! Woe to Satan! God commanded the son of Adam to prostrate and for him is Paradise, and he obeyed. And He commanded me to prostrate, and I disobeyed, so for me is the Fire."],[4988,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if I increase beyond three days? He said: Then it is better."],[4989,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined upon me three things that I will never abandon, whether at home or travelling: sleeping on witr, fasting three days of every month, and the two rak'ahs of duha. He said: Then Hasan became confused after that and put in place of the two rak'ahs of duha the ghusl of Friday."],[4991,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray the Duha prayer: two, four, six, and eight (rakʿahs)."],[4993,"He used to pray the Duha prayer, and it was said: What is this? He said: \"A prayer of desire and fear.\""],[4994,"Whoever prays twelve rakʿahs in a day, God builds for him a house in Paradise, and whoever builds a mosque, God builds for him something more spacious than it."],[4995,"O son of Adam, are you unable to pray four rakʿahs at the beginning of the day? I will suffice you at its end."],[4996,"She entered upon the Messenger of God ﷺ on the day of the conquest while he was in a tent of his, and she found that he had bathed with water that was in a large bowl, and I could see in it traces of dough, and I saw him praying the Duha prayer."],[4997,"The Prophet ﷺ was screened, and he bathed in the forenoon, then prayed eight rakʿahs. It was not known whether its standing was longer, or its bowing, or its prostration."],[4998,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed eight rakʿahs in the forenoon; its standing, bowing, and prostration were nearly equal."],[4999,"The Messenger of God ﷺ camped on the day of the conquest at the upper part of Makkah, and I went to him. Abu Dharr came to him with a large bowl containing water. She said: I could see in it traces of dough. He said: \"Abu Dharr screened him, and he bathed. Then the Prophet ﷺ screened Abu Dharr, and he bathed. Then he prayed eight rakʿahs, and that was in the forenoon.\""],[5000,"I went to the Prophet ﷺ in the year of the conquest and found him bathing while his daughter Fatimah was screening him with a garment. I greeted him, and that was in the forenoon. He said: \"Who is this?\" I said: Umm Haniʾ bt. Abi Talib. He said: \"Welcome to Umm Haniʾ.\" When he finished his bath, he prayed eight rakʿahs wrapped in a single garment, then left. I said: O Messenger of God, the son of my mother claims he will kill a man whom I have granted protection, so-and-so son of Umayyah. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"We grant protection to whom Umm Haniʾ grants protection.\""],[5001,"I asked him about the Duha prayer, and he said: The companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray in the midday heat—or he said: \"at the midday heat—and the Messenger of God ﷺ never prayed the Duha prayer at all except on the day of the conquest of Makkah and when he returned from a journey.\""],[5002,"The Prophet ﷺ arrived in Madinah from the expedition of Tabuk at forenoon and prayed two rak'ahs in the mosque. He said: When he came from a journey, he used to do that."],[5003,"The Prophet ﷺ would not arrive from a journey except during the day, at forenoon. When he arrived, he would begin with the mosque and pray two rak'ahs in it, then sit in it."],[5004,"When this prayer that people introduced was mentioned to Ali b. Abi Talib, he would say: Pray as much as you are able, for God does not punish for prayer."],[5005,"A'ishah used to pray eight rak'ahs of Duha and say: If my father were resurrected for me, I would not abandon them."],[5006,"The Messenger of God ﷺ did not use to pray the Duha prayer as a supererogatory devotion. He said: A'ishah used to pray it, and she used to say: The Messenger of God ﷺ would leave a deed out of fear that people would adopt it as a practice and it would become obligatory on them, and he loved what was light upon people."],[5007,"Uthman was killed while no one prayed it, and people did not introduce anything more beloved to me than it."],[5008,"Uthman was struck down while no one prayed it, and indeed it is among the most beloved of what people introduced to me."],[5009,"There remained in my soul something about the Duha prayer until I recited: 'We subjected the mountains with him to glorify in the evening and at sunrise.'"],[5010,"The Duha prayer is in the Qur'an, but no one plunges into it except one who plunges deep. Then he recited: 'They glorify in the evening and at sunrise.' Tawus said: By God, Ibn Abbas did not pray it until he died, except when he circumambulated the House."],[5011,"The first to pray it were the Bedouins. When one of them sold his goods, he would come to the mosque and say the takbir and prostrate, except that Tawus says: 'God is greater, God is greater, God is greater,' then the Bedouin prostrates."],[5012,"The Duha prayer is when the shadows cease."],[5013,"Abd Allah b. Mas'ud would not pray the Duha prayer, and he would pray between Zuhr and Asr along with a long portion of the night."],[5014,"I frequented Abd Allah b. Mas'ud for a year and never saw him pray the Duha prayer or fast any day outside Ramadan. He said: While we were with him one night, someone came and said to him: This is the messenger of al-Walid. Abd Allah said: \"Extinguish the lamp.\" Then he entered and said to him: The amir says to you: Abandon these words that you say. He said: \"And what are they?\" He said: \"These words?\" He kept repeating them. He said: Your saying: Every innovation is a misguidance. He said: \"I will never abandon them.\" He said: Then he says to you: Then leave. He said: \"Then I am leaving.\" So he left for Madinah."],[5015,"I never saw him pray it."],[5016,"I said to Abd Allah b. Umar: Why do I not see you pray the Duha prayer? He said: I did not see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray it."],[5017,"Whoever prays the Duha prayer eight rak'ahs will be written among the repentant (awwabin): {For He is ever forgiving to the repentant}."],[5018,"I have not prayed the Duha prayer since I embraced Islam."],[5019,"He said regarding a man who prays following the imam's prayer: If there is a river, a road, or a wall between them, he should not follow him."],[5020,"I asked Amir al-Sha'bi about a woman who prays following the imam's prayer while there is a road between them. He said: \"She may not do that.\""],[5021,"He said regarding a man who prays following the imam's prayer while there is a wall between them: \"It is acceptable, as long as there is no road or women between them.\""],[5022,"She would pray following the imam's prayer in her house while he was in the mosque."],[5023,"A woman may pray following the imam's prayer even if there is a road or a wall between them, as long as she hears the takbir; there is no harm in that."],[5024,"I came once with my father and found the mosque full, so we prayed following the imam's prayer in a house near the mosque with a road between them."],[5025,"We prayed in the house of Humayd b. 'Abd al-Rahman."],[5026,"He saw Anas b. Malik pray Jumu'ah in the house of Humayd b. 'Abd al-Rahman following the prayer of al-Walid b. 'Abd al-Malik, with a road between them."],[5027,"He saw Abu Hurayrah praying on the roof of the mosque following the imam's prayer while he was below him."],[5028,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with the people to pray for rain. He led them in two rak'ahs, reciting aloud in them, turned his cloak inside out, supplicated, and faced the qiblah."],[5029,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed for rain, faced the qiblah, and turned his cloak inside out."],[5030,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked for rain and prayed two rak'ahs at the prayer place."],[5031,"Indeed, Allah laughs at you when you despair of the rain being near. A man from Bahilah said, 'O Messenger of Allah, does our Lord indeed laugh?' He said, 'Yes.' The man said, 'By Allah, we will not be deprived of good from a Lord who laughs.'"],[5032,"A commander among the commanders sent me to Ibn 'Abbas to ask him about the rain prayer. Ibn 'Abbas said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out humble, beseeching, and submissive, and he delivered a sermon, but not like this sermon of yours. Then he supplicated and prayed two rak'ahs as he prayed on the Eid.'"],[5033,"I think he used to say the takbir in the Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, and rain prayers: seven in the first rak'ah and five in the last."],[5034,"'Ali used to say the takbir in the Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, and rain prayers: seven in the first and five in the last, and he prayed before the sermon and recited aloud. He said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman used to do that.'"],[5035,"The sunnah of the rain prayer is like the sunnah of Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha in the takbir."],[5036,"When the Prophet ﷺ prayed for rain, he turned his cloak inside out, putting its right side on his left and its left side on his right, then faced the qiblah, then descended and prayed two rak'ahs."],[5037,"He attended Umar b. Abd al-Aziz when he was governor of Madinah: he sought rain from the pulpit, then descended and prayed."],[5038,"Ibn al-Zubayr went out to seek rain with the people, and he delivered a sermon, then prayed without any adhan or iqama. He said: Among the people that day were al-Bara' b. Azib and Zayd b. Arqam."],[5039,"He used to recite in the two rak'as of the rain prayer: 'By the sun and its morning brightness' and 'By the night when it covers.'"],[5040,"Sulayman b. Musa asked Ata': Is there a prayer in the rain-seeking? He did not distinguish for him anything from those who had passed. Sulayman said: It was mentioned to us that Umar b. al-Khattab went out with the people to the prayer place, supplicated and sought forgiveness, then descended and returned without praying."],[5041,"Umar b. al-Khattab went out to seek rain with the people, and he did no more than seek forgiveness until he returned. They said: O Commander of the Faithful, we did not see you seek rain. He said: I sought rain by the means of heaven through which rain is brought down: 'And say: Seek forgiveness of your Lord; indeed He is ever Forgiving. He will send the heaven upon you with abundant rain, and extend you with wealth and sons.' 'Seek forgiveness of your Lord; then turn to Him. He will send the heaven upon you with abundant rain and add strength to your strength.'"],[5042,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Maymun b. Mihran: I have written to the people of the garrison cities that they should go out on such-and-such a day of such-and-such a month to seek rain. Whoever is able to fast and give charity, let him do so, for God says: 'He has succeeded who purifies himself, and remembers the name of his Lord and prays.' And say as your father said: 'Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will surely be among the losers.' And say as Nuh said: 'And unless You forgive me and have mercy on me, I will be among the losers.' And say as Musa said: 'Indeed I have wronged myself, so forgive me, and He forgave him. Indeed He is the Forgiving, the Merciful.' And say as Yunus ﷺ said: 'There is no god but You; glory be to You. Indeed I was among the wrongdoers.'"],[5043,"He said about the rain-seeking: 'When you go out, praise God and extol Him as He deserves, and send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and seek forgiveness; for the rain-seeking is seeking forgiveness.' He said: And Ali said: The Prophet ﷺ turned his cloak while standing when he wanted to supplicate."],[5044,"A man, while he was watering a crop, saw a cloud with a voice in it: 'Water the land of so-and-so.' He followed the voice until he reached the land that was named, and he asked its owner: What is your practice regarding it? He said: I restore a third of it, give a third in charity, and withhold a third for my family."],[5045,"Ibn Mas'ud used to send him to his land and ordered him to do likewise with it."],[5046,"O God, help me against Mudar with a year of drought. A man from Mudar came to him and said: O Prophet of God, by God, no camel of ours moves and no shepherd of ours takes provision. He repeated his words to him, but he turned away. Then he remained as long as God willed, then he called the Mudari and said: What did you say? He repeated it to him, and the Prophet ﷺ said: 'O God, I called upon You and You answered me, and I asked You and You gave me. O God, give us abundant rain, wholesome, productive, covering, swift not slow, beneficial not harmful.' No sooner was it evening than the sky was clothed with clouds and it rained. No one came from any direction without reporting rain. We said to him: What does 'yakhtir' mean? He said: It bellows."],[5047,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, Mudar has perished; seek rain from God for them, or he said: supplicate for them. The Prophet ﷺ then said: 'O God, give us rain, wholesome, pleasant, productive, covering, swift not slow, beneficial not harmful.' He said: They did not remain but a week before the people were revived."],[5048,"A man stood up to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, you supplicated against Mudar with a year of drought; no camel of theirs grunts and no child of theirs cries. He said: The Prophet ﷺ stood on the pulpit and said: 'O God, give us abundant rain, productive, blessed, wholesome, beneficial, covering, swift not slow.' He said: That day had not passed before they were rained upon, or seven days had not passed before the people were satisfied with vegetation."],[5049,"The Messenger of God ﷺ once prayed for rain, and the people were rained upon for three days or as long as God willed. Then it did not clear away from them. He said: So it was said, 'O Messenger of God, the walls have collapsed, the riders have been cut off, and we fear drowning.' He said: Then he supplicated and said, 'O God, around us and not upon us.' He said: Then I saw the clouds split apart from Madinah until it became like a ring, while all around it was dark, and I know that it was raining."],[5050,"I was present with the Prophet ﷺ when a man came to him and complained of drought while he was on the pulpit, and he prayed for rain—he did not mention his words. The sky became overcast with clouds, and it rained until the following Friday. It was said to him, 'O Messenger of God, we are drowning, the livestock have perished, and the traveler cannot go out.' He smiled, then said, 'O God, around us and not upon us.' He said: I saw him when the clouds were clearing away from Madinah and dispersing, until I was in an open space."],[5051,"The Prophet of God ﷺ used to pray for rain, saying, 'O God, give water to Your servants and Your animals, spread Your mercy, and revive Your dead land.'"],[5052,"Umar prayed for rain at the prayer place and said to al-Abbas, 'Stand and pray for rain.' Al-Abbas stood and said, 'O God, You have clouds, and You have water, so spread the clouds, then send down water into them, then send it down upon us. Strengthen thereby the roots, make the crops grow tall, and make the udders flow with milk. O God, accept our intercession for ourselves and our families. O God, we have interceded with You on behalf of those who cannot speak, for our beasts and cattle. O God, give us a gentle, ample, covering, general, life-giving rain. O God, we desire none but You alone, with no partner. O God, we complain to You of the hunger of every hungry one, the debt of every debtor, the starvation of every starving one, the nakedness of every naked one, and the fear of every fearful one'—in his supplication."],[5053,"The people were afflicted with a year of drought, and there was a man in the desert. He went out and prayed two rak'ahs with his companions and prayed for rain, then slept. In his sleep he saw that the Messenger of God ﷺ came to him and said, 'Convey greetings to Umar and tell him that God has answered you—Umar had also gone out and prayed for rain—and command him to fulfill the covenant and tighten the knot.' He said: The man set out until he came to Umar and said, 'Seek permission for the messenger of the Messenger of God ﷺ.' He said: Umar heard him and said, 'Who is this fabricating lies against the Messenger of God ﷺ?' The man said, 'Do not rush me, O Commander of the Faithful,' and he told him the news, and Umar wept."],[5054,"The Messenger of God ﷺ supplicated for a people that they be given rain, but they were not rained upon. He said, 'I supplicated for you while there was something in my heart against you, so you were not given rain. But now you will be given rain.' He supplicated for them, and they were rained upon."],[5055,"Jesus son of Mary went out to pray for rain, and he went out with the people. Then he said to them, 'Whoever among you has committed a sin, let him turn back.' He said: The people began to turn back until none remained except a one-eyed man. Jesus said to him, 'Supplicate and I will say amīn.' He said: He supplicated, and Jesus said amīn, and God gave them water."],[5056,"When one of you sees lightning or rain, he should not point at it, but should describe it or mention its qualities."],[5057,"He forbade pointing at the rain."],[5058,"The Prophet ﷺ said one day, 'Where is Tibnayn?' They said, 'A valley among the valleys of Yemen.' He said, 'This is a cloud commanded to go to Tibnayn. How does its owner deal with it there?' They said, 'He divides its fruit into three: a third for him and his family, a third for his charity, and a third he reinvests in it.' He said, 'All of that is in the way of God.'"],[5059,"Silence is in three places: during the Friday prayer, during the prayer for rain, and during the two Eids."],[5060,"Solomon son of David went out with his companions to pray for rain, and he saw an ant standing, raising one of its legs, praying for rain. He said to his companions, 'Return, for you have been given water. This ant prayed for rain and it was answered.'"],[5061,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood and led the people in prayer, lengthening the recitation. Then he bowed and lengthened the bowing. Then he raised his head and lengthened the recitation, but it was shorter than his first recitation. Then he bowed and lengthened the bowing, but it was shorter than his first bowing. Then he raised his head and prostrated twice. Then he stood and did the same in the second rak'ah. Then he turned away and said: 'The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone or for his life, but they are two signs among the signs of God. So when you see that, hasten to prayer.'"],[5062,"So when you see that, give charity and pray."],[5063,"The Prophet ﷺ stood and led them in prayer. He stood, then bowed. Then he raised his head and stood, but for less than the first standing. Then he bowed. Then he raised his head and prostrated. Then he stood and did the same in the second rak'ah, except that its standing and bowing were less than the first in every rak'ah of the two rak'ahs."],[5064,"A Jewish woman came to me and said: 'May God protect you from the punishment of the grave.' She said: Then the Messenger of God ﷺ entered, and I said: 'O Messenger of God, will we be punished in our graves?' He said: 'The Jews lie.' Then one day the Messenger of God ﷺ mounted a riding animal, and the sun eclipsed. She said: I went out with some women and we were among the rooms when the Prophet ﷺ came from his mount and went to his prayer place. He stood for a long time, making it long. Then he bowed for a long time, making his bowing long. Then he raised his head and stood for a long time, but shorter than his first standing. Then he bowed for a long time, but shorter than his first bowing. Then he raised his head and prostrated for a long time. Then he stood for a long time, but shorter than his first standing, and did as he had done in the first rak'ah. Then he sat. She said: Then I heard him seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave."],[5065,"The sun eclipsed, and the Messenger of God ﷺ prayed with the people. He stood for a long time, about the length of Surah al-Baqarah. Then he bowed for a long time. Then he raised his head and stood for a long time, but shorter than the first standing. Then he bowed, but shorter than the first bowing. Then he prostrated. Then he stood and did the same in the second rak'ah, but his standing in it was shorter than the first standing, and his bowing and prostration were shorter than what he had done in the first rak'ah. Then he turned away, and the sun had become clear. Then he said: 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of God Almighty. They do not eclipse for the death of anyone or for his life. So when you see that, remember God.' They said: 'O Messenger of God, we saw you reach for something from this place of yours, then we saw you draw back.' He said: 'I was shown Paradise—or I saw Paradise—and I reached for a bunch of grapes from it. If I had taken it, you would have eaten from it as long as the world remains. And I saw the Fire, and I have never seen a sight like today. I saw that most of its inhabitants were women.' It was said: 'Why, O Messenger of God?' He said: 'Because of their ingratitude.' It was said: 'Do they disbelieve in God?' He said: 'They are ungrateful to their husbands and ungrateful for kindness. If you were to be kind to one of them for a lifetime, and then she saw something from you, she would say: I have never seen any good from you.'"],[5066,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He stood with the people for a very long time, standing and then bowing, standing and then bowing, standing and then bowing. He bowed in two rak'ahs, with three bowings in each rak'ah. He bowed the third time and then prostrated. He did not depart until the sun became clear, and until some men that day fainted, such that buckets of water were poured on them because of how long he stood with them. When he bowed he said: 'God is greatest,' and when he raised his head he said: 'God hears those who praise Him.' Then he stood and praised God and extolled Him. Then he said: 'The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone or for his life, but they are two signs among the signs of God by which He frightens you. So when they eclipse, hasten to the remembrance of God until it clears.' And it was added on the authority of Ata' in this sermon: 'But it may be that the righteous die in remote parts of the earth, and the jinn spread that, and so there is that darkness.'"],[5067,"The Messenger of God ﷺ went out on the day the sun eclipsed. He took a coat of mail and put it on until it was covered by his cloak. He stood with the people for a very long time, standing and then bowing. If someone had come after he bowed, he would not have known that he had bowed at all, nor would he have imagined that he had bowed, because of the length of the standing. She said: I began to look at the woman who was older than me and the woman who was weaker than me, standing, and I said: 'I have more right to endure the long standing than you.'"],[5068,"Ibn Abbas recited in the first rak'ah during the eclipse the Fatihah and al-Baqarah, and in the second rak'ah the Fatihah and Al Imran."],[5069,"He prayed during the earthquake in al-Basrah and lengthened the qunut, then bowed. Then he raised his head and lengthened the qunut, then bowed. Then he bowed, then prostrated. Then he prayed the second rak'ah in the same way. Thus his prayer became three bowings and four prostrations. And he said: 'This is the prayer of the signs.'"],[5070,"Hudhayfah prayed in al-Mada'in with his companions a prayer like the prayer of Ibn Abbas during the signs."],[5071,"He prayed during the earthquake in al-Basrah. They both agreed that he bowed six times in two rak'ahs, three in each rak'ah. But they differed: Asim said: 'He recited between every two bowings,' while Khalid said: 'He recited in the first of each rak'ah among them, then repeated after that.'"],[5072,"When he prayed with them, he said: 'This is the prayer of the signs.'"],[5073,"He recited in the first unit Surah al-Baqarah, and in the last one Al ʿImrān."],[5074,"Ibn ʿAbbās, when the sun eclipsed, prayed on the back of the Zamzam platform two units, with four bowings in each unit."],[5075,"He prayed for the eclipse of the sun. He recited, then bowed four times in each prostration, except that when he raised his head from bowing he recited, then prostrated, then stood, and in the second unit he did the same as in the first."],[5076,"He led the people in the mosque for the eclipse of the sun. He said: “He recited aloud. He stood and recited, then bowed, then stood and supplicated, then bowed four bowings in one prostration, supplicating in them after the bowing. Then in the second unit he did the same.” Sufyān said: “I heard them estimate ʿAlī's standing in recitation as the length of al-Rūm, or Yāsīn, or al-ʿAnkabūt.”"],[5077,"Prayer for the eclipse of the sun and the moon is two units, similar to our prayer."],[5078,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed with them on the day the sun eclipsed, the day his son Ibrāhīm died. He stood with the people, and it was said: he will not bow, but he bowed; and it was said: he will not raise, but he raised; and it was said: he will not prostrate, but he prostrated; and it was said: he will not raise, but he sat; and it was said: he will not prostrate, but he prostrated; and it was said: he will not raise; then he stood in the second unit and did the same, and the sun became clear."],[5079,"The sun eclipsed while al-Mughīra b. Shuʿba was over Kufa. “He stood and prayed with the people. I was where I could not hear, so I estimated the length of a sūra from the two hundred, then he bowed, then raised, then recited, then bowed. Then the sun became clear, so he bowed and prostrated, then stood in the second unit and recited a light recitation, then bowed and prostrated.”"],[5080,"A man said to ʿAṭāʾ: “Do you think if the moon eclipses, I should pray as the Prophet ﷺ prayed when the sun eclipsed?” He said: “Yes, unless there is a congregational prayer.”"],[5081,"The moon eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and they said, “The moon has been bewitched.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split” until “continuing.”"],[5082,"I asked al-Zuhrī about a sign occurring after ʿaṣr. He said: “Supplication, and there is no prayer after ʿaṣr.” I said: “From whom do you report this?” He said: “Thus they used to do.”"],[5083,"The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone, but when our Lord, Blessed and Exalted, manifests Himself to one of His creatures, it submits to Him."],[5084,"The Prophet ﷺ, whenever he bowed a bowing and raised his head, sent a man to see whether it had become clear."],[5085,"He used to say: From where did the people take the qunut? And he marveled, and said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ only performed qunut for some days, then he abandoned that.\""],[5086,"The Messenger of God ﷺ passed away, and Abu Bakr and Umar, while they did not perform qunut."],[5087,"Umar led us in prayer for a time and did not perform qunut."],[5088,"We prayed the dawn prayer behind Umar b. al-Khattab, and he did not perform qunut."],[5089,"Ibn Mas'ud did not perform qunut in the dawn prayer."],[5090,"Ibn Umar did not perform qunut in the morning prayer, nor in the witr either."],[5091,"I prayed the dawn prayer behind Umar, and he did not perform qunut in it."],[5092,"Ibn Umar did not perform qunut in the dawn prayer."],[5093,"Ibn Abbas prayed the morning prayer and did not perform qunut.\" And Ibn al-Mujalid said, from his father, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, they said: The Messenger of God ﷺ did not perform qunut in any of the prayers except when he fought a war, for he would perform qunut in all the prayers. Neither Abu Bakr nor Umar nor Uthman performed qunut until they died, and Ali did not perform qunut until he fought the people of Syria, then he would perform qunut in all the prayers. Muawiyah also performed qunut, and each of them would supplicate against the other.\""],[5094,"Ibn Abbas prayed the morning prayer and did not perform qunut.\" And Ibn al-Mujalid said, from his father, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, they said: The Messenger of God ﷺ did not perform qunut in any of the prayers except when he fought a war, for he would perform qunut in all the prayers. Neither Abu Bakr nor Umar nor Uthman performed qunut until they died, and Ali did not perform qunut until he fought the people of Syria, then he would perform qunut in all the prayers. Muawiyah also performed qunut, and each of them would supplicate against the other.\""],[5095,"I asked Ibn Umar about the qunut in the dawn prayer, and he said: \"I did not realize that anyone does it.\""],[5096,"I asked Salim b. Abd Allah: Did Umar b. al-Khattab perform qunut in the morning prayer? He said: \"No, it is only something the people introduced afterward.\""],[5097,"Umar did not perform qunut in the morning prayer."],[5098,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to perform the qunut with these words in the Morning Prayer and in the witr at night: O God, guide me among those You have guided, and grant me well-being among those You have granted well-being, and take me as a friend among those You have taken as friends, and bless me in what You have given, and protect me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed, You decree and none can decree against You, and none whom You befriend is humiliated. Blessed are You, our Lord, and exalted."],[5099,"I prayed behind Rabi' b. Khuthaym, and he performed the qunut before the rak'ah."],[5100,"That 'Umar b. al-Khattab prayed the Morning Prayer, and when he finished the recitation he performed the qunut, then he said the takbir when he bowed."],[5101,"That 'Ali said the takbir when he performed the qunut in the dawn prayer, then he said the takbir when he bowed."],[5102,"That he performed the qunut in the dawn prayer, and he said the takbir when he finished the recitation, then he said the takbir when he finished the qunut."],[5103,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed the qunut in the dawn prayer, and Abu Bakr and 'Umar after the ruku'. When 'Uthman's turn came, he performed the qunut before the ruku' so that the people could catch the rak'ah."],[5104,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed the qunut in the Morning Prayer after the ruku', supplicating against some clans of the Arabs. His qunut before that and after it was before the ruku'."],[5105,"The Messenger of God ﷺ did not cease performing the qunut in the dawn prayer until he departed this world."],[5106,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed the qunut in the dawn prayer after the ruku'."],[5107,"I said to him: How did you use to perform the qunut? He said: All of that, before the ruku' and after it."],[5108,"That Ibn Mas'ud used not to perform the qunut in the Morning Prayer."],[5109,"I prayed the Morning Prayer behind 'Umar b. al-Khattab, and he performed the qunut after the ruku'. He said: I heard him say: O God, we seek Your help, and we seek Your forgiveness, and we praise You and do not disbelieve in You. We believe in You and we renounce and forsake those who disobey You. O God, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; indeed, Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers. O God, punish the disbelievers, and cast terror into their hearts, and divide their unity, and send down upon them Your wrath and punishment. O God, punish the disbelievers, the People of the Book who turn away from Your path, and deny Your messengers, and fight Your allies. O God, forgive the believing men and believing women, and the Muslim men and Muslim women, and set right their relations, and unite their hearts, and place faith and wisdom in their hearts, and make them firm upon the religion of Your Prophet, and inspire them to fulfill the covenant You made with them, and grant them victory over Your enemy and their enemy, O God of truth, and make us among them."],[5110,"He narrated from ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb regarding the qunūt: He used to say: “O Allah, forgive the believing men and believing women, and the Muslim men and Muslim women, and unite their hearts, and set aright the matters between them, and grant them victory over Your enemy and their enemy. O Allah, curse the disbelievers among the People of the Book who deny Your messengers and fight Your allies. O Allah, sow discord among their word, and make their feet slip, and send down upon them Your punishment which You do not avert from the wrongdoing people. In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. O Allah, we seek Your help, and we seek Your forgiveness, and we praise You and do not disbelieve in You, and we renounce and forsake whoever disobeys You. In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. O Allah, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy, and we fear Your punishment. Indeed, Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers.”"],[5111,"He used to say: “O Allah, we seek Your help, and we seek Your forgiveness, and we praise You and do not disbelieve in You, and we renounce and forsake whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We fear Your punishment, and we hope for Your mercy. Indeed, Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers.”"],[5112,"ʿUmar used to perform qunūt in the morning prayer for the length of a hundred verses of the Qur’an."],[5113,"ʿUmar used to perform qunūt in the dawn prayer with two sūrahs."],[5114,"Ibn ʿAbbās led us in the morning prayer during his governorship over Baṣrah, and he performed qunūt before the bowing."],[5115,"ʿAlī used to perform qunūt in the morning prayer before the bowing, and in the witr before the bowing."],[5116,"The Prophet ﷺ used to perform qunūt in the dawn and sunset prayers."],[5117,"ʿAlī performed qunūt in the sunset prayer and supplicated against certain people and their followers, and he performed qunūt before the bowing."],[5118,"I saw al-Ḥasan meet Abū Rāfiʿ the goldsmith. He said: “I am between the two.” Al-Ḥasan said: “The qunūt is before the bowing.” Abū Rāfiʿ said: “No, after the bowing. We did it with ʿUmar.” Al-Ḥasan said: “How long?” He said: “Two months.” Abū Rāfiʿ said: “Rather, two years.” He said: And ʿAbd al-Karīm pointed with his finger, meaning in the morning prayer."],[5119,"ʿAlī used to perform qunūt with these two sūrahs in the dawn prayer, except that he put the latter one first and say: “O Allah, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy, and we fear Your punishment. Indeed, Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers. O Allah, we seek Your help, and we seek Your guidance, and we praise You with all good, and we thank You and do not disbelieve in You, and we believe in You, and we renounce and forsake whoever disobeys You.” Al-Ḥakam said: And Ṭāwūs informed me that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: ʿUmar performed qunūt before the rakʿah with these two sūrahs, except that he put first the one ʿAlī put last, and put last the one ʿAlī put first, but the wording is the same."],[5120,"ʿUmar performed qunūt. He said: Our companions informed me from al-Mukhāriq, from Ṭāriq, that he said the takbīr when he performed qunūt—meaning when he finished the recitation—then he said the takbīr when he went down."],[5121,"ʿUmar performed qunūt. He said: Our companions informed me from al-Mukhāriq, from Ṭāriq, that he said the takbīr when he performed qunūt—meaning when he finished the recitation—then he said the takbīr when he went down."],[5122,"We prayed the dawn prayer behind ʿUmar, and he performed qunūt after the bowing. One of the two said: He raised his hands."],[5123,"He used to perform qunut in the last rak'ah of the noon prayer, the last night prayer, and the morning prayer after saying: 'Allah listens to the one who praises Him,' and he would supplicate for the believers and curse the disbelievers. He mentioned that the Prophet ﷺ used to do it."],[5124,"The qunut in the witr and the morning prayer is: 'O Allah, we seek Your help, and we seek Your forgiveness, and we praise You with goodness, and we do not disbelieve in You, and we believe in You, and we cast off and forsake whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy and fear Your severe punishment; indeed, Your severe punishment will overtake the disbelievers. O Allah, punish the disbelievers and the polytheists, and cast terror into their hearts, and cause discord among their words, and send down upon them Your wrath and Your punishment. O Allah, punish the disbelievers of the People of the Book who block people from Your path and deny Your messengers. O Allah, forgive the believing men and women, and the Muslim men and women. O Allah, reconcile their hearts, and unite their hearts, and place faith and wisdom in their hearts, and inspire them to be grateful for Your favor which You bestowed upon them, and to fulfill Your covenant which You made with them, and cause them to die upon the religion of Your Messenger, and grant them victory over Your enemy and their enemy, O God of truth, and include us among them.' He would say this, then fall into prostration. He would not add anything to this from the prayer upon the Prophet ﷺ. Some of those who asked him would say: 'O Abu Sa'id, does he add anything to this from the prayer upon the Prophet ﷺ, supplication, glorification, and takbir?' He would say: 'I do not forbid you, but I heard the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ not adding anything to this, and he would become angry if they wanted him to add more.'"],[5125,"The qunut is obedience to Allah. He used to perform qunut with four verses from the beginning of al-Baqarah, then the verse 'Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth...', and the verse 'Allah – there is no deity except Him, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of existence', and the verse 'To Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth...' until the end of al-Baqarah, then 'Say: He is Allah, [who is] One', then 'Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak', then 'Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind'. Then he would say: 'O Allah, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We fear Your punishment and hope for Your mercy; indeed, Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers. O Allah, we seek Your help and Your forgiveness, and we praise You, so we do not disbelieve in You, and we believe in You, and we cast off and forsake whoever disbelieves in You.' They mentioned that there are two surahs from al-Baqarah, and that their place is after 'Say: He is Allah, [who is] One'."],[5126,"I said to al-Hasan b. 'Ali: 'What was I like on the day the Prophet ﷺ died, and what do I remember from him?' He said: 'I remember that a man came to him one day and asked him about something, and he said: \"Leave what causes you doubt for what does not cause you doubt, for evil causes doubt, and good brings tranquility.\" And I remember from him that one day I passed in front of him by a trough of dates from the sadaqah, and I took a date and threw it into my mouth. He grabbed me by the back of my neck, then put his hand into my mouth and pulled it out while I was swallowing it, then threw it back into the trough. His companions said: \"If only you had left the boy to eat it.\" He said: \"Sadaqah is not lawful for the family of Muhammad ﷺ.\" He said: And he taught me words to supplicate with at the end of the qunut: \"O Allah, guide me among those You have guided, and grant me well-being among those You have granted well-being, and take me as an ally among those You have taken as allies, and bless me in what You have given, and protect me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed, You decree and none decrees against You, and none is humiliated whom You befriend. Blessed and Exalted are You, our Lord.\"'"],[5127,"The Prophet ﷺ taught him to say in the qunut: O Allah, guide me among those You have guided, and grant me well-being among those You have granted well-being, and take me as an ally among those You have taken as allies, and bless me in what You have given, and protect me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed, You decree and none decrees against You, and none is humiliated whom You befriend. Blessed and Exalted are You, our Lord."],[5128,"'Umar performed qunut after the ruku'."],[5129,"Regarding a man who missed one rak'ah of the morning prayer and prayed one rak'ah with the imam and performed qunut with him, he said: 'When he makes up the last rak'ah, he should also perform qunut.' Ma'mar said: Qatadah said: 'He should not perform qunut.' Ma'mar said: 'If he performs qunut, it is good; if he does not perform qunut, there is no harm.'"],[5130,"I prayed behind 'Abidah and he performed qunut in the dawn prayer before the rak'ah."],[5131,"Others say in the qunut: 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. O Allah, to You we pray and to You we prostrate, and You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy and fear Your severe punishment; indeed, Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers. O Allah, we seek Your help and Your forgiveness, and we praise You and do not disbelieve in You, and we believe in You, and we cast off and forsake whoever disobeys You. O Allah, we have submitted our souls to You, and turned our faces to You, and leaned our backs upon You, out of desire and fear of You. There is no refuge or escape from You except to You. I believe in Your Book which You revealed and Your Messenger whom You sent. O Allah, forgive the believing men and women and the Muslim men and women, and reconcile their hearts, and unite their hearts, and place faith and wisdom in their hearts, and inspire them to fulfill Your covenant which You made with them, and cause them to die upon the religion of Your Prophet, and grant them victory over Your enemy and their enemy, O God of truth. O Allah, punish the disbelievers, and cast terror into their hearts, and cause discord among their words, and send down upon them Your wrath and Your punishment. O Allah, punish the disbelievers of the People of the Book who deny Your messengers and block people from Your path. O Allah, forgive us and have mercy on us and be pleased with us.'"],[5132,"Ubayy b. Ka'b performed qunut in the witr after the ruku'."],[5133,"Ibn Mas'ud used to perform qunut all year in the witr."],[5134,"He performed qunut in the witr before the rak'ah."],[5135,"The qunut in the witr is part of the Sunnah throughout the entire year, before the bowing (rak'ah)."],[5136,"Al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin used to perform the qunut in the witr before the bowing (rak'ah). And 'Abd al-Razzaq said: He says the takbir when he raises his head from the bowing, then he also says the takbir when he prostrates; and this is what we follow."],[5137,"There is no qunut in the Sunnah throughout the entire year except in the latter half of Ramadan."],[5138,"He used to perform the qunut throughout the entire year in the witr, except in the first half of Ramadan."],[5139,"He used to prefer to say in the qunut of the witr these two surahs: 'O Allah, we seek Your help and Your forgiveness, and we praise You and do not disbelieve in You; we renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; indeed, Your punishment will overtake the disbelievers.'"],[5140,"The hands were not raised in the witr during Ramadan."],[5141,"The hands were not raised in the witr during Ramadan."],[5142,"I said to 'Ata': The supplication of the people of Makkah after they finish the witr in the month of Ramadan? He said: It is an innovation (bid'ah). He said: I found the people not doing that in Makkah until it was newly introduced as something new."],[5143,"He says the takbir when he finishes the recitation in the last rak'ah of the witr, then he performs the qunut and raises his voice, then when he wants to bow he says the takbir again."],[5144,"I came to al-Aswad while he was ill; I stood up while a man was supporting him, and he prolonged it, fearing that he would fall short of what he used to do in the qunut."],[5145,"I came to al-Aswad while he was ill; I stood up while a man was supporting him, and he prolonged it, fearing that he would fall short of what he used to do in the qunut."],[5146,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to raise his hands level with his chest when he supplicated, then he would wipe his face with them."],[5147,"Shall I not give you a gift? Shall I not grant you something? Shall I not bestow upon you? Shall I not prefer you? Shall I not? Shall I not? - until I thought he would cut off for me the water of the two seas (i.e., grant me great wealth). He said: You pray four rak'ahs, reciting the Mother of the Qur'an (al-Fatihah) in each rak'ah along with a surah. Then you say: 'All praise is due to Allah, Glory be to Allah, Allah is the Greatest, there is no god but Allah,' counting it as one, until you have counted it fifteen times. Then you bow and say it ten times while bowing. Then you rise and say it ten times while standing. Then you prostrate and say it ten times while prostrating. Then you rise and say it ten times while sitting. Then you prostrate and say it ten times while prostrating. Then you rise and say it ten times while sitting. That makes seventy-five, and in the last three (rak'ahs) likewise. So the total is three hundred when combined, and when you separate them it is one thousand two hundred. He used to prefer to recite a surah after the Mother of the Qur'an of twenty verses or more. You may perform them in your day, your night, your week, your month, your year, or your lifetime. If your sins were as numerous as the stars of the sky, or the drops of rain, or the sand of 'Alij, or the days of eternity, Allah would forgive you."],[5148,"Whoever deliberately abandons the ‘Aṣr prayer, Allah nullifies his deeds."],[5149,"There is nothing between any of you and falling into disbelief except that he abandons an obligatory prayer."],[5150,"There is nothing between the servant and polytheism except that he abandons the prayer."],[5151,"Whoever deliberately abandons the prayer, the protection of Allah is free from him."],[5153,"Abandoning the prayer is polytheism."],[5154,"There is no share in Islam for anyone who abandons the prayer."],[5155,"Islam is built upon eight portions: the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, establishing the prayer, giving the zakāh, the pilgrimage to the House, fasting the month of Ramaḍān, jihād, and enjoining good and forbidding evil. And whoever has no portion has failed."],[5156,"I was sitting with Ibn ʿUmar when a young man came to him and said, 'Will you not engage in jihād?' He remained silent and turned away from him. Then the man repeated, and he remained silent and turned away from him. Then he asked him again, and Ibn ʿUmar said, 'Islam is built upon four pillars: establishing the prayer, giving the zakāh—do not separate the two—fasting Ramaḍān, and pilgrimage to the House for whoever can find a way. Jihād and charity are part of good deeds.'"],[5157,"Pray the prayer that Allah has made obligatory upon you at its proper time, for in neglecting it is destruction."],[5158,"I asked the Prophet ﷺ, saying, 'Which is best?' He said, 'The deeds: prayers at their times, devotion to parents, and jihād in the path of Allah.'"],[5159,"A woman performs the iqāmah for herself when she wants to pray."],[5160,"ʿĀʾishah used to give the adhān and the iqāmah."],[5161,"ʿĀʾishah used to give the adhān and the iqāmah."],[5162,"Women are not obligated to perform the iqamah."],[5163,"If a man is present with the women, then it is not forbidden for them to give the adhan and to perform the iqamah in that case."],[5164,"Women are not obligated to perform the iqamah."],[5165,"Women are not obligated to give the adhan or perform the iqamah."],[5166,"Women are not obligated to give the adhan or perform the iqamah."],[5167,"Women are not obligated to give the adhan or perform the iqamah."],[5168,"Women are not obligated to perform the iqamah."],[5169,"Women are not obligated to give the adhan or perform the iqamah."],[5170,"Mamar, from Yahya b. Abi Kathir, from a man, from Ikrimah, something similar: Women are not obligated to give the adhan or perform the iqamah."],[5171,"A woman may pray in a long gown and a head cover."],[5172,"I saw Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, pray in a long gown and a head cover."],[5173,"She asked Umm Salamah, \"In how much clothing may a woman pray?\" She replied, \"In a head cover and a full-length gown that conceals the tops of her feet.\""],[5174,"In how many garments may a woman pray? She said to him: \"Ask Ali, then come back to me and tell me what he says to you.\" He said: So he went to Ali and asked him, and he said: \"In the khimar and the ample dir'.\" Then he returned to Aisha and told her, and she said: \"He has spoken the truth.\""],[5175,"I said to Ibn Abbas: \"In how many garments may a woman pray?\" He said: \"In a dir' and a khimar.\""],[5176,"She saw Aisha, the Mother of the Believers, praying in the house wearing an izar, and a dir' and a thick khimar, with nothing else on her."],[5177,"Umm Habiba, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, prayed in a dir' and an izar which she used as a head covering until it touched the ground, and she did not wrap it around her waist, and she had no khimar on."],[5178,"If a woman takes a garment and covers her head with it so that nothing of her hair is seen, it suffices her in place of the khimar."],[5179,"Ikrima was asked: \"May a woman pray in a dir' and a khimar?\" He said: \"Yes, if it is not translucent.\""],[5180,"Her dir' suffices her if it is ample, I do not think he said anything except: \"with the khimar.\""],[5181,"\"A woman prays in her dir', her khimar, and her izar, and making use of the jilbab is dearer to me.\" I said: \"What do you think if her dir' and her khimar, one of them, is thin?\" He said: \"Then the jilbab in that case, because of the angels, that they are with her.\" I said: \"Her dir' reaching to the knees?\" He said: \"No, not until it is ample and thick.\" He said: \"And let her wrap the izar and tie it around her waist.\""],[5182,"Do not belittle concealing the waist, for if what is below the waist is hidden, it is more concealing, and if there is anything in it, it is more hidden for it."],[5183,"Any girl who has menstruated and does not cover her head, Allah will not accept her prayer."],[5184,"A woman should not go out except wearing a waist-wrapper, and Amr b. Dinar said: \"That used to be said.\""],[5185,"If a free woman who has menstruated prays without a khimar, Allah will not accept her prayer."],[5186,"When a woman menstruates, she covers her head; it is obligatory upon her as it is upon her mother."],[5187,"When a woman menstruates, no prayer is accepted from her until she covers her head and conceals her hair."],[5188,"A woman fell from her mount, and her garments were uncovered, while the Prophet ﷺ was near her. He turned away from her. It was said, 'She has trousers on.' He said, 'May Allah have mercy on the women who wear trousers.'"],[5189,"He disliked a woman praying without a necklace on her neck. I said, 'Why?' He said, 'So that she does not resemble men.'"],[5190,"Umm al-Fadl bt. Ghaylan, who is the daughter of Yazid b. al-Muhallab, wrote to Anas b. Malik: 'May a woman pray without a necklace on her neck?' He said: He wrote back to her: 'A woman should not pray except with a necklace on her neck.' He said: 'Even if she finds only a strap.'"],[5191,"There was a woman in Madinah called Sharr, and her name was Damalmakah. Umar ordered her to put on the jilbab."],[5192,"I said to Ata: 'A young girl who has not menstruated, when she prays?' He said: 'Her waist-wrapper is sufficient for her.'"],[5193,"There is no head cover or jilbab required for one who has not menstruated."],[5194,"The khimar is only that which covers the hair and the skin."],[5195,"The Prophet ﷺ entered upon her while she was covering her head. He said: 'One turn, not two turns.'"],[5196,"He disliked a woman praying while her ear was protruding from the khimar."],[5197,"I said to Ata: 'What is the minimum clothing that suffices a slave woman?' He said: 'We say about her what Umar said: \"She threw her fur garment behind the house, and her waist-wrapper and shift suffice her.\" He said: \"And she puts part of her shift over her head.\" I said: 'What if she is married to a slave?' He said: 'Likewise, a slave woman with a slave.' I said: 'What if she is married to a free man?' He said: 'Then let her wrap that around herself, to pray in her waist-wrapper, shift, and khimar.'"],[5198,"He heard al-Hasan commanding the female slave, when she married a slave or a free man, to cover her head with a khimar. He said: al-Hasan did not consider a khimar obligatory upon a female slave unless she married or her master had intercourse with her."],[5199,"I said to Ata: May a woman pray in a durra'ah? He said: Yes. I was told that female slaves during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ and after him would not pray until one of them placed her wrapper on her head as a veil, or a khimar, or a cloth in which her head was concealed."],[5200,"Female slaves, when they prayed, would cast a cloth over their heads. Thus they used to do during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[5201,"A female slave may pray without a khimar; she prays as she goes out."],[5202,"I said to Ata: May a female slave who has menstruated pray without a khimar? He said: Yes."],[5203,"Khimars are required for female slaves when they menstruate, but jalabib are not required for them."],[5204,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to forbid female slaves from wearing jalabib, so that they would not imitate free women."],[5206,"I said to Ata: May a woman wear a jilbab while she has no khimar? He said: It does no harm."],[5207,"Umar saw a slave girl who came out of the house of Hafsa adorned, wearing a jilbab—or from the house of one of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ. Umar entered the house and said: Who is this slave girl? They said: She is a slave girl of ours—or they said: A slave girl belonging to the family of so-and-so. He became angry with them and said: Do you send out your slave girls with their adornment, tempting people?"],[5208,"Umar, while he was addressing the people, saw a female slave who came out of the house of Hafsa, mingling among the people, dressed in the clothing of free women. When he finished, he entered upon Hafsa bt. Umar and said: Who is the woman who came out from your place mingling with the men? She said: That is a slave girl, the slave girl of Abd al-Rahman. He said: What prompts you to clothe your brother's slave girl in the clothing of free women? I came in upon you and did not see her except as a free woman, and I wanted to punish her."],[5209,"When a female slave prays, she conceals her head with her khimar or a cloth. Thus they used to do during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ and after him."],[5210,"Umar struck a female slave belonging to the family of Anas whom he saw veiled. He said: Uncover your head; do not imitate free women."],[5211,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to forbid slave women from wearing jilbabs."],[5212,"I said to Ata: Does a woman gesture with her hands like men in takbir? He said: \"She does not raise her hands in that like men,\" and he gestured, lowering his hands greatly and drawing them close to himself, and he said: \"A woman has a posture that is not for a man.\""],[5213,"A woman draws her hands together in her standing as much as she is able."],[5214,"When a woman prostrates, she draws herself together as much as she is able and does not spread out, so that she does not raise her buttocks."],[5215,"A woman draws herself together when she bows, raising her hands to her belly, and she draws together as much as she is able. When she prostrates, she should draw her hands to herself and press her belly and chest to her thighs, and draw together as much as she is able."],[5216,"The Prophet ﷺ prohibited \"a woman's attaching anything to her head.\""],[5217,"A woman was commanded to place her forearm and belly on her thighs when she prostrates, and not to spread out as a man spreads out, so that she does not raise her buttocks."],[5218,"When a woman prostrates, she should draw herself in and press her thighs to her belly."],[5219,"When she raises her head from prostration in other than the first sitting, she does not sit on her heels, but she sits as she sits in the first sitting."],[5220,"Safiyya bt. Abi Ubayd, when she sat in the first or fourth sitting, would sit cross-legged."],[5221,"A woman's sitting between the two prostrations is on her left side with her weight on it, and her sitting for the tashahhud is cross-legged."],[5222,"A woman's sitting between the two prostrations is like her sitting in the first sitting."],[5223,"The woman is commanded in prayer, in the position of sitting on one side (i.e., during tashahhud), to press her thighs together from one side."],[5224,"The woman sits in the position of sitting on one side however she wishes, as long as she is gathered together."],[5225,"The woman sits in the position of sitting on one side however it is easy for her."],[5226,"A woman leads women in prayer without going out in front of them, but rather she stands level with them in the obligatory prayer and the voluntary prayer.\" I said: \"Even if they are many, until they are two rows or more?\" He said: \"And that she stand in their midst.\""],[5227,"A woman leads women in the obligatory and voluntary prayer, standing in their midst."],[5228,"Umm Salamah led us in the afternoon prayer. She stood among us."],[5229,"A woman leads women, standing in their midst."],[5230,"There is no harm in a woman leading women in prayer during Ramadan. She stands in their midst."],[5231,"A woman leads women in Ramadan and stands with them in the row. Ma'mar said: 'And someone who heard 'Ikrimah informed me that he said the same.'"],[5232,"That 'A'ishah led them and stood among them in an obligatory prayer."],[5233,"That 'A'ishah used to lead women in voluntary prayer, standing with them in the row."],[5234,"When the man does not recite anything of the Qur'an, then he leads, and the woman stands behind him and prays following his prayer."],[5235,"When a man does not recite along with women, he goes forward, and the woman recites from behind him. When he says the takbīr, he bows, and she bows with his bowing and prostrates with his prostration."],[5236,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ about the hair that is attached to the head and the padding placed in the hair on the head: “If the woman wishes, she may place it on her head.” He said: As for attachment, the Messenger of God ﷺ cursed the one who attaches and the one who has it attached. Anas then said: And the consumer of ribā, the one who gives it, the witness, the scribe, the tattooer, the one tattooed, the one who practices incantations, and the one who has incantations done. ʿAṭāʾ said: We have heard that. He said: Arab women used to tattoo their hands. He said: As for these two, it is something you have innovated, but it did not exist at the time of the Prophet ﷺ, so let the woman remove it at prayer. I said: Do you consider every tattoo by which a woman increases beauty? He said: There is no good in it. I said: Her tattooing her lips and then applying ithmid to them? He said: There is no good in it."],[5237,"When a woman places hair on her head without attachment? He said: Let her remove it when she stands for prayer, for it is an innovation."],[5238,"There is no harm in a woman placing hair on her head without attachment."],[5239,"I saw Muʿāwiya on the pulpit in Madinah, and in his hand was a lock of hair. Then he said something I do not now remember."],[5240,"He saw Muʿāwiya giving a sermon on the pulpit, and in his hand was a lock of hair. He said: I heard him say: Where are your scholars, O people of Madinah? I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ forbid the attachment of this, and he said: The Children of Israel were punished when their women took up this. The Children of Israel were punished when their women took up this."],[5241,"He heard Muʿāwiya b. Abī Sufyān, with a lock of hair in his hand, saying: I witnessed the Messenger of God ﷺ forbidding the like of this, and he said: The Children of Israel were punished when their women took up this."],[5242,"The Prophet ﷺ prohibited a woman from attaching anything to her head."],[5243,"A woman of the Anṣār came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, we have married off a young girl of ours, and she was ill, so her hair fell out. Shall we attach hair for her? The Messenger of God ﷺ said: God has cursed the one who attaches and the one who has it attached."],[5244,"He detests attachment with wool."],[5245,"The women of the Children of Israel attached their hair, so God cursed them and prevented them from entering the Holy House. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: God has cursed the one who attaches and the one who has it attached."],[5246,"The consumer of ribā, the one who gives it, its scribe, and its witness, when they know of it; the tattooer and the one tattooed; the one who withholds charity and the one who transgresses in it; the habitual drinker of wine; and the one who reverts to Bedouinism after his emigration—cursed upon the tongue of Muḥammad ﷺ on the Day of Resurrection."],[5247,"Four were cursed: the tattooer, the one who asks for tattooing, the plucker of facial hair, and the one who has her teeth filed for beauty."],[5248,"I asked al-Zuhri about tattooing, and he said: \"It is from the adornment of the people of the Jahiliyyah.\""],[5249,"May Allah curse the women who tattoo, those who ask for tattooing, those who pluck facial hair, and those who file their teeth for beauty, altering Allah's creation. He said: This reached a woman from Banu Usayd called Umm Ya'qub. She said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, it has reached me that you cursed such-and-such. He said: \"And why should I not curse those whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed and who are mentioned in the Book of Allah?\" She said: I read what is between the two covers, and I do not find it. He said: \"If you are a reader, you have indeed found it. Have you not read: 'And whatever the Messenger gives you, take it; and whatever he forbids you, abstain from it' (59:7)?\" She said: Yes. He said: \"For the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade it.\" She said: I think your family do some of that. He said: \"Go and look.\" He said: \"She entered and did not see anything of what she sought. 'Abdullah said: 'If she had been like that, she would not have lived with us.'\""],[5250,"She was with 'A'ishah when a woman asked her: O Mother of the Believers, there are hairs on my face; should I pluck them to beautify myself for my husband? 'A'ishah said: \"Remove the harm from yourself, and adorn yourself for your husband as you would adorn yourself for a visit. When he commands you, obey him; when he makes an oath to you, fulfill it; and do not permit anyone he dislikes into his house.\""],[5251,"I said: What do you think about the women who go out during the day when they hear the call to prayer; is it obligatory for them to attend the prayer? He said: \"If she wishes to attend it, and if she does not, there is no blame.\" I said: His saying, 'O you who believe, when the call to prayer is made on the day of Friday' (62:9), is it not for women along with men? He said: \"No.\""],[5252,"I said to him: Is it obligatory for women, when they hear the call to prayer, to answer it as it is obligatory for men? He said: \"No, by my life.\""],[5253,"Do not prevent the maidservants of Allah from praying in the mosque. A son of 'Abdullah said: We do prevent them. He said: \"He insulted him severely,\" and said: We narrate to you from the Prophet ﷺ and you say, we do prevent them?"],[5254,"Give permission to women to go to the mosque at night. His son said: By Allah, we will not give them permission, lest they take that as a means of deceit. He said: \"May Allah do such-and-such to you. You hear me saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, and you say: No.\""],[5255,"Whoever among you believes in Allah and the Last Day, let her not raise her head until we raise our heads."],[5256,"A man asked Anas b. Malik: Did women attend the prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: Indeed, by Allah. Otherwise, why did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'The best rows of women are the last rows, and the worst rows of women are the first rows; and the best rows of men are the first rows, and the worst rows of men are the last rows'?"],[5257,"'Atikah bt. Zayd b. 'Amr b. Nufayl, who was married to 'Umar b. al-Khattab, used to attend the prayer in the mosque. 'Umar would say to her: \"By Allah, you know that I do not like this.\" She said: By Allah, I will not stop unless you forbid me. He said: \"I will not forbid you.\" She said: When 'Umar was stabbed on the day he was stabbed, she was in the mosque."],[5258,"If the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw the women today, he would have forbidden them from going out."],[5259,"Had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seen what the women have introduced after him, he would have barred them from the mosques, just as the women of the Children of Israel were barred.\" He said: I said: \"O you woman, were the women of the Children of Israel indeed barred?\" She said: \"Yes."],[5260,"The women of the Children of Israel used to make wooden stilts to look over at the men in the mosques, so Allah forbade the mosques to them, and menstruation was imposed upon them."],[5261,"Among the Children of Israel, men and women used to pray together. A woman who had a close friend would wear two clogs to appear taller for her friend. Then menstruation was imposed upon them. Ibn Mas'ud used to say: Put them back where Allah put them. So we said to Abu Bakr: What are the two clogs? He said: \"Two wooden lifts."],[5262,"A woman's prayer in her house is better than her prayer elsewhere.\" Then he said: \"When a woman goes out, Satan makes her appear attractive."],[5263,"A man came and said: It used to be said: A woman's prayer in her room is better than her prayer in her house. Abu 'Umar said to him: Why do you make it lengthy? I heard the owner of this house, meaning Ibn Mas'ud, swearing and striving in his oath: There is no prayer place for a woman better than her house, except for Hajj or 'Umrah, unless she is a woman who has despaired of marriage, in which case she may go about in her two sandals."],[5264,"He had three wives, and not one of them ever prayed in the mosque of the neighborhood."],[5265,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about women going out. He said: \"They should go out unadorned."],[5266,"They should go out unadorned."],[5267,"Do not prevent the maidservants of Allah from the mosques of Allah, but they should not go out except unadorned."],[5268,"If one of your wives asks permission to go to the mosque, do not prevent her."],[5270,"There is no harm in a man leading women in prayer."],[5271,"'Umar b. al-Khattab ordered Sulayman b. Abi Hathmah to lead the women in prayer at the rear of the mosque during the month of Ramadan. Sufyan said: Our companions dislike that, and they say: Consider, if he loses his ritual purity, who will step forward? And they say: Voluntary prayer is easier."],[5272,"Ali used to order the people to stand in prayer during the month of Ramadan, and he would appoint an imam for the men and an imam for the women.\" He said: \"He ordered me, so I led the women."],[5273,"The mosques used to be built plainly, and the cities used to have battlements."],[5274,"I was not commanded to raise the mosques high. He said: And Ibn Abbas said: Indeed, by Allah, you will surely decorate them."],[5275,"Ali would pass by a mosque of Taym that was lofty, and he would say: This is the church of Taym."],[5276,"The mosque of the Prophet ﷺ was built of mud bricks, its pillars were of wood, and its roof was of palm branches. Then the Prophet ﷺ passed away, and Abu Bakr took charge and did not alter it until he died. Then Umar took charge and expanded it, keeping its wooden pillars as they were and roofing it with palm branches. Then when Uthman came, he expanded it and built it with carved stones and roofed it with teak wood."],[5277,"It reached me that it was revealed to the Prophet ﷺ: 'Take a mosque as a booth like the booth of Moses, reaching a cubit into the sky.'"],[5278,"Your mosques will be decorated as the Jews and Christians decorated their places of worship."],[5279,"When you adorn your mushafs and decorate your mosques, then ruin is upon you."],[5280,"No nation ever did evil in its deeds except that it decorated its mosques, and no nation ever perished except because of its scholars."],[5281,"When people adorn their mosques, their deeds become corrupt."],[5282,"Ubayy b. Kab and Abu al-Darda measured the mosque, then they came to the Prophet ﷺ with the cubit. He said: Rather, a booth like the booth of Moses, with dry grass and pieces of wood; the matter is more urgent than that."],[5283,"Umar b. al-Khattab entered the mosque and prayed one rakah. It was said to him, and he said: It is only voluntary, so whoever wishes may add and whoever wishes may reduce. I disliked taking it as a thoroughfare."],[5284,"I entered the mosque with Ibn Masʿūd. He bowed, and a man passed by him while he was bowing and greeted him. He said: “Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth.” When he finished, he said: It was said: Among the signs of the Hour are that a man greets another only on account of acquaintance, that mosques are taken as passageways, that women compete in riding horses, that prices become cheap and do not rise until the Day of Resurrection, and that a man and a woman strip together."],[5285,"Among the signs of the Hour are the loud voice of the wicked in the mosques, rain without vegetation, that mosques are taken as passageways, and that children of adultery become prominent."],[5286,"I said to al-Ḥasan: “Do you not dislike that a man passes through the mosque and does not pray in it?” He said: “Yes, indeed.”"],[5287,"Among the signs of the Hour are that a passerby passes by a mosque and does not bow two rakʿahs in it, that a young boy is sent by the youths as a messenger between the two horizons, that greetings are only for acquaintance, and that the barefoot naked shepherds of sheep are in houses of mud."],[5288,"Yaḥyā b. Zakarīyā was sent and commanded to tell his people five words and to strike parables for them. They pleased him, so he kept them to himself. It was said to ʿĪsā: “Go to Yaḥyā and command him to convey the words he was commanded with, otherwise you convey them.” When he came to him, he said: “I will convey them.” So he said to his people: The parable of associating partners with Allah is like a man who bought a slave with his own wealth, treated him well, and freed him, then said: “Go.” So he went and acquired good things, and he gave his good things and his gain to a man other than the one who freed him. That is the parable of associating partners with Allah. The parable of prayer is like a man who came to an awesome ruler, not hoping that he would permit him to speak, but he came to him and permitted him to say whatever he wished. That is the parable of the one praying: when he is in prayer, Allah grants him from his supplication what he loves. The parable of zakāh is like a man captured by the enemy. They said: “Kill him; what are you waiting for?” He said: “What will you gain by killing me? Instead, assign me installments, and I will pay you the price of my freedom.” So they assigned him installments. Each time he paid an installment, a part of his slavery was released until he was freed. Thus charity expiates sins. The parable of fasting is like a man who witnessed battle and took up weapons until he saw that nothing could reach him. That is the parable of fasting: fasting is a shield from the Fire. The parable of the Qurʾān is like a people in a fortified stronghold: the enemy does not come to them except that he finds them alert. Thus is the parable of the companion of the Qurʾān against Satan."],[5290,"One day I was alone, wanting to strive in praising my Lord and supplicating, but I was at a loss for words. Then I heard a speaker saying, though I saw no one: “Say: O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs all praise, to You belongs all dominion, in Your hand is all good, and to You returns the whole affair, its open and its secret. You are worthy to be praised; indeed, You have power over all things. O Allah, forgive me all my past sins, protect me for the rest of my life, and grant me pure deeds with which You are pleased with me.” He said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him. He said: “That was an angel teaching you how to praise your Lord and supplicate.”"],[5291,"The drying of the earth is its purification. May Allah bless Muḥammad and grant him peace abundantly."],[5292,"The people of Madinah held the Friday prayer before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived and before the Friday prayer was revealed. They were the ones who named it al-Jumuʿah. The Anṣār said: “The Jews have a day on which they gather every seven days, and the Christians likewise. Come, let us make a day on which we gather and remember Allah, pray, and thank Him” – or as they said. They said: “Saturday is for the Jews, Sunday is for the Christians, so make it the Day of ʿArūbah.” They used to call Friday the Day of ʿArūbah. So they gathered to Asʿad b. Zurārah, and he prayed with them that day and reminded them. They named it al-Jumuʿah. When they gathered to him, Asʿad b. Zurārah slaughtered a sheep for them, and they had lunch and dinner from a single sheep, because of their small number. Then Allah revealed concerning that afterwards: “When the call is made for prayer on the day of Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah.”"],[5293,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “Who was the first to hold the Friday prayer?” He said: “A man from Banū ʿAbd al-Dār, they claimed.” I said: “Was it by the command of the Prophet ﷺ?” He said: “So what?”"],[5294,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Muṣʿab b. ʿUmayr b. Hāshim to the people of Madinah to teach them the Qurʾān. He asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for permission to hold the Friday prayer with them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him permission. At that time he was not a governor, but he went to teach the people of Madinah. Maʿmar said: Al-Zuhrī used to say: Wherever there is a governor, he admonishes his companions on Friday and prays two rakʿahs with them."],[5295,"He was with ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz while he was staying in the desert at al-Suwaydāʾ, during his governorship of the Ḥijāz. He said: “When Friday came, they prepared for him a seat of pebbles. Then the muezzin called the prayer, and he came out to them and sat on that seat. Then they gave another call. Then he delivered the sermon to them. Then the prayer was commenced, and he prayed two rakʿahs with them, reciting aloud in it. Then when he finished his prayer, he said to them: ‘The imam holds the Friday prayer wherever he is.’”"],[5296,"ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz came to Makkah for Hajj or ʿUmrah and held the Friday prayer with them while he was traveling."],[5297,"Maslama b. ʿAbd al-Malik wrote to him: I am in a village with much wealth, people, and folk; shall I lead them in the Friday prayer though I am not a governor? He wrote back: Musʿab b. ʿUmayr asked the Messenger of God ﷺ for permission to lead the people of al-Madīnah in the Friday prayer, and he permitted him, so he led them while they were then few. If you think you should write to Hishām so that he permits you, then do so."],[5298,"The Friday prayer may be attended from two farsakhs."],[5299,"It has reached me that the people of Dhū al-Ḥulayfah used to attend the Friday prayer with the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[5300,"The Friday prayer is obligatory upon anyone whom the night shelters while returning to his family."],[5301,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ: From how far may the Friday prayer be attended? He said: Ten miles up to a barīd."],[5302,"The people used to come down for the prayer on Friday from four or six miles away."],[5303,"ʿAmr b. Shuʿayb was asked, while I listened: From how far may the Friday prayer be attended? He said: From the distance at which the voice carries."],[5304,"He sent to Ibn al-Musayyab to ask him: Upon whom is the Friday prayer obligatory? He said: Upon whoever hears the call."],[5305,"My father would be six or eight miles from al-Madīnah, and sometimes he would attend the Friday prayer in al-Madīnah and sometimes he would not attend it."],[5306,"Anas would be at his land, three miles between him and al-Baṣrah, and he would attend the Friday prayer in al-Baṣrah."],[5307,"ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ would be at al-Wahṭ and would not attend the Friday prayer with the people in al-Ṭāʾif, even though it was only four or three miles between him and al-Ṭāʾif."],[5308,"Abū Hurayrah would be five miles from al-Madīnah and would lead the Friday prayer and come down."],[5309,"Muawiya used to call the people to attend Jumu'ah from the minbar in Damascus, saying: 'Attend Jumu'ah, O people of such-and-such, O people of such-and-such,' until he called the people of Matirin and the people of Fa'in, which is at that time from Damascus at twenty-four miles, and he would say: 'Attend, O people of Fa'iz.'"],[5310,"Mu'ādh b. Jabal would stand on his minbar and say: \"O people of Quradā, and O people of Dāmirah—two villages among the villages of Damascus, one of them four farsakhs away and the other five—Jumu'ah is obligatory upon you, and there is no Jumu'ah except with us.\""],[5311,"That some men from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ who had witnessed Badr lost their eyesight during the lifetime of the Prophet ﷺ and after him, yet they did not abandon attending the Friday prayer. So we do not consider it permissible for anyone who has the means to reach it to abandon the Friday prayer."],[5312,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"O you who believe, when the call is made for prayer on Friday\"—are women not with the men? He said: \"No.\""],[5313,"Whoever hears the call to prayer for three Fridays and then does not attend is recorded among the hypocrites."],[5314,"Does one of you have to take a piece of land at a distance of two or three miles from Madinah, then come to Friday prayer but not attend it, then come to Friday prayer but not attend it? Then Allah sealed his heart."],[5315,"5203 5167 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, each one of them, from a man, from Muhammad b. 'Abbad b. Ja'far, from the Prophet ﷺ. Hidden text: Should any of you take up a property with a life interest at the head of two or three miles from Madinah, then come to Friday prayer and not attend it, then come to Friday prayer and not attend it, and so Allah sets a seal upon his heart?"],[5316,"Peoples must cease from staying away from Jumu'ah, or Allah will seal their hearts and they will be recorded among the heedless."],[5317,"Whoever abandons the Friday prayer for four consecutive Fridays without excuse has cast Islam behind his back."],[5318,"I have considered ordering a man to lead the people in prayer, then going off to burn the houses of a people who do not attend Friday prayer."],[5319,"I command my young men to gather bundles of firewood."],[5320,"He stayed behind one day from Jumu'ah, and it was said to him—so he said: 'This mud and mire prevented me.'"],[5321,"Whoever misses Jumu'ah a single day will have no expiation for it short of the Day of Resurrection."],[5322,"That I drink a cup of wine - or he said: an uqiyyah - is dearer to me than deliberately missing Jumu'ah."],[5323,"There is no Jumu'ah and no tashriq except in a large town where [Friday] prayer is held in congregation."],[5324,"And no i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."],[5325,"There is no Jumu'ah and no tashriq except in a large town where [Friday] prayer is held in congregation. He used to count the amsar as: al-Basrah, al-Kufah, al-Madinah, al-Bahrayn, Misr, al-Sham, al-Jazirah, and sometimes he said: al-Yaman and al-Yamamah."],[5326,"There is no Jumu'ah and no tashriq except in a large town where [Friday] prayer is held in congregation. He used to count the amsar as: al-Basrah, al-Kufah, al-Madinah, al-Bahrayn, Misr, al-Sham, al-Jazirah, and sometimes he said: al-Yaman and al-Yamamah."],[5327,"Wasit is a large town."],[5328,"I said to 'Ata': What is a comprehensive village? He said: One with a congregation, a governor, qisas, and dwellings that are clustered together, not separated, adjoining one another, like the situation of Jeddah. He said: And qisas. He said: So Jeddah is comprehensive, and al-Ta'if. He said: And if you are in a comprehensive village and the call to prayer is made on Friday, then it is incumbent upon you to attend it, whether you hear the adhan or not."],[5329,"He commanded the people of Quba, the people of Dhu al-Hulayfah, and the people of the small villages around it not to hold the Jumu'ah, but to attend the Jumu'ah in al-Madinah."],[5330,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote to the people of the watering places between Makkah and al-Madinah to hold the Jumu'ah. 'Ata' said at that: It has reached us that there is no Jumu'ah except in a large town where [Friday] prayer is held in congregation."],[5331,"The Messenger of God ﷺ held the Jumu'ah with his companions while traveling, and he delivered the sermon to them leaning on a bow."],[5332,"We have heard that there is no Jumu'ah except in a comprehensive village."],[5333,"If the mosque is one in which the prayer is held in congregation, then the Jumu'ah should be prayed in it."],[5334,"Ibn Umar used to see the people of the watering places between Makkah and Madinah holding Jumu'ah, and he did not find fault with them."],[5335,"Arafah is not a place of Jumu'ah, nor al-Zahran, nor Saraf, nor the people of this valley of ours."],[5336,"If you are in a village that is not a place of Jumu'ah, and its people hold Jumu'ah, then if you wish you may pray Jumu'ah with them, and if you wish you may not, unless you hear the call. If you pray Jumu'ah with them, then when the imam concludes with two rak'ahs, add two rak'ahs, and do not shorten with them."],[5337,"I asked him about a village that is not a place of Jumu'ah, where they hold Jumu'ah and shorten the prayer. He said: I asked, 'Should I pray Jumu'ah with them and shorten?' He said, 'Yes.'"],[5338,"There is no Jumu'ah, no Adha, and no Fitr except for one who attends the imam."],[5339,"I said to Ata', 'What do you think about the people of Basra? The grand mosque cannot accommodate them. What should they do?' He said, 'Each group should have a mosque in which they hold Jumu'ah, and that will suffice them.' Ibn Jurayj said, 'The people disapproved of that, that they should hold Jumu'ah except in the grand mosque.'"],[5340,"I prayed the Jumu'ah prayer with a man who did not give the khutbah, and he prayed four rak'ahs. I deemed him mistaken, but when I asked about that, behold, he had been correct."],[5341,"He prayed with an imam who did not give the khutbah on Friday, and the imam prayed two rak'ahs. Then al-Dahhak stood up and prayed two rak'ahs after the prayer had concluded, making them four."],[5342,"He disliked that the imam of a village that is not a place of Jumu'ah should give the khutbah and then pray four rak'ahs."],[5343,"If the imam does not give the khutbah on Friday, he prays four rak'ahs."],[5344,"He prays two rak'ahs in every case."],[5345,"Jumu'ah is not obligatory upon women and slaves."],[5346,"There is no Jumu'ah prayer for the traveler."],[5347,"He would not perform the ritual bath on Friday while traveling, and he would say: \"The traveler has no Jumu'ah.\""],[5348,"There is no Jumu'ah prayer for the woman, nor for the slave, nor for the traveler, nor for the child."],[5349,"Indeed, whoever was engaged in something forbidden and then Allah turned him away from it to something else, Allah will forgive him. And whoever does good, whether a believer or a disbeliever, his reward is guaranteed by Allah either in this world or in the Hereafter. And whoever prays one prayer for me, I pray ten upon him. And whoever supplicates for me once, his sins are forgiven. And Jumu'ah is a right upon every Muslim\" — or he said: \"whoever believes in Allah, Jumu'ah is a right upon him — except for a slave, or a woman, or a child, or a sick person. Whoever seeks enrichment through entertainment or trade, Allah dispenses with him, and Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.\""],[5350,"He saw Ibn Mas'ud turning out women from the mosque, saying: \"Go out to your homes; it is better for you.\""],[5351,"They would not perform Jumu'ah while traveling, and they would pray only two rak'ahs."],[5352,"There is no Jumu'ah prayer for the traveler."],[5353,"Any slave who pays the kharaj must attend Jumu'ah. If no kharaj is due from him or his master's work occupies him, then there is no Jumu'ah upon him."],[5354,"I asked him about a traveler who passes by a village and stops there on Friday. He said: \"If he hears the call to prayer, let him attend Jumu'ah.\""],[5355,"There is no Jumu'ah prayer for the traveler."],[5356,"There is no Jumu'ah prayer for women and slaves."],[5357,"Every feast is when the forenoon becomes intense: Jumu'ah, the sacrifice, and the breaking of the fast."],[5358,"I went early on Friday. When the sun declined, Umar came out, ascended the pulpit, and the mu'adhdhin began his call to prayer."],[5359,"I attended Friday prayer with Abu Bakr, and he completed his prayer and sermon before midday. Then I attended Friday prayer with Umar, and he completed his prayer and sermon when the sun declined."],[5360,"We used to pray Friday with Uthman, then we would return and take a midday nap."],[5361,"Uthman used to lead the Friday prayer, then the people would take a midday nap after the prayer."],[5362,"We used to pray Friday with Nafi' b. Abd al-Malik in the Hijr, and Ata' said: \"That has reached us.\""],[5363,"Mu'adh b. Jabal arrived from Syria and found the people of Makkah praying Friday in the Hijr. He rebuked them for praying it until the Ka'bah's shadow fell from its face, which is the time of zawal."],[5364,"He found Utbah b. Abi Sufyan leading Friday prayer with the people in the Hijr at the height of the day, standing on the ground with nothing beneath him."],[5365,"We used to pray Friday with Ali b. Abi Talib, then we would depart. Sometimes there was shadow, and sometimes there was not. We did not see it—he says: we sometimes saw it, and sometimes we did not see it."],[5366,"We used to pray Friday with Ammar b. Yasir, and I do not know whether the sun had declined or not."],[5367,"When he intended the prayer, he said: 'The resolve is at the reminder,' meaning when he delivered the sermon."],[5368,"When the call to prayer was given, he said: 'It is the time.'"],[5369,"We used to pray Friday with Ibn Mas'ud, then we would return and take a midday nap."],[5370,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray Jumu'ah with us when the least shadow had fallen."],[5371,"When the imam gives the first call to prayer, all crafts become forbidden; they are in the same position as buying and selling."],[5372,"When the call to prayer is given on the day of Jumu'ah, once the sun has passed its zenith, buying and selling becomes forbidden."],[5373,"The call to prayer during which buying and selling becomes forbidden is the call at the time of the imam's appearance. Al-Zuhri said: \"And I think that buying and selling should now be abandoned at the first call to prayer.\""],[5374,"When the call to prayer is given on the day of Jumu'ah, buying and selling become forbidden."],[5375,"If a man makes a purchase after the sun has passed its zenith, the sale is invalid."],[5376,"I left the mosque and Muslim b. Nawfal met me on Friday and said: \"Have you prayed?\" I said: \"No.\" He said: \"I prayed it with Umar b. al-Khattab, and he placed the pulpit in the Hijr.\""],[5377,"I said to Ata': \"If there is a village that is not a central mosque town, it is not appropriate for them to pray Jumu'ah until the sun has declined and risen high, at the time of Zuhr.\""],[5378,"\"Do you know of anything that becomes forbidden when the first call to prayer is given besides buying and selling?\" He said: \"Yes, crafts.\" I said: \"What about a document someone wanted to write at that time?\" He said: \"Nor anything.\" I said: \"What about goods he wanted to prepare?\" He said: \"No.\" I said: \"What if someone wanted to take a midday nap at that time?\" He said: \"Neither sleep, nor going to his wife at that time when the first call is given.\" I said: \"When the first call is given, is it obligatory to set out at that moment?\" He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"Because of His saying: 'When the call to prayer is given on the day of Jumu'ah'?\" He said: \"Yes, so let him leave everything at that time and set out.\""],[5379,"\"Is raising the voice in recitation on the day of Jumu'ah a Sunnah?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[5380,"Abu Hurayrah used to lead us in Jumu'ah prayer and recite Surat al-Jumu'ah in the first rak'ah and \"When the hypocrites come to you\" in the second rak'ah. Ubayd Allah said: I caught up with Abu Hurayrah when he had finished, and I said: \"O Abu Hurayrah, I heard you reciting two surahs that Ali b. Abi Talib used to recite in Kufa.\" Abu Hurayrah said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite them, and Abu Bakr followed that.\""],[5381,"Ali used to recite Surat al-Jumu'ah and \"When the hypocrites come to you\" in the Jumu'ah prayer. He said: I mentioned that to Abu Hurayrah, and he said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that.\""],[5382,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting these two surahs with tarji' on Friday: Surat al-Jumu'ah and “When the hypocrites come to you.”"],[5383,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite on Friday at dawn “The revelation of the Prostration” and “Has there come upon man?” And he used to recite in the Friday prayer Surat al-Jumu'ah and “When the hypocrites come to you.”"],[5384,"The Prophet ﷺ used to recite on the two 'ids and on Friday: “Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High” and “Has there come to you the story of the Overwhelming?”"],[5385,"Al-Dahhak b. Qays wrote to al-Nu'man b. Bashir asking him what the Prophet ﷺ used to recite on Friday along with Surat al-Jumu'ah. He said: “Has there come to you the story of the Overwhelming?”"],[5386,"The Prophet ﷺ recited in the Friday prayer Surat al-Jumu'ah and “O Prophet, when you divorce women.”"],[5387,"The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the Friday prayer Surat al-Jumu'ah and “Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High,” and in the morning prayer on Friday “Alif Lam Mim. The revelation” and “Blessed is He in whose hand is the dominion.”"],[5388,"He used to recite on Friday at dawn “Alif Lam Mim. The revelation of the Prostration” and “Has there come upon man?”"],[5389,"He used to recite in the dawn prayer “Alif Lam Mim. The revelation” and a surah from the mufassal, and sometimes he said “Has there come upon man?”"],[5390,"My pulpit is upon a garden from the gardens of Paradise. Whoever swears by it falsely upon a green toothstick, let him take his seat in the Fire. Let those present convey to those absent."],[5391,"The legs of my pulpit are firmly fixed in Paradise."],[5392,"Between my house and my pulpit is a garden from the gardens of Paradise, and my pulpit is upon my cistern."],[5393,"Baqul, the freedman of al-'As b. Umayya, made the Prophet's ﷺ pulpit for him from tamarisk with three steps. When Mu'awiya came to Madinah, he added to it, and the sun was eclipsed on that day."],[5394,"What is between my pulpit and my house is a garden from the gardens of Paradise."],[5395,"Did the Prophet ﷺ stand, when he delivered a sermon, leaning on a staff? He said: Yes, he used to lean on it firmly."],[5397,"An angel came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: Your Lord gives you the choice between being a prophet-servant or a prophet-king. He looked at Jibril as if consulting him, and he gestured to him to be humble. So he said: \"Rather, a prophet-servant.\" After that, the Prophet ﷺ was never seen eating reclining. Al-Zuhri said: The angel had not come to him before that, nor after."],[5398,"He used to carry a straight stalk from the palms of Tabah. Sufyan said: It is a straight stalk; it may have a bend, and then it is straightened. He said: With that stalk he struck Sawadah b. Ghaziyyah al-Ansari, who said: O Messenger of God, retaliation! He said: \"Yes.\" That distressed the people. They said: O Messenger of God, he is needy; he only wanted you to give him something. The Prophet ﷺ allowed him retaliation, and he kissed between his eyes. Then the Prophet ﷺ gave him something after that."],[5399,"Jibril, may the blessings of God be upon them both, or an angel, came to the Prophet ﷺ while he had a staff. He said: Do not break the horns of your ummah."],[5400,"He used to lean on a staff while delivering the Friday sermon, when he used to preach standing by the trunk. When the pulpit was made, he stood on it and leaned on the staff as well."],[5401,"The Prophet ﷺ gave Abd Allah b. Unays al-Sulami a staff and said: \"Take this and carry it as a walking stick. Know that those who carry such a staff on the Day of Resurrection will be few.\" He said: When Abd Allah b. Unays died, that staff was buried with him."],[5402,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to stand on Fridays by a trunk of a palm tree that had been erected in the mosque and deliver the sermon, until he decided to have a pulpit made. He consulted the people of sound judgment among the Muslims, and they advised him to make one. So he had a pulpit made. When Friday came, the Prophet ﷺ walked over and sat on the pulpit. When the trunk missed him, it moaned with a sound that alarmed the people. The Prophet ﷺ rose from his seat, came to it, stood by it, and stroked it, and it became calm. No moaning was heard from it after that."],[5403,"When the Prophet ﷺ delivered a sermon, he used to lean against one of the pillars of the mosque. When his pulpit was made for him and he stood on it, that pillar shook like the yearning cry of a she-camel, so that the people of the mosque heard it, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came down to it and embraced it, and it fell silent."],[5404,"What happened to the trunk that the Prophet ﷺ used to stand by when he preached? He said: \"It was buried in the mosque.\""],[5405,"The Prophet ﷺ used to sit between the two sermons on Friday and preach. His prayer was moderate, and his sermon was moderate, and he would recite verses from the Quran on the pulpit."],[5406,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ deliver the Friday sermon standing, then he sat and did not speak, then he stood and delivered another sermon. Whoever tells you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered the sermon sitting has lied."],[5407,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to deliver the Friday sermon standing. Then Uthman continued to do that until standing became difficult for him, so he would deliver the sermon standing, then sit, then stand again and deliver the sermon. When Mu'awiya's time came, he delivered the first sermon sitting, then stood and delivered the second sermon standing."],[5408,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to deliver the Friday sermon standing, not sitting, except in the pause between the two sermons. The first to sit was Mu'awiya. When Abd al-Malik's time came, he delivered the sermon standing, struck the pulpit with his foot, and said, 'This is the Sunnah.' Later, when it became too long for him, he sat after that."],[5409,"The Prophet's ﷺ sermon on Friday consisted of two parts delivered standing, with a sitting between them."],[5410,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to deliver two sermons on Friday, with a sitting between them."],[5411,"The Prophet's ﷺ sermon on Friday consisted of two parts delivered standing, with a sitting between them."],[5412,"Uthman developed a tremor, and when he stood on the pulpit he would rest for a while, then stand and deliver the sermon."],[5413,"On Friday, when the Prophet ﷺ settled on the pulpit, he would sit. When he sat, the muezzins would call the adhan. When they finished, he would stand and deliver the sermon. When he finished the first sermon, he sat, then stood and delivered the second sermon."],[5414,"When Mu'awiya's time came, he asked the people's permission to sit during one of the two sermons and said, 'I have grown old and I want to sit during one of the two sermons.' So he sat during the first sermon."],[5415,"The Prophet ﷺ never sat on a pulpit until he died; he only delivered the sermon standing. How much you would like people to feel it. The Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman—when one of them ascended the pulpit, he stood upright; he did not sit on the pulpit until he ascended it, nor did he sit on it afterward until he descended. His entire sermon was delivered standing. They would only recite the testimony of faith once at the beginning. There was no pulpit except the pulpit of the Prophet ﷺ until Mu'awiya came when he performed the pilgrimage with his pulpit and left it there, and they continued to deliver sermons from pulpits after that."],[5416,"Who was the first to introduce sitting during the sermon? He said: 'Uthman in his later days when he grew old and developed a tremor. He would sit for a little while, then stand.' I said: 'Did he deliver the sermon while sitting?' He said: 'I do not know.'"],[5417,"I went out with my father to Friday prayer when I was a boy. When Ali came out and ascended the pulpit, my father said, 'O Amr, stand up and look at the Commander of the Faithful.' He said: 'So I stood and saw him standing on the pulpit, with a white head and beard, wearing only a waist-wrapper and a cloak, no shirt.' He said: 'I did not see him sit on the pulpit until he descended from it.' I said to Abu Ishaq: 'Did he perform the qunut?' He said: 'No.'"],[5418,"How did Ibn al-Zubayr deliver the sermon? He said: 'He would sit and deliver the sermon sitting, then stand and deliver it again, and his sitting was most of that time.'"],[5419,"I saw Abu Mahdhurah, when Khalid b. Sa'id came out from the gate of Banu Makhzum on Friday, he would call the adhan the moment he came out, and Khalid would not reach his place where he used to deliver the sermon except that Abu Mahdhurah had already finished. He said: \"Thus did those who came before use to do.\""],[5420,"I saw Khalid b. al-'As delivering the sermon standing on the ground, leaning against the House, with no sitting in that, neither before nor after — a single sermon — until Khalid fell ill, and then he would sit on a ladder. He said: Thus they used to deliver sermons standing on the ground, except the Prophet ﷺ on his minbar."],[5421,"Mu'awiyah b. Abi Sufyan delivered a sermon standing for nearly a year. Then it was said to him: You seek the blood of 'Uthman and yet you act contrary to him. So he delivered the sermon standing and sitting."],[5422,"I said to 'Ata': I see the imams, when they descend from the minbar, touch the Corner before they come to the Maqam. Has anything reached you about it? He said: \"No.\" I said: Do you deem it recommended? He said: \"No, except that touching the Corner, the more you do it, the better it is.\""],[5423,"'Abdullah b. Mas'ud came to us on Friday and said: When you women pray with the imam on Friday, pray two rak'ahs, and when you pray in your houses, pray four. Sufyan said: The slave is of that same standing."],[5424,"Not a year comes upon you except that it is worse than the one before it. And the death of my household is easier for me than a number of them like dung beetles. And you are only approached from the side of your rulers. And a bad servant of God am I if I have lied."],[5425,"When women attend the Friday prayer, they pray two rak'ahs."],[5426,"Women perform the Friday prayer, even if they are in the houses nearest to the mosque."],[5427,"The Prophet ﷺ did not raise his hands in supplication."],[5428,"I asked him about raising the hands on Friday, and he said: \"It is an innovation. The first one to introduce it was 'Abd al-Malik.\""],[5429,"He saw Bishr b. Marwan raising his hands on Friday, and he cursed him and said: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ one day say: \"Except like this,\" and he gestured with his index finger."],[5430,"He saw them raising their hands on Friday while the imam was delivering the sermon, and he said: \"O God, cut off their hands.\""],[5431,"When he ascended the pulpit, he turned his face toward the people and said: \"Peace be upon you.\""],[5432,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ ascended the pulpit, he turned his face toward the people and said: \"Peace be upon you.\" He said: Abu Bakr and Umar used to do that after the Prophet ﷺ."],[5433,"On Friday he used to recite on the pulpit: ﴿Say: O disbelievers﴾ and ﴿Say: He is God, One﴾."],[5434,"Ammār b. Yāsir recited on the pulpit on Friday: ﴿When the sky is split apart﴾, then he descended and prostrated."],[5435,"I heard Hudhayfah on Friday while he was on the pulpit recite: ﴿The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split﴾, then he said: \"The Hour has drawn near, and the moon has split. Today is the training ground, and tomorrow is the race.\""],[5436,"The Prophet ﷺ recited ﴿When the sky is split apart﴾ while he was on the pulpit. When he reached the prostration in it, he descended and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him."],[5437,"There is no qunūt in the Friday prayer. Maʿmar said: And someone who heard al-Ḥasan informed me that he said something similar."],[5438,"There is no qunūt in the Friday prayer. Maʿmar said: And someone who heard al-Ḥasan informed me that he said something similar."],[5439,"I said to him: The qunūt in the two rakʿahs of Friday? He said: \"I have not heard of qunūt in the prescribed prayers except in the morning prayer,\" and he disapproved of there being qunūt in the Friday prayer."],[5440,"Raising the hands and qunūt in the Friday prayer is an innovation."],[5441,"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ while he was on the pulpit saying: Whoever of you comes to Friday prayer, let him perform ghusl."],[5442,"Ibn Shihāb informed me, from Sālim b. ʿAbd Allāh, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet ﷺ something similar. Hidden matn: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ while he was on the pulpit saying: Whoever of you comes to Friday prayer, let him perform ghusl."],[5443,"While ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb was standing delivering the sermon on Friday, a man from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ entered. ʿUmar called out to him: \"What hour is this?\" He said: \"I was busy today, and I did not return to my family until I heard the call, so I did no more than perform ablution.\" ʿUmar said: \"And ablution too! You know that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to command ghusl.\""],[5444,"While 'Umar was giving the khutbah, a man entered. 'Umar said: \"What kept you?\" He said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, I only managed to perform wudu' when I heard the call, then I came.\" When the prayer was over, Ibn 'Abbas said to him: \"Did you not hear what he said, O Commander of the Faithful?\" He said: \"Indeed, he knew that we were commanded to perform ghusl.\""],[5445,"He came while 'Umar was giving the khutbah on Friday. 'Umar turned aside to where the man was to sit until he finished the remembrance. Then 'Uthman said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, I only managed to hear the first call, so I performed wudu' and came out.\" 'Umar said: \"You knew it is not [sufficient] with wudu'.\""],[5446,"It is a duty upon every Muslim who has reached puberty to purify himself one day every seven days for Allah, and if he is not junub, let him wash his head and his skin on Friday."],[5447,"It is a duty upon every Muslim to perform ghusl once every seven days on Friday, to use the siwak, and to apply some perfume of his family."],[5448,"It is a duty upon every adult to perform ghusl one day every seven days, washing his head and the rest of his body."],[5449,"For Allah, upon every Muslim, is to perform ghusl one day every seven days, washing every part of himself, and to touch perfume if his family has any."],[5450,"I met the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, those among them who witnessed Badr or gave the pledge under the tree. When Friday came and one of them wanted to go out, he performed ghusl as he would for janabah, wore his best clothes, and applied perfume if he had any."],[5451,"Whoever trims his nails on Friday, clips his moustache, and uses the siwak has completed the Friday."],[5452,"They heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a Friday among Fridays, while he was on the minbar, saying: \"O assembly of Muslims, this is a day which Allah has made a festival for the Muslims, so perform ghusl on it with water. Whoever has perfume, it does not harm him to apply some of it. And you should use this siwak.\""],[5453,"He heard Ibn 'Abbas being asked about ghusl on Friday. He said: \"Perform ghusl, and if your family has perfume, it does not harm you to apply some of it.\" 'Ata' said: \"Without considering one who leaves it sinful.\" He said: I said to 'Ata': \"Do you dislike leaving it on that day if you find it?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[5454,"He mentioned the saying of the Prophet ﷺ about ghusl on Friday. He said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: \"And should he touch perfume or oil if his family has it?\" He said: \"I do not know it.\""],[5455,"I asked 'Ata', saying to him: \"Is ghusl on Friday obligatory?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[5456,"Ghusl on Friday is obligatory, like the ghusl for janabah. A man said to him: 'From the Prophet ﷺ?' He said: 'No,' and he became angry."],[5457,"He would not go out to the Friday prayer except that he had oiled and perfumed himself, unless he was in a state of ihram."],[5458,"The Messenger of God ﷺ made ghusl obligatory on Friday for every one who has reached puberty."],[5459,"I heard Umar b. al-Khattab, regarding something he said: 'Then I would be more incapable than one who does not perform ghusl on Friday.'"],[5460,"I love to bathe after five things: the bath, major ritual impurity, cupping, shaving, and on Friday.” He said: I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: “They did not consider any bath obligatory except the bath for major ritual impurity, but they recommended the bath on Friday.”"],[5461,"Whoever clips his nails on Friday, God removes illness from him and brings healing to him."],[5462,"Whoever performs wudu on Friday, it is valid and good; but whoever performs ghusl, it is better."],[5463,"Whoever performs ablution on Friday, it is sufficient and excellent, and whoever performs ghusl, it is better."],[5464,"5349 5313 - Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from a man, from Abu Nadra, from Jabir b. 'Abd Allah, from the Prophet ﷺ, something similar. Hidden text: Whoever performs wudu' on Friday, it suffices and is good, and whoever performs ghusl, it is better."],[5465,"Whoever does not perform ghusl on Friday, let him wash thoroughly, meaning wash his lower areas."],[5466,"People were only workers for themselves,” and it was said, “If only you would perform ghusl.”"],[5467,"Ghusl on Friday is sunnah."],[5468,"Ibn Umar used to perform a single ghusl for both janabah and Friday."],[5469,"Three things are incumbent upon every Muslim on Friday: ghusl, using the siwak, and applying perfume if he can find any."],[5470,"They used to recommend that if a man performed ghusl at the beginning of the day on Friday and then invalidated his ablution, he should perform another ghusl."],[5471,"He used to recommend that he perform a fresh ghusl with which to pray the Friday prayer."],[5472,"If he performs ghusl at the beginning of the day on Friday before going out, and then invalidates his ablution, then wudu suffices him."],[5473,"If a man performs ghusl on Friday after dawn breaks, it suffices him, and if he invalidates his ablution, he performs wudu."],[5474,"He would invalidate his ablution on Friday after the ghusl, then perform wudu and not repeat the ghusl."],[5475,"He would not perform ghusl while traveling on Friday."],[5476,"He would not perform ghusl on Friday while traveling, nor pray the Duha prayer while traveling."],[5477,"I never saw him perform ghusl while traveling on Friday at all."],[5478,"I was with Talha b. Abd Allah on a journey, and when Friday came he ordered me to screen him, so he performed ghusl and said: 'Screen me from the direction of the qiblah.' He said: 'Then he screened me and I performed ghusl.'"],[5479,"They used to perform ghusl while traveling on Friday."],[5480,"Would one of you not take two garments for his Friday, besides his two work garments? He said: And they used to wear striped cloaks."],[5481,"People used to come to the congregation wearing one striped cloak or two, which he would tie around himself. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: “What is wrong with one of you—or what is wrong with you—if he can afford it, not to take two garments for his Friday besides his two work garments?”"],[5482,"He used to wear on every eid day a striped Yemeni cloak of his, made of hibara."],[5483,"The sunnah of Friday is the ritual bath, the tooth-stick, perfume, and wearing your cleanest clothes."],[5484,"Regarding the words of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: “Take your adornment at every mosque” [7:31], he said: “It means clothing.” He said: And Tawus said: “It is the shumlah, part of adornment.”"],[5485,"I said to Ata: If I go early on Friday and leave at midday? He said: “If it is winter, no; but if it is summer, yes, until the shadows lean over.”"],[5486,"Friday is all prayer. He says: “He prays half the day for Allah.” Ma‘mar said: I kept hearing that from others; they used to say: “Prayer until the afternoon.”"],[5487,"Friday is all prayer."],[5488,"When a man enters the mosque on Friday, let him say: “O Allah, make me the best of those who turn to You, the nearest of those who draw near to You, and the most successful of those who ask You and seek from You.” He said: And it was said: The best of people on Friday is the one who sends the most blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ."],[5489,"Send abundant blessings upon me on Friday."],[5490,"The adhan on Friday in the past was only one, then the iqamah. That adhan was called when the imam came out, and the imam would not stand upright where he was to deliver the sermon until the mu’adhdhin finished, or around that time. That is when trade becomes forbidden, and that is when the first adhan is called. As for the adhan that is called now before the imam comes out and sits on the minbar, it is invalid."],[5491,"The first to add the adhan in Madinah was Uthman."],[5492,"Uthman was the first to add the first adhan on Friday. When the people became numerous, he added it, and it was called on al-Zawra'. He said: \"As for the first to add it in our lands, it was al-Hajjaj.\""],[5493,"The adhan on Friday during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Umar was a single adhan until the imam came out. When it was the time of Uthman, the people became numerous, so he added the first adhan, intending that the people prepare for the Friday prayer."],[5494,"The adhan on Friday during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ was a single adhan, when the imam came out; then the iqama was made after the sermon."],[5495,"I saw Ibn al-Zubayr: no adhan was called for him until he sat on the pulpit, and only one adhan was called for him on Friday."],[5496,"About a man who came when the imam had already prayed on Friday, he said: \"He makes the iqama, because he is praying a prayer other than the imam's.\""],[5497,"\"So proceed to the remembrance of God\" (Q. 62:9), and it is like His saying: \"Indeed, your striving is diverse\" (Q. 92:4). Ma'mar said: I heard someone else say: If you are engaged in it, then you are engaged in it. He says: \"If you are preparing for it, then you are striving toward it.\""],[5498,"Regarding His saying: \"So hasten to the remembrance of God\" (Q. 62:9), he said: \"The going is walking.\""],[5499,"Indeed, Umar died not reciting this verse in Surat al-Jumu'ah except as: \"So proceed to the remembrance of God.\""],[5500,"\"When the call is made for prayer on Friday, then hasten to the remembrance of God\" (Q. 62:9). Abdullah said: If I recited it as \"hasten\", I would hasten until my cloak fell. But he used to recite it as \"proceed\"."],[5501,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to recite it as \"So proceed to the remembrance of God.\""],[5502,"The coming out of the imam cuts off prayer; his speech cuts off talk."],[5503,"Umar used to come and sit on the pulpit while the muezzin was calling the adhan, and we would be talking. When the muezzin finished his adhan, our talk would cease."],[5504,"I asked al-Zuhrī about people's speech when the imam descends and before the prayer, and he said: There is no harm in that. A person with him disapproved of that. Al-Zuhrī said: The Messenger of God ﷺ used to be spoken to when he descended from the pulpit."],[5505,"I saw ʿAṭāʾ speaking when the imam descended and before the prayer."],[5506,"I asked ʿAlqama: When is speech disliked on Friday? He said: When the imam delivers the sermon, or he said: When the imam comes out—he was uncertain. I said: What do you think of a man reading to himself? He said: Perhaps that does not harm him."],[5507,"He used to forbid speech after the imam's descent from the pulpit and before the prayer."],[5508,"Ṭāwūs spoke to them after Sulaymān b. ʿAbd al-Malik's descent on Friday."],[5509,"Ibn ʿAbbās and Saʿīd b. Zayd spoke on Friday after the imam came out and before he delivered the sermon, while they were beside the pulpit and ʿUmar was on the pulpit."],[5510,"There is no harm in speech on Friday while the imam is on the pulpit and the muezzin is calling the adhān. Maʿmar said: Someone who heard al-Ḥasan informed me that he said: Silence is recommended."],[5511,"When the imam comes out on Friday and you are praying, do not sit until the imam sits. He said: I said: What if the imam comes out while I am praying standing; does it harm me not to sit as long as he is walking, if he has not sat and I am standing? He said: No."],[5512,"There is no harm in speech while the imam is seated on the pulpit and the muezzins are calling the adhān. Attentive silence is not obligatory until the imam speaks."],[5513,"Regarding two men entering the mosque while the imam is delivering the Friday sermon, he said: They may speak in the mosque as long as they have not sat down."],[5514,"5399 5363 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihāb, similar to it. Matn abbreviated: Regarding two men entering the mosque while the imam is delivering the Friday sermon, he said: They may speak in the mosque as long as they have not sat down."],[5515,"Ibn ʿUmar used to pray on Friday, and when he expected the imam to come out, he would sit before his coming out."],[5516,"The people on Friday are of three types: a man who attends it with calmness, dignity, and silence—that is the one forgiven between the two Fridays. He said: I think he said: And an additional three days. He said: And a witness who attends it with idle talk—that is his share from it. And a man who prays after the imam comes out—that is not sunnah; if He wills, He gives him, and if He wills, He withholds from him."],[5517,"When the Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit on Friday, he said, \"Sit down.\" A man from the Ansar heard the Prophet's ﷺ words while he was on the road and had not entered the mosque, so he sat down among the Banu Ghanm. He said: When the prayer was established, the man entered, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him, \"Did you not go (to the mosque)?\" So he told him the news, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him, \"Good.\""],[5518,"It reached me that Ibn Rawaha heard the Prophet ﷺ while he was on the road saying, \"Sit down,\" so he sat down in the road. The Prophet ﷺ passed by him and said to him, \"What is the matter with you?\" He said, \"I heard you say, 'Sit down,' so I sat down.\" The Prophet ﷺ said to him, \"May God increase you in obedience.\""],[5519,"While the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon, he said, \"Sit down.\" Ibn Mas'ud heard him and sat down at the door of the mosque, inside the mosque. The Prophet ﷺ said to him, \"Come here, O 'Abd Allah.\""],[5520,"What necessitates silence on Friday? He said: His saying: \"When the Qur'an is recited, listen to it and be silent.\" He said: \"Thus they claimed regarding prayer and on Friday.\" He said: I said: And silence for the one listening to the sermon is like silence for the one listening to the Qur'an? He said: \"Yes.\""],[5521,"I said to 'Ata': May I glorify and declare tahlil on Friday while I understand the sermon? He said: \"No, except something small, and keep it between you and yourself.\" It was said to him: May a person remember God while the imam is delivering the sermon on the day of 'Arafah or the day of Fitr, and he understands the words of the imam? He said: \"No, all of that is an 'eid, so do not speak unless the imam goes into other than the remembrance of God.\""],[5522,"When the imam asks for rain, then supplicate; he commands you then."],[5523,"The reward of the silent one who does not hear the sermon is like the reward of the silent one who hears the sermon."],[5524,"He used to say in his sermon: It is rare for the imam to omit saying when he stands: Listen and be silent, for the silent one who does not hear the sermon has the like of what the listening silent one has. When the prayer is established, straighten the rows and align the shoulders, for the straightness of the row is part of the completeness of the prayer. Then he does not say takbir until men come whom he has entrusted with straightening the rows and inform him that they are straight, then he says takbir."],[5525,"I read my portion when I do not listen to the sermon on Friday."],[5526,"Al-Zuhri was asked about glorification and takbir while the imam is delivering the sermon. He said: \"Silence used to be commanded.\" He said: I said: The imam goes into other than the remembrance of God on Friday? He said: \"Speak if you wish.\" Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said: If they innovate, do not innovate."],[5527,"If I cannot hear the imam, may I declare tahlil, takbir, glorify, supplicate to God, and supplicate for my family, naming them and naming my debtor? He said: \"Yes.\" He said: I said: Even if the imam has not supplicated? He said: \"Yes.\""],[5528,"Speaking is prohibited while the imam is on the pulpit, and if he goes into other than that, the remembrance of God [is then permitted]."],[5529,"On Friday, when the imam is delivering the sermon on the pulpit, no one should supplicate for anything, nor remember God, except if the imam remembers [Him]."],[5530,"He disliked on Friday, while the imam is on the pulpit, fidgeting, movement, and yawning. He said: \"People cannot help but do that on Friday due to the length of the sermon.\""],[5531,"Someone who heard Ikrimah narrated to me that he forbade turning over pebbles and cracking the fingers on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon."],[5532,"When you come to Friday prayer, be silent, do not fidget with pebbles, and if a man near you is speaking, nudge him, and if he is far away, gesture to him."],[5533,"When Abd Allah b. Atik al-Ansari and his companions killed Sallam b. Abi al-Huqayq al-A'war, one of the Jews, they entered the mosque while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon on Friday. When he saw them, he said: \"May the faces prosper.\""],[5534,"Take control of the dough, for it is the better of the two quarters, or he said: \"the better of the two flours.\" Hisham said: \"He saw it as his duty to command them with what he would command his own family.\""],[5535,"I saw Uthman sitting on the pulpit on Friday while the muezzins were calling the adhan, and he was asking the people about their prices and their news."],[5536,"I said to Ata: Were they careful not to mix the sermon with anything except the remembrance of God Almighty? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Did they recite the tashahhud in the sermons on Friday? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Then why are we called al-Hamidin? He said: \"We say: Praise be to God, Lord of the worlds.\" I said: What about seeking rain or seeking healing? He said: \"No harm.\""],[5537,"After everything the imam says on the pulpit, if he commands good, forbids evil, [mentions] selling, buying, a measure, or a weight, it is remembrance."],[5538,"The Prophet ﷺ would supplicate on the pulpit on Friday, and the people would say ameen."],[5539,"Umar b. al-Khattab said to a man while he was on the pulpit on Friday: \"Did you buy for us? And did you bring us this?\" and he gestured with a fingertip of his fingers."],[5540,"I said to 'Ata': people talking to the ruler while he is delivering the sermon, singling him out for a conversation, or asking him about something of remembrance? He said: \"I dislike that.\" He said: I said: What about people talking to the imam while he is on the pulpit, praising him? He said: \"I dislike that too. Friday is only for remembrance.\""],[5541,"I asked al-Zuhri about people facing the imam on Friday. He said: \"That is what they used to do.\""],[5542,"Ibn 'Umar used to face the imam on Friday."],[5543,"Shurayh used to face the imam on Friday."],[5544,"'Ubayd b. 'Umayr came to narrate stories here—and he pointed toward the direction of Banu Makhzum—while Sinan b. Ya'la or Sa'id b. Ya'la was facing the House. Ya'la called him and said: What made you do what you did? Face the remembrance. Then 'Abbad b. Abi 'Abbad said: He is Sinan b. Ya'la."],[5545,"I said to 'Ata': What about someone who is level with the pulpit—should he face the imam and turn away from the House? He said: \"Yes, he should face the House.\""],[5546,"I was sitting with Ibn 'Umar while people were asking him questions, and 'Ubayd b. 'Umayr was narrating stories. Ibn 'Umar said: \"Let us and our reminder be.\""],[5547,"I entered the mosque and prayed the afternoon prayer with Ibn 'Umar. Then he sat down, and his companions gathered around him in a circle. He turned his back toward the storyteller. He said: Then he launched into narrating hadith. He said: The storyteller raised his hand to supplicate, but Ibn 'Umar did not raise his hand."],[5548,"I said to 'Ata': What about this storyteller's narrating, other than the imam's sermon on Friday—may I remember Allah while I hear him and understand? He said: \"Yes, and sit with him as long as you wish, and stand when you wish, and raise your voice with some remembrance.\" I said: What if someone sneezes and praises Allah—should I say 'yarhamuk Allah' to him? He said: \"Yes, by my life.\" I said: May I and another person talk while we are listening to him? He said: \"Yes, but for you to glorify Allah and remember Him is dearer to me.\""],[5549,"I said to 'Ata': Has it reached you that silence is not obligatory during battle? He said: Yes, by my life, it is obligatory. Then he recited: \"When you meet those who disbelieve advancing in battle, do not turn your backs to them.\"—and remember Allah. He said: So remembrance became obligatory on that day. He said: \"And there is no talking on that day except remembrance.\" I said: Do you say the remembrance out loud? He said: \"Yes.\""],[5550,"When the imam comes out on Friday, separate with some speech before he delivers the sermon. I said: If the imam greets and I return the greeting, does that count as a separation? He said: \"I would like you to add also the words of greeting in the Quran.\""],[5551,"The first one to narrate stories was Tamim al-Dari in the time of 'Umar. He asked his permission to stand every Friday, and he permitted him, so he would stand. He said: Then he asked him for another standing, and he granted it. When it was the time of 'Uthman, he asked for another standing, so he would narrate stories on Friday three times.\" Ma'mar said: I heard someone other than al-Zuhri say: When 'Umar passed by him while he was narrating stories, he would pass the sword over his throat."],[5552,"The first one to narrate stories was Tamim al-Dari in the time of 'Umar. He asked his permission to stand every Friday, and he permitted him, so he would stand. He said: Then he asked him for another standing, and he granted it. When it was the time of 'Uthman, he asked for another standing, so he would narrate stories on Friday three times.\" Ma'mar said: I heard someone other than al-Zuhri say: When 'Umar passed by him while he was narrating stories, he would pass the sword over his throat."],[5553,"He used to come out of the mosque and a man would meet him and say: What is the matter with you, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman? He would say: \"The storyteller drove me out.\" Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Ibn al-Musayyib used to hear them reciting the prostration verse, and he would not prostrate, saying: \"I did not sit with them.\""],[5554,"He used to come out of the mosque and a man would meet him and say: What is the matter with you, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman? He would say: \"The storyteller drove me out.\" Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Ibn al-Musayyib used to hear them reciting the prostration verse, and he would not prostrate, saying: \"I did not sit with them.\""],[5555,"'Ubayd b. 'Umayr entered upon 'A'ishah and she asked: Who is this? He said: I am 'Ubayd b. 'Umayr. She said: \"'Umayr b. Qatadah?\" He said: Yes, O my mother. She said: \"Has it not reached me that you sit and people sit with you?\" He said: Yes, O Mother of the Believers. She said: \"Beware of making people despair and destroying them.\""],[5556,"He narrated stories and said in his storytelling: The one who says is truthful: The one who dies and finds rest is not dead; rather, the dead is the dead among the living. Ma'mar said: I saw 'Ata' al-Khurasani narrating stories according to the Sunnah."],[5557,"He narrated stories and said in his storytelling: The one who says is truthful: The one who dies and finds rest is not dead; rather, the dead is the dead among the living. Ma'mar said: I saw 'Ata' al-Khurasani narrating stories according to the Sunnah."],[5558,"Ibn 'Umar would not sit with the storytellers except the storyteller of the congregation."],[5559,"I saw Ibn 'Umar raising his hands at the storyteller. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And I saw him—meaning Ma'mar—do that."],[5560,"I saw Ibn 'Umar raising his hands at the storyteller. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And I saw him—meaning Ma'mar—do that."],[5561,"'A'ishah sent to Marwan complaining about al-Sa'ib, who was a storyteller, and said: \"By Allah, I cannot speak to my servant.\" Marwan forbade him, but he returned, so she complained about him again. Marwan met him and struck him on the chest, or said: \"He slapped him.\""],[5562,"It reached me that 'Ali passed by a storyteller and said: \"Do you know the abrogating from the abrogated?\" He said: No. He said: \"You have perished and caused others to perish.\" He said: And he passed by another and said: \"What is your kunyah?\" He said: Abu Yahya. He said: \"Rather, you are Abu 'Irifuni.\""],[5563,"A storyteller was mentioned to Ibn Mas'ud who sat at night and told the people: Say such-and-such, say such-and-such. He said: \"When you see him, inform me.\" So they informed him. He said: 'Abdullah came veiled and said: \"Whoever knows me knows me, and whoever does not know me, I am 'Abdullah b. Mas'ud. Do you know that you are more guided than Muhammad and his companions? Indeed, you are clinging to the tail of misguidance.\""],[5564,"It reached 'Abdullah b. Mas'ud that some people sat from Maghrib until 'Isha', glorifying Allah and saying: Say such-and-such, say such-and-such. 'Abdullah said: \"If they sit, inform me about them.\" When they sat, they informed him. He went when they informed him, entered, and sat with them wearing a burnus. They began their glorification. 'Abdullah uncovered the burnus from his head and said: I am 'Abdullah b. Mas'ud. The people fell silent. Then he said: \"You have brought a dark innovation, or you have surpassed the companions of Muhammad ﷺ in knowledge.\" A man from Banu Tamim said: We have not brought a dark innovation, nor have we surpassed the companions of Muhammad ﷺ in knowledge. 'Amr b. 'Utbah b. Farqad said: I seek Allah's forgiveness, O Ibn Mas'ud, and I repent to Him. He said: He ordered them to disperse. Ibn Mas'ud saw two circles in the mosque of Kufah and said: \"Which of you was before its companion?\" One of them said: We were. He said to the other: \"Move over to them,\" and he made them one."],[5565,"Ibn Mas'ud heard of people going out to the desert with a storyteller who said: Glorify Allah. Then he said: \"I am 'Abdullah b. Mas'ud. You have surpassed the companions of Muhammad ﷺ in knowledge, or you have brought a dark innovation. If you follow their way, they have gone far ahead; if you oppose them, you have gone far astray. What are you enumerating of Allah's command?\""],[5566,"Abu Bakr looked at a storyteller who had gone on too long and said: \"If this one were told: Stand and pray two rak'ahs and recite in them such-and-such, he would tire of that.\""],[5567,"The sermon is delivered on Friday, whether short or long."],[5568,"The sermon on Friday is before the prayer."],[5569,"If you say to your companion, 'Listen,' while the imam is delivering the sermon, you have engaged in idle talk."],[5570,"Ibn Shihab said: And 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to me, from Ibrahim b. 'Abd Allah b. Qariz, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ the like of it. Hidden text: If you say to your companion, 'Listen,' while the imam is delivering the sermon, you have engaged in idle talk. Hidden chain: 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab said: And 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to me, from Ibrahim b. 'Abd Allah b. Qariz, from Abu Hurayrah."],[5571,"If you say to your companion, 'Listen,' while the imam is delivering the sermon on Friday, you have engaged in idle talk."],[5572,"'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah b. 'Ubayd Allah b. 'Utbah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Then he mentioned the like of the first hadith of Ibn Jurayj. Hidden text: If you say to your companion, 'Listen,' while the imam is delivering the sermon on Friday, you have engaged in idle talk."],[5573,"If you say to the people, 'Be quiet,' on Friday while they are speaking and the imam is delivering the sermon, you have engaged in idle talk against yourself."],[5574,"If he says, 'Hush,' he has engaged in idle talk, and if he engages in idle talk, he has invalidated his Jumu'ah."],[5575,"Whoever catches the sermon has caught the Jumu'ah, and whoever does not catch the sermon has caught the prayer. Whoever draws near to the imam and listens and remains silent will have a double portion of reward, and whoever does not listen and does not remain silent will have a double portion of sin. And whoever says, 'Hush,' while the imam is delivering the sermon has engaged in idle talk, and whoever engages in idle talk has no Jumu'ah—or he said, 'then he has nothing.'"],[5576,"The Prophet ﷺ recited the verse of Jumu'ah, and Ibn Mas'ud said, 'O Ubayy b. Ka'b, is this how you recite it?' Ubayy remained silent, and they were in the Friday prayer. When the Prophet ﷺ finished, Ubayy said to Ibn Mas'ud, 'You did not perform Jumu'ah today.' So he went to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him, and the Prophet ﷺ said, 'Ubayy spoke the truth.'"],[5577,"I said to 'Ata': Do you know of anything that invalidates the Jumu'ah of Islam such that he must pray four rak'ahs, from speech, or stepping over people's necks, or anything else? He said, 'No.'"],[5578,"It is said: Whoever speaks, his speech is his share of the Jumu'ah—meaning, from the reward of the Jumu'ah. As for completing four rak'ahs, then no."],[5579,"While the Prophet ﷺ was on the pulpit on Friday, he recited a verse, and Abu Dharr heard it. Abu Dharr said to Ubayy b. Ka'b, 'When was this verse revealed?' Ubayy remained silent three times, each time remaining silent, until when the Prophet ﷺ descended, Ubayy said to Abu Dharr, 'You have nothing from your Jumu'ah except what has already passed.' Then Abu Dharr asked the Prophet ﷺ about that, and he said, 'Ubayy spoke the truth.'"],[5580,"He certainly regarded it as vain talk for a man to gesture to another man with his hand, meaning: 'Be quiet,' when he spoke."],[5581,"Ibn Umar threw pebbles at two men who were talking while the imam was delivering the Friday sermon."],[5582,"Ibn Umar threw pebbles at two men who were talking while the imam was delivering the Friday sermon."],[5583,"Ibn Umar saw a beggar asking while the imam was delivering the Friday sermon, and threw pebbles at him."],[5584,"He saw Ibn Umar gesturing to a man on Friday while the imam was delivering the sermon."],[5585,"He saw a beggar while the imam was delivering the sermon, and gestured with his hand, meaning: 'Be quiet.'"],[5586,"I saw him gesturing to Muhammad b. Sa'd while al-Hajjaj was delivering the sermon. He was talking, so he gestured to him, meaning: 'Be quiet.'"],[5587,"I saw Amir al-Sha'bi and Abu Burdah talking while al-Hajjaj was delivering the sermon, when he said: 'May Allah curse the liars, and may Allah curse...' I said: 'Are you two talking while the imam is delivering the sermon?' They said: 'We were not commanded to listen to this one.'"],[5588,"I saw Ibrahim al-Nakha'i talking to a man while the imam was delivering the Friday sermon in the time of al-Hajjaj."],[5589,"A man may drink water if he is thirsty while the imam is delivering the Friday sermon."],[5590,"If a person sneezes on Friday and praises Allah, and you hear him and you hear the sermon, do not say 'yarhamuk Allah' to him. Even if you do not hear the sermon, also do not say it to him."],[5591,"If a person sneezes on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon, and he praises Allah, and you hear him and hear the sermon, then say 'yarhamuk Allah' to him silently to yourself. But if you do not hear the sermon, then say it to him and let him hear you."],[5592,"Regarding the man who sneezes on Friday, he said: “Then respond to him with a prayer for mercy.”"],[5593,"I witnessed ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī responding to the sneezer while the imam was delivering the sermon on Friday."],[5594,"My father sent me to Ibn al-Musayyib to ask him about the man who sneezes on Friday while the imam is delivering the Friday sermon—should I respond to him? He said: “No.”"],[5595,"Regarding the man who greets another man while he is in the sermon, they both said: “He should return the greeting and make him hear it.”"],[5596,"A man should not return the greeting while the imam is delivering the sermon on Friday."],[5597,"He returns the greeting while the imam is delivering the sermon."],[5598,"If a man greets on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon, then if you can hear the sermon, return the greeting within yourself; but if you cannot hear the sermon, then return the greeting and make him hear it."],[5599,"When the scrolls are read on Friday, do not speak to anyone; if they introduce something new, do not introduce it."],[5600,"He disliked the reading of scrolls on Friday. He said: “If they are read, do not speak.” He said: “Reading scrolls on Friday is an innovation they have introduced.”"],[5601,"Saʿīd b. Jubayr used to speak when the scrolls were read on Friday."],[5602,"I said: If the scrolls are read while I am near the pulpit and I hear their recitation, may I glorify Allah, declare His oneness, remember Allah within myself, and supplicate for my family, naming them by their names, and say: ‘O Allah, extract for me from my debtor’ naming him by his name? He said: “Yes.”"],[5603,"He disliked that a man should lean on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon, except due to illness, old age, or sickness."],[5604,"Ibn 'Umar, when the imam lengthened the sermon, would lean on me."],[5605,"Abu Hurayrah would lean on him on Friday while the imam was delivering the sermon."],[5606,"I said to 'Ata': For one who did not attend the sermon but heard it—is it Jumu'ah for him, and he sat in the shade and kept away from the preacher? He said: 'Glory be to Allah! Yes, and what is wrong with him that it should not be Jumu'ah for him? He went out for Allah desiring none but Allah.' 'Ata' said: 'And if he comes near him, that is dearer to me; if he endures the sun, that is better for him.'"],[5607,"I said to 'Ata': 'The mu'adhdhins sit in the minaret overlooking the mosque and do not sit with the people; may they shorten [the prayer]?' He said: 'Yes.' 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And I asked Ma'mar about it, and he said: 'They may shorten.'"],[5608,"I said to 'Ata': 'The mu'adhdhins sit in the minaret overlooking the mosque and do not sit with the people; may they shorten [the prayer]?' He said: 'Yes.' 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And I asked Ma'mar about it, and he said: 'They may shorten.'"],[5609,"'Whoever does not pray on Friday in the mosque has no Jumu'ah.' Ma'mar said: 'If he is compelled, al-Hasan saw no harm in praying it in the road or in the courtyard of the mosque. Wherever he is compelled because of tightness or crowding, let him pray two rak'ahs.' He said: 'We said to al-Hasan: \"But there is dung of animals there,\" and he said: \"He prays.\"'"],[5610,"I came once with my father, and we found the mosque full on Friday, so we prayed following the people's prayer in a house near the mosque with a road between them. He said: I think he said: In the house of Humayd b. 'Abd al-Rahman."],[5611,"He saw Anas b. Malik pray Jumu'ah in the house of Humayd b. 'Abd al-Rahman following the imam's prayer, with a road between them."],[5612,"I prayed with Zirr, and he led me, and I had missed Jumu'ah. I asked Ibrahim, and he said: ''Abd Allah did that with 'Alqamah and al-Aswad.' Sufyan said: 'And sometimes al-A'mash and I did it.'"],[5613,"He used to dislike it if people missed Jumu'ah that they should pray in congregation. And Sufyan's statement is dearer to me."],[5614,"He disliked that they should pray Jumu'ah in congregation."],[5615,"Zayd b. Thabit came to the mosque on Friday and met the people dispersing, so he entered a house and prayed there. It was said to him: 'Why did you not come to the mosque?' He said: 'Whoever is not shy before people is not shy before Allah.'"],[5616,"He used to command one who missed Jumu'ah to go to the mosque and pray in it."],[5617,"A man met the people on Friday after they had dispersed. Hudhayfah said to him: \"Avoid the paths of the people, for there is no good in one who has no shame.\""],[5618,"Whoever is unable to pray on Friday due to crowding, he prays four rak'ahs when they are crowded and he cannot bow or prostrate. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan say informed me: \"He bows two rak'ahs, for he has entered with them in their prayer.\""],[5619,"If you wish, prostrate on the back of a man, and if you wish, when the imam stands up, then prostrate."],[5620,"A man prostrates on the back of a man when he finds no place to prostrate on."],[5621,"When crowding becomes intense on Friday, let one of you prostrate on the back of his brother."],[5622,"When the heat harms one of you on Friday, let him prostrate on his garment."],[5623,"When crowding becomes intense, prostrate on the leg of a man. Sufyan said: If you cannot prostrate on the leg of a man, then stand until the people stand, then you prostrate."],[5624,"When people crowd on Friday and a man is crowded so that he does not bow and does not prostrate while he is standing, then when he is able, he only has to bow and prostrate, and he is in the position of one sleeping, and the imam's recitation suffices him."],[5625,"Whoever is distressed by heat on Friday in the mosque, let him pray on his garment, and whoever people crowd, let him prostrate on the back of his brother."],[5626,"When a man catches one rak'ah on Friday, he prays another rak'ah to it."],[5627,"When a man catches one rak'ah on Friday, he prays another rak'ah to it, and if he finds them sitting, he prays four."],[5628,"When a man catches one rak'ah on Friday, he prays another rak'ah to it, and if he finds them sitting, he prays four."],[5629,"If a man catches on Friday one rak'ah, he prays to it another rak'ah. If he finds them sitting, he prays four."],[5630,"If a man catches on Friday one rak'ah, he prays to it another rak'ah. If he finds them sitting, he prays four."],[5631,"If a man catches on Friday one rak'ah, he prays to it another rak'ah. If he finds them sitting, he prays four."],[5632,"If a man catches on Friday one rak'ah, he prays to it another rak'ah. If he finds them sitting, he prays four."],[5633,"If a man catches on Friday one rak'ah, he prays to it another rak'ah. If he finds them sitting, he prays four."],[5634,"Whoever catches the rak'ah has caught the Friday prayer, and whoever does not catch the rak'ah should pray four."],[5635,"Whoever catches one rak'ah of the prayer has caught the prayer."],[5636,"Whoever misses the last rak'ah should pray four."],[5637,"If he finds them sitting at the end of the prayer on Friday, he prays two rak'ahs. Ma'mar said: Qatadah said: He prays four. It was said to Qatadah: Ibn Mas'ud came upon them sitting at the end of the prayer, and he said to his companions, 'Sit, you have caught it, if Allah wills.' Qatadah said: 'Infaya' [1] meaning: you have caught the reward."],[5638,"If a man catches the imam on Friday while he is sitting and has not yet said the salam, he should pray according to his prayer, two rak'ahs. He is in the position of a traveler. Al-Thawri said: And four is more preferable to us because he has missed the Friday prayer."],[5639,"A man came to 'Imran b. al-Husayn and said: A man has missed the Friday prayer; how much should he pray? 'Imran said: Why would he miss the Friday prayer? When the man turned away, 'Imran said: Indeed, if I missed the Friday prayer, I would pray four."],[5640,"On Friday, the angels stand at the doors of the mosque and write down the people according to their ranks: the first, then the next. If a man among them is delayed from his rank, the angels supplicate for him, saying: 'O Allah, if he is sick, heal him; O Allah, if he has a need, fulfill his need.' They continue in this manner until when the imam comes out, the pages are folded and then sealed. Whoever comes after the imam descends has caught the prayer but has not caught the Friday prayer."],[5641,"Whoever catches the khutbah has caught the prayer."],[5642,"The khutbah takes the place of the two rak'ahs. Whoever misses the khutbah prays four."],[5643,"I said to 'Ata': What is it that, when a person catches it on Friday, he shortens, and otherwise he completes the prayer? He said: \"The khutbah.\" He said: I said: So I did not sit until the imam descended? He said: \"He has not caught the imam.\" He said: I said: So I sat before he descended? He said: \"That is sufficient for you; you have caught it.\""],[5644,"A man said to 'Ata': I did not catch the khutbah except when he was discussing the measure and the balance. He said: \"God has commanded that, so that is part of the remembrance; so shorten.\""],[5645,"Whoever does not catch the khutbah prays four."],[5646,"About a man who had a nosebleed while the imam was giving the khutbah, so he got up and performed ablution but did not return until the imam had prayed and finished. He said: \"He prays two rak'ahs; he already attended the khutbah.\""],[5647,"He prays four rak'ahs."],[5648,"About a man who did not attend the khutbah and came when the imam stood for prayer, then the imam had a mishap and wanted to put him forward. He said: \"No one should come forward except one who attended the khutbah. If he had already prayed part of his prayer with the imam, there is no harm in putting him forward; he should complete two rak'ahs. As for the imam who had the mishap and then returned: if he had spoken, he prays four; if he had not spoken, he prays two. If the imam puts forward a man who did not attend anything of his khutbah or his prayer with him, he prays four.\""],[5649,"About a man who did not attend the khutbah and came when the imam stood for prayer, then the imam had a mishap and wanted to put him forward. He said: \"No one should come forward except one who attended the khutbah. If he had already prayed part of his prayer with the imam, there is no harm in putting him forward; he should complete two rak'ahs. As for the imam who had the mishap and then returned: if he had spoken, he prays four; if he had not spoken, he prays two. If the imam puts forward a man who did not attend anything of his khutbah or his prayer with him, he prays four.\""],[5650,"About a man who prayed one rak'ah with the imam on Friday, then had a mishap and left without speaking. He said: \"Yes, he performs ablution and completes what remains; if he speaks, he prays four.\""],[5651,"I said to 'Ata': Suppose you were near the minbar while the imam was giving the khutbah, and you were called for help concerning a child, would you get up and leave the Friday prayer? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: What about a child, a brother, or a cousin? He said: \"I would not get up except for something good or a tie of kinship, and the world would not distract me from Friday.\""],[5652,"A call for help was made concerning Sa'id b. Zayd b. 'Amr b. Nufayl on Friday after the day had advanced, so he went out to him and did not attend Friday that day."],[5653,"Ibn 'Umar was called to Sa'id b. Zayd while he was dying, and Ibn 'Umar was standing performing the Friday ghusl. He went to him and left the Friday prayer."],[5654,"Ibn Umar was summoned to Sa'id b. Zayd while he was dying, and Ibn Umar was preparing for the Friday prayer standing, so he went to him and left the Friday prayer."],[5655,"Ibn Umar was called urgently for Sa'id b. Zayd on Friday after the forenoon had risen, so Ibn Umar came to him at al-Aqiq."],[5656,"A man came stepping over people's necks while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the khutbah. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his khutbah and prayer, he said: \"O so-and-so, did you pray the Friday prayer today?\" He said: \"Did you not see me, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Indeed I saw you, and you harmed and delayed.\""],[5657,"A man came stepping over people's necks while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the khutbah. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his khutbah and prayer, he said: \"O so-and-so, did you pray the Friday prayer today?\" He said: \"Did you not see me, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Indeed I saw you, and you harmed and delayed.\""],[5658,"If you see a gap ahead of you before the imam comes out, there is no harm in going to it so long as you do not harm anyone."],[5659,"I said to Ata: If I see a gap ahead of me with people before it, should I step over them to reach it? He said: \"No.\" I said: What if I weave through them to reach it? He said: \"And how?\" I said: As if two men are not touching each other. He said: \"Yes, if you do not step over anyone.\" A person said to him: What if two people are touching their knees, should I step over their knees? He said: \"No.\""],[5660,"I said to Ata: Should I step over people's necks while they are seated and the imam has not yet come out? He said: \"No.\" I said: What if they are standing praying and the imam has not come out, should I weave through the people? He said: \"If you do not push anyone aside, harm them, or precede anyone, then yes; but if any of that occurs, then do not harm anyone.\""],[5661,"The first row on Friday and the foremost row in the path of Allah are equal in merit. Whoever makes a man move from his place, that place's reward is for him."],[5662,"That I pray the Friday prayer at al-Rawha is dearer to me than stepping over people's necks on Friday."],[5663,"I would not love to have red camels and that I missed the Friday prayer; rather, to perform it at the edge of al-Harra is dearer to me than stepping over people's necks when they have taken their seats."],[5664,"I would not love to have red camels and that I missed the Friday prayer; rather, to perform it at the edge of al-Harra is dearer to me than stepping over people's necks when they have taken their seats."],[5665,"A person asked Makhul, while I was listening and he was sitting with Ata, about the words of Allah: \"Only the believers are those who believe in Allah\" up to His words \"and when they are with him on a matter of common concern\". Makhul said: \"It is acted upon now, so it is proper that no one should leave on Friday or in a military expedition until he asks permission of the imam.\" He said: And likewise in any matter of common concern. Do you not see that He says: \"And when they are with him on a matter of common concern\"? Ata then said: By my life, I saw people in the past asking permission of the imam when they stood up while he was delivering the khutbah. I said: How did you see them asking permission? He said: A man would gesture with his hand, and Ata gestured to me with his right hand. I said: He gestures and does not speak? He said: Yes. I said: And when the imam gives permission? He said: He gestures and does not speak. I said: Does a person not put his hand over his nose or his garment? He said: No."],[5666,"Concerning His saying: “And when they are with him upon a collective matter” he said: “On Friday.” Maʿmar said: “And I heard Qatādah say: On Friday, and also on military expeditions.”"],[5667,"The people used to seek permission during the Friday sermon and say “like this,” gesturing with three fingers. When Ziyād took charge, they crowded upon him and he became distressed, so he said: “Whoever holds his nose, that is his permission.”"],[5668,"A man, when he had a need on Friday while the imam was giving the sermon and wanted to leave because something had come upon him urgently, would place his hand over his nose and then leave."],[5669,"Concerning His saying: “And when they are with him upon a collective matter” he said: “In military expeditions and on Friday. The imam’s permission on Friday is to gesture with his hand.”"],[5670,"A man from the Anṣār came on Friday while the Prophet ﷺ was giving the sermon, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “Have you prayed?” He said: “No.” He said: “Then pray two rakʿahs.”"],[5671,"A man came while the Prophet ﷺ was on the pulpit on Friday giving the sermon, and he said to him: “Have you prayed two rakʿahs?” He said: “No.” He said: “Then pray.”"],[5672,"A man called Sulayk from Ghaṭafān came while the Prophet ﷺ was standing giving the sermon, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: “O Sulayk, stand and pray two short rakʿahs.”"],[5673,"I saw him pray two rakʿahs while the imam was giving the sermon on Friday."],[5674,"A man came while the Prophet ﷺ was on the pulpit on Friday giving the sermon, and he said to him: “Have you prayed two rakʿahs?” He said: “No.” He said: “Then pray.”"],[5675,"They asked him about a man praying while the imam is giving the sermon. He said: “Do you not see that if all of them did that, it would be good?”"],[5676,"On Friday he would come to the mosque. If the imam had not yet come out, he prayed two rakʿahs. If he had already come out, he did not pray, but sat with his knees drawn up, faced the imam, and did not turn right or left."],[5677,"I asked Qatādah about a man who arrives while the imam is giving the Friday sermon and he has not yet prayed: should he pray? He said: “As for me, I would rather be sitting.”"],[5678,"I asked him: If you arrive while the imam is delivering the Friday sermon, should you pray two rak‘ahs? He replied: “As for when the imam is delivering the sermon, I would not pray.”"],[5679,"I asked ‘Ata’: I have heard that you pray twelve rak‘ahs before Friday prayer. What has reached you about that? He replied: Umm Habibah daughter of Abu Sufyan informed ‘Anbasah b. Abi Sufyan that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever prays twelve rak‘ahs...”"],[5680,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray two rak‘ahs after Friday prayer in his house."],[5681,"He saw Ibn ‘Umar praying after Friday prayer. He said: “He would move slightly away from his prayer spot, then pray two rak‘ahs, then walk on a little, then pray four rak‘ahs.”"],[5682,"I saw Ibn ‘Umar, when he finished the Friday prayer, move forward slightly from his prayer spot and pray two rak‘ahs, then move forward again and pray four."],[5683,"Ibn Mas‘ud used to pray four rak‘ahs before Friday prayer and four rak‘ahs after it.” Abu Ishaq said: “And ‘Ali used to pray six rak‘ahs after Friday prayer.”"],[5684,"‘Abdullah used to instruct us to pray four rak‘ahs before Friday prayer and four after it, until ‘Ali came to us and instructed us to pray two rak‘ahs after it, then four."],[5685,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray two rak‘ahs after Friday prayer in his house."],[5686,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray two rak‘ahs after Friday prayer in his house."],[5687,"‘Amr b. Shu‘ayb prayed Friday prayer, then prayed two rak‘ahs immediately after it in the mosque. Ibn ‘Umar forbade him from doing that and said: “As for the imam, no. When you have prayed, go back and pray in your house as much as you like, unless you are performing tawaf. As for the people, they may pray in the mosque.”"],[5688,"Whoever among you prays after Friday prayer, let him pray four rak‘ahs."],[5689,"‘Imran b. Husayn prayed Friday prayer with Ziyad, then stood and prayed four rak‘ahs after it. The people said: “He did not consider Ziyad’s prayer valid.” That reached ‘Imran, and he said: “If daggers were plunged into my belly, that would be dearer to me than doing this.” The next Friday, he prayed Friday prayer with him, then sat and did not pray anything until he prayed ‘Asr."],[5690,"I asked al-Hasan b. ‘Ali: “Do the two rak‘ahs of Friday prayer suffice instead of praying anything else?” He said: “Yes.”"],[5691,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: The imam passes the evening in the mosque, so should he pray in it at night or during the day? He said: Yes, that is good."],[5692,"When you have prayed the Friday prayer, do not connect it with two light rakʿahs until you separate between them by moving away or speaking."],[5693,"Nāfiʿ b. Jubayr sent him to al-Sāʾib b. Yazīd to ask him about something Muʿāwiyah had seen him do in prayer. He said: I prayed the Friday prayer with him in the enclosure, and when he said the taslīm I stood in my place and prayed. When he went in he sent for me and said: Do not repeat what you did. When you have prayed the Friday prayer, do not connect it with a prayer until you speak or leave, for the Prophet ﷺ commanded that, and we follow it."],[5694,"Ibn ʿUmar saw a man praying in the place where he had prayed the Friday prayer and forbade him from that, saying: Do I not see you praying in your place?"],[5695,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb saw a man wearing traveling clothes after he had finished the Friday prayer and said: What is the matter with you? He said: I wanted to travel, but I disliked going out until I had prayed. ʿUmar said to him: The Friday prayer does not prevent you from traveling as long as its time has not arrived."],[5696,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb saw a man with traveling gear, and the man said: Today is Friday, and were it not for that I would have set out. ʿUmar said: The Friday prayer does not hold back a traveler, so go out as long as the time for going has not come."],[5697,"Abū ʿUbaydah set out on one of his journeys early in the morning on a Friday, and he did not wait for the prayer."],[5698,"Sālim b. ʿAbdallāh left Makkah on a Friday."],[5699,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set out traveling on a Friday in the forenoon before the prayer."],[5700,"I asked Yaḥyā b. Abī Kathīr: May a man go out on a Friday? He disliked it. I kept telling him of the concession for it, and he said to me: Rarely does a man go out on a Friday but that he sees what he dislikes; and if you looked into that you would find it so."],[5701,"When a man travels on a Friday, the day calls against him, that he not be helped in his need nor accompanied on his journey."],[5703,"I said: Has it reached you that it used to be said: If he spends the evening in a village where Friday prayer is held on the night before Friday, he should not leave until he prays the Friday prayer? He said: That is indeed disliked. I said: What about from Thursday? He said: No, that is daytime, so it does not harm him."],[5704,"He used to pray the morning prayer on Friday in Madinah, then ride to his palace at al-‘Aqiq and not pray the Friday prayer, and between that is less than a barid, or about that."],[5705,"When a person becomes drowsy on Friday, let him rise from that seat of his and sit in another seat, or strike his head three times, for that is only from Satan. He gestured—and there he was clenching his hand, then striking with the edges of the fingers, with the hand still closed, the nails drawn together."],[5706,"Malik b. Abi Sahm informed me that he became drowsy while the imam was delivering the khutbah. He said: “Either Ibn ‘Umar pointed to him, or Ibn ‘Umar gestured to him to rise from that place of his and move back from it.”"],[5707,"When a man becomes drowsy during the Friday prayer while the imam is delivering the khutbah, it is the seat of Satan, so let him rise from it."],[5708,"When a man becomes drowsy on Friday while the imam is delivering the khutbah, he is ordered to rise and sit in a seat other than his own."],[5709,"When a person becomes drowsy on Friday, he leaves his seat. As for stepping over people, no; rather let him shift aside, and let those around him wake him."],[5710,"Ibn ‘Umar used to throw pebbles at those who slept while the imam was delivering the khutbah."],[5712,"I saw Ibn al-Musayyab sitting with his knees drawn up on Friday beside the enclosure while the imam was delivering the khutbah."],[5713,"He saw al-Hasan sitting with his knees drawn up on Friday while the imam was delivering the khutbah."],[5714,"I saw ‘Ata’ sitting with his knees drawn up while the imam was delivering the khutbah on Friday."],[5715,"He used to sit with his knees drawn up on Friday, facing the imam, and not turn to the right or to the left."],[5716,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man from sitting with his knees drawn up on Friday while the imam is delivering the khutbah."],[5717,"There is no day greater in the sight of Allah than Friday. On it Allah decreed the creation of the heavens and the earth, and on it the Hour will occur. The sun does not rise on Friday but that the land, the sea, the rocks, the trees, and everything Allah created are in fear—except the two heavy ones (humans and jinn). And in it there is an hour in which no Muslim asks Allah for anything but that He gives it to him."],[5719,"Allah did not create a day greater than Friday. On it the creation of the heavens and the earth was decreed, and on it the Hour will occur. The sun does not rise on Friday but that at its rising the land, the sea, the rocks, and everything Allah created are alarmed—except the two heavy ones (humans and jinn). And in Friday there is an hour in which a Muslim servant does not ask Allah for anything but that He gives it to him."],[5720,"Abu Hurayrah and Ka'b met. Abu Hurayrah said: In Friday there is an hour in which no Muslim man asks Allah, exalted be He, for good but that He grants it to him. Ka'b said: Shall I not narrate to you about Friday? Ka'b said: \"When Friday comes, the heavens and the earth, the land and the sea, the trees and the moist earth, the water and all creatures are alarmed—except the son of Adam and Satan.\" He said: \"The angels surround the doors of the mosque and write down those who come, first then next. When the imam comes out, they fold up their scrolls. Whoever comes after that comes for the right of Allah and for what is prescribed for him. It is obligatory upon every adult man to perform ghusl on it like his ghusl from janabah. The sun has not risen nor set on a day greater than Friday, and charity on it is greater than on other days.\" Ibn Abbas said: This is the hadith of Abu Hurayrah and Ka'b. I think that if a man's family has perfume, he should touch some of it on that day."],[5721,"The days were presented to me, and I saw Friday. Its splendor and light pleased me, and I saw in it something like a black dot. I said: What is this? It was said: On it the Hour will occur."],[5722,"The days were presented to me, and Friday was presented to me in a mirror—or he said: like a mirror—and I saw in it a black dot. I said: What is this? It was said: On it the Hour will occur."],[5723,"The Prophet ﷺ said to Salman: Do you know what Friday is? On it your father Adam was assembled—that is, his clay was assembled."],[5724,"When Friday comes, the angels sit at the doors of the mosque and write down those who come to Friday prayer. When the imam comes out, the angels fold up the scrolls and enter to listen to the remembrance. He said: The Prophet ﷺ said: The one who comes early to Friday prayer is like one who offers a camel, then like one who offers a cow, then like one who offers a sheep, then like one who offers a chicken, then like one who offers—I think he said—an egg."],[5725,"When Friday comes, the angels sit at the doors of the mosque and write down those who come to Friday prayer. When the imam comes out, the angels fold up the scrolls and enter to listen to the remembrance. He said: The Prophet ﷺ said: The one who comes early to Friday prayer is like one who offers a camel, then like one who offers a cow, then like one who offers a sheep, then like one who offers a chicken, then like one who offers—I think he said—an egg."],[5726,"The sun does not rise nor set on a day better than Friday. There is no creature but that it is alarmed for Friday except these two heavy ones: jinn and humans. At every door of the mosque there are two angels writing down the first then the next. So it is like a man who presents a cow, like a man who presents a sheep, like a man who presents a bird, and like a man who presents an egg. When the imam sits, the scrolls are folded up."],[5727,"When Friday comes, the angels sit at the doors of the mosque and write down people according to their coming. When the imam sits, the scrolls are folded up and the virtues cease. Whoever comes then has come only for the right of the prayer. Their virtue is like the virtue of the one who offers a camel over the one who offers a cow and over the one who offers a sheep."],[5729,"When Friday comes, if one of you performs ghusl as he performs ghusl from janabah, then goes early in the first hour, he will have a reward like that of a camel, and the first hour and its end are equal. Then the second hour is like a bull, and its beginning and end are equal. Then the third is like a horned ram, its beginning and end are equal. Then the fourth hour is like a chicken, its beginning and end are equal. Then like an egg. When the imam sits, the scrolls are folded up and the angels come to listen to the remembrance. Then he is forgiven if he listens and remains silent, for what is between the two Fridays and three additional days."],[5730,"When Friday comes, if one of you washes his head, then performs ghusl, then goes early, then draws near and listens and remains silent, he will have for every step he takes like fasting a year and standing in prayer a year."],[5731,"The Hour will not occur on Saturday, nor on Sunday, nor on Monday, nor on Tuesday, nor on Wednesday, nor on Thursday. Then he was silent."],[5732,"On Friday the Hour will be established."],[5733,"That is the best day on which the sun rises: Friday. On it Adam was created, and on it the Hour will be established. When Allah created Adam, He breathed the spirit into him and it moved through him. Then He breathed into him again, and he sat upright. He sneezed, and Allah placed on his tongue: 'Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds.' The angels said: 'May Allah have mercy on you.'"],[5734,"Whoever performs the major ablution and takes a bath, goes early and arrives early, draws near the imam, and listens attentively, will have for every step he takes the reward of fasting a year and standing in prayer for a year. That is easy for Allah."],[5735,"Indeed, on Friday there is an hour in which no Muslim coincides while praying and asking Allah for something except that He gives it to him."],[5736,"Indeed, on Friday there is an hour—and he indicated with his palm as if he were making it small—no Muslim servant coincides with it asking Allah for something except that He gives it to him. He indicated to us how the Prophet ﷺ indicated: he pressed his fingers together, bent them slightly, then clenched them without spreading them."],[5737,"Indeed, on Friday there is an hour in which no Muslim asks Allah for something while praying except that He gives it to him. He said: Abu Hurayrah would indicate with his hand, making it small."],[5738,"He used to seek the hour in which supplication is answered on Friday after the Asr prayer."],[5739,"I asked al-Zuhri about the hour in which supplication is answered on Friday. He said: 'I have not heard anything about it that I can narrate, except that Ka'b used to say: \"If a person were to divide his entire prayer into Fridays, he would come upon that hour.\"'"],[5740,"A man was seeking the hour in which supplication is answered on Friday. He dozed off briefly on Friday and was visited in his sleep. He was told: 'Wake up, for this is the hour you were seeking—that is, at the sun's decline from its zenith.' After that, al-Hasan would seek it at the sun's decline from its zenith."],[5741,"The hour that occurs on Friday is between the Asr prayer until the sun sets."],[5742,"He was in the Asr prayer on Friday, with the people behind him, when a dog appeared passing in front of them. The dog collapsed and died before it passed. When the Prophet ﷺ came, he faced the people and said: 'Which of you supplicated against this dog?' A man said: 'I supplicated against it.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'You supplicated against it in an hour in which supplication is answered.'"],[5743,"Like the day, it has twelve hours. The hour mentioned on Friday is the last hour of the day."],[5745,"He was asked about that hour. He said: 'Allah created Adam after 'Asr on Friday, and He created him from the surface layer of the whole earth, its red, its black, its good, and its bad. That is why among his descendants are black, red, good, and bad. He prostrated His angels to him and lodged him in His garden. By Allah, that day had not ended before he disobeyed Him and He expelled him from it.'"],[5746,"I said to Ibn 'Abbas: 'Abu 'Abbas, the hour that is mentioned on Friday?' He said, 'Allah knows best' several times. 'Allah created Adam in the last hours of Friday. He created him from the surface layer of the whole earth, its red, its black, its good, its bad, its rough, and its smooth. That is why among his descendants are good, bad, red, black, easygoing, and rough. Then He breathed into him of His spirit, lodged him in His garden, commanded the angels, and they prostrated to him. He made a covenant with him, but he forgot, so he was named insan (human). By Allah, the sun had not set that day before He expelled him from it.'"],[5747,"The hour on Friday is the one in which the Hour will be established, the one in which Allah sent down Adam, and the one in which no Muslim supplicates Allah with a righteous supplication except that it is answered for him, from when the sun yellows until it sets."],[5748,"Abu Hurayrah went to Syria and met Ka'b. Abu Hurayrah narrated from the Prophet ﷺ, and Ka'b narrated from the Torah until he came to the hour on Friday. Abu Hurayrah said: 'The Prophet ﷺ said: In Friday there is an hour in which the Muslim servant does not ask Allah for anything except that He gives it to him.' Ka'b said: 'But it is only in one Friday of the year.' Abu Hurayrah said: 'No.' Ka'b said: 'Indeed, Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth: every Friday.' Then Abu Hurayrah came to Madinah and met 'Abdullah b. Salam. He mentioned to him what Ka'b had said about Friday. 'Abdullah said: 'He lied.' Abu Hurayrah said: 'He has retracted it.'"],[5749,"And in Friday there is indeed an hour: no Muslim servant coincides with it asking Allah for good except that He gives it to him, and it is after 'Asr."],[5750,"I know that hour. I said to him: 'My brother, I am not a man who would begrudge it to you; tell me it.' He said: 'It is the last hour of Friday before the sun sets.' I said: 'Did you not say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a Muslim servant does not coincide with it while he is in prayer, and that hour is not a time of prayer?' He said: 'Did you not hear the Prophet ﷺ say: Whoever prays then sits waiting for the prayer remains in prayer until the next prayer comes to him?' He said: 'And in it Adam was created, in it he was sent down from the Garden, in it he was forgiven, in it he was taken, and in it the Hour will be established.'"],[5751,"I said to Abu Hurayrah: 'They claim that the Night of Qadr has been lifted.' He said: 'Whoever said that has lied.' I said: 'Is it in every Ramadan that I will face?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: I said: 'Did they claim that the hour on Friday, in which no Muslim supplicates except that he is answered, has been lifted?' He said: 'Whoever said that has lied.' I said: 'Is it in every Friday that I will face?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[5752,"In Friday there is an hour: no servant coincides with it while he is praying or waiting for the prayer, supplicating Allah for something, except that He answers him."],[5753,"Friday to Friday is an expiation, and the five prayers are expiations for what is between them, as long as major sins are avoided.' He said: A man said: 'O Prophet of Allah, does Friday expiate until Friday?' He said: 'Yes, and an addition of three days.'"],[5754,"Whoever bathes on Friday and performs the bath well, wears the best of his clothes, touches what Allah has decreed for him of his family's perfume or his oil, then goes to the Friday prayer and does not separate two people, he will be forgiven what is between the two Fridays and an addition of three days."],[5755,"Whoever uses the tooth stick on Friday, then bathes as he bathes from janabah, then touches perfume, then wears his two garments, then goes early to the mosque and does not separate two people, and does not speak until the imam stands, he will be forgiven what is between the two Fridays."],[5756,"None of you should make his brother get up on Friday and then take his seat, but let him say: Make room."],[5757,"None of you should make his brother get up from his seat and then take his place. I said to him: 'Even on Friday?' He said: 'On Friday and other days.' Nafi' said: 'A man would get up from his seat for Ibn Umar, but he would not sit in it.'"],[5758,"None of you should make his brother get up and then sit in his place. A man would get up from his seat for Ibn Umar, but he would not sit in his place."],[5759,"But he should say: 'Make room and spread out.'"],[5760,"Whoever dies on the night of Friday – or the day of Friday – is absolved from the trial of the grave, or he said: is protected from the trial of the grave, and is recorded as a martyr."],[5761,"He is absolved from the trial of the grave."],[5762,"5634 – Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Muttalib b. Abd Allah b. Hantab, from the Prophet ﷺ something similar. Whoever dies on the night of Friday or the day of Friday is absolved from the trial of the grave."],[5763,"I asked Ata about praying before the imam comes out on the day of Fitr. He said: 'When the sun has risen, then pray.'"],[5764,"Mujahid used to pray between them."],[5765,"Anas, Abu Hurayrah, al-Hasan, his brother Sa'id, and Jabir b. Zayd used to pray before the imam came out and after him."],[5766,"I saw Anas b. Malik and al-Hasan praying before the Eid prayer."],[5767,"I saw Anas b. Malik, al-Hasan, his brother Sa'id, and Jabir b. Zayd Abu al-Sha'tha praying on the day of Eid before the imam came out."],[5768,"The Adha prayer is like the Fitr prayer: two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs."],[5769,"Some people from among the companions of the Prophet ﷺ came to us on the day of Eid before the imam came out and they prayed. Then Ibn Umar came and did not pray. A man said to Ibn Umar: \"Some people from among the companions of Muhammad ﷺ came and prayed, and you came and did not pray.\" Ibn Umar said: \"Allah, Blessed and Exalted, would not reject a servant's good deed. I think so.\""],[5770,"Ali went out on the day of Eid and found the people praying before he came out. It was said to him: \"If only you would forbid them.\" He said: \"I am not one to forbid a servant if he prays it, but I will tell you what we witnessed,\" or he said, \"what we attended.\""],[5771,"Ibn Mas'ud and Hudhayfah used to forbid the people - or he said - \"they would make sit down anyone they saw praying before the imam came out on the day of Eid.\""],[5772,"Alqamah b. Qays was asked about praying before the imam comes out on the day of Eid, and he said: \"The companions of the Prophet ﷺ would not pray before it.\" The questioner said: \"What do you think, since I have already prayed?\" He said: \"I have told you about the practice of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ, and you know best.\""],[5773,"I went out with him on a day of Eid, and he did not pray before it or after it."],[5774,"He would not pray before the imam came out."],[5775,"The companions of the Prophet ﷺ would not pray until the Prophet ﷺ came out."],[5776,"He would not pray anything before the two Eids or after them."],[5777,"He would not pray on that day until the day had declined."],[5778,"5650 5613 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn Umar, something similar. Hidden text: He would not pray anything before the two Eids or after them, nor would he pray on that day until the day had declined."],[5779,"He would pray the morning prayer on the day of Eid while wearing his clothes, then he would go out to the prayer place."],[5780,"We did not know anyone who would pray before the imam came out on the day of Eid or after it."],[5781,"Amr b. Shuayb used to command us not to pray before it or after it."],[5782,"The Messenger of God ﷺ went out on the day of Fitr — or Adha — and prayed two rak'ahs, not praying before them or after them."],[5783,"The Prophet ﷺ did not pray before the Fitr prayer or after it, and the Prophet ﷺ did not pray anything before the Adha prayer or after it."],[5784,"He used not to pray anything before the two Eid prayers, and he would pray four after them."],[5785,"Ibn Mas'ud used to pray four after the two Eid prayers."],[5786,"He used to pray four rak'ahs or eight after it, and he used not to pray before it."],[5787,"I saw Amir praying two rak'ahs after the two Eid prayers."],[5788,"I said to Ata: 'Have you received any report about any prayer of glorification being performed after the Fitr prayer?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'Except that which you do more of is dearer to you?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[5789,"He does not pray before it or after it."],[5790,"There is no prayer before the Adha or after it, and none before the Fitr prayer or after it until the sun declines."],[5791,"We went out with Ali b. Abi Talib on the day of Eid to the prayer ground. He saw people praying before the imam's prayer and said in wonder: 'Do you not see these people praying?' We said: 'Will you not forbid them?' He said: 'I dislike being like the one who forbids a servant when he prays.' Then he began the prayer before the khutbah, and he did not pray before it or after it."],[5792,"Neither on the day of Fitr nor on the day of Adha was the call to prayer given."],[5793,"He sent word to Ibn al-Zubayr when he was first given allegiance: 'No call to prayer used to be given for the prayer on the day of Fitr, so do not call it.' He said: 'So Ibn al-Zubayr did not call it that day, and he sent word to him along with that: \"The sermon is only after the prayer, and that used to be done.\"' He said: 'So Ibn al-Zubayr prayed before the sermon. Then his companions asked him—Ibn Safwan and companions of his—they said, \"Why did you not call us? The prayer passed them by that day.\" Then when relations between him and Ibn Abbas soured, Ibn al-Zubayr did not return to Ibn Abbas's instruction.'"],[5794,"He attended the Eid with Umar, Uthman, and Ali, and all of them prayed without an adhan or an iqamah."],[5795,"He attended al-Mughirah b. Shubah on an Eid day, and he prayed with them before the sermon without an adhan or an iqamah. Then he came being led on his camel until he delivered the sermon after the prayer on his camel."],[5796,"The Prophet ﷺ stood on the day of Fitr and prayed, beginning with the prayer before the sermon. Then he delivered the sermon to the people. When the Prophet of God ﷺ finished, he descended and came to the women and admonished them while leaning on Bilal, and Bilal was spreading out his garment while the women were dropping charity into it."],[5797,"I attended the prayer on the day of Fitr with the Prophet ﷺ and with Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, all of them praying it before the sermon, then delivering the sermon afterward. He said: 'The Prophet of God ﷺ descended, and it is as if I can see him when he seated the men with his hand. Then he proceeded, making his way through them until he came to the women, with Bilal accompanying him. Then he said: \"[O Prophet, when believing women come to you to pledge allegiance to you that they will not associate anything with God]\" and he recited this verse until he finished it. Then when he finished it, he said, \"Are you upon that?\" One woman answered him and none of the women besides her responded: \"Yes, O Prophet of God\"—Hasan does not know who she was. He said: 'Then give charity.' He said: 'Bilal spread out his garment and then said, \"Come, for you, may my father and mother be ransom for you.\" They began dropping rings and signet rings into Bilal's garment.'"],[5798,"I attended the Prophet ﷺ when he prayed on the day of Eid, then he preached. He thought that he had not made the women hear, so he came to them and admonished them, and said: 'Give charity.' He said: 'The woman began throwing the ring, the earring, and such things. Then he ordered Bilal and he placed them in a garment until he distributed them.'"],[5799,"He would go out on the day of Eid and the day of Fitr and pray those two rak'ahs, then give the salutation. Then he would stand, face the people while they were seated around him, and say: 'Give charity, give charity.' The most charitable were the women, with the ring, the earring, and such things. If the Prophet ﷺ had a need to send out an expedition against the people, he would mention it; otherwise he would depart."],[5800,"He would begin on the day of Fitr and Adha with the prayer before the sermon, then deliver the sermon; in his sermon would be the command for an expedition or a raiding party."],[5801,"He attended the Eid with Umar b. al-Khattab, and he prayed before preaching without an adhan or an iqamah. Then he preached and said: 'O people, the Messenger of God ﷺ forbade fasting these two days: one of them is the day of your breaking the fast and your Eid, and the other is the day on which you eat your sacrificial animals.' He said: 'Then I attended it with Uthman, and that was on a Friday, and he prayed before preaching without an adhan or an iqamah. Then he preached to the people and said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade fasting these two days: one of them is the day of your breaking the fast and your Eid, and the other is the day on which you eat your sacrificial animals.\" He said: 'Then I attended it with Uthman, and that was on a Friday, and he prayed before preaching without an adhan or an iqamah. Then he preached to the people and said: \"O people, this is a day on which two Eids have come together for you. Whoever among you is from the people of the outskirts, we have given him permission to return, and whoever wishes may attend the prayer.\" He said: 'Then I attended it with Ali, and he prayed before preaching without an adhan or an iqamah. Then he preached and said: \"O people, the Messenger of God ﷺ forbade that you eat your sacrificial animals after three nights, so do not eat them after that.\"'"],[5802,"He attended al-Mughirah b. Shubah on an Eid day; he prayed without an adhan or an iqamah. Then he came being led on his camel until he delivered the sermon after the prayer on his camel."],[5803,"He attended al-Mughirah b. Shubah when he prayed before the sermon, then mounted a Bukhti camel of his and preached to them. When he finished, he urged it on."],[5804,"I witnessed with Abu Bakr on an Eid day, and he began with the prayer before the khutbah, without adhan or iqamah."],[5805,"I said to Ata: Does a person remember Allah while the imam is delivering the khutbah on the day of Arafah or the day of Fitr, and he understands the imam's words? He said: No, every Eid, one should not speak during it."],[5806,"Silence is required in four places: Jumu'ah, the two Eids, and istisqa' (prayer for rain)."],[5807,"Attentive listening is obligatory in four places: Jumu'ah, Fitr, Adha, and istisqa'."],[5808,"I said to Ata: Do you know who was the first to give the khutbah on the day of Fitr then pray? He said: I do not know; I found the people doing that."],[5809,"The first one to begin with the khutbah before the prayer on the day of Fitr was Umar b. al-Khattab, when he saw the people leaving, so when he prayed he detained them with the khutbah."],[5810,"The first one to begin with the khutbah before the prayer on the day of Fitr was Uthman b. Affan, when he saw the people leaving, so when he prayed he detained them with the khutbah."],[5811,"The first one to begin with the khutbah before the prayer was Mu'awiyah."],[5812,"The first one to give the khutbah was Mu'awiyah on the Eid, or Uthman at the end of his caliphate."],[5813,"I went out with Marwan on the day of Eid al-Fitr - or Adha - he was between me and Abu Mas'ud until we reached the prayer place, and there was Kathir b. al-Salt al-Kindi who had built for Marwan a minbar of bricks and clay. Marwan turned toward the minbar until he was beside it, and I pulled him to begin with the prayer. He said: O Abu Sa'id, what you know has been abandoned. He said: No, by the Lord of the easts and the wests, three times, you will not bring anything better than what we know. Then he began with the khutbah."],[5814,"The first one to put the khutbah before the prayer on the day of Eid was Marwan. A man stood up to him and said: O Marwan, you have opposed the Sunnah. Marwan said: O so-and-so, what was there has been abandoned. Abu Sa'id said: As for this one, he has fulfilled his duty. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever among you sees an evil, let him change it with his hand; if he cannot, then with his tongue; if he cannot, then with his heart, and that is the weakest of faith.'"],[5815,"I said to Ata: When did those who have passed use to come out from their homes on the day of Fitr for the prayer? He said: They used to come out until the forenoon extended, then they would pray, then they would give the khutbah for a short while, making their khutbah brief. He said: They would not detain the people at all. Then they would descend and the people would leave. He said: The Prophet ﷺ did not sit on a minbar until he died; he only gave the khutbah standing. So how could it be feared that they would detain the people? They only gave the khutbah standing, they did not sit. The Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman - each of them would ascend the minbar and stand as he was, not sitting on the minbar until he ascended it, and not sitting on it after descending. His entire khutbah was standing. They only said the tashahhud once, the first one. He said: There was no minbar except the minbar of the Prophet ﷺ, until Mu'awiyah came when he performed Hajj with the minbar and left it. He said: So they continued giving the khutbah on minbars after that."],[5816,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to ʿAmr b. Ḥazm when he dispatched him to Najrān: 'Delay the fiṭr prayer, remind the people, and perform the aḍḥā early.'"],[5817,"The Prophet's ﷺ Friday sermon consisted of two parts delivered while standing."],[5818,"The people used to deliver two Friday sermons, with a sitting in between."],[5819,"When the Prophet ﷺ would deliver a sermon, he would lean on a palm trunk among the pillars of the mosque. When the minbar was made for him and he stood upon it, that pillar trembled like the moaning of a she-camel, until the people of the mosque heard it, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended and embraced it, and it became still."],[5820,"On the day of fiṭr and the day of aḍḥā he would deliver the sermon on his mount after the prayer. He said: 'He would utter the tashahhud, then recite a sūrah from the Qur'an, make some supplications, then depart.'"],[5821,"He witnessed al-Mughīrah b. Shuʿbah on a day of ʿĪd pray without adhān or iqāmah, then he came being led by his camel until he delivered the sermon after the prayer on his camel."],[5822,"He witnessed al-Mughīrah 'pray it before the sermon, then he mounted a bukhtī camel of his, then he delivered the sermon to them, and when he finished he urged it on.'"],[5823,"On the day of aḍḥā, the Prophet ﷺ came to al-Baqīʿ, and a bow was handed to him, and he delivered the sermon leaning on it."],[5824,"I saw ʿAbdullāh b. al-Zubayr delivering a sermon with a staff in his hand."],[5825,"Additional textAdditional textThe Prophet ﷺ did not have a minbar brought out for him, nor did his companions, on the day of ʿĪd. The first to bring out the minbar was Marwān. A man said to him: 'You have brought out the minbar though it was not brought out, and you began with the sermon before the prayer though that was not done, and you sat during the sermon though sitting was not done.' He said: 'That sunnah has been abandoned.'"],[5826,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would go out on the day of fiṭr with a short spear and plant it in the prayer place before him and pray toward it."],[5827,"When he delivered a sermon he would lean on his staff firmly."],[5828,"I saw him coming to the Eid on foot."],[5829,"Ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote encouraging them regarding the two Eids: Whoever is able to come to them on foot, let him do so."],[5830,"He used to dislike riding to the Eid and the Friday prayer."],[5831,"Going out on the day of Fiṭr equals an ʿumrah, and going out on the day of Aḍḥā equals a ḥajj."],[5832,"It is from the Sunnah that you come to the prayer place on the day of Eid on foot."],[5833,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade taking out weapons on the day of Eid."],[5834,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade taking out weapons on the day of Eid, unless they fear an enemy and go out."],[5835,"When we have finished the prayer, whoever wishes may wait for the sermon, and whoever wishes may leave."],[5836,"I heard that the takbīr is said on Eid nine times and seven times."],[5837,"The imam says takbīr on the day of Fiṭr before delivering the sermon nine times when he intends to stand, and seven times in the second sermon. I tried to get him to explain this better than that but he could not, so I thought that his saying 'when he intends to stand' refers to the last sermon."],[5838,"The Sunnah is to say takbīr on the pulpit on the day of Eid: he begins his first sermon with nine takbīrs before delivering it, and begins the second with seven."],[5839,"The Sunnah is to say takbīr on the pulpit on the day of Eid: he begins his first sermon with nine takbīrs before delivering it, and begins the second with seven."],[5840,"Between every two takbirs is a prayer upon the Prophet ﷺ."],[5841,"The takbir in the prayer on the day of Fitr is thirteen takbirs, which he says while standing: seven in the first rak'ah—among them the opening takbir of the prayer, among them the takbir of the rak'ah, among them six before the recitation, and among them one after it—and in the other, six takbirs: among them a takbir for the rak'ah, among them five before the recitation, and one after it. I said to him: 'Yusuf b. Mahak informed me that Ibn al-Zubayr used to say only four takbirs in each rak'ah, the same in both, which we heard from him.' 'Ata' said: 'The one from whom I took this hadith is, by Allah, more knowledgeable than Ibn al-Zubayr.' I said: 'Who?' He said: 'Ibn 'Abbas.'"],[5842,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the takbir on the day of Fitr in the first rak'ah seven times, then recited, then said the takbir of the rak'ah; then in the other he said the takbir five times, then recited, then said the takbir, then bowed."],[5843,"'Ali: he says the takbir in the Adha, Fitr, and istisqa' prayers seven times in the first, and five in the other, and he prays before the khutbah, and recites aloud.\" He said: \"And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman used to do that."],[5844,"He used to say the takbir in the Adha and Fitr seven times in the first, and five in the last."],[5845,"I attended the Eid with Abu Hurayrah: he said the takbir in the first seven times, and in the last five times before the recitation."],[5846,"I attended the Eid with Abu Hurayrah: he said the takbir in the first seven times, and in the last five times before the recitation."],[5847,"I attended the Eid with Abu Hurayrah: he said the takbir in the first seven times, and in the last five times before the recitation."],[5848,"The takbir on the day of Eid before the recitation: seven and five."],[5849,"He used to say the takbir on the day of Fitr, Adha, and istisqa' as one set: seven in the first, and five in the other."],[5850,"In the first, five takbirs including the takbir of the rak'ah and the opening takbir; and in the other rak'ah, four including the takbir of the rak'ah."],[5851,"He used to say the takbir in the two Eids nine times each: four before the recitation, then he said the takbir and bowed; and in the second, he recited, and when he finished he said the takbir four times, then bowed."],[5852,"Ibn Mas'ud was sitting and with him were Hudhayfah and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari. Sa'id b. al-'As asked them about the takbir in the prayer on the day of Fitr and Adha. One of them kept saying: Ask this one, and the other kept saying: Ask this one. Hudhayfah said to him: Ask this one, 'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud. So he asked him, and Ibn Mas'ud said: \"He says takbir four times, then recites, then says takbir and bows, then stands in the second rak'ah and recites, then says takbir four times after the recitation.\""],[5853,"He asked Masruq about the imam's takbir. He said: \"The imam says takbir once, then says takbir four times, then recites, then says takbir and prostrates, then stands in the last rak'ah and recites, then says takbir three times, then says one takbir with which he bows.\""],[5855,"I witnessed Ibn 'Abbas say takbir nine times in the Eid prayer in Basra, and he made the two recitations consecutive."],[5856,"I said to 'Ata': On the day of Adha, is there an established sunnah for the people of the regions in anything they should do? He said: \"One prayer like Fitr, and it is not obligatory except in congregation, two rak'ahs only, and a sacrifice if he wishes.\" And he said: \"They must attend it just as they must attend the Fitr prayer.\""],[5857,"That in Adha there is among them as much takbir as there is among them in Fitr."],[5858,"I do not dislike in the two rak'ahs as much takbir on the day of Adha as on the day of Fitr, and that has not reached me from anyone."],[5859,"The takbir on the day of Eid in the first rak'ah is four times, and in the last three times. So the takbir is seven besides the takbir of the prayer."],[5860,"The sunnah of Adha is the sunnah of Fitr except the sacrifice.\" He said: \"And it is the same in going out, the sermon, and the takbir except the sacrifice."],[5861,"The imam stands and says the opening takbir for the prayer, then remains for a while supplicating and remembering to himself, without any specific words or supplication having reached them. Then he says the second takbir, then remains like that for a while supplicating to himself and says takbir. Then likewise between every two takbirs there is a while in which he supplicates and remembers to himself, until he has said six takbirs including the opening takbir. Then he recites, and when he finishes he says the seventh takbir for the rak'ah. Then he stands in the second rak'ah, and when he is fully upright he says takbir, then remains for a while supplicating to himself and remembering, then says the second takbir, then likewise until he has said five takbirs before the recitation. Then when he finishes he says the sixth takbir, so that is thirteen takbirs, all of which the imam says while standing.\" He said that more than once, and the takbir of prostration is not counted among them."],[5862,"That between every two takbirs there is the amount of a word."],[5863,"I said to 'Ata': Is there any tahlil, tasbih, or hamd to be said on that day as takbir is said, such that it is proper to do it in the prayer, after it, before it, or on the minbar? He said: \"It has not reached me.\""],[5864,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “Does the imam raise his hands every time he utters this additional takbīr in the ṣalāt al-fiṭr?” He said: “Yes, and the people also raise their hands.”"],[5865,"Regarding the recitation in the two Eids: make those near you hear it."],[5866,"He used to recite in the prayer on the day of Fiṭr: “Iqtarabat al-sāʿah” (Q 54). He said: “I do not know but that he mentioned it from the Prophet ﷺ.”"],[5867,"He used to recite in the prayer on the day of Eid: Qāf (Q 50) and “Iqtarabat al-sāʿah” (Q 54)."],[5868,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb went out on an Eid day and asked Abū Wāqid al-Laythī: “With what did the Messenger of God ﷺ use to recite in the prayer on the day of Eid?” He said: “With Qāf and Iqtarabat.”"],[5869,"The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the prayer on the day of Eid: “Sabbiḥ isma rabbika al-aʿlā” (Q 87) and “Hal atāka” (Q 88)."],[5870,"The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the two Eids: in the first rakʿah the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb and “Sabbiḥ isma rabbika al-aʿlā” (Q 87), and in the second the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb and “Hal atāka ḥadīthu al-ghāshiyah” (Q 88)."],[5871,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite on the day of Jumuʿah and on the two Eids: “Sabbiḥ isma rabbika al-aʿlā” (Q 87) and “Hal atāka ḥadīthu al-ghāshiyah” (Q 88)."],[5872,"It is from the Sunnah to come to the prayer on the day of Eid."],[5873,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “Is the prayer on the day of Fiṭr obligatory upon all people?” He said: “No, except in congregation.” He said: “Is Jumuʿah not more obligatory in that it is attended than it? Only in congregation. So how about Fiṭr?” ʿAṭāʾ said: “They are not completed as four [rakʿahs] in congregation or otherwise.” He said: I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “Is it a right upon the people of a village to attend the ṣalāt al-Fiṭr just as it is a right upon them to attend the day of Jumuʿah?” He said: “Yes.” He said this repeatedly, and he had told me another time before this: “That is a right. But as for like the right of Jumuʿah, no. They were commanded to Jumuʿah.” Then he said: “There is no day greater than the day of Jumuʿah. It is the greatest of all days, greater than the day of ʿArafah and the day of Fiṭr. It has reached us that there is nothing—land or sea, tree or stone—but it ceases not to supplicate on that day until the sun rises, except for the two heavy ones: jinn and humans.”"],[5874,"I have not seen them consider anything more obligatory than Jumuʿah compared to Fiṭr. They say: this is a farīḍah and that is a sunnah."],[5875,"The prayer of Aḍḥā is like the prayer of Fiṭr: two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs."],[5876,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think if I prayed the fiṭr prayer without ablution, and then remembered after the imam had finished? He said: 'You repeat it.' And ʿAmr b. Dīnār said that to me."],[5877,"If you fear that you will miss the prayer on the two Eids while you are holding back urine, then urinate, then perform tayammum."],[5878,"Whoever misses the two Eid prayers should pray four [rakʿas]."],[5879,"Regarding a man who misses a rakʿa of the Eid prayer, he said: 'He prays with the imam, then makes up the rakʿa that he missed, and he says takbīr as the imam says takbīr, and if he finds the imam reciting, he says takbīr as the imam says takbīr.'"],[5880,"Whoever misses the Eid prayer with the imam, no takbīr is required of him."],[5881,"Whoever misses the prayer on the day of fiṭr prays as the imam prays. Maʿmar said: If a person misses the sermon or the prayer on the day of fiṭr or aḍḥā, then attends after that, he prays two rakʿas."],[5882,"The Messenger of God ﷺ sent word to distant villages—Fadak, Yanbuʿ, and similar villages, at a distance of three [nights' journey] from Madinah—that they should hold the Friday prayer and pray the two Eids."],[5883,"Abū ʿIyāḍ and Mujāhid were in hiding during the time of al-Ḥajjāj, and it was the day of fiṭr. Abū ʿIyāḍ was spoken to, and he supplicated for them and led them in two rakʿas. He said: And Shuʿba informed us, from Qatāda, from ʿIkrima, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, that he used to say the like of that."],[5884,"Abū ʿIyāḍ and Mujāhid were in hiding during the time of al-Ḥajjāj, and it was the day of fiṭr. Abū ʿIyāḍ was spoken to, and he supplicated for them and led them in two rakʿas. He said: And Shuʿba informed us, from Qatāda, from ʿIkrima, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, that he used to say the like of that."],[5885,"There is no Friday prayer and no tashrīq except in a large congregational city."],[5886,"The traveler is not required to pray the aḍḥā prayer or the fiṭr prayer, unless he is in a large city or village and attends the prayer with them."],[5887,"My husband went on twelve military expeditions with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and I went out with him on five of them. We used to attend to the sick and treat the wounded. We were commanded on the two Eids that whoever did not have a jilbāb, her companion should let her wear one of her own jilbābs with her. Ḥafṣa said: 'Then Umm ʿAṭiyya al-Anṣāriyya came to us, and I mentioned that to her. She said: \"Yes, by his father and mother, he commanded us to bring out on the two Eids the young unmarried women, those in seclusion, and the menstruating women.\" She said: \"As for the menstruating women, they keep away from the prayer place, but they witness the good and the supplication of the Muslims.\""],[5888,"My husband went on twelve military expeditions with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and I went out with him on five of them. We used to attend to the sick and treat the wounded. We were commanded on the two Eids that whoever did not have a jilbāb, her companion should let her wear one of her own jilbābs with her. Ḥafṣa said: 'Then Umm ʿAṭiyya al-Anṣāriyya came to us, and I mentioned that to her. She said: \"Yes, by his father and mother, he commanded us to bring out on the two Eids the young unmarried women, those in seclusion, and the menstruating women.\" She said: \"As for the menstruating women, they keep away from the prayer place, but they witness the good and the supplication of the Muslims.\""],[5889,"The wife of 'Alqama was a noble woman, and she used to go out on the two Eids."],[5890,"He used not to take his women out on the Eid."],[5891,"If Friday and the day of Fitr coincide on one day, he should combine them and pray two rak'ahs only, where he prays the Fitr prayer, then it is the same until 'Asr. Then he told me at that point: \"The day of Fitr and the day of Jumu'ah coincided on one day in the time of Ibn al-Zubayr. Ibn al-Zubayr said: 'Two Eids have come together on one day,' and he combined them both into one. He prayed on Friday two rak'ahs early in the morning as the Fitr prayer, then he did not add to it until he prayed 'Asr.\" He said: \"As for the jurists, they said nothing about that, but those without understanding disapproved of it.\" He said: \"I myself disapproved of it, and I prayed Zuhr that day.\" He said: \"Until it later reached us that when the two Eids coincided like that, they were prayed as one.\""],[5892,"Abu al-Zubayr informed me about Ibn al-Zubayr's combining them on the day he combined them. He said: We heard that Ibn 'Abbas said: \"Two Eids coincided on one day, and he hit the mark.\""],[5893,"One of them suffices for the other."],[5894,"Two Eids coincided during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Fitr and Jumu'ah—or Adha and Jumu'ah. He said: The Prophet ﷺ came out and said: \"You have attained remembrance and good. We shall hold the Jumu'ah. Whoever wishes to attend Jumu'ah, let him attend, and whoever wishes to stay back, let him stay back.\""],[5895,"In the time of the Prophet ﷺ, a day of Jumu'ah and a day of Fitr, or a day of Jumu'ah and Adha, coincided. He prayed the first Eid with the people, then he gave the sermon, and he permitted the Ansar to return to the 'Awali and leave the Jumu'ah. The matter remained like that thereafter."],[5897,"They coincided while 'Ali was in Kufa. He prayed, then he prayed Jumu'ah, and when he prayed Fitr he said: \"Whoever is here, we have permitted him,\" as if for those around him, meaning the Jumu'ah."],[5898,"Two Eids coincided on one day. He said: \"Whoever wishes to attend Jumu'ah, let him attend, and whoever wishes to stay back, let him stay back.\""],[5899,"I witnessed 'Uthman when Fitr and Jumu'ah coincided. 'Uthman addressed the people after the prayer, then said: \"These two Eids have coincided on one day. Whoever is from the people of the 'Awali and wishes to remain until he witnesses Jumu'ah, let him do so; and whoever wishes to leave, we have permitted him.\""],[5900,"When the two coincided on one day, 'Ali used to pray the Eid at the beginning of the day, and pray Jumu'ah at the end of the day."],[5901,"If you can, let none of you go out on the day of Fitr until he eats something; let him do so. He said: I have never failed to eat before going out since I heard that from Ibn 'Abbas. I eat from the edge of a sarifah. We said to him: What is a sarifah? He said: Thin bread, a morsel, or I drink some milk, nabidh, or water. I said: How do you interpret this? He said: I heard him say—I think on the authority of the Prophet ﷺ—: \"They would not go out until the forenoon had extended, and they would say: 'We eat so that we do not hurry away from the prayer.'\" He said: Sometimes I would go out having tasted nothing but water."],[5902,"A person used to be instructed to eat on the day of Fitr before the Imam went out to the prayer place. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri used to eat on the day of Fitr before going out in the morning, and he did not eat on the day of Sacrifice until they had slaughtered."],[5903,"Did he used to eat on the day of Fitr before going out in the morning?"],[5904,"He would not go out on the day of Fitr until he had eaten; he used to enjoin that."],[5905,"They used to consider it recommended to eat on the day of Fitr before going out to the prayer place."],[5906,"I saw Amir al-Sha'bi on the day of Fitr while we were with him, and his neighbors gathered around him. He came out with a loaf of bread in his hand, and he gave each person a piece, and they ate it. Then he set off to the mosque—or he said: to the prayer place."],[5907,"Ibn Umar used to go out on the morning of the day of Fitr from the mosque. He said: 'And I do not know that he ate anything.'"],[5908,"The people used to eat on the day of Fitr before they went out."],[5909,"Do not eat before you go out on the day of Fitr, if you wish."],[5910,"Ibn Umar used not to eat on the day of Fitr."],[5911,"Using the tooth-stick on Friday is a sunnah."],[5912,"I mentioned to Umar b. Abd al-Aziz the day Uthman b. Affan descended from the pulpit on Friday and his saying: 'O people, I forgot the tooth-stick.' So he descended and cleaned his teeth, then returned to the pulpit. Umar said: 'Indeed, part of the sunnah regarding the tooth-stick on the day of Eid is like its status on Friday.'"],[5913,"Bathing on the day of the two Eids is a sunnah. He said: And Ibn al-Musayyib said: \"Like the bath of janabah.\""],[5914,"I said to Ata: 'Cleaning the teeth on the day of Fitr?' He said: 'It has not reached me that it was specifically enjoined on the day of Fitr. But it has reached us from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: Were it not that I would impose hardship on my community, I would have commanded them to use the tooth-stick for every prayer.'"],[5915,"Ibn Umar used to bathe on the day of Fitr before going out."],[5916,"He used to command bathing on the day of Fitr and say: \"It is not obligatory, but it is a good and recommended practice.\""],[5917,"Bathing on the day of Fitr is good, because it is a day of Eid, and I would not fail to bathe on the day of Fitr. I said: Should one be diligent about bathing on it as one is diligent about bathing for janabah? He said: No."],[5918,"Bathing on the day of the two Eids is a sunnah. He said: And Ibn al-Musayyib said: \"Like the bath of janabah.\""],[5919,"Ali used to bathe on the day of Fitr and the day of Adha before going out."],[5920,"And he would apply perfume."],[5921,"Ibn Umar used to bathe on the day of Fitr before going out."],[5922,"I never saw Ibn Umar bathe for Eid at all. He would spend the night of Fitr in the mosque, then go out from it after praying Fajr, and he would not go to his house."],[5923,"They used to pray Fajr in their clothes, then go out to the prayer place on the day of Fitr."],[5924,"Indeed I bathe on the day of Fitr, the day of Sacrifice, the day of Arafah, and the day of Jumu'ah, and from janabah and wet dreams, and from the bathhouse, and when I have been cupped."],[5925,"I would love to give zakat al-fitr by today's measure, a measure that we take and by which we feed ourselves."],[5926,"He used to give zakat al-fitr by the mudd with which he fed his family."],[5927,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if I were in a city other than my own, and their measuring vessel was larger than mine, should I pay the fitr with it – or should I pay – with the measuring vessel of my city? He said: 'You have no obligation except that, and increase in goodness is good.' He said: How much has it reached you between the measuring vessel today and the measuring vessel that was in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ and Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman? He said: 'I do not know, except that that measuring vessel was smaller.'"],[5928,"That the mudd of the Prophet ﷺ was one third of the mudd that Marwan b. al-Hakam established. Ibn Jurayj said: Abu Bakr informed me, he said: 'With us it is four and a half ratls.'"],[5929,"That the mudd of the Prophet ﷺ was one third of the mudd that Marwan b. al-Hakam established. Ibn Jurayj said: Abu Bakr informed me, he said: 'With us it is four and a half ratls.'"],[5931,"Zakat al-Fitr is due on every free person and slave, male and female, young and old, rich and poor: a sa' of dates, or half a sa' of wheat."],[5932,"The Messenger of God ﷺ obligated zakat al-Fitr on male and female, free and slave: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley.' Ibn Umar said: 'Afterwards people equated it with two mudds of wheat.' Ibn Umar said: 'He liked to give dates.'"],[5933,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded zakat al-Fitr on every free Muslim slave, young and old: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley."],[5934,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded zakat al-Fitr on every free Muslim slave, young and old: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley."],[5935,"On every man, slave or free, free woman or female slave, and people are equal in that, young and old, except slaves who are maintained: two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of barley or dates.' Ata' said: 'Then pay on behalf of your slave, and if the slave pays on his own behalf, it suffices his master.'"],[5936,"That he heard Ibn al-Zubayr say on the pulpit: 'Zakat al-Fitr is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley; free and slave are equal.'"],[5937,"Zakat al-Fitr is due on every slave or free person, young and old. Whoever pays raisins, it is accepted from him; whoever pays dates, it is accepted from him; whoever pays barley, it is accepted from him; whoever pays sult, it is accepted from him: a sa' each."],[5938,"Zakat al-Fitr is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley."],[5939,"Two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley."],[5940,"On the free and the slave: two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates, and corn is double wheat."],[5941,"Everything except wheat is a sa', and wheat is half a sa'.\""],[5942,"Zakat al-fitr is due on every Muslim, young and old, slave or free: two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates, or barley."],[5943,"Upon those whom you support: half a sa' of wheat, or a sa' of dates."],[5944,"Abu Bakr al-Siddiq added to it half a sa' of wheat between two men."],[5945,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded for zakat al-fitr a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley. 'Abd Allah said: So the people made two mudds of wheat equivalent to it."],[5946,"He gave to Abu Bakr half a sa' of wheat between two men."],[5947,"Abu Bakr gave two mudds as zakat al-fitr."],[5948,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote: for every two persons a dirham - meaning zakat al-fitr. Ma'mar said: This is according to the calculation of what is given by measure."],[5949,"We used to give, when the Messenger of God ﷺ was among us, zakat al-fitr on behalf of every young and old, free and slave: a sa' of dried curd, a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins. We continued to give it like that until Mu'awiya came as a pilgrim - or as one performing 'umra - and spoke to the people from the pulpit. Among what he said to them was: I think two mudds of Syrian wheat are equivalent to a sa' of dates. So the people adopted two mudds. Abu Sa'id said: As for me, I will continue to give it as I used to give it forever."],[5950,"Zakat al-fitr at the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ was a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins, a sa' of dried curd. When Mu'awiya came and Syrian wheat arrived, he saw that two mudds were equivalent to one mudd."],[5951,"We used to give zakat al-fitr at the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins, until the time of Mu'awiya when wheat became plentiful and it was given."],[5952,"He used to be commanded to give before going out a sa' of dates or half a sa' of wheat.\""],[5953,"Ibn ʿUmar used to prefer giving dates."],[5954,"I asked ʿUrwa b. al-Zubayr and Saʿīd b. Jubayr about the fiṭr food. They both said: A ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of barley, or a mudd of wheat."],[5955,"The Messenger of God ﷺ addressed the people a day or two before fiṭr and said: Give a ṣāʿ of wheat, or a qamḥ between two people, or a ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of barley, on behalf of every person, young or old."],[5956,"The zakat al-fiṭr for those who fast is two mudds of ḥinṭa, or a ṣāʿ of dates."],[5958,"We used to pay zakat al-fiṭr in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ from three kinds: barley, aqiṭ, and dates.\" ʿIyāḍ said: I said to him: What about ḥinṭa? He said: \"It became plentiful later, and it was paid in the time of Muʿāwiya.\""],[5959,"They liked to pay zakat al-fiṭr on behalf of the young and the old, even for the fetus in its mother's womb."],[5960,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A fetus that is not yet moving in its mother's womb—should I pay zakat on its behalf? He said: No, because you do not know whether it will be complete or not, whether it will come out dead or alive."],[5961,"I asked him about the fetus: Is zakat paid on its behalf? He said: \"Yes.\""],[5962,"It was recommended for the people of the desert to go out on the day of ʿĪd and for one of them to lead them in prayer, and to pay zakat al-fiṭr."],[5963,"Ibn al-Zubayr sat on the pulpit a day or two before fiṭr and said: Zakat al-fiṭr is due on every Muslim: two mudds of qamḥ, or a ṣāʿ of dates. Let a man pay on behalf of himself, his child, and his slaves.\" Abū al-ʿAbbās said: I said: And on behalf of the people of the desert? He said: \"Yes. Are they not Muslims? I think they are.\""],[5964,"Ibn al-Zubayr sat on the pulpit a day or two before fiṭr and said: Zakat al-fiṭr is due on every Muslim: two mudds of qamḥ, or a ṣāʿ of dates. Let a man pay on behalf of himself, his child, and his slaves. Abū al-ʿAbbās said: I said: And on behalf of the people of the desert? He said: Yes. Are they not Muslims? I think they are."],[5965,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Our valleys—Marr, Nakhla, and ʿArafa—do their people owe zakat al-fiṭr? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Do they pay it here with us or with them? He said: \"Rather with us.\""],[5966,"The desert dwellers: “He has certainly succeeded who purifies himself” [87:14]."],[5967,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What do you think of His saying, ‘He has certainly succeeded who purifies himself’ [87:14] — is it about the fiṭr?” He said: “It is about all charity.”"],[5968,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “Are the desert dwellers liable for any zakāh?” He said: “No, I have not heard of it except upon the people of the villages.”"],[5969,"ʿAṭāʾ said to me: “The concern of the desert dwellers is concern for themselves — shepherds of their livestock and its workers.”"],[5970,"Zakāt al-fiṭr is a sunnah; it is upon the desert dwellers."],[5971,"The qasāmah existed in the pre-Islamic era regarding blood and regarding a man born on his bed whom another man claimed; they would swear fifty oaths concerning him, like the qasāmah of blood, and take him away. When the Prophet ﷺ performed the pilgrimage, al-ʿAbbās b. ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib said to him: “So-and-so is my son, and we swear concerning him.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “No, the child belongs to the bed, and the fornicator gets the stone.” Then he sent a crier to proclaim among the people of Makkah: “Indeed, zakāt al-fiṭr is a due obligation upon every Muslim — male and female, free or slave, young or old, present or desert-dwelling: two mudds of wheat, or a ṣāʿ of other food besides that. Indeed, the child belongs to the bed, and the fornicator gets the athlab — meaning the stone.” Thus the Prophet ﷺ confirmed the qasāmah of blood as it had been in the pre-Islamic era."],[5972,"We asked Saʿd b. Qays b. ʿUbādah about zakāt al-fiṭr. He said: “The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded us to give it before the zakāh was revealed. When the zakāh was revealed, he neither commanded us nor forbade us, and we still do it.”"],[5973,"If someone has slaves who are being managed, he should not impose it upon them."],[5974,"If they fast Ramadan with you until they break the fast, then feed on their behalf."],[5975,"Pay it on behalf of your slave. If the slave pays it for himself, it suffices for his master. If he is a mukātab and pays it for himself, it suffices for his master. If he does not pay it for himself, then his master should pay it for him, for he remains a slave until he is freed. If you are absent on the day of fiṭr, then when you arrive, pay it for yourself. If you have Christian slaves who are not being managed, pay it for them. And pay it on behalf of your traveling slave."],[5976,"Ibn ʿUmar had two mukātabs, and he would not pay zakāt al-fiṭr on their behalf."],[5977,"5845 5806 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Abd Allah b. Umar, from Nafi' the like of it. Hidden text: Ibn Umar had two mukatab slaves, and he would not pay the sadaqat al-fitr on their behalf."],[5978,"A man does not pay sadaqat al-fitr on behalf of his mukatab slave if he [the slave] wishes [not to]."],[5979,"Regarding Christian slaves, he said: \"They are not to be favored [with it].\" He said: \"They are property, so it should be discharged on their behalf.\""],[5980,"Do not discharge it except on behalf of one who has prayed and fasted."],[5981,"A man feeds [as sadaqat al-fitr] on behalf of his slave even if he is a Christian."],[5982,"A man feeds [as sadaqat al-fitr] on behalf of his slave even if he is a Magian."],[5983,"A man pays sadaqat al-fitr on behalf of his mukatab slave and on behalf of every slave he owns, even if he is a Jew or a Christian."],[5984,"We used to pay sadaqat al-fitr on behalf of every soul we supported, even if he was a Christian."],[5985,"If your slave has small free-born sons, their father does not pay [sadaqat al-fitr] on their behalf except with the permission of his master."],[5986,"He does not discharge it on their behalf except with the permission of his master."],[5987,"It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Let every one of you pay—young or old, free or enslaved, poor or rich—half a sa' of wheat, or a sa' of dates. As for our poor person, more returns to him than was taken from him, and as for the rich person, he will find [it to be so]."],[5988,"Sadaqat al-fitr was [incumbent] upon every rich and poor person."],[5989,"A person said to ʿAṭāʾ: He gives the zakāt al-fiṭr on behalf of himself and his dependents; may he take from it when it is distributed? He said: \"Yes, if he is in need.\""],[5990,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A poor, needy person who is also in debt — does he give it? He said: \"Yes.\" Then he said: \"A person — may he take from it?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[5991,"The poor person is given the zakāt al-fiṭr, even if he takes it."],[5992,"If a poor person receives zakāt on the day of fiṭr, he does not have to pay it on his own behalf."],[5993,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think of a poor person who cannot find it — may he beg until he can pay it? He said: \"No, it is only obligatory on one who has the means.\""],[5994,"If a poor person receives zakāt on the day of fiṭr, he does not have to pay it on his own behalf."],[5995,"5863 5824 - Wakīʿ from Shuʿba, from al-Ḥakam, from Ibrāhīm, the like of it. Hidden text: If a poor person receives zakāt on the day of fiṭr, he does not have to pay it on his own behalf."],[5996,"ʿAṭāʾ was asked: Is there zakāt on a slave who tends a walled garden or livestock? He said: \"No, because the wealth he is in charge of has already been assessed for zakāt.\""],[5997,"ʿAbd al-Malik b. Marwān wrote to Ibn ʿAlqama concerning a slave who is in charge of livestock or a walled garden: there is no zakāt al-fiṭr on him, because the walled garden and livestock he is in charge of have already had zakāt assessed on them through him."],[5998,"He used to pay zakāt — or he said: give — on behalf of the workers of his land."],[5999,"Ibn ʿUmar used to pay the zakāt al-fiṭr in Madinah on behalf of his slaves who worked on his land, and on behalf of his wife's slaves, and on behalf of every person he supported."],[6000,"He used to pay zakāt on behalf of his slaves who were on his land and with his livestock."],[6001,"It is upon the shepherds."],[6002,"He asked Ibn al-Musayyib, ʿUrwah b. al-Zubayr, and Abū Salamah b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān about a man’s slaves among his livestock, and they said: “He should feed on their behalf.”"],[6003,"Zakāt al-fiṭr is not obligatory upon farm laborers and hired herders."],[6004,"Ibn ʿUmar used to pay zakāt al-fiṭr on behalf of every slave he owned, whether present, absent, or on a farm, to the extent that he would perhaps pay on behalf of sixty or seventy."],[6005,"If you are able, pay your zakāh before the prayer, or in advance of the prayer – meaning the fiṭr prayer."],[6006,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “When do you order your food to be given?” He said: “I go out in the pre-dawn, then command it to be sent out after me, before the prayer.”"],[6007,"Command your food, when you leave for the prayer, to be taken away."],[6008,"Ibn ʿUmar used to send the ṣadaqah of Ramaḍān when those who collected it sat down, and that was one or two days before fiṭr."],[6009,"Ibn ʿUmar used to send the ṣadaqah of Ramaḍān when those who collected it sat down, and that was one or two days before fiṭr."],[6010,"Ibn ʿUmar used to send out zakāt al-fiṭr before going out to the prayer place, when those who collected it sat down, and that was one or two days before fiṭr."],[6011,"He used to be ordered to pay the zakāh before going out to the prayer place."],[6012,"There is no harm in paying zakāt al-fiṭr one or two days before it, or one or two days after fiṭr.” He added: “He himself used to pay it before setting out in the morning.”"],[6013,"I said to 'Ata': Is there any harm if I delay it until after breaking the fast? He said: \"No.\""],[6014,"I met Salim b. 'Abd Allah and others of our scholars and elders, and they would not give it except when they went out."],[6015,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that the zakat al-fitr be given before the people go out to the prayer place."],[6016,"The Prophet ﷺ stood and commanded the people to give it before they go out to the prayer place, as a sunnah."],[6017,"The people would give their zakat and eat before going out to the prayer place."],[6018,"Al-Hasan was asked about zakat al-fitr, and he commanded us to give it. It was said: But they demand it. He said: \"Do not give it to them, and do not grant them comfort.\""],[6019,"He would collect the zakat al-fitr in the mosque of his neighborhood, then give it to the monks. Al-Thawri said: Others would give it to the Muslims."],[6020,"Ayyub would send the zakat al-fitr to his neighbors on plates."],[6021,"He allowed the people who are not near the congregational mosque in Basra to give their zakat, the zakat al-fitr, to the needy among their relatives. We said to 'Abd al-Razzaq: Do you put it in the congregational mosque? He said: If they do not store it, then yes. But if I know that they store it, I distribute it among my neighbors. We said to him: Ma'mar used to send it to the mosque, and at that time they did not store it."],[6022,"It was recommended for the desert dwellers to go out on the day of Eid and for one of them to lead them in prayer."],[6023,"If the desert dwellers wish, they need not pray the Eid al-Fitr prayer except in a central village."],[6024,"The Prophet ﷺ sent to the villages of Uraynah, Fadak, Yanbuʿ and similar villages, a journey of three days from Madinah, that they should gather and attend the two eids."],[6025,"He used to be at his residence in al-Zawiyah, and if he did not attend the eid in al-Basrah, he would gather his family, his children, and his clients, then he would order his client ʿAbd Allah b. Abi ʿUtbah and he would lead them in two rakʿahs."],[6026,"Tawus would not leave any black slave girl of his or others except that he ordered them to dye their hands and feet for the day of Fitr and the day of Adha, saying: 'It is a day of eid.'"],[6027,"I was informed that the wives of the Prophet ﷺ used to dye after the last [night] prayer until morning."],[6028,"It reached me that you narrated from your father that the Prophet ﷺ used to wear a burd for each of the two eids. He said: I did not say that, but I was informed from my father that he said: The Prophet ﷺ wore on the Farewell Pilgrimage on the day of Arafah a hullah—or a burd."],[6029,"The prostrations of the Quran are ten: al-Aʿraf, al-Nahl, al-Raʿd, Bani Israʾil, Maryam, al-Hajj, al-Furqan, the middle Tā Sīn, Alif Lām Mīm Tanzil, and Ḥā Mīm al-Sajdah. I said: Did Ibn Abbas not say that there is a prostration in Ṣād? He said: No."],[6030,"He heard Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar counting how many prostrations are in the Quran, and they said: Al-Aʿraf, al-Raʿd, al-Nahl, Bani Israʾil, Maryam, al-Hajj [the first of them], al-Furqan, Tā Sīn, Alif Lām Mīm Tanzil, Ṣād, and Ḥā Mīm al-Sajdah—eleven."],[6031,"In the Quran there are eleven prostrations, and he counted them."],[6032,"He asked Ibn Abbas: Is there a prostration in Ṣād? He said: Yes. Then he recited 'And We granted him...' until he reached '...so follow their guidance.' He said: He is one of them. And Ibn Abbas said: I saw Umar recite Ṣād on the minbar, then he descended and prostrated in it, then he climbed back onto the minbar."],[6033,"The ʿazāʾim are four: Alif Lām Mīm Tanzil, Ḥā Mīm al-Sajdah, al-Najm, and 'Read in the name of your Lord who created.' [1]"],[6034,"I saw ʿUthman prostrate in Ṣād."],[6035,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ prostrate in Ṣād, though Ṣād is not one of the ʿazāʾim."],[6036,"The Messenger of God ﷺ recited Sad on the pulpit, then he descended and prostrated."],[6037,"I saw Ibn Abbas prostrate in Sad."],[6038,"He heard Ibn Abbas when he was asked, “Is there a prostration in Sad?” He said, “Yes, ‘Those are they whom God has guided, so follow their guidance’ (Q 6:90).”"],[6039,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of God, I saw as if a man were writing the Quran while a tree was beside him. When he passed by the place of the prostration in Sad, the tree prostrated and said, ‘O God, cause through it gratitude for me, magnify through it reward for me, and remove through it sin from me.’” The Prophet ﷺ said, “We are more deserving than the tree.”"],[6040,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said about the prostration in Sad, “David prostrated it as repentance, and I prostrated it as gratitude.”"],[6041,"His father used to prostrate in Sad."],[6042,"There is a prostration in Sad."],[6043,"It is but the repentance of a prophet that was mentioned. So he would not prostrate in it — meaning Sad."],[6044,"Ibn Abbas used to prostrate at the last one in Ha Mim, “while they do not weary” (Q 41:38)."],[6045,"Ibn Abbas said to a man who prostrated at the first one, “If it is Him you worship” (Q 41:37), “You were hasty.”"],[6046,"He used to prostrate at the last one, “while they do not weary” (Q 41:38)."],[6047,"He used to prostrate in it, “while they do not weary” (Q 41:38)."],[6048,"Al-Hasan used to prostrate in the first one: “If it is Him you worship.”"],[6049,"He used to prostrate in the first one: “If it is Him you worship.”"],[6050,"Umar prostrated in al-Najm, then stood and joined it to a surah."],[6051,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ prostrate in al-Najm, and the people prostrated with him. Al-Muttalib said: I did not prostrate with them—he was a polytheist that day. Al-Muttalib said: I will never fail to prostrate in it."],[6052,"He recited Yusuf in the dawn prayer and bowed, then recited in the second unit al-Najm, stood and prostrated, then recited: “When the earth is shaken with its [final] earthquake.”"],[6053,"Ammar prostrated in “When the sky has split open.”"],[6054,"I saw Umar and Abd Allah prostrating in “When the sky has split open”—then he said: or one of them, and this is what we follow."],[6055,"Abu Hurayrah used to prostrate in “When the sky has split open.”"],[6056,"Abu Hurayrah used to prostrate in it, and Abu Hurayrah said: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ prostrating in it."],[6057,"We prostrated with the Messenger of God ﷺ in “When the sky has split open” and “Recite in the name of your Lord who created.”"],[6058,"I prostrate in “When the sky has split open.”"],[6059,"He attended Umar b. al-Khattab on a Friday when he recited Surah al-Nahl on the pulpit until he reached the prostration, then he descended and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him. The following Friday he recited it until he reached the prostration and said: “O people, we only pass by the prostration. Whoever prostrates has done right and well, and whoever does not prostrate, no sin is upon him.” He said: “And Umar did not prostrate.”"],[6060,"Prostration is not obligatory upon us unless we wish."],[6061,"Umar and Ibn Umar used to make two prostrations in (Surah) al-Hajj. He said: Ibn Umar said: \"If I prostrated once in it, the other prostration would be more beloved to me.\" He said: Ibn Umar said: \"This surah is distinguished with two prostrations.\""],[6062,"I saw Ibn Umar prostrating twice in (Surah) al-Hajj."],[6063,"In Surah al-Hajj, the first is decisive, and the latter is instructive, and he used not to prostrate in it."],[6064,"Ibn Umar, when he recited (Surah) al-Najm, would prostrate in it while in prayer; if he did not prostrate, he would bow."],[6065,"Surah al-Hajj is distinguished with two prostrations."],[6066,"He saw Umar at al-Jabiyah prostrate in (Surah) al-Hajj twice."],[6067,"Ibn Umar used to prostrate in “When the sky is split open” (al-Inshiqaq 84:1)."],[6068,"Ibn Umar, when he recited (Surah) al-Najm, would prostrate, and when he recited “In the Name of your Lord who created” (al-Alaq 96:1) in prayer, he would say takbir, bow and prostrate; and when he recited them outside prayer, he would prostrate in both."],[6069,"When you prostrate in a prostration, do not bow until you recite some verses after it."],[6070,"He asked Zayd b. Thabit about (Surah) al-Najm: Is there a prostration in it? Zayd said: I recited it before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he did not prostrate."],[6071,"There is no prostration in the Mufassal."],[6072,"There is no prostration in the Mufassal."],[6073,"It is like that from Ibn Abbas."],[6074,"I said to Ata: Is prostration obligatory? He said: No. It has reached me that Umar b. al-Khattab, while he was reciting a surah containing a prostration and those around him prostrated, said: “Had you not prostrated, I would not have prostrated, and it is not in the prayer.”"],[6075,"There is no prostration in the mufassal."],[6076,"It is like that from Ata."],[6077,"The Prophet ﷺ prostrated in the mufassal when he was in Makkah, saying: “Then he did not prostrate after that.”"],[6078,"I said to Ata: Is prostration obligatory? He said: No. It has reached me that Umar b. al-Khattab, while he was reciting a surah containing a prostration and those around him prostrated, said: “Had you not prostrated, I would not have prostrated, and it is not in the prayer.”"],[6079,"Uthman passed by a storyteller who recited a prostration so that Uthman would prostrate with him. Uthman said: “Prostration is only upon the one who listens.” Then he went on and did not prostrate."],[6080,"I recited the prostration verse in the presence of Ibn Masud and looked at him. He said: “What are you looking at? You recited it, so if you prostrate, we will prostrate.”"],[6081,"The prostration is only upon the one who sits for it. If you pass by and they prostrate, you are not required to prostrate."],[6082,"Salman passed by a group of people sitting and they recited the prostration verse and prostrated. It was said to him, and he said: “It is not for that that we came.”"],[6083,"Imran b. al-Husayn passed by a storyteller, and the storyteller recited a prostration verse. Imran went on and did not prostrate with him, and said: “The prostration is only upon the one who sits for it.”"],[6084,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite the Quran to us, and when he came to a prostration verse he said takbir and prostrated, and we prostrated with him."],[6085,"Umar recited on the pulpit a surah containing a prostration verse, then descended and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him. He recited that same surah on the following Friday, and when he approached the prostration verse, the people prepared to prostrate. He said: “It is only upon us if we wish.” Then he recited it and did not prostrate."],[6086,"I said to 'Ata': Is the prostration obligatory in prayer? He said: \"No.\" Then he said: \"If it were obligatory upon you in prayer, it would be obligatory upon you in recitation.\" I said: Which is more beloved to you? He said: \"The prostration.\""],[6087,"A man recited a surah in which there is a prostration in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, and when he finished he said: O Messenger of God, is there no prostration in this surah? He said: \"Yes, but you were the imam, so had you prostrated we would have prostrated.\""],[6088,"When you reach the prostration, then if you wish you may make it a rak'ah. Ibn Jurayj said: And Ibn Tawus said it."],[6089,"'A'ishah was asked about the prostration of the Quran, and she said: \"It is a right due to God that you render, or a voluntary act that you perform voluntarily. There is no Muslim who prostrates to God a prostration except that God raises him a degree thereby, or removes from him a sin thereby, or combines both of them for him.\""],[6090,"I compiled the Quran and recited it in one night, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I fear that time will be long for you and that you will become weary. Recite it in a month.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength and my youth.' He said: 'Recite it in twenty.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength and my youth.' He said: 'Recite it in ten.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength and my youth.' He said: 'Recite it in seven.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength,' but he refused."],[6091,"There is no servant who prostrates to God a prostration except that God raises him a degree thereby and removes from him a sin thereby."],[6093,"If the prostration is the seal of the surah, then if you wish you may bow, and if you wish you may prostrate."],[6095,"Al-A'raf, Bani Isra'il, and \"Recite in the name of your Lord,\" \"By the star,\" and \"When the sky is split asunder\"—if he wishes he may bow, and if he wishes he may prostrate."],[6096,"If you pass by the star, and \"When the sky is split asunder,\" and \"Recite in the name of your Lord who created,\" and Bani Isra'il, and the end of al-A'raf, then if you wish you may prostrate and then connect to it something of the Quran, and if you wish you may bow."],[6097,"When you reach the prostration, then if you wish you may make it a rak'ah. Ibn Jurayj said: And Ibn Tawus said it."],[6098,"That his father sometimes bowed in \"Alif Lam Mim. The revelation\" when he reached the prostration, and he would not leave it every night but that he would recite it."],[6099,"He said: I said, “O Messenger of God, which mosque was placed on earth first?” He said: “The Sacred Mosque.” He said: I said, “Then which?” He said: “The Farthest Mosque.” He said: I said, “How much is between them?” He said: “Forty years.” Then he said: “Wherever prayer overtakes you, pray, for it is a mosque.” Additional text: Al-Taymi would sometimes recite a prostration verse while passing by and would prostrate just as he was on the road."],[6100,"Regarding a man who recites a prostration verse in prayer and prostrates, then adds another prostration to it, he said: “When he finishes, he should perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness.”"],[6101,"When you recite a prostration verse near the Kaʿbah, face the Kaʿbah and gesture with a nod."],[6102,"He would recite a prostration verse while walking and would gesture with a nod."],[6104,"The two of them, when they recited a prostration verse, would say takbir when they prostrated and would say taslim when they finished."],[6106,"He would recite to us while we were facing Banu Sulaym, not facing the qiblah, and he would come upon a prostration verse and would gesture with a nod, then say taslim."],[6107,"There is no taslim in the prostrations."],[6108,"I was with Ibn ʿUmar when a storyteller recited a prostration verse after the dawn prayer. Ibn ʿUmar shouted at him, and the storyteller prostrated, but Ibn ʿUmar did not prostrate. When the sun rose, Ibn ʿUmar made it up, saying: “He prostrated it.”"],[6109,"If you hear a prostration verse while you are not in a state of ritual purity, perform tayammum, then prostrate."],[6111,"Ibn Umar used to shout at them when he saw them — meaning the storytellers — prostrating after Fajr."],[6112,"If you hear the prostration while you are praying, then prostrate; but if you are bowing or prostrating, it suffices you for the prostration."],[6114,"He was asked about a man killing a scorpion during prayer. He said: “In prayer there is indeed enough to occupy one.”"],[6116,"Is it disliked for a person to divide his recitation by one surah before another? He said: No."],[6117,"I was with Aishah when an Iraqi came to her and said: Which shroud is best? She said: Woe to you, and what does it matter to you? He said: O Mother of the Believers, show me your mushaf so that perhaps I can arrange the Quran according to it, for we recite it unarranged. She said: And what does it matter to you which you recite first? It was revealed beginning with a surah of the mufassal containing mention of Paradise and Hell, until when the people had settled into Islam, the lawful and the unlawful were revealed. Had it been revealed first, “Do not drink wine,” they would have said, “We will never give up wine,” and had it been revealed, “Do not approach women,” they would have said, “We will never give them up.” Indeed, in Makkah — while I was a young girl playing — there was revealed to Muhammad ﷺ, “And the Hour is more grievous and more bitter,” and Surat al-Baqarah was not revealed except while I was with him. He said: So she brought out the mushaf and dictated to him the verses of the surahs."],[6118,"Ibn Sirin used to recite the Quran in portions, then he would add to them another surah from the Quran, until sometimes he would add to them a seventh of the Quran, and he used to recite the Quran in seven days."],[6120,"Whoever recites the Qur'an has for each verse ten good deeds. I do not say Alif Lam Mim is ten, but Alif is ten, Lam is ten, and Mim is ten—thirty good deeds."],[6121,"Whoever recites the Qur'an has for each verse ten good deeds. I do not say Alif Lam Mim is ten, but Alif is ten, Lam is ten, and Mim is ten—thirty good deeds."],[6122,"Do not recite the Quran in less than three days; recite it in seven. A man should keep to his portion day and night."],[6123,"Indeed we recite it, or indeed I recite it, in eight."],[6125,"I asked Zayd b. Thabit about a man who recites the Quran in seven, and he said: 'It is good, but for me to recite it in fifteen—or twenty—is more beloved, for I pause in it and reflect.'"],[6130,"He used to recite the Quran in Ramadan every three days, but when the last ten days entered, he would recite it in two nights and perform ghusl every night."],[6132,"He asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in how many days the Quran should be recited. He said: 'In forty.' He said: 'I can do more than that.' He said: 'In a month.' He said: 'I can do more than that.' He said: 'In fifteen.' Then he said: 'In ten.' Then he said: 'In seven.' He did not go below seven."],[6133,"Abdullah b. Amr asked the Prophet ﷺ: 'In how many days should the Quran be recited?' He said: 'In a month.' He said: 'I can do more than that.' He mentioned the same as the hadith of Simak, until he reached three. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Whoever recites it in less than three does not understand it.'"],[6134,"When the Prophet ﷺ sent Mu'adh b. Jabal and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari to Yemen, he said to them: 'Make things easy and do not make them difficult, do not separate, and cooperate.' Abu Musa said: 'A drink is made in our land from honey called bit', and from barley called mizr.' The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Every intoxicant is forbidden.' Mu'adh said to Abu Musa: 'How do you recite the Quran?' He said: 'I recite it in my prayer, on my mount, lying down, and sitting, taking it bit by bit.' Mu'adh said: 'But I sleep, then I get up and recite it—meaning his portion—and I count my sleep as I count my standing.' So it was as if Mu'adh b. Jabal surpassed him."],[6135,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a short man called Zanim and fell down in prostration, then raised [his head] and said: \"I ask Allah for well-being.\""],[6136,"When Allah turned to him in mercy and his repentance was revealed, he fell down in prostration."],[6137,"I was with Ali on the day of al-Nahrawan, and he said: \"Search for Dhu al-Thudayya.\" They searched for him but could not find him. Ali's forehead began to sweat, and he said: \"By Allah, I have not lied, nor have I been told a lie. Search for him.\" He said: Then we found him in a water channel—or a stream—under the slain. He was brought to Ali, and he fell down in prostration."],[6139,"The Prophet ﷺ went out and saw a short man—and al-nughashi means the short one."],[6140,"Do not make the Quran a pillow. By the One in whose hand is my soul, it slips away faster than hobbled camels—or he said: hobbled—to their watering place. By the One in whose hand is my soul, there is no verse in it but that it has an outward and an inward, and there is no letter in it but that it has a limit, and for every limit there is a place of ascent."],[6141,"Keep watch over the Quran, for it slips away from a man's chest faster than camels from their hobbles. How bad it is for one of you to say: \"I forgot such-and-such a verse.\" Rather, he was made to forget."],[6142,"Keep watch over the Quran, for it is wild; it slips away faster than camels from their hobbles. None of you should say: \"I forgot such-and-such a verse.\" Rather, he was made to forget."],[6143,"How bad it is for a man or a woman to say: \"I forgot such-and-such a surah.\" Rather, he was made to forget."],[6144,"Whoever learns the Quran and then forgets it without an excuse will have a degree removed from him for every verse, and he will come as an adversary."],[6145,"The example of the Quran, when its companion makes a pact with it, then supplicates and recites during the watches of the night and the ends of the day, is like a man who has camels: if he hobbles them, he preserves them, and if he lets their hobbles loose, they go astray. And thus is the companion of the Quran."],[6146,"Abu Bakr al-Siddiq had a son in whom there was some of what Abu Bakr did not approve, and he used to look down on him for that. He fell ill, and his father entered upon him. The boy said to him: 'I am sending you to the Messenger of God ﷺ with a message: convey to him on my behalf that I bear witness that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is His servant and His messenger.' Abu Bakr set out until he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and told him of that. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Convey to your son that he will have Paradise.' He said: Abu Bakr went out, and Umar met him and he told him. Umar said to him: 'Return with us to the Messenger of God ﷺ so that we may verify it from him.' They returned to the Prophet ﷺ and he said to him the like of this. Umar said: 'O Messenger of God, this is for the dead, so how about the living?' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'The like of it, and the like of it, and the like of it,' until he counted some thirty-odd times. He said: And al-Qasim gestured with his hand, thirty-four."],[6147,"There is no sin with which a servant meets on the Day of Resurrection, after what God has forbidden, greater than forgetting a sūrah he had memorized."],[6148,"There is no envy except in two cases: a man to whom God gave the Qurʾān and he stands with it through the hours of night and day, and a man to whom God gave wealth and he spends from it—meaning charity and the like—through the hours of night and day."],[6149,"May God have mercy on so-and-so; how often he reminded me of the verse and the verses that I had indeed forgotten."],[6150,"The Prophet ﷺ recited one night Ḥā-Mīm ʿAyn-Sīn-Qāf and repeated it several times—Ḥā-Mīm ʿAyn-Sīn-Qāf—while he was in Maymūnah's house. He said: \"O Maymūnah, do you have Ḥā-Mīm ʿAyn-Sīn-Qāf with you?\" She said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then recite it to me, for I have been made to forget what is between its beginning and its end.\""],[6151,"The rewards of my community were shown to me, even the speck—or the dung—that a man removes from the mosque. And the sins of my community were shown to me, and I saw no sin greater than a verse or a sūrah a man was given and then he was made to forget it."],[6152,"For shooting stars to strike my chest is dearer to me than to drop anything from the Qurʾān."],[6153,"Keep looking at the muṣḥaf. If you disagree about a yāʾ or a tāʾ, make it: 'Remind me of the Qurʾān.'"],[6154,"\"This Qurʾān will surely be snatched away from among you.\" He said: I said: \"O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, how will it be snatched away when we have fixed it in our hearts and fixed it in our maṣāḥif?\" He said: \"It will be taken up by night, and nothing of it will remain in the heart of any servant or in any muṣḥaf. The people will wake up poor, like beasts.\" Then ʿAbd Allāh recited: \"And if We willed, We could surely take away that which We have revealed to you. Then you would find no guardian for you against Us in that.\""],[6155,"\"The first thing you will lose of your religion is trustworthiness, and the last thing that will remain of your religion is prayer. People who have no religion will surely pray, and the Qurʾān will surely be snatched away from among you.\" They said: \"O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, do we not recite the Qurʾān, and have we not fixed it in our maṣāḥif?\" He said: \"It will be taken up by night and removed from the hearts of men, and nothing of it will remain.\""],[6156,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ in the morning and said: \"I had a sūrah with me, but I went to recite it and could not.\" Another man said to him: \"I too had one with me, but I could not.\" He said: \"I do not know if there were two men or three.\" They entered upon the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"It has been raised, among a Qurʾān that was raised.\""],[6157,"A delegation came to the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah and asked him to leave them alone for their need. He said: \"Last night I missed my portion of the Qurʾān.\""],[6158,"Indeed this Qur'an has been recited by children and slaves who have no knowledge of its interpretation, and they have not approached the matter from its beginning.\" And he said: \"﴿[This is] a blessed Book which We have revealed to you that they might reflect upon its verses﴾, and reflecting upon its verses is nothing but following it with knowledge. By Allah, it is not about memorizing its letters while wasting its limits, to the point that one of them says: By Allah, I have recited the entire Qur'an and I have not omitted a single letter from it, but he has omitted all of it. You do not see for him in the Qur'an any character or action. Even one of them says: By Allah, I recite a surah in a single breath. By Allah, these are not the reciters, nor the scholars, nor the wise, nor the pious. When did the reciters ever say such a thing? May Allah not increase the likes of these among the Muslims.\""],[6159,"Turn the deceased to face the Qibla."],[6160,"Turn the deceased to face the Qibla."],[6161,"The best reciter of this Qur'an among the people is the hypocrite; he does not leave out an alif or a waw from it, wrapping it with his tongue as a cow wraps up grass with its tongue."],[6162,"When one of you asks his companion: How do you recite such-and-such a verse? Let him ask him about the one before it."],[6163,"Whoever learns the Qur'an and then forgets it will meet Allah mutilated."],[6164,"\"How many verses do you recite from Surah al-Ahzab?\" I said: \"Eighty-some verses.\" He said: \"We used to recite it with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as long as Surah al-Baqarah or longer, and we used to recite in it the verse of stoning: 'The old man and the old woman, stone them absolutely as a punishment from Allah, and Allah is Mighty, Wise.'\""],[6165,"Learn the Qur'an, for it will intercede for its companions on the Day of Resurrection. Learn al-Baqarah and Al 'Imran; learn the two radiant ones, for they will come on the Day of Resurrection like two clouds—or two canopies—or like two flocks of birds in rows, pleading for their companion. And learn al-Baqarah, for learning it is a blessing and abandoning it is a regret, and the sorcerers cannot handle it—meaning the idle."],[6166,"If it were said to one of you: 'If you go early to the village, you will have four young camels,' he would spend the night saying: 'It is time for me to go early.' If one of you were to go early and learn a verse from the Book of Allah, it would be better for him than four and four and four, until he counted many things. Abu Ishaq said: And Abu 'Ubaydah informed me that when Ibn Mas'ud woke up in the morning and went out, people came to his house, and he would say: 'Stay where you are.' Then he would pass by those whom he was teaching the Qur'an and say: 'O so-and-so, which surah are you on?' They would tell him, and he would say: 'Which verse?' and he would prompt him with the following verse, then say: 'Learn it, for it is better for you than what is between the heaven and the earth.' He said: So the man would think that there is no verse in the Qur'an better than it. Then he would pass by another and say the same to him, until he had said that to all of them."],[6167,"Whoever recites the Qur'an has for each verse ten good deeds. I do not say Alif Lam Mim is ten, but Alif is ten, Lam is ten, and Mim is ten—thirty good deeds."],[6168,"Allah chose speech, and He chose the Qur'an; from it He chose Surah al-Baqarah; from Surah al-Baqarah He chose the Throne Verse. He chose the lands and chose the Sanctuary; He chose the Sanctuary and chose the Mosque; He chose the Mosque and chose the site of the House."],[6169,"The best of you is the one who learns the Qur'an and teaches it."],[6170,"The deceased is washed an odd number of times."],[6171,"Hud and its sisters have made me gray: Surat al-Waqi'ah, Surat al-Qiyamah, (and the mursalat), (when the sun is folded up), (when the sky is split open), and (when the sky is rent asunder)."],[6173,"This Quran is God's banquet. Whoever can learn something from it, let him do so. The emptiest house of good is the house in which there is nothing of the Book of God Most High. A house in which there is nothing of the Book of God is ruined like the ruin of a house that has no inhabitant. Satan leaves the house in which he hears Surat al-Baqarah being recited."],[6174,"A house in which the Quran is recited has abundant good, its family is granted expansion, angels attend it, and devils abandon it. A house in which it is not recited is constricted for its family, its good is little, angels abandon it, and devils attend it. A house in which the Quran is recited and pondered shines for the people of heaven as a star shines for the earth.\" He said: Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Give glad tidings of light from God on the Day of Resurrection to those who walk in darkness to the mosques."],[6175,"Three will recite the Quran: a man who recites it seeking God's pleasure and hoping for His reward from Him — his reward is upon God; a man who recites it for show and reputation, to eat by it in this world — that is against him, not for him; and a man who recites it but his recitation does not pass — or he said: his mark — his collarbone."],[6176,"Which verse in the Book of God is greatest? He said: God and His Messenger know best, repeating it several times. Then Ubayy said: The Verse of the Throne. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"May knowledge be pleasant for you, Abu al-Mundhir. By the One in whose hand is my soul, it has a tongue and two lips that sanctify the King at the foot of the Throne.\""],[6177,"We sat in the mosque and they came to us. One of them said to his companion: He has not come to us except that we should narrate to them. Either you narrate to them and I confirm you, or I narrate to them and you confirm me. One of them said: I heard 'Abd Allah say: The greatest verse in the Quran is the Verse of the Throne. The other said: You have spoken truth. The other said: I heard 'Abd Allah say: The most comprehensive verse in the Quran is (Indeed, God commands justice and excellence). He said: You have spoken truth. And I heard him say: The verse in the Quran that gives most trust in God is (Whoever fears God, He will make a way out for him). He said: He said: You have spoken truth. He said: And I heard him say: The verse in the Quran with the greatest hope of relief is (O My servants who have transgressed against themselves). He said: You have spoken truth."],[6178,"Whoever washes a deceased person, let him perform ghusl."],[6179,"(Say: He is God, the One) equals a third of the Quran."],[6180,"(Say: He is God, the One) equals a third of the Quran."],[6181,"(Say: He is God, the One) equals a third of the Quran."],[6183,"“When the earth is shaken” is half of the Quran, and “Say: O disbelievers” is a quarter of the Quran.”"],[6184,"Everything has a heart, and the heart of the Quran is Ya Sin. Whoever recites it, it equals the Quran—or he said: it equals reciting the entire Quran. And whoever recites “Say: O disbelievers,” it equals a quarter of the Quran, and “When the earth is shaken” is half of the Quran."],[6185,"The Quran is an intercessor whose intercession is accepted, and an advocate who is believed. Whoever puts it in front of him, it will lead him to Paradise, and whoever puts it behind him, it will drive him to the Fire."],[6186,"She is buried as she is."],[6187,"She is buried as she is."],[6188,"She is buried as she is."],[6189,"The Quran will come on the Day of Resurrection in the form of a pale, estranged person and will say to its companion: Do you recognize me? He will say: Who are you? It will say: I am your intimate friend, your bedfellow, your compassionate companion, the one who kept you awake at night and wearied you by day, and who went with you wherever you went. Every trader has obtained from his trade, and today I am for you behind every trader. He will be given sovereignty in his right hand and eternity in his left hand, and the crown of dignity will be placed on his head, and it will be said to him: Go in lasting bliss. His parents will be clothed in two garments that the world could never have provided for them. They will say: Why this, when we did not act for it? It will be said: Because your son took to the Quran. Then it will be said: Recite and ascend. Whoever used to recite it carefully will be according to that, and whoever used to recite it quickly will be according to that."],[6190,"What an excellent treasure for the poor is Surah Al Imran. He rises at the end of the night and stands reciting it."],[6192,"The one skilled in the Quran will be with the noble, righteous scribes, and the one who recites it with difficulty will have two rewards."],[6193,"This Quran is the banquet of Allah, so learn from His banquet as much as you can. This Quran is the rope of Allah that He has commanded, and it is the clear light and the beneficial healing—a protection for those who hold fast to it and a salvation for those who cling to it. It does not bend so as to be straightened, nor does it deviate so as to be corrected. Its wonders never cease, and it does not wear out from repetition. Recite it, for Allah will reward you for every letter with ten good deeds. I do not say that alif lam mim is one letter, but alif is a letter, lam is a letter, and mim is a letter."],[6194,"The Prophet ﷺ sent a group and appointed the youngest of them as their leader. They mentioned that, so he said: He has the most Quran among you. The example of the companion of the Quran is like a leather bag containing musk: if you open it—or it is opened—its fragrance spreads, and if it is tied shut, it is tied over something fragrant."],[6195,"Everything has a hump, and the hump of the Quran is Surah al-Baqarah. In it is a verse that is the mistress of the verses of the Quran: the Verse of the Throne. It is not recited in a house in which there is a devil except that he leaves."],[6196,"He would seek out the places of his prostration with perfume."],[6197,"6062 6021 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman b. Yazid, from Abu Masud al-Ansari, from the Prophet ﷺ, the like of it. And he added: Abd al-Rahman said: And Alqamah narrated it to me, from Abu Masud. He said: I met Abu Masud during tawaf and asked him about it, and he narrated it to me while he was performing tawaf. Hidden text: Whoever recites the two verses at the end of Surat al-Baqarah, they will suffice him."],[6198,"Whoever recites ten verses from the beginning of Surat al-Kahf will be protected from the trial of the Dajjal. And whoever recites its end - or he said: recites it to its end - it will be a light for him from his head to his foot."],[6199,"Whoever performs wudu and completes his wudu, then says: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise. I bear witness that there is no god but You. I seek Your forgiveness and turn to You in repentance, a seal will be placed upon it and it will be set under the Throne, and it will not be broken until the Day of Resurrection. And whoever recites Surat al-Kahf as it was revealed and then encounters the Dajjal, he will not be given power over him, nor will he have any way against him. And whoever recites the concluding part of Surat al-Kahf, its light will shine from where he recites it, between him and Makkah."],[6200,"A man died and the angels of punishment came and sat at his head. It (his head) said: You have no way against him; he used to recite Surat al-Mulk for me. Then they sat at his feet, and they said: You have no way against him; he used to stand upon us reciting Surat al-Mulk. Then they sat at his belly, and it said: You have no way against him; he contained Surat al-Mulk within me. So it was named al-Mani'ah (the Protector)."],[6201,"The man will be approached in his grave, and his feet will be approached, and they will say: You have no way against what is before us; he used to recite Surat al-Mulk upon us. Then his inside will be approached, and it will say: You have no way against me; he had contained Surat al-Mulk within me. Then his head will be approached, and it will say: You have no way against what is before me; he used to recite Surat al-Mulk through me."],[6203,"When we were learning ten verses of the Quran, we would not learn the ten after them until we learned what they contain of the lawful, the unlawful, the command, and the prohibition."],[6204,"Whoever recites one hundred verses in a night will not be recorded among the heedless. Whoever recites two hundred verses will have the qunut of that night recorded for him. Whoever recites from five hundred to a thousand will wake up with a qintar of reward.\" He said: Ibn Umar was asked, \"How much is a qintar?\" He said, \"Seventy thousand.\""],[6206,"There were two men in the past, one of whom used to adhere to 'Blessed is He in whose hand is the dominion' (Surat al-Mulk), and it argued on his behalf until he was saved. As for the companion of the small prostration (i.e., a surah ending with a prostration), it split into two parts in his grave: one part at his head and one part at his feet, until he was saved. So it was named al-Munqasimah (the Divider)."],[6209,"The likeness of the Quran when its companion keeps watch over it, reciting it night and day, is that of a man who has camels: if he hobbles them, he keeps them; if he releases their hobbles, they go away. And so it is with the companion of the Quran."],[6210,"Whoever reads the Quran and follows what is in it, Allah guides him from misguidance in this world, and protects him on the Day of Resurrection from the reckoning. That is because Allah, exalted is He, says: “So whoever follows My guidance will neither go astray nor suffer.”"],[6211,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to some people who were reciting the Quran, and said: “Recite, for all of it is a book belonging to Allah, before there come a people who straighten it as an arrow is straightened, who hasten it and do not delay it.”"],[6213,"Umar b. al-Khattab addressed the people and said: A time passed over us when we thought that no one learned the Book of Allah, exalted is He, except wanting Allah by it, until when it came to this later time, I thought that some people learn the Quran wanting by it the people and what they have. So intend Allah by your deeds and your recitation. We used to know you while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among us and revelation was descending and informing us of your news. As for today, I only know you by your statements. Whoever shows us good, we think good of him and love him for it, and whoever shows us evil, we think evil of him and hate him for it. Your secrets are between you and Allah."],[6214,"He used to breathe three times during the praise."],[6215,"When revelation descended upon the Prophet ﷺ, a sound like the buzzing of bees was heard near his face. It descended upon him and we remained for an hour. He faced the qiblah and raised his hands and said: “O Allah, increase us and do not decrease us, honor us and do not humiliate us, give us and do not deprive us, prefer us and do not prefer others over us, and be pleased with us.” Then he said: “Ten verses were sent down to me; whoever establishes them will enter Paradise.” Then he recited to us “The believers have certainly succeeded” until he completed the ten."],[6216,"He would seek out the places of his prostration with perfume."],[6218,"I asked Ubayy b. Ka'b about the two mu'awwidhat, and he said: I asked the Prophet ﷺ about them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “It was said to me,” so I said: Ubayy said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Thus we say.”"],[6219,"Was it commanded to dictate to the sick person when death is present with him? He said: “Indeed I love that.”"],[6220,"Do not mention your dead except with good, and dictate to them the testimony that there is no god but Allah."],[6221,"Attend your dying ones and make them repeat “there is no god but Allah,” close their eyes, and recite the Quran near them."],[6222,"Abu Bakr al-Siddiq had a son in whom there was some of what Abu Bakr did not approve, and he used to look down on him for that. He fell ill, and his father entered upon him. The boy said to him: 'I am sending you to the Messenger of God ﷺ with a message: convey to him on my behalf that I bear witness that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is His servant and His messenger.' Abu Bakr set out until he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and told him of that. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Convey to your son that he will have Paradise.' He said: Abu Bakr went out, and Umar met him and he told him. Umar said to him: 'Return with us to the Messenger of God ﷺ so that we may verify it from him.' They returned to the Prophet ﷺ and he said to him the like of this. Umar said: 'O Messenger of God, this is for the dead, so how about the living?' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'The like of it, and the like of it, and the like of it,' until he counted some thirty-odd times. He said: And al-Qasim gestured with his hand, thirty-four."],[6223,"Whoever says at the time of his death: 'There is no god but God,' it will save him one day of the age, whatever had befallen him before that."],[6224,"He would seek out the places of his prostration with perfume."],[6226,"Prompt your dead with 'There is no god but God,' for it demolishes sins. It was said to him: 'How about the living?' He said: 'It is more demolishing, and more demolishing.'"],[6227,"Five things by which God confirms the servant when he says them: when he says 'There is no god but God, and God is greater,' God says: 'My servant has spoken truth.' When he says 'There is no god but God, and praise belongs to God,' God says: 'My servant has spoken truth.' When he says 'There is no god but God, He has no partner,' God says: 'My servant has spoken truth.' When he says 'There is no god but God alone,' He says: 'My servant has spoken truth.' When he says 'There is no god but God, to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise,' He says: 'My servant has spoken truth.'\" He said: I met Shu'bah, and he narrated this to me from al-Agharr, from [additional text] Abu Hurayrah, and he added in them: [additional text] 'Whoever says them at the time of his death, the Fire will not touch him.'"],[6229,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered upon Abu Salamah while he was ill, and he heard weeping from behind a screen. He said: 'The angels attend the deceased and say ameen to what his family says, for the eyesight follows the spirit.' And the Prophet ﷺ closed the eyes of Abu Salamah."],[6230,"When you close the eyes of the deceased, say: 'In the name of God, upon the death of the Messenger of God ﷺ.' And when you carry the deceased, say: 'In the name of God, and glorify Him.'"],[6231,"She was called to a deceased person who was in his death throes. Umm Salamah said to her: 'When you attend him, say: Peace be upon the messengers, and praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.'"],[6232,"From Alqamah b. Qays, when death approached him, he said: 'Do not announce my death to anyone, like the practice of the pre-Islamic age.'"],[6233,"He would seek out the places of his prostration with perfume."],[6234,"Ibn ʿUmar used to wait for a moment of people's inattention regarding his funeral procession."],[6235,"Do not announce my death to anyone; sufficient for me are those who carry me to my grave."],[6236,"There is no harm if a man dies that his close friend be informed. They used to dislike it being circulated in gatherings—announcing so-and-so's death—like the practice of jahiliyyah."],[6237,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced the deaths of the companions of Muʾtah one by one. He began with Zayd b. Ḥārithah, then Jaʿfar b. Abī Ṭālib, then he said: ʿAbd Allāh b. Rawāḥah, then he said: Then Khālid b. al-Walīd took the banner, and he is a sword among the swords of Allah."],[6238,"Was a person, when death approached him, commanded to be purified by washing? He said: That is indeed good."],[6239,"Have you considered turning the dying person's face toward the Qibla when his death is imminent, on his right side—is that a sunnah? He said: Glory be to Allah, I know of no one who understands that he would leave that for his dying person. By Allah, a man's bed would be moved until he is turned, if he is unable to do that himself."],[6240,"He would seek out the places of his prostration with perfume."],[6241,"I asked al-Shaʿbī about the deceased being turned toward the Qibla. He said: If you wish, turn him, and if you wish, do not turn him; but make the grave face the Qibla. The grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the grave of ʿUmar, and the grave of Abū Bakr are toward the Qibla."],[6242,"When death approached Ibn al-Musayyib while he was lying on his back, a person said: Turn him. He said: Am I not already facing it?"],[6243,"A man entered upon Ibn al-Musayyib while he was ill, lying on his back, and said: Turn him toward the Qibla. Saʿīd became angry and said: Am I not facing the Qibla?"],[6245,"The angels turned Ādam when death approached him, then they closed his eyes."],[6246,"When you attend the dying person or the sick person, say good words; for the angels say amin to what you say."],[6247,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered upon Abu Salamah while he was sick, and the Prophet's ﷺ entry coincided with the departure of his soul. He heard weeping and said: \"Do not supplicate against yourselves except for good; for the angels are present with the dying person,\" or he said: \"the family of the house, and they say amin to their supplication.\" Then he said: \"O God, forgive him his sins, make his grave spacious, magnify his light, and illuminate for him in his grave. O God, raise the degree of Abu Salamah among the guided ones, and be his successor among his descendants who remain, and forgive him, Lord of the worlds.\" Then he said: \"The sight fixed on the soul. Do you not see how his eyes are staring?\""],[6248,"When the sick person sees the angel, knowledge departs."],[6249,"Do you not see a person when his gaze becomes fixed?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"That is when his sight follows his soul.\""],[6250,"Al-Sha'bi was asked about a sword being placed on the belly of the deceased. He said: \"It is only done to prevent swelling, and it does not harm you whether you do it or not.\" He was asked about bringing shoes into the grave. He said: \"It is only disliked because of the fear of slipping.\""],[6252,"The consoler of calamities is clothed with a garment of faith that will be a screen for him from the Fire."],[6253,"He reported that he found a scroll tied to Safwan's scabbard or to his sword, and on it was written: \"This is what Ibrahim asked his Lord: 'O Lord, what is the reward of one whose tears moisten his face out of fear of You?' He said: 'My blessings.' He said: 'What is the reward of one who comforts the sorrowful seeking Your countenance?' He said: 'I will clothe him in garments of faith with which he will dwell in Paradise and be shielded from the Fire.' He said: 'What is the reward of one who supports the widow seeking Your countenance?' He said: 'What does he support?' He said: 'He provides for her. I will place him in My shade and admit him to My Paradise.' He said: 'What is the reward of one who follows the funeral procession seeking Your countenance?' He said: 'My angels will pray over his body and accompany his soul.'\" He said: \"And in it was visiting the sick, but I forgot it.\""],[6254,"Whoever consoles a believer in a calamity that has befallen him, God will clothe him on the Day of Resurrection with a robe that he will be adorned with."],[6255,"The deceased is washed an odd number of times: three, five, or seven, all with water and sidr, and in each washing his head is washed with the rest of his body.\" He said: I said: \"Is one washing sufficient?\" He said: \"Yes, if they clean him.\""],[6256,"Ibn Umar would let one end of the turban hang over the face of the deceased, then wrap it around his head from under the chin, then twist it around his head, then let the other end also hang over his face. We said to Abd al-Razzaq: And how? He said: Ma'mar showed us like this: he placed the end of the turban and let it hang over his face, then he folded back what hung over the face toward the throat, then he placed the turban over what hung over the face, folding it back under the chin, then he twisted it around his head, then he brought the end of the turban back onto his forehead, then he let what remained of it hang over his face as well."],[6257,"A woman is shrouded in five garments: a shift, a head-cover, a wrapper, a waist-wrapper, and an outer cloak."],[6258,"The shroud and the perfume are a debt."],[6260,"The shroud and the perfume are a debt."],[6261,"The washing of the deceased is three times. Whoever washes a dead person should cast a cloth over his face, then begin by performing ablution on him and washing his head. When he wants to wash his private parts, he should not touch them directly, but should take a rag and place it on his hand, then insert his hand beneath the cloth and wipe his belly so as to expel from it any impurity."],[6262,"The shroud is from all the wealth."],[6263,"He begins with his right side. He said: \"When he wants to perform ablution on him, he removes the one over his face, but as for the one over his private parts, he should not move it. Instead, he places a rag on his hand, then inserts it beneath the rag.\""],[6264,"When they do not find lote-tree leaves, they wash him with potash if his illness was prolonged and severe."],[6265,"If the deceased's illness was prolonged, he may be washed with potash if they wish."],[6266,"The Prophet ﷺ had a shirt on, so it was announced: Do not remove it."],[6267,"The shroud is from all the wealth."],[6268,"The shroud and the perfume are a debt."],[6269,"The shroud and the perfume are a debt."],[6270,"The Prophet ﷺ was washed in a shirt, and 'Ali, al-Fadl b. 'Abbas, and Salih b. Sa'dan, the client of the Prophet ﷺ, descended into his grave."],[6271,"The angels descended when death came to Adam. When he saw them, he recognized them, so they took his soul, washed him, shrouded him, prayed over him, and buried him while his sons looked on."],[6272,"This is the sunnah of your son."],[6273,"It is disliked to shave the pubic hair of the deceased."],[6274,"I saw him wash a deceased person and then dry his head with the censer."],[6275,"The daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died. He said: 'Wash her three times, or five, or more than that if you see fit. Wash her with water and lote tree leaves, and put some camphor in the last washing. When you finish, inform me.' She said: When we finished, we informed him, and he threw his waist wrapper to us and said: 'Shroud her with it.' She said: We made her hair into three braids and let them hang behind her."],[6276,"The daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died."],[6277,"The daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died. He said: 'Wash her three times, or five, or more than that if you see fit. Wash her with water and lote tree leaves, and put some camphor in the last washing. When you finish, inform me.' She said: When we finished, we informed him, and he threw his waist wrapper to us and said: 'Shroud her with it.' She said: We made her hair into three braids and let them hang behind her."],[6278,"\"Should a woman's hair be let down and washed loose with her?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[6279,"It is disliked to shave the pubic hair of the deceased."],[6281,"Ali sought from the Prophet ﷺ what is sought from the deceased and found nothing. He said: 'May my father and mother be your ransom, pure in life and pure in death.'"],[6282,"It is disliked to shave the pubic hair of the deceased."],[6283,"He is washed three times. If something comes out, what came out is washed, and it is not increased beyond three times."],[6284,"Whoever washes a deceased person, he comes out of his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him. Ibn Jurayj said: It reached me from al-Shaʿbī the like of that except that he added in his saying: \"Whoever washes a deceased person and then does not disclose anything about him,\" all of that from the Prophet ﷺ: \"Whoever washes a deceased person, he comes out of his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him.\""],[6285,"Whoever washes a deceased person, he comes out of his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him. Ibn Jurayj said: It reached me from al-Shaʿbī the like of that except that he added in his saying: \"Whoever washes a deceased person and then does not disclose anything about him,\" all of that from the Prophet ﷺ: \"Whoever washes a deceased person, he comes out of his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him.\""],[6286,"Whoever washes a deceased person and fulfills the trust in it, he will be [free] of his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him."],[6287,"He heard Yūsuf, who was a Jew and embraced Islam, say: In the Torah: \"Whoever shrouds a deceased person is like one who sponsors a young child until he grows up.\""],[6288,"The example of the one who shrouds a deceased person is like the one who sponsored him as a young child until he died."],[6289,"Ibn ʿAbbās was asked: Does the one who washes a deceased person have to perform ghusl? He said: \"No, in that case they would have deemed their companion impure; rather, it is wudu.\""],[6290,"He was asked: Should the one who washes a deceased person perform ghusl? He said: \"If he was ritually impure, then perform ghusl; otherwise, wudu is sufficient for one of you.\""],[6291,"Four of the Companions of the Tree washed your father, and they did no more than tuck up their garments; when they finished, they performed wudu and prayed over him."],[6292,"If he was ritually impure, then perform ghusl."],[6293,"They both did not consider ghusl obligatory upon the one who washes a deceased person, and they said: \"If your companion was ritually impure, then perform ghusl.\""],[6294,"Should I perform ghusl from [washing] a deceased person? He said: \"Is he a believer?\" I said: \"I hope so.\" He said: \"Then wipe yourself because of the believer, but do not perform ghusl from it.\""],[6297,"I was with ʿAlī in a funeral procession. He said: ʿAlī was holding my hand while we were behind it, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar were walking in front of it. He said: The virtue of the one walking behind it over the one walking in front of it is like the virtue of the congregational prayer over the prayer of one praying alone. They both know of that what I know, but they did not like to make things difficult for the people."],[6300,"Whoever washes a deceased person should perform ghusl, and whoever lowers him into his grave should perform wudu."],[6301,"Whoever prays over a funeral and follows it has two qīrāṭs of reward, like Uḥud, and whoever prays but does not follow has one qīrāṭ, like Uḥud."],[6302,"We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."],[6303,"Ibn Umar shrouded Sa'id b. Zayd, then prayed over him and carried him, then entered the mosque to pray without performing wudu."],[6304,"Ibn Umar shrouded Sa'id b. Zayd and carried him, then entered the mosque to pray without performing wudu."],[6305,"The wife of Abu Bakr washed him when he died; he had bequeathed that."],[6307,"Abu Bakr was washed by his wife Asma', and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari was washed by his wife Umm 'Abd Allah."],[6309,"If a woman dies among the people, the woman washes her husband, and the man washes his wife."],[6311,"The people most entitled to wash the woman and pray over her is her husband."],[6313,"Asma' bt. 'Umays, the wife of Abu Bakr, washed him when he died. Then she went out and asked those of the Muhajirun who were present, saying: 'I am fasting, and this is a very cold day; is washing required of me?' He said: 'No.'"],[6314,"Abu Bakr commanded his wife Asma' to wash him, and she was fasting. He insisted that she break her fast. She called for water before sunset and drank, and said: 'I will not follow him today with a sin in his grave.'"],[6315,"If a woman dies and they cannot find a woman to wash her, her husband or her son washes her. If they find a Jewish or Christian woman, she washes her."],[6316,"When death approached Fatimah, she told 'Ali to prepare water for her. She washed and purified herself, and called for her shroud garments. Thick garments were brought to her, and she wore them and applied some hanut. Then she told 'Ali not to uncover her when she died, and to wrap her as she was in her garments. He said: I said to him: 'Do you know anyone who did that?' He said: 'Yes, Kathir b. 'Abbas, and he wrote on the edges of his shrouds: Kathir b. 'Abbas bears witness that there is no god but Allah.'"],[6317,"A man who dies in a barren land: he said: 'He is given tayammum, and his face is wiped with dust.'"],[6318,"A slave girl of Safiyyah bt. Abi 'Ubayd fell ill with them on a journey. Nafi' said to her: 'Your slave girl fell ill with us.' Safiyyah said: 'Tell me, if she died, how would you deal with her?' I said: 'I don't know.' She said: 'She is buried as she is.'"],[6319,"She is buried as she is."],[6320,"I asked 'Ata': If I pray over a funeral bier and I am not following it? He said: I enter and do not wait to see it lifted."],[6322,"If a man dies among women when there is no man among them, he is given tayammum."],[6325,"When a man dies among women, or a woman among men, they are to be given tayammum and buried. They have the same status as one who cannot find water."],[6326,"If Abu 'Amr makes me disappear and lowers me into my grave, then he is free."],[6327,"The woman is placed in the prayer area at the feet of the man."],[6329,"Ibn 'Umar was asked about musk for the deceased. He said: 'Is it not among the finest of your perfumes?'"],[6330,"He used to perfume the deceased with musk, sprinkling it as a powder."],[6331,"Ibn 'Umar used to apply musk to the flexures and joints of the deceased."],[6332,"Salman obtained musk from Balanjar and gave it to his wife to keep. When he was dying, he said to her: 'Where is that which I entrusted to you?' She said: 'Here it is,' and brought it to him. He said: 'Sprinkle it around me, for there will come to me creatures of Allah's creation who do not eat food or drink drink; they perceive scent.'"],[6333,"Is musk disliked as a hanut? He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'What about ambergris?' He said: 'No. Ambergris and musk are but a drop from an animal.'"],[6334,"What about khaluq for the deceased? He said: 'That is yellow, and yellow was disliked.'"],[6335,"‘Ammār said: I perfumed myself with khaluq, then I came to the Prophet ﷺ and he rebuked me, saying: \"Go, O son of Umm ‘Ammār, and wash it off yourself.\" He said: So I returned and washed it off myself, then I came back to him and he rebuked me again, and ordered me to go back and perform a full bath. I did so, then I came back to him and he rebuked me and ordered me to go back and perform a full bath three times."],[6336,"The woman is placed in the prayer area at the feet of the man."],[6338,"He used to dislike saffron being put in any of the perfume of the deceased."],[6339,"When al-Ash‘ath b. Qays died, al-Hasan b. ‘Alī said: When you have washed him, do not disturb him until you bring him to me. When his washing was finished, he was brought to him, and he called for camphor and performed ablution on him with it, and put some on his face, and in his hands, head, and feet. Then he said: \"Wrap him.\""],[6340,"He saw Ayyūb sprinkling dry perfume on the deceased, his beard, and his chest."],[6341,"Should fire be used to follow the deceased with? Meaning the incense burner. He said: There is no good in that. He said: What about fumigating his garments? He said: \"That is fine, there is no harm in it.\""],[6342,"She said to her family: Fumigate my garments when I die, then shroud me, then perfume me, but do not sprinkle perfume on my shroud."],[6343,"Ibn al-Musayyib enjoined his family not to follow him with an incense burner."],[6344,"Abū Hurayrah enjoined his family not to set up a tent over his grave, nor to follow him with an incense burner, and to hasten with him."],[6346,"I do not think Ayyūb other than that he would dry the head of the deceased with an incense burner."],[6347,"The imam stands at the chest of the man and the shoulder of the woman."],[6348,"The imam stands at the chest of the man and the shoulder of the woman."],[6349,"I was with Sa'id b. Jubayr while he was following a funeral procession with an incense burner that was being followed with it. He threw it and broke it, and said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: Do not imitate the People of the Book."],[6350,"When the deceased is perfumed with incense, it should begin with his head until it reaches his feet, and you perfume an odd number of times. I was informed that the Prophet ﷺ commanded that."],[6351,"Ma'qil b. Yasar, at his death, instructed that no firebrand, meaning an incense burner, should come near him, nor should he be washed with hot water, and that prayer be said over him at his grave."],[6352,"The Prophet ﷺ saw an incense burner with a woman at a funeral, when he wanted to pray over it, and he shouted until it disappeared among the thickets of Madinah."],[6354,"The Prophet ﷺ was buried in a niche grave, then bricks were set up over his niche."],[6355,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in two thin white garments and a red burd."],[6356,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in two white burds and a red burd."],[6357,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in two Sahari garments and a hibara garment."],[6358,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in a Yemeni suit and a shirt."],[6360,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in a cloak and a shirt, and a niche grave was dug for him."],[6361,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in three white Sahuli garments."],[6362,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in three white Sahuli garments made of cotton, with no shirt or turban among them."],[6363,"The Prophet ﷺ was wrapped in a striped Hibara garment and was dried in it. Then it was removed and replaced with the Sahuli garment. The striped garment belonged to Abdullah b. Abi Bakr."],[6364,"The Prophet ﷺ was covered with a striped Hibara garment."],[6365,"Abu Bakr was shrouded in three garments, and prayer was said over him in the mosque, and he was buried at night."],[6366,"Abu Bakr asked Aisha in how many garments the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded. She said: 'In three garments.' He said: 'Then shroud me in three: this garment of mine, which has a mark on it, along with two other garments. And wash the garment he used to wear.' Aisha said: 'Should we not buy new ones for you?' He said: 'No, the living are more in need of new things. It is only for the pus.' On which day did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ die? She said: 'On Monday.' He said: 'What day is this?' She said: 'Monday.' He said: 'I hope to make it until night.' He died when evening came, and was buried that very night before morning."],[6367,"The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in a Yemeni suit and a shirt."],[6368,"Abu Bakr said about his two garments in which he was being nursed: 'Wash them and shroud me in them.' Aisha said: 'Should we not buy new ones for you?' He said: 'No, the living are more in need of new things than the dead.'"],[6369,"Abu Bakr was shrouded in three garments: two Yemeni striped sheets and a garment he used to wear. And he said: 'The living are more in need of new things. It is only for the pus.'"],[6370,"Ibn Umar used to shroud his family in five garments, among them a turban, a shirt, and three wrappers."],[6371,"Maymunah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, died at Sarif. I took my cloak and spread it under her, but Ibn Abbas took it and threw it away."],[6372,"Ibn Umar used to lengthen the standing in the funeral prayer, and the imam would say the takbir four times."],[6373,"Ibn Umar would let one end of the turban hang over the face of the deceased, then wrap it around his head from under the chin, then twist it around his head, then let the other end also hang over his face. We said to Abd al-Razzaq: And how? He said: Ma'mar showed us like this: he placed the end of the turban and let it hang over his face, then he folded back what hung over the face toward the throat, then he placed the turban over what hung over the face, folding it back under the chin, then he twisted it around his head, then he brought the end of the turban back onto his forehead, then he let what remained of it hang over his face as well."],[6374,"Umar was shrouded in three garments: two Sahuli garments and a garment that he used to wear."],[6375,"He would shroud in an odd number: a shirt and two wrappers. He would put the shirt on and wrap him in the two wrappers, and stuff cotton and dharirah."],[6376,"I saw Ayyub stuffing cotton."],[6377,"He would not recite anything in any of the takbirs, and he would say: 'O Allah, forgive the believing men and the believing women, and the Muslim men and the Muslim women, and bring their hearts together, and make their hearts follow the hearts of their best ones. O Allah, raise his degree among the rightly guided, and be his successor among those left behind regarding his inheritance. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not lead us astray after him.'"],[6378,"The deceased is shrouded in an odd number: a shirt and two wrappers. The shirt is put on, and one wrapper is spread over the other, then he is rolled up in them, and the shirt remains on him."],[6379,"The deceased is dressed in a shirt and a waist-wrapper and wrapped in the third. If there is only one garment, he is wrapped in it."],[6380,"Do not put a turban on me, nor a shirt, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not given a turban or a shirt."],[6381,"The deceased is not dressed in a waist-wrapper, nor is a cloak thrown over him, but he is wrapped in them in a wrapping."],[6383,"Should the deceased be wrapped with a turban? He said: \"No.\" I said: Should cotton be inserted? He said: \"Yes, so that nothing leaks from him.\""],[6384,"Hamzah was shrouded in a single garment."],[6386,"Hamzah was killed on the day of Uhud, and a man from the Ansar was killed with him. Safiyyah bt. Abd al-Muttalib came with two garments to shroud Hamzah in them, but there was no shroud for the Ansari. The Prophet ﷺ drew lots between the two garments, then each of them was shrouded in one garment."],[6387,"We emigrated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking the face of Allah, so our reward became due upon Allah. Among us are those who passed away without partaking of any of his reward, among them Musab b. Umayr. He was killed on the day of Uhud and left behind a burdah. When we placed it over his feet, his head was exposed, and when we placed it over his head, his feet were exposed. So the Prophet ﷺ ordered us to place it over his head and to put some idhkhir over it. Among us are those whose fruit has ripened, and he is plucking it."],[6388,"In those days a man would be shrouded in a single garment: if his head was covered, his feet were uncovered, and if his feet were covered, his head was uncovered. He said: Abu Bakr ordered either Aishah or Asma bt. Umays to wash two garments in which he had been treated during his illness. Aishah said: Or new clothes, or better than them? He said: \"The living are more entitled to that.\""],[6389,"What has reached you about what is recommended for a deceased person's shroud? He said: \"White is the least of it.\" I said: I see people have taken to hanging up qabati. He said: That is an innovation. Where were qabati in those days? Is it pride in life and pride in death?"],[6390,"Upon you is this whiteness: let your living wear it, and shroud your dead in it, for it is among the best of your garments."],[6391,"Wear white garments, for they are purer and cleaner, and shroud your dead in them."],[6392,"Wear white from your garments, for they are among the best of your garments, and shroud your dead in them. Among the best of your kohls is ithmid, for it makes hair grow and clears the sight."],[6394,"I attended the funeral of Hammam b. Munabbih, and Umar b. Abd al-Hamid, a man from the descendants of Umar b. al-Khattab, attended it. He was the first governor of the Abbasids, and nothing like him had ever been seen. He prayed, and there was a cloth over the bier. They wanted to place it in his grave to shroud him in it, but he pulled it away and said: His shroud is only what he was brought out from his house wearing. He said: \"So he did not let them shroud him in it.\""],[6395,"He was present when Abu Said al-Khudri was dying. Abu Said said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"Indeed, the deceased will be resurrected in the clothes in which his soul was taken.\" Then Abu Said said: I have instructed my family not to follow me with fire, and not to set up a tent over my grave. Carry me on a purple velvet cloth."],[6396,"When Abu Sa'id al-Khudri died, a red velvet cloth was placed for him. A man said, \"Indeed, I heard him narrate from the Prophet ﷺ that he saw redness and said, 'Indeed, redness has overcome you.'\""],[6397,"The Prophet ﷺ was lowered into the grave from the direction of his head, and the people after him."],[6398,"I witnessed Muḥammad b. al-Ḥanafiyyah when Ibn ʿAbbās died: he took hold of him from the direction of the qiblah when he brought him into the grave."],[6399,"The first tent struck over the grave of any of the Muslims was over the grave of Zaynab bt. Jahsh, and it was a hot day."],[6400,"Whoever takes charge of his brother, let him make his shroud good. It has reached me that they visit one another in their shrouds."],[6401,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that shrouds be of good quality."],[6402,"Hudhayfa b. al-Yaman sent me and another man to buy a shroud for him. I bought a good red garment for him for three hundred dirhams. When we came to him, he said, \"Show me what you bought.\" We showed him, and he said, \"Return it, and do not go to excess in the shroud. Buy for me two clean white garments, for they will not remain on me except for a short while until I am clothed in something better than them or worse than them.\""],[6403,"When Hudhayfa was near death, Hudhayfa said to Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, \"What part of the night is this?\" He said, \"The greater pre-dawn.\" He said, \"Seeking refuge with Allah from the Fire. Buy for me two garments and do not go to excess for yourselves. If your companion is pleasing, he will be clothed in something better than them; otherwise, he will be stripped with a swift stripping,\" or he said, \"quickly.\""],[6405,"When I die, buy for me a shroud for thirty dirhams. He said: And he was well-off. And he said, \"No, do not let anyone know about me except those who will carry me to my grave.\""],[6406,"I said to 'Ata': \"In how many garments is a woman shrouded?\" He said, \"In three garments and a garment over her in which she is wrapped.\" I said, \"And no head-cover?\" He said, \"No, but she is tied together with bands. She has an appearance like the appearance of a man.\""],[6407,"A woman is shrouded in her shift, a head-cover, and a wrapper in which she is rolled up."],[6408,"A woman is shrouded in five garments: a shift, a head-cover, and three wrappers."],[6409,"A woman is shrouded in five garments: a shift, a head-cover, a wrapper, a waist-wrapper, and an outer cloak."],[6410,"A woman is shrouded in five garments: a shift, a head-cover, a piece of cloth, and two wrappers."],[6412,"The cloth for the waist is placed over her shift."],[6413,"The deceased woman is veiled as the living woman is veiled, and she is covered from the head-cover a cubit's length, letting it hang over her face."],[6414,"The shroud is from all the wealth."],[6415,"The shroud and the perfume are a debt."],[6417,"He said: 'Begin with the shroud, then the debt, then the bequest.' I said: 'What about the grave digger's fee and washing the shroud?' He said: 'That is part of the shroud.'"],[6418,"The shroud is from all the wealth."],[6421,"Nothing is taken from the hair of the deceased, nor from his nails."],[6422,"If his hair is long, excessively long, something is taken from it; and his nails likewise."],[6423,"A man said to 'Ata': A deceased person dies with long hair, is anything taken from it? He said: \"No, if he has died then no. A person's dandruff flies from his head and then it is picked up, gathered, and buried with him when he dies. So nothing is removed from him. But before he dies, then yes.\""],[6424,"When something from the hair of the deceased's head was plucked out, it was gathered and buried with him."],[6425,"Regarding hair and nails that fall from the deceased, he said: \"You put it with him in his shroud.\""],[6426,"'A'ishah saw a woman whose head they were combing, and she said: \"Why are you grooming your deceased?\""],[6427,"Hammad was asked about trimming the nails of the deceased. He said: \"Do you think that if he were uncircumcised you would circumcise him?\""],[6429,"If it is excessive, something is taken from it."],[6430,"Sa'd b. Malik shaved the pubic hair of a deceased person."],[6431,"I said to 'Ata': Who was the first to bring the bier for a woman? He said: \"Asma' bt. 'Umays; I think she saw that in the land of Abyssinia.\""],[6433,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Is the yellow velvet disliked for the bier? He said: I do not know. He said: What about the red one? He said: ʿAlī b. Abī Ṭālib said: The Prophet ﷺ forbade me from wearing a gold ring, from wearing qassī (meaning silk garments), from using a purple velvet, and from using a mīthara—a thing that used to be placed under a man like a small carpet, resembling a fine padded saddle with red and yellow fringes."],[6434,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Regarding the saying, 'Seek forgiveness for him, may Allah forgive you'? He said: It is an innovation. And it reached me that the Prophet ﷺ said regarding Dhū al-Bijādayn: Seek forgiveness for him, may Allah forgive you."],[6435,"He was present when Abu Said al-Khudri was dying. Abu Said said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"Indeed, the deceased will be resurrected in the clothes in which his soul was taken.\" Then Abu Said said: I have instructed my family not to follow me with fire, and not to set up a tent over my grave. Carry me on a purple velvet cloth."],[6436,"I was with Ibn al-Musayyib at a funeral when he heard a man saying: Seek forgiveness from Allah. He said: What is this reciter of theirs saying? I have made it binding upon my family that this reciter of theirs should not recite with me, nor say: Saʿīd b. al-Musayyib has died, so bear witness. Sufficient for me is the One Who will turn me over to my Lord. And that they should not walk with me with an incense burner, for if I have any good with my Lord, then what is with Allah is more fragrant than your perfume."],[6438,"Saʿīd b. Jubayr heard a man saying: Seek forgiveness for her. He said: May Allah not forgive you."],[6439,"A son of Abū Bakr who used to drink wine died. Abū Hurayrah said: Seek forgiveness for him, for forgiveness is sought only for a wrongdoer like him."],[6440,"Abū Juḥayfah held the legs of the bier of ʿAmr b. Shuraḥbīl and did not leave it until he reached the grave, saying: O Allah, forgive Abū Maysarah."],[6441,"I saw ʿAbd Allāh b. Ḥasan holding the legs of the bier of Ṭāwūs. Indeed I saw that some of his clothes were torn on him and his cap fell off, yet he did not leave it until he reached the grave. I said to Abū Bakr: He said: Between the two poles of the bier. We said: And where did Ṭāwūs die? He said: In Makkah."],[6442,"Hasten with your funerals, for if the deceased was righteous, you are speeding him to good, and if he was otherwise, you are ridding yourselves of him and taking him off your necks."],[6443,"Hasten with your funerals, and do not walk slowly as the People of the Book walk slowly."],[6444,"It used to be said: Stride out with funerals, and do not creep along like the creeping of the Jews and Christians."],[6445,"There is no funeral bier but it appeals to its bearers: if it was a believer and Allah is pleased with it, it says, 'I adjure you by Allah to hasten me along.' And if it was a disbeliever in Allah and Allah is wrathful with it, it says, 'I adjure you by Allah to turn back with me.' There is nothing but hears it except the two heavy ones (humans and jinn). Were a human to hear it, he would be terrified and alarmed. Al-kharaʿ means weakness and fear."],[6446,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: How is the walking with a man? Should we hasten with him? He said: 'Yes.' I said: And the woman? He said: 'Hasten with her too, but slightly less than with the man. The woman has a form that the man does not have.' It was said: 'What is this ḥiyākatukum or ḥibātukum of yours?' He said: 'And it is so.'"],[6447,"Nāfiʿ was present with Ibn ʿAbbās at the funeral of Maymūnah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, at Sarif. Ibn ʿAbbās said: 'This is the wife of the Prophet ﷺ' or he said: 'This is the spouse of the Prophet ﷺ. So when you raise her bier, do not jolt it, and do not shake it, and be gentle. For the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had nine [wives], and he used to apportion [nights] to eight, and did not apportion to one.' ʿAṭāʾ said: 'The one to whom he did not apportion was Ṣafiyyah bt. Ḥuyayy b. Akhṭab.' Ibn Jurayj said: 'And ʿĀʾishah commanded hastening with funeral biers.'"],[6448,"I witnessed a funeral with ʿAbd Allāh b. Jaʿfar. He sat in the graveyard, then began looking at the funeral approaching while they were slow. He said: 'Glory be to Allah! What the people have introduced into funerals! I used to hear a man reminding another man and frightening him, saying: Fear Allah; you will soon be carried swiftly. No, by Allah, walking with funeral biers was only swift.'"],[6449,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day when a funeral bier was brought past him. He said: 'One at rest, or one from whom rest is taken.' He said: We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is one at rest and one from whom rest is taken?' He said: 'The righteous servant rests from the toil of this world and its worries to the mercy of Allah, and the wicked servant—the servants, the trees, and the animals rest from him.'"],[6450,"The soul of the deceased is in the hand of the angel, saying: 'Listen to what is said in your praise when you are washed and when you are carried. Then when you are buried, the earth speaks to you'—and it says: 'Do you not know that I am the house of estrangement, loneliness, and worms? So what have you prepared for me?'"],[6451,"Breaking the bones of the deceased is like breaking them when alive."],[6452,"Breaking the bones of the deceased is like breaking them when alive."],[6453,"Maʿmar reported to us from Saʿīd b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Jaḥshī, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the like of it. Hidden text: Breaking the bones of the deceased is like breaking them when alive."],[6456,"I saw Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb striking the people, making them go in front of the funeral bier of Zaynab bt. Jaḥsh."],[6457,"I heard al-ʿAyzār ask Anas b. Mālik about walking in front of the funeral bier. Anas said to him: 'You are only a mourner escorting it. So walk, if you wish, in front of it; if you wish, behind it; if you wish, on its right; and if you wish, on its left.'"],[6459,"I was with ʿAlī in a funeral procession. He said: ʿAlī was holding my hand while we were behind it, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar were walking in front of it. He said: The virtue of the one walking behind it over the one walking in front of it is like the virtue of the congregational prayer over the prayer of one praying alone. They both know of that what I know, but they did not like to make things difficult for the people."],[6460,"The Messenger of God ﷺ walked in front of the bier of Saʿd b. Muʿādh."],[6461,"We asked our Prophet ﷺ about walking with funerals, and he said: It is only the one followed, not the follower, and there is no one with it who precedes it."],[6462,"The angels walk behind it. He said: And I was told from Ibn Masʿūd that he used to forbid whoever attended a funeral from taking a path ahead of it."],[6463,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī came to ʿAlī b. Abī Ṭālib while he was sitting and had drawn up his knees. He greeted him, and he returned the greeting. He said: Abā al-Ḥasan, tell me about walking in front of a funeral when I attend it: which is better, behind it or in front of it? He said: ʿAlī frowned between his eyes, then said: Glory be to God! Would someone like you ask about this? Abū Saʿīd said: Yes, by God, someone like me would ask about something like this. Who would ask about something like this except someone like me? ʿAlī said: By the One who sent Muḥammad with the truth, the virtue of the one walking behind it over the one walking in front of it is like the virtue of the prescribed prayer over the voluntary prayer. Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said to him: Abā al-Ḥasan, do you say this by your own opinion or by something you heard from the Messenger of God ﷺ? He said: He became angry and said: Glory be to God, Abā Saʿīd! Would I say something like this by my own opinion? No, by God, rather I heard him say it many times, not once, nor twice, nor three times, until he counted seven times. Abū Saʿīd said: By God, I have not sat in a sitting since I attended a funeral except for a man from the Anṣār, so Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and all the Companions attended it. I looked at Abū Bakr and ʿUmar walking in front of it. He said: ʿAlī laughed and said: Did you see them doing that? Abū Saʿīd said: Yes. ʿAlī said: If anyone other than you had told me this, I would not have believed him, but I know that lying is not your way. May God forgive them both. The best of this community is Abū Bakr b. Abī Quḥāfah and ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, then God knows best where the good is. If you saw them doing that, they know that the virtue of the one walking behind it over the one walking in front of it is like the virtue of the prescribed prayer over the voluntary prayer, just as they know that before tomorrow there is a night. They heard that from the Messenger of God ﷺ as I heard it, but they disliked that people would crowd together and be constricted, so they preferred to go ahead and make it easy. They knew that they would be followed as examples, so for that reason they went ahead. Abū Saʿīd said: Abā al-Ḥasan, if I attend a funeral, is carrying it obligatory on whoever attends it? He said: No, but it is better. Whoever wishes may take it up and whoever wishes may leave it. When you attend a funeral, place it in front of you and make it a goal before your eyes, for it is only an admonition, a reminder, and a lesson. If it seems good to you to carry it, look at the front of the bier and look at its left side, and place it on your right shoulder. When you come to the cemetery and pray over it, do not sit down; stand at his grave, for you will see a great matter. I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: Your brother, your brother, he used to vie with you in this world and dispute with you over it. The plains of the earth became too narrow for him because of its shortness, and now he enters the hollow of a grave, turned on his side. If they do not stop you, do not fail to stand until he is lowered into his pit, even if they fight you fiercely."],[6464,"Whoever prays over a funeral has a qīrāṭ of reward, and whoever waits for it until it is placed in the grave niche has two qīrāṭs. It was said: What are the two qīrāṭs? He said: Like two great mountains."],[6465,"Whoever follows a funeral and prays over it has one qīrāṭ, and whoever waits until its matter is decided has two qīrāṭs, the smaller of which is like Uḥud."],[6466,"Ibn ʿUmar passed by Abū Hurayrah while he was narrating from the Prophet ﷺ: Whoever follows a funeral and prays over it has a qīrāṭ, and if he witnesses its burial he has two qīrāṭs, greater than Uḥud. Ibn ʿUmar said: Part of your hadith, Abū Hurayrah, is from the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: Abū Hurayrah stood up, took Ibn ʿUmar by the hand, and went with him to ʿĀʾishah. Abū Hurayrah said to her: I adjure you by God, did you hear the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'Whoever follows a funeral and prays over it has a qīrāṭ, and if he witnesses its burial he has two qīrāṭs, and the qīrāṭ is greater than Uḥud'? ʿĀʾishah said: O God, yes. Abū Hurayrah said: Planting palm shoots and trading in the markets did not keep me busy; I only sought from the Messenger of God ﷺ a morsel to feed me or a word to teach me. Ibn ʿUmar said to him: You, Abū Hurayrah, were the most constant of us in accompanying the Messenger of God ﷺ and the most knowledgeable of us of his hadith."],[6467,"Whoever prays over a funeral and follows it has two qīrāṭs of reward, like Uḥud, and whoever prays but does not follow has one qīrāṭ, like Uḥud."],[6468,"Ibn ʿAbbās saw a man leave after praying over a funeral, and Ibn ʿAbbās said to him: This one has left with one qīrāṭ of reward."],[6469,"ʿAṭāʾ followed a funeral and prayed near the house of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. A man said to him: What reward is there for me? He said: According to how much you followed."],[6470,"Mujahid was asked: Is voluntary prayer better or following the funeral? He said: \"Rather, following the funeral.\""],[6471,"I said to Ata: If one follows a funeral, may someone without wudu carry it? He said: \"Yes, and I prefer that he be pure, but no one should pray over it except one with wudu, and the menstruating woman should not pray over it.\" He was the one who said: I said to him: Going to the rituals and the two pushes with wudu? He said: \"And without wudu.\""],[6472,"He should not pray over a funeral without wudu. If he does, he should repeat the prayer as long as the deceased has not been buried. Once he is buried, his prayer has passed."],[6473,"He should not pray over a funeral without wudu. If a funeral comes to him while he does not have wudu and he fears missing it, he should perform tayammum and pray over it."],[6474,"If he attends a funeral without wudu, let him perform tayammum."],[6475,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz prayed over a funeral and began ordering his family and entourage to perform wudu. Abu Qilaba said: What is this, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: It has reached me, I think from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: The one who prays over a funeral should perform wudu. Abu Qilaba said: It was lifted to you on other than its proper face. A funeral passed by while people were in their markets, and they started following the funeral like this. The Prophet ﷺ said: Whoever prays over a funeral should perform wudu, meaning no one should pray over it except one with wudu. Umar said to him: For the like of this, I used to love your nearness to me."],[6476,"If a funeral surprises you while you do not have wudu, pray over it."],[6477,"I encountered the Companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ preferring lowering the voice at funerals, at recitation of the Quran, and at fighting."],[6478,"When he followed a funeral, he was mostly silent and mostly talking to himself."],[6479,"When they attended a funeral, that was known in them for three [days]."],[6480,"The Messenger of God ﷺ never rode with a funeral. He said: And I do not think he said anything but: Nor Abu Bakr and Umar."],[6481,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed over Ibn al-Dahdah. He said: When he finished with the funeral, a horse was brought, and a man hobbled it, and the horse was bare. The Prophet ﷺ rode it, and it began prancing with him while we ran around him."],[6484,"We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made binding upon us."],[6486,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was following a funeral procession, and there was an old woman following it. The Messenger of God ﷺ became angry until anger was seen on his face. He ordered that she be turned back. Then the bier was placed, and he did not say the takbīr over her until they said: By the One who sent you with the truth, she has disappeared among the palm-fenced huts of Madinah. He said: Then he said the takbīr over her."],[6487,"The Messenger of God ﷺ went out for a funeral, saw a woman, ordered that she be driven away until he no longer saw her, then he said the takbīr."],[6488,"The Prophet ﷺ followed a funeral procession and saw a woman behind it. He ordered that the funeral be held back, and sent a man to turn the woman back, until when the houses hid her from view, they proceeded with it."],[6490,"Women have a share in the funeral procession."],[6491,"About women going out to funeral processions? He said: “They will be a temptation.”"],[6492,"Women going out to funeral processions is an innovation."],[6493,"He was asked: Should a woman pray over funerals? He said: “She should not pray over it, whether pure or menstruating.”"],[6494,"The Prophet ﷺ went out in a funeral procession and saw women. He said, \"Will you carry him among those who carry him?\" They said, \"No.\" He said, \"Will you place him in the grave among those who place him?\" They said, \"No.\" He said, \"Will you sprinkle dust among those who sprinkle?\" They said, \"No.\" He said, \"Then return, burdened with sin, not rewarded.\""],[6495,"Umar saw women with a funeral procession and said, \"Return, burdened with sin, not rewarded. By Allah, you do not carry nor bury. O annoyers of the dead and tempters of the living.\""],[6496,"He used to throw dust in their faces. If they went on, he would return."],[6497,"Do not give up a right for a falsehood."],[6498,"I went out with Ibn Umar in a funeral procession. When he reached the cemetery, he heard a wailing woman, or a loud cry. He said: He faced her and said harsh words to her, and said to Mujahid, \"You went out seeking reward, but this one wants to burden you with sin. Indeed, we have been forbidden to follow a funeral procession accompanied by wailing.\" He said: So he returned, and she returned with him."],[6499,"Ibn Umar followed a funeral procession and saw women following it and screaming. He turned to them and said, \"Uff to you, harm to the dead and temptation to the living,\" three times."],[6500,"A daughter of Wahb died. When the men went out, he locked the door and did not let the women follow her."],[6501,"When one of you sees a funeral procession, let him stand until it passes him by or is put down."],[6506,"The Prophet ﷺ and his companions stood for the funeral procession of a Jew until it disappeared."],[6507,"Marwan was sitting, and with him was Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, when a funeral passed by, so he stood up. Marwan said: \"What is this?\" He said: \"He commanded us,\" meaning the Prophet ﷺ. He said: \"Then bring me evidence for that.\""],[6508,"We were with Ali when a funeral passed by, and some people stood up for it. Ali said: \"Who gave you this fatwa?\" They said: \"Abu Musa.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did that only once, and he was imitating the People of the Book, but when it was forbidden he stopped.\""],[6509,"That he attended a funeral with Ali b. Abi Talib in Kufa, and he saw people standing waiting for the funeral to be placed, so he gestured to them with a whip he had, or a stick: \"Sit down, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat after he used to stand.\""],[6512,"I was in Madinah and attended the funeral of Umm Amr bt. al-Zubayr. When the prayer was offered over her, Ibn al-Musayyab sat down, but I stood up. Ibn al-Musayyab said to me: \"Sit down.\" I said: \"I have heard that Ibn Umar disliked that.\" He said: \"Sit down, there is no harm upon you.\""],[6513,"Ibn Umar would go ahead of the funeral until he reached al-Baqi' and sat down. Then when he saw it, he stood up. Nafi' said: \"So I would shield him so that he would not see it.\""],[6514,"Ibn Umar followed a funeral, and with him were Ubayd b. Umayr and Ibn Abi Aqrab, and I was following them. He said: \"He went ahead of it and sat down, until when it came level with him, he stood up until it had passed him.\""],[6521,"We went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ in a funeral procession, and we found the grave had not yet been given a lahd, so he sat down and we sat down."],[6522,"When you have prayed over the funeral bier, stand until it is lifted. Then people altered it and said: Stand until it is put down."],[6523,"I asked 'Ata': If I pray over a funeral bier and I am not following it? He said: I enter and do not wait to see it lifted."],[6524,"If a funeral bier passes by you, stand up; and if you follow it, do not sit down until it is put down."],[6525,"When there are men and women, the men are to be placed closest to the imam, and the women behind that."],[6526,"Men before women, and the older before the younger."],[6527,"He used to pray over funeral biers, placing the men closest to the imam and the women before that."],[6528,"I prayed with Abu Hurayrah and Ibn 'Umar over a man and a woman. He placed the man closest to the imam and the woman behind that, and he said the takbir four times."],[6529,"Men are placed closest to the imam, and women behind that."],[6530,"He placed the man closest to the imam and the woman before that."],[6531,"When the funerals of men and women are gathered together, the men are placed next to the imam, and the women beyond that."],[6532,"Ali said to Abu Hayyaj: \"I am sending you on what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me: Do not leave any raised grave without leveling it—meaning the graves of the Muslims—nor any image in a house without effacing it.\""],[6533,"Ibn Umar prayed over Umm Kulthum bt. Ali b. Abi Talib and Zayd b. Umar, and he placed Zayd next to him and the woman beyond that."],[6534,"I heard Nafi claim that Ibn Umar prayed over nine funerals all together, and he placed the men next to the imam and the women facing the qiblah, arranging them in a row. The funeral of Umm Kulthum, daughter of Ali, wife of Umar b. al-Khattab, and a son of hers called Zayd were placed together. The imam that day was Said b. al-As, and among the people were Ibn Abbas, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Said, and Abu Qatadah. The boy was placed next to the imam. A man said: I disapproved of that, so I looked at Ibn Abbas, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Said, and Abu Qatadah and said: What is this? They said: It is the Sunnah."],[6535,"He was with Saʿīd b. Jubayr at a funeral, and Saʿīd carried. He began with the front end of the pole that was at the head, placing it on his right shoulder, then he went back to its end that was by the foot and carried it on his left shoulder, then he came to its end that was by the head and placed it on his left shoulder, then he turned away on his right, and said: 'This is how funerals are carried.'"],[6536,"When Wathilah b. al-Asqa prayed over women and men together, he placed the men next to him and the women beyond that."],[6537,"I saw him come to the funerals of men and women and say: Where are the saafiqah, or what do the saafiqah say? Meaning those who criticize. He said: Then he placed the men next to the imam and the women beyond that, one after another. Then he mentioned that Ibn Umar did that with Umm Kulthum and Zayd, and there were men from Banu Hashim present. He said: I think he mentioned Hasan and Husayn."],[6538,"The men are placed facing the qiblah and the women next to the imam."],[6539,"Two commanders, yet they are not commanders: the man who is with the funeral and prays over it—he has no right to return until he seeks permission from its guardian; and the menstruating woman—her companions have no right to depart until they seek permission."],[6540,"I saw al-Shabi pray over the funeral of two men, and he placed one behind the other, then said: Do with them like this, even if they are ten."],[6541,"Shurayh used to bury at night."],[6542,"I was in Madinah with Ibn ‘Umar during the fitnah, when ‘Abbas b. Sahl, a man of the Ansar, came and said: “O Abu ‘Abd al-Rahman, ‘Aqil b. Abi Talib has been placed at the mosque gate to be prayed over, and that is after ‘Asr.” He said: “O Ibn Yasar, look: has the sun set?” He said: “No.” So he refused to get up. He said: Then he came back to him and said: “Look: has the sun set?” I looked and said: “No.” So he refused to pray over him. He said: Then they took him away and prayed over him, while they wanted Ibn ‘Umar to lead them; Ibn al-Zubayr was then in Makkah."],[6543,"When he prayed over men and women, he placed the women's heads at the men's knees. He said: 'And the Companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ were with him, and they did not object to that.'"],[6544,"He informed us that whoever prayed with Abu al-Darda' or Fadalah b. 'Ubayd over funerals, the two of them would place the woman at the man's shoulder."],[6545,"Ibn Umar said: Umar was prayed over in the mosque."],[6546,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over the child of adultery and its mother, who died during her post-partum bleeding."],[6547,"When there is a funeral of a man and a woman, the man is given precedence with the head when they are placed in the prayer area."],[6548,"The one who kills himself: prayer is offered over him, and the stoned one: prayer is offered over him."],[6549,"The imam stands at the chest of the man and the shoulder of the woman."],[6551,"The man stands at her chest when he prays over a woman."],[6552,"I saw al-Sha'bi bring the funerals of two men and line one of them behind the other. He said: 'Do this with them, even if they are ten.'"],[6553,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed over those killed at Uhud."],[6555,"The imam raises his hands each time he pronounces takbir over the bier, and the people behind him [do likewise]."],[6556,"One of the family of Talha b. 'Ubayd Allah saw in a dream that he saw him and said: You have buried me in a place where water has reached me, so move me from it. So they moved him and brought him out as if he were a fresh vegetable; nothing of him had changed except some hairs of his beard."],[6557,"Ibn 'Umar used to raise [his hands] during the four takbirs over the bier."],[6558,"He used to raise his hands in the first takbir only when praying over the dead, then he did not raise them after that."],[6559,"Ibn 'Abbas used to raise his hands in the first takbir only, then he did not raise them after that, and he used to pronounce four takbirs."],[6560,"When a funeral passed by him, he would say: Depart, for we are leaving early, or leave early, for we are departing – a powerful admonition and a swift heedlessness. The first goes and the last remains, with no sense."],[6561,"May God have mercy on 'Umar and Ibn 'Umar. They heard something but did not remember it correctly. The Prophet ﷺ passed by a deceased person whose family was weeping over him, and the Prophet ﷺ said, 'Indeed, his family are weeping over him, and he is being punished.' She said: The man had been in a state of ihram."],[6562,"Al-Zubayr prayed over 'Umar and buried him, and he had been made his executor."],[6563,"I prayed over 'A'ishah when the imam that day was Abu Hurayrah."],[6564,"The one who used to lead her in prayer during her life should pray over her. He said: That is because a woman died among another people, and Suwayd b. Ghafalah said that."],[6565,"Their imams used to pray over their funerals. He said: \"When a woman died among another people, the imam of that clan in which she died would pray over her.\""],[6566,"I witnessed Husayn when Hasan died, pushing Sa'id b. al-'As from behind and saying: \"Go forward, for were it not for the Sunnah I would not have put you forward.\" Sa'id was governor of Madinah at that time. He said: When they had prayed over him, Abu Hurayrah stood up and said: \"Do you begrudge the son of your Prophet ﷺ a piece of earth in which to bury him?\" Then he said: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever loves them has loved me, and whoever hates them has hated me.'\""],[6567,"The people most entitled to pray over the woman are the father, then the husband, then the son, then the brother."],[6568,"I asked permission from the Prophet's ﷺ wife to pray over her."],[6569,"Greeting the graves is: \"Peace be upon the Muslim men and Muslim women, and the believing men and believing women from the dwellers of these abodes, and may Allah have mercy on those of us who have gone before and those who remain behind, and we, if Allah wills, shall join you.\""],[6571,"A wife of Abu Bakrah died, and her brothers came disputing with him over praying over her. Abu Bakrah said: \"Were I not more entitled to pray over her, I would not dispute with you about that.\" He said: Then he went forward and prayed over her. Then he entered the grave and was brought out unconscious, and at that time he had thirty or forty sons and daughters. They cried out over him and he revived and said: \"There is no soul on earth, not even the soul of a fly, dearer to me than that it leave my body.\" It was said to him: Why? He said: \"Out of fear that a time may overtake me in which I do not enjoin good or forbid evil. What good am I then?\""],[6572,"The person most entitled to pray over the woman is her husband."],[6573,"No one led them in prayer over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They entered in groups—men, women and children—into the house and the room where he was, supplicated, then left, and others entered, until the people had finished."],[6574,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ died on Monday, and he was not buried that day or that night, until it was the end of Tuesday. He said: He was washed with his shirt on, and shrouded in three garments: two Sahari garments and a striped cloak. Prayer was performed over him without an imam. 'Umar b. al-Khattab called out among the people: \"Leave the funeral bier and its family.\" A niche grave was dug for him, and bricks were placed over his niche."],[6575,"Wathilah b. al-Asqa' would, when he buried men and women together, place the man in the grave on the side facing the qiblah and place the woman behind him in the grave."],[6578,"Four men took charge of washing the Prophet ﷺ, burying him, and placing him in the grave apart from the people: 'Ali, al-'Abbas, al-Fadl, and Salih Shuqran, the freed slave of the Prophet ﷺ. They dug a niche grave for him, and set bricks over him upright."],[6580,"When the Prophet ﷺ died, there were two men in Madinah: one who made niche graves and one who dug trench graves. The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ gathered and said, “O Allah, choose what is best for him.” He said, “The one who made niche graves came, so he made a niche for him.”"],[6581,"There were two men in Madinah: one of them made niche graves and the other dug trench graves. When the Prophet ﷺ died, they said, “Whichever of them comes, we will command him to do his work.” The one who made niche graves came, so they commanded him, and he buried the Prophet ﷺ in a niche."],[6582,"The niche grave is for us, and the trench grave is for others."],[6583,"They used to prefer the niche grave and dislike the trench grave. They disliked bricks in the grave and preferred unbaked bricks and reeds. They also disliked it when the earth was leveled over the deceased for the guardian to stand at his grave and receive condolences."],[6584,"The one who made the niche grave of the Prophet ﷺ was Abu Talhah, and the one who placed the velvet cloth was the Prophet’s ﷺ freedman, Ibn Shuqran."],[6585,"A worn velvet cloth that the Prophet ﷺ used to ride on during his life was spread in his grave. We said to Abu Bakr, “If only people would do that.” He said, “No, the Prophet ﷺ is not like anyone else.”"],[6586,"A Fadak velvet cloth was spread in the grave of the Prophet ﷺ."],[6587,"Maymunah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, died at Sarif. I took my cloak and spread it under her, but Ibn Abbas took it and threw it away."],[6588,"The Prophet ﷺ was given a brick as a pillow, placed by his head to support it, not under his cheek."],[6589,"A niche was made for the Prophet ﷺ, and bricks were presented to him and set up."],[6590,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced the death of the Negus to his Companions while they were in Madinah. They lined up behind him, and he prayed over him and said four takbirs."],[6592,"They used to say seven, five, and four takbirs in the time of the Prophet ﷺ, until the time of ʿUmar. He gathered them and asked them, and each man among them informed him of what he had seen, so he united them upon four takbirs, like the longest prayer—that is, ẓuhr."],[6593,"Zayd b. Thābit said four takbirs over his mother, and he did not envy her any good."],[6594,"ʿUmar said four takbirs over Zaynab bt. Jaḥsh, and he asked the wives of the Prophet ﷺ, “Who will enter her into her grave?” They said, “Someone who used to see her during her lifetime.”"],[6595,"ʿAlī said four takbirs over Yazīd b. al-Mukaffaf al-Nakhaʿī."],[6596,"ʿAlī prayed over Sahl b. Ḥunayf and said six takbirs over him."],[6597,"ʿAlī said five takbirs over a funeral bier."],[6599,"He used to gather the people for the ḥamd and say three takbirs over funeral biers."],[6600,"ʿAlī prayed over Sahl b. Ḥunayf and said six takbirs over him, then he turned to us and said, “He was a Badrī.”"],[6601,"I saw ʿAbd Allāh b. Abī Awfā pray over a daughter of his and say four takbirs over her, then he stood for a while and they said subḥān Allāh to him, so he said, “You think I will say five takbirs, but I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say four.” He said, “Then he rode with her, and he began saying to his guide, ‘Do not put me in front of her.’ The women began weeping, and he said, ‘Do not lament, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid elegies.’”"],[6602,"The takbir over a man and a woman is four. I said, “Night and day?” He said, “Yes.” I said, “What if they place two men together?” He said, “Four takbirs are said over both of them.” The questioner said, “Some people say three, just as maghrib is three.” He said, “We have not heard of that.”"],[6603,"Today a righteous man of the Abyssinians, Ashamah, has died. Come and pray over him. He said: So we formed rows, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed, and we were with him."],[6604,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed at the place of the funeral and said the takbir four times, then said: 'Do you know over whom I prayed?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Over Ashamah.'"],[6605,"They did not differ that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over the Negus at Baqi' al-Musalla."],[6606,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed over Umm Kulthum, the sister of Sawdah bt. Zam'ah. She died in Makkah, and he prayed over her at al-Baqi', Baqi' al-Musalla, and said the takbir over her four times."],[6607,"Ibn Umar used to lengthen the standing in the funeral prayer, and the imam would say the takbir four times."],[6608,"If the imam said the takbir over a funeral and then another was brought, he would say the takbir over it four times, making four for the latter and five for the former. Ibrahim disliked that the last thing the deceased should hear be a flute, or that a rider pass in front of the funeral, or that a man stand between the two posts of the bier at the front or back, or that the family of the deceased pass in front of the funeral or close behind it, magnifying the deceased by that. If a funeral surprised him while he was not in a state of ritual purity, he would perform tayammum and pray over it. If he missed part of the takbir, he would hasten before it was lifted and say the takbir for what he had missed."],[6609,"If you miss part of the takbir with the imam, say the takbir for what you missed."],[6611,"If you miss part of the takbir, hasten before it is lifted. This is what we follow."],[6612,"If he missed some of the takbir over the funeral, he made up what he missed."],[6613,"He was at a funeral and if he missed part of the takbir, he would not make it up."],[6614,"If you come and the imam has already said the takbir over the deceased, and you stand in the row but do not say the takbir until they do, then say the takbir with them."],[6615,"He said the takbir over a funeral three times, then left forgetting, and spoke and people spoke to him. They said: 'O Abu Hamzah, you said the takbir three times.' He said: 'Form rows!' They did, and he said the fourth takbir."],[6616,"When you pray over a funeral, nothing that passes in front of you harms you. He says: What interrupts the prayer?"],[6617,"He used to say in supplication for the deceased: O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, our young and our old, our males and our females, our absent and our present. O Allah, whomever of us You keep alive, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomever of us You take, take him upon faith."],[6618,"Regarding what is said over the deceased: O Allah, Your servant and the son of Your servant. You created him and You took his soul. You guided him to Islam, and You know best his inner and outward state. We have come to intercede for him, so forgive him."],[6619,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to say over funerals three times: 'O Allah, Your servant so-and-so has entered the morning,' if it was morning, and if it was evening, he said: 'Your servant has entered the evening. He has departed from this world and left it to its people. He has become in need of You while You are free of need from him. He used to bear witness that there is no god but You and that Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger. So forgive him and pardon him.'"],[6620,"He used to say over the deceased: 'O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, unite our hearts, reconcile our differences, and make our hearts conform to the hearts of the best among us. O Allah, forgive him. O Allah, have mercy on him. O Allah, return him to a better state than he was in. O Allah, grant him Your pardon.' And when news of the death of an absent man came to him, he would say: 'Indeed we belong to Allah and to Him we return. O Allah, raise his degree among the rightly guided, and be his successor among those who remain regarding what he left behind, and we seek his reward from You, O Lord of the worlds. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not test us after him.'"],[6621,"He used to say in the funeral prayer: 'O Allah, bless him, send prayers upon him, forgive him, and bring him to the basin of Your Messenger ﷺ.'"],[6622,"He used to say in the funeral prayer: 'O Allah, bless him, send prayers upon him, forgive him, and bring him to the basin of Your Messenger ﷺ.'"],[6623,"How do you pray over funerals? Abu Hurayrah said: I, by the life of Allah, will tell you. I follow it with its family, and when they place it down, I say the takbir, praise Allah, and send prayers upon His Prophet ﷺ. Then I say: 'O Allah, Your servant and the son of Your servant and the son of Your female servant. He used to bear witness that there is no god but You and that Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger, and You know best about him. O Allah, if he was a doer of good, increase his good, and if he was a wrongdoer, pardon him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not test us after him.'"],[6624,"And I asked him about the funeral prayer, and he informed me from Abu Salih al-Zayyat who said: You begin with sending prayers upon the Prophet ﷺ, then you say: 'O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, reconcile our differences, unite our hearts, and make our hearts conform to the hearts of the best among us. O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, and return him to a better state than he was in, and make this day the best day that came upon him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not test us after him.'"],[6626,"The Sunnah in the funeral prayer is that one says the takbir, then recites the Mother of the Quran, then sends prayers upon the Prophet ﷺ, then sincerely supplicates for the deceased. One does not recite except in the first takbirah, then one greets quietly to one's right."],[6628,"I compiled forty books on the funeral prayer, and I retained from them one book in which it says: 'He says the takbir, then recites the Mother of the Quran, then sends prayers upon the Prophet ﷺ, then says: O Allah, Your servant so-and-so, You created him. If You punish him, it is for his sin, and if You forgive him, You are the Oft-Forgiving, the Merciful. O Allah, raise his soul to heaven, widen the earth away from his body, O Allah, illuminate for him in his grave, make spacious for him in Paradise, and be his successor for his family. O Allah, do not let us go astray after him, do not deprive us of his reward, and forgive us and him.'"],[6630,"He used to recite in all of the takbirs the Opening of the Book (al-Fatihah), saying: 'O Allah, Your servant so-and-so — magnify his reward, illuminate him, and join him to his Prophet ﷺ, and make his grave spacious for him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not lead us astray after him.'"],[6632,"He would not recite anything in any of the takbirs, and he would say: 'O Allah, forgive the believing men and the believing women, and the Muslim men and the Muslim women, and bring their hearts together, and make their hearts follow the hearts of their best ones. O Allah, raise his degree among the rightly guided, and be his successor among those left behind regarding his inheritance. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not lead us astray after him.'"],[6634,"The first takbir over the deceased is praise of Allah, the second is a prayer upon the Prophet ﷺ, the third is supplication for the deceased, and the fourth is the salutation (taslim)."],[6635,"Is there anything specified over the deceased? He said: 'I do not know of it.' Sufyan said: 'It has reached us that Ibrahim said: \"Upon him is supplication and seeking forgiveness.\"'"],[6636,"We do not know of any specific recitation or supplication in the prayer over the deceased."],[6637,"They used to recite the Opening of the Book, supplicate, and seek forgiveness after each of the three takbirs, then make the fourth takbir and depart without reciting."],[6639,"When he prayed over a funeral, he would say: 'O Allah, make him a forerunner for us, and make Paradise an appointed meeting between us and him. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not lead us astray after him.'"],[6640,"Zayd b. Thabit used to say over the funeral: 'O Allah, Your servant and the son of Your servant. You gave him life as long as You willed, and You took him when You willed, and You will raise him when You will. O Allah, if he was pure, then purify him further, and if he was sinful, then overlook his sins. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not lead us astray after him. O Allah, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith.'"],[6641,"When you make takbir behind the imam over the funeral, let yourself hear it."],[6643,"When the imam prays over the funeral bier, he performs the taslim quietly, to his right."],[6644,"Ibn Abbas performed a light taslim over the funeral bier."],[6645,"The imam performs the taslim over the funeral bier to his right, a light taslim."],[6647,"He performs a light taslim."],[6648,"He performed the taslim over a funeral bier until those next to him heard him."],[6649,"The imam performs the taslim over the funeral bier as he performs the taslim in the prayer, and those behind him also perform the taslim."],[6650,"When he prayed over a funeral bier, he would perform the taslim until those next to him heard him."],[6651,"When he finished the prayer over the funeral bier, he performed the taslim to his right."],[6653,"When a man dies, his seat is shown to him morning and evening. If he is from the people of Paradise, then Paradise; if he is from the people of the Fire, then the Fire. It is said: 'This is your seat until you are resurrected to it on the Day of Resurrection.'"],[6654,"He was with a brother of his while he was driving camels, and his forehead began to bead with sweat. Alqamah laughed, so Yazid b. Aws said to him: “What makes you laugh, O Abu Shibl?” He said: “I heard Abd Allah b. Masud say: The soul of the believer comes out as sweat, and the soul of the disbeliever comes out from the side of his mouth as the soul of a donkey comes out. The believer is made to suffer at his death for a bad deed he had done, so that it may be requited thereby; and the disbeliever is made to have it easy at his death for a good deed he had done, so that it may be requited thereby.”"],[6655,"Ali, al-Fadl, and Shuqran descended into the grave of the Prophet ﷺ."],[6656,"It is as if I am looking at them in the grave of the Prophet ﷺ—four: Ali, al-Fadl, Abd al-Rahman b. Awf, and Usama or Abbas."],[6657,"Ali and al-Fadl descended into the grave of the Prophet ﷺ, and Ali took charge of the lower part of the grave. A man from the Ansar descended with them. The Ansar said: \"We had a share in his life, so give us a share in his death.\" So they let that Ansari down with them, and I have heard that he was Khawli b. Aws."],[6658,"I asked Ata about what is said when the deceased is lowered into the grave. He said: A son of Ubayd b. Umayr died. When he was taken from above the grave, I heard Ubayd saying: \"In the name of Allah, upon the faith of Ibrahim, hanif, and he was not of the polytheists. Our prayer, our sacrifice, our living, and our dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds. He has no partner, and with that we have been commanded, and we are of the Muslims.\""],[6659,"It used to be said over the deceased in the grave when he was lowered: \"In Your name, O Allah, and in Your path, and upon the faith of Your Messenger ﷺ. O Allah, accept him from You with a good acceptance and bring him to the best returning place. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward and do not test us after him.\""],[6660,"When you lower the deceased into his lahd, say: \"In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the faith of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. O Allah, make his grave spacious for him and illuminate his grave for him, and join him to his Prophet while You are pleased with him, not angry.\""],[6661,"They used to prefer to say over the deceased: \"In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the faith of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. O Allah, protect him from the punishment of the Fire, the punishment of the grave, and the evil of Satan.\""],[6662,"When you place me in my pit, say: \"O Allah, bless this house and bless its occupant.\""],[6664,"Abu Bakr al-Siddiq used to say when the deceased was placed in the lahd: \"In the name of Allah, and upon the faith of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and with certainty in the resurrection after death.\""],[6665,"I attended the funeral of al-Harith al-A'war al-Kharifi, who was a companion of Ali and Ibn Mas'ud. I saw Abd Allah b. Yazid al-Ansari uncover the bier cloth from him when he was placed in the grave and say: \"He is only a man.\" He said: \"I saw the powdered perfume on his shroud, and he pulled him out from the direction of the foot of the grave.\" Then he said: \"Like this.\""],[6666,"Do not make anyone aware of me, and lower me gently into the earth to my Lord."],[6668,"Nothing invalidates the prayer. He said: Sometimes I saw a man whom I had forbidden to pass in front of Amir while he was praying, so he would take him by the hand and make him walk in front of him."],[6669,"The Prophet ﷺ was lowered into the grave from the direction of his head, and the people after him."],[6670,"And Abū Bakr died at the same age."],[6672,"ʿAlī took Yazīd b. al-Mukaffaf from the direction of the qiblah."],[6673,"I witnessed Muḥammad b. al-Ḥanafiyyah when Ibn ʿAbbās died: he took hold of him from the direction of the qiblah when he brought him into the grave."],[6674,"Asmāʾ bt. Abī Bakr instructed that no perfume be sprinkled on the cloth of her bier."],[6675,"I saw ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz prohibiting perfume powder being sprinkled over the bier."],[6676,"Al-Ḥārith al-Khārifī died, and I saw ʿAbd Allāh b. Yazīd say: 'Remove this cloth, for he is only a man,' meaning the cloth covering the grave."],[6677,"The Prophet ﷺ ordered a cloth to be placed over the grave when Saʿd b. Muʿādh was lowered into it."],[6678,"The young one is counted."],[6679,"When Ibn Abbas buried Zayd b. Thabit, he threw dust over him and then said: \"This is how knowledge is buried.\" Ali b. Zayd said: I narrated this to Ali b. al-Husayn, and he said: \"By Allah, with Ibn Abbas much knowledge has been buried.\""],[6680,"He threw dust over Yazid b. al-Mukaffaf on the deceased three times—he or someone else."],[6681,"The Prophet passed by a grave that had been sprinkled with water and said: \"Had we already prayed over this one?\" They said: No. So he prayed over it."],[6682,"Sprinkling water on the grave was practiced in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[6683,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by al-Baqi' and saw a fresh grave. He asked about it, and they said: \"O Messenger of Allah, this is al-Suwayda' who was among the Banu Ghanm. She died and was buried at night.\" He said: \"So he prayed over her.\""],[6684,"The grave of the Prophet ﷺ was raised a span, and they made its back into a mound with no hump."],[6685,"The young one is counted."],[6686,"Do not make my grave long."],[6687,"Ali said to Abu Hayyaj: \"I am sending you on what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me: Do not leave any raised grave without leveling it—meaning the graves of the Muslims—nor any image in a house without effacing it.\""],[6688,"He forbade a man from sitting on a grave, and from plastering it and building over it."],[6689,"The young one is counted."],[6692,"I was present with Musa b. Talha and he attended a funeral and said: \"Make the graves level, evenly flat. It is said: 'It is not raised nor made into a hump.'\""],[6693,"A man died during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he attended his burial. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Lighten [the burden] upon your companion.\""],[6694,"He disliked building over a grave, plastering it, or defecating near it, and he used to say: \"Do not take the graves of your brothers as toilets.\""],[6695,"When a grave in Makkah has [stood] for ten years, do with it whatever you wish—a house, a mosque, cultivation, or whatever. But in your lands, [let it be] twenty years."],[6696,"A paternal uncle of mine died at al-Janad. I entered with my father upon Ibn Tawus, and he said: \"O Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman, do you think I should plaster the grave of my brother?\" He laughed and said: \"Glory be to God, O Abu Shayba! It is better for you not to know his grave unless you come to it to seek forgiveness for him and supplicate for him. Do you not know that the Messenger of God ﷺ forbade building over the graves of Muslims, plastering them, or cultivating them? Indeed, the best of your graves are those that are unknown.\""],[6697,"He was asked about a rakīyah among graves, and he disliked drinking from it or performing ablution from it. I said: 'What is a rakīyah?' He said: 'Some say it is a well, others say it is a pool that exists among graves.' I asked: 'Which do you say?' He said: 'We say it is a well.' I asked: 'Do you dislike performing ablution from it?' He said: 'Yes.' I asked: 'Why?' He said: 'Because when rain is heavy, graves become submerged, and therefore I dislike ablution from it.'"],[6698,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade plastering graves, adorning them, and writing on them."],[6699,"The young one is counted."],[6700,"While the Messenger of God ﷺ was sitting on the grave of his son, he saw a gap and said to the gravedigger: \"Bring me a clod of mud to fill it. Indeed, it neither harms nor benefits, but it pleases the eye of the living.\""],[6701,"He was sitting by a grave while it was being given a lahd, and he said to the one making the lahd: \"Make it wider from the side of his feet.\""],[6702,"My father was killed on the day of Uhud, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Dig and make wide and make good, and bury two or three in a single grave, and put forward the one who knew more of the Quran.\" My father was the third of three and was the one who knew more of the Quran, so he was put forward."],[6703,"I saw Ibn ʿAbbās: when they had finished [filling] the grave of ʿAbd Allāh b. al-Sāʾib, and the people were with him, Ibn ʿAbbās stood and positioned himself over it and supplicated for him. He [the narrator] said: Did you hear anything of what he said? He said: No."],[6704,"The Prophet ﷺ stood over the grave of Saʿd b. Muʿādh when it had been finished, and he supplicated for him and prayed over him. It is from there that this practice was taken."],[6705,"Ibn al-Munkadir stood over a grave after it had been finished and said: “O Allah, make him steadfast. He is now being questioned.”"],[6706,"When ʿUmar had leveled the grave over a deceased person, he said: “O Allah, he has been surrendered to You along with family, wealth, and kinsfolk. His sin is great, so forgive him.”"],[6707,"ʿAlī said the takbīr four times over Yazīd b. al-Mukaffaf, and he sat on the grave while he was being buried. He said: “O Allah, Your servant and the son of Your servant has come down to You today, and You are the best with whom one alights. O Allah, make spacious for him his entry, and forgive him his sin. We know nothing of him but good, and You know him best.”"],[6708,"It has reached me that Ibn ʿAbbās, when he had finished the burial of Maymūnah, stood over the grave and supplicated for a while, then departed."],[6709,"He forbade water spouts [near] graves — the water spouts that are set up for hunting."],[6710,"It is disliked to perform ablution on graves or to sit on them. I said to him: Should I step over it? He said: I dislike it. He said: When we reach the grave of one of them, we indeed tread on it."],[6711,"He came to the cemetery of Makkah, and it was said to him: Will you tread on the grave? He said: “Then where should I tread? Here?” And he pointed to the mountain pass of the Madinans."],[6712,"If I were to sit on a live coal and it burned my cloak, then my shirt, then my lower garment, then reached my skin, that would be dearer to me than sitting on the grave of a Muslim man."],[6713,"If I were to tread on embers of ghaḍā wood, that would be dearer to me than treading on the grave of a Muslim man."],[6715,"He was with Saʿīd b. Jubayr at a funeral, and Saʿīd carried. He began with the front end of the pole that was at the head, placing it on his right shoulder, then he went back to its end that was by the foot and carried it on his left shoulder, then he came to its end that was by the head and placed it on his left shoulder, then he turned away on his right, and said: 'This is how funerals are carried.'"],[6716,"I saw him carry a bier, and he began with the front of the bier, placing it on his right shoulder."],[6718,"When one of you follows a funeral, let him take hold of all its sides, for that is from the Sunnah; then after that let him volunteer or leave off."],[6719,"Whoever carries the bier by its four sides has fulfilled what is due upon him."],[6720,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said to ʿAlī: 'O Abū al-Ḥasan, do you think that if I attend a funeral, carrying it is obligatory upon the one who attends it?' He said: 'No, but it is good; so whoever wishes may take hold, and whoever wishes may leave off. When you attend a funeral, place it before you and set it directly between your eyes, for it is only an admonition, a reminder, and a lesson. If it seems good to you to carry, then look to the front of the bier, and look to its left side and place it on your right shoulder.'"],[6721,"I saw Ibn ʿUmar at a funeral carry by the four sides of the bier. He said: 'He began with its right sides, then he withdrew from it, so he was from it in the position of one driving away a dog.'"],[6723,"Al-Miswar b. Makhramah, when he prayed over a funeral, would not leave until permission was given to him."],[6724,"Two commanders, yet they are not commanders: the man who is with the funeral and prays over it—he has no right to return until he seeks permission from its guardian; and the menstruating woman—her companions have no right to depart until they seek permission."],[6726,"When you have prayed over a funeral, you have fulfilled what is due upon you, so leave it and its family. Thus he would leave and not seek their permission."],[6728,"When you have prayed over the funeral bier, you have fulfilled what is upon you, so leave it between it and its family."],[6730,"He saw al-Qasim b. Muhammad and 'Urwah b. al-Zubayr following a funeral bier, and they heard the call to prayer before it was finished, so they stood up when they heard the call before they had finished with it."],[6731,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz went out with a funeral bier, and when it was placed in the grave, he departed and did not seek permission."],[6732,"My community will remain upon a firm hold of its religion as long as they do not entrust the funerals to their families."],[6733,"Every human being is buried in the soil from which he was created."],[6734,"Bodies are only buried where the souls are taken."],[6735,"There is no newborn that is born except that God sends an angel who takes dust from the earth and places it on the place where his umbilical cord is cut, and in it is his healing, and his grave is in the place where the dust was taken from him."],[6736,"The companions of the Prophet ﷺ did not know where to bury the Prophet ﷺ until Abu Bakr said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: No prophet was buried except where he died. He said: So they moved his bed aside and dug for him beneath his bed."],[6737,"Had I been present for Abd al-Rahman—meaning her brother—he would not have been buried except where he died. He died at al-Hubshi, so he was buried in upper Makkah. Al-Hubshi is near Makkah."],[6738,"I offered condolences to Aisha for her brother, and she said: May God have mercy on my brother. The greatest thing I find concerning my brother is that he was not buried where he died."],[6739,"Had I been present for him—meaning her brother—he would have been buried beneath his bed."],[6740,"A person used to stand at the mosque and clean from it something he found, and he died. The Prophet ﷺ asked about him some days after that, and they said: He died, O Messenger of God. He said: Why did you not inform me, for my prayer over them is a light in their graves."],[6741,"Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Bakr died six miles from Makkah, so we carried him until we brought him to Makkah and buried him. Then A'ishah came to us after that and criticized us for that. Then she said: Where is my brother's grave? We showed it to her, and she was placed in her howdah at his grave and prayed over him."],[6743,"A black woman used to be in the mosque, and she died, so the Prophet ﷺ prayed over her after she had been buried."],[6744,"A woman from the people of al-Awali fell ill. The Messenger of God ﷺ used to ask about her, and he was the best of people—or he said: the best of people—in visiting the sick. He said: If she dies, inform me. She died at night, and in the morning the Prophet ﷺ asked and they told him her news, and that they had buried her at night. He said: So the Prophet ﷺ came to her grave and prayed over her and said the takbir four times."],[6745,"Some people came after the funeral prayer had been performed over Sahl b. Hunayf, so Ali ordered Qarazah al-Ansari to lead them, and he prayed over him after he had been buried."],[6748,"Ibn Umar arrived after his brother Asim had died, and he asked about him and said: Where is my brother's grave? They showed it to him, and he came to it and supplicated for him."],[6749,"When he missed the funeral prayer, he would not pray over it."],[6750,"When Qatadah missed the funeral prayer, he would pray over it."],[6751,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Are there charity dues on working camels and ploughing oxen? He said: No. And ʿAmr b. Dīnār said to me: We heard that. And ʿAbd al-Karīm said: That is what we say: no charity is due on working camels or ploughing oxen, and he did not trace it back to anyone."],[6752,"No charity is due on ploughing oxen."],[6754,"We did not perceive the burial of the Prophet ﷺ until we heard the sound of shovels in the last part of the night."],[6755,"That Abu Bakr was buried at night, and the funeral prayer was performed over him in the mosque."],[6756,"That Umar buried Abu Bakr after the last evening prayer, after he had performed it."],[6758,"She had instructed him to do that."],[6759,"That Ali buried Fatimah at night, and did not inform Abu Bakr of it."],[6760,"Shurayh used to bury at night."],[6761,"Shurayh used to deliberately bury his dead at night, and when morning came he was asked about it, and he said: “He has become still, and we hope that he has found rest.”"],[6762,"There was a man from the people of Najd who, when he supplicated, raised his voice; when he prayed, he raised his voice; and when he recited, he raised his voice. Abu Dharr complained about him to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of God, this bedouin has harmed me. When he supplicates, he raises his voice; and when he recites, he raises his voice.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Leave him, for he is awwah (full of sighs).” Abu Dharr said: Then when it came to the expedition of Tabuk, I saw a fire at night, and I said: I will go to this fire and see what is around it. And there was a funeral being prepared, and a man in the grave, and he was saying: “Come, bring your companion near to me, bring your companion near to me.” And in the grave was the Prophet ﷺ, and the bedouin was the funeral."],[6763,"I said to Nafi': Did Ibn Umar pray over funerals after Asr and Subh? He said: “Yes, as long as they had not prayed it at its proper time.”"],[6765,"I said to Nafi': Did Ibn Umar pray over funerals after Asr and Subh? He said: “Yes, as long as they had not prayed it at its proper time.”"],[6766,"He used to dislike that funeral prayer be offered when the sun rises until it has risen a little."],[6767,"Take out the funerals before the sun starts to decline for setting."],[6768,"Ibn ‘Umar said on the day the funeral of Rafi‘ b. Khadij was placed in Baqi‘ al-Gharqad: “They want to pray over it after Subh, before the sun rises. Ibn ‘Umar shouted to the people: ‘Do you not fear God? It is not proper for you to pray over funerals after Subh until the sun rises, nor after ‘Asr until the sun sets.’” So the people stopped and did not pray over it until the sun rose."],[6769,"There is no harm in praying over a funeral as long as the sun has not set."],[6770,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Are there charity dues on working camels and ploughing oxen? He said: No. And ʿAmr b. Dīnār said to me: We heard that. And ʿAbd al-Karīm said: That is what we say: no charity is due on working camels or ploughing oxen, and he did not trace it back to anyone."],[6771,"I was in Madinah with Ibn ‘Umar during the fitnah, when ‘Abbas b. Sahl, a man of the Ansar, came and said: “O Abu ‘Abd al-Rahman, ‘Aqil b. Abi Talib has been placed at the mosque gate to be prayed over, and that is after ‘Asr.” He said: “O Ibn Yasar, look: has the sun set?” He said: “No.” So he refused to get up. He said: Then he came back to him and said: “Look: has the sun set?” I looked and said: “No.” So he refused to pray over him. He said: Then they took him away and prayed over him, while they wanted Ibn ‘Umar to lead them; Ibn al-Zubayr was then in Makkah."],[6772,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade us to pray in three hours and to bury our dead during them: when the sun rises until it becomes white and rises high, when it sets until its setting becomes clear, and at midday during intense heat."],[6773,"I said to Nafi': Did Ibn Umar dislike praying in the midst of graves? He said: We prayed over Aisha and Umm Salama in the midst of al-Baqi'. He said: The imam on the day we prayed over Aisha ؓ was Abu Hurayra, and Abdullah b. Umar attended that."],[6774,"When an obligatory prayer and a funeral are both present, one begins with the obligatory prayer."],[6775,"I saw al-Hasan when a funeral was placed at Maghrib prayer time, and he began by praying over the funeral, then prayed Maghrib after that. I mentioned that to Qatadah, who said: “If only he had begun with the obligatory prayer.”"],[6776,"When the funeral and the obligatory prayer coincide, begin with the obligatory prayer."],[6778,"I was informed that a funeral was placed in the cemetery of Basra when the sun had turned yellow, and it was not prayed over until the sun had set. Then Abu Barza ordered the announcer, and he made the call. Then he stood up and Abu Barza came forward and led them in the Maghrib prayer, and among the people was Anas b. Malik. Then he prayed over the funeral."],[6779,"My father saw people leaving the mosque to pray over a funeral, and he said, 'What are these people doing? Abu Bakr was not prayed over except in the mosque.'"],[6781,"She ordered that the funeral of Sa'd b. Malik be brought past her in the mosque when he died so that she could supplicate, and the people objected to that. Aisha said: 'How quickly people have forgotten! The Messenger of God ﷺ did not pray over Suhayl b. Bayda' except in the mosque.'"],[6782,"Whoever prays over a funeral in the mosque, there is nothing for him."],[6783,"I will certainly know that I have not prayed over a funeral in the mosque."],[6784,"There is no man who dies and a community of people pray over him and seek forgiveness for him except that they are granted intercession."],[6785,"A man came to Ali b. Abi Talib and said, 'Will you not stand and pray over this funeral?' He said, 'We are standing, and nothing prays over him except his own deeds.'"],[6786,"Concerning working camels: for every five, one sheep."],[6787,"The people of her religion attend to her, and she is buried with them."],[6788,"He buried a woman from the People of the Book who was pregnant from a Muslim in the cemetery of the Muslims."],[6789,"Wathila b. al-Asqaʿ handed over a Christian woman who had died while pregnant from a Muslim to a cemetery that was neither the Christians' cemetery nor the Muslims' cemetery, between the two.\" Sulayman said: \"Those of her religion should attend to her."],[6790,"I said to ʿAtaʾ: Are people obliged to straighten their rows for funerals as they straighten them in prayer? He said: \"No, they are only people who say takbir and ask forgiveness.\""],[6791,"When he prayed over a child, he said: \"O Allah, make him a forerunner for us and make him a reward for us.\""],[6792,"Nothing is due on working animals."],[6793,"A man came to Abu al-Darda' and asked him about a verse, but he did not tell him. The man turned away saying: 'Indeed, those who conceal what We have sent down of clear signs and guidance...' Abu al-Darda' said: 'How will you be when you enter your grave and two black, blue-eyed angels are brought out to you, stepping on their hair and digging with their fangs? They will ask about Muhammad ﷺ. What kind of man will you be if you remain steadfast concerning him?' And he mentioned that they have an iron club that if the two weighty ones—or he said the people of Mina—were to gather against it, they could not bear it. 'How will you be when the bridge of Hell is placed? What kind of man will you be if you pass over it or are saved? And how will you be when there is no place on earth except where your foot stands, and no shade except the shade of the Throne of the Most Merciful? What kind of man will you be if you seek shade under it? Go away; by Allah, besides whom there is no deity, this is indeed the truth.'"],[6794,"If the newborn cries out, prayer is performed over it. Al-Zuhri said: \"And it inherits if it cries out.\""],[6795,"It does not inherit until it cries out even if it moves. He said: \"If it sneezed, in my view it would be like crying out.\""],[6796,"If the soul remained in it for three days, it does not inherit until it cries out."],[6797,"Midwives testified about a child who moved but did not cry out, and Shurayh did not make it inherit."],[6798,"If it cries out, prayer is performed over it, blood money is paid for it, and it inherits."],[6799,"Masruq and I entered upon 'A'ishah and said: 'Ibn Mas'ud said: Whoever loves to meet Allah, Allah loves to meet him; and whoever hates to meet Allah, Allah hates to meet him—and death is before meeting Allah.' She said: 'May Allah have mercy on Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. He told you a hadith but you did not ask him about its end. I will tell you about that: When Allah intends good for His servant, He appoints an angel for him a year before his death, who guides him and makes things easy for him until he dies in the best state possible. When death approaches and he sees his reward from Paradise, he begins to gasp for his soul, wishing it would come out. Then he loves to meet Allah, and Allah loves to meet him. And when Allah intends evil for a servant, He appoints a devil for him a year before his death, who turns him away, leads him astray, and tempts him until he dies in the worst state possible. People say: So-and-so died in the worst state. When death approaches and he sees his punishment from the Fire, he begins to swallow his soul, wishing it would not come out. Then he hates to meet Allah, and Allah hates to meet him.'"],[6800,"I said to 'Ata': 'Do you pray over one who has cried out and upward?' He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'And a child born dead, three times?' He said: 'I have not heard that such a one is prayed over.'"],[6801,"Regarding the miscarried fetus that is born alive — and the small child — one does not pray over it until it cries out loudly."],[6802,"Ibn 'Umar was asked about a miscarried fetus that falls dead: is it prayed over? He said: 'No, until it cries out. When it cries out, it is prayed over and inherited.'"],[6803,"Ibn 'Umar prayed over a small newborn, a miscarriage — I do not know whether it cried out or not. He prayed over it in his house, then it was sent and buried."],[6805,"When its creation is complete and the soul is breathed into it, it is prayed over even if it does not cry out. Qatadah said: 'And it is named, for it will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection by its name — or he said: it will be called by its name.'"],[6806,"The miscarried fetus is prayed over, and one supplicates for its parents for well-being and mercy."],[6807,"If its creation is not complete, it is buried but not prayed over."],[6808,"Ali entered upon his son al-Hasan while al-Ashari was with him. He said: “What has brought you, old man?” He said: “I heard of the illness of my nephew’s son and wished to visit him.” He said: “Indeed, what is in our hearts does not prevent us from telling you what we have heard: whoever visits a sick person by day, seventy thousand angels pray for him until evening; and if he visits him at night, seventy thousand angels pray for him until morning.”"],[6809,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over the son of Mariyah the Copt, and he was sixteen months old."],[6810,"Ibn al-Zubayr said to Husayn b. 'Ali: 'Who bears the ransom of the captive?' He said: 'The land for which we are fighting.' He said: 'And I asked him about the newborn, when does its share become obligatory?' He said: 'When it cries out, its share becomes obligatory.'"],[6811,"And Abū Bakr died at the same age."],[6812,"Regarding the newborn whose cry is heard: 'It inherits when its voice is heard.'"],[6813,"A young sister of mine died. My father ordered a client of hers to bury her, and he did not come out to her, nor follow her — he said: I think he said: — nor pray over her."],[6814,"I saw Abu Hurayrah pray over a stillborn who had never committed a sin, and he said: \"O Allah, protect him from the punishment of the grave.\""],[6815,"The young one is counted."],[6817,"Regarding the child of adultery: \"If he dies as a small infant, prayer is not offered over him.\""],[6818,"I asked Ata' about the child of adultery when he is born after he has cried: should prayer be offered over him? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"How, when he is like that?\" He said: \"Because he was born upon the fitrah, the fitrah of Islam.\" I said: \"What if he grows up and becomes an evil man?\" He said: \"Prayer is still offered over him.\" I said: \"What if his mother dies during her post-partum bleeding?\" He said: \"I would not leave her,\" while he also said: {Indeed, Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him}. And Ata' said to me after that: \"Prayer is offered over the child of adultery if he cried at birth, and over his mother if she dies from post-partum bleeding, and over the two who have sworn mutual cursing, and over the one who is executed in retribution, and over the stoned one, and over the one who is engaged in battle and flees and is killed, and over the one who dies an evil death.\" He said: \"I do not abandon prayer over anyone who said 'There is no god but Allah.'\" I said: \"Even after it becomes clear to him that he is one of the people of Hell?\" He said: \"Who knows that these are among the people of Hell?\""],[6819,"No charity is due on working cattle."],[6823,"A man died during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and a man came to him and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so has died.\" He said: \"He has not died.\" Then he came to him a second time, then a third time. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"How did he die?\" He said: \"He slaughtered himself with a blade he had.\" So he did not pray over him."],[6824,"No charity is due on working cattle."],[6825,"The Prophet ﷺ stoned two men and prayed over one of them but did not pray over the other."],[6827,"Pray over the one who says: There is no god but Allah, even if he is an extremely wicked man. Say: O Allah, forgive the believing men and believing women and the Muslim men and Muslim women.\" He said: \"And I do not know anyone among the people of knowledge who avoided praying over the one who says: There is no god but Allah.\""],[6830,"When Ali stoned Shuraḥah al-Hamdaniyyah, her relatives came and said: What shall we do with her? He said to them: Do with her what you do with your dead, meaning washing her, praying over her, and the like."],[6832,"Abd Allah b. Abd Allah b. Ubayy said to the Prophet ﷺ: Let me kill my father, for he harms Allah and His Messenger. The Prophet ﷺ said: Do not kill your father. Then he went away, then returned to him and said: Let me kill him. He said: \"Do not kill your father.\" Then he came the third time, and he said the like of that to him. He said: Perform ablution, O Messenger of Allah, so that perhaps I may give him water to drink, perhaps his heart will soften. He said: So the Prophet ﷺ performed ablution and gave him the water. He said: I gave you to drink the ablution water of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: You gave me to drink your mother's urine. Ibn Abbas said: When his illness in which he died came, the Prophet ﷺ came to him and they spoke words between them. Abd Allah said: I understood what you say; grant me a favor, so shroud me in this shirt of yours and pray over me. He said: So the Prophet ﷺ shrouded him in that shirt of his and prayed over him. Ibn Abbas said: And Allah knows best what prayer it was, and Muhammad ﷺ never deceived any person ever."],[6834,"The Prophet ﷺ came to Abd Allah b. Ubayy after he had been placed in his grave, and he ordered that he be brought out to him. He placed him on his knees, put his shirt on him, and blew on him from his saliva, and Allah knows best."],[6835,"The Prophet ﷺ rode to Banu al-Ḥārith and saw a funeral on a piece of wood. He said: \"What is this?\" It was said: A slave of ours. He was a bad slave, hated, harsh. He said: \"Was this one praying?\" They said: Yes. He said: \"Was he saying Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" They said: Yes. He said: The angels nearly came between me and him. Return and make good his washing, shrouding, and burial."],[6836,"The Prophet ﷺ saw in al-Baqīʿ a black slave carrying a dead person. He said: \"Who is carrying him? What is this?\" They said: A slave of so-and-so. He said: \"What is he?\" They said: The wickedest of people, the most thieving, the most runaway, and the most rebellious, among other things of evil they mentioned about him. He said: \"Bring me his master.\" He asked him about him, and he mentioned similar to what was mentioned. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Was he praying?\" They said: Yes. He said: \"And did he bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah?\" They said: Yes. He said: \"By the One in whose hand is my soul, the angels almost came between me and him just now.\" He called a blacksmith and had his iron removed, then ordered that he be washed, then shrouded from his own possessions, then prayed over him."],[6838,"If a child is from the captives or others and is between his two parents while they are polytheists, then funeral prayer is not offered over him. But if he is not between his two parents, then he is a Muslim: if he dies while a child, funeral prayer is offered over him."],[6839,"On the day of Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ looked over the martyrs who were killed that day and said: “I have witnessed over these, so shroud them in their blood.” He would bury two men or three in one grave and ask which of them had memorized more of the Qur'an, and they would put him forward. Jabir said: My father and my paternal uncle were buried in one grave that day."],[6840,"Nothing is due on working animals."],[6841,"I used to hear for a time that they used to say: 'Take from us what the Prophet ﷺ took.' I was surprised that they did not accept that from them, until al-Zuhrī told me that the Prophet ﷺ wrote a document containing these obligations, and the Prophet ﷺ passed away before writing to the governors. Abu Bakr followed it and enforced it after him according to what he had written. I do not think he mentioned anything but cattle as well."],[6844,"I did not see them wash the martyr, perfume him, or shroud him. I said: How do we pray over him? He said: “Just as prayer is offered over any other person who is not a martyr.”"],[6845,"Mu'awiya ordered the killing of Hujr b. 'Adi al-Kindi. Hujr said: “Do not loosen any shackle from me—or he said: any iron—and shroud me in my clothes and my blood.”"],[6846,"“Do not wash any blood from me, and do not remove any garment except the two leather socks, and bury me in the ground. For I am a man who will argue and contend on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[6847,"Bury us with whatever blood of ours has touched the soil."],[6848,"He was called ‘the reciter’ in the time of the Prophet ﷺ. He met an enemy and fled from them. 'Umar said to him: “Would you like to go to Syria? Perhaps Allah will grant you martyrdom.” He said: “No, except against those from whom I fled.” He said: Then he addressed them at al-Qadisiyya and said: “We will meet the enemy tomorrow, if Allah wills, and we will be martyred. Do not wash any blood from us, and do not shroud us except in the garment that was on us.”"],[6849,"We asked Sulayman b. Musa: How is the prayer over a martyr among them? He said: “The same form as over anyone else.” He said: And we asked about burying a martyr. He said: “If he is on the battlefield, we bury him as he is, without washing, shrouding, or perfuming him. But if we carry him away while he still has life in him, then we wash him, shroud him, and perfume him. We found the people upon that practice, and so did those of the people who passed before us.”"],[6850,"When the martyr dies on the battlefield, he is buried as he is; but if he dies after being brought back, he is treated as the other is treated."],[6851,"Umar was the best of martyrs, yet he was washed, prayed over, and shrouded, because he lived after his stabbing."],[6852,"Ali was washed, shrouded, and prayed over."],[6853,"If a martyr dies on the spot, he is not washed. If he is carried away alive, he is washed."],[6854,"He was asked about a man whom thieves killed, and he said: “He is not washed.”"],[6855,"Prayer is performed over the martyr, and he is not washed, for God has already purified him."],[6857,"A man from the Bedouins came to the Prophet ﷺ, believed in him, and followed him, and said: “I will emigrate with you.” The Prophet ﷺ entrusted him to one of his companions. When it was the battle of Khaybar — or he said: Hunayn — the Messenger of God ﷺ obtained some spoil to be distributed, and he allotted a share for him. He gave his companions what he allotted, and the man was tending their riding animals. When he came, they handed it to him. He said: “What is this?” They said: “A share the Prophet ﷺ allotted for you.” He took it and brought it to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “What is this, Muhammad?” He said: “A share I allotted for you.” He said: “It was not for this that I followed you. Rather, I followed you so that I would be shot here — and he pointed with his hand to his throat — with an arrow, and die, and enter Paradise.” He said: “If you are true to God, He will be true to you.” They stayed a little while, then they rose to fight the enemy. He was brought to the Prophet ﷺ being carried, having been struck by an arrow where he had pointed. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Is it he? Is it he?” They said: “Yes.” He said: “He was true to God, and He was true to him.” Then the Prophet ﷺ shrouded him in the Prophet’s ﷺ jubbah, then brought him forward and prayed over him. Among what appeared of his prayer was: “O God, this is Your servant who went out as an emigrant in Your path and was killed as a martyr.”"],[6859,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed over Hamzah on the day of Uhud seventy prayers — every time a man was brought, he prayed over him, and Hamzah was placed and prayed over along with him."],[6861,"From the slain man are removed his leather socks, his trousers, and his cap — or he said: his turban — and a garment is added, or a garment is subtracted, until it becomes an odd number."],[6862,"When Mu'awiya wanted to run the water channel, he said: Whoever has a slain relative, let him come for his slain one – meaning the slain of Uhud. He said: He brought them out fresh, bending. He said: The shovel struck the leg of one of them, and it gushed blood. Abu Sa'id said: After this, no denier should ever deny."],[6863,"One of the family of Talha b. 'Ubayd Allah saw in a dream that he saw him and said: You have buried me in a place where water has reached me, so move me from it. So they moved him and brought him out as if he were a fresh vegetable; nothing of him had changed except some hairs of his beard."],[6864,"We carried the slain on the day of Uhud to bury them, but the caller of the Prophet ﷺ came and said: Bury the slain where they fell. So we returned them."],[6865,"The martyr is not buried in sandals, leather socks, shoes, weapons, or a ring. He said: We bury him in the belt and clothes."],[6866,"A woman who died in the desert was mentioned to 'Umar. People kept passing by her and did not bury her, until Kulayb passed by her and buried her. 'Umar said: I hope for good for Kulayb because of her. He said: 'Umar asked 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar about her, and he said: I did not see her. He said: Had you seen her and not buried her, I would have made you an example."],[6867,"Umar b. al-Khattab did not allow any non-Arab to enter Madinah. So al-Mughirah b. Shu'bah wrote to Umar: 'I have a servant who is a carpenter, engraver, and blacksmith, in whom there is benefit for the people of Madinah. If you see fit to permit me to send him, I will do so.' He allowed him, and he had imposed on him two dirhams every day. He was called Abu Lu'lu'ah, and he was originally a Magian. He remained as long as Allah willed. Then he came to Umar complaining of the heaviness of his tax. Umar said to him: 'What crafts are you skilled in?' He said: 'Carpenter, engraver, blacksmith.' Umar said: 'Your tax is not much considering what crafts you are skilled in.' He went away resentful. Then he passed by Umar while he was sitting and said: 'Was I not told that you say: If I wished to make a mill that grinds by wind, I would do it?' Abu Lu'lu'ah said: 'I will make a mill that people will talk about.' He went on. Umar said: 'The slave has just threatened me.' When he resolved on what he resolved, he took a dagger and concealed it on himself, then sat in wait for Umar in a corner of the mosque. Umar used to go out at dawn and wake people for prayer. He passed by him, and he sprang at him and stabbed him three times: one below his navel, which was the one that killed him, and he stabbed twelve men of the people of the mosque, of whom six died and six survived. Then he slit his own throat with his dagger and died."],[6868,"When a funeral passed by him, he would say: Depart, for we are leaving early, or leave early, for we are departing – a powerful admonition and a swift heedlessness. The first goes and the last remains, with no sense."],[6869,"It is said when a funeral is seen: God is greatest; this is what God and His Messenger promised, and God and His Messenger spoke the truth. O God, increase us in faith and submission. We are at peace with God our Lord. By this we abide – meaning salam: submission; that is, we are Yours."],[6870,"When Abu Hurayra was asked about the funeral, he would say: It is you; if you refuse, then I."],[6871,"Providing food for the deceased is from the affair of the Jahiliyyah, a woman spending the night at the house of the deceased is from the affair of the Jahiliyyah, and wailing is from the affair of the Jahiliyyah."],[6872,"When the news of Ja'far's death came, the Messenger of God ﷺ said: Prepare food for the family of Ja'far, for something has come to them that preoccupies them."],[6873,"When Ja'far was struck down, the Messenger of God ﷺ came to me and said: O Asma', do not say anything indecent and do not strike your chest. She said: Fatima came forward saying, 'O cousin!' The Prophet ﷺ said: For one like Ja'far, let the weepers weep. She said: Then the Prophet ﷺ turned to his family and said: Prepare food for the family of Ja'far, for they are preoccupied today."],[6874,"Patience is at the first shock, and tears are not something the son of Adam controls – the remnant of a man's affection for his brother."],[6875,"That the Prophet ﷺ passed by a woman who had been afflicted by the loss of her child, and he heard from her what he disliked, so he stopped by her to admonish her. She said to him, 'Go away, for there is not in your heart what is in my heart.' He turned away from her, and it was said to her, 'Woe to you! Do you not know who stopped by you? He is the Messenger of God ﷺ.' She followed him and said, 'O Messenger of God, I did not recognize you.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said, 'Go away, for patience is only at the first shock.'"],[6876,"That the Prophet ﷺ passed by a woman who had been afflicted by the loss of her child, and he heard from her what he disliked, so he stopped by her to admonish her. She said to him, 'Go away, for there is not in your heart what is in my heart.' He turned away from her, and it was said to her, 'Woe to you! Do you not know who stopped by you? He is the Messenger of God ﷺ.' She followed him and said, 'O Messenger of God, I did not recognize you.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said, 'Go away, for patience is only at the first shock.'"],[6877,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade taking anything from horses."],[6878,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered, leaning on 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf, while Ibrahim, the son of the Prophet ﷺ, was breathing his last. When he saw him, his eyes shed tears. 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf said to him, 'O Messenger of God, do you weep? Whenever the Muslims see you weeping, they will weep.' He said: When his tears flowed, he said, 'This is only compassion, and whoever does not show compassion will not be shown compassion. I only forbid people from wailing and from lamenting the dead with what is not in him.' When he passed away, he said, 'Were it not that it is a comprehensive promise and a path that is trodden, and that the last of us will join the first, we would have found grief other than what we have found. We are surely saddened by you, O Ibrahim. The eye sheds tears, and the heart grieves, but we do not say what displeases the Lord. And the excellence of his suckling is in Paradise.'"],[6879,"That Fatimah wept for the Messenger of God ﷺ, saying: 'O my father, to his Lord how near he is! O my father, to Jibril we announce his death. O my father, the Garden of Firdaws is his abode.'"],[6880,"He was sitting with Ibn 'Umar one day in the market, and a funeral procession passed by with people weeping over it. Ibn 'Umar disapproved of that and rebuked them. Salama b. al-Azraq said to him, 'Do not say that, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, for I testify concerning Abu Hurayra: I heard him say: A woman from the clan of Marwan died, and I attended her funeral. Marwan ordered that the women who were weeping be struck. Abu Hurayra said, \"Leave them, O Abu 'Abd al-Malik, for the Prophet ﷺ passed by a funeral procession with people weeping over it, and I was with him and 'Umar b. al-Khattab was with him. 'Umar rebuked the women who were weeping, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him, 'Leave them, O Ibn al-Khattab, for the soul is afflicted and the eye is tearful, and the covenant is recent.'\"' He said, 'Did you hear it?' He said, 'Yes.' He said, 'God and His Messenger know best.'"],[6881,"‘Uthmān used to levy zakat on horses."],[6882,"That Suhayb said to 'Umar, 'O my brother, O my companion!' 'Umar said, 'Be quiet, woe to you! Have you not heard us saying that the one mourned over is punished?'"],[6883,"That Suhayb said to 'Umar, 'O my brother, O my companion!' 'Umar said, 'Be quiet, woe to you! Have you not heard us saying that the one mourned over is punished?'"],[6884,"I heard Ibn 'Umar say while he was at the funeral of Rafi' b. Khadij, and the women stood weeping over Rafi'. He made them sit down repeatedly, then said to them, 'Woe to you! Rafi' b. Khadij is an old man with no strength to endure the punishment, and indeed the deceased is punished by the weeping of his family over him.'"],[6885,"Do not buy back your charity until it is taken from you."],[6886,"When Abu Bakr died, he was mourned. Umar said: \"The Prophet ﷺ said: 'The deceased is tormented by the weeping of the living,'\" but they refused to do anything but weep. Umar said to Hisham b. al-Walid: \"Get up and bring out the women.\" Aisha said: \"I will bring you out.\" Umar said: \"Enter, for I have given you permission.\" He said: So he entered. Aisha said: \"Are you going to bring me out, my son?\" He said: \"As for you, I have given permission.\" He said: He began to bring them out one woman at a time, striking them with the whip until he brought out Umm Farwa. He separated them, or he said: \"He separated the wailing women.\""],[6887,"When Khalid b. al-Walid died, women gathered in the house of Maymunah weeping. Umar came with Ibn Abbas, and he had the whip. He said: \"O Abu Abdullah, enter upon the Mother of the Believers and tell her to veil herself, and bring the women out to me.\" He said: He began bringing them out and he was striking them with the whip. The head covering of one of the women fell, and they said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, her head covering!\" He said: \"Leave her! There is no sanctity for her.\""],[6888,"Umar b. al-Khattab heard a wailing woman in Madinah at night. He went to her, entered, and separated the women. He caught the wailing woman and began striking her with the whip. Her head covering fell, and they said: \"Her hair, O Commander of the Faithful!\" He said: \"Yes, indeed, there is no sanctity for her.\""],[6889,"He is not one of us who tears open the collars, strikes the cheeks, and calls with the call of the Jahiliyyah."],[6890,"We entered upon al-Ash'ari and the mother of his child wept over him. We forbade her and said: \"Would you weep over the like of Abu Musa?\" He said: \"Leave her to pour out a bucket or two of her tears. But I call you to witness that I am innocent of anyone who shaves, wails loudly, or tears clothes.\""],[6891,"They said to Umar: \"Women from Banu al-Mughirah have gathered in the house of Khalid b. al-Walid, weeping over him. We dislike annoying you, but perhaps you should forbid them.\" He said: \"What harm is it for them to pour out a bucket or two of their tears over Abu Sulayman, as long as there is no dust-throwing or loud wailing?\""],[6892,"Four things remain from the affair of the Jahiliyyah: boasting about lineages, slandering lineages, seeking rain by the stars, and wailing. Indeed, when the wailing woman dies without having repented, she will be clothed with garments of tar and a coat of fire.\""],[6893,"Four things remain from the affair of the Jahiliyyah: boasting about lineages, slandering lineages, seeking rain by the stars, and wailing. Indeed, when the wailing woman dies without having repented, she will be clothed with garments of tar and a coat of fire.\""],[6895,"Providing food for the deceased is from the affair of the Jahiliyyah, a woman spending the night at the house of the deceased is from the affair of the Jahiliyyah, and wailing is from the affair of the Jahiliyyah."],[6896,"The Prophet ﷺ took a pledge from the women when they gave allegiance that they would not wail. They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, women helped us in the Jahiliyyah, so should we help them in Islam?\" He said: \"There is no helping in Islam, no shighar marriage in Islam, no hamstringing in Islam, no jalab, no janab, and whoever plunders is not one of us.\""],[6897,"The Prophet ﷺ took a pledge from the women when he accepted their allegiance that they would not wail or bring men for conversation. Abd al-Rahman b. Awf said to him: \"We are often absent and we have guests.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"I do not mean those.\""],[6898,"Hafsah asked permission to enter upon her father, and he said to those with him, “Arise.” She entered, and while she was with him he lost consciousness, so she wept. He said, “Did you know—or have you not heard—that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Indeed the dead person is punished by the weeping of the living.’”"],[6899,"My father was killed on the day of Uhud. He was brought to the Prophet ﷺ and placed before him, mutilated—he had been disfigured. He said: I bent over him weeping, and the people were consoling me while the Prophet ﷺ saw me and did not forbid me, until he was lifted. The Prophet ﷺ said: “The angels kept surrounding him until he was lifted.” He said: My father had a debt, and the creditors would come to the palm trees, look at them, and consider them insufficient. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: “When you want to harvest, inform me.” He said: I came to him, and he went with me until he stood among them and invoked blessing. He said: I paid off what was owed by my father, and abundant food remained for us."],[6900,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Uhud, he heard wailing and weeping from the people of Madinah. He said, “What is this?” It was said, “The Ansar are weeping over their slain.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “But Hamzah has no weepers.” That reached the Ansar, so they gathered their women and brought them into the house of Hamzah to weep over him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard them and said, “What is this?” It was said, “When the Ansar heard you say, ‘But Hamzah has no weepers,’ they gathered their women to weep over him.” The Prophet ﷺ spoke well of the Ansar and forbade them from wailing."],[6901,"The Prophet ﷺ came to Abd Allah b. Thabit Abu al-Rabi to visit him during his illness twice. He died when he came to him on the last of those two visits. The Prophet ﷺ cried out once or twice, then said: “A barrier has been placed between us and Abu al-Rabi. Indeed we belong to Allah and to Him we return.” When his daughters and the daughters of his brother heard that, they began to weep. Jabr b. Atik said to them: “Do not annoy the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Leave them, O Abu Abd Allah; let them weep for Abu al-Rabi as long as he is among them, but when it becomes obligatory, they should not weep for him.” His daughter said: “You had already prepared your equipment in the way of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “The reward of Abu al-Rabi has fallen according to his intention. What do you count as martyrdom?” They said: “Being killed in the way of Allah.” He said: “Then the martyrs of my community would be few.” They said: “What are the martyrs, O Messenger of Allah?” He said: “The one who dies of plague is a martyr, the one who dies of a stomach ailment is a martyr, the one who dies by fire is a martyr, the one who drowns is a martyr, the one who dies of pleurisy is a martyr, the one who dies of grief is a martyr, and the woman who dies in childbirth is a martyr.” The Prophet ﷺ shrouded him in his shirt."],[6902,"A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “You are the Messenger of Allah and revelation descends upon you. My two sons have been struck where you know. If they were believers, we would speak of them with what we know; but if they were hypocrites, we would not weep for them nor comfort our eyes.” He said: “Rather they are believers, and they are from the people of Paradise.” She said: “Now I will go to great lengths in weeping over them.”"],[6903,"Ibn Rawahah lost consciousness, and his wife began saying: “Woe for such and such! Woe for such and such!” When he regained consciousness, he said: “There is nothing you said except that it is said to me, ‘Are you really like that?’ and I say, ‘No.’”"],[6904,"When Zayd b. Harithah was killed, Usamah stayed away from the Prophet ﷺ and did not come to him. Then he came to him after that, and stood before the Prophet ﷺ, and his eyes shed tears. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wept, and when his tears had flowed, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Why did you stay away from us, and then come to grieve us?” He said: The next day he came to him, and when the Prophet ﷺ saw him approaching, he said: “Today I will meet from you what I met from you yesterday.” When he drew near, his eyes shed tears, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wept."],[6905,"Abu Bakr was seized by the swoon of death, and she wept over him—meaning Aishah—with a line of poetry: “He whose tears do not cease to be restrained must one day be shed.” He said: Then he regained consciousness and said: “Rather, the agony of death has come with the truth—that is what you were trying to avoid.”"],[6906,"“When one of you is struck by a calamity, let him remember his calamity with me, and that will console him for his calamity.” He said: He said: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, are there horses in Paradise? For I love horses.” He said: “Allah will admit you, if Allah wills, into Paradise, and you will not wish to ride a horse of red ruby that flies with you in whichever garden you wish except that you will do so.”"],[6907,"“There is no Muslim who is struck by a calamity and says: ‘Indeed we belong to Allah and to Him we return. O Allah, I seek reward for my calamity with You; O Allah, replace it for me with something better than it,’ except that Allah will replace it for him with something better.” She said: I kept saying to myself, “Who is better than Abu Salamah?” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and proposed to me, and I married him."],[6908,"The first drop that falls from the blood of a martyr forgives him by it all his previous sins. Then Allah sends to him two angels with sweet basil from Paradise and a fine cloth. At the edges of the sky there are angels saying: “Glory be to Allah; today a good fragrance and a good breeze has come from the earth.” He does not pass by any door except that it is opened for him, nor by any angel except that he prays over him and escorts him until he is brought before the Most Merciful. He prostrates to Him before the angels, and the angels prostrate after him. Then he is commanded to the martyrs, and he finds them in green meadows and garments of silk, near a bull and a fish that are given a lick each day unlike the lick they were given yesterday. The fish remains in the rivers of Paradise, and when evening comes the bull gores it with its horn and slaughters it for them, and they eat from its flesh and find in its flesh the taste of every fragrance from the rivers of Paradise. The bull remains grazing in Paradise, and when morning comes the fish comes upon it and gores it with its tail and slaughters it for them, and they eat from its flesh and find in its flesh the taste of every fruit from the fruits of Paradise. They look at their dwellings morning and evening, calling upon Allah that the Hour may be established. When a believer dies, Allah sends to him two angels with sweet basil from Paradise and a piece of cloth from Paradise in which his soul is taken. It is said: “Come out, O good soul, to rest and sweet basil, and your Lord is not angry with you.” So it comes out like the best fragrance anyone has ever smelled with his nose. At the edges of the sky there are angels saying: “Glory be to Allah; today a good fragrance and a noble breeze has come from the earth.” It does not pass by any door except that it is opened for it, nor by any angel except that he prays over it and escorts it until it is brought before the Most Merciful. The angels prostrate before it and it prostrates after them. Then Mikha’il is summoned and it is said: “Take this soul and place it with the souls of the believers until I ask you about them on the Day of Resurrection.” He is commanded to his grave, and it is widened for him seventy cubits in length and seventy in width, and sweet basil is scattered for him in it, and he is covered with silk. If he had anything of the Quran with him, he is clothed with its light, and if he did not have anything, a light like the sun is made for him. His likeness is like that of a bride whom no one awakens except the dearest of her family to her. When a disbeliever dies, Allah sends to him two angels with a piece of coarse cloth, stinkier than every stench and rougher than every rough thing. It is said: “Come out, O evil soul, and evil is what you have sent ahead for yourself.” So it comes out like the foulest odor anyone has ever smelled with his nose. Then he is commanded to his grave, and it is constricted for him until his ribs interlace. Then snakes are sent upon him like the necks of Bactrian camels that eat his flesh. Mute, deaf, and blind angels are appointed for him who do not hear his voice nor see him to have mercy on him, and they do not tire when they strike. They call upon Allah to make that continue for him until he reaches the Fire."],[6909,"When the deceased is placed in his grave, prayer is at his head, zakah at his right, fasting at his left, and charity, maintaining ties, kindness, and benevolence to people are at his feet. He is approached from the direction of his head, and prayer says: “There is no entrance before me.” Then he is approached from his right, and zakah says: “There is no entrance before me.” Then he is approached from his left, and fasting says: “There is no entrance before me.” Then he is approached from his feet, and charity says: “There is no entrance before me.” He said: Then he is made to sit up. Abu Hurayrah said: He hears the striking of their sandals. He said: He is made to sit, and the sun is shown to him as if it has neared setting. He says: “Let me pray.” It is said to him: “You will do so.” Then it is said to him: “What do you say about this man?” He says: “Muhammad?” They say: “Yes.” He says: “I bear witness that he came with the truth from Allah.” He said: It is said to him: “Upon that you lived, upon that you died, and upon that you will be raised, if Allah wills.” He said: That is His saying: “Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter.” He said: A door to the Fire is opened for him, and he looks at his dwellings in it. It is said to him: “If you had disobeyed, these would be your dwellings,” and he increases in joy and happiness, and his grave is made spacious for him—seventy. Abd al-Rahman b. Yahya b. Hantab said: Then it is said: “Sleep the sleep of a bride whom no one awakens except the dearest of creation to him.” The hadith returns to Abu Hurayrah, who said: His soul is placed in the good breeze in the bodies of birds that hang among the trees of Paradise, or hang from the trees of Paradise. He said: And the bodies return to that for which they were created. He said: As for the disbeliever, he is approached from the direction of his head and nothing is found for him. He is made to sit, then it is said to him: “What did you use to say about this man?”—twice. He does not remember until he is prompted, and he says: “Muhammad? I used to say what the people say.” It is said to him: “You have spoken truthfully; upon that you lived, upon that you died, and upon that you will be raised, if Allah wills.” Then a door to Paradise is opened, and he sees its dwellings. It is said to him: “If you had acted and obeyed Allah, these would be your dwellings,” and he increases in regret and destruction. He said: Then it is closed upon him, and a door to the Fire is opened, and he sees his dwellings in it and what Allah has prepared for him of punishment, and he increases in regret and destruction. His grave is constricted for him until his ribs interlace. That is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: “a life of narrowness.” He said: And his soul is placed in Sijjin."],[6912,"Had the Messenger of God ﷺ not forbidden visiting graves, I would have visited my daughter’s grave."],[6913,"When the collector finds an age above the due age or below it, they return to him, in place of the difference between them, twenty dirhams or two sheep.” Al-Thawrī said: “This applies only to camels. But if they are for trade, they are valued in dirhams.”"],[6914,"I had forbidden you from visiting graves, but now visit them, for they remind of the Hereafter. I had forbidden you from nabīdh made in earthen jars, but now make nabīdh in any vessel and avoid every intoxicant. I had forbidden you from eating sacrificial meat after three days, but now eat it, take it as provision, and store it."],[6916,"A man came to Ibn Umar asking him about the zakah of his wealth. He said: \"Hand it over to the ruler.\" He said: Our commanders are the dahaqin. He said: \"And what are the dahaqin?\" He said: Among the polytheists. He said: \"Then do not hand it over to the polytheists.\""],[6917,"“Come to your dead, greet them, and invoke blessing upon them, for in them is a lesson for you.” Ibn Abī Mulaykah said: “I saw ʿĀʾishah visiting the grave of her brother ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Abī Bakr. He died at al-Ḥubshī and was buried in Makkah.”"],[6918,"She said: “Shall I not tell you about myself and the Prophet ﷺ?” We said: “Yes.” She said: “When it was my night, he turned over, placed his sandals by his feet, and laid down his cloak until he spread the edge of his lower garment over his bed. He remained only as long as he thought I had fallen asleep. Then he slowly put on his sandals and slowly took his cloak. I put my shift over my head and veiled myself, then wrapped myself in my lower garment and followed his tracks until he came to al-Baqīʿ. He raised his hands three times and prolonged his standing. Then he turned away and I turned away. He quickened his pace and I quickened mine. He jogged and I jogged. He ran and I ran, and I overtook him and entered. No sooner had I lain down than he entered and said: ‘What is with you, O ʿĀʾishah, that you are out of breath and panting?’ I said: ‘Nothing.’ He said: ‘Will you tell me, or the Subtle, the All-Aware will inform me?’ I said: ‘O Messenger of God, may my father and mother be your ransom,’ and I told him the matter. He said: ‘Were you the dark figure I saw in front of me?’ I said: ‘Yes.’ She said: He poked me in the chest with a poke that hurt me, then said: ‘Did you think that God and His Messenger would treat you unjustly?’ I said: ‘Whatever people conceal, God knows it. Yes.’ He said: ‘Gabriel came to me when you saw, and he would not enter upon you while you had taken off your clothes. He called me but concealed it from you. I answered him and concealed it from you, thinking you had fallen asleep. I disliked waking you and feared you would feel lonely. He ordered me to go to the people of al-Baqīʿ and ask forgiveness for them.’” She said: I said: “How should I say?” He said: “Say: Peace be upon the inhabitants of these dwellings, among the believers and the Muslims. May God have mercy on those of us who have gone before and those who come later. And we, if God wills, shall join you.”"],[6919,"Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of God ﷺ, used to visit the grave of Ḥamzah every Friday."],[6920,"One day the Messenger of God ﷺ went out and we went out with him until we reached the graveyard. He ordered us to sit, and we sat. Then he stepped over the graves until he came to one of them and sat beside it. He conversed with it at length. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ raised his voice in loud weeping, and we wept because of his weeping. Then the Prophet ﷺ came forward, and ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb met him and said: “What made you weep, O Messenger of God?” He said: “It made us weep and alarmed us.” He took ʿUmar by the hand, then gestured to us, and we came to him. He said: “Did my weeping alarm you?” We said: “Yes, O Messenger of God.” He said: “The grave you saw me at is the grave of my mother Āminah bt. Wahb. I asked my Lord permission to visit her, and He permitted me. Then I asked Him permission to ask forgiveness for her, but He did not permit me. It was revealed: ‘It is not for the Prophet and those who believe to ask forgiveness for the polytheists’ (Q 9:113), and ‘Abraham’s asking forgiveness for his father’ (Q 9:114). I was overcome by the tenderness a child feels toward a parent, and that made me weep. Indeed, I had forbidden you three things: visiting graves, eating sacrificial meat beyond three days, and nabīdh in certain vessels. Now visit graves, for they make one renounce this world and remind of the Hereafter. Eat sacrificial meat and spend from it as you wish. I only forbade you when the good was scarce, to give people more room. Indeed, no vessel makes anything unlawful; every intoxicant is unlawful.”"],[6921,"Āminah bt. Wahb, the mother of the Prophet ﷺ, was buried in the mountain pass of Abū Dubb."],[6922,"The Prophet ﷺ used to come to the graves of the martyrs at the turn of the year and say: \"Peace be upon you for what you patiently endured, and excellent is the final home.\" He said: Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman also used to do that."],[6923,"Fatimah bt. the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to come to the grave of Hamzah, and she had placed a marker on it so that she might recognize it."],[6925,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a graveyard—or he said: by al-Baqiʿ—then he said: \"Peace be upon the dwellers of these abodes, those among them who are Muslims; the abode of a people who have died, and we are in their footsteps—or he said: in your footsteps—soon to join them.\""],[6926,"The Prophet ﷺ used to go out with groups of his companions to the burial ground of Baqiʿ al-Gharqad and say: \"Peace be upon you, O people of the graves. If you only knew from what Allah has saved you and what is to come after you.\" Then he would turn to his companions—among whom that day were the most virtuous—and say: \"Are you better or are these?\" They would say: \"We hope they are not better than us; we migrated as they migrated and we strove as they strove.\" He would say: \"Rather, they are better than you. They have passed on and have not eaten anything of their rewards, while you are eating of your rewards. These have passed on, and I have testified for them, and I do not know what you will bring about after me.\""],[6929,"Abu Hurayrah and a companion of his passed by a grave. Abu Hurayrah said: \"Greet it.\" The man said: \"Should I greet a grave?\" Abu Hurayrah said: \"If he ever saw you in this world, even for a single day, he would surely recognize you now.\""],[6932,"He heard Ibn al-Musayyib, and he saw people greeting the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"No prophet remained on earth for more than forty days.\""],[6933,"He saw a group of people at the grave and forbade them, and said: The Prophet ﷺ said: Do not take my house as a place of festival, and do not make your houses graves, and send prayers upon me wherever you are, for your prayers reach me."],[6934,"I passed by Musa on the night I was taken on the Night Journey, and he was standing, praying in his grave."],[6935,"Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas fell ill during the Prophet's ﷺ absence from Makkah, when the Prophet ﷺ had gone to al-Ta'if. When he returned, the Prophet ﷺ said to 'Amr al-Qari: \"If he dies, then here,\" and he pointed to the road to Madinah."],[6936,"The Prophet ﷺ left a man in charge of Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas while he was in Makkah, and said: \"If he dies, do not bury him until you have taken him out of it.\""],[6937,"We visited Abu Waqid al-Bakri during the illness in which he died. He died and was buried in the graves of the Muhajirun. He said: \"And some of the Ansar, companions of the Prophet ﷺ, died and were buried there, in the graves of the Muhajirun.\" He said: \"And I followed some of them.\" It has reached me that these are the graves below Fakhkh. I kept hearing, while I was a boy, that they are the graves of the Muhajirun."],[6938,"Whoever dies and is buried in that cemetery will be raised secure on the Day of Resurrection. He said: \"Before that I used to hear that whoever dies in the Haram, that is for him.\""],[6939,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded al-Sa'ib b. 'Abd al-Qari, saying: \"If Sa'd dies, do not bury him in Makkah.\""],[6940,"Ibn 'Umar instructed them: \"Do not bury him.\" But 'Abd Allah b. Khalid overcame them until they buried him in the Haram."],[6941,"When the Prophet ﷺ overlooked the cemetery while he was on its first road, he gestured with his hand behind the yellow [mark], and said: \"What an excellent cemetery.\" I said to the one who informed me: \"Did he single out the mountain pass?\" He said: \"Thus we used to hear that the Prophet ﷺ singled out the mountain pass facing the House.\""],[6942,"I would not like to be buried in al-Baqi'. To be buried elsewhere is dearer to me than to be buried in it. One of two men: either a wrongdoer, so I would not like to be with him in his grave, or a righteous man, so I would not like his bones to be displaced."],[6943,"When people are gathered on the Day of Resurrection, I will be raised among the people of al-Baqi'."],[6944,"Charity is not lawful for Muhammad ﷺ nor for the family of Muhammad ﷺ."],[6945,"The Prophet ﷺ said to 'Umar: \"How will you be, O 'Umar, with the two trial-bringers of the grave when they come to you, digging with their fangs and trampling with their hair? Their eyes are like flashing lightning, their voices like crashing thunder, and with them is a sledgehammer; if all the people of Mina gathered to lift it, they could not.\" 'Umar said: \"And I will be as I am today?\" He said: \"And you will be as you are today.\" He said: \"Then I will suffice against them, if Allah wills.\""],[6946,"We went out with Mu'adh b. Jabal to a funeral. He said: \"You will not leave while they are burying him until your companion hears the sound of your sandals. Then the attendant of the grave comes to him from the direction of his head. His tongue says: 'Do not come to him from my direction, for he used to stand with the Book of Allah, Mighty and Exalted, and exert himself, so this is when he rests.' Then he comes to him from the direction of his feet. His feet say: 'Do not come to him from our direction, for he used to walk with us to the prayers.' Then he comes to him from his right side. It says: 'Do not come to him from my direction, for he used to extend his right hand in charity.' Then he comes to him from his left side. His left side says: 'Do not come to him from my direction, for he used to carry weapons upon me' – or he said: 'in me – weapons in the way of Allah.' Then he stands before his face and strikes him, saying: 'What do you say about this man?' Allah will keep him firm. If he was doubtful, he says: 'I do not know; I heard people saying something.' Then he strikes him with a blow that everything present hears except the two heavy species (jinn and humans).\""],[6947,"A man came to Abu al-Darda' and asked him about a verse, but he did not tell him. The man turned away saying: 'Indeed, those who conceal what We have sent down of clear signs and guidance...' Abu al-Darda' said: 'How will you be when you enter your grave and two black, blue-eyed angels are brought out to you, stepping on their hair and digging with their fangs? They will ask about Muhammad ﷺ. What kind of man will you be if you remain steadfast concerning him?' And he mentioned that they have an iron club that if the two weighty ones—or he said the people of Mina—were to gather against it, they could not bear it. 'How will you be when the bridge of Hell is placed? What kind of man will you be if you pass over it or are saved? And how will you be when there is no place on earth except where your foot stands, and no shade except the shade of the Throne of the Most Merciful? What kind of man will you be if you seek shade under it? Go away; by Allah, besides whom there is no deity, this is indeed the truth.'"],[6948,"Regarding 'For him is a life of hardship' (Quran 20:124), he said: 'His grave is made narrow for him until his ribs interlock.'"],[6949,"One day the Prophet ﷺ entered a palm grove of Banu al-Najjar and heard the voices of men from Banu al-Najjar who had died in the pre-Islamic era being punished in their graves. The Prophet ﷺ came out alarmed from the grave and ordered his companions to seek refuge from the punishment of the grave."],[6950,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave."],[6951,"That he heard Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh forbidding the sale of charity before it is given."],[6952,"When a man dies, his seat is shown to him morning and evening. If he is from the people of Paradise, then Paradise; if he is from the people of the Fire, then the Fire. It is said: 'This is your seat until you are resurrected to it on the Day of Resurrection.'"],[6953,"When the collector comes to you, hand over your zakat to him, and do not buy it back from him. And entrust to him from it what he takes charge of.” By Allah, they used to say: “We will leave it for you,” and I would say: “No.” Then they would say: “Buy it,” and we would say: “No; it belongs only to Allah.”"],[6954,"That he heard Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh forbidding the sale of charity before it is given."],[6955,"Whoever loves to meet Allah, Allah loves to meet him; and whoever hates to meet Allah, Allah hates to meet him. We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, all of us hate death.' He said: 'When Allah intends to take the believer, He reveals to him what delights him, so then he loves to meet Allah, and Allah loves to meet him.'"],[6956,"Masruq and I entered upon 'A'ishah and said: 'Ibn Mas'ud said: Whoever loves to meet Allah, Allah loves to meet him; and whoever hates to meet Allah, Allah hates to meet him—and death is before meeting Allah.' She said: 'May Allah have mercy on Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. He told you a hadith but you did not ask him about its end. I will tell you about that: When Allah intends good for His servant, He appoints an angel for him a year before his death, who guides him and makes things easy for him until he dies in the best state possible. When death approaches and he sees his reward from Paradise, he begins to gasp for his soul, wishing it would come out. Then he loves to meet Allah, and Allah loves to meet him. And when Allah intends evil for a servant, He appoints a devil for him a year before his death, who turns him away, leads him astray, and tempts him until he dies in the worst state possible. People say: So-and-so died in the worst state. When death approaches and he sees his punishment from the Fire, he begins to swallow his soul, wishing it would not come out. Then he hates to meet Allah, and Allah hates to meet him.'"],[6958,"There will come after you a people who deny the punishment of the grave, deny the Most Merciful, deny the Dajjal, deny the Pool, and deny a people who will come out of the Fire."],[6959,"A man died, and when he was placed in his grave, the angels came to him and said: 'We will flog you one hundred lashes of Allah's punishment.' He said: So he mentioned his prayer, his fasting, and his striving. He said: So they lightened it for him until it reached ten. Then he asked them until they lightened it for him until it came to one. They said: 'We will flog you one lash, there is no escape from it.' So they flogged him one lash, and his grave blazed with fire in the daytime, and he lost consciousness. When he regained consciousness, he said: 'For what did they flog me this lash?' They said: 'You urinated one day, then you prayed without performing ablution, and you heard a man crying out for help as one wronged, and you did not help him.'"],[6960,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by two graves while he was on a mule, and it shied with him. He said: 'It shied, and it had a right to. The occupants of these two graves are being punished without a great sin or trial. As for this one—meaning one of them—he used not to protect himself from urine. As for this one, he used to eat people's flesh (backbite).' Then he broke a palm branch and planted one on each grave. It was said to him: 'What benefit will this be to them?' He said: 'Perhaps it will be lightened for them as long as they remain moist.'"],[6961,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by two graves and said: 'This is the grave of so-and-so, and this is the grave of so-and-so, and they are being punished without a great sin—yet it is great. As for one of them, he used not to shield himself from his urine, and as for the other, he used to slander people.' Then he took a fresh palm branch, broke it, and placed one on this grave and one on that, and said: 'Perhaps the punishment will be lightened for them as long as the two remain moist—or as long as they remain fresh.'"],[6963,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: \"O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave, and from the trial of life and death, and from the evil of the trial of the false messiah.\""],[6964,"He was with Sulayman and Umar b. Abd al-Aziz in the bathhouse. Sulayman was in the inner room, and I and Umar b. Abd al-Aziz were in the second room with no one else with us. He said: He began asking me about my courage, and I told him. Then Umar said to me: 'O Abu Khalid, I will tell you a hadith. One part of it is a secret, and the other is public. As for the secret: I went down into the grave of al-Walid b. Abd al-Malik when they lowered him into his grave. When we took him from his bier and placed him on our hands, he convulsed in his shrouds, so we placed him in his grave. His son said: \"My father is alive! My father is alive!\" I said: \"Your father is not alive, but they experience this in their graves.\" As for the public: This man has appointed you over Iraq, so fear Allah regarding them, for they have suffered tribulation from al-Hajjaj and suffered from Qutayba b. Muslim.'"],[6965,"Only two men are tested: a believer and a hypocrite. As for the believer, he is tested for seven days, and as for the hypocrite, he is tested for forty mornings. As for the disbeliever, he is not asked about Muhammad and does not know him."],[6966,"Ubayd b. Umayr said, mentioning Munkar and Nakir: 'They come out with fire in their mouths and eyes, wearing haircloth, and the earth trembles with them, until when he is cut off from his intellect and cannot reason anything with his intellect except what Allah puts upon his tongue, they say: \"Who is your Lord?\"'"],[6968,"The earth consumes the son of Adam entirely except the coccyx bone, and from it he is composed—or he said: connected. He said: And he said: 'The earth will be rained upon with a rain that grows the bodies of people until they become bodies without souls, then the soul will be breathed into them.'"],[6969,"That is Munkar and Nakir. They come out with fire in their mouths and eyes, wearing haircloth, and the earth trembles with them, until when he is cut off from his intellect and cannot reason anything with his intellect except what Allah puts upon his tongue, they say: 'Who is your Lord?' He says: 'Allah.' 'What is your religion?' He says: 'Islam.' 'Who is your Prophet?' He says: 'Muhammad ﷺ.' They say: 'How do you know? Have you seen him?' He says: 'No, but the Book of Allah came with that, so I believed in it and affirmed it.' They say: 'You have spoken truthfully. By Allah, upon that you lived, upon that you died, and upon that you will be resurrected, if Allah wills. Look, may Allah have mercy on you, at what Allah has turned away from you of the Fire, and look at your seat in Paradise.' Then his shroud is replaced with garments from the garments of Paradise, and his grave is widened for him as far as his sight reaches, and an opening is made between him and Paradise from which its breeze, fragrance, coolness, and scent enter upon him. As for the hypocrite, he is struck with an iron club one blow, and he sits up. They say to him: 'Who is your Lord?' He says: 'Allah.' 'What is your religion?' He says: 'Islam.' 'Who is your Prophet?' He says: 'Muhammad.' They say: 'Why do you say that? Have you seen him?' He says: 'No, by Allah, I do not know.'"],[6970,"The visitor of the sick is in the harvest of Paradise until he returns."],[6971,"Visit the sick, and follow funeral processions, for they remind of the Hereafter."],[6972,"Answer the inviter, visit the sick, and free the captive."],[6973,"Anas b. Malik said to him: “O Abu Abd Allah, we used to relate that the visitor of a sick person plunges into mercy. If he asks about the sick person while standing, mercy bridles him; and if he sits, it immerses him.”"],[6974,"Anas b. Malik said to him: “O Abu Abd Allah, we used to relate that the visitor of a sick person plunges into mercy. If he asks about the sick person while standing, mercy bridles him; and if he sits, it immerses him.”"],[6975,"Whoever visits a sick person, follows a funeral procession, and is enabled to fast that day, reaches evening with Paradise having become incumbent for him."],[6976,"A man said to the Prophet ﷺ: “How are you, O Messenger of God?” He said: “Better than a man who has not fasted today and has not visited a sick person.” The man said: “And what is visiting the sick, O Messenger of God?” He said: “Like fasting.”"],[6977,"Ali entered upon his son al-Hasan while al-Ashari was with him. He said: “What has brought you, old man?” He said: “I heard of the illness of my nephew’s son and wished to visit him.” He said: “Indeed, what is in our hearts does not prevent us from telling you what we have heard: whoever visits a sick person by day, seventy thousand angels pray for him until evening; and if he visits him at night, seventy thousand angels pray for him until morning.”"],[6978,"Wealth accumulated with me. He said: I went to Ibn Umar, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas. I came to each man of them alone and said: Wealth has accumulated with me, and these people put it where you see, and I have found another place for it. What do you think? All of them said: \"Hand it over to them.\""],[6979,"Amr b. Hurayth visited Husayn b. Ali and met Ali. Ali said to Amr: “Have you visited Husayn?” He said: “Yes.” He said: “Despite what is in the heart?” He said: “You, O Abu al-Hasan, cannot remove what is in the heart.” He said: “That does not prevent me from giving you sincere advice: whichever man visits a sick person, seventy thousand angels are appointed over him who pray for him until the like of it on the morrow; and if he sits, he sits in the meadows of Paradise and in the mercy of God.”"],[6980,"What the family of a sick person meets from the visitation of foolish reciters is harder than what they meet from their sick person."],[6981,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to sweat during the illness in which he died."],[6982,"He was with a brother of his while he was driving camels, and his forehead began to bead with sweat. Alqamah laughed, so Yazid b. Aws said to him: “What makes you laugh, O Abu Shibl?” He said: “I heard Abd Allah b. Masud say: The soul of the believer comes out as sweat, and the soul of the disbeliever comes out from the side of his mouth as the soul of a donkey comes out. The believer is made to suffer at his death for a bad deed he had done, so that it may be requited thereby; and the disbeliever is made to have it easy at his death for a good deed he had done, so that it may be requited thereby.”"],[6983,"Charity is not lawful for Muhammad ﷺ nor for the family of Muhammad ﷺ."],[6984,"Abu Bakr came to the house in which the Messenger of God ﷺ died, which was in Aisha’s dwelling. He uncovered from his face a striped cloak with which he was covered. He looked at the face of the Prophet ﷺ, then leaned over him and kissed him, then said: “By God, God will not combine two deaths upon you. You have died the death after which there is no death.”"],[6985,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered upon Uthman b. Mazun when he was dead, leaned over him and kissed him, then wept until I saw the tears flowing on his cheeks."],[6986,"Sudden death is a mercy for the believer and a regret for the disbeliever."],[6987,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ while he was dividing dates from charity, and al-Hasan b. Ali was in his lap. When he finished, the Prophet ﷺ carried him on his shoulder, and his saliva flowed onto the Prophet's cheek ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ raised his head to him, and there was a date in his mouth. The Prophet ﷺ put his hand in and extracted it from him, then said to him: \"Do you not know that charity is not lawful for the family of Muhammad ﷺ?\""],[6989,"Ḥudhayfah used to regard sudden death as severe, a seizure in displeasure."],[6992,"Sudden death is a mercy for the believer and a grievous seizure for the disbeliever."],[6993,"The Prophet ﷺ died when he was sixty-three years old, receiving the Qurʾān for ten of those years in Makkah and ten in Madinah."],[6995,"Revelation came down upon the Prophet ﷺ when he was forty. He stayed in Makkah for thirteen years and in Madinah for ten, and he died at sixty-three."],[6996,"Mīkāʾīl was entrusted with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he was forty, for three years, teaching him the means of prophethood. When he became forty-three, Jibrīl was entrusted with him, and he brought down the Qurʾān upon him in Makkah for ten years and in Madinah for ten years. Then he died at sixty-three.\" ʿUmar also died at sixty-three."],[6997,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was neither tall nor short, neither curly-haired nor straight-haired, neither dark brown nor white. Revelation came down upon him when he was forty. He stayed in Makkah for ten years and in Madinah for ten years, and he was taken at the age of sixty. He did not have twenty white hairs on his head or in his beard."],[6998,"I asked ʿUrwah b. al-Zubayr: How long did the Prophet ﷺ stay in Makkah? He said, \"Ten years.\" I said, \"But Ibn ʿAbbās said, 'a few more than ten.'\" He said, \"He lied; he only took it from the poet's saying.\" ʿAmr b. Dīnār said: I disliked ʿUrwah when he called him a liar."],[6999,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ while he was dividing dates from charity, and al-Hasan b. Ali was in his lap. When he finished, the Prophet ﷺ carried him on his shoulder, and his saliva flowed onto the Prophet's cheek ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ raised his head to him, and there was a date in his mouth. The Prophet ﷺ put his hand in and extracted it from him, then said to him: \"Do you not know that charity is not lawful for the family of Muhammad ﷺ?\""],[7001,"The Prophet ﷺ died at the age of sixty-five, and Abu Bakr was the same, and Umar b. al-Khattab was fifty-six, and Uthman was sixty-one."],[7002,"The Prophet ﷺ died at the age of sixty-three. Ibn Shihab said: Aisha said: Abu Bakr died at sixty-three. Ibn Shihab said: Umar died at fifty-five."],[7003,"Regarding the charity on honey, he said: “For every ten afrāq, one faraq.”"],[7004,"No sadaqah is due from the slave on his property."],[7005,"No sadaqah is due from the slave on his property."],[7006,"Some people came to my father and complained about Uthman's collectors. My father said: Take this document and go to Uthman b. Affan and say to him: My father said: Some people have come and complained about your collectors. This is the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding the obligatory sadaqat, so let them follow it. I departed with the document and entered upon Uthman. I said to him: My father sent me to you, and he mentioned that some people complained about your collectors. This is the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding the obligatory sadaqat, so order them to follow it. He said: We have no need of your document. He said: I returned to my father and informed him. My father said: It does not matter; return the document to where you took it from. He said: If he had remembered anything about Uthman, he would have mentioned it—meaning something bad. He said: The document contained only what is in the hadith of Ali."],[7007,"Whoever acquires wealth, no zakat is due on it until a year has passed over it."],[7008,"Zakat is due on jewelry of gold and silver."],[7009,"Whatever exceeds two hundred [dirhams], then according to that calculation."],[7010,"‘Abd Allāh b. ‘Umar used to hold the wealth of orphans, and he would borrow their wealth to safeguard it from loss, then he would pay its zakāh from their wealth every year."],[7011,"If you pay the zakat of your wealth, it is not a treasure, even if it is buried. If you do not pay it, it is a treasure, even if it is visible."],[7012,"What has had its zakat paid is not a treasure, even if it is under seven layers of earth; and what is visible but its zakat is not paid is a treasure."],[7013,"Abū Bakr b. ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUbayd Allāh b. ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wrote to him a letter about charity, copying it for him – Abū Bakr claimed – from a page he found tied to the scabbard of ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb: For twenty-four camels and below, in every five camels one sheep [is due]. Above that up to thirty-five, a bint makhāḍ [is due]. If a bint makhāḍ is not available, then a male ibn labūn. And above that…"],[7014,"There is no charity on less than five camels. In five camels, one sheep [is due]; in ten, two sheep; in fifteen, three sheep; in twenty, four sheep; in twenty-five, a bint makhāḍ up to thirty-five. If a bint makhāḍ is not available, then a male ibn labūn. If it increases by one, then a bint labūn [is due] up to forty-five. If it increases by one, then a ḥiqqah, one that the stallion mounts, up to sixty. If it increases by one, then a jadhaʿah up to seventy-five. If it increases by one, then two bint labūn [are due] up to ninety. If it increases by one, then two ḥiqqahs, ones that the stallion mounts, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases further, then begin the obligatory dues anew: when it reaches fifty, then in every fifty a ḥiqqah [is due]. Sufyān said: The explanation of our ḥadīth from Ibrāhīm: when it exceeds one hundred and twenty, then in every five one sheep [is due], in every ten two sheep, in fifteen three sheep, in every twenty four sheep. When it reaches one hundred and forty, then two ḥiqqahs and four sheep [are due]. When it reaches one hundred and forty-five, then two ḥiqqahs and a bint makhāḍ [are due] – meaning until it reaches fifty – then three ḥiqqahs [are due]. If it increases further, you begin the obligatory dues anew as you began at the start."],[7015,"Separate [livestock] should not be combined, nor combined [livestock] separated."],[7016,"The jadhaʿ – meaning the one that is weaned from its mother – is not taken for charity."],[7017,"They used not to ask except about the one in need."],[7019,"If a man has sheep and goats, and only one sheep is due from them, the collector takes from the larger of the two groups."],[7020,"Sufyān b. ʿAbd Allāh, while collecting charity in the rural districts of al-Ṭāʾif, the livestock owners complained to him about counting the newborn lambs. They said: \"If you count the newborns, then take charity from them.\" Sufyān did not concede anything to them until he met ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb. He said: \"The livestock owners complain to me that I count the newborns, and they say: If you count them, then take charity from them.\" He said: \"Tell them: We count all the newborns, even the lamb that the shepherd brings carrying it on his hand.\" He said: And he said: \"I do not take from them the fattened animal, nor the ram of the flock, nor the nursing ewe, nor the pregnant one; but I take the young she-goat, the jadhaʿah, and the thaniyyah. That is a just balance between the newborns and the best of the wealth. And tell them: We count all the newborns, even the lamb.\""],[7021,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wrote to them: Give from the charity to one whom the year has left with sheep and their shepherd, and do not give from it to one whom the year has left with two sheep and two shepherds."],[7022,"The sheep are divided into two halves: the owner of the sheep chooses one of them, and the collector chooses from the other group."],[7023,"It is divided into three groups: the owner of the sheep chooses the best of them, and the collector takes from the middle."],[7024,"Umar said regarding the zakah of sheep: \"Its owner selects them — meaning he chooses them — sheep by sheep until a third of them is set apart, then he divides the flock into two halves, and the collector chooses from one of the two.\""],[7025,"Umar b. al-Khattab met Sa'd and said: \"Where do you intend to go?\" He replied: \"I am going on a military expedition.\" Umar said to him: \"Return to your companion, meaning Ya'la b. Umayya, for a rightful deed is a good jihad. When you collect zakah from livestock, do not forget the good deed, and do not make its owner forget it. Then divide it into three parts, and the owner of the sheep chooses one third, then you choose from the remaining two thirds.\" Sa'd said: \"We would go out to collect zakah and return with nothing but our whips.\""],[7026,"The Prophet ﷺ sent a zakah collector and said: \"Take the old camel, the old she-camel, and the one-eyed.\" He (the narrator) said: \"I do not think he said anything but: 'Then the obligatory shares came later.'\""],[7027,"Muhammad b. Yusuf appointed Tawus over the district of Hakam to collect zakah from their wealth. He said: He collected it and did not return with a single dirham. I said to him: \"How did you use to do it, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman?\" He said: \"We would stand by a man among his family and wealth and say: 'Give charity, may God have mercy on you, from what God has given you.' If he brought out to us what we considered to be the due amount, we accepted it. Otherwise we said to him: 'Make amends, may God have mercy on you.' If he did, [we accepted]; if not, we accepted from him what he gave. Then we looked for the neediest household and gave it to them.\" I said to him: \"What if a man brought his zakah to you and stood with it before you, then took it back?\" He said: \"In that case we would not make him return it.\""],[7028,"He came to Umar, who had appointed him over al-Ta'if, and said to him: \"O Commander of the Faithful, the livestock owners claim that we count the young ones against them but do not take them.\" He said: \"Count them against them and do not take them, even the suckling lamb that the shepherd brings back in his arms. And tell them: 'We leave the nursing ewe, the ram, the pregnant one, and the meat sheep. Take from the young she-goats, and that is the middle ground between us and you: the nursing ewe is the one whose young walks with her, and the pregnant one is the one with young in her belly.'\" He said: Then Safwan b. Umayya sent him a large bowl of meat carried by a group of men, and it was placed before Umar, and that was in the Sacred Mosque. He said: Then the people who carried it withdrew, and Umar said to them: \"Come near. May God fight a people who disdain these!\" A man said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, they do not disdain them, but they prefer themselves over them.\" He said: \"That was lighter in his view.\" He said: Then Abu Mahdhura gave the call to prayer, and Umar said: \"Were you not afraid that your little cloak would split?\" He said: \"I wanted you to hear me, O Commander of the Faithful.\" Umar said: \"Your land, O people of Tihama, is hot, so wait until it cools, then wait again, then give the call to prayer, then say the iqama, then I will come to you.\" Then he entered the house of Safwan b. Umayya, and they had covered it with painted leather. Umar said: \"If you had put rough cloth in place of this, it would have held dust better than this.\""],[7029,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz took a fifth of ambergris."],[7030,"He had come to the Prophet ﷺ and he named him Bashir. He said: We came to him and said: \"The zakah collectors transgress against us. Should we conceal from them the amount they take in excess?\" He said: \"No, but gather it, and when they take it, tell them to pray for you.\" Then he recited: \"And pray for them; indeed your prayer is a comfort for them\" (Q 9:103). He said: We said: \"We have neighbors from Banu Tamim. No sheep of ours strays but they take it, and some of their wealth is hidden from us. Should we take it?\" He said: \"No.\""],[7031,"It has reached us that zakah on livestock is from the middle third of the wealth. He said: \"If it is camels, the obligatory shares that are selected for zakah are taken out: the owner of the livestock selects one share and the collector selects one share until the collector fulfills his due. If it is cattle, a cow is taken from the middle of the wealth, a musinnah or a thaniyyah or older. If it is sheep, the flock is divided into three thirds: the owner of the wealth chooses one third, and the collector chooses his due from the third that follows it.\""],[7032,"I lived to see and was told that whatever the owner of wealth brought out, they accepted it from all livestock, provided he did not bring out a young one, a male, one with a defect, or a decrepit one."],[7033,"I asked Abu Hurayrah: \"In which part of wealth is zakah?\" He said: \"In the middle third. When the collector comes to you, bring out for him the middle third: the jadha'ah and the thaniyyah.\" He said: \"If he takes it, it is his right; if he refuses, do not prevent him or insult him, but feed him from your food and speak to him kindly.\""],[7034,"I was told from one of the Ansar that Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to one of his governors a letter instructing him: Take zakah from the Muslims as a purification for their deeds and a purification for their wealth, and as one of God's ordinances. Exceeding it is injustice and wrongdoing to the Muslims, and falling short of it is compromising the truth and betraying the trust. Call the people with their wealth to the gentlest gathering places and the closest to their best interests. Do not keep the first of them waiting for the last, for crowding is hard on livestock and it has hardships. Do not drive them in a way that makes their watering place far from pasture. When a man presents his flock to you, do not take the best of his sheep, and do not take from the lowest of them. Take zakah from the middle of them. Do not take from a man if you do not find in his camels the age that is due from him except that same age from the best of his camels or an equivalent value. And look at the milk-producing and pregnant ones among those from which zakah is due and avoid them for the benefit of the Muslims, for they are the wealth of their present, the provision of their travelers — or their raiders — and the reserve of their time. Then distribute to the poor, beginning with the weak among the destitute, the orphans, the widows, and the elderly. Whoever of the poor comes together to you as a household who take turns and help one another, distribute to them camels that they may take turns carrying them. If it is sheep, give them as a grant. Whoever is alone, do not reduce every five of them from a share or ten of anything up to fifteen sheep."],[7035,"When the collector comes to you, say: \"This is my wealth, and this is my zakah.\" If he is satisfied, [fine]; if not, turn your face away from him and leave him alone, whatever he does, and do not curse him."],[7036,"No sadaqah is due from the slave on his property."],[7037,"Whoever acquires wealth, he pays zakat on it along with his [existing] wealth."],[7038,"The Prophet ﷺ urged the people regarding charity, and he was approached. It was said: O Messenger of God, this is Abū Jahm b. Ḥudhayfah, Khālid b. al-Walīd, and ʿAbbās, the paternal uncle of the Messenger of God ﷺ; they have withheld charity. He said: Ibn Jamīl finds fault with us only because he was poor, and God and His Messenger enriched him. As for Khālid b. al-Walīd, he has withheld his coats of mail and his war equipment in the way of God. As for ʿAbbās, the paternal uncle of the Messenger of God ﷺ, it is due from him, and the like of it along with it."],[7039,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Are there charity dues on working camels and ploughing oxen? He said: No. And ʿAmr b. Dīnār said to me: We heard that. And ʿAbd al-Karīm said: That is what we say: no charity is due on working camels or ploughing oxen, and he did not trace it back to anyone."],[7040,"I asked Jabir b. 'Abd Allah about jewelry: Is there zakat on it? He said: No. I said: Even if it is a thousand dinars? He said: A thousand is a lot."],[7041,"O 'Ali, I have exempted the zakat on horses and slaves. As for camels, cattle, and sheep, it is not exempted, but bring one quarter of the tithe: from every two hundred dirhams five dirhams, and from every twenty dinars half a dinar. Nothing is due on two hundred dirhams until a year passes over them. When a year has passed, five dirhams are due. For whatever exceeds, then one dirham for every forty dirhams."],[7042,"No charity is due on working cattle."],[7043,"There is no zakat on debt."],[7045,"Umar used to pay zakat on the orphan's wealth."],[7046,"No sadaqah is due from the slave on his property."],[7047,"Wealth used to come to him, and he would not pay zakah on it until a year had passed over it, even if he spent all of it. He used to spend it on what was right and on need, and he used to say: 'There is no charity on wealth until a year has passed over it. When a year has passed over it, then on every two hundred dirhams five dirhams are due, and whatever is in excess is calculated accordingly.'"],[7048,"Zakat is due on jewelry every year."],[7049,"If a man has forty sheep in a town that he milks, there is no zakah on them—meaning domesticated ones."],[7050,"When two partners work their wealth, their wealth is not combined for charity. I informed Ata of the statement of Tawus concerning that, and he said, “I consider it to be true.”"],[7052,"Zakat is due on jewelry every year."],[7053,"The Prophet ﷺ sent him to Yemen and commanded him to take from every thirty cattle a tabi (one-year-old male or female), and from every forty a musinnah (two-year-old), and from every adult one dinar or its equivalent in maafir cloth."],[7054,"Concerning cattle: for thirty a tabi or tabiah, and for forty a musinnah."],[7055,"I will not take anything from the waqs (intermediate numbers) of cattle until I go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. So he went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he commanded something concerning them."],[7056,"Muadh b. Jabal remained in al-Janad from when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Yemen until he died, and Abu Bakr, then he came to Umar and he sent him back to what he had been upon."],[7057,"For thirty cattle a tabi jadha (one-year-old), and for forty cattle a cow. He said, “I did not hear from him anything beyond that.”"],[7058,"The agents of Ibn al-Zubayr and Ibn Awf and his agents used to take from every fifty cattle one cow, and from eighty two cows, then if they increased, for every fifty one cow.” I said, “Which cow?” He said, “Thus.”"],[7059,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Uthman b. Muhammad b. Abi Suwayd: “Take from every thirty cattle a tabi, and from every forty cattle a cow, not exceeding that.” He said: So Uthman commanded his agents to take that, and if the cattle increased beyond that, then from every thirty cattle a tabi, and for every forty a musinnah."],[7062,"There is nothing due on fewer than thirty cattle. When they reach thirty, a one-year-old calf, male or female (tabīʿ or tabīʿah), is due until they reach forty. When they reach forty, a two-year-old cow (musinnah) is due. For cattle beyond that, on every thirty a one-year-old calf, and on every forty a two-year-old cow."],[7063,"On thirty: a one-year-old female calf (tabīʿah); on every forty: a two-year-old cow (musinnah). There is nothing due between forty and sixty. On sixty: two one-year-old calves (tabīʿatān or tabīʿān). On seventy: a two-year-old cow and a one-year-old calf. On eighty: two two-year-old cows. On ninety: three one-year-old calves. On one hundred: two one-year-old calves and a two-year-old cow. On one hundred and ten: two two-year-old cows and a one-year-old calf. On one hundred and twenty: three two-year-old cows. Their young and old are counted, and water buffalo are counted with cattle. As for cattle held for trade, they are valued; nothing is taken according to this calculation, but they are only valued, and when they reach two hundred dirhams, zakah is due on them."],[7064,"Zakat is due on jewelry every year."],[7065,"I used to hear for a time that they used to say: 'Take from us what the Prophet ﷺ took.' I was surprised that they did not accept that from them, until al-Zuhrī told me that the Prophet ﷺ wrote a document containing these obligations, and the Prophet ﷺ passed away before writing to the governors. Abu Bakr followed it and enforced it after him according to what he had written. I do not think he mentioned anything but cattle as well."],[7066,"Zakat is due on jewelry every year."],[7067,"Simāk b. al-Faḍl gave me a letter from the Prophet ﷺ to Mālik b. Kafalānis and the Muṣʿabiyyīn. I read it, and in it: \"On what the sky and the rivers water: one-tenth; and on what is watered by the masnā (irrigation channel): half of one-tenth.\""],[7068,"He took from cattle: from thirty a one-year-old calf, and from forty a two-year-old cow. They asked him about what is less than thirty, and he said: 'I did not hear anything from the Prophet ﷺ about it, and he did not command me anything regarding it.'"],[7069,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Uthman b. Muhammad b. Abi Suwayd: “Take from every thirty cattle a tabi, and from every forty cattle a cow, not exceeding that.” He said: So Uthman commanded his agents to take that, and if the cattle increased beyond that, then from every thirty cattle a tabi, and for every forty a musinnah."],[7070,"Whoever has camels and does not pay their due—or he said: their zakah—will be made to lie on his back for them on the Day of Resurrection on a wide, level plain, on a day whose length is fifty thousand years. They will trample him with their hoofs and bite him with their mouths, their first passing over their last, until judgment is passed among the people. Then he will see his way. Whoever has sheep and does not pay their due will be made to lie on his back for them on the Day of Resurrection on a wide, level plain, on a day whose length is fifty thousand years. They will trample him with their cloven hoofs and gore him with their horns, their first passing over their last, until judgment is passed among the people. Then he will see his way. Whoever has gold or silver and does not pay what is due on it, it will be made into plates of fire on the Day of Resurrection, and they will be placed on his side, his back, and his forehead, until judgment is passed among the people. Then he will see his way."],[7071,"There is no charity for the slave without his master's permission."],[7072,"Excellent camels are thirty camels: their zakat is given, their sturdy mount is ridden, their fat one is slaughtered, and their abundant milker is given in grant.\" He said: \"And has it reached you concerning that — and the milking on the day they come to water among camels?\" He said: \"No, that is enough.\" And he said: \"If there is no surplus in the camels beyond what their owners need, then you should not milk them on the day they come to water."],[7074,"Whoever has camels and does not give God's due in them, they will come on the Day of Resurrection as vicious as they ever were, trampling him with their padded feet. It was asked: \"What is their due?\" He said: \"Then he mentioned four\" — Abd Allah said: \"I do not know with which of them he began\" — he said: \"They are milked at the resting place by the water, their young are carried, their fat ones are slaughtered, and their milkers are given in grant.\""],[7075,"Whoever has wealth and does not pay its due, it will be made for him into a bald-headed serpent with two protrusions in its mouth that will pursue him until he places his hand in its mouth, and it will keep biting it until judgment is passed among the servants."],[7076,"There is no zakat on jewelry."],[7077,"Give glad tidings to the hoarders of treasure of a branding on the foreheads, the sides, and the backs."],[7078,"He owes no zakat."],[7080,"For sheep there is a due like that which is in camels."],[7081,"A man from Banu Nahd said: \"O Messenger of God, I possess much wealth.\" He said: \"How much is your wealth?\" He said: \"The valley where I settle cannot contain it.\" He said: \"How are you with the grant of milk animals?\" He said: \"A hundred every year.\" He said: \"How are you with the covering of camels by a stallion?\" He said: \"The camels go out in the morning and the people go out, and whoever wishes to take a male camel takes one.\" He said: \"How are you with hospitality?\" He said: \"By God, O Messenger of God, I adhere to the old she-camel, the decrepit one, the aged one, and the one with little milk.\" He said: \"Is your wealth dearer to you or the wealth of your heirs?\" He said: \"Rather, my wealth.\" He said: \"Then you only have from your wealth what you eat and consume, or wear and wear out, or spend and send forth; and what remains belongs to your heirs.\""],[7082,"Excellent wealth is thirty camels: the abundant milker is given in grant, the fat one is slaughtered, the stallion is made available for covering, and the riding camel is lent for riding. Thirty are better than forty. Woe to the owners of two hundred, for how many of their dues they do not pay!"],[7083,"Whoever has camels and does not pay their due, they will come on the Day of Resurrection as vicious as they ever were, trampling him with their padded feet. It was said: \"What is their due?\" He said: \"You grant the people their milk, you lend the riding camel, you milk them at the resting place by the water, and you slaughter the fat one — I think he said — and the stallion is made available for covering.\""],[7084,"He was asked about donkeys: is there zakat on them? He said: \"No, even if they reach such-and-such, a great number, two hundred or three hundred.\" Sufyan said: \"But we say: unless they are for trade.\""],[7085,"The Muslims used to prefer, when one of them acquired wealth, to pay its zakāh, and when a year passed over his wealth, to pay zakāh on it along with what had not yet had a year pass over it."],[7087,"I do not think charity is due except on cash."],[7088,"I said to al-Zuhrī: A man pays zakāh on livestock, keeps it for eleven months, then sells it. He said: \"The charity is upon the buyer.\""],[7089,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"Indeed, charities are for the poor,\" and I recited the verse to him. I said: \"Are all charities for them?\" He said: \"Yes, if you place your zakat in one category, or two, or three. If it were abundant, I would command him to distribute it among all of them.\""],[7090,"The collector came to him when his livestock had reached thirty-nine sheep, counting them one by one, until when he passed by, one of them gave birth, and the collector had turned away. He said: They say: \"There is no charity on it.\""],[7091,"There is no charity upon a Muslim for his slave or his horse."],[7092,"O ʿAlī, did you not know that I have waived the charity on horses and slaves?"],[7093,"I have excused you from the charity on horses."],[7094,"For the nose, the full diyah; for the glans, the full diyah; for the tongue, the full diyah; for the hand, half the diyah; for the foot, half the diyah; for the tooth, five camels; for the revealing wound, five camels; for the bone-displacing wound, fifteen camels; for the head wound, a third of the diyah; for the penetrating wound, a third of the diyah; for each finger, ten camels. For five camels, one sheep; for every ten, two sheep; for fifteen, three sheep; for every twenty, four sheep; for twenty-five, five sheep; for twenty-six, a bint makhad; if there is no bint makhad, then a male ibn labun, until thirty-five. If it increases by one, then a bint labun, until forty-five. If it increases by one, then a hiqqah mounted by the stallion, or he said: the camel, until sixty. If it increases by one, then a jadh'ah, until seventy-five. If it increases by one, then two bint labun, until ninety. If it increases by one, then two hiqqahs mounted by the stallion, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then for every fifty, a hiqqah, and for every forty, a bint labun. For cattle: for every thirty cattle, a tabi' that is one year old; for every forty cattle, a musinnah. For sheep: for every forty sheep, one sheep; nothing below forty, until one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then two sheep, up to two hundred. If it increases by one, then three sheep, up to three hundred. If it increases, then for every hundred, one sheep. An old one or one with a defect is not taken, unless the collector wishes. Separate herds are not combined, and combined herds are not separated. For what the sky and wells water, one-tenth; for what is watered by rope, half of one-tenth. For silver, when a year passes, for every two hundred dirhams, five dirhams; nothing below two hundred dirhams, and if it increases, then by that reckoning. I have exempted the zakat of horses and slaves."],[7095,"There is no zakat on cotton or wars (a yellow dye plant)."],[7097,"Al-Hajjaj sent Musa b. Mughirah over the Sawad, and he wanted to take from the green produce of the Sawad. Musa b. Talhah said: “I have the document of Muadh b. Jabal, from the Messenger of God ﷺ: He commanded him to take from wheat, barley, raisins, and dates.” He said: I mentioned that to al-Hajjaj, and he said, “He spoke the truth.”"],[7098,"No zakat is due on pasturing horses."],[7099,"I said: Do you think that if my palms were estimated and then I sold them after the estimation to some people, and I established proof that it was less than what was estimated, do you think they should return the surplus to me? He said: \"If the estimation was according to the practice of the Prophet ﷺ, then it is not for them to return the surplus to you.\" I said: They estimated my palms, and when I gathered up my dates, they were more than their estimation; should I pay the surplus to them? He said: \"No, if it was according to the practice of the Prophet ﷺ.\" I said: Do you think that if I sold the fruit of my property before the estimator came, do they have the right to annul my sale and then estimate? He said: \"Yes, they estimate it if the estimation is according to the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; that is more correct. Otherwise, you have sold to them and to yourself. If someone sold fruit for gold, then the zakāh is on the fruit as it actually is, and not on the gold.\""],[7100,"There is no zakat on any of the riding animals unless they are for trade, except sheep, camels, and cattle.”"],[7101,"The people of Syria came to ‘Umar and said: Our wealth consists only of horses and slaves, so take charity from us. He said: “I do not want to take something that was not taken before me.” Then he consulted the people. ‘Alī said: If they willingly give it, it is good, provided it does not become a jizya taken from them after you. So ‘Umar took from horses ten dirhams and from slaves ten dirhams, ten dirhams every year. And he provisioned the horses: every horse ten ajriba every month, and he provisioned the slaves two jarībs, two jarībs every month.” Ma‘mar said: I heard someone other than Abū Isḥāq say: “When Mu‘āwiya came, he calculated that, and found that what he gave them was more than what he took from them, so he left them alone and did not take from them or give to them.”"],[7103,"On it, one-tenth."],[7104,"He used to dislike selling the zakat of animals before it was taken in hand, but he saw no harm in that regarding food.”"],[7105,"As for selling food, there is no harm. As for livestock, it is disliked, though it is not riba."],[7106,"Do not buy back your charity until it is taken from you."],[7107,"He said to ‘Uthmān b. Muḥammad b. Abī Suwayd: I do not think it is lawful for you to sell the zakat until you have taken it in hand. ‘Uthmān said to Ṭāwūs: This Ibrāhīm claims that it is not lawful for us to sell the zakat until it is taken in hand. Ṭāwūs said: “By the Lord of this House, and he was in its shade, it is not lawful for you to sell it before it is taken in hand, nor after it is taken in hand. You were not obligated to do that. If you must, then take it in hand and mark it.” [or: and brand them]"],[7108,"Those who passed away used to dislike buying back their charities. He said: “If you do it after it is taken from you, there is no harm, though I prefer that you do not do it.”"],[7109,"I said to Ibn Ṭāwūs: May I sell the zakat before it is taken in hand? He said: “No.” I said: Do you give the buyer the option? He said: “We have heard that it should not be bought until it is taken in hand.”"],[7110,"On it, one-tenth."],[7111,"I asked Ibn ʿUmar, saying: “Suppose a camel due as zakat falls to the collector and I tie it up; may I buy it?” He said: “No, may Allah not bless it. Do not buy the purification of your wealth.”"],[7112,"On it, one-tenth."],[7113,"He forbade that charity be purchased until it has been hobbled and branded."],[7114,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of charities until they are taken possession of."],[7115,"On it, one-tenth."],[7116,"When the collector takes from the camels an age above the due age, he returns to them ten dirhams or two sheep; and when he takes an age below the due age, they return to him ten dirhams; and when he takes, in place of a bint labūn, an ibn labūn, then ten dirhams or two sheep."],[7117,"When the collector finds an age above the due age or below it, they return to him, in place of the difference between them, twenty dirhams or two sheep.” Al-Thawrī said: “This applies only to camels. But if they are for trade, they are valued in dirhams.”"],[7118,"If the age below it is not found, the age above it is taken, and the livestock owner is given two sheep or ten dirhams."],[7119,"That ʿUmar wrote to some of his governors: “Do not take from a man who does not find in his camels the age due from him except that same age from an equivalent of his camels, or the value of a just equivalent.”"],[7120,"I said to Ibn Ṭāwūs: “I was told that you say: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: ‘If he does not find the age, then its value.’” He said: “I never said it.” He said: “Rather I said: ‘He gives whatever he wishes.’” He said: “Perhaps I said it, but I did not hear anything from him about it.”"],[7121,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a zakat collector, and he found that a man owed a bint makhāḍ. The man said: “I will not give, as the first zakat taken from me, a she-camel that has no back and no belly”—or he said: “no udder”—“but choose from among them a fine she-camel.” He said: The collector mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “Inform him of what is due from him, and if he voluntarily gives something extra, accept it from him.”"],[7123,"The Prophet ﷺ came to a man from among those who had converted, and he wanted to take from him the age that is taken from him for charity, so he said to him: 'Do not ever leave an age better than the age you take, for no collector for God stood before you for it.'"],[7124,"The collectors of the Prophet ﷺ used to assess people at their watering places and in their courtyards."],[7125,"They would be brought wherever they were."],[7126,"'Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to one of his collectors: 'Summon the people with their wealth to the gathering places most gentle to them and closest to their interests. Do not detain the people, the first of them for the last, for the confined livestock in a flock is severe, it has a destroyer, and do not drive them a drive that takes them far from pasturage and their watering place.'"],[7127,"The people would not delay their charity in drought or fertility, in leanness or fatness, until Mu'awiya came and delayed it for them and made them liable for it."],[7128,"Fear God, for you owe two charities; if they give me one, I take it, or two, I take them."],[7129,"If two charities come in succession, only the first is taken, like the jizya."],[7130,"If I had asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about one who withholds his charity, and he said: 'I put it in its proper place—is he to be fought?' That would be dearer to me than red camels. He said: 'Abu Bakr held the view that he should be fought.'"],[7131,"When Abu Bakr prepared—or he said: when Abu Bakr was ready—to fight the people of apostasy, he said to him: 'How will you fight the people, O Abu Bakr, when the Messenger of God ﷺ said: I was commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but God, and if they say it, they protect their blood and their wealth from me except by its right, and their reckoning is upon God?' Abu Bakr said: 'By God, I will fight whoever separates between prayer and zakāh, for zakāh is the right of wealth. By God, if they withheld from me a hobble-cord that they used to give to the Messenger of God ﷺ, I would fight them for it.' 'Umar said: 'By God, it was only that I saw that God had opened Abu Bakr's chest to fighting, and I knew that it was the truth.'"],[7132,"I said to 'Ata': 'Do you permit that I put the charity of my wealth in its proper places, or must it be to the commanders?' He said: 'I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: \"If you put it in its proper places, as long as you do not give any of it to someone you yourself name, there is no harm.\" I heard it from him more than once, tracing it to Ibn 'Abbas.' He said: 'And 'Ata' said to me: \"Ibn 'Umar used to say: 'Give the zakāh to the commanders.'\" He said: 'A man said to him while he was disputing with him: \"They do not put it in its proper places.\" He said: \"Even so.\"'"],[7134,"The Prophet ﷺ called on the people for charity, and it was brought, and it was said: 'O Messenger of God, this is Abu Jahm, Khalid b. al-Walid, and 'Abbas, the uncle of the Messenger of God ﷺ; they have withheld charity.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'He finds fault with us only because he was poor and God and His Messenger enriched him. As for Khalid b. al-Walid, he has withheld his coats of mail and his equipment in the way of God. As for 'Abbas, the uncle of the Messenger of God ﷺ, it is due upon him and the like of it with it.'"],[7135,"On it, one-tenth."],[7136,"He saw that if charity were put in its proper places, should I put it in its proper places or hand it over to the authorities? He said – without hesitation –: \"That is not for you if they put it in its proper places.\" I said then: Ibn Abbas only said that because they do not put it in its proper places? He said: \"Yes.\" And he said the same about zakah al-fitr and every charity of livestock or crops. He said: \"It will suffice you to hand it over to them, and the reward will be yours, and they will bear what they bear.\""],[7137,"He said to Tawus: We have lands; should we put their charity in its proper places or hand it over to them? He said: \"If you are able to take them by their hands, then do so.\" And Ibn al-Musayyib said: \"If you then put it in its proper places, your authority over it will not be weakened in what is unavoidable of gifts and frontier posts, then there is no harm; otherwise no.\""],[7138,"Wealth accumulated with me. He said: I went to Ibn Umar, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas. I came to each man of them alone and said: Wealth has accumulated with me, and these people put it where you see, and I have found another place for it. What do you think? All of them said: \"Hand it over to them.\""],[7139,"It is not to be handed over to them if they do not put it in its proper places."],[7140,"A man came to Ibn Umar and said: I have wealth; should I pay zakah on it? Ibn Umar said: \"May the far one be debased!\" They said: He is saying: I have wealth, so where should I put its zakah? He said: Why does he not say it like this: a lump of the lumps of Jahannam came to me, wearing a black cloak of dog's fur. Hand it over to your governors, even if they shred the flesh of dogs on their tables."],[7141,"A man came to Ibn Umar asking him about the zakah of his wealth. He said: \"Hand it over to the ruler.\" He said: Our commanders are the dahaqin. He said: \"And what are the dahaqin?\" He said: Among the polytheists. He said: \"Then do not hand it over to the polytheists.\""],[7143,"I entered upon Ibn Umar, I and a shaykh older than me. He said: I think he said: Ibn al-Musayyib. I asked him about charity: should I hand it over to the commanders? He said: \"Yes.\" He said: I said: Even if they buy with it cheetahs and falcons? He said: \"Yes.\" Then when we went out I said to the shaykh: Do you say what Ibn Umar said? He said: No. Then I said to Maymun b. Mihran: Do you say what Ibn Umar said? He said: No."],[7144,"I entered upon al-Hasan while he was hiding during the time of al-Hajjaj in the house of Abu Khalifah. A man said to him: I asked Ibn Umar: Should I hand over zakah to the commanders? Ibn Umar said: Put it among the poor and the needy. He said: Al-Hasan said to me: Did I not tell you that Ibn Umar, when he felt safe with a man, would say: \"Put it among the poor and the needy.\""],[7145,"I never asked al-Hasan about anything as I asked him about this. He said: Sometimes he said to me: \"Hand it over to them,\" and sometimes he said to me: \"Do not hand it over to them.\""],[7146,"Whatever they take from you, count it as reward."],[7148,"Do not pay zakat to one who is unjust in it."],[7149,"What they take from you suffices on your behalf, and what is hidden from them, put it in its proper places."],[7150,"What they take from you suffices on your behalf."],[7152,"Abu Bakr said in what he advised Umar: \"Whoever pays zakat to other than its rightful recipients, his zakat will not be accepted, even if he gives charity with the entire world. And whoever fasts the month of Ramadan outside of it, his fast will not be accepted from him, even if he fasts the entire age.\""],[7153,"What they take from you, count it on your behalf, and what is hidden for you, put it in its proper places."],[7154,"I asked Hammad about a man who sent his zakat with a man to hand it to the ruler, and it perished on the way: does it suffice on his behalf? He said: He laughed and said: You people of Basra are nothing but a piece of the people of Sham; you have settled among the people of Iraq. It does not suffice on his behalf, even if it reaches. It is in the position of a debt. I said to him: What about Ibn Umar, who said: \"Give it to them, even if they tear the flesh of dogs on their tables\"? He said: God forbid that Ibn Umar said that."],[7155,"I asked Hammad about a man who sent his zakat with a man to hand it to the ruler, and it perished on the way: does it suffice on his behalf? He said: He laughed and said: You people of Basra are nothing but a piece of the people of Sham; you have settled among the people of Iraq. It does not suffice on his behalf, even if it reaches. It is in the position of a debt. I said to him: What about Ibn Umar, who said: \"Give it to them, even if they tear the flesh of dogs on their tables\"? He said: God forbid that Ibn Umar said that."],[7156,"If a man takes out his zakat and it is stolen, he is liable for it; it is in the position of a debt.\" Al-Thawri said: Hammad said it. Sufyan said: Another opinion is more beloved to me: \"There is no liability in it as long as he has not set it aside or turned it into something.\""],[7158,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ while he was dividing dates from charity, and al-Hasan b. Ali was in his lap. When he finished, the Prophet ﷺ carried him on his shoulder, and his saliva flowed onto the Prophet's cheek ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ raised his head to him, and there was a date in his mouth. The Prophet ﷺ put his hand in and extracted it from him, then said to him: \"Do you not know that charity is not lawful for the family of Muhammad ﷺ?\""],[7159,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade us—and I do not say he forbade you—to mate a donkey with a horse, and he commanded us to perform ablution thoroughly, and not to eat charity."],[7160,"And I brought her charity with which I had been commanded, and she said: \"I warn our young men; for Maymun, or Mihran, the client of the Prophet ﷺ, informed me that he passed by the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: 'O Maymun,' or 'O Mihran, we are a household forbidden from charity, and our clients are of ourselves, so do not eat charity.'\""],[7161,"It was said to him: 'Who are the family of Muhammad ﷺ?' He said: 'Those for whom charity is unlawful.' It was said: 'Who are they?' He said: 'The family of ʿAlī, the family of ʿAqīl, the family of Jaʿfar, and the family of ʿAbbās.'"],[7162,"Indeed I enter my house and find a date thrown on my bed, and were it not that I fear it might be from charity, I would eat it."],[7163,"The Prophet ﷺ picked up a hair from the ground between his two fingers and said: 'Charity is not lawful for me, nor for anyone of my household, not even the like of this hair.'"],[7164,"ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz sent to ʿAbd Allāh b. al-Faḍl—he said: A man said to me, when I narrated this, 'Rather to ʿAlī b. al-Ḥusayn'—saying: 'I want to appoint you over the collection of such-and-such charity.' He replied: 'The Prophet ﷺ said: Charity is not lawful for the Banū Hāshim or the Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.' He said: 'Then from where is your stipend and provision?' He said: 'I returned nothing to him.' Then I went to Ibn al-Musayyib, and he said to me: 'What did he say to you?' So I told him his news and his statement, 'Then from where is your stipend and provision?' He said: 'Why did you not say: The stipend and provision are only from the Muslims, wherever you and your companions are, while charity belongs to its rightful recipients.'"],[7165,"They came to Ibn Masʿūd in the time of ʿUthmān and said: 'Give us our stipends.' He said: 'I have no stipend for you. Your stipend is from your war booty and your poll tax, while charity belongs to its rightful recipients.' When they kept coming back to him, he brought the keys to ʿUthmān, threw them down, and said: 'I am not a treasurer.'"],[7166,"Regarding the policeman who is used for something concerning charity, may he be given from it a dirham or two dirhams? He said: 'No. He is only given from the war booty and the poll tax, while charity belongs to its rightful recipients.'"],[7167,"The Prophet ﷺ appointed ʿUbāda b. al-Ṣāmit as a collector, then said: 'O Abū al-Walīd, do not come on the Day of Resurrection with a young camel that bellows, a cow that lows, or a sheep that bleats.' ʿUbāda said: 'By the One who sent you with the truth, I will never work over anything again.'"],[7168,"On it, one-tenth."],[7169,"The Prophet ﷺ appointed Ibn al-Utbiyya, a man from the Azd, over charity. When the Prophet ﷺ settled accounts with him, he said: 'This is for you.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Why did you not sit in the house of your father and mother and see whether anything would be gifted to you or not?' Then the Prophet ﷺ stood as a preacher and said: 'What is the matter with men whom we appoint over work from what Allah has entrusted to us, then one of them comes and says: This is what is for you, and this was gifted to me. Why did he not sit in the house of his father and mother and see whether anything would be gifted to him or not? By the One in whose hand is my soul, none of you embezzles anything—or he said: of that anything—except that he brings it on the Day of Resurrection on his neck. If it is a camel, he brings it bellowing; if it is a cow, he brings it lowing; and if it is a sheep, he brings it bleating.' Then he raised his hands and said: 'Have I conveyed?' Then he raised his hands until the pale color of his armpit appeared."],[7170,"On what is watered by manual irrigation (naḍḥ) and ropes and buckets: half of one-tenth. Maʿmar said: \"I do not know of any disagreement about it. And on what is baʿl, and on what is watered by kaẓāʾim, and on what is a spring (najl): one-tenth.\" Maʿmar said: \"I have not heard any disagreement about it.\""],[7171,"The Prophet ﷺ appointed ʿUbāda b. al-Ṣāmit—or Saʿd b. ʿUbāda—and said: 'Beware of coming on the Day of Resurrection with a camel that you carry on your back, bellowing.' He said: 'I will not come with it, nor will I betray.' So he did not work."],[7172,"The Prophet ﷺ sent Muʿādh to Yemen. Then the Prophet ﷺ died suddenly, and Abū Bakr was appointed as successor. He said: Abū Bakr sent ʿUmar over the pilgrimage season. Muʿādh came on the Day of ʿArafa with servants whom he had set aside. ʿUmar met them and said: 'What are these?' He said: 'These are for Abū Bakr from the poll tax, and these were gifted to me as a present.' ʿUmar said: 'Obey me and hand them over to Abū Bakr. If he gives them to you, take them.' Muʿādh said: 'No, by Allah, I will not do it. I will not take a gift that was given to me and give it to Abū Bakr.' The next day, Muʿādh met ʿUmar and said: 'I think I will do what you told me. Last night I saw myself brought to the Fire while you were holding my waist-wrapper.' So Muʿādh went to Abū Bakr and handed them over to him, saying: 'These were gifted to me, so take them; you are more entitled to them.' He said: Abū Bakr accepted them and took them, and went with them to his house. When the prayer was called, they were in the row behind him. After he prayed, he said: 'Did you pray?' They said: 'Yes.' He said: 'For whom?' They said: 'For Allah.' He said: 'Go, for you are for Allah.'"],[7173,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us and said: O people, whoever among you we appoint over some task and he conceals from us a needle or anything greater, that is a misappropriation he will bring on the Day of Resurrection. Then a black man of the Anṣār stood up—it is as if I can see him now—and said: O Messenger of Allah, take back my appointment. He said: Why is that? He said: I heard you say what you just said. He said: And I say it: whoever among you we appoint over some task, let him bring its small and its great; whatever he is given from it he takes, and whatever he is forbidden he refrains from."],[7174,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “And invoke blessings upon them; indeed your invocation is a reassurance for them” [Qur’an 9:103]—has any saying reached you that is said when taking charity? He said: No."],[7175,"When a people brought charity to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he would say: O Allah, bless them. He said: My father brought charity to him, and he said: O Allah, bless Abū Awfā."],[7176,"None of you should milk the livestock of another man without his permission. Would any of you like his storeroom to be approached, his cupboard broken into, and its food taken away? Indeed, the udders of their livestock store their food for them. So let no one milk the livestock of another except with his permission."],[7177,"All zakāh on fruits and crops: whether palm trees, grapevines, or crops of wheat, barley, or hull-less barley (sult) — what is baʿl, or is watered by a river, or is watered by a spring, or is ʿatharī watered by rain: on it one-tenth — one out of every ten. And what is watered by manual irrigation (naḍḥ): on it half of one-tenth — one out of every twenty."],[7178,"When you are three, appoint one of you as leader—meaning on a journey. When you pass by a camel herder or a sheep herder, call out to him three times. If one of them answers you, ask him for a drink. Otherwise, dismount, milk, and drink, then tie back—I said to him: What does “tie back” mean? He said: He ties its udder."],[7179,"Camels we pass by—shall we milk them? He said: No; perhaps their owners are in need of them."],[7180,"The Prophet ﷺ and Ḥamza were discussing the world, and the Prophet ﷺ said: The world is green and sweet. Whoever takes its due portion will be blessed, but many who plunge into the wealth of Allah and the wealth of His Messenger will have the Fire on the Day of Resurrection."],[7181,"I heard ʿAṭāʾ being asked about prodding camels. He said: If that is for boasting and showing off, then no. But if he intends to improve the sale, there is no harm. He said: I said: What is prodding them? He said: He strikes them and pokes them with a stick in their flanks."],[7182,"They asked him about what is less than thirty cattle, and about honey. He said: I have not been commanded anything regarding them."],[7183,"ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz sent me to Yemen, and I wanted to take charity from honey. He said: Al-Mughīra b. Ḥakīm said to me: There is nothing due on it. So I wrote to ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz about it. He said: He spoke the truth, and he is upright and acceptable. There is nothing due on it."],[7184,"ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz asked me about honey: Is there charity due on it? I said: There is no honey in our land, but I asked al-Mughīra b. Ḥakīm about it, and he said: There is nothing due on it. ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: He is upright and trustworthy; he spoke the truth."],[7185,"He asked Ibn ʿUmar: Do you know whether ʿUmar took tithes from the Muslims? He said: “I do not know it. I do not know it.”"],[7186,"I wrote to Ibrahim b. Maysara asking him about that, and he wrote to me: Your letter about the trade routes has come to me. Two men from them arrived with a letter to Uthman b. Muhammad, claiming it was from the Prophet ﷺ, studied, and Uthman ordered it renewed for them in the revival of some of the mountain passes of the people of Tihama. He said: I think he said for Qays or Sunbula. And he mentioned, I think, that “a companion of theirs came to the Prophet ﷺ with two water-skins, one charity and one a gift. He accepted the gift and ordered the charity to be received by someone.”"],[7187,"Sufyan b. Abd Allah, the governor of al-Ta’if, wrote to Umar b. al-Khattab that the people before me are asking me to protect a mountain for them — or he said: bees for them. Umar wrote to them: It is only rain flies; no one has more right to it than another. So if they acknowledge charity to you, protect it for them. He wrote that they had acknowledged charity. So Umar wrote to him: Protect it for them and take tithes from them."],[7189,"Regarding the charity on honey, he said: “For every ten afrāq, one faraq.”"],[7190,"The Messenger of God ﷺ wrote to the people of Yemen: “That tithes be taken from honey owners.”"],[7192,"He wrote to Umar b. Abd al-Aziz asking him about the charity on ambergris. Urwa claimed that he wrote to him: “Write to me how the early people used to take it? Or how was it taken from them? Then write to me.” He said: It has been established with me that it was treated as spoils of war, so a fifth was taken from it. Urwa claimed that he wrote to him: “Take the fifth, and give what remains after the fifth to the one who found it.”"],[7193,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz took a fifth of ambergris."],[7194,"Ibrahim b. Saʿd asked him about ambergris, and he said: “If there is anything in ambergris, then a fifth is due on it.”"],[7195,"We do not see a fifth on ambergris, saying: It is something the sea thrust out."],[7196,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Urwa b. Muhammad: Ask those before you how the early people used to take from ambergris. He wrote to him that it has been established with me that it was treated as spoils of war, a fifth taken from it. So Umar wrote to him: “Take a fifth of it, and give what remains of it after the fifth to the one who found it.”"],[7197,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz took one-fifth from ambergris."],[7198,"Ata was asked, \"Is there charity on the orphan's unproductive wealth?\" He was astonished and said, \"Why should it not have charity?\" He said, \"Yes, on the orphan's unproductive wealth, on cultivated land, on livestock, and on other kinds of his property.\""],[7199,"Regarding one who manages an orphan's wealth, Jabir said, \"He gives its zakat.\""],[7200,"Seek profit in the orphan's wealth lest zakat consume it."],[7201,"Aʾishah used to invest our wealth in sea trade, and she would pay its zakat."],[7202,"We were orphans under Aʾishah's care, and she used to pay zakat on our wealth. Then she handed it over as a profit-sharing investment, and it was blessed for us."],[7203,"Our wealth was with Aʾishah, and she used to pay zakat on it while we were orphans."],[7204,"Ali sold land for us for eighty thousand. When we wanted to collect what was ours, it had decreased. He said, \"I used to pay zakat on it,\" and we were orphans under his care."],[7205,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to pay zakat on an orphan's wealth. He said to Uthman b. Abi al-As, \"I have wealth belonging to an orphan, and zakat has depleted it quickly. Do you have merchants to whom I can entrust it?\" He said, \"So he gave him ten thousand, and he departed with it. He had a servant. When a year had passed, he came to Umar, and Umar said to him, 'What happened to the orphan's wealth?' He said, 'I have brought it to you.' He said, 'Was there any profit?' He said, 'Yes, it reached one hundred thousand.' He said, 'How did you manage?' He said, 'I gave it to the merchants and informed them of the orphan's standing with you.' Umar said, 'No one before you was more likely, in our estimation, to feed us unlawful gain than you. Return our capital; we have no need of your profit.'\""],[7206,"A wasq is sixty sa's."],[7207,"Trade with the wealth of orphans and give its charity."],[7208,"Seek profit in the wealth of orphans before zakat consumes it."],[7209,"I asked al-Zuhri about the awsuq, and he verified them for me."],[7210,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb gathered a group of Muslims and said, 'I want to put this fayʾ in its proper place, so let each man among you come to me in the morning with his opinion.' When morning came, he said, 'I found a verse from the Book of Allah, Exalted is He — or he said, verses — in which Allah has not left any Muslim with a share in this wealth without naming him. Allah says: \"And know that whatever you gain, for Allah is one-fifth of it and for the Messenger\" until he reached \"And whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain from it\" — the verse. Then he recited: \"For the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes\" up to \"Those are the truthful\" — this is for the Emigrants. Then he recited: \"And those who had settled in the abode and in faith before them\" until he reached \"And whoever is protected from the stinginess of his soul, those are the successful.\" Then he said, 'This is for the Anṣār.' Then he recited: \"And those who came after them, saying, 'Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith'\" until he reached \"Kind and Merciful.\" Then he said, 'There is no Muslim on earth who does not have a right in this wealth, whether he is given it or denied it.'"],[7211,"Seek out for the orphans in their wealth."],[7212,"Regarding the zakat of the orphan's wealth: \"No zakat is due on it, just as no prayer is due on him.\""],[7213,"I asked him about the orphan's wealth, and he said: \"I have wealth belonging to my brother's son, and I do not pay zakat on it.\""],[7214,"No zakat is due on the orphan's wealth."],[7215,"He was asked about the wealth of orphans, and he said: \"When they reach puberty, inform them of what zakat has become due on it. If they wish, they may pay it; if they wish, they may leave it.\""],[7216,"Ibn Umar used to have an orphan's wealth in his possession, and he would borrow it to safeguard it from ruin, while paying its zakat from their own wealth."],[7218,"Al-Zuhri was asked about the orphan's wealth, how it should be handled. He said: \"All of that used to be done. Some would borrow it to safeguard it from ruin. Others would say: 'It is a trust, so I will not leave it until I return it to its owner.' Others would take it as a qirad (profit-sharing investment). All of that depends on the intention.\""],[7219,"Abdullah b. Umar used to have the wealth of orphans in his possession, and he would borrow their wealth to safeguard it from ruin, paying its zakat every year from their wealth."],[7220,"No charity is due on a male slave, nor a female slave, nor a mukatib. He said: \"It has reached us that the slave was not entered in the diwan, nor was zakat taken from him.\""],[7221,"There is no zakah due on a debt, nor on a slave, nor on a mukatib, nor on one who buys on credit."],[7224,"There is no sadaqah due from a slave on his property, nor from the master on the property of his slave.\" Ma'mar said: 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote concerning the mukatib: \"No sadaqah is taken from him.\""],[7226,"I asked Ibn 'Umar about the sadaqah on a slave's property. He said: \"Is he not a Muslim?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then he owes five dirhams on every two hundred dirhams, and what exceeds that is calculated accordingly.\""],[7227,"Tawus used to say: \"There is zakah on the property of a slave.\""],[7228,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb gathered a group of Muslims and said, 'I want to put this fayʾ in its proper place, so let each man among you come to me in the morning with his opinion.' When morning came, he said, 'I found a verse from the Book of Allah, Exalted is He — or he said, verses — in which Allah has not left any Muslim with a share in this wealth without naming him. Allah says: \"And know that whatever you gain, for Allah is one-fifth of it and for the Messenger\" until he reached \"And whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain from it\" — the verse. Then he recited: \"For the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes\" up to \"Those are the truthful\" — this is for the Emigrants. Then he recited: \"And those who had settled in the abode and in faith before them\" until he reached \"And whoever is protected from the stinginess of his soul, those are the successful.\" Then he said, 'This is for the Anṣār.' Then he recited: \"And those who came after them, saying, 'Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith'\" until he reached \"Kind and Merciful.\" Then he said, 'There is no Muslim on earth who does not have a right in this wealth, whether he is given it or denied it.'"],[7229,"My mother, who was a mukatabah, passed by Masruq with some cows of hers, and he did not take anything from her.\" He said: \"And he was in charge of the silsilah (the chain, i.e., the zakah administration).\""],[7231,"I went back to 'Ata' concerning the property of my slave, and I said: \"It is deposited with me, and I know that it is cash, with no debt owed to anyone, and he does not trade in anything nor mix with people.\" He said: \"Do you know that he owes nothing to anyone?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"And there is no zakah on it.\" He said: \"It is said: 'A slave is not enrolled in a diwan, nor is zakah taken from him.'\" I said: \"Zakah on the property?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[7233,"A slave may not give sadaqah without the permission of his master."],[7234,"The slave is not permitted to give anyone anything from his wealth, nor to free a slave, nor to give any of it in charity except with his master's permission. Rather, he may eat in a reasonable manner and clothe himself, his child, and his wife."],[7235,"Nothing of his master's wealth is lawful for the slave except that he eat, clothe himself, or spend in a reasonable manner."],[7236,"A slave may not give sadaqah without the permission of his master."],[7237,"I was with Abd Allah b. Abbas when a man came and said, 'I am a slave, and a passerby passed by me asking for a drink of milk, so shall I give him a drink?' He said, 'No.' The man said, 'What if I fear that he might die of thirst?' He said, 'Give him a drink just enough to sustain him until someone else takes over, then seek your family's permission regarding what you gave him to drink.'"],[7238,"I asked Amir al-Sha'bi whether a slave may give charity. He said, 'No, and his testimony is not valid.'"],[7239,"The slave may give a small amount of charity, one that is of no consequence."],[7240,"He complained to Abu Hurayrah about his masters and asked him whether he could give charity. Abu Hurayrah said to him, 'Nothing of your wealth is lawful for you except that you eat in a reasonable manner, or that you hand a poor person a morsel to eat from your hand.'"],[7244,"He asked al-Qasim b. Muhammad about a mukatab of his with whom he had agreed on a large sum of money: is zakat due on what he took from him? Al-Qasim said, 'Abu Bakr al-Siddiq would not take zakat from any wealth until a year had passed over it. When he gave a man a stipend, he would ask him, “Do you have any wealth on which zakat is due?” If he said yes, he would deduct from his stipend the zakat of that wealth; otherwise he would hand over his stipend in full.'"],[7246,"On newly acquired wealth, when it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due."],[7247,"398 397 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Isrāʾīl, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, similar to it. Hidden text: A woman among the wives of the Prophet ﷺ bathed from janābah. The Prophet ﷺ came and performed wuḍūʾ from her leftover water. She said, 'I bathed from it.' He said, 'Water is not made impure by anything.'"],[7248,"I asked al-Zuhri about a man who has wealth and between him and paying its zakah there remains a month or two, then he wants to spend it. He said: 'The Muslims used to prefer that a man pay his zakah before spending it.'"],[7249,"When I received my stipend from Uthman, he would say: 'Do you have wealth on which zakah has become obligatory?' If I said yes, he would take the zakah of that wealth from my stipend; otherwise, he would hand over my stipend to me."],[7251,"The Prophet ﷺ said concerning the crescent of the month of Ramadan: \"If you see it, then fast; then if you see it, break the fast. If it is obscured from you, then estimate for it thirty days.\""],[7252,"Wealth used to come to him, and he would not pay zakah on it until a year had passed over it, even if he spent all of it. He used to spend it on what was right and on need, and he used to say: 'There is no charity on wealth until a year has passed over it. When a year has passed over it, then on every two hundred dirhams five dirhams are due, and whatever is in excess is calculated accordingly.'"],[7253,"There is no charity on wealth until a year has passed over it. I said: 'And is there none if it is held for more than a year?' He said: 'No.' I said to him: 'Its charity is gone. Then it remained with me eleven months, and I decided to sell it. Is charity due on it from me?' He said: 'No, but if you give it as charity, that is greater for blessing.' I said: 'Livestock remained with me eleven months and I sold it. Then the collector came out after it had remained with the buyer for a month. On whom is the charity?' He said: 'On the one who bought it.'"],[7254,"This estimation of theirs was, they claimed, in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[7255,"Regarding newly acquired wealth, when it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due on it."],[7256,"He used to give and then take its zakah."],[7258,"If you put wealth before the year has passed into something you are not circulating, there is no charity on it."],[7259,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: When are palms estimated? He said: \"When they become edible.\""],[7260,"If you have wealth that you intend to pay zakat on, and between you and the year there is a month or two, then you acquire wealth, pay zakat on it together with it; pay zakat on both of them together."],[7262,"If he acquires wealth after zakat has become due on his wealth, and even if he has not paid zakat on it, the newly acquired wealth starts a new year. Sufyan said: \"If a man has wealth equal to the zakat threshold, then that wealth is lost and only one dirham remains, and between him and the time he used to pay zakat there is a month, then he acquires wealth, he pays zakat on the newly acquired wealth together with that dirham. But if the wealth is exhausted and nothing remains, he does not pay zakat on the newly acquired wealth until the year that he begins anew.\""],[7263,"No charity is taken from profits if the capital has already been subject to zakat, until a year has passed over it."],[7264,"About a man who acquired wealth and waited until there remained between him and the due date of zakat only one day, then acquired a thousand? He said: He pays zakat on both of them together. If he has wealth that he used to pay zakat on, and it is gone except for one dirham, then he acquires wealth before the time of its zakat by a month or two, or less, then that dirham is stolen, he said: He pays zakat on the wealth he acquired, because he had acquired the wealth while the dirham was in his possession. Sufyan said: If he buys cloth for two hundred, and it increases at the time of the year until it reaches a thousand, he pays zakat on the thousand; but if it decreases after reaching a thousand to one hundred, he does not pay zakat on it. Sufyan said: About a man who buys an animal or a commodity for trade for one hundred and ninety, then it grows until its value reaches a thousand or more? He said: There is no zakat on it until he converts it into something else, because the price he bought it with did not have zakat due on it. If he converts it into something else, he does not pay zakat until a year has passed over it. If he buys it for two hundred and it reaches ten dirhams, then there is no zakat on it; but if he buys it for two hundred and it reaches a thousand, then zakat is due on the thousand, because the original had zakat due. He said: If a man buys a commodity for trade, then later decides to keep it, he has annulled the trade; if he later decides to put it into trade, there is no zakat on it until he converts it. Sufyan said about a man who is owed two hundred dirhams by another man, and he is paid one hundred dirhams: There is no zakat on it until he receives the rest, unless he has other wealth and combines it with his wealth, then he pays zakat on it. If he receives the two hundred and has no other wealth besides them, he pays zakat on the two hundred once, because if he took five dirhams from it, the remainder would not reach the zakat threshold. He said: Whoever has cloth and appraises it at a value reaching a thousand dirhams, but does not pay zakat until it decreases to five hundred dirhams, he must pay zakat on the thousand. If he appraised it at five hundred, then left it until it reached a thousand, he only pays zakat on five hundred. If he has cloth and appraises it at one hundred, then it reaches a thousand, there is no zakat on it."],[7265,"I asked 'Amir al-Sha'bi about the zakat on jewelry. He said: Its zakat is lending it out. 'Umar said: My father advised me to pay zakat on a necklace on my sister's neck. My father said: \"It used to be said that a stored thing, if it has been given zakat once, is not subject to zakat again until it is converted into something else.\""],[7266,"I asked Jabir b. 'Abd Allah about jewelry: Is there zakat on it? He said: \"No.\" I said: Even if it is a thousand dinars? He said: \"A thousand is a lot.\""],[7267,"What the rivers, the sky, and the springs water: one-tenth; and what is watered by the rope and bucket (rishāʾ): half of one-tenth."],[7268,"On what is watered with buckets and watering camels: half of one-tenth."],[7269,"There is no charity due on what is less than five awsuq, nor on what is less than five awaq, nor on what is less than five dhawd."],[7270,"There is no zakat on endowed charity, meaning the zakat.\" We said to ʿAbd al-Razzāq, 'Why?' He said, 'Because he made it for the poor, and when they take something from it, is it not given to the poor?' We said, 'Yes.' He said, 'So there is no charity due on it.'"],[7271,"She asked Aisha about jewelry of hers: is there any charity due on it? She said: \"No.\""],[7272,"Aisha used to adorn her brother's daughters with gold and pearls, and she did not pay zakat on it. Their jewelry at that time was small."],[7275,"A woman asked him about jewelry of hers: is there zakat on it? He said: \"When it reaches two hundred dirhams, then pay zakat on it.\" She said: \"I have orphans in my care; should I give it to them?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[7276,"The wife of Abdullah said: \"I have jewelry; should I pay zakat on it?\" He said: \"When it reaches two hundred dirhams, then pay zakat on it.\" She said: \"In my care are orphan sons of my brother; should I put it toward them?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[7277,"He used to adorn his daughters with gold—he mentioned more than two hundred dirhams, I think he mentioned a thousand or more—and he used to pay zakat on it."],[7279,"We were with Ammar b. Yasir on the day about which there is uncertainty regarding Ramadan, when a roasted sheep was brought. A man from the group withdrew, so he said, 'Come close.' The man said, 'I am fasting, and it is only a fast I have been observing.' Ammar said, 'Do you believe in Allah and the Last Day? Then eat.'"],[7280,"He asked Ibn al-Musayyib: Is there zakat on jewelry of gold and silver? He said: \"Yes.\" He said: I said: \"Then it will be exhausted.\" He said: \"Even so.\""],[7281,"Charity is due on raw gold and raw silver if it is traded; and if it is not traded, and if it is cast and stored, and if it is in a woman's jewelry. He said: \"There is no charity on pearls, chrysolite, rubies, gems, or goods that are not traded. If any of those are traded, then charity is due on their price when sold.\""],[7282,"There is no zakat on jewels and rubies unless it is for trade."],[7283,"Regarding ornaments: gold and silver, zakat is due on them, and there is no zakat on beads unless they are for trade."],[7284,"There is no zakat on rubies and the like, unless something of it is circulated [in trade]."],[7285,"Two Yemeni women came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and he saw on their hands rings of gold. He said, \"Do you two pay its zakat?\" They said, \"No.\" He said, \"Would it please you two that God seal you on the Day of Resurrection with rings of fire?\" Or he said, \"Would it please you two that He bracelet you on the Day of Resurrection with two bracelets of fire?\" They said, \"No.\" He said, \"Then pay its zakat.\""],[7286,"We say: the ornament of a sword, and the belt, and every gold and silver that you include with your wealth—when you pay zakat, you pay zakat on it. And when the foodstuffs of every kind are a wasq or two wasqs, nothing is due on them until one kind alone reaches five wasqs. However, gold and silver have a criterion unlike others: when it is ten mithqals of gold and one hundred dirhams, you pay zakat on it."],[7287,"Umar b. al-Khattab said to al-Abbas at the time of zakat: \"Pay the zakat of your wealth.\" The Prophet ﷺ had commanded that. Al-Abbas said, \"I paid it before that.\" Umar mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ said, \"He has spoken the truth; he paid it before.\""],[7288,"I asked him about a man who sees the time for his zakat [approaching] and pays it in advance. He said, \"There is no harm in paying in advance.\""],[7289,"A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, \"O Messenger of God! I have seen the crescent.\" He said, \"Do you testify that there is no god but God and that I am the Messenger of God?\" He said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Then the Prophet ﷺ ordered Bilal to announce to the people that they should fast.\""],[7290,"He was asked about that and said, \"Why would he pay his zakat in advance?\" as if he disliked that."],[7292,"Anas b. Malik sent me to Ayla. I said, \"You have sent me to the worst of your work.\" He said, \"Then he brought out to me a letter from Umar b. al-Khattab: 'Take from the Muslims one dirham for every forty dirhams, and from the people of the covenant one dirham for every twenty dirhams, and from those who have no covenant one dirham for every ten dirhams.'\""],[7293,"Whoever eats forgetfully or drinks forgetfully, there is no harm upon him. God has fed him and given him drink."],[7294,"On two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due, and whatever exceeds that is according to the same calculation. He said: I said, \"What does his statement 'and whatever exceeds that is according to the same calculation' mean?\" He said, \"Some of them say: when it exceeds two hundred and the excess is forty dirhams, then one dirham is due on it. Others say: 'and whatever exceeds that is according to the same calculation'—if it is ten, then a quarter of a dirham is due on it.\""],[7295,"Whatever exceeds two hundred [dirhams], then according to that calculation."],[7296,"Whoever acquires wealth, no zakat is due on it until a year has passed over it. When it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due. If it is less than two hundred, nothing is due. If it exceeds two hundred, then proportionately."],[7297,"O 'Ali, I have exempted the zakat on horses and slaves. As for camels, cattle, and sheep, it is not exempted, but bring one quarter of the tithe: from every two hundred dirhams five dirhams, and from every twenty dinars half a dinar. Nothing is due on two hundred dirhams until a year passes over them. When a year has passed, five dirhams are due. For whatever exceeds, then one dirham for every forty dirhams."],[7298,"Whatever exceeds two hundred [dirhams], nothing is taken from it until it reaches forty."],[7299,"7445 7390 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Hisham b. 'Urwah, from his father, the like of it, and Ibn Jurayj said it from 'Ata'. Hidden text: If a man breaks his fast in Ramadan and then the sun appears, he must make it up; but if he eats at dawn while thinking it is still night, he does not have to make it up."],[7300,"Three is a congregation."],[7301,"Whatever exceeds two hundred [dirhams], then according to that calculation."],[7302,"Whoever acquires wealth, no zakat is due on it until a year has passed over it. When it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due. If it is less than two hundred, nothing is due. If it exceeds two hundred, then proportionately."],[7304,"Zakat is not due on wealth until it reaches twenty dinars. When it reaches twenty dinars, half a dinar is due. Then for every four dinars the wealth increases, one dirham is due, until the wealth reaches forty dinars, then one dinar for every forty dinars. He said: 'On twenty-four dinars, half a dinar and a dirham.' I said: 'So on twenty dinars is half a dinar, accepted?' He said: 'Yes, unless it is after that for a while.' I said to him: 'If a man has nineteen dinars, he has nothing else, and the exchange rate is twelve or thirteen per dinar, is zakat due on it?' He said: 'Yes, if when exchanged it would reach two hundred dirhams. That was when silver was not gold.' He said: 'Zakat is not due on silver until it reaches two hundred dirhams. When it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due. Then for every forty dirhams the wealth increases, one dirham.' And 'Amr b. Dinar said that. He said: 'Ata' said: 'Until the wealth reaches four hundred dirhams, then for every four hundred dirhams ten dirhams.' I said: 'Two hundred dirhams and twenty dirhams?' He said: 'Nothing is due on twenty dirhams.' And 'Amr b. Dinar said that to me."],[7305,"Until it reaches forty dirhams, then it is six. Then nothing until it reaches two hundred eighty, then it is seven. Then in the same way. 'Ata' said: 'If it is twenty-three dinars, then on the twenty there is half a dinar. If the exchange rate reached forty-three dirhams, then one dirham is due, otherwise nothing.' He said: 'Abd al-Karim said to me similar to the statement of 'Ata' regarding the forty and odd dirhams: a dirham, and nothing is due on what is below the forty and odd.'"],[7307,"On two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due. Nothing is due on anything after two hundred until it reaches forty dirhams."],[7308,"Nothing is due on what is below two hundred dirhams. When it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due. He said: 'In the letter of the Prophet ﷺ to 'Amr b. Hazm: \"On the silver of any of them, when it reaches five uqiyyahs, one quarter of the tithe.\"'"],[7309,"This is the month of your zakat. Whoever has a debt, let him pay it. Then let him pay zakat on what remains."],[7310,"When the stipend was distributed, Uthman would give a sermon and say: 'Whoever owes a debt, let him pay it off, then pay zakat on his wealth.' Ata' then said to me: 'By my life, there is no zakat due on a man's wealth while he owes a debt.' Ata' said: 'If they pay zakat on a man's stipend after his debt, then the master of the stipend has not wronged anyone.' I said to him: 'What do you think if I owe a debt, and I have wealth, and I have slaves that would reduce the debt for me—should I pay zakat on my behalf?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[7311,"When your dates or crops are ready, take account of whatever debt you owe, old or new, and set it aside. Then pay zakat on what remains if it reaches five wasqs."],[7312,"Regarding a man's crop whose debt is greater than his wealth when it is harvested—should he pay its due on the day it is harvested? He said: 'I do not think that a man whose debt is greater than his wealth owes any zakat on livestock, or on the principal, or that he must pay its due on the day of harvest.'"],[7313,"He owes no zakat."],[7314,"Zakat is only due on what you secure after you eat from it, and after you give wages or spend on grinding and the like, until you secure it in your house—except if you sell something, then zakat is due on what you sold."],[7315,"Whatever you give of your food for your own expense is counted as food, and likewise what you eat, except something you measure out for your family—that is, you measure it for them."],[7316,"Regarding a hunter who keeps his catch for a year, or birds that he keeps for a year, no zakat is due on them until he keeps them as part of something he circulates for trade. Sufyan said: 'And anyone who inherits something owes no zakat on it until he disposes of it, except one who inherits cattle, sheep, camels, gold, silver, or crops.'"],[7317,"Whoever has merchandise for trade, and it remains with him for years without selling it, zakat is due on it every year—he pays its zakat. He said: And al-Sha'bi said: 'No zakat is due on it after the first time.'"],[7318,"Food from my father's land would remain with him for two or three years while he intended to sell it, but he would not pay zakat on it after the first zakat, waiting for prices to rise. Abd al-Razzaq said: '[He used] a word I do not like to say: waiting for prices to rise.'"],[7319,"If I had sheep and paid zakat on them, then sold some of their wool and milk for two hundred dirhams, no zakat would be due on the two hundred until a year has passed over them, since the necks of the sheep had already been assessed for zakat. He said: And al-Hakam b. Utayba said the same."],[7320,"I asked al-Ju'fi about a man who has grain from his land, intending to sell it, and he has already paid zakat on the original crop. He said: Al-Sha'bi said: 'No zakat is due on it until it is sold.' He said: And al-Nakha'i said: 'Zakat is due on it.'"],[7321,"7445 7390 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Hisham b. 'Urwah, from his father, the like of it, and Ibn Jurayj said it from 'Ata'. Hidden text: If a man breaks his fast in Ramadan and then the sun appears, he must make it up; but if he eats at dawn while thinking it is still night, he does not have to make it up."],[7322,"We have not heard anything about it other than the first [opinion]."],[7323,"‘Umar passed by me and said: “Pay the zakāh on your wealth.” He said: I said: “I have no wealth to pay zakāh on except in leather socks and tanned hides.” He said: “Then assess its value, and pay its zakāh.”"],[7324,"Regarding cloth: “If it is circulated like slaves, its value is subject to zakāh.”"],[7325,"Regarding a man who has various grains, on none of which zakāh is individually due, he said: “He combines them and then pays zakāh on them.”"],[7326,"There is no zakāh on goods that are not circulated except gold and silver, for if it is unworked nuggets lying idle—even if not circulated—it is subject to zakāh."],[7327,"Among what counted as wealth: slaves, or riding animals, or cloth circulated for trade—zakāh was due every year."],[7328,"On trade goods that are circulated, zakāh is due every year; zakāh is not taken from them until that month comes in the following year."],[7330,"I heard that it is “the value of the trade goods on the day its zakāh is taken out.”"],[7331,"[He was asked about] a loan that a man gives. He said: “Neither the owner of the wealth nor the one to whom he lent it owes any zakāh. In that case it is like a debt regarding zakāh, except that it is greater in reward than a debt, they claim: the gift of gold is the loan—he is the one saying it.”"],[7332,"A man informed him that an orphan had wealth in the possession of Ibn ‘Umar, and it was said: “Pay zakāh on it.” Ibn ‘Umar said: “Later.”"],[7333,"Ibn ‘Umar loaned out an orphan’s wealth, and it remained owed to him for three years, and he used to pay zakāh on it while it was owed to him during those three years, taking it from their wealth."],[7334,"Abdullah b. Umar used to have the wealth of orphans in his possession, and he would borrow their wealth to safeguard it from ruin, paying its zakat every year from their wealth."],[7335,"7480 7424 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Abd Allah b. Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, similar to it. Hidden text: He used to forbid kissing for the fasting person."],[7336,"If your debt is with a trustworthy person, pay zakat on it; but if you fear its loss, do not pay zakat on it until you receive it."],[7338,"On every commodity from which cash is received and on every debt that is hoped for, there is zakat."],[7340,"He used to kiss while he was fasting."],[7341,"He was asked about a man who is owed a debt by another man. He said: 'What prevents him from paying zakat?' He said: 'He cannot get it.' He said: 'If he is truthful, let him pay what is absent from him.'"],[7344,"I was sitting with 'Abd al-Rahman b. Nafi' when Ziyad the doorkeeper came to him and said: 'The Commander of the Faithful—referring to Ibn al-Zubayr—says: Send the zakat of your wealth.' He said: 'Did he send you?' He said: 'Yes.' He did not respond to anything else until he stood up and brought out one hundred dirhams. He said: 'Convey peace to him and say: Zakat is only due on cash.' Nafi' said: 'I later met Ziyad and asked: Did you convey to him what he said?' He said: 'Yes.' I asked: 'What did he say?' He said: 'He spoke the truth.'"],[7345,"7174 Ibn Jurayj said: And 'Ubayd Allah b. Abi Yazid narrated to me something similar from Ziyad. Hidden Matn: I was sitting with 'Abd al-Rahman b. Nafi' when Ziyad the doorkeeper came to him and said: 'The Commander of the Faithful—referring to Ibn al-Zubayr—says: Send the zakat of your wealth.' He said: 'Did he send you?' He said: 'Yes.' He did not respond to anything else until he stood up and brought out one hundred dirhams. He said: 'Convey peace to him and say: Zakat is only due on cash.' Nafi' said: 'I later met Ziyad and asked: Did you convey to him what he said?' He said: 'Yes.' I asked: 'What did he say?' He said: 'He spoke the truth.' Hidden Chain: Ibn Jurayj said: And 'Ubayd Allah b. Abi Yazid narrated to me something similar from Ziyad."],[7347,"Regarding a debt owed by one man to another, should he pay its zakat? He said: 'Yes.'"],[7348,"A man said to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb: “O Commander of the Believers, we have funds, so I hasten to give charity, spend on my family, and pay off my debt.” He said: “Do not hasten with it. When it comes, calculate your debt and what you owe, gather all of that together, then pay zakat on it.”"],[7349,"A man said to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb: “The time for my zakat comes, and I have a debt?” So he commanded him to pay zakat on it."],[7350,"He used to kiss while he was fasting."],[7352,"Regarding a man from whom the enemy seized a thousand dirhams, and he recovered them after a year, he said: “He owes no zakat on it until a full year passes from the day he took it, because it was considered lost. Had the Muslims seized it, they would have divided it.”"],[7353,"ʿUrwa b. Muḥammad wrote to ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz about wealth in which the people were wronged, and it was in the hands of the tax collectors. He wrote that it should be returned to them and its zakat taken from them. His governor corresponded with him about that, whether to take it every year or only one year. He wrote to him: “If it is ḍimār wealth, pay zakat on it for one year.” I said to him: “What is ḍimār?” He said: “That which is lost.”"],[7354,"I said to Qatāda: “Does absent wealth have zakat?” He said: “If it is not ḍimār or in tawā (ruin, loss), then pay zakat on it.”"],[7356,"There is no zakat on debt until it is received. When it is received, he pays zakat on it once."],[7357,"I asked al-Zuhrī about a man who has a debt: should he pay zakat on it? He said: “Yes, if it is with someone trustworthy. But if he fears tawā (loss) for it, he should not pay zakat on it. When he receives it, he pays zakat for what was absent from him.”"],[7358,"Sa'd b. Malik used to rub her private part with his hand while fasting."],[7360,"He used to take trade goods for zakat and assign them to a single category of people."],[7361,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"Indeed, charities are for the poor,\" and I recited the verse to him. I said: \"Are all charities for them?\" He said: \"Yes, if you place your zakat in one category, or two, or three. If it were abundant, I would command him to distribute it among all of them.\""],[7362,"If you place it in one category of these categories, it suffices you."],[7363,"If you place it in one category of these categories, it suffices you. God only said: \"Indeed, charities are for the poor,\" and so on, so that you do not place it outside these categories."],[7364,"Every worker is given according to the extent of his work."],[7365,"ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote to ʿUrwa b. Muḥammad: Do not distribute charity according to prices; rather, give every worker according to his status, and the poor and needy according to their need and their chronic disability."],[7366,"If you pay the zakat of your wealth, it is not a treasure, even if it is buried. If you do not pay it, it is a treasure, even if it is visible."],[7367,"Whatever has had its zakat paid is not a treasure, even if it is under seven layers of earth; and whatever is visible but its zakat is not paid is a treasure."],[7368,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ use the toothstick while he was fasting more times than I can count."],[7369,"If you pay the zakat of your wealth, it is not a treasure, even if it is buried; and if you do not pay its zakat, it is a treasure, even if it is visible."],[7370,"The treasure that God mentioned in His Book is only that whose zakat has not been paid."],[7371,"If you take out the charity of your wealth, you have removed its evil, and it is not a treasure."],[7372,"A man sold another man a walled garden of his, or property, for a great amount of wealth. ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb said to him, “Put this wealth in a good place.” The man said to him, “Where shall I place it, O Commander of the Faithful?” ʿUmar said, “Place it under the woman's seat.” The man said, “Is it not a treasure, O Commander of the Faithful?” ʿUmar said, “It is not a treasure if you pay its zakāh.”"],[7373,"Zakāh is a bridge between the Fire and Paradise. Whoever pays his zakāh crosses the bridge."],[7374,"Whoever earns pure wealth, withholding zakāh makes it foul; and whoever earns foul wealth, zakāh does not make it pure."],[7375,"There is no prayer without zakāh."],[7376,"Four thousand dirhams and less is spending; what is above that is a treasure."],[7377,"Charity is not lawful for a rich person except for five: an agent appointed over it, a man who bought it with his own money, a debtor, a fighter in the path of God, or a poor person who was given charity from it and then gave a gift from it to a rich person."],[7379,"They used not to ask except about the one in need."],[7380,"The Prophet ﷺ was distributing on the Day of the Conquest, and two men came to him and asked him. He raised his gaze toward them and lowered it – or he said: and brought it down – and Maʿmar said: Additional text, meaning they were sturdy. The Prophet ﷺ said to them, “Whatever you wish, but there is no right in it for a rich person, nor for a strong one who earns.”"],[7381,"Charity is not lawful for a rich person, nor for one who has full strength and is sound."],[7382,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wrote to them: Give from the charity to one whom the year has left with sheep and their shepherd, and do not give from it to one whom the year has left with two sheep and two shepherds."],[7383,"No one who has fifty dirhams is to be given from the charity, and no one is to be given more than fifty dirhams from it unless he is a debtor burdened with debt."],[7384,"Whoever has fifty dirhams should not take from charity unless he is in debt."],[7385,"He may be given from charity one hundred up to two hundred."],[7387,"Give the zakat of your wealth to your relatives; if there are none, then your freed clients; if there are none, then your neighbors."],[7388,"Give it to the people of your kinship among whom you are; if you do not find them, then those next after them."],[7389,"If a man has only a house and a servant, he may take zakat. He said: Our companions say that. And al-Hasan did not consider pilgrimage obligatory for one who has only a house and a servant."],[7390,"There is no harm in putting your zakat in its proper place. If you do not give any of it to someone you are supporting yourself, then there is no harm in it."],[7391,"I said to Sa'id b. Jubayr: Should I give the maternal aunt from the zakat? He said: Yes, as long as you do not close a door on her—meaning as long as she is not among your dependents."],[7392,"He used to dislike kohl for the fasting person."],[7393,"Neither a Jew nor a Christian is given from zakat; they are given from voluntary charity."],[7395,"He used to give zakat al-fitr to monks from the people of the covenant, and others said: Give it to the Muslims."],[7396,"He would collect the zakat al-fitr in the mosque of his neighborhood, then give it to the monks. Al-Thawri said: Others would give it to the Muslims."],[7397,"A man should not give the zakat of his wealth to those of his relatives whose maintenance is obligatory upon him, nor should he give it for the shroud of a deceased person, nor for the debt of a deceased person, nor for building a mosque, nor for purchasing a copy of the Qur'an, nor should Hajj be performed with it, nor should he give it to his mukatab (a slave who has contracted to buy his freedom), nor should he use it to buy a slave to set free, nor should he give it to Jews or Christians, nor should he hire someone with it to carry it from one place to another."],[7398,"I said to Ibrahim, \"May I give my zakat to my sister?\" He said, \"Yes.\""],[7400,"There is no sadaqah except on palm trees or grapes or crops. Amr b. Dinar and Abd al-Karim b. Abi al-Mukhariq said that. I said to Ata, \"Is there sadaqah on all grains?\" He said, \"Yes,\" and he named for me all grains. He said, \"I said, 'What about sorghum, millet, khulkhan (a type of grain), lentils, and ihriz (a type of grain)?' He said, 'Yes, on all grains.' He said, 'I said, \"What about taqdidah (a type of grain)?\" He said, 'There is sadaqah on it; it is a grain. Sadaqah is on all grains.' I said, 'And is there no sadaqah on anything besides that?' He said, 'No.'\""],[7401,"There is no zakat on cotton or wars (a yellow dye plant)."],[7403,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ with a piece of silver and said, \"O Messenger of God, take its zakat from this.\" He said, \"Where is it from?\" He said, \"It is from the mine of the family of so-and-so.\" The Prophet ﷺ said, \"Rather, we will give you the like of it, and we will not go back to it.\""],[7404,"They used to take from us, for what we worked and extracted with our own hands, five dirhams for every two hundred dirhams. But if we found a treasure (rikaz) in the mines, the fifth would be taken from us."],[7406,"The Prophet ﷺ sent Ali b. Abi Talib to a rikaz in Yemen and he levied the fifth on it."],[7407,"I heard that if a man buys land or a house and finds in it ancient (ʿādī) treasure, it is his, and it is spoils of war. But if he finds money from the wealth of this community, it is his, unless the previous owner brings clear evidence and a recognized sign."],[7408,"The well is jubar (no liability), the mine is jubar, the animal is jubar, and on rikaz the fifth is due."],[7409,"Abu Hakim went off to Marwan with the zakah of his wealth. Marwan said to him, “Are you on the payroll?” He said, “No.” He said, “Then take away the zakah of your wealth, for we will not take it from you.” He said: So the next day Marwan assigned him a stipend."],[7410,"Ibn Umar did not have cupping done while fasting."],[7411,"There is no zakah on herbs, sugar cane, rocket, cucumbers, cotton, safflower, fruits, citron, apples, walnuts, figs, pomegranates, peaches—he counts all fruits: there is no sadaqah on them, and they are only eaten—unless something of them is sold for gold that reaches the amount on which sadaqah is due. If something of them is sold for gold that reaches the amount on which sadaqah is due, then at that time there is on it the like of the sadaqah on gold. Abd al-Karim and Amr b. Dinar said that to me."],[7412,"There is no sadaqah on green vegetables."],[7413,"Al-Hajjaj sent Musa b. Mughirah over the Sawad, and he wanted to take from the green produce of the Sawad. Musa b. Talhah said: “I have the document of Muadh b. Jabal, from the Messenger of God ﷺ: He commanded him to take from wheat, barley, raisins, and dates.” He said: I mentioned that to al-Hajjaj, and he said, “He spoke the truth.”"],[7414,"They had taken from grains of his on his land, and I heard him say to Abd al-Hamid—when he entered upon him—“Between me and you is the document of Muadh b. Jabal: He did not take anything from green produce.”"],[7415,"There is no sadaqah on green produce: herbs, apples, and cucumbers."],[7416,"A man came to Madinah and entered upon the Prophet ﷺ for some need of his while the Prophet ﷺ was eating. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Come near.' He said: 'I am fasting.' Then he said: 'Come near, for the traveler has been relieved of fasting and half of the prayer, and so have the pregnant and nursing women.'"],[7417,"There is no sadaqah on summer crops—meaning grains, lentils, and the like."],[7418,"Umar b. al-Khattab took zakah from legumes. Legumes are lentils, chickpeas, and the like."],[7419,"On green produce and fruit, if its price reaches two hundred dirhams, then on it is five dirhams."],[7420,"On olives he said: “It is measured by volume, so on it is a tenth if it is not irrigated, and half a tenth if it is irrigated by buckets.”"],[7421,"There is no zakāh on vegetables. He said: I mentioned it to Ibrāhīm, and he said: 'He spoke the truth.'"],[7422,"On everything the earth produces, a tenth is due."],[7423,"ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote: 'A tenth is to be taken from what the earth produces, whether little or much.'"],[7424,"Regarding His statement: “So whoever does good voluntarily,” they both said: “He feeds another poor person.”"],[7425,"There is no zakat on cotton or wars (a yellow dye plant)."],[7426,"This estimation of theirs was, they claimed, in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[7427,"The Prophet ﷺ sent a man from the Anṣār of the Banū Bayāḍa called Farwa b. ʿAmr, and he estimated the fruit of the people of Madinah. He said: \"I have not heard of estimation except for palms and grapes.\""],[7428,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7429,"When Ibn Rawāḥah came to them, they gathered jewelry from their women's jewelry and gave it to him as a gift. He said: 'O company of Jews, by God, you are the most hateful of God's creation to me, but that will not make me wrong you. As for what you offered me of this bribe, it is unlawful gain, and we do not consume it.' Then he estimated upon them, then gave them the choice to take it or he would take it. They said: 'By this the heavens and the earth are upheld,' and they took it according to that estimation."],[7430,"The Prophet ﷺ had no workers to work the date palms and crops of Khaybar, so the Prophet ﷺ called the Jews of Khaybar and handed Khaybar over to them on condition that they work it for half, and they would deliver it to the Prophet ﷺ and his companions. The Prophet ﷺ said to them: 'I will keep you in it as long as God keeps you.' The Messenger of God ﷺ used to send ʿAbd Allāh b. Rawāḥah to them, and he would estimate upon them when the first fruit ripened before any of it was eaten, then he would give the Jews the choice to take it according to the estimation, or to hand it over to them according to that estimation."],[7431,"About the Prophet's ﷺ agreement with the Jews of Khaybar that half the fruit is ours and half is theirs. He said: They would do the work until their fruit ripened, then they came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'Our fruit has ripened, so send an estimator between us and you.' The Prophet ﷺ sent Ibn Rawāḥah. When he went around their date palms and looked at them, he said: 'By God, I know of no one in God's creation with a greater lie and more hostile to the Messenger of God ﷺ than you. By God, God has created no one more hateful to me than you. By God, that will not make me wrong you by the weight of a speck, and I know it.' He said: Then he estimated all of it, what was theirs and what was the Jews', eighty thousand wasqs. Then the Jews said: 'You have robbed us.' Ibn Rawāḥah said: 'If you wish, give us forty thousand wasqs and we will leave you, and if you wish, we will give you forty thousand wasqs and you leave us.' They looked at one another and said: 'By this the heavens and the earth are upheld, and by this they will overcome you.'"],[7432,"Ibn Rawāḥah estimated it at forty thousand wasqs, and he claimed that when Ibn Rawāḥah gave the Jews the choice, they took the dates, and they owed twenty thousand wasqs."],[7433,"It is the duty of the estimator, when the owner of the property disputes the estimation, to give him the choice, as Ibn Rawāḥah gave the choice. He said: Yes, by my life, and what sunnah is better than the sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ?"],[7434,"About Khaybar he said: The Prophet ﷺ conquered it, and it was entirely his, with its fields and palm trees. The Prophet ﷺ and his companions had no servants, so the Prophet ﷺ made a truce with the Jews on the condition that you will do the work for us and you will have half of the fruit, on the condition that I will let you remain as long as Allah and His Messenger wish. That was when the Prophet ﷺ sent Ibn Rawāḥah to estimate the produce between them. When he gave them the choice, the Jews took the fruit. Khaybar remained in the hands of the Jews under the Prophet's ﷺ truce until ʿUmar, who expelled them. The Jews said: Did not the Prophet ﷺ make a truce with us on such and such terms? He said: Yes, on the condition that we let you remain as long as Allah and His Messenger wish. Now it seems good to me to expel you, so he expelled them. Then he divided it among the Muslims who had conquered it with the Prophet ﷺ and did not give any of it to anyone who had not been present at its conquest. He said: Its people now are Muslims; there are no Jews in it."],[7435,"The Prophet ﷺ handed over Khaybar to the Jews on the condition that they work in it and have half of it. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr continued on that, and a part of ʿUmar's caliphate, then ʿUmar was informed that the Prophet ﷺ said in his illness in which he died: \"Two religions shall not coexist in the land of the Hijaz—or in the land of the Arabs.\" He investigated that until he found confirmation of it, then he said: Whoever has a covenant from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, let him bring it; otherwise I will expel you. He said: So he expelled them. And the Prophet ﷺ had said that in his illness in which he died."],[7436,"He used to send Farwah b. ʿAmr to estimate the palms. When he entered the orchard, he would count the clusters in it, then strike some of them against others according to what he saw in them, and he would not be mistaken."],[7437,"When the Prophet ﷺ sent an estimator, he would command him not to estimate the ʿarāyā."],[7439,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7440,"Palms and grapes are estimated, but grain is not estimated."],[7441,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded ʿAttāb b. Asīd when he appointed him over Makkah, saying: \"Estimate the grapes as you estimate the palms, then take their zakah from raisins as you take the zakah of palms from dates.\" Ibn Jurayj said: And ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote: \"Regarding the sadaqah of dates, the barnī should be taken from barnī, and the lawn from lawn, and lawn should not be taken from barnī, and it should be taken from the threshing floor, and they should not combine them.\""],[7442,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded ʿAttāb b. Asīd when he appointed him over Makkah, saying: \"Estimate the grapes as you estimate the palms, then take their zakah from raisins as you take the zakah of palms from dates.\" Ibn Jurayj said: And ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote: \"Regarding the sadaqah of dates, the barnī should be taken from barnī, and the lawn from lawn, and lawn should not be taken from barnī, and it should be taken from the threshing floor, and they should not combine them.\""],[7443,"They used to estimate the fruit when it ripened, when it was still unripe dates, then they would leave it to its owners, and they would eat it as unripe dates, ripe dates, and dried dates, then they would take according to that estimation."],[7444,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded the estimation of Khaybar when its fruit ripened."],[7445,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7446,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7447,"She said, while mentioning the affair of Khaybar: \"The Prophet ﷺ used to send Ibn Rawāḥa to the Jews, and he would estimate the palms when the first fruit ripened, before it was eaten, then he would give the Jews the choice to take it according to that estimation or to hand it over to them accordingly. The Prophet ﷺ ordered that estimation only so that the zakāh could be calculated before the fruits were eaten and dispersed.\""],[7448,"The Prophet ﷺ used to say to the estimators when he sent them: \"Make allowance for the owners of property in the fallen fruit, the trodden fruit, and what is due on fruit as a right.\""],[7449,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb used to say to the estimator: \"Leave for them the amount that falls and the amount they eat.\""],[7450,"ʿUmar used to say to the estimator: \"If you find a people who have already begun harvesting—meaning they have settled in their orchard—then estimate the amount you think they eat, for it is not estimated against them.\""],[7451,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7452,"I said to Ayyūb: I sold my fruit for two hundred dinars. He said: \"Then on them, for every ten dinars, one dinar, if it has already been missed.\" He said: \"But if he catches it, he takes from the fruit, and the buyer recovers from the seller one tenth of what he gave.\""],[7453,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7454,"No one hosts an estimator. If he hosts him, the host is not obliged to host him; if he wishes, or otherwise he milks—meaning he milks the livestock for him."],[7455,"It has reached me that the estimators in that time did not host anyone; they only ate from the property."],[7456,"The Prophet ﷺ sent, for the entirety of his life, a man from the Anṣār as an estimator, called ʿAbd Allāh b. al-Tayyihān Abū al-Haytham. When the Prophet ﷺ died, Abū Bakr sent for him, but he refused and said: \"You used to estimate for the Prophet ﷺ.\" He said: I used to do so, then I would come and he would seek forgiveness for me. Who will seek forgiveness for me now? So Abū Bakr sent another man."],[7457,"The Prophet ﷺ sent a man from the Anṣār of the Banū Bayāḍa called Farwa b. ʿAmr, and he estimated the fruit of the people of Madinah. He said: \"I have not heard of estimation except for palms and grapes.\""],[7458,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7460,"On what the sky waters, and on baʿl (crops watered by roots), and on rivers: one-tenth; and on what is watered by manual irrigation with buckets: half of one-tenth."],[7461,"What is irrigated by flooding (fatḥan) or what the sky waters: on it one-tenth; and what is irrigated by the large bucket (gharb): half of one-tenth."],[7462,"On what is watered by manual irrigation (naḍḥ) and ropes and buckets: half of one-tenth. Maʿmar said: \"I do not know of any disagreement about it. And on what is baʿl, and on what is watered by kaẓāʾim, and on what is a spring (najl): one-tenth.\" Maʿmar said: \"I have not heard any disagreement about it.\""],[7463,"What the rivers, the sky, and the springs water: one-tenth; and what is watered by the rope and bucket (rishāʾ): half of one-tenth."],[7464,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7465,"On what is watered with buckets and watering camels: half of one-tenth."],[7466,"Everything that entails no toil in watering it: on it one-tenth; and everything that entails toil in watering it with a bucket: on it half of one-tenth."],[7467,"All zakāh on fruits and crops: whether palm trees, grapevines, or crops of wheat, barley, or hull-less barley (sult) — what is baʿl, or is watered by a river, or is watered by a spring, or is ʿatharī watered by rain: on it one-tenth — one out of every ten. And what is watered by manual irrigation (naḍḥ): on it half of one-tenth — one out of every twenty."],[7468,"Simāk b. al-Faḍl gave me a letter from the Prophet ﷺ to Mālik b. Kafalānis and the Muṣʿabiyyīn. I read it, and in it: \"On what the sky and the rivers water: one-tenth; and on what is watered by the masnā (irrigation channel): half of one-tenth.\""],[7469,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7470,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7471,"I asked al-Ju'fi about a man who has grain from his land, intending to sell it, and he has already paid zakat on the original crop. He said: Al-Sha'bi said: 'No zakat is due on it until it is sold.' He said: And al-Nakha'i said: 'Zakat is due on it.'"],[7472,"We say regarding crops: if its zakāh is paid the first time, then a year passes while it is with you, do not pay zakāh on it again; the first suffices you.” He said: ʿAṭāʾ said to me: “The first suffices you.” He said: ʿAmr b. Dīnār said to me: “I have not heard anything about it other than the first.”"],[7473,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7474,"Regarding crops irrigated by a large bucket (gharb) and by rain: on which basis is its zakāh? He said: “On whichever gives it life, and on whichever predominates over it; its zakāh is according to that.”"],[7475,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7476,"He asked Ibn ʿUmar: Do you know whether ʿUmar took tithes from the Muslims? He said: “I do not know it. I do not know it.”"],[7477,"There is no zakāh on less than five awsuq; no zakāh on less than five camels; and no zakāh on less than five awāq."],[7478,"There is no zakāh on less than five awāq; no zakāh on less than five awsuq of grain; and no zakāh on less than five awsuq of dates."],[7479,"There is no zakāh on less than five awāq, nor on less than five awsuq, nor on less than five camels."],[7480,"The Prophet ﷺ gestured with his hand and five fingers: “There is no zakāh on less than five awāq, no zakāh on less than five awsuq, and no zakāh on less than five camels.” Ibn Jurayj said: “He means five camels.”"],[7481,"There is no zakāh on less than five awsuq; no zakāh on less than five camels; and no zakāh on less than five awāq."],[7482,"There is no charity due on grain or fruit until it reaches five awsuq, and there is no charity due on what is less than five awaq, and there is no charity due on what is less than five dhawd."],[7484,"There is no charity due on what is less than five awsuq, nor on what is less than five awaq, nor on what is less than five dhawd."],[7485,"There is no charity due on what is less than five awsuq, and there is no charity due on what is less than five awaq, or he said: zakah, and there is no charity due on what is less than five camels."],[7486,"There is no charity due on what is less than five awsuq of dates, and there is no charity due on what is less than five awaq of silver, and there is no charity due on what is less than five dhawd of camels."],[7487,"A wasq is sixty sa's, and five awsuq are three hundred sa's."],[7488,"A wasq is sixty sa's."],[7489,"There is no charity due on what is less than five awsuq. I said to him: How much is the sa'? He said: \"Four mudds by the mudd of the Prophet ﷺ.\" I said: How much is the mudd? He said: Some say: \"A ratl and a half,\" and others say: \"Two ratls.\""],[7490,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7491,"About the saying of Allah: \"And give its due on the day of its harvest,\" he said: \"At the time of sowing he gives a qabs, and at the time of harvest he gives a qabd, and he leaves them to follow the traces of the harvest.\" I said: What is qabd? He said: \"A handful of ears of grain.\" We said: What is qabs? He said: \"When you sow, you give them from the seed with the tips of your fingers,\" and he gestured with them."],[7492,"At the time of the date harvest, a bunch of dates would be cut off and people would eat from it."],[7494,"“And give its due on the day of its harvest.” He said: “The prescribed charity.” Saʿīd said: “And His statement: ‘And do not be extravagant.’ He said: ‘Do not withhold the charity and thus disobey.’”"],[7495,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7496,"He used to forbid closing the gate of the garden on the day the dates are cut and the grapes are picked, so that the poor could eat what falls from the palms and the grapes. They were left free with everything that falls, but they are left until a person may glean the grain. Thus they are left, along with what falls from the ears after what is given to them as a due in kindness.” He said: “That suffices you once you have lifted it from the threshing floor.”"],[7497,"Let no palms be cut at night, and let no milk be mixed with water for sale."],[7499,"When a woman spends from her husband's earnings without his command, he gets half its reward."],[7501,"If a person falls ill in Ramadan, then recovers but does not make it up until another Ramadan reaches him, he should fast the one that has newly come, then make up the other, and feed a poor person for each day."],[7502,"When a woman spends from her husband's food without being wasteful, she has her reward, and her husband has the like of that. Neither of them diminishes the other's reward at all. The treasurer has the like of that: for her what she spent, and for him what he earned."],[7503,"A woman was with ʿĀʾishah, and a woman asked her: “May a woman give charity from her husband's house?” She said: “Yes, as long as she does not protect her wealth with his wealth.”"],[7504,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage: “Allah has given every possessor of a right his right, so there is no bequest for an heir. The child belongs to the bed, and the stone belongs to the fornicator, and their reckoning is with Allah. Whoever claims descent from other than his father or allies himself with other than his patrons, upon him is the curse of Allah, continuing until the Day of Resurrection. And let no woman spend anything from his house except with her husband's permission.” It was said: “O Messenger of Allah, not even food?” He said: “That is the best of our wealth.” Then he said: “The borrowed item is to be returned, the gift (manīḥah) is to be given back, the debt is to be paid, and the guarantor is liable.”"],[7505,"A woman came to him and said, 'Is it lawful for me to take from my husband's dirhams?' He said, 'Is it lawful for him to take from your jewelry?' She said, 'No.' He said, 'His right over you is greater.'"],[7507,"He makes up both of them by fasting."],[7508,"No one is made to swear by the muṣḥaf; whoever pays something, it is accepted from him. They are entrusted with their zakat as they are entrusted with their prayer.\" ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: 'Rajāʾ b. Rūḥ wrote to al-Thawrī: Are people made to swear regarding their zakat by the muṣḥaf? He wrote back to him with this, while al-Thawrī was in Makkah.'"],[7509,"He should be fed for it."],[7510,"The owner of the wealth should pay what he recalls of the wealth, and he is not summoned to produce his wealth, nor made to swear."],[7511,"Deceive them, swear to them, lie to them, and scheme against them, and do not give them anything if they do not put it in its proper places."],[7514,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb gathered a group of Muslims and said, 'I want to put this fayʾ in its proper place, so let each man among you come to me in the morning with his opinion.' When morning came, he said, 'I found a verse from the Book of Allah, Exalted is He — or he said, verses — in which Allah has not left any Muslim with a share in this wealth without naming him. Allah says: \"And know that whatever you gain, for Allah is one-fifth of it and for the Messenger\" until he reached \"And whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain from it\" — the verse. Then he recited: \"For the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes\" up to \"Those are the truthful\" — this is for the Emigrants. Then he recited: \"And those who had settled in the abode and in faith before them\" until he reached \"And whoever is protected from the stinginess of his soul, those are the successful.\" Then he said, 'This is for the Anṣār.' Then he recited: \"And those who came after them, saying, 'Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith'\" until he reached \"Kind and Merciful.\" Then he said, 'There is no Muslim on earth who does not have a right in this wealth, whether he is given it or denied it.'"],[7515,"There is no zakat on endowed charity, meaning the zakat.\" We said to ʿAbd al-Razzāq, 'Why?' He said, 'Because he made it for the poor, and when they take something from it, is it not given to the poor?' We said, 'Yes.' He said, 'So there is no charity due on it.'"],[7516,"He should be fed for it."],[7518,"The child is commanded to pray when he knows his right from his left, and to fast when he is able to."],[7519,"In making up Ramadan: Keep count of the number, and fast however you wish."],[7520,"A man said to the Prophet ﷺ: \"I saw the Night of Decree as if it was such-and-such a night.\" He said: \"I see that your visions agree on the last ten, so seek it in the ninth, in an odd-numbered night.\""],[7522,"The boy is commanded to pray before fasting, because prayer is easier."],[7523,"He is beaten for it at ten years, and commanded to do it at seven years."],[7524,"The child was commanded to pray when he shed his milk teeth."],[7525,"When is prayer prescribed for the girl? He said: \"When she menstruates.\" He said: I said: And the boy? He said: \"When he has a wet dream.\""],[7526,"Ibn Abbas used to say: \"Wake the child to pray, even if only with a prostration.\""],[7527,"Be diligent with your children in prayer."],[7528,"If the boy fasts three consecutive days, the fasting of the month of Ramadan becomes obligatory upon him."],[7529,"If you do not see the crescent of Ramadan, then complete Sha'ban as thirty days; and if you do not see the crescent of Shawwal, then complete Ramadan as thirty days."],[7531,"Count the crescent of Sha'ban for the sighting of the month of Ramadan. If you see it, then fast; then if you see it, break the fast. If it is obscured from you, then complete the number."],[7532,"He disliked that Ramadan be made up in the ten days."],[7533,"If you see the crescent, then fast; and if you see it, then break the fast. If it is obscured from you, then fast thirty."],[7534,"Indeed, God has made the crescents as markers of time for the people. So fast at its sighting, and break the fast at its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then count for it thirty days."],[7535,"The Prophet ﷺ said concerning the crescent of Ramadan: If you see it, then fast; then if you see it, break the fast. If it is obscured from you, then complete thirty. Your fasting is on the day you fast, and your breaking the fast is on the day you break the fast."],[7536,"We fasted with Ali for twenty-eight days, and on the day of breaking the fast he ordered us to make up one day."],[7537,"I have not fasted twenty-nine more than I have fasted thirty."],[7538,"He entered with a man accompanying him upon Aisha on the day of Arafah. Aisha said: O girl, mix for them some sawiq and sweeten it. Were it not that I am fasting, I would taste it. They said: Are you fasting, O Mother of the Believers, while you do not know, perhaps it is the day of sacrifice? She said: \"Sacrifice is only when the imam slaughters and the majority of people do, and breaking the fast is when the imam breaks the fast and the majority of people do.\""],[7539,"I was with Ibn Abbas a day or two before Ramadan, and his lunch was brought near. He said: \"Break your fast, O fasting people. Do not join Ramadan with anything, and separate.\" He said: \"Ibn Abd al-Qari was fasting, and I think he broke his fast.\""],[7540,"‘Amr b. Shu‘ayb prayed Friday prayer, then prayed two rak‘ahs immediately after it in the mosque. Ibn ‘Umar forbade him from doing that and said: “As for the imam, no. When you have prayed, go back and pray in your house as much as you like, unless you are performing tawaf. As for the people, they may pray in the mosque.”"],[7542,"He said: \"Will it suffice for you to separate with the Day of Breaking the Fast?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Fast days before it or after it.\""],[7543,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade anticipating the month of Ramadan by fasting a day or two before it, except for a man who was accustomed to fasting regularly, in which case that day would coincide with his fasting."],[7544,"Separate between Sha'ban and Ramadan by breaking the fast for a day or two, or something like that."],[7545,"One day they woke up uncertain about fasting, and that was in Ramadan. I went to Anas b. Malik and found that he had gone out for some need, so I asked his family, 'Did he wake up fasting or not fasting?' They said, 'He drank a drink of milk, then went out.'"],[7546,"We were with Ammar b. Yasir on the day about which there is uncertainty regarding Ramadan, when a roasted sheep was brought. A man from the group withdrew, so he said, 'Come close.' The man said, 'I am fasting, and it is only a fast I have been observing.' Ammar said, 'Do you believe in Allah and the Last Day? Then eat.'"],[7547,"I saw him command a man after noon to break his fast, and he said, 'Whoever fasts this day has disobeyed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'"],[7548,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on six days: the day before Ramadan, the Day of Sacrifice, the Day of Breaking the Fast, and the three days of Tashriq."],[7549,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz delivered a sermon during his caliphate and said: 'Look for the crescent of Ramadan. If you see it, then fast; if you do not see it, then complete thirty days.' He said: The people woke up, some fasting and some not fasting, and they had not seen the crescent. Then news came to them that the crescent had been sighted. He said: The people spoke to Umar, and he sent guards through the army saying: 'Whoever woke up fasting should complete his fast, for he has been guided to it. Whoever woke up not fasting and has not tasted anything should complete the rest of his day. Whoever has eaten something should complete the rest of his day and make up a day in its place later. Indeed, today I licked a lick of honey, so I am fasting for the remainder of my day, then I will replace it later.'"],[7550,"If a man wakes up not fasting and has not tasted anything, then learns of the sighting at the beginning of the day or at its end, he should fast for the remainder of the day and does not have to make it up."],[7551,"The Prophet ﷺ led the people in prayer for three nights that remained of Ramadan."],[7552,"From Ibn Umar: When there were clouds, he would wake up fasting; when there were no clouds, he would wake up not fasting."],[7553,"There is no continuous fasting in fasting."],[7555,"I asked al-Qasim b. Muhammad about fasting the day about which there is doubt regarding Ramadan. He said: “If it is cloudy and it is thought that it is from Ramadan, he should not fast it.”"],[7556,"I said to ʿAtaʾ: A traveler entered a village and he had begun the day not fasting, but he had not tasted anything. He said: “He completes it.”"],[7557,"I said to ʿAmr b. Dinar: Is it not said regarding the one who has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan that he should complete that day and then make it up? And likewise the one who has intercourse with his wife during Hajj? He said: “Yes.”"],[7558,"That I break the fast on a day of Ramadan without relying on it is dearer to me than that I fast the day about which there is doubt regarding Shaʿban."],[7560,"I said to him: A person is not fasting on the day about which there is doubt, then the news comes. He said: He may eat and drink."],[7561,"ʿUmar wrote to us while we were at Khaniqin: If you see the crescent during the day, do not break the fast until two men testify that they saw it yesterday."],[7562,"ʿUmar wrote to ʿUtbah b. Farqad: If you see the crescent during the day before the sun begins to decline, completing thirty days, then break the fast; and if you see it after the sun begins to decline, do not break the fast until evening comes."],[7565,"We were with Salman b. Rabiʿah al-Bahili at Balanjar. He said: I saw the crescent at mid-morning for the completion of thirty days. I came to Salman b. Rabiʿah and told him. He came with me, and I showed it to him from a shaded place under a tree. He ordered the people, and they broke the fast."],[7566,"The people began the day fasting in the time of the Prophet ﷺ. Then two bedouins came and testified by Allah, besides whom there is no god, saying: “We saw it yesterday.” The Prophet ﷺ ordered the people, and they broke the fast."],[7567,"We said to Maʿmar: What do you think if two men testify that they saw it yesterday, and they testify at the end of the day, and they had arrived from a journey—should the people break the fast that evening? He said: Yes, and they go out the next day."],[7568,"Do not anticipate the crescent of this month until you see the crescent or complete the number before it. Then fast, and do not break the fast until you see the crescent or complete the number after it."],[7569,"Two men saw the crescent while they were traveling, and they hurried until they reached Madinah in the forenoon and informed Umar b. al-Khattab of that. Umar said to one of them, \"Are you fasting?\" He said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Why?\" He said, \"Because I disliked that the people be fasting while I am not fasting, and I disliked differing from them.\" Then he said to the other, \"And you?\" He said, \"I began the day not fasting.\" He said, \"Why?\" He said, \"Because I saw the crescent, and I disliked to fast.\" He said to the one who had broken his fast, \"Were it not for this one—meaning the one who fasted—we would have rejected your testimony and given you a beating on your head.\" Then he ordered the people, and they broke their fast and went out."],[7570,"The crescent of Shawwal was obscured from us, so we began the day fasting. Then riders came to the Prophet ﷺ at the end of the day and testified that they had seen the crescent the day before. The Prophet ﷺ ordered the people to break their fast that day and to go out for their Eid the next day."],[7571,"The crescent of Shawwal was sighted during the day, but Abdullah did not break his fast until evening, and he went out to the prayer place the next day."],[7572,"I said to Ata, \"I began the day fasting, and news came at the end of the day of its sighting.\" He said, \"Break your fast.\" Ibn Jurayj said, \"And Amr b. Dinar said, 'They break their fast whenever the news comes to them.'\""],[7573,"I said to Ata, \"I began the day fasting, and news came at the end of the day of its sighting.\" He said, \"Break your fast.\" Ibn Jurayj said, \"And Amr b. Dinar said, 'They break their fast whenever the news comes to them.'\""],[7575,"Umar b. al-Khattab accepted the testimony of a single man for sighting the crescent for breaking the fast or for the sacrifice (Eid al-Adha)."],[7576,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz used to accept a single man for the sighting of the crescent for fasting, but he did not accept fewer than two men for breaking the fast."],[7577,"If a man begins the day fasting in the month of Ramadan, then sets out as a traveler during the day, he should not break his fast that day unless he fears thirst for himself. If he fears it, he may break his fast, and he must make it up later. Then, if he wishes afterwards, he may break his fast, and if he wishes, he may fast."],[7578,"Only two men are acceptable for the sighting of the crescent."],[7579,"Uthman refused to accept Hashim b. Utbah al-Awar alone for the sighting of the crescent of Ramadan."],[7580,"I said to Ata, \"What do you think if a man saw the crescent of Ramadan one night before the people—should he fast before them and break his fast before them?\" He said, \"No, unless the people have also seen it. I fear that he may have been mistaken, unless there are two.\" I said, \"No, unless he saw it and watched it for a while?\" He said, \"Even so, until there are two.\""],[7581,"A man came to Umar b. al-Khattab and said, \"I saw the crescent of the month of Ramadan.\" He said, \"Did anyone else see it with you?\" He said, \"No.\" He said, \"What did you do?\" He said, \"I fasted with the fasting of the people.\" Umar said, \"What a good jurist you are!\""],[7582,"The cupper and the one who seeks cupping have broken their fast."],[7583,"When the Prophet ﷺ saw the crescent moon, he would say: “I believe in the One who created you, fashioned you, and proportioned you.”"],[7584,"While I was traveling, I saw the crescent moon and heard a speaker—whom I could not see—saying: “O Allah, bring it upon us with safety and Islam, security and faith, righteousness and piety, and success in what You love and are pleased with.” He kept repeating it until I memorized it."],[7585,"When the Prophet ﷺ saw the crescent moon, he would say “Allahu Akbar” three times, then say: “A crescent of good and guidance” three times. Then he would say: “I believe in the One who created you” three times. Then he would say: “Praise be to Allah who has taken away the month of such-and-such and brought the month of such-and-such.”"],[7586,"It has reached me that Ibrahim used to say regarding a traveler who arrives not fasting, or a menstruating woman who becomes pure at the end of her day: “They should not eat until evening.”"],[7587,"He should not eat until evening."],[7588,"They were asked about a menstruating woman who becomes pure before sunset. They said: “She may eat and drink.”"],[7590,"From Ata regarding a woman who began the day fasting but was menstruating: “If she becomes pure in the first part of the day, she should complete her day; otherwise, she should not.”"],[7591,"From Qatadah regarding a Christian or Jew who embraces Islam during part of the month of Ramadan: “He should fast what remains of the month.”"],[7592,"If a Christian embraces Islam during part of Ramadan, he should fast what has passed of it. If he embraces Islam at the end of the day, he should fast that day."],[7593,"From Ikrimah regarding a Christian who embraces Islam in the remaining days of Ramadan: “He should fast what he has caught up with and make up what he missed. If he embraces Islam on the last day of Ramadan, he is like a traveler who joins the prayer of the residents.”"],[7594,"If he embraces Islam during part of the month of Ramadan, he observes the fast for all of it."],[7595,"From al-Thawrī concerning a Christian who embraced Islam at the end of the day: He said: Whoever follows the view of ʿAṭāʾ says: He prays Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr. Whoever follows the view of al-Ḥasan says: He prays ʿAṣr and does not pray Ẓuhr. And he said: If he embraces Islam during the month of Ramadan, he does not fast that day on which he embraced Islam, but he is commanded not to eat until evening."],[7597,"If two men look at the dawn and one of them is uncertain, let them both eat until it becomes clear to them."],[7598,"\"Give me a drink, O boy!\" He said: \"It is morning.\" I said: \"No, by no means.\" Ibn ʿAbbās said: \"Doubt, by the life of God! Give me a drink.\" So he drank."],[7599,"God has made drink lawful for you as long as you are in doubt, until you are no longer in doubt."],[7602,"Ibn ʿAbbās was asked about a man who hears the call to prayer while night still rests upon him. He said: \"Let him eat.\" It was said: \"Even if he heard another caller to prayer?\" He said: \"One of them bore witness for the other.\""],[7603,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"Do you dislike that I drink while I am in the house, not knowing whether morning has come?\" He said: \"There is no harm in that; it is doubt.\""],[7604,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had cupping done while fasting."],[7605,"If he had himself cupped out of forgetfulness or ignorance, then no makeup is due from him."],[7606,"He completes his fast and does not make it up; Allah fed him and gave him drink."],[7607,"If a man has intercourse with his wife while fasting forgetfully in Ramadan, there is nothing upon him for that."],[7608,"I asked Ata about a man who had intercourse with his wife forgetfully in Ramadan. He said: This is not all forgotten; he must make it up. Allah has not made an excuse for him."],[7610,"A person came to Abu Hurayrah and said: I began the morning fasting, but I forgot and ate and drank. He said: No harm; Allah fed you and gave you drink. He said: Then I entered upon another person and forgot and ate and drank. He said: \"No harm; Allah fed you and gave you drink.\" He said: Then I entered upon another person and forgot and ate. Abu Hurayrah said: \"You are a person who has not returned to fasting.\""],[7611,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7612,"From Ibrahim regarding a man who rinses his mouth while fasting and water enters his throat: He said: If it was for the obligatory prayer, then he does not have to make it up; if it was voluntary, then he must make it up."],[7613,"From Ibn Abbas regarding a man who rinses his mouth while fasting and it enters his stomach: He said: If it was for the obligatory prayer, then there is nothing upon him; if it was voluntary, then he must make it up."],[7615,"The Prophet ﷺ said about the month of Ramadan: Indeed this month has arrived, and it is a blessed month. Allah has made its fasting obligatory. In it the gates of Hell are closed, the gates of Paradise are opened, and the devils are chained. In it there is a night better than a thousand months; whoever is deprived of it has indeed been deprived."],[7616,"When the month of Ramadan begins, the gates of mercy are opened, the gates of Hell are closed, and the devils are chained."],[7617,"When the first night of the month of Ramadan begins, the gates of Paradise are opened, and not one of them is closed for the entire month; the gates of the Fire are closed, and not one of them is opened for the entire month; and the rebellious jinn are chained. Then there are for Allah those freed from the Fire at the time of every breaking of the fast: male slaves and female slaves."],[7618,"We were mentioning the month of Ramadan. 'Utbah b. Farqad said: 'What are you mentioning?' He said: 'We were mentioning the month of Ramadan.' He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"In it the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of Hell are closed, the devils are chained, and a caller calls out every night: 'O seeker of good, come forward; O seeker of evil, desist.'\"'"],[7619,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What does ‘can bear it with hardship’ mean?” He said: “They are burdened with it.”"],[7620,"That ʿUmar and ʿUthmān used to pray Maghrib in Ramadan before breaking their fast."],[7621,"If a man breaks his fast in Ramadan and then the sun appears, he must make it up; but if he eats at dawn while thinking it is still night, he does not have to make it up."],[7623,"He completes it and makes up a day in its place. And if he eats while thinking that night is still upon him, and then it turns out that morning has come, he must make it up."],[7624,"The people broke their fast in the month of Ramadan on a cloudy day. Then someone looked and saw the sun. 'Umar b. al-Khattab said: 'The matter is simple; we have exercised judgment and we will make up a day.'"],[7625,"We were with 'Umar b. al-Khattab in the month of Ramadan when a large platter of food was brought. The muezzin said: 'O you people, the sun has risen.' 'Umar said: 'May Allah protect us, or may Allah spare us from your evil! We did not send you as a watcher over the sun; we sent you to call to prayer. O people, whoever has broken his fast, making up one day is easy; and whoever has not broken his fast, let him complete his fast.'"],[7626,"We were with 'Umar b. al-Khattab in Ramadan, and the sky was cloudy. Some sawiq was brought, and the sun appeared. He said: 'Whoever has broken his fast, let him make up a day in its place.'"],[7627,"Complete this day of yours, then make up a day."],[7628,"The people broke their fast during the time of 'Umar. He said: I saw large bowls being brought out from the house of Hafsah, and they drank in Ramadan. Then the sun emerged from behind clouds, and that seemed hard on the people. They said: 'Shall we make up this day?' 'Umar said: 'Why? By Allah, we have not inclined to any sin.'"],[7629,"'Whoever is overtaken by dawn while in a state of janabah has no fast.' He said: So I and my father went and entered upon 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, and asked them about that. They informed us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would wake up in a state of janabah not from a wet dream, then he would fast. He said: Then we entered upon Marwan and informed him of their statement and the statement of Abu Hurayrah. He said: 'I adjure you both to go to Abu Hurayrah and inform him of their statement.' He said: We met Abu Hurayrah at the gate of the mosque, and my father said to him: 'The amir has adjured us concerning a matter to mention it to you.' He said: 'What is it?' He said: My father told him. He said: The face of Abu Hurayrah changed color, then he said: 'This is how al-Fadl b. al-'Abbas narrated it to us, and he knows best.'"],[7630,"He would be overtaken by dawn while he was in a state of janabah from his wife, then he would perform ghusl and fast."],[7631,"Whoever dawn overtakes while junub, there is no fast for him."],[7632,"By the Lord of this House, whoever dawn overtakes while junub, let him break his fast; but Muhammad ﷺ said..."],[7633,"I said to Ata: May a man spend the night junub in Ramadan, deliberately doing so, until morning comes, and then fast? He said: As for Abu Hurayrah, he used to forbid that; as for Aishah, she used to say: There is no harm in that. When they differed before Ata, he said: He should complete that day, and make up another day."],[7634,"I do not mind having intercourse with my wife, then waking up junub, then fasting; I came to what is lawful."],[7635,"A man from the tribe came to me and said: I passed by my wife in the moonlight and she pleased me, so I had intercourse with her in Ramadan, then I slept until morning. I said: Go to Abdullah b. Masud or to Abu Hakim al-Muzani. So he went to Abdullah and asked him, and he said: You were junub, so prayer was not lawful for you; then you performed ghusl, and prayer became lawful for you, and fasting became lawful for you, so fast."],[7636,"A man came to Abu al-Darda and said: I had intercourse with my wife, then sleep overcame me until morning, while I intended to fast. Abu al-Darda said: You came to your wife, and she was lawful to you; then you were overcome; then Allah restored your soul to you, so you prayed when you regained your senses, and you fasted when you regained your senses."],[7637,"If the muezzin gave the call to prayer while Abdullah was between the legs of his wife and he intended to fast, he would complete his fast."],[7638,"Whoever dawn overtakes while junub, deliberately doing so, must make up the fast; and whoever that happens to unintentionally must not make it up."],[7639,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to forbid the fasting person from kissing. It was said to him: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to kiss while fasting,' so he said: 'Who has the self-restraint and protection that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had?'"],[7640,"The Prophet ﷺ used to kiss while fasting. Then Ibn Abbas said: 'The Prophet ﷺ used to touch the heads (i.e., kiss their heads) while fasting.'"],[7641,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to kiss some of his wives while fasting."],[7642,"When the night approaches, the day retreats, and the sun sets, the fasting person has broken the fast."],[7643,"The Messenger of God ﷺ reached for me to kiss me, so I said, “I am fasting.” He said, “And I am fasting,” then he kissed me."],[7644,"She was with ʿĀʾisha, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, when her husband, ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, entered upon her; he was fasting in Ramadan. ʿĀʾisha said to him, “What prevents you from approaching your wife, playing with her, and kissing her?” He said, “Should I kiss her while I am fasting?” She said, “Yes.”"],[7646,"I heard Ibn ʿAbbās being asked about the kiss for a fasting person. He said, “There is no harm in it, if he restrains himself from what follows it.” Someone said, “May he touch her leg?” He also said, “Excuse the fasting person; he should not touch her leg.”"],[7647,"I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say the like of the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj. [Hidden text]: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās being asked about the kiss for a fasting person, and he said, “There is no harm in it, if he restrains himself from what follows it.” Someone said, “May he touch her leg?” He also said, “Excuse the fasting person; he should not touch her leg.”"],[7649,"He was asked about the kiss for a fasting person. He said, “It leads to something else, and abstaining is more prudent.”"],[7650,"Someone who heard the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ forbidding one another from kissing while fasting told me, and they would say, “Perhaps you will be led to something greater than it.”"],[7651,"An old man came to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the kiss while he was fasting, and he permitted it for him. Then a young man came to him, and he forbade him."],[7652,"I was sitting with Ibn Abbas when a man came to him and said: Two Ramadans have passed over me successively. Ibn Abbas said: \"By God, has this happened?\" He said: Yes. He said: \"No.\" He said: So the man left. Then another came and said: A man has had two Ramadans pass over him successively. He said: \"By God, has this happened?\" He said: Yes. Ibn Abbas said: \"One of seven: he fasts two months and feeds sixty poor people.\""],[7654,"It was said to Abu Hurayrah: Do you kiss while you are fasting? He said: Yes, and I engage in mouth-to-mouth contact, meaning he opens his mouth to her mouth."],[7655,"A man asked Abu Hurayrah: A man kissed his wife while he was fasting; has he broken his fast? He said: No. He said: What about other than that? He said: Abu Hurayrah turned away."],[7657,"He makes it up consecutively."],[7658,"Ibn Umar was asked: May a man kiss while he is fasting? He said: Would he not kiss a live coal?"],[7659,"From Ibn Mas'ud concerning a man who kisses while he is fasting: He said: He makes up a day in its place."],[7661,"Ali said concerning kissing for the fasting person: What need has he for the bad breath of her mouth?"],[7662,"Consecutively."],[7663,"They asked Ibn al-Musayyab about a man who kisses his wife while he is fasting. He said: If you kiss, it does not break your fast, but it diminishes your fast."],[7665,"Fast however you wish. Allah said: \"Then a number of other days.\""],[7666,"That it reduces from the fast of the one who touches or undresses, but you may take her hand and the nearest part of her body, and leave the farthest part."],[7667,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a man who has intimate contact while fasting. He said: \"He should repent ten times.\""],[7668,"He said regarding intimate contact for the fasting person: \"There is no harm in it; it is only like a morsel he smelled.\" He said: \"Allah has permitted that he take her hand and the nearest part of her body, and not take the farthest part.\""],[7669,"I said to 'Ata': \"He has intimate contact with her, skin to skin, during the day?\" He said: \"His fast is not invalidated, but he should make up a day in place of that day, and he does not break the fast.\" I said: \"He had intimate contact with her, skin to skin, until morning?\" He said: \"There is no harm in that, whether he was seeking warmth or not, if nothing came out of him.\" Then he said after that: \"If it was at the time of dawn, then no.\""],[7670,"I said to 'Ata': \"He had intimate contact with her during the day, touching her upper part?\" He said: \"He should not do that.\" I said: \"He has intimate contact with her during the day with a cloth between them?\" He said: \"As for something he deliberately does of that, then no.\""],[7672,"I asked 'A'ishah: \"What is lawful for a man from his wife while he is fasting?\" She said: \"Everything except sexual intercourse.\""],[7673,"I saw al-Hasan meet Abu Rafi'. He said: \"I was between them.\" He said: \"Al-Hasan said to him: 'May the fasting person kiss and have intimate contact?' Abu Rafi' said: 'He should neither kiss nor have intimate contact.'\""],[7674,"We set out as pilgrims, and we discussed the fasting person kissing and having intimate contact. A man from al-Nakha' said: \"He has fasted for two years and stood in prayer for them, and he is muscular. I was about to take this bow of mine and strike you with it.\" They came to 'A'ishah and said to 'Alqamah: \"O Abu Shibl.\" He said: \"I am not one to speak obscenely in her presence today.\" She heard him and said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to kiss and have intimate contact while fasting, and he was the most able of you to control his desire.\""],[7675,"Ibn Mas'ud used to have intimate contact with his wife in the middle of the day while fasting."],[7676,"Fasting is a shield. When one of you is fasting on a day, he should not behave ignorantly or speak obscenely. If a man fights him, let him say: 'I am fasting.'"],[7677,"Sa'd b. Malik used to rub her private part with his hand while fasting."],[7678,"I asked 'Ata': He gripped her vulva directly. He said: He should not do it, and if he does, he need not make up another day in place of that day."],[7679,"I asked 'Ata': May he feel and touch repeatedly what is under the garment? He said: No. I asked: What about what is over the garment? He said: I do not like that."],[7680,"Have you considered if he uncovered and examined, and sat between her legs, then withdrew without emitting ejaculatory fluid (al-ma' al-dafiq)? He said: His fast is not invalidated, but he should make up a day in place of that day, and it does not break his fast."],[7681,"I asked 'Ata': If he intended to satisfy his need without reaching her private part, then withdrew without emitting ejaculatory fluid (al-ma' al-dafiq)? He said: His fast is not invalidated, but he should make up a day in place of that day, and it does not break his fast."],[7682,"If ejaculatory fluid comes from his playful activity, then upon him is the same obligation as upon the one who has intercourse."],[7683,"Regarding a man who kisses during the day in Ramadan, or has skin contact, or engages in foreplay and emits pre-ejaculate (madhy): He said: Nothing is upon him, though what he did is bad. If the ejaculatory fluid (al-ma' al-dafiq) comes out from him, then he is in the same position as one who has had intercourse. He said: And Qatadah said: If the ejaculatory fluid comes out from him, then upon him is only to fast a day."],[7684,"If a man engages in playful touching with his wife while fasting until the ejaculatory fluid comes from him, then upon him is the expiation (al-zakah). He said: I asked 'Ata': What do you think about whatever moves the penis of the fasting person in the month of Ramadan, and pre-ejaculate (madhy) comes out with his moving it? He said: There is nothing upon him in that as long as there was no direct skin contact or something approaching that."],[7685,"Whoever gazes at the form of a woman while fasting has invalidated his fast."],[7686,"'Isa son of Maryam said: Looking plants desire in the heart, and it is enough as a trial for its owner."],[7687,"You do not have the right to stare persistently at a woman while she is wearing her clothes."],[7688,"Whoever does not abandon false speech and obscene talk, then Allah has no need for him to abandon his food and drink."],[7689,"I asked 'Ata': Has it reached you that a person, when invited to food, should say: I am fasting? He said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: When you are fasting, do not be ignorant or insult, and if someone is ignorant toward you, say: I am fasting."],[7690,"I had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan. Then he mentioned something like the hadith of Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd b. 'Abd al-Rahman. Hidden text: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, I am ruined. He said: \"And what is that?\" He said: I had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan. He said: \"Can you find a slave?\" He said: No. He said: \"Can you fast two consecutive months?\" He said: No. He said: \"Then feed sixty poor persons.\" He said: I cannot find, O Messenger of God. He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ was brought a basket of dates – and the basket is the large basket – and said: \"Go and give this in charity.\" He said: To someone poorer than me? By the One who sent you with the truth, there is no household between its two lava fields more in need than mine. The Messenger of God ﷺ laughed, then said: \"Take it to your family.\""],[7691,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, the last one is ruined. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"And what is that?\" He said: I touched my wife in Ramadan. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"Can you free a slave?\" He said: No. He said: \"Then sacrifice a she-camel.\" He said: I cannot find one. He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ was brought a basket containing fifteen sa's. He said: \"Give this in charity.\" The man complained to him of need, so he said: \"It is for you and your family,\" or he said: \"Twenty sa's.\""],[7692,"A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ striking his chest, pulling out his hair, and saying: The furthest one is ruined. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"What is the matter with you?\" He said: I transgressed in the month of Ramadan. He said: \"Can you free a slave?\" He said: No. He said: \"Then sacrifice.\" He said: Do you mean a she-camel? He said: \"It is nothing but that.\" He said: I cannot find one. He said: \"Then sit.\" So he sat, and a man came with a basket containing twenty sa's of dates, or fifteen sa's. Then he said to the Bedouin: \"Give these in charity.\" He complained to him of need, so he said: \"It is for you and your family.\""],[7693,"If he wishes, he may separate it."],[7695,"That the Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"Give charity, and fast a day in its place.\""],[7696,"A slave, then a she-camel."],[7698,"If he has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan, then eats and drinks, it is a single expiation, like the expiation for intercourse."],[7699,"About the one who has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan? He said: The Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"Free a slave.\" He said: I cannot find one. He said: \"Then give something in charity.\" He said: I do not know except that he said: \"Then make up a day in its place.\""],[7700,"I said: \"What do you think if a man owes some days from Ramadan, and he begins the day not intending to fast, then later after morning he decides to fast – can he count it as making up Ramadan, even though he did not intend it before dawn?\" He said: \"Let him fast it, and count it as making up Ramadan.\""],[7701,"He disliked that Ramadan be made up in the ten days."],[7702,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyab about a man who ate intentionally in Ramadan. He said: 'He must fast a month.' He said: I said: 'Two days?' He said: 'Fasting a month.' He said: Then I counted some days, and he said: 'Fasting a month.'"],[7703,"Ubayy used to lead the people during the time of Umar in Ramadan. When it reached the middle of the month, he recited the qunut aloud after the rak'ah. When twenty nights were completed, he departed to his family, and Abu Halima Mu'adh al-Qari led the people, reciting the qunut aloud in the last ten nights. They would hear him say: O Allah, the rain has been withheld—and they would say: Amin. Then he would say: How quick you are to say Amin! Let me finish supplicating."],[7704,"I asked al-Sha'bi about a man who broke his fast for a day in Ramadan? He said: 'What do the strict ones say about it?' He said: Then al-Sha'bi said: 'He fasts a day in its place and seeks forgiveness from Allah.'"],[7708,"Whoever breaks his fast on a day of Ramadan without a dispensation from Allah, fasting for a lifetime will not make up for it, even if he fasts it."],[7709,"Whoever breaks his fast on a day of Ramadan without a dispensation from Allah will meet Allah with it, even if he fasts the entire time. If He wills, He will forgive him, and if He wills, He will punish him."],[7710,"He disliked that a person should insert anything during the day in Ramadan. If he does so, he should replace that day, but he should not break his fast on that day."],[7711,"The one who has an enema with wine breaks his fast, but the legal punishment is not inflicted on him."],[7712,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ use the toothstick while he was fasting more times than I can count."],[7713,"If a man has relations with his wife while making up Ramadan, he replaces that day, and no expiation is due from him.' I said: 'What if he kisses her?' He said: 'He also replaces that day, but he does not break his fast.'"],[7714,"He grants a concession for someone who is extremely thirsty while making up a Ramadan fast to break it. Ibn Jurayj said: I sent a man to ask him: 'Is making up a Ramadan fast to be treated like a voluntary fast?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[7716,"Consecutively."],[7717,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ use the toothstick while he was fasting more times than I can count."],[7718,"I have never seen a man more diligent with the siwak than Umar b. al-Khattab while fasting, but only with a dry stick."],[7719,"I have made my mouth bleed twice today while fasting from using the siwak."],[7720,"I said to Ata: 'May the fasting person use the siwak?' He said: 'Yes.' It was said to him: 'May he swallow his saliva?' He said: I said: 'So if he does that, has he broken his fast?' He said: 'No, but it is discouraged.' He said: I said: 'What if he swallows it while he is being told that it is discouraged?' He said: 'Then he has broken his fast,' and he repeated that more than once."],[7722,"He heard Maymun b. Mihran disapproving of the siwak for the fasting person at the end of the day, so he asked al-Hasan, who said: 'There is no harm in it at the end of the day. It is only a means of purification, so let him use it at the beginning and the end of the day.'"],[7723,"I said to Ata: 'What is discouraged regarding the siwak?' He said: 'If the siwak is dry and no moisture comes from it.' I said: 'What is meant by the moisture of the siwak?' He said: 'The saliva that is on it, coming from the head and the mouth.' I said: 'What if the siwak is dry and has no sap?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[7724,"He used to clean his teeth with a fresh siwak while fasting."],[7725,"He did not see any harm in using a fresh siwak for the fasting person."],[7726,"Consecutively."],[7727,"He used to dislike a fresh palm branch that a fasting person uses as a siwak, because of its taste."],[7730,"There is no harm in using a green siwak for a fasting person."],[7731,"I said to 'Ata': May a fasting person chew gum? He said: No. I said: He spits out the saliva of the gum and does not swallow it or suck it. He said: If he does not swallow its saliva, it is a means of quenching for him, but if he swallows its saliva, while saying he is forbidden from that, then he has broken his fast."],[7732,"'Umar b. al-Khattab summoned the companions of Muhammad ﷺ and asked them about the Night of Decree, and they agreed that it is in the last ten. Ibn 'Abbas said: I said to 'Umar: \"I know, or I think I know, which night it is.\" 'Umar said: \"Which night is it?\" I said: \"The seventh that passes, or the seventh that remains, of the last ten.\" 'Umar said: \"How did you know that?\" He said: \"Allah created seven heavens, seven earths, and seven days, and time revolves in seven; Allah created man from seven, he eats from seven, he prostrates on seven, the circumambulation of the House is seven, and the stoning of the jamarat is seven – for things he mentioned –.\" 'Umar said: \"You have perceived something we did not perceive.\""],[7734,"I asked 'Ata' about rinsing the mouth for a fasting person other than for prayer, and he said: I only dislike it because of the saying of Abu Hurayra: I heard him say: The bad breath of a fasting person is sweeter to Allah than the fragrance of musk."],[7735,"There is no harm if a fasting person swallows his saliva."],[7736,"I said to 'Ata': He rinsed his mouth while fasting, then he spat out the water; does it harm him if he swallows it? He said: It does not harm him, and what remains in his mouth?"],[7737,"'Ata' used to say: \"He may delay it as long as another Ramadan has not overtaken him.\""],[7738,"I saw Uthman b. Abi al-As at Arafah while he was fasting, sipping water and pouring water over himself."],[7739,"When one of you is fasting and breaks his fast, he should not rinse his mouth and then spit it out; rather, he should drink it, for its first part is its best. And he should not wipe his hand with a towel until he licks it or has it licked."],[7740,"He rinsed his mouth once while fasting at the time of iftar, then spat it out. A man said to him, 'Is this not disliked?' He said, 'Yes, but I forgot.'"],[7741,"The Prophet ﷺ, while at al-Arj, used to pour water over his head while fasting."],[7742,"The Prophet ﷺ set out in Ramadan on the day of the conquest while fasting. When he reached al-Arj, fasting became hard for him, so he poured water over his head while fasting."],[7743,"I asked Hammad about a fasting woman tasting broth, and he saw no harm in that. He said: 'They say: nothing is more effective in that than the water with which the fasting person rinses his mouth.'"],[7744,"He did not see any harm in a fasting woman chewing for her child."],[7745,"I saw him chew food for the child while fasting. He said: 'He chews it, then takes it out of his mouth and puts it into the child's mouth.' Yunus said: 'I used to visit him while he was fasting in intense heat, and he would rinse his mouth with water and spit it out from midday until afternoon, and that was in Rajab.'"],[7746,"He disliked the fasting person applying kohl of aloe, but saw no harm in antimony."],[7747,"I said to Ata': 'Aloe — may the fasting person apply it as kohl?' He said: 'Yes, if he wishes.'"],[7748,"He asked Ibrahim about aloe for the fasting person. He said: 'Apply it as kohl, but do not snuff it.'"],[7749,"There is no harm in kohl for the fasting person."],[7750,"If the fasting person applies kohl, he must make up a day in its place. He said: \"His father used to dislike kohl for the fasting person.\""],[7751,"Ablution is only from what comes out, and fasting is from what goes in, and it is not from what comes out."],[7752,"The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast."],[7753,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by a man being cupped on the eighteenth of Ramadan while I was with him, and he said: \"The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.\""],[7754,"7577 - 7521 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Isma'il b. Abd Allah, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-Sana'ani, from Shaddad b. Aws, the like of it, and he added: \"That was on the Day of the Conquest.\" The hidden matn: The Prophet ﷺ passed by a man being cupped on the eighteenth of Ramadan while I was with him, and he said: \"The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.\""],[7756,"The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast."],[7757,"The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast."],[7758,"The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast."],[7759,"The cupper and the one who seeks cupping have broken their fast."],[7760,"I asked Abu Hurayrah about the fasting person being cupped. He said: \"They say the cupper and the cupped have broken their fast, but if I were cupped, I would not care.\""],[7762,"I asked Abu Hurayrah about a man who has cupping done while fasting. He said, \"What do you think if he loses consciousness?\""],[7763,"Ibn Umar did not have cupping done while fasting."],[7764,"Ibn Umar used to have cupping done while fasting, then he abandoned it. After that, when the sun set, he would have cupping done."],[7765,"Ibn Umar used to have cupping done while fasting, then he abandoned it. He would prepare the cups, and when the sun set, he would order him to make the incisions. He said, \"I do not know whether he disliked it or whether something reached him.\""],[7766,"In Ramadan, Ibn Umar would prepare the cupper, his cups, and his needs, so that when the fasting person broke his fast, he would have cupping done at night."],[7767,"I said to Ata, \"What do you think if a person has cupping done in Ramadan? Does he make up a day in place of that day?\" He said, \"Yes, he has broken his fast, and he must expiate according to what the Prophet ﷺ said.\" I asked, \"What do you think if a person cups his shin?\" He said, \"It is the same; he has broken his fast.\""],[7768,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade cupping for the fasting person and continuous fasting, but he did not make them unlawful, as a concession for his companions. They said, \"O Messenger of Allah! You practice continuous fasting until dawn.\" He said, \"I practice continuous fasting until dawn, and my Lord feeds me and gives me drink.\""],[7769,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had cupping done while fasting."],[7770,"He asked al-Qasim b. Muhammad whether the fasting person may have cupping done. He said, \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had cupping done while fasting.\""],[7771,"The one who vomits, the one who has cupping done, and the one who has a wet dream do not break their fast."],[7773,"Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas and A'ishah saw no harm in it, and they would have cupping done while fasting."],[7774,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had himself cupped while he was fasting and in the state of ihram between Makkah and Madinah."],[7775,"From Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that she used to have herself cupped while she was fasting."],[7776,"We fasted with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during Ramadan, and he did not lead us in night prayer at all during the month until seven nights remained. Then he led us until about a third of the night had passed. Then he did not lead us on the fourth night, but led us on the following night until about half the night had passed. We said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you would grant us voluntary prayers for the rest of this night of ours. He said: When a man stands with the imam until he finishes, the rest of his night is reckoned for him. Then he did not lead us on the sixth night, but led us on the seventh night, and he sent for his family and the people gathered. He led us until we feared we would miss the falah. I said: What is the falah? He said: The pre-dawn meal."],[7777,"Husayn b. 'Ali b. Abi Talib had himself cupped while he was fasting."],[7778,"If he had himself cupped out of forgetfulness or ignorance, then no makeup is due from him."],[7779,"That he \"used to have himself cupped while fasting, then he would not break the fast.\""],[7780,"I said to 'Ata': A person induced vomiting out of forgetfulness or ignorance. He said: He does not make up that day, and he completes it. He said: 'Ata' said: \"If a person deliberately induces vomiting in Ramadan, then he has broken his fast, and if he forgot, then he has not broken his fast.\""],[7781,"The Messenger of God ﷺ induced vomiting, so he broke his fast; water was brought and he performed ablution."],[7782,"He induced vomiting in Ramadan? He said: He makes up that day and performs expiation according to what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. But if he was forgetful or ignorant—"],[7783,"Whoever induces vomiting has broken his fast and must make it up, but whoever is overcome by vomiting has not broken his fast."],[7784,"Whoever induces vomiting has broken his fast and must make it up, but whoever is overcome by vomiting has not broken his fast."],[7785,"If you vomit or induce vomiting inadvertently, you have not broken your fast."],[7787,"He used to complete the Quran in the month of Ramadan every three days, but when the last ten entered, he would complete it in two nights and perform a ritual bath every night."],[7788,"The pregnant woman who is in her month and the nursing woman who fears for her child break their fast and each of them feeds a poor person every day, and they do not have to make up the fasts."],[7790,"The pregnant woman who is in her month and the nursing woman who fears for her child break their fast and each of them feeds a poor person every day, and they do not have to make up the fasts."],[7791,"The pregnant and nursing women break their fast in Ramadan when they fear for their children in summer.\" He said: \"And in winter when they fear for their children.\""],[7792,"Abd Allah b. 'Amr b. 'Uthman sent me to Ibn 'Umar to ask him about a woman who was pregnant during Ramadan. He said: 'She breaks her fast and feeds a poor person every day.'"],[7793,"Umar did not gather the people of Makkah behind one reciter because of circumambulation; he left whoever wished to circumambulate."],[7794,"A man came to Madinah and entered upon the Prophet ﷺ for some need of his while the Prophet ﷺ was eating. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Come near.' He said: 'I am fasting.' Then he said: 'Come near, for the traveler has been relieved of fasting and half of the prayer, and so have the pregnant and nursing women.'"],[7795,"The pregnant woman, if she fears for herself in Ramadan, breaks her fast and feeds, and there is no makeup for her."],[7796,"If I had only two surahs with me, repeating them would be dearer to me."],[7799,"They both make up the fast, in the same position as a sick person: he breaks the fast and makes it up, and the breastfeeding woman likewise."],[7800,"A woman came to 'Alqamah and said: 'I am pregnant, and I am able to fast, but my husband forbids me.' 'Alqamah said to her: 'Obey your Lord and disobey your husband.'"],[7801,"He used to command a pregnant slave girl of his to break the fast in the month of Ramadan, and he said: 'You are in the same position as an elderly person who is not able to fast, so break the fast and feed half a sa' of wheat for each day.'"],[7802,"I said to 'Ata': 'On account of what ailment may one break the fast in Ramadan?' He said: 'On account of any of it.' I said: 'He fasts until [.... ] he breaks the fast?' He said: 'Yes, as Allah said.'"],[7803,"'Ata' was asked: 'Does a man have a dispensation to compel his servant to break the fast in the month of Ramadan?' He said: 'No.' A man said to him: 'Does a shepherd have a dispensation to break the fast?' He said: 'I have not heard of any dispensation for him.' He said: 'He does not see the property except as a quarter or a third.' He said: 'He does not break the fast.'"],[7804,"Anas b. Malik became old until he was not able to fast, so he used to break the fast and feed others."],[7807,"Ibn 'Abbas said about this verse: 'And upon those who are able to fast, a ransom of feeding a poor person.' No other verse abrogated it: 'So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it.'"],[7808,"It is not abrogated. He used to recite it as 'they are made to bear it.' It concerns the elderly person who is obligated to fast but is not able to do it, so he breaks the fast and feeds others."],[7809,"He used to recite it as: 'And upon those who are made to bear it,' and he would say: 'It is the elderly person who is not able to fast, so he breaks the fast and feeds a poor person half a sa' of wheat for each day.'"],[7810,"I said to Ata: What about fasting while traveling? He said: \"Break your fast when you shorten the prayer, and fast when you complete the prayer.\""],[7811,"'A'ishah used to recite: 'And they are made to bear it.'"],[7813,"He abrogated His statement: “And upon those who can bear it with hardship” with “So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it.”"],[7814,"He used to recite: “And upon those who can bear it with hardship.” He said: “It applies to the elderly man and the elderly woman when they are unable to fast: they must feed a poor person each day, each one of them. If they cannot find the means, then nothing is required of them.”"],[7815,"He feeds a poor person each day a makkūk of wheat or a makkūk of dates."],[7816,"I asked Ṭāwūs about my mother, who had a chronic thirst and was unable to fast Ramadan. He said: “She feeds a poor person each day a mudd of wheat.” I said: “With which mudd?” He said: “The mudd of your land.”"],[7817,"Regarding His statement: “So whoever does good voluntarily,” they both said: “He feeds another poor person.”"],[7819,"He prays alone."],[7820,"It applies to the elderly man when he is unable to fast: he redeems each day by feeding a poor person a mudd of wheat."],[7821,"When one of you breaks his fast, let him break it with dates; if he cannot find any, let him break it with water, for water is purifying."],[7822,"7643 7587 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq, from al-Thawrī, from ʿĀṣim, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from al-Rabāb, from Salmān b. ʿĀmir al-Ḍabbī, from the Prophet ﷺ, the same as it. Hidden text: When one of you breaks his fast, let him break it with dates; if he cannot find any, let him break it with water, for water is purifying."],[7824,"I was sitting with ʿUmar when a rider came to him from Syria, and ʿUmar began inquiring about their condition. He said: \"Do the people of Syria hasten the breaking of the fast?\" He said: Yes. He said: \"They will remain in good as long as they do that and do not wait for the stars as the people of Iraq wait.\""],[7825,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to the people of the garrison towns: Do not be among those who are overtaken by your iftar, nor among those who wait for your prayer until the stars interweave."],[7826,"The companions of Muḥammad ﷺ were the fastest of people to break the fast and the slowest to take the pre-dawn meal."],[7827,"People will remain in good as long as they hasten the breaking of the fast."],[7828,"I used to bring Ibn ʿUmar a cup at his breaking of the fast and shield him from the people, and he had nothing but shyness—he says: from the speed with which he broke the fast."],[7829,"We were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, and he said to a man of the people: 'Dismount and mix for me something,' while he was fasting. He said: 'The sun, O Messenger of God.' He said: 'Dismount and mix for me.' He said: So he dismounted and mixed for him, and he drank. And he said: 'Even if someone on his camel could see it—meaning the sun—he would see it.' Then the Prophet ﷺ pointed with his hand toward the east and said: 'When you see the night approaching from here, the fasting person has broken the fast.'"],[7830,"When the night approaches, the day retreats, and the sun sets, the fasting person has broken the fast."],[7831,"A person is commanded to break the fast before praying, even if with a sip."],[7832,"I used to attend Ibn ʿAbbās at the breaking of the fast in Ramaḍān. His food would be set out, then he would order a watcher to watch the sun. When he said: 'It has set,' he said: 'Eat.' He said: Then we would break the fast before the prayer."],[7833,"Take the pre-dawn meal, for in the pre-dawn meal there is blessing."],[7834,"Take the pre-dawn meal, even if with a gulp of water."],[7835,"Come to the wholesome, blessed meal—meaning the pre-dawn meal—for a man."],[7836,"Take the predawn meal (sahur), for truly in the predawn meal there is blessing."],[7837,"The difference between our fast and the fast of the People of the Book is eating the predawn meal."],[7838,"Seek help through the midday nap for standing in prayer at night, and through eating the predawn meal for fasting during the day."],[7839,"What an excellent aid is the midday nap for standing in prayer at night."],[7840,"He prays alone."],[7841,"I went with Zirr b. Hubaysh to Hudhayfah, who was in the house of al-Harith b. Abi Rabi'ah. We sought permission to enter, and he came out to us. He brought milk and said: \"Drink.\" We said: \"We intend to fast.\" He said: \"And I intend to fast.\" Then he drank, then handed it to Zirr, who drank, then handed it to me, and I drank, while the mu'adhdhin was calling the adhan in the mosque. He said: \"When we entered the mosque, the prayer had commenced, and they were performing it in the dark before dawn.\""],[7842,"\"He ate the predawn meal with Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas in Kufa during Ramadan, then he went out and I was with him. He came to the mosque and the prayer had commenced.\" He said: I said: \"How much was between his house and the mosque?\" He said: \"What is between the grave of Ziyad b. Fayruz and the Great Mosque.\""],[7843,"Bilal came to the Prophet ﷺ while the Prophet ﷺ was eating the predawn meal, and said: \"The prayer, O Messenger of God!\" He said: He remained as he was, eating. Then he came to him again and said: \"The prayer,\" while he was still in that state. Then he came a third time and said: \"The prayer, O Messenger of God! By God, it has become morning for you.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"May God have mercy on Bilal. Were it not for Bilal, we would have hoped for a concession until the sun rose.\""],[7844,"I came to 'Ali while he was encamped at Dayr Abi Musa and he was eating the predawn meal. He said: \"Come close.\" I said: \"I intend to fast.\" He said: \"And I intend to fast.\" When he finished, he said to the mu'adhdhin: \"Commence the prayer.\""],[7845,"Part of seventy parts of prophethood is delaying the pre-dawn meal, hastening the breaking of the fast, and a man's pointing with his finger during the prayer."],[7847,"They were uncertain about the Prophet's ﷺ fasting at Arafah, so Umm al-Fadl said: 'I will inform you about that.' She sent him a wooden bowl of milk, and he drank from it."],[7848,"Ibn Umm Maktum used to give the call to prayer for the Prophet ﷺ while he was blind. He would not give the call to prayer until it was said to him: 'It is morning.'"],[7850,"Among the character traits of the prophets (peace be upon them) are: hastening to break the fast, delaying the pre-dawn meal, and placing the right hand over the left in prayer."],[7851,"Some people from Thaqif came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he lodged them in the cemetery, and that was in Ramadan. The Prophet ﷺ sent them their pre-dawn meal after Bilal's adhan, after the first dawn had broken, and it became very bright, so they ate, and Bilal ate with them. Then they all fasted. Then Bilal was sent to them with their iftar when they thought the sun had set, while they were uncertain, so they broke their fast, and he broke his fast with them."],[7852,"The Prophet ﷺ sent Abu Qatadah on an errand for me, and he came to him after it had become very bright, meaning after the first dawn. The Prophet ﷺ offered him a pre-dawn meal, but he said: 'O Messenger of God! Morning has come for me.' He said: 'Eat the pre-dawn meal.' And the Prophet ﷺ closed the door, shutting it so that the brightness would not be apparent to him. When he finished, he went out and found that it had become very bright, meaning after the first dawn."],[7853,"Abu Bakr used to say: 'Close the doors so that dawn does not surprise us.'"],[7854,"We ate the pre-dawn meal with Abdullah, then we went out and the prayer was established."],[7855,"Whoever reaches Ramadan while sick, then recovers but does not make it up until another Ramadan reaches him, he should fast the one he has reached, then fast the first one, and feed for each day half a sa' of wheat."],[7856,"If a person falls ill in Ramadan, then recovers but does not make it up until another Ramadan reaches him, he should fast the one that has newly come, then make up the other, and feed a poor person for each day."],[7857,"He feeds in place of the month that has passed, because he recovered and neglected to make it up until Ramadan reached him. I said to Ata: 'How much have you heard he should feed?' He said: 'A mudd, they claimed.'"],[7858,"Whoever is reached by another Ramadan while he is sick and has not recovered between them, he should make up the latter of them by fasting, and make up the first of them by feeding a mudd of wheat, and he does not fast."],[7859,"Whoever falls ill in Ramadan, then another Ramadan reaches him while he is sick, and he does not fast this latter one, then he should fast the first, and feed a mudd for each day of the first Ramadan. He said: And that reached me from Umar b. al-Khattab."],[7860,"There is no harm in fasting on the day of Arafah."],[7861,"He makes up both of them by fasting."],[7862,"Whoever is overtaken by two Ramadans while sick, not having recovered between them, he makes up both of them by fasting."],[7863,"I was sitting with Ibn Abbas when a man came to him and said: Two Ramadans have passed over me successively. Ibn Abbas said: \"By God, has this happened?\" He said: Yes. He said: \"No.\" He said: So the man left. Then another came and said: A man has had two Ramadans pass over him successively. He said: \"By God, has this happened?\" He said: Yes. Ibn Abbas said: \"One of seven: he fasts two months and feeds sixty poor people.\""],[7864,"We went out on a military expedition in the land of Rum. While we were in the land of Rum and with us was Awf b. Malik al-Ashja'i, he said: He addressed us, and I heard him say: I heard Umar, the Commander of the Believers, say: Whoever fasts a day outside Ramadan, and feeds a poor person, and combines them in ransom, then they equal a day of Ramadan."],[7865,"Regarding a man who is sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, he said: \"Nothing is required of him. But if he recovers and does not fast until he dies, then food is given on his behalf for each day: half a sa' of wheat.\""],[7867,"If a man falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, nothing is required of him. But if he recovers and does not make it up until he dies, then food is given on his behalf for each day: a makkuk of wheat and a makkuk of dates."],[7868,"If a man falls sick in Ramadan and does not recover until he dies, nothing is required of him; he was overcome by his condition and his making up."],[7869,"If a man falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, nothing is required of him. But if he recovers and does not make it up until he dies, then food is given on his behalf for each day: a makkuk of wheat and a makkuk of dates."],[7870,"Whoever falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, food is not given on his behalf. But if he recovers and does not make it up until he dies, food is given on his behalf."],[7871,"If a man falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, food is given on his behalf for each day: one mudd of wheat to a poor person."],[7872,"There is no harm in fasting on the day of Arafah."],[7873,"I mentioned to Ibn Sīrīn the statement of Ṭāwūs and he was not impressed by it."],[7874,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: He fell ill in Ramadan, then recovered but did not make it up until three Ramadans passed while he was healthy. He said: \"He feeds once, thirty poor people with thirty mudds.\""],[7875,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What about a man who was ill for all of Ramadan and remained ill until another Ramadan passed? He said: \"He feeds only once.\" I said to him: What about a man who was ill for all of Ramadan and remained ill until the next Ramadan came while he was still ill? He said: \"He only makes up the first one, and he does not feed.\""],[7876,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: A man was ill in Ramadan until another Ramadan came while he was ill, and he was ill for all of it, then he recovered but did not make up either of them until the third Ramadan came. He said: How much should he feed? He said: \"Sixty poor people with sixty mudds.\""],[7877,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What about a man who was ill for all of Ramadan, then recovered but did not make it up until he died? He said: \"Thirty poor people should be fed on his behalf with thirty mudds.\" I said: What about a man who was ill for all of Ramadan, then recovered but did not make it up until another Ramadan came and he died during it or after it? He said: \"Sixty poor people should be fed on his behalf with sixty mudds.\""],[7878,"There is no harm in fasting on the day of Arafah."],[7879,"When a man dies owing the fast of another Ramadan, half a ṣāʿ of wheat should be fed for each day on his behalf."],[7880,"A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, my mother died owing a month of fasting. He said: Fast on her behalf."],[7881,"When a man dies owing the fast of Ramadan, some of his relatives should make it up on his behalf."],[7882,"A woman died owing a year of fasting, and she left behind her husband and her three sons. Ṭāwūs said: \"Fast on her behalf for a year, all of you.\""],[7883,"About a man who died owing a vowed fast and did not make it up, he said: \"Some of his relatives should fast on his behalf.\""],[7884,"There is no harm in fasting on the day of Arafah."],[7885,"About a man who died while owing Ramadan and owing a vow to fast another month, he said: \"Sixty needy persons are fed on his behalf.\""],[7886,"A needy person is fed on his behalf for each day instead of Ramadan, and one of his guardians fasts the vow on his behalf."],[7888,"Regarding the two months that are made up by the traveler, he said: \"Like the sick person, exactly.\" I said: \"A man who broke the fast for some days of Ramadan while traveling, then died during that journey before settling?\" He said: \"Nothing is due from him, and no one is fed on his behalf.\""],[7889,"I said to ʿAtaʾ: \"What about a man who broke the fast in Ramadan while traveling, then remained traveling until the next Ramadan caught up with him, traveling throughout?\" He said: \"Nothing is due from him except that he makes up the first one, and he does not have to feed anyone.\" I said: \"What about a man who broke the fast of Ramadan while traveling, then settled, and did not make it up until the next Ramadan found him traveling? May he break the fast if he wishes?\" He said: \"Yes, then he feeds thirty needy persons, thirty mudds.\""],[7890,"About a man who breaks the fast for some days while traveling, then dies on his journey, he said: \"Nothing is due from him,\" and this falls under the saying of Ibn ʿAbbas, al-Nakhaʿi, al-Hasan, ʿAtaʾ, and al-Zuhri."],[7891,"There is no harm in fasting on the day of Arafah."],[7892,"Ibn Umar used to fast the sacred months."],[7893,"Fast it as you broke it."],[7894,"It was revealed: \"then a number of other days, consecutive,\" then \"consecutive\" was dropped."],[7898,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade fasting on six days: one day before Ramaḍān, the day of Aḍḥā, the day of Fiṭr, and the three days of tashrīq."],[7899,"Fast it however you wish, and keep count of the number."],[7900,"He used to prefer it consecutively."],[7903,"Fast however you wish. Allah said: \"Then a number of other days.\""],[7905,"Fast however you wish when you have counted its fast."],[7906,"Keep count of the number, and fast however you wish."],[7907,"In making up Ramadan: Keep count of the number, and fast however you wish."],[7908,"If you wish, separate them; it is only a number of other days."],[7909,"He was asked about making up Ramadan: together or separately? He said: \"Whichever of that he wishes; Allah said: two consecutive months, and if He wished He said: 'So whoever makes up Ramadan, then together,' but He did not say anything about it, and the righteous people did not forbid it, so they follow what is permissible.\""],[7910,"She asked Abu Hurayrah about making up Ramadan, and he said: \"There is no harm in breaking it up; it is only a number of days from other days.\""],[7911,"Fast however you wish, and count the number."],[7913,"I said: \"What do you think if a man owes some days from Ramadan, and he begins the day not intending to fast, then later after morning he decides to fast – can he count it as making up Ramadan, even though he did not intend it before dawn?\" He said: \"Let him fast it, and count it as making up Ramadan.\""],[7914,"I was with Ibn al-Musayyab when a Bedouin came to him in the afternoon, or after the afternoon, and said: \"I have not eaten anything today; shall I fast?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"But I owe a day from Ramadan, so shall I count this one in its place?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[7915,"I used to owe some days from Ramadan, then I would not be able to make them up until Sha'ban came."],[7916,"I used to owe days from Ramadan, and I would not make them up except in Sha'ban."],[7917,"'Ata' used to say: \"He may delay it as long as another Ramadan has not overtaken him.\""],[7918,"He prohibited a woman from fasting voluntarily except with her husband's permission."],[7919,"A man said to the Prophet ﷺ: \"I saw the Night of Decree as if it was such-and-such a night.\" He said: \"I see that your visions agree on the last ten, so seek it in the ninth, in an odd-numbered night.\""],[7920,"Seek the Night of Decree in the last ten, in the remaining nine, in an odd-numbered night."],[7922,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed i'tikaf during the first ten days of Ramadan. It was said to him: \"What you are seeking is ahead of you.\" So he observed i'tikaf during the middle ten days of Ramadan. It was said to him: \"What you are seeking is ahead of you.\" So he observed i'tikaf during the last ten days. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Seek it in the last ten, in an odd-numbered night,\" meaning the Night of Decree."],[7923,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed i'tikaf during the first ten days of Ramadan. It was said to him: \"What you are seeking is ahead of you.\" So he observed i'tikaf during the middle ten days of Ramadan. It was said to him: \"What you are seeking is ahead of you.\" So he observed i'tikaf during the last ten days. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Seek it in the last ten, in an odd-numbered night,\" meaning the Night of Decree."],[7925,"We discussed Laylat al-Qadr among a group of Quraysh. I went to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who was a friend of mine, and said, 'Will you come out with us to the palm groves?' He said, 'Yes.' He came out wearing a cloak of his. I said to him, 'Did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention Laylat al-Qadr?' He said, 'Yes. We observed i'tikaf with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the middle ten days of the month of Ramadan, and we came out on the morning of the twentieth.' He said, 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us and said: I saw Laylat al-Qadr, then I was made to forget it. So seek it in the last ten days, on an odd night. And I saw myself prostrating in water and mud. Whoever observed i'tikaf with me, let him return to his place of i'tikaf.' He said, 'We returned, and there was not a cloud in the sky. Then a cloud came and we were rained upon until the roof of the mosque leaked, and it was made of palm branches. The prayer was established, and when he finished, I saw the trace of mud on the forehead and nose of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, meaning the night of the twenty-first.'"],[7926,"Ibn Abbas used to sprinkle water on his family on the night of the twenty-third."],[7927,"The Prophet ﷺ was among a group of his companions and said, 'Shall I inform you of Laylat al-Qadr?' They said, 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah!' He was silent for a while, then said, 'I said to you what I said a short while ago, and I know it. Indeed I know it, then I was made to forget it. Do you remember the day we were at such-and-such a place—which night was it? In a military expedition he undertook.' They said, 'We traveled and did such-and-such until the group of people settled on it being the night of the twenty-third.'"],[7928,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I saw in a dream Laylat al-Qadr as if it were the seventh night.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'I see that your visions have concurred on the seventh night. So whoever among you is seeking it, let him seek it on the seventh night.'"],[7929,"Al-Juhani came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I am a man of the desert and livestock, so advise me about Laylat al-Qadr, in which I should stand in prayer.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Or two nights?' He said, 'Rather one night.' He called him and whispered to him, and no one knew what he commanded him. The people said, 'Watch the night on which Al-Juhani stands.' When it was the night of the twenty-third, he came down to his family and stood that night."],[7930,"Al-Juhani, Abdullah b. Unays, came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I have burdens and property'—and he was a farmer—'so command me with a night.' He said, 'Or two nights?' He said, 'Rather one night.' He called him and whispered to him twice or three times, and commanded him with the night of the twenty-third. He would spend that night in the mosque and not leave it until morning, and he would not attend anything in Ramadan before it or after it, nor on the day of Eid al-Fitr."],[7931,"Abdullah b. Unays al-Juhani said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I am a man whose home is far away, so command me with a night in which I should come down.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Come down on the night of the twenty-third.'"],[7933,"He used to consider it the night of the twenty-third. Al-Hasan b. al-Hurr narrated to him from Abda b. Abi Lubaba that he said, 'The night of the twenty-seventh, and that he had tested that by various things and by the stars.' Makhul did not pay attention to that."],[7935,"Aisha used to wake us up on the night of the twenty-third of Ramadan."],[7936,"Ali used to seek out the Night of Decree on the night of the nineteenth, the twenty-first, and the twenty-third."],[7937,"Seek out the Night of Decree on the night of the seventeenth, the morning of Badr, or the twenty-first, or the twenty-third."],[7938,"I observed the sun for twenty years, and I saw it rise on the morning of the twenty-fourth of Ramadan with no rays."],[7940,"I said: O Abu al-Mundhir, meaning Ubayy b. Kab, tell me about the Night of Decree, for Ibn Umm Abd says: Whoever keeps vigil the whole year will catch it. He said: May Allah have mercy on Abu Abd al-Rahman. He knew that it is in Ramadan, but he made it unclear to people so they do not become complacent. By the One who sent down the Book upon Muhammad ﷺ, it is indeed in the month of Ramadan, and it is indeed the night of the twenty-seventh. I said: O Abu al-Mundhir, how did you know that? He said: By the sign that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed us of. We saw it and memorized it. By Allah, it is indeed that, with no exception. He said: I said to Zirr: What is the sign? He said: That the sun rises on that morning like a brass tray, with no rays."],[7941,"I saw Zirr b. Hubaysh while al-Hajjaj stood on the pulpit mentioning the Night of Decree, and it was as if he said: Some people mention the Night of Decree. Zirr wanted to leap at him, but the people restrained him. Zirr said: It is the night of the twenty-seventh. Whoever catches it should perform a ritual bath, break his fast on milk, and let his breaking of fast be at dawn."],[7942,"When the last ten nights of Ramadan entered, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would wake his family and tighten his waist wrapper."],[7944,"When the last ten nights of Ramadan entered, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would wake his family and tighten his waist wrapper."],[7945,"He used to complete the Quran in the month of Ramadan every three days, but when the last ten entered, he would complete it in two nights and perform a ritual bath every night."],[7946,"We fasted with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during Ramadan, and he did not lead us in night prayer at all during the month until seven nights remained. Then he led us until about a third of the night had passed. Then he did not lead us on the fourth night, but led us on the following night until about half the night had passed. We said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you would grant us voluntary prayers for the rest of this night of ours. He said: When a man stands with the imam until he finishes, the rest of his night is reckoned for him. Then he did not lead us on the sixth night, but led us on the seventh night, and he sent for his family and the people gathered. He led us until we feared we would miss the falah. I said: What is the falah? He said: The pre-dawn meal."],[7947,"I said to Abu Hurayrah: They claim that the Night of Decree has been lifted. He said: Whoever said that has lied. I said: Then is it in every Ramadan I face? He said: Yes."],[7948,"The Night of Decree is in every Ramadan; it comes."],[7950,"He was asked about Abu Dharr at Mina, and he said: Has the Night of Decree been lifted, or is it in every Ramadan? Abu Dharr said: I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ, saying: O Messenger of God, has the Night of Decree been lifted? He said: Rather, it is in every Ramadan."],[7952,"He disliked making up Ramadan in the ten days."],[7953,"Ramadan is not to be made up in Dhu al-Hijjah."],[7956,"I heard Abu Hurayrah when a man asked him, saying: I owe days from Ramadan; shall I fast the ten days as voluntary? He said: No. And why? Begin with God's right, then volunteer afterward as much as you wish."],[7957,"It was disliked for a man to voluntarily fast in the ten days when he owes an obligatory fast. He said: No, but fast the ten days and count them as make-up."],[7958,"No, rather until you fulfill the right."],[7959,"Whoever owes the fast of Ramadan and volunteers a fast, let him count what he volunteered toward making up Ramadan."],[7960,"He used to encourage standing in prayer during Ramadan without commanding them as a firm obligation, and he would say: Whoever stands in prayer during Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins will be forgiven. The Messenger of God ﷺ passed away with the matter on that, then the matter was like that during the caliphate of Abu Bakr and the beginning of the caliphate of Umar."],[7961,"He used to encourage standing in prayer during Ramadan without commanding them as a firm obligation, and he would say: Whoever stands in prayer during Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins will be forgiven. The Messenger of God ﷺ passed away with the matter on that, then the matter was like that during the caliphate of Abu Bakr and the beginning of the caliphate of Umar."],[7962,"The Prophet ﷺ led the people in prayer for three nights that remained of Ramadan."],[7963,"Ali used to order the people to stand in prayer during the month of Ramadan, and he would appoint an imam for the men and an imam for the women.\" He said: \"He ordered me, so I led the women."],[7964,"One night Umar went out with Abd al-Rahman b. Awf, and that was during Ramadan, while the people were scattered groups: a man praying by himself, and a man praying and a small group praying with his prayer. Umar b. al-Khattab said: I think if we gathered these people behind one reciter, it would be better. So he resolved to gather them behind one reciter, and he commanded Ubayy b. Ka'b, who led them. Then he went out one night while the people were praying with the prayer of their reciter, and he said: What an excellent innovation this is! But the one you sleep through is better than the one you stand in—meaning the last part of the night. They used to stand in the first part of the night."],[7965,"The fasting person is in worship as long as he does not backbite."],[7968,"He gathered the people behind Ubayy b. Ka'b and Tamim al-Dari, and Ubayy would perform Witr with three rak'ahs."],[7970,"Ubayy b. Ka'b used to perform qunut in the latter half of Ramadan after the ruku'."],[7972,"Umar gathered the people in Ramadan behind Ubayy b. Ka'b and Tamim al-Dari for twenty-one rak'ahs, reciting the long surahs and departing at the break of dawn."],[7973,"They used to recite thirty-nine or forty-one [verses?]. He said: The people in Makkah during the time of Umar and others used to fast and circumambulate until al-Qasri gathered them."],[7974,"Umar commanded three reciters to recite in Ramadan. He commanded the fastest of them to recite thirty verses, commanded the middle one to recite twenty-five, and commanded the slowest to recite twenty."],[7975,"We would finish the night prayer during the time of Umar when the branches of dawn had drawn near, and the night prayer during the time of Umar was twenty-three rak'ahs."],[7976,"I found the people only while they were cursing the disbelievers in the month of Ramadan. He said: 'The reciters would stand [in prayer] with Surah al-Baqarah in eight rak'ahs, and when the reciters stood with it in twelve rak'ahs, the people considered that he had made it lighter for them.'"],[7977,"The night prayer during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Ramadan: a small group would stand and a man likewise here, and a small group behind the man. Umar was the first to gather the people behind one reciter."],[7978,"Umar did not gather the people of Makkah behind one reciter because of circumambulation; he left whoever wished to circumambulate."],[7979,"One of their commanders — Mu'awiyah or someone else — wanted to gather the people of Makkah behind one reciter, so a reluctant Karabbus said: 'Do not do it. Leave the people: whoever wishes may circumambulate, and whoever wishes may pray with the reciter's prayer.' So he did."],[7980,"I was told that the first to lead the people of Makkah in night prayer during the caliphate of Umar b. al-Khattab was Zayd b. Qunfudh b. Zayd b. Jud'an. Whoever wished stood with him, whoever wished stood by himself, and whoever wished circumambulated."],[7981,"The people would stand in prayer in Ramadan and pray Isha when a quarter of the night had passed, then they would finish while another quarter remained upon them."],[7982,"Umar invited me to have the pre-dawn meal with him, and to have the midday meal in the month of Ramadan. Umar heard the clamor of the people when they left the mosque, and he said: 'What is this?' I said: 'The people when they left the mosque.' He said: 'What remains of the night is more beloved to me than what has passed.'"],[7984,"A man came to Ibn Umar and said: 'Should I pray behind the imam in Ramadan?' He said: 'Do you recite the Quran?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Would you then keep silent as if you were a donkey? Pray in your house.'"],[7986,"If I had only two surahs with me, repeating them would be dearer to me."],[7988,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed one night in the month of Ramadan in the mosque, and some people were with him. Then he prayed the second night, and that night more people gathered than the first. When it was the third or fourth night, the mosque was filled to capacity with its people, but he did not come out to them. The people began calling out to him, 'The prayer!' When morning came, Umar b. al-Khattab said, 'The people kept waiting for you last night, O Messenger of Allah!' He said, 'Indeed, their affair was not hidden from me, but I feared it would be made obligatory upon them.'"],[7989,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out one night in the middle of the night and prayed in the mosque. Some men gathered and prayed with him following his prayer. When morning came, they spoke about how the Prophet ﷺ had gone out and prayed in the mosque in the middle of the night. The following night, more people gathered than before. The Prophet ﷺ went out in the middle of the night and prayed, and they prayed with him following his prayer. Then morning came and they spoke about that. On the third night, many people gathered, until the people filled the mosque. She said: The Prophet ﷺ went out in the middle of the night and prayed, and they prayed with him. She said: When it was the fourth night, the people gathered until the mosque was almost unable to accommodate its people. She said: The Prophet ﷺ sat and did not come out. She said: Until I heard some of them saying, 'The prayer!' But the Prophet ﷺ did not come out to them. When he had prayed the dawn prayer, he said the taslim, then stood among the people and recited the tashahhud, then said: 'Now then, your situation last night was not hidden from me, but I feared that it would be made obligatory upon you and you would be unable to perform it.'"],[7990,"When Umar b. al-Khattab entered the first night of Ramadan, he would pray the maghrib prayer, then say, 'Sit down.' Then he would deliver a brief sermon, saying, 'To proceed: this month its fasting has been prescribed upon you, but its standing in prayer has not been prescribed upon you. Whoever among you is able to stand, let him stand, for these are the supererogatory acts of good which Allah has mentioned. Whoever is not able, let him sleep on his bed. Let each of you beware of saying: I will fast if so-and-so fasts, and I will stand if so-and-so stands. Whoever among you fasts or stands, let him do that for Allah. Let each of you know that he is in prayer as long as he is waiting for a prayer. Reduce idle talk in the houses of Allah,' twice or three times. Then he would say, 'Beware, none of you should anticipate the month,' three times. 'Beware, do not fast until you see it' — or 'do not fast until they see it' — 'unless it is obscured for you. If it is obscured for you, then count to thirty. Then do not break your fast until you see the night grow dark over the mountain passes.'"],[7991,"Sa'id b. Jubayr used to lead us in prayer during the month of Ramadan. He would recite in both readings together. One night he would recite according to the reading of Ibn Mas'ud, and he would pray five tarwihahs. When it was the last ten nights, he would pray six tarwihahs."],[7992,"If a man is praying between the two tarwihahs in Ramadan, and the imam says the takbir before he bows, there is no harm in his praying his prayer with the imam's prayer, and he does not bow."],[7993,"He used to observe continuous fasting from one pre-dawn meal to the next."],[7994,"He used to observe continuous fasting from one pre-dawn meal to the next."],[7995,"Do not observe continuous fasting. They said, 'O Messenger of Allah, but you observe continuous fasting?' He said, 'I am not like you. I spend the night with my Lord feeding me and giving me drink.' He said, 'They did not refrain from continuous fasting, so the Prophet ﷺ continued fasting with them for two days and two nights. Then they saw the new moon, and the Prophet ﷺ said, 'If the new moon had been delayed, I would have increased for you,' as if rebuking them."],[7996,"Beware of continuous fasting, beware of continuous fasting. They said, 'But you observe continuous fasting?' He said, 'In that I am not like you. I remain with my Lord feeding me and giving me drink. So take on of deeds what you have the strength for.'"],[7997,"Do not practice continuous fasting. They said: But you practice continuous fasting? He said: \"I am not like you. I spend the night with food and drink provided for me.\""],[7998,"The Prophet ﷺ forbade continuous fasting. They said: But you practice continuous fasting. He said: What makes you know? Perhaps my Lord feeds me and gives me drink."],[8002,"The fasting person is in worship as long as he does not backbite."],[8003,"When a man begins Ramadan among his family and fasts some days of it, then travels, he may fast if he wishes or break his fast if he wishes."],[8005,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out for Makkah in the year of the conquest during Ramadan until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke his fast. Breaking the fast was the latter of the two practices."],[8006,"4516 4484 - Ibn Uyaynah said: And Amr b. Dinar informed me, from Kulthum b. Jabr, from a man, from Umar, something similar. [Hidden matn]: Umar b. al-Khattab ordered a man who fasted the month of Ramadan while traveling to make it up."],[8007,"Umm Dharr entered upon Aishah to greet her, and that was during Ramadan. Aishah said to her: Will you travel during Ramadan? I would not like to travel during Ramadan. If it came upon me while I was traveling, I would stay."],[8008,"I said to Ata: What about fasting while traveling? He said: \"Break your fast when you shorten the prayer, and fast when you complete the prayer.\""],[8009,"If a man begins the day fasting in the month of Ramadan, then sets out as a traveler during the day, he should not break his fast that day unless he fears thirst for himself. If he fears it, he may break his fast, and he must make it up later. Then, if he wishes afterwards, he may break his fast, and if he wishes, he may fast."],[8011,"He saw no harm in a person breaking a voluntary fast. He gave examples for that: a man who circumambulates seven times and then stops without completing it—he has what he intended; or prays one rak'ah and does not pray another before it—he has what he intended; or goes with wealth to give as charity and gives some of it and withholds some."],[8012,"Fasting is like charity: you intended to fast and then changed your mind, and you intended to give charity and then changed your mind."],[8014,"Whoever begins the day fasting voluntarily, if he wishes he may fast, and if he wishes he may break his fast, and he owes no makeup."],[8016,"Umar b. al-Khattab said to his companions one day: What do you think I owe? I began this day fasting, then I saw a slave girl of mine and had intercourse with her. Ali said: You fasted voluntarily, and you came to something lawful. I do not think you owe anything."],[8017,"I entered upon Ibn Abbas at the beginning of the day and found him fasting. Then I entered upon him at the end of the day and found him not fasting. I said: What is your situation? He said: I saw a slave girl of mine and she pleased me, so I had intercourse with her. Indeed, I will tell you another thing: she had committed an indecency, so we kept her chaste."],[8018,"Abu al-Darda, when he began the day, would ask his family for the midday meal. If there was none, he would say: I am fasting."],[8019,"They said: Except for the obligatory fast."],[8020,"He would come to his family until midday and ask them: Is there any midday meal? We would find it or not find it. He would say: Only this day, so he would fast it, even though he had begun the day not fasting."],[8021,"Abu Talhah would come to his family and say: Is there any midday meal? If they said no, he would fast that day. Qatadah said: Muadh b. Jabal used to do that."],[8022,"The one fasting has the choice as long as the midday meal has not arrived."],[8023,"He has the choice until midday, as long as he has not eaten food, or unless he had made it obligatory at night."],[8024,"Whoever decides to fast after the sun has passed its zenith, let him fast."],[8025,"Abu Hurayrah and Abu Talhah would enter the morning not fasting, and they would say: 'Is there any food?' If they found it [they ate], or if they did not find it, they would complete that day [fasting]."],[8026,"A man came to 'Ali b. Abi Talib and said: 'I entered the morning not intending to fast.' He said: 'You have the choice up to midday. Once midday has passed, you may not break the fast.'"],[8027,"I used to fast one day and break the fast one day. While I was traveling, it was my day to break the fast, and we journeyed and did not stop until after midday, or at the time of prayer. He said: I said: 'I will certainly fast this day,' and I fasted. I mentioned that to Ibn al-Musayyab, and he said: 'You did right.'"],[8029,"Ibn 'Umar, when he resolved to fast, would not break the fast; and when he resolved to break the fast, he would not fast."],[8030,"There is no fast for one who does not resolve to fast at night."],[8031,"There is no fast for one who does not resolve to fast at night."],[8032,"If a man resolves to fast at night, then enters the morning fasting, he gets the reward of the night and the reward of the day. If he breaks the fast, he must make it up."],[8034,"A'ishah and Hafsah woke up fasting, and food was gifted to them that they liked, so they broke their fast. When the Prophet ﷺ entered upon them, Hafsah hastened to him—she was her father's daughter—and asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered them to fast one day in its place."],[8035,"Whoever breaks his fast in a voluntary fast, should he make it up?"],[8036,"The Prophet ﷺ entered upon me one day and said: 'Do you have any food?' She said: I said: 'No.' He said: 'Then I will fast today.' She said: Then he entered another time, and I said: 'We have been gifted hubaysh or hays—'Abd al-Razzaq was uncertain—so he said: 'Then I will break my fast today, and I had already intended to fast.'"],[8037,"The Prophet ﷺ entered upon me one day, and I brought him some hays, and he ate from it and said: 'I had wanted to fast today, but I will fast a day in its place.'"],[8038,"Umar b. al-Khattab entered the mosque and prayed one rakah. It was said to him, and he said: It is only voluntary, so whoever wishes may add and whoever wishes may reduce. I disliked taking it as a thoroughfare."],[8039,"Sulayman b. Musa asked Ata': Was it said: A man may break his fast outside Ramadan for his guest? He said: 'Yes.'"],[8040,"If the reciter finds you praying the last Isha in Ramadan and you had said the takbir before him, make your prayer the Isha—pray it with his prayer if he completes it; otherwise, differ from him and do not pray with his prayer.' I said: 'He said the takbir before me while I want to pray Isha?' He said: 'Then say the takbir and make it Isha if he completes it; otherwise, make it a voluntary prayer, then pray Isha afterward.'"],[8041,"When a man comes to the night prayer of Ramadan and has not prayed the obligatory prayer, he prays with them and counts it as the obligatory prayer with them."],[8042,"It is recommended for the fasting person to apply oil so that the dustiness of fasting goes away from him."],[8043,"I heard Wahb praying alone, and I asked him about people entering the mosque in the month of Ramadan after having prayed the last Isha, while they are standing in voluntary prayer—should they pray behind the imam in the mosque, led by one of them? He said: 'No, they pray individually.'"],[8045,"Perfume and women have been made beloved to me, and the delight of my eye has been placed in prayer."],[8046,"I came to the people while they were in the night prayer, and I had not prayed Isha. So I prayed Isha for myself alone while they were praying. Then I mentioned that to Ibn al-Musayyib, and he said, 'You did right.' He said, then he said to me, 'What kept you from the prayer?' I made an excuse to him, and he said, 'I have not seen the people for forty years' — meaning, 'I have not seen them leaving without my having caught it.'"],[8047,"Abu al-Darda' used to keep the night of Friday alive in worship and fast its day. Salman came to him — and the Prophet ﷺ had made them brothers — and he stayed the night with him. Abu al-Darda' wanted to get up for his night prayer, but Salman went to him and did not leave him until he slept and broke his fast. He said: Then Abu al-Darda' came to the Prophet ﷺ and told him, and the Prophet ﷺ said, 'Uwaymir, Salman knows better than you. Do not single out the night of Friday for prayer, nor its day for fasting.'"],[8048,"The Prophet ﷺ entered upon one of his wives on Friday while she was fasting. He said, 'Did you fast yesterday?' She said, 'No.' He said, 'Do you intend to fast tomorrow?' She said, 'No.' So he ordered her to break her fast."],[8050,"By the Lord of this Kaaba, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid fasting on Friday unless one connects it with fasting."],[8051,"The slaughtering is begun before the shaving."],[8053,"He asked Jabir b. Abd Allah while he was circumambulating the House, saying, 'Did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid fasting on Friday?' He said, 'Yes, by the Lord of this House.'"],[8055,"We set out as pilgrims and stopped at Abu Dharr's. He prepared food for us, and it was Friday. Among us was a man fasting. Then Abu Dharr said, 'I adjure you to eat, unless you have begun the month afresh.' And he adjured him a second time, or twice. He said, 'Friday is a day of Eid, so for you to break your fast is better.'"],[8056,"Do not deliberately fast on Friday."],[8057,"Whoever of you is voluntarily fasting some days of the month, let his fasting include Thursday, and let him not deliberately fast Friday, for it is a day of Eid, food, and drink. Thus two righteous days come together for him: his fasting day and his day of ritual with the Muslims."],[8058,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ broke his fast at Arafah; Umm al-Fadl prepared milk for him, and he drank at Arafah."],[8059,"They were uncertain about the Prophet's ﷺ fasting at Arafah, so Umm al-Fadl said: 'I will inform you about that.' She sent him a wooden bowl of milk, and he drank from it."],[8060,"He saw Ibn Abbas not fasting at Arafah, eating pomegranate."],[8061,"Abdullah b. Abbas invited to food on the day of Arafah, and Abdullah said: 'Do not fast, for the Prophet ﷺ was brought a milking vessel containing milk on the day of Arafah and he drank. So do not fast, for people follow your example.'"],[8062,"The slaughtering is begun before the shaving."],[8063,"I entered upon Ibn Umar while he was eating on the day of Arafah. He said: 'Come near.' I said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'Come near.' I said: 'If you wish, I will do so.' He said: 'Will you inform people that I ordered you to break your fast?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then he remained silent about me and neither ordered me nor forbade me.'"],[8064,"Ibn Abbas said on the day of Arafah: 'No one intending to fast should accompany us, for it is a day of takbir, eating, and drinking.' Abd al-Razzaq said: 'Al-Thawri also forbade me from fasting the day of Tarwiyah and the day of Arafah.'"],[8067,"I asked Ata: 'Do you fast on the day of Arafah?' He said: 'I fast it in winter but do not fast it in summer.'"],[8068,"Ibn Umar used to dislike fasting on the day of Arafah."],[8069,"He is named, then his 'aqiqah is performed on his seventh day, then his head is shaved,\" and he would say: \"His head is smeared with the blood."],[8072,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about fasting on the Day of Arafah, and he said: 'It is an expiation of two years: the past year and the coming year.'"],[8074,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about the fara'ah. He said: \"Offer the fara'ah if you wish.\""],[8075,"A man came to Hasan and Husayn on the Day of Arafah and found one of them fasting and the other not fasting. He said: 'I came to ask you two about a matter in which you differ.' They said: 'We do not differ. Whoever fasts, it is good, and whoever does not fast, there is no harm.'"],[8076,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about fasting on Ashura, and he said: 'It is an expiation for the year.'"],[8077,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about fasting on Ashura, and he said: 'It is an expiation for the year.'"],[8078,"He said concerning fasting on the day of Ashura: 'It expiates the year.'"],[8079,"When the Prophet ﷺ came to Madinah, he said to a man from Aslam: 'Go to your people and command them to fast this day, the day of Ashura.' He said: 'What do you think if I find some of them have already eaten lunch?' He said: 'Then command them to complete the fast.'"],[8081,"The Prophet ﷺ was at Qudayd, and a man came to him. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Have you eaten anything today, on the day of Ashura?' He said: 'No, except that I drank water.' He said: 'Then do not eat anything after this until sunset, and command those behind you to fast this day.'"],[8082,"I have not seen anyone more emphatic in commanding the fast of the day of Ashura than Ali and Abu Musa."],[8083,"There is no retreat except with fasting."],[8084,"Umar b. al-Khattab sent to Abd al-Rahman b. al-Harith on the night of Ashura: 'Take the pre-dawn meal and begin the day fasting.' He said: So Abd al-Rahman began the day fasting."],[8085,"He heard Ibn Abbas say concerning the day of Ashura: 'Differ from the Jews; fast the ninth and the tenth.'"],[8086,"He has his stipulation."],[8088,"We used to be commanded to fast the day of Ashura, so when the fast of the month of Ramadan was sent down, whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished left it."],[8089,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came to Madinah and found the Jews fasting the day of Ashura. He said, 'What is this?' They said, 'This is a great day on which God saved Musa and drowned the people of Pharaoh.' He said: So he fasted it out of gratitude. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: 'I am closer to Musa and more entitled to fast it than you.' So he fasted it and commanded it be fasted."],[8090,"There is no i'tikaf except with fasting."],[8091,"He has his stipulation."],[8092,"We asked Saʿd b. Qays b. ʿUbādah about zakāt al-fiṭr. He said: “The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded us to give it before the zakāh was revealed. When the zakāh was revealed, he neither commanded us nor forbade us, and we still do it.”"],[8093,"Ibn Umar would not fast the day of Ashura when he was traveling, but when he was resident he fasted it."],[8094,"The Prophet ﷺ came to Madinah and found the Jews fasting the day of Ashura. They said, 'This is a great day which the Jews venerate.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'We are more entitled to venerate it.' So he fasted it and commanded it be fasted. Then when its fasting was sent down, whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished left it."],[8095,"Nuh boarded the ship in Rajab, when ten days remained, and disembarked from the ship on the day of Ashura."],[8096,"He has his stipulation."],[8097,"He has his stipulation."],[8098,"He has his stipulation."],[8100,"He has his stipulation."],[8101,"He has his stipulation."],[8102,"A'ishah, during her i'tikaf, when she went out to her house for some need, she would pass by a sick person and ask about him while she was just passing by, not stopping for him."],[8104,"A people who fast Rajab were mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Where are they from Sha'ban?\" Zayd said: \"And the most the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted after Ramadan was Sha'ban.\""],[8105,"I asked Aisha about the fasting of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: He would fast until we said: He has fasted. And he would break his fast until we said: He has broken his fast. I did not see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fast in any month more than his fasting in Sha'ban, except for what was in Ramadan. He used to fast all of Sha'ban except a little. He said: And I asked her about his prayer. She said: The prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Ramadan and at other times was thirteen rak'ahs, including the two rak'ahs of Fajr."],[8106,"I asked Aisha about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed standing, he bowed standing; and when he prayed sitting, he bowed sitting. He said: And I asked her about his fasting. She said: When he fasted, he fasted until we said: He has fasted, he has fasted, he has fasted. And when he broke his fast, he broke his fast until we said: He has broken his fast, he has broken his fast, he has broken his fast. And I did not know the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to fast a complete month since he came to Madinah."],[8107,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast until we said: He will not break his fast. And he would break his fast until we said: He will not fast. I did not see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ever complete a month except Ramadan. And I did not see him in any month fast more than he did in Sha'ban."],[8108,"The one in I'tikaf does not sell and does not buy."],[8109,"It reached the Prophet ﷺ that I was fasting continuously and praying at night. Either he sent for me or I met him, and he said: “Have I not been told that you fast without breaking it and pray? Do not do that, for your eyes have a share over you and your soul has a share over you. Fast and break the fast, and fast one day out of every ten, and you will have the reward of nine.” He said: “I find myself stronger than that, O Prophet of God.” He said: “Then fast the fast of David.” He said: “How did he use to fast, O Messenger of God?” He said: “He used to fast one day and break the fast one day, and he would not flee when he met the enemy.” ‘Ata’ said: I do not know how he mentioned perpetual fasting, but the Prophet ﷺ said: “May he not fast who fasts perpetually; may he not fast who fasts perpetually.”"],[8110,"The one residing in the mosque (mujawir) does not sell and does not buy."],[8111,"A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him: “How is your fasting?” He turned away from him, and when he was asked about something he disliked it was known from his face. He remained silent until the Prophet’s ﷺ anger subsided. Then ‘Umar said to him: “What do you say, O Messenger of God, about fasting perpetually?” He said: “He has neither fasted nor broken the fast,” or he said: “He has not fasted and he has not broken the fast.” He said: “What do you say about fasting two days and breaking the fast one day?” He said: “Who can bear that?” He said: “Fasting one day and breaking the fast two days?” He said: “I wish I could bear that.” He said: “Fasting one day and breaking the fast one day?” He said: “That is the fasting of David.” He said: “What do you say about fasting three days of every month?” He said: “That is fasting perpetually.” He said: “Fasting on Monday?” He said: “That is the day on which I was born and the day on which revelation first came to me.” He said: “Fasting on ‘Ashura’?” He said: “It expiates a year.” He said: “Fasting on the day of ‘Arafah?” He said: “It expiates a year and the year before it.”"],[8112,"Whoever fasts perpetually, God will make Hellfire thus constricted for him — and he tied a knot with his ten fingers."],[8113,"A man said: “O Messenger of God, what do you think of a man who fasts all the time?” He said: “I wish he would never eat anything.” He said: “Then two-thirds of it?” He said: “More.” He said: “Then half of it?” He said: “More.” He said: “Then a third of it?” He said: “That has not been revealed. Shall I not tell you what removes the rancor of the heart? Fasting three days of every month.”"],[8114,"He came to the Prophet ﷺ and he said: “Who are you?” He said: “I am the one who came to you last year.” He said: “You seem to have been sturdier than I find you now, or better in body than I see you now.” He said: “I have not eaten except at night since I left you.” He said: “Who commanded you to torment yourself? — three times.” He said: “I am strong.” He said: “Then fast the month of patience and one day of every month.” He said: “I am stronger.” He said: “Then fast the month and three days of every month.” He said: “I am stronger.” He said: “Then fast the sacred months and break the fast.”"],[8115,"My father fasted for forty years or thirty years, breaking the fast only on the day of Eid al-Fitr or the day of sacrifice, and he died while he was fasting."],[8116,"Abu Talhah used to fast the least during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he was a veteran of Badr, because of raiding. When the Messenger of God ﷺ died, I never saw him break the fast except on the day of sacrifice or the day of Eid al-Fitr."],[8117,"We were with ‘Umar b. al-Khattab when food was brought to him. A man from the group withdrew, so he said: “What is the matter with him?” They said: “He is fasting.” He said: “What is his fast?” They said: “Perpetual fasting.” He began striking his head with a cane he had, saying: “Eat, O perpetual faster; eat, O perpetual faster!”"],[8118,"Fasting the month of patience and fasting three days of every month remove the rancor of the heart."],[8119,"He was commanded to fast the white days: the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth."],[8120,"Who among us was present on the day of al-Qāḥa when a rabbit was brought to the Prophet ﷺ? Abū Dharr said: I was. A bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ with a rabbit and said: 'I saw it bleeding.' He said: 'Eat from it,' and he mentioned that he himself did not eat. The bedouin said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'And what is your fast?' He mentioned something, so he said: 'Where are you from the bright white days—the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth?'"],[8121,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined upon me three things that I will never abandon, whether at home or travelling: sleeping on witr, fasting three days of every month, and the two rak'ahs of duha. He said: Then Hasan became confused after that and put in place of the two rak'ahs of duha the ghusl of Friday."],[8122,"Three things I will not abandon until I meet Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ: to spend every night on witr, to pray ḍuḥā, and to fast three days every month."],[8123,"A bedouin came to us while we were at al-Mirbad and said: 'Is there among you a reader who can read this letter?' We said: 'We all read.' He said: 'Then read it for me.' He said: 'This is a letter Muḥammad the Messenger of God ﷺ wrote for me to Banū Zuhayr b. Uqaysh, a clan of ʿUkl: If you bear witness that there is no god but God and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of God, establish prayer, give zakāh, and give the fifth of the spoils, the Prophet's share ﷺ and his ṣafī, then you are secure with God's protection.' He said: 'We said: The Messenger of God ﷺ wrote this letter for you?' He said: 'Yes. Do you think I would lie about the Messenger of God ﷺ?' He became angry and struck his hand on the letter and took it. He said: 'We followed him and said: Tell us, O Abū ʿAbd Allāh, something you heard from the Messenger of God ﷺ.' He said: 'I heard him say: Among what removes much of the rancor of the chest is fasting the month of patience and fasting three days of every month.'"],[8124,"I went out to al-Rabadha seeking Abū Dharr, but I did not find him. I greeted his wife and said: 'Where is Abū Dharr?' She said: 'He has gone to work.' He said: 'I sat down, and then Abū Dharr came leading two camels, one tied to the tail of the other, and on the neck of each was a water skin. He made the camels kneel and carried the water skins. I greeted him, and he spoke to his wife about something, and it seemed she replied sharply. He repeated and she repeated, so he said: 'You do no more than what the Messenger of God ﷺ said: A woman is like a rib; if you straighten her, she breaks, yet in her there is benefit and curvature.' Then he brought a bowl containing something like a small bird and said: 'Eat, for I am fasting.' Then he stood to pray, then returned and ate with him. Nuʿaym said: 'Indeed we belong to God, O Abū Dharr! Whoever among people lies to me—but you, I did not think you would lie to me.' He said: 'I did not lie to you. I said I was fasting, then I ate. Now I tell you: I am fasting. I fasted three days of this month, so its fasting became due for me and its breaking became lawful for me.'"],[8125,"He forbade fasting on these two days, meaning al-Fiṭr and al-Aḍḥā. He said: 'As for one of them, it is the day of your breaking fast from your fasting; as for the other, it is a day on which you eat from your sacrificial animals.'"],[8126,"It was disliked for the person in spiritual retreat to perfume himself."],[8127,"I said to ʿAmr: 'What do you think if he joined the two ends of the garment on his right side?' He said: 'I have only seen them dislike that, if he did so.'"],[8129,"He forbade two sales and two ways of dressing, prayer at two times, two ways of eating, and fasting on two days. As for the two sales and two ways of dressing, they are as al-Zuhrī said. As for prayer at two times, they are after ʿaṣr and after ṣubḥ. As for fasting on two days, they are the day of Fiṭr and the day of Aḍḥā. As for the two ways of eating, one is pairing two dates together, and the other is eating while standing."],[8131,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on six days: the day before Ramadan, the Day of Sacrifice, the Day of Breaking the Fast, and the three days of Tashriq."],[8132,"A woman should not fast voluntarily while her husband is present except with his permission, nor give permission in his house while he is present except with his permission. Whatever she spends from his earnings without his command, half of its reward is his."],[8133,"He prohibited a woman from fasting voluntarily except with her husband's permission."],[8134,"Sulayman b. Musa said to ʿAtaʾ: It used to be said: The woman should break her fast for her husband, and the man for his guest? He said: Yes, and if she is praying, she should turn to him."],[8135,"It is not lawful for a woman to fast voluntarily except with her husband's permission."],[8136,"He prohibited a woman from fasting a day outside Ramadan except with her husband's permission."],[8137,"Every deed of the son of Adam is for him except fasting, for fasting is for Me and I reward it. The breath of the fasting person is more pleasant to God than the scent of musk."],[8138,"By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, the breath of the fasting person is more pleasant to God than the scent of musk. He leaves his desire, his food, and his drink for My sake, so fasting is for Me and I reward it."],[8139,"Every good deed the son of Adam does is multiplied tenfold to seven hundredfold, except fasting. It is for Me and I reward it. He gives up his desire for My sake, and he gives up his food for My sake. The fasting person has two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast and a joy when he meets his Lord. And the breath of his mouth is more pleasant to God than the scent of musk. Fasting is for Me and I reward it."],[8140,"Fasting is a man's shield, like the shield of one of you in battle. The master of days is Friday, and the master of months is Ramadan. Consider people by their close friends, for a man befriends only one whose way or condition pleases him."],[8141,"The fasting person is in worship as long as he does not backbite anyone, even if he is sleeping on his bed. Hafsa used to say: How lovely is worship while I am sleeping on my bed."],[8142,"The fasting person is in worship as long as he does not backbite anyone, even if he is sleeping on his bed. Hafsa used to say: How lovely is worship while I am sleeping on my bed."],[8143,"The people raided by land and sea, and I was among those who raided by sea. While we were sailing in the sea, we heard a voice saying: O people of the ship, stop and I will inform you. We looked right and left, but saw nothing except the vast sea. Then it called a second time until it called seven times, saying the same. Abu Musa said: When it was the seventh, I stood and said: What do you inform us of? He said: I inform you of a decree God Almighty has decreed upon Himself: that whoever makes himself thirsty for God on a hot day, He will quench his thirst on the Day of Resurrection. Abu Burda said: So Abu Musa would never let a hot day pass without fasting it, and he would writhe in it from thirst."],[8144,"The fasting person has two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord. And the breath of a fasting person is more fragrant to God than the scent of musk."],[8145,"It was disliked for the person in spiritual retreat to perfume himself."],[8146,"Umm Ayman set out as an emigrant to God and His Messenger ﷺ while she was fasting, with no provision, no mount, and no water-skin, in the intense heat of Tihamah. She had nearly died of hunger and thirst until, at the time when the fasting person breaks the fast, she heard a rustling above her head. She raised her head and saw a bucket suspended by a white rope. She said: “I took it with my hands and drank from it until I was quenched, and I was never thirsty after that.” He said: “She used to fast and circumambulate in order to become thirsty during her fast, but she was never able to become thirsty until she died.”"],[8148,"I never saw 'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud fasting at all except for two days besides Ramadan. She said: I do not know what the matter of those two days was."],[8149,"'Abd Allah used to fast little. We said to him, “You fast little.” He said, “When I fast, I become too weak for prayer, and prayer is dearer to me than fasting.”"],[8150,"We were with 'Abd Allah when a drink was brought to him. He said, “Pass it to the people.” They said, “We are fasting.” He said, “But I am not fasting,” and he drank. Then he recited: “They fear a day when hearts and eyes will turn about.” [Quran 24:37]"],[8151,"Whoever gives a fasting person food to break his fast, feeding him and giving him drink, will have the like of his reward without any decrease in his reward."],[8152,"Whoever gives a fasting person food to break his fast, feeding him and giving him drink, will have the like of his reward."],[8153,"The Prophet ﷺ ate oil at Sa'd b. 'Ubadah's house, then said: “May the fasting break their fast with you, the righteous eat your food, and the angels descend upon you.”"],[8154,"A woman invited him to break his fast at her place, and he did so. He said: “I tell you that there is no man who breaks his fast with a household except that they will have the like of his reward.” She said: “I wish you would choose a time — or something like that — to break your fast with me.” He said: “I want to make it for my own household.”"],[8156,"When food is eaten in the presence of a fasting person, his joints glorify Allah."],[8158,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us and we offered him food. Some of those with him were fasting, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"When you eat in the presence of a fasting person, the angels send blessings upon him.\""],[8159,"It is recommended for the fasting person to apply oil so that the dustiness of fasting goes away from him."],[8160,"Jesus son of Mary used to say: \"When it is the fasting day of one of you, let him oil his beard and wipe his lips so that when he goes out to the people, they will say: 'He is not fasting.' And when one of you prays, let him draw his door curtain close to him, for Allah distributes praise just as He distributes provision. And when one of you gives, let him give with his right hand and conceal it from his left.\""],[8161,"The gates of heaven are opened every Monday and Thursday, and Allah forgives every servant who does not associate anything with Allah except those who bear mutual grudges. The angels say: \"Leave them until they reconcile.\""],[8162,"Fast on Monday and Thursday, for they are two days in which deeds are raised up, and Allah forgives every servant who does not associate anything with Him except one who harbors rancor. Allah says: \"Leave him until he repents.\""],[8165,"Whoever fasts the month of Ramadan and follows it with six days of Shawwal, it is written for him as fasting the whole year."],[8167,"Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six days of Shawwal, it is written for him as fasting a year."],[8168,"Whoever fasts the month of Ramadan and follows it with six days of Shawwal, that is fasting the entire age.\" He said: I said: \"For each day ten?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[8169,"I asked Ma'mar about fasting the six days after the Day of Breaking the Fast. They said to him: Is it to be fasted one day after the breaking of the fast? He said: God forbid! They are days of festival, eating, and drinking. Rather, three days are to be fasted before the White Days, or the three White Days themselves or after them. The White Days are the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth."],[8170,"God looks down upon His servants on the night of the middle of Sha'ban and forgives the people of the earth, except for a man who is a polytheist or one harboring enmity."],[8171,"Qays b. Sa'd narrated to me, from Makhul, from Kathir b. Murrah, tracing it back to the Prophet ﷺ, similar to the hadith of Muhammad b. Rashid. [Hidden matn: God looks down upon His servants on the night of the middle of Sha'ban and forgives the people of the earth, except for a man who is a polytheist or one harboring enmity.]"],[8172,"Lifespans are decreed on the middle of Sha'ban, to the extent that a man may set out as a traveler while he has been transferred from the living to the dead, and he may marry while he has been transferred from the living to the dead."],[8173,"A man may walk in the markets while his name is among the dead."],[8174,"There are five nights in which supplication is not rejected: the night of Friday, the first night of Rajab, the night of the middle of Sha'ban, and the two nights of the festivals."],[8175,"It was said to Ibn Abi Mulaykah: Ziyad al-Minqari, who was a storyteller, says that the reward of the night of the middle of Sha'ban is like the reward of the Night of Decree. Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: If I heard him say that while I had a stick in my hand, I would strike him with it."],[8176,"She heard Umar b. al-Khattab while he was delivering a sermon saying: O assembly of women, when you dye your hands, beware of patterning and marking the fingertips. Let each of you dye her hands up to here, and he pointed to the place of the bracelet."],[8177,"I asked Ibn Abbas about dyeing for women. He said: As for our women, they dye when they have prayed the evening prayer, then they release their hands for the morning prayer, then they reapply it until the noon prayer and perfect the dye, and it does not prevent them from praying."],[8178,"A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ to pledge allegiance to him. He said: \"What is the matter with you that you do not dye? Do you have a husband?\" She said: Yes. He said: \"Then dye, for a woman dyes for two reasons: if she has a husband, let her dye for her husband; and if she does not have a husband, let her dye for her betrothal.\" Then he said: God has cursed mannish women and effeminate men."],[8179,"It was disliked for the person in spiritual retreat to perfume himself."],[8180,"Whoever applies perfume for the sake of God will come on the Day of Resurrection with a scent more fragrant than musk, and whoever applies perfume for other than God will come on the Day of Resurrection with a stench more foul than a carcass."],[8182,"We used to bring ʿUmar ghāliyah and dharīrah, including that musk, and he would begin by dyeing his beard with khaluq, then smear his beard with ghāliyah, apply dharīrah, and use incense."],[8183,"The Prophet ﷺ slaughtered at the prayer place, or he said, \"he slaughtered [a camel]\"."],[8184,"The Messenger of God ﷺ saw me while I was perfumed with khaluq, and said, “Do you have a wife?” I said, “No.” He said, “Go and wash it off, then do not do it again,” three times. He said: So I washed it off, then I washed it again, then I will not do it again."],[8185,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was taking the oath of allegiance from people when a man came to him with traces of khaluq on him. He took his allegiance with the tips of his fingers. The Prophet ﷺ said, “The best perfume for men is that whose scent is apparent and whose color is subtle, and the best perfume for women is that whose color is apparent and whose scent is subtle.”"],[8186,"Perfume and women have been made beloved to me, and the delight of my eye has been placed in prayer."],[8187,"He used to dislike that the muṣḥaf be marked with vowel signs or that anything be added to it."],[8188,"He used to perform Hajj and would not offer a sacrifice."],[8189,"Ibn Masʿūd used to dislike the marking of tenths in the muṣḥaf."],[8190,"He used to dislike in the muṣḥaf the dotting and the marking of tenths (taʿshīr)."],[8191,"Strip the Qur'an bare—he means: do not mix with it what is not from it."],[8192,"ʿAlī used to dislike that muṣḥaf copies be made small."],[8193,"He used to perform Hajj and would not offer a sacrifice."],[8194,"O people, learn the Qur'an, for one of you does not know when it may be taken from him. A man came to him and said: \"O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, what do you think of a man who recites the Qur'an in reverse order?\" He said: \"That is one whose heart is reversed.\" Then a decorated and gilded codex was brought to him, and 'Abd Allah said: \"The best adornment of a codex is its recitation in truth.\""],[8195,"Should we offer a sacrifice on behalf of someone who is absent? He said: \"There is no harm in it.\""],[8196,"\"Let me strive tonight in supplication.\" Then softness overtook him, and he was unable to do anything. Then he heard a speaker saying: \"Say: O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs all praise. In Your hand is all good. To You returns the whole affair, its open and its secret. You are worthy to be praised. You have power over all things. O Allah, forgive me the sins I have committed before, and protect me for what remains of my life, and grant me righteous deeds by which You may be pleased with me.\""],[8197,"Ja'far b. Abi Talib married Asma' bt. 'Umays al-Khath'amiyya. He was killed while married to her, then Abu Bakr married her and died, then 'Ali b. Abi Talib married her after Fatimah."],[8198,"If a master gives his slave in marriage, he does not have the right to separate them."],[8200,"She asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the 'aqiqah. He said: \"For a boy two equivalent sheep, and for a girl one sheep.\" She said: \"I said: What is equivalent?\" He said: \"Two similar ones. Sheep (da'n) are more beloved to me than goats, their males more beloved to him than their females, as his opinion.\""],[8201,"She asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the 'aqiqah. He said: \"Yes, for a boy two, and for a girl one, and it does not harm you whether they are male or female.\""],[8202,"\"Indeed, for a boy two sheep, and for a girl one sheep, and it does not harm you whether it is male or female.\" She narrates that from the Prophet ﷺ, saying: \"I heard him say.\""],[8203,"I and Ibn Mulayka entered upon Hafsa bt. 'Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Bakr, who had given birth to a boy for al-Mundhir b. al-Zubayr. I said: \"Why did you not perform 'aqiqah with a camel for your son?\" She said: \"God forbid! My aunt 'A'isha used to say: For a boy two sheep, and for a girl one sheep.\""],[8204,"For a boy two sheep."],[8205,"For the boy there is an ʿaqīqah, so shed blood on his behalf and remove the harm from him."],[8207,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered an ʿaqīqah for himself after he was sent with prophethood."],[8208,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the ʿaqīqah, and he said: \"I do not like ʿuqūq,\" as if he disliked the name. They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, we ask you about one of us to whom a child is born.\" He said: \"Whoever of you wishes to offer a sacrifice on behalf of his child, let him do so: for a boy two similar sheep, and for a girl one sheep.\""],[8209,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered ʿaqīqah for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn with two rams."],[8210,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered ʿaqīqah for al-Ḥasan with two sheep and for al-Ḥusayn with two sheep, slaughtering them on the seventh day.\" She said: \"He smeared them and ordered that harm be removed from their heads.\" She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Slaughter in his name and say: In the name of Allah; O Allah, for You and to You; this is the ʿaqīqah of so-and-so.\" She said: The people of the Jāhiliyyah used to dye a piece of cotton with the blood of the ʿaqīqah, and when they shaved the infant's head, they would place it on his head. The Prophet ﷺ ordered them to put khāliq (perfume) in place of the blood, meaning by \"smeared them\": he placed on their heads a clay of mashq similar to khāliq."],[8211,"The one in ihram may leave it if he wishes."],[8212,"Half a sa' for each day."],[8213,"The boy is held in pledge for his ʿaqīqah. He used to relate it, and if a sacrifice is offered on his behalf, that suffices for him in place of the ʿaqīqah."],[8214,"If no ʿaqīqah is offered for him, his sacrificial animal suffices him."],[8215,"For a boy a sheep is offered as ʿaqīqah, but no ʿaqīqah is offered for a girl; there is no ʿaqīqah upon her."],[8216,"For a rock hyrax, if it is eaten, a sheep is due."],[8217,"We set out as pilgrims, and while we were traveling, the people argued a lot about which is faster in running, the antelope or the horse. Then an antelope appeared to us, and the sunuh is like this—and he gestured from left to right—and a man among us shot it and did not miss its khushasha', so it collapsed in its blood, and he was at a loss until we came to Umar. We came to him while he was at Mina, and I sat before him with him, and he told him the news. He said: \"How did you hit it, by mistake or intentionally?\" Sufyan said: Mis'ar said: \"I deliberately aimed at it, but I did not intend to kill it.\" He said: And I remember that he said: The man became confused and said: \"I did not hit it by mistake or intentionally.\" So Mis'ar said: He said to him: \"You have combined intention and mistake.\" So he leaned toward a man, and by God, his face was like a heart, and consulted him, then turned to us and said: \"Take a sheep, shed its blood, give its meat as charity, and use its hide as a waterskin.\" So we got up from his presence, and I said: \"O seeker of fatwa from Ibn al-Khattab, his fatwa will not avail you anything before God. So sacrifice your camel and exalt the rituals of God. By God, Umar did not know until he asked the man beside him.\" The man with the two eyes went and reported it to Umar. By God, I was only aware when he came at my companion with the whip, striking him, then said: \"May God fight you, do you transgress in the fatwa and kill the sacred game?\" Then he turned to me, and I said: \"O Commander of the Believers, I will not make lawful for you what God has made forbidden to you.\" So he took hold of my clothes at the chest and said: \"I see you are a man eloquent of tongue and broad of heart, and a man may have ten traits, nine good and one bad, and the bad trait corrupts the nine good ones. Beware of the flights of youth,\" or he said: \"the deceptions of youth.\""],[8219,"He is named, then his 'aqiqah is performed on his seventh day, then his head is shaved,\" and he would say: \"His head is smeared with the blood."],[8220,"An 'aqiqah is performed for him, and he is named on his seventh day. If no 'aqiqah is performed, the udhiyah (sacrifice) suffices for him."],[8221,"Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whenever a child was born to her, she would give instructions concerning him. His head was shaved, then she would give in charity the weight of his hair in silver.\" She said: \"My father used to do that."],[8222,"Whenever Fatimah gave birth, she would shave the child's hair, then give in charity its weight in silver."],[8224,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach a boy of Banu Hashim, when he spoke clearly, seven times: ﴿Praise be to Allah, who has not taken a son and has had no partner in dominion﴾, to the end of the surah."],[8225,"They used to consider it recommended that the first thing a child is taught when he speaks clearly is 'La ilaha illa Allah' (There is no god but Allah) seven times, and that would be the first thing he uttered."],[8227,"The Prophet ﷺ named Husayn on his seventh day, and he derived the name Husayn from Hasan. He mentioned that there was nothing between them except the pregnancy."],[8228,"Al-Hasan b. 'Ali was the one among them who most resembled the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[8229,"When Fatimah gave birth to al-Hasan b. 'Ali, she brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he named him Hasan. When she gave birth to Husayn, she brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, this one is more beautiful than that one,' meaning Husayn. So he derived a name for him from his own name and called him Husayn."],[8230,"My mother did nothing for me on the day I was circumcised except make 'asidah (a dish) with dates."],[8231,"A boy was born to me tonight, so I named him after my father, Ibrahim."],[8233,"When 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz had a child born to him, he would take him just as he was in his cloth, then pronounce the adhan in his right ear and the iqamah in his left, and name him on the spot."],[8234,"For the pigeon, a sheep."],[8235,"The Prophet ﷺ used to seek protection for Hasan and Husayn, saying: 'I seek protection for you two with the perfect words of Allah from every devil and poisonous creature, and from every evil eye.' He said: And the Prophet ﷺ said: 'Seek protection with these words for your children, for Ibrahim ﷺ used to seek protection with them for his two sons, Isma'il and Ishaq.'"],[8237,"The people of the Jahiliyyah used to sacrifice one of every fifty (camels) as the fara'ah. When Islam came, the Prophet ﷺ was asked about that. He said: 'If you wish, do it, but he did not make it obligatory.'"],[8238,"The people of the Jahiliyyah used to offer the fara'ah. When Islam came, the Prophet ﷺ was asked about that. He said: 'If you wish, offer the fara'ah, but to leave it until it matures and you carry (loads) on it in the way of Allah is better than slaughtering it so that its meat mixes with its hair.'"],[8239,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the fara'. He said: 'Offer the fara' if you wish, but to leave it until it matures and is carried on in the way of Allah, or you use it to maintain kinship ties, is better than slaughtering it so that its meat mixes with its hair.'"],[8240,"He said regarding the fara'ah: 'It is a duty, but do not slaughter it while it is a small, glue-like (weak) animal that sticks to your hand. Rather, let it have milk until, when it is among the best of the property, then slaughter it.'"],[8241,"Abu Hurayrah was asked about the fara'ah. He said: 'It is a duty, but it does not mean that you slaughter it while it is a small, weak (animal). Rather, you let it have milk until, when it is the most precious of your property, you slaughter it or carry (loads) on it.'"],[8242,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about the fara'ah. He said: \"Offer the fara'ah if you wish.\""],[8243,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about the fara'ah. He said: \"The fara'ah is a right, but that you leave it until it becomes a shughrufi, a b. makhad or a b. labun, and then carry [someone] on it in the path of God, or give it to a widow, is better than slaughtering it, when its flesh would stick to its fur, and you would overturn your vessel, and bereave your she-camel.\""],[8244,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about the fara'. He said: \"It is a right, but that you leave it until it becomes a b. makhad or a b. labun, zahzabba, is better than that you overturn your vessel, bereave your she-camel, and slaughter it, so that its hair becomes mixed—or he said—sticks to its flesh.\""],[8245,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded the fara'ah: one from every fifty [camels]."],[8246,"There is no fara' and no 'atirah. The fara' is the first offspring; it used to be produced for them and they would slaughter it."],[8247,"A man asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about the 'atirah. He said: \"We used to slaughter a sheep in Rajab in the Jahiliyyah, calling it the 'atirah. Should we slaughter it today?\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Slaughter for God in whatever month it is, act piously toward God, and feed others.\""],[8248,"The people of the Jahiliyyah used to slaughter a sheep for every household in Rajab, calling it the 'atirah. When Islam came, some men, among them 'Abd Allah b. 'Amr, asked the Messenger of God ﷺ: \"Something we used to do in the Jahiliyyah, O Messenger of God, which we called the 'atirah. We used to slaughter it for the people of every household in Rajab. Should we do it in Islam?\" He said: \"Yes, and call it the rajibah.\""],[8249,"I reached the Prophet ﷺ on the day of 'Arafah while he was saying: \"Do you know it?\" He said: \"I do not know what they replied to him.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Upon the people of every household is to slaughter a sheep in every Rajab, and at every 'Adha a sheep.\""],[8250,"I said to Mujahid: \"I heard a man in the mosque of Kufah say: 'By the Lord of this mosque, I indeed slaughtered the 'atirah in the Jahiliyyah and in Islam.'\" He asked me: \"Where did you hear this?\" He said: I said: \"In the mosque of Kufah.\" He said: \"I have not seen a land more deserving that knowledge be heard in it that has not been heard, than the mosque of Kufah,\" or he said: \"than Kufah.\""],[8251,"For the sparrow, half a dirham."],[8253,"The entire Haram is a mosque; he may observe i'tikaf in whichever part of it he wishes, and if he wishes, in his dwelling, except that he should not pray except in congregation."],[8254,"For the sparrow, half a dirham."],[8255,"For the sparrow, half a dirham."],[8257,"There is no i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."],[8258,"There is no i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."],[8259,"They both used to permit i'tikaf in the mosques of the tribes in which the prayer is established."],[8261,"Abu al-Ahwas used to perform i'tikaf in the mosque of his people."],[8262,"Hudhayfah came to 'Abd Allah and said: Shall I not amaze you about people performing i'tikaf between your house and the house of al-Ash'ari? 'Abd Allah said: Perhaps they were right and you were wrong. Hudhayfah said: \"I do not care whether I perform i'tikaf in it or in these houses of yours. I'tikaf is only in these three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the mosque of Madinah, and the Aqsa Mosque.\" And those who performed i'tikaf - whom Hudhayfah criticized - were in the Great Mosque of Kufah."],[8263,"A man performed i'tikaf in the mosque in a tent of his, and the people threw pebbles at him. He said: \"The man sent me to 'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud, and 'Abd Allah came and drove the people away and considered that good.\""],[8264,"Hudhayfah said to 'Abd Allah: People are performing i'tikaf between your house and the house of Abu Musa; will you not forbid them? 'Abd Allah said to him: Perhaps they were right and you were wrong, and they remembered and you forgot. Hudhayfah said: \"There is no i'tikaf except in these three mosques: the mosque of Madinah, the mosque of Makkah, and the mosque of Iliya'.\""],[8265,"There is no i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."],[8266,"There is no spiritual retreat except in a congregational mosque. Then he said: \"There is no spiritual retreat except in the mosque of Makkah and the mosque of Madinah.\""],[8268,"I said to 'Ata': What do you think if a person from the people of these waters vowed a retreat—I named for him al-Zahran and 'Usfan—in their mosque? He said: \"He fulfills it if he made it binding upon himself in that mosque.\" I said: He vowed a retreat in the mosque of Mina? He said: \"Then let him retreat in it, for it has a special status.\" I said: May he set up his structure there at Mina in the house? He said: \"No, because of the threshold of the door.\" I said: Then in our mosque likewise? He said: \"No, that threshold belongs to the house, and it is not like the form of this mosque of ours.\" Then he said afterwards: \"There is no retreat except in the mosque of Makkah and the mosque of Madinah.\" He said: \"And the people of al-Basra retreat in their mosque until one of them retreats in the mosque of his own house.\""],[8269,"I said to 'Ata': What about the mosque of Ilya'? He said: \"He should not retreat except in the mosque of Makkah and the mosque of Madinah.\""],[8270,"'A'ishah made i'tikaf between Hira' and Thabir, and we used to come to her there, and a slave of hers led her in prayer."],[8271,"That 'A'ishah vowed a retreat in the hollow of Thabir on the side facing Mina. I said: So she did retreat? He said: Yes, and 'Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Bakr had forbidden her to retreat fearing it would be taken as a sunnah, so 'A'ishah said: \"It was a need within my soul.\""],[8272,"I asked Ibrahim about a woman who made i'tikaf in the mosque of her house: may she pass through its portico? He said: \"Yes, it is a path.\" I said: She made i'tikaf in its portico, may she pass through her house? He said: \"No.\""],[8275,"For a gazelle, a sheep."],[8276,"We have not heard that the fox is to be redeemed."],[8277,"For a rabbit, a sheep is due."],[8278,"For a rock hyrax, if it is eaten, a sheep is due."],[8279,"When the Prophet ﷺ returned from Hunayn, Umar asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a vow he had made in the pre-Islamic era: a day's i'tikaf. He ordered him to fulfill it."],[8280,"The Prophet ﷺ intended to observe i'tikaf during the last ten days of Ramadan. She said: I asked his permission, and he granted it to me. Hafsa asked his permission, and he granted it to her. Zaynab heard about that. She said: The Prophet ﷺ, when he intended to observe i'tikaf, would pray Fajr, then go to his place of i'tikaf, and he ordered a structure to be built, so it was erected. She said: When he prayed Fajr, suddenly there were four structures. He said, \"What is this?\" They said: Aisha, Hafsa, and Zaynab. He said, \"Do you say they intend righteousness by this?\" So he removed his structure. She said: He did not observe i'tikaf during the last ten days of Ramadan, and he observed i'tikaf for ten days in Shawwal."],[8281,"I heard Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah mention that his mother died while she owed an i'tikaf. He said: I rushed with my brothers to Ibn Abbas and asked him. He said: Observe i'tikaf on her behalf and fast."],[8282,"For a rabbit, a sheep is due."],[8283,"For a rabbit, a sheep is due."],[8284,"For the pigeon, a sheep."],[8285,"For the pigeon, a sheep."],[8286,"For the pigeon, a sheep."],[8289,"A woman vowed to observe i'tikaf for a month during the time of Ziyad, and he used to prevent i'tikaf because of the Kharijites. It was interceded for her, but he refused to give her permission. They asked Shurayh, and he said: She should fast and break her fast each day by feeding a poor person—two rites in place of one."],[8291,"The one in I'tikaf has what he stipulated at the time of his I'tikaf."],[8292,"For the Syrian pigeon, its value; no increase upon you in it."],[8293,"Ali and Ibn Mas'ud said regarding the one staying in the mosque: \"He has his intention.\""],[8294,"For the sparrow, half a dirham."],[8295,"The one in I'tikaf stipulates [attending] the Friday prayer, funerals, and visiting the sick, and if a need arises for him."],[8296,"For a rabbit, a sheep is due."],[8297,"This is not I'tikaf."],[8298,"Whoever is in I'tikaf should not speak obscenely or insult anyone. He should attend the Friday prayer and funerals. He may instruct his family if he has a need while standing, and he should not sit with them."],[8299,"The one in I'tikaf may visit the sick, follow funerals, and respond to a commander if he invites him."],[8300,"The one in I'tikaf does not leave except for a necessity he cannot avoid, such as defecation or urination. He does not follow funerals, visit the sick, answer an invitation, touch a woman, or engage in foreplay with her."],[8301,"Regarding the louse: a handful or a morsel. If you kill it unknowingly, nothing is due from you.” I said, “Is the locust like it?” He said, “Like it.”"],[8302,"The one in I'tikaf does not leave except for a necessity he cannot avoid, such as defecation or urination. He does not follow funerals, visit the sick, answer an invitation, touch a woman, or engage in foreplay with her."],[8303,"The person performing i'tikaf does not answer an invitation, does not visit the sick, and does not follow a funeral procession. And there is no i'tikaf except with fasting, and there is no i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."],[8304,"A'ishah, during her i'tikaf, when she went out to her house for some need, she would pass by a sick person and ask about him while she was just passing by, not stopping for him."],[8305,"She would pass by a sick person from her family while she was just passing by and would not turn to him."],[8307,"He used to grant a concession to the person performing i'tikaf to visit the sick without sitting down, and he used to grant him a concession to accompany a funeral procession."],[8308,"He saw no harm if a person performing i'tikaf went out for a need and a man met him and asked him to stop so he could talk with him."],[8309,"I said to 'Ata': 'What do you think if his child or a close relative dies?' He said: 'Glory be to Allah! Should we let him follow the funeral procession and cut off his seclusion?' I said: 'He prays over people's funeral prayers.' He said: 'If his seclusion is at the door of the mosque, then yes; but if his seclusion is in the middle of it, then no.'"],[8310,"For the sparrow, half a dirham."],[8311,"A'ishah, during her i'tikaf, would enter her house for some need and pass by a sick person and ask about him while she was passing by, not stopping for him."],[8312,"The person performing i'tikaf does not visit the sick, does not answer an invitation, and does not follow a funeral procession."],[8313,"I said to 'Ata': 'Is it obligatory for him to spend the night in the mosque?' He said: 'No. If he has a tent at the door of the mosque, it does not matter in which of them he spends the night, but I prefer that he spend the night in the mosque.'"],[8314,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was performing i'tikaf, and I came to him at night and talked with him. Then I got up, and he got up with me to accompany me back. Her dwelling was in the room of Usamah b. Zayd. Two men of the Ansar passed by, and when they saw the Prophet ﷺ, they hurried away. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Slow down! It is Safiyyah bt. Huyayy.' They said: 'Glory be to Allah, O Messenger of Allah!' He said: 'Satan flows through the human being like blood, and I feared that he might cast something into your hearts' - or he said: 'evil.'"],[8315,"The Prophet ﷺ was in I'tikaf in the mosque, and his wives gathered around him, then they dispersed. He said to Safiyya bt. Huyayy: “I will escort you to your house.” So he went with her until he brought her into her house, while he was in I'tikaf."],[8316,"Sawda, the Prophet's wife ﷺ, went out one night. 'Umar b. al-Khattab saw her and said: “You will not be hidden from us,” for she was tall. That was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ while he was eating a bone with meat on it, and he did not put it down until it was revealed to him: “You have been permitted to go out for your needs at night.”"],[8317,"I asked about the eggs of pigeons that the muhrim kills, and he said: 'A ruling is made upon him at the time he kills it for its value.'"],[8318,"The one in I'tikaf does not sell and does not buy, and does not go out to a ruler to litigate before him unless he intends that."],[8319,"There is no harm in the one in I'tikaf litigating before a commander in the mosque, or settling with a debtor, or advising his family about their work and the welfare of their livelihood, and writing a letter concerning his need."],[8321,"There is no harm in game entering the Haram and then being slaughtered."],[8323,"'Ali gave Ja'da b. Hubayra six hundred dirhams, helping him with it toward the price of a servant. He met him and said: “Have you bought the servant?” He said: “I am in I'tikaf.” He said: “What would it matter to you if you went out to the market and bought her?”"],[8326,"He does not go across the land except to attend a prayer or to go for relieving himself."],[8327,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “If a creditor of his comes to him during his retreat, and he gives him his due?” He said: “There is no harm in that.” I said: “Should he be brought [merchandise] during his retreat—may he buy and sell in it?” He said: “There is no harm in that.”"],[8328,"From al-Zuhrī regarding one who has intercourse with his wife while he is in iʿtikāf, he said: “Nothing has reached us concerning that, but we hold that he should free a slave, like the expiation of one who has intercourse with his wife during Ramadan.”"],[8329,"From al-Ḥasan regarding one who has intercourse with his wife while he is in iʿtikāf, he said: “He should free a slave; if he cannot find one, then he should fast two consecutive months; and if he is unable, then feed sixty poor persons.”"],[8330,"If the one in iʿtikāf has intercourse with his wife, he should start his iʿtikāf anew."],[8331,"The one in iʿtikāf should not approach his wife at night or during the day. He would say: “He should not have intercourse with his wife, nor kiss, nor touch, nor feel, nor lie with her—he should keep away from her as much as he can.”"],[8332,"Nothing interrupts his retreat except intercourse itself, like fasting and Hajj."],[8333,"From al-Shaʿbī regarding a woman who vowed to observe iʿtikāf for fifty days, then her husband turned her back, he said: “She should make up what remains upon her.”"],[8334,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “If a disputant comes to him during his retreat?” He said: “He should defend himself and argue with him.”"],[8335,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What do you think—if this one in retreat is brought goods in his tent to sell or buy, should he do it?” He said: “Yes, he may sell during his retreat.”"],[8336,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “What do you think—if he goes to the market just to look?” He said: “I dislike that; it is only about remembrance and worship.” I said: “May he write during his retreat to a governor seeking worldly gain, or to a servant of his?” He said: “There is no harm in that.”"],[8337,"There is no harm in the one in I'tikaf litigating before a commander in the mosque, or settling with a debtor, or advising his family about their work and the welfare of their livelihood, and writing a letter concerning his need."],[8338,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: “Was it said that he should not enter a house, nor pass under a roof or a threshold?” He said: “Yes.”"],[8339,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: The one staying near the door of the mosque, may he sit under a canopy? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Is he then under a roof? He said: \"The mosque is not like anything.\" A person said: What if he goes to relieve himself? He said: \"In the mountains and on the elevations.\" I said: A person staying in the middle of the mosque, may he set up his tent at its door for his needs if he wishes? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: Have you considered, if he goes to relieve himself, may he pass under a roof? He said: \"No.\" I said: May he pass under an arched vault, or stones, with no threshold in it and no wood? He said: \"Yes.\""],[8340,"ʿAmr b. Dīnār informed me concerning the arched vault. He said: What threshold is more severe than the arched vault? I said: \"What about a stone with a crossbeam?\" He said: \"Yes, that is a threshold; he should not pass under it.\""],[8341,"ʿAṭāʾ differentiated for me between the spiritual retreat of the villager and the bedouin. He said: \"As for the villager, when he vows the retreat, he abandons his house, abandons the wife, and fasts. The bedouin is not from the people of Makkah, so when he vows the retreat, all of Makkah becomes for him... He may retreat in whichever part of Makkah he wishes, and in whichever of its houses he wishes, and he does not fast, and he may have intercourse with women if he wishes, and he may sell and buy, and attend gatherings, and enter houses, and visit the sick, and follow the funeral procession, unless he intends within himself that his retreat should be at the door of the mosque and that he abstain from what is forbidden during retreat. And he made the people of ʿArafah from the people of Makkah.\" And he recited that for someone whose family are not present at the Sacred Mosque. He said: \"And we have heard that said.\" I said: May he go out to his family for a need in an matter that has become urgent for him? He said: \"No.\" I said: Then why does he perform Hajj and why does he perform ʿUmrah and why do they differ? He said: \"Hajj and ʿUmrah are better than what he is in.\""],[8342,"He said concerning the bedouin: if he vows a retreat without intending it at the door of the mosque, then he may retreat in whichever village he wishes."],[8343,"One who is not from its people may retreat wherever he wishes in it, while its people retreat at the door of the mosque if he intended the retreat at its door. Sleeping in the mosque is disliked."],[8344,"The entire Haram is a mosque; he may observe i'tikaf in whichever part of it he wishes, and if he wishes, in his dwelling, except that he should not pray except in congregation."],[8345,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ about a person who vowed a retreat for a year. He said: \"Let him perform Hajj, and make up what he missed during the Hajj, and he should not start a new year afresh.\""],[8346,"When a woman menstruates while she is in retreat, she goes out to her house; when she becomes pure, she makes up for that."],[8347,"When she menstruates while in retreat, she returns to her house; when she becomes pure, let her return to her retreat."],[8349,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: She became pure during part of the day. He said: \"Let her go that day, and she should not count that day.\""],[8350,"When a woman retreats and then menstruates, let her pitch a tent in her house; when she becomes pure, she makes up those days."],[8352,"I said to 'Ata': 'Do you think that if she becomes pure while she is in her house, may her husband touch her that day?' He said: 'No, unless that cuts off her spiritual retreat.' I said: 'And may he not kiss her?' He said: 'No.' I said: 'During her menstruation, may her husband kiss her?' He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'And may he have contact with her upper body?' He said: 'Yes.' Ibn Jurayj said: 'And 'Amr b. Dinar said the same.' 'Ata' said: 'He may have from her what a man has from his menstruating wife outside spiritual retreat.'"],[8353,"I said to 'Ata': 'If she complains of an illness that prevents her from fasting?' He said: 'She returns to her house if she wishes until she recovers.' I said: 'May her husband touch her during her illness?' He said: 'She has God then, unless she cuts off her spiritual retreat.' I said: 'Not even a kiss or anything?' He said: 'No.'"],[8354,"There is no harm in a woman in spiritual retreat being married during her retreat. Ibn Jurayj said: 'Ata' was asked: 'May a woman in spiritual retreat perfume herself and adorn herself?' He said: 'No. Do you want her husband to fall upon her? She should not perfume herself.' I said: 'If she does so, does that cut off her retreat?' He said: 'No. And why would she do that while she is in worship and humility? The perfume and adornment of a woman are only for her husband.'"],[8355,"He performed Umrah with Uthman in a caravan. When they were at al-Rawha', bird meat was presented to them. Uthman said, \"Eat,\" but he disliked eating from it. Amr b. al-As said, \"Should we eat from what you are not eating?\" He said, \"I am not in that matter like you. It was only hunted for me and killed on my account—or he said: for my sake.\""],[8357,"A woman went out perfumed during the time of 'Umar b. al-Khattab. He smelled her scent and struck her with the whip, then said: 'You go out perfumed, so men smell your scent. The hearts of men are only at their noses. Go out unperfumed.'"],[8358,"If he slaughters it and then eats it, then two expiations are due."],[8359,"ʿĀʾishah had a spear with which she used to kill geckos."],[8360,"A prophet among the prophets alighted under a tree, and his baggage was beneath it. An ant bit him, so he ordered his baggage to be lifted, then he ordered the tree, and it was burned. Then Allah, Exalted is He, revealed to him: “Why not just one ant?” – meaning the one that bit him."],[8361,"A woman went out adorned, with her husband's permission. 'Umar b. al-Khattab was informed of her, so he sought her but could not find her. He stood up to deliver a sermon and said: 'This woman who went out, and this one who sent her—if I get hold of them, I will make an example of them.' Then he said: 'A woman goes out to her father, he struggles with himself, and to her brother, he struggles with himself. When she goes out, let her wear her working clothes; when she returns, let her take her adornment in her house and adorn herself for her husband.'"],[8362,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said to 'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud's wife: 'If one of you women wants to attend the evening prayer, she should not touch perfume.'"],[8363,"She sent to Hafsa, her sister, asking her about perfume, and she wanted to go out. Hafsa, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: 'Perfume is only for the bed.'"],[8364,"To crowd against a camel that has been tarred is dearer to me than to crowd against a woman who has perfumed herself, and that a man's belly be filled with pus is better for him than that it be filled with poetry."],[8365,"To crowd against a camel that has been tarred is dearer to me than to crowd against a woman who has perfumed herself, and that a man's belly be filled with pus is better for him than that it be filled with poetry."],[8366,"Ibrahim's wife asked his permission to visit some of her family, and he gave her permission. When she went out, he noticed a pleasant scent from her and said, \"Return, for when a woman perfumes herself and then goes out, it is only fire and disgrace.\""],[8367,"Umar b. al-Khattab walked among the rows of women and noticed a pleasant scent from a woman's head. He said, \"If I knew which of you it was, I would do such and such. Let one of you perfume herself for her husband, and when she goes out, let her wear the worn-out garments of her slave girl.\" He said, \"It reached me that the woman who had perfumed herself urinated in her clothes out of fear.\""],[8368,"There is no deed more excellent than a deed in the ten days of Dhu al-Hijjah. It was said, \"O Messenger of God, not even jihad in the path of God?\" He said, \"Not even jihad in the path of God, unless it reaches the point of killing.\""],[8369,"There is no deed in the days of the year more excellent than a deed in the ten days of Dhu al-Hijjah. He said, \"And it is the ten which God completed for Musa.\""],[8370,"Masruq was asked about 'the dawn and the ten nights.' He said, \"They are the best days of the year.\""],[8371,"Castration is mutilation."],[8372,"Adi b. Artah said to al-Hasan, \"Will you not go out with the people and stand with them at Arafah?\" That was in Basra. Al-Hasan said, \"The standing is only at Arafah.\""],[8373,"I entered upon al-Hasan while he was praying, and I discussed something of the Quran with his son. He turned to us and said, \"What are you two discussing?\" I said, \"Ta-Sin-Mim and Ha-Mim.\" He said, \"They are openings with which the Quran is opened.\""],[8374,"The first to hold the standing in our land was Ibn Abbas. He would prepare on the evening of Arafah and recite the Quran, al-Baqarah, verse by verse, and he was a flowing spring of knowledge."],[8375,"The best supplication is the supplication on the day of Arafah, and the best that I and the prophets before me have said is: 'There is no god but God alone, with no partner.'"],[8376,"There is no day on which Iblis is more driven away, more cast out, or more enraged than the Day of Arafah, because of what he sees of the descent of mercy and Allah, exalted is He, overlooking grave matters — except what he saw on the Day of Badr.” It was said, “And what did he see on the Day of Badr?” He said, “He saw Jibril marshalling the angels.”"],[8377,"Fasting one day of the ten [days] is equivalent to two months."],[8378,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was never seen fasting during the ten [days]."],[8380,"He used to sacrifice in Madinah two horned, black and white rams."],[8381,"The Prophet ﷺ sacrificed two rams."],[8382,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a donkey that had been branded on its face and said: \"May Allah curse the one who did this.\""],[8383,"The Messenger of God ﷺ sacrificed a black-eyed, horned, sturdy ram."],[8385,"There is no proper slaughter except in the throat (nahr) or the upper chest (dhabh)."],[8386,"There is no harm in the Magian's bread."],[8387,"Eat what your trained dog has eaten from, even if it has eaten."],[8388,"'Ali sacrificed two rams."],[8389,"The sacrificial animals are of no consequence, or he said: It is not obligatory. Whoever wishes may offer a sacrifice, and whoever wishes may not offer one."],[8390,"No one of his family would ask him in Madinah for a sacrificial animal but that he would offer a sacrifice on his behalf; but he would not offer a sacrifice for them in Mina."],[8391,"I saw Abu Bakr and Umar, and they were not offering sacrifices."],[8393,"He disliked killing game with a broad-headed arrow and then eating it."],[8394,"Permission was granted for the pilgrim and the traveler not to offer a sacrifice."],[8395,"They used to perform Hajj carrying money with them, but they would not offer sacrifices."],[8396,"When they were present, they would offer sacrifices, but when they traveled, they would not offer sacrifices."],[8397,"Sa'd b. Malik sent to us while we were in Mina: 'We have not slaughtered or offered a sacrifice, so feed us.'"],[8398,"Ibn Abbas sent me to buy meat for him for two dirhams, and he said: 'Say this is the sacrifice of Ibn Abbas.'"],[8399,"That I should not offer a sacrifice is dearer to me than that I should consider it obligatory upon me."],[8400,"I considered abandoning the sacrifice, though I am among the wealthiest of you with regard to it, for fear that it would be thought to be an obligatory duty."],[8401,"I certainly leave off the sacrifice, even though I am well-off, for fear that my neighbors may think it is obligatory upon me."],[8402,"My family drove me to coarseness after I had come to know the Sunnah. The people of a household used to sacrifice a sheep or two sheep, but now our neighbors make us stingy."],[8405,"The Prophet ﷺ distributed sheep among us, and I received a jadhaʿ. I sacrificed it on behalf of my household, then I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'It suffices for you.'"],[8406,"We were known only for that until the people of Iraq mixed with us. He said: The people of a household used to sacrifice a sheep, so they sacrificed one sheep for each individual."],[8407,"I performed Hajj three times and did not shed blood in any of them. He said: To leave it while I am well-off is more beloved to me than to sacrifice while I am in hardship."],[8408,"It would not matter to me if I sacrificed a rooster. But giving its price in charity to an orphan or one covered with dust is more beloved to me than sacrificing it."],[8409,"That I sacrifice a jadhaʿ is more beloved to me than sacrificing an old animal. Allah is more deserving of richness and generosity. The most beloved of them to me to sacrifice is the one most beloved to me to keep as property."],[8410,"None of you should offer to Allah what he would be ashamed to offer to his honored friend. Allah is the most generous of the generous, and the most deserving of having the best chosen for Him."],[8411,"I reached the Prophet ﷺ on the day of 'Arafah while he was saying: \"Do you know it?\" He said: \"I do not know what they replied to him.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Upon the people of every household is to slaughter a sheep in every Rajab, and at every 'Adha a sheep.\""],[8412,"She used to slaughter a sheep for herself at Mina, but she did not slaughter on our behalf."],[8413,"When a man buys his sacrificial animal and it falls ill while with him, or a disease afflicts it, it remains permissible."],[8415,"Silver has not taken a path in anything of its measure more excellent than the price of a badanah."],[8416,"I came to Madinah with my camels, and said: If only I entered the mosque. He said: So I entered the mosque, and there was Umar b. al-Khattab delivering a sermon saying: O people of Madinah, perform Hajj and offer hady, for Allah loves the hady. He said: So I returned to my camels, and there was every man embracing a camel from them. He said: Then Umar came and looked at them, and said: These are the camels of a Muhajir man."],[8417,"The blood of a white one is more beloved to Allah than the blood of two black ones."],[8418,"That I sacrifice a sheep is more beloved to me than that I give a hundred dirhams in charity."],[8419,"Sacrifice, and feel good about them yourselves; for there is no Muslim who directs his sacrificial animal toward the qiblah except that its blood, its dung, and its wool become good deeds presented in his scale on the Day of Resurrection."],[8420,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Aishah or to Fatimah: Witness your sacrifice; for you will be forgiven at the first drop of its blood."],[8421,"He used to command his daughters to slaughter their sacrificial animals with their own hands."],[8422,"A slave girl of Ka'b b. Malik used to tend sheep of his, and a sheep fell ill, so she slaughtered it with a sharp stone. He asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he commanded him to eat it."],[8423,"He commanded him to slaughter a camel while he was in ihram."],[8424,"Regarding His saying: 'Allah will surely test you with something of the game that your hands and spears reach' (Quran 5:94), he said: Your taking them from their eggs and their chicks, and your spears are what you threw or thrust with."],[8426,"If he takes it deliberately regarding its inviolability, deliberately killing it, no judgment is passed against him. And if he takes it deliberately for it but forgetful of his ihram, judgment is passed against him."],[8427,"8236 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And Ibn Jurayj said it, from 'Ata'. Hidden text: Judgment is passed against the one who takes game once in the case of intentional killing. Then he retracted and said: Judgment is passed against him in intentional, accidental, and forgetful cases, and every time he takes game. 'Ata' said: “God has pardoned what is past.” He said: “In the pre-Islamic era. And whoever takes it in Islam, God will not leave him until He exacts retribution from him, and along with that is the expiation.” Hidden chain: 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And Ibn Jurayj said it, from, from."],[8430,"Judgment is passed against him in intentional cases, and in accidental cases it is the Sunnah. Abu Bakr said: “And that is the saying of the people, and we adopt it.”"],[8431,"There is no harm in their slaughter."],[8432,"There is no harm in their slaughter."],[8435,"That 'Umar “ruled regarding accidental cases”"],[8436,"Concerning the pilgrim in ihram who kills game: judgment is passed against him, then he repeats. He said: \"Judgment is not passed against him again. If God wills, He pardons him, and if He wills, He seizes him.\" He said: And he recited this verse: “But whoever repeats, God will take retribution from him” (Q 5:95)."],[8437,"A man intentionally killed game in the sanctuary twice, and a fire came and struck him and burned him. Ma‘mar said: It reached me that a man in the Jahiliyyah took a gazelle in the sanctuary and held it by its neck until the gazelle urinated. He said: Then a snake came and coiled around the man’s neck, and it kept choking him until he urinated; then it released him."],[8438,"Killing game in the sanctuary was permitted once, but if he repeats, God will not leave him until He takes retribution from him in the matter of an intentional killing."],[8440,"Does the imam punish him for it? He said: \"No. It is a sin he committed between himself and his Lord.\" Al-Thawri reported from his companions: \"But let him offer a ransom.\""],[8441,"About the slave who kills game? He said: \"He fasts.\" It was said to him: What if his master gives him something to sacrifice? He said: \"Fasting is more beloved to me.\" Then he said: Layth reported to me from Mujahid who said: The slave owes nothing but fasting."],[8443,"The man has a choice between fasting, charity, and sacrificial animal as compensation for game killed."],[8445,"About His words: “Then compensation of the like of what he killed from livestock, as judged by two just men among you” (Q 5:95). He said: \"They judge against him a sacrificial animal, if he finds it; otherwise the sacrificial animal is valued in food, then the food is valued in fasting: for each poor person’s portion of food, one day of fasting.\" Mujahid said: \"For every two mudds, one day of fasting.\""],[8446,"About a man who killed game and could not find its compensation. He said: \"It is valued in dirhams, then the dirhams are valued in food, then he fasts two days for each sa‘.\""],[8447,"There is no harm in their slaughter."],[8449,"What is “or the equivalent of that in fasting” (5:95)? He said: If he kills a mountain goat, the goat is valued in food, then in place of each day a mudd is set, which he fasts."],[8450,"The least of game is a mountain goat."],[8451,"There is no harm in their slaughter."],[8452,"Regarding the compensation for game when the muhrim cannot find it, he said: “He fasts three days during the interval between it and ten days.”"],[8454,"For an ostrich, a camel; for a wild donkey, a cow; for a wild cow, a cow; for the great powerful mountain goat, a cow; for what is below the mountain goat, a sheep; and for the hyrax, a sheep."],[8455,"As for what has already been ruled and the sunnah has passed, for an ostrich a slaughtered camel."],[8456,"That 'Umar b. al-Khattab, 'Ali b. Abi Talib, 'Uthman b. 'Affan, and Zayd b. Thabit said: For an ostrich killed by a muhrim, a camel from the camels."],[8457,"I asked about the ransom for game. He said: The assessment: two just men assess it at that time. If you look at what has been ruled, high prices and low prices break it, but it is assessed against him when he kills it."],[8458,"Abu al-Malih b. Usama wrote to Abu 'Ubaydah b. 'Abd Allah asking him about an ostrich killed by a muhrim. Abu 'Ubaydah wrote back to him: “For it is a camel.”"],[8459,"For an ostrich, a camel; for a wild donkey, a cow; for a wild cow, a cow; for the great powerful mountain goat, a cow; for what is below the mountain goat, a sheep; and for the hyrax, a sheep."],[8460,"For an ostrich, a camel; for a wild donkey, a cow; for a wild cow, a cow; for the great powerful mountain goat, a cow; for what is below the mountain goat, a sheep; and for the hyrax, a sheep."],[8461,"For a wild cow, a cow."],[8462,"For a wild cow, a cow."],[8463,"For the large, old male ibex, a cow; and for anything less than that among ibex, a ram."],[8464,"If a slaughterer slaughters and severs the head, then eat as long as he did not intend that."],[8465,"For a sheep from the gazelles, a sheep; and the least that is due for game is a sheep."],[8466,"Abu Malih b. Usamah wrote to Abu Ubaydah b. Abd Allah asking him about a wild donkey that a pilgrim in ihram kills. He wrote back to him: \"A sacrificial camel is due for it,\" or he said: \"A cow.\""],[8467,"Umar b. al-Khattab ruled for a gazelle: a sheep."],[8468,"Slaughtering is cutting the jugular veins. I said, \"What if someone slaughters but does not cut its jugular veins?\" He said, \"I think he has already made it lawful to eat, so let him eat it.\""],[8469,"Umar b. al-Khattab ruled for a jerboa: a young she-goat.\" Ma'mar said: \"Al-Zuhri said: 'A ruling of compensation.'\""],[8470,"Ibn Mas'ud said: \"For a man who threw a sack over a jerboa and killed it while he was in ihram, he ruled a young he-goat,\" or he said: \"a young she-goat.\""],[8471,"For every animal with canine teeth, a sheep; and for a jerboa, a sheep."],[8472,"For a jerboa, a newborn lamb."],[8473,"We set out as pilgrims, and there we were with snakes as if they were boiling pots, so we killed them. He said: A man among us made his camel trample a dabb lizard and crushed its back. I asked ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb about the snakes, and he said: \"You have killed an enemy.\" And we asked him about the dabb, so he turned to me and to a man and said: \"Do you deem for me a kid that has reached water and herbage sufficient for it?\" He said: Yes. So he ordered him to give it."],[8474,"We set out as pilgrims, and a man among us called Arbad b. ʿAbd Allāh made his mount trample a dabb lizard. We came to ask ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, and Arbad asked him. ʿUmar said to him: \"Give a ruling for it.\" He said: You are better than I and more knowledgeable. He said: \"I only commanded you to give a ruling.\" He said: I said: For it a kid that has gathered water and herbage. He said: \"Then for it is that.\" He said: And we came upon snakes in the sand while we were in iḥrām, and we asked ʿUmar about them. He said: \"They are an enemy; kill them wherever you find them.\""],[8475,"Regarding the dabb: a handful of food, because the Messenger of God ﷺ did not eat it."],[8476,"He deemed the hyena game and ruled for it a ram."],[8477,"He ruled for the hyena a ram, for the gazelle a sheep, for the hare a young she-goat, and for the jerboa a jafrah."],[8478,"For the hyena: a ram."],[8479,"The Messenger of God ﷺ classed it as game, and decreed for it a Najdi ram."],[8480,"Had I a ruling, I would have ruled for the fox a kid."],[8481,"Slaughtering is cutting the jugular veins. I said, \"What if someone slaughters but does not cut its jugular veins?\" He said, \"I think he has already made it lawful to eat, so let him eat it.\""],[8482,"For the fox: a sheep."],[8483,"We have not heard that the fox is to be redeemed."],[8484,"He ruled for the hare a kid, or a young she-goat."],[8485,"He ruled for the hyena a ram, for the gazelle a sheep, for the hare a young she-goat, and for the jerboa a jafrah."],[8486,"He ruled, he and Ibn Abbas: \"For a rabbit, a jadha' or a weaned young.\""],[8487,"When it has hair or wool, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother."],[8488,"If you strike a fish with your stick and kill it, or throw a stone at it and it dies, then eat it in all cases, and locusts are like that."],[8489,"ʿAlī saw no harm in eating hyena, and he considered it game."],[8490,"I said to Ata: A mouse falls into solid rendered fat, or non-solid. He said: It has reached us that if it is solid, take what is around it and throw it away, and eat what remains. I said: What about non-solid? He said: Nothing has reached me about it, but I think it should be used as fuel for a lamp and not eaten."],[8491,"A man killed an antelope while in ihram, so he came to Ali and asked him, and he said: \"Sacrifice a ram from the sheep.\""],[8492,"I was in ihram and saw an antelope, so I shot it and hit its khushasha'—meaning the base of its horn—so it collapsed in its blood, and I felt uneasy about that. I came to Umar b. al-Khattab to ask him, and when I arrived, I found a fair-skinned man with a delicate face, and he was Abd al-Rahman b. Awf. So I asked Umar, and he turned to Abd al-Rahman and said: \"Do you think a sheep would suffice for him?\" He said: \"Yes.\" So he ordered me to slaughter a sheep. We got up from his presence, and a companion of mine said: \"The Commander of the Believers could not give you a proper fatwa until he asked the man.\" Umar heard his words and struck him with the whip. Then Umar turned to me to strike me, but I said: \"O Commander of the Believers, I did not say anything; it was he who said it.\" So he left me, then said: \"You wanted to kill the sacred game and transgress in the fatwa.\" He said: \"In a person there are ten traits, nine good and one bad, and that one bad trait corrupts them.\" And he said: \"Beware of the slip of youth.\""],[8493,"We set out as pilgrims, and while we were traveling, the people argued a lot about which is faster in running, the antelope or the horse. Then an antelope appeared to us, and the sunuh is like this—and he gestured from left to right—and a man among us shot it and did not miss its khushasha', so it collapsed in its blood, and he was at a loss until we came to Umar. We came to him while he was at Mina, and I sat before him with him, and he told him the news. He said: \"How did you hit it, by mistake or intentionally?\" Sufyan said: Mis'ar said: \"I deliberately aimed at it, but I did not intend to kill it.\" He said: And I remember that he said: The man became confused and said: \"I did not hit it by mistake or intentionally.\" So Mis'ar said: He said to him: \"You have combined intention and mistake.\" So he leaned toward a man, and by God, his face was like a heart, and consulted him, then turned to us and said: \"Take a sheep, shed its blood, give its meat as charity, and use its hide as a waterskin.\" So we got up from his presence, and I said: \"O seeker of fatwa from Ibn al-Khattab, his fatwa will not avail you anything before God. So sacrifice your camel and exalt the rituals of God. By God, Umar did not know until he asked the man beside him.\" The man with the two eyes went and reported it to Umar. By God, I was only aware when he came at my companion with the whip, striking him, then said: \"May God fight you, do you transgress in the fatwa and kill the sacred game?\" Then he turned to me, and I said: \"O Commander of the Believers, I will not make lawful for you what God has made forbidden to you.\" So he took hold of my clothes at the chest and said: \"I see you are a man eloquent of tongue and broad of heart, and a man may have ten traits, nine good and one bad, and the bad trait corrupts the nine good ones. Beware of the flights of youth,\" or he said: \"the deceptions of youth.\""],[8494,"Two pilgrims raced to the hill of al-Batin, and one of them hit an antelope and killed it. He came to Umar b. al-Khattab, and he said: \"Slaughter a dust-colored sheep.\""],[8495,"Maymunah or Umm al-Fadl—Abu Bakr was uncertain—locked the door of her house in Makkah on a cat and her two kittens, and went out to Mina and Arafah. She found them dead, so she mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered her to free a slave for each one of them."],[8496,"Ibn Abbas was asked about hunting locusts in the Haram, and he forbade it. Either I said, or a man from the people said: \"Your people take them while they are sitting in the mosque.\" He said: \"They do not know.\""],[8497,"I was with Ibn ‘Abbās when a man asked him about a locust he had killed while in iḥrām. He said: “For it is a handful of wheat, and indeed you are taking a handful of locusts.”"],[8498,"I asked Sa‘īd b. Jubayr: I killed locusts, and I do not know how many they were, while I was in iḥrām. He said: “Then take dates; you do not know how many they were, so give charity accordingly.”"],[8499,"‘Umar b. al-Khaṭṭāb was asked about a locust killed by a muḥrim, and he said: “A date is better than a locust.”"],[8500,"Ka‘b asked: O Commander of the Faithful, while we were kindling a fire, I threw a locust into it while I was in iḥrām, and I gave a dirham in charity. ‘Umar said: “Indeed, O people of Ḥimṣ, your leaves are many. A date is more beloved to me than your locusts.”"],[8502,"I saw Sa‘īd b. Jubayr in Makkah going out and seeing locusts in the hands of children, and he would kill them from their hands. He considered it game."],[8503,"The least thing a muḥrim may afflict is the locust, and there is no compensation for what is below it. For it is a date."],[8504,"‘Umar ruled concerning the locust with a date."],[8505,"There is no harm in eating rabbit."],[8506,"I mentioned to him a hadith that Abdullah b. Abi Awfa narrated to me about the meat of donkeys. Said said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade them absolutely.\""],[8507,"For the louse, the ant, and similar creatures: “If a muḥrim kills them, a handful of food.”"],[8508,"If a muḥrim kills it, for it is a handful of food."],[8509,"Regarding the louse: a handful or a morsel. If you kill it unknowingly, nothing is due from you.” I said, “Is the locust like it?” He said, “Like it.”"],[8510,"You may kill the louse while you are in Makkah and not in ihram, and you may pick it up and throw it away if you see it on your garment or skin while in ihram, but do not kill it. Do not deliberately kill it while in ihram."],[8511,"I was with Ibn Umar, leaning on me, when a man came and said, “What do you say about a muhrim who kills a louse?” Ibn Umar said, “He slaughters a camel.” I laughed, and he looked at me and said, “Do not blame me. By God, he asks me about a louse while one of them attacks his brother with a sword.”"],[8513,"I asked Sa'id b. Jubayr about a muhrim killing a louse. He said, “Anything you feed in expiation for it is better than it.”"],[8514,"Ibn ʿUmar was asked about eating locusts, and he said: \"It is a lawful slaughter; eat it.\""],[8515,"Ibn Umar was asked about a muhrim killing a louse. He said, “Would one of you kill his Muslim brother, yet ask about a louse?” Then a woman came and said, “She killed a louse while in ihram. What is its expiation?” Ibn Umar said, “We do not consider the louse to be game.” She repeated, and he said the same. She repeated a third time, and he said, “A sheep is better than a louse,” and he looked at me so I could testify with him. I said, “Indeed, a sheep is better than a louse.”"],[8516,"He heard a man ask Ibn Abbas, saying, “I threw away a louse in Makkah while I was in ihram without remembering, then I sought it but did not find it.” Ibn Abbas said, “That is the lost thing that is not sought.”"],[8517,"Abd Allah b. Uthman b. Humayd came to Ibn Abbas and said, “My son killed a pigeon in Makkah.” Ibn Abbas said, “Provide a sheep and give it in charity.”"],[8518,"It reached him that ʿAlī used not to eat the flesh of al-jirrīth (a type of fish), nor enter a house containing an image, nor eat the spleen. He said: As for the spleen, the Messenger of God ﷺ found it repulsive and did not eat it, and said: It is merely a congealed lump of blood; so ʿAlī did not eat it. As for a house containing an image, the Prophet ﷺ would not enter a house containing an image. As for al-jirrīth, it is a fish that the People of the Book do not eat."],[8519,"Umar and Ibn Abbas ruled that a sheep is due for the pigeons of Makkah."],[8520,"Umar passed by a pigeon, and it flew and landed on al-Marwah, and a snake took it and killed it, so Umar imposed a sheep for it."],[8521,"That a pigeon was on the House and it dropped dung on Umar's hand. Umar gestured with his hand, so it flew and landed in one of the dwellings of Makkah. Then a snake came and ate it, so Umar made its compensation a sheep."],[8522,"For the pigeon of the Haram, a sheep; and for the pigeon of the Hill, a dirham."],[8524,"Whoever sets out for this House, and nothing moves him except prayer at it and touching the Stone, his previous sins will be expiated."],[8526,"That a man locked his door upon a pigeon and its two young, then he went to Mina and Arafat, and when he returned they had died. He said: So he came to Ibn Umar and mentioned that to him, and he imposed upon him three sheep, and he appointed an arbitrator along with him."],[8527,"We have heard that the righteousness of Hajj is good food and good speech."],[8529,"That he asked Salim and al-Qasim b. Muhammad about a partridge that he slaughtered while at Makkah, having forgotten. One of them said to his companion: Is a partridge in a man's belly better, or two-thirds of a mudd? He said: Rather two-thirds of a mudd. He said: Is it better, or half a mudd? He said: Half a mudd. He said: Is it better, or a third of a mudd? He said: I said to them: Would a sheep suffice for me? They said: Do you know? He said: Yes. They said: Then go."],[8530,"A sandgrouse in place of a partridge, and he did not say a partridge."],[8531,"I asked Sa'id b. Jubayr about a partridge that I slaughtered while I was not in ihram in Makkah, and he did not see any harm upon me. He said: How do you buy it? He said: Twenty for a dirham. He said: Then I will guide you to someone who sells it, forty for a dirham."],[8532,"For the small birds of prey, a mudd, a mudd — that is, the vulture and its like."],[8533,"Indeed the hoopoe is below the pigeon and above the sparrow; for it is a dirham. As for the ka't, it is a sparrow. As for the bat, it is above the sparrow and below the hoopoe; for it is two-thirds of a dirham. Whatever bird does not reach being a pigeon and is above the sparrow, for it is a dirham."],[8534,"For the wahaziyy or its like, and the dubsiyy, and the qatah, and the hubara, and the qamariyy, and the hajal, a sheep, a sheep."],[8536,"A man came to Ibn Abbas and said, 'I killed a quail while I was in ihram.' Ibn Abbas ruled against him a sheep."],[8537,"For the sparrow, half a dirham."],[8538,"Uthman b. Affan set out as a pilgrim and locked the door upon pigeons, and found they had died, so he ruled a sheep for each pigeon."],[8539,"He was asked about a muhrim man who killed a pigeon from the pigeons of the Haram, and he said: 'Two just men among you judge concerning it.' He said: 'A sheep; then for each egg, a dirham.'"],[8540,"For an egg from the eggs of the pigeons of Makkah, half a dirham; but if it is broken and contains a chick, then for it is a dirham."],[8542,"For an egg from the eggs of pigeons of the hill, a mudd."],[8543,"I stepped on a nest of Makkah pigeons while I was in Makkah, containing feathered chicks and an egg, and I killed the chick and broke the egg. I asked Ata, and he said: 'For the dead one, a sheep, but go to that circle, for in it is a shaykh, and he is Ubayd b. Umayr, and ask him; if he tells you anything, come back to me and inform me.' So I asked Ubayd, and he said: 'As for the chick that has feathers, for it is a sheep; and as for the egg, for it is half a dirham.' I said to him, 'What should I do?' He said: 'Slaughter the sheep, and buy food with half a dirham, grind it, and look to the poor near you and feed them; and if you are strangers or in need, then keep some of it.' Then I passed by Ata and informed him, and he said: 'Thus Ibn Abbas informed me.'"],[8544,"For two eggs, a dirham."],[8545,"I asked him about the eggs of ostriches that the muhrim kills, and he said: 'The ruling, a judgment is made upon him at the time he kills it for its value.'"],[8546,"A man from the Ansar trod upon an ostrich nest while he was in ihram—meaning its nest—and broke an egg. He asked Ali, who said, “You owe a camel fetus,” or he said, “the covering of a camel.” The Ansari went out and came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ said, “I have heard what Ali said, but come to the concession: fasting or feeding a poor person.”"],[8547,"Abu Mulayh b. Usama wrote to Abu Ubayda b. Abd Allah asking him about ostrich eggs that a pilgrim in ihram might damage. Abu Ubayda wrote back to him that Abd Allah b. Masud used to say, “For it is fasting a day or feeding a poor person.”"],[8553,"Regarding ostrich eggs and the like that a pilgrim in ihram damages, its value."],[8554,"A man asked Umar about ostrich eggs that a pilgrim in ihram damages. Umar said to him, “Go ask Ali, for we have been commanded to consult him.”"],[8555,"He ruled regarding a person in ihram who pointed out ostrich eggs to a person not in ihram; he ruled for it a day of fasting or feeding a poor person."],[8556,"Ali ruled regarding ostrich eggs that a pilgrim in ihram damages: “Send the stud camel upon your camels, and when their pregnancy becomes clear, you name the number of eggs you damaged.” I said, “This is a sacrificial animal, then you have no liability for what is spoiled.” Ibn Abbas said, “Muawiya was amazed at Ali’s ruling.” Ibn Abbas said, “Why is Muawiya amazed at something amazing? It is nothing but what the eggs were sold for in the market, given as charity.”"],[8557,"If you have no camels, then for each egg two dirhams."],[8558,"The Prophet ﷺ ruled regarding ostrich eggs that a pilgrim in ihram damages, its price."],[8560,"Whatever you hunt while you are not in ihram, and what is hunted while you are in ihram, do not eat it."],[8561,"Ibn Abbas used to forbid eating game if it was brought into the Haram alive. Ibn Jurayj said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir b. Abd Allah being asked about it, and he said: If it were slaughtered outside the Haram, that would be more beloved to me."],[8562,"Ibn Abbas used to forbid eating game if it was brought into the Haram alive. Ibn Jurayj said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir b. Abd Allah being asked about it, and he said: If it were slaughtered outside the Haram, that would be more beloved to me."],[8563,"There is no harm in eating game meat in the Haram. He said: 'Game is not slaughtered in the Haram, but if it were slaughtered outside the Haram and then brought into the Haram already slaughtered, there would be no harm in it.'"],[8565,"If game is brought into the Haram alive, there is no harm in eating it. It was said to Amr: 'Ata has retracted this statement of his.' He said: 'My knowledge of him is that he eats it.' And Amr saw no harm in eating it."],[8567,"Abd Allah b. Amir gave Ibn Umar some slaughtered gazelles as a gift while he was in Makkah, but he did not accept them."],[8568,"A man asked him, “O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what about a man employed over the alms who takes from them and performs Hajj with that?” Ibn Umar said, “What if someone stole the baggage of pilgrims and performed Hajj with it?” The man said, “May Allah forgive, O Abu Abd al-Rahman.” Ibn Umar said, “Verily, these alms are for the poor.”"],[8569,"He gave Ibn Umar live gazelles as a gift, and he returned them, saying: 'Could he not have slaughtered them before they entered the Haram? Once they entered the Haram, their place of safety, I have no need for his gift.'"],[8570,"Wiping the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner sheds sins absolutely."],[8572,"Ibn Umar, when he touched the Corner, would say: \"Bismillah, Allahu Akbar\"."],[8573,"Ibn Umar disliked eating game, even if it was brought into Makkah already slaughtered."],[8574,"Ibn Umar considered domesticated birds and gazelles to be in the category of game."],[8575,"I saw game being sold in Makkah alive during the rule of Ibn al-Zubayr."],[8576,"Ibn Umar disliked a pilgrim in ihram buying game outside the Haram and then slaughtering it inside the Haram."],[8577,"I have not omitted touching these two corners since I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ touching them. Nafiʿ said: Ibn ʿUmar used to crowd at the two corners until his nose bled, then he would come and wash it."],[8578,"He used to be in the mosque, and when he wanted to leave the mosque, he would touch the corner, then leave."],[8579,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by me while I was at al-Abwa. I gave him a wild donkey as a gift, but he returned it to me. When he saw the displeasure on my face, he said: It is not that we are refusing it to you, but we are in ihram."],[8580,"Zayd b. Arqam came, and Ibn Abbas asked him to recall: How did you tell me about the meat that was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in ihram? He said: Yes, a limb of game meat was gifted to him, and he returned it to him, saying: We do not eat it, for we are in ihram."],[8581,"A dried strip of gazelle meat was gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in ihram, and he did not eat it."],[8583,"That his father used to touch all the corners."],[8584,"He disliked game meat while he was in ihram, and he recited this verse: 'Lawful to you is game from the sea and its food as provision for you and the travelers, but forbidden to you is game from the land as long as you are in ihram.'"],[8585,"Mu'adh b. Jabal forbade them from eating game meat while they were in ihram."],[8586,"He used to dislike game meat for the muhrim. He said: I do not know but that Ibn Tawus informed me from his father that the Prophet ﷺ disliked it."],[8587,"It is left unspecified, regarding His saying: 'Forbidden to you is land game as long as you are in ihram' (5:96)."],[8588,"He heard Tawus being asked about people in ihram who passed by non-muhrim people who had caught a hyena and they ate from it with them. Tawus said: 'Glory be to God!' The one asking about them said: 'What do they slaughter? A sheep each?' Tawus said: 'Yes, if they do so voluntarily; otherwise a single sheep suffices for them each day.'"],[8589,"There is no harm in drinking while circumambulating the House."],[8591,"If a muhrim is forced to eat game, he may hunt it and no compensation is due from him; but if he finds carrion, he should begin with the carrion and leave the game."],[8592,"Al-Thawri was asked, while I listened, about a muhrim who is forced and finds carrion, pork, and game meat—which does he eat? He said: 'He eats the pork and the carrion.'"],[8593,"Talha b. 'Ubayd Allah asked the Messenger of God ﷺ whether a muhrim may eat game meat if it was slaughtered outside the sanctuary. He said: 'Yes.'"],[8594,"I went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ at the time of al-Hudaybiyya. His companions entered ihram, but I did not. I saw a wild donkey, so I attacked it and hunted it. I mentioned its affair to the Messenger of God ﷺ and mentioned that I had not entered ihram and that I had only hunted it for you. So he ordered his companions to eat, but he did not eat from it when I told him that I had hunted it for him."],[8595,"I went out with the Prophet ﷺ until when we were at the sanctuary, some of us were in ihram and some not. I saw people looking at something. I said: 'What are you looking at?' They were silent toward me. I looked, and there was a wild donkey. I saddled my horse and took the spear and the whip, then mounted. The whip fell from me when I mounted, so I said to them: 'Hand it to me.' They said: 'We will not help you with it in anything.' So I reached down and took it. Then I came upon it from behind a hill and stabbed it—or he said: killed it. Some of them said: 'It is not lawful to eat it,' and others said: 'It is lawful to eat it.' I went to the Messenger of God ﷺ—or he said: we went to the Messenger of God ﷺ while he was ahead of us—and he said: 'Eat it, for it is lawful.'"],[8596,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ was at Sifah al-Rawha' or near al-Rawha', there was a wild donkey wounded for the people. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'A man has hit this, and he will soon come to it.' Al-Bahzi came and said: 'O Messenger of God, I hunted this donkey, so it is yours to deal with.' The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered Abu Bakr to distribute it among the traveling parties, while we were in ihram. Then we went on until we were at Uthayat al-'Araj, and there was a gazelle bending its neck in a hollow. The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered Abu Bakr to stand by it until the people passed beyond it."],[8597,"When I set out for the al-Jar journey, Umar went out as a pilgrim for Hajj or Umrah. He said, \"Come with us so we can pass by al-Jar and look at the ships, and praise God who moves them.\" Al-Damri said: He singled me out to travel with him among seven men. Night brought us to a Bedouin's tent, and there was a pot bubbling—meaning boiling. Umar said, \"Is there any food?\" They said, \"No, except gazelle meat we obtained yesterday.\" He said, \"Then bring it near,\" and he ate while he was in ihram."],[8598,"Ka'b asked Umar b. al-Khattab about game meat that was brought to him. He said: I think he said it was wild donkey meat obtained by a man who was not in ihram, while they were in ihram. He said: So we ate from it. Umar said, \"If you had left it, I would have thought you do not understand anything.\""],[8599,"Some people in ihram asked me about people not in ihram who gifted them game, and I ordered them to eat it. Then I saw Umar and asked him, and he said, \"How did you rule for them?\" I informed him, and he said, \"If you had ruled for them otherwise, I would have caused you pain.\""],[8601,"A man asked Ibn Umar: May he eat game meat while he is in ihram? He said: Ibn Umar was informed of the statement of Umar and Abu Hurayrah, and he said: \"Umar is better than me, and Abu Hurayrah is better than me.\" Amr said: Ibn Umar used not to eat it."],[8602,"A man from the people of Syria sought his ruling about game meat he had obtained while in ihram, and he ordered him to eat it. He said: Then I met Umar and informed him of the man's question, and he said to him, \"What did you rule for him?\" I said, \"To eat it.\" He said, \"By the One in Whose hand Umar's soul is, if you had ruled for him otherwise, I would have struck you with the whip.\""],[8603,"He performed Umrah with Uthman in a caravan. When they were at al-Rawha', bird meat was presented to them. Uthman said, \"Eat,\" but he disliked eating from it. Amr b. al-As said, \"Should we eat from what you are not eating?\" He said, \"I am not in that matter like you. It was only hunted for me and killed on my account—or he said: for my sake.\""],[8604,"Uthman disliked eating quails that were hunted for them while they were in ihram. He said, \"It was only hunted for me and killed on my account.\""],[8605,"I was with Uthman between Makkah and Madinah while we were in ihram, and game was hunted for him. He ordered his companions to eat, but he did not eat. He said, \"It was hunted or killed on my account.\" He said: Ali stood up, and it was said to Uthman that he disliked eating it. So he sent for him, and Ali said: \"Forbidden to you is game of the land while you are in ihram.\" Amr said to him, \"May dust fill your mouth.\" Ali said to him, \"Rather, may dust fill your mouth.\""],[8606,"We used to take provisions of strips of wild game while we were in ihram."],[8607,"Game of the land is lawful for you while you are in ihram, except what you hunt or what is hunted for you."],[8608,"Ka'b al-Ahbar came from Syria in a group of people in ihram. When they were on part of the road, they found game meat, and Ka'b ruled for them to eat it. When they came to Umar, they mentioned that to him, and he said, \"Who ruled this for you?\" They said, \"Ka'b.\" He said, \"I have appointed him over you until you return.\" Then when they were on part of the road to Makkah, a swarm of locusts passed by, and Ka'b ordered them to take and eat. When they came to Umar, they mentioned that to him, and he said, \"What led you to rule this for them?\" He said, \"It is from the game of the sea.\" He said, \"And what tells you that?\" He said, \"O Commander of the Faithful! By the One in Whose hand my soul is, it is only the sneeze of a fish that it sneezes twice every year.\""],[8612,"Concerning a group of people who shared in (killing) game while they were in a state of ihram, he said: \"They owe a single expiation.\""],[8616,"I was with Ibn Abbas when a woman came and said: I pointed to a rabbit, and the hirer threw at it. Ibn Abbas said: \"Two just men from among you shall judge concerning it.\" He (the narrator) said: I said to the woman: Say: You judge. So she said that to him. He said: \"There must be another with me.\" I said to her: Say to him: Choose whomever you wish. So he placed his hand on me and said: \"Who is this?\" I said: Amr b. Habashi. He said: \"Give us a verdict concerning an animal that grazes on trees and drinks water into a stomach that has not yet teethed.\" He (the narrator) said: I said: \"That, in our view, is the weaned young, the female that follows (its mother), and the one in its second year.\" So he said to her: \"Choose from these if you wish.\" She said: I find more than that. He said: \"Then give whatever you wish.\""],[8617,"I asked Amir al-Sha'bi about a man who pointed to game while he was in a state of ihram, and someone killed it. He said: \"Each one of the two owes a compensation.\" He (the narrator) said: I said to him: But Hammad said that a single expiation suffices for both of them. He said: \"By Allah?\" He said: I said: Yes. He said: \"If he said that, he has gone mad.\" He said: Then I went to al-Harith al-Ukli and told him their statements, and he was the one among the people whose agreement I most loved. He said to me: The correct saying is that of Amir. Do you not see that if a group kills a man, each one of them owes an expiation? He (the narrator) said: I said: This saying is the saying of Hammad. Do you not see that they owe a single blood-money?"],[8618,"He was among a group of people who caught a hyena while they were in a state of ihram. He said: We went to Ibn Umar and asked him. He said: \"You owe one ram.\" A man among us said: A ram for each man? Ibn Umar said: \"He is indeed honored by you: one ram upon you all.\""],[8619,"I asked Marwan b. al-Hakam, while we were in Wadi al-Azraq, about things we find in the Qur'an that have no equivalent, which a muhrim kills. He said: \"Assess its value and send it to the Kaaba.\""],[8620,"Beware of game as long as you are in a state of ihram: do not pursue it and do not guide to it. If you have a need for it for your Hajj, then slaughter it before you enter ihram."],[8621,"A person ordered me to (catch) game, so I slaughtered it. He laughed and said: \"That is enough for you; you have incurred liability for it.\" I said: I bought game while I was in a state of ihram and did not slaughter it until I exited ihram. When I exited ihram, I slaughtered it. He said: \"No problem.\" So I said to Ata: I bought game while I was not in ihram, and I did not slaughter it until I entered ihram. He said: \"You incurred liability for it.\" He said: \"And if you bought it while in ihram and slaughtered it while in ihram, you also incurred liability for it.\" I said: I bought game while in ihram and kept it with me, then it died. He said: \"Then you incur liability for it.\" I said to Ata: I bought it while in ihram and gave it as a gift to people who were not in ihram, and they slaughtered it in my sanctuary (i.e., the Haram). He said: \"You incur liability for it.\" He said: I said: They did not slaughter it until I exited ihram. He said: \"Its liability is upon you.\""],[8622,"And I asked al-Thawri about a muhrim who slaughters game: is it permissible for someone else to eat it? He said: Layth informed me from Ata that he said: \"It is not permissible for anyone to eat it.\" Al-Thawri said: And Ash'ath informed me from al-Hakam b. Utayba that he said: \"There is no harm in eating it.\" Al-Thawri said: And the saying of al-Hakam is more beloved to me."],[8624,"If a person performs part of his seven [circuits] in the Hijr, then he must circumambulate the House from beyond the Hijr for what he did in the Hijr, if he erred."],[8626,"If you lie in wait for game after you have saved it from the talons of the hawk or the dog, and then it dies, do not eat it."],[8628,"I shot game and hit it fatally, but I found it still alive, and the time for slaughtering it had passed. He said: 'Do not eat it.'"],[8629,"Do not shoot game while you are outside the Sacred Precincts and it is inside the Sacred Precincts. If you do, you must pay compensation. And do not eat game that you shot and hit, but it entered the Sacred Precincts before you caught it."],[8630,"If you shoot game outside the Sacred Precincts and it enters the Sacred Precincts and dies there, do not eat it, but no compensation is due from you for it."],[8631,"If you shoot game outside the Sacred Precincts and hit it, then you wait until it enters the Sacred Precincts and dies there, do not eat it, and nothing is due from you. He said: 'They say regarding a dog sent out in the outside area that goes beyond and catches game in the Sacred Precincts, that nothing is due from the sender.'"],[8632,"He disliked a man sending his dogs while he is inside the Sacred Precincts after game that is outside the Sacred Precincts. If he does so and they kill it, he must pay full compensation for it. 'Ata' said: 'If you send your dogs in the outside area and they kill in the Sacred Precincts, no compensation is due from you, but do not eat it.' I said to him: 'What if I caught it outside the Sacred Precincts, then I entered the Sacred Precincts and found it alive?' He said: 'Leave it; it is not yours.' He said: 'What if I killed it in the Sacred Precincts?' He said: 'It is not yours; do not eat it either.'"],[8633,"If you hit game—meaning if you shoot it outside the Sacred Precincts and it dies inside the Sacred Precincts—then make expiation. And if you hit it inside the Sacred Precincts and it enters the outside area and dies, then make expiation."],[8635,"Everything that is not eaten, if you kill it while you are in ihram, no compensation is due from you for it. It is forbidden to kill it, unless it is an enemy or harms you."],[8636,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded the killing of five noxious creatures in the Sacred Precincts and outside them: the kite, the crow, the mouse, and the rabid dog."],[8638,"Five kinds of animals are to be killed in the outside area and the Sacred Precincts: the crow, the scorpion, the mouse, the kite, and the rabid dog."],[8640,"I saw Ibn al-Zubayr circumambulate the House and quicken his walking."],[8641,"He asked Abu Hurayrah about the vicious dog. He said, \"It is the lion.\""],[8642,"He asked Abu Hurayrah about the vicious dog. He said, \"It is the lion.\""],[8643,"Umar b. al-Khattab commanded us, \"to kill the snake, the scorpion, and the hornet—which resembles the bee—and it is the dabar, and the mouse—Sufyan was uncertain—while we were in ihram.\""],[8644,"Umar b. al-Khattab commanded us to kill the snake, the scorpion, and the hornet—which resembles the bee—and it is the dabar, and the mouse—Sufyan was uncertain—while we were in ihram."],[8645,"Umar was asked about killing the snake. He said, \"It is an enemy, so kill it wherever you find it.\""],[8646,"I saw Ibn al-Zubayr circumambulate the House and quicken his walking."],[8647,"Five the muhrim may kill: the scorpion, the snake, the crow, the dog, and the wolf."],[8648,"Five the muhrim may kill: the scorpion, the snake, the crow, the dog, and the wolf."],[8650,"The muhrim may kill the wolf if it confronts him, and he may kill among wild beasts whatever confronts him."],[8651,"The wolf may be killed in the Haram."],[8652,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ in a cave when it was revealed to him: “By those sent forth in succession” (Q 77:1). I took it from his mouth, and his mouth was moist with it. I do not know whether it ended with: “Then in what statement after it will they believe?” (Q 77:50) or “And when it is said to them, ‘Bow,’ they do not bow” (Q 77:48). He said: A snake escaped into a hole, so he said: “You have been protected from its evil, and it has been protected from your evil.”"],[8653,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded killing geckos, and he called them little transgressors (fuwaysiq)."],[8654,"Whoever kills a gecko, God raises him by nine degrees and erases from him nine sins.” Al-Qasim said: ‘A’ishah said: “Whoever kills a gecko, then turns and prays two rak‘ahs, it will be for him the equivalent of a slave.”"],[8655,"The frog used to extinguish the fire from Abraham, while the gecko used to blow on it. So killing this one was forbidden, while killing that one was commanded."],[8656,"Protect the frog, for the sound you hear from it is glorification, sanctification, and magnification. The beasts asked their Lord for permission to extinguish the fire from Abraham, so He gave permission to the frogs, and they piled onto it. Then God gave them water in place of the heat of the fire."],[8657,"Whoever kills a gecko, God will expiate seven sins from him."],[8658,"He commanded her to kill geckos."],[8659,"Whoever kills a gecko, he has charity for it."],[8660,"Whoever kills a gecko, he will have a qirat of reward."],[8661,"Kill geckos, for it is a devil."],[8662,"The mouse is a metamorphosed creature, by the sign that the milk of milch camels is brought near it and it does not taste it, while the milk of sheep is brought near it and it drinks it.” Hammam said to him: “Is this something you heard from the Messenger of God ﷺ?” Abu Hurayrah said: “Has the Torah been revealed to me?”"],[8663,"ʿĀʾishah had a spear with which she used to kill geckos."],[8665,"He used to perform tawaf around the House seven rounds, then enter the House and pray the two rak'ahs of tawaf inside it."],[8666,"Saʿīd b. al-Musayyib was asked about a man who killed a tick or a louse while he was in iḥrām. He said: “He should give in charity a date or two dates.”"],[8667,"I was a butcher. Ibn ʿAbbās said – and I had entered iḥrām –: “Get up and remove the ticks from this camel.” I said: “I am in iḥrām.” When we reached al-Suqyā, he said: “Get up and slaughter this she-camel.” So I slaughtered it. He said – Wahb in his ḥadīth – “May you have no mother! – and Hishām said: May the other have no mother – how many – woe to you – do you think you killed of ticks and camel-ticks?”"],[8668,"Tick-removal was mentioned in the presence of Ibn ʿAbbās, and I disliked it. When we were on part of the road, he ordered me, so I slaughtered a she-camel. Then he said: “May you have no mother! How many do you think are in it of ticks, camel-ticks, and ḥamnānah?”"],[8669,"I was a butcher. Ibn ʿAbbās said – and I had entered iḥrām –: “Get up and remove the ticks from this camel.” I said: “I am in iḥrām.” When we reached al-Suqyā, he said: “Get up and slaughter this she-camel.” So I slaughtered it. Then he said: “May you have no mother! How many do you think you killed in it of ticks and camel-ticks?” ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: “”"],[8671,"The muḥrim may remove ticks from his camel, and he rubs it with tar."],[8672,"I saw ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb removing ticks from his camel at al-Suqyā while he was in iḥrām, in mud."],[8674,"A prophet among the prophets alighted under a tree, and his baggage was beneath it. An ant bit him, so he ordered his baggage to be lifted, then he ordered the tree, and it was burned. Then Allah, Exalted is He, revealed to him: “Why not just one ant?” – meaning the one that bit him."],[8676,"Whoever kills a sparrow or anything smaller without right, it will cry out to Allah\" or he said: \"it will raise a complaint to Allah on the Day of Resurrection,\" saying: \"O Lord, so-and-so killed me without benefit.\""],[8677,"No person kills a sparrow without its right except that Allah will question him about it. They said: 'And what is its right?' He said: \"That he slaughters it and eats it, and not cut off its head and throw it away.\""],[8678,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade killing four creatures: the ant, the bee, the hoopoe, and the shrike."],[8679,"If an ant harms you, then kill it."],[8684,"Do not kill the frog, for its croaking that you hear is glorification and sanctification."],[8685,"He used to forbid the pilgrim in ihram from killing the vulture or lice in the Haram."],[8686,"A man called Arbad caught a lizard and came to Umar. Umar said to him: \"Pass judgment on it.\" So he passed judgment, and Umar confirmed his ruling."],[8687,"He witnessed Ibn Umar and Ibn Safwan when a man who had caught game came to them and said: 'Judge for me.' Ibn Umar said to Ibn Safwan: \"Either you speak and I will confirm you, or I speak and you confirm me.\" Ibn Safwan said: 'Speak, and I will confirm you.' Ibn Umar said: \"For it is such-and-such,\" and Ibn Safwan confirmed him."],[8688,"I asked Ata about waterless desert fish that are not from sea game. He said: \"No,\" and he recited to me: \"This is fresh and sweet, and this is salty and bitter.\""],[8689,"As for that which lives in both sea and land, if a pilgrim catches it, he owes compensation."],[8691,"He used to dislike killing beasts of burden and killing monks."],[8692,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade al-mujaththamah — meaning from eating it."],[8693,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade taking a living creature as a target."],[8694,"He passed by people who had set up a chicken to shoot at and said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed whoever mutilates beasts.'"],[8695,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade that a beast be detained and made a target, and he forbade eating it when it is taken as a target and played with."],[8696,"I heard Tawus, and a man asked him, saying: 'I scratched myself while in ihram and killed some ants?' He said: 'Give in charity a few handfuls.'"],[8697,"A man killed a Umm Hubayn, and Uthman ruled against him that he owed a lamb."],[8698,"Regarding a monkey killed in the Haram? He said: 'Two just men among you shall judge it.'"],[8699,"That the Prophet ﷺ said to Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib: \"O Banu 'Abd Manaf, if you have any authority, let me not find any of you preventing anyone from performing tawaf around the House or praying near it at any hour of the night or day.\" He (the narrator) said: \"Then 'Abd al-Malik came as a pilgrim and prohibited tawaf after dawn for a day or two, then later permitted it at that time. We were told that this hadith reached him.\""],[8700,"He saw Mujahid while at Arafah: an ant stung him on his chest, and he crushed it until he severed its head on his chest."],[8701,"I asked Salim b. Abdullah about bugs while I was in ihram, and he said: 'Kill them, for they are enemies.'"],[8703,"That he used to perform tawaf after 'Asr and Fajr, and pray at that time for his seven circuits."],[8704,"That he castrated a mule of his."],[8705,"Were it not for licking the lips, I would not care to rinse my mouth."],[8706,"He used to dislike castration, and say: 'In it is the increase of creation.'"],[8707,"Umar forbade the castration of sheep. He said: 'Is increase anything but in the males?'"],[8708,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas: 'No horse is to be castrated.'"],[8709,"He castrates horses, then he mounts them in the cause of Allah."],[8710,"Regarding His saying: 'They will surely change the creation of Allah', he said: 'Part of changing the creation of Allah is castration.'"],[8711,"Mujahid ordered me to ask Ikrimah about His saying: 'They will surely change the creation of Allah.' He said: 'It is castration.' He said: So I informed Mujahid, and he said: 'He erred; \"They will surely change the creation of Allah\" means the religion of Allah.'"],[8712,"Castration is mutilation."],[8713,"Castration is mutilation."],[8714,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a camel that had been branded on its face, and said: \"Who branded this?\" They said: Al-Abbas. He said: \"You brand on the face, while you are the uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" He said: By Allah, I will only brand it on the furthest part from the face. So he used to brand on the two hips."],[8715,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the sea, and he said: \"It is the lawful; its dead are lawful, its water is purifying.\""],[8717,"I entered upon the Prophet ﷺ at the animal pen while he was branding sheep."],[8718,"Do not eat floating fish."],[8719,"He was asked about a man who shoots game and finds his arrow in it the next day. He said: If I knew that your arrow killed it, I would command you to eat it, but I do not know; perhaps cold or something else killed it."],[8720,"A man came to him and said: I shoot game, and I kill it outright, and I track it after wounding. He said: \"What you kill outright, eat; but what you track after wounding, do not eat.\""],[8721,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah! I shot game and it disappeared from me for a night. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"The vermin of the night are many.\""],[8722,"If you find an arrow in game and it has died, do not eat it, for you do not know who shot it, nor whether he pronounced the name of Allah or not."],[8723,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, I shoot game and it disappears from me for a night? He said: \"If you find your arrow in it and you find no mark other than it, then eat it.\""],[8724,"I asked Abu al-Darda about game I shot that disappeared from me for a night, then I found my arrow in it and found nothing other than it. He said: \"As for me, I would eat it.\""],[8725,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I shot game and it fell, and I kept looking at it until it died. He said: \"Eat it.\" He said: \"But if it is hidden from you by the mountains or the hills, and its place of falling is hidden from you, then leave it.\""],[8726,"A man brought the Prophet ﷺ a gazelle that he had hit yesterday, and it was dead. He said: O Messenger of God, I recognized my arrow in it, and I had shot it yesterday. He said: \"If I knew that your arrow killed it, I would eat it; but I do not know—the creatures of the night are many. If I knew that your arrow killed it, I would eat it.\""],[8727,"When one of you shoots a bird while it is on a mountain and it dies, he should not eat it, for I fear that its fall killed it; or it fell into water and died, he should not eat it, for I fear that the water killed it."],[8728,"There is no harm in eating rabbit."],[8729,"If you shoot a bird and it falls into water before you slaughter it, do not eat it."],[8730,"He was brought the meat of a bird that a man shot and then slaughtered, then he left it and it flew and fell into water and died. He refused to eat from it, and said: \"It helped against itself.\""],[8731,"There is no harm in eating rabbit."],[8732,"I said to him: I shot game and hit its vital spot, then it fell down or fell into water while I was looking at it, and it died. He said: \"Do not eat it.\""],[8733,"If you strike game and a limb falls from it, then it runs away alive, do not eat that limb, and eat the rest of it that contains the head. But if it died when you struck it, then eat all of it—what fell from it and what did not."],[8734,"If you strike it and a limb falls from it, then it runs away, do not eat what fell, and eat the rest of it."],[8735,"If you shoot a bird with a stone and cut off a limb from it, and you find it alive, then that limb is carrion; slaughter what remains of it and eat it. And if you thrust your spear into game and kill it, or strike it with your sword and cut it in two, then it is that [i.e., properly killed], so eat it."],[8736,"If he cuts off the two thighs and separates them, he should not eat the two thighs, and he should eat what contains the head. But if with the two thighs there is what is less than half of the animal, he should not eat it, and he should eat what is next to the head. And if the two halves are equal, he may eat both of them. And eat whatever is more from the side of the head. This is the opinion of Abū Ḥanīfah."],[8737,"I asked al-Zuhri about game of the Sacred Precinct when it is found in the profane land. He said: \"If you find it in the profane land, hunt it and eat it.\""],[8738,"A man had a wild donkey that escaped in his house, and they could not catch it, so he struck its neck with a sword and invoked the name of Allah. He asked Ibn Mas'ud about that, and he ordered him to eat it."],[8739,"A wild donkey belonging to the family of 'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud resisted them, overpowered them, and kicked them, so they killed it. Ibn Mas'ud said: \"The swiftest slaughter,\" and he saw no harm in it."],[8740,"We came to the house of 'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud, and his servant boys had caught a wild donkey, and one of them struck it with his sword on its snout. He said: Do you think 'Abd Allah will eat from it? He said: We sat with him to see what he would do. He said: A dish of it was brought to us. He said: We mentioned to him what we had seen, and he said: \"It is only game.\""],[8742,"A man came to 'Ali and said: A camel of mine bolted, and I stabbed it with a spear. 'Ali said: \"Give me its hindquarter as a gift.\""],[8743,"Whatever beast escapes you is in the position of game."],[8744,"A man struck the neck of a camel with a sword and severed it. He asked 'Ali b. Abi Talib about it, and he said: \"A swift slaughter.\""],[8745,"He was asked about a man who had a gazelle, and he feared it would escape, so he shot it with an arrow. He said: \"He may eat it.\""],[8746,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Dhu al-Hulayfah in Tihamah, and the people captured camels and sheep, and they hastened with them and boiled them in pots. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them and ordered the pots to be overturned, so they were overturned. He equated ten sheep with a slaughtered camel. He said: A camel bolted from them, and a man shot it with an arrow and stopped it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: These camels have wild traits like those of wild game. If any of them overwhelms you, do with it like this. He said: Then Rafi' b. Khadij came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, we fear we may meet the enemy—or it is expected we will meet the enemy—tomorrow, and we have no knives, so may we slaughter with reeds? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Whatever lets the blood flow and the name of Allah is mentioned, eat it, except the tooth and the nail. I will tell you about them: as for the tooth, it is bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians.\" Rifa'ah said: Then a watering camel fell into a well in Madinah, and it was slaughtered from its flank—meaning its side—and 'Umar took from it a tenth for a dirham."],[8747,"There is no harm in eating rabbit."],[8748,"He disliked eating the slaughter of vain play. He said: \"If you stab it or slaughter it with a sword in vain play, do not eat it.\""],[8749,"There is no harm in eating rabbit."],[8750,"If a stallion attacks a man and kills him, he said: They say he is liable for it. 'Ata' said: 'And its meat is not to be eaten.'"],[8751,"There is no proper slaughter except in the throat (nahr) or the upper chest (dhabh)."],[8753,"Wherever your weapon falls on game, eat it. But as for domesticated animals, no, unless it is slaughtered by cutting the throat or the upper chest."],[8754,"If a camel falls into a well, then stab it in its flank, invoke the name of Allah, and eat."],[8755,"That a sheep fell into a well, and Masruq said: 'Slaughter it from its flank.'"],[8756,"Slaughter it from wherever you are able to do so."],[8757,"Regarding a Muslim who borrows a dog from a Magian and sends it after game? He said: 'His dog is like his knife.' He says: 'There is no harm in it.' Qatada said: 'Al-Hasan disliked it.'"],[8759,"If you send a Magian's dog, and it has been trained, and it kills, then eat."],[8761,"Do not eat the game caught by a Magian's dog, nor what his arrow hits.' 'Ata' said something similar, and there is no harm in his bread."],[8762,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prohibited eating every beast of prey with a fang and eating every bird with talons."],[8763,"Among birds, whatever eats carrion was disliked."],[8764,"I asked Qatada about eating mule meat? He said: \"It is nothing but the semen of a donkey.\""],[8766,"When you send your trained dog and your hawk, then eat, even if they kill."],[8767,"I asked Ata about the dog? He said: \"It has reached us that eating it is forbidden.\""],[8768,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, my land is a land of game. He said: \"When you send your trained dog and pronounce the name of Allah, then eat of what your dog holds back for you, even if it kills. But if it eats, then do not eat from it, for it has only held it back for itself. And when you send your dog and other dogs that have not had the name of Allah pronounced over them mix with it, then do not eat, for you do not know which of them killed it.\" He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I shoot at game and it disappears from me for a night. He said: \"If you find your arrow in it and find nothing else in it, then eat it.\""],[8769,"I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, write for me the land of such-and-such, which had not yet been conquered at that time. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Will you not listen to what this one says?\" Abu Thalabah said: By the One in whose hand is my soul, it will surely be conquered, O Messenger of Allah! He said: \"So he wrote it for him.\" He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, our land is a land of game, so I send my trained dog and my dog that is not trained. He said: \"When you send your trained dog and pronounce the name of Allah, then eat of what your dog holds back for you, even if it kills. And when you send your dog that is not trained, and you catch up with it while it is still alive and slaughter it, then eat. And eat of what your arrow brings back to you, even if it kills.\" He said: I said: \"And pronounce the name of Allah.\" He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, our land is the land of the People of the Book, and they eat pork and drink wine, so how should we deal with their vessels and cooking pots? He said: \"If you find no others, then wash them with water and cook in them and drink.\" He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is lawful for us from what is forbidden to us? He said: \"Do not eat the flesh of domestic donkeys, nor any beast of prey with fangs.\""],[8770,"Al-Hasan was asked about a man who finds game with his dog, but finds nothing with which to slaughter it, so he leaves it in his hand and it kills it. He said: \"There is no harm in eating it.\""],[8771,"If your dog takes game and you pull it away from it while it is still alive, and it dies in your hand before you slaughter it, then do not eat it."],[8772,"I heard a man asking Qatadah about a man who was training a falcon of his, and while it was circling around him, it saw a bird and swooped down on it, and the man pronounced the name of Allah. He said: \"Do not eat it unless you catch up with it while it is still alive and slaughter it, because he did not send it himself.\""],[8773,"They used to dislike sending a hunting dog after carrion."],[8774,"The game caught by a solid black dog is disliked, because the Messenger of God ﷺ commanded killing it."],[8775,"About a man who shot an arrow and killed [game] but forgot to mention God's name, he said: \"He may eat it.\""],[8776,"The fox is a predatory animal and is not eaten."],[8780,"When a trained dog eats, do not eat; but as for the falcon and the hawk, if they eat, it may be eaten."],[8783,"ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ said about the locust: \"It is only the scattering of a fish.\""],[8784,"About a trained dog that eats from what it catches, he said: \"Eat, even if it has eaten two-thirds of it.\""],[8785,"He hunts with birds such as hawks and others; if you reach it while it is still alive and can slaughter it, then eat, otherwise do not eat it. As for the trained dog, eat from what it has held for you, even if it has eaten from it."],[8786,"Ibn Umar was asked about cheese made by the Magians? He said: \"What I find in the Muslim market I buy, and I do not ask about it.\""],[8787,"Ibn Abbas did not see any harm in cheese made by the Jews and Christians."],[8788,"Any wild animal that you kill with a stone, a pellet, a piece of wood, or ... then eat it. And if you shoot and forget to say the name of Allah, then say it and eat."],[8789,"\"Any wild animal that you kill with a stone or a pellet, then eat. If you refuse to eat it, then bring it to me.\" The man said: I hurried and forgot to mention the name of Allah. He said: \"Mention it and eat.\""],[8790,"If you shoot with a stone or a pellet, and then mention the name of Allah, then eat."],[8792,"I shot game with a stone, and Ibn Umar took it. He said: O Abu Nafi'. Then he said: \"O my son, bring me something with which to slaughter it.\" He said: \"I hurried and brought him the adze, and he began to slaughter it with the blade of the adze. It died in his hand, so he threw it away.\""],[8793,"They disliked the game of the pellet bow, unless its slaughtering is caught."],[8794,"If you shoot game with a pellet, and you catch it while it can still be slaughtered, then eat it; otherwise do not eat it."],[8796,"If you shoot game and it dies in your hand, then eat it. But if you take it and intend to keep it alive, and it dies in your hand, then do not eat it."],[8797,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the game of the broad-headed arrow? He said: \"If it pierces, then eat.\""],[8798,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the game of the broad-headed arrow? He said: \"Do not eat from it except what you have properly slaughtered.\""],[8799,"Regarding game taken with a broad-headed arrow: “If it pierces, there is no harm in it. And if you shoot with an arrow that has no iron head and it falls, then eat it.”"],[8800,"The people of Madinah went out to a gathering of theirs, and there I saw a bald man, left-handed yet using his right, who towered above the people by a forearm’s length, wearing a coarse lower garment and a coarse cotton cloak, wrapped in it, saying: “O people, perform hijrah and do not merely claim it. None of you should strike a hare with a stick or a stone and then eat it. Let the asal—the spears—and the arrows make it lawful for you.” I said, “Who is this?” They said, “Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ.”"],[8801,"“O people, perform hijrah and do not merely claim it. Let each of you be wary of a hare that he strikes with a stick or throws a stone at. Rather, let the asal—the spears—and the arrows make it lawful for you.”"],[8802,"Bilal threw a stick at a hare, breaking its legs, then slaughtered it and ate it."],[8803,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyab about game taken with a pellet bow and a broad-headed arrow. He said, “Salman was asked about it and said: ‘If you will not eat it, then bring it to me and I will eat it.’”"],[8804,"Regarding the broad-headed arrow: “If it falls [struck by the broad head], then eat it; otherwise it is carrion. And if you shoot with an arrow that has no iron head, it is carrion. And if you shoot with an arrow that has an iron head, then likewise [if it does not pierce, it is carrion].”"],[8805,"“The Muslim is a name from among the names of Allah. So if one of you forgets to mention the name [of Allah] over the slaughtered animal, let him mention it and eat.”"],[8806,"“With the Muslim is the remembrance of Allah. If he slaughters and forgets to mention the name, let him mention it and eat. But if a Magian were to mention the name of Allah over his slaughtered animal, it would not be eaten.”"],[8808,"He was asked about a man who slaughters and forgets to mention the name. He said: “No harm. Mention the name over it and eat it.”"],[8809,"Some people had embraced Islam during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and came to Madinah with meat that they were selling. The companions of the Prophet ﷺ felt aversion toward it and said, “Perhaps the name of Allah was not mentioned over it.” So they asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: “Then you mention the name and eat.”"],[8810,"“The slaughtered animal that poets slaughter for boasting is not to be eaten, nor is the slaughtered animal of gambling.”"],[8811,"Humaid b. 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf had a tame sheep among his flock, and it urinated on his bed, so he rose to it in anger and slaughtered it while angry, and he did not pronounce the name. He said: I came to Abu Hurairah and mentioned that to him, and he said: \"There is no harm; let him pronounce the name over it when he eats.\""],[8812,"I asked 'Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Laila about the slaughter of a Muslim who forgets to mention the name of Allah. He said: \"It is eaten. Slaughtering is only upon the religion. Do you not see that if a Magian mentioned the name of Allah over his slaughter, it would not be eaten?\""],[8813,"He indeed distinguished that by the Book."],[8815,"In the Muslim is the name of Allah, so if he slaughters and forgets the name of Allah, let him eat. But if a Magian slaughters and mentions the name of Allah, do not eat it."],[8816,"A slave girl of Ka'b b. Malik used to tend sheep of his, and a sheep fell ill, so she slaughtered it with a sharp stone. He asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he commanded him to eat it."],[8817,"'Abd Allah b. 'Umar informed us, from Nafi', from Sulaiman b. Yasar, that a slave girl of Ka'b's, and he mentioned something similar from the Prophet ﷺ. Hidden text: A slave girl of Ka'b b. Malik used to tend sheep of his, and a sheep fell ill, so she slaughtered it with a sharp stone. He asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he commanded him to eat it."],[8818,"He was asked about Jerusalem, and he reported its virtues. A man from the people of Syria said: O Abu Abbas, you mention Jerusalem often, but you do not mention this House often. Ka'b said to him: 'By the One in whose hand is the soul of Ka'b, Allah has not created on the face of the earth a house more virtuous than this House. It has a tongue and two lips, and they speak. It has a heart with which it understands.' A man called Abu Hafs said: O Abu Ishaq, you keep telling us extraordinary tales—do stones speak? Ka'b said: 'By the One in whose hand is my soul, the Ka'bah complained to its Lord and said: O Lord, my visitors have become few, and my pilgrims have become few. Allah, exalted is He, revealed to it: I shall send down upon you a new Torah and devoted servants who keep night vigil, bowing and prostrating, yearning for you as a dove yearns for its egg, and flocking to you like eagles. Whoever performs tawaf around you seven times will have the reward of freeing a slave. And no one shaves his head near this House except that for every hair he has a light on the Day of Resurrection.'"],[8819,"Whoever slaughters, whether young or old, male or female, then eat."],[8820,"I heard al-Shaʿbī dislike residing in Makkah. Zakariyyā said: So I asked Jābir why… Did ʿĀmir dislike residing in Makkah? He said: Because of the Prophet’s ﷺ letter to Khuzāʿah: “Whoever of you stays among his family is a migrant, unless he settles, except for Hajj or ʿumrah.”"],[8821,"There is no harm in the slaughter of a boy or a woman among the Muslims, or the People of the Book."],[8822,"My father was asked about the slaughter of a boy? He said: \"If he holds the knife.\""],[8823,"He considered there to be no harm in the slaughter of a young boy if he understood the slaughter and pronounced the name of Allah."],[8824,"I asked Ibn Umar, saying: We travel to the lands, and the Bedouin and the young boy meet us and feed us meat. We do not know what it is. He said: Eat whatever the Muslim feeds you."],[8825,"Umar b. al-Khattab came to the butchers and said: Who slaughters for you? They said: This non-Arab (ilj). Umar asked him... but he did not do it properly, so Umar gave him several lashes, then said: None should slaughter for you except one who understands the prayer."],[8826,"A group of people were in the market, and their Islam was recent; they had no understanding of fiqh and were not proficient in slaughtering. He said: Umar b. al-Khattab expelled them from the market and ordered their expulsion."],[8827,"A slave woman belonging to Kab b. Malik used to tend sheep for him at Sal. Death came to one of the sheep, so she took a sharp stone, broke it, and slaughtered it. The Prophet ﷺ ordered him to eat it."],[8828,"He used to dislike eating the slaughter of the Bedouins that is hamstrung at their graves."],[8829,"Ibn Abbas disliked the slaughter of the uncircumcised and said: His testimony is not valid, nor is his prayer accepted. Mamar said: I asked Hammad about it, and he said: There is no harm in his slaughter, his testimony is valid, and his prayer is accepted."],[8831,"I said to Qatada: An uncircumcised person milked a sheep or a cow. He said: There is no harm in it."],[8832,"I returned to my family and found a sheep of ours slaughtered. I said to my family: What happened to it? They said: We feared it would die. He said: In the house there was a slave boy of ours, a captive who had not yet prayed, and he slaughtered it. I went to Ibn Masud and asked him, and he said: Eat it."],[8833,"He would not eat the slaughter of the Zanj. He said: I said to Ibn Tawus: Why? He said: My father used to say: Have you ever seen any good in a Zanji?"],[8834,"I asked al-Shabi about the slaughter of the mute, and he said: He points to the sky."],[8835,"I asked Tawus and Ikrima about the slaughter of a thief, and they both disliked it and forbade me from eating it."],[8836,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a slave who stole a sheep or a cow and slaughtered it? He did not see any harm in his slaughter."],[8838,"ʿAlī used to dislike the slaughter of the Christians of Banū Taghlib, saying: \"They do not adhere to Christianity except by drinking wine.\""],[8839,"I asked al-Zuhrī about the slaughter of the Arab Christians, and he said: \"Whoever adopts a religion is of its people,\" and he did not see any harm in their slaughter."],[8840,"There is no harm in their slaughter. Have you not heard God say: \"And among them are unlettered ones who do not know the Book\"?"],[8842,"They used to prefer to set out early to Mina, and they used to like to touch the Stone when they arrived, when they circumambulated, when they completed, on the Day of Sacrifice, and on the Day of Departure."],[8843,"Indeed what God has made lawful is their slaughter, \"and your Lord is never forgetful.\""],[8844,"An official wrote to ʿUmar that there are people near us called the Samaritans who read the Torah, observe the Sabbath, and do not believe in the resurrection. What does the Commander of the Faithful think about their slaughter? ʿUmar wrote to him: \"They are a sect of the People of the Book; their slaughter is the slaughter of the People of the Book.\""],[8845,"There is no harm in the slaughter of the People of the Book, but he disliked that a Muslim hand over his sheep to a Jew to slaughter it."],[8846,"You have settled in a land where no Muslims slaughter; there are only the Nabateans - or he said: the Nabīṭ - and Persians. So when you buy meat, ask. If it is the slaughter of a Jew or a Christian, then eat it, for their food is lawful for you."],[8847,"He permitted their slaughter, but disliked their women."],[8848,"He was asked about a Jew who slaughtered a sheep and erred regarding it so that it became forbidden to him. He said: It is not lawful for a Muslim to eat it. If a man from the People of the Book offers you food, tell him to eat; if he eats, then eat, and if he does not eat, then do not eat it."],[8849,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz appointed some Muslims over some Christians so that when they slaughtered, they would pronounce the name of God, and they would not leave them to invoke other than God."],[8850,"Camels and cows: if you wish, you may slaughter them, and if you wish, you may stab them."],[8851,"Slaughter was among them, and stabbing was among them, in His saying: 'So they slaughtered her, and they almost did not do it,' and He said: 'So pray to your Lord and sacrifice.'"],[8852,"God mentioned the slaughter of the cow in the Quran. If you slaughter something that is normally stabbed, it suffices you."],[8853,"Slaughtering is cutting the jugular veins. I said, \"What if someone slaughters but does not cut its jugular veins?\" He said, \"I think he has already made it lawful to eat, so let him eat it.\""],[8854,"Ibn 'Umar used to dislike eating a slaughtered animal whose slaughter was not facing the qibla."],[8855,"I asked him about a man who slaughters facing the qibla but then turns away from the qibla. He said: There is no harm in it. He said: And Jabir said: It does not harm you whether you face the qibla or you do not face it."],[8856,"It was recommended that the slaughtered animal be faced toward the qibla."],[8857,"I asked al-Qasim about a man who slaughtered not facing the qibla: Is his slaughtered animal eaten? He said: What harm is there in that?"],[8858,"I have not omitted touching these two corners since I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ touching them. Nafiʿ said: Ibn ʿUmar used to crowd at the two corners until his nose bled, then he would come and wash it."],[8859,"Regarding a sheep whose spinal cord is severed, he said: It is disliked, but there is no harm in eating it."],[8860,"Ibn 'Umar would not eat a sheep if its spinal cord was severed."],[8861,"He was asked about a rooster slaughtered from the back of its neck? He said: \"If you wish, then eat.\""],[8862,"He was asked about a slaughtered animal that is slaughtered, and the knife passes through and cuts the entire neck? He said: \"A swift slaughter,\" he said: \"There is no harm in eating it.\""],[8864,"He was asked about a slaughtered animal that is slaughtered, and the knife passes through and cuts the entire neck? He said: \"A swift slaughter,\" he said: \"There is no harm in eating it.\""],[8865,"The chicken, when its head is severed, is a swift slaughter. Indeed, I eat it."],[8866,"He was asked about a chicken that is slaughtered, and the knife slips and cuts off the head. He said: \"If he did not do it intentionally, then let him eat it.\""],[8867,"Whoever slaughters a camel from behind it intentionally, it is not eaten; and if he slaughters a sheep from its joint intentionally, meaning the joint, intentionally, it is not eaten."],[8868,"If a slaughterer slaughters and severs the head, then eat as long as he did not intend that."],[8869,"Al-Zuhrī was asked about a man who slaughtered with his sword and cut off the head. He said: \"What a bad thing he did.\" The man said: \"Should he eat it?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[8870,"If a man were to slaughter a kid and cut off its head, there would be no harm in eating it."],[8871,"He was asked about a slaughtered animal that is slaughtered, and the knife passes through and cuts the entire neck? He said: \"A swift slaughter,\" he said: \"There is no harm in eating it.\""],[8872,"I memorized two things from the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: \"God is kind and loves kindness in everything, so when you kill, kill well, and when you slaughter, slaughter well. Let one of you sharpen his blade and give rest to his slaughtered animal.\""],[8873,"I memorized two things from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: that he said: 'Allah has prescribed excellence in everything. So when you kill, kill well; and when you slaughter, slaughter well. Let each of you sharpen his blade and give rest to his slaughtered animal.'"],[8874,"Umar b. al-Khattab saw a man dragging a sheep by its leg to slaughter it, and said to him: 'Woe to you! Drive it to death with a gentle driving.'"],[8875,"When one of you sharpens the blade, let him not sharpen it while the sheep is looking at him."],[8877,"The Prophet ﷺ saw a man lay down a sheep, placed his foot on its neck, and began sharpening his blade. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Woe to you! Do you want to cause it multiple deaths? Why didn't you sharpen your blade before laying it down?'"],[8878,"A butcher opened a gate for a sheep in order to slaughter it, but it escaped from him and came to the Prophet ﷺ. He followed it and grabbed it, dragging it by its leg. The Prophet ﷺ said to it: 'Be patient for the command of Allah.' And to the butcher: 'As for you, drive it to death with a gentle driving.'"],[8880,"The people of the Jahiliyyah used to cut off the humps and tails of animals. They asked the Prophet ﷺ about that, and he said: 'Whatever is cut from an animal while it is alive is dead meat.'"],[8882,"A wolf attacked a sheep and tore open its belly, and something fell from it to the ground. I asked Ibn Abbas, and he said: 'Look at what fell to the ground, and do not eat it.' And he ordered him to slaughter it properly and then eat it."],[8883,"You slaughter animals that are not lawful; you rush upon the slaughter animal. Umar said: 'We are more entitled to guard against that, Abu Hayyan. The slaughter is in the throat and the upper chest for whoever is able. Leave the souls until they depart.'"],[8885,"Slaughtering is cutting the jugular veins. I said, \"What if someone slaughters but does not cut its jugular veins?\" He said, \"I think he has already made it lawful to eat, so let him eat it.\""],[8886,"I asked Ibn Abbas about rabbits that I slaughtered with my fingernail. He said, \"Do not eat it, for it is the strangled one.\""],[8887,"Whatever causes the blood to flow, and the name of Allah is mentioned over it, then eat, except the tooth and the fingernail. I will tell you: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the fingernail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians."],[8888,"It may be slaughtered with anything except four: the tooth, the fingernail, the horn, and the bone."],[8889,"Everything that cuts the jugular veins and lets the blood flow, except the fingernail, the canine tooth, and the bone."],[8890,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about game that I hunt? He said, \"Let the blood flow with whatever you wish, and mention the name of Allah over it.\""],[8891,"Regarding a man who slaughters with a stick, he said, \"If it cuts, and does not merely bruise or break, then there is no harm in it.\""],[8892,"If you do not have a knife, then you slaughter a sheep with a peg, it suffices you."],[8893,"Slaughter with a stick if it cuts the jugular veins, not merely bruising."],[8894,"Safinah, the freed slave of the Prophet ﷺ, caused the blood of a camel to flow with a wooden block, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to eat it."],[8895,"A young boy of the Ansar from Banu Harithah was tending a milch camel at Uhud, and death came to it while he had no iron with which to slaughter it. So he took a wooden peg and slaughtered it with it, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to eat it."],[8896,"A young boy of the Ansar was tending a camel of his at Uhud, and he feared it would die, so he slaughtered it with a wooden peg. He asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he commanded him to eat it."],[8897,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a camel slaughtered with a wooden stick, and he said: \"If it pierced through it properly, then eat; and if it did not pierce through it, then do not eat it.\""],[8898,"Everything that cuts, slaughter with it when you are compelled to do so."],[8899,"When it severs the jugular veins, then eat."],[8900,"There is no proper slaughter except with a sharp blade."],[8901,"I asked him about a man who sets down his reaping hook, and then a bird passes by it and it cuts open its belly and kills it. He disliked eating it.\" He said: \"And I asked Salim b. 'Abd Allah about it, and he saw no harm in it.\""],[8902,"When you slaughter it and it lashes its tail or moves, that is sufficient for you."],[8903,"When it strikes with its tail or its leg, or blinks its eye, then it is properly slaughtered."],[8904,"The animal beaten to death, the one that falls, the one gored, and that which the wild beast has eaten from, he said: \"If you slaughter it while its eye blinks or one of its legs is standing, then there is no harm in it.\""],[8905,"He found a sheep of theirs dying, so he slaughtered it and it moved. He said: \"I asked Zayd b. Thabit, and he said: 'Indeed, a dead animal can move.' He said: 'And he asked Abu Hurayrah, and he said: \"Eat it if its eye blinks or a leg from its legs moves.\"\""],[8907,"When it blinks or lashes its tail, or moves, then it has become lawful."],[8908,"A person asked 'Ata': \"A sheep fell and its head was severed, and it is moving but has not died; should it be slaughtered?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"I asked him again, and he said: 'Beware of it, and keep away from it.'\""],[8909,"Regarding the fetus: \"When it has hair or wool, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.\""],[8910,"The companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ used to say: When the fetus has hair, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother."],[8911,"Regarding the fetus: When it comes out dead, and it has hair or wool, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother."],[8912,"Regarding the fetus: \"When it has hair or wool, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.\""],[8913,"Regarding the fetus: \"When its mother casts it out dead after she is slaughtered, then eat it, because she cast it out after she had been slaughtered.\""],[8914,"Its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother, whether it has hair or not, unless it is found repulsive."],[8915,"I asked Ibrahim about the fetus of a cow? He said: \"It is only a limb of its limbs.\""],[8916,"I lodged in a house in Madinah and slaughtered a she-camel there, and it cast out a dead fetus from its belly - meaning the fetus that had not yet grown hair. I asked Ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: \"Eat it.\" He said: So I returned and took it, and I had from it liver and hump as much as I wished."],[8917,"If the fetus comes out alive, then dies before you slaughter it, do not eat it."],[8918,"The slaughter of the fetus is the slaughter of its mother, whether it has hair or not."],[8920,"Regarding His saying, Exalted is He: \"The game of the sea and its food as provision for you\" - he said: \"Its game is what you catch from it, and its food is what you take as provision salted on your journey.\""],[8921,"Its food is what it casts up, and its game is what you catch."],[8922,"Fish are slaughtered alive and dead."],[8923,"I testify that Abu Bakr said: The floating fish is lawful, so whoever wants it may eat it."],[8924,"Every creature in the sea that God has slaughtered, eat it."],[8925,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the sea, and he said: \"It is the lawful; its dead are lawful, its water is purifying.\""],[8926,"Al-Mughira b. Abd Allah b. Abd was asked: Some people from Banu Mudlij asked the Prophet ﷺ, saying: \"O Messenger of God, we ride rafts of ours, and one of us carries a little water for his drinking. If we perform ablution with seawater, we find something in ourselves; and if we perform ablution with it, we thirst.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"It is the purifying; its water is purifying, its dead are lawful.\""],[8927,"Everything from the game of the sea is slaughtered."],[8928,"Do not eat floating fish."],[8929,"If you find it floating, do not eat it; for its taking is its slaughter — meaning fish in the sea."],[8930,"\"The food of the sea is all that is in it.\" Amr said: I mentioned it to Abu al-Sha'tha', and he said: \"We used to speak only of its food being salty, and we dislike the floating from it. As for what the water recedes from, then eat.\""],[8931,"Whatever you find floating, do not eat it; and whatever is on its shores, eat it."],[8932,"Fish and locusts are all slaughtered."],[8933,"The sea cast up many dead fish, so we sought a ruling from Abu Hurayrah, and he ordered eating them. But we turned away from Abu Hurayrah's ruling, so we instructed Marwan, and he sent to Zayd b. Thabit asking him. He said: \"It is lawful, so eat it.\""],[8935,"The Prophet ﷺ sent us on a military expedition and provisioned us with a bag of dates. When we set out, we spent what we had with us and exhausted the provisions, until nothing remained with us except the bag. He said: Each one of us was given a single date. I said: \"Did it benefit you?\" Jabir said: \"Indeed, we felt its loss.\" He said: We traveled until we came to the seashore. He said: Abu Ubaydah b. al-Jarrah was our commander, and we found on the shore a huge fish that Allah had cast out for us. He said: \"It is carrion.\" Abu Ubaydah said: \"It is provision Allah has provided for you.\" He said: We stayed for eighteen days eating from its meat and anointing ourselves with its fat, and we were three hundred men. He said: Abu Qatadah ordered one of the ribs of that fish to be set up, and a rider passed beneath it."],[8936,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us out, three hundred riders, with our commander Abu Ubaydah b. al-Jarrah, to watch for the caravan of Quraysh. He said: We set out and stayed on the coast. Severe hunger struck us until we ate leaves. He said: Then the sea cast out for us a creature called al-anbar (sperm whale), and we ate from it for half a month and anointed ourselves with its fat until our bodies recovered. He said: Abu Ubaydah took a rib from it and looked for the tallest camel in the army and the tallest man among them, then loaded him on the camel. Then he passed beneath the rib. He said: A man of the people had slaughtered three fat camels, then slaughtered three fat camels, then slaughtered three more. Then Abu Ubaydah forbade him. Amr said: I heard Abu Salih say: Qays b. Sa'd said to his father: I was in the army and they became hungry. He said: Slaughter. He said: I have slaughtered. He said: Then they became hungry. He said: Slaughter. He said: I have slaughtered. He said: Then they became hungry. He said: Slaughter. He said: I have slaughtered. Then they became hungry. He said: Slaughter. He said: I have been forbidden. So I think he had slaughtered the fat camels on that day."],[8937,"And we had some of it with us, so some of the people sent to him and he ate from it. Jabir said: The Prophet ﷺ had provisioned us with a bag of dates, and he would take a handful for us from it, then date by date. We would suck them and then drink water over them until night. Then what was in the bag ran out, and when it was exhausted, we felt its loss. Hidden matn: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us out, three hundred riders, with our commander Abu Ubaydah b. al-Jarrah, to watch for the caravan of Quraysh. He said: We set out and stayed on the coast. Severe hunger struck us until we ate leaves. He said: Then the sea cast out for us a creature called al-anbar, and we ate from it for half a month and anointed ourselves with its fat until our bodies recovered. He said: Abu Ubaydah took a rib from it and looked for the tallest camel in the army and the tallest man among them, then loaded him on the camel. Then he passed beneath the rib. He said: A man of the people had slaughtered three fat camels, then slaughtered three fat camels, then slaughtered three more. Then Abu Ubaydah forbade him. Jabir said: We mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: Provision Allah has brought out for you. If you have any of it with you, feed us. He said: We had some of it with us, so some of the people sent to him and he ate from it. Jabir said: The Prophet ﷺ had provisioned us with a bag of dates, and he would take a handful for us from it, then date by date. We would suck them and then drink water over them until night. Then what was in the bag ran out, and when it was exhausted, we felt its loss."],[8938,"Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Hurayrah asked him about fish that the sea cast out: are they carrion? He said: \"Yes,\" and he forbade eating them. When he entered the house, he called for the mushaf and recited: \"Lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food as provision for you and for the travelers.\" He said: Then he sent to him and said: \"The game of the sea and its food have been made lawful to you. What comes out of it, eat it; there is no harm in it, even if it is dead.\""],[8941,"If you strike a fish with your stick and kill it, or throw a stone at it and it dies, then eat it in all cases, and locusts are like that."],[8942,"Two roasted dabb lizards were brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Khalid b. al-Walid was with him. The Prophet ﷺ reached out his hand to eat it, but it was said: \"It is a dabb.\" So he withheld his hand. Khalid said: \"Is it forbidden, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"No, but it is not found in the land of my people and I find myself averse to it.\" He said: So Khalid ate while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ watched him."],[8943,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the dabb lizard? He said: \"I do not eat it and I do not forbid it.\""],[8944,"That the Prophet ﷺ was asked about the dabb lizard? He said: \"I do not eat it and I do not forbid it.\""],[8946,"Meat of a dabb lizard was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"My father—or my forefathers—did not eat it.\" Khalid b. al-Walid said: \"But my father used to eat it.\" He said: So Khalid ate from it while the Prophet ﷺ watched him."],[8947,"The sister of Maymūna sent her some dabb lizards — or a dabb lizard — and milk. He said: The Prophet ﷺ was brought some of those dabb lizards, so he spat, and said to Khālid b. al-Walīd and ...: \"Eat.\" He said: Then the Messenger of God ﷺ was brought a vessel containing milk, and he drank. I was on his right, and he said to me: \"The drink is yours, so if you wish, O Ibn ʿAbbās, to give it to Khālid, you may do so.\" He said: I said: \"I will not give the leftover of the Messenger of God ﷺ to anyone.\" He said: So I drank, then I gave it to Khālid and he drank. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever God feeds food, let him say: O God, bless it for us and feed us better than it. And whoever God gives milk to drink, let him say: O God, bless it for us and increase it for us.\" He said: \"For I know of nothing that suffices for both food and drink except milk.\""],[8948,"A man was a shepherd and complained to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb of hunger in his land. ʿUmar said to him: \"Are you not in a land of dabb lizards?\" He said: \"Yes, O Commander of the Faithful.\" ʿUmar said: \"I would not like to have red camels in exchange for dabb lizards.\""],[8949,"We, the companions of Muhammad ﷺ, would prefer that one of us be gifted a roasted dabb lizard over a chicken."],[8950,"The Prophet ﷺ was brought a dabb lizard, and he said: \"A tribe of the Children of Israel, among those upon whom God was angry, went astray. If it is in the earth, then it is this.\""],[8951,"The Prophet ﷺ was brought a dabb lizard, and he refused to eat it, saying: \"I do not know; perhaps it is from the early generations that were transformed.\""],[8953,"I asked Jābir b. ʿAbdallāh about the hyena, and he said: \"It is lawful.\" I said to him: \"From the Prophet ﷺ?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[8954,"I asked Jābir b. ʿAbdallāh about the hyena. He said: I said: \"May I eat it?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: I said: \"Is it game?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: I said: \"Did you hear that from the Prophet of God ﷺ?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[8955,"Saʿd b. Abī Waqqāṣ used to eat hyenas, and Ibn ʿUmar did not object to it."],[8957,"I heard ʿIkrima, the client of Ibn ʿAbbās, when he was asked about it, say: \"I saw it on the table of Ibn ʿAbbās.\""],[8958,"The Prophet ﷺ circumambulated the House on his camel, touching the corner with his staff. He said: Then 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf came, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'What did you do, O Abu Muhammad, regarding touching the corner?' He said: 'All of it: I touched and I left off.' He said: 'You did right.'"],[8959,"A man from the people of Syria came and asked Ibn al-Musayyib about eating hyena, and he forbade him. The man said to him: \"But your people eat it\" — or something like this. He said: \"My people do not know.\" Sufyān said: \"And this view is more beloved to me.\" I said to Sufyān: \"Then what about what has been reported from Ibn ʿUmar, ʿAlī, and others?\" He said: \"Did not the Prophet ﷺ forbid eating every beast of prey with fangs? So leaving it is more beloved to me.\""],[8960,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about eating hyena, and he said: 'Eating it is not permissible.' A shaykh who was with him said: 'If you wish, I will tell you what I heard from Abu al-Darda'. He said: I heard him say: The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade every animal with a predatory snatch, every snatched morsel — meaning what is cut off from a living animal — every animal that is tied up as a target, and the eating of every fanged beast of prey.' Sa'id said: 'You have spoken the truth.'\""],[8962,"I asked 'Ata' al-Khurasani about jerboa, Egyptian vulture, kite, crows, eagles, and vultures. He said: 'No report has reached me about them, and I do not declare them forbidden, but I find them distasteful.' 'Amr b. Dinar said: 'I see no harm in eating them as long as you do not find them distasteful.'"],[8964,"Safwan b. Fulan, or Fulan b. Safwan, hunted two rabbits and asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he commanded him to eat them."],[8966,"Who among us was present on the day of al-Qāḥa when a rabbit was brought to the Prophet ﷺ? Abū Dharr said: I was. A bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ with a rabbit and said: 'I saw it bleeding.' He said: 'Eat from it,' and he mentioned that he himself did not eat. The bedouin said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'And what is your fast?' He mentioned something, so he said: 'Where are you from the bright white days—the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth?'"],[8967,"A man came to him and asked him about the rabbit. He said: 'And what forbids it?' He said: 'They claim that it menstruates.' He said: 'Does it not become pure?' He said: 'I do not know.' He said: 'The One who knows when it menstruated knows when it becomes pure. God did not leave anything without clarifying it to you, unless He forgot it. What God has said is as God has said, and what the Messenger of God ﷺ has said is as the Messenger of God ﷺ has said. What neither God nor His Messenger has said is left to God's pardon and mercy, so leave it and do not investigate it. It is merely a pregnant animal among these pregnant animals.'"],[8968,"He used to be in the mosque, and when he wanted to leave the mosque, he would touch the corner, then leave."],[8969,"I heard a man ask Ma'mar: 'Did you hear Qatadah narrate from Ibn al-Musayyib that rabbits were served to Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas and 'Amr b. al-'As, and Sa'd ate while 'Amr did not?' Ibn al-Musayyib said: 'We eat what Sa'd ate, and we pay no attention to what 'Amr did.' Ma'mar said: 'Yes, I heard Qatadah narrate it.'"],[8970,"Bilal threw a stick at a rabbit and broke its legs, then he slaughtered it and ate it."],[8971,"I asked 'A'ishah: 'Have you seen the Messenger of God ﷺ eat rabbit?' She said: 'I did not see him eat it, but it was given to us as a gift while I was asleep, and he set aside the hindquarter for me. When I woke up, he gave it to me and I ate it.'"],[8972,"A Bedouin came with a rabbit that he had caught, or slaughtered with a flint, and said: 'O Messenger of God, I caught it and there is some blood on it; I think it menstruates.' He said: 'Eat.' They said: 'What prevents you from it?' He said: 'I am fasting.' Then the Prophet ﷺ said to the Bedouin: 'If you are inevitably fasting, then fast three days of each month, and make them the white days.'"],[8973,"A group of scholars disliked eating the kite and the crow because the Prophet ﷺ named them among the vermin of animals that are killed in the Haram."],[8975,"He used to be in the mosque, and when he wanted to leave the mosque, he would touch the corner, then leave."],[8976,"Among birds, everything that eats carrion was disliked."],[8977,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prohibited eating every beast of prey with a fang."],[8978,"On the day of Khaybar, the Messenger of God ﷺ prohibited pregnant women from being approached, the sale of spoils until they are distributed, eating every beast of prey with a fang, and the flesh of domestic donkeys."],[8979,"On the day of Khaybar, the Messenger of God ﷺ prohibited eating every clawed animal, eating every beast of prey with a fang, the flesh of domestic donkeys, pregnant women from being approached, and the sale of spoils until they are distributed."],[8980,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prohibited eating every beast of prey with a fang and eating every bird with talons."],[8981,"Aisha was asked about eating every beast of prey with a fang, and she recited: \"Say, 'I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden to one who eats it'\" up to \"poured-forth blood,\" then said, \"Indeed we see in the cooking pot the yellowness of blood.\""],[8982,"Ibn Abbas recited this verse: \"Say, 'I do not find...'\" the verse, and said, \"Whatever is other than this is lawful.\""],[8983,"Ibn Umar bought camels that ate dung, so he sent them to the protected grazing land, and they grazed until they became good, then he rode them for Hajj."],[8984,"He disliked riding the animal that eats dung or performing Hajj on it."],[8985,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the meat of jallalah camels and their milk, and he disliked performing Hajj on them."],[8986,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the meat of jallalah animals and their milk."],[8988,"Umar b. al-Khattab arrived at Makkah and was told that a mawla of Amr b. al-As called Najdah had driven jallalah camels. So he sent to them: \"Take them out of Makkah.\" He said: \"We gather firewood on them and carry loads on them.\" He said: \"Then do not perform Hajj on them, nor Umrah.\""],[8989,"A man said to Ibn Umar: \"I want to accompany you.\" He said: \"Do not accompany me on a jallalah animal.\""],[8990,"He used to confine the chicken for three days if he wanted to eat its eggs."],[8993,"The caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called out that Allah and His Messenger forbid you the meat of donkeys, for it is impure - meaning domestic donkeys."],[8994,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade on the day of Khaybar the meat of domestic donkeys."],[8995,"I mentioned to him a hadith that Abdullah b. Abi Awfa narrated to me about the meat of donkeys. Said said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade them absolutely.\""],[8996,"On the day of Khaybar we captured donkeys outside the village and slaughtered them. He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade eating them.\" Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani said: \"I met Said b. Jubayr and mentioned that to him, and he said: 'He only forbade them because they used to eat dung.'\""],[8997,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the meat of domestic donkeys and mut'ah with women on the day of Khaybar."],[8998,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade the meat of domestic donkeys, cooked or raw."],[8999,"I was kindling fire under the cooking pots—or he said: over the pots—with donkey meat, when the caller of the Messenger of God ﷺ announced: God forbids you the meat of donkeys."],[9000,"A man from his people asked the Prophet ﷺ about the meat of the domestic donkey, and mentioned something of their situation?—He said: I do not know what it was—so he permitted it for him."],[9001,"Ibn Abbas was asked about the meat of domestic donkeys, and he said: The Messenger of God ﷺ only forbade it on the day of Khaybar because they were the beasts of burden. Then he recited: \"Say: I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden,\" the verse."],[9002,"Two men from Muzaynah—or one of them—asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he mentioned that the year had left them nothing with which to feed their families except donkeys. The Prophet ﷺ said: Feed your family from the fat of your wealth, for I only deemed distasteful for you the village scavengers."],[9003,"I said to Abu al-Sha'tha': They say that the Prophet ﷺ commanded them to overturn the cooking pots containing donkey meat. He said: Al-Hakam b. Amr al-Ghifari used to say that."],[9005,"We slaughtered a horse in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ and ate it."],[9006,"Some of the companions of Abdullah slaughtered a horse and ate it, and they saw no harm in it."],[9007,"We used to eat horse meat. He said: I said: What about mules? He said: No."],[9009,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade the meat of donkeys, and fed us horse meat."],[9010,"He disliked mule meat."],[9011,"I asked Qatada about eating mule meat? He said: \"It is nothing but the semen of a donkey.\""],[9012,"I saw the companions of the mosque, the companions of Ibn al-Zubayr, eating horse and packhorse."],[9014,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about eating dog meat? He said: \"It is a food of the Age of Ignorance, and God has made [us] self-sufficient away from it.\""],[9015,"Eat whatever God, the Exalted, has created except three: carrion, blood, and the flesh of swine.\""],[9018,"He used to touch the Yemeni corner and the Black corner, and he did not touch the other two."],[9019,"He did not consider eating fox meat objectionable."],[9020,"It is not a predatory animal, and he permitted eating it."],[9021,"I asked Ata about the hyena and the fox? He said: \"Eat them both because they are harmful, and any game that is harmful is game.\""],[9022,"Mujahid was asked about eating monkey? He said: \"It is not among the grazing livestock.\""],[9023,"Regarding a monkey killed in the Haram? He said: 'Two just men among you shall judge it.'"],[9024,"I was with Ibn al-Musayyab when a man from Ghatafan came to him and asked him about eating monitor lizard? He said: \"There is no harm in it, and if you have any of it with you, then feed us.\""],[9027,"Eating the bat and the [large lizards called] sawālī was disliked."],[9028,"Locusts were mentioned to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb at al-Rabadha, and he said: I wish we had a basket or two of them."],[9029,"ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ said about the locust: \"It is only the scattering of a fish.\""],[9030,"Ibn ʿUmar was asked about eating locusts, and he said: \"It is a lawful slaughter; eat it.\""],[9032,"Indeed, God, Blessed and Exalted, created Adam, and some of his clay remained in His hand, so He created from it the locust. Thus, it is an army of the armies of God, and there is no army greater than it.\""],[9033,"God did not create anything after Adam except the locust; some of his clay remained, and from it He created the locust.\""],[9034,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the locust, and he said: An army of the armies of God, and there is no army greater than it. I do not eat it, nor do I forbid it. And he used to say: Whatever is not forbidden is lawful for us."],[9036,"I saw ʿUmar, Ṣuhayb, and Salmān eating locusts."],[9037,"Locusts are like the game of the sea."],[9039,"He asked ʿAbd Allāh b. Abī Awfā about locusts? He said: I went on seven expeditions, or six expeditions, with the Messenger of God ﷺ, eating locusts."],[9040,"The wives of the Messenger of God ﷺ used to exchange gifts of locusts on trays."],[9041,"He asked Ibn ʿAbbās about locusts? He said: “There is no harm in eating them.”"],[9042,"Salmān was asked about cheese, furs, and clarified butter? He said: God's lawful is what He made lawful in the Qur'an, and God's unlawful is what He made unlawful in the Qur'an, and whatever is other than that is something pardoned."],[9043,"The Prophet ﷺ said in his illness in which he died: Let not the people hold anything back from me; for I do not make lawful except what God made lawful in His Book, and I do not make unlawful except what God made unlawful in His Book."],[9044,"God made lawful His lawful, and made unlawful His unlawful; so what He made lawful is lawful, and what He made unlawful is unlawful, and what He was silent about is pardoned.”"],[9045,"God made lawful and made unlawful; so what He made lawful, make it lawful, and what He made unlawful, avoid it. And He left some things that He did not make unlawful nor make lawful; that is a pardon from God. Then he recites: “O you who believe, do not ask about things…” the verse."],[9047,"The elephant is a pig; its meat is not eaten and its milk is not drunk,” or he said: “Its udder is not milked and its hoof is not brought.”"],[9048,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to dislike seven parts of the sheep: the blood, the gallbladder, the testicles, the gland, the penis, the bladder, and the bile; and he used to prefer the forepart of the sheep."],[9050,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to have an aversion to the spleen."],[9051,"ʿAlī used to dislike the spleen of a sheep, the eel-like fish (al-jirrī), and every bird with talons."],[9052,"I asked him about the spleen and the eel-like fish (al-jirrī), so he recited this verse: {Say: I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden}."],[9054,"It reached him that ʿAlī used not to eat the flesh of al-jirrīth (a type of fish), nor enter a house containing an image, nor eat the spleen. He said: As for the spleen, the Messenger of God ﷺ found it repulsive and did not eat it, and said: It is merely a congealed lump of blood; so ʿAlī did not eat it. As for a house containing an image, the Prophet ﷺ would not enter a house containing an image. As for al-jirrīth, it is a fish that the People of the Book do not eat."],[9055,"I passed by ʿAlī with a [fish called] jirriyyah in a basket, its two ends protruding from the basket. He said: How much? I said: For a quarter of flour. ʿAlī said: How good this is!"],[9056,"He was asked about al-jirrīth, so he said: There is no harm in it; it is only something the Jews disliked."],[9057,"The Messenger of God ﷺ ate liver while it was dripping with fresh blood."],[9058,"She asked Umm Salamah about eating cheese, so she said: Put the knife into it, then say 'In the name of God,' then eat."],[9059,"It was said to ʿUmar: Some people make cheese and put into it rennet from dead animals. ʿUmar said: Mention God's name and eat."],[9061,"They asked him about rennet? He said: \"Milk does not die.\""],[9064,"I asked Umar about cheese? He said: \"Eat, for it is only milk or colostrum.\""],[9066,"Ibn Abbas did not see any harm in cheese made by the Jews and Christians."],[9067,"I asked Ibn Umar about cheese? His answer was: \"Nothing comes to us from Iraq more admirable to us than cheese.\""],[9068,"Ibn Umar was asked about silk? He said: \"We have heard that whoever wears it in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter.\" And I asked him about cheese? He said: \"About which aspect of it do you ask me?\" I said: \"They put in it—or we fear they put in it—the rennet of carrion.\" He said: \"Leave what causes you doubt for what does not cause you doubt.\""],[9069,"I was with Ibn Umar when a man asked him about tila'—meaning thickened grape juice—and he said: \"The Commander of the Believers used to drink it and give it as provision to our servants.\" I said: \"But they cook it while it is wine.\" He said: \"If you know it is wine, then do not drink it.\" I said: \"What about cheese?\" He said: \"It is brought from Iraq and we eat it and feed it to our servants.\" I said: \"But they put carrion in it.\" He said: \"If you know that there is carrion in it, then do not eat it.\""],[9072,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought a cheese in the expedition of Tabuk, and it was said: \"O Messenger of Allah, this is a food made by the people of Persia; I fear that there may be carrion in it.\" He said: \"Pronounce the name of Allah over it, and eat.\""],[9073,"Keep up Hajj and Umrah in succession, for performing them consecutively removes poverty and sins just as the bellows removes impurities from iron."],[9074,"Keep up Hajj and Umrah in succession."],[9075,"There is no reward for an accepted Hajj except Paradise, and two Umrahs expiate what is between them."],[9076,"And the accepted Hajj has no reward except Paradise."],[9077,"Whoever performs Hajj to this House and does not engage in obscenity or wickedness will return as on the day his mother bore him."],[9078,"Whoever sets out for this House, and nothing moves him except prayer at it and touching the Stone, his previous sins will be expiated."],[9079,"Umar b. al-Khattab went out and saw a caravan. He said, \"Who is the caravan?\" They said, \"Pilgrims.\" He said, \"Did nothing else move you?\" three times. They said, \"No.\" He said, \"If the caravan knew to whom they have made their camels kneel, their eyes would be delighted with grace after forgiveness. By the One in whose hand is Umar's soul, no camel raises its hoof or sets it down except that Allah raises him a degree, removes a sin from him, and records a good deed for him.\""],[9080,"The delegations of Allah are three: the pilgrim, the performer of Umrah, and the warriors. Allah called them and they answered Him; they asked Allah and He gave them.\""],[9081,"When the pilgrim or the performer of Umrah says the takbir — he said, I do not know whether he mentioned the warrior — the one next to him says takbir, then the one next to him, until it reaches the horizon."],[9082,"A man passed by Abu Dharr while he was at al-Rabadha and asked him, \"Where do you intend to go?\" He said, \"Hajj.\" He said, \"Did nothing else move you?\" He said, \"No.\" He said, \"Then begin your deeds anew.\" The man said: I departed until I reached Madinah and stayed as long as Allah willed. Suddenly the people were crowding around a man, so I pushed through, and there was the old man I had met at al-Rabadha, meaning Abu Dharr. When he saw me, he said, \"He is the one about whom I told you.\""],[9083,"While Umar b. al-Khattab was sitting between al-Safa and al-Marwah, a caravan arrived and made their camels kneel at the gate of the mosque. They circumambulated the House while Umar watched them. Then they went out and performed sa'i between al-Safa and al-Marwah. When they finished, he said, \"Bring them to me.\" They were brought. He said, \"From where are you?\" They said, \"From the people of Iraq.\" He said — I think they said, \"From the people of Kufa\" — \"What brought you here?\" They said, \"Pilgrimage.\" He said, \"Have you come for trade, inheritance, or seeking a debt?\" They said, \"No.\" He said, \"Have you worn out your mounts?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Have you become exhausted?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Have your feet become sore?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Then begin your deeds anew.\""],[9084,"A man asked him about the pilgrim. He said, \"The pilgrim intercedes for four hundred households of his people, and he is blessed in forty camels' mothers that carried him, and he emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him.\" He said: A man said to him, \"O Abu Musa, I used to perform Hajj regularly, but now I have become weak and old. Is there anything equal to Hajj?\" He said to him, \"Can you free seventy believing slaves from the descendants of Isma'il? As for the journey and departure, I find no equal for it — or he said, no equivalent.\""],[9085,"When you set down the saddles, then set out for Hajj and Umrah, for it is one of the two jihads."],[9086,"A man asked the Prophet ﷺ about jihad, and he said: “Shall I not guide you to a jihad that has no fighting in it? Hajj.”"],[9087,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “I am a coward and cannot face the enemy.” He said: “Shall I not guide you to a jihad in which there is no fighting?” He said: “Yes, O Messenger of God.” He said: “Upon you is Hajj and Umrah.”"],[9088,"We asked the Prophet ﷺ about jihad, and he said: “Sufficient for you is Hajj,” or “Your jihad is Hajj.”"],[9089,"The Prophet ﷺ performed Hajj with his wives on the Farewell Hajj, then said: “This is only it, then the backs of the mats,” meaning “Stay upon the backs of the mats in your homes.”"],[9090,"God boasts to His angels about the people of Arafah, saying: “Look at My servants: they have come to Me disheveled, dusty, and exposed to the sun.” And there is no day on which more are freed from the Fire than that day, and no forgiveness is given therein to an arrogant person."],[9091,"We used to say that whoever is sealed with one of three things—either he said: “Paradise is assured for him,” or he said: “He is free from the Fire”—whoever fasts the month of Ramadan and dies when the month ends, or whoever goes out for Hajj and dies when he returns from his Hajj, or whoever goes out for Umrah and dies when he returns from his Umrah."],[9092,"Hajjs in succession and Umrahs in order repel an evil death and the poverty of destitution."],[9094,"Which pilgrim is best? He said: “One who feeds food and restrains his tongue.” He said: “And al-Thawri reported to us: We heard that it is part of the righteousness of Hajj.”"],[9095,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked: “What is the righteousness of the pilgrim?” He said: “Feeding food and refraining from speech.”"],[9097,"A pilgrim never becomes destitute—meaning he never becomes poor."],[9098,"Perform Hajj and you will become self-sufficient, and go on military expeditions and you will become healthy."],[9099,"We have heard that the righteousness of Hajj is good food and good speech."],[9100,"While I was sitting with Ibn Abbas, a man came to him and said: I applied perfume while I was in ihram. Ibn Abbas said: 'Then I and Abu Bakr judge that you owe a sheep.' Then another man came and said: I have completed my rituals except the tawaf. He said: 'Perform tawaf around the House, then come back to me.' He said: So he returned to him and said: I have performed tawaf. Ibn Abbas said to him: 'Go and start the actions anew.'"],[9101,"Tawus was asked: After the obligatory, is Hajj better or charity? He said: 'Where is the halting, the departure, the staying awake at night, the toil, the tawaf around the House, the prayer near it, the standing at Arafah, the gathering [at Muzdalifah], and the stoning of the jamarat? It is as if he was saying: Hajj.'"],[9102,"A man asked him: After the obligatory, is Hajj better or charity? He said: Abu Miskin informed me, from Ibrahim, that he said: 'If one has performed Hajj multiple times, then charity.'"],[9103,"Seven circuits [of tawaf] equal [the reward of freeing] a slave."],[9104,"Whoever performs tawaf around the House and prays two rak'ahs, speaking only good, it is like [the reward of freeing] a slave."],[9105,"A servant to whom I have granted ample provision, yet he does not come to Me on pilgrimage every four years, is indeed deprived."],[9106,"If people were to abandon visiting this House for a single year, they would not be given rain."],[9107,"He was asked about Jerusalem, and he reported its virtues. A man from the people of Syria said: O Abu Abbas, you mention Jerusalem often, but you do not mention this House often. Ka'b said to him: 'By the One in whose hand is the soul of Ka'b, Allah has not created on the face of the earth a house more virtuous than this House. It has a tongue and two lips, and they speak. It has a heart with which it understands.' A man called Abu Hafs said: O Abu Ishaq, you keep telling us extraordinary tales—do stones speak? Ka'b said: 'By the One in whose hand is my soul, the Ka'bah complained to its Lord and said: O Lord, my visitors have become few, and my pilgrims have become few. Allah, exalted is He, revealed to it: I shall send down upon you a new Torah and devoted servants who keep night vigil, bowing and prostrating, yearning for you as a dove yearns for its egg, and flocking to you like eagles. Whoever performs tawaf around you seven times will have the reward of freeing a slave. And no one shaves his head near this House except that for every hair he has a light on the Day of Resurrection.'"],[9108,"A man died at Mina on the last of the days of tashriq. A man came and said: O Commander of the Faithful, our nephew has died; will you bury him? Umar said: What prevents me from burying a man who has not sinned since he was forgiven?"],[9109,"You have come asking about your prayer, your bowing, your prostration, and your fasting, saying: What do I get for it? He said: Yes, by the One who sent you with the truth. He said: Pray at the beginning of the night and at its end, and sleep in its middle. He said: If you pray in its middle, then you are [left to your own]. He said: When you stand for prayer and bow, place your hands on your knees and spread your fingers. Then raise your head until every limb returns to its joint. When you prostrate, place your forehead firmly on the ground. He said: Fast the white nights: the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth. Then he turned to the Ansari and said: Ask about your need, and if you wish I will tell you. He said: That is more pleasing to me. He said: You have come asking me about your departure from your home heading for the Sacred House, saying: What do I get for it? You have come asking about your standing at Arafah, saying: What do I get for it? And about your stoning the jamarat, saying: What do I get for it? He said: Yes, by the One who sent you with the truth. He said: As for your departure from your home heading for the Sacred House, for every step your mount takes, Allah writes for you a good deed and erases from you a bad deed. As for your standing at Arafah, Allah, blessed and exalted is He, descends to the lowest heaven and boasts about them to the angels, saying: These are My servants who have come disheveled and dusty from every deep valley, hoping for My mercy and fearing My punishment, though they have not seen Me. How would it be if they saw Me? If you had sins like the sand of Alij, or like the days of this world, or like the drops of rain, Allah would wash them away from you. As for your stoning the jamarat, it is stored for you. As for your shaving your head, for every hair that falls you have a good deed. And when you perform tawaf around the House, you come out of your sins as on the day your mother gave birth to you."],[9110,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the Day of Arafah: 'O people, Allah has bestowed His favor upon you on this day: He forgives you except for wrongs between one another, and He gives your wrongdoers to your good-doers, and He grants your good-doers what they ask. Move on in the name of Allah.' When he was at Muzdalifah, he said: 'Allah has forgiven your righteous ones, and your righteous ones have interceded for your wicked ones. Forgiveness descends and encompasses them all. Then forgiveness spreads throughout the lands and falls upon every repentant person who guarded his tongue and his hand. Iblis and his troops are on the mountains of Arafat watching what Allah does with them. When forgiveness descends, he and his troops cry out with woe and say: I used to incite them for ages, then forgiveness came and covered them. So they scatter, crying woe and destruction.'"],[9111,"There is no day on which Iblis is more driven away, more cast out, or more enraged than the Day of Arafah, because of what he sees of the descent of mercy and Allah, exalted is He, overlooking grave matters — except what he saw on the Day of Badr.” It was said, “And what did he see on the Day of Badr?” He said, “He saw Jibril marshalling the angels.”"],[9112,"That she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man who performed Hajj and did so frequently — should he spend his wealth on maintaining ties or freeing slaves? The Prophet ﷺ said, “A sevenfold circumambulation with no idle talk in it equals a slave.”"],[9113,"Whoever heads to this House seeking this world or the Hereafter, it will be given to him."],[9114,"That this House complained to Allah, exalted is He, about ruin. A man said to him, “Does it have a tongue with which it speaks?” He said, “Yes, and a heart with which it reasons.” Then he said, “I will replace you with a Torah, and I will make for you dwellers who incline toward you as a wet nurse inclines toward her young, and they will hasten to you as vultures hasten to their nests. They asked you for prescribed limits, prostrating.”"],[9115,"A man said in the presence of Ibn Umar, “How numerous the pilgrims are!” Ibn Umar said, “How few they are!” Then Ibn Umar saw a man on a camel with a worn saddle, its halter a rope, and said, “Perhaps this one.”"],[9116,"The pilgrims are few, and the riders are many."],[9117,"I was sitting with Jabir b. Abd Allah when a group from Yemen passed by, having carried water and firewood. Jabir b. Abd Allah said, “I have not seen anyone more resembling us with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than these.”"],[9118,"Allah is good and accepts only what is good. He commanded the believers with what He commanded the messengers, and said: “O messengers, eat from the good things and act righteously” (Q 23:51). Then He said: “O you who believe, eat from the good things We have provided for you” (Q 2:172). He said, then he mentioned a man who prolongs travel, disheveled and dusty, stretching his hand to the sky saying, “O Lord, O Lord,” while his food is unlawful, his clothing is unlawful, and he has been nourished by the unlawful — how could he be answered?"],[9119,"Four in four are not accepted in Hajj, nor in Umrah, nor in jihad, nor in charity: treachery, theft, misappropriation, and the property of an orphan."],[9120,"A man asked him, “O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what about a man employed over the alms who takes from them and performs Hajj with that?” Ibn Umar said, “What if someone stole the baggage of pilgrims and performed Hajj with it?” The man said, “May Allah forgive, O Abu Abd al-Rahman.” Ibn Umar said, “Verily, these alms are for the poor.”"],[9121,"The stay of the emigrant in Makkah after completing his rites is three days."],[9122,"I heard Ibn Abd al-Aziz, while he was governor of Madinah, asking his companions, “What have you heard about residing in Makkah?” Al-Sa'ib b. Yazid said to him, “Al-Ala' b. al-Hadrami said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘The stay of the emigrant after completing his rites is three days.’”"],[9123,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, when he came to Makkah and completed his rites, said: “You are not a place of staying or residence.”"],[9124,"The companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ used to perform Hajj and then return, and perform ʿumrah and not reside as neighbours."],[9128,"I heard al-Shaʿbī dislike residing in Makkah. Zakariyyā said: So I asked Jābir why… Did ʿĀmir dislike residing in Makkah? He said: Because of the Prophet’s ﷺ letter to Khuzāʿah: “Whoever of you stays among his family is a migrant, unless he settles, except for Hajj or ʿumrah.”"],[9129,"I saw Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh, Ibn ʿUmar, and Abū Hurayrah—and I think he also mentioned Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī—performing Hajj, then residing as neighbours, and performing ʿumrah and Hajj."],[9130,"Stay in this land—meaning Makkah—even if you eat bitter shrubs or tree leaves."],[9131,"Abū Dharr came as a muʿtamir, stayed with them for three days, then departed."],[9132,"“Do not reside in Makkah.” ʿUthmān was a handsome man. He said: I thought he meant that, so I said: “O Abū Muḥammad, I hope that God will protect me.” He said: “I do not mean that. But if you reside in the Ḥaram, you will soon commit in it what is committed outside it when a long time passes upon you, for error in it is greater.”"],[9133,"Seals from al-Baṣrah for the House were sent with me, and they were lost. I asked al-Qāsim b. Muḥammad: “Is there anything due in them?” He said: “No.” Then he said: “What do they do with a gift to the House? To give a dirham in charity is dearer to me than to gift to this House a hundred thousand. If this valley flowed with wealth for me, I would not gift any of it to the House.”"],[9134,"To give a dirham in charity is dearer to me than to gift to the Kaʿbah such-and-such, because of something I heard."],[9135,"I sat with Ibn Umar and greeted him, intending to ask him about the gift for the House. He said: I think he said: I made a vow upon myself that I would give a gift to it, when a man asked him about that. He said: \"What would the House do with that?\" The man said: I have already done it. He said: \"Then fulfill what you said.\" I said: And I, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: \"And you as well?\" I said: Yes. He said: \"Then fulfill it,\" and he had disapproved of that for both of them and commanded both of them to fulfill it."],[9136,"I was with Aisha when she was asked about a man who gave a gift to the House. She said: \"Let them put it among the poor, for this House is maintained from the wealth of God.\""],[9138,"She used to circumambulate the House while wearing a face veil."],[9140,"He disliked her circumambulating the House while wearing a face veil."],[9143,"Ibrahim the Prophet ﷺ was the first to set up the boundary markers of the sanctuary, and Gabriel pointed out to him the places for them."],[9145,"In the time of ignorance, the people of the sanctuary did not commit any offense within it except that punishment was hastened for them. Then there came the matter that you have seen. Soon no one will commit any offense within it except that punishment will be hastened for him, to the point that if a black slave woman sought refuge in it, no one would oppose her."],[9146,"A slave woman in the time of ignorance sought refuge at the House, and her mistress came and pulled her, and her hand withered. He said: Islam came while her hand was still withered."],[9147,"A woman's forearm was exposed while she was circumambulating the House in the time of ignorance, and a man placed his hand on her forearm, and his hand stuck to her hand. So he went to a man who said: Go to the place where you did that and make a covenant with the Lord of this House that you will not return to it. He said: So he did that, and it was released."],[9148,"The Prophet ﷺ stood at al-Hazwara and said: \"I know that you are the best land of Allah and the most beloved land to Allah. Were it not that your people expelled me, I would not have left.\""],[9149,"I know that you are the best of Allah's lands."],[9150,"I saw Abd Allah b. Amr b. al-As at Arafah, and his dwelling was in the hill and his prayer-place was in the Haram. It was said to him: Why do you do this? He said: \"Because the deed in it is more excellent, and the error is greater in it.\""],[9151,"That I commit seventy errors at Rukbah is dearer to me than that I commit a single error at Makkah."],[9152,"Umar b. al-Khattab warned Quraysh, and there were in it three Arab tribes, and they perished: That I commit twelve errors at Rukbah is dearer to me than that I commit a single error at its corner."],[9153,"The House is equivalent to the Throne of Allah, and were the Frequented House to fall, it would fall upon it. It is the center of the earth, and from it the earth was spread out."],[9154,"The Frequented House which is in heaven is called al-Durah, and it is above the Sacred House. Were it to fall, it would fall upon it. Every day seventy thousand angels frequent it who have never seen it before. And in the seventh heaven there is a sanctuary equal to its sanctuary."],[9155,"He asked Ali about the Frequented House, what is it? Ali said: \"That is al-Durah in the seven heavens at the Throne. Every day seventy thousand angels enter it, and they do not return to it until the Day of Resurrection.\""],[9156,"The Station was beside the House, and they feared floods would overpower it, and they used to circumambulate behind it. Umar said to al-Muttalib b. Abi Wada'ah al-Sahmi: 'Do you know where its original place was?' He said: 'Yes, I estimated the distance between it and the Black Stone, between it and the door, between it and Zamzam, and between it and the corner near the Stone.' He said: 'So where is its measure?' He said: 'With me.' He said: 'Bring its measure.' He brought its measure, and he placed it in its current place."],[9157,"Wiping the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner sheds sins absolutely."],[9158,"By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Ibn Abbas, no Muslim servant aligns himself with the Corner asking Allah for good but He gives it to him."],[9159,"Touching the Corner erases sins completely."],[9160,"Whoever touches the Corner (the Black Stone), then supplicates, his supplication is answered. He said: It was said to Ibn Abbas: Even if he is quick? He said: \"Even if he is quicker than flashing lightning.\""],[9161,"No one places his hand on the Yemeni Corner, then supplicates, except that his supplication is almost answered. So come, let us place our hands, then supplicate."],[9162,"The Corner and the Station (Maqam Ibrahim) will come on the Day of Resurrection greater than Abu Qubays, each of them having two eyes, two tongues, and two lips, testifying for those who fulfilled their covenant at them."],[9163,"He was sitting between Zamzam, the Corner, and the Station while people were crowding at the Corner. He said to his companions: \"Do you know what this is?\" They said: Yes, it is the Stone. He said: \"I know, but it is among the stones of Paradise that God created with His hand. It will surely be gathered on the Day of Resurrection with two eyes, two lips, and a tongue, testifying for whoever touched it with truth.\""],[9164,"They used to prefer to set out early to Mina, and they used to like to touch the Stone when they arrived, when they circumambulated, when they completed, on the Day of Sacrifice, and on the Day of Departure."],[9165,"He liked to touch the Stone when he began and when he finished. If he was unable to do so, he would say takbir and send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ and continue."],[9166,"I saw Sa'id b. Jubayr circumambulating the House. When he came parallel to the Corner and did not touch it, he faced it and said takbir."],[9167,"I saw Ibn Umar, when he wanted to pray behind the Station, put between himself and the Station a row or two rows or one man or two men."],[9169,"If you are able to touch the Corner, then do so; otherwise, face it, proclaim tahlil, and say takbir. He used to like to begin with the Stone and end with it in the circumambulation in which he walks briskly (raml), the circumambulation in which he exits ihram, and the circumambulation in which he departs. He used to like to crowd at the Stone in these three: when he touched it, began with it, and ended with it."],[9170,"The Station and the Stone will come on the Day of Resurrection like Abu Qubays, each of them having two eyes and two lips, calling out with their loudest voices, testifying for those who fulfilled their covenant at them."],[9172,"When Allah took the covenant from the Children of Israel or Adam, He placed it in the Corner. So fulfilling Allah's covenant is touching the Stone."],[9173,"Has any saying reached you that is recommended when touching the Corner? He said: \"It is as if he instructs to say takbir.\""],[9175,"I circumambulated behind Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, and I did not hear any of them speak during circumambulation."],[9176,"When he touched the Corner, he would glorify Allah and say takbir, then say: \"O Allah, I seek refuge in You from disbelief, poverty, and the stations of humiliation.\""],[9177,"La ilaha illa Allah, Allahu Akbar. O Allah, in affirmation of Your Book and the Sunnah of Your Prophet ﷺ."],[9178,"When he touched it, he would say: \"O Allah, in faith in You and affirmation of Your Book and the Sunnah of Your Prophet ﷺ.\""],[9179,"When touching the Stone, he would say: \"O Allah, in fulfillment of Your covenant, affirmation of Your Book, and following the Sunnah of Your Prophet ﷺ.\""],[9180,"The Prophet ﷺ said to Abd al-Rahman b. Awf: \"How did you do, O Abu Muhammad, regarding touching the Stone?\" He said: \"Sometimes I touched it and sometimes I left it.\" He said: \"You have done right.\""],[9182,"I have not left touching the two Corners in ease or hardship since I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ touching them."],[9183,"I have not left touching the two Corners in ease or hardship since I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ touching them. And Ibn Umar would crowd around the Stone until his nose bled, then he would come and wash it."],[9184,"I have not omitted touching these two corners since I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ touching them. Nafiʿ said: Ibn ʿUmar used to crowd at the two corners until his nose bled, then he would come and wash it."],[9185,"Ibn ʿUmar would not fail to touch the two Yemeni corners in every circumambulation."],[9186,"I and Salim used to crowd for ʿAbd Allah b. ʿUmar at the two corners."],[9187,"I asked al-Qasim b. Muhammad about crowding at the corner, and he said: Crowd, O nephew, for I saw ʿAbd Allah b. ʿUmar crowd until his nose bled."],[9188,"If you find crowding at the corner, do not harm anyone and do not be harmed, and pass on."],[9189,"I would wish that the one who crowds at the corner, meaning the Stone, would turn away with neither gain nor loss."],[9190,"ʿUmar used to crowd at the corner, and the Prophet ﷺ said: O Abu Hafs! You are a strong man, and you harm the weak. If you find an empty space, touch the corner; otherwise, say tahlil and takbir and pass on."],[9191,"When he found crowding at the corner, he would say takbir, raise his hand, and pass on without touching it."],[9192,"He saw Ibn ʿAbbas come on the Day of Tarwiyah with his hair matted. He said: I saw him kiss the corner, then prostrate on it, then kiss it, then prostrate on it, then kiss it, then prostrate on it."],[9193,"ʿUmar kissed the corner, meaning the Stone, then prostrated on it."],[9194,"The Black Corner does not perish from Paradise: meaning, were it not from Paradise, it would have perished."],[9195,"Were it not that those with impurities from the Age of Ignorance touched it, no afflicted person would touch it but be healed, and there is nothing on earth from Paradise except it."],[9196,"that: \"The Corner was, before the fire, the color of the Maqām (Station).\""],[9199,"They say: Indeed it is from the stones of Paradise, but it is only a stone from one of these valleys. I think he said: Allah wanted to make it a sign."],[9200,"The Corner — meaning the Stone — is the right hand of Allah on earth, with which He shakes hands with His creation as a man shakes hands with his brother. It testifies for the one who touches it with righteousness and fidelity. By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Ibn ʿAbbās, no Muslim servant aligns himself with it asking Allah, exalted is He, for good except that He grants it to him."],[9201,"I circumambulated behind Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, and I did not hear any of them speak during circumambulation."],[9202,"The Corner is the right hand of Allah, with which He shakes hands with His servants."],[9203,"The Corner and the Station are two rubies from the rubies of Paradise. Allah extinguished their light, and were it not for that, they would have illuminated all between the east and the west."],[9204,"The Corner used to be placed on Abū Qubays, and the entire town would shine from its light."],[9205,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What do you think of people kissing their hands when they touch the Corner? Was this from those who passed in everything? He said: Yes. I saw Ibn ʿUmar, Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, Jābir b. ʿAbd Allāh, and Abū Hurayrah when they touched it they kissed their hands. He said: I said: And Ibn ʿAbbās? He said: And Ibn ʿAbbās, I think he said. I said: Do you dislike leaving off kissing your hand when you touch? He said: Yes, if he touched it then he would kiss it, and I want its blessing."],[9206,"Two are dearer to me than three."],[9207,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ circumambulated the House on his riding camel, touching the Corner with his staff, then bringing it to his mouth."],[9209,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived while he was ill, he circumambulated the House on his riding camel, touching the corner with his staff, then kissed the tip of his staff."],[9210,"The Prophet ﷺ circumambulated the House on his camel, touching the corner with his staff. He said: Then 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf came, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'What did you do, O Abu Muhammad, regarding touching the corner?' He said: 'All of it: I touched and I left off.' He said: 'You did right.'"],[9211,"The Prophet ﷺ circumambulated on a camel so that the people would not be pushed away from him."],[9212,"That he saw Ibn al-Zubayr and 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz, when they touched, they wiped their faces with their hands."],[9213,"I saw Salim b. 'Abd Allah, when he touched the corner, he placed his hand on his cheek."],[9214,"I did not see anyone touch except that he kissed his hand, and we found the people upon that. He said: And indeed I saw Ayyub often wiping his face with his hand when he touched after kissing his hand."],[9216,"The Prophet ﷺ circumambulated the House on the night of al-Ifadah on his camel, touching the corner with his staff."],[9217,"The Prophet ﷺ circumambulated the House on his riding camel, out of dislike that he would turn the people away from himself, touching the corner with his staff."],[9218,"When Ibn al-Zubayr wanted to remove the watering place from the mosque, Ibn Abbas said to him: 'You have not followed the piety of one more pious than you, nor the wickedness of one more wicked than you.'"],[9220,"I passed by the mosque without being in a state of ablution—should I touch the corner? He said: 'No.' I said: 'Nor anything of the Ka'bah?' He said: 'No.'"],[9221,"Al-Ashall, who had a paralyzed right hand, asked: “Should he touch [the Corner] with the back of his hand or with his left hand?” He replied: “Rather, he should say takbir and not touch with either of his hands, and whichever of those he does, it is fine.” He added: “I had heard him say before that: ‘He touches with his right hand, even if it is paralyzed.’”"],[9222,"I passed by the mosque without being in a state of ablution—should I touch the corner? He said: 'No.' I said: 'Nor anything of the Ka'bah?' He said: 'No.'"],[9223,"That his father was informed of the statement of A’ishah: “Indeed, part of the Hijr belongs to the House,” so Ibn Umar said: “By Allah, I think that if A’ishah heard this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then I do not think the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded leaving off touching them except that they are not on the foundations of Ibrahim, and the people did not circumambulate from behind the Hijr except for that reason.”"],[9224,"He would not touch the two western corners, but rather the two eastern ones."],[9225,"That he did not see Ibn Umar touch the two western corners, but he would hardly pass the two eastern ones without touching them.”"],[9226,"I was with Ibn Abbas and Muawiyah while they were circumambulating the House. Muawiyah would not pass by any corner except that he touched it. Ibn Abbas said to him: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to touch only the Yamani Corner.” Muawiyah replied: “Nothing of the House is to be abandoned.”"],[9227,"And Ibn al-Zubayr pointed with his hand toward al-Madinah."],[9228,"He circumambulated with Ibn Umar around the House. He said: “I saw that he did not fail to touch the two Yamani corners in every circumambulation.” He said: “And I saw that he did not approach the other two.”"],[9229,"“And whoever avoids touching anything of the House.” He said: Ibn al-Zubayr “used to touch all of them when he began and when he finished.”"],[9230,"I do not care whether I pray in the Hijr or inside the House."],[9231,"Nothing of its corners is to be abandoned."],[9232,"I did not see him."],[9233,"Between the two corners is a basin upon which are seventy thousand who say 'Ameen' for the one who supplicates; and if he forgets, they say: O Allah, forgive him."],[9234,"I did not see him."],[9235,"I did not see him."],[9236,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Umar during part of his caliphate used to pray in the area of the House, until Umar prayed behind the Station."],[9237,"I did not see him."],[9238,"I did not see him."],[9240,"Ibn al-Zubayr saw people wiping the Station and forbade them, saying: 'You were not commanded to wipe it.' He said: 'You were only commanded to pray.'"],[9241,"I saw al-Hajjaj intending to place his foot on the Station, and Ibn al-Hanafiyyah deterred him from that and forbade him from doing so."],[9242,"I did not see him."],[9243,"I did not see him."],[9244,"I did not see him."],[9245,"That he heard the Messenger of God ﷺ saying between the corner of Banu Madh-hij and the Black Stone: “Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.”"],[9246,"I watched Ibn ʿUmar as he was circumambulating the House, saying: “There is no god but God alone, with no partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise. In His hand is all good, and He has power over all things.” Then he said: “Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.”"],[9247,"I did not see him."],[9248,"I heard ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb constantly reciting around the House: “Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.”"],[9249,"Whoever circumambulates the House should refrain from idle talk and remember God, except for talk in which there is no harm. It is more beloved to me that he leaves all talk except the remembrance of God and the Qur’an."],[9252,"He was asked about reciting during circumambulation, and he said: “The people introduced it as an innovation.”"],[9253,"I did not see him."],[9254,"Circumambulation is merely prayer, so when you circumambulate, speak little."],[9255,"Inscribed on the Station: 'The Sacred House of God in Makkah. The dwellings of its people are in water and meat. God has guaranteed the provision of its people. It is approached from three roads: the upper part of the valley, its lower part, and the mountain pass. The first to declare it sacred shall not make it lawful.'"],[9256,"Inscribed on the Station: 'The Sacred House of God is blessed for its people in meat and water. Upon God is the provision of its people from three roads. The first to declare it sacred shall not make it lawful.'"],[9257,"Circumambulation is prayer, but you have been permitted to speak. So whoever speaks, let him not speak except with good."],[9258,"The Prophet ﷺ caught up with a man during circumambulation and said: How have you become this morning? How much do you find? How much is with you?"],[9259,"While Umar b. al-Khattab was circumambulating the Kaaba, he heard two men behind him speaking in a foreign tongue. He turned to them and said: Seek a way to Arabic."],[9260,"I was circumambulating with Tawus, and he said: Let us touch this with you, for us five. He said: I thought that he liked to touch on the odd [number]."],[9262,"The Prophet ﷺ drank while circumambulating the House."],[9263,"It was said to Muhammad b. Ali: What is the best thing to say during these days, the days of Hajj or the days of... He said: There is no god but God, and God is greatest. He said: That is it."],[9264,"I heard Ibn Umar say: There is no god but God, and God is greatest. He said: That is it. I said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what is 'that is it'? He said: 'And He bound them to the word of piety, and they were more entitled to it and worthy of it' [Quran 48:26]."],[9265,"Ibn Umar used to prefer to circumambulate seven at night and five during the day."],[9268,"Ibn Sirin used to prefer odd numbers in everything, even to eat an odd number."],[9272,"I heard ʿAṭāʾ being asked: Are three circuits dearer to you or four? He said: Three. Then when it was said to him: What about six? He said: If you wish, you may increase; but three are dearer to me than four."],[9274,"On every camel’s hump there is a devil, so remember God as you have been commanded, then make use of them for yourselves, for God carries [you] on them."],[9275,"He prefers to depart on an odd number of circumambulations."],[9276,"Every seven is odd, and four are dearer to me than three."],[9277,"Whoever circumambulates the House fifty times, it is as the day his mother gave birth to him."],[9278,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I doubted in the circumambulation whether it was two or three. He said: Complete it according to the greater certainty. I said: I performed it with another man and we differed. He said: His less and mine less. I said: He refused. He said: Then do what is more certain in your minds. I said: I performed it and said: All of it is with me. He said: Then start a new seven."],[9279,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I performed seven circuits, then it occurred to me that I had performed eight circuits. He said: Then perform another seven, and count them as six circuits."],[9280,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I performed seven circuits and prayed, then it occurred to me that I had performed six circuits. He said: Then perform another seven, and count them as eight circuits. ʿAṭāʾ said: If you have performed six circuits, then perform one more and pray two rakʿahs."],[9281,"I was in the guard of Ibn al-Zubayr, and he performed eight circuits until he reached the people at the middle of the stone. He was told about that, so he completed seven circuits and said: Circumambulation is only odd-numbered."],[9282,"Ali used to say concerning a man who circumambulates the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa three circuits: he should circumambulate fourteen."],[9283,"I heard Sa'id b. Jubayr being asked about a man who circumambulates between al-Safa and al-Marwa a hundred circuits. He said: Nothing is upon him."],[9284,"Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Bakr circumambulated during the governorship of Amr b. Sa'id over Makkah. Amr went out to prayer, and Abd al-Rahman said to him: Wait for me so that I may finish on an odd number. He finished on three circuits, then he did not repeat that seven."],[9285,"He circumambulated with Sa'id b. Jubayr and prayer interrupted them while two circuits remained for them. Sa'id did not repeat them and finished on five circuits."],[9286,"Prayer interrupted him while something remained of his circumambulation. He did not repeat what remained, and I think he finished on five circuits."],[9287,"I said to Ata': Prayer interrupted me; should I complete what remains? He said: Yes. A person said to him: So I returned? He said: Then complete what has passed. I said: Prayer interrupted me and I prayed at the Maqam, or near the house of Ibn al-Zubayr, or from your side? He said: Leave that circumambulation and do not count it. I said: Do you think if I prayed from your side, should I not continue when I finish as I am on my way to the corner and not count it as anything? He said: Yes, if you wish, until after that. I said: The circumambulation that prayer interrupts while I am in it? He said: I prefer that you do not count it. I said: If I counted it, would it suffice? He said: Yes, if Allah wills, you have circumambulated."],[9288,"I said to Ata': How are you? He said: When I see that it (prayer) has begun and I am at the corner and have not yet circumambulated... I said: Then it begins while you have passed the corner? He said: If I think I can complete that circuit, I continue and circumambulate; otherwise I cut it short. I said: Prayer interrupted my seven, so I finished and wanted to pray two rak'ahs before completing my seven? He said: No, complete your seven, unless you are prevented from circumambulating, then pray if you wish, until you are allowed."],[9289,"I said to Ata': How long should I sit after the imam's salutation if I am interrupted? He said: Nothing, and do not sit for conversation. I said: Should I cut short my circumambulation to attend a funeral and pray over it, then return? He said: No."],[9290,"Travel; you will be healthy and provided for."],[9291,"From Ata' concerning a man who circumambulated some circuits, then the prayer was called or prayer presented itself to him, so he left. He said: If his circumambulation was voluntary, then if it is an odd number, it suffices him, and he may pray two rak'ahs; if he wishes, he completes his circumambulation. And if it is an even or odd number and then he prays, he liked not to leave except on an odd number of that seven."],[9292,"He circumambulated with Sa'id b. Jubayr five circuits, then the prayer was called for Asr. He completed what remained of his circumambulation, then prayed the two rak'ahs of circumambulation after Asr."],[9293,"Whoever circumambulates the House and then has a need, let him leave on an odd number and pray two rak'ahs, and he should not repeat the rest of his seven."],[9294,"If the prayer interrupts you during your seven [circuits], then complete it from where you were cut off."],[9296,"He saw Ibn ʿUmar perform three circuits on a hot day, then sit in the Hijr and rest, then stand up and complete what remained."],[9298,"I saw Ibn al-Zubayr circumambulate the House and quicken his walking."],[9300,"If a person performs part of his seven [circuits] in the Hijr, then he must circumambulate the House from beyond the Hijr for what he did in the Hijr, if he erred."],[9303,"If I had authority over the House, I would have included all of the Hijr in it, so that it would not be circumambulated from beyond it."],[9304,"It has reached me that the obligatory prayer suffices for the two rakʿahs of the seven [circuits]."],[9305,"The obligatory prayer suffices for the two rakʿahs of the seven [circuits]."],[9306,"Travel; you will be healthy and provided for."],[9307,"Travel; you will be healthy and provided for."],[9308,"He performed tawaf with Ibn Umar before sunset. He said: 'We finished and the iqamah was called, so we prayed Maghrib. Then he stood and did not pray, and began another seven rounds. I said, 'You did not pray for your seven rounds.' He said, 'Did we not already pray?' Then he said, 'The obligatory prayer suffices for the two rak'ahs of the seven rounds.'"],[9309,"I asked Salim b. Abdullah: 'I finished tawaf and the iqamah was called for prayer.' He said, 'The prayer suffices you for your tawaf.'"],[9310,"The two rak'ahs of Fajr suffice for the two rak'ahs after the seven rounds."],[9311,"It was said to him: 'The obligatory prayer suffices for the two rak'ahs after the seven rounds.' He said, 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never performed tawaf seven rounds except that he prayed two rak'ahs after it.'"],[9312,"He performed tawaf around the House, then prayed the obligatory prayer, then prayed the two rak'ahs of tawaf."],[9313,"I asked Sa'id b. Jubayr about tawaf after Asr. He said: 'If you wish, pray two rak'ahs when the sun has set; if you wish, the obligatory prayer suffices you; and if you wish, pray them after the obligatory prayer.'"],[9314,"I said to Ata: 'Does my seven rounds suffice if I do not pray until I come to the House and pray them?' He said: 'Yes, if you wish.' I said: 'What do you think if I pray the two rak'ahs of the seven rounds before it—would that suffice for the two rak'ahs after it?' He said: 'Glory be to Allah, I do not know.' He said: I said: 'No, not until I pray them after it?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[9315,"Pray them wherever you wish as long as you have not left the Haram."],[9316,"My father used to perform tawaf around the House and see it open, so he would enter and pray, then come out and pray the two rak'ahs of tawaf outside the House."],[9317,"He used to perform tawaf around the House seven rounds, then enter the House and pray the two rak'ahs of tawaf inside it."],[9318,"I was sitting with ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUmar when a young man came to him and said: Will you not wage jihad? He remained silent and turned away from him. Then Ibn ʿUmar said: Islam is built on four pillars: establishing prayer and giving zakat, between which no separation is made, fasting the month of Ramadan, and performing Hajj to the House for whoever is able to find a way there. And jihad and charity are among good deeds."],[9319,"Extra text: that Tawus and Ibn Sabit used to pray four rak'ahs for every week (i.e., every seven circuits). Mandal said: I narrated that to Ibn Jurayj, and he said: 'Ata' narrated to me: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray two rak'ahs for every seven circuits.\""],[9320,"That the Prophet ﷺ said to Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib: \"O Banu 'Abd Manaf, if you have any authority, let me not find any of you preventing anyone from performing tawaf around the House or praying near it at any hour of the night or day.\" He (the narrator) said: \"Then 'Abd al-Malik came as a pilgrim and prohibited tawaf after dawn for a day or two, then later permitted it at that time. We were told that this hadith reached him.\""],[9321,"O Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib, O Banu 'Abd Manaf: let me not find that you have prevented anyone from praying near this House at whatever hour he wishes, night or day."],[9322,"He mentions that he saw Ibn 'Abbas on the Day of Tarwiyah \"perform tawaf after 'Asr seven circuits, then pray two rak'ahs, as both a pilgrim (hajj) and one performing 'umrah. Then he would stand after the Fajr prayer and perform tawaf seven circuits, and pray two rak'ahs.\" We said to him: \"He only does that because of his arrival, until he stayed among us.\" Then he stood when he prayed Fajr, performed tawaf, then prayed two rak'ahs, then touched the corner and ascended. He (the narrator) says: he left the mosque."],[9323,"That he used to perform tawaf after 'Asr and Fajr, and pray at that time for his seven circuits."],[9325,"That he performed tawaf with 'Umar after the Fajr prayer around the Ka'bah. When 'Umar finished his tawaf, he looked and did not see the sun, so he mounted and did not pray supererogatory prayer until he dismounted at Dhu Tuwa, where he prayed two rak'ahs for his tawaf."],[9326,"I saw Sa'id b. Jubayr and Mujahid performing tawaf after 'Asr once, seven circuits, then they sat and did not pray until the sun set."],[9327,"Abu Sa'id al-Khudri came as a pilgrim or for 'umrah, and he performed tawaf after Fajr. He (the narrator) said: \"They said: 'Watch what he does.' When he finished his seven circuits, he sat down, and when the sun rose, he prayed two rak'ahs.\""],[9328,"I asked 'Ata' b. Abi Rabah about tawaf after 'Asr and after Fajr. He said: \"I saw Ibn 'Umar perform tawaf after dawn, then pray.\" Musa said: I went to Nafi' and informed him, and he said: \"'Ata' lied.\" So I returned to 'Ata' and informed him, and he said: \"I saw Ibn 'Umar doing that before Nafi' was taken captive.\" Musa said: I went to Salim b. 'Abd Allah and asked him, and he said: \"'Ata' spoke the truth. Ibn 'Umar used to perform tawaf after Fajr once, seven circuits, then pray for it at that time.\" Musa said: \"I went to Nafi' and mentioned to him the statement of Salim, and he remained silent.\""],[9329,"That Ibn 'Umar used to dislike combining tawaf (i.e., performing two sets of seven circuits without praying in between), and he would say: \"For every seven circuits there are two rak'ahs.\" And he himself would not combine two sets of seven circuits."],[9331,"Ata' saw no harm in joining tawaf circuits, and he gave fatwa for it. He mentioned that Tawus and al-Miswar b. Makhramah used to do it. He said: A man asked Ata' about performing seven circuits without any prayer between them until he prays the rak'ahs for all of them after finishing them. He said: \"I heard of that from al-Miswar b. Makhramah and from Tawus, and I think that is only something that reached them.\" I said to Ata': \"Did you hear of that from anyone other than the two of them?\" He said: \"And why should I not do it?\" He said: \"I see no harm in it if you do it.\""],[9333,"I performed tawaf with Sa'id b. Jubayr on the day of Eid al-Fitr before the Eid prayer, and he joined three sets of seven circuits. I said: \"Why are you joining them?\" He said: \"Indeed, no prayer is performed before the Eid prayer.\""],[9334,"A'ishah stayed in the residence of 'Utbah b. Muhammad b. al-Harith. She would perform tawaf after the last evening prayer. When she wanted to perform tawaf, she ordered the lamps of the mosque to be extinguished, all of them, then she performed tawaf. When she finished seven circuits, she sought refuge between the corner and the door, then returned to the corner and touched it, and performed another seven circuits. When she finished, she sought refuge between the corner and the door, then returned and joined three sets of seven circuits, then went behind the row of Zamzam, then prayed two rak'ahs, then spoke, then prayed two rak'ahs, separating each two rak'ahs with speech. With her was a freedwoman and Umm Hakim bt. Khalid b. al-'As, and Umm Hakim bt. 'Abd Allah b. Abi Rabi'ah. The freedwoman said: We mentioned Hassan, and we discussed his lineage. A'ishah said: \"The son of al-Furay'ah,\" saying it quietly. She forbade us from insulting him, and she absolved him from being among those who slandered her. She said: \"I hope that Allah admits him to Paradise because of his saying: 'You satirized Muhammad, but I answered on his behalf, and with Allah is the reward for that. For my father, his father, and my honor are a shield for Muhammad's honor from you.'\" And A'ishah recited these two verses while she was performing tawaf around the House."],[9335,"She performed tawaf with A'ishah around the House, three sets of seven circuits, without praying between them. When she finished, she prayed two rak'ahs for each set of seven."],[9336,"Ibn Hisham forbade women from performing tawaf with men. He reported and said: \"How can you forbid them from tawaf when the wives of the Prophet ﷺ performed tawaf with men?\" I said: \"After the hijab?\" He said: \"Yes, by my life, I was present, by my life, after the hijab.\" I said: \"How did they mix with men?\" He said: \"They would not do so. A'ishah would perform tawaf apart from the men, not mixing with them. A woman with her said: 'Come with us, O Mother of the Believers, to touch the corner.' She pulled her and said: 'Go away from me,' and she refused to touch it. They would go out veiled at night, and they would perform tawaf with men, not mixing with them.\" He said: \"But when they entered the House, they were screened when they entered, then the men were sent out.\" He said: \"'Ubayd b. 'Umayr and I used to come to A'ishah when she was in residence in the heart of Thabir. I said: 'What was her screen then?' He said: 'She was in a Turkish tent of hers, with a covering over it, between us and her.' He said: 'But I saw her wearing a saffron-dyed gown when I was a boy.'\""],[9337,"The Prophet ﷺ ordered Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, to perform tawaf riding in her howdah, behind the worshippers, in the middle of the mosque.\" I said: \"During the day or at night?\" He said: \"I do not know.\" I said: \"Which seven?\" He said: \"I do not know.\""],[9338,"Sawdah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, went out one night and 'Umar saw her; she was tall. He said: \"Indeed, you will not be hidden from us.\" That was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ while he was eating a bone with meat on it, and he did not put it down until it was revealed to him: \"That permission has been given to you to go out for your needs at night.\""],[9339,"She said: I sent to the Prophet ﷺ, or I complained to the Prophet ﷺ, that I was ill. He said: \"Perform tawaf behind the people while riding.\" She said: I performed tawaf while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was leading the people in prayer beside the House, and he was reciting: \"By the Mount, and by a Book inscribed.\""],[9340,"I said to Ata': \"Is it disliked for a person to perform tawaf before the prayer while the imam is being awaited to come out?\" He said: \"It does not harm him.\" I said: \"What about during the yellowing of the sun, at the time when prayer is disliked, if he delays his two rak'ahs until a time when prayer is not disliked?\" He said: \"It does not harm him if he does not pray at the time when prayer is disliked.\""],[9341,"Tawus, Mujahid, and Ata' forbade him from performing tawaf beyond the Maqam, and they said: \"Between the House and the Maqam.\""],[9342,"I said to Ata': \"A boy who has not reached puberty, is tawaf performed with him around the House so that he performs wudu?\" He said: \"No problem. There is no problem for one who has reached understanding to seek blessing through his wudu.\""],[9345,"I used to hear strangers asking 'Ata': Is tawaf better for us or prayer? He would say: \"As for you, tawaf is better, because you are not able to perform tawaf in your own land, but you are able to pray there.\""],[9346,"He came to Madinah, and 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote to him asking: Is prayer better for strangers or tawaf? Anas said to him: \"Rather, prayer and enjoying the House is better.\""],[9347,"I saw Sa'id b. Jubayr saying to strangers when he saw them praying: \"Leave and perform tawaf around the House.\""],[9348,"When a stranger stays in Makkah for forty days, prayer becomes better for him than tawaf.\""],[9349,"'Umar b. al-Khattab, may Allah have mercy on him, passed by a leprous woman while she was performing tawaf around the House. He said to her: \"O slave woman of Allah, do not harm people. If only you would stay in your house.\" She did so. Then a man passed by her after that and said: \"The one who forbade you has died, so come out.\" She said: \"I would not obey him while he was alive and disobey him when he is dead.\""],[9350,"I said to 'Ata': Kissing the corner? He said: \"It is good.\""],[9351,"I saw 'Umar b. al-Khattab kissing the corner, and he would say: \"By Allah, I kiss you while I know that you are a stone, and I know that Allah is my Lord, but I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ kiss you, so I kissed you.\""],[9352,"I saw 'Umar b. al-Khattab kissing the Stone and saying: \"By Allah, I know that you are a stone, but I saw Abu al-Qasim ﷺ being devoted to you.\""],[9353,"Horses and barādhīn are equal."],[9354,"Then he wiped the corner with his garment and then kissed it."],[9355,"The Prophet ﷺ did not seek refuge.\" He said: And he informed me that he did not see Abu Hurayrah, Jabir, Abu Sa'id, or Ibn 'Umar clinging to any part of the House from Zamzam. I said: Has it reached you that the Prophet ﷺ used to touch anything of its interior or its staircases to seek refuge by it? He said: \"No.\" I said: Nor from any of his companions? He said: \"No.\" I said: Nor did you see anyone from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ doing that? He said: \"No.\" I said: Do you yourself cling to the House? He said: \"No, but I place my hand toward the House and I do not touch it at all.\" I said: What about the outside of the House, do you cling to it? He said: \"No.\" He said: \"And as for your seeking refuge by any part of it, I would not care which part you sought refuge by; I would not then be following anything.\""],[9356,"He sought refuge at the House, and al-Harith b. Abd Allah said to him: \"Do you know, O Commander of the Believers, who was the first to do this?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"The old women of your people, the old women of Quraysh.\" He said: \"I think Abd al-Malik abandoned that afterwards.\""],[9357,"She is only your sweet basil."],[9358,"His father used to seek refuge between the Corner and the Door."],[9359,"It is disliked for a man to place his forehead on the House, but his hand [is acceptable]."],[9360,"I saw Ayyub pressing his chest and hands against the House."],[9361,"I performed tawaf with Abd Allah b. Amr. When we finished the seven [circuits], we prayed two rak'ahs behind the Ka'bah. I said: \"Will you not seek refuge?\" He said: \"I seek refuge in Allah from the Fire.\" Then he walked and touched the Corner, then stood between the Stone and the Door and pressed his chest, hands, and cheek to it, then said: \"This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do.\""],[9362,"Muhammad - his grandfather - performed tawaf with his father Abd Allah b. Amr. When they completed their seven, Muhammad said to Abd Allah where people seek refuge: \"Seek refuge.\" Abd Allah said: \"I seek refuge in Allah from Satan.\" Then when he touched the Corner, he sought refuge between the Corner and the Door, and pressed his forehead and chest against the House, then said: \"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do this.\""],[9363,"I came to Ibn Abbas while he was seeking refuge between the Corner and the Door, leaning on the hand of Ikrimah, his freed slave. I said: \"Is it 'two magicians supporting each other' or 'two sorceries'?\" He did not answer either. Ikrimah said: \"'Two magicians supporting each other' - you have pressed him too much.\""],[9364,"I said: \"When you have performed tawaf between the sixth and seventh?\" I said: \"Then I cling to the House between the Black Corner and the Yamani Corner, then I seek refuge in Allah.\""],[9365,"This is the Multazam between the Corner and the Door."],[9366,"He used to press his chest, hand, and belly against the House."],[9367,"The Messenger of God ﷺ gave the cavalryman two shares and the infantryman one share."],[9368,"He used to seek refuge between the corner and the door."],[9369,"Ibn ʿUmar ؓ would not cling to any part of the House."],[9370,"I saw Mujāhid pass by a man standing supplicating between the corner and the door; he touched him with his hand and said, “Cling, cling.”"],[9371,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Has it reached you that the Prophet ﷺ or some of his companions used to face the House upon exiting and supplicate? He said: No. Then ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ informed me that he said to some of those who face the House thus—supplicating upon exiting at the time of his departure—: “Why do they do this? This is the practice of the Jews in their scripture. Supplicate inside the House as much as you wish, then exit.”"],[9372,"When he came opposite a door in the house of Yaʿlā near the perfume sellers, he would face the House and supplicate, and the daughters of Ghazwān, who were Muslim women, would come out to him and supplicate with him."],[9373,"When he came opposite a place in the house of Yaʿlā—ʿUbayd Allāh forgot it—he would face the House and then supplicate. Ibn Jurayj said: I was performing ṭawāf with ʿAbd Allāh b. Kathīr al-Dārī until when we reached that place, he faced the House and then supplicated, and said: “Something has reached me about this place.”"],[9374,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: “You have been commanded only to perform ṭawāf, and you have not been commanded to enter it.” He said: “He did not forbid entering it, but I heard him say: Usāma b. Zayd informed me from the Prophet ﷺ that when he entered the House, he supplicated in all its corners and did not pray inside it until he exited. When he exited, he prayed two rakʿahs facing the qibla, and said: ‘This is the qibla.’” I said: What are its corners? In its angles? He said: “Rather, in every direction of the House.”"],[9375,"He entered the House with the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ did not pray in the House when he entered it, but when he came out and descended, he prayed two rakʿahs at the door of the House."],[9376,"The Prophet ﷺ entered the House and supplicated in its corners, then came out and prayed two rakʿahs."],[9377,"Face all of it, and do not place any of it behind you."],[9378,"He would not pray inside the House; when he came out, he would pray two rakʿahs."],[9379,"The Prophet ﷺ entered the House, then came out, and it was not mentioned that he prayed inside it."],[9383,"A slave of mine ran away on the day of al-Yarmuk, then the Muslims captured him, and they returned him to me."],[9384,"The Prophet ﷺ came on the day of the conquest on a camel belonging to Usama b. Zayd, with Usama riding behind the Prophet ﷺ, and with him were Bilal and Uthman b. Talha. When he reached the House, he sent Uthman b. Talha, who brought him a key. He opened it, and the Prophet ﷺ, Usama b. Zayd, Uthman b. Talha, and Bilal entered. They remained in the House for a long time and closed the door. Then the Prophet ﷺ came out to them, and they hastened to the House. Abdullah b. Umar and another man with him beat them to it. Abdullah asked Bilal: 'Where did the Prophet ﷺ pray?' He showed him where he prayed, but he did not ask him how many rakʿahs he prayed. He said: Whenever Abdullah b. Umar entered the Kaʿbah, he would walk straight ahead, putting the door behind his back, then walk until there was about three cubits between him and the wall, then pray, aiming for the spot where Bilal told him the Prophet ﷺ had prayed."],[9385,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in the House, or in the Kaʿbah. Another one will come and forbid you; do not obey him."],[9388,"He used to perform tawaf around the House seven rounds, then enter the House and pray the two rak'ahs of tawaf inside it."],[9389,"That Muhammad b. al-Hanafiyya entered the Kaʿbah and prayed two rakʿahs in each corner."],[9391,"The Prophet ﷺ came walking between Usama b. Zayd and Bilal until he entered the Kaʿbah, and inside it there was a crossbeam. When Bilal came out, I asked him: 'What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do?' He said: 'He left one third of the beam on his right and prayed in the remaining two thirds.' He said: I said: 'How many rakʿahs did he pray?' He said: 'I did not ask Bilal about that.'"],[9392,"The House should not be entered with shoes, weapons, or khuffs. Ata and Mujahid considered the Stone to be part of the House."],[9393,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Uthman b. Talha on the day of the conquest: 'Bring me the key of the Kaʿbah.' He was slow in coming, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood waiting for him until sweat like pearls dripped from him, and he said: 'What is keeping him?' A man hurried to him, and the woman who had the key—he said: I think he said: she was Umm Uthman—kept saying: 'If he takes it from you, they will never give it back to you.' He kept at her until she gave him the key. He brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ opened the House. Then he came out while the people were with him and sat by the watering place. Ali said: 'If we have been given prophethood, and we have been given the watering place, and we have been given the guardianship of the Kaʿbah, no people have a greater share than us.' He said: It was as if the Prophet ﷺ disliked his statement. Then he called Uthman b. Talha and handed the key to him, and said: 'Conceal it.'"],[9396,"The Prophet ﷺ took the key of the Kaaba on the day of the conquest, while the people were present, and the Prophet ﷺ said: 'Is there anyone who will speak?' Then he called Talha, then he called Uthman b. Talha and handed the key over to him."],[9397,"The Prophet ﷺ called Uthman b. Talha on the day of the conquest for the key of the Kaaba, and he came with it openly until he handed it to the Prophet ﷺ. Al-Abbas said: 'O Prophet of God, combine for me the guardianship with the water-giving.' And revelation descended upon the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: 'Summon Uthman b. Talha for me.' So he was summoned, and the Prophet ﷺ handed it to him, and covered it over him. He said: 'The Messenger of God ﷺ was the first to cover it over him.' Then he said: 'Take it, O sons of Talha, with no one seizing it from you except an oppressor.'"],[9398,"Ka'b b. al-Ashraf used to satirize the Prophet ﷺ and harm him. So the Prophet ﷺ ordered Sa'd b. Mu'adh to send five men to him. They brought him while he was in the assembly of his people at al-'Awali. When he saw them, he was startled by them and said, 'What has brought you?' They said, 'We have come to you for a need.' He said, 'Let one of you come close and tell me his need.' One of them came close to him, and they said, 'We have come to pledge allegiance to you for armor we have.' He said, 'By Allah, if you do, you have been hard-pressed since this man settled among you'—or he said, 'with you.' They made an appointment with him to come after the night had grown still. They came to him, and he rose to meet them. His wife said, 'What brings these people at this hour with anything you would like?' He said, 'They have told me of their need.' When he drew near to them, Abu 'Abs embraced him, and Muhammad b. Maslama came upon him with the sword and stabbed him in his side with his dagger, and they killed him. When morning came, the Jews went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'Our companion has been killed by treachery.' The Prophet ﷺ reminded them of how he had been satirizing him in his poetry and harming him. He said, 'Then the Prophet ﷺ invited them to write a pact between him and them.'—He said, 'I think he said: And that document is with 'Ali.' Al-Zuhri or someone else said: A man among those who claimed Islam said to Abu 'Abs, 'You killed Ka'b by treachery.' Abu 'Abs swore that he would never see him able to kill him but that he would kill him. So whenever he saw him, he would run after him until the other man eluded him."],[9399,"I said to Ata: 'Should someone who appears on it pray on the roof of the Kaaba?' He said: 'I do not like that.' I said: 'What do you think if the custodians are upon it when the prayer time comes, would you dislike them praying on it at that moment?' He said: 'Yes, I dislike it.'"],[9401,"Heraclius wrote to Muawiya asking him about three things: which place, if you pray in it, you would think you had not prayed toward the qibla; which place the sun rose in once and had not risen in before or after; and about the obliteration on the moon. He said: Muawiya sought knowledge of that, and he would have liked to learn it from someone other than Ibn Abbas but did not find it, so he wrote about it to Ibn Abbas. He wrote to them: 'As for the place where if you pray in it you would think you had not prayed toward the qibla, it is the roof of the Kaaba. As for the place where the sun rose once and had not risen in before or after, it is the sea when God parted it for Moses. As for the obliteration on the moon, God Almighty says: 'And We obliterated the sign of the night' – that is the obliteration.'"],[9402,"Shayba b. Uthman stripped the Kaaba before the fire of the garments with which the people of the Jahiliyya had clothed it, and he perfumed it and scented it. He said: He left in it the two horns of the ram on its exterior, in the building, approximately toward the qibla of the Maqam. I said: 'And what were those garments?' He said: 'Of every kind, coverings and better than that.'"],[9403,"And I asked him: 'Were there in the House the two horns of a ram?' He said: 'Yes, they were in it.' I said: 'Did you see them?' He said: 'I think so, but Abd al-Rahman b. Babayh informed me that he had seen them.' He said: 'And others besides him have seen them in it.' He said: 'And they say that they are the horns of the ram that Ibrahim sacrificed.'"],[9404,"I asked Uthman: 'Why did the Prophet ﷺ send for you after he came out of the Kaaba?' He said: 'He sent for me and said: I saw the two horns of the ram, but I did not command you to cover them; for it is not proper that there be anything in the House that distracts a worshipper.'"],[9405,"Umar b. al-Khattab said: 'If we took what is in this House, meaning the Kaaba, and divided it.' Ubayy b. Kab said to him: 'By God, that is not for you.' He said: 'Why?' He said: 'Because God has made clear the place of every wealth, and the Messenger of God ﷺ confirmed it.' He said: 'You have spoken the truth.'"],[9407,"Tubba was the first to clothe the Kaaba with woven striped cloths, and it was covered with them. Ibn Jurayj said: 'Some of our scholars have claimed it was Ismail the Prophet ﷺ, and God knows best about that.'"],[9408,"Abdullah b. al-Zubayr was the first to clothe the Ka'bah with silk brocade."],[9409,"I asked Aisha: Should we clothe the Ka'bah? She said: \"The rulers suffice you in that, but you women should perfume it with fragrance.\""],[9410,"I asked Ibn Abbas about His saying: ﴿And His Throne was upon the water﴾. I said: \"Upon what was the water before anything was created?\" He said: \"Upon the back of the wind.\" Ibn Jurayj said: Sa'id b. Jubayr said: Ibn Abbas said: \"Vapor like the vapor of rivers used to rise to the sky, then it became solid and returned as a mass. That is His saying: ﴿Then He directed Himself to the heaven while it was smoke﴾.\""],[9411,"When Allah sent Adam down, his feet were on the earth and his head in the sky, hearing the speech of the inhabitants of the sky and their supplication, so he became intimate with them. The angels were in awe of him until they complained to Allah in their supplication and prayer, so Allah lowered him to the earth. When he lost what he used to hear from them, he felt lonely until he complained to Allah in his supplication and prayer. So He directed him to Makkah, and the place of his foot became a village and his stride a desert until he reached Makkah. Allah sent down a ruby from the rubies of Paradise, and it was at the site of the House now. It continued to be circumambulated until Allah sent the flood, and that ruby was lifted up. Then Allah sent Ibrahim and he built it. That is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: ﴿And when We assigned to Ibrahim the site of the House﴾."],[9412,"The House was lifted up on the day of the flood."],[9413,"Adam said: \"O Lord, why do I not hear the voices of the angels?\" He said: \"Because of your sin. But descend to the earth and build a House for Me, then circumambulate it as you saw the angels circumambulate My House in the sky.\" It is claimed that he built it from five mountains: Hira, Lebanon, al-Judi, Mount Zita, and Mount Sinai, and its foundation was from Hira. This was the building of Adam, then Ibrahim ﷺ built it."],[9415,"The Ka'bah was built from five mountains: Lebanon, Mount Zita, al-Judi, Mount Sinai, and Hira, and its foundation was from Hira.\""],[9416,"Some people said: Allah sent a cloud containing a head, and the head said: \"O Ibrahim, your Lord commands you to take the extent of this cloud.\" He began looking at it and outlining its size. The head said: \"Have you done it?\" He said: \"Yes.\" Then it rose, and he dug, uncovering a firm foundation in the earth.\""],[9417,"The king, the shrike, and tranquility (sakinah) came with Ibrahim ﷺ from Syria. The sakinah said: \"O Ibrahim, settle upon the House.\" He said: \"Therefore no coarse Bedouin or any king circumambulates the House except that you see dignity and tranquility upon him.\""],[9418,"Allah placed the House with Adam. Allah sent Adam down to the earth, and his landing place was in the land of India. His head was in the sky and his feet on the earth, and the angels were in awe of him, so he was reduced to sixty cubits. Adam grieved when he lost the voices of the angels and their glorification, so he complained about that to Allah, the Exalted. Allah said: \"O Adam, I have sent down a House for you, so circumambulate it as one circumambulates around My Throne, and pray at it as one prays at My Throne.\" Adam went out to it, and his stride was extended, so between each step there was a desert, and those deserts remained after that. Adam came to the House and circumambulated it, and after him the prophets did so."],[9419,"Allah created the site of this House two thousand years before He created anything of the earth, and its pillars are in the seventh earth.\""],[9420,"The House was foam upon the water forty years before the earth was created, and from it the earth was spread out."],[9421,"And God had entrusted the Corner to Abu Qubays. When Abraham came, Abu Qubays called out to him: 'O Abraham, this Corner is with me, take it.' So he dug it out and placed it. When Abraham finished building it, he said: 'We have done it, O Lord, so show us our rituals; make them clear to us; teach them to us.' So God sent Gabriel, and he performed the pilgrimage with him until he came to Arafah. He said: 'I have known [it],' for he had come to it once before that; therefore it was named Arafah. When it was the Day of Sacrifice, Satan appeared to him and said: 'Throw pebbles.' So he threw seven pebbles, then on the second and third days, and he blocked what was between the two mountains—meaning the accursed Iblis—which is why the stoning of the Jamarat is done. He said: 'Climb onto Thabir.' So he climbed it and called out at the top of his voice: 'O servants of God, answer God! O servants of God, obey God!' His call was heard by those between the seven seas who had in their hearts an atom's weight of faith. This is what God gave to Abraham in the rituals: his saying, 'Labbaik Allahumma labbaik, Allahumma labbaik labbaik.' There have never ceased to be on the face of the earth seven Muslims or more; were it not for that, the earth and those upon it would have perished."],[9422,"When Abraham was commanded to proclaim the pilgrimage to the people, he stood on the Station and said: 'O servants of God, answer God.' They said: 'Labbaik Rabbana labbaik.' So whoever performs the pilgrimage is among those who answered Abraham's call."],[9423,"This Station grew tall for Abraham when God said to Abraham: 'And proclaim the pilgrimage to the people' (Qur'an 22:27), in the pilgrimage, until it became the tallest mountain on earth. Then he called out with a call that was heard between the seven seas, saying: 'O servants of God, answer God! O servants of God, obey God!' They said: 'Labbayk Allahumma ajabnak, labbayk Allahumma ata'nak.' He said: 'So whoever performs the pilgrimage until the Hour arises is among those who answered Abraham.'"],[9424,"The sheep used to leap over the top of the House from the Hijr because of its low height, until Abraham and Ishmael built it.\" He said: \"And they built it fifteen years before the flood came out to him.\""],[9425,"Place a cloth, then put it in it, then let one man from each tribe take it. They did so, and he himself took the Corner, placing his hand beneath it, and he was the one who raised it with them until he set it with them in its place, which is where it is now."],[9426,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ reached puberty, a woman perfumed the Ka'bah with incense, and a spark flew from her incense burner into the Ka'bah's cloth coverings, and it burned. Quraysh consulted about demolishing it, but they feared to demolish it. Al-Walid b. al-Mughira said to them: 'What do you intend by demolishing it? Do you intend repair or harm?' They said: 'We intend repair.' He said: 'God will not destroy the one who repairs.' They said: 'Who will climb it and demolish it?' Al-Walid b. al-Mughira said: 'I will climb it and demolish it.' So al-Walid b. al-Mughira climbed onto the top of the House with an axe, then said: 'O God, we intend nothing but repair,' then he demolished. When Quraysh saw that he had demolished part of it and no harm came to them, they demolished with him. When they built and reached the place of the Corner, Quraysh disputed over the Corner—which tribe would have the honor of lifting it—until they almost came to blows. They said: 'Come, let us appoint as arbiter the first man who comes out to us from this alley.' They agreed on that. The Messenger of God ﷺ came out to them, a young lad wearing a striped waistcloth. They made him arbiter. He ordered that the Corner be placed on a cloth, then he ordered the chief of each tribe and gave him a side of the cloth to hold. Then he himself climbed up, and they lifted the Corner to him, and he placed it."],[9427,"When the House was demolished in the pre-Islamic era, then they rebuilt it, when they reached the place of the Corner, a snake came out at them, its neck like a camel's neck. The people feared to approach it. He said: 'Then a bird came and shaded half of Makkah, seized it with its talon, then flew off with it until it cast it into the sea.'\" Mujahid said: \"And one day they went out on one of their festivals, and a man removed a stone from the House, then stole from its ornaments and slipped away. Then he returned to steal again, and the two stones stuck to his head. The people came to him with his head stuck between them.\""],[9428,"In the pre-Islamic era, the Ka'bah was built of unshaped stones without mud. It was only high enough for a young goat to leap over it. It had no roof; its cloth coverings were simply laid on top and draped down. The Black Stone was placed on its wall, visible. It had two corners, like the shape of this ring. A ship came from the land of the Romans, and when they were near Jeddah, the ship broke apart. Quraysh went out to take its wood, and they found a Roman there. They took the wood, and he gave it to them. The ship had been heading for Abyssinia. The Roman who was on the ship was a carpenter. They brought the wood and they brought the Roman. Quraysh said: 'We will build the House of our Lord with this wood.' When they wanted to demolish it, there was a snake on the wall of the House, like a piece of a beam, black-backed and white-bellied. Whenever anyone approached the House to demolish it or take from its stones, it would rush at him with its mouth open. Quraysh gathered at the sanctuary and cried out to God, saying: 'Our Lord, we were not alarmed [without cause]; we intended to honor Your House and set it in order. If You are pleased with that, then [let it be], otherwise whatever You will, do.' Then they heard a lowing sound in the sky, and there was a bird larger than a vulture, black-backed and white-bellied and white-legged. It sank its talons into the back of the snake's neck and went off dragging it, its tail—larger than such-and-such—drooping, until it went with it toward Ajyad. Then Quraysh demolished it and began to rebuild it with stones from the valley, which Quraysh carried on their necks. They raised it twenty cubits into the sky. While the Prophet ﷺ was carrying stones from Ajyad, wearing a striped cloth, the cloth became too tight for him. He went to put the cloth on his shoulder, and his private parts became exposed because the cloth was too small. He was called: 'O Muhammad, cover your private parts.' He was never seen naked after that. Between [this event] and God's sending down [revelation] to him was five years, and between his departure [i.e., the start of his mission] and its building was fifteen years.\" Then when the army of al-Husayn b. Numayr came, he mentioned its burning in the time of Ibn al-Zubayr."],[9429,"The custodians of this House before you were Tasm, and they were negligent about it and did not honor its sanctity, so God destroyed them. Then after them Jurhum were its custodians, and they were negligent about it and did not honor its sanctity, so God destroyed them. So do not be negligent about it, and honor its sanctity."],[9430,"When Abraham and Ishmael finished the foundations of the House, Abraham said to Ishmael: 'Bring me a stone to set as a marker by which people may be guided.' He brought him a stone, but he was not pleased with it. He said: 'Go and bring me a stone other than this.' He said: 'And Abraham was brought the Black Stone. Ishmael came with the stone, and Abraham said to him: \"He who has not left me to your stone has brought it to me.\""],[9431,"The Stone remained on Abu Qubays for forty years, like a white thaghamah plant."],[9432,"Drinking Zamzam is by taking the bucket, then facing the Qiblah, and drinking from it until one is fully satisfied, for no hypocrite ever drinks his fill from it."],[9433,"I was with Ibn 'Abbas when a man came and sat beside him. Ibn 'Abbas said to him, \"Where have you come from?\" He said, \"I drank from Zamzam.\" He said, \"Did you drink it as it should be drunk?\" He said, \"And how should it be drunk, O Ibn 'Abbas?\" He said, \"Face the qibla, mention the name of Allah, then drink, and breathe three times. When you finish, praise Allah Almighty, and drink your fill of it, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'The sign between us and the hypocrites is that they do not drink their fill of Zamzam.'\""],[9434,"Ibn 'Abbas drank from Zamzam then said, \"I ask You for beneficial knowledge, abundant provision, and healing from every disease.\""],[9435,"When 'Abd al-Muttalib dug Zamzam, he built a basin over it, and he and his son al-Harith began drawing water and filling that basin, giving the pilgrims to drink from it. But some people, out of envy of Quraysh, would break it at night, and 'Abd al-Muttalib would repair it in the morning. When they kept damaging it repeatedly, 'Abd al-Muttalib called upon his Lord and was shown something in a dream. He said: 'Say: O Allah, I do not permit it for one who bathes, but it is permitted and lawful for one who drinks.' Then you will be spared them. 'Abd al-Muttalib said when Quraysh rushed into the mosque, he called out with what he had been shown, then departed. After that, no one damaged his basin except that he was struck with a disease in his body, until they left his basin and his water-giving alone.\""],[9436,"He heard 'Abbas b. 'Abd al-Muttalib say while standing at Zamzam, lifting his clothes with his hand, saying: \"O Allah, I do not permit it for one who bathes, but it is for one who drinks—I think he said: and one who performs ablution—permitted and lawful.\""],[9437,"9209 9115 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he also heard Ibn 'Abbas say the same while standing at Zamzam. Matn: He heard 'Abbas b. 'Abd al-Muttalib say while standing at Zamzam, lifting his clothes with his hand, saying: \"O Allah, I do not permit it for one who bathes, but it is for one who drinks—I think he said: and one who performs ablution—permitted and lawful.\""],[9438,"Zamzam is blessed, reserved; it has been reserved for you. The first for whom it was brought forth was Isma'il.\""],[9439,"When a Bedouin entered Zamzam, he entered it with his camel, then drank from it and poured some over his clothes. It was said to him, \"Why are you wetting your clothes, O Bedouin?\" He said, \"You do not know this: in the Book of Allah it is blessed, the drink of the righteous, Zamzam; it is not exhausted nor blamed. Its name is Riwa', food that nourishes, and a cure for sickness.\""],[9440,"The best of two valleys among people is the valley of Makkah, and a valley in India where Adam ﷺ descended, containing the perfume you use. The worst of two valleys among people is the valley of al-Ahqaf, and a valley in Hadramawt called Barhut. The best well among people is Zamzam, and the worst well among people is Balahut, a well in Barhut where the souls of disbelievers gather.\""],[9441,"I heard it said: The best water on earth is the water of Zamzam, and the worst water on earth is the water of Barhut—a ravine among the ravines of Hadramawt. The best places on earth are the mosques, and the worst places on earth are the markets.\""],[9442,"We used to call it Shuba'a—meaning Zamzam—and we found it an excellent help for the family.\""],[9443,"We find it in the Book of Allah—meaning Zamzam—the drink of the righteous—meaning Zamzam—reserved, food that nourishes, and a cure from sickness; it is not drained nor blamed.\""],[9444,"Zamzam is food that nourishes and a cure for sickness.\""],[9446,"Zamzam is for whatever it is drunk for. If you drink it seeking healing, Allah will heal you. If you drink it intending to quench your thirst, it will quench it. If you drink it intending to be filled, it will fill you. It is the strike of Jibril and the drink of Allah for Isma'il.\""],[9447,"He named Zamzam, calling it Zamzam, Barrah, and Madnunah."],[9448,"When Ibn al-Zubayr wanted to remove the watering place from the mosque, Ibn Abbas said to him: 'You have not followed the piety of one more pious than you, nor the wickedness of one more wicked than you.'"],[9449,"The Prophet ﷺ wrote to Suhayl b. Amr: 'If my letter reaches you at night, do not let the morning come, or by day, do not let the evening come, before you send me water from Zamzam.' Suhayl's wife Uthayla al-Khuzaiyya, the grandmother of Ayyub b. Abdullah b. Zuhayr, sought help, so she and some slave girls set out by night, and morning did not come before they had filled two water-skins and sealed them, placed them in two Ghuti saddle-bags, and then filled them with water, and sent them to the Prophet ﷺ."],[9450,"Isma'il was buried between Zamzam and the Corner and the Station."],[9451,"I performed tawaf with him until when we were between the corner and the station, he mentioned such-and-such, until he mentioned the grave of Isma'il there — I think — he mentioned about ninety prophets or seventy."],[9452,"Indeed, this curved [place] is the grave of the virgins, daughters of Ismāʿīl, and it is the elevated place, opposite the gate of Banū Sahm, toward the corner."],[9455,"That he heard Ibn al-Zubayr say on the pulpit: 'A prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred prayers in other mosques besides it.' He said: 'And he did not mention the Mosque of Madinah, so it seemed to me that he only meant the Mosque of Madinah.'"],[9456,"And Ibn al-Zubayr pointed with his hand toward al-Madinah."],[9457,"A prayer in this mosque of mine is more virtuous than a thousand prayers in any other, except the Mosque of the Kaʿbah."],[9461,"That he heard Ibn al-Zubayr say on the pulpit: 'A prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred prayers in other mosques besides it.' He said: 'And he did not mention the Mosque of Madinah, so it seemed to me that he only meant the Mosque of Madinah.'"],[9463,"Al-Sharīd came to the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of the Conquest and said, “I vowed that if Allah granted you victory over Makkah, I would pray in Jerusalem.” He said: The Prophet ﷺ said to him, “Here is more virtuous,” three times. Then he said, “By the One in whose hand is my soul, if you pray here it will suffice you.” Then he said, “A prayer in this mosque is more virtuous than a thousand prayers in any other mosque.”"],[9464,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb entered the mosque of Qubāʾ and said, “By Allah, to pray a single prayer in this mosque is more beloved to me than to pray four prayers in Jerusalem, after I have prayed a single prayer in Jerusalem. And if this mosque were at one of the horizons, we would strike the camels' flanks to reach it.”"],[9466,"If a man expectorates in the Ḥijr, there is no harm as long as he buries it."],[9467,"I was told that Abū ʿUbaydah b. al-Jarrāḥ expectorated in the mosque and then left without burying it. So they brought a lamp with him, and he began picking it up with his cloak and searching for it with it."],[9468,"Whoever expectorates in the mosque while in a state of purity, a sin is recorded against him, so let one of you bury his sputum."],[9469,"He was asked about a man who is in the Kaʿbah and wants to spit. He said, “He should spit into his garment.”"],[9470,"If you are going to do it, do not leave the people without a qiblah. Put poles at its corners and hang curtains over it so that the people pray towards it. He did so, until when it was Sunday, he ascended the minbar and said: O people, what do you think about demolishing the House? No one disagreed with him, and they said: We think you should not demolish it. He remained silent, until when their opinions were exhausted, he said: One of you keeps patching its foundation on his head, while you see the bird land on it and its stones scatter. Verily, I am demolishing it tomorrow. That coincided with the funeral of a man from Banu Bakr. Those who wished to follow it and those who did not wish to follow it followed it. A cushion was broken for him at the Maqam, then men climbed over it from behind the curtains, and the people finished their funeral. One went to Mina and another went to the well of Maymun, thinking only that a calamity from the sky would befall them. When the people were told: Enter, for by Allah it has been demolished, the people entered. He dug until he demolished it down to a foundation in the Hijr. There it was, parts holding onto each other, not stable. He called the elders of Quraysh and showed it to them. Ibn Muti' took the pickaxe from the side of the foundation facing the house of Banu Humayd and made it all collapse completely. Then he rebuilt it until he named it. He made for it two doors placed on the ground, eastern and western; people enter from this door and exit from this one. He built it. When he finished building it, there was in the mosque an unsightly pit, mounds, and sitting places. The people returned to its gravel, and a man would spread gravel with a hundred camels' load of it, until a man would go out in his cloak and shirt to Dhu Tuwa and come back with gravel in the edge of his robe, seeking reward for that. When the people grew weary, he took its mortar and spread gravel until it was level. Then he said: O people, I think you should perform 'umrah from al-Tan'im on foot. Whoever is wealthy should sacrifice a camel, otherwise a cow, otherwise a sheep. He said: I remembered the Day of Resurrection from the multitude of people. The earth crawled with them, its plains and mountains, old people and young people, virgins and married women, women, and shaving heads. He said: We came to the House and circumambulated with him, and ran between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then we slaughtered and sacrificed. I have never seen heads, hooves, and arms in one place more than on that day."],[9471,"Someone who saw those foundations being shaken with the pickaxe informed me that the House almost shook. He said: It was like kneeling camels."],[9472,"The Hijr is part of the House. He said: 'And let them circumambulate the Ancient House' (Qur'an 22:29). He said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ circumambulated from behind it."],[9473,"Al-Harith b. 'Abd Allah came as a delegate to 'Abd al-Malik during his caliphate. 'Abd al-Malik said: I do not think Abu Khubayb heard from 'A'ishah what he claimed to have heard from her. He said: Al-Harith was truthful and did not lie. He said: You heard her say what? He said: I heard her say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Your people shortened the building of the House, and were it not for their recent time with polytheism, I would have restored what they left out of it. If your people decide to rebuild it after me, come and let me show you what they left out of it. He showed her about seven cubits."],[9474,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to 'A'ishah: Did you not see that your people fell short of the foundations of Ibrahim? She said: Will you not restore it, O Messenger of Allah, to the foundations of Ibrahim? He said: Your people are recent in disbelief, or they are recent in disbelief."],[9475,"'Umar came to Makkah and sent for an old man from Banu Zuhrah to ask him about a child born in the Jahiliyyah. He said: The women of the Jahiliyyah did not have a waiting period. He said: He told me that he went with the old man to 'Umar and found him sitting in the Hijr. He asked him, and he said: As for the sperm, it is from so-and-so, and as for the child, it belongs to the bed of so-and-so. 'Umar said: You have spoken the truth, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruled by the bed. He said: When the old man stood up, 'Umar said: Come, tell me about the building of the Ka'bah. He said: Quraysh gathered strength to build the Ka'bah in the Jahiliyyah, but they fell short and were unable, and they left part of its building in the Hijr. 'Umar said: You have spoken the truth."],[9476,"'A'ishah had a disagreement with her brother 'Abd al-Rahman, and he swore not to speak to her. She wanted him to come to her, but he refused. It was said to her: He has an hour at night when he circumambulates. She waited for him at the door of the Hijr, until when he passed by her, she grabbed his garment and pulled him until she entered the Hijr. Then she said: So-and-so is free from you, and so-and-so is free from you, by the One in whose House I am. She kept swearing to him and apologizing to him."],[9477,"She asked him to open the Ka'bah for her at night, but he refused — they claimed it was Shaybah b. 'Uthman. 'A'ishah said to Umm Kulthum: Go and enter the Ka'bah. So she entered the Hijr."],[9478,"I do not care whether I pray in the Hijr or inside the House."],[9479,"'A'ishah prayed in the Hijr and said: I will pray in the House — meaning the Hijr — even if the nose of so-and-so is rubbed in the dust — referring to one of the doorkeepers. He had prevented her from entering the House at night."],[9480,"He attended that. He said: Ibn al-Zubayr brought seventy men from the best and most senior of Quraysh to 'A'ishah. She told them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her: Were it not for your people's recent time with polytheism, I would have built the House on the foundations of Ibrahim and Isma'il. Do you know why they fell short of the foundations of Ibrahim? She said: No. He said: The funds fell short for them. He said: The Ka'bah had been weakened by the fire of the people of Syria. He said: He demolished it while I was in Makkah that day. He uncovered a foundation in the Hijr, parts holding onto each other, and left it uncovered for eight days to call witnesses. He said: I saw that foundation like the flanks of camels, five stones, one stone on one side and two stones on the other. He said: I saw a man take the pickaxe and shake it from the side of the corner, and the other corner shook. He said: Then he built on that foundation, and made for it two doors attached to the ground, eastern and western. When Ibn al-Zubayr was killed, al-Hajjaj demolished it from the direction of the Hijr and restored it to what it had been. 'Abd al-Malik wrote to him: I wish you had left Ibn al-Zubayr and what he undertook."],[9481,"The saddlebeasts are journeyed to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, this mosque of mine, and the Aqsa Mosque."],[9482,"A man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ met [him], and it was said: \"From where have you come?\" He said: \"From Mount Sinai.\" He said: \"Had I met you, I would not have let you go.\" Then he narrated to him, saying: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Saddlebeasts are journeyed to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the Aqsa Mosque, and this mosque of mine.'\""],[9483,"Saddlebeasts are journeyed to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the Mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Aqsa Mosque."],[9485,"Saddlebeasts are journeyed to two mosques: the Mosque of Makkah and the Mosque of Madinah."],[9486,"Riding beasts are not worked except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, then the Mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Mosque of Jerusalem."],[9487,"Umar b. al-Khattab came and said: \"If the Mosque of Quba were in one of the horizons, we would strike the livers of the riding beasts toward it.\""],[9488,"While Umar was among some charity camels, two men passed by him. He said: \"From where have you come?\" They said: \"From Jerusalem.\" He struck them with the whip and said: \"A Hajj like the Hajj of the House?\" They said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, we came from such-and-such a land and passed by it, and we prayed in it.\" He said: \"If that is so,\" and left them."],[9489,"A man came and asked Umar's permission to go to Jerusalem. Umar said: \"Prepare yourself, and when you are ready, let me know.\" When he had prepared, he came to him. He said: \"Make it an Umrah.\""],[9490,"If there were two farsakhs between me and Jerusalem, I would not go to it."],[9491,"I heard al-Sha'bi swear by Allah: Muhammad ﷺ was not turned away from Jerusalem except out of displeasure."],[9492,"I said to al-Muthanna: \"I want to go to Madinah.\" He said: \"Do not do it. I heard Ata say—and a man asked him—he said to him: 'Circumambulating the House seven times is better than your journey to Madinah.'\""],[9494,"Circumambulating seven times is better for you than your journey to Madinah. I said: \"Then should I go to Jeddah?\" He said: \"No. You were only commanded to circumambulate.\" He said: I said: \"Then should I go out to the tree and perform Umrah from it?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"And some scholars said: 'My feet have not ceased since I arrived in Makkah.'\" He said: I said: \"Is travelling back and forth more beloved to you or residing nearby?\" He said: \"Rather travelling back and forth.\""],[9495,"I said to Ibn ʿUmar, 'I want to go to al-Ṭūr.' He said, 'Riding beasts are only journeyed to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the Mosque of the Prophet ﷺ, and al-Aqsa Mosque. Leave al-Ṭūr aside and do not go to it.'"],[9496,"Whoever looks at the House in veneration of it and in recognition of its right, a good deed is written for him thereby and an evil deed is erased from him thereby. And whoever comes to it as a visitor, venerating it and recognizing it, his sins fall away when he looks at it as leaves fall from a tree."],[9497,"Looking at the House is an act of worship, and a good deed is written for him thereby, and the angels send blessings upon him as long as he looks at it."],[9498,"Aban b. ʿAbdallah al-Bajali reported to me from ʿAtaʾ something similar: Looking at the House is an act of worship, and a good deed is written for him thereby, and the angels send blessings upon him as long as he looks at it."],[9499,"It has reached me that for every look that is directed at the House there is a good deed."],[9500,"Regarding the Kaaba: you will destroy it, O community, three times, then it will be taken up on the fourth, so enjoy it."],[9501,"Leave the Abyssinians alone as long as they leave you, for no one will extract the treasure of the Kaʿbah except Dhu al-Suwayqatayn from Abyssinia."],[9502,"Increase your circumambulation of the House before you are prevented from it, for I can almost see a small-headed, small-eared man climbing it and demolishing it with his shovel."],[9503,"It is as if I am looking at him: a small-headed, small-eared man who has climbed it with his shovel."],[9504,"It is as if I am looking at him: a small-headed, small-eared man standing over it with his shovel."],[9505,"When Ibn al-Zubayr wanted to demolish it, we fled from Makkah and stayed away for three days, fearing that punishment would descend upon us."],[9506,"It reached me that al-Husayn b. Numayr, when he set up the mangonel against the Kaaba, a white cloud appeared toward Abu Qubays, then it thundered, then it sent a thunderbolt, so the mangonel burned, and seventy men beneath it burned.”"],[9507,"This House will surely be destroyed by the hand of a man from the descendants of Ibn al-Zubayr."],[9508,"Regarding the Kaaba: you will destroy it, O community, three times, then it will be taken up on the fourth, so enjoy it."],[9509,"I was circumambulating the House with Abd Allah b. Amr after the burning of the House, when he said: “O Sa’id, do you regard what the House has done as great?” He said: I said: “What is greater than it?” He said: “The blood of a Muslim that is shed without right.”"],[9510,"Whoever looks at his Muslim brother with a look to frighten him, God will frighten him on the Day of Resurrection."],[9511,"I said to al-Zuhri: “Has it reached you that the Prophet ﷺ said: ‘Abraham made Makkah sacred, and I make Madinah sacred’?” He said: “I have heard of that, but it reached me that the Messenger of God ﷺ said: ‘People did not make Makkah sacred, but God made it sacred. It is sacred until the Day of Resurrection. Among the most insolent of people toward God on the Day of Resurrection are a man who killed in the sanctuary, a man who killed someone other than his killer, and a man who acted upon the vengeances of the people of the pre-Islamic era.’”"],[9512,"The Prophet ﷺ stood on the day of the conquest and said: “God made Makkah sacred on the day He created the heavens and the earth. It is sacred by God’s sanctity until the Day of Resurrection. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor for anyone after me. It was never lawful for anyone except for an hour of time, and it is sacred by God’s sanctity until the Day of Resurrection. Its game must not be startled, its thornbushes must not be cut, its fresh herbage must not be cut, and its lost property is not lawful except for one who announces it.” Al-Abbas b. Abd al-Muttalib said: “Except for idhkhir, O Messenger of God, for it is indispensable; it is for the blacksmith and for the houses.” The Prophet ﷺ fell silent, then said: “Except for idhkhir, for it is lawful.”"],[9513,"and its secure person is not to be frightened."],[9514,"Abd al-Karim informed me of this sermon of the Messenger of God ﷺ from Mujahid, or he said: I heard Ikrima mention from Ibn Abbas. [Hidden matn:] The Prophet ﷺ stood on the day of the conquest and said: “God made Makkah sacred on the day He created the heavens and the earth. It is sacred by God’s sanctity until the Day of Resurrection. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor for anyone after me. It was never lawful for anyone except for an hour of time, and it is sacred by God’s sanctity until the Day of Resurrection. Its game must not be startled, its thornbushes must not be cut, its fresh herbage must not be cut, and its lost property is not lawful except for one who announces it.” Al-Abbas b. Abd al-Muttalib said: “Except for idhkhir, O Messenger of God, for it is indispensable; it is for the blacksmith and for the houses.” The Prophet ﷺ fell silent, then said: “Except for idhkhir, for it is lawful.”"],[9515,"When the Prophet ﷺ entered the mosque on the day of the conquest, he ordered regarding those idols — he said: I think he said: they were around the Kaaba — so they were tipped onto their faces. Then he ordered them and they were dragged until they were taken out of the Sacred Mosque, while he was saying: “Truth has come and falsehood has vanished; indeed falsehood is bound to vanish.” He said: Then he gave a sermon, then said: “God made Makkah sacred on the day He created the heavens and the earth. It is sacred by God’s sanctity until the Day of Resurrection. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor for anyone after me. God made it lawful for me only for an hour of the day. Its game must not be startled, its thornbushes must not be cut, its fresh herbage must not be cut, and its lost property is not lawful except for one who announces it.” He said: Al-Abbas said: “Except for idhkhir, O Messenger of God, for it is for our houses, our goldsmiths, and our blacksmiths?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Except for idhkhir, for it is lawful.”"],[9516,"Its fresh herbage must not be cut, its game must not be startled, its thorny trees must not be cut, and its lost property is not lawful except for one who announces it.” Al-Abbas said: “Except for idhkhir, O Messenger of God?” He said: “Except for idhkhir.”"],[9517,"Regarding the doha tree: if it is cut in the sanctuary, a cow is due — meaning, if it is cut."],[9518,"He used to cut down the large tree from a wall that was in the valley of Mina, and the tree, and the salam tree, and pay a cow for every large tree."],[9519,"For the large tree, five dinars or six to be given as charity in Makkah."],[9520,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz forbade carrying horses into the land of India."],[9521,"He saw a man uprooting a samura tree and said: \"Its thorny trees are not to be cut.\""],[9523,"There is no harm in the siwak and sticks you take from the Haram. He said: \"And ʿAṭāʾ disliked it.\""],[9524,"There is no harm in pulling out the mays tree, the young palm shoots, and the siwak from the bashama tree in the Haram.\" He said: We do not think that by his saying 'its fresh herbage is not to be cut' he meant anything except for grazing animals. ʿAmr said: \"And the leaves of senna for walking; and by my life, if it is more effective from its root, it will be pulled out just as young palm shoots and the nahīs are pulled out from it. As for trade, then no.\""],[9525,"ʿAṭāʾ and ʿAmr disliked what grew on one's own water in the Haram from the trees of the Haram. ʿIkrima went back to ʿAṭāʾ and said: If what grows on my water in the Haram is forbidden to me, then my cotton will surely be forbidden to me, for strange plants grow in it, and greens and herbs grow in it, and then people would not be able to have their greens. He said: \"What grows on your water is permitted to you, even if you did not plant it yourself.\""],[9526,"ʿAṭāʾ disliked for me to bring a branch close to my camel or my sheep. He said: \"And I say you guaranteed it if you break it, and that is cutting fresh herbage.\""],[9527,"While he was delivering a sermon in Mina, he noticed a man from Yemen cutting a tree. He sent for him and said: \"What are you doing?\" He said: I am cutting fodder for my camel; I have no fodder. He said: \"Do you know where you are?\" He said: No. He said: So ʿUmar ordered provision for him."],[9528,"He disliked taking stones from the Haram to make loops for sacks to be tied on them."],[9529,"Mujāhid disliked taking anything out from the stones of the Haram."],[9530,"Do not cut the green plants of 'Urana and Namira."],[9531,"The Prophet ﷺ forbade cutting down trees, for they are a protection for the animals in times of drought."],[9532,"Do not cut down trees, for they are a safeguard for livestock in times of drought."],[9533,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz forbade carrying horses into the land of India."],[9534,"O people of Makkah, do not put doors on your houses, so that the desert dweller may alight wherever he wishes."],[9537,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz forbade carrying horses into the land of India."],[9538,"Tawus used to say: \"There is zakah on the property of a slave.\""],[9541,"It is not lawful to sell the houses of Makkah or to rent them."],[9542,"'A'ishah asked the Prophet ﷺ for permission to build a lavatory in Mina, but he did not grant her permission."],[9543,"Inscribed on the Station: 'The Sacred House of God in Makkah. The dwellings of its people are in water and meat. God has guaranteed the provision of its people. It is approached from three roads: the upper part of the valley, its lower part, and the mountain pass. The first to declare it sacred shall not make it lawful.'"],[9544,"Inscribed on the Station: 'The Sacred House of God is blessed for its people in meat and water. Upon God is the provision of its people from three roads. The first to declare it sacred shall not make it lawful.'"],[9545,"The wife of Isma'il said to Ibrahim—Ibn Jurayj said in his hadith—that she said to Ibrahim: 'Stop and we will feed you.' Ibrahim said: 'What is your food?' She said: 'Meat.' He said: 'What is your drink?' She said: 'Water.' He said: 'O God, bless them in meat and water.' He said: 'These two are such that no one outside Makkah can do without them without finding them unsuitable.'"],[9547,"It is written on the stone: 'I am Allah, Lord of Bakkah. I made it on the day I made the sun and the moon. I surrounded it with seven righteous angels. Blessed are its people in meat and milk, and the first of its people to make it lawful shall not make it lawful.' And he said: 'It shall not cease until the two Akhshab cease, and the two Akhshab are the two great mountains.'"],[9548,"It was found on a stone in Makkah: 'I am Allah, Lord of Bakkah. I made it on the day I made the sun and the moon. It shall not cease until the two Akhshab cease. I blessed its people in fat and the fat one. Its provision comes to it from three ways, and I surrounded it with seven righteous angels. The first to make it lawful for its people is its people.'"],[9549,"Selling food in Makkah is ilhad (a violation)."],[9550,"Ilhad in the Haram is wronging a servant or anything beyond that."],[9551,"Indeed, no shedder of blood, no trader in usury, and no spreader of malicious gossip shall dwell in it."],[9552,"I said to Ata: What does 'And whoever enters it is safe' mean? He said: 'Everything that enters it is safe.' He said: 'Even if he had shed blood in it?' He said: 'Unless he killed within the Haram, then he is killed within it.' He said: And he recited: '…near the Sacred Mosque until they fight you within it.' So if he killed elsewhere and then entered it, he is safe until he leaves it."],[9553,"Regarding His saying, 'was safe,' he said: 'Whoever kills or steals in the profane land, then enters the Haram, he is not to be sat with, spoken to, or given shelter, but he is to be sought until he leaves, then what he did is carried out upon him. If he kills or steals in the profane land and is brought into the Haram, and they want to carry out upon him what he did, they take him out of the Haram to the profane land and carry it out upon him. But if he kills or steals within the Haram, it is carried out upon him within the Haram.'"],[9554,"Ibn Abbas faulted Ibn al-Zubayr regarding a man who was seized in the profane land, then brought into the Haram, then taken out to the profane land and killed. He said: 'He brought him into the Haram and then took him out,' saying: 'He brought him in under protection.' The man had been suspected by Ibn al-Zubayr in some matter, and he had assisted Abd al-Malik against him. Ibn Abbas did not consider him deserving of execution. He said: Ibn al-Zubayr did not remain after that except a short while until he perished."],[9555,"If I found in it the killer of al-Khattab, I would not touch him until he leaves it."],[9556,"If I found in it the killer of Umar, I would not accost him."],[9557,"It reached us that Tubba marched to the Kaaba intending to destroy it, and rabbis of the Jews marched with him until, when they were at Marr or Sarif—and some scholars say he reached al-Tanim—darkness covered the land upon them. He summoned the rabbis and asked them, and they said: 'Have you resolved in yourself something concerning this House?' He said: 'Yes, I resolved to destroy it.' They said: 'That is why this darkness has come.' So Tubba made a covenant with Allah: if that darkness were lifted from him, he would venerate the Kaaba and clothe it. Then Allah lifted that darkness, and Tubba traveled until, when he reached the boundary stones of the Haram, he dismounted from his mount, then removed his sandals in reverence for the Haram and as repentance for what he had intended. He said: Then he entered Makkah on foot, barefoot, and circumambulated the House and clothed the Kaaba with the wasa'il [cloth], so it was covered with them. Then he unloaded his baggage and his kitchen in the mountain pass of Abdullah b. Amir b. Karim, and it was called al-Matabikh from that day to this day of the people. And he unloaded his weapons in the mountain pass of Abdullah b. al-Zubayr, and it was called Buqayqi'an from that day to this day of the people. And he unloaded his horses in the mountain pass of Banu Makhzum, and those two passes were called Ajyad al-Asghar and Ajyad al-Akbar to this day of the people. They mentioned that only two men from Hudhayl advised him to destroy the Kaaba. When Allah lifted that darkness, Tubba ordered the two of them, and they were taken out of the Haram and crucified. Some of our scholars claim that the first to clothe the Kaaba was Ismail the Prophet ﷺ, and Allah knows best about that."],[9559,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ went out on a journey, he would say: “O God, I seek refuge in You from the toil of travel, the gloom of return, from loss after gain, and from an evil sight in family and property.”"],[9560,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz forbade carrying horses into the land of India."],[9561,"They had known what they were being called to."],[9562,"Praise be to God who created me when I was not a thing mentioned. O God, help me against the terror of this world, the calamities of time, and the misfortunes of nights and days. O God, accompany me in my journey, and be my successor over my family. To You I turn, so guide me; and upon a sound character, so set me right. To You, O Lord, so make me beloved; and to the people, so do not entrust me to them. Lord of the weak, You are my Lord. I seek refuge in Your noble face, by which the light of the heavens and the earth shone, and by which You dispelled darkness and set right the affair of the first and the last, from You bringing down Your wrath upon me or sending down Your anger upon me. To You belongs the right to reproach me as much as I can bear. There is no power and no strength except in God.”"],[9563,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ returned from a journey and passed a hard, elevated ground or a hillock, he would say “God is greatest” three times, then say: “There is no god but God alone, with no partner; His is the dominion and His is the praise, and He has power over all things.” Then he would say: “Returning, repenting, worshipping, prostrating, praising our Lord. God has fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone.”"],[9564,"I accompanied Ibn ʿUmar on a journey. When dawn broke, he would raise his voice and say: “Let a hearer hear with praise of God, His mercy, and His good favour upon us. O God, accompany us and bestow Your bounty upon us, seeking refuge with You from the Fire.”"],[9565,"When it was the morning of a journey while he was travelling, he would say—I said it several times—: “Let a hearer hear with praise of God and His favour upon us. O God, accompany us and bestow Your bounty upon us, seeking refuge with God from Hell.”"],[9566,"When the Prophet ﷺ went out travelling for Hajj or ʿUmrah and passed a hard, elevated ground or a hillock, he would say “God is greatest” three times."],[9567,"On every camel’s hump there is a devil, so remember God as you have been commanded, then make use of them for yourselves, for God carries [you] on them."],[9568,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ returned from a journey, he would say: “Returning, repenting, worshipping, praising our Lord.”"],[9569,"When the Prophet ﷺ returned from a journey, he would say: “Returning, if God wills, repenting; if God wills, worshipping; if God wills, praising our Lord. O God, we seek refuge in You from the toil of travel, the gloom of return, and an evil sight in family and property.”"],[9570,"When they returned from Hajj or ʿUmrah, they would say: “Returning, if God wills, repenting, worshipping, prostrating, praising our Lord. God has fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone.”"],[9572,"We used to call the enemy, and we call."],[9573,"When the Prophet ﷺ and his armies ascended the mountain passes, they would say takbir, and when they descended, they would say tasbih. The prayer was established upon that."],[9574,"The people used to say takbir when they ascended the mountain passes, and when they descended, they would raise their voices very loudly. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, you are not calling upon one who is deaf or absent, but you are calling upon One who is Hearing and Seeing. Indeed, He is with you.” And he commanded them to be quiet."],[9575,"When you find abundant pasture, let the animals reach their humps, and do not pass by the stopping places. When you find barren land, then travel, and upon you is the night journey, for the earth is folded up by night. Do not alight upon the main roads, for they are the abode of snakes and beasts of prey. Beware of relieving yourselves upon them, for they are among the places of cursing. When the ghouls appear to you, then call the adhan."],[9576,"The ghouls were mentioned in the presence of Ibn Abbas, and he said: “That is a generation that has perished.”"],[9577,"The ghouls were mentioned in the presence of Umar, and he said: “Indeed, nothing changes from the form in which it was created, but among them are sorcerers from your sorcerers. So when you see anything of that, then call the adhan.”"],[9578,"Scatter from death and make the head two heads. Do not tarry in a land of incapacity. Make your night shelters sound, and frighten the vermin before they frighten you."],[9579,"Indeed, Allah is Gentle and loves gentleness, and is pleased with it, and aids upon it what He does not aid upon harshness. So when you ride these dumb beasts, alight with them at their resting places. If the land is barren, then escape upon them with their marrow. Upon you is traveling by night, for the earth is folded up by night what is not folded up by day. Beware of making camp for the night on the road, for it is the path of beasts and the abode of snakes."],[9580,"When the ghouls appear to you, then call the adhan."],[9581,"When one of you buys a camel, let him buy one that is tall and large, for if its goodness misses him, its market value will not miss him. Do not clothe your women with the fine Egyptian linen (qabati), for if it does not reveal, it describes. Make your night shelters sound, and frighten the vermin before they frighten you, for no Muslim appears from them."],[9582,"If the people knew what Allah provides for carrying the weak, they would not be extravagant in their mounts."],[9583,"Travel is only a portion of torment; it prevents one of you from his food and drink. So when one of you has fulfilled his need from his journey, let him hasten the return to his family."],[9584,"Abu Salamah b. 'Abd al-Rahman and Sa'id b. Jubayr met. Sa'id said to Abu Salamah, 'Narrate, for we will follow you.' Abu Salamah said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'When there are three on a journey, let the one among them most versed in the Qur'an lead them, even if he is the youngest of them. If he leads them, he is their commander.' Abu Salamah said: 'That is a commander whom the Messenger of God ﷺ appointed.'"],[9585,"When you set out as a traveler, pray two rak'ahs in your house; and when you come from your journey, pray two rak'ahs in your house."],[9586,"When he arrived from a journey, he would pray two rak'ahs in the mosque."],[9587,"He arrived from a journey and prayed two rak'ahs on a carpet in his house."],[9588,"Whoever halts at a place and says: 'I seek refuge in the complete words of God, all of them, from the evil of what He has created,' nothing will harm him until he departs from it."],[9589,"9355 9261 - 'Abd al-Razzaq said: As for Malik, he mentioned it from Ya'qub b. 'Abd Allah b. al-Ashajj, from Busr b. Sa'id, from Sa'd, from Khawlah bt. Hakim, from the Prophet ﷺ similarly. Hidden text: Whoever halts at a place and says: 'I seek refuge in the complete words of God, all of them, from the evil of what He has created,' nothing will harm him until he departs from it."],[9590,"Whoever recites this verse: 'Praise be to God, who has not taken a son and has no partner in dominion' to the end of the surah, theft will not befall him. He said: I heard my father, when he alighted at a place, say while still on his mount: 'We have alighted at the best stopping place, and its best for one alighting, by praise of the Lord of caravans. Its most righteous and most God-fearing, its most filling and most quenching.' And he would keep saying it until he finished unloading."],[9591,"When we alighted at a place, we would not cease glorifying God until the saddles were untied."],[9592,"On every camel’s hump there is a devil, so remember God as you have been commanded, then make use of them for yourselves, for God carries [you] on them."],[9594,"It has reached me that some people were on a journey, and they would not pray in congregation nor dismount at the stopping place. Then their eyes were blinded, and al-Khidr ﷺ appeared to them. They told him of their situation, so he supplicated for them and God restored their sight to them."],[9595,"He says to the pilgrim when he arrives: 'May Allah magnify your reward—or make your reward great—and accept your rites, and replace your expenditure for you.'"],[9596,"When you are three on a journey, appoint one of you as leader. When you pass by a shepherd, call out three times; if anyone answers you, then fine, otherwise dismount, untie, milk, and drink, then tie up again."],[9597,"Travel; you will be healthy and provided for."],[9598,"He used to prefer to set out on Thursday when he wanted to travel."],[9610,"A hired worker has no share."],[9613,"I dislike selling the fifth until it is divided."],[9635,"If a man has only a house and a servant, he may take zakat. He said: Our companions say that. And al-Hasan did not consider pilgrimage obligatory for one who has only a house and a servant."],[9638,"I am the group of every Muslim."],[9645,"If the apostate repents, they deem it preferable for him to start anew with a Hajj, if he had performed Hajj before his apostasy."],[9655,"Were it not that some of the believers would be unwilling to stay behind me, and I do not find what I can mount them on, no expedition would go out raiding in the path of God without me being with them. By God, I would love to be killed in the path of God, then brought back to life, then killed, then brought back to life, then killed."],[9694,"Every piece of skin is removed from the martyr."],[9729,"The Prophet ﷺ performed Hajj with his wives on the Farewell Hajj, then said: “This is only it, then the backs of the mats,” meaning “Stay upon the backs of the mats in your homes.”"],[9781,"Pray them wherever you wish as long as you have not left the Haram."],[9801,"I prayed with the Messenger of God ﷺ the noon prayer in Madinah four [rakʿahs], and the afternoon prayer at Dhu al-Hulayfah two [rakʿahs]."],[9922,"Ibn Abbas said to me: Whatever you disobey me in concerning anything, do not disobey me in three things: when you go out as a traveler, pray two rak'ahs until you return to your family; do not fast until you return to your home; and do not enter Makkah except in a state of ihram."],[9948,"A raid in the sea is better than ten raids on land. Whoever crosses the sea is as if he crossed the valleys, and the one who suffers seasickness in the ship is like the one who wallows in his blood."],[9954,"It reached Umar b. al-Khattab that his governors were taking jizyah from wine, so he adjured them three times. Bilal said: \"They indeed do that.\" He said: \"Do not do that, but put them in charge of selling it; for the Jews were forbidden fats, yet they sold them and consumed their price.\""],[9955,"When a man dies leaving his son a slave or a Christian, and the son is then manumitted, if the inheritance has not been divided, then it is his—meaning he inherits."],[9958,"A Muslim may bequeath to a disbeliever."],[9961,"Do not follow their funerals, even if there is kinship between you and them."],[9986,"Ali came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “This misguided old man—meaning Abu Talib—has died!” He said: “Wash him, then perform ghusl as you would for major ritual impurity, then bury him.” He said: “I will not do it.” He said: “Then command someone else.”"],[9995,"I said to Ata: Can a Muslim slave be sold to a disbeliever? He said: No, as a matter of opinion. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr b. Dinar said to me: No, as a matter of opinion."],[9996,"The Prophet ﷺ handed over Khaybar to the Jews on condition that they work it and have half of its fruit. The Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and the early part of Umar's caliphate continued on that basis. Then Umar was informed that the Prophet ﷺ said during the illness of which he died: “Two religions shall not coexist in the land of the Arabs,” or he said: “in the land of the Hijaz.” So he investigated that until he found confirmation of it, then he summoned them and said: “Whoever has a covenant from the Messenger of God ﷺ, let him bring it; otherwise, I will expel you!” So Umar expelled them."],[10001,"There is no hadd punishment upon him."],[10011,"There is no hadd punishment upon him."],[10014,"There is no hadd punishment upon him."],[10016,"There is no harm in their slaughtered animals."],[10031,"There is no harm in combining four from the People of the Book."],[10032,"A woman from the People of the Book is not married except in a covenant."],[10041,"He has more right to her as long as he does not take her out of the land of her emigration."],[10042,"He has more right to her as long as he does not take her out of the land of her emigration."],[10056,"A condition on them: a day and a night of hospitality."],[10072,"Umar b. al-Khattab sent Ammar b. Yasir, Abdullah b. Mas'ud, and Uthman b. Hunayf to Kufa. He put Ammar in charge of prayer and fighting, put Abdullah b. Mas'ud in charge of judiciary and the treasury, and put Uthman b. Hunayf in charge of land surveying. He assigned them each day a sheep: half of it and its offal for Ammar, a quarter for Ibn Mas'ud, and a quarter for Uthman b. Hunayf. Then he said: \"I do not think any village from which a sheep is taken every day will not quickly be ruined.\" Then he said to them: \"I have placed you and myself with regard to this wealth like the guardian of an orphan; whoever is rich should abstain, and whoever is poor may eat in a reasonable manner.\" He said: Uthman distributed to every head of the dhimmis twenty-four dirhams every year, and he did not impose anything of that on women and children. He surveyed the sawad of Kufa from the land of the dhimmis, and he imposed on a jarib of palm trees ten dirhams, on a jarib of grapes eight dirhams, on a jarib of sugar cane six dirhams, on a jarib of wheat four dirhams, and on a jarib of barley two dirhams. He took from the merchants of the dhimmis one dirham for every twenty dirhams, and he reported that to Umar, who approved of it."],[10073,"His inheritance belongs to the people of his religion."],[10082,"There is no hadd punishment upon him."],[10087,"There is no hadd punishment upon him."],[10101,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: 'Is raiding obligatory upon all the people?' He and ʿAmr b. Dīnār said: 'We do not know.'"],[10102,"That raiding is obligatory upon all the people? He was silent; he knew that if he disapproved of what I said he would have clarified it for me. I said to Ibn al-Musayyib: 'I prepared myself; nothing stirred me except that until I was stationed at a frontier post.' He said: 'It has sufficed for you.'"],[10103,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'I am a coward; I cannot bear meeting the enemy.' He said: 'Shall I not guide you to a struggle in which there is no fighting?' He said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Upon you is Hajj and ʿUmrah.'"],[10104,"I was informed that the Prophet ﷺ, then he mentioned the like of Maʿmar's hadith. Hidden text: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'I am a coward; I cannot bear meeting the enemy.' He said: 'Shall I not guide you to a struggle in which there is no fighting?' He said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Upon you is Hajj and ʿUmrah.'"],[10105,"There is no household from which a warrior does not go out, or they equip a warrior, or they look after his family in his absence, but Allah will strike them with a calamity before death."],[10106,"He lied. Upon you are three journeys: he lied. Upon you are Hajj, ʿUmrah, and jihad in the path of Allah, and that a man seeks profit from his surplus wealth; the spender and the giver of charity. He says: Upon you are Hajj, ʿUmrah, and jihad."],[10107,"He lied. Upon you are Hajj and ʿUmrah. He says: Upon you are Hajj and jihad."],[10108,"Upon you is jihad in the path of Allah, for it is a gate among the gates of Paradise; through it Allah removes deceit and worry."],[10109,"I was sitting with Ibn ʿUmar when a young man came to him and said, 'Will you not engage in jihād?' He remained silent and turned away from him. Then the man repeated, and he remained silent and turned away from him. Then he asked him again, and Ibn ʿUmar said, 'Islam is built upon four pillars: establishing the prayer, giving the zakāh—do not separate the two—fasting Ramaḍān, and pilgrimage to the House for whoever can find a way. Jihād and charity are part of good deeds.'"],[10110,"Islam is built on eight shares: bearing witness that there is no god but God and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of God, establishing prayer, giving zakat, fasting Ramadan, performing Hajj, enjoining good, and forbidding evil. He who has no share has indeed failed."],[10111,"He would face the qiblah, then swear ten oaths that military raiding is obligatory upon you, then say: \"If you wish, I will add more for you.\""],[10112,"When you set down the saddles, then set out for Hajj and Umrah, for it is one of the two jihads."],[10113,"A man asked the Prophet ﷺ about jihad, and he said: “Shall I not guide you to a jihad that has no fighting in it? Hajj.”"],[10115,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I have come to pledge allegiance to you for emigration, and I left my parents weeping. He said: Then return to them and make them laugh as you made them weep."],[10116,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I want to go on jihad. He said: \"Do you have a mother?\" He said: Yes. He said: \"Then stay with her.\""],[10117,"The Prophet ﷺ dispatched a military expedition, and with him was a young man whom he used to hold by the hand when he stood. The young man asked him to send him on the expedition, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him: Have you left anyone elderly among your family? He said: No, only young children. He said: \"Then return to them, for in them there is a good struggle.\""],[10118,"Regarding parents, if they permit going on a military expedition, he said: \"If you think they prefer you to stay, then stay.\" He was asked what dutifulness to parents is, and he said: \"To give them what you own and to obey them in what they command you, unless it involves disobedience (to God).\""],[10120,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, I want to go on a military expedition, and I have come to consult you. He said: \"Do you have a mother?\" He said: Yes. He said: \"Stay with her, for Paradise is at her feet.\" Then he repeated this a second and a third time."],[10121,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade his companions to fight from one side of a mountain. The men turned away from them, but one man remained and fought them. They shot him and killed him. He was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"After we forbade fighting?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He left him and did not pray over him."],[10122,"I heard a man when we were defeated at al-Jamājim, and we mentioned him to our companions. They said: \"This is al-Maʿrūr b. Suwayd.\" He said: \"A man was mentioned to ʿUmar who had gone out from the battle line and was killed. ʿUmar said: 'That I die on my bed is better for me than that I fight in front of a battle line.'\""],[10124,"The harshest hadith we heard from the Prophet ﷺ,\" he said, \"was his statement about Saʿd b. Muʿādh, and his statement about the matter of the grave. When it was the expedition of Tabūk, he said: 'No one shall go out with us except a man who is strong.'\" He said: \"A man went out on a difficult young camel of his, and it threw him down, and he died. The people said: 'A martyr, a martyr.' The Prophet ﷺ ordered Bilāl to call out among the people: 'A disobedient one will not enter Paradise.'\""],[10125,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said to us on the day of Badr: \"Whoever sees the polytheists, if they come close to you, shoot them with arrows, and do not draw swords until they overwhelm you.\""],[10126,"He said to his companions one day while facing the enemy: \"Let none of you fight.\" One of them then deliberately shot at the enemy and fought them, and they killed him. It was said to the Prophet ﷺ: \"So-and-so has been martyred.\" He said: \"After I forbade fighting?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"A disobedient one will not enter Paradise.\""],[10127,"Food is not to be taken in enemy territory except with the permission of the imam.\" Al-Zuhrī said: \"If the imam permits him and he takes something from it and sells it for gold or silver, then the fifth is due on it.\""],[10128,"Concerning taking food in enemy territory, he said: \"They used to grant them license regarding food and fodder as long as they did not make it into property.\""],[10129,"These people want to make me slip away from my religion. By God, I will die while I am upon my religion. What is sold from it for gold or silver, whether food or other than that, then upon it is the fifth of God and the shares of the Muslims.\""],[10130,"Salmān was brought a basket containing bread and cheese,\" meaning also property. He said: \"He took away the property and ate the bread and cheese.\""],[10132,"It used to be said: When they are in enemy territory, they eat; when they bring it to the land of the Muslims, they hand it over to the imam. They should not sell it in enemy territory. If they sell it for gold or silver, then the fifth is due on it."],[10133,"I said to Sulayman b. Musa: A man carried food to his family. He said: There is no harm in that."],[10135,"I said to al-Hasan: What did you used to obtain on the road? He said: Straw and firewood. He said: I said: A man who passes by fruits? He said: He may eat but not carry."],[10136,"Food does not remain in enemy territory, and the commander is not asked permission for it. Whoever reaches it first takes it, unless the commander forbids it, in which case it is left because of his prohibition. If he sells any of the food for silver or gold, it is not lawful for him; at that point it is from the spoils. He said: This is the Sunnah and the truth in our view."],[10137,"That he asked Ibn al-Musayyib and al-Qasim b. Muhammad about what a raiding party finds in Byzantine grain silos. He said: As for gold, silver, clothes, and food, it is put into the spoils. As for whatever food there is, even if abundant—oil, ghee, or honey—it belongs to that raiding party, to the exclusion of the army. They eat and give gifts, but do not sell."],[10138,"That he said to al-Hasan: Should a man charge at the enemy or stay in the rank? He said: Rather, he should stay in the rank, and when they advance, advance with them. He said: And al-Hasan said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to a man: Stay in the rank, and when the Muslims charge, charge with them."],[10140,"That a man was in the expedition of Khaybar with the Prophet ﷺ while the spoils were before him. The Prophet ﷺ said: Give me this for a ball of spun thread—I will bind a bone of my leg with it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: As for my share of it, it is yours."],[10141,"The commander may not give away any of the spoils without the owner's permission, except what he allocates to a guide or a shepherd."],[10142,"That Anas was with Ubayd Allah b. Abi Bakra in an expedition he undertook, and they captured captives. He wanted to give him some of the captives before they were divided. Anas said: No, but divide and give me from the fifth. Ubayd Allah said: No, except from all the spoils. Anas refused to accept from him, and Ubayd Allah refused to give him anything from the fifth."],[10143,"Horses raided in Syria. The thoroughbreds caught up the same day, and the workhorses caught up by mid-morning of the following day. Al-Mundhir b. Abi Hamza al-Hamdani, who was in command of the people, said: I will not make the share of one who caught up like one who did not. He wrote about that to Umar b. al-Khattab, and Umar wrote back: May his mother be bereft of the Wadi'i—he has achieved it. Carry it out as he said."],[10145,"There is no share except for two horses. If a man has several horses with him, then two horses get four shares, and the man gets one share. The shares of horses and barādhīn are equal."],[10146,"There is no share from the horses except for two horses, even if he has a thousand horses with him when he enters enemy territory."],[10147,"He was allotted shares during the governorship of Saʿīd b. ʿUthmān: two shares for his two horses, and one share for himself."],[10149,"The Messenger of God ﷺ gave the Arabian horse two shares and its rider one share on the day of Khaybar."],[10150,"The Messenger of God ﷺ gave the cavalryman two shares and the infantryman one share."],[10151,"If a man brings several horses, each horse gets two shares. I said: Even if the enemy fought over them? He said: Yes."],[10152,"It has reached me that he gave the muqrif horse one share and the infantrymen one share."],[10153,"The Prophet ﷺ divided shares for thirty-six horses on the day of al-Naḍīr, two shares for each horse, and he divided on the day of Khaybar for two hundred horses, two shares for each horse."],[10154,"Al-Zubayr attended Khaybar with two horses, and the Prophet ﷺ gave him five shares."],[10155,"Abū Mūsā wrote to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb that there had been death and hardship among the purebred horses, and that after them there were things that did not reach the levels of the purebreds—barādhīn and the like—and he would like to hear his opinion about them. ʿUmar wrote back to him: Give the Arabian horse two shares, the muqrif one share, and the mule one share."],[10156,"If a man's child is born after he leaves the land of the Muslims and the land of treaty, then that newborn has a share. He said: And they named the man for whom the Prophet ﷺ ruled in favor of his child."],[10157,"If a man dies after entering enemy territory, and after leaving the territory of the Muslims and the territory of the peace treaty, his share goes to his family."],[10158,"The Jews used to raid with the Prophet ﷺ, and he would allot them shares like the shares of the Muslims."],[10160,"I asked him about the polytheists who raid with the Muslims: What do they get along with the Muslims? He said to them: \"Whatever they agreed upon. If it was said to you such-and-such, then that is for them.\""],[10161,"I was present with the Messenger of God ﷺ when he gave as bonus spoil a third."],[10162,"Habib b. Maslama — who was ill — used to give the raiding parties a third as bonus spoil after the fifth, when setting out."],[10163,"The Prophet ﷺ gave as bonus spoil a third after the fifth."],[10164,"He used to give a quarter as bonus spoil at the outset, and a third upon return."],[10165,"We were on a raiding party, and our shares reached eleven camels for each man among us. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ gave each of us an extra camel after that."],[10166,"The Messenger of God ﷺ sent a raiding party toward Najd, and I was among them. We captured many camels, and our shares reached eleven camels for each man among us. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ gave each person an extra camel after that."],[10167,"People used to be given as bonus spoil more than a third, until it was the time of Umar b. Abd al-Aziz. He wrote that \"it has not reached us that the Prophet ﷺ gave as bonus spoil more than a third.\" After that, it continued to be practiced accordingly."],[10168,"If a raiding party goes out with the commander's permission, whatever they capture, the imam takes a fifth of it, and the rest belongs to that raiding party. If they go out without his permission, the imam takes a fifth of it, and the rest is shared among the entire army."],[10169,"There is no harm in their slaughtered animals."],[10170,"The army is reinforced by the raiding parties, and the raiding parties are reinforced by the army."],[10171,"There is no nafl in the spoils of the Muslims except from the fifth of the fifth."],[10175,"No nafl until the fifth is divided, and no nafl until the first of the booty is divided, in the Book of God Most High, among the believers."],[10177,"There is no nafl in the first thing taken from the spoils. He said: \"That is known; it has been practiced in the past until today.\""],[10178,"What the polytheists have seized, then the Muslims captured it, it is theirs as long as it is not a free person or a muʿāhid; it is not returned to its owner."],[10179,"Regarding a Christian woman who is under a Christian man and she becomes Muslim before he has consummated the marriage with her, he said: \"She separates from him, and she gets no dower.\""],[10180,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: Property that the enemy takes from the Muslims, then God restores it to them? He said: \"If no precedent has been established regarding it, returning it to him is preferable, as long as it has not been divided. If it has been divided, then he has no right to it.\""],[10181,"We heard that whatever the enemy seizes belongs to the Muslims; they divide it among themselves."],[10182,"Abdullah b. Umar's horse was taken by the enemy. When the enemy was defeated, Khalid b. al-Walid found his horse and returned it to Abdullah b. Umar."],[10183,"A slave of mine ran away on the day of al-Yarmuk, then the Muslims captured him, and they returned him to me."],[10184,"Whatever is recognized before it is divided, he returns it to its owner. Whatever is not recognized until the shares have been allotted, they do not return it."],[10186,"It is returned if it is recognized before or after the division."],[10188,"The enemy captured the she-camel of a Muslim man, and a Muslim man bought it from the enemy. Its owner recognized it and established clear proof for it. They brought their dispute to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ ruled that he should pay to the buyer the price he had paid for it to the enemy; otherwise, he should leave it to the buyer."],[10189,"Whatever the polytheists take from the property of the Muslims, and then the Muslims later capture it: if its owner finds it before the shares of the Muslims are allotted for it, he has a greater right to it. If the shares of the Muslims have been allotted for it, there is no way for him to it except by paying its value."],[10190,"Two men brought a dispute before Shurayh concerning a female slave who had been taken captive from the Muslims, and then a man had bought her from the enemy. Shurayh said, \"The most entitled to return it to the Muslim is his brother.\" The other said, \"She has become pregnant from me.\" Shurayh said, \"Set her free; it is the ruling of the Commander—meaning Umar b. al-Khattab.\""],[10191,"Regarding a Christian woman who is under a Christian man and she becomes Muslim before he has consummated the marriage with her? He said: \"They are to be separated, and there is no dowry.\""],[10192,"A mukatab (slave with a contract of manumission) whom the enemy had captured, and then a man bought him. Bakr b. Qirwash asked Ali about him, and Ali ﷺ said, \"What do you say about it, O Bakr b. Qirwash?\" He said, \"Allah knows best.\" Ali said, \"I am the servant of Allah and the cousin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. If his master ransoms him, then he remains on the remainder of his contract. If his master refuses to ransom him, then he belongs to the one who bought him.\""],[10193,"When the enemy seizes any of the Muslims' property, it belongs to its owner as long as it has not been divided. If they divide it, its owner is more entitled to its price."],[10195,"Regarding a man who finds his commodity in the possession of another man and says: 'Did you buy it from the enemy?' He said: 'If he bought it with proof, its owner takes it in exchange for the price. If he establishes proof of the purchase but does not know the price, the word of the purchaser is accepted.'"],[10196,"Regarding the polytheist: 'If he takes something of the Muslims' property, then sells it before securing it to the land of polytheism, his sale is void; its owner may take it wherever he finds it.'"],[10197,"I said to 'Ata': 'Free women whom the enemy captured and a man purchased them—may he have intercourse with them?' He said: 'He must not enslave them, but he may free them in exchange for what he paid for them, without increasing anything over them.' He said: 'Abd al-Karim said the same about this: if they are from the people of the covenant, it is also like that.'"],[10198,"Regarding a free man whom the enemy captures and then the Muslims purchase, it is similar to his saying about the women.' And 'Amr b. Dinar said the same."],[10199,"'Ata' was asked about a Muslim whom the enemy captures and who then kills a Muslim there, or commits adultery there, and afterward the Muslims capture him. He said: 'I do not think he is liable for anything he committed there.'"],[10200,"'Umar b. al-Khattab wrote: 'The commander of the army or the commander of a raiding party must not impose a hadd punishment on any Muslim until he emerges from the mountain pass on his return, for I fear that partisanship might carry him to join the polytheists.'"],[10201,"Shurahbil b. al-Simt was in command of an army and said to his troops: 'You have camped in a land with many women and much drink—meaning wine—so whoever among you commits an offense deserving a hadd punishment, let him come to us so that we may purify him.' Some people came to him, and this reached 'Umar b. al-Khattab, who wrote to him: 'Are you—may you have no mother—the one who commands the people to tear apart the veil of Allah with which He covered them?'"],[10202,"The commander of the army—who was al-Walid b. 'Uqba—drank and became drunk. The people said to Abu Mas'ud and Hudhayfa b. al-Yaman: 'Carry out the hadd punishment on him.' They said: 'We will not do it while we are facing the enemy; we dislike that they learn of it, for it would be boldness from them against us and weakness on our part.'"],[10203,"A Muslim man stole a horse and entered Byzantine territory, then returned with it among the Muslims. They wanted to amputate his hand, but 'Ali b. Abi Talib said: 'Do not amputate until he leaves Byzantine territory.'"],[10205,"Abu Bakr al-Siddiq sent armies to Syria and sent commanders, then he sent Yazid b. Abi Sufyan and said to him while he was walking: 'Either you ride or I dismount.' Abu Bakr, ؓ , said: 'I will not ride and you will not dismount. I have accounted my steps in the path of Allah.' At that time Yazid was in command of a quarter of the quarters. He said: 'You will find a people who claim that they have confined themselves to Allah, so leave them and that which they claim to have confined themselves to for Him. You will find a people who have shaved the middle of their heads of hair and left locks like headbands, so strike with the sword what they have shaved. I counsel you with ten things: do not kill a woman, nor a child, nor an elder; do not hamstring a palm tree nor burn it; do not be cowardly; and do not steal from the spoils. Those who have shaved their heads are the monks, and those who have confined themselves are those in the monasteries.'"],[10206,"Abu Bakr accompanied Yazid b. Abi Sufyan to Syria and said to him while he was walking: 'Either you ride or I dismount.' Abu Bakr, ؓ , said: 'I will not ride and you will not dismount. I have accounted my steps in the path of Allah.' At that time Yazid was in command of a quarter of the quarters. He said: 'You will find a people who claim that they have confined themselves to Allah, so leave them and that which they claim to have confined themselves to for Him. You will find a people who have shaved the middle of their heads of hair and left locks like headbands, so strike with the sword what they have shaved. I counsel you with ten things: do not kill a woman, nor a child, nor an elder; do not hamstring a palm tree nor burn it; do not be cowardly; and do not steal from the spoils. Those who have shaved their heads are the monks, and those who have confined themselves are those in the monasteries.'"],[10207,"When Abu Bakr sent his armies to Syria, he would say: 'You will find a people who have shaved their heads with swords, and you will find a people who have confined themselves in monasteries, so leave them with their sins.'"],[10208,"When Abu Bakr sent his armies to Syria, he would say: 'You will find a people who have shaved their heads with swords, and you will find a people who have confined themselves in monasteries, so leave them with their sins.'"],[10209,"He forbade killing the servants and the hired laborers; the hired laborer is the employee."],[10211,"The Prophet ﷺ forbade cutting down trees, for they are a protection for the animals in times of drought."],[10212,"We raided with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we passed by a woman who had been killed, and people were gathered around her, so they made way for the Prophet ﷺ. He said: 'She was not one to fight.' Then he said: 'Go and catch up with Khalid and tell him: Do not kill a child nor a hired laborer.'"],[10213,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by a slain woman on the day of Hunayn and said: 'Did I not forbid this?' A man said: 'I made her ride behind me, and she wanted to kill me, so I killed her.' The Prophet ﷺ ordered that she be buried."],[10214,"The army is reinforced by the raiding parties, and the raiding parties are reinforced by the army."],[10215,"I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we strike in night raids and hit the children of the polytheists.' He said: 'They are from them.'"],[10216,"The Prophet ﷺ sent an expedition to Khaybar, and the killing reached the children. That reached the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: 'What makes you kill the children?' They said: 'Are they not the children of the polytheists?' He said: 'Are not the best of you the children of the polytheists?' Then he addressed us and said: 'Indeed, every newborn is born upon the natural disposition until his tongue expresses it for him.'"],[10217,"He made a night raid on one of the enemies at night."],[10218,"Ka'b b. al-Ashraf used to satirize the Prophet ﷺ and harm him. So the Prophet ﷺ ordered Sa'd b. Mu'adh to send five men to him. They brought him while he was in the assembly of his people at al-'Awali. When he saw them, he was startled by them and said, 'What has brought you?' They said, 'We have come to you for a need.' He said, 'Let one of you come close and tell me his need.' One of them came close to him, and they said, 'We have come to pledge allegiance to you for armor we have.' He said, 'By Allah, if you do, you have been hard-pressed since this man settled among you'—or he said, 'with you.' They made an appointment with him to come after the night had grown still. They came to him, and he rose to meet them. His wife said, 'What brings these people at this hour with anything you would like?' He said, 'They have told me of their need.' When he drew near to them, Abu 'Abs embraced him, and Muhammad b. Maslama came upon him with the sword and stabbed him in his side with his dagger, and they killed him. When morning came, the Jews went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'Our companion has been killed by treachery.' The Prophet ﷺ reminded them of how he had been satirizing him in his poetry and harming him. He said, 'Then the Prophet ﷺ invited them to write a pact between him and them.'—He said, 'I think he said: And that document is with 'Ali.' Al-Zuhri or someone else said: A man among those who claimed Islam said to Abu 'Abs, 'You killed Ka'b by treachery.' Abu 'Abs swore that he would never see him able to kill him but that he would kill him. So whenever he saw him, he would run after him until the other man eluded him."],[10219,"He disliked killing the polytheists patiently and would recite: 'So tighten their bonds, and thereafter either grace or ransom' (Q 47:4). He said, 'And I say: Then it was abrogated by: \"So seize them and kill them wherever you find them\" (Q 4:89). And it was revealed—they claimed—specifically about the Arabs.' The Prophet ﷺ killed 'Uqba b. Abi Mu'ayt on the day of Badr patiently."],[10220,"The Prophet ﷺ crucified 'Uqba b. Abi Mu'ayt on a tree and said, 'Am I among Quraysh?' He said, 'Yes.' He said, 'Then who will care for the children?' He said, 'The Fire.'"],[10221,"He wrote to him about the commander, saying, 'Give me such and such for him.' He said, 'Kill him. Killing a man from the polytheists is dearer to me than such and such.'"],[10222,"I never saw 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz kill a captive at all, except one from the Turks. He said, 'Captives from the Turks were brought.' He said, 'He ordered that they be enslaved.' A man among those who brought them said, 'O Commander of the Faithful, if you had seen this one of them killing among the Muslims, you would have wept much for them.' He said, 'Then you—go and kill him.' He said, 'So he arose and killed him.'"],[10224,"The Prophet ﷺ ransomed the captives of Badr, and the ransom of each one of them was four thousand. He killed 'Uqba b. Abi Mu'ayt before the ransom. 'Ali b. Abi Talib rose to him and killed him patiently. He said, 'Who will care for the children, O Muhammad?' He said, 'The Fire.'"],[10225,"Banu 'Amir had captured two men from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ captured a man from Thaqif, and they took a she-camel on which the pilgrims used to race. The Prophet ﷺ passed by him while he was bound, and he said, 'O Muhammad, O Muhammad!' So he turned to him, and he said, 'For what am I being held and the racing camel of the pilgrims taken?' He said, 'For the crime of your allies from Banu 'Amir.' Banu 'Amir were allies of Thaqif. Then the Prophet ﷺ went on, and he called him again, 'O Muhammad!' He answered him, and he said, 'I am a Muslim.' He said, 'If you had said that while you had control of your affair, you would have prospered completely.' He said, 'Then the Prophet ﷺ went on, and he called him again, so he returned to him. He said, 'Feed me, for I am hungry.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'This is your need.' He ordered food for him. Then the Prophet ﷺ ransomed the man for the two men who had been captured from his companions. He said, 'Some people raided an area of Madinah and seized a she-camel and also seized a woman, and they took them to their camp. The woman arose during part of the night to their camels, and they would let them rest near their courtyards. Every time she approached a camel to mount it, it bellowed, until she came to the she-camel of the Prophet ﷺ, which was a docile camel, and it did not bellow. She sat on its hindquarters, then urged it on. The people became aware of her and rode in pursuit of her. She made a vow while fleeing, with them following her: if Allah delivered her on it, she would certainly slaughter it. She escaped, and when she arrived in Madinah, the Prophet ﷺ was told, 'This is your she-camel; so-and-so came on it; Allah delivered her on it.' The Prophet ﷺ brought the woman and asked her, 'What did you do?' She told him: 'I vowed while they were pursuing me that if Allah delivered me on it, I would slaughter it.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'What a bad reward you would give it, then. There is no fulfillment of a vow in disobedience to Allah, nor in what the son of Adam does not own.'"],[10226,"A captive was taken, and the Prophet ﷺ ordered him to be killed. He said, 'I am a Muslim.' The Prophet ﷺ was informed, so he left him and said, 'Among you are men whom I entrust to their faith; among them is Furat b. Hayyan.'"],[10227,"I was with Mujahid on a military expedition. A captive belonging to a man among us ran away, and a man pursued him and killed him. Mujahid disapproved of that."],[10228,"The Prophet ﷺ said to the captives of Badr: 'None of you shall be killed except with the stroke of one man or by ransom.'"],[10229,"From Muti' b. al-Aswad—whose name was al-'As, and the Prophet ﷺ named him Muti'—he said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said on the Day of the Conquest: 'No Qurayshi shall be killed patiently [i.e., in captivity] after today.'"],[10230,"The Prophet ﷺ said concerning the captives of Badr: 'If al-Mut'im b. 'Adi were alive and spoke to me about these stinking ones, I would let them go.'"],[10231,"When the Prophet ﷺ took the captives of Badr, Abu Wada'a b. Subara al-Sahmi was among them. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'He has a clever son in Makkah; he is al-Muttalib b. Abi Wada'a.' He was the first to come with his father's ransom."],[10232,"Gabriel ﷺ descended upon the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Badr and said: 'Your Lord gives you the choice: if you wish, kill these captives; if you wish, ransom them—and a like number of your companions will be killed.' He consulted his companions, and they said: 'We will ransom them, gain strength from them, and God will honor with martyrdom whomever He wills.'"],[10233,"Two things the Messenger of God ﷺ did: his permitting the hypocrites, and his taking [ransom] from the captives."],[10234,"I said to Mujahid: 'It has reached me that Ibn 'Abbas said: \"Captives are not lawful, because God, Blessed and Exalted, said: 'Thereafter either grace or ransom until the war lays down its burdens' (Q 47:4).\"' Mujahid said: 'He pays no attention to this at all. I met the companions of Muhammad ﷺ; all of them rejected this and said: \"This is abrogated. It was only during the period [of truce] between the Prophet of God ﷺ and the polytheists. But today, [it is] according to God's word: 'Kill the polytheists wherever you find them' (Q 9:5). If they are from the Arab polytheists, nothing is accepted from them except Islam; if they refuse, they are killed. As for others, when they are captured, the Muslims have a choice: if they wish, they kill; if they wish, they spare them; and if they wish, they ransom them, as long as they do not change from their religion. But if they profess Islam, they are not ransomed.\"'"],[10235,"Regarding His statement: 'Thereafter either grace or ransom' (Q 47:4), they said: 'It was abrogated by the verse \"Kill the polytheists\" (Q 9:5).' Al-Suddi also said this."],[10236,"The Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Badr gave each man among his companions the captive whom he had captured, and he himself would ransom him."],[10237,"Suhayl b. 'Amr bore [responsibility for] the ransom of the Badr captives, and the Prophet ﷺ bore [the task of] informing him of what Quraysh intended in his expedition. He ransomed Abu Wada'a for four thousand."],[10238,"Carrying weapons into enemy territory was disliked. I said: 'Are horses taken to them?' He refused that and said: 'As for what they use to strengthen themselves in fighting, it is not carried to them; other than that, there is no harm.' 'Amr b. Dinar also said this."],[10242,"Khalid b. al-Walid burned some people from the apostates. Umar said to Abu Bakr: \"Will you leave this one who tortures with the punishment of Allah?\" Abu Bakr said: \"I will not sheathe a sword Allah has drawn against the polytheists.\""],[10243,"Ali killed a people who disbelieved after their Islam and burned them with fire. That reached Ibn Abbas, and he said: \"If it were I, I would have killed them and not burned them, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever changes—or he said: whoever reverts from—his religion, kill him, and do not torture with the punishment of Allah,' meaning fire.\" He said: \"Ibn Abbas's statement reached Ali, and he said: 'Woe to Ibn Abbas!'\""],[10244,"We were with the Prophet ﷺ and passed by a village of ants that had been burned. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"It is not fitting for a human to torture with the punishment of Allah.\""],[10246,"He disliked letting the garment hang down."],[10247,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent out a raiding party and said: \"If you seize Habbar b. al-Aswad, place him between two branches of firewood, then cast fire into it.\" Then he said: \"Glory be to Allah! It is not fitting for anyone to torture with the punishment of Allah. If you find him, cut off his hand, then his foot, then cut off his hand, then his foot.\" He said: \"That raiding party did not get him, but a transfer to Madinah got him.\" He said: \"He was a man who insulted, so the Prophet ﷺ was told, 'This is Habbar b. al-Aswad, insulting, and what insults!'\" He said: \"The Prophet ﷺ came to him walking until he stood over him—and Habbar was a Muslim—and said to him: 'Insult whoever insulted you, insult whoever insulted you.'\""],[10248,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him and a group with him as a raiding party against a man from his enemy, and he said to them: \"If you gain power over so-and-so, burn him in the fire.\" They departed, and when they were hidden from him, he called to them and sent for them and brought them back. He said to them: \"If you gain power over him, kill him and do not burn him with fire, for no one tortures with fire except the Lord of Fire.\""],[10249,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a detachment against some people and ordered them to kill them all if they gained power over them. The bearer of good news came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him that they had attacked them in the morning and began killing them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began rejoicing and smiling at what he was telling him. While he was like that, the man said: \"A man passed by and ran until he climbed a tall, massive tree. We shot arrows at him while he was in it, then we lit a fire and burned the tree.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry when burning with fire was mentioned to him.\" The man said: \"The man fell, and it turned out the arrows had killed him.\""],[10250,"The Prophet ﷺ advised Muadh b. Jabal when he sent him to Yemen, saying: \"You will come upon some people of the Book. Call them to tawhid. If they affirm that, then say: 'Allah has made obligatory upon you five prayers by night and day.' If they affirm that, then say: 'Allah has made obligatory upon you fasting one month in twelve months.' If they affirm that, then say: 'Allah has made obligatory upon you zakah on your wealth, taken from your rich.' If they affirm that, then take from their wealth, but avoid the best of their wealth. Beware of the supplication of the wronged, for there is no veil between it and Me.\""],[10251,"The Prophet ﷺ did not fight Banu Qurayza until he called them to Islam, and they refused, so he fought them."],[10252,"The Prophet ﷺ called Banu al-Nadir to give a covenant they would pledge to him, and they refused, so he fought them."],[10253,"Khalid b. al-Walid wrote to Mihran b. Zadan and another with him whom he named: Now then, I call you to Islam. If you refuse, I call you to give the jizya. If you refuse, I have with me a people who love fighting as the Persians love drinking wine."],[10254,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ sent Ali, he sent a man behind him and said: \"Follow Ali and do not call him from behind, but follow him and take him by the hand and say to him: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Stop until he comes to you.'\" He said: So he stopped until the Prophet ﷺ came and said: Do not fight a people until you call them."],[10255,"We used to call the enemy, and we call."],[10256,"He has more right to her as long as he does not take her out of the land of her emigration."],[10258,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ appointed a commander over an army or an expedition, he enjoined him in his own person to fear God and to treat those with him among the Muslims well. Then he said: Go forth in the name of God, in the path of God. Fight those who disbelieve in God. Go forth and do not act treacherously, do not mutilate, do not steal from the spoils, and do not kill a child. When you meet your enemy among the polytheists, call them to three qualities or traits. Whichever of them they respond to, accept from them and refrain from them. Call them to Islam; if they respond, accept from them and refrain from them. Then call them to move from their land to the land of the emigrants, and tell them that they will have what the emigrants have and be subject to what the emigrants are subject to. If they refuse to move from their land to the land of the emigrants, tell them that they will be like the Bedouin Muslims: the judgment of God will apply to them as it applies to the believers, and they will have no share in the fay' and the booty, unless they fight alongside the Muslims. If they refuse to enter Islam, then ask them to give the jizya. If they do, accept from them and refrain from them. If they refuse, seek help from God and fight them. If you besiege the people of a fortress and they ask you to grant them the protection of God and the protection of His Prophet ﷺ, do not grant them the protection of God or the protection of His Prophet, but grant them your protection and the protection of your father and the protections of your companions, for if you violate your protection and the protection of your fathers, that is easier for you than violating the protection of God and the protection of His Messenger ﷺ. If you besiege the people of a fortress and they ask you to bring them down according to the judgment of God, do not bring them down according to the judgment of God, but bring them down according to your judgment, for you do not know whether you will hit upon the judgment of God concerning them or not."],[10259,"Umar wrote to us while we were at Khanqin: When you besiege a fortress, do not say, 'Come down according to the judgment of God and our judgment,' but bring them down according to your judgment, then judge among them as you wish. When a man meets a man and says to him, 'matras' (do not fear), he has given him safety. When he says, 'la tadhal' (do not be afraid), he has given him safety. When he says, 'la takhaf' (do not fear), he has given him safety, for God knows the tongues."],[10260,"When he sent out an army, he said: \"Go forth in the name of God and with God, and in the path of God. You will fight those who disbelieve in God. I send you out on condition that you do not steal from the spoils, do not be cowardly, do not mutilate, do not kill a child, do not burn a church, and do not cut down a palm tree.\" He sent a person to a person who had lied to him in Yemen and said: \"Burn him.\" Then he said: \"Do not punish with the punishment of God.\""],[10261,"Umar wrote to us while we were at Khanqin: The crescent moons are some larger than others. When you see the crescent, do not break your fast until two men testify that they saw it yesterday. When you besiege the people of a fortress, do not bring them down according to the judgment of God and the judgment of His Messenger, but bring them down according to your judgment, then judge among them as you wish. Do not say, 'Do not fear,' 'Do not be afraid,' or 'matras,' for God knows the tongues."],[10262,"A man from the people of Syria came to Ibn al-Musayyab and said to him: O Abu Muhammad, shall I tell you what we do in our campaigns? He said: No. He said: Then tell me what the Prophet ﷺ and his companions used to do. He said: When the Messenger of God ﷺ encamped at a village, he called its people to Islam. If they followed him, he mixed them with himself and his companions. If they refused, he called them to give the jizya. If they gave it, he accepted it from them. If they refused, he proclaimed war against them on equal terms. And the least of them, if he gave them the pact, they all fulfilled it for him."],[10264,"The Prophet ﷺ sent Khalid b. al-Walid to Judhayma. He called them to Islam, but they did not know how to say 'we have become Muslims,' and they kept saying 'saba'na, saba'na' (we have apostatized). Khalid began killing and taking prisoners among them. He handed over to each man among us a prisoner, until on one day he ordered each of us to kill his prisoner. Abd Allah b. Umar said: I said: By God, I will not kill my prisoner, and no man among my companions will kill his prisoner. Then we came to the Prophet ﷺ and he raised — meaning his hands — and said: O God, I disavow to You what Khalid did, twice."],[10265,"Any man who calls a man from the polytheists and points to the sky, God has granted him protection, for he has only descended with God's covenant and His pact."],[10266,"I witnessed a town of the towns of Persia called Shahirta, and we besieged it for a month, until one day when we hoped to attack them in the morning, we withdrew from them at the time of the midday rest. A slave of ours remained behind, and they asked him for protection. He wrote to them on an arrow a letter of protection, then shot it to them. When we returned to them, they came out in their clothes and laid down their weapons. We said, 'What is your matter?' They said, 'You granted us protection,' and they brought out to us the arrow containing the letter of their protection. We said, 'This is a slave, and a slave has no power over anything.' They said, 'We do not know your slave from your free man, and they have come out under protection.' We said, 'Then return under protection.' They said, 'We will never return to it.' So we wrote to Umar part of their story, and Umar wrote: 'The Muslim slave is from the Muslims; his protection is their protection.' He said: 'Thus we missed what we had been on the verge of from their spoils.'"],[10267,"If a woman used to take [protection] upon the Muslims, saying: 'You are granted protection.'"],[10268,"Umm Hani brought two men, and Ali wanted to kill them. She came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him, and he said: 'We have granted protection to what Umm Hani has granted protection.'"],[10269,"She entered upon the Prophet ﷺ on the day of the conquest and said: 'O Messenger of God, the son of my mother claims that he will kill so-and-so, a man whom I have granted protection.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'We have granted protection to what Umm Hani has granted protection.'"],[10270,"Zaynab said: 'O Messenger of God, if Abu al-As b. al-Rabi is brought near, he is a cousin; if he is kept far, he is the father of a child, and I have granted him protection.' The Prophet ﷺ ratified it."],[10271,"Abu al-As b. al-Rabi b. Abd al-Uzza b. Abd Shams b. Abd Manaf, and he was the husband of Khadija's daughter, was brought to the Prophet ﷺ in a leather strap, and Zaynab, the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, untied him."],[10272,"Zaynab, the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, granted protection to her husband Abu al-As b. al-Rabi, and the Prophet ﷺ ratified her protection."],[10273,"Two cavalry forces met, a cavalry of the Daylam and a cavalry of the Arabs. The cavalry was routed, and there was a fallen man among them. He said: 'The Arabs came forward and thought he was one of them, and said: No harm.' When they surrounded him, behold, he was a man from the Daylam. Some of them said: 'By God, we have granted him protection.' Others said: 'By God, we did not grant him protection; we only thought he was one of us.' Their opinion agreed that they had granted him protection."],[10274,"When the Prophet ﷺ prayed the dawn prayer, Zaynab stood up and said: 'It has been mentioned that my husband has been brought, and I have granted him protection.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'I have no knowledge of this matter, but the lowest of the people may grant protection on behalf of the group.'"],[10275,"The Muslims are one hand against those besides them; their blood is equal, and the protection granted by the lowest of them is binding. A Muslim is not killed for a disbeliever, nor a person under covenant while in his covenant. The lowest of them is entitled to the same as the farthest, the one who goes out on expedition over the one who stays behind, and the strong over the weak. He says: regarding the spoils."],[10276,"The Prophet ﷺ ratified the protection granted by his daughter Zaynab."],[10277,"Amr b. Shu'ayb said to us: \"A slave has no share with the Muslims.\" He said: \"And Amr b. Shu'ayb informed us at that time that a slave found buried treasure in the time of Umar b. al-Khattab. Umar took it from him, then bought him from it, freed him, gave him some wealth from it, and put the rest into the wealth of the Muslims.\""],[10278,"It has reached us that it is said: \"A slave is not enrolled in the register, and no zakah is taken from him.\""],[10279,"That slaves of theirs were present at Badr, and Umar b. al-Khattab used to give them three thousand, three thousand every year."],[10280,"That they were with the Prophet ﷺ on a campaign. He said: \"Among us were slaves.\" He said: \"So he did not allot any share for them.\""],[10281,"Najdah wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the slave and the woman: are they given anything from the khums? He said: \"They have nothing from the khums.\""],[10282,"The slave and the woman used to be given gifts from the spoils of the people. He said: \"And I say the statement of Ibn Abbas regarding the slave and the woman who attend the battle: they have no fixed share, except that they may be given gifts from the spoils of the people.\""],[10283,"The slave has no portion of the spoils."],[10284,"I was present at Khaybar with the Prophet ﷺ, and he did not allot a share for me, but he gave me something from the odds and ends of the baggage."],[10285,"That Najdah wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the share of the near relatives, about killing children, and about slaves: were they given anything from the spoils? Ibn Abbas wrote back to him: \"You wrote to me about the share of the near relatives. It was ours until our people deprived us of it. You wrote about killing children. If you know about them what the companion of Musa knew, then otherwise it is not lawful for you to kill them. You wrote about slaves, whether they were given anything from the spoils. They used to be given small gifts without any share being allotted for them.\""],[10287,"That Abd al-Rahman b. Awf said to a man from the poor emigrants: \"Will you go out with me, O so-and-so, on a military expedition?\" He said: \"Yes.\" So he promised him. When the time of departure came, he called him, but he refused to go out with him. Abd al-Rahman said to him: \"Did you not promise me? Are you lying to me and breaking your promise to me?\" He said: \"I cannot go out.\" He said: \"What prevents you?\" He said: \"My dependents and my family.\" He said: \"What will satisfy you so that you go out?\" He said: \"Three dinars,\" on condition that he go out with him. So he went out with him. When they defeated the enemy and obtained the spoils, he said to Abd al-Rahman: \"Give me my share of the spoils.\" Abd al-Rahman said to him: \"I will mention your matter to the Messenger of God ﷺ.\" So he mentioned it, and the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"These three dinars are his lot and his share from his expedition, in the affairs of his world and his hereafter.\""],[10289,"The one who stayed behind would grant a share to the warrior. As for a man selling his military service, I do not know what that is."],[10290,"I asked Ibn Umar about rewards, and he said: 'I would not accept a bribe except what Allah has given me.'"],[10291,"I asked Ibn Abbas about rewards, and he came out to us with one out of every four and one out of every three. He said: 'If you make it in provisions for horses or weapons, there is no harm; but if you make it in a slave, a slave-girl, or sheep, it is of little benefit.'"],[10293,"Masruq would appoint a substitute for himself when the expedition set out."],[10294,"O Abu al-Walid, O Ubadah b. al-Samit, when you see charity being concealed and becoming scarce, and people being hired for military expeditions, and ruins being inhabited and inhabited places being ruined, and a man becoming accustomed to betraying his trust as a camel becomes accustomed to rubbing against trees, then you and the Hour are like these two—and he pointed with his index finger and the one next to it."],[10295,"The battle cry of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Musaylimah was: 'O companions of Surat al-Baqarah!'"],[10296,"Regarding marrying polytheist women outside a covenant: he disliked their women and permitted their slaughtered animals in the land of war."],[10297,"If you are attacked at night, say: 'Ha Mim, they will not be helped.'"],[10298,"Al-Bara' b. Malik, the brother of Anas, fought in single combat with Marzuban al-Za'rah and killed him, taking his spoils. His spoils reached thirty thousand. This reached Umar b. al-Khattab, who said to Abu Talhah: 'We used not to take a fifth of the spoils of single combat, but the spoils of al-Bara' have reached a great amount of wealth, and I think I will take a fifth of it.'"],[10299,"Al-Bara' b. Malik lay on his back and began to hum. Anas said to him: 'Remember Allah, my brother.' He sat up straight and said: 'O Anas, do you think I will die on my bed when I have killed a hundred of the polytheists in single combat, besides those I shared in killing?'"],[10300,"A man from Banu Qurayzah stood up and said: 'Who will fight me in single combat?' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Rise, O Zubayr.' Safiyyah said: 'Is he to be my only one, O Messenger of Allah?' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Whichever of the two overcomes his companion will kill him?' Then al-Zubayr overcame him and killed him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him his spoils as extra reward."],[10301,"We kept hearing from long ago that when the Muslims and the disbelievers meet, and a Muslim man kills a man from the disbelievers, the slain man's spoils belong to him, unless it occurs in the heat of battle."],[10303,"We were at al-Qadisiyyah when a man came out from among them, armed and well-equipped. He said: 'One on one! One on one!' meaning: man against man. I offered my companions the chance to fight him, but they refused. I was a short man. He said: I went forward to him. He shouted, and I said the takbir. He roared, and I said the takbir. He attacked me and struck, and his horse veered with him. I grabbed his dagger, leapt onto his chest, and slew him. He said: I took his belt, a sword, two banners, a coat of mail, and two bracelets; their value was estimated at twelve thousand. I brought them to Sa'd b. Malik. He said: 'Come to me later, and bring the spoils.' So I went to him later, and he stood on the pulpit and said: 'This is the spoils of Shabr b. 'Alqamah. Take it all, may it be pleasant and wholesome to you.' So he gave it all to me as a bonus."],[10304,"On the day of Banu Musaylimah, al-Bara' b. Malik met a man called Himar al-Yamamah, who was a tall man with a white sword in his hand. Al-Bara' was a short man. Al-Bara' struck at his legs with the sword, but it seemed he missed him, and the man fell on his back. He said: I took his sword and sheathed my own."],[10305,"If you have no weapon except the weapon of the enemy, then fight with it, and afterwards return it to the spoils of war."],[10306,"I fought a man in single combat on the day of Hunayn and killed him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me his spoils."],[10307,"A man from the polytheists insulted the Prophet ﷺ, so he said: 'Who will suffice me against my enemy?' Al-Zubayr said: 'I will.' So al-Zubayr fought him in single combat and killed him, and the Prophet ﷺ gave him the man's spoils."],[10308,"Al-Ahwaz was conquered while their commander was Abu Musa or someone else. He summoned Majza'ah or Shaqiq b. Thawr — Abu Bakr was uncertain — and said: 'Find me a man from your people whom I can send on an expedition.' He said: 'If this matter you intend is good, I would not like anyone from my people to beat me to it; and if it is otherwise, I would not like to cause anyone from my people to fall into it. So send me.' He said: 'We have been guided to an underground passage through which one can enter the city.' So he sent him with some people — I think he also said — and al-Bara' b. Malik was over them. Majza'ah or Shaqiq entered the passage, and when he came out they threw a rock at him and killed him. Then the people entered until they became many, and Allah granted them victory. He said: We heard that he was a young man of twenty years."],[10309,"'Ali administered the khums according to the practice of the two."],[10310,"Ibn 'Abbas was asked about the share of the near relatives, and he said: 'It was ours, but our people denied it to us. So 'Umar summoned us and said: \"Your widows may be married from it, and your debtors may be paid from it.\" We refused, and 'Umar ؓ refused.'"],[10311,"Concerning His saying: 'And know that of anything you gain as spoils, a fifth is for Allah' — it is divided into five fifths: for the Messenger, for the near relatives, for the orphans, for the needy, and for the wayfarer."],[10312,"I asked al-Hasan b. Muhammad b. 'Ali b. al-Hanafiyyah about the saying of Allah, Exalted is He: 'And know that of anything you gain as spoils, a fifth is for Allah.' He said: 'This is the opening phrase of the statement. To Allah belong this world and the Hereafter, and to the Messenger, and to the near relatives. After the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ they differed concerning these two shares. One party said: \"The share of the near relatives belongs to the relatives of the Prophet ﷺ.\" Another party said: \"The share of the near relatives belongs to the relatives of the caliph.\" The companions of Muhammad ﷺ agreed unanimously to allocate these two shares for horses and equipment in the cause of Allah, and that was during the caliphate of Abu Bakr and 'Umar.' I said to him: '...' He said: 'He disliked that it be claimed he opposed the two.'"],[10313,"On the day of Badr, the Messenger of God ﷺ said: “Whoever kills an enemy shall have such-and-such, and such-and-such.” They killed seventy and took seventy captives. Abu al-Yasar b. ‘Amr brought two captives and said: “O Messenger of God, you promised us that whoever kills an enemy shall have such-and-such, and whoever captures a prisoner shall have such-and-such. I have brought two captives.” Sa‘d b. ‘Ubada stood and said: “O Messenger of God, it was not lack of desire for the Hereafter that held us back, nor cowardice before the enemy, but we stood in this position for fear that the polytheists would cut you off. If you give to these, nothing will remain for your companions.” He said: Then these began to speak, and those began to speak, and it was revealed: “They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war belong to God and the Messenger. So fear God and set things right among yourselves” [Q 8:1]. He said: So they surrendered the booty to the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: Then it was revealed: “And know that whatever you take as spoils, a fifth belongs to God” [Q 8:41]."],[10314,"He used to say in supplication for the deceased: O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, our young and our old, our males and our females, our absent and our present. O Allah, whomever of us You keep alive, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomever of us You take, take him upon faith."],[10315,"The share of the Prophet ﷺ was called the safiyy. If he wished, it could be a slave, or if he wished, a horse. He would select it before the fifth, and a share would be allotted for him whether he was present or absent. Safiyya bt. Huyayy was among the safiyy."],[10318,"On the day of Khaybar."],[10319,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prohibited on the day of Khaybar the meat of domestic donkeys, approaching pregnant women, selling spoils of war until they are divided, and eating any fanged beast of prey."],[10322,"A prophet among the prophets went on a military expedition and said: “No one who has married a woman and not yet consummated the marriage shall go with me, nor a man who has sheep awaiting their offspring, nor a man who has built a building and not yet finished it.” When he reached the place he intended, the time of ‘Asr came, and he said to the sun: “You are commanded, and I am commanded. O God, hold it back for me for a while.” So God held it back for him for a while. Then God granted him victory. Then the booty was placed, and fire came but did not consume it. He said: “There is misappropriation among you, so let one man from each tribe pledge allegiance to me.” He said: His hand stuck to the hands of two or three men. He said: “The misappropriation is among you.” He said: So they brought out something like the head of a cow made of gold and threw it into the booty. Then the fire came and consumed it. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: “It was not lawful for anyone before us, and that is because God Almighty saw our weakness and made it good for us.”"],[10323,"When the Prophet ﷺ obtained spoils, he would send a crier: “No man should misappropriate a needle or anything less, for he will come on the Day of Resurrection carrying it on his back, and it will bellow if it is a camel, and neigh if it is a horse.”"],[10324,"‘Aqil b. Abi Talib came, and his wife said to him: “We know that you fought, so have you brought us anything?” He said: “Here is a needle to sew your clothes.” He said: The Prophet ﷺ sent a crier: “No man should misappropriate a needle or anything less.” ‘Aqil said to his wife: “I think your needle has escaped you.”"],[10325,"Regarding His saying: “And know that whatever you gain…” he said: “Even a needle from anything.”"],[10326,"Someone who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me while he was in Wadi al-Qura, standing on his horse, and a man from Bulqin came to him and said: “Your slave has been martyred” or he said: “Your client so-and-so.” He said: “Rather, he is now being dragged to the Fire in a cloak he misappropriated from Allah and His Messenger.”"],[10327,"While he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on his return from Hunayn, the Bedouins clung to him asking him, so he was forced against a thorny tree, and it snatched his cloak while he was on his mount. He stopped and said: “Return my cloak to me. Do you fear stinginess from me? If there were as many livestock as these thorn trees, I would divide them among you, and then you would not find me stingy, nor cowardly, nor a liar.”"],[10328,"When it was time for the division of the fifth, a man came to him asking him to allow a thread or a needle. He said: “Return the thread and the needle, for misappropriation is disgrace, Fire, and shame.” He said: Then he lifted some hairs or a piece of wool from his camel and said: “I have no right to what Allah has granted you as spoils, not even the like of this, except the fifth, and it is returned to you.”"],[10329,"Allah does not accept prayer without purification, nor charity from misappropriated booty."],[10330,"Four things in four contexts are not accepted: in Hajj, in Umrah, in jihad, and in charity: fraud, theft, misappropriation, and the property of an orphan."],[10331,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Khaybar when a man from Ashjaʿ died, and the Prophet ﷺ did not pray over him. They went to inspect his belongings and found among them beads from the beads of the Jews worth no more than two dirhams."],[10332,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Khaybar when a man from Ashjaʿ died, and the Prophet ﷺ did not pray over him. They went to inspect his belongings and found among them beads from the beads of the Jews worth no more than two dirhams."],[10333,"On the day of Hunayn, the Prophet ﷺ came to the tribes at their encampments, supplicating for them and greeting them, but he left one tribe among those tribes and did not come to it. They searched among them and found in a man's saddlecloth a necklace of onyx that he had misappropriated. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them and prayed over them as one prays over the dead."],[10334,"A man named Kirkirah was in charge of the Prophet's baggage, and he died. The Prophet ﷺ said: “He is in the Fire.” They went to look at him and found a cloak on him that he had misappropriated."],[10335,"The Prophet ﷺ was told about a man who had been holding the head of his mount during the fighting: “So-and-so has been martyred.” He said: “Right now he is tossing about in the Fire.” It was said: “Why, O Messenger of Allah?” He said: “He misappropriated a woolen cloak on the day of Khaybar.” A man from the people said: “O Messenger of Allah, I took two sandal straps on such-and-such a day.” He said: “Two sandal straps of Fire.”"],[10336,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Uhud and said: “O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so has misappropriated such-and-such.” The Prophet ﷺ said to him: “O so-and-so, did you do that?” He said: “No.” He said: Then the Prophet ﷺ looked at the man who had informed him, and he said: “O Messenger of Allah, dig here.” So they dug and brought out a velvet cloth. They said: “O Messenger of Allah, ask forgiveness for him.” He said: “Leave us alone from Abu Kharth,” meaning excrement."],[10337,"Regarding His saying: “Is then one who follows the pleasure of Allah like one who draws upon himself wrath from Allah?” He said: “The one who misappropriates.”"],[10338,"A man, when he misappropriated, would be commanded: his saddle would be burned, and he would be deprived of his share of the booty."],[10339,"A man, when he misappropriated, would be commanded regarding his saddle: it would be brought out and burned.” He said: “And 'Amr said, from al-Hasan: ‘And he would be deprived of his share of the booty.’”"],[10340,"He witnessed a man named Ziyad being pursued for misappropriation in the way of Allah in the land of al-Rum. So a ruling was sought from Salim b. 'Abd Allah, 'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz, and Raja' b. Haywah, and all of them advised: “That he be given a painful flogging, and his belongings—except animals—be gathered and burned, then he be let go in his trousers, and his sword be given to him alone.”"],[10341,"His saddle is gathered and burned."],[10343,"Do not wish to meet the enemy, for you do not know—perhaps you will be tested by them. Ask Allah for well-being. Then when they come to you flashing their swords, chanting, and shouting, then [stay close to] the ground, the ground, sitting down. Then say: “O Allah, our Lord and their Lord, our forelocks and their forelocks are in Your hand; indeed only You kill them.” Then when they come near you, rise up against them, and know that Paradise lies beneath the flashing swords."],[10344,"It has reached me that a woman from the People of the Book is not married except in a covenant."],[10345,"'Abd Allah b. Abi Awfa wrote to 'Ubayd Allah b. Ma'mar, then he mentioned something similar to the hadith of Ibn Abi Awfa from Musa b. 'Uqbah, from Abu al-Nadr. He wrote on behalf of a man from Aslam among the companions of the Prophet ﷺ called 'Abd Allah b. Abi Awfa, that he wrote to 'Umar b. 'Ubayd Allah when he marched against the Haruriyyah, informing him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, during the days in which he met the enemy, would wait until the sun had declined, then he would stand among them and say: “O people, do not wish to meet the enemy. Ask Allah for well-being. If you meet them, be patient, and know that Paradise lies beneath the shade of the swords.” Then the Prophet ﷺ stood and said: “O Allah, Sender down of the Book, Mover of the clouds, Defeater of the confederates, defeat them and grant us victory over them.” He also mentioned that it reached him that the Prophet ﷺ supplicated in a similar situation, saying: “O Allah, our Lord and their Lord, we are Your servants and they are Your servants, our forelocks and their forelocks are in Your hand, so grant us victory over them.”"],[10346,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the Day of the Confederates: “O Allah, Sender down of the Book, Swift in reckoning, Mover of the clouds, Defeater of the confederates. O Allah, defeat them and shake them.”"],[10347,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met the enemy, he would say: “O Allah, You are my support and my helper; by You I move and by You I attack, and by You I fight.”"],[10348,"Do not wish to meet the enemy; ask Allah for well-being. When you meet them, be steadfast, remember Allah, and if they clamor and shout, then you must keep silent."],[10349,"I said to 'Ata': \"Fleeing from the advancing army?\" He said: \"The one who flees is not the one who turns aside for fighting, nor the one who withdraws to a group—God's saying.\" I said: \"If a man flees not in an advancing army?\" He said: \"There is no harm in that; that is only in the advancing army.\""],[10350,"Regarding His saying: \"When you meet those who disbelieve, advancing [in battle]\" until \"and evil is the destination,\" he said: \"They held that this was on the day of Badr. Do you not see that He says: 'And whoever turns his back to them that day'?\""],[10351,"This was only on the day of Badr, and the Muslims had no group to which they could withdraw."],[10352,"'Umar appointed Abu 'Ubayd al-Thaqafi over an army, and he was killed in the land of Persia, he and his army. So 'Umar said: \"If they had withdrawn to me, I would have been a group for them.\""],[10353,"'Umar b. al-Khattab said to the Muslims: \"I am your group, so whoever of you withdraws, let him withdraw to the armies.\""],[10354,"I am the group of every Muslim."],[10355,"It was imposed on the Muslims—against a man, ten of the disbelievers—in His saying: \"If there be of you a hundred steadfast, they shall overcome two hundred; and if there be of you a thousand, they shall overcome two thousand by God's permission.\" So if a man meets two men and flees, or one man and flees, it is a major sin; but if he meets three and flees from them, there is no harm."],[10356,"Regarding His saying: \"If there be of you twenty steadfast,\" he said: \"This was obligatory upon them, that not one should flee from ten; then God lightened it for them.\""],[10357,"Regarding marrying polytheist women outside a covenant: he disliked their women and permitted their slaughtered animals in the land of war."],[10358,"Every wound that a Muslim receives in the path of God will be in the same condition as when it was inflicted, gushing blood. He said: \"The color is the color of blood, and the scent is the scent of musk.\""],[10359,"Were it not that I would burden my community, I would not stay behind any raiding party that goes out in the path of God. But I do not find means to mount them, and they do not find means to follow me, and they are not content to stay behind after me."],[10360,"The likeness of the one who strives in the path of God—and God knows best who strives in His path—is that of one who stands in prayer and fasts. God has guaranteed for the one who strives in His path that if He takes him in death, He will admit him to Paradise, or He will return him safe with whatever he has obtained of reward or spoils."],[10361,"Whoever is wounded in the path of God will come on the Day of Resurrection bleeding, his scent like musk and his color the color of blood."],[10362,"Were it not that some of the believers would be unwilling to stay behind me, and I do not find what I can mount them on, no expedition would go out raiding in the path of God without me being with them. By God, I would love to be killed in the path of God, then brought back to life, then killed, then brought back to life, then killed."],[10363,"Killing washes away the stain, and killing is of two kinds: an expiation and a rank."],[10364,"No Muslim man fights in the path of God for the interval between two milkings of a camel without Paradise becoming obligatory for him. Whoever asks God for death in battle sincerely from himself, then dies or is killed, has the reward of a martyr. Whoever is wounded in the path of God or receives a blow, it will come on the Day of Resurrection as profuse as it ever was, its color like saffron and its scent like musk. Whoever goes out in the path of God bears the seal of the martyrs."],[10365,"There is no living soul on earth for whom death with God Almighty is better, and who would love to return to you even if it had the world, except the one killed in the path of God; for he would love to return and be killed one more time."],[10366,"When the fighter goes out from his house, as many as he left behind of the people of the qibla, the people of the covenant, and the animals, for each one of them, a qirat is recorded for him, every night, like a mountain—or he said: like Uhud."],[10367,"The example of the fighter is like the one who fasts constantly and prays at night."],[10368,"His action confirmed his words. He used to address us and say: 'Remember God's blessing upon you, how good is the trace of God's blessing upon you. If you could see what I see of green and yellow, and in the saddles is what is in them.' He said: It used to be said: 'When the people are lined up for battle, or they form rows in prayer, the gates of heaven, the gates of Paradise, and the gates of Hell are opened, and the houris of wide beautiful eyes are adorned, and they look out. When he advances, they say: O God, grant him victory; and when he retreats, they hide from him and say: O God, forgive him. So strike hard at the faces of the enemy—may my father and mother be your ransom—and do not disgrace the houris of wide beautiful eyes.' He said: 'The first drop of his blood that is shed, God expiates by it every sin he has committed.' He said: 'Two houris of wide beautiful eyes descend to him, wipe the dust from his face, and say: It is time for you; and he says: It is time for you two. Then he is clothed in a hundred garments, not from the weaving of the children of Adam, but from the plants of Paradise; if they were placed between two fingers, they would fit.' He said: He used to say: 'I have been informed that swords are the keys of Paradise. On the Day of Resurrection, it will be said: O so-and-so, this is your light; and O so-and-so, son of so-and-so, no light for you.'"],[10369,"Whoever fights in the path of God for the interval between two milkings of a camel, whether he is killed or dies, enters Paradise. Whoever shoots an arrow that reaches the enemy or falls short, it is like freeing a slave. Whoever grows a gray hair in the path of God, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection. Whoever is wounded, the wound will come on the Day of Resurrection with its scent like musk and its color like saffron."],[10370,"When the two rows meet, the houris of wide beautiful eyes descend to the lowest heaven. When they see a man whose advance pleases them, they say: O God, make him steadfast; and if he turns back, they hide from him. If he is killed, two of them descend to him, wipe the dust from his face, and say: O God, cover with dust the one who covered him with dust, and earth the one who earthed him."],[10371,"The one who is striving to free himself from a contract of manumission is aided, the one who marries is aided, and the fighter is aided, guaranteed by God for whatever reward or booty he obtains until he returns to his family; and if he dies, he enters Paradise."],[10372,"Three are entitled to God's assistance: the fighter in the path of God, the one who marries seeking chastity, and the one under a contract of manumission who intends to fulfill it."],[10373,"A morning expedition or an evening expedition in the path of Allah is better than the world and what it contains, and the standing of one of you in the battle rank is better than the worship of a man for sixty years."],[10374,"Whoever grows a gray hair in the path of Allah, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, it is like the manumission of a slave."],[10375,"We were with a reciter who was reciting, and he came to this verse: 'Allah has preferred the strivers' up to 'and forgiveness and mercy.' He said to the reciter: 'Stop. It has reached me that it is seventy degrees, between each two degrees seventy years for a swift, well-trained horse.'"],[10376,"People will certainly be called on the Day of Resurrection as the deficient ones. It was said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, what are the deficient ones? He said: \"One of them decreases his prayer in his ablution and his turning around.\""],[10377,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the expedition of Tabuk and overlooked Madinah, he said: 'Indeed in Madinah there are people: you have not traversed a path, nor crossed a valley, except that they are with you. Excuse has detained them.'"],[10378,"Whoever grows a gray hair in the path of Allah, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it misses or hits, it is like the manumission of a slave from the descendants of Isma'il."],[10379,"An evening expedition or a morning expedition in the path of Allah is better than the world and what it contains."],[10380,"O Allah, I ask You for martyrdom in Your path in the city of Your Messenger ﷺ."],[10381,"That I die on my bed - Wasil said: He said: I think he said - patiently and expecting reward is dearer to me than that I advance against a people whom I do not want to kill me. He said: Does not Allah bring about martyrdom while the man is greatly concerned about his companions, honored for his position?"],[10382,"Abdullah b. Jahsh said on the day of Uhud: 'O Allah, I adjure You that I meet the enemy, and when I meet the enemy, they kill me, then they split open my belly, then they mutilate me. When I meet You, You will ask me and say: For what was this?' He said: He met the enemy and was killed, and that was done to him. Ibn al-Musayyab said: I hope Allah will fulfill the last part of his oath as He fulfilled its beginning."],[10383,"It has reached us that the souls of the martyrs are in the forms of white birds, eating from the fruits of Paradise. And al-Kalbi said, from the Prophet ﷺ: In the form of white birds that resort to lanterns suspended beneath the Throne."],[10384,"We asked Abdullah about this verse: 'And do not reckon those who were killed in the path of Allah' up to 'they are provided for.' He said: 'The souls of the martyrs are with Allah like birds that have lanterns suspended at the Throne, roaming in Paradise wherever they wish. He said: Then your Lord looked upon them with a glance and said: Do you desire anything so that I may increase you? They said: Our Lord, do we not roam in Paradise wherever we wish? Then He looked upon them a third time and said: Do you desire anything so that I may increase you? They said: Return our souls to our bodies so that we may fight in Your path and be killed once again. He said: So He fell silent regarding them.'"],[10385,"He said in the third [time], when he said: \"Do you desire anything so that I may increase it for you?\" They said: \"Convey our greeting to our Prophet, and inform him that we are pleased and He is pleased with us.\""],[10386,"The souls of the martyrs are in the forms of green birds, suspended in the lanterns of Paradise, which God will restore on the Day of Resurrection."],[10387,"It has reached us that the souls of the martyrs are in the forms of white birds, eating from the fruits of Paradise. And al-Kalbi said, from the Prophet ﷺ: In the form of white birds that resort to lanterns suspended beneath the Throne."],[10388,"The souls of the martyrs are transformed into green birds that cling to the fruit of Paradise."],[10390,"Indeed, for the martyr with God there are nine qualities—I am uncertain—God forgives his sin at the first gush of his blood, he sees his seat in Paradise, he is adorned with the ornament of faith, he is protected from the punishment of the grave, he is married to the wide-eyed houris, he is secured from the greatest terror, a crown of dignity is placed on his head, each ruby of which is better than this world and all it contains, he is married to seventy-two wives from the wide-eyed houris, and he intercedes for seventy people among his relatives."],[10391,"In Paradise there is a dwelling that none will inhabit except a prophet, a steadfast affirmer of truth, a martyr, a just ruler, or one who is given the choice between being killed and disbelief and chooses death over disbelief."],[10392,"The martyr was mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: \"The earth will not dry from his blood before his two wives rush to him, as if they were two she-camels that have lost their young in an open stretch of land. Each one appears in a garment better than this world and all it contains.\""],[10393,"They were shown to me in Paradise in a tent of pearl, each one of them on a couch. I saw Zayd and Ibn Rawāḥah with their necks turned aside, but Jaʿfar was upright with no turning. He said: \"I asked, or it was said: When death overcame them, it was as if they turned away, or turned their faces aside; but Jaʿfar did not do so.\""],[10394,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb passed by some people who were mentioning an expedition that had perished. Some of them said: \"They are its martyrs, in Paradise,\" while others said: \"They have what they hoped for.\" ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb said: \"What are you mentioning?\" They said: \"We mention these people. Some of us say they were killed in the path of God, and some of us say they did not hope for reward.\" ʿUmar said: \"Among people are those who fight for show, and among people are those who fight seeking worldly gain, and among people are those who fight when fighting presses upon them and they find no alternative, and among people are those who fight out of zeal, and among people are those who fight seeking the face of God. Those are the martyrs. Indeed, every soul will be resurrected upon what it died. No soul knows about this man who was killed that he has—that his past and future sins have been forgiven.\""],[10395,"Ḥarām b. Milḥān, the maternal uncle of Anas b. Mālik, when he was stabbed on the day of Biʾr Maʿūnah, took some of his blood in his hand and sprinkled it on his face and head. He said: \"I have triumphed, by the Lord of the Kaaba, I have triumphed, by the Lord of the Kaaba.\""],[10396,"A man came to Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī while Ḥudhayfah was with him and said: \"What do you think of a man who took his sword and fought with it until he was killed—does he have Paradise?\" Al-Ashʿarī said: \"Yes.\" Ḥudhayfah said: \"Ask the man to clarify and make him understand.\" He said: \"What did you say?\" The man repeated his first statement, and Abū Mūsā gave the same answer. Ḥudhayfah again said: \"Ask the man to clarify and make him understand.\" He said: \"What did you say?\" The man repeated his statement, and Abū Mūsā said: \"I have nothing but this.\" Ḥudhayfah said: \"Whoever does this in such and such a way will surely enter the Fire. But whoever strikes with his sword in the path of God seeking the truth, for him is Paradise.\" Abū Mūsā said: \"He has spoken the truth.\""],[10398,"They said: O Messenger of God, a man who fights out of zeal, and a man who fights out of bravery—which of those is in the way of God? He said: Whoever fights so that the word of God may be supreme is in the way of God."],[10399,"The martyr is only the one who, if he died on his bed, would enter Paradise—meaning the one who dies on his bed with no sin."],[10400,"It is special to the martyr."],[10401,"Every believer is a martyr. Then he recited: “And those who believe in God and His messengers—those are the truthful and the witnesses.”"],[10402,"To swear nine times that the Messenger of God ﷺ was killed is dearer to me than to swear once that he did not say that, for God made him a prophet and took him as a martyr."],[10403,"Whoever falls from mountaintops, is eaten by wild beasts, or drowns in the sea is a martyr with God."],[10404,"We were present with the Messenger of God ﷺ at Khaybar—or he said: When the Messenger of God ﷺ was at Khaybar, he said about a man among those with him who claimed to be a Muslim: “This one is from the people of the Fire.” When the fighting began, he fought and was wounded. It was said: He has died. He was brought to the Prophet ﷺ and it was said: The man whom you said is from the people of the Fire fought fiercely today and has died. The Prophet ﷺ said: “To the Fire.” Some of the people seemed to doubt. He said: While they were thus, it was said: He has not died, but he has severe wounds. During the night he could not bear the wounds, so he killed himself. The Prophet ﷺ was informed of that, and he said: “God is most great. I bear witness that I am God’s servant and His messenger.” Then he ordered Bilal, who called out: “No soul enters Paradise except a believing soul, and God may support this religion with a sinful man.”"],[10406,"What do you count as a martyr among you? They said: One who is killed in the way of God. He said: “Then the martyrs of my community are few. Being killed in the way of God is martyrdom; the belly is martyrdom; drowning is martyrdom; the plague is martyrdom; and the woman who dies in childbirth is martyrdom.”"],[10407,"Four are martyrdom for the Muslims: the plague, the woman who dies in childbirth, drowning, and the belly."],[10408,"What do you count as a martyr among you? They said: One who is killed in the way of God is a martyr. He said: “Then the martyrs of my community are few. One who is killed in the way of God is a martyr; the one who dies of plague is a martyr; the one who dies of a belly ailment is a martyr; the one who drowns is a martyr; and the woman who dies in childbirth is a martyr.”"],[10409,"The martyr is: the one who dies of plague, the one who dies of a belly ailment, the one who drowns, the woman who dies in childbirth, and the one who is crushed under a falling building."],[10410,"A man from Quraysh looked down upon the Prophet ﷺ and his companions from the top of a hill. They said: How sturdy this man is; if only his sturdiness were in the way of God. The Prophet ﷺ said: Is there no one in the way of God except one who is killed? Then he said: “Whoever goes out into the land seeking lawful provision to support his family is in the way of God; whoever goes out seeking lawful provision to support himself is in the way of God; and whoever goes out seeking to amass wealth is in the way of Satan.”"],[10412,"On the day of Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ looked over the martyrs who were killed that day and said: “I have witnessed over these, so shroud them in their blood.” He would bury two men or three in one grave and ask which of them had memorized more of the Qur'an, and they would put him forward. Jabir said: My father and my paternal uncle were buried in one grave that day."],[10413,"The Prophet ﷺ said concerning the martyrs on the day of Uhud: 'These have passed on, and I have borne witness for them; they have not consumed anything of their rewards, while you consume from your rewards, and I do not know what you will introduce after me.'"],[10414,"The Messenger of God ﷺ made peace with idol worshippers on the jizya, except those among them who were Arabs, and he accepted the jizya from the people of al-Bahrayn, who were Magians."],[10416,"I did not see them wash the martyr, perfume him, or shroud him. I said: How do we pray over him? He said: “Just as prayer is offered over any other person who is not a martyr.”"],[10417,"Mu'awiya ordered the killing of Hujr b. 'Adi al-Kindi. Hujr said: 'Do not loosen any fetter—or he said: any iron—from me, and shroud me in my blood and my garments.'"],[10418,"“Do not wash any blood from me, and do not remove any garment except the two leather socks, and bury me in the ground. For I am a man who will argue and contend on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[10419,"Bury us with whatever blood of ours has touched the soil."],[10421,"He was called ‘the reciter’ in the time of the Prophet ﷺ. He met an enemy and fled from them. 'Umar said to him: “Would you like to go to Syria? Perhaps Allah will grant you martyrdom.” He said: “No, except against those from whom I fled.” He said: Then he addressed them at al-Qadisiyya and said: “We will meet the enemy tomorrow, if Allah wills, and we will be martyred. Do not wash any blood from us, and do not shroud us except in the garment that was on us.”"],[10422,"We asked Sulayman b. Musa: How is the prayer over a martyr among them? He said: “The same form as over anyone else.” He said: And we asked about burying a martyr. He said: “If he is on the battlefield, we bury him as he is, without washing, shrouding, or perfuming him. But if we carry him away while he still has life in him, then we wash him, shroud him, and perfume him. We found the people upon that practice, and so did those of the people who passed before us.”"],[10423,"When the martyr dies on the battlefield, he is buried as he is; but if he dies after being brought back, he is treated as the other is treated."],[10424,"Umar was one of the best of martyrs, so he was washed, shrouded, and prayed over, because he lived after being stabbed."],[10425,"He was asked about the polytheists, what is taken from them when they trade in the land of the Muslims? Umar said: They do not take from you except on oil and wheat, so take from them a half-tenth."],[10426,"Ali was washed, shrouded, and prayed over."],[10427,"He was asked about a man killed by robbers. He said: \"He is not washed.\""],[10428,"Prayer is performed over the martyr, and he is not washed, for God has already purified him."],[10430,"A man from the Bedouins came to the Prophet ﷺ and believed in him and followed him. He said: \"I will emigrate with you,\" and the Prophet ﷺ entrusted him to some of his companions. When the expedition of Khaybar or Hunayn took place, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ obtained spoils and distributed them, and allotted a share to him. He gave his companions what had been allotted to him, as he was tending their riding animals. When he came, they handed it to him, and he asked: \"What is this?\" They said: \"A share which Allah and His Messenger ﷺ have allotted to you.\" He took it and brought it to the Prophet ﷺ, asking: \"What is this, Muhammad?\" He said: \"A share I allotted to you.\" He said: \"It was not for this that I followed you. Rather, I followed you so that I might be shot here—pointing to his throat—with an arrow and enter Paradise.\" He said: \"If you are truthful to Allah, He will be truthful to you.\" They remained for a short while, then they rose to fight the enemy. He was brought carried, having been struck by an arrow where he had pointed. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Is it he? He was truthful to Allah, and He was truthful to him.\" The Prophet ﷺ shrouded him in a jubba of his, then he ﷺ brought him forward and prayed over him. Among what appeared of his prayer over him was: \"O Allah, this is Your servant who went out as an emigrant in Your path and was killed as a martyr, and I am a witness to that for him.\""],[10433,"On the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over Hamza seventy prayers. Each time he prayed and a man was brought, he prayed over him, with Hamza placed there and prayed over along with him."],[10435,"His leather socks, trousers, and cap—or he said turban—are removed from the slain man, and one garment is added or one garment is removed until it is an odd number."],[10436,"When Muawiya wanted to run the irrigation channel, he said: \"Whoever has a slain relative, let him come to his slain one\"—meaning the slain of Uhud. He said: \"So he brought them out fresh, bending. He said: \"The spade struck the leg of one of them, and it gushed blood.\" He said: \"Abu Said said: \"After this, no denier will ever deny.\""],[10437,"9697 9603 - Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail b. Abi Khalid, from Qays b. Abi Hazim, who said: One of the family of Talhah b. Ubayd Allah saw him in a dream and said: 'You buried me in a place where water has harmed me, so move me from it.' He said: 'So they moved him and took him out as if he were a fresh leaf; nothing of him had changed except a few hairs of his beard.'"],[10438,"We carried the slain on the day of Uhud to bury them, but the caller of the Prophet ﷺ came and said: Bury the slain where they fell. So we returned them."],[10439,"The martyr is not to be buried in sandals, or shoes, or weapons, or a ring. He said: 'He is buried in a belt and clothes.' He said: And it reached me from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i that he said: 'His face-veil is not to be buried [with him].'"],[10440,"If the body of a slain person is found in a house or a place, the funeral prayer is offered over it and blood-money is paid; but if a head or a leg is found, neither prayer is offered nor blood-money paid."],[10441,"Abu Ayyub al-Ansari went on a military expedition with Yazid b. Mu'awiyah, the expedition in which he died."],[10442,"Abu Ayyub al-Ansari used to go on expeditions with Yazid b. Mu'awiyah. He fell ill while with him, and Yazid entered to visit him. He said to him: 'What is your need?' He said: 'When I die, carry me into the enemy's land as far as you can, then bury me.' He said: 'When he died, he carried him until he penetrated a day's journey or part of a day into Byzantine territory, then he dismounted and buried him.'"],[10443,"I asked Jundub b. Abd Allah: 'Did you use the non-Arabs as forced guides?' He said: 'We used them from one village to another; they would show us the way, then we would let them go.'"],[10444,"I said to Ibn Abbas: 'We go on expeditions with these commanders, and they fight for worldly gain.' He said: 'Then you fight for your share of the Hereafter.'"],[10445,"Do not testify that your community has committed shirk, and do not declare them disbelievers for a sin. Jihad is not harmed by the tyranny of a tyrant or the justice of a just ruler. Jihad will continue until the last of this community is sent, and [so is] faith in the divine decree, its good and its evil."],[10446,"I asked al-Sha'bi about military expeditions and whether the soldiers of the regular army are superior or the volunteer. He said: 'Rather, the soldiers of the regular army; the volunteer can return whenever he wishes.'"],[10447,"I said to al-Hasan: 'We go on expeditions with the commanders, but they do not inform us of their plans; however, we make peace when they make peace, and we fight when they fight.' He said: 'Fight the enemy along with the Muslims.'"],[10449,"A man of the Ansar came to Umar b. al-Khattab and said, \"Where were you?\" He said, \"At the frontier guard post.\" He said, \"How long did you stand guard?\" He said, \"Thirty [days].\" He said, \"Would that you had completed forty.\""],[10450,"Guarding one night at the seacoast beyond the vulnerable frontier of the Muslims is dearer to me than observing Laylat al-Qadr in one of the two mosques, the Mosque of the Ka'bah or the Mosque of the Messenger ﷺ in Madinah. Guarding three days is equivalent to a year, and the complete guard duty is forty nights."],[10451,"Salman al-Farisi passed by Shurahbil b. al-Simt while he was standing guard at a fortress in the land of Persia. Salman said to him, \"Shall I relate to you a hadith that may be a help to you in what you are doing? I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: Guarding one day in the way of God is better than fasting a month and standing in prayer during it. Whoever dies while standing guard in the way of God will be protected from the torment of the grave, and his righteous deeds will continue to grow for him until the Day of Resurrection.\""],[10452,"Guarding one day in the way of God is better than standing in prayer for a month and fasting it: one would stand without sitting and fast without breaking the fast. Whoever dies while standing guard in the way of God will be saved from the torment of the grave, and his righteous deeds will continue for him until the Day of Resurrection."],[10453,"We were in the land of Persia, and fatigue and hardship befell us. Salman al-Farisi came to us and said, \"Rejoice! Rejoice! There is no Muslim who stands guard in the way of God but it is like fasting a month and standing in prayer during it. Whoever dies while standing guard in the way of God, his deeds will continue for him until the Day of Resurrection, and he will be protected from the trial of the grave.\""],[10454,"Salman al-Farisi passed by al-Simt b. Thabit while he was at a guard post and it had become burdensome for him, and he was thinking of moving away from it. He said, \"Shall I not tell you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of God ﷺ?\""],[10455,"Keep to jihad as long as it is sweet and green, before it becomes stubble, or becomes decayed, or becomes broken pieces. When expeditions become distant, spoils are consumed, and sacred things are violated, then keep to guard duty, for it is the best of your fighting."],[10456,"Whoever dies while standing guard dies a martyr, is protected from the two tempters of the grave, and is provided morning and evening with his sustenance from Paradise, and his deeds continue for him."],[10457,"Umar disliked carrying the Muslims as raiders on the sea."],[10458,"I asked Ata about a naval expedition, and he disliked it and said, \"I fear...\""],[10459,"Umar b. al-Khattab sent Alqamah b. Mujazziz with some people to Abyssinia, and they were afflicted at sea. Umar swore by God he would never carry anyone on it again."],[10460,"Whoever believes in God and the Last Day, and whoever believes in God and His Messenger, let him not expose his offspring to the polytheists."],[10461,"He used to dislike riding the sea except for three: a raider, a pilgrim, or one performing 'umrah."],[10462,"The Messenger of God ﷺ slept, then woke up laughing. I said, 'Are you laughing at me, O Messenger of God?' He said, 'No, but at a people from my community who go out as raiders in the sea, whose likeness is like kings on couches.' Then he slept, then woke up again and laughed. I said, 'Are you laughing at me, O Messenger of God?' He said, 'No, but at a people from my community who go out as raiders in the sea and return with little booty, forgiven.' She said, 'Pray to God to make me one of them.' So he prayed for her. 'Ata' b. Yasar said: 'I saw her in a raid led by al-Mundhir b. al-Zubayr to the land of the Romans, and she was with us, and she died in the land of the Romans.'"],[10463,"A raid in the sea is better than ten raids on land. Whoever crosses the sea is as if he crossed the valleys, and the one who suffers seasickness in the ship is like the one who wallows in his blood."],[10464,"Whoever did not take part in the raid with me, let him raid in the sea, for the reward of a day in the sea is like the reward of a month on land. Killing in the sea is like two killings on land. The one who suffers seasickness in the ship is like the one who wallows in his blood. The best martyrs of my community are the Companions of the Cave.\" They said, 'Who are the Companions of the Cave, O Messenger of God?' He said, 'A people whom you will entrust with your ships in the way of God.'"],[10465,"A raid in the sea equals ten on land, and the one who suffers seasickness in the sea is like the one who wallows in his blood in the way of God."],[10466,"Maslama b. Makhlad said to a people who embarked on a raid in the sea: 'They left behind none of their sins.'"],[10467,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari used to raid in the sea."],[10468,"May God have mercy on the people of the cemetery.\" 'A'ishah said, 'The people of al-Baqi'.' He said, 'May God have mercy on the people of the cemetery.' 'A'ishah said, 'The people of al-Baqi'.' until he said it three times. Then he said, 'The cemetery of Ashkelon.'"],[10469,"He used to mention that eating, drinking, food, and marriage are more excellent in Ashkelon."],[10470,"On the day of Khaybar he said: 'I will give the banner to a man whom God and His Messenger love, and who loves God and His Messenger.' Then he called 'Ali, and he had sore eyes, and he spat in his eyes, then gave it to him, and God granted him victory."],[10471,"Sa'd b. 'Ubadah was the bearer of the banner of the Messenger of God ﷺ with the Messenger of God ﷺ on the day of Badr and elsewhere."],[10472,"The banner of the Prophet ﷺ used to be with ʿAlī b. Abī Ṭālib, and it was among the Anṣār wherever they turned."],[10473,"The banner of the Prophet ﷺ was with ʿAlī b. Abī Ṭālib, and the banner of the Anṣār was with Saʿd b. ʿUbādah. When the fighting intensified, the Prophet ﷺ would be under the banner of the Anṣār."],[10474,"He saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tie a black banner for ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ."],[10475,"The banner of the Prophet ﷺ was with Saʿd b. ʿUbādah on the day of the conquest, and Saʿd handed it to his son Qays."],[10477,"During the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a man, when he feared [for his life], would take off his weapon and give it to this one and give it to that one—parts of his weapon—until they became unrecognizable, so that the two would not be recognized."],[10478,"He prohibited, if an animal became slow in enemy territory, that it be hamstrung. He said: 'As for the weapon, let him bury it.'"],[10479,"Al-Zubayr was the first to draw a sword in the path of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ was in the lower part of Makkah, and al-Zubayr was in Makkah. He was told that the Prophet ﷺ had been killed, so he went out with his sword drawn, cutting through the people with it until he reached the Prophet ﷺ and found him unharmed. He said: The Prophet ﷺ asked him about that, so he informed him. He said: 'He supplicated for him and for his sword.'"],[10480,"The first man to draw a sword in Allah['s cause] was al-Zubayr. A blast from Satan was blown: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has been seized'—while the Prophet ﷺ was in the upper part of Makkah. Al-Zubayr went out cutting through the people with his sword and met the Prophet ﷺ, who said to him: 'What is the matter with you, O Zubayr?' He said: 'I was told that you had been seized.' He said: 'So he supplicated for him and for his sword.'"],[10481,"The one who bloodied the face of the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Uḥud was a man from Hudhayl called Ibn al-Qamiʾah. His doom was that Allah sent a billy goat against him, and it gored him and killed him."],[10482,"ʿUtbah b. Abī Waqqāṣ broke the front tooth of the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Uḥud and made his face bleed. The Prophet ﷺ supplicated against him, saying: 'O Allah, do not let a year pass over him until he dies a disbeliever.' And a year did not pass over him before he died a disbeliever, [destined] for the Fire."],[10483,"A man embraced Islam during the time of Umar b. al-Khattab and said: Remove the jizyah from my land! Umar said: Your land was taken by force."],[10484,"Umar sent an army, and he used to send successive armies one after another. They stayed for a while, and no relief force came to them, so they returned. The commander of the detachment wrote to Umar that they had returned, leaving their frontier post, and had set a bad precedent for the people. Umar sent for them, and no one else witnessed that. He became angry with them and threatened them with a severe threat that alarmed them. They said, 'O Umar, why do you separate us? You have abandoned among us the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of sending relief forces for the raiders, one after another.' He said, 'I am not separating you on my own, but because of matters that did not exist among the companions of the Prophet ﷺ from the Ansar.'"],[10485,"Ata was asked about a man from the polytheists who comes to a Muslim without a treaty. He said, 'Give him the choice: either you may grant him protection, or you may convey him to a place of safety.'"],[10486,"Regarding a man from the people of war who enters with a guarantee of safety, and then some of his relatives in the lineage of which he is an heir die: if he had demonstrated residence among the Arabs before the relative died, then he has inheritance; otherwise, no. They said regarding a woman from the People of the Book, from the people of war, who enters the land of the Arabs with a guarantee of safety: if she demonstrates residence in the land of the Arabs, there is no harm in Muslims marrying her; but if she only shows that at the time of the proposal, she is not to be married."],[10487,"Ata was asked about a man from the people of the covenant (dhimmah) who is captured among the polytheists, though the condition had been imposed on them that he should not come to them. He says, 'I did not intend to help them.' Ata disliked killing him except with clear evidence. One of the people of knowledge said to him, 'If he violates any one thing of what is upon him, he has broken the treaty.'"],[10488,"Regarding a man who made a peace agreement for himself and his sons, young and old, and then these sons betrayed him: there is no disagreement about it; they say they are made lawful [to be fought] by the betrayal of these sons, if they were the ones who made the agreement on their own behalf."],[10489,"Tustar was in a peace treaty, but its people disbelieved. The Muhajirun raided them, killed them, defeated them, and took them captive. The Muslims took their women, and children were born to them from those women. He said, 'I saw their children from those births.' Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ ordered that anyone who had been taken captive from them be returned to them under their jizyah, and he separated them from their captors."],[10490,"When the Prophet ﷺ made peace with the people of Khaybar, he made peace with them on condition that he would have their property, and that they would be safe regarding their blood, their offspring, and their women. The Prophet ﷺ called the two sons of Abu al-Huqayq and said, 'Where is the wealth that you two brought out from the Banu al-Nadir?' They said, 'We spent it, and it is gone.' He said, 'Do you think if you two are lying, then your blood, your property, and your women become lawful to me?' They said, 'Yes.' And he made them testify to that. Then he said, 'You two have hidden it in such-and-such a place.' He sent someone with them, and the Prophet ﷺ found the wealth as he had described. So he struck their necks, took their property, and took their women captive. Safiyyah was the wife of one of them."],[10491,"It was disliked to marry women of the People of the Book except under a treaty."],[10492,"The Prophet ﷺ went on eighteen expeditions. He said, 'And another time I heard him say, \"Twenty-four expeditions.\" I do not know whether it was a mistake from him or something he heard after that.'"],[10493,"The raiding parties were twenty-four, and the expeditions were eighteen or nineteen."],[10494,"The name of the female servant of the Prophet ﷺ was Khadrah, his donkey was Ya'fur, his she-camel was al-Qaswa', his mule was al-Shahba', and his sword was Dhu al-Faqar."],[10495,"The name of the sword of the Prophet ﷺ was Dhu al-Faqar, and the name of his coat of mail was Dhat al-Fudul."],[10498,"The sword of 'Umar b. al-Khattab was adorned with silver."],[10499,"If a person gives something in the path of Allah and then completes his military expedition and brings it back, or part of it, he should not consume it; rather, he should spend it in that same path. He said: And if he withholds a she-camel in the path of Allah and it gives birth, its offspring has the same status as it."],[10500,"If anything remains, he should put it into something similar."],[10503,"I asked him about that, and he said: \"It is his, unless he made it an endowment.\""],[10504,"He was asked about a man who is given something in the path of Allah, and Sa'id said: \"When he reaches the farthest point of his expedition, it is like his own property.\""],[10506,"And he was asked about the jihad of women, and he said: \"They used to attend with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, tending the wounded and giving water to the fighters, and I have not heard of any woman being killed with him. The women of Quraysh fought on the day of al-Yarmuk, when the masses of the Romans pressed upon them until they intermingled with the Muslim camp, and on that day the women struck with swords during the caliphate of 'Umar ؓ.\""],[10509,"Additional text: that a man came to al-Zubayr and said: Should I kill ʿAlī? He said: Yes. He said: And how will you do it? He said: I will pretend to him that I am with him, then I will assassinate him and kill him. Al-Zubayr said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: The fetter of faith is assassination; a believer does not assassinate."],[10510,"Additional text: that a man came to al-Zubayr and said: Should I kill ʿAlī? He said: Yes. He said: And how will you do it? He said: I will pretend to him that I am with him, then I will assassinate him and kill him. Al-Zubayr said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: The fetter of faith is assassination; a believer does not assassinate."],[10511,"Al-Mughīrah b. Shuʿbah accompanied a people in the pre-Islamic era, then he killed them and took their wealth, then he came and embraced Islam. The Prophet ﷺ said: As for Islam, I accept it; as for the wealth, I have nothing to do with it. Maʿmar said: And I heard that they had taken an assurance from al-Mughīrah that he would not betray them until he notified them. So they halted at a stopping place, and he began digging with the point of his sword. They said: What are you doing? He said: I am digging your graves. By that he deemed them lawful to be killed, so they drank and then slept, and he killed them. None of them escaped except al-Sharīd, and for that reason he was called al-Sharīd."],[10512,"A man entered upon al-Mukhtār b. Abī ʿUbayd while he had wrapped his sword around him. He said: Al-Mukhtār began lying about God and His Messenger ﷺ. He said: I resolved to strike him with my sword, but I remembered a hadith that ʿAmr b. al-Ḥamiq or ʿAmr b. so-and-so narrated to me. He said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: Any man who grants another man safety for his blood and his wealth, then kills him, the protection of God is disavowed from the killer, even if the slain one was a disbeliever."],[10513,"I asked Ḥammād about some people of war with whom the Muslims made peace for a thousand head each year, and some of them would capture others and hand them over. He said: There is no harm in that; they hand them over from wherever they wish."],[10514,"When a man leaves enemy territory with a slave, if the slave converts to Islam then he belongs to him, but if the slave precedes him and converts to Islam then he is free."],[10515,"He went out to the Messenger of God ﷺ while he was besieging the people of al-Ṭāʾif with twenty-three slaves, and the Messenger of God ﷺ freed them. They are those who are called the freed ones."],[10516,"Whoever fasts a day in the path of God, God distances his face from the Fire a distance of one hundred years of the gallop of a swift, well-trained horse."],[10517,"Whoever fasts a day in the path of God, God distances his face from the Fire a distance of one hundred years."],[10518,"Whoever fasts a day in the path of God, God distances his face from the Fire seventy autumns."],[10520,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to a people besieging the enemy during Ramadan, 'Do they fast?' (He said): 'Do not fast.'"],[10521,"The Prophet ﷺ said on the day of the conquest of Makkah: 'This is a day of fighting, so break your fast.'"],[10524,"O Messenger of God, what do you think of a man who is the protector of the people, defending his companions—will his share be like the share of others? The Prophet ﷺ said: 'May your mother be bereaved of you, O son of Umm Sa'd! Are you provided for and helped except through your weak ones?'"],[10525,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas: 'Distribute to those Muslims who reach you as long as the slain of Persia have not burst open.'"],[10526,"About the first person to hold a horse race, Umar b. al-Khattab said: 'I think...'"],[10530,"Hudhayfa raced people on a gray horse of his. He said: 'He beat them.' He said: 'We entered his house upon him.' He said: 'There he was at his feeding trough, and he was on a sand dune, dripping sweat onto a cloak of his, with Hudhayfa b. al-Yaman sitting by him at his feet, his buttocks not touching the ground.' He said: 'People began to enter upon him congratulating him and saying: \"May the victory be pleasant for you.\"' He said: 'A man entered and did not say anything, so a man said: \"Will you not congratulate him?\" He said: \"For what?\" He said: \"His horse won.\" He said: \"I fear that news will reach the commander.\"' He said: 'At that time Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas was in charge of the people.' Then Hudhayfa said: 'By God, the Hour will not come until over you is appointed a ruler who on the Day of Resurrection will not weigh a tenth of a mosquito.'"],[10533,"The mantles of the warriors are swords."],[10534,"A head was brought to Abu Bakr, and he said: \"You have transgressed.\""],[10536,"A head was never brought to the Prophet ﷺ, not even on the day of Badr. A large head was brought to Abu Bakr, and he said: 'What have I to do with their corpses being carried to the city of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?' Then after him, during the time of the fitna, heads were not carried to Marwan or to anyone else, until the time of Ibn al-Zubayr, and he was the first to institute that. The head of Ziyad and his companions were carried to him, and they cooked their heads in pots."],[10537,"A man from the polytheists insulted the Prophet ﷺ, so he said: 'Who will suffice me against my enemy?' Al-Zubayr said: 'I will.' So al-Zubayr fought him in single combat and killed him, and the Prophet ﷺ gave him the man's spoils."],[10538,"There was a woman who used to insult the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said, \"Who will take care of my enemy for me?\" Khalid b. al-Walid went out to her and killed her."],[10539,"Ayyūb b. Yaḥyā went out to Aden, and a Christian man who had insulted the Prophet ﷺ was brought before him. He consulted about him, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Yazīd al-Ṣanʿānī advised him to kill him, so he killed him, and he narrated a ḥadīth to him about that. He said: He had met ʿUmar and heard much knowledge from him. Ayyūb wrote about that to ʿAbd al-Malik, or to al-Walīd b. ʿAbd al-Malik, and he wrote back commending that."],[10541,"Regarding the one who lied about the Prophet ﷺ: \"His neck is to be struck.\""],[10544,"Emigration has ceased after the conquest, but there remains jihad and intention, and if you are called to march, then march."],[10546,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of the conquest of Makkah: “There is no emigration, but there is jihad and intention, and if you are called to march, then march.”"],[10547,"A man was taken captive by the Daylam. They said: “We will release you and you give us a covenant and pact that you will send to us such and such; if he does not do it, he will come to them himself.” Indeed, he cannot find it. So how do you command him? He said: “He should go to them.” He said: “They are people of polytheism.” He said: “He should fulfill the covenant.” He said: “They are people of polytheism.” He said: “He should fulfill the covenant to them, for the covenant will be asked about.”"],[10548,"Fay' and ghanimah are different. As for ghanimah: it is what the Muslims took and came into their hands from the disbelievers; the fifth of that goes to the commander, and he places it wherever Allah has commanded. The remaining four-fifths belong to those who gained the spoils. As for fay': it is what resulted from a peace treaty between the imam and the disbelievers regarding their persons, their land, their crops, and what they agreed upon from what the Muslims did not take by force, nor did they seize it, nor did they overpower it, until a peace treaty occurred between them regarding it. That peace treaty belongs to the imam, and he places it wherever Allah has commanded."],[10549,"My father brought me to the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Uhud, and I was fourteen years old, but the Prophet ﷺ did not permit me. Then he brought me on the day of the Trench, and I was fifteen years old, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ assigned a stipend for me.” Nafi' said: “I narrated this to Umar b. Abd al-Aziz, and he commanded that a stipend should not be assigned except for a fifteen-year-old.”"],[10550,"I was presented to the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Uhud, and I was fourteen years old. Then he mentioned something similar to the hadith of Abdullah b. Umar. He said: Umar would not assign a stipend to anyone until he reached puberty and had a wet dream, except one hundred dirhams. And he would not assign a stipend to a newborn until he was weaned. While he was walking around the prayer place one night, a baby cried, so he said to its mother: “Nurse him.” She said: “The Commander of the Faithful does not assign a stipend to a newborn until he is weaned, and I have weaned him.” Umar said: “I almost had him killed. Nurse him, for the Commander of the Faithful will soon assign a stipend for him.” Then after that he assigned a stipend for the newborn when he was born."],[10552,"The first thing that began the revelation to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was truthful dreams; he would not see a dream except that it came like the break of dawn. Then solitude was made beloved to him, and he would go to Hira and practice tahannuth there—that is, worship for a number of nights—and he would take provisions for that. Then he would return to Khadijah and take provisions for that again, then he would return to Khadijah and take provisions for the like of it. When the truth came to him while he was in the cave of Hira, the angel came to him and said: “Read.” He said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “Read.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I said: “I am not a reader.” He took me and pressed me until it exhausted me, then he released me and said: “Read.” I said: “I am not a reader.” He took me and pressed me a second time until it exhausted me, then he released me and said: “Read.” I said: “I am not a reader.” He took me and pressed me a third time until it exhausted me, then he released me and said: “Read in the name of your Lord who created” until he reached “what he did not know.” He returned with his heart trembling, until he entered upon Khadijah and said: “Wrap me up, wrap me up.” They wrapped him until the terror left him. He said to Khadijah: “What is wrong with me?” and he told her the news. He said: “I feared for myself.” She said: “No, by Allah, Allah will never disgrace you. You maintain kinship ties, speak the truth, honor the guest, and help in times of hardship.” Then Khadijah took him and brought him to Waraqah b. Nawfal b. Rashid b. Abd al-Uzza b. Qusayy, who was the cousin of Khadijah, the brother of her father. He had become a Christian in the pre-Islamic era, and he wrote the Arabic script, and he wrote in Arabic from the Gospel as much as Allah willed. He was an old man who had gone blind. Khadijah said: “O cousin, listen to your nephew.” Waraqah said: “Nephew, what do you see?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him what he had seen. Waraqah said: “This is the Namus who was sent down to Moses ﷺ . Would that I were young when your people drive you out.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Will they drive me out?” Waraqah said: “Yes, no one has come with what you have come with except that he was opposed and harmed. If I live to see your day, I will support you with strong support.” Then Waraqah died soon after. The revelation paused for a period, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grieved, as we have heard, so much that he would go out several times wanting to throw himself from the tops of high mountains. When he climbed to the top of a mountain, Gabriel ﷺ appeared to him and said: “O Muhammad, you are truly the Messenger of Allah.” Then his heart would calm and his soul would be at ease, and he would return. If the pause of revelation became long for him, he would go back to something like that, and when he climbed to the top of a mountain, Gabriel ﷺ appeared to him and said the same thing."],[10553,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speaking about the pause of revelation, and he said in his hadith: While I was walking, I heard a voice from the sky. I raised my head, and there was the one who had come to me at Hira sitting on a chair between the sky and the earth. I was terrified by him, then I returned and said: “Wrap me up, wrap me up, and cover me.” Then Allah, Exalted, revealed: “O you wrapped in garments” up to “and shun the idols,” before prayer was made obligatory, and that is the idols."],[10554,"Khadijah died, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was shown in Paradise a house for Khadijah of hollow pearls, with no noise in it and no toil; it is the stem of pearls.” He said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about Waraqah b. Nawfal, as we have heard, and he said: “I saw him in a dream with white clothes on him, and I think that if he were from the people of the Fire, I would not have seen white on him.” He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called to Islam secretly and openly, and to abandon the idols."],[10555,"The first person to believe in him was Ali b. Abi Talib ؓ, and he was fifteen or sixteen years old."],[10557,"So whoever Allah willed from among the young men and the weak of the people responded to him, until those who believed in him became many. The disbelievers of Quraysh were deniers of what he said, saying when he passed by them in their gatherings, pointing to him: \"This youth of Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib is, they claim, spoken to from heaven.\" Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: No one from the nobles of his people followed him except two men: Abu Bakr and 'Umar (may Allah have mercy on them). 'Umar was harsh toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the believers. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"O Allah, strengthen Your religion with Ibn al-Khattab.\" The beginning of 'Umar's Islam, after many people had already embraced Islam before him, was that he was told his sister Umm Jamil bt. al-Khattab had embraced Islam, and that she had a shoulder-blade on which she had written some of the Quran, reading it in secret. He was also told that she did not eat from the dead animal that 'Umar ate from. So he entered upon her and said: \"What is this shoulder-blade that was mentioned to me, with which you read what Ibn Abi Kabshah says?\"—meaning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: \"I have no shoulder-blade.\" So 'Umar slapped her—or he said struck her—then he got up and searched for the shoulder-blade in the house until he found it. When he found it, he said: \"I have been told that you do not eat my food that I eat from.\" Then he struck her with the shoulder-blade, gashing her twice. Then he went out with the shoulder-blade until he called a reciter, and he recited to him. 'Umar did not write. When it was recited to him, his heart was moved when he heard the Quran, and Islam fell into his soul. When evening came, he went until he drew near to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying and reciting aloud. He heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting: \"You did not recite any book before it, nor did you write it with your right hand\" until he reached \"the wrongdoers.\" And he heard him reciting: \"those who disbelieve, you are not a messenger\" until he reached \"the knowledge of the Book.\" He said: So 'Umar waited for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he finished his prayer. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to his family. 'Umar quickened his pace behind him. When he saw him, he said: \"Wait for me, O Muhammad.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"I seek refuge in Allah from you.\" 'Umar said: \"Wait for me, O Muhammad, O Messenger of Allah.\" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ waited for him, and 'Umar believed in him and affirmed him. When 'Umar ؓ embraced Islam, he went until he entered upon Khalid b. al-Walid b. al-Mughirah and said: \"O my maternal uncle, bear witness that I believe in Allah and His Messenger, and I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger ﷺ. Inform your people of that.\" Al-Walid said: \"O son of my sister, be firm in your affair, for you are in a state known among people: a man wakes up in one state and ends the day in another.\" 'Umar said: \"By Allah, the matter has become clear to me, so inform your people of my Islam.\" Al-Walid said: \"I will not be the first to mention that about you.\" So 'Umar entered and sought... Then when 'Umar knew that al-Walid had not mentioned anything of his affair, he entered upon Jamil b. Ma'mar al-Jumahi and said: \"Inform that I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger.\" So Jamil b. Ma'mar got up dragging his cloak in haste, going to every gathering of Quraysh saying: \"'Umar b. al-Khattab has become a Sabian.\" Quraysh did not respond to him. 'Umar was the chief of his people, so they were in awe of denying him. When he saw that they did not deny it, he walked until he came to their gatherings, the fullest they were, and entered the Hijr and leaned his back against the Ka'bah and said: \"O assembly of Quraysh, do you know that I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger?\" So they rose up, and some men fought him fiercely. He fought them most of the day until they left him. He proclaimed his Islam and began going to them morning and evening, bearing witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. So they left him alone, and they did not leave him after their first uprising. That weighed heavily on the disbelievers of Quraysh against every man who embraced Islam. They tortured a group of Muslims."],[10559,"The obligatory prayers were imposed on the Prophet ﷺ on the night he was taken on the night journey as fifty, then they were reduced to five. Then it was called out: \"O Muhammad: 'The word in My presence is not changed,' and you have, for the five, fifty.\""],[10560,"I stood in the Hijr when my people called me a liar, and the Holy House was raised for me until I began to describe it to them."],[10561,"When he was taken on the night journey, he said: \"I met Musa.\" He described him: \"And there was a man, I think he said, with loose hair, as if he were from the men of Shanu'ah.\" He said: \"And I met 'Isa ﷺ , and he described him, saying: 'Of medium height, ruddy, as if he had just come out of a bathhouse.'\" He said: \"And I saw Ibrahim, and I am the most resembling of his offspring to him.\" He said: \"Two vessels were brought, in one of them milk and in the other wine.\" He said: \"Take whichever you wish.\" So I took the milk and drank it. It was said to me: \"You have been guided to the natural disposition—or you have attained the natural disposition. If you had taken the wine, your community would have gone astray.\""],[10564,"The scribe of the document on the day of Hudaybiyyah was 'Ali b. Abi Talib."],[10565,"I asked al-Zuhri about it, and he laughed and said: \"It was 'Ali b. Abi Talib. If you asked them—meaning the Umayyads—they would say: 'Uthman.\""],[10574,"When al-'Abbas was taken captive among the prisoners on the day of Badr, the Messenger of God ﷺ heard his groaning while he was in bonds. The Prophet ﷺ could not sleep that night, and sleep would not come to him. A man from the Ansar noticed this and said, 'O Messenger of God, you have been sleepless since the night.' He said, 'Al-'Abbas is in pain from his bonds, and that has kept me awake.' He said, 'Shall I go and loosen something for him?' He said, 'If you wish, do that on your own.' The Ansari went and loosened his bonds, and he became quiet and still, and the Messenger of God ﷺ slept."],[10581,"The Prophet ﷺ remained in Makkah for fifteen years, of which four or five he called to Islam secretly while he was afraid, until God sent against the men concerning whom He revealed: \"Indeed, We are sufficient for you against the mockers\"—\"Who made the Qur'an into parts.\" Al-'idin in the tongue of Quraysh means magic; a sorceress is called 'adihah. Then He commanded enmity toward them and said: \"Then declare what you are commanded and turn away from the polytheists.\" Then he was commanded to migrate to Madinah, and he arrived when eight nights had passed of the month of Rabi' al-Awwal. Then came the Battle of Badr. Concerning them, God revealed: \"And remember when God promised you one of the two groups.\" Concerning them was revealed: \"The gathering will be defeated.\" Concerning them was revealed: \"Until when We seize their affluent ones with punishment.\" Concerning them was revealed: \"That He might cut off a part of those who disbelieve.\" Concerning them was revealed: \"Not for you is the decision.\" God intended the people, while the Messenger of God ﷺ intended the caravan. Concerning them was revealed: \"Have you not seen those who exchanged the favor of God for disbelief\"—the verse. Concerning them was revealed: \"Have you not seen those who left their homes\"—the verse. Concerning them was revealed: \"There was certainly a sign for you in the two armies that met\"—concerning the caravan—\"and the caravan was below you\"; they took the lower part of the valley. All of this was concerning the people of Badr. Two months before Badr there was a company, the day al-Hadrami was killed. Then came Uhud, then the Day of the Confederates, two years after Uhud. Then came al-Hudaybiyah, which is the Day of the Tree. The Prophet ﷺ made peace with them on condition that he would perform 'umrah the following year in that month. Concerning that was revealed: \"The sacred month is for the sacred month.\" So the month of the first year corresponded to the month of the second year, and sacred things were subject to retribution. Then came the conquest after the 'umrah. Concerning that was revealed: \"Until when We open upon them a door of severe punishment, immediately they will be therein in despair.\" That is because the Prophet of God ﷺ raided them, and they had not prepared for war. Four men of Quraysh were killed, and of their allies from Banu Bakr, fifty or more. Concerning them was revealed when they entered the religion of God: \"And it is He who produced for you hearing and vision.\" Then he went out to Hunayn after twenty nights, then to al-Ta'if, then returned to Madinah. Then he appointed Abu Bakr over the Hajj, then the Messenger of God ﷺ performed Hajj the following year, then bade farewell to the people, then returned and passed away when two nights had passed of the month of Rabi'. When Abu Bakr returned from Hajj, the Messenger of God ﷺ raided Tabuk."],[10585,"O God, I adjure You by Your covenant and Your promise. O God, if You will that You not be worshiped... While they were in that state, the Prophet ﷺ sent to 'Uyaynah b. Hisn b. Badr al-Fazari, who was then the chief of the polytheists of Ghatafan and was with Abu Sufyan: 'What do you think if I give you a third of the produce of the Ansar; will you withdraw with those of Ghatafan who are with you and cause dissension among the confederates?' 'Uyaynah sent back: 'If you give me half, I will do it.' The Prophet sent for Sa'd b. Mu'adh, the chief of the Aws, and Sa'd b. 'Ubadah, the chief of the Khazraj, and said to them, 'Uyaynah b. Hisn has asked me for half of your produce on condition that he withdraw with those of Ghatafan who are with him and cause dissension among the confederates. I offered him a third, but he refused except half. What do you think?' They said, 'O Messenger of God, if you have been commanded something, carry out God's command.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said, 'If I had been commanded something, I would not consult you. But this is my opinion; I present it to you.' They said, 'We do not think we should give him anything but the sword.' He said, 'Good then.' Ma'mar said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me that they said to him, 'By God, O Messenger of God, when God brought Islam, we used to give them that.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Good then.' Al-Zuhri said in his hadith from Ibn al-Musayyab: While they were thus, Nu'aym b. Mas'ud al-Ashja'i came to them. Both parties trusted him; he had a truce with them. He said, 'I was with 'Uyaynah and Abu Sufyan when the messenger of Banu Qurayzah came to them: \"Hold firm, for we will come upon the Muslims from behind their homes.\"' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Perhaps we ordered them to do that.' Nu'aym was a man who did not conceal a conversation, so he acted upon the word of the Prophet ﷺ. 'Umar came to him and said, 'O Messenger of God, if this matter is from God, carry it out. If it is an opinion of yours, then the affair of Quraysh and Banu Qurayzah is less than that anyone should have a say over you in it.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Bring the man back.' They brought him back, and he said, 'Consider what we mentioned to you; do not mention it to anyone, for that only incited him.' He went until he came to 'Uyaynah and Abu Sufyan and said, 'Have you ever heard Muhammad say a statement except that it was true?' They said, 'No.' He said, 'When I mentioned to him the matter of Qurayzah, he said, \"Perhaps we ordered them to do that.\"' Abu Sufyan said, 'We will know that if it is a scheme.' He sent to Banu Qurayzah: 'You ordered us to hold firm and said you would come upon the Muslims from behind their homes. Give us hostages for that.' They said, 'The night of the Sabbath has begun, and we do not decide anything on the Sabbath.' Abu Sufyan said, 'You are in a scheme from Banu Qurayzah, so depart.' God sent the wind upon them and cast terror into their hearts. It extinguished their fires and cut the ropes of their horses. They departed defeated without fighting. He said: That is when He says: \"And God sufficed the believers in battle, and God is ever Strong and Mighty.\" He said: The Prophet ﷺ called upon his companions to pursue them, so they pursued them until they reached Hamra' al-Asad. He said: Then they returned. He said: The Prophet ﷺ laid down his armor, bathed, and used perfume. Then Gabriel called to the Prophet ﷺ: 'Your excuse from a fighter! Do I not see that you have laid down your armor while we have not yet laid ours down?' The Prophet ﷺ stood up in alarm and said to his companions, 'I adjure you not to pray 'Asr until you reach Banu Qurayzah.' The sun set before they reached them. A group of the Muslims said, 'The Prophet ﷺ did not intend that you abandon prayer, so pray.' Another group said, 'We are under the Prophet's ﷺ adjuration, and there is no harm upon us.' One group prayed out of faith and seeking reward, and another group omitted it out of faith and seeking reward. He said: The Prophet ﷺ did not blame either of the two parties. The Prophet ﷺ went out and passed by gatherings between him and Banu Qurayzah and said, 'Has anyone passed by you?' They said, 'Yes, Dihyah al-Kalbi passed by us on a gray mule with a brocade saddlecloth beneath him.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'That was not he, but Gabriel, sent to Banu Qurayzah to shake their fortresses and cast terror into their hearts.' The companions of the Prophet ﷺ besieged them. When the companions of the Prophet ﷺ arrived, he ordered them to cover him with their shields to protect him from stones so he could hear their words. They did so, and he called to them, 'O brothers of apes and pigs.' They said, 'O Abu al-Qasim, you were not one to use obscene language.' He called them to Islam before fighting them, but they refused to answer him to Islam. The Messenger of God ﷺ fought them and those with him of the Muslims until they submitted to the judgment of Sa'd b. Mu'adh, and they refused to submit to the judgment of the Prophet ﷺ. They submitted to a disease, and they were brought forward while Sa'd b. Mu'adh was carried on a donkey until they reached the Messenger of God ﷺ. Qurayzah began reminding him of their alliance, and Sa'd b. Mu'adh kept turning to the Messenger of God ﷺ seeking instruction, waiting for him regarding what he intended to judge, and the Messenger of God ﷺ would answer him, intending to say, 'Proceed with what I am judging.' The Messenger of God ﷺ kept saying, 'Yes.' Sa'd said, 'I judge that their warriors be killed, their wealth be divided, and their women and children be taken captive.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'You have hit upon the right judgment.' He said: Huyayy b. Akhtab had summoned the polytheists against the Messenger of God ﷺ and then came to Banu Qurayzah. He sought to enter upon them at night, but their chief said, 'This is an ill-omened man; do not let Huyayy bring misfortune upon you.' He called to them, 'O Banu Qurayzah, will you not respond? Will you not admit me? Will you not host me? I am a gathering deceived.' Banu Qurayzah said, 'By God, we will open for him.' They continued until they opened for him. When he entered their fortress, he said, 'O Banu Qurayzah, I have come to you in the might of time. I have come to you with a cold cloud that nothing can withstand.' Their chief said to him, 'You promise us a passing cold cloud that will lift from us, and you leave us at a lasting sea that will not leave us. You only promise us deception.' He said: He made a covenant with them and pledged that if the gatherings of the confederates dispersed, he would come and enter their fortress with them. They obeyed him then with treachery against the Prophet ﷺ and the Muslims. When God dispersed the gatherings of the confederates, he set out until he was at al-Rawha', then he remembered the covenant and pact he had given them, so he returned and entered with them. When Banu Qurayzah were brought, he was brought bound with leather straps. Huyayy said to the Prophet ﷺ, 'Indeed, by God, I do not blame myself for my enmity toward you, but whoever God forsakes will be forsaken.' The Prophet ﷺ ordered him, and his neck was struck."],[10586,"O God, I adjure You by Your covenant and Your promise. O God, if You will that You not be worshiped... While they were in that state, the Prophet ﷺ sent to 'Uyaynah b. Hisn b. Badr al-Fazari, who was then the chief of the polytheists of Ghatafan and was with Abu Sufyan: 'What do you think if I give you a third of the produce of the Ansar; will you withdraw with those of Ghatafan who are with you and cause dissension among the confederates?' 'Uyaynah sent back: 'If you give me half, I will do it.' The Prophet sent for Sa'd b. Mu'adh, the chief of the Aws, and Sa'd b. 'Ubadah, the chief of the Khazraj, and said to them, 'Uyaynah b. Hisn has asked me for half of your produce on condition that he withdraw with those of Ghatafan who are with him and cause dissension among the confederates. I offered him a third, but he refused except half. What do you think?' They said, 'O Messenger of God, if you have been commanded something, carry out God's command.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said, 'If I had been commanded something, I would not consult you. But this is my opinion; I present it to you.' They said, 'We do not think we should give him anything but the sword.' He said, 'Good then.' Ma'mar said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me that they said to him, 'By God, O Messenger of God, when God brought Islam, we used to give them that.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Good then.' Al-Zuhri said in his hadith from Ibn al-Musayyab: While they were thus, Nu'aym b. Mas'ud al-Ashja'i came to them. Both parties trusted him; he had a truce with them. He said, 'I was with 'Uyaynah and Abu Sufyan when the messenger of Banu Qurayzah came to them: \"Hold firm, for we will come upon the Muslims from behind their homes.\"' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Perhaps we ordered them to do that.' Nu'aym was a man who did not conceal a conversation, so he acted upon the word of the Prophet ﷺ. 'Umar came to him and said, 'O Messenger of God, if this matter is from God, carry it out. If it is an opinion of yours, then the affair of Quraysh and Banu Qurayzah is less than that anyone should have a say over you in it.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Bring the man back.' They brought him back, and he said, 'Consider what we mentioned to you; do not mention it to anyone, for that only incited him.' He went until he came to 'Uyaynah and Abu Sufyan and said, 'Have you ever heard Muhammad say a statement except that it was true?' They said, 'No.' He said, 'When I mentioned to him the matter of Qurayzah, he said, \"Perhaps we ordered them to do that.\"' Abu Sufyan said, 'We will know that if it is a scheme.' He sent to Banu Qurayzah: 'You ordered us to hold firm and said you would come upon the Muslims from behind their homes. Give us hostages for that.' They said, 'The night of the Sabbath has begun, and we do not decide anything on the Sabbath.' Abu Sufyan said, 'You are in a scheme from Banu Qurayzah, so depart.' God sent the wind upon them and cast terror into their hearts. It extinguished their fires and cut the ropes of their horses. They departed defeated without fighting. He said: That is when He says: \"And God sufficed the believers in battle, and God is ever Strong and Mighty.\" He said: The Prophet ﷺ called upon his companions to pursue them, so they pursued them until they reached Hamra' al-Asad. He said: Then they returned. He said: The Prophet ﷺ laid down his armor, bathed, and used perfume. Then Gabriel called to the Prophet ﷺ: 'Your excuse from a fighter! Do I not see that you have laid down your armor while we have not yet laid ours down?' The Prophet ﷺ stood up in alarm and said to his companions, 'I adjure you not to pray 'Asr until you reach Banu Qurayzah.' The sun set before they reached them. A group of the Muslims said, 'The Prophet ﷺ did not intend that you abandon prayer, so pray.' Another group said, 'We are under the Prophet's ﷺ adjuration, and there is no harm upon us.' One group prayed out of faith and seeking reward, and another group omitted it out of faith and seeking reward. He said: The Prophet ﷺ did not blame either of the two parties. The Prophet ﷺ went out and passed by gatherings between him and Banu Qurayzah and said, 'Has anyone passed by you?' They said, 'Yes, Dihyah al-Kalbi passed by us on a gray mule with a brocade saddlecloth beneath him.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'That was not he, but Gabriel, sent to Banu Qurayzah to shake their fortresses and cast terror into their hearts.' The companions of the Prophet ﷺ besieged them. When the companions of the Prophet ﷺ arrived, he ordered them to cover him with their shields to protect him from stones so he could hear their words. They did so, and he called to them, 'O brothers of apes and pigs.' They said, 'O Abu al-Qasim, you were not one to use obscene language.' He called them to Islam before fighting them, but they refused to answer him to Islam. The Messenger of God ﷺ fought them and those with him of the Muslims until they submitted to the judgment of Sa'd b. Mu'adh, and they refused to submit to the judgment of the Prophet ﷺ. They submitted to a disease, and they were brought forward while Sa'd b. Mu'adh was carried on a donkey until they reached the Messenger of God ﷺ. Qurayzah began reminding him of their alliance, and Sa'd b. Mu'adh kept turning to the Messenger of God ﷺ seeking instruction, waiting for him regarding what he intended to judge, and the Messenger of God ﷺ would answer him, intending to say, 'Proceed with what I am judging.' The Messenger of God ﷺ kept saying, 'Yes.' Sa'd said, 'I judge that their warriors be killed, their wealth be divided, and their women and children be taken captive.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'You have hit upon the right judgment.' He said: Huyayy b. Akhtab had summoned the polytheists against the Messenger of God ﷺ and then came to Banu Qurayzah. He sought to enter upon them at night, but their chief said, 'This is an ill-omened man; do not let Huyayy bring misfortune upon you.' He called to them, 'O Banu Qurayzah, will you not respond? Will you not admit me? Will you not host me? I am a gathering deceived.' Banu Qurayzah said, 'By God, we will open for him.' They continued until they opened for him. When he entered their fortress, he said, 'O Banu Qurayzah, I have come to you in the might of time. I have come to you with a cold cloud that nothing can withstand.' Their chief said to him, 'You promise us a passing cold cloud that will lift from us, and you leave us at a lasting sea that will not leave us. You only promise us deception.' He said: He made a covenant with them and pledged that if the gatherings of the confederates dispersed, he would come and enter their fortress with them. They obeyed him then with treachery against the Prophet ﷺ and the Muslims. When God dispersed the gatherings of the confederates, he set out until he was at al-Rawha', then he remembered the covenant and pact he had given them, so he returned and entered with them. When Banu Qurayzah were brought, he was brought bound with leather straps. Huyayy said to the Prophet ﷺ, 'Indeed, by God, I do not blame myself for my enmity toward you, but whoever God forsakes will be forsaken.' The Prophet ﷺ ordered him, and his neck was struck."],[10615,"The Messenger of God ﷺ obligated zakat al-Fitr on male and female, free and slave: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley.' Ibn Umar said: 'Afterwards people equated it with two mudds of wheat.' Ibn Umar said: 'He liked to give dates.'"],[10618,"Pour upon me from seven waterskins whose mouths have not been untied, so that I may make a covenant with the people. Aisha said: So we seated him in a copper washing tub belonging to Hafsa, and we poured water upon him from them until he began gesturing to us that you have done enough, then he went out."],[10647,"When allegiance was pledged to Abu Bakr, Ali stayed behind in his house. Umar met him and said: Have you withheld from pledging allegiance to Abu Bakr? He said: I swore an oath when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died not to put on a cloak except for the prescribed prayer until I compile the Quran, for I feared the Quran would slip away. Then he went out and pledged allegiance to him."],[10648,"I asked Ibn Umar about Ali and Uthman. He said: As for Ali, this is his house—meaning his house is near the house of the Prophet ﷺ in the mosque—and I will tell you about him—meaning Uthman. As for Uthman, may Allah have mercy on him, he committed a great sin between himself and Allah, and He forgave him; but he committed a small sin between himself and you, and you killed him."],[10649,"When allegiance was pledged to Abu Bakr ؓ, Abu Sufyan came to Ali and said: The most humble family in Quraysh has overcome you in this matter. By Allah, I will fill it with cavalry and infantry. He said: I said: You have always been an enemy of Islam and its people, but that has not harmed Islam or its people. We saw Abu Bakr as worthy of it."],[10650,"A man said to Ali: Tell me about Quraysh. He said: The most forbearing among us are our brothers the Banu Umayyah, and the most courageous at the meeting, and the most generous with what the right hand possesses ... the Banu Hashim, and the aromatic herb of Quraysh among whom we smell is Banu al-Mughirah. Away from me with the rest today."],[10651,"A man said to Ali: Tell me about Quraysh. He said: As for us, Banu Hashim, we are courageous and glorious, guiding and generous. As for our brothers Banu Umayyah, they are refined and defenders. The aromatic herb of Quraysh among whom we smell is Banu al-Mughirah."],[10668,"When Umar was stabbed, he summoned Ali, Uthman, Abd al-Rahman b. Awf, and al-Zubayr — he said: I think he also said Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas — and said: 'I have considered the people's affair and have not seen discord among them. If there is discord, it is among you. Then your people will appoint one of you, whichever of the three. If you are charged with anything of the people's affair, O Ali, fear Allah and do not burden Banu Hashim on the people's necks.' Ma'mar said: Someone other than al-Zuhri said: 'Do not burden Banu Abi Rukanah on the people's necks.' Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said in his hadith, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: 'And if you, O Uthman, are charged with anything, fear Allah and do not burden Banu Abi Mu'ayt on the people's necks. And if you are charged with anything of the people's affairs, O Abd al-Rahman, fear Allah and do not burden your relatives on the people's necks. So consult one another, then appoint one of yourselves.' He said: They rose to consult. Abd Allah b. Umar said: Uthman called me and consulted me, though Umar did not include me in the shura. When they called me repeatedly, I said: 'Will you not fear Allah? Will you appoint while the Commander of the Believers is still alive?' He said: It was as if I woke Umar, so he called them and said: 'Wait. Let Suhayb lead the people in prayer, then consult, then settle your matter among the three, and gather the commanders of the armies. Whoever of you takes command without consultation of the Muslims, kill him.' Ibn Umar said: 'By Allah, I would not love to have been with them, for I rarely saw Umar move his lips except that some of what he said came to pass.' Al-Zuhri said: When Umar died, they gathered, and Abd al-Rahman b. Awf said to them: 'If you wish, I will choose for you from among you,' and they entrusted that to him. Al-Miswar said: I have never seen anyone like Abd al-Rahman. By Allah, he did not leave anyone among the Muhajirun and the Ansar, nor any other person of sound judgment, without consulting them that night."],[10673,"On the day of al-Qadisiyyah, Qays b. Makshuh al-Absi was over the cavalry, al-Mughirah b. Shu'bah al-Thaqafi over the infantry, and Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas over the people. Qays said: 'I have witnessed the day of al-Yarmuk, the day of Ajnadayn, the day of Abs, and the day of Fahl, but I have never seen anything like today in numbers, iron weapons, or readiness for battle. By Allah, their two flanks cannot be seen.' Al-Mughirah b. Shu'bah said: 'This is foam from the foam of Satan. If we charge them, Allah will throw some of them upon others. Do not let me find you, when I charge them with my infantry, charging them with your cavalry at their rear. Instead, hold back your cavalry from us and charge those near you.' He said: A man stood up and said: 'Allah is greatest! I can see the ground behind them.' Al-Mughirah said: 'Sit down. Standing and speaking at the time of battle is failure. When one of you wants to... in the socket of his spear.' Then he said: 'I will shake my mount three times. When I shake it the first time, get ready. When I shake it the third time, get ready for the charge' — or he said: 'charge, for I am charging.' He said: He shook it the third time, then charged, and he had on two coats of mail. He said: We did not reach his person until he met them with two thrusts and slipped between them, and the victory came. He said: Allah threw some of them upon others until they were in heaps, and we did not wish to take two men from them and kill them except that we did."],[10676,"She watched the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he kept supplicating for them both specifically, not associating anyone with them in his supplication, until he disappeared into his rooms. Thus in the edition of al-Maktab al-Islami and the manuscript, and more than one transmitted it from the author likewise; the correct reading is: (Hanzalah b. Sabrah). See the sources of extraction."],[10677,"That Ali, when he married Fatimah, she said to the Prophet ﷺ: 'You married me to a small-eyed man with a big belly?' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'I have married you to him, and he is the first of my companions to submit, the most knowledgeable of them, and the greatest of them in forbearance.'"],[10680,"Two men from Muzaynah were wicked men who had cut off the road and killed. The Prophet ﷺ passed by them, so they performed ablution and prayed, then gave allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we had intended to come to you, but Allah shortened our steps!' He said: 'What are your names?' They said: 'The two humiliated.' He said: 'Rather, you are the two honored.'"],[10682,"I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with my hand, and he stipulated upon me sincerity to every Muslim, for indeed I am sincere to you."],[10683,"He saw the Prophet ﷺ pledging allegiance to the people on the day of the conquest. He said: He sat near Qarn Masqalah, and Qarn Masqalah is the one towards which the houses of Ibn Abi Umamah pour, and it is the house of Ibn Samurah and what is around it, and the one into which what recedes from it pours upon the house of Ibn Amir, and what approaches from it upon the house of Ibn Samurah and what is around it. Al-Aswad said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ sat by it, and the people came to him, young and old and women, and they pledged allegiance to him upon Islam and the testimony. I said: What is the testimony? He said: Muhammad b. al-Aswad informed me that he pledged allegiance to them upon faith in Allah and the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His messenger."],[10684,"That when he pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, he took upon him not to associate anything with Allah, to establish the prayer, to give the zakah, to be sincere to the Muslim, and to part with the polytheist."],[10685,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pledge allegiance to us upon hearing and obedience, then he would instruct us: \"In what you are able.\""],[10686,"When the people pledged allegiance to Abd al-Malik b. Marwan, Ibn Umar wrote to him: \"To proceed: I acknowledge hearing and obedience to Abdullah Abd al-Malik, Commander of the Believers, upon the Sunnah of Allah and the Sunnah of His Messenger ﷺ in what I am able, and my sons have acknowledged the like of that. Peace.\""],[10689,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pledge allegiance to the people by speech with this verse: \"That they do not associate anything with Allah,\" and the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never touched the hand of a woman, except a woman he possessed."],[10690,"I came among women to pledge allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, and he stipulated upon us not to commit fornication, not to steal, and this verse. She said: So we pledged allegiance to him, and the Prophet ﷺ stipulated upon us, saying: \"In what you are able and capable.\" She said: We said: Allah and His Messenger are more merciful to us than ourselves! She said: We said: Shall we not shake your hand, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I do not shake hands with women; my saying to one woman is like my saying to a hundred women."],[10691,"Fatimah bt. Utbah b. Rabiah came to pledge allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, and he took upon her not to associate anything with Allah—the verse. She said: She placed her hand on her head out of modesty, and what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw of her pleased him. Aishah said: Accept, O woman, for by Allah he did not pledge allegiance to us except upon this. She said: Then yes, so he pledged allegiance to her upon the verse."],[10692,"The Prophet ﷺ used to make them swear that they did not come out except out of desire for Islam and love for Allah and His Messenger ﷺ."],[10693,"The Prophet ﷺ took a pledge from the women when they gave allegiance that they would not wail. They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, women helped us in the Jahiliyyah, so should we help them in Islam?\" He said: \"There is no helping in Islam, no shighar marriage in Islam, no hamstringing in Islam, no jalab, no janab, and whoever plunders is not one of us.\""],[10694,"The Prophet ﷺ took upon the women when he pledged allegiance to them not to wail and not to seclude themselves with men's talk."],[10695,"The Prophet ﷺ used to take the pledge from them, and he would say: 'I do not shake hands with women.'"],[10696,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to shake hands with women with a cloth over his hand."],[10698,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ while I wanted to embrace Islam, so I embraced Islam. The Prophet ﷺ then ordered me to perform ghusl with water and lote-tree leaves, so I performed ghusl with water and lote-tree leaves."],[10699,"Thumamah al-Hanafi was taken prisoner, and the Prophet ﷺ would go to him in the morning and say: 'What do you have, O Thumamah?' He would reply: 'If you kill, you kill one whose blood will be avenged; if you show favour, you show favour to one who is grateful; and if you want wealth, you will be given from it whatever you wish.' The companions of the Prophet ﷺ were fond of ransom and would say: 'What would we do by killing this man?' One day the Prophet ﷺ passed by him and he embraced Islam, so he released him and sent him to the garden of Abu Talhah, ordering him to perform ghusl. He performed ghusl and prayed two rak'ahs. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Your brother's Islam has become excellent.'"],[10700,"He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'I have embraced Islam!' The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Cast off the hair of disbelief and get circumcised,' meaning: shave. Another narrator informed me from him that the Prophet ﷺ said to another man: 'Cast off the hair of disbelief and get circumcised.'"],[10701,"Regarding the one who embraces Islam: 'He is ordered to perform ghusl.'"],[10703,"When you meet the polytheists on a road, do not initiate the greeting of salam with them, and force them to its narrowest side."],[10704,"We were commanded not to say more to the People of the Book than 'And upon you.'"],[10709,"When you pass by a gathering in which there are Muslims and disbelievers, greet them."],[10710,"He was with Abdullah on a journey, and some people from the People of the Book accompanied him. When they parted from him, he said, \"Where are you going?\" They said, \"Here.\" So he followed them and greeted them."],[10711,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by a gathering in which there was a mix of Muslims, Jews, and polytheists, and he greeted them."],[10714,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to Heraclius: \"In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, the great one of the Romans. Peace be upon whoever follows guidance.\""],[10720,"The people of two different religions do not inherit from each other."],[10721,"I said, \"O Messenger of Allah, where will you stay tomorrow?\" That was during the Prophet's ﷺ farewell pilgrimage. He said, \"Has 'Aqil b. Abi Talib left us any place?\" Then he said, \"A Muslim does not inherit from a disbeliever, nor does a disbeliever inherit from a Muslim.\" Then he said, \"We will stay tomorrow at Khayf Bani Kinanah, where Quraysh swore an oath upon disbelief.\""],[10723,"Abu Talib was inherited by 'Aqil and Talib, and 'Ali did not inherit anything from him. He said, \"Because of that we left our share of the mountain pass.\""],[10724,"Abu Talib was inherited by 'Aqil and Talib, and 'Ali did not inherit anything from him. He said, \"Because of that we left our share of the mountain pass.\""],[10726,"The people of polytheism: we do not inherit from them, nor do they inherit from us."],[10727,"The people of two different religions do not inherit from each other."],[10728,"A paternal aunt of his who was Jewish died, so he went to Umar b. al-Khattab seeking her inheritance, but Umar refused to make him inherit from her, and gave the inheritance to the Jews."],[10729,"A paternal aunt of his who was Jewish died in Yemen, and al-Ash'ath b. Qays mentioned that to Umar b. al-Khattab, so Umar said: 'No one inherits from her except the people of her religion.'"],[10732,"O Commander of the Faithful, my sister was married to one of the maqawil (chiefs), and he made her a Jewess. She has died; who inherits from her? Umar said: 'The people of her religion.'"],[10733,"The people of two different religions do not inherit from each other."],[10734,"The people of two different religions do not inherit from each other."],[10736,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz freed a Christian slave of his, who then died. He ordered me to place his inheritance in the bayt al-mal (public treasury)."],[10738,"He heard Ikrimah when he was asked about a man who manumitted a Christian slave of his, then the slave died and left wealth, and he said: \"His inheritance belongs to the people of his religion.\""],[10739,"If a Christian slave of yours dies and you find gold in kind from him, the price of wine, then take it; but if you find wine and pork, then do not."],[10740,"A Muslim does not inherit from a disbeliever as long as he has a relative from the people of his religion. If he has no inheriting relative, then the Muslims inherit from him by virtue of Islam."],[10741,"The people of two different religions do not inherit from each other."],[10743,"Whoever embraces Islam at the hands of a man, he is his mawla. Ibn al-Mubarak said: And he inherits from him if he has no heir. So I mentioned it to al-Thawri, and he said: \"He inherits from him; he is more entitled than others.\""],[10747,"Regarding a man who came from the people of shirk and embraced Islam, and allied himself with a man, he said: \"He has his wala and his inheritance, and he may not ally himself with anyone else.\""],[10749,"Regarding His saying: \"And if you fear poverty, Allah will enrich you from His bounty,\" he said: \"Allah enriched them with the jizyah, which flows month by month and year by year.\""],[10757,"No jizyah is upon them; their protection is the protection of the Muslims."],[10759,"It reached Umar b. al-Khattab that his governors were taking jizyah from wine, so he adjured them three times. Bilal said: \"They indeed do that.\" He said: \"Do not do that, but put them in charge of selling it; for the Jews were forbidden fats, yet they sold them and consumed their price.\""],[10762,"If a Muslim dies leaving a Christian child, his inheritance is not divided until his Christian child embraces Islam – then he has no right; the inheritance took effect before he embraced Islam. The same applies to a slave whose father died free: his inheritance is not divided until he is freed."],[10763,"Whoever embraces Islam after the inheritance has taken effect, having not been Muslim before, has no right to it, because the inheritances took effect before he embraced Islam. The slave is in the same position."],[10765,"Concerning a Jewish household whose father died and whose inheritance had not been divided until they embraced Islam: 'It is not according to the division of Islam; the inheritances came into effect before they embraced Islam.'"],[10766,"If a Muslim dies leaving a child ... a Muslim and a disbeliever, and his inheritance is not divided until the disbeliever embraces Islam, he inherits together with the believer; they both inherit. But I did not like what he said. And someone said to me: That is the inheritance of the people of the Jahiliyyah. Whatever Islam came upon and was not divided is according to the division of Islam. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: No, the inheritances came into effect in Islam, and someone other than me said that."],[10767,"Whatever was subject to division in the Jahiliyyah remains according to the division of the Jahiliyyah, and whatever Islam came upon and had not been divided is according to the division of Islam."],[10769,"When a man dies leaving his son a slave or a Christian, and the son is then manumitted, if the inheritance has not been divided, then it is his—meaning he inherits."],[10770,"When a man dies leaving his son a slave, and the son is manumitted before the inheritance is divided, he receives nothing."],[10771,"When a claimant to an inheritance embraces Islam after the death of the owner of the inheritance, he receives nothing from it."],[10772,"Ata said to me—and I asked him—and he said: If two parents are Christians and their father embraces Islam while they have young children, and then their children die leaving wealth, their Muslim father does not inherit from them; rather their mother inherits from them, and what remains goes to the people of their religion. I said: But they are young and have no religion. He said: But they were born in Christianity upon Christianity. And he had once told me: The Muslim inherits from them, his share of inheritance from their father. And I do not think he ceased to say: Their young child inherits from both of them, and they both inherit from him until a religion either unites them or separates them. I discussed this with Amr b. Dinar, saying: But their parents are Christians! He said: I would give their wealth to their child. I said to Amr: How, when the child is upon the fitrah? He said: Then why are the children of the polytheists taken captive? They are upon the fitrah, and they are Muslims."],[10774,"Concerning two Christians who have a young child between them and one of them embraces Islam, he said: The Muslim has a better right to him; they both inherit from him and he inherits from both of them."],[10776,"The established practice among our predecessors, which is acted upon and not doubted, and upon which we are now, is that two Christians who have their young child between them inherit from him, and he inherits from them, until a religion either separates them or unites them. If his mother embraces Islam, she inherits from him according to the Book of God, and what remains goes to the Muslims. If his father is a Christian while he is young, and he has a maternal brother who is Muslim or a maternal sister who is Muslim, his brother or sister inherits from him according to the Book of God, and then what remains goes to the Muslims. He said: Funeral prayer is not performed over the children of a Christian, nor do we console him for them, nor do they follow them to their graves; they bury them in their own cemetery. He said: If a Muslim kills one of their children intentionally, he is not executed for it, and his blood money is the blood money of a Christian. I said to Sulayman: What about a young child between two polytheists, when one of them embraces Islam while their child is young, and then their father dies? He said: Their child inherits from the Muslim parent, and the disbelieving parent does not inherit from either of them. At that point, the inheritance is between the Muslim and the child, and the child does not then inherit from the disbelieving parent."],[10777,"Concerning two Christians who have a young child between them and one of them embraces Islam, he said: The Muslim has a better right to him; they both inherit from him and he inherits from both of them."],[10780,"If a Magian marries his daughter and she bears him two daughters, then he dies, and then they embrace Islam, and one of his daughters dies—her full sister receives half, and her mother receives one-sixth; she is excluded by herself because she is the sister of her daughter, and her remaining daughter, who is her daughter's sister, also excludes her. Then the mother also receives what the paternal sister would receive.\" And al-Thawri said something similar to their statement: \"Her full sister receives half, and the paternal sister receives one-sixth to complete the two-thirds, and she also receives one-sixth because she is the mother who excludes herself; and because she is a sister, she receives one-third.\" Al-Thawri said: \"This is the statement of Ibrahim: 'They inherit from two capacities.'\""],[10781,"Regarding a Christian who dies while his wife is pregnant, then she embraces Islam before giving birth, then she gives birth and dies. He said: \"Their child inherits from both of them together, because he acquired the inheritance of his father when his father died, and then his mother died, so he followed her in her religion and inherited from her.\""],[10785,"Regarding a Magian who marries his sister, and she bears him a daughter, then they embrace Islam, and then he dies—he said: \"His daughter inherits half, and half goes to his sister, because she is an 'asaba.\" And he said regarding a Magian who marries his mother, and she bears him two daughters, then they embrace Islam, and the man dies: \"His two daughters receive two-thirds, and his mother receives one-sixth. Then one of the daughters dies; her sister inherits half, and the mother became both mother and grandmother, so she excludes herself, and we give her the inheritance of a mother, and we do not give her the inheritance of a grandmother.\" And he says: \"When they embraced Islam, the marriage was dissolved for him, so it is not appropriate for him to remain married to his mother or his sister after Islam; we give him inheritance on the basis of kinship.\""],[10786,"That 'Ali used to give the Magian inheritance from two capacities."],[10788,"Whoever steals wine from the People of the Book is to have his hand cut off."],[10791,"Safiyya, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, sold a house of hers to Mu'awiya for one hundred thousand. She said to a Jewish relative of hers: \"Embrace Islam, for if you embrace Islam, you will inherit from me!\" But he refused, so she bequeathed to him. Some of them said: Thirty thousand."],[10792,"That Safiyya bt. Huyayy bequeathed to a Jewish nephew of hers."],[10793,"The bequest of a Muslim to a Christian is valid."],[10794,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What is the meaning of His saying: “unless you do a kindness to your allies”? He said: “Giving.” I said to him: “Is it a believer giving to a disbeliever between whom there is kinship?” He said: “Yes, his giving to him while alive and his bequest to him.”"],[10796,"Regarding His saying: “unless you do a kindness to your allies,” he said: “Unless you have a relative who is not of your religion, and you bequeath something to him. He is your ally in lineage, but not your ally in religion.” He said: “And al-Ḥasan said the like of it.”"],[10798,"A Muslim may visit a disbeliever and say: “How are you this morning? How are you this evening?” Then when he leaves he says: “O God, destroy him, give the Muslims relief from him, and spare them his burden.”"],[10799,"If there is close kinship between a Muslim and a disbeliever, the Muslim should visit the disbeliever.” ʿAmr b. Dīnār said it as his own opinion."],[10800,"“unless you fear them out of piety” — he said: “Unless there is kinship between him and him, so he maintains ties with him for that reason.”"],[10801,"We visit the Christians even if there is no kinship between us and them."],[10802,"Abū Ṭālib fell ill, and the Messenger of God ﷺ came to visit him."],[10804,"If there is close kinship between a Muslim and a disbeliever, he should follow his funeral procession.” ʿAmr said it as his own opinion."],[10805,"The mother of al-Ḥārith b. Abī Rabīʿa died, and she was a Christian, and the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ escorted her funeral."],[10806,"A man asked Ibn Umar: “My mother died while she was a Christian; should I attend her burial?” Ibn Umar said to him: “Walk ahead of it, for you are not with it.”"],[10808,"The mother of Khalid b. Abd Allah al-Qasri died, and she was a Christian. He summoned the Christian bishops in Damascus and said: “Do for her what you do for the daughters of your kings, for she is one of the daughters of kings.” He said: “And he ordered his wives, and they were the ones who attended to her, and they were the ones who instructed the [other] women.” He said: “When they finished and she was carried, he mounted, and the notable people mounted with him, and he walked alongside her. When he reached the grave with her, he turned his mount’s head away and said: ‘This is the last kindness we can show to the mother of Jarir.’ Then he said: ‘Indeed I did for her only what Abd Allah b. Abi Zakariyya did for his mother.’” Muhammad said: “Abd Allah b. Abi Zakariyya was among the devout, jurists, and notables of the people of Syria. Makhul used to learn from him.”"],[10809,"The Prophet ﷺ followed the funeral of Abu Talib, walking alongside it, and did not pray over him, saying: “May kinship reach you, and may you be rewarded with good.” He said: An additional text: “And he did not stand at his grave.”"],[10810,"Do not follow their funerals, even if there is kinship between you and them."],[10812,"It has reached me that al-Harith b. Abi Rabi'a did not follow the funeral of his mother, and al-Harith’s mother was a disbeliever."],[10816,"When Abu Talib died, Ali went to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, your misguided old uncle has died; who will bury him?” The Prophet ﷺ said to him: “Go and bury your father, and when you finish, do not do anything until you come to me.” He said: “So I came to him, and he ordered me, and I performed ghusl. Then he supplicated for me with supplications such that nothing on earth would please me more than them.”"],[10817,"The father of a man died, and he was a Jew, so his son did not follow him. That was mentioned to Ibn Abbas, and Ibn Abbas said: “What harm would it have done him if he had washed him, followed him, and sought forgiveness for him while he was alive?”—meaning he would have supplicated for him while the father was alive. He said: “Then Ibn Abbas recited: ‘But when it became clear to him that he was an enemy of Allah, he disassociated himself from him,’ saying: ‘when he died upon his disbelief.’”"],[10818,"The Prophet ﷺ came to Abd Allah b. Ubayy b. Salul after he had been placed in his grave, and he met him, and he ordered that he be taken out. He placed him on his knees, put his own shirt on him, and blew on him from his saliva—and Allah knows best."],[10820,"Al-'Abbas said to the Messenger of God ﷺ, 'What have you availed your uncle Abu Talib? For he used to protect you and become angry for you.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'He is in a shallow part of the Fire, and were it not for me, he would have been in the lowest depth of the Fire.'"],[10821,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote concerning those of the slaves of the People of the Covenant who embraced Islam: 'They are to be sold.'"],[10825,"If a Jew or Christian were with me and died, and there was no one of his own religion with him, then I would bury him and not leave the beasts to eat him, but I would not wash him or pray over him."],[10828,"They used to follow our funeral processions."],[10829,"Solomon son of David died, and the Jews and Christians followed him along with the Muslims. Ma'mar said, 'There is no harm in it.'"],[10831,"A Muslim may offer condolences to a dhimmi, saying, 'To God belongs dominion and might. Live, O son of Adam, as long as you live; death is inevitable.'"],[10837,"The disbeliever does not carry the Muslim's bier. Amr b. Dinar said: He may carry his bier."],[10838,"The disbeliever does not carry the Muslim's bier. Amr b. Dinar said: He may carry his bier."],[10841,"The disbeliever does not enslave a Muslim."],[10842,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning the slaves of the dhimmis who become Muslims: He orders that they be sold. Al-Thawri said: And that is what we say: They are to be sold."],[10843,"When a Christian slave becomes Muslim, he is compelled to sell him."],[10844,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to his father: To proceed: I have written to our governors that they should not leave with a Christian any Muslim slave except that he be taken and sold, nor a Muslim woman under a Christian except that they separate them. So carry that out in what is before you."],[10846,"Concerning an umm walad of a Christian who became Muslim, he said: Her value is assessed for her, and she works to pay off her value, and she is separated from him. If he dies, she is freed. If he becomes Muslim after her striving, she is sold and does not return to him. If he dies while Muslim or Christian, there is no striving upon her. Al-Thawri said concerning a Christian mudabbar the like of what he said concerning his umm walad. Al-Thawri said concerning a dhimmi with whom a slave becomes Muslim, and he makes him disappear or conceals him: He is disciplined and sold."],[10848,"An umm walad of a Christian from the people of Palestine became Muslim, and he wrote about her to Umar b. Abd al-Aziz, and Umar wrote to him: Send men to assess her value, and when her value is determined, pay it to him from the public treasury, for she is a woman of the Muslims."],[10849,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning those of the slaves of the dhimmis who become Muslim: They are to be sold, and do not leave between the dhimmis and their enslaving them. Pay their prices to their masters. Whoever you find after your advance warning has enslaved any of the Muslims' captives who has become Muslim and prayed, then free him."],[10850,"He was asked about non-Arab slaves who come out from the sea and elsewhere: may they be sold to Jews and Christians? He said: If they are adults, Islam is offered to them. If they accept Islam, so be it; otherwise, they may be sold to Jews and Christians if their owner wishes. What is preferable in this matter is that when a Muslim acquires Jews by sale or captivity, he should invite them to Islam. If they refuse except to hold fast to their religion, the Muslim may, if he wishes, sell them to the protected people (ahl al-dhimmah), but he may not sell them to anyone from the people of war (ahl al-harb). If they are of no religion, such as the Indians and the Zanj, the Muslim may not sell them to anyone from the protected people or from the people of war; he may sell them only to Muslims, because they respond when invited and have no religion to which they cling. Jews and Christians should not be allowed to Judaize or Christianize them. If the non-Arabs are minors, they may not be sold to Jews and Christians; they may be sold only to Muslims."],[10851,"He was asked about non-Arab slaves who come out from the sea and elsewhere: may they be sold to Jews and Christians? He said: If they are adults, Islam is offered to them. If they accept Islam, so be it; otherwise, they may be sold to Jews and Christians if their owner wishes. What is preferable in this matter is that when a Muslim acquires Jews by sale or captivity, he should invite them to Islam. If they refuse except to hold fast to their religion, the Muslim may, if he wishes, sell them to the protected people (ahl al-dhimmah), but he may not sell them to anyone from the people of war (ahl al-harb). If they are of no religion, such as the Indians and the Zanj, the Muslim may not sell them to anyone from the protected people or from the people of war; he may sell them only to Muslims, because they respond when invited and have no religion to which they cling. Jews and Christians should not be allowed to Judaize or Christianize them. If the non-Arabs are minors, they may not be sold to Jews and Christians; they may be sold only to Muslims."],[10852,"He was asked about Muslim merchants who enter the lands of the non-Arabs and some of them enslave others: is it permissible for him to buy them while he knows? He said: Yes."],[10853,"Do not buy the slaves of the protected people, for they are people of kharaj; some of them pay on behalf of others from their ancestral property."],[10854,"Ibn Shihab said concerning a man from the People of the Book who bought a Muslim slave woman secretly, and she bore him children: He is punished and she is taken away from him."],[10856,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote concerning those of the slaves of the People of the Covenant who embraced Islam: 'They are to be sold.'"],[10857,"A Christian manumitted a Muslim, and Umar b. Abd al-Aziz said: Give him his value from the public treasury, and his wala' belongs to the Muslims."],[10859,"A hadith was narrated to me and traced back to Ali b. Abi Talib concerning a Jew or Christian who became a zindiq. He said: Leave him; he may move from one religion to another."],[10863,"I heard Bajalah al-Tamimi narrating to Abu al-Shatha' and Amr b. Aws at the bench of Zamzam during the governorship of Mus'ab b. al-Zubayr. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj. Hidden text: I was a scribe for Jaz' b. Muawiyah, the paternal uncle of al-Ahnaf b. Qays. A letter came from Umar a year before his death: Kill every sorcerer, separate between every mahram among the Magians, and forbid them from zamzamah. He said: So we killed three female sorcerers. He said: Jaz' prepared much food and invited the Magians. They threw down the skewers they used for eating, a mule-load or two mule-loads of silver, and they ate without zamzamah. He said: Umar had not taken the jizyah from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman b. Awf testified that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it from the Magians of Hajar. Hidden isnad: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, saying: Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr b. Dinar, who said: I heard Bajalah al-Tamimi narrating from."],[10864,"A man from Taghlib came to Umar, and Umar said to him: You had a share in the Jahiliyyah, so take your share from Islam. He made a peace agreement with him on condition that he double the jizyah upon them and that they would not Christianize the children."],[10865,"I witnessed the Messenger of God ﷺ when he made peace with the Christians of Banu Taghlib on the condition that they would not make their children Christian. If they did, they would have no covenant.” He said: And Ali said: “If I were free, I would fight them.”"],[10866,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi b. Artat, asking al-Hasan: Why were the Magians left to marry mothers and sisters? So he asked him, and he said: “The polytheism they are upon is greater than that, and they were only left to it for the sake of the jizya.”"],[10868,"Umar would not allow a Christian, a Jew, or a Magian, if they entered Medina, to remain there more than three days, enough time to sell their goods. When Umar was struck, he said: “I had commanded you that none of them should enter upon us, and if the one struck were other than me, he would have authority in it.” He said: “And it was said: Two religions shall not meet there.”"],[10870,"The Jews, Christians, and other disbelievers, whoever of them came to Medina as travelers, were not allowed to stay more than three days in the time of Umar. I do not know whether that was done to them before that or not."],[10872,"No polytheist enters the entire Haram, and he recited: “After this year of theirs.”"],[10873,"His saying: “So let them not come near the Sacred Mosque after this year of theirs” means the entire Haram."],[10874,"Regarding this verse: “The polytheists are impure, so let them not come near the Sacred Mosque,” except that one be a slave or one of the people of the jizya."],[10875,"I lived to see that no Jew or Christian was left to enter the Haram, and they would not tread it except by stealth."],[10877,"Two religions shall not meet in the land of the Arabs—or he said: in the land of the Hijaz. He said: Umar investigated that until he found clear evidence for it. Al-Zuhri said: For that reason Umar expelled them. Al-Zuhri said: Umar would not allow the protected people to stay in Medina more than three days if they wanted to sell food, and the women of the Jews and Christians were commanded to veil and adorn themselves."],[10878,"I will certainly expel the Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula until I leave none in it but a Muslim."],[10881,"The Jews of Banu al-Nadir and Qurayzah fought the Messenger of God ﷺ, so the Messenger of God ﷺ expelled Banu al-Nadir and allowed Qurayzah to remain, showing them favor, until Qurayzah fought after that. Then he killed their men and divided their women, children, and property among the Muslims, except some of them who joined the Messenger of God ﷺ, so he granted them safety and they became Muslims. The Messenger of God ﷺ expelled all the Jews of Medina: Banu Qaynuqa, who are the people of Abdullah b. Salam, the Jews of Banu Harithah, and every Jew who was in Medina."],[10885,"Ibn Abbas said to me: “Thursday—and what is Thursday?” Then he wept until his tears wet the pebbles. I said: “O Abu al-Abbas, what is Thursday?” He said: “The day on which the Messenger of God's ﷺ illness became severe. He said: ‘Bring me [writing materials] so that I may write for you a document after which you will never go astray.’ They disputed, and it is not proper to dispute in the presence of a prophet. They said: ‘What is wrong with him? Is he delirious? Ask him for clarification.’ He said: ‘Leave me, for what I am in is better than what you are calling me to.’ And he gave three instructions at his death, saying: ‘Expel the polytheists from the Arabian Peninsula, and honor delegations as I used to honor them.’” He said: “Either Sa'id intentionally omitted the third, or he mentioned it and I forgot it.”"],[10886,"The Prophet ﷺ instructed at his death: “Let no Jew or Christian be left in the land of the Hijaz, and let Usama's army be dispatched to Syria, and treat the Copts well, for they have kinship.”"],[10887,"If you are given authority after me, expel the people of Najran from the Arabian Peninsula."],[10888,"The Jews and Christians shall not share in your cities unless they embrace Islam. Whoever among them apostatizes and refuses, nothing will be accepted from him except his blood."],[10890,"If you conquer the Copts, treat them well, for they have a covenant and kinship."],[10893,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote that the Christians in Syria be prevented from striking the naqus. He said: 'And they are to be forbidden from parting their hair, cutting their forelocks, tightening their belts, riding on saddles, wearing turbans, and raising their crosses above their churches. If any of them is caught doing any of that after being warned, his belongings are for the one who finds him.' He said: 'And he wrote that their women be prevented from riding on pack saddles.' Amr b. Maymun said: Umar consulted me about demolishing their churches, and I said: 'Do not demolish them; this is what they were granted by treaty,' so Umar left them."],[10894,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Urwa b. Muhammad: 'Demolish the churches that are in the amsar (garrison towns) of the Muslims.' He said: I witnessed Urwa b. Muhammad ride until he stopped at them, then he called me, and I bore witness to Umar's letter and Urwa's demolition of them, and he demolished them."],[10895,"He passed with Hisham by Hudda, and a church had been newly built there. He consulted about demolishing it, and Hisham demolished it."],[10896,"It is from the sunna that churches in the amsar, old and new, be demolished."],[10897,"Ibn Abbas was asked: May the polytheists build churches in the land of the Arabs? Ibn Abbas said: As for what the Muslims have established as amsar, no church, no synagogue, no fire temple, and no cross may be raised in them, nor may a horn be blown in them, nor a naqus struck in them, nor wine or pigs be brought into them. As for any land that was reconciled by treaty, the Muslims must fulfill their treaty for them."],[10898,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to us: 'Do not let pigs be near you, and do not let a cross be raised among you, and do not eat at a table on which wine is drunk, and train the horses, and walk between the two targets.'"],[10899,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote that the Christians in Syria be prevented from striking the naqus. He said: 'And they are to be forbidden from parting their hair, cutting their forelocks, tightening their belts, riding on saddles, wearing turbans, and raising their crosses above their churches. If any of them is caught doing any of that after being warned, his belongings are for the one who finds him.' He said: 'And he wrote that their women be prevented from riding on pack saddles.' Amr b. Maymun said: Umar consulted me about demolishing their churches, and I said: 'Do not demolish them; this is what they were granted by treaty,' so Umar left them."],[10901,"Muhammad b. Abi Bakr wrote to Ali asking him about a Muslim who committed zina with a Christian woman. He wrote to him: 'Carry out the hadd for Allah on the Muslim, and hand the Christian woman over to the people of her religion.'"],[10904,"Regarding the People of the Book when they are brought before the judges of the Muslims, they (two authorities) said: 'If the governor wishes, he may judge between them, and if he wishes, he may turn away from them; but if he judges between them, he judges by what Allah has revealed.'"],[10905,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi b. Artat: 'When the People of the Book come to you, judge between them.'"],[10906,"This verse was abrogated: 'So judge between them or turn away from them' (Q. 5:42), by His saying: 'And judge between them by what Allah has revealed' (Q. 5:49)."],[10907,"If a man from the People of the Book commits adultery with a Muslim woman or steals something from a Muslim, the hadd punishment is carried out on him, and the ruler does not turn away from that. He says: everything between the Muslims and the People of the Book, the ruler does not turn away from it."],[10909,"There is no hadd punishment upon one who throws at a Jew or a Christian."],[10910,"I asked my father: Is there a hadd punishment upon one who slanders the people of the covenant? He said: \"I do not consider that there is a hadd punishment upon him.\""],[10916,"The Jews of Banu Zurayq bewitched the Prophet ﷺ, and it is not mentioned that he killed any of them."],[10917,"A Jewish woman gifted the Prophet ﷺ a roasted sheep at Khaybar. He said to her: \"What is this?\" She said: \"A gift.\" She was cautious not to say \"from charity,\" lest he not eat it. He ate it and his companions ate. Then he said to them: \"Hold back.\" He said to the woman: \"Did you poison this sheep?\" She said: \"Yes.\" She said: \"Who informed you?\" He said: \"This bone,\" referring to its leg bone, which was in his hand. She said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Why?\" She said: \"I intended that if you are a liar, the people would be rid of you, and if you are a prophet, it would not harm you.\" He said: The Prophet ﷺ had cupping done on the shoulder, and he ordered them to have cupping done, and some of them died. Al-Zuhri said: She accepted Islam, so he left her alone. Ma'mar said: As for the people, they mention that he killed her."],[10919,"I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. When they say: There is no god but Allah, they have protected their blood and their property from me, except for its right, and their reckoning is with Allah."],[10920,"I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. When they do that, they have protected their blood and their property from me, except for its right, and their reckoning is with Allah."],[10921,"When Abu Bakr resolved to fight the people of apostasy, 'Umar said to him: How will you fight the people, O Abu Bakr, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. When they say it, they have protected their blood and their property from me, except for its right, and their reckoning is with Allah\"? Abu Bakr said: \"By Allah, I will fight whoever separates prayer from zakat, for zakat is the right of wealth. By Allah, if they withheld from me a young goat that they used to give to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I would fight them over it!\" 'Umar said: By Allah, it was only that I saw Allah had opened Abu Bakr's chest to fighting, and I knew that it was the truth."],[10923,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ: Are the Magians People of the Book? He said: No. I said: What about the Asbādhiyyūn? He said: \"The letter of the Prophet ﷺ to them was found, or so they claimed, after ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wanted to take the jizya from them. When he found it, he left them alone. He said: They claimed that.\""],[10924,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb did not want to take the jizya from the Magians until ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf testified that the Messenger of God ﷺ took it from the Magians of Hajar."],[10925,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb went out and passed by some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, among them ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf. He said: I do not know what to do about these people who are neither Arabs nor People of the Book—meaning the Magians. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"Treat them according to the practice of the People of the Book.\""],[10927,"I asked al-Zuhrī: Is jizya taken from those who are not People of the Book? He said: \"Yes. The Messenger of God ﷺ took it from the people of al-Baḥrayn, ʿUmar from the people of al-Sawād, and ʿUthmān from the Berbers.\""],[10928,"The Prophet of God ﷺ took the jizya from the Magians of Hajar, ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb took it from the Magians of al-Sawād, and ʿUthmān took it from the Berbers.\""],[10930,"Al-Mustawrid b. ʿAlqama was in a gathering with Farwa b. Nawfal al-Ashjaʿī. A man said: There is no jizya on the Magians. Al-Mustawrid said: You say this when the Messenger of God ﷺ took it from the Magians of Hajar! By God, what you have concealed is more wicked than what you have shown! He took him until he entered upon ʿAlī, who was sitting in a pavilion in a palace. He said: O Commander of the Faithful, this man claims that there is no jizya on the Magians, and you know that the Messenger of God ﷺ took it from the Magians of Hajar! ʿAlī said: Al-badā, meaning: Sit down. By God, there is no one on earth today more knowledgeable about that than I. The Magians were People of a Book they knew and knowledge they studied. One of their leaders drank wine and became drunk, and fell upon his sister. A group of Muslims saw him. When morning came, his sister said: You did such-and-such to her, and a group saw you who will not cover for you. He summoned the greedy ones and gave them [gifts], then said to them: You know that Adam married his sons to his daughters. Those who had seen him came and said: Woe to the farthest one! On your back there is a punishment from God! Those who were with him killed them. Then a woman came and said to him: Rather, I saw you! He said to her: Woe to the prostitute of Banū so-and-so! She said: Yes, by God, she was a prostitute then she repented. So he killed her. Then what was in their hearts and their books was taken away by night, and nothing remained sound with them.\""],[10932,"The people of al-Sawād had no covenant. When the kharāj was taken from them, they had a covenant."],[10934,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"The Christians of the Arabs!\" He said: \"Muslims do not marry their women, and their slaughtered animals are not eaten. He did not consider Jews to be anyone but the Children of Israel at all, and when asked about the Christians, likewise. And if you ask him about the zakāt of their wealth, how is it taken? He placed them in the position of the People of the Book.\""],[10935,"The women of the Christian Arabs are not to be married, and their slaughtered animals are not to be eaten."],[10936,"ʿAlī used to dislike the slaughtered animals of the Christians of Banū Taghlib, and he said: \"They do not hold to Christianity except by drinking wine.\""],[10941,"Indeed what God has made lawful is their slaughter, \"and your Lord is never forgetful.\""],[10942,"I asked al-Zuhri about the slaughtered animals of the Christians of the Arabs, and he said: \"There is no harm in them. Whoever adopts a religion is of its people.\" He said: \"And their women may be married.\""],[10943,"Whoever from the Arabs enters [Christianity], he is in their religion; it is difficult."],[10944,"There is no harm in their slaughter. Have you not heard God say: \"And among them are unlettered ones who do not know the Book\"?"],[10945,"An official wrote to ʿUmar that there are people near us called the Samaritans who read the Torah, observe the Sabbath, and do not believe in the resurrection. What does the Commander of the Faithful think about their slaughter? ʿUmar wrote to him: \"They are a sect of the People of the Book; their slaughter is the slaughter of the People of the Book.\""],[10947,"It reached Umar that his governors were taking wine as part of the jizya. He adjured them three times, and Bilal said: \"They do that.\" He said: \"Do not do that, but let them sell it themselves, for the Jews were forbidden fat, so they sold it and ate its price.\""],[10948,"When Allah revealed Surat al-Baqarah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and recited it, then forbade trading in wine."],[10951,"Regarding a Christian who advanced a loan to a Christian for wine, then one of them became Muslim, he said: \"He takes his capital. If one of them lends his companion wine and the lender becomes Muslim, he takes nothing. But if the borrower becomes Muslim, he returns to the Christian the price of the wine.\""],[10953,"A man came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of God, I have wealth belonging to an orphan, and I bought wine with it. Do you permit me to sell it so I can return the orphan's wealth to him?\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"May God fight the Jews! Fat was forbidden to them, so they sold it and ate its price.\" And the Prophet ﷺ did not permit him to sell the wine."],[10954,"Umar found wine in the house of a man from Thaqif, and he had already flogged him for wine. So he burned his house and said: \"What is your name?\" He said: \"Ruwayshid.\" He said: \"Rather, you are Fuwaysiq.\""],[10956,"Ata' was asked about a Magian who combined a woman and her daughter in marriage, then became Muslim. He said: \"I prefer that he separate from both of them.\""],[10960,"There is no harm in marrying women of the People of the Book, but the women of the Christian Arabs are not to be married."],[10961,"There is no harm in marrying women of the People of the Book, but the women of the Christian Arabs are not to be married."],[10962,"Hudhayfah married a Jewish woman during the time of Umar. Umar said: \"Divorce her, for she is a live coal!\" He said: \"Is she forbidden?\" He said: \"No.\" So Hudhayfah did not divorce her because of his statement, until after that he divorced her."],[10964,"Talhah b. Ubayd Allah married the daughter of a Jewish leader. He said: \"Umar made him swear to divorce her.\""],[10965,"Talhah b. Ubayd Allah married a Jewish woman."],[10968,"There is no harm in combining four from the People of the Book."],[10969,"A woman from the People of the Book, her waiting period, her divorce, and her apportionment are like those of the Muslims."],[10970,"A woman from the People of the Book is like a free Muslim woman: her waiting period and her divorce, and apportionment is made for her when she is with a Muslim woman.\" He said: \"And she may be married in addition to a Muslim wife, and whoever marries her has thereby become muhsan; they are called muhsanat.\""],[10971,"The affair of the Jewish woman and the Christian woman among them in Syria is like the affair of the free Muslim woman in divorce and waiting period, and in apportionment between them and the free Muslim woman."],[10976,"Regarding a man who has a Muslim slave woman and a Christian slave man, may he marry the slave man to the slave woman? He said: \"No.\""],[10978,"Regarding a Christian woman who is under a Christian man and she becomes Muslim before he has consummated the marriage with her, he said: \"She separates from him, and she gets no dower.\""],[10981,"Regarding a Christian woman who is under a Christian man and she becomes Muslim before he has consummated the marriage with her? He said: \"They are to be separated, and there is no dowry.\""],[10983,"Regarding a polytheist who divorced a polytheist woman but she did not complete her waiting period until after she became Muslim, he said: \"She should observe a waiting period of three menstrual cycles.\" He said: \"And there is no inheritance for her.\" And he said regarding a polytheist who died leaving a polytheist wife, and she became Muslim before her waiting period ended, he said: \"She should observe a waiting period of three months and ten days, and what has passed of her waiting period during the time of polytheism before she became Muslim is to be counted.\""],[10986,"Al-Thawri said: \"If the woman apostatizes and she has a husband who has not consummated the marriage with her, there is no dowry for her, and the bond between them is severed. If he has consummated the marriage with her, she is entitled to the full dowry.\""],[10987,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz said regarding a man who is taken captive and becomes Christian, he said: \"When that is known, his wife is acquitted from him and she observes a waiting period of three menstrual cycles.\""],[10988,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about an apostate: How long should his wife observe a waiting period? He said: \"Three menstrual cycles.\" I said: If he is killed? He said: \"Four months and ten days.\""],[10992,"The women of the People of the Book are lawful for us, and our women are forbidden to them."],[10993,"A woman from the people of al-Hira became Muslim while her husband did not. Umar b. al-Khattab wrote concerning her: \"Give her the choice: if she wishes, she may part from him; and if she wishes, she may remain with him.\""],[10995,"He has more right to her as long as he does not take her out of the land of her emigration."],[10998,"Regarding marrying polytheist women outside a covenant: he disliked their women, but permitted their slaughtered animals in the land of war."],[10999,"Regarding marrying polytheist women outside a covenant: he disliked their women and permitted their slaughtered animals in the land of war."],[11002,"Umar wrote to the commanders of the armies: \"Do not impose the jizya upon women or children, and impose the jizya upon adult men upon whom the razor has passed, and seal their necks, and shave their forelocks — whoever among them grows hair — and require them to wear belts, and prevent them from riding except sideways on pack saddles.\" He said: He means: \"His two legs are on one side.\" Abdullah said: \"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz did that to them when he took office.\" Abdullah said, in the hadith of Nafiʿ, from Aslam: \"Umar imposed the jizya upon those of them in Syria at four dinars per man, with two mudds of food and two or three qists of oil. He imposed upon those in Egypt four dinars and two irdabbs of food and something he mentioned. He imposed upon those in Iraq forty dirhams and fifteen qafizes and something I do not remember. Along with that, he imposed upon them the entertainment of Muslims who passed by them for three days, and he imposed upon them clothing, and he mentioned something he did not remember.\""],[11003,"The Messenger of God ﷺ made peace with idol worshippers on the jizya, except those among them who were Arabs, and he accepted the jizya from the people of al-Bahrayn, who were Magians."],[11004,"The Messenger of God ﷺ imposed upon a Christian in Makkah called Mawhab, a dinar every year as jizya."],[11005,"I asked ʿAtaʾ about the jizya. He said: \"We know nothing fixed except what they made peace upon; then they secured all their wealth.\" He said: \"Amr b. Dinar told me that.\""],[11006,"I said to Mujahid: \"Why is it that the People of the Book in Syria have four dinars taken from them in jizya, while the people of Yemen have one dinar?\" He said: \"That is because of ease.\""],[11007,"He imposed the jizya on every man who had reached puberty: forty dirhams or four dinars. He made silver for those of them in Iraq, because it is a land of silver, and he made gold for the people of Syria, because it is a land of gold. Along with that, he imposed on them their provisions and the clothing that Umar used to clothe people with, and the hospitality of Muslims who stopped with them for three nights and their days."],[11009,"Umar imposed the jizya and wrote about that to the commanders of the armies: 'Do not impose the jizya except on those upon whom the razor has been used, and do not impose it on a child or a woman.' He imposed on the people of Iraq forty dirhams on every man, and he also imposed on the people of Iraq fifteen saa's. He imposed on the people of Syria four dinars on every man, and he also imposed on the people of Syria two mudds of wheat, three qists of oil, and such-and-such amount of honey and fat, which Ayyub or Nafi did not remember. He imposed on the people of Egypt four dinars on every man, and he also imposed on the people of Egypt an irdabb of wheat, and something he did not remember, and the clothing of the Commander of the Believers as a fixed levy. And they owe the hospitality of Muslims for three days: they feed them from what they eat, from what is lawful for Muslims from their food. When Umar came to Syria, they complained to him that they were burdening us with chickens. Umar said: 'Do not feed them except from what you eat, from what is lawful to them from your food.'"],[11010,"A condition on them: a day and a night of hospitality."],[11011,"Umar imposed on the dhimmis in Yemen one dinar on every adult, and on the Romans in Syria four dinars, and on the people of the Sawad forty-eight dirhams."],[11012,"The Prophet ﷺ sent Mu'adh b. Jabal to Yemen and ordered him to take from every adult male and adult female among the dhimmis one dinar or its value in Ma'afiri cloth."],[11014,"If two jizyas come due on a man, the first one is taken."],[11016,"He asked Ibn Abbas, saying: 'We pass by the dhimmis and they slaughter for us a chicken or a sheep.' He asked: 'And what do you say?' He replied: 'He says: \"There is no way against us from the unlettered people.\"' Ibn Abbas said: 'When they pay the jizya, their wealth is not lawful for you except with their willing consent.'"],[11017,"An army passed by the crops of a dhimmi man. They let their animals loose in it. A man among them restrained his animal and kept following with it the pasture, preventing it from the crops. The dhimmi owner of the crops came to the one who restrained his animal and said: 'May God suffice you,' or he said: 'God has sufficed me through you. Were it not for you, I would have been spared these people. But it is only because of you that these people are prevented.'"],[11018,"We were with a commander among the commanders. He saw us refraining from taking any of the fruit of the dhimmis. He said: 'Among what Umar made peace with them on was a day and a night for the traveler.'"],[11019,"Perhaps you will fight a people and prevail over them, and they will shield themselves from you with their wealth rather than their own lives and their children, and make peace with you. So do not take from them anything besides that."],[11023,"I asked Ibn 'Umar, saying: \"What do you think about buying land?\" He said: \"It is fine.\" He said: \"They take from me from every jarib a qafiz and a dirham.\" He said: \"Do not put humiliation on your neck.\""],[11024,"I would not like all the land to be mine as jizya for five dirhams, acknowledging humiliation upon myself."],[11025,"I entered upon Ibn Umar, I and a shaykh older than me. He said: I think he said: Ibn al-Musayyib. I asked him about charity: should I hand it over to the commanders? He said: \"Yes.\" He said: I said: Even if they buy with it cheetahs and falcons? He said: \"Yes.\" Then when we went out I said to the shaykh: Do you say what Ibn Umar said? He said: No. Then I said to Maymun b. Mihran: Do you say what Ibn Umar said? He said: No."],[11026,"A man from the people of Najran became Muslim, and they wanted to take from him—meaning jizya, or as he said—and he refused. 'Umar b. al-Khattab said: \"You are only seeking refuge.\" The man said: \"In Islam there is indeed a refuge if I do that!\" 'Umar said: \"You have spoken the truth, by God; in Islam there is indeed a refuge.\""],[11028,"Anas b. Malik appointed me over Ayla, and I said: \"You have appointed me over the toll from your work.\" He said: \"Take what 'Umar b. al-Khattab used to take from the people of Islam: when it reached two hundred dirhams, from every forty dirhams one dirham; and from the people of the covenant from every twenty dirhams one dirham; and from those who are not from the people of the covenant from every ten dirhams one dirham.\""],[11029,"He sent him to Ayla. He said: \"I said: 'You have sent me to the worst of your work!'\" He said: \"Then he brought out to me the letter of 'Umar b. al-Khattab.\""],[11030,"'Umar b. al-Khattab ruled concerning the wealth of the people of the covenant, when they passed with it by the collectors of zakat, half of the tithe; and concerning the wealth of the merchants of the polytheists—those who were from the people of the covenant—half the tithe."],[11031,"The first collector of tithes in Islam was I, and we did not tithe a Muslim or one with a treaty. He said: \"I said: 'Then whom did you tithe?'\" He said: \"The Christians of Banu Taghlib.\" Ibrahim said: \"Then a man narrated to me from Ziyad, saying: 'I asked him: How much did you tithe them?' He said: 'Half the tithe.'\""],[11032,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote to him: Whoever passes by you among the Muslims having wealth with which he trades, take from it its zakat: from every forty dinars one dinar; what is less than that down to twenty is calculated proportionally down to ten dinars; if it is less by a third of a dinar, take nothing from it. And whoever passes by you from the People of the Book or from the people of the covenant among those who trade, take from him from every twenty dinars one dinar; what is less is calculated proportionally down to ten dinars; if it is less by a third of a dinar, take nothing from it."],[11033,"The first to take half the tithe from the people of the covenant when they traded was 'Umar b. al-Khattab, and he used to take it from the merchants of the Nabataeans of Syria when they came to Madinah."],[11034,"The people of Manbij and those beyond the sea of Adan wrote to 'Umar b. al-Khattab offering to bring their trade into the land of the Arabs, with the tithes from it for them. 'Umar consulted the companions of the Prophet ﷺ about that, and they unanimously agreed to it; so he was the first to take tithes from them."],[11035,"Twice what is taken from the Muslims is taken from the People of the Book, from the people of gold and silver.” He said: “Umar b. al-Khattab and Umar b. Abd al-Aziz did that."],[11037,"Umar asked the Muslims: How do the Abyssinians treat you when you enter their land? They said: They take a tenth of what we have. He said: Then take from them the like of what they take from you."],[11039,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz took one-fifth from ambergris."],[11040,"We used to collect tithes during the governorship of Umar b. al-Khattab, and we did not tithe a covenanter or a Muslim. He said: I said to him: From whom did you collect tithes? He said: From the merchants of the people of war, just as they tithe us when we come to them. He said: Ziyad b. Hudayr was a governor for Umar b. al-Khattab."],[11041,"Umar sent him as a collector, and he ordered him to take a tenth from the Christians of Banu Taghlib, and from the Christians of the Arabs a half-tenth."],[11042,"Umar used to take a tenth from the Nabataeans on wheat and oil, intending thereby to increase the transport to Madinah, and he took a half-tenth on pulses, meaning chickpeas, lentils, and the like."],[11043,"He was asked about the polytheists, what is taken from them when they trade in the land of the Muslims? Umar said: They do not take from you except on oil and wheat, so take from them a half-tenth."],[11045,"Umar b. al-Khattab sent Ammar b. Yasir, Abdullah b. Mas'ud, and Uthman b. Hunayf to Kufa. He put Ammar in charge of prayer and fighting, put Abdullah b. Mas'ud in charge of judiciary and the treasury, and put Uthman b. Hunayf in charge of land surveying. He assigned them each day a sheep: half of it and its offal for Ammar, a quarter for Ibn Mas'ud, and a quarter for Uthman b. Hunayf. Then he said: \"I do not think any village from which a sheep is taken every day will not quickly be ruined.\" Then he said to them: \"I have placed you and myself with regard to this wealth like the guardian of an orphan; whoever is rich should abstain, and whoever is poor may eat in a reasonable manner.\" He said: Uthman distributed to every head of the dhimmis twenty-four dirhams every year, and he did not impose anything of that on women and children. He surveyed the sawad of Kufa from the land of the dhimmis, and he imposed on a jarib of palm trees ten dirhams, on a jarib of grapes eight dirhams, on a jarib of sugar cane six dirhams, on a jarib of wheat four dirhams, and on a jarib of barley two dirhams. He took from the merchants of the dhimmis one dirham for every twenty dirhams, and he reported that to Umar, who approved of it."],[11046,"A man embraced Islam during the time of Umar b. al-Khattab and said: Remove the jizyah from my land! Umar said: Your land was taken by force."],[11047,"A man came to Umar b. al-Khattab and said: The people of such and such a land can bear more kharaj than what is upon them. He said: There is no way to them; they were only granted peace through a treaty."],[11048,"I had land subject to its jizya, so my agent wrote about it to Umar b. Abd al-Aziz, and Umar wrote: \"Collect the jizya and the tithes, then take the surplus from him.\""],[11049,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote concerning a village headwoman from the people of Nahr al-Malik who had embraced Islam and who owned much land. He wrote to Umar about her, and he wrote: \"Give her back her land, and she shall pay the kharaj for it.\""],[11051,"Ali b. Abi Talib said to a village head: \"If you embrace Islam, I will remit the dinar from your head and take it from your wealth.\""],[11054,"Any village you come upon, your share is in it—or words to that effect. And any village that disobeys God and His Messenger, its land belongs to God and His Messenger ﷺ, then it is yours."],[11056,"Ali was brought an old man who had been a Christian, then embraced Islam, then apostatized from Islam. Ali said to him: \"Perhaps you apostatized only to obtain an inheritance, then to return to Islam.\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Perhaps you proposed to a woman and they refused to marry her to you, and you wanted them to give her to you in marriage, then to return to Islam?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then return to Islam!\" He said: \"Not until I meet the Messiah!\" So Ali ordered that he be executed, and his inheritance was given to his Muslim children."],[11057,"Al-Mustawrid al-Ijli apostatized from Islam. Ali asked him to repent, but he refused, so he killed him, divided his wealth among his heirs, and ordered his wife to observe an idda of four months and ten days."],[11058,"Ibn Masud ruled concerning the inheritance of an apostate in the same way as Ali's saying."],[11059,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning a Muslim man who was taken captive and became a Christian: when that is known, his wife is separated from him, and she observes an idda of three menstrual cycles, and his wealth is given to his Muslim heirs."],[11060,"Concerning an apostate: if he is killed, his wealth belongs to his heirs; and if he joins the territory of war, his wealth belongs to the Muslims. I do not know whether he added: \"unless he has an heir of the same religion in the territory, who is more entitled to it.\""],[11062,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about the apostate: how long should his wife observe the waiting period? He said: \"Three menstrual cycles.\" He said: I said: \"He was killed.\" He said: \"Then four months and ten days.\" He said: I said: \"Is his inheritance passed on?\" He said: \"What do you mean, is his inheritance passed on?\" He said: \"Do his sons inherit from him?\" He said: \"We inherit from them, but they do not inherit from us.\""],[11063,"The people of polytheism: we inherit from them, but they do not inherit from us."],[11064,"The inheritance of the apostate belongs to the Muslims, though they used to make it pleasant for his heirs."],[11065,"He heard Ikrimah when he was asked about a man who manumitted a Christian slave of his, then the slave died and left wealth, and he said: \"His inheritance belongs to the people of his religion.\""],[11066,"If the apostate repents, they deem it preferable for him to start anew with a Hajj, if he had performed Hajj before his apostasy."],[11067,"People are of two groups: among them are those who say the inheritance of the apostate belongs to the Muslims, because the moment he disbelieves, it is withheld from him, and he cannot dispose of any of it until it is seen whether he becomes Muslim or remains a disbeliever. Among them are al-Nakha'i, al-Sha'bi, and al-Hakam b. 'Utaybah. Another group says it belongs to the people of his religion."],[11069,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote: \"Execute the bequest of the captive.\""],[11071,"I said: O Prophet of Allah ﷺ, our land is the land of the People of the Book, and they eat pork, so what should we do with their vessels and cooking pots? He said: \"If you find no others, then wash them.\""],[11073,"Ibrahim al-Nakha'i was with them in the cavalry, and with him was a Magian woman who served him and prepared his food and drink."],[11074,"Sa'id b. Jubayr was with them for years in Isfahan, and he had a Magian servant boy who served him and prepared his food and drink."],[11075,"Sa'id b. Jubayr was with them for years in Isfahan, and he had a Magian servant boy who served him and handed him the mushaf in its cover."],[11077,"There is no harm in eating the cheese of a Magian."],[11079,"Convey from me even a single verse, and narrate from the Children of Israel without restriction. Whoever tells a lie about me, let him take his seat in the Fire."],[11080,"Do not ask the People of the Book about anything, for they will not guide you when they have already led themselves astray.\" He said: We said, \"O Messenger of God, may we then narrate from the Children of Israel?\" He said: \"Narrate, there is no restriction."],[11081,"How can you ask the People of the Book about anything when the Book of God that was sent down to you is among you pure and unadulterated? It is the most recent of reports from God. God has informed you about the People of the Book that they wrote books with their own hands and then said, 'This is from God,' to sell it for a small price. They altered it and distorted it from its proper places. Does not what has come to you from God forbid you from asking them? By God, we have not seen any of them asking you about what was sent down to you."],[11082,"Abu Namlah informed him that while he was sitting with the Messenger of God ﷺ, a man from the protected people came to him and said, \"O Muhammad, does this corpse speak?\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"God knows best.\" The Jew said, \"It speaks.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Whatever the People of the Book tell you, neither believe them nor disbelieve them, but say, 'We believe in God and His Books.' If it is false, you will not have believed it; if it is true, you will not have disbelieved it.\""],[11083,"The Jews used to narrate to the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and they would listen as if in amazement. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Do not believe them and do not disbelieve them, but say: 'We believe in what was sent down to us and what was sent down to you. Our God and your God is one, and to Him we submit.'\""],[11084,"Do not ask the People of the Book about anything, for they will not guide you when they have gone astray, lest you disbelieve a truth and believe a falsehood. There is no one among the People of the Book but that in his heart there is a caller inviting him to God and His Book, like the craving for wealth."],[11085,"Umar b. al-Khattab passed by a man reading a book. He listened for a while and admired it. He said to the man, \"Do you copy from this book?\" He said, \"Yes.\" So he bought a hide for himself, then brought it to him and copied it on both sides. Then he brought it to the Prophet ﷺ and began reading it to him. The face of the Messenger of God ﷺ began to change color. A man from the Ansar struck the book with his hand and said, \"May your mother be bereaved, O Ibn al-Khattab! Do you not see the face of the Messenger of God ﷺ all day while you read this book?\" The Prophet ﷺ said then, \"I was sent only as an opener and a seal. I was given the comprehensive words and their beginnings, and speech was made concise for me. So let not the confused ones destroy you.\""],[11086,"Umar b. al-Khattab came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, \"O Messenger of God, I passed by a brother of mine from Qurayza and he wrote for me a collection from the Torah. Shall I present it to you?\" He said: The face of the Messenger of God ﷺ changed. Abdullah said: I said, \"May God disfigure your mind! Do you not see what is on the face of the Messenger of God ﷺ?\" Umar said, \"I am content with God as Lord, Islam as religion, and Muhammad ﷺ as Prophet.\" He said: So the Prophet ﷺ was relieved, then said, \"By the One in whose hand is Muhammad's soul, if Moses were to appear among you and you followed him and left me, you would go astray. You are my share among the nations, and I am your share among the prophets.\""],[11087,"Hafsa, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, came to the Prophet ﷺ with a book of the stories of Joseph on a shoulder blade. She began reading it to him, and the Prophet's ﷺ face changed color. He said, \"By the One in whose hand is my soul, if Joseph came to you while I was among you and you followed him and left me, you would go astray.\""],[11088,"In Kufa there was a man seeking the books of Daniel and that kind. A letter came from Umar b. al-Khattab that he be brought to him. The man said, \"I do not know why I am being brought.\" When he came to Umar, he struck him with the whip. Then he began reciting to him, \"Alif Lam Ra. These are the verses of the clear Book\" until he reached \"the heedless.\" He said: \"I knew what he wanted.\" I said, \"O Commander of the Faithful, leave me, for by God, I will not leave anything of those books in my possession but I will burn it.\" He said: Then he let him go."],[11090,"A Jewish or Christian man goaded a Muslim woman, then threw dirt on her, wanting her for himself. This was brought to Umar b. al-Khattab, and Umar said: \"These people have a covenant as long as they fulfill their covenant with you. When they do not fulfill their covenant with you, they have no covenant.\" He said: So Umar crucified him."],[11091,"A Muslim woman hired a Jew or a Christian, and he went with her. When they came to a hillock, he hid behind it and then violated her. Abu Salih said: I had been watching her when he violated her, so I struck him and did not leave him until I thought I had killed him. He said: He went to Abu Hurayrah and informed him, so he called me and I informed him. He sent for the woman, and she corroborated my report. Abu Hurayrah said: \"We did not give you the covenant for this,\" and he ordered him to be killed."],[11092,"A Jew or a Christian goaded a Muslim woman and she fell, so Umar struck his neck and said: \"We did not make peace with you for this.\""],[11094,"A man from the Jews killed a girl from the Ansar for her jewelry, then threw her into a well and smashed her head with stones. He was seized and brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who ordered that he be stoned until he died. So he was stoned until he died."],[11097,"He saw Abdullah b. Muhayriz shaking hands with a Christian man in Damascus."],[11098,"They used to dislike eating with Jews and Christians, and shaking hands with them."],[11099,"They did not see any harm in shaking hands with the Jew or the Christian."],[11101,"You have settled in a land where Muslims do not slaughter; only the Nabateans and Persians do. So when you buy meat, ask. If it is the slaughter of a Jew or a Christian, eat it, for their food is lawful for you."],[11102,"It was said to both of them: \"The People of the Book mention other than Allah over their sacrifices.\" They replied: \"Allah, when He permitted their sacrifices, knew what they say over their sacrifices.\""],[11103,"The sacrifices of the People of the Book may be eaten even if it is slaughtered for other than Allah,\" or he said: \"even if it is consecrated to other than Allah.\""],[11104,"There is no harm in the sacrifices of the People of the Book, even if they are among the people of war, nor in the game caught by their dogs."],[11105,"Regarding His saying: “And that which is dedicated to other than Allah — then whoever is compelled...” He said: “He says: in the name of the Messiah,” and he said: “There is no harm in their sacrifices.”"],[11106,"When a Jew slaughters his sacrifice and it becomes invalid for him according to his religion, it is not lawful for a Muslim to eat it."],[11107,"Regarding His saying: “And the food of those who were given the Scripture is lawful for you” — he said: “Their sacrifices.”"],[11109,"And that which is dedicated to other than Allah — Allah has made it lawful, because He knew that they would say such a thing."],[11110,"He used to dislike eating it if he heard him invoking a name other than Allah, unless he moved out of sight so that he would not hear it. He said: “His invocation is to say: in the name of the Messiah.”"],[11111,"There were some Christians who used to slaughter in Syria and then sell the meat to the Muslims. So ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz appointed some Muslims to attend their slaughtering, to ensure that they invoked the name of Allah, and to prevent them from associating anything with Allah over their sacrifices."],[11112,"A man asked Ibn ʿUmar about the sacrifice of a Jew or a Christian. He recited to him: “Lawful for you are the good things, and the food of those who were given the Scripture is lawful for you,” and he recited: “And do not eat of that over which the name of Allah has not been mentioned,” and he recited: “And that which is dedicated to other than Allah.” He said: The man repeated the question to him, so Ibn ʿUmar said: “May Allah curse the Jews, the Christians, and the disbelieving Bedouins, for this man and his companions ask me, and when I do not agree with them they come and argue with me.”"],[11113,"If a Jew offers you food, tell him to eat from it first. If he eats, then eat; but if he refuses, do not eat from it."],[11114,"Regarding a Christian who slaughtered a sheep for baptism, but mistook it in intention, so that eating it became forbidden for him — he said: “Then a Muslim should also not eat it.”"],[11115,"Regarding an animal to be slaughtered jointly by a Muslim, a Jew, and a Christian — he said: “He should not slaughter for you; rather, you slaughter, because our religion prevails over theirs.”"],[11117,"I asked Saʿīd b. Jubayr and Murrah b. Shurāḥīl about a Magian who mentions the name of Allah when slaughtering. He said: “Do not eat it.”"],[11122,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the kunya to Safwan b. Umayya, while he was at that time a polytheist. He came to him on a horse, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Dismount, Abu Wahb.'"],[11123,"Umar b. al-Khattab gave the kunya to al-Furafisa al-Hanafi while he was a Christian, saying to him: 'Abu Hassan.'"],[11124,"I said to al-Zuhri: 'Is it said to him: Marhaban (welcome)?' He said: 'If he has done you a favor that you have not repaid, there is no harm.'"],[11125,"A Christian said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'I have become Muslim!' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Abu al-Harith.' The Christian said: 'I have become Muslim!' The Prophet ﷺ said the third time: 'Abu al-Harith!' He said: 'I became Muslim before you!' He said: 'You have lied; three traits have come between you and Islam: your drinking wine' – and he did not say 'your drinking' – 'your eating pork, and your claiming a child for Allah.'"],[11126,"I heard Mujahid say to a Christian slave of his: 'O Jarir, become Muslim.' Then he said: 'This is how it was said to them.'"],[11128,"I said to him: 'A Muslim who frees a Christian or a Jew – is there reward in it?' He said: 'No,' and he disliked freeing them."],[11130,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz freed a Christian slave of his, who then died. He ordered me to place his inheritance in the bayt al-mal (public treasury)."],[11131,"His father freed a Magian slave of his, and he freed a child of fornication."],[11133,"He was asked about a Muslim borrowing the dog of a Magian. He said: 'His dog is like his knife,' meaning: 'There is no harm in it.'"],[11135,"He disliked the game of a Magian's dog."],[11137,"The Sabians are a people who worship the angels, pray toward the qiblah, and recite the Psalms."],[11138,"The Sabians are between the Magians and the Jews; they have no religion."],[11139,"Ibn Abbas was asked about the Sabians, and he said: \"They are a people between the Jews and the Christians; their slaughtered animals are not lawful, nor is marriage with them.\""],[11141,"Regarding His saying: \"So ask the people of the Reminder if you do not know\" (Q 16:43), he said: \"The people of the Torah—ask them: have any but men come to them to whom revelation was given?\""],[11142,"Regarding His saying: \"And ask those of Our messengers whom We sent before you\" (Q 43:45), he says: \"Ask the People of the Book: did the messengers come to them with monotheism? Did they come to them with sincere devotion?\""],[11143,"Regarding His saying: \"So if you are in doubt about what We have sent down to you, ask those who recite the Book before you\" (Q 10:94), he said: It has reached us that the Prophet ﷺ said: \"I do not doubt, nor do I ask.\""],[11146,"I said to Ata: the blood money of a Magian! He said: \"Eight hundred dirhams.\""],[11147,"Umar ruled that the blood money of a Magian is eight hundred dirhams, and he said: \"He is not of the People of the Book; he is only a slave.\""],[11148,"Abu Musa wrote to Umar b. al-Khattab that the Muslims fall upon the Magians and kill them, so what do you think? Umar wrote to him: \"They are only slaves, so set their value among you.\" Abu Musa wrote to him: Eight hundred dirhams, and Umar set that for the Magians."],[11151,"That ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz set the blood money for a Magian at half the blood money of a Muslim."],[11152,"That ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb set the blood money for a Magian at eight hundred dirhams."],[11156,"The blood money for a woman from the People of the Book is four thousand dirhams. He said: I said: What about Arab Christians? He said: \"The same as them.\""],[11158,"That a Muslim man killed a man from the people of the covenant deliberately, and it was brought to ʿUthmān, and he did not execute him, but he increased the blood money upon him, like the blood money of a Muslim."],[11159,"The blood money for a Jew, a Christian, and a Magian is the same as the blood money of a Muslim."],[11165,"He used to permit the testimony of the People of the Book, some of them against others."],[11166,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz permitted the testimony of a Magian against a Christian, or a Christian against a Magian."],[11169,"Kab b. Sawr used to make the People of the Book swear; he would place the Gospel on his head, then bring him to the altar and he would swear by Allah."],[11171,"He used to make them swear by Allah, and he used to say: 'Allah sent down: \"And judge between them by what Allah has sent down.\"'"],[11173,"When a Christian woman becomes pregnant by a Muslim and dies while pregnant, she is buried with the people of her religion."],[11174,"The people of her religion attend to her, and she is buried with them."],[11175,"He buried a woman from the People of the Book who was pregnant from a Muslim in the cemetery of the Muslims."],[11176,"Wathila b. al-Asqaʿ handed over a Christian woman who had died while pregnant from a Muslim to a cemetery that was neither the Christians' cemetery nor the Muslims' cemetery, between the two.\" Sulayman said: \"Those of her religion should attend to her."],[11178,"He went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ on an expedition he undertook. Khalid b. al-Walid was in command of the vanguard. Rabah and the companions of the Messenger of God ﷺ passed by a slain woman, among those the vanguard had struck. They stopped at her, looking, marveling at her form. Until the Messenger of God ﷺ came up on a she-camel of his, and they made way from the woman. The Messenger of God ﷺ stopped, looking at her, and said, \"This one was not one to fight!\" Then he looked into the faces of the people and said to one of them, \"Catch up with Khalid and say: Do not kill a child, nor a hireling.\""],[11180,"Whoever divorces in jest or marries in jest, it is valid."],[11181,"Three things in which the jester is like the serious: marriage, divorce, and manumission."],[11182,"Gabriel led me in prayer at the House. He prayed Zuhr with me when the sun declined, and it was the length of a sandal strap. Then he prayed Asr with me when the shadow of every thing was equal to it. Then he prayed Maghrib with me when the fasting person breaks his fast. Then he prayed Isha with me when the twilight disappeared. Then he prayed Fajr with me when food and drink became forbidden for the fasting person. He said: Then the next day he prayed Zuhr with me when the shadow of every thing was equal to it. Then he prayed Asr with me when the shadow of every thing was twice its length. Then he prayed Maghrib with me when the fasting person breaks his fast. Then he prayed Isha with me in the first third of the night. Then he prayed Fajr with me and it became bright. Then he turned to me and said: O Muhammad, this is the time of the prophets before you; the time is between these two times."],[11183,"Three things in which there is no jest: marriage, divorce, manumission, and charity."],[11184,"Three things in which the jester and the serious are equal: divorce, charity, and manumission."],[11185,"Whoever divorces while jesting, his divorce is valid; whoever manumits while jesting, his manumission is valid; and whoever gives in marriage while jesting, his marriage is valid."],[11186,"Whoever divorces or marries in jest, it is valid."],[11187,"A matter in which there is no return except through marriage, divorce, manumission, and vow."],[11188,"Three things in which there is no jesting: marriage, divorce, and emancipation."],[11189,"Marriage, divorce, and taking back are not valid except with two witnesses. If he takes her back and forgets to have witnesses while he is having intercourse with her, then when he knows, he should return to the Sunnah by having two upright witnesses testify."],[11192,"A man asked 'Imran b. Husayn: \"A man divorced without witnesses and took back without witnesses!\" He said: \"How bad is what he did! He divorced in an innovation and took back contrary to the Sunnah. Let him have witnesses for what he did.\""],[11193,"A man asked him: \"I divorced without witnesses and took back without witnesses!\" He said: \"You divorced outside the prescribed waiting period and took back contrary to the Sunnah.\""],[11194,"If he has intercourse, then his entering is a taking back, but he should have witnesses."],[11200,"I heard Ayyub ask Matar al-Warraq about a man who said: 'His wife is divorced if she enters so-and-so's house,' and she entered while he did not know, and he kept having intercourse with her while he did not know! Matar said: 'Al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyib used to say: \"His having intercourse with her is a taking back, but let him have witnesses.\""],[11201,"If he does not have witnesses to the taking back until the waiting period ends, then claims the taking back after the end of the waiting period, he is not to be believed, and even if he brings witnesses for that, he is not to be believed."],[11204,"About a man who divorced his wife once, then she remained three years, then gave birth. He said: 'I have taken you back,' but she said: 'You did not take me back with a taking back, because the child could only have come from intercourse after the divorce, and intercourse is a taking back.' He said: 'If that is two years or less, he is asked for clear evidence of the taking back, otherwise the child is attributed to him and she is separated from him, because the child can be from up to two years.'"],[11206,"Al-Ash'ath b. Qays went out bidding farewell to a man—I think from Quraysh—and saw his wife, or a woman with him, and she pleased him. It was decreed for the man to die on his journey, and his family returned to Kufah. Al-Ash'ath proposed to that woman, and she said: 'I marry you according to my judgment!' So he married her. When he had consummated the marriage and remained as long as he remained, he divorced her, then said: 'Judge for yourself what you want!' She said: 'I judge so-and-so and so-and-so, slaves of his father.' He said: 'As for those, no, but judge from my wealth.' She brought a dispute to Umar b. al-Khattab. He said: 'O Commander of the Believers, I fell in love with this woman.' He said: 'That is as long as you do not possess her.' He said: 'Then I married her according to her judgment, then I divorced her before appeasing her.' Umar rejected that and said: 'A woman of the Muslims has what any woman of the Muslims has.' He did not make any judgment for her, but gave her the dower of a woman of her peers."],[11208,"Ali said about a man who marries a woman according to her judgment: 'The marriage is valid, and she has the dower of her peers, without reduction or excess.'"],[11211,"If he enters upon her before fixing a dower, she has the like of the dower of her peers."],[11212,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to consult his daughters when he married them off. He said: He would sit at the curtain of the betrothed woman and say, \"So-and-so mentions So-and-so.\" If she moved the curtain, he would not marry her off; and if she remained silent, he would marry her off."],[11213,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to consult his daughters when he married them off. He said: He would sit at the curtain of the betrothed woman and say, \"So-and-so mentions So-and-so.\" If she moved the curtain, he would not marry her off; and if she remained silent, he would marry her off."],[11214,"Consult virgins concerning themselves, for they are shy. If she remains silent, that is her consent."],[11216,"The previously married woman has more right to herself than her guardian, and the virgin is asked for permission."],[11217,"The previously married woman has more right to herself than her guardian, and the virgin is asked for permission."],[11218,"The previously married woman is in control of her own affair, and the virgin is consulted concerning herself; her silence is her assent."],[11219,"I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about a girl whose family marries her off: is she consulted or not? The Messenger of God ﷺ said to her: \"Yes, she is consulted.\" Aisha said: I said, \"But she is shy and remains silent.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"That is her permission if she remains silent.\""],[11220,"The previously married woman is consulted, and the virgin is asked for permission. They said, \"What is her permission, O Prophet of God?\" He said: \"That she remain silent.\""],[11221,"I said to Ata: Are women consulted concerning their marriages, both previously married and virgin? He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"Does the father consult?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[11223,"Zaynab bt. the Prophet ﷺ was married off in the pre-Islamic era, while Ali and Uthman married in Islam. The Prophet ﷺ used to come to the curtained litter of the daughter whose hand was being sought and say: “So-and-so is asking for the hand of So-and-so.” If she struck the curtain of her litter with her hand, that was a refusal from her, and he would not marry her off. But if she did not strike the curtain of her litter with her hand, the Prophet ﷺ would marry her off, and she would remain silent."],[11225,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to consult his daughters when he married them off. He said: He would sit at the curtain of the betrothed woman and say, \"So-and-so mentions So-and-so.\" If she moved the curtain, he would not marry her off; and if she remained silent, he would marry her off."],[11228,"The father’s marriage arrangement is valid for a virgin, but not for a previously married woman."],[11231,"The orphan girl is consulted concerning herself, and if she remains silent, that is her approval."],[11232,"The orphan girl is consulted concerning herself, and if she remains silent, that is her approval."],[11233,"The guardian has no authority over a previously married woman, but the orphan girl is consulted, and her silence is her consent."],[11234,"The father’s decision is binding on a virgin in marriage, provided he is not incompetent."],[11503,"Umar said while he was on the pulpit: Indeed something descends like pearls, or like a bead, and I do not care about it."],[12014,"The ears are part of the head."],[12031,"No crier from the people of the earth calls to prayer until a crier from the people of heaven calls: 'Arise, O children of Adam, and extinguish your fires.' He said: Then the muezzin stands and gives the call to prayer, then the people rise for prayer."],[12463,"The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution — or drank — from a pool into which dog meat was thrown. He said: 'And I do not think but that he said: and carcasses.' That was mentioned to him, and he said to him: 'Water is not made impure by anything.'"],[13133,"I caught one rak'ah of the residents' prayer while I was a traveler. He said: \"Pray according to their prayer.\""],[13209,"906 898 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar, from Qatādah the like of it, and from Juwaybir, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk b. Muzāḥim the like of it. Hidden text: If a traveller fears thirst for himself and has water, he performs tayammum."],[13459,"'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ishaq b. 'Abd Allah b. Abi Talhah, from a woman, from her mother, who was married to Abu Qatadah: similar to the hadith of Malik. Hidden text: Abu Qatadah entered upon her, and she poured water for his ablution; then a cat came and drank, and he tilted the vessel for it until it drank. Kabshah said: He saw me looking at him, and he said, \"Are you amazed, daughter of my brother?\" She said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, 'It is not impure; it is merely one of the frequent visitors—males and females.'\" Hidden chain: 'Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ishaq b. 'Abd Allah b. Abi Talhah, from a woman, from her mother; Abu Qatadah said."],[13520,"The people should be led by the one who recites the Qur'an best. If they are equal in recitation, then the one who made hijrah earliest. If they are equal in hijrah, then the most knowledgeable in the Sunnah. If they are equal in knowledge, then the eldest. And no man should lead another in his sphere of authority, nor sit on his seat of honor in his house except with his permission."],[13600,"I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about an apostate: How long should his wife observe a waiting period? He said: \"Three menstrual cycles.\" I said: If he is killed? He said: \"Four months and ten days.\""],[13637,"Regarding a Christian woman who is under a Christian man and the woman becomes Muslim, he said: \"A Christian cannot have authority over a Muslim woman; they are to be separated.\""],[13639,"The women of the People of the Book are lawful for us, and our women are forbidden to them."],[13643,"A woman from the people of al-Hira became Muslim while her husband did not. Umar b. al-Khattab wrote concerning her: \"Give her the choice: if she wishes, she may part from him; and if she wishes, she may remain with him.\""],[13645,"He has more right to her as long as he does not take her out of the land of her emigration."],[13648,"The women of the People of the Book are lawful for us, and our women are forbidden to them."],[13649,"There is no harm in marrying women of the People of the Book, but the women of the Christian Arabs are not to be married."],[13651,"Hudhayfah married a Jewish woman during the time of Umar. Umar said: \"Divorce her, for she is a live coal!\" He said: \"Is she forbidden?\" He said: \"No.\" So Hudhayfah did not divorce her because of his statement, until after that he divorced her."],[13655,"Talhah b. Ubayd Allah married the daughter of a Jewish leader. He said: \"Umar made him swear to divorce her.\""],[13656,"Talhah b. Ubayd Allah married a Jewish woman."],[13658,"There is no harm in marrying them."],[13675,"The affair of the Jewish woman and the Christian woman among them in Syria is like the affair of the free Muslim woman in divorce and waiting period, and in apportionment between them and the free Muslim woman."],[13676,"A woman from the People of the Book, her waiting period, her divorce, and her apportionment are like those of the Muslims."],[13683,"Regarding a Christian woman who is under a Christian man and she becomes Muslim before he has consummated the marriage with her, he said: \"She separates from him, and she gets no dower.\""],[13696,"ʿAlī used to dislike the slaughtered animals of the Christians of Banū Taghlib, and he said: \"They do not hold to Christianity except by drinking wine.\""],[13698,"ʿAlī used to dislike the slaughtered animals of the Christians of Banū Taghlib, and he said: \"They do not hold to Christianity except by drinking wine.\""],[13699,"I asked al-Zuhri about the slaughtered animals of the Christians of the Arabs, and he said: \"There is no harm in them. Whoever adopts a religion is of its people.\" He said: \"And their women may be married.\""],[13703,"Indeed what God has made lawful is their slaughter, \"and your Lord is never forgetful.\""],[13705,"Regarding marrying polytheist women outside a covenant: he disliked their women and permitted their slaughtered animals in the land of war."],[13774,"398 397 - ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Isrāʾīl, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, similar to it. Hidden text: A woman among the wives of the Prophet ﷺ bathed from janābah. The Prophet ﷺ came and performed wuḍūʾ from her leftover water. She said, 'I bathed from it.' He said, 'Water is not made impure by anything.'"],[14118,"A man said to 'Ata': Was it ever said: When a man passes by the mosque, let him pray two rak'ahs in it? He replied: I have not heard that about it, but it is good."],[14369,"When Ali stoned Shuraḥah al-Hamdaniyyah, her relatives came and said: What shall we do with her? He said to them: Do with her what you do with your dead, meaning washing her, praying over her, and the like."],[14513,"Nafi' b. al-Azraq disputed with Ibn al-'Abbas and said: Do you find the five prayers in the Quran? He said: Yes. Then he recited to him: “So glory be to Allah when you enter the evening and when you enter the morning”—that is Maghrib and Fajr—“and in the afternoon”—that is 'Asr—“and when you reach midday”—that is Zuhr. He said: “And after the 'Isha prayer.”"],[14961,"Her mistress sent her with jashish or rice as a gift to 'A'ishah. She brought it while 'A'ishah was praying, so she set it down. A cat came near and ate from it, while some women were with 'A'ishah. When she finished praying, she called for it, and when she saw the women avoiding the spot where the cat had eaten, 'A'ishah ؓ placed her hand on the spot where the cat had eaten and said, \"It is not impure.\""],[15039,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over the son of Mariyah the Copt, and he was sixteen months old."],[15827,"If Abu 'Amr makes me disappear and lowers me into my grave, then he is free."],[15891,"It reached Umar that his governors were taking wine as part of the jizya. He adjured them three times, and Bilal said: \"They do that.\" He said: \"Do not do that, but let them sell it themselves, for the Jews were forbidden fat, so they sold it and ate its price.\""],[15893,"I saw Umar turning his palms over and saying: \"May God fight Samurah! He was a worker of ours in Iraq. He mixed into the booty of the Muslims a price for us from wine and swine. For it is forbidden, and its price is forbidden.\""],[15894,"Regarding a Christian who advanced a loan to a Christian for wine, then one of them became Muslim, he said: \"He takes his capital. If one of them lends his companion wine and the lender becomes Muslim, he takes nothing. But if the borrower becomes Muslim, he returns to the Christian the price of the wine.\""],[15923,"Food from my father's land would remain with him for two or three years while he intended to sell it, but he would not pay zakat on it after the first zakat, waiting for prices to rise. Abd al-Razzaq said: '[He used] a word I do not like to say: waiting for prices to rise.'"],[15961,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of charities until they are taken possession of."],[16031,"He forbade two days of fasting, two sales, and two ways of dressing. As for the two days, they are the day of Fiṭr and the day of Aḍḥā. As for the two sales, they are mulāmasa and munābadha. Mulāmasa is that each of them touches the other's garment without examining it. As for munābadha, it is that each of them throws his garment to the other without either looking at the other's garment. As for the two ways of dressing, one is that a man wraps himself in a single garment with his private parts exposed. As for the other way of dressing, it is that he puts the inside of his izār and its outside over one of his shoulders and exposes his side.' ʿAmr said: 'They hold that if he wraps himself in a garment and covers his private parts, there is no harm.'"],[16426,"The son of adultery may marry, and his daughter may be given in marriage, his testimony is valid, and he may lead."],[16500,"Only two men are acceptable for the sighting of the crescent."],[16586,"He used to permit the testimony of the People of the Book, some of them against others."],[16588,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz permitted the testimony of a Magian against a Christian, or a Christian against a Magian."],[16589,"10303 10234 - Al-Thawri said, regarding a Christian who died, and a Muslim man came with two Christian witnesses that he was owed one thousand dirhams by him, and a Christian man came with Christian witnesses that he was owed one thousand dirhams by him. He said: 'It belongs to the Muslim, because the Christian's testimony harms the right of the Muslim.' Al-Thawri said: 'Disbelief is one religion, and Islam is one religion.'"],[16796,"If you have a nosebleed during prayer, pinch your nostril and pray as you are. If any blood comes out, perform ablution, then do not speak until you build upon what has preceded."],[17049,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage: “Allah has given every possessor of a right his right, so there is no bequest for an heir. The child belongs to the bed, and the stone belongs to the fornicator, and their reckoning is with Allah. Whoever claims descent from other than his father or allies himself with other than his patrons, upon him is the curse of Allah, continuing until the Day of Resurrection. And let no woman spend anything from his house except with her husband's permission.” It was said: “O Messenger of Allah, not even food?” He said: “That is the best of our wealth.” Then he said: “The borrowed item is to be returned, the gift (manīḥah) is to be given back, the debt is to be paid, and the guarantor is liable.”"],[17060,"Banu 'Amir had captured two men from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ captured a man from Thaqif, and they took a she-camel on which the pilgrims used to race. The Prophet ﷺ passed by him while he was bound, and he said, 'O Muhammad, O Muhammad!' So he turned to him, and he said, 'For what am I being held and the racing camel of the pilgrims taken?' He said, 'For the crime of your allies from Banu 'Amir.' Banu 'Amir were allies of Thaqif. Then the Prophet ﷺ went on, and he called him again, 'O Muhammad!' He answered him, and he said, 'I am a Muslim.' He said, 'If you had said that while you had control of your affair, you would have prospered completely.' He said, 'Then the Prophet ﷺ went on, and he called him again, so he returned to him. He said, 'Feed me, for I am hungry.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'This is your need.' He ordered food for him. Then the Prophet ﷺ ransomed the man for the two men who had been captured from his companions. He said, 'Some people raided an area of Madinah and seized a she-camel and also seized a woman, and they took them to their camp. The woman arose during part of the night to their camels, and they would let them rest near their courtyards. Every time she approached a camel to mount it, it bellowed, until she came to the she-camel of the Prophet ﷺ, which was a docile camel, and it did not bellow. She sat on its hindquarters, then urged it on. The people became aware of her and rode in pursuit of her. She made a vow while fleeing, with them following her: if Allah delivered her on it, she would certainly slaughter it. She escaped, and when she arrived in Madinah, the Prophet ﷺ was told, 'This is your she-camel; so-and-so came on it; Allah delivered her on it.' The Prophet ﷺ brought the woman and asked her, 'What did you do?' She told him: 'I vowed while they were pursuing me that if Allah delivered me on it, I would slaughter it.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'What a bad reward you would give it, then. There is no fulfillment of a vow in disobedience to Allah, nor in what the son of Adam does not own.'"],[17063,"Banu 'Amir had captured two men from the companions of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ captured a man from Thaqif, and they took a she-camel on which the pilgrims used to race. The Prophet ﷺ passed by him while he was bound, and he said, 'O Muhammad, O Muhammad!' So he turned to him, and he said, 'For what am I being held and the racing camel of the pilgrims taken?' He said, 'For the crime of your allies from Banu 'Amir.' Banu 'Amir were allies of Thaqif. Then the Prophet ﷺ went on, and he called him again, 'O Muhammad!' He answered him, and he said, 'I am a Muslim.' He said, 'If you had said that while you had control of your affair, you would have prospered completely.' He said, 'Then the Prophet ﷺ went on, and he called him again, so he returned to him. He said, 'Feed me, for I am hungry.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'This is your need.' He ordered food for him. Then the Prophet ﷺ ransomed the man for the two men who had been captured from his companions. He said, 'Some people raided an area of Madinah and seized a she-camel and also seized a woman, and they took them to their camp. The woman arose during part of the night to their camels, and they would let them rest near their courtyards. Every time she approached a camel to mount it, it bellowed, until she came to the she-camel of the Prophet ﷺ, which was a docile camel, and it did not bellow. She sat on its hindquarters, then urged it on. The people became aware of her and rode in pursuit of her. She made a vow while fleeing, with them following her: if Allah delivered her on it, she would certainly slaughter it. She escaped, and when she arrived in Madinah, the Prophet ﷺ was told, 'This is your she-camel; so-and-so came on it; Allah delivered her on it.' The Prophet ﷺ brought the woman and asked her, 'What did you do?' She told him: 'I vowed while they were pursuing me that if Allah delivered me on it, I would slaughter it.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'What a bad reward you would give it, then. There is no fulfillment of a vow in disobedience to Allah, nor in what the son of Adam does not own.'"],[17162,"As for me, when I have intercourse with my wife, I perform ghusl."],[17635,"I heard Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah mention that his mother died while she owed an i'tikaf. He said: I rushed with my brothers to Ibn Abbas and asked him. He said: Observe i'tikaf on her behalf and fast."],[17763,"When a person has a nosebleed while praying, he leaves, washes the blood off, then returns and completes the remainder based on what has preceded, as long as he has not spoken; and no ablution is required of him."],[17915,"Three things in which there is no jest: marriage, divorce, manumission, and charity."],[17938,"A woman came to him and said, 'Is it lawful for me to take from my husband's dirhams?' He said, 'Is it lawful for him to take from your jewelry?' She said, 'No.' He said, 'His right over you is greater.'"],[17940,"When a woman spends from her husband's food without being wasteful, she has her reward, and her husband has the like of that. Neither of them diminishes the other's reward at all. The treasurer has the like of that: for her what she spent, and for him what he earned."],[17941,"A woman was with ʿĀʾishah, and a woman asked her: “May a woman give charity from her husband's house?” She said: “Yes, as long as she does not protect her wealth with his wealth.”"],[18174,"His father freed a Magian slave of his, and he freed a child of fornication."],[18285,"I had forbidden you from visiting graves, but now visit them, for they remind of the Hereafter. I had forbidden you from nabīdh made in earthen jars, but now make nabīdh in any vessel and avoid every intoxicant. I had forbidden you from eating sacrificial meat after three days, but now eat it, take it as provision, and store it."],[18418,"Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to us: 'Do not let pigs be near you, and do not let a cross be raised among you, and do not eat at a table on which wine is drunk, and train the horses, and walk between the two targets.'"],[18437,"The one who has an enema with wine breaks his fast, but the legal punishment is not inflicted on him."],[18468,"Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine."],[18470,"There is no harm in the urine of that which has a stomach."],[18558,"When he prayed witr and there was still night remaining, he would sit and recite until morning."],[18650,"For the nose, the full diyah; for the glans, the full diyah; for the tongue, the full diyah; for the hand, half the diyah; for the foot, half the diyah; for the tooth, five camels; for the revealing wound, five camels; for the bone-displacing wound, fifteen camels; for the head wound, a third of the diyah; for the penetrating wound, a third of the diyah; for each finger, ten camels. For five camels, one sheep; for every ten, two sheep; for fifteen, three sheep; for every twenty, four sheep; for twenty-five, five sheep; for twenty-six, a bint makhad; if there is no bint makhad, then a male ibn labun, until thirty-five. If it increases by one, then a bint labun, until forty-five. If it increases by one, then a hiqqah mounted by the stallion, or he said: the camel, until sixty. If it increases by one, then a jadh'ah, until seventy-five. If it increases by one, then two bint labun, until ninety. If it increases by one, then two hiqqahs mounted by the stallion, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then for every fifty, a hiqqah, and for every forty, a bint labun. For cattle: for every thirty cattle, a tabi' that is one year old; for every forty cattle, a musinnah. For sheep: for every forty sheep, one sheep; nothing below forty, until one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then two sheep, up to two hundred. If it increases by one, then three sheep, up to three hundred. If it increases, then for every hundred, one sheep. An old one or one with a defect is not taken, unless the collector wishes. Separate herds are not combined, and combined herds are not separated. For what the sky and wells water, one-tenth; for what is watered by rope, half of one-tenth. For silver, when a year passes, for every two hundred dirhams, five dirhams; nothing below two hundred dirhams, and if it increases, then by that reckoning. I have exempted the zakat of horses and slaves."],[18828,"The Prophet ﷺ wrote a document for them, in which: for the nose, if it is cut off, one hundred camels; the penetrating wound is a third of the diyah; the head wound is the same; the eye is fifty; the hand is fifty; the foot is fifty; for each finger of the hands and feet, ten; the tooth is five; the revealing wound is five. For sheep: from forty to one hundred and twenty, one sheep; if it exceeds, up to two hundred, two sheep; if it exceeds two hundred, up to three hundred, three sheep; if it reaches more than that, count one sheep for every hundred. For camels: from twenty-five to thirty-five, a bint makhad; if no bint makhad is found among the camels, then a male ibn labun. From thirty-six to forty-five, a bint labun. From forty-six to sixty, a hiqqah. From more than that to seventy-five, a jadh'ah. From more than that to ninety, two bint labun. From more than that to one hundred and twenty, two hiqqahs. If more than that, count a hiqqah for every fifty, and for less than twenty-five, for every five, one sheep, not an old one or one with a defect. For cattle: for every thirty, a tabi'; for every forty, a musinnah."],[18833,"For the nose, the full diyah; for the glans, the full diyah; for the tongue, the full diyah; for the hand, half the diyah; for the foot, half the diyah; for the tooth, five camels; for the revealing wound, five camels; for the bone-displacing wound, fifteen camels; for the head wound, a third of the diyah; for the penetrating wound, a third of the diyah; for each finger, ten camels. For five camels, one sheep; for every ten, two sheep; for fifteen, three sheep; for every twenty, four sheep; for twenty-five, five sheep; for twenty-six, a bint makhad; if there is no bint makhad, then a male ibn labun, until thirty-five. If it increases by one, then a bint labun, until forty-five. If it increases by one, then a hiqqah mounted by the stallion, or he said: the camel, until sixty. If it increases by one, then a jadh'ah, until seventy-five. If it increases by one, then two bint labun, until ninety. If it increases by one, then two hiqqahs mounted by the stallion, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then for every fifty, a hiqqah, and for every forty, a bint labun. For cattle: for every thirty cattle, a tabi' that is one year old; for every forty cattle, a musinnah. For sheep: for every forty sheep, one sheep; nothing below forty, until one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then two sheep, up to two hundred. If it increases by one, then three sheep, up to three hundred. If it increases, then for every hundred, one sheep. An old one or one with a defect is not taken, unless the collector wishes. Separate herds are not combined, and combined herds are not separated. For what the sky and wells water, one-tenth; for what is watered by rope, half of one-tenth. For silver, when a year passes, for every two hundred dirhams, five dirhams; nothing below two hundred dirhams, and if it increases, then by that reckoning. I have exempted the zakat of horses and slaves."],[18967,"For the nose, the full diyah; for the glans, the full diyah; for the tongue, the full diyah; for the hand, half the diyah; for the foot, half the diyah; for the tooth, five camels; for the revealing wound, five camels; for the bone-displacing wound, fifteen camels; for the head wound, a third of the diyah; for the penetrating wound, a third of the diyah; for each finger, ten camels. For five camels, one sheep; for every ten, two sheep; for fifteen, three sheep; for every twenty, four sheep; for twenty-five, five sheep; for twenty-six, a bint makhad; if there is no bint makhad, then a male ibn labun, until thirty-five. If it increases by one, then a bint labun, until forty-five. If it increases by one, then a hiqqah mounted by the stallion, or he said: the camel, until sixty. If it increases by one, then a jadh'ah, until seventy-five. If it increases by one, then two bint labun, until ninety. If it increases by one, then two hiqqahs mounted by the stallion, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then for every fifty, a hiqqah, and for every forty, a bint labun. For cattle: for every thirty cattle, a tabi' that is one year old; for every forty cattle, a musinnah. For sheep: for every forty sheep, one sheep; nothing below forty, until one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then two sheep, up to two hundred. If it increases by one, then three sheep, up to three hundred. If it increases, then for every hundred, one sheep. An old one or one with a defect is not taken, unless the collector wishes. Separate herds are not combined, and combined herds are not separated. For what the sky and wells water, one-tenth; for what is watered by rope, half of one-tenth. For silver, when a year passes, for every two hundred dirhams, five dirhams; nothing below two hundred dirhams, and if it increases, then by that reckoning. I have exempted the zakat of horses and slaves."],[19027,"The Prophet ﷺ wrote a document for them, in which: for the nose, if it is cut off, one hundred camels; the penetrating wound is a third of the diyah; the head wound is the same; the eye is fifty; the hand is fifty; the foot is fifty; for each finger of the hands and feet, ten; the tooth is five; the revealing wound is five. For sheep: from forty to one hundred and twenty, one sheep; if it exceeds, up to two hundred, two sheep; if it exceeds two hundred, up to three hundred, three sheep; if it reaches more than that, count one sheep for every hundred. For camels: from twenty-five to thirty-five, a bint makhad; if no bint makhad is found among the camels, then a male ibn labun. From thirty-six to forty-five, a bint labun. From forty-six to sixty, a hiqqah. From more than that to seventy-five, a jadh'ah. From more than that to ninety, two bint labun. From more than that to one hundred and twenty, two hiqqahs. If more than that, count a hiqqah for every fifty, and for less than twenty-five, for every five, one sheep, not an old one or one with a defect. For cattle: for every thirty, a tabi'; for every forty, a musinnah."],[19028,"For the nose, the full diyah; for the glans, the full diyah; for the tongue, the full diyah; for the hand, half the diyah; for the foot, half the diyah; for the tooth, five camels; for the revealing wound, five camels; for the bone-displacing wound, fifteen camels; for the head wound, a third of the diyah; for the penetrating wound, a third of the diyah; for each finger, ten camels. For five camels, one sheep; for every ten, two sheep; for fifteen, three sheep; for every twenty, four sheep; for twenty-five, five sheep; for twenty-six, a bint makhad; if there is no bint makhad, then a male ibn labun, until thirty-five. If it increases by one, then a bint labun, until forty-five. If it increases by one, then a hiqqah mounted by the stallion, or he said: the camel, until sixty. If it increases by one, then a jadh'ah, until seventy-five. If it increases by one, then two bint labun, until ninety. If it increases by one, then two hiqqahs mounted by the stallion, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then for every fifty, a hiqqah, and for every forty, a bint labun. For cattle: for every thirty cattle, a tabi' that is one year old; for every forty cattle, a musinnah. For sheep: for every forty sheep, one sheep; nothing below forty, until one hundred and twenty. If it increases by one, then two sheep, up to two hundred. If it increases by one, then three sheep, up to three hundred. If it increases, then for every hundred, one sheep. An old one or one with a defect is not taken, unless the collector wishes. Separate herds are not combined, and combined herds are not separated. For what the sky and wells water, one-tenth; for what is watered by rope, half of one-tenth. For silver, when a year passes, for every two hundred dirhams, five dirhams; nothing below two hundred dirhams, and if it increases, then by that reckoning. I have exempted the zakat of horses and slaves."],[19591,"A man from the Jews killed a girl from the Ansar for her jewelry, then threw her into a well and smashed her head with stones. He was seized and brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who ordered that he be stoned until he died. So he was stoned until he died."],[19655,"If the body of a slain person is found in a house or a place, the funeral prayer is offered over it and blood-money is paid; but if a head or a leg is found, neither prayer is offered nor blood-money paid."],[19701,"It does not inherit until it cries out even if it moves. He said: \"If it sneezed, in my view it would be like crying out.\""],[19834,"The blood money for a woman from the People of the Book is four thousand dirhams. He said: I said: What about Arab Christians? He said: \"The same as them.\""],[19844,"I said to Ata: the blood money of a Magian! He said: \"Eight hundred dirhams.\""],[19848,"That ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz set the blood money for a Magian at half the blood money of a Muslim."],[19850,"That ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb set the blood money for a Magian at eight hundred dirhams."],[19853,"That a Muslim man killed a man from the people of the covenant deliberately, and it was brought to ʿUthmān, and he did not execute him, but he increased the blood money upon him, like the blood money of a Muslim."],[19860,"The blood money for a Jew, a Christian, and a Magian is the same as the blood money of a Muslim."],[19886,"A man from the Jews killed a girl from the Ansar for her jewelry, then threw her into a well and smashed her head with stones. He was seized and brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who ordered that he be stoned until he died. So he was stoned until he died."],[20015,"Ali killed a people who disbelieved after their Islam and burned them with fire. That reached Ibn Abbas, and he said: \"If it were I, I would have killed them and not burned them, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever changes—or he said: whoever reverts from—his religion, kill him, and do not torture with the punishment of Allah,' meaning fire.\" He said: \"Ibn Abbas's statement reached Ali, and he said: 'Woe to Ibn Abbas!'\""],[20028,"When Abu Bakr prepared—or he said: when Abu Bakr was ready—to fight the people of apostasy, he said to him: 'How will you fight the people, O Abu Bakr, when the Messenger of God ﷺ said: I was commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but God, and if they say it, they protect their blood and their wealth from me except by its right, and their reckoning is upon God?' Abu Bakr said: 'By God, I will fight whoever separates between prayer and zakāh, for zakāh is the right of wealth. By God, if they withheld from me a hobble-cord that they used to give to the Messenger of God ﷺ, I would fight them for it.' 'Umar said: 'By God, it was only that I saw that God had opened Abu Bakr's chest to fighting, and I knew that it was the truth.'"],[20056,"I heard Bajalah al-Tamimi narrating to Abu al-Shatha' and Amr b. Aws at the bench of Zamzam during the governorship of Mus'ab b. al-Zubayr. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj. Hidden text: I was a scribe for Jaz' b. Muawiyah, the paternal uncle of al-Ahnaf b. Qays. A letter came from Umar a year before his death: Kill every sorcerer, separate between every mahram among the Magians, and forbid them from zamzamah. He said: So we killed three female sorcerers. He said: Jaz' prepared much food and invited the Magians. They threw down the skewers they used for eating, a mule-load or two mule-loads of silver, and they ate without zamzamah. He said: Umar had not taken the jizyah from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman b. Awf testified that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it from the Magians of Hajar. Hidden isnad: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, saying: Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr b. Dinar, who said: I heard Bajalah al-Tamimi narrating from."],[20376,"Convey from me even a single verse, and narrate from the Children of Israel without restriction. Whoever tells a lie about me, let him take his seat in the Fire."],[20377,"The Jews used to narrate to the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and they would listen as if in amazement. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Do not believe them and do not disbelieve them, but say: 'We believe in what was sent down to us and what was sent down to you. Our God and your God is one, and to Him we submit.'\""],[20381,"How can you ask the People of the Book about anything when the Book of God that was sent down to you is among you pure and unadulterated? It is the most recent of reports from God. God has informed you about the People of the Book that they wrote books with their own hands and then said, 'This is from God,' to sell it for a small price. They altered it and distorted it from its proper places. Does not what has come to you from God forbid you from asking them? By God, we have not seen any of them asking you about what was sent down to you."],[20382,"A Jewish or Christian man goaded a Muslim woman, then threw dirt on her, wanting her for himself. This was brought to Umar b. al-Khattab, and Umar said: \"These people have a covenant as long as they fulfill their covenant with you. When they do not fulfill their covenant with you, they have no covenant.\" He said: So Umar crucified him."],[20384,"We visit the Christians even if there is no kinship between us and them."],[20385,"The Prophet ﷺ had a Jewish neighbor of good character, and he fell ill, so the Messenger of God ﷺ visited him with his companions. He said: “Do you bear witness that there is no god but God and that I am the Messenger of God?” He looked at his father, and his father remained silent, and the young man remained silent. Then a second time, then a third time. On the third time his father said: “Say what he says to you!” So he did, and then he died. The Jews wanted to take charge of him, but the Messenger of God ﷺ said: “We have a better right to him than you!” So the Prophet ﷺ washed him, shrouded him, embalmed him, and prayed over him."],[20387,"I heard Mujahid say to a Christian slave of his: 'O Jarir, become Muslim.' Then he said: 'This is how it was said to them.'"],[20388,"He saw the Prophet ﷺ pledging allegiance to the people on the day of the conquest. He said: He sat near Qarn Masqalah, and Qarn Masqalah is the one towards which the houses of Ibn Abi Umamah pour, and it is the house of Ibn Samurah and what is around it, and the one into which what recedes from it pours upon the house of Ibn Amir, and what approaches from it upon the house of Ibn Samurah and what is around it. Al-Aswad said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ sat by it, and the people came to him, young and old and women, and they pledged allegiance to him upon Islam and the testimony. I said: What is the testimony? He said: Muhammad b. al-Aswad informed me that he pledged allegiance to them upon faith in Allah and the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His messenger."],[20390,"He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'I have embraced Islam!' The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Cast off the hair of disbelief and get circumcised,' meaning: shave. Another narrator informed me from him that the Prophet ﷺ said to another man: 'Cast off the hair of disbelief and get circumcised.'"],[20391,"He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'I have embraced Islam!' The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Cast off the hair of disbelief and get circumcised,' meaning: shave. Another narrator informed me from him that the Prophet ﷺ said to another man: 'Cast off the hair of disbelief and get circumcised.'"],[20392,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ while I wanted to embrace Islam, so I embraced Islam. The Prophet ﷺ then ordered me to perform ghusl with water and lote-tree leaves, so I performed ghusl with water and lote-tree leaves."],[20395,"A hadith was narrated to me and traced back to Ali b. Abi Talib concerning a Jew or Christian who became a zindiq. He said: Leave him; he may move from one religion to another."],[20398,"It is from the sunna that churches in the amsar, old and new, be demolished."],[20406,"Regarding the People of the Book when they are brought before the judges of the Muslims, they (two authorities) said: 'If the governor wishes, he may judge between them, and if he wishes, he may turn away from them; but if he judges between them, he judges by what Allah has revealed.'"],[20409,"If a man from the People of the Book commits adultery with a Muslim woman or steals something from a Muslim, the hadd punishment is carried out on him, and the ruler does not turn away from that. He says: everything between the Muslims and the People of the Book, the ruler does not turn away from it."],[20415,"I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. When they say: There is no god but Allah, they have protected their blood and their property from me, except for its right, and their reckoning is with Allah."],[20416,"I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. When they do that, they have protected their blood and their property from me, except for its right, and their reckoning is with Allah."],[20417,"I asked ʿAṭāʾ: Are the Magians People of the Book? He said: No. I said: What about the Asbādhiyyūn? He said: \"The letter of the Prophet ﷺ to them was found, or so they claimed, after ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb wanted to take the jizya from them. When he found it, he left them alone. He said: They claimed that.\""],[20418,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb went out and passed by some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, among them ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf. He said: I do not know what to do about these people who are neither Arabs nor People of the Book—meaning the Magians. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"Treat them according to the practice of the People of the Book.\""],[20420,"I asked al-Zuhrī: Is jizya taken from those who are not People of the Book? He said: \"Yes. The Messenger of God ﷺ took it from the people of al-Baḥrayn, ʿUmar from the people of al-Sawād, and ʿUthmān from the Berbers.\""],[20423,"The people of al-Sawād had no covenant. When the kharāj was taken from them, they had a covenant."],[20425,"The Prophet of God ﷺ took the jizya from the Magians of Hajar, ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb took it from the Magians of al-Sawād, and ʿUthmān took it from the Berbers.\""],[20426,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb did not want to take the jizya from the Magians until ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. ʿAwf testified that the Messenger of God ﷺ took it from the Magians of Hajar."],[20429,"I asked ʿAtaʾ about the jizya. He said: \"We know nothing fixed except what they made peace upon; then they secured all their wealth.\" He said: \"Amr b. Dinar told me that.\""],[20432,"Umar imposed the jizya and wrote about that to the commanders of the armies: 'Do not impose the jizya except on those upon whom the razor has been used, and do not impose it on a child or a woman.' He imposed on the people of Iraq forty dirhams on every man, and he also imposed on the people of Iraq fifteen saa's. He imposed on the people of Syria four dinars on every man, and he also imposed on the people of Syria two mudds of wheat, three qists of oil, and such-and-such amount of honey and fat, which Ayyub or Nafi did not remember. He imposed on the people of Egypt four dinars on every man, and he also imposed on the people of Egypt an irdabb of wheat, and something he did not remember, and the clothing of the Commander of the Believers as a fixed levy. And they owe the hospitality of Muslims for three days: they feed them from what they eat, from what is lawful for Muslims from their food. When Umar came to Syria, they complained to him that they were burdening us with chickens. Umar said: 'Do not feed them except from what you eat, from what is lawful to them from your food.'"],[20436,"I said to Mujahid: \"Why is it that the People of the Book in Syria have four dinars taken from them in jizya, while the people of Yemen have one dinar?\" He said: \"That is because of ease.\""],[20437,"Perhaps you will fight a people and prevail over them, and they will shield themselves from you with their wealth rather than their own lives and their children, and make peace with you. So do not take from them anything besides that."],[20443,"The first to take half the tithe from the people of the covenant when they traded was 'Umar b. al-Khattab, and he used to take it from the merchants of the Nabataeans of Syria when they came to Madinah."],[20445,"Twice what is taken from the Muslims is taken from the People of the Book, from the people of gold and silver.” He said: “Umar b. al-Khattab and Umar b. Abd al-Aziz did that."],[20447,"I had land subject to its jizya, so my agent wrote about it to Umar b. Abd al-Aziz, and Umar wrote: \"Collect the jizya and the tithes, then take the surplus from him.\""],[20452,"I asked Ibn 'Umar, saying: \"What do you think about buying land?\" He said: \"It is fine.\" He said: \"They take from me from every jarib a qafiz and a dirham.\" He said: \"Do not put humiliation on your neck.\""],[20457,"Concerning an apostate: if he is killed, his wealth belongs to his heirs; and if he joins the territory of war, his wealth belongs to the Muslims. I do not know whether he added: \"unless he has an heir of the same religion in the territory, who is more entitled to it.\""],[20458,"The people of polytheism: we inherit from them, but they do not inherit from us."],[20464,"People are of two groups: among them are those who say the inheritance of the apostate belongs to the Muslims, because the moment he disbelieves, it is withheld from him, and he cannot dispose of any of it until it is seen whether he becomes Muslim or remains a disbeliever. Among them are al-Nakha'i, al-Sha'bi, and al-Hakam b. 'Utaybah. Another group says it belongs to the people of his religion."],[20467,"The people of two different religions do not inherit from each other."],[20471,"The people of two different religions do not inherit from each other."],[20473,"He heard Ikrimah when he was asked about a man who manumitted a Christian slave of his, then the slave died and left wealth, and he said: \"His inheritance belongs to the people of his religion.\""],[20475,"Abu Talib was inherited by 'Aqil and Talib, and 'Ali did not inherit anything from him. He said, \"Because of that we left our share of the mountain pass.\""],[20476,"A Muslim does not inherit from a disbeliever as long as he has a relative from the people of his religion. If he has no inheriting relative, then the Muslims inherit from him by virtue of Islam."],[20479,"If a Muslim dies leaving a child ... a Muslim and a disbeliever, and his inheritance is not divided until the disbeliever embraces Islam, he inherits together with the believer; they both inherit. But I did not like what he said. And someone said to me: That is the inheritance of the people of the Jahiliyyah. Whatever Islam came upon and was not divided is according to the division of Islam. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: No, the inheritances came into effect in Islam, and someone other than me said that."],[20487,"Regarding a Christian who dies while his wife is pregnant, then she embraces Islam before giving birth, then she gives birth and dies. He said: \"Their child inherits from both of them together, because he acquired the inheritance of his father when his father died, and then his mother died, so he followed her in her religion and inherited from her.\""],[20489,"Concerning a Jewish household whose father died and whose inheritance had not been divided until they embraced Islam: 'It is not according to the division of Islam; the inheritances came into effect before they embraced Islam.'"],[20490,"When a man dies leaving his son a slave, and the son is manumitted before the inheritance is divided, he receives nothing."],[20494,"When a man dies leaving his son a slave, and the son is manumitted before the inheritance is divided, he receives nothing."],[20495,"If a Magian marries his daughter and she bears him two daughters, then he dies, and then they embrace Islam, and one of his daughters dies—her full sister receives half, and her mother receives one-sixth; she is excluded by herself because she is the sister of her daughter, and her remaining daughter, who is her daughter's sister, also excludes her. Then the mother also receives what the paternal sister would receive.\" And al-Thawri said something similar to their statement: \"Her full sister receives half, and the paternal sister receives one-sixth to complete the two-thirds, and she also receives one-sixth because she is the mother who excludes herself; and because she is a sister, she receives one-third.\" Al-Thawri said: \"This is the statement of Ibrahim: 'They inherit from two capacities.'\""],[20496,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi b. Artat, asking al-Hasan: Why were the Magians left to marry mothers and sisters? So he asked him, and he said: “The polytheism they are upon is greater than that, and they were only left to it for the sake of the jizya.”"],[20499,"Regarding a Magian who marries his sister, and she bears him a daughter, then they embrace Islam, and then he dies—he said: \"His daughter inherits half, and half goes to his sister, because she is an 'asaba.\" And he said regarding a Magian who marries his mother, and she bears him two daughters, then they embrace Islam, and the man dies: \"His two daughters receive two-thirds, and his mother receives one-sixth. Then one of the daughters dies; her sister inherits half, and the mother became both mother and grandmother, so she excludes herself, and we give her the inheritance of a mother, and we do not give her the inheritance of a grandmother.\" And he says: \"When they embraced Islam, the marriage was dissolved for him, so it is not appropriate for him to remain married to his mother or his sister after Islam; we give him inheritance on the basis of kinship.\""],[20500,"I said to ʿAṭāʾ: What is the meaning of His saying: “unless you do a kindness to your allies”? He said: “Giving.” I said to him: “Is it a believer giving to a disbeliever between whom there is kinship?” He said: “Yes, his giving to him while alive and his bequest to him.”"],[20501,"Regarding His saying: “unless you do a kindness to your allies,” he said: “Unless you have a relative who is not of your religion, and you bequeath something to him. He is your ally in lineage, but not your ally in religion.” He said: “And al-Ḥasan said the like of it.”"],[20502,"My mother came while she was a polytheist during the treaty of Quraysh, when they made a pact with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and during their period, along with her father. I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for a ruling: “O Messenger of Allah, my mother has come and she is desirous [of good relations]; should I maintain ties with her?” He said: “Yes, maintain ties with your mother.”"],[20503,"The bequest of a Muslim to a Christian is valid."],[20505,"The bequest of a Muslim to a Christian is valid."],[20508,"Ibn Shihab was asked about a Christian who had under him a Christian slave-girl of his, and she bore him a child, then she became Muslim. He said: Islam separates them, and she and his child are freed. Ibn Jurayj said: But I say: She is not freed until her master is summoned to Islam; if he refuses to become Muslim, she is freed, and if he becomes Muslim, she remains his slave-girl."],[20510,"Umar b. Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning the slaves of the dhimmis who become Muslims: He orders that they be sold. Al-Thawri said: And that is what we say: They are to be sold."],[20512,"A Christian manumitted a Muslim, and Umar b. Abd al-Aziz said: Give him his value from the public treasury, and his wala' belongs to the Muslims."],[20513,"He was asked about Muslim merchants who enter the lands of the non-Arabs and some of them enslave others: is it permissible for him to buy them while he knows? He said: Yes."],[20515,"Ibn Shihab said concerning a man from the People of the Book who bought a Muslim slave woman secretly, and she bore him children: He is punished and she is taken away from him."],[20517,"Regarding this verse: “The polytheists are impure, so let them not come near the Sacred Mosque,” except that one be a slave or one of the people of the jizya."],[20519,"Two religions shall not meet in the land of the Arabs—or he said: in the land of the Hijaz. He said: Umar investigated that until he found clear evidence for it. Al-Zuhri said: For that reason Umar expelled them. Al-Zuhri said: Umar would not allow the protected people to stay in Medina more than three days if they wanted to sell food, and the women of the Jews and Christians were commanded to veil and adorn themselves."],[20520,"Umar would not allow a Christian, a Jew, or a Magian, if they entered Medina, to remain there more than three days, enough time to sell their goods. When Umar was struck, he said: “I had commanded you that none of them should enter upon us, and if the one struck were other than me, he would have authority in it.” He said: “And it was said: Two religions shall not meet there.”"],[20521,"When Umar was stabbed, he sent to the people among the Muhajirun, including Ali, and said: Was this from a group of you? Ali said: “God forbid that it would be from a group of us, and if we could add from our lifespans to your lifespan, we would do so!” He said: “I had forbidden you that any of them should enter upon us.”"],[20524,"The Jews of Banu al-Nadir and Qurayzah fought the Messenger of God ﷺ, so the Messenger of God ﷺ expelled Banu al-Nadir and allowed Qurayzah to remain, showing them favor, until Qurayzah fought after that. Then he killed their men and divided their women, children, and property among the Muslims, except some of them who joined the Messenger of God ﷺ, so he granted them safety and they became Muslims. The Messenger of God ﷺ expelled all the Jews of Medina: Banu Qaynuqa, who are the people of Abdullah b. Salam, the Jews of Banu Harithah, and every Jew who was in Medina."],[20527,"Two religions shall not meet in the land of the Arabs—or he said: in the land of the Hijaz. He said: Umar investigated that until he found clear evidence for it. Al-Zuhri said: For that reason Umar expelled them. Al-Zuhri said: Umar would not allow the protected people to stay in Medina more than three days if they wanted to sell food, and the women of the Jews and Christians were commanded to veil and adorn themselves."],[20529,"The Prophet ﷺ handed over Khaybar to the Jews on condition that they work it and have half of its fruit. The Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and the early part of Umar's caliphate continued on that basis. Then Umar was informed that the Prophet ﷺ said during the illness of which he died: “Two religions shall not coexist in the land of the Arabs,” or he said: “in the land of the Hijaz.” So he investigated that until he found confirmation of it, then he summoned them and said: “Whoever has a covenant from the Messenger of God ﷺ, let him bring it; otherwise, I will expel you!” So Umar expelled them."],[20531,"Ibn Abbas said to me: “Thursday—and what is Thursday?” Then he wept until his tears wet the pebbles. I said: “O Abu al-Abbas, what is Thursday?” He said: “The day on which the Messenger of God's ﷺ illness became severe. He said: ‘Bring me [writing materials] so that I may write for you a document after which you will never go astray.’ They disputed, and it is not proper to dispute in the presence of a prophet. They said: ‘What is wrong with him? Is he delirious? Ask him for clarification.’ He said: ‘Leave me, for what I am in is better than what you are calling me to.’ And he gave three instructions at his death, saying: ‘Expel the polytheists from the Arabian Peninsula, and honor delegations as I used to honor them.’” He said: “Either Sa'id intentionally omitted the third, or he mentioned it and I forgot it.”"],[20532,"The Prophet ﷺ instructed at his death: “Let no Jew or Christian be left in the land of the Hijaz, and let Usama's army be dispatched to Syria, and treat the Copts well, for they have kinship.”"],[20533,"If you are given authority after me, expel the people of Najran from the Arabian Peninsula."],[20534,"The Jews and Christians shall not share in your cities unless they embrace Islam. Whoever among them apostatizes and refuses, nothing will be accepted from him except his blood."],[20540,"A Jew or a Christian goaded a Muslim woman and she fell, so Umar struck his neck and said: \"We did not make peace with you for this.\""],[20544,"Whoever steals wine from the People of the Book is to have his hand cut off."],[20546,"When a Christian slave becomes Muslim, he is compelled to sell him."],[20552,"I heard Bajalah al-Tamimi narrating to Abu al-Shatha' and Amr b. Aws at the bench of Zamzam during the governorship of Mus'ab b. al-Zubayr. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj. Hidden text: I was a scribe for Jaz' b. Muawiyah, the paternal uncle of al-Ahnaf b. Qays. A letter came from Umar a year before his death: Kill every sorcerer, separate between every mahram among the Magians, and forbid them from zamzamah. He said: So we killed three female sorcerers. He said: Jaz' prepared much food and invited the Magians. They threw down the skewers they used for eating, a mule-load or two mule-loads of silver, and they ate without zamzamah. He said: Umar had not taken the jizyah from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman b. Awf testified that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it from the Magians of Hajar. Hidden isnad: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, saying: Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr b. Dinar, who said: I heard Bajalah al-Tamimi narrating from."],[20553,"A man from Taghlib came to Umar, and Umar said to him: You had a share in the Jahiliyyah, so take your share from Islam. He made a peace agreement with him on condition that he double the jizyah upon them and that they would not Christianize the children."],[20554,"I witnessed the Messenger of God ﷺ when he made peace with the Christians of Banu Taghlib on the condition that they would not make their children Christian. If they did, they would have no covenant.” He said: And Ali said: “If I were free, I would fight them.”"],[20557,"It reached Umar that his governors were taking wine as part of the jizya. He adjured them three times, and Bilal said: \"They do that.\" He said: \"Do not do that, but let them sell it themselves, for the Jews were forbidden fat, so they sold it and ate its price.\""],[20559,"The first collector of tithes in Islam was I, and we did not tithe a Muslim or one with a treaty. He said: \"I said: 'Then whom did you tithe?'\" He said: \"The Christians of Banu Taghlib.\" Ibrahim said: \"Then a man narrated to me from Ziyad, saying: 'I asked him: How much did you tithe them?' He said: 'Half the tithe.'\""],[20569,"He saw Abdullah b. Muhayriz shaking hands with a Christian man in Damascus."],[20570,"They used to dislike eating with Jews and Christians, and shaking hands with them."],[20571,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the kunya to Safwan b. Umayya, while he was at that time a polytheist. He came to him on a horse, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Dismount, Abu Wahb.'"],[20625,"When you pass by a gathering in which there are Muslims and disbelievers, greet them."],[20771,"Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas and A'ishah saw no harm in it, and they would have cupping done while fasting."],[20821,"O Allah, I ask You for martyrdom in Your path in the city of Your Messenger ﷺ."],[20823,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say when he said the taslim: 'There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise. O Allah, none can withhold what You give, and none can give what You withhold, and no possessor of fortune can benefit from You his fortune.' Warrad said, 'Then afterwards I came as a delegate to Muʿawiyah, and I heard him on the minbar commanding the people to say that and teaching them.'"],[20936,"Al-Bara' b. Malik lay on his back and began to hum. Anas said to him: 'Remember Allah, my brother.' He sat up straight and said: 'O Anas, do you think I will die on my bed when I have killed a hundred of the polytheists in single combat, besides those I shared in killing?'"],[21053,"You slaughter animals that are not lawful; you rush upon the slaughter animal. Umar said: 'We are more entitled to guard against that, Abu Hayyan. The slaughter is in the throat and the upper chest for whoever is able. Leave the souls until they depart.'"],[21171,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade me from reciting while bowing and prostrating, from wearing a gold ring, from wearing qassī clothing, and from wearing garments dyed with safflower. I asked him: 'What is qassī?' He said: 'Silk.'"],[21175,"The last prayer the Messenger of God ﷺ prayed was in a rose-colored sheet, wrapping himself in it."],[21447,"The gates of heaven are opened every Monday and Thursday, and Allah forgives every servant who does not associate anything with Allah except those who bear mutual grudges. The angels say: \"Leave them until they reconcile.\""],[21505,"Indeed, Allah laughs at you when you despair of the rain being near. A man from Bahilah said, 'O Messenger of Allah, does our Lord indeed laugh?' He said, 'Yes.' The man said, 'By Allah, we will not be deprived of good from a Lord who laughs.'"],[21516,"'Ali called for water for ablution, and it was brought near to him. He washed his palms three times before putting them into his ablution water, then rinsed his mouth three times, sniffed water three times, then washed his face three times, then washed his right hand up to the elbow three times, then the left likewise, then wiped his head with one wipe, then washed his right foot up to the ankles three times, then the left likewise. Then he stood upright and said to me: 'Hand it to me.' So I handed him the vessel containing the leftover water of his ablution, and he drank from the leftover water of his ablution standing. I was astonished, and when he saw my astonishment, he said: 'Do not be astonished, for I saw your father, the Prophet ﷺ, do the same as you have seen me do,' meaning with this ablution of his and his drinking of the leftover water of his ablution standing."],[21569,"When the Prophet ﷺ saw the crescent moon, he would say: “I believe in the One who created you, fashioned you, and proportioned you.”"],[21921,"The Prophet ﷺ used to take upon those who entered Islam, saying: \"You establish the prayer, give the zakah, perform Hajj to the House, fast Ramadan, and that you do not see the fire of a polytheist except that you are at war with him.\""],[21922,"The Prophet ﷺ used to take the pledge from them, and he would say: 'I do not shake hands with women.'"],[22166,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ went out on a journey, he would say: “O God, I seek refuge in You from the toil of travel, the gloom of return, from loss after gain, and from an evil sight in family and property.”"],[22167,"Travel; you will be healthy and provided for."],[22171,"The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ said: “O Messenger of God, a man whom we used to consider righteous falls ill and is made to suffer at his death, and a man in whom we used to see no good falls ill and is made to have it easy at his death.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “The believer has some of his sins remaining, so he is made to suffer at his death so that he may meet God with no sin; and the hypocrite has some of his good deeds remaining, so it is made easy for him so that he may meet God with no good deed.”"],[22258,"The Prophet ﷺ took allegiance from a group, and I was among them, and he recited to them the verse of the women: 'That you do not associate anything with Allah...' the verse. Then he said: 'Whoever fulfills it, his reward is with Allah. Whoever commits any of that and is punished for it in this world, it is purification and expiation for him. Whoever commits any of that and Allah conceals him, his affair is up to Allah; if He wills, He forgives him, and if He wills, He punishes him.'"],[22259,"Fatimah bt. Utbah b. Rabiah came to pledge allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, and he took upon her not to associate anything with Allah—the verse. She said: She placed her hand on her head out of modesty, and what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw of her pleased him. Aishah said: Accept, O woman, for by Allah he did not pledge allegiance to us except upon this. She said: Then yes, so he pledged allegiance to her upon the verse."]]);
